window.BOOK = {"layers":[{"id":"language","name":"C# language","note":"the code you write"},{"id":"runtime","name":"Runtime & platform","note":"CLR, BCL, SDK, tooling"},{"id":"framework","name":"Application frameworks","note":"ASP.NET Core, EF Core, hosting"},{"id":"arch","name":"Application architecture","note":"how you organise the code"},{"id":"data","name":"Data & state","note":"databases, caches, storage"},{"id":"comms","name":"Service communication","note":"HTTP, messaging, events"},{"id":"infra","name":"Hosting & infrastructure","note":"containers, cloud, pipelines"},{"id":"cross","name":"Cross-cutting concerns","note":"testing, security, observability, performance"}],"parts":[{"id":"p0","num":"0","title":"Orientation","chapters":["big-picture"]},{"id":"p1","num":"I","title":"The .NET Platform","chapters":["what-is-dotnet","how-code-runs","projects-sdk-nuget"]},{"id":"p2","num":"II","title":"The C# Language","chapters":["type-system","oop","generics","delegates-events","collections","linq","exceptions-disposal","async-await","modern-csharp","advanced-csharp"]},{"id":"p3","num":"III","title":"ASP.NET Core","chapters":["aspnet-hosting","dependency-injection","configuration-options","middleware","routing","building-apis","errors-logging","auth","http-client","caching-ratelimit-cors","background-work","api-design","app-models"]},{"id":"p4","num":"IV","title":"Data Access","chapters":["relational-fundamentals","ado-dapper","efcore-fundamentals","efcore-querying","efcore-advanced","nosql-redis"]},{"id":"p5","num":"V","title":"Architecture & Design","chapters":["design-principles","design-patterns","app-architectures","cqrs-mediator-result","ddd","microservices"]},{"id":"p6","num":"VI","title":"Testing","chapters":["unit-testing","integration-testing"]},{"id":"p7","num":"VII","title":"Distributed Systems","chapters":["messaging-events","resilience","observability-aspire"]},{"id":"p8","num":"VIII","title":"Cloud & DevOps","chapters":["cloud-azure-overview","docker","azure-compute","azure-data-messaging","azure-identity-secrets","cicd-iac","kubernetes"]},{"id":"p9","num":"IX","title":"Performance & Internals","chapters":["memory-gc","threading-concurrency","performance-engineering"]},{"id":"p10","num":"X","title":"Security","chapters":["web-security","oauth-oidc-identity"]},{"id":"p11","num":"XI","title":"Interview Preparation","chapters":["system-design","study-plan-resources"]}],"chapters":{"big-picture":{"id":"big-picture","title":"The Big Picture: How Everything in .NET Fits Together","summary":"Before any detail, the whole map. What the pieces of the .NET world are, which layer each belongs to, and how a single HTTP request touches almost all of them.","layer":"runtime","where":"Orientation › The whole map","fits":"<p>This chapter sits <em>above</em> every other chapter. Come back to it whenever a new term shows up and you can&#39;t tell what it is or what it is next to.</p>\n","prereqs":[],"next":["what-is-dotnet","aspnet-hosting"],"levels":["b"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/big-picture/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>.NET is organised in <strong>8 layers</strong>, each built on the one below: Language → Runtime → Frameworks → Architecture → Data → Service comms → Hosting/infra → Cross-cutting.</li>\n<li>Most confusion comes from one English word (&quot;service&quot;, &quot;context&quot;, &quot;scope&quot;, &quot;handler&quot;) meaning something different at each layer — know the layer, know the meaning.</li>\n<li>A single HTTP request touches almost every layer on its way in and back out; that end-to-end trip is the backbone of the &quot;walk me through a request&quot; interview question.</li>\n<li>Microsoft reuses a few product-name families (the platform family, the web family, the data family, the <code>Microsoft.Extensions.*</code> glue, the cloud family) — recognising the family tells you what something is for even if you&#39;ve never seen its exact name.</li>\n<li>Read Beginner sections everywhere first for a shallow full map, then go deeper where interviews actually probe (C#/async, DI, middleware, EF Core, SOLID, REST, auth, testing, cloud).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"why-net-feels-like-a-maze\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/big-picture/why-net-feels-like-a-maze\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Why .NET feels like a maze <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>You feel lost because you learned the pieces one by one, from tutorials that each assume you already know the other pieces. &quot;Add a scoped service&quot;, &quot;register the middleware&quot;, &quot;inject <code>IOptions&lt;T&gt;</code>&quot;, &quot;deploy to Container Apps&quot;: each sentence comes from a different layer of the system, and nobody showed you the layers.</p>\n<p>The fix is a <strong>map with layers</strong>. Every concept in .NET belongs to exactly one layer, and each layer only builds on the layers beneath it. Once you know the layer, you know:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>what it depends on</strong> (the layers below it),</li>\n<li><strong>what depends on it</strong> (the layers above it),</li>\n<li><strong>what it is <em>not</em></strong> (a middleware is not a design pattern; a DI lifetime is not a cloud setting).</li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout c-analogy\"><div class=\"callout-title\">A building</div><p>Think of a backend system as a building. <strong>C#</strong> is the bricks. <strong>The runtime</strong> (CLR) is the foundation and the plumbing: water (memory) and electricity (threads) are supplied without you thinking about them. <strong>ASP.NET Core and EF Core</strong> are pre-built floors you move into. <strong>Architecture</strong> is how you arrange the rooms. <strong>Data</strong> is the storage in the basement. <strong>Communication</strong> is the corridors and lifts between buildings. <strong>Infrastructure</strong> is the land, the city and the roads (the cloud). <strong>Cross-cutting concerns</strong> are the building codes (security, fire inspections and maintenance) that apply to every floor.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"the-eight-layers\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/big-picture/the-eight-layers\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The eight layers <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>This is the map the whole book uses. Every chapter opens with this stack and highlights the layer you are in.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ 8  CROSS-CUTTING        Testing · Security · Observability · Performance     │  ← applies to every layer\n├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 7  HOSTING &amp; INFRA      Docker · Azure App Service/Functions/Container Apps  │  where it runs\n│                         Kubernetes · CI/CD · Infrastructure as Code          │\n├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 6  SERVICE COMMS        REST/HTTP · gRPC · SignalR · Message brokers         │  how services talk\n│                         Events · Outbox · Sagas · Resilience (Polly)         │\n├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 5  DATA &amp; STATE         SQL Server/Postgres · EF Core · Dapper · Redis       │  where state lives\n│                         Cosmos DB · Blob storage · Caching                   │\n├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 4  ARCHITECTURE         SOLID · Design patterns · Clean/Onion · Vertical     │  how code is organised\n│                         slices · CQRS · DDD · Microservices                  │\n├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 3  FRAMEWORKS           ASP.NET Core: hosting, DI, config, middleware,       │  pre-built machinery\n│                         routing, APIs, auth · EF Core · Worker services      │\n├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 2  RUNTIME &amp; PLATFORM   CLR · JIT · GC · Base Class Library · SDK · NuGet    │  what executes code\n├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ 1  LANGUAGE             C#: types, OOP, generics, LINQ, async/await …        │  what you write\n└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Layer</th>\n<th>Question it answers</th>\n<th>Typical interview question</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Language</td>\n<td><em>How do I express logic?</em></td>\n<td>&quot;Struct vs class?&quot;, &quot;What does <code>await</code> do?&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Runtime &amp; platform</td>\n<td><em>What runs my code and manages memory?</em></td>\n<td>&quot;What is the JIT?&quot;, &quot;How does the GC work?&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Frameworks</td>\n<td><em>What machinery handles HTTP, DI, config, DB mapping?</em></td>\n<td>&quot;Explain middleware order&quot;, &quot;Scoped vs singleton?&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Architecture</td>\n<td><em>How should I organise my own code?</em></td>\n<td>&quot;Explain Clean Architecture&quot;, &quot;When CQRS?&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Data &amp; state</td>\n<td><em>Where does data live and how do I get it efficiently?</em></td>\n<td>&quot;N+1 problem?&quot;, &quot;Clustered index?&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Service communication</td>\n<td><em>How do separate processes talk reliably?</em></td>\n<td>&quot;Outbox pattern?&quot;, &quot;Circuit breaker?&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Hosting &amp; infrastructure</td>\n<td><em>Where does it run and how does it get there?</em></td>\n<td>&quot;App Service vs AKS?&quot;, &quot;Multi-stage Dockerfile?&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Cross-cutting</td>\n<td><em>Is it correct, secure, visible and fast?</em></td>\n<td>&quot;How do you test this?&quot;, &quot;How do you find a slow endpoint?&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-tip\"><div class=\"callout-title\">How to use the map when you&#39;re lost</div><p>When you meet a new term, ask &quot;which layer?&quot; first. <code>IOptionsMonitor</code> is layer 3 (an ASP.NET Core/hosting feature). <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> is layer 1 and 2 (a C# type that the runtime treats specially). &quot;Saga&quot; is layer 6. &quot;Managed identity&quot; is layer 7, and it shows up in layer 5 when you connect to a database without a password. Once you know where something lives, it stops being random.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"the-same-thing-at-different-layers\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/big-picture/the-same-thing-at-different-layers\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The same thing at different layers <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>A lot of confusion comes from one word meaning different things on different layers, or one idea showing up at several layers. Here are the most common:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Word</th>\n<th>Layer 1–2 meaning</th>\n<th>Layer 3 meaning</th>\n<th>Layer 4+ meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Pipeline</strong></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>The middleware pipeline (request → middleware → endpoint)</td>\n<td>CI/CD pipeline (build → test → deploy); MediatR &quot;pipeline behaviours&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Host</strong></td>\n<td>The process that loads the CLR</td>\n<td>The <strong>Generic Host</strong>: object that owns DI, config, logging, lifetime</td>\n<td>The machine/container/App Service that runs your app</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Service</strong></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>A class registered in the DI container</td>\n<td>A deployable application (&quot;the Orders service&quot;)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Handler</strong></td>\n<td>An event handler (delegate)</td>\n<td>Route handler, authorization handler, <code>DelegatingHandler</code></td>\n<td>Command/query handler in CQRS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Filter</strong></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>MVC/endpoint filters around an action</td>\n<td>EF Core global query filters (data layer)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Context</strong></td>\n<td><code>SynchronizationContext</code>, <code>ExecutionContext</code></td>\n<td><code>HttpContext</code> (one request)</td>\n<td><code>DbContext</code> (one unit of work), DDD &quot;bounded context&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Scope</strong></td>\n<td>Variable scope</td>\n<td>DI scope (= one HTTP request)</td>\n<td>Logging scope, transaction scope</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Repository</strong></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Data-access pattern (layer 4/5); also &quot;Git repository&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Event</strong></td>\n<td>C# <code>event</code> keyword</td>\n<td><code>IHostApplicationLifetime</code> events</td>\n<td>Domain events, integration events, Event Grid</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">The single biggest mix-up</div><p>&quot;Service&quot; in <strong><code>builder.Services.AddScoped&lt;IOrderService, OrderService&gt;()</code></strong> means &quot;a class the DI container can create&quot;. &quot;Service&quot; in <strong>&quot;microservices&quot;</strong> means &quot;a separately deployed application&quot;. An app with 200 DI services can still be one monolith.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"follow-one-request-through-every-layer\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/big-picture/follow-one-request-through-every-layer\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Follow one request through every layer <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>This trace touches nearly every chapter in the book, so it&#39;s the best way to see how the pieces connect. Imagine a mobile app calls:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>HTTP</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>POST <span class=\"tk-ty\">https</span>:<span class=\"tk-com\">//api.shop.com/orders</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Authorization</span>: Bearer eyJhbGciOi...\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Content-Type</span>: application/json\n\n{ <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;customerId&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;lines&quot;</span>: [ { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;sku&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;KB-01&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;qty&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> } ] }</code></pre></figure>\n<h3 id=\"1-infrastructure-the-request-finds-your-code-layer-7\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/big-picture/1-infrastructure-the-request-finds-your-code-layer-7\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>1. Infrastructure: the request finds your code (layer 7)</h3>\n<p>DNS points <code>api.shop.com</code> at an <strong>Azure Front Door</strong> or <strong>Application Gateway</strong>. It ends the TLS connection and forwards the request to one of several running instances of your app. Those instances run in <strong>Azure Container Apps</strong>, as a container built from your <strong>Dockerfile</strong> and deployed by your <strong>CI/CD pipeline</strong>. There are several instances because the app is <strong>stateless</strong> and <strong>scaled out</strong>: any instance can handle any request. → <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cloud-azure-overview\">Cloud &amp; Azure overview</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/docker\">Docker</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-compute\">Azure compute</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-runtime-a-process-is-already-running-layer-2\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/big-picture/2-runtime-a-process-is-already-running-layer-2\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>2. Runtime: a process is already running (layer 2)</h3>\n<p>Inside the container, <code>dotnet Shop.Api.dll</code> started earlier. The <strong>CLR</strong> loaded your assemblies. The <strong>JIT</strong> compiled each method to machine code the first time it ran. The <strong>GC</strong> manages every object you allocate, and the <strong>thread pool</strong> supplies the threads that do the work. → <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/how-code-runs\">How code runs</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/memory-gc\">Memory &amp; GC</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-framework-kestrel-and-the-host-layer-3\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/big-picture/3-framework-kestrel-and-the-host-layer-3\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>3. Framework: Kestrel and the host (layer 3)</h3>\n<p><strong>Kestrel</strong>, ASP.NET Core&#39;s web server, reads the bytes off the socket and turns them into an <strong><code>HttpContext</code></strong>. The <strong>Generic Host</strong> that <code>Program.cs</code> built owns the <strong>DI container</strong>, <strong>configuration</strong> and <strong>logging</strong> for the whole app. For this one request, a new <strong>DI scope</strong> is created. → <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting\">Hosting</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/dependency-injection\">Dependency injection</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/configuration-options\">Configuration</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"4-framework-the-middleware-pipeline-layer-3\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/big-picture/4-framework-the-middleware-pipeline-layer-3\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>4. Framework: the middleware pipeline (layer 3)</h3>\n<p>The request walks through middleware, in the order you registered it:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>ExceptionHandler → HTTPS redirection → Routing → CORS → Authentication → Authorization\n→ Rate limiting → (your custom middleware) → Endpoint</code></pre></figure>\n<p><strong>Authentication</strong> checks the JWT — its signature, issuer, audience and expiry — and fills in <code>HttpContext.User</code>. <strong>Authorization</strong> then checks the endpoint&#39;s policy, for example <code>RequireAuthorization(&quot;CanOrder&quot;)</code>. → <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/middleware\">Middleware</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/routing\">Routing</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/auth\">Auth</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/oauth-oidc-identity\">OAuth &amp; OIDC</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"5-framework-binding-validation-your-endpoint-layers-1-3\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/big-picture/5-framework-binding-validation-your-endpoint-layers-1-3\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>5. Framework: binding, validation, your endpoint (layers 1 + 3)</h3>\n<p><strong>Model binding</strong> turns the request JSON into a <code>PlaceOrderRequest</code> record — an ordinary C# type (layer 1). <strong>Validation</strong> rejects bad input with a <code>400 ProblemDetails</code> response. If it passes, your <strong>minimal API handler</strong> or <strong>controller action</strong> runs, with its dependencies already injected. → <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/building-apis\">Building APIs</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/errors-logging\">Errors &amp; logging</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"6-architecture-your-codes-shape-layer-4\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/big-picture/6-architecture-your-codes-shape-layer-4\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>6. Architecture: your code&#39;s shape (layer 4)</h3>\n<p>The handler doesn&#39;t talk to SQL directly. It sends a <code>PlaceOrder</code> <strong>command</strong> to a <strong>handler</strong> (this is CQRS). That handler loads a <code>Customer</code> <strong>aggregate</strong> and calls <code>Order.Create(...)</code>, a <strong>DDD</strong> rich model that enforces rules like &quot;an order needs at least one line&quot;. It returns a <strong>Result</strong> instead of throwing on failure. The project is structured as Domain / Application / Infrastructure / API, which is <strong>Clean Architecture</strong>, and its interfaces follow <strong>SOLID</strong>. → <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/design-principles\">Design principles</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/app-architectures\">App architectures</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cqrs-mediator-result\">CQRS</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ddd\">DDD</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"7-language-async-all-the-way-down-layer-1\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/big-picture/7-language-async-all-the-way-down-layer-1\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>7. Language: async all the way down (layer 1)</h3>\n<p>Every I/O call is <code>await</code>ed with a <code>CancellationToken</code>. While the database does its work, the thread goes back to the pool and serves other requests instead of sitting idle. → <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/async-await\">async/await</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"8-data-ef-core-and-sql-layer-5\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/big-picture/8-data-ef-core-and-sql-layer-5\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>8. Data: EF Core and SQL (layer 5)</h3>\n<p><strong>EF Core</strong> turns LINQ into SQL. The <strong><code>DbContext</code></strong>, scoped to this one request, tracks the new <code>Order</code>, and <code>SaveChangesAsync</code> writes it inside a <strong>transaction</strong>. In that same transaction, an <code>OrderPlaced</code> message is written to an <strong>Outbox</strong> table, so it can&#39;t be lost even if the next step fails. A product lookup earlier was served from <strong>Redis</strong> via <code>HybridCache</code> instead of hitting the database. → <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals\">EF Core</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-querying\">Querying</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals\">Relational fundamentals</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/nosql-redis\">NoSQL &amp; Redis</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"9-communication-telling-other-services-layer-6\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/big-picture/9-communication-telling-other-services-layer-6\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>9. Communication: telling other services (layer 6)</h3>\n<p>A <strong>background service</strong> reads the outbox and publishes <code>OrderPlaced</code> to <strong>Azure Service Bus</strong>. The Payments service and the Email service each pick it up independently. Calls out to the external payment provider go through <code>HttpClient</code> wrapped in a <strong>retry + circuit breaker</strong> policy, so one flaky response doesn&#39;t take the whole request down. → <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events\">Messaging &amp; events</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/resilience\">Resilience</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/http-client\">HttpClient</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"10-response-and-observability-layers-3-8\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/big-picture/10-response-and-observability-layers-3-8\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>10. Response and observability (layers 3 + 8)</h3>\n<p>The handler returns <code>201 Created</code>, and the response travels <strong>back out through the middleware, in reverse order</strong>. Along the way, every step emitted <strong>structured logs</strong>, <strong>metrics</strong> and <strong>trace spans</strong> via <strong>OpenTelemetry</strong> — so in Application Insights you can see this one request traced across all three services. Finally the DI scope is disposed, which disposes the <code>DbContext</code> with it. → <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/observability-aspire\">Observability</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"11-and-before-any-of-this-happened-layer-8\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/big-picture/11-and-before-any-of-this-happened-layer-8\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>11. And before any of this happened (layer 8)</h3>\n<p>Before this code ever shipped, it passed <strong>unit tests</strong> (checking domain rules) and <strong>integration tests</strong> (the real pipeline plus a SQL container via Testcontainers) in CI, and was checked for <strong>OWASP</strong> issues. → <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/unit-testing\">Unit testing</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/integration-testing\">Integration testing</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/web-security\">Web security</a>.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-interview\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Use this in interviews</div><p>&quot;Walk me through what happens when a request hits your API&quot; is one of the most common senior .NET questions. The 11 steps above are a complete answer. Practise saying it in two minutes.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"what-belongs-to-what-the-ecosystem-by-family\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/big-picture/what-belongs-to-what-the-ecosystem-by-family\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What belongs to what: the ecosystem by family <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>The second source of confusion is product names. Microsoft ships many things with similar names. Here they are grouped by family, with the layer each lives in.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-platform-family-layer-2\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/big-picture/the-platform-family-layer-2\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The platform family (layer 2)</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Name</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>.NET</strong> (5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10…)</td>\n<td>The modern, cross-platform platform: runtime + libraries + SDK.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>.NET Framework</strong> (4.x)</td>\n<td>The old Windows-only platform. Still runs legacy apps. Not getting new features.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>.NET Core</strong> (1.0–3.1)</td>\n<td>The old name for modern .NET before version 5.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>.NET Standard</strong></td>\n<td>A <em>specification</em> of APIs, used to write libraries that run on both Framework and modern .NET.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CLR</strong></td>\n<td>The runtime engine (JIT, GC, type system, threads).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>BCL</strong></td>\n<td>Base Class Library: <code>System.*</code> types like <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>HttpClient</code>, <code>Task</code>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SDK / <code>dotnet</code> CLI</strong></td>\n<td>Build tools: compiler (Roslyn), MSBuild, templates.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>NuGet</strong></td>\n<td>The package manager and package format.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Mono / Xamarin</strong></td>\n<td>Older cross-platform runtime used for mobile; merged into modern .NET.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-web-family-layer-3\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/big-picture/the-web-family-layer-3\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The web family (layer 3)</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Name</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>Use it for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>ASP.NET Core</strong></td>\n<td>The web framework (the umbrella)</td>\n<td>Everything web</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>↳ <strong>Minimal APIs</strong></td>\n<td>Lightweight HTTP endpoints as functions</td>\n<td>JSON APIs, microservices</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>↳ <strong>MVC / Web API controllers</strong></td>\n<td>Class-based endpoints</td>\n<td>JSON APIs in larger/traditional codebases</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>↳ <strong>Razor Pages</strong></td>\n<td>Page-focused server-rendered HTML</td>\n<td>Server-rendered web apps</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>↳ <strong>Blazor</strong></td>\n<td>C# UI components running on the server or in the browser (WebAssembly)</td>\n<td>Interactive web UIs without JavaScript</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>↳ <strong>SignalR</strong></td>\n<td>Real-time push over WebSockets</td>\n<td>Chat, live dashboards</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>↳ <strong>gRPC</strong></td>\n<td>High-performance binary RPC over HTTP/2</td>\n<td>Service-to-service calls</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Kestrel</strong></td>\n<td>The web server inside ASP.NET Core</td>\n<td>Always there</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ASP.NET</strong> (no &quot;Core&quot;)</td>\n<td>The old Framework-only web stack (<code>System.Web</code>, Web Forms, MVC 5)</td>\n<td>Legacy only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>YARP</strong></td>\n<td>A reverse proxy library built on ASP.NET Core</td>\n<td>API gateways</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-data-family-layer-5\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/big-picture/the-data-family-layer-5\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The data family (layer 5)</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Name</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>ADO.NET</strong></td>\n<td>The low-level DB access API (<code>DbConnection</code>, <code>DbCommand</code>). Everything else is built on it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>EF Core</strong></td>\n<td>The full ORM: LINQ to SQL, change tracking, migrations.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Entity Framework 6</strong></td>\n<td>The old ORM for .NET Framework.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Dapper</strong></td>\n<td>A micro-ORM: you write SQL, it maps results to objects.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Microsoft.Data.SqlClient / Npgsql</strong></td>\n<td>The ADO.NET drivers for SQL Server / PostgreSQL.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-microsoft-extensions-family-the-glue-layer-3\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/big-picture/the-microsoft-extensions-family-the-glue-layer-3\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The &quot;Microsoft.Extensions&quot; family: the glue (layer 3)</h3>\n<p>These packages are the plumbing that ASP.NET Core, worker services and even console apps share. Recognising this family resolves a lot of confusion: <strong>DI, configuration, logging, options and hosting are not ASP.NET Core features. They are general .NET libraries that ASP.NET Core uses.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Package family</th>\n<th>Provides</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection</code></td>\n<td>The DI container (<code>IServiceCollection</code>, <code>IServiceProvider</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration</code></td>\n<td>Layered configuration (<code>IConfiguration</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Microsoft.Extensions.Options</code></td>\n<td><code>IOptions&lt;T&gt;</code>, validation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Microsoft.Extensions.Logging</code></td>\n<td><code>ILogger&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting</code></td>\n<td>The Generic Host, <code>IHostedService</code>, <code>BackgroundService</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Microsoft.Extensions.Http</code></td>\n<td><code>IHttpClientFactory</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.*</code></td>\n<td><code>IMemoryCache</code>, <code>IDistributedCache</code>, <code>HybridCache</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Microsoft.Extensions.Resilience</code></td>\n<td>Polly-based resilience pipelines</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-cloud-family-layer-7\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/big-picture/the-cloud-family-layer-7\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The cloud family (layer 7)</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Azure service</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Run a web app/API</td>\n<td>App Service, Container Apps, AKS, Functions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relational DB</td>\n<td>Azure SQL Database, Azure Database for PostgreSQL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Document DB</td>\n<td>Cosmos DB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Files/blobs</td>\n<td>Blob Storage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cache</td>\n<td>Azure Managed Redis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Messaging</td>\n<td>Service Bus (commands), Event Grid (event routing), Event Hubs (streams)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Secrets</td>\n<td>Key Vault</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Identity</td>\n<td>Microsoft Entra ID (formerly Azure AD), managed identities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Monitoring</td>\n<td>Azure Monitor / Application Insights</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gateway</td>\n<td>API Management, Front Door, Application Gateway</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-vocabulary-every-chapter-assumes\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/big-picture/the-vocabulary-every-chapter-assumes\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The vocabulary every chapter assumes <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Short definitions so no chapter surprises you. Each one is covered in depth later.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Assembly</strong>: a compiled <code>.dll</code> (or <code>.exe</code>): IL code plus metadata. The unit you deploy and reference.</li>\n<li><strong>Namespace</strong>: a naming scope (<code>System.Collections.Generic</code>). Has nothing to do with files or assemblies.</li>\n<li><strong>IL</strong>: Intermediate Language, the CPU-independent bytecode C# compiles to.</li>\n<li><strong>JIT</strong>: the compiler inside the runtime that turns IL into machine code at runtime.</li>\n<li><strong>GC</strong>: the garbage collector, which frees memory you no longer reference.</li>\n<li><strong>Managed code</strong>: code running under the CLR (memory managed for you).</li>\n<li><strong>Task</strong>: an object representing work that completes later; the basis of <code>async</code>/<code>await</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>DI container</strong>: the object that creates your classes and passes in their dependencies.</li>\n<li><strong>Lifetime</strong>: how long the container keeps an instance: transient, scoped or singleton.</li>\n<li><strong>Middleware</strong>: a component in the request pipeline that can act before and after the next one.</li>\n<li><strong>Endpoint</strong>: the final handler for a route (a controller action or a minimal API delegate).</li>\n<li><strong>DTO</strong>: Data Transfer Object, a simple type used to move data across a boundary (HTTP, messages).</li>\n<li><strong>Entity</strong>: an object with identity that you persist (an <code>Order</code> with an <code>Id</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>ORM</strong>: Object-Relational Mapper (EF Core): maps objects to tables.</li>\n<li><strong>Migration</strong>: a versioned, code-generated change to the database schema.</li>\n<li><strong>Idempotent</strong>: doing it twice has the same effect as doing it once.</li>\n<li><strong>Eventual consistency</strong>: data in different places will agree <em>eventually</em>, not instantly.</li>\n<li><strong>Stateless</strong>: no request depends on memory held by one specific server instance.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-read-this-book\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/big-picture/how-to-read-this-book\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>How to read this book <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>First pass: breadth.</strong> Read every chapter&#39;s Beginner sections, plus the &quot;Where this fits&quot; box. Skip Advanced sections. The goal is a complete, shallow map in your head.</li>\n<li><strong>Second pass: depth where interviews go.</strong> The most-asked areas for .NET backend roles are, in order: <strong>C# fundamentals and async</strong>, <strong>DI lifetimes</strong>, <strong>middleware and the request pipeline</strong>, <strong>EF Core performance</strong>, <strong>SOLID and patterns</strong>, <strong>REST API design</strong>, <strong>authentication with JWT</strong>, <strong>testing</strong>, then <strong>cloud and distributed systems</strong> for senior roles.</li>\n<li><strong>Third pass: Advanced sections and internals.</strong> GC, async state machines, threading, Kubernetes, sagas.</li>\n<li><strong>Every day: the question bank.</strong> Use drill mode. Say answers out loud before revealing them. If you can&#39;t explain it simply, go back to the linked section.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Each section heading has a level chip:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Beginner</strong>: vocabulary and core usage. Expected of every candidate.</li>\n<li><strong>Intermediate</strong>: how it works and how to use it well. Expected with 2+ years of experience.</li>\n<li><strong>Advanced</strong>: internals, trade-offs and edge cases. Senior and lead interviews.</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the difference between .NET, .NET Framework, .NET Core and ASP.NET Core?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>.NET Framework is the original Windows-only platform. .NET Core was its cross-platform rewrite; from version 5 it is simply called &quot;.NET&quot; and is the current platform. ASP.NET Core is the web framework that runs on .NET (layer 3), not a platform itself.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Are dependency injection, configuration and logging part of ASP.NET Core?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>They are used by ASP.NET Core, but they live in the general-purpose <code>Microsoft.Extensions.*</code> libraries and work in any .NET app: worker services, console apps, Azure Functions. ASP.NET Core&#39;s <code>WebApplication.CreateBuilder</code> simply wires them up for you.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Walk me through what happens when an HTTP request reaches a .NET API.</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Infrastructure (load balancer/gateway) forwards it to an instance; Kestrel parses it into an HttpContext and a DI scope is created; it passes through middleware in order (exception handling, HTTPS, routing, CORS, authentication, authorization, rate limiting); the endpoint runs (model binding, validation, filters, handler); the handler calls application/domain code and EF Core asynchronously; the result is serialized; the response flows back out through the middleware; the scope is disposed. Logs, metrics and traces are emitted throughout.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the difference between a DI &quot;service&quot; and a microservice?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A DI service is any class registered in the dependency injection container in one process. A microservice is a separately deployable application with its own data, communicating over the network. They share a word and nothing else.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/big-picture/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/architecture/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">.NET architecture overview</a> — Microsoft&#39;s own layered view of the platform, close to the map used here.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet-core/fundamentals/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ASP.NET Core fundamentals</a> — the canonical source for the middleware pipeline order shown in step 4 of the request trace.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/choosing-core-framework-server\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">.NET and .NET Framework — what&#39;s the difference</a> — backs the &quot;.NET vs .NET Framework vs .NET Core&quot; claims in the platform family table.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/big-picture/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Most claims here are about <em>names and layers</em>, not runtime behaviour, so there&#39;s little to assert about infrastructure or cloud pieces — trust <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/big-picture/further-reading\">Further reading</a> for those. What <em>is</em> checkable locally: that managed code really runs under the CLR, that a <code>Task</code> really represents work completing later, and that the GC really reclaims unreachable objects. Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (plain console app, .NET 8/9, no extra packages).</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: &quot;Managed code&quot; runs under the CLR, which reports its own runtime version.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> asm = <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Reflection</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Assembly</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetExecutingAssembly</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsNullOrEmpty</span>(asm.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ImageRuntimeVersion</span>))\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: no CLR runtime version reported — not managed code?&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: running under CLR {asm.ImageRuntimeVersion} (managed code)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: a Task represents work that completes later, not immediately.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; slow = <span class=\"tk-ty\">SlowlyComputeAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (slow.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsCompleted</span>)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: task completed synchronously, can't demonstrate 'completes later'&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: Task is pending immediately after starting (completes later)&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> result = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> slow;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!slow.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsCompletedSuccessfully</span> || result != <span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: task did not complete successfully with the expected result&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: awaited Task completed successfully with result 42&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: the GC frees memory you no longer reference.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> weak = <span class=\"tk-ty\">AllocateAndDrop</span>();          <span class=\"tk-com\">// helper method so the object is truly out of scope</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">GC</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Collect</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">GCCollectionMode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Forced</span>, blocking: <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">GC</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WaitForPendingFinalizers</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">GC</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Collect</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">GCCollectionMode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Forced</span>, blocking: <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (weak.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsAlive</span>)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: unreferenced object was not collected&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: GC reclaimed the unreferenced object&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">WeakReference</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AllocateAndDrop</span>()\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span> obj = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>[<span class=\"tk-num\">1_000_000</span>];\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">WeakReference</span>(obj);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: &quot;idempotent&quot; = doing it twice has the same effect as doing it once.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HashSet</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>); <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> != <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: HashSet.Add was not idempotent in this check&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: adding the same item twice to a set is idempotent (count stays 1)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">SlowlyComputeAsync</span>()\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Delay</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">50</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>;\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here — see Further reading:</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - exact middleware execution order (needs a real ASP.NET Core host)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - JIT compiling IL to machine code on first call (observable via profilers, not plain code)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - cloud/infra claims (Front Door, Container Apps, Service Bus, Key Vault, etc.)</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"why-net-feels-like-a-maze","text":"Why .NET feels like a maze","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"the-eight-layers","text":"The eight layers","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"the-same-thing-at-different-layers","text":"The same thing at different layers","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"follow-one-request-through-every-layer","text":"Follow one request through every layer","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"1-infrastructure-the-request-finds-your-code-layer-7","text":"1. Infrastructure: the request finds your code (layer 7)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"2-runtime-a-process-is-already-running-layer-2","text":"2. Runtime: a process is already running (layer 2)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"3-framework-kestrel-and-the-host-layer-3","text":"3. Framework: Kestrel and the host (layer 3)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"4-framework-the-middleware-pipeline-layer-3","text":"4. Framework: the middleware pipeline (layer 3)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"5-framework-binding-validation-your-endpoint-layers-1-3","text":"5. Framework: binding, validation, your endpoint (layers 1 + 3)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"6-architecture-your-codes-shape-layer-4","text":"6. Architecture: your code's shape (layer 4)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"7-language-async-all-the-way-down-layer-1","text":"7. Language: async all the way down (layer 1)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"8-data-ef-core-and-sql-layer-5","text":"8. Data: EF Core and SQL (layer 5)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"9-communication-telling-other-services-layer-6","text":"9. Communication: telling other services (layer 6)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"10-response-and-observability-layers-3-8","text":"10. Response and observability (layers 3 + 8)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"11-and-before-any-of-this-happened-layer-8","text":"11. And before any of this happened (layer 8)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"what-belongs-to-what-the-ecosystem-by-family","text":"What belongs to what: the ecosystem by family","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"the-platform-family-layer-2","text":"The platform family (layer 2)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-web-family-layer-3","text":"The web family (layer 3)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-data-family-layer-5","text":"The data family (layer 5)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-microsoft-extensions-family-the-glue-layer-3","text":"The \"Microsoft.Extensions\" family: the glue (layer 3)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-cloud-family-layer-7","text":"The cloud family (layer 7)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-vocabulary-every-chapter-assumes","text":"The vocabulary every chapter assumes","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"how-to-read-this-book","text":"How to read this book","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":3661,"minutes":17,"num":"0","part":"p0"},"what-is-dotnet":{"id":"what-is-dotnet","title":"What .NET Actually Is","summary":"The platform under everything: runtime, libraries and SDK; the history behind the confusing names; versions and support; and what \"cross-platform\" really means.","layer":"runtime","where":"Runtime & platform › The platform itself","fits":"<p>.NET is layer 2. C# (layer 1) compiles <em>for</em> it, and every framework in layer 3 (ASP.NET Core, EF Core) is a set of libraries that <em>runs on</em> it. When people say &quot;the runtime&quot;, &quot;the BCL&quot; or &quot;the SDK&quot;, they mean parts of this layer.</p>\n","prereqs":["big-picture"],"next":["how-code-runs","projects-sdk-nuget"],"levels":["b","i"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/what-is-dotnet/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>.NET is a <strong>platform</strong> (runtime + BCL + SDK), not a language — C#, F# and VB all compile to it.</li>\n<li>The confusing names are historical: <strong>.NET Framework</strong> (old, Windows-only) → <strong>.NET Core</strong> (rewrite, cross-platform) → renamed to just <strong>.NET</strong> from version 5 onward.</li>\n<li>Pick <strong>LTS</strong> versions (even numbers: 6, 8, 10) for production — 3 years of support vs 2 for STS (odd numbers).</li>\n<li>Deployment mode is a trade-off: framework-dependent (small, needs a runtime installed) vs self-contained vs Native AOT (fastest startup, some reflection limits).</li>\n<li>The BCL already ships most things you&#39;d reach for a NuGet package for: JSON, HTTP, collections, crypto, regex, metrics.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"net-is-a-platform-not-a-language\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/what-is-dotnet/net-is-a-platform-not-a-language\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>.NET is a platform, not a language <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>.NET is a <strong>software platform</strong>: everything needed to build and run applications. C# is the most popular language for it, but F# and Visual Basic compile to the same format and run on the same platform.</p>\n<p>The platform has four parts:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Part</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>Where you meet it</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Runtime (CLR)</strong></td>\n<td>The engine that loads compiled code, JIT-compiles it to machine code, manages memory (GC), threads, exceptions and type safety</td>\n<td>Always running underneath your app</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Base Class Library (BCL)</strong></td>\n<td>Thousands of ready-made types: <code>string</code>, <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>Dictionary&lt;K,V&gt;</code>, <code>Task</code>, <code>HttpClient</code>, <code>File</code>, <code>JsonSerializer</code>, <code>DateTime</code>…</td>\n<td>Every <code>using System...</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SDK</strong></td>\n<td>Build-time tools: the C# compiler (Roslyn), MSBuild, the <code>dotnet</code> CLI, project templates</td>\n<td>Your dev machine and CI servers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>App frameworks (shared frameworks)</strong></td>\n<td>Big library sets shipped with the runtime: <strong>Microsoft.NETCore.App</strong> (base), <strong>Microsoft.AspNetCore.App</strong> (web), <strong>Microsoft.WindowsDesktop.App</strong> (WPF/WinForms)</td>\n<td>What your project&#39;s SDK attribute pulls in</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-analogy\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Runtime vs SDK</div><p>The <strong>runtime</strong> is like a DVD player: it plays discs. The <strong>SDK</strong> is the studio that makes discs. A production server only needs the player (the runtime). A developer machine and a CI server need the studio (the SDK), which <em>includes</em> a runtime so you can run what you build.</p>\n</aside>\n<h3 id=\"runtime-only-installs\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/what-is-dotnet/runtime-only-installs\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Runtime-only installs</h3>\n<p>You&#39;ll see three runtime downloads:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>.NET Runtime</strong>: console apps and libraries.</li>\n<li><strong>ASP.NET Core Runtime</strong>: the above plus the web framework. Use this for web servers.</li>\n<li><strong>.NET Desktop Runtime</strong>: Windows only, for WPF/WinForms.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Docker images mirror this: <code>mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/sdk</code> (build), <code>.../aspnet</code> (run web apps), <code>.../runtime</code> (run console apps), <code>.../runtime-deps</code> (only native dependencies, for self-contained apps).</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-history-that-explains-the-names\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/what-is-dotnet/the-history-that-explains-the-names\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The history that explains the names <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Most confusion about .NET comes from its renaming. Here is the timeline:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Year</th>\n<th>Event</th>\n<th>Why it matters today</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>2002</td>\n<td><strong>.NET Framework 1.0</strong>: Windows only, installed into the OS</td>\n<td>Legacy enterprise apps still run on 4.8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2002–2019</td>\n<td>Framework 2.0 → 4.8: generics, LINQ, async, WPF, WCF, ASP.NET MVC</td>\n<td>Old tutorials, <code>web.config</code>, <code>System.Web</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2004</td>\n<td><strong>Mono</strong>: an open-source, cross-platform reimplementation</td>\n<td>Became Xamarin (mobile) and Unity&#39;s scripting runtime</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2016</td>\n<td><strong>.NET Core 1.0</strong>: a ground-up rewrite. Cross-platform, open source, modular, fast</td>\n<td>The start of modern .NET</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2016</td>\n<td><strong>ASP.NET Core</strong>: a rewrite of ASP.NET for .NET Core</td>\n<td>Today&#39;s web framework</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2019</td>\n<td>.NET Core 3.1 (LTS)</td>\n<td>Was the &quot;standard&quot; version for years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2020</td>\n<td><strong>.NET 5</strong>: &quot;Core&quot; dropped from the name; skipped version 4 to avoid confusion with Framework 4.x</td>\n<td>One .NET going forward</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2021</td>\n<td>.NET 6 (LTS): minimal APIs, hot reload</td>\n<td>Still found in many companies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2023</td>\n<td>.NET 8 (LTS): Native AOT for APIs, keyed DI, <code>TimeProvider</code></td>\n<td>The most common production version in 2025–26</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2024</td>\n<td>.NET 9 (STS): <code>HybridCache</code>, built-in OpenAPI, LINQ <code>CountBy</code>/<code>Index</code></td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2025</td>\n<td><strong>.NET 10 (LTS)</strong>, C# 14</td>\n<td>Current LTS</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">&quot;ASP.NET&quot; vs &quot;ASP.NET Core&quot;</div><p><strong>ASP.NET</strong> (Web Forms, MVC 5, Web API 2, <code>System.Web</code>, <code>Global.asax</code>, <code>web.config</code>) runs only on .NET Framework. <strong>ASP.NET Core</strong> is the modern rewrite. They share concepts (controllers, Razor) but the internals, configuration and hosting are completely different. If a tutorial has <code>Global.asax</code> or <code>HttpContext.Current</code>, it is about the old one.</p>\n</aside>\n<h3 id=\"why-the-rewrite-happened\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/what-is-dotnet/why-the-rewrite-happened\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Why the rewrite happened</h3>\n<p>.NET Framework had a few structural problems. None of them could be fixed without breaking apps that already depended on the old behaviour, so Microsoft started over instead:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Windows only.</strong> Meanwhile the industry moved to Linux and containers.</li>\n<li><strong>Machine-wide installs.</strong> Upgrading Framework on a server upgraded it for <em>every</em> app on that server, so companies ended up freezing versions for years rather than risk breaking something else. .NET Core fixes this: versions can run <strong>side by side</strong>, and an app can even be <strong>self-contained</strong>, carrying its own runtime with it.</li>\n<li><strong>Monolithic <code>System.Web</code>.</strong> ASP.NET was tied to IIS and loaded a huge pipeline even for a tiny API. ASP.NET Core is modular instead: you add only the middleware you actually use.</li>\n<li><strong>Performance.</strong> .NET Core was designed for throughput from day one. ASP.NET Core regularly places among the fastest mainstream web frameworks in TechEmpower benchmarks.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"versions-support-and-release-cadence\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/what-is-dotnet/versions-support-and-release-cadence\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Versions, support and release cadence <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>A new major version ships <strong>every November</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Versions</th>\n<th>Support length</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>LTS</strong> (Long Term Support)</td>\n<td>Even numbers: 6, 8, 10</td>\n<td>3 years</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>STS</strong> (Standard Term Support)</td>\n<td>Odd numbers: 7, 9, 11</td>\n<td>2 years (extended from 18 months starting with .NET 9)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Patch releases come monthly (&quot;Patch Tuesday&quot;) with security fixes. You get them by updating the runtime or rebuilding on the latest base image.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-tip\"><div class=\"callout-title\">What to say in an interview</div><p>&quot;We target the current LTS in production because it gives three years of patches. We upgrade once per LTS cycle, and upgrading is usually just changing the target framework, updating packages and fixing a few breaking changes listed in the official docs.&quot;</p>\n</aside>\n<h3 id=\"target-framework-monikers-tfms\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/what-is-dotnet/target-framework-monikers-tfms\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Target framework monikers (TFMs)</h3>\n<p>Your project declares which .NET it targets:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>TFM</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>net10.0</code>, <code>net8.0</code></td>\n<td>Modern .NET, any OS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>net8.0-windows</code></td>\n<td>Modern .NET with Windows-only APIs (WPF, WinForms)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>net8.0-android</code>, <code>net8.0-ios</code></td>\n<td>Mobile via .NET MAUI</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>netstandard2.0</code></td>\n<td>A library usable from Framework 4.6.1+ <strong>and</strong> all modern .NET</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>net48</code></td>\n<td>.NET Framework 4.8</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>You can multi-target a library with <code>&lt;TargetFrameworks&gt;net8.0;netstandard2.0&lt;/TargetFrameworks&gt;</code> (note the plural).</p>\n<h2 id=\"net-standard-a-contract-not-a-runtime\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/what-is-dotnet/net-standard-a-contract-not-a-runtime\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>.NET Standard: a contract, not a runtime <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>During the transition years there were three runtimes (Framework, Core, Mono/Xamarin) with different API sets. <strong>.NET Standard</strong> was a <strong>specification</strong>: a list of APIs that every compliant runtime promises to implement. A library targeting <code>netstandard2.0</code> runs on all of them.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>.NET Standard 2.0</strong> is the useful one: supported by Framework 4.6.1+, .NET Core 2.0+, all modern .NET, Mono, Unity.</li>\n<li><strong>.NET Standard 2.1</strong> dropped .NET Framework support, which made it much less useful.</li>\n<li><strong>No new versions</strong> will be made. Modern .NET (<code>net8.0</code> etc.) is the &quot;standard&quot; now.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>When to target it today:</strong> only for libraries that must also be consumed by .NET Framework apps. Otherwise target <code>net8.0</code>/<code>net10.0</code> to get modern APIs (<code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> overloads, newer collections, etc.).</p>\n<h2 id=\"cross-platform-open-source-and-who-builds-it\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/what-is-dotnet/cross-platform-open-source-and-who-builds-it\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Cross-platform, open source, and who builds it <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Runs on</strong> Windows, Linux, macOS; x64, x86, Arm64 (including Apple Silicon, AWS Graviton, Azure Cobalt). Linux containers are the dominant production deployment.</li>\n<li><strong>Open source</strong> under the MIT licence, developed on GitHub (<code>dotnet/runtime</code>, <code>dotnet/aspnetcore</code>, <code>dotnet/efcore</code>, <code>dotnet/roslyn</code>), stewarded by the <strong>.NET Foundation</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Built by</strong> Microsoft with large community contribution. Design discussions (API proposals) happen in the open on GitHub issues.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-runs-on-net\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/what-is-dotnet/what-runs-on-net\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What runs on .NET</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>App type</th>\n<th>Technology</th>\n<th>Layer-3 framework</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Web APIs, websites</td>\n<td>ASP.NET Core (minimal APIs, MVC, Razor Pages, Blazor)</td>\n<td>Microsoft.AspNetCore.App</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Background processing</td>\n<td>Worker Service (Generic Host)</td>\n<td>Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Serverless</td>\n<td>Azure Functions (isolated worker), AWS Lambda</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Desktop</td>\n<td>WPF, WinForms (Windows), .NET MAUI, Avalonia</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mobile</td>\n<td>.NET MAUI (iOS, Android)</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Games</td>\n<td>Unity (Mono/IL2CPP based), Godot, MonoGame</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cloud-native distributed apps</td>\n<td>.NET Aspire for orchestration</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ML</td>\n<td>ML.NET, ONNX Runtime</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>This book focuses on <strong>backend</strong>: web APIs, workers, data and cloud.</p>\n<h2 id=\"framework-dependent-vs-self-contained-deployment\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/what-is-dotnet/framework-dependent-vs-self-contained-deployment\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Framework-dependent vs self-contained deployment <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>When you <code>dotnet publish</code>, you choose how the app ships:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Mode</th>\n<th>What&#39;s in the output</th>\n<th>Pros</th>\n<th>Cons</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Framework-dependent</strong> (default)</td>\n<td>Your DLLs only</td>\n<td>Small; shared runtime is patched centrally</td>\n<td>Target machine needs the right runtime installed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Self-contained</strong> (<code>--self-contained -r linux-x64</code>)</td>\n<td>Your DLLs + the entire runtime</td>\n<td>No runtime needed on the machine; pin exact version</td>\n<td>Larger (≈70 MB+); you must rebuild to get runtime patches</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Single file</strong> (<code>-p:PublishSingleFile=true</code>)</td>\n<td>One executable (self-contained or not)</td>\n<td>Easy distribution</td>\n<td>Startup extraction quirks for some native libs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Native AOT</strong> (<code>-p:PublishAot=true</code>)</td>\n<td>One native binary, no JIT</td>\n<td>Instant startup, low memory</td>\n<td>Reflection limits; not all libraries are compatible</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ReadyToRun</strong> (<code>-p:PublishReadyToRun=true</code>)</td>\n<td>IL + precompiled native code</td>\n<td>Faster startup, still JIT-capable</td>\n<td>Larger files</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The <strong>RID</strong> (runtime identifier) such as <code>linux-x64</code>, <code>linux-musl-arm64</code> (Alpine), <code>win-x64</code>, <code>osx-arm64</code> says which platform a self-contained/native build targets.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-bcl-a-tour-of-what-you-get-for-free\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/what-is-dotnet/the-bcl-a-tour-of-what-you-get-for-free\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The BCL: a tour of what you get for free <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Knowing what is already in the box saves you writing (or installing) it. Highlights by namespace:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Namespace</th>\n<th>Key types</th>\n<th>Used for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>System</code></td>\n<td><code>String</code>, <code>DateTime</code>, <code>DateTimeOffset</code>, <code>TimeSpan</code>, <code>Guid</code>, <code>Math</code>, <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>Exception</code>, <code>TimeProvider</code></td>\n<td>Basics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>System.Collections.Generic</code></td>\n<td><code>List&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>Dictionary&lt;K,V&gt;</code>, <code>HashSet&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>Queue&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>PriorityQueue&lt;T,P&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Data structures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>System.Collections.Concurrent</code></td>\n<td><code>ConcurrentDictionary</code>, <code>ConcurrentQueue</code>, <code>BlockingCollection</code></td>\n<td>Thread-safe collections</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>System.Collections.Immutable</code> / <code>Frozen</code></td>\n<td><code>ImmutableList</code>, <code>FrozenDictionary</code></td>\n<td>Snapshots, read-mostly lookups</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>System.Linq</code></td>\n<td><code>Enumerable</code>, <code>Queryable</code></td>\n<td>Querying</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>System.Threading</code> / <code>.Tasks</code></td>\n<td><code>Task</code>, <code>CancellationToken</code>, <code>SemaphoreSlim</code>, <code>Channel&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>Parallel</code></td>\n<td>Async and concurrency</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>System.Text.Json</code></td>\n<td><code>JsonSerializer</code>, <code>JsonDocument</code>, <code>Utf8JsonWriter</code></td>\n<td>JSON (the default serializer)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>System.Net.Http</code></td>\n<td><code>HttpClient</code></td>\n<td>Calling HTTP APIs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>System.IO</code></td>\n<td><code>File</code>, <code>Stream</code>, <code>Path</code>, <code>FileSystemWatcher</code></td>\n<td>Files and streams</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>System.IO.Pipelines</code></td>\n<td><code>Pipe</code>, <code>PipeReader</code></td>\n<td>High-performance I/O (used by Kestrel)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>System.Diagnostics</code></td>\n<td><code>Activity</code>, <code>Stopwatch</code>, <code>Process</code>, <code>Metrics.Meter</code></td>\n<td>Tracing, timing, metrics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>System.Security.Cryptography</code></td>\n<td><code>SHA256</code>, <code>Aes</code>, <code>RandomNumberGenerator</code>, <code>X509Certificate2</code></td>\n<td>Crypto</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>System.Text.RegularExpressions</code></td>\n<td><code>Regex</code> (with source generator)</td>\n<td>Pattern matching on text</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">DateTime vs DateTimeOffset</div><p><code>DateTime</code> doesn&#39;t reliably carry a time zone offset (its <code>Kind</code> is often <code>Unspecified</code>). For timestamps stored in databases and sent over APIs, prefer <strong><code>DateTimeOffset</code></strong> or always use UTC (<code>DateTime.UtcNow</code>). For testable time, inject <strong><code>TimeProvider</code></strong> (.NET 8) instead of calling <code>DateTime.Now</code> directly.</p>\n</aside>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the difference between the .NET SDK and the .NET runtime?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The runtime contains the CLR and the libraries needed to <em>run</em> a compiled app. The SDK contains the compiler, MSBuild, CLI and templates needed to <em>build</em> apps, and it includes a runtime. Production servers and runtime Docker images need only the runtime (for web apps, the ASP.NET Core runtime); dev machines and CI need the SDK.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why was .NET Core created instead of continuing .NET Framework?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Framework was Windows-only, installed machine-wide (so upgrades affected every app), tied to IIS via a monolithic System.Web, and hard to make fast without breaking compatibility. .NET Core was a cross-platform, open-source, modular rewrite with side-by-side installs and much better performance.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is .NET Standard and should you still target it?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A specification of APIs that multiple .NET implementations promise to support, so one library could run everywhere during the transition. Target <code>netstandard2.0</code> only if the library must also be used by .NET Framework apps; otherwise target a modern TFM such as <code>net8.0</code> or <code>net10.0</code>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What do LTS and STS mean? Which would you choose for production?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>LTS (even versions) gets 3 years of support; STS (odd versions) gets 2 years. Most companies pick LTS for production to reduce upgrade frequency, while trying STS releases in non-critical services to get features earlier.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Framework-dependent vs self-contained deployment?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Framework-dependent publishes only your app and relies on an installed shared runtime: small, centrally patched. Self-contained bundles the runtime: no install needed and version-pinned, but larger, and you must redeploy to pick up runtime security patches.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Name some things in the BCL you&#39;d use instead of a third-party package.</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>System.Text.Json for JSON, HttpClient + IHttpClientFactory for HTTP, Channel&lt;T&gt; for producer/consumer queues, PriorityQueue, TimeProvider for testable time, System.Diagnostics.Metrics and Activity for telemetry, built-in rate limiting, and the source-generated Regex.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/what-is-dotnet/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/introduction\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">What is .NET? (Microsoft Learn)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://dotnet.microsoft.com/en-us/platform/support/policy/dotnet-core\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">.NET support policy</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/frameworks\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Target frameworks</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/deploying/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Application publishing overview</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/what-is-dotnet/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Version/support-cycle dates and &quot;which OS runs .NET&quot; aren&#39;t things one running process can prove about itself — trust <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/what-is-dotnet/further-reading\">Further reading</a> for those. What the program below checks are the BCL behaviours this chapter calls out: the <code>DateTime</code> vs <code>DateTimeOffset</code> gotcha, <code>TimeProvider</code> being injectable/testable, and <code>System.Text.Json</code> being usable with zero extra packages. Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (plain console app, .NET 8/9, no extra packages).</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Text</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Json</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: DateTime doesn't reliably carry a time zone offset — Kind is often Unspecified.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> parsed = <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTime</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parse</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;2026-01-01T10:00:00&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (parsed.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Kind</span> != <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeKind</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Unspecified</span>)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected DateTime.Parse without a zone to be Kind.Unspecified&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: a plain DateTime.Parse carries no reliable zone info (Kind.Unspecified)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// DateTimeOffset, by contrast, always carries an explicit offset.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> withOffset = <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parse</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;2026-01-01T10:00:00+02:00&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (withOffset.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Offset</span> != <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromHours</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>))\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected DateTimeOffset to preserve the +02:00 offset&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: DateTimeOffset preserves the offset that DateTime would have lost&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: inject TimeProvider instead of calling DateTime.Now directly, so time is testable.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> fixedTime = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2030</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">6</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Zero</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeProvider</span> fakeClock = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FixedTimeProvider</span>(fixedTime);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (fakeClock.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetUtcNow</span>() != fixedTime)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: a fake TimeProvider should let you control 'now' in a test&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: TimeProvider can be substituted with a fixed clock for deterministic tests&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: System.Text.Json is the default serializer, usable with no extra NuGet package.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> json = <span class=\"tk-ty\">JsonSerializer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Serialize</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Ana&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">42.5m</span> });\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> roundTripped = <span class=\"tk-ty\">JsonSerializer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Deserialize</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>&gt;&gt;(json);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (roundTripped <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span> || roundTripped.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> != <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: JSON round-trip through System.Text.Json failed&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: System.Text.Json serialized and parsed JSON with zero extra packages: {json}&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here — see Further reading:</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - which OSes/architectures .NET runs on (needs multiple machines, not one process)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - LTS/STS support windows and release cadence (a policy, not a runtime fact)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - framework-dependent vs self-contained vs Native AOT publish sizes/startup time (needs `dotnet publish`)</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FixedTimeProvider</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span> now) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeProvider</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetUtcNow</span>() =&gt; now;\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"net-is-a-platform-not-a-language","text":".NET is a platform, not a language","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"runtime-only-installs","text":"Runtime-only installs","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-history-that-explains-the-names","text":"The history that explains the names","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"why-the-rewrite-happened","text":"Why the rewrite happened","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"versions-support-and-release-cadence","text":"Versions, support and release cadence","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"target-framework-monikers-tfms","text":"Target framework monikers (TFMs)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"net-standard-a-contract-not-a-runtime","text":".NET Standard: a contract, not a runtime","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"cross-platform-open-source-and-who-builds-it","text":"Cross-platform, open source, and who builds it","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"what-runs-on-net","text":"What runs on .NET","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"framework-dependent-vs-self-contained-deployment","text":"Framework-dependent vs self-contained deployment","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"the-bcl-a-tour-of-what-you-get-for-free","text":"The BCL: a tour of what you get for free","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":2339,"minutes":11,"num":"1.1","part":"p1"},"how-code-runs":{"id":"how-code-runs","title":"How Your Code Runs: Compilation, IL, the CLR and the JIT","summary":"What happens between saving a .cs file and the CPU executing instructions: Roslyn, IL, assemblies and metadata, the CLR's job, the JIT with tiered compilation and PGO, and the ahead-of-time alternatives.","layer":"runtime","where":"Runtime & platform › Execution engine (CLR)","fits":"<p>This is the bottom of layer 2, directly under the language. Everything in C# eventually becomes IL that this machinery executes. The GC (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/memory-gc\">Memory &amp; GC</a>) and the thread pool (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/threading-concurrency\">Threading</a>) are other services of the same runtime.</p>\n","prereqs":["what-is-dotnet"],"next":["projects-sdk-nuget","type-system","memory-gc"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Compiling happens <strong>twice</strong>: Roslyn turns C# into <strong>IL</strong> (a CPU-independent format) at build time; the <strong>JIT</strong> turns IL into real machine code at run time, the first time each method is actually called.</li>\n<li>Splitting it this way means one build runs on any CPU, lets C#/F#/VB interoperate (same IL), and lets the runtime optimise based on how your code <em>actually</em> behaves, not just a guess.</li>\n<li>The JIT compiles most methods <strong>twice</strong>: a rough <strong>Tier 0</strong> pass immediately, then a fully optimised <strong>Tier 1</strong> pass once a method proves it&#39;s called often — using <strong>Dynamic PGO</strong> data recorded from real usage.</li>\n<li><strong>ReadyToRun</strong> and <strong>Native AOT</strong> skip some or all of this at startup, trading flexibility (reflection, dynamic code) for faster cold starts.</li>\n<li>&quot;The first request is slow&quot; has one root cause: everything on that code path is being JIT-compiled, DI/EF Core models built, and connection pools filled for the very first time.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"lets-follow-one-line-of-code-start-to-finish\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/lets-follow-one-line-of-code-start-to-finish\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Let&#39;s follow one line of code, start to finish <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Here&#39;s the smallest program you can write:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"cHVibGljIHN0YXRpYyBpbnQgQWRkKGludCBhLCBpbnQgYikgPT4gYSArIGI7\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> a, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> b) =&gt; a + b;</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>You&#39;ve written this. You know what it does. But what actually <em>happens</em> to it between you hitting save and a CPU somewhere producing the number <code>5</code>? Let&#39;s trace it, step by step, because every term in this chapter is really just a closer look at one moment in this sequence.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You save the file and run <code>dotnet build</code>.</li>\n<li>The C# compiler reads <code>Add</code> and writes it down — but not as machine code. It writes it in a different, in-between format. That happens <strong>once</strong>, right now, at build time, and never again for this particular method.</li>\n<li>The result gets saved into a file called an <strong>assembly</strong>.</li>\n<li>Time passes. Maybe seconds, maybe you deploy it and it&#39;s months. You run the app with <code>dotnet run</code>. Nothing from step 2 has been turned into something your CPU can actually execute yet — that hasn&#39;t happened. Not even once.</li>\n<li>Then, the moment something finally <em>calls</em> <code>Add(2, 3)</code> — and not a moment before — the runtime translates that in-between format into real instructions for your exact CPU, runs them, and hands back <code>5</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Notice something odd there: step 2 and step 5 are <em>both</em> &quot;compiling&quot;. Same word, two completely different jobs, done by two completely different pieces of software, at two completely different times. Why would .NET deliberately do that twice instead of once? That question is the whole chapter.</p>\n<h2 id=\"so-why-split-it-into-two-steps-at-all\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/so-why-split-it-into-two-steps-at-all\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>So why split it into two steps at all? <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Here&#39;s a fair challenge: C++ doesn&#39;t do this. It compiles straight to machine code, once, at build time, and never touches the result again. Why does .NET add this extra step?</p>\n<p>Start with the problem that forces the split. Machine code isn&#39;t universal — it&#39;s written for one specific CPU family. Code compiled for an Intel/AMD chip (what&#39;s called x64) simply will not run on an Arm chip, the kind in a lot of phones and, increasingly, servers too. If .NET compiled your C# straight to machine code the moment you hit build, you&#39;d need to know, right then, exactly which CPU your app would eventually run on — and build a separate version for each one you cared about.</p>\n<p>So .NET stops one step short at build time. It compiles down to an in-between format instead of all the way to machine code, and saves the <em>last</em> translation — the CPU-specific one — for later, on whichever machine actually ends up running the code. That machine, by definition, already knows exactly what CPU it has. One build. Any CPU.</p>\n<p>That in-between format has a name: <strong>IL</strong> (Intermediate Language — you&#39;ll also see it called CIL or MSIL; don&#39;t let the three names fool you, they&#39;re all the same thing). The compiler that writes it is <strong>Roslyn</strong>. The piece that reads IL later and turns it into real machine code is the <strong>JIT</strong> — short for &quot;just-in-time&quot; compiler, because it compiles each piece exactly when it&#39;s needed, not before. The specific JIT built into .NET even has its own name, <strong>RyuJIT</strong>, which you&#39;ll see mentioned in performance discussions.</p>\n<p>Once you accept that the translation happens <em>later</em>, on the real machine, a few more nice things fall out of it almost for free — and they&#39;re worth knowing, because they come up again and again later in this book:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The CPU gets code tuned for itself.</strong> Since the translation happens on the actual machine, the JIT can use whatever that specific CPU is good at — a fast modern instruction set on a beefy server, something plainer on an older box. A build-time-only compiler could never know that in advance; it wouldn&#39;t know what machine you&#39;d eventually run on.</li>\n<li><strong>The runtime can watch your code run and keep making it faster.</strong> This sounds almost magical the first time you hear it, but it&#39;s real, and you&#39;ll see exactly how in the Dynamic PGO section further down.</li>\n<li><strong>Different .NET languages can call each other directly, with zero friction.</strong> C#, F# and VB all compile down to the very same IL. By the time anything is actually running, there&#39;s no language boundary left — it&#39;s just IL calling IL.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>One more payoff, and it has nothing to do with CPUs at all: IL is <strong>verifiable</strong>, and it comes bundled with rich <strong>metadata</strong> — a structured description of every type and method in your code, sitting right there in the file alongside the IL itself. That one detail is what makes garbage collection, reflection and serialization possible, and it&#39;s worth slowing down for, which is exactly what the next section does.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-build-time-actually-hands-you\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/what-build-time-actually-hands-you\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What build time actually hands you <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>  build time                                       run time\n┌──────────┐  Roslyn   ┌──────────────────┐  CLR loads  ┌──────┐  ┌───────────────┐\n│ .cs files│ ────────▶ │ assembly (.dll)  │ ──────────▶ │ JIT  │─▶│ native x64/Arm│ ─▶ CPU\n└──────────┘ (csc)     │  IL + metadata   │             └──────┘  │ machine code  │\n                       └──────────────────┘                       └───────────────┘</code></pre></figure>\n<p>The file Roslyn produces — the <strong>assembly</strong> — is a PE file, the same container format Windows uses for a plain <code>.exe</code>. Open one up conceptually and you&#39;ll find four things packed inside it:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Part</th>\n<th>Contents</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Manifest</strong></td>\n<td>Assembly name, version, culture, public key token, list of referenced assemblies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Metadata tables</strong></td>\n<td>Every type, method, field, property, attribute and its exact signature</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>IL code</strong></td>\n<td>The actual method bodies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Resources</strong></td>\n<td>Embedded files, localisation strings</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>That metadata row deserves more than a passing glance, because it quietly explains three things you probably already take for granted. Ever wonder why .NET doesn&#39;t need separate header files the way C does? The metadata already says, precisely, what every type looks like — there&#39;s nothing left for a header file to repeat. Ever notice IntelliSense working perfectly against a compiled library you&#39;ve never once seen the source code for? Same reason — the metadata is right there in the <code>.dll</code>, fully readable. And <strong>reflection</strong> — code that asks &quot;what properties does this type have?&quot; <em>while the program is running</em>, instead of knowing the answer at compile time — only works at all because that metadata exists to answer it. Without it, reflection would have nothing to inspect.</p>\n<h3 id=\"lets-watch-add-actually-become-il\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/lets-watch-add-actually-become-il\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Let&#39;s watch <code>Add</code> actually become IL</h3>\n<p>Back to our one-line method. Here&#39;s exactly what the compiler writes down for it:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"cHVibGljIHN0YXRpYyBpbnQgQWRkKGludCBhLCBpbnQgYikgPT4gYSArIGI7\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> a, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> b) =&gt; a + b;</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>.method public hidebysig static int32 Add(int32 a, int32 b) cil managed\n{\n  ldarg.0      // push a\n  ldarg.1      // push b\n  add          // pop two, push sum\n  ret          // return top of stack\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<p>If that looks a little strange, here&#39;s the trick to reading it: IL doesn&#39;t use named variables sitting in CPU registers the way the final machine code eventually will. It works with a small, temporary pile of values called a <strong>stack</strong> — you can only ever add to the top of it, or take from the top of it. Nothing else. Let&#39;s walk through those four lines and watch exactly what&#39;s sitting on that stack at each point:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Instruction</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>Stack after</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>ldarg.0</code></td>\n<td>push argument <code>a</code></td>\n<td><code>[a]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ldarg.1</code></td>\n<td>push argument <code>b</code></td>\n<td><code>[a, b]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>add</code></td>\n<td>pop two values, push their sum</td>\n<td><code>[a+b]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ret</code></td>\n<td>return whatever is on top of the stack</td>\n<td><em>(returns <code>a+b</code>)</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>That&#39;s genuinely the entire thing. Four instructions, each doing one small, obvious job to a pile of values. It reads almost like a recipe for a very literal-minded assistant — and that literalness is the whole point: it&#39;s simple enough that <em>any</em> CPU&#39;s JIT can turn it into real instructions, no matter how wildly different that CPU&#39;s actual registers are underneath.</p>\n<p>Want to see this for yourself, right now, on real code? Go to <strong>SharpLab.io</strong>, paste in any C# snippet, and it shows you the IL <em>and</em> the JIT&#39;s final machine-code output, side by side. It&#39;s genuinely one of the best ways to understand what <code>async</code>, <code>foreach</code> and closures are secretly expanding into — each gets its own full chapter later in this book, and this trick will make those chapters click faster. <strong>ILSpy</strong> and <strong>dotPeek</strong> do the same thing for compiled <code>.dll</code> files you didn&#39;t write yourself.</p>\n<h3 id=\"three-words-that-sound-alike-but-arent-the-same-thing\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/three-words-that-sound-alike-but-arent-the-same-thing\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Three words that sound alike but aren&#39;t the same thing</h3>\n<p>Before moving on: there&#39;s a classic source of confusion worth clearing up here, because it&#39;ll save you grief later.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Project</strong> (<code>.csproj</code>)</td>\n<td>Build instructions: what to compile, what to reference</td>\n<td><code>Shop.Api.csproj</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Assembly</strong></td>\n<td>The compiled <em>output</em> of one project — one file</td>\n<td><code>Shop.Api.dll</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Namespace</strong></td>\n<td>A naming scope <em>inside</em> your code, with no file-system meaning</td>\n<td><code>Shop.Api.Orders</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>These three sound like they should line up one-to-one, and they just don&#39;t. One assembly can quite happily contain many namespaces. One namespace — <code>System.Collections.Generic</code> is a good example — can be spread across several different assemblies. A namespace is purely about keeping names from colliding in your <em>code</em>; it has nothing whatsoever to say about how that code gets packaged or shipped.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-run-time-actually-does-with-all-this\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/what-run-time-actually-does-with-all-this\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What run time actually does with all this <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>So build time is done. You&#39;re sitting on a <code>.dll</code> full of IL and metadata that, remember, no CPU can run yet. Run time is where that finally changes.</p>\n<p>When you start the app, the <strong>CLR</strong> (Common Language Runtime — the engine underneath everything .NET does while it&#39;s actually running) loads your assembly into memory. Here&#39;s the part that surprises people: it does <strong>not</strong> translate every method the moment it loads. It&#39;s lazier than that, on purpose. Watch:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Your code calls <code>Add(2, 3)</code> for the very first time.</li>\n<li>The CLR checks: has this method&#39;s IL ever been translated before? No. So it hands the IL over to the JIT.</li>\n<li>The JIT translates <em>just that one method&#39;s</em> IL into real x64 or Arm64 machine code, right there, for whatever CPU it happens to be running on at that moment.</li>\n<li>That native code gets kept in memory for the rest of the process&#39;s life — it isn&#39;t thrown away after use.</li>\n<li>Every later call to <code>Add</code> just reuses the native code from step 4. No re-translating, ever again, for this process.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>So the translation cost is paid exactly <strong>once per method</strong>, the first time — and only the first time — it&#39;s called. Not once per call. That distinction matters a lot for everything that follows, because almost this entire chapter from here is about what happens around that one moment in step 3, plus one clever alternative that skips it entirely.</p>\n<h2 id=\"meanwhile-the-clr-is-doing-a-lot-more-than-just-this\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/meanwhile-the-clr-is-doing-a-lot-more-than-just-this\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Meanwhile, the CLR is doing a lot more than just this <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Loading assemblies and running the JIT is only one of the CLR&#39;s jobs — it&#39;s just the one this chapter cares about most. The whole time your process is alive, it&#39;s quietly doing several other things underneath your code, continuously:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Service</th>\n<th>What it means for you</th>\n<th>Chapter</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Loading</strong></td>\n<td>Finds and loads assemblies on demand (via <code>AssemblyLoadContext</code>)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>JIT compilation</strong></td>\n<td>IL → machine code, the first time each method is called</td>\n<td>this chapter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Memory management</strong></td>\n<td>Allocates objects on the managed heap; the GC frees them automatically</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/memory-gc\">Memory &amp; GC</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Type safety</strong></td>\n<td>You can&#39;t silently treat an <code>Order</code> as a <code>string</code>; casts are checked; array bounds are checked</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/type-system\">Type system</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Exception handling</strong></td>\n<td>Structured exceptions that unwind cleanly across method calls</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/exceptions-disposal\">Exceptions</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Threading</strong></td>\n<td>Managed threads, the thread pool, synchronisation primitives</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/threading-concurrency\">Threading</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Interop</strong></td>\n<td>Calling native code (P/Invoke), COM</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/advanced-csharp\">Advanced C#</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Security</strong></td>\n<td>Verification, no buffer overruns in safe code</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/web-security\">Web security</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Keep this table in your back pocket — it&#39;s basically a preview of the rest of Part I and II. Nearly every chapter coming up is really just one row of this table, told in full.</p>\n<p>Here&#39;s a definition worth having precisely: code the CLR is doing all of this for is called <strong>managed code</strong>. Anything outside its control — a native C/C++ library, a raw OS file handle, a socket — is <strong>unmanaged code</strong>, and here&#39;s the catch: nothing cleans that up for you automatically. Nobody&#39;s watching it. That single fact is the entire reason <code>IDisposable</code> exists in C#: it&#39;s the language&#39;s way of flagging &quot;this object is holding onto something the CLR genuinely cannot manage on your behalf — you, personally, have to let it go.&quot;</p>\n<h2 id=\"now-zoom-into-the-jit-why-does-it-translate-a-method-twice\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/now-zoom-into-the-jit-why-does-it-translate-a-method-twice\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Now, zoom into the JIT: why does it translate a method <em>twice</em>? <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>You already know, from the run-time walkthrough above, that the JIT translates a method&#39;s IL the first time it&#39;s called. What that walkthrough didn&#39;t mention is this: for most methods, it actually does that translation <strong>twice</strong> — a quick, rough pass first, and then, sometimes, a second, much better pass later. Let&#39;s figure out why anyone would want that.</p>\n<p><strong>Think about the problem first.</strong> If the JIT fully, carefully optimised every single method the very first time it was called, your app would feel painfully slow to start up. Every method on the path from &quot;the process launches&quot; to &quot;the very first response goes out&quot; — <em>including</em> methods that might only ever run once in the app&#39;s whole lifetime — would sit there paying a heavy optimisation tax before anything could happen at all.</p>\n<p><strong>Here&#39;s the fix .NET landed on, since .NET Core 3.0:</strong> compile fast and rough the first time, and only come back and do it properly for the methods that actually prove they&#39;re worth the extra effort.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>first call ──▶ Tier 0: quick, unoptimised code (fast to produce)\n                │  method called ≥ 30 times (and loops hit OSR)\n                ▼\n             Tier 1: fully optimised code (slower to produce, fast to run)</code></pre></figure>\n<p><img src=\"visuals/jit-tiers.svg\" alt=\"How the JIT warms up a method from a quick first draft to a fully optimized rewrite\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"lets-trace-this-with-real-numbers-not-just-the-diagram\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/lets-trace-this-with-real-numbers-not-just-the-diagram\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Let&#39;s trace this with real numbers, not just the diagram</h3>\n<p>Say your <code>PlaceOrder</code> method gets called, over and over, as orders come in. Here&#39;s exactly what happens, call by call:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Call #1:</strong> nothing has been compiled for this method yet, anywhere. The JIT bangs out <strong>Tier 0</strong> code almost instantly — it&#39;s crude and unoptimised, but it&#39;s ready in microseconds. This very first call runs on that.</li>\n<li><strong>Calls #2 through #29:</strong> still running on that same Tier 0 code. The JIT hasn&#39;t done anything more yet — it&#39;s watching, but not acting.</li>\n<li><strong>Call #30:</strong> somewhere around here, the JIT&#39;s internal counter decides enough is enough — this method is genuinely &quot;hot&quot;, called often enough that a better version would actually pay off. It kicks off a proper <strong>Tier 1</strong> recompile, <em>in the background</em>, while calls #30 onward just keep using the existing Tier 0 code. Your app never pauses for this.</li>\n<li><strong>The moment Tier 1 finishes compiling:</strong> every call <em>after</em> that — #45, #100, #10,000, whatever comes next — gets silently switched over to the new, fully optimised version instead.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Notice what this means for a method that&#39;s only ever called twice in your app&#39;s entire life: it never earns a Tier 1 recompile at all, and that&#39;s completely fine — the cost of optimising it properly would never have paid for itself.</p>\n<p>Here&#39;s a trickier case worth thinking through: what if a method gets stuck inside one <em>very</em> long-running loop on its very first call — before it&#39;s ever had the chance to be called a <em>second</em> time and pick up Tier 1 the normal way? It can&#39;t, because it never returns long enough to be &quot;called again&quot;. This is exactly what <strong>OSR (On-Stack Replacement)</strong> is for. The JIT can swap that one <em>already-running</em> method over to its optimised Tier 1 version <strong>mid-loop</strong>, without waiting for it to finish first. The name makes more sense once you know why: the method&#39;s stack frame is still sitting there, mid-execution, &quot;on the stack&quot;, at the exact moment the swap happens underneath it.</p>\n<p>One more wrinkle, briefly: if the assembly was published with <strong>ReadyToRun</strong> (you&#39;ll meet this properly later in the chapter), methods don&#39;t start at Tier 0 at all — they start out already precompiled. But even then, they can still get re-jitted up to Tier 1 later if they turn out to be genuinely hot.</p>\n<h3 id=\"dynamic-pgo-the-jit-actually-learns-from-your-programs-real-\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/dynamic-pgo-the-jit-actually-learns-from-your-programs-real-\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Dynamic PGO: the JIT actually learns from your program&#39;s real behaviour</h3>\n<p>Tiered compilation explains <em>when</em> the JIT bothers recompiling a method. This next part explains something arguably more interesting: how that second compile can be <em>smarter</em> than a generic optimisation pass, because the JIT has been quietly watching.</p>\n<p>Here&#39;s how, and it&#39;s on by default since <strong>.NET 8</strong>: while a method is running at Tier 0, the JIT adds tiny counters to it that record what actually happens, live, each time it runs — which branch of an <code>if</code> usually gets taken, which concrete class usually turns up behind an interface call. <strong>PGO stands for Profile-Guided Optimisation</strong> — and &quot;profile&quot; here means exactly what it means in &quot;user profile&quot;: a recorded summary of real, observed behaviour, not a guess made in advance.</p>\n<p>So when Tier 1 finally compiles, it isn&#39;t optimising blind — it&#39;s optimising <em>for the specific pattern this exact program has already shown it</em>. The clearest, most concrete payoff of this is called <strong>guarded devirtualisation</strong>, and it&#39;s worth slowing down for because it sounds intimidating and really isn&#39;t:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Calling a method through an interface — something like <code>IRepository repo; repo.Get(id);</code> — normally costs a little extra: the CPU can&#39;t know in advance which concrete class&#39;s <code>Get</code> method to actually jump to, because <code>repo</code> could be any class that implements <code>IRepository</code>. Working that out at the last second, every single call, is called <strong>virtual dispatch</strong>. (Haven&#39;t met interfaces and virtual methods properly yet? Here&#39;s the short version: an interface lets totally different classes stand in for each other through one shared contract — great for flexibility, but it means the runtime genuinely has to re-check, every single time, which actual class it&#39;s dealing with.)</li>\n<li>Now suppose Dynamic PGO&#39;s counters show that, in this program, <code>IRepository</code> is <em>almost always</em>, in practice, actually <code>SqlRepository</code>. Tier 1 can then emit a fast shortcut: &quot;quickly check — is this really a <code>SqlRepository</code>? If yes, jump straight there, no lookup needed.&quot; The slow, fully general lookup only ever runs for the rare case where it turns out to be something else.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If you&#39;re wondering whether any of this is just theoretical: it isn&#39;t. This is a genuine, measurable reason why .NET 8 and later apps got faster without a single line of application code changing. The exact same source, simply recompiled targeting .NET 8, started optimising itself around its own observed behaviour automatically.</p>\n<h3 id=\"a-quick-tour-of-the-other-things-the-jit-does-along-the-way\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/a-quick-tour-of-the-other-things-the-jit-does-along-the-way\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>A quick tour of the other things the JIT does along the way</h3>\n<p>You don&#39;t need to memorise this list — just recognise the names when they come up elsewhere:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inlining:</strong> pastes a small method&#39;s body directly into wherever it&#39;s called from, removing the overhead of a separate call entirely. Simple property getters (<code>public int Count =&gt; _count;</code>) almost always get inlined this way — so don&#39;t worry that using a property instead of a raw field is &quot;slower&quot;; by the time Tier 1 is done, it usually isn&#39;t.</li>\n<li><strong>Bounds-check elimination:</strong> a loop like <code>for (int i = 0; i &lt; arr.Length; i++)</code> can safely skip the usual per-access array-bounds check, because the JIT can mathematically prove every access in that loop stays in range.</li>\n<li><strong>Devirtualisation of <code>sealed</code> classes:</strong> the same idea as guarded devirtualisation above, except the JIT doesn&#39;t even need to guess — if a class is marked <code>sealed</code>, there is only ever one possible type, full stop, so the virtual-dispatch check can be skipped entirely rather than just made fast. This is a genuinely real reason to mark classes <code>sealed</code> when you never intended them to be base classes.</li>\n<li><strong>Constant folding, dead-code elimination, loop optimisations:</strong> the classic compiler-textbook tricks you may already know from other languages — .NET just does them here, at JIT time, instead of purely ahead of time.</li>\n<li><strong>SIMD vectorisation</strong>, via <code>Vector128/256/512&lt;T&gt;</code>: crunching several values with a single CPU instruction instead of one at a time. The BCL leans on this constantly behind the scenes — string search, LINQ&#39;s <code>Sum</code>, <code>Span</code> operations all quietly benefit from it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"heres-what-all-of-this-actually-looks-like-in-milliseconds\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/heres-what-all-of-this-actually-looks-like-in-milliseconds\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Here&#39;s what all of this actually looks like in milliseconds</h3>\n<p>Everything above explains <em>why</em> a freshly started process behaves so differently from one that&#39;s been running a while. Let&#39;s make that concrete, because &quot;the first request is slow&quot; is a sentence every .NET backend developer hears constantly, and now you&#39;ll actually know why instead of just repeating it:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Request</th>\n<th>Typical latency</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>1st request ever</strong></td>\n<td>hundreds of ms</td>\n<td>Every method on the path gets jitted at Tier 0 for the first time ever; static constructors, DI&#39;s resolution plans and EF Core&#39;s model are <em>also</em> all being built for the first time; connection pools start completely empty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>~30th request to the same endpoint</strong></td>\n<td>starts dropping noticeably</td>\n<td>The hottest methods on that path have now crossed the Tier 1 threshold and are being recompiled in the background</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Steady state</strong></td>\n<td>single-digit ms</td>\n<td>Everything on the path is running fully optimised Tier 1 code, and every pool is warmed up</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Lay it out this way and the usual list of &quot;fixes&quot; for slow cold starts stops being a random grab-bag you have to memorise — each one maps directly onto one row of that table. <strong>ReadyToRun</strong> and <strong>Native AOT</strong> remove row 1&#39;s Tier 0 cold compile entirely, before it can even happen. Warm-up requests (fired at a deployment slot or a readiness probe before real traffic arrives) absorb row 1&#39;s cost on a request nobody&#39;s actually waiting on. EF Core compiled models specifically remove the &quot;building the model for the first time&quot; piece of row 1. Nothing here is a coincidence — it&#39;s the same underlying cause, attacked from different angles.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-if-you-skipped-the-jit-completely\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/what-if-you-skipped-the-jit-completely\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What if you skipped the JIT completely? <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Here&#39;s a thought worth having explicitly: everything described so far happens <em>as the app runs</em>, method by method, the first time each one is called. What if, instead, you translated absolutely everything, for every single method, before the app ever even starts — so that by the time it&#39;s running, there&#39;s no translation left to do, ever?</p>\n<p>That&#39;s exactly what the next two options do, each taking it further than the last.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>How</th>\n<th>Startup</th>\n<th>Peak perf</th>\n<th>Restrictions</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>JIT (default)</strong></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Slowest</td>\n<td>Best (with PGO)</td>\n<td>None</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ReadyToRun (R2R)</strong></td>\n<td><code>&lt;PublishReadyToRun&gt;true&lt;/PublishReadyToRun&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Faster</td>\n<td>Same as JIT, once Tier 1 kicks in</td>\n<td>Bigger binaries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Native AOT</strong></td>\n<td><code>&lt;PublishAot&gt;true&lt;/PublishAot&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Fastest (milliseconds)</td>\n<td>Good, sometimes even below JIT+PGO</td>\n<td>No runtime code generation, limited reflection, trimming required</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>ReadyToRun</strong> is the gentler of the two. Methods are precompiled and shipped bundled inside the assembly itself, so there&#39;s no Tier 0 cold-start cost sitting in your way for them. But here&#39;s the thing: the JIT doesn&#39;t disappear — it&#39;s still present at runtime, quietly watching, and it can still step in and re-optimise a method up to Tier 1 later if Dynamic PGO&#39;s counters show it&#39;s genuinely hot.</p>\n<p><strong>Native AOT</strong> goes all the way and removes the JIT from the picture, full stop. The entire app — your code, <em>plus</em> whatever bits of the .NET runtime it actually needs to function — gets compiled into one single native executable at publish time. <strong>There is no JIT at runtime. Not a reduced one. None.</strong> Nothing is ever translated on the fly, which is exactly why startup time for a Native AOT app is measured in milliseconds rather than the hundreds you saw in the cold-start table above.</p>\n<p>That speed isn&#39;t free, and it&#39;s worth really understanding the trade-off rather than just filing it away as a rule to follow:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Native AOT needs <strong>trimming</strong> — the process of stripping out every bit of code the app can&#39;t prove it actually uses, so the final executable doesn&#39;t end up enormous.</li>\n<li>Here&#39;s where it bites: reflection, which you met earlier as something metadata makes possible, is precisely the kind of thing a trimmer <em>cannot</em> see coming. If nothing in your <em>visible</em> code ever calls a particular method by name, the trimmer has no way to predict that reflection might go hunting for it later at runtime — so, to keep things small, it just gets removed.</li>\n<li>This is exactly why AOT-friendly libraries lean so heavily on <strong>source generators</strong> instead of reflection: code that gets generated and made fully, explicitly visible at <em>compile</em> time (System.Text.Json&#39;s source generator, the minimal API request delegate generator, the configuration binding generator, <code>[LoggerMessage]</code>) sidesteps the whole problem — there&#39;s nothing left for the trimmer to have to guess about, because it&#39;s right there in plain sight.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So who actually wants Native AOT? It&#39;s a genuinely strong fit for serverless and scale-to-zero workloads, where that cold-start row from the earlier table <em>is</em> the entire problem you&#39;re trying to solve, plus CLI tools, small containers, and high-density hosting where every millisecond and every megabyte counts. It doesn&#39;t yet support MVC controllers or Razor, though, and EF Core support is still limited — both of those rely on patterns that clash with Native AOT&#39;s restrictions in ways that haven&#39;t been fully solved yet.</p>\n<h2 id=\"two-more-services-coming-up-properly-in-their-own-chapters\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/two-more-services-coming-up-properly-in-their-own-chapters\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Two more services, coming up properly in their own chapters <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Two more things the CLR is doing, the whole time, deserve a full chapter each rather than a rushed paragraph here — but you should at least know they exist, and that they&#39;re part of the exact same machinery this chapter has been describing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The GC</strong> (garbage collector): generational, with a &quot;Server GC&quot; mode on by default in ASP.NET Core, responsible for freeing memory your code no longer needs. See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/memory-gc\">Memory &amp; GC</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>The thread pool</strong>: a pool of worker threads that runs your request handlers and every <code>await</code> continuation. Blocking those threads (with <code>.Result</code> or <code>Thread.Sleep</code>) causes <strong>thread pool starvation</strong>, a classic production bug. See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/async-await\">async/await</a> and <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/threading-concurrency\">Threading</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-type-system-across-languages-cts-and-cls\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/the-type-system-across-languages-cts-and-cls\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The type system across languages: CTS and CLS <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Earlier in this chapter, you saw the claim that C#, F# and VB all compile down to the same IL, and can therefore call each other directly with zero friction. It&#39;s a nice claim, but <em>why</em> is it actually true? It&#39;s only possible because all three languages secretly agree, underneath their very different-looking syntax, on exactly what a &quot;type&quot; fundamentally is.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>CTS (Common Type System):</strong> the runtime&#39;s shared rulebook for types — classes, structs, interfaces, enums, delegates, and the value-vs-reference distinction you&#39;ll meet properly in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/type-system\">Type system</a>. Every .NET language maps its own type keywords onto this one shared system underneath. That&#39;s the real reason <code>int</code> in C# and <code>Integer</code> in VB turn out to be, underneath, literally the exact same thing: <code>System.Int32</code>. Not &quot;similar&quot; — the same.</li>\n<li><strong>CLS (Common Language Specification):</strong> a smaller, stricter subset of rules that applies specifically to anything you expose <em>publicly</em>, chosen so that <em>every</em> .NET language — even one with narrower type support than C# has — can still consume it safely. A public <code>uint</code>, for example, actually breaks CLS compliance, because some .NET languages simply don&#39;t have unsigned integers at all. If you want the compiler to warn you whenever you&#39;ve accidentally broken that promise, mark a library with <code>[assembly: CLSCompliant(true)]</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"assembly-loading-and-versioning\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/assembly-loading-and-versioning\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Assembly loading and versioning <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>One last practical layer: a running .NET app isn&#39;t really just &quot;the assembly&quot; on its own — it&#39;s a small set of files that, together, tell the CLR exactly which assembly, and which <em>version</em> of it, to actually load.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Assemblies are identified by name, version, culture and, optionally, a public key token from something called <strong>strong naming</strong>. Worth knowing, but don&#39;t over-invest here: modern .NET mostly ignores strong-name <em>validation</em> at runtime these days — it&#39;s largely a legacy identity mechanism now, not a real security boundary.</li>\n<li><strong><code>AssemblyLoadContext</code></strong> is what replaced .NET Framework&#39;s old AppDomains for isolating loaded assemblies, and for loading and unloading plugins while the app is running.</li>\n<li>Two generated files sit right next to your published app and quietly drive all of this loading. <strong><code>.deps.json</code></strong> lists every dependency assembly your app needs, and exactly where to find each one. <strong><code>.runtimeconfig.json</code></strong> says which shared framework and version the app expects, plus runtime settings like GC mode.</li>\n<li><strong>Roll-forward</strong> decides what happens when the <em>exact</em> version your app was built for isn&#39;t installed on the machine. By default, an app built for <code>8.0.x</code> happily runs on whatever the latest installed <code>8.0</code> <em>patch</em> release is — it rolls forward automatically to pick up fixes, no questions asked. Rolling forward across a <em>major</em> version — say, from 8 to 9 — is a different story: it&#39;s off by default, and has to be explicitly opted into with <code>&lt;RollForward&gt;Major&lt;/RollForward&gt;</code>, precisely because major versions are allowed to carry breaking changes that a patch release never would.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>JSON</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Shop.Api.runtimeconfig.json (generated)</span>\n{\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;runtimeOptions&quot;</span>: {\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;tfm&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;net10.0&quot;</span>,\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;framework&quot;</span>: { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;name&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Microsoft.AspNetCore.App&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;version&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;10.0.0&quot;</span> },\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;configProperties&quot;</span>: { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;System.GC.Server&quot;</span>: true }\n  }\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Explain how C# code gets executed.</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Roslyn compiles C# into IL plus metadata inside an assembly, at build time. At run time the CLR loads the assembly; when a method is first called, the JIT compiles its IL to native machine code — quickly at Tier 0, then re-optimised at Tier 1 if it&#39;s called often (&quot;hot&quot;), using Dynamic PGO data gathered while it ran. The CLR also provides GC, type safety, exceptions and threading throughout the process&#39;s life.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is IL and why does .NET use an intermediate language?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A CPU-independent, stack-based bytecode produced by the compiler instead of final machine code. It makes assemblies portable across OS/CPU (the JIT compiles it for whatever machine it actually runs on), lets multiple languages interoperate because they all compile down to the same format, enables verification and rich metadata (reflection, GC info), and lets the JIT optimise for the actual hardware and runtime behaviour rather than guessing at build time.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is tiered compilation and Dynamic PGO?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Tiered compilation JITs methods first with minimal optimisation (Tier 0) for fast startup, then recompiles frequently called ones with full optimisation (Tier 1) in the background. Dynamic PGO instruments Tier 0 code to observe what actually happens — such as which concrete type sits behind an interface call — and Tier 1 uses that recorded data to devirtualise, inline and lay out code better than a generic optimisation pass could.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the difference between JIT, ReadyToRun and Native AOT?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>JIT compiles at runtime: slowest startup, best peak performance, no restrictions. ReadyToRun ships precompiled code alongside IL for faster startup while keeping the JIT available to re-optimise hot methods later. Native AOT compiles everything to one native binary with no JIT at all: fastest startup and smallest memory, but it requires trimming unused code and avoids runtime reflection and code generation, which is why AOT-friendly libraries use source generators instead.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is managed vs unmanaged code?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Managed code runs under the CLR&#39;s control, which provides GC, type safety and exception handling automatically. Unmanaged code runs outside that control (native libraries, raw OS handles), so nothing frees its resources for you — you must release them explicitly, typically through IDisposable/SafeHandle.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why might marking a class sealed improve performance?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The JIT knows no subclass can ever override its virtual methods, so it can devirtualise calls to it (skip the indirect virtual-dispatch lookup and jump straight to the method) and inline them, and type checks (<code>is</code>/casts) become cheaper since there&#39;s only one possible type to check against.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the difference between an assembly and a namespace?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>An assembly is a compiled deployment unit — one physical .dll file. A namespace is a purely logical naming scope inside your code, with no connection to files or packaging. They&#39;re independent: one assembly can contain many namespaces, and a single namespace can span several different assemblies.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/clr\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Common Language Runtime overview</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/managed-execution-process\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Managed execution process</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/deploying/native-aot/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Native AOT deployment</a></li>\n<li>Book: <em>CLR via C#</em> by Jeffrey Richter, the classic deep dive (written for Framework; the concepts still apply).</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://sharplab.io\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SharpLab</a>: see IL and JIT output for any snippet.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://devblogs.microsoft.com/dotnet/performance-improvements-in-net-8/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Performance Improvements in .NET 8</a> (.NET Blog) — backs the Dynamic PGO / guarded devirtualisation claims in this chapter.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>The JIT&#39;s internal tiering decisions, Dynamic PGO&#39;s counters, and exact ReadyToRun/Native AOT startup numbers aren&#39;t something a plain program can observe about itself from the inside — trust <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/further-reading\">Further reading</a> and tools like SharpLab/dotnet-trace for those. What the program below <em>can</em> check: that a method&#39;s IL really only gets translated once per process (not once per call, by timing a &quot;cold&quot; vs &quot;warm&quot; call), that <code>sealed</code> genuinely changes what the runtime can prove about a type, and that reflection really can see metadata the compiler didn&#39;t hard-code. Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (plain console app, .NET 8/9, no extra packages).</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Diagnostics</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Reflection</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: the first call to a method pays a one-time JIT/warm-up cost; later calls are far cheaper.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> sw = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Stopwatch</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartNew</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> firstCallTicks = sw.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ElapsedTicks</span>;\nsw.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Restart</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; i &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">100_000</span>; i++) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(i, i);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> hundredKCallsTicks = sw.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ElapsedTicks</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> avgWarmCallTicks = hundredKCallsTicks / <span class=\"tk-num\">100_000.0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (avgWarmCallTicks &gt;= firstCallTicks)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected warmed-up calls to be far cheaper per-call than the very first one&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: first call ≈{firstCallTicks} ticks, average of 100,000 later calls ≈{avgWarmCallTicks:F3} ticks each — later calls are far cheaper&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: a sealed class has exactly one possible runtime type, so a type check against it is exact.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">SealedThing</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsSealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: SealedThing should report IsSealed == true&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: the runtime itself reports a sealed type as sealed (nothing can subclass it)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: metadata lets reflection discover a type's shape purely at runtime, with no compile-time reference to it.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Type</span> discovered = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Assembly</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetExecutingAssembly</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetTypes</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">First</span>(t =&gt; t.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;SealedThing&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> prop = discovered.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetProperty</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Value&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (prop <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span> || prop.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PropertyType</span> != <span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>))\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: reflection should have found SealedThing.Value : int from metadata alone&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: reflection discovered a property's name and type purely from assembly metadata&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> a, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> b) =&gt; a + b;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SealedThing</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here — see Further reading:</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - Tier 0 vs Tier 1 compilation actually happening (needs dotnet-trace / ETW events, not a stopwatch)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - Dynamic PGO and guarded devirtualisation (observable via profilers, not plain timing)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - ReadyToRun / Native AOT startup-time comparisons (needs `dotnet publish` with each mode)</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"lets-follow-one-line-of-code-start-to-finish","text":"Let's follow one line of code, start to finish","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"so-why-split-it-into-two-steps-at-all","text":"So why split it into two steps at all?","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"what-build-time-actually-hands-you","text":"What build time actually hands you","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"lets-watch-add-actually-become-il","text":"Let's watch Add actually become IL","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"three-words-that-sound-alike-but-arent-the-same-thing","text":"Three words that sound alike but aren't the same thing","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"what-run-time-actually-does-with-all-this","text":"What run time actually does with all this","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"meanwhile-the-clr-is-doing-a-lot-more-than-just-this","text":"Meanwhile, the CLR is doing a lot more than just this","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"now-zoom-into-the-jit-why-does-it-translate-a-method-twice","text":"Now, zoom into the JIT: why does it translate a method twice ?","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"lets-trace-this-with-real-numbers-not-just-the-diagram","text":"Let's trace this with real numbers, not just the diagram","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"dynamic-pgo-the-jit-actually-learns-from-your-programs-real-","text":"Dynamic PGO: the JIT actually learns from your program's real behaviour","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"a-quick-tour-of-the-other-things-the-jit-does-along-the-way","text":"A quick tour of the other things the JIT does along the way","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"heres-what-all-of-this-actually-looks-like-in-milliseconds","text":"Here's what all of this actually looks like in milliseconds","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"what-if-you-skipped-the-jit-completely","text":"What if you skipped the JIT completely?","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"two-more-services-coming-up-properly-in-their-own-chapters","text":"Two more services, coming up properly in their own chapters","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"the-type-system-across-languages-cts-and-cls","text":"The type system across languages: CTS and CLS","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"assembly-loading-and-versioning","text":"Assembly loading and versioning","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":5984,"minutes":27,"num":"1.2","part":"p1"},"projects-sdk-nuget":{"id":"projects-sdk-nuget","title":"Projects, the SDK, NuGet and the Daily Toolbox","summary":"How a .NET codebase is physically organised: SDK-style project files, MSBuild, solutions, project references, NuGet packages, central package management, shared build settings, and the tools you'll use every day.","layer":"runtime","where":"Runtime & platform › Build system & tooling","fits":"<p>This is the build-time half of layer 2. The runtime runs assemblies; this chapter is about how those assemblies get produced from your source and packages. Architecture decisions (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/app-architectures\">App architectures</a>) are physically enforced here, through project references.</p>\n","prereqs":["what-is-dotnet"],"next":["type-system","app-architectures","cicd-iac"],"levels":["b","i"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/projects-sdk-nuget/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>One <code>.csproj</code> → one assembly. A <code>.sln</code>/<code>.slnx</code> just groups projects for the IDE/build — it has no effect on the compiled output.</li>\n<li>SDK-style project files are short because they include every file in the folder by default and inherit sensible defaults; the csproj only lists what&#39;s <em>different</em>.</li>\n<li><code>Directory.Build.props</code> shares settings across every project in the repo tree; <code>global.json</code> pins the SDK version everyone (and CI) builds with.</li>\n<li>NuGet resolves the full dependency graph on restore: it picks the lowest version that satisfies every constraint, and a direct reference always wins over a transitive one.</li>\n<li><strong>Central Package Management</strong> (<code>Directory.Packages.props</code>) stops different projects in one solution from drifting to different versions of the same package.</li>\n<li>Debug builds skip optimizations for easy debugging; Release builds are optimized — always benchmark and ship Release.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-physical-structure-of-a-net-codebase\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/projects-sdk-nuget/the-physical-structure-of-a-net-codebase\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The physical structure of a .NET codebase <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Shop/                              ← git repository root\n├── Shop.slnx                      ← solution: groups projects for IDE/build\n├── Directory.Build.props          ← settings shared by every project\n├── Directory.Packages.props       ← package versions shared by every project\n├── global.json                    ← pins the SDK version\n├── .editorconfig                  ← code style rules\n├── src/\n│   ├── Shop.Api/Shop.Api.csproj           → Shop.Api.dll\n│   ├── Shop.Application/…csproj           → Shop.Application.dll\n│   ├── Shop.Domain/…csproj                → Shop.Domain.dll\n│   └── Shop.Infrastructure/…csproj        → Shop.Infrastructure.dll\n└── tests/\n    ├── Shop.Domain.Tests/…csproj\n    └── Shop.Api.IntegrationTests/…csproj</code></pre></figure>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>project</strong> (<code>.csproj</code>) produces <strong>one assembly</strong>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>solution</strong> (<code>.sln</code>, or the new XML <strong><code>.slnx</code></strong> format supported since .NET 9 SDK / VS 17.13) groups projects. It is only for tooling; it doesn&#39;t affect the compiled output.</li>\n<li><strong>Project references</strong> say which assembly may use which. They are how architecture rules (Domain depends on nothing) are enforced physically.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"sdk-style-project-files\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/projects-sdk-nuget/sdk-style-project-files\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>SDK-style project files <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>XML</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>&lt;Project <span class=\"tk-ty\">Sdk</span>=<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web&quot;</span>&gt;\n\n  &lt;PropertyGroup&gt;\n    &lt;TargetFramework&gt;net10.<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>&lt;/TargetFramework&gt;\n    &lt;Nullable&gt;enable&lt;/Nullable&gt;\n    &lt;ImplicitUsings&gt;enable&lt;/ImplicitUsings&gt;\n    &lt;RootNamespace&gt;Shop.Api&lt;/RootNamespace&gt;\n    &lt;UserSecretsId&gt;7b8f…&lt;/UserSecretsId&gt;\n  &lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;\n\n  &lt;ItemGroup&gt;\n    &lt;PackageReference <span class=\"tk-ty\">Include</span>=<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Serilog.AspNetCore&quot;</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Version</span>=<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;9.0.0&quot;</span> /&gt;\n    &lt;ProjectReference <span class=\"tk-ty\">Include</span>=<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;..\\Shop.Application\\Shop.Application.csproj&quot;</span> /&gt;\n    &lt;ProjectReference <span class=\"tk-ty\">Include</span>=<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;..\\Shop.Infrastructure\\Shop.Infrastructure.csproj&quot;</span> /&gt;\n  &lt;/ItemGroup&gt;\n\n  &lt;ItemGroup&gt;\n    &lt;InternalsVisibleTo <span class=\"tk-ty\">Include</span>=<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop.Api.IntegrationTests&quot;</span> /&gt;\n  &lt;/ItemGroup&gt;\n\n&lt;/Project&gt;</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Element</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Sdk=&quot;Microsoft.NET.Sdk&quot;</code></td>\n<td>Plain library/console app</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Sdk=&quot;Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web&quot;</code></td>\n<td>Adds the ASP.NET Core shared framework, web defaults, <code>launchSettings.json</code> support</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Sdk=&quot;Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Worker&quot;</code></td>\n<td>Worker service (background processing)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Sdk=&quot;Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor&quot;</code> / <code>BlazorWebAssembly</code></td>\n<td>Razor class libraries / Blazor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&lt;TargetFramework&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Which .NET you build for (<code>net10.0</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&lt;Nullable&gt;enable</code></td>\n<td>Turn on nullable reference type analysis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&lt;ImplicitUsings&gt;enable</code></td>\n<td>Auto-add common <code>using</code>s (<code>System</code>, <code>System.Linq</code>, <code>Microsoft.AspNetCore.Builder</code>…) as <strong>global usings</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&lt;PackageReference&gt;</code></td>\n<td>A NuGet dependency</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>&lt;ProjectReference&gt;</code></td>\n<td>A dependency on another project in the repo</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-why\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Why SDK-style projects are short</div><p>Old .NET Framework projects listed every <code>.cs</code> file explicitly, so adding a new file meant editing the project file too — and that caused constant merge conflicts. SDK-style projects fix this by <strong>including every file in the folder by default</strong> (globbing) and importing sensible defaults from the SDK. The csproj then only needs to list what&#39;s <em>different</em> from the defaults, which is why they&#39;re short enough to read and edit by hand.</p>\n</aside>\n<h3 id=\"msbuild-the-engine-under-the-cli\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/projects-sdk-nuget/msbuild-the-engine-under-the-cli\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>MSBuild: the engine under the CLI</h3>\n<p><code>dotnet build</code> runs <strong>MSBuild</strong>, which evaluates the project XML (properties, items, targets) and executes <strong>targets</strong> (Restore → Compile → Copy → Publish). Useful things to know:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Properties</strong> (<code>&lt;PropertyGroup&gt;</code>) are scalar settings; <strong>items</strong> (<code>&lt;ItemGroup&gt;</code>) are lists (files, references).</li>\n<li>You can pass properties on the command line: <code>dotnet build -p:Version=1.2.3</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Conditions</strong> let settings vary: <code>&lt;PropertyGroup Condition=&quot;&#39;$(Configuration)&#39; == &#39;Release&#39;&quot;&gt;</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Build configurations</strong>: <code>Debug</code> (no optimisation, full debug info) and <code>Release</code> (optimised). Always benchmark and ship Release.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"sharing-settings-directory-build-props-and-global-json\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/projects-sdk-nuget/sharing-settings-directory-build-props-and-global-json\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Sharing settings: Directory.Build.props and global.json <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>MSBuild automatically imports the nearest <strong><code>Directory.Build.props</code></strong> walking up from each project. Put repo-wide settings there instead of repeating them in every csproj:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>XML</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>&lt;!-- Directory.Build.props at the repo root --&gt;\n&lt;Project&gt;\n  &lt;PropertyGroup&gt;\n    &lt;TargetFramework&gt;net10.<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>&lt;/TargetFramework&gt;\n    &lt;Nullable&gt;enable&lt;/Nullable&gt;\n    &lt;ImplicitUsings&gt;enable&lt;/ImplicitUsings&gt;\n    &lt;TreatWarningsAsErrors&gt;true&lt;/TreatWarningsAsErrors&gt;\n    &lt;AnalysisLevel&gt;latest-recommended&lt;/AnalysisLevel&gt;\n    &lt;EnforceCodeStyleInBuild&gt;true&lt;/EnforceCodeStyleInBuild&gt;\n    &lt;LangVersion&gt;latest&lt;/LangVersion&gt;\n  &lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;\n&lt;/Project&gt;</code></pre></figure>\n<p><code>Directory.Build.targets</code> is the same idea but imported <em>after</em> the project (for targets and overrides).</p>\n<p><strong><code>global.json</code></strong> pins the SDK version so everyone and CI build with the same compiler:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>JSON</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>{ <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;sdk&quot;</span>: { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;version&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;10.0.100&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;rollForward&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;latestFeature&quot;</span> } }</code></pre></figure>\n<h2 id=\"the-dotnet-cli\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/projects-sdk-nuget/the-dotnet-cli\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The dotnet CLI <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Command</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>dotnet new list</code> / <code>dotnet new webapi -n Shop.Api</code></td>\n<td>List templates / create a project</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dotnet new sln</code> / <code>dotnet sln add src/**/*.csproj</code></td>\n<td>Create a solution / add projects</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dotnet add reference ../Shop.Domain</code></td>\n<td>Add a project reference</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dotnet add package Polly</code></td>\n<td>Add a NuGet package</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dotnet restore</code></td>\n<td>Download packages (also runs implicitly on build)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dotnet build -c Release</code></td>\n<td>Compile</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dotnet run</code> / <code>dotnet watch</code></td>\n<td>Run / run with hot reload</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dotnet test --filter &quot;Category=Unit&quot;</code></td>\n<td>Run tests</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dotnet publish -c Release -o out</code></td>\n<td>Produce deployable output</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dotnet publish /t:PublishContainer</code></td>\n<td>Build a container image without a Dockerfile</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dotnet format</code></td>\n<td>Apply <code>.editorconfig</code> style and analyzer fixes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dotnet list package --outdated --vulnerable</code></td>\n<td>Audit dependencies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dotnet tool install -g dotnet-ef</code></td>\n<td>Install a global tool</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dotnet user-secrets set &quot;Key&quot; &quot;Value&quot;</code></td>\n<td>Store dev secrets outside the repo</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dotnet dev-certs https --trust</code></td>\n<td>Trust the local HTTPS dev certificate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Local tools</strong> (<code>dotnet new tool-manifest</code>, then <code>dotnet tool install dotnet-ef</code>) are pinned per repo in <code>.config/dotnet-tools.json</code> and restored with <code>dotnet tool restore</code>. They&#39;re better than global tools for team consistency.</p>\n<h2 id=\"nuget-in-depth\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/projects-sdk-nuget/nuget-in-depth\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>NuGet in depth <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><strong>NuGet</strong> is .NET&#39;s package manager. A <strong>package</strong> (<code>.nupkg</code>) is a zip containing compiled assemblies for one or more TFMs (<code>lib/net8.0/</code>, <code>lib/netstandard2.0/</code>), a <code>.nuspec</code> manifest with its dependencies, and optionally build props, analyzers or content files.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Feeds:</strong> nuget.org (public), plus private feeds such as Azure Artifacts, GitHub Packages or JFrog, configured in <code>nuget.config</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Restore</strong> resolves the full dependency graph and writes <code>obj/project.assets.json</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Transitive dependencies:</strong> packages your packages depend on. You get them automatically.</li>\n<li><strong>Version resolution:</strong> for each package, NuGet picks the <em>lowest</em> version that satisfies all constraints (&quot;lowest applicable version&quot;). A direct reference wins over a transitive one (&quot;direct dependency wins&quot;).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"central-package-management-cpm\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/projects-sdk-nuget/central-package-management-cpm\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Central Package Management (CPM) <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h3>\n<p>Here&#39;s the problem CPM solves: in a multi-project solution, it&#39;s easy for one project to reference <code>EntityFrameworkCore 9.0.1</code> while another references <code>9.0.3</code>. At runtime that mismatch can cause binding errors that are confusing to diagnose, because the symptom shows up far from the actual cause. <strong>CPM</strong> fixes this by putting every package&#39;s version in one shared file, so there&#39;s only ever one version to drift from:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>XML</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>&lt;!-- Directory.Packages.props --&gt;\n&lt;Project&gt;\n  &lt;PropertyGroup&gt;\n    &lt;ManagePackageVersionsCentrally&gt;true&lt;/ManagePackageVersionsCentrally&gt;\n    &lt;CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled&gt;true&lt;/CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled&gt;\n  &lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;\n  &lt;ItemGroup&gt;\n    &lt;PackageVersion <span class=\"tk-ty\">Include</span>=<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer&quot;</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Version</span>=<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;10.0.0&quot;</span> /&gt;\n    &lt;PackageVersion <span class=\"tk-ty\">Include</span>=<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FluentValidation&quot;</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Version</span>=<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;12.0.0&quot;</span> /&gt;\n    &lt;PackageVersion <span class=\"tk-ty\">Include</span>=<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;xunit.v3&quot;</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Version</span>=<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;3.0.0&quot;</span> /&gt;\n  &lt;/ItemGroup&gt;\n&lt;/Project&gt;</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>XML</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>&lt;!-- any .<span class=\"tk-ty\">csproj</span>: no Version attribute --&gt;\n&lt;PackageReference <span class=\"tk-ty\">Include</span>=<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer&quot;</span> /&gt;</code></pre></figure>\n<p><strong>Transitive pinning</strong> lets you upgrade a vulnerable transitive package centrally without referencing it directly everywhere.</p>\n<h3 id=\"package-hygiene\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/projects-sdk-nuget/package-hygiene\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Package hygiene <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lock files</strong> (<code>&lt;RestorePackagesWithLockFile&gt;true&lt;/RestorePackagesWithLockFile&gt;</code> → <code>packages.lock.json</code>) make restores reproducible: everyone and CI resolve to the exact same package versions, not just versions that satisfy the constraints. Use <code>--locked-mode</code> in CI so a build fails loudly instead of silently resolving something new.</li>\n<li><strong>Vulnerability auditing:</strong> <code>dotnet restore</code> warns about known CVEs (NuGet Audit, on by default since .NET 8). Fail the build on them in CI.</li>\n<li><strong>Licences:</strong> check them before adopting a dependency. Some popular libraries have moved parts of their functionality to commercial licenses in recent years (check each library&#39;s current license page — this changes over time, so don&#39;t rely on this book for the current status of any specific package). Interviewers sometimes ask how you evaluate a new dependency, and licensing is part of a real answer.</li>\n<li><strong>Package source mapping</strong> in <code>nuget.config</code> stops dependency-confusion attacks (where an attacker publishes a malicious package with the same name as an internal one, hoping your build pulls theirs instead) by mapping package ID prefixes to specific feeds.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"creating-your-own-package\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/projects-sdk-nuget/creating-your-own-package\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Creating your own package</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Shell</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>dotnet pack -c Release -<span class=\"tk-ty\">p</span>:<span class=\"tk-ty\">PackageVersion</span>=<span class=\"tk-num\">1.4.0</span>\ndotnet nuget push bin/Release/Shop.Contracts.<span class=\"tk-num\">1.4.0</span>.nupkg <span class=\"tk-attr\">--source</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;company-feed&quot;</span> <span class=\"tk-attr\">--api-key</span> az</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Companies often publish a shared <strong>contracts</strong> package (message types, DTOs) used by several services.</p>\n<h2 id=\"code-quality-tooling\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/projects-sdk-nuget/code-quality-tooling\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Code quality tooling <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Tool</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Roslyn analyzers</strong> (built in)</td>\n<td>Warnings for bugs, performance and API misuse (<code>CA1848</code> use LoggerMessage, <code>CA2007</code> ConfigureAwait…)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><code>.editorconfig</code></strong></td>\n<td>Naming, formatting and severity of each rule; enforced in IDE and build</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><code>dotnet format</code></strong></td>\n<td>Applies fixes from the command line / CI</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Third-party analyzers</strong></td>\n<td>SonarAnalyzer, Roslynator, Meziantou.Analyzer, StyleCop</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SonarQube / SonarCloud</strong></td>\n<td>Quality gates, coverage, code smells in CI</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Nullable warnings as errors</strong></td>\n<td>Prevents most <code>NullReferenceException</code>s</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>ini</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-com\"># .editorconfig</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">root</span> = true\n[*.cs]\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">indent_style</span> = space\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">indent_size</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">4</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">csharp_style_namespace_declarations</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">file_scoped</span>:warning\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">dotnet_diagnostic.CA2016.severity</span> = error   <span class=\"tk-com\"># forward CancellationToken</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">dotnet_naming_rule.private_fields_underscore.severity</span> = warning</code></pre></figure>\n<h2 id=\"the-daily-toolbox\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/projects-sdk-nuget/the-daily-toolbox\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The daily toolbox <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Job</th>\n<th>Tools</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>IDE</strong></td>\n<td>Visual Studio 2022/2026 (Windows), JetBrains <strong>Rider</strong> (all OSes), <strong>VS Code + C# Dev Kit</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Call your API</strong></td>\n<td><code>.http</code> files (built into VS, Rider, VS Code), Postman, Bruno, Insomnia, <code>curl</code>; Scalar/Swagger UI from OpenAPI</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Inspect compiled code</strong></td>\n<td>ILSpy, dotPeek, SharpLab.io</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Databases</strong></td>\n<td>SSMS, Azure Data Studio (being retired in favour of the VS Code MSSQL extension), DBeaver, DataGrip, <code>dotnet ef</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Containers</strong></td>\n<td>Docker Desktop / Podman / Rancher Desktop, <code>docker compose</code>, .NET Aspire</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Diagnostics</strong></td>\n<td><code>dotnet-counters</code>, <code>dotnet-trace</code>, <code>dotnet-dump</code>, <code>dotnet-gcdump</code>, PerfView, IDE profilers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Benchmark / load</strong></td>\n<td>BenchmarkDotNet; k6, NBomber, JMeter, Azure Load Testing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Source control &amp; CI</strong></td>\n<td>Git, GitHub/Azure DevOps, GitHub Actions/Azure Pipelines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cloud CLIs</strong></td>\n<td>Azure CLI (<code>az</code>), Azure Developer CLI (<code>azd</code>), <code>kubectl</code>, Bicep/Terraform</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>HTTP</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-com\">### Shop.Api.http: committed next to the project so anyone can try endpoints</span>\n@<span class=\"tk-ty\">host</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">https</span>:<span class=\"tk-com\">//localhost:7180</span>\n@<span class=\"tk-ty\">token</span> = {{$dotenv TOKEN}}\n\nGET {{host}}/orders?<span class=\"tk-ty\">page</span>=<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>&amp;<span class=\"tk-ty\">pageSize</span>=<span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Authorization</span>: Bearer {{token}}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">###</span>\nPOST {{host}}/orders\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Content-Type</span>: application/json\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Authorization</span>: Bearer {{token}}\n\n{ <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;customerId&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;lines&quot;</span>: [{ <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;sku&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;KB-01&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;qty&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> }] }</code></pre></figure>\n<h2 id=\"launchsettings-json-and-environments\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/projects-sdk-nuget/launchsettings-json-and-environments\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>launchSettings.json and environments <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><code>Properties/launchSettings.json</code> is <strong>only used on your dev machine</strong> (by <code>dotnet run</code> and the IDE). It sets URLs and environment variables such as <code>ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Development</code>. It is not deployed, and production ignores it. Production gets settings from real environment variables, App Service configuration or Kubernetes manifests. See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/configuration-options\">Configuration</a>.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the difference between a project, a solution and an assembly?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A project (.csproj) is the build definition for one assembly. An assembly (.dll) is the compiled output. A solution groups projects for the IDE and for building together; it has no effect on the output.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is Directory.Build.props for?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>MSBuild imports it automatically into every project in that folder tree. Teams use it for shared settings such as target framework, nullable, warnings-as-errors, analyzers and versioning, so each csproj stays small and consistent.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you keep NuGet package versions consistent across many projects?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Central Package Management: <code>Directory.Packages.props</code> with <code>ManagePackageVersionsCentrally</code>, versions declared once with <code>PackageVersion</code>, and csproj files referencing packages without versions. Add transitive pinning and lock files for reproducibility.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you handle a vulnerable transitive dependency?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Find it with <code>dotnet list package --vulnerable --include-transitive</code>. Upgrade the parent package if a fixed version exists; otherwise pin the transitive package to a patched version (directly, or via CPM transitive pinning). Fail CI on NuGet audit warnings to catch it early.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What&#39;s the difference between Debug and Release builds?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Debug disables optimisations and emits full debugging info, so code maps one-to-one to source. Release enables compiler and JIT optimisations (inlining, dead-code removal). Performance measurements and deployments must use Release.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you enforce code style across a team?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>An .editorconfig in the repo, analyzers enabled with <code>AnalysisLevel</code> and <code>EnforceCodeStyleInBuild</code>, warnings as errors in CI, <code>dotnet format --verify-no-changes</code> in the pipeline, plus optional SonarQube quality gates.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/projects-sdk-nuget/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/project-sdk/overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">.NET project SDKs</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/nuget/consume-packages/central-package-management\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Central Package Management</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/tools/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">dotnet CLI overview</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/fundamentals/code-analysis/overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Code analysis in .NET</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/projects-sdk-nuget/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (plain console app, .NET 8/9, BCL only). This can&#39;t exercise a real MSBuild/NuGet restore, but it can verify the two resolution rules the chapter states, plus the Debug/Release mechanism itself.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: Debug builds skip optimizations and keep full debug info; Release builds optimize.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// The DEBUG symbol is exactly how the compiler tells your own code which configuration built it.</span>\n#<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DEBUG</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: this is a Debug build (DEBUG symbol defined) — run `dotnet run -c Release` and this branch disappears entirely&quot;</span>);\n#<span class=\"tk-kw\">else</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: this is a Release build (DEBUG symbol not defined) — optimizations are on&quot;</span>);\n#endif\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: NuGet picks the LOWEST version that satisfies every constraint (&quot;lowest applicable version&quot;),</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// and a DIRECT reference always wins over a TRANSITIVE one, even if the transitive one asks for something newer.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Model NuGet's resolution rules directly (simplified, but the same two rules described in the chapter).</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> constraints = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">PackageRef</span>&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Newtonsoft.Json&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Version</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">12</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>), <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsDirect</span>: <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>), <span class=\"tk-com\">// transitive: wants &gt;= 12.0.0</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Newtonsoft.Json&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Version</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">13</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>), <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsDirect</span>: <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>), <span class=\"tk-com\">// transitive: wants &gt;= 13.0.1 (higher wins among these)</span>\n};\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> resolvedWithoutDirect = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Resolve</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Newtonsoft.Json&quot;</span>, constraints);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (resolvedWithoutDirect != <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Version</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">13</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>))\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected the highest transitive floor (13.0.1) to win among transitive-only constraints, got {resolvedWithoutDirect}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: with no direct reference, resolution settles on {resolvedWithoutDirect} (the highest of the transitive floors)&quot;</span>);\n\nconstraints.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Newtonsoft.Json&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Version</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">11</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>), <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsDirect</span>: <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>)); <span class=\"tk-com\">// app adds its OWN older direct reference</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> resolvedWithDirect = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Resolve</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Newtonsoft.Json&quot;</span>, constraints);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (resolvedWithDirect != <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Version</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">11</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>))\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected the direct reference (11.0.0) to win over transitive ones, got {resolvedWithDirect}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: adding a direct reference ({resolvedWithDirect}) overrides the transitive versions, even though it's lower — \\&quot;direct dependency wins\\&quot;&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here (needs real MSBuild/NuGet restore, a multi-project solution, or CI) — see Further reading:</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - Real SDK-style project globbing, Directory.Build.props inheritance, actual NuGet restore/version resolution</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - Central Package Management end to end</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - dotnet CLI commands and their real behavior</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All checkable claims verified.&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Version</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Resolve</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> packageId, <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">PackageRef</span>&gt; allConstraintsForThisPackage)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> r <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> allConstraintsForThisPackage)\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsDirect</span> &amp;&amp; r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> == packageId) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MinVersion</span>; <span class=\"tk-com\">// direct dependency wins outright</span>\n\n    <span class=\"tk-com\">// otherwise: lowest version that satisfies every transitive constraint</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> allConstraintsForThisPackage.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(r =&gt; r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> == packageId).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Max</span>(r =&gt; r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MinVersion</span>)!;\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PackageRef</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Version</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">MinVersion</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsDirect</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-physical-structure-of-a-net-codebase","text":"The physical structure of a .NET codebase","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"sdk-style-project-files","text":"SDK-style project files","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"msbuild-the-engine-under-the-cli","text":"MSBuild: the engine under the CLI","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"sharing-settings-directory-build-props-and-global-json","text":"Sharing settings: Directory.Build.props and global.json","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"the-dotnet-cli","text":"The dotnet CLI","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"nuget-in-depth","text":"NuGet in depth","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"central-package-management-cpm","text":"Central Package Management (CPM)","depth":3,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"package-hygiene","text":"Package hygiene","depth":3,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"creating-your-own-package","text":"Creating your own package","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"code-quality-tooling","text":"Code quality tooling","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"the-daily-toolbox","text":"The daily toolbox","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"launchsettings-json-and-environments","text":"launchSettings.json and environments","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":2431,"minutes":11,"num":"1.3","part":"p1"},"type-system":{"id":"type-system","title":"The Type System: Value Types, Reference Types and Everything Between","summary":"The most important idea in C#. How data is stored and copied, stack vs heap, structs vs classes vs records, boxing, nullable value types, enums, equality, and parameter passing with ref/out/in.","layer":"language","where":"C# language › Types › Value vs reference","fits":"<p>This is the foundation of layer 1, and it touches layer 2 directly: how the runtime lays out memory, and how much work the GC (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/memory-gc\">Memory &amp; GC</a>) does, depends on the choices made here. Every later chapter assumes you know what &quot;copy semantics&quot; means.</p>\n","prereqs":["how-code-runs"],"next":["oop","generics","memory-gc"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/type-system/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Every type in C# answers one question: does assignment copy the <strong>data</strong> (value types: <code>struct</code>, <code>int</code>, <code>enum</code>, …) or copy a <strong>reference</strong> to shared data (reference types: <code>class</code>, <code>string</code>, arrays, …)?</li>\n<li>&quot;Value types live on the stack&quot; is a popular simplification, not the real rule: value types live wherever their <em>container</em> lives — which can be the heap (a struct field inside a class, or inside an array). Physically: the <strong>stack</strong> is a small, fixed, per-thread block (≈1 MiB by default on Windows); the <strong>heap</strong> is one shared, growable region per process that the GC manages.</li>\n<li><strong>Boxing</strong> wraps a value type in a heap object so it can pretend to be an <code>object</code>/interface; it&#39;s an easy-to-miss allocation (<code>ArrayList.Add</code>, <code>string.Format</code> with value args) and generics (<code>List&lt;int&gt;</code>) exist partly to avoid it.</li>\n<li>Three different things all look like &quot;equality&quot; in C# — <code>==</code>, <code>Equals</code>, <code>ReferenceEquals</code> — and classes/structs/records each default to different answers; overriding <code>Equals</code> obligates you to override <code>GetHashCode</code> too.</li>\n<li>Everything is passed <strong>by value</strong>, including references — so a method can mutate an object through a reference parameter but can&#39;t make the caller&#39;s variable point somewhere else unless you add <code>ref</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"one-question-that-predicts-a-huge-amount-of-c-behaviour\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/type-system/one-question-that-predicts-a-huge-amount-of-c-behaviour\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>One question that predicts a huge amount of C# behaviour <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Here&#39;s a question worth asking about <em>any</em> type in C#: when you assign one variable to another, do you get an independent copy, or two names for the same thing? The answer splits every single type in the language into exactly two camps, and once you know which camp a type is in, you can predict how it behaves in dozens of situations you haven&#39;t even seen yet.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Value types</th>\n<th>Reference types</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Examples</strong></td>\n<td><code>int</code>, <code>long</code>, <code>double</code>, <code>decimal</code>, <code>bool</code>, <code>char</code>, <code>DateTime</code>, <code>Guid</code>, <code>TimeSpan</code>, any <code>struct</code>, any <code>enum</code>, tuples <code>(int, string)</code>, <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td><code>class</code>, <code>string</code>, <code>object</code>, arrays (<code>int[]</code>), delegates, <code>record</code> (class), interfaces, <code>dynamic</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Variable holds</strong></td>\n<td>The data itself</td>\n<td>A reference (an address) to an object on the heap</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Assignment copies</strong></td>\n<td>The whole value</td>\n<td>Only the reference; both variables now point at the same object</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Default value</strong></td>\n<td>All bits zero (<code>0</code>, <code>false</code>, <code>default(DateTime)</code>)</td>\n<td><code>null</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Can be null?</strong></td>\n<td>No, unless wrapped as <code>Nullable&lt;T&gt;</code> (<code>int?</code>)</td>\n<td>Yes (but see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/modern-csharp\">nullable reference types</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Inheritance</strong></td>\n<td>Can&#39;t inherit or be inherited (can implement interfaces)</td>\n<td>Single inheritance of classes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Allocated</strong></td>\n<td>Inline: in the containing object, array, or stack frame</td>\n<td>Always on the managed heap; freed by the GC</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Let&#39;s actually watch the difference happen instead of just reading about it:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Run as-is: a complete program, not a fragment.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">struct</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PointS</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">X</span>; }     <span class=\"tk-com\">// value type</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span>  <span class=\"tk-ty\">PointC</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">X</span>; }     <span class=\"tk-com\">// reference type</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> s1 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PointS</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">X</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> };\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> s2 = s1;          <span class=\"tk-com\">// copies the 4 bytes that make up X — s2 is now independent of s1</span>\ns2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">X</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">99</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(s1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">X</span>); <span class=\"tk-com\">// 1   ← independent copy</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> c1 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PointC</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">X</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> };\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> c2 = c1;          <span class=\"tk-com\">// copies only the 8-byte reference — c1 and c2 now point at the SAME object</span>\nc2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">X</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">99</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(c1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">X</span>); <span class=\"tk-com\">// 99  ← same object, seen through a second variable</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Both lines do <code>var x2 = x1; x2.X = 99;</code>. Both <em>look</em> identical. And yet <code>s1.X</code> stays <code>1</code> while <code>c1.X</code> becomes <code>99</code> — the exact same two lines of code, producing opposite outcomes, purely because <code>struct</code> and <code>class</code> disagree about what <code>=</code> actually copies. For <code>PointS</code>, <code>s2 = s1</code> copies the 4 bytes that make up <code>X</code>, so <code>s2</code> is a completely independent value from that point on. For <code>PointC</code>, <code>c2 = c1</code> copies only the <em>reference</em> — a pointer-sized address — so <code>c1</code> and <code>c2</code> end up pointing at the exact same object in memory, and changing one changes what you see through the other.</p>\n<p>This exact mix-up is a real, common bug: someone treats a <em>class</em> like it&#39;s a cheap, independent value — hands what they think is a &quot;copy&quot; of an <code>OrderTotals</code> object to a background job — without realising the dashboard and the job are now both quietly editing the exact same object. Had <code>OrderTotals</code> been an immutable <code>struct</code> or <code>record</code>, that bug couldn&#39;t have happened: there&#39;d have been no shared object left to edit by accident.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-analogy\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Value vs reference</div><p>A value type is like <strong>handing someone a photocopy</strong> of a document: they can scribble all over it and your original stays untouched. A reference type is like <strong>handing someone the address of a shared Google Doc</strong>: you each hold your own copy of the <em>address</em>, but you&#39;re both looking at, and editing, the exact same document.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"value-types-live-on-the-stack-a-useful-lie\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/type-system/value-types-live-on-the-stack-a-useful-lie\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>&quot;Value types live on the stack&quot; — a useful lie <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>You will hear this sentence constantly: &quot;value types live on the stack, reference types live on the heap.&quot; It&#39;s a reasonable first approximation, and it&#39;s <strong>not actually true</strong> — knowing the precise version, and <em>why</em> the simple version is wrong, is a genuinely good way to stand out in an interview.</p>\n<p>First, what the stack and heap actually are <em>physically</em>, since the sentence above leans on both without saying where either one actually lives:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The stack</strong> is a small, fixed-size block of memory the operating system reserves <strong>per thread</strong>, the moment that thread is created — on Windows the default is 1 MiB for the main thread (configurable per-thread, and other platforms pick their own default); it isn&#39;t shared with any other thread. Local variables and parameters live in <strong>stack frames</strong> inside it, one frame per currently-running method call, stacked on top of each other exactly like the name suggests. Calling a method pushes a new frame on top; returning pops it straight back off. Allocating on the stack is just moving one pointer up or down — there&#39;s no GC involved at all, which is why it&#39;s essentially free.</li>\n<li><strong>The managed heap</strong>, by contrast, is <strong>one shared region per process</strong>, not per thread — every thread in your program allocates objects into the same heap. It isn&#39;t fixed-size: the CLR asks the operating system for address space in large chunks as it needs more, and organises what it gets into GC <strong>generations</strong> (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/memory-gc\">Memory &amp; GC</a> covers exactly how). An object placed there outlives the method call that created it — it survives for as long as <em>anything, anywhere</em> still has a reference to it, which the GC tracks and eventually reclaims once nothing does.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><img src=\"visuals/stack-and-heap-residents.svg\" alt=\"A stack frame for one method call sitting next to the shared heap: a local value lives inline in the frame, while two reference variables in that same frame point into heap objects that will outlive the frame once it&#39;s popped\"></p>\n<p>That picture is the one fact worth memorising from this section: a stack frame and the references inside it vanish together, the instant the method returns — but whatever those references <em>pointed at</em> doesn&#39;t necessarily vanish with them. It only disappears once nothing, anywhere in the whole process, still points to it.</p>\n<p>Here&#39;s the precise rule, and it&#39;s genuinely simpler than the popular version once you see it stated correctly: <strong>reference-type objects always live on the heap. Value types live wherever their container lives</strong> — which might be a stack frame, but might just as easily be a slot inside a heap object.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Quantity</span>;        <span class=\"tk-com\">// value type field: lives INSIDE the Order object, on the heap</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTime</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreatedAt</span>;  <span class=\"tk-com\">// same</span>\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">M</span>()\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> local = <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>;              <span class=\"tk-com\">// local value type: in M's stack frame (or a CPU register)</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> order = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>();    <span class=\"tk-com\">// the reference 'order' is on the stack; the Order object is on the heap</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>[] nums = { <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span> };   <span class=\"tk-com\">// the array object (with its 3 ints inline) is on the heap</span>\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Look at <code>Order.Quantity</code> closely: it&#39;s an <code>int</code>, a value type — and yet it&#39;s sitting on the heap, because an <code>Order</code> <em>object</em> is a reference type, and <code>Quantity</code> lives wherever its containing <code>Order</code> lives. The value type didn&#39;t move to the heap on its own; it&#39;s just physically embedded inside something that did. That&#39;s the whole rule, applied consistently: a value type&#39;s location is never decided by the value type itself, only by whatever&#39;s holding it.</p>\n<p>There are a couple of exceptions worth flagging now, even though they&#39;re explained properly in later chapters: locals <strong>captured by a lambda</strong> (a closure) and locals inside <strong><code>async</code> methods or iterators</strong> stop being ordinary stack locals and become fields of a compiler-generated object on the heap instead. You&#39;ll see exactly why, and exactly what that generated object looks like, in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/delegates-events\">Delegates</a> and <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/async-await\">async/await</a>.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-why\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Why this distinction actually matters in backend code</div><p>Every heap allocation adds work for the garbage collector — you saw the mechanics of that in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/memory-gc\">Memory &amp; GC</a>. If a hot API endpoint allocates thousands of small temporary objects per request, you get frequent GCs and the latency spikes that come with them. Modelling small, short-lived data as structs (or processing it with <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code>) sidesteps that, because a struct embedded in a stack frame never touches the GC at all. But the trade-off genuinely runs the other way too: copying a <em>big</em> struct around is slower than copying an 8-byte reference, because you&#39;re copying the whole thing every single time instead of just an address. There&#39;s no universally right answer here — pick whichever shape actually fits the size and lifetime of your data.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"when-a-struct-actually-earns-its-keep\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/type-system/when-a-struct-actually-earns-its-keep\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>When a struct actually earns its keep <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Given that trade-off, when should you actually reach for <code>struct</code> instead of the default, <code>class</code>? The <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/design-guidelines/choosing-between-class-and-struct\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Framework Design Guidelines</a> spell out four conditions, word for word, and the honest reading of their own wording is: <strong>avoid</strong> a struct <em>unless</em> all four hold.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>It logically represents a single value (a point, money, a coordinate, an ID).</li>\n<li>Its instance size is <strong>under 16 bytes</strong>.</li>\n<li>It is <strong>immutable</strong>.</li>\n<li>It won&#39;t be boxed frequently (you&#39;ll meet boxing properly in the next section).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Fail even one of those, and a class is almost always the better choice.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">struct</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Amount</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Currency</span>)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">operator</span> +(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span> a, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span> b) =&gt;\n        a.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Currency</span> == b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Currency</span> ? <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(a.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Amount</span> + b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Amount</span>, a.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Currency</span>)\n                                 : <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Currency mismatch&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">struct</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>)   <span class=\"tk-com\">// strongly typed ID: can't mix up with CustomerId</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>();\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Two keywords in those examples are doing real work, so let&#39;s take each on its own.</p>\n<p><code>readonly struct</code> is a compiler-enforced promise: nothing about this type can ever change after it&#39;s constructed, not even internally. That promise lets the compiler skip something called a <strong>defensive copy</strong>. Here&#39;s what that means: normally, when you pass an ordinary (non-readonly) struct into a method using <code>in</code> — a modifier that means &quot;you only get to read this, not change it&quot; — the compiler still quietly makes a safety copy first, just in case that method secretly tries to mutate it anyway. A <code>readonly struct</code> removes the need for that safety net: there&#39;s nothing to protect against, since the type itself can&#39;t be mutated, so the compiler skips the copy and you get the performance benefit of <code>in</code> for free.</p>\n<p><code>record struct</code> (C# 10) is a struct that also gets the same three conveniences a <code>record</code> class gets: <strong>value-based equality</strong> (two instances count as equal if their fields match — the <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/type-system/equality-three-different-questions-similar-syntax\">&quot;Equality&quot; section</a> later in this chapter covers exactly what that means and why it&#39;s not the default for plain structs), a readable default <code>ToString()</code>, and <strong><code>with</code>-expressions</strong> — shorthand for &quot;copy this value, but change just one field,&quot; e.g. <code>money with { Amount = 50 }</code> produces a new <code>Money</code> with the same <code>Currency</code> and a different <code>Amount</code>.</p>\n<p><code>OrderId</code> above demonstrates a different, popular pattern worth naming on its own: a <strong>strongly typed ID</strong>. Instead of passing a bare <code>Guid</code> around for every kind of ID in your system, you wrap it in a tiny dedicated struct. The payoff: <code>GetOrder(customerId)</code> — accidentally passing the wrong kind of ID into the wrong method — becomes a <strong>compile error</strong> instead of a runtime bug, because <code>OrderId</code> and <code>CustomerId</code> are genuinely different types to the compiler, even though they&#39;re both &quot;just a <code>Guid</code>&quot; underneath.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Mutable structs bite, and in a genuinely surprising way</div><figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"dmFyIHBvaW50cyA9IG5ldyBMaXN0PFBvaW50Uz4geyBuZXcoKSB7IFggPSAxIH0gfTsKcG9pbnRzWzBdLlggPSA1OyAgIC8vIGNvbXBpbGUgZXJyb3IgQ1MxNjEyOiB0aGUgaW5kZXhlciByZXR1cm5zIGEgQ09QWSwgbW9kaWZ5aW5nIGl0IGlzIHBvaW50bGVzcw==\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> points = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">PointS</span>&gt; { <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>() { <span class=\"tk-ty\">X</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> } };\npoints[<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>].<span class=\"tk-ty\">X</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>;   <span class=\"tk-com\">// compile error CS1612: the indexer returns a COPY, modifying it is pointless</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Here&#39;s why that&#39;s a compile error rather than silently doing nothing: <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code>&#39;s indexer is a <em>method</em> (<code>get_Item</code>), and calling it returns a <strong>copy</strong> of the struct stored inside the list — so <code>points[0].X = 5</code> would modify a temporary copy that&#39;s immediately thrown away, achieving nothing. The compiler refuses to compile code that can&#39;t possibly do what it looks like it does. With a plain array, though, <code>array[0].X = 5</code> <em>does</em> work and modifies in place, because an array element is a real, addressable variable, not something returned from a method call. Same-looking syntax, two different outcomes, depending on whether you&#39;re indexing an array or a <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code> — which is exactly the kind of inconsistency that makes &quot;keep structs immutable&quot; such a firmly held rule rather than just a style preference.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"boxing-what-happens-when-a-value-type-has-to-pretend-to-be-a\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/type-system/boxing-what-happens-when-a-value-type-has-to-pretend-to-be-a\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Boxing: what happens when a value type has to pretend to be an object <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Here&#39;s a scenario that comes up constantly: you have an <code>int</code>, but something — an older API, a non-generic collection, an interface — needs an <code>object</code>. An <code>int</code> is a value type; <code>object</code> is a reference type. How does a value with no heap presence at all get treated as something that&#39;s supposed to live on the heap?</p>\n<p><strong>Boxing</strong> is the answer: the runtime allocates a small object on the heap, copies the value type&#39;s bits into it, and hands you a reference to that box — now indistinguishable, from the outside, from any other heap object. <strong>Unboxing</strong> is the reverse: given a boxed reference, copy the value back out into an ordinary value-type variable.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> n = <span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span> o = n;           <span class=\"tk-com\">// BOX: allocate an object on the heap, copy 42 into it</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> m = (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>)o;         <span class=\"tk-com\">// UNBOX: type check + copy back out</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">IComparable</span> c = n;      <span class=\"tk-com\">// also boxes: interface reference to a value type</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice that last line — assigning <code>n</code> to an <code>IComparable</code> variable boxes it too, for exactly the same reason: an interface reference is a reference-type concept, so a value type being treated as &quot;any type implementing this interface&quot; needs the same heap wrapper as being treated as a bare <code>object</code> does.</p>\n<p>The dangerous part is that boxing often happens <strong>without you writing anything that looks like a box</strong>:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"Ly8gSGlkZGVuIGJveGluZwp2YXIgbGlzdCA9IG5ldyBBcnJheUxpc3QoKTsgICAvLyBub24tZ2VuZXJpYywgc3RvcmVzIG9iamVjdApsaXN0LkFkZCgxKTsgICAgICAgICAgICAgICAgICAvLyBib3hlcwpzdHJpbmcgcyA9IHN0cmluZy5Gb3JtYXQoInswfSIsIDQyKTsgIC8vIGJveGVzIHRoZSBpbnQgKGludGVycG9sYXRlZCBzdHJpbmdzIGF2b2lkIHRoaXMp\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Hidden boxing</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> list = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ArrayList</span>();   <span class=\"tk-com\">// non-generic, stores object</span>\nlist.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);                  <span class=\"tk-com\">// boxes</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> s = <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Format</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;{0}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>);  <span class=\"tk-com\">// boxes the int (interpolated strings avoid this)</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><code>ArrayList.Add</code> takes an <code>object</code> parameter — a holdover from before generics existed — so every <code>int</code> you add gets silently boxed on the way in. <code>string.Format(&quot;{0}&quot;, 42)</code> has the same issue: its <code>object[]</code> parameter list boxes <code>42</code> before the method body even runs. Neither line <em>looks</em> expensive, and that&#39;s exactly the trap: each one is a heap allocation, a copy, and — on the way back out — a type check, all paid for something that looks like a free, instant operation. Do this in a hot loop processing millions of items, and you&#39;ve built a real, measurable performance bug out of code that compiles without a single warning — a classic real-world version of this is a hot path that logs thousands of order IDs a second through an old <code>ArrayList</code>-based audit buffer, each one boxed on the way in.</p>\n<p>The actual fix, worth remembering as a rule rather than a one-off trick: <strong>generics avoid boxing structurally</strong>. <code>List&lt;int&gt;</code> stores <code>int</code>s directly, inline, with no boxing at all — because the JIT generates a genuinely separate, specialised version of <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code> for <code>int</code> specifically, rather than forcing everything through a shared <code>object</code>-based implementation. You&#39;ll see exactly how and why that works in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/generics\">Generics</a>.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">A boxed value is frozen at the moment it was boxed</div><figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"UG9pbnRTIHAgPSBuZXcoKSB7IFggPSAxIH07Cm9iamVjdCBib3hlZCA9IHA7CnAuWCA9IDI7CkNvbnNvbGUuV3JpdGVMaW5lKCgoUG9pbnRTKWJveGVkKS5YKTsgIC8vIDE6IHRoZSBib3ggaG9sZHMgYSBjb3B5IHRha2VuIGF0IGJveGluZyB0aW1l\"><code><span class=\"tk-ty\">PointS</span> p = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>() { <span class=\"tk-ty\">X</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> };\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span> boxed = p;\np.<span class=\"tk-ty\">X</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(((<span class=\"tk-ty\">PointS</span>)boxed).<span class=\"tk-ty\">X</span>);  <span class=\"tk-com\">// 1: the box holds a copy taken at boxing time</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>This surprises people who expect <code>boxed</code> to somehow track <code>p</code>. It doesn&#39;t, because boxing is a <strong>copy</strong>, not a reference back to <code>p</code> — the exact same copy-semantics rule from the very start of this chapter, just applied one level deeper. Once <code>p</code> is boxed, the two are completely independent; changing one never touches the other.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"strings-a-reference-type-dressed-up-to-feel-like-a-value\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/type-system/strings-a-reference-type-dressed-up-to-feel-like-a-value\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Strings: a reference type dressed up to feel like a value <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><code>string</code> is, by the table at the top of this chapter, a reference type — but it behaves so differently from a typical reference type (<code>PointC</code>, say) that it&#39;s worth its own section. Three deliberate design choices explain why:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It is <strong>immutable</strong>: every &quot;modification&quot; — <code>+</code>, <code>Replace</code>, <code>ToUpper</code>, anything — actually creates a brand-new string and leaves the original completely untouched.</li>\n<li><code>==</code> compares <strong>contents</strong>, not references, because the <code>==</code> operator is specifically overloaded for <code>string</code> to do that. (Most reference types don&#39;t do this — <code>PointC == PointC</code> above would compare references, not field values, unless you overload it yourself.)</li>\n<li>Identical string <strong>literals</strong> are <strong>interned</strong>: the runtime recognises <code>&quot;hello&quot;</code> appearing twice in your source and has both refer to the exact same single object in memory, rather than allocating two copies of an identical string.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> a = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;hello&quot;</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> b = a;\nb += <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot; world&quot;</span>;          <span class=\"tk-com\">// creates a NEW string; a is still &quot;hello&quot;</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Concatenation in a loop creates n intermediate strings: O(n²) copying</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> s = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> w <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> words) s += w;            <span class=\"tk-com\">// bad for large n</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> sb = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">StringBuilder</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> w <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> words) sb.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Append</span>(w);      <span class=\"tk-com\">// one growing buffer</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> result = sb.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>();\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> joined = <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Join</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;, &quot;</span>, words);      <span class=\"tk-com\">// best when you have a collection</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>That second block is worth understanding precisely, not just avoiding by rote: every single <code>s += w</code> inside the loop throws away the previous string entirely and allocates a brand-new, slightly longer one — because strings are immutable, there&#39;s no other way to &quot;append&quot; to one. Over <code>n</code> iterations, you&#39;re not doing <code>n</code> units of work, you&#39;re doing roughly <code>1 + 2 + 3 + ... + n</code> units of copying, which is <code>O(n²)</code>. <code>StringBuilder</code> exists specifically to avoid this: it maintains one resizable internal buffer and only produces a final immutable <code>string</code> once, when you call <code>ToString()</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Comparison</strong> deserves its own rule, because getting it wrong is a genuine, recurring bug category: always be deliberate about which comparison rules you&#39;re invoking. For identifiers, keys, file paths and protocol values, use <strong>ordinal</strong> comparison — <code>string.Equals(a, b, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)</code> — which compares raw character codes with no linguistic interpretation at all. Reserve culture-sensitive comparison (<code>CurrentCulture</code>) for text you&#39;re actually displaying to a user in their own language. The classic cautionary tale here is the &quot;Turkish I problem&quot;: call <code>.ToUpper()</code> on <code>&quot;i&quot;</code> under Turkish culture settings and you get <code>&quot;İ&quot;</code>, not <code>&quot;I&quot;</code> — which has broken more than one case-insensitive lookup that assumed <code>ToUpper</code>/<code>ToLower</code> meant the same thing everywhere in the world.</p>\n<h2 id=\"nullable-value-types-giving-a-value-type-a-no-value-state\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/type-system/nullable-value-types-giving-a-value-type-a-no-value-state\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Nullable value types: giving a value type a &quot;no value&quot; state <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Value types, as the very first table in this chapter said, can&#39;t be <code>null</code> — an <code>int</code> always has <em>some</em> integer value, even if that value is just <code>0</code> by default. But plenty of real-world data genuinely has a &quot;no value&quot; state that isn&#39;t the same thing as zero: a discount that might not apply, a <code>ShippedAt</code> date for an order that hasn&#39;t shipped yet. <code>Nullable&lt;T&gt;</code>, almost always written as the shorthand <code>T?</code>, is how C# adds that missing state to a value type without turning it into a reference type.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>? discount = <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (discount.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasValue</span>) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(discount.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> effective = discount ?? <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;                 <span class=\"tk-com\">// null-coalescing</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> alsoEffective = discount.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetValueOrDefault</span>();\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTime</span>? shippedAt = order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShippedAt</span>;         <span class=\"tk-com\">// database NULL ↔ C# null</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Here&#39;s the detail that makes <code>Nullable&lt;T&gt;</code> click: it isn&#39;t magic, it&#39;s just an ordinary struct, roughly <code>{ bool hasValue; T value; }</code> — a flag plus the underlying value, packaged together. That&#39;s why <code>int?</code> is still, underneath, a value type with all the copy semantics you&#39;d expect, not some special case the runtime handles differently. One genuinely subtle consequence: boxing a <em>null</em> <code>int?</code> produces an actual <code>null</code> reference, not a box containing &quot;no value&quot; — while boxing a <em>non-null</em> <code>int?</code> boxes just the underlying <code>int</code>, discarding the wrapper entirely. The box, once created, has no memory that it ever came from a <code>Nullable&lt;T&gt;</code> at all.</p>\n<p>Don&#39;t confuse this with <strong>nullable reference types</strong> (<code>string?</code>), which look almost identical in syntax but are a completely different mechanism — compile-time-only annotations that add nothing whatsoever at runtime. <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/modern-csharp\">Modern C#</a> covers the distinction in full.</p>\n<h2 id=\"enums-named-integers-with-a-sharp-edge\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/type-system/enums-named-integers-with-a-sharp-edge\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Enums: named integers with a sharp edge <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>An <code>enum</code> is a named set of integer constants — a value type, backed by <code>int</code> unless you say otherwise.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">enum</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Draft</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Placed</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Paid</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Shipped</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Cancelled</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">9</span> }\n\n<span class=\"tk-attr\">[Flags]</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">enum</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Permissions</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">None</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Read</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Write</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Delete</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">4</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Admin</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Read</span> | <span class=\"tk-ty\">Write</span> | <span class=\"tk-ty\">Delete</span> }\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> p = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Permissions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Read</span> | <span class=\"tk-ty\">Permissions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Write</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> canWrite = p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasFlag</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Permissions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Write</span>);       <span class=\"tk-com\">// or (p &amp; Permissions.Write) != 0</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice <code>Permissions</code> assigns <code>1, 2, 4</code> — powers of two, not <code>1, 2, 3</code>. That&#39;s deliberate: each value occupies a distinct bit, so they can be combined with <code>|</code> (bitwise OR) without any of them colliding, and checked individually with <code>HasFlag</code> or a raw bitwise <code>&amp;</code>. The <code>[Flags]</code> attribute signals this intent to tooling (and to <code>ToString()</code>, which then prints <code>&quot;Read, Write&quot;</code> instead of a meaningless number) — but doesn&#39;t change the underlying mechanics at all; it&#39;s purely a hint.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Enums are not validated — a value outside the named set compiles and runs just fine</div><p><code>(OrderStatus)42</code> compiles, runs, and produces a perfectly valid-looking <code>OrderStatus</code> value that happens to correspond to no named member at all. This isn&#39;t a hypothetical edge case: values coming from JSON, from a database column, from a different version of your own code, can easily land outside the set you defined. Check with <code>Enum.IsDefined</code>, or a <code>switch</code> with a <code>default</code> arm that handles the unexpected case explicitly, anywhere an enum value arrives from outside your own code. One more easily-missed trap: if you ever persist enum values as their underlying numbers, give every member you store an <strong>explicit</strong> number. Without one, the compiler assigns them in declaration order starting from 0 — and simply reordering your <code>enum</code> declaration, with no other change at all, silently changes what every previously stored number now means.</p>\n</aside>\n<p>Given that fragility, prefer serialising enums as <strong>strings</strong> in public APIs (<code>JsonStringEnumConverter</code>), not as their numeric values — a new member added later doesn&#39;t break clients reading the string name, the way it could break clients hard-coded against specific numbers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"equality-three-different-questions-similar-syntax\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/type-system/equality-three-different-questions-similar-syntax\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Equality: three different questions, similar syntax <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>&quot;Are these two equal?&quot; sounds like one question. In C#, depending on exactly how you ask it, it&#39;s actually at least three, and they don&#39;t always agree with each other:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Default for classes</th>\n<th>Default for structs</th>\n<th>Records</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>==</code></td>\n<td>Reference equality (unless overloaded, e.g. <code>string</code>)</td>\n<td>Not defined unless you overload it</td>\n<td>Value equality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Equals(object)</code></td>\n<td>Reference equality</td>\n<td>Field-by-field value equality (via reflection, slow)</td>\n<td>Value equality (generated, fast)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ReferenceEquals</code></td>\n<td>Same object?</td>\n<td>Always false (boxed copies)</td>\n<td>Same object?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The struct row for <code>Equals</code> is worth a second look: it works, but it&#39;s genuinely slow, because the default implementation uses <strong>reflection</strong> to compare every field one by one — exactly the kind of runtime cost <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/what-build-time-actually-hands-you\">How code runs</a> warned reflection carries. If you&#39;re going to compare structs for equality often, overriding <code>Equals</code> yourself (or using <code>record struct</code>, which generates a fast version for you) is a real, measurable win, not just tidiness.</p>\n<p>If you override <code>Equals</code> yourself, there&#39;s a rule you <strong>must</strong> follow or you&#39;ll get genuinely confusing bugs much later: you must override <code>GetHashCode</code> too, consistently — equal objects have to produce equal hash codes, or <code>Dictionary</code>/<code>HashSet</code> will silently fail to find items you know are in them. Implementing <code>IEquatable&lt;T&gt;</code> alongside both gives you a fast, non-boxing <code>Equals(T)</code> overload on top.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IEquatable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span>&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Code</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> code) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Code</span> = code.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToUpperInvariant</span>();\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Equals</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span>? other) =&gt; other <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">not</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span> &amp;&amp; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Code</span> == other.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Code</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Equals</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>? obj) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Equals</span>(obj <span class=\"tk-kw\">as</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetHashCode</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Code</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetHashCode</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">operator</span> ==(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span>? a, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span>? b) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Equals</span>(a, b);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">operator</span> !=(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span>? a, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span>? b) =&gt; !<span class=\"tk-ty\">Equals</span>(a, b);\n}\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Or just: public sealed record Sku(string Code);  ← the compiler writes all of the above</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>That final comment is worth taking seriously as practical advice, not just trivia: every single member in that <code>Sku</code> class — <code>Equals</code>, <code>GetHashCode</code>, both operators — is exactly what the compiler generates automatically for a <code>record</code>. Unless you have a real reason to write a class by hand here, a one-line <code>record</code> gets you the same correctness with none of the five-method ceremony above it.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">A mutable hash key is a silent, permanent bug</div><p>If an object&#39;s hash code depends on a field that changes <em>after</em> you&#39;ve already added it to a <code>HashSet</code> or used it as a <code>Dictionary</code> key, you will never find it again through that collection — not an exception, not a warning, just a lookup that quietly fails forever, because the collection stored it under the hash code it had <em>then</em>, and is now searching under the hash code it has <em>now</em>. Keys must be immutable, full stop.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"passing-parameters-what-by-value-actually-means-for-a-refere\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/type-system/passing-parameters-what-by-value-actually-means-for-a-refere\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Passing parameters: what &quot;by value&quot; actually means for a reference <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Everything in C# is passed by value, with no exceptions</strong> — including reference types, which is the part that confuses people, because it genuinely sounds like it should mean the opposite. Here&#39;s the precise mechanics: for a reference type, the thing being passed &quot;by value&quot; is the <em>reference itself</em> — so the method you&#39;re calling gets its own copy of that reference, pointing at the same object, which means it <strong>can</strong> mutate the object through that reference, but it <strong>cannot</strong> make the caller&#39;s own variable point somewhere else.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Rename</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span> c)      { c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;New&quot;</span>; }        <span class=\"tk-com\">// caller sees this (same object)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Replace</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span> c)     { c = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>(); }     <span class=\"tk-com\">// caller does NOT see this</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReplaceRef</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">ref</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span> c) { c = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>(); }  <span class=\"tk-com\">// caller DOES see this</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Those three methods look almost identical, and produce three genuinely different outcomes. <code>Rename</code> mutates the object the reference points at — both the caller and the method are looking at the same <code>Customer</code>, so the caller sees the change. <code>Replace</code> reassigns the <em>local copy</em> of the reference to point at a brand-new object entirely — but that local copy is the method&#39;s own, private variable; the caller&#39;s original reference never moves, and still points at the original <code>Customer</code>. <code>ReplaceRef</code> adds the one modifier that changes this: <code>ref</code> passes the caller&#39;s actual variable, not a copy of it, so reassigning <code>c</code> inside the method genuinely does reassign the caller&#39;s variable too.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Modifier</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Caller must</th>\n<th>Typical use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><em>(none)</em></td>\n<td>Copy of value / copy of reference</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Default</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ref</code></td>\n<td>Pass the variable itself; method can read and write</td>\n<td>Initialise first, write <code>ref</code> at call site</td>\n<td>Swapping, large structs you mutate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>out</code></td>\n<td>Method <strong>must</strong> assign it; caller needn&#39;t initialise</td>\n<td>Write <code>out</code></td>\n<td><code>TryParse</code>, <code>TryGetValue</code> patterns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>in</code></td>\n<td>Read-only reference; avoids copying large structs</td>\n<td>(optional <code>in</code>)</td>\n<td>Performance with big readonly structs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ref readonly</code> (C# 12)</td>\n<td>Like <code>in</code>, but the call site must pass a reference</td>\n<td></td>\n<td>API clarity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>out</code> is the one you&#39;ve almost certainly already used without necessarily naming it:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"aWYgKGludC5UcnlQYXJzZShpbnB1dCwgb3V0IHZhciBxdHkpICYmIGRpY3QuVHJ5R2V0VmFsdWUoa2V5LCBvdXQgdmFyIHZhbHVlKSkgeyAvKiAuLi4gKi8gfQ==\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryParse</span>(input, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> qty) &amp;&amp; dict.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGetValue</span>(key, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>)) { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* ... */</span> }</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The contract <code>out</code> makes is the mirror image of a normal parameter: instead of the caller promising to provide a value, the <em>method</em> promises to assign one before it returns — which is exactly why <code>TryParse</code> and <code>TryGetValue</code> can return a clean <code>bool</code> for success/failure while still handing back the actual result through the same call, with no uninitialised-variable risk on the caller&#39;s side.</p>\n<h2 id=\"type-conversions-and-checks\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/type-system/type-conversions-and-checks\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Type conversions and checks <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span> o = <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetSomething</span>();\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Pattern matching (preferred)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (o <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> order &amp;&amp; order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">100</span>) { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* order is typed */</span> }\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// as: returns null on failure (reference/nullable types only)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> maybe = o <span class=\"tk-kw\">as</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Cast: throws InvalidCastException on failure</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> sure = (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>)o;\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Numeric conversions</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">300</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span> b = (<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>)i;                <span class=\"tk-com\">// 44: silently wraps (unchecked)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span> c = <span class=\"tk-kw\">checked</span>((<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>)i);       <span class=\"tk-com\">// throws OverflowException</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">long</span> big = i;                    <span class=\"tk-com\">// implicit widening: always safe</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> price = <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parse</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;19.99&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CultureInfo</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">InvariantCulture</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The first three options above aren&#39;t interchangeable — they&#39;re three different answers to &quot;what should happen if this doesn&#39;t work?&quot; Pattern matching (<code>is</code>) checks and gives you a safely typed variable in one step, failing gracefully into the <code>else</code> branch. <code>as</code> returns <code>null</code> on failure, which only makes sense for reference and nullable types (there&#39;s no &quot;null&quot; a plain cast could fall back to for a non-nullable value type). A plain cast <code>(Order)o</code> is the least forgiving: it throws <code>InvalidCastException</code> immediately if the object genuinely isn&#39;t an <code>Order</code>.</p>\n<p>The numeric half of that block hides a real footgun: <strong>implicit conversions</strong> (<code>int</code> → <code>long</code>) are always safe, because the destination type can represent every possible value of the source — nothing is ever lost. <strong>Explicit casts</strong> carry no such guarantee, and <code>(byte)300</code> is the proof: 300 doesn&#39;t fit in a <code>byte</code>&#39;s 0–255 range, so it silently wraps around to 44, with no warning, no exception, just a wrong number that looks like a right one. That silent wraparound is also the <em>default</em> behaviour for everyday integer arithmetic in C#, not just explicit casts — <code>int.MaxValue + 1</code> overflows back to a huge negative number without a peep, unless you opt into <code>&lt;CheckForOverflowUnderflow&gt;</code> project-wide or wrap specific risky arithmetic in a <code>checked</code> block.</p>\n<p>One more rule worth having memorised, not just known: use <strong><code>decimal</code></strong> for money, never <code>double</code>. <code>decimal</code> is base-10 internally, so it represents a value like <code>0.1</code> <em>exactly</em>. <code>double</code> is binary floating point, and — this is the famous, often-demonstrated gotcha — <code>0.1 + 0.2 != 0.3</code> under binary floating point, because neither <code>0.1</code> nor <code>0.2</code> can be represented exactly in base 2 in the first place. For currency, that&#39;s not a rounding curiosity, it&#39;s a correctness bug waiting to happen.</p>\n<h2 id=\"var-dynamic-and-object-three-ways-to-avoid-naming-a-type-for\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/type-system/var-dynamic-and-object-three-ways-to-avoid-naming-a-type-for\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>var, dynamic and object: three ways to avoid naming a type, for three different reasons <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>Type checked</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>var</code></td>\n<td>Compiler infers the static type from the right-hand side. <strong>Still strongly typed.</strong></td>\n<td>Compile time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>object</code></td>\n<td>The base of all types; you need casts to use it</td>\n<td>Compile time (as <code>object</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dynamic</code></td>\n<td>Bypasses compile-time checks; resolved at runtime by the DLR</td>\n<td>Runtime (errors become exceptions)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>It&#39;s worth being precise about <code>var</code>, because its name invites a misconception: <code>var</code> is <strong>purely syntax</strong>, resolved entirely at compile time, with zero runtime effect. <code>var x = 5;</code> doesn&#39;t produce some loosely typed variable — it compiles to exactly, literally, <code>int x = 5;</code>. The compiler already knows the right-hand side&#39;s type perfectly well; <code>var</code> just saves you typing it out a second time on the left.</p>\n<p><code>dynamic</code> is the genuine outlier in that table: it defers all member resolution — does this object even <em>have</em> a <code>.Foo()</code> method? — all the way to runtime, via the DLR (Dynamic Language Runtime). That trades away IntelliSense, compile-time error checking, and performance, all at once, in exchange for flexibility you&#39;ll rarely actually need in backend code. Reach for it almost never: COM interop and a handful of dynamic-JSON scenarios are about the only legitimate uses you&#39;ll run into.</p>\n<h2 id=\"memory-layout-and-size\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/type-system/memory-layout-and-size\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Memory layout and size <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Every heap object carries a <strong>header</strong> before its own fields even begin — an 8-byte sync block index plus an 8-byte method table pointer on a 64-bit process — so even a completely empty object costs roughly <strong>24 bytes</strong> before a single field of your own is counted. This specific number is a CLR implementation detail rather than something the C# language spec promises you, documented in community/internals references like <em>Pro .NET Memory Management</em> (Konrad Kokosa) and <em>CLR via C#</em> rather than on Microsoft Learn itself — but it&#39;s stable and well-known enough to rely on when budgeting memory. That overhead is invisible in isolation, but allocate thousands of tiny objects per request and it adds up into real, measurable memory pressure.</li>\n<li>The runtime is free to reorder a class&#39;s fields in memory for better packing; structs, by contrast, default to <strong>sequential layout</strong> — the order you declared them in — which matters specifically for native interop, and can be controlled explicitly with <code>[StructLayout]</code>.</li>\n<li><code>sizeof(int)</code> is 4 bytes, <code>long</code>/<code>double</code> are 8, <code>decimal</code> is 16, <code>Guid</code> is 16, <code>DateTime</code> is 8, and a reference is 8 bytes on a 64-bit process.</li>\n<li><code>Unsafe.SizeOf&lt;T&gt;()</code> gives you the exact size of any type at runtime; <strong>ObjectLayoutInspector</strong> and BenchmarkDotNet&#39;s <code>MemoryDiagnoser</code> are the practical tools for actually measuring this in your own code rather than reasoning about it in the abstract.</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the difference between a value type and a reference type?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A value type variable contains its data, and assignment copies the data. A reference type variable contains a reference to a heap object, and assignment copies the reference so both variables see the same object. Value types can&#39;t be null (without <code>Nullable&lt;T&gt;</code>) and can&#39;t be inherited; reference types are garbage-collected objects that support inheritance.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Do value types always live on the stack?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>No. Reference-type objects always live on the heap, but value types live wherever their container lives: locals on the stack (unless captured by a lambda or used in an async method or iterator), fields inside a class object on the heap, elements inline inside an array on the heap. The defining difference is copy semantics, not location.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>When would you use a struct instead of a class?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>For a small (≈16 bytes or less), immutable value that logically represents a single value and isn&#39;t boxed often: Money, coordinates, strongly typed IDs. Make it a <code>readonly struct</code> or <code>readonly record struct</code>. Use classes for anything with identity, behaviour, inheritance or larger size.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is boxing and how do you avoid it?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Converting a value type to <code>object</code> or an interface, which allocates a heap object and copies the value; unboxing copies it back out with a type check. Avoid it with generics (<code>List&lt;int&gt;</code> instead of <code>ArrayList</code>), generic constraints instead of interface parameters, <code>IEquatable&lt;T&gt;</code>, and interpolated strings instead of <code>string.Format</code> with value arguments.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why is string immutable, and what are the consequences?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Immutability makes strings thread-safe, safe to use as dictionary keys, and allows interning. The consequence is that every modification allocates a new string, so build strings in loops with StringBuilder or string.Join/Concat.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>If you override Equals, what else must you do?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Override GetHashCode consistently (equal objects ⇒ equal hash codes), ideally implement <code>IEquatable&lt;T&gt;</code>, and overload <code>==</code>/<code>!=</code> so they agree. Or use a record, which generates all of this.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Are reference types passed by reference in C#?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>No. Everything is passed by value by default. For a reference type, the value passed is the reference, so the method can modify the object but reassigning the parameter doesn&#39;t affect the caller. Use <code>ref</code> to pass the variable itself.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>ref vs out vs in?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p><code>ref</code>: the caller passes an initialised variable, and the method can read and write it. <code>out</code>: the method must assign it, and the caller needn&#39;t initialise it (TryParse pattern). <code>in</code>: a read-only reference, used to avoid copying large structs.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why use decimal instead of double for money?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p><code>decimal</code> is a base-10 type that represents values like 0.1 exactly, with 28–29 significant digits. <code>double</code> is binary floating point, so many decimal fractions can&#39;t be represented exactly and rounding errors accumulate.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the difference between var and dynamic?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p><code>var</code> is compile-time type inference: the variable is still statically typed. <code>dynamic</code> defers all member resolution to runtime, so errors appear as runtime exceptions and you lose IntelliSense and performance.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/type-system/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/language-reference/builtin-types/value-types\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Value types (C# reference)</a> — confirms copy semantics; deliberately doesn&#39;t commit to &quot;stack vs heap&quot; wording, for the reason this chapter explains.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/design-guidelines/choosing-between-class-and-struct\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Choosing between class and struct</a> — the exact four-condition struct rule quoted above, and the &quot;value types are allocated either on the stack or inline in containing types&quot; sentence this chapter&#39;s stack/heap section is built on.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/programming-guide/types/boxing-and-unboxing\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Boxing and unboxing</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/language-reference/builtin-types/nullable-value-types#boxing-and-unboxing\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Nullable value types — boxing and unboxing rules</a> — the exact source for &quot;boxing a null <code>T?</code> returns the null reference; boxing a non-null one boxes just the underlying value.&quot;</li>\n<li>Book: <em>C# in Depth</em> by Jon Skeet. Book: <em>CLR via C#</em> by Jeffrey Richter, chapter on type fundamentals. Book: <em>Pro .NET Memory Management</em> by Konrad Kokosa, for the object-header/memory-layout numbers in the Advanced section above.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/type-system/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Exact memory layout (object header size, struct field packing) needs a profiler like ObjectLayoutInspector, not plain code — see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/type-system/further-reading\">Further reading</a> for that. Everything else in this chapter is observable behaviour, so the program below asserts it directly. Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (plain console app, .NET 8/9, no extra packages).</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Rename</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span> c) =&gt; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Renamed&quot;</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Replace</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span> c) =&gt; c = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Should not be visible&quot;</span> };\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReplaceRef</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">ref</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span> c) =&gt; c = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Brand New&quot;</span> };\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: assigning a struct copies the data; assigning a class copies only the reference.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> s1 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PointS</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">X</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> };\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> s2 = s1;\ns2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">X</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">99</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (s1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">X</span> != <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: struct assignment should have copied, not shared, the data&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: struct assignment copies the value (s1.X stayed 1)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> c1 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PointC</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">X</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> };\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> c2 = c1;\nc2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">X</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">99</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (c1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">X</span> != <span class=\"tk-num\">99</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: class assignment should share the same object&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: class assignment copies the reference, so both see the change (c1.X became 99)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: a boxed value is frozen at the moment it was boxed — later changes to the original don't show through.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">PointS</span> p = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>() { <span class=\"tk-ty\">X</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> };\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span> boxed = p;\np.<span class=\"tk-ty\">X</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (((<span class=\"tk-ty\">PointS</span>)boxed).<span class=\"tk-ty\">X</span> != <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: the box should hold a copy taken at boxing time&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: a boxed value type is an independent copy, frozen at boxing time&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: enums are not validated — a value outside the named set compiles and runs.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> bogus = (<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span>)<span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Enum</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsDefined</span>(bogus)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: 42 should not be a defined OrderStatus member&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: (OrderStatus)42 = {bogus} compiles and runs, but Enum.IsDefined correctly flags it as undefined&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: decimal represents 0.1 + 0.2 exactly as 0.3; double does not.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> d = <span class=\"tk-num\">0.1m</span> + <span class=\"tk-num\">0.2m</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> f = <span class=\"tk-num\">0.1</span> + <span class=\"tk-num\">0.2</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (d != <span class=\"tk-num\">0.3m</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: decimal 0.1 + 0.2 should equal exactly 0.3&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (f == <span class=\"tk-num\">0.3</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected binary double 0.1 + 0.2 to NOT equal 0.3 exactly&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: decimal 0.1m+0.2m == 0.3m exactly; double 0.1+0.2 = {f:R} != 0.3 exactly (binary rounding)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: everything is passed by value — `ref` is what lets a method reassign the caller's variable.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> customer = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Original&quot;</span> };\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Rename</span>(customer);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (customer.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> != <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Renamed&quot;</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: mutating through the reference should be visible to the caller&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: Rename mutated the shared object — caller sees 'Renamed'&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Replace</span>(customer);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (customer.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> != <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Renamed&quot;</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: reassigning the local copy of the reference must NOT affect the caller&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: Replace reassigned only its own local copy — caller's variable is untouched&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReplaceRef</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">ref</span> customer);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (customer.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> != <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Brand New&quot;</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: ref should let the method reassign the caller's variable&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: ReplaceRef (with `ref`) reassigned the caller's own variable&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: boxing a null Nullable&lt;T&gt; produces an actual null reference, not a box of &quot;no value&quot;.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>? maybeNull = <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>? boxedNullable = maybeNull;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (boxedNullable <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">not</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: boxing a null int? should produce a real null reference&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: boxing a null int? produces an actual null reference&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">struct</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PointS</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">X</span>; }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PointC</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">X</span>; }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;</span>; }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">enum</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Draft</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Placed</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Paid</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Shipped</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Cancelled</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">9</span> }</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"one-question-that-predicts-a-huge-amount-of-c-behaviour","text":"One question that predicts a huge amount of C# behaviour","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"value-types-live-on-the-stack-a-useful-lie","text":"\"Value types live on the stack\" — a useful lie","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"when-a-struct-actually-earns-its-keep","text":"When a struct actually earns its keep","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"boxing-what-happens-when-a-value-type-has-to-pretend-to-be-a","text":"Boxing: what happens when a value type has to pretend to be an object","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"strings-a-reference-type-dressed-up-to-feel-like-a-value","text":"Strings: a reference type dressed up to feel like a value","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"nullable-value-types-giving-a-value-type-a-no-value-state","text":"Nullable value types: giving a value type a \"no value\" state","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"enums-named-integers-with-a-sharp-edge","text":"Enums: named integers with a sharp edge","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"equality-three-different-questions-similar-syntax","text":"Equality: three different questions, similar syntax","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"passing-parameters-what-by-value-actually-means-for-a-refere","text":"Passing parameters: what \"by value\" actually means for a reference","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"type-conversions-and-checks","text":"Type conversions and checks","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"var-dynamic-and-object-three-ways-to-avoid-naming-a-type-for","text":"var, dynamic and object: three ways to avoid naming a type, for three different reasons","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"memory-layout-and-size","text":"Memory layout and size","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":7262,"minutes":33,"num":"2.1","part":"p2"},"oop":{"id":"oop","title":"Object-Oriented Programming in C#","summary":"Classes and their members, constructors and initialisation order, the four pillars, inheritance and polymorphism (virtual/override/new), abstract classes vs interfaces, access modifiers, static, sealed, partial, and composition over inheritance.","layer":"language","where":"C# language › Types › Classes, interfaces & OOP","fits":"<p>Still layer 1. Everything in layers 3 and 4 is expressed with these tools. DI (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/dependency-injection\">Dependency injection</a>) works because you code against interfaces. SOLID (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/design-principles\">Design principles</a>) and design patterns (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/design-patterns\">Design patterns</a>) are rules and recipes built on top of this chapter.</p>\n","prereqs":["type-system"],"next":["generics","design-principles","dependency-injection"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oop/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A class bundles <strong>state</strong> (fields/properties) and <strong>behaviour</strong> (methods); most of OOP is about controlling how much of that state the outside world can see or change.</li>\n<li>Construction order is <strong>derived field inits → base field inits → base constructor body → derived constructor body</strong> — the opposite of what intuition suggests, and the reason calling a <code>virtual</code> method from a constructor is dangerous.</li>\n<li><code>override</code> is runtime (<strong>virtual dispatch</strong>, decided by the object&#39;s real type); <code>new</code> is compile-time (decided by the variable&#39;s <em>declared</em> type) — they look alike and behave completely differently.</li>\n<li>Interface vs abstract class is a design decision, not just vocabulary: interfaces for swappable &quot;can-do&quot; contracts, abstract classes for related types that share real code/state.</li>\n<li>Prefer <strong>composition</strong> (small, swappable collaborators) over deep inheritance chains, which get brittle fast (the fragile base class problem, the &quot;one class per combination&quot; explosion).</li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout c-analogy\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Pico Mart</div><p>Picture a small online shop, <strong>Pico Mart</strong>. Its backend needs customers, accounts, payments, orders — the usual cast. Every section below is a real problem Pico Mart&#39;s code ran into, in the order it ran into them. By the end you&#39;ll have watched one small codebase grow from &quot;a class with some fields&quot; into something that actually resembles a real OOP design, instead of four definitions recited for an interview.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"a-class-is-two-things-bundled-together\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oop/a-class-is-two-things-bundled-together\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>A class is two things bundled together <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Pico Mart&#39;s first customer record was just a bag of public fields — name, email, a list of addresses — and that already breaks within a day, because nothing stops other code from putting garbage into it. A <strong>class</strong> is a blueprint for objects, and every single thing you can put inside one falls into exactly two buckets: <strong>state</strong> (data the object carries around — its fields) and <strong>behaviour</strong> (what it can do — its methods). Everything else in this chapter is really about controlling how those two buckets interact with each other and with the outside world.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">namespace</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Shop</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Domain</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>                     <span class=\"tk-com\">// type declaration</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Address</span>&gt; _addresses = [];   <span class=\"tk-com\">// field: private state</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">const</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">MaxAddresses</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>;                 <span class=\"tk-com\">// compile-time constant</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> _created;                       <span class=\"tk-com\">// static field: shared by all instances</span>\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> email, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> name)          <span class=\"tk-com\">// constructor</span>\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">ArgumentException</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ThrowIfNullOrWhiteSpace</span>(email);\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Email</span> = email;\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> = name;\n        _created++;\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NewGuid</span>();          <span class=\"tk-com\">// get-only auto-property (set in ctor/initialiser)</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Email</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }          <span class=\"tk-com\">// public read, private write</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }                   <span class=\"tk-com\">// read/write auto-property</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsVip</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">TotalSpent</span> &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">10_000m</span>;         <span class=\"tk-com\">// computed (expression-bodied) property</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TotalSpent</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IReadOnlyList</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Address</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Addresses</span> =&gt; _addresses;   <span class=\"tk-com\">// expose read-only view</span>\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAddress</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Address</span> a)                  <span class=\"tk-com\">// method: behaviour that protects invariants</span>\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (_addresses.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> &gt;= <span class=\"tk-ty\">MaxAddresses</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Too many addresses&quot;</span>);\n        _addresses.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(a);\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">event</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">EventHandler</span>? <span class=\"tk-ty\">BecameVip</span>;              <span class=\"tk-com\">// event</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;{Name} &lt;{Email}&gt;&quot;</span>;\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Member</th>\n<th>Purpose</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Field</strong></td>\n<td>Raw storage. Keep private.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Property</strong></td>\n<td>Controlled access with <code>get</code>/<code>set</code>/<code>init</code> accessors. The public face of state.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Method</strong></td>\n<td>Behaviour.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Constructor</strong></td>\n<td>Initialises a new instance; establishes invariants.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Static members</strong></td>\n<td>Belong to the type, not an instance.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Constant / readonly</strong></td>\n<td><code>const</code> is compile-time and baked into callers; <code>readonly</code> is set once at runtime (in the constructor).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Indexer</strong></td>\n<td><code>this[int i]</code> lets instances be used like arrays.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Event</strong></td>\n<td>Notification to subscribers. See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/delegates-events\">Delegates &amp; events</a>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Operator</strong></td>\n<td>Overloads like <code>+</code>, <code>==</code>, implicit/explicit conversions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Nested type</strong></td>\n<td>A type declared inside another.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Finalizer</strong></td>\n<td><code>~Customer()</code>: last-chance cleanup by the GC. Rarely needed.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">const vs static readonly look similar but behave completely differently across assemblies</div><p>A <code>public const</code> isn&#39;t really a variable at all by the time your code ships — the compiler <strong>copies its literal value directly into every assembly that uses it</strong>, at compile time. That has a sharp consequence: if you change a <code>const</code> inside a library and ship a new version, every <em>already-compiled</em> caller keeps using the <em>old</em> value, baked in, until they themselves are recompiled against the new library. <code>static readonly</code> doesn&#39;t have this problem — it&#39;s read fresh from the library at runtime, every time — which is exactly why it&#39;s the safer choice for anything that might ever change between versions. Reserve <code>const</code> for values that are genuinely, permanently constant: <code>Math.PI</code>, a fixed protocol value, that sort of thing.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"properties-the-public-face-a-field-doesnt-get\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oop/properties-the-public-face-a-field-doesnt-get\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Properties: the public face a field doesn&#39;t get <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }                       <span class=\"tk-com\">// auto-property</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">init</span>; }                       <span class=\"tk-com\">// settable only during object initialisation</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">required</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Email</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">init</span>; }            <span class=\"tk-com\">// caller MUST set it (C# 11)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }             <span class=\"tk-com\">// encapsulated setter</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> _qty;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Quantity</span>                                    <span class=\"tk-com\">// full property with validation</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span> =&gt; _qty;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span> =&gt; _qty = <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span> &gt;= <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span> ? <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span> : <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ArgumentOutOfRangeException</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">nameof</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>));\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Slug</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">field</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Trim</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToLowerInvariant</span>(); }  <span class=\"tk-com\">// C# 14 'field' keyword</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>A bare field is just memory — anyone with access to it can set it to anything, including nonsense. A property wraps that memory behind <code>get</code>/<code>set</code> accessors that are really just methods in disguise, which means you can put real logic in them, as <code>Quantity</code> does above: reject a negative value the instant someone tries to set it, instead of discovering the bad data three layers downstream. <code>init</code> narrows that further — settable only while an object is being constructed via an object initialiser, then locked — and <code>required</code> (C# 11) goes one step beyond that, making the compiler refuse to compile any code that constructs the type without setting that property at all.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"dmFyIHAgPSBuZXcgUHJvZHVjdCB7IFNrdSA9ICJLQi0wMSIsIEVtYWlsID0gInhAeS56IiwgTmFtZSA9ICJLZXlib2FyZCIgfTs=\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> p = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;KB-01&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Email</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;x@y.z&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Keyboard&quot;</span> };</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Worth knowing precisely: an object initialiser like this one runs its property assignments <strong>after</strong> the constructor has already finished, not during it — so if your constructor logic depends on a property that&#39;s only ever set this way, it&#39;ll see the property&#39;s default value, not whatever gets assigned a moment later.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-order-things-actually-happen-in-when-you-call-new\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oop/the-order-things-actually-happen-in-when-you-call-new\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The order things actually happen in, when you call <code>new</code> <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Pico Mart&#39;s bug tracker once had a ticket titled &quot;logger is null in the base constructor, but only sometimes&quot; — which turned into exactly this question: when <code>OrderService</code> extends <code>Base</code>, which code runs first?</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Base</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ILogger</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Log</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Base</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ILogger</span> log) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Log</span> = log; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Base ctor&quot;</span>); }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderService</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Base</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderRepository</span> _repo;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> _retries = <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>;                     <span class=\"tk-com\">// field initialiser</span>\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderService</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderRepository</span> repo, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ILogger</span> log) : <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>(log)   <span class=\"tk-com\">// calls Base first</span>\n    {\n        _repo = repo;\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Derived ctor&quot;</span>);\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderService</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderRepository</span> repo) : <span class=\"tk-kw\">this</span>(repo, <span class=\"tk-ty\">NullLogger</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Instance</span>) { }  <span class=\"tk-com\">// chain</span>\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>When you call <code>new OrderService(...)</code>, it&#39;s tempting to assume everything in <code>OrderService</code> runs before anything in <code>Base</code> — it&#39;s the type you actually asked for, after all. The real order is the opposite, and it matters enough to walk through exactly:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Derived field initialisers</strong> run first (<code>_retries = 3</code>) — before any constructor body at all.</li>\n<li><strong>Base field initialisers</strong> run next.</li>\n<li>The <strong>base constructor&#39;s body</strong> runs.</li>\n<li>Only <em>after</em> the base is fully constructed does the <strong>derived constructor&#39;s body</strong> run.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><img src=\"visuals/construction-order.svg\" alt=\"Four numbered steps lighting up in order: derived field initialisers, then base field initialisers, then the base constructor body, then finally the derived constructor body\"></p>\n<p>The reasoning behind that order is simple once you see it: a derived class&#39;s constructor body is allowed to assume the base class is already fully, safely built — calling <code>base</code>&#39;s methods, reading <code>base</code>&#39;s fields — and that assumption would be false if the order ran the other way. (Static constructors are a separate thing entirely: they run exactly once per type, automatically, before that type is first used, and the runtime guarantees this happens in a thread-safe way even under concurrent access.)</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Calling a virtual method from a constructor is a trap, and now you know exactly why</div><p>The base constructor&#39;s body runs <em>before</em> the derived constructor&#39;s body — you just watched that happen in the trace above. So if the base constructor calls a <code>virtual</code> method that the derived class overrides, that override runs <strong>during step 3</strong>, while the object is still, by definition, only half-built: every field the derived constructor was going to set in step 4 hasn&#39;t been set yet. The override executes against an object in a state it was never designed to handle.</p>\n</aside>\n<h3 id=\"primary-constructors-the-modern-shorthand\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oop/primary-constructors-the-modern-shorthand\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Primary constructors: the modern shorthand</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"cHVibGljIGNsYXNzIE9yZGVyU2VydmljZShJT3JkZXJSZXBvc2l0b3J5IHJlcG8sIElMb2dnZXI8T3JkZXJTZXJ2aWNlPiBsb2cpCnsKICAgIHB1YmxpYyBUYXNrPE9yZGVyPz4gR2V0QXN5bmMoaW50IGlkKSB7IGxvZy5Mb2dJbmZvcm1hdGlvbigiR2V0IHtJZH0iLCBpZCk7IHJldHVybiByZXBvLkZpbmRBc3luYyhpZCk7IH0KfQ==\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderService</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderRepository</span> repo, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ILogger</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderService</span>&gt; log)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>?&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id) { log.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogInformation</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Get {Id}&quot;</span>, id); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> repo.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FindAsync</span>(id); }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>A primary constructor&#39;s parameters (<code>repo</code>, <code>log</code> above) are in scope for the <em>entire</em> class body, not just inside a constructor method — which is why <code>GetAsync</code> can reach <code>repo</code> and <code>log</code> directly, with no explicit field ever declared. The catch worth knowing: these parameters are <strong>not</strong> automatically <code>readonly</code> fields. They&#39;re ordinary, reassignable parameters that happen to stay in scope — so if you genuinely need the immutability guarantee a <code>readonly</code> field would give you, assign one explicitly: <code>private readonly IOrderRepository _repo = repo;</code>. In practice, primary constructors have become the standard, idiomatic style for DI-injected services specifically because most services never need to reassign their dependencies anyway.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-four-pillars-with-a-reason-each-one-earns-its-place\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oop/the-four-pillars-with-a-reason-each-one-earns-its-place\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The four pillars, with a reason each one earns its place <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>These four words get recited so often in interviews that they risk becoming empty vocabulary. Here&#39;s each one tied back to an actual problem Pico Mart&#39;s code ran into — and, unlike a lot of OOP explainers, each one below gets its <strong>own small program you can actually run</strong>, not just a paragraph pointing at someone else&#39;s code.</p>\n<h3 id=\"encapsulation-the-object-protects-its-own-rules\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oop/encapsulation-the-object-protects-its-own-rules\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Encapsulation: the object protects its own rules</h3>\n<p>The problem: a <code>Customer</code> with a public <code>List&lt;Address&gt;</code> lets <em>any</em> calling code add a 51st address, silently breaking the &quot;max 5 addresses&quot; rule that exists for a reason you presumably had. Run this and watch it happen:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Run with: dotnet run (plain console app, no packages)</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Version 1: no encapsulation. The rule exists only as a comment. ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">LeakyCustomer</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Addresses</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>();   <span class=\"tk-com\">// public field: anyone can touch it, no questions asked</span>\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> leaky = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">LeakyCustomer</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>; i &lt;= <span class=\"tk-num\">7</span>; i++) leaky.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Addresses</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;Address #{i}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;LeakyCustomer ended up with {leaky.Addresses.Count} addresses — the \\&quot;max 5\\&quot; rule was never real code, just a wish.&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Version 2: encapsulated. The field is hidden; a method is the only door in. ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; _addresses = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>();           <span class=\"tk-com\">// private: invisible outside this class</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">const</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">MaxAddresses</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IReadOnlyList</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Addresses</span> =&gt; _addresses;        <span class=\"tk-com\">// read-only VIEW: callers can look, not modify</span>\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAddress</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> address)                       <span class=\"tk-com\">// the only way in — and it enforces the rule</span>\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (_addresses.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> &gt;= <span class=\"tk-ty\">MaxAddresses</span>)\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;Customers can't have more than {MaxAddresses} addresses.&quot;</span>);\n        _addresses.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(address);\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> safe = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>; i &lt;= <span class=\"tk-num\">7</span>; i++) safe.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAddress</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;Address #{i}&quot;</span>);\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span> ex)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;Customer stopped at {safe.Addresses.Count} addresses: \\&quot;{ex.Message}\\&quot;&quot;</span>);\n}\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// safe.Addresses.Add(&quot;sneaky&quot;);   // doesn't even compile: IReadOnlyList&lt;T&gt; has no Add method</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Running it prints <code>7</code> for the leaky version and <code>5</code> (with the exception message) for the encapsulated one. Nothing about the <em>data</em> changed between the two versions — only who&#39;s allowed to touch it, and through which door. That&#39;s the entire idea: <strong>hide the state, expose only behaviour that keeps it valid.</strong></p>\n<h3 id=\"inheritance-sharing-one-real-is-a-relationship\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oop/inheritance-sharing-one-real-is-a-relationship\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Inheritance: sharing one real &quot;is-a&quot; relationship</h3>\n<p>The problem: <code>SavingsAccount</code> and <code>CheckingAccount</code> genuinely share a lot of behaviour — they&#39;re both, fundamentally, accounts, with a balance and deposit/withdraw rules. Writing that twice would mean fixing every bug twice, forever. <strong>Inheritance</strong> lets <code>SavingsAccount</code> extend <strong>one</strong> base class and inherit its non-private members, modelling a genuine &quot;is-a&quot; relationship: a <code>SavingsAccount</code> <em>is an</em> <code>Account</code>, not merely similar to one.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Account</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Owner</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Balance</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }      <span class=\"tk-com\">// protected: derived classes can change it, outsiders can't</span>\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Account</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> owner, <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> openingBalance)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Owner</span> = owner;\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Balance</span> = openingBalance;\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">virtual</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Withdraw</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> amount)          <span class=\"tk-com\">// shared rule, but subclasses may specialise it</span>\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (amount &gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Balance</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Insufficient funds.&quot;</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Balance</span> -= amount;\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SavingsAccount</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Account</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InterestRate</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SavingsAccount</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> owner, <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> openingBalance, <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> rate) : <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>(owner, openingBalance)\n        =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">InterestRate</span> = rate;\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ApplyMonthlyInterest</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Balance</span> += <span class=\"tk-ty\">Balance</span> * <span class=\"tk-ty\">InterestRate</span>;   <span class=\"tk-com\">// behaviour only savings accounts have</span>\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CheckingAccount</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Account</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">const</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OverdraftLimit</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">100m</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CheckingAccount</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> owner, <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> openingBalance) : <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>(owner, openingBalance) { }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Withdraw</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> amount)        <span class=\"tk-com\">// specialises the shared rule instead of duplicating it</span>\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (amount &gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Balance</span> + <span class=\"tk-ty\">OverdraftLimit</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Insufficient funds, even with overdraft.&quot;</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Balance</span> -= amount;\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> savings = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SavingsAccount</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Alice&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1000m</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">0.02m</span>);\nsavings.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ApplyMonthlyInterest</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;Savings balance after interest: {savings.Balance}&quot;</span>);     <span class=\"tk-com\">// 1020</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> checking = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CheckingAccount</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Bob&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">50m</span>);\nchecking.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Withdraw</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">120m</span>);                                                     <span class=\"tk-com\">// allowed: overdraft covers it</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;Checking balance after overdraft withdrawal: {checking.Balance}&quot;</span>);  <span class=\"tk-com\">// -70</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;Is a SavingsAccount really an Account? {savings is Account}&quot;</span>);       <span class=\"tk-com\">// True — a real &quot;is-a&quot; check</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><code>ApplyMonthlyInterest</code> only exists on <code>SavingsAccount</code> — <code>CheckingAccount</code> has no idea it&#39;s there, and doesn&#39;t need to. What both share — <code>Owner</code>, <code>Balance</code>, the basic shape of <code>Withdraw</code> — lives exactly once, in <code>Account</code>. That&#39;s inheritance earning its keep: one real relationship, modelled once, instead of two accounts that happen to look similar by coincidence.</p>\n<h3 id=\"polymorphism-one-call-many-bodies\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oop/polymorphism-one-call-many-bodies\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Polymorphism: one call, many bodies</h3>\n<p>The problem: Pico Mart needs to notify a customer when an order ships — sometimes by email, sometimes by SMS, and next quarter probably by push notification too. Writing <code>if (type == &quot;email&quot;) ... else if (type == &quot;sms&quot;) ...</code> everywhere that notification happens would mean touching every one of those call sites again each time a new channel is added. <strong>Polymorphism</strong> (&quot;many forms&quot;) is the fix: write the call <strong>once</strong>, against a shared type, and let the <strong>runtime type</strong> of the actual object decide which body runs.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">INotifier</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Notify</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> customerName, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> message);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">EmailNotifier</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">INotifier</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Notify</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> customerName, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> message) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;[Email to {customerName}] {message}&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SmsNotifier</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">INotifier</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Notify</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> customerName, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> message) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;[SMS to {customerName}] {message}&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// This method knows nothing about Email or Sms specifically — just the interface.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShipOrder</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> customerName, <span class=\"tk-ty\">INotifier</span> notifier)\n{\n    notifier.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Notify</span>(customerName, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Your order has shipped!&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">INotifier</span>&gt; channels = [<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">EmailNotifier</span>(), <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SmsNotifier</span>()];\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> channel <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> channels)\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShipOrder</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Priya&quot;</span>, channel);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// same call, same line of code — two different bodies run</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><code>ShipOrder</code>&#39;s own code — <code>notifier.Notify(...)</code> — never changes, no matter how many notifier types exist, including ones that don&#39;t exist yet. Each time through the loop, the <strong>compile-time type</strong> of <code>notifier</code> is the same (<code>INotifier</code>), but the <strong>runtime type</strong> is different (<code>EmailNotifier</code>, then <code>SmsNotifier</code>), and that runtime type is what actually decides which <code>Notify</code> body executes. The <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/oop/virtual-override-and-new-three-keywords-that-look-similar-an\">&quot;virtual, override and new&quot;</a> section below shows exactly how the runtime performs that lookup.</p>\n<h3 id=\"abstraction-depending-on-what-not-how\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oop/abstraction-depending-on-what-not-how\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Abstraction: depending on <em>what</em>, not <em>how</em></h3>\n<p>The problem: <code>OrderService</code> needs to charge a payment, but it shouldn&#39;t need to know, or care, whether that charge goes through Stripe, a bank API, or a fake used only in tests — knowing that detail would couple order logic to one specific vendor for no reason, and would make testing <code>OrderService</code> require a real payment gateway. <strong>Abstraction</strong> is exposing <em>what</em> something does while hiding <em>how</em> — interfaces and abstract classes are the two tools C# gives you for drawing that line deliberately.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGateway</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> amount);     <span class=\"tk-com\">// the WHAT: &quot;charge this much&quot; — no mention of HOW</span>\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Production implementation: the HOW is hidden inside here, and nowhere else.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">StripeGateway</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGateway</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> amount)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;[Stripe] calling a real payment API to charge {amount:C}...&quot;</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromResult</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>);\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Test double: a completely different HOW, behind the exact same WHAT.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FakeGateway</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGateway</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Charges</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> amount) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Charges</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(amount); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromResult</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>); }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderService</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGateway</span> gateway)     <span class=\"tk-com\">// depends only on the interface</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> total)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;Placing order for {total:C}...&quot;</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> gateway.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeAsync</span>(total);\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> prod = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderService</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">StripeGateway</span>());\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> prod.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">49.99m</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> fake = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FakeGateway</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> test = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderService</span>(fake);                    <span class=\"tk-com\">// same OrderService code, zero changes</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> test.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">49.99m</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;Fake gateway recorded {fake.Charges.Count} charge(s) — no real API was ever called.&quot;</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><code>OrderService</code> is written once and never edited to support either case — it depends entirely on the shape described by <code>IPaymentGateway</code>, not on which class actually implements it. That&#39;s the line abstraction draws: the <strong>interface is the promise</strong>, the <strong>implementation is the secret</strong>, and <code>OrderService</code> only ever gets to see the promise.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">abstract</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentMethod</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">abstract</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentResult</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span> amount);          <span class=\"tk-com\">// must override</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">virtual</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Describe</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetType</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>;                      <span class=\"tk-com\">// may override</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">LogAttempt</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ILogger</span> log) =&gt; log.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogInformation</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Charging via {M}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Describe</span>()); <span class=\"tk-com\">// fixed</span>\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CardPayment</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> last4) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentMethod</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentResult</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span> amount) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-com\">/* call card gateway */</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromResult</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentResult</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Describe</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;Card ****{last4}&quot;</span>;\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentMethod</span> pm = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CardPayment</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;4242&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> pm.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;EUR&quot;</span>));   <span class=\"tk-com\">// runs CardPayment.ChargeAsync: runtime type wins</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>That same <code>PaymentMethod</code> abstract class is worth one more look, because it shows three different relationships to overriding, sitting side by side: <code>ChargeAsync</code> is <code>abstract</code> — no body at all, every derived class <strong>must</strong> supply its own. <code>Describe</code> is <code>virtual</code> — it has a default body, and derived classes <strong>may</strong> override it if they want something more specific. <code>LogAttempt</code> is neither — plain, fixed behaviour that every <code>PaymentMethod</code> shares identically, with no variation point at all. Three keywords, three different amounts of flexibility deliberately granted to subclasses.</p>\n<h2 id=\"virtual-override-and-new-three-keywords-that-look-similar-an\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oop/virtual-override-and-new-three-keywords-that-look-similar-an\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>virtual, override and new: three keywords that look similar and aren&#39;t <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Keyword</th>\n<th>On</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>virtual</code></td>\n<td>Base method/property</td>\n<td>Derived classes <em>may</em> override</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>abstract</code></td>\n<td>Method in abstract class</td>\n<td>No body; derived classes <em>must</em> override</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>override</code></td>\n<td>Derived member</td>\n<td>Replaces the base implementation; <strong>dynamic dispatch</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>sealed override</code></td>\n<td>Derived member</td>\n<td>Overrides and stops further overriding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>new</code></td>\n<td>Derived member</td>\n<td><strong>Hides</strong> the base member; no polymorphism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>sealed</code></td>\n<td>Class</td>\n<td>Can&#39;t be inherited</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>base.Method()</code></td>\n<td>Inside override</td>\n<td>Call the base implementation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The last two rows of this table — <code>override</code> and <code>new</code> — are genuinely easy to confuse, because both let a derived class provide its own version of a method with the same name. They do fundamentally different things underneath, and the only way to really see the difference is to watch what each one actually looks up at the moment of a call:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">A</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">virtual</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Who</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A&quot;</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Plain</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A&quot;</span>; }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">B</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">A</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Who</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;B&quot;</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Plain</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;B&quot;</span>; }\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">A</span> x = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">B</span>();\nx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Who</span>();    <span class=\"tk-com\">// &quot;B&quot;: override, decided by runtime type</span>\nx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Plain</span>();  <span class=\"tk-com\">// &quot;A&quot;: new/hiding, decided by COMPILE-TIME type of x</span>\n((<span class=\"tk-ty\">B</span>)x).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Plain</span>(); <span class=\"tk-com\">// &quot;B&quot;</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><img src=\"visuals/virtual-dispatch.svg\" alt=\"Virtual dispatch looks up the real object&#39;s method table at runtime; hiding is decided at compile time from the variable&#39;s declared type\"></p>\n<p>Here&#39;s precisely what the diagram is showing, and it&#39;s worth internalising because it&#39;s the mechanical reason behind every &quot;override vs new&quot; interview answer: every object that has any <code>virtual</code> members carries, alongside its actual fields, a pointer to a <strong>method table</strong> — a lookup structure with one slot per virtual method, and that slot holds whichever override is correct <em>for this object&#39;s real, runtime type</em>. When you call <code>x.Who()</code>, the runtime doesn&#39;t look at what <em>type the variable <code>x</code> was declared as</em> — it follows the actual <code>B</code> object&#39;s own method table, finds the <code>Who</code> slot, sees it&#39;s been overridden to point at <code>B.Who()</code>, and jumps there. The compile-time type of <code>x</code> (<code>A</code>) never enters into it at all. That&#39;s what &quot;dynamic dispatch&quot; means, literally: the dispatch decision happens dynamically, at the call, based on the real object.</p>\n<p><code>Plain()</code> tells the opposite story, because it was never <code>virtual</code> in the first place — there&#39;s no method-table slot for it at all. <code>new</code> doesn&#39;t override anything; it just declares a <em>completely separate</em>, unrelated method on <code>B</code> that happens to share a name with one on <code>A</code>. Which one actually gets called is decided the moment the code compiles, purely from the <strong>declared type of the variable</strong> — <code>x.Plain()</code> compiles straight into &quot;call <code>A.Plain()</code>&quot;, full stop, because <code>x</code> was declared as <code>A</code>, and that decision is baked into the compiled code before the program ever runs. Change the variable&#39;s declared type to <code>B</code> (as the final line does, with an explicit cast) and the exact same object now resolves to <code>B.Plain()</code> instead — nothing about the object changed, only which door you called through.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-deep\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Why this indirection costs something, and why sealed can help</div><p>A virtual call genuinely does more work than a direct one: read the object&#39;s method-table pointer, read the right slot from that table, <em>then</em> jump — one extra hop compared to jumping straight to a known address. That extra hop also blocks a common optimisation: the JIT can&#39;t inline a virtual call the way it inlines a direct one, because it doesn&#39;t know, in general, which concrete method will actually run until the object shows up at runtime — unless it can <strong>devirtualise</strong> the call. <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/dynamic-pgo-the-jit-actually-learns-from-your-programs-real-\">How code runs</a> covers exactly how Dynamic PGO does this by guessing from observed behaviour; <code>sealed</code>, covered later in this chapter, does it unconditionally, because a sealed class guarantees there&#39;s only ever one possible method-table slot value to begin with. That&#39;s the real, mechanical reason &quot;mark it sealed for a small performance win&quot; is genuine advice and not folklore.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"interfaces-a-contract-with-no-implementation-of-its-own\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oop/interfaces-a-contract-with-no-implementation-of-its-own\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Interfaces: a contract with no implementation of its own <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>An <strong>interface</strong> is a contract: a set of members a type promises to provide, with no say in <em>how</em>. Unlike base classes, a single class can implement <strong>as many interfaces as it needs</strong>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderRepository</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>?&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">FindAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct = <span class=\"tk-kw\">default</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> order);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IUnitOfWork</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct = <span class=\"tk-kw\">default</span>); }\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">EfOrderRepository</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span> db) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderRepository</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IUnitOfWork</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>?&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">FindAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct = <span class=\"tk-kw\">default</span>) =&gt; db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FindAsync</span>([id], ct).<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsTask</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> order) =&gt; db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(order);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct = <span class=\"tk-kw\">default</span>) =&gt; db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>(ct);\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h3 id=\"three-newer-interface-capabilities-worth-knowing\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oop/three-newer-interface-capabilities-worth-knowing\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Three newer interface capabilities worth knowing</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Default interface methods</strong> (C# 8): an interface member can carry an actual body. This exists for one specific, narrow purpose — <strong>evolving a published interface without breaking everyone who already implements it</strong> — not as a general substitute for sharing implementation the way a base class does.</li>\n<li><strong>Static abstract members</strong> (C# 11): interfaces can now require <em>static</em> members too, not just instance ones, which is specifically what makes <strong>generic math</strong> possible — <code>T Sum&lt;T&gt;(T[] xs) where T : INumber&lt;T&gt;</code> can call static operators like <code>+</code> on a type it only knows through a generic constraint.</li>\n<li><strong>Explicit implementation</strong>: <code>void IDisposable.Dispose() { }</code> is written so it&#39;s only callable <em>through</em> the interface reference, not through the class directly. This resolves name clashes between two interfaces that happen to declare the same member name, or deliberately hides a member from a class&#39;s &quot;normal&quot; public surface.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"abstract-class-vs-interface-a-decision-not-just-a-vocabulary\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oop/abstract-class-vs-interface-a-decision-not-just-a-vocabulary\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Abstract class vs interface: a decision, not just a vocabulary question <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Abstract class</th>\n<th>Interface</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Multiple per type</td>\n<td>No (one base class)</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fields / state</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>No instance fields</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Constructors</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Access modifiers on members</td>\n<td>Any</td>\n<td>Public by default (others allowed since C# 8)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Implementation</td>\n<td>Full</td>\n<td>Default methods only (limited)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relationship</td>\n<td>&quot;is-a&quot; family with shared code</td>\n<td>&quot;can-do&quot; capability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Versioning</td>\n<td>Adding a non-abstract member is safe</td>\n<td>Adding a member breaks implementers (unless default)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Value types</td>\n<td>Can&#39;t inherit</td>\n<td>Structs can implement</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Turn that table into a rule of thumb: reach for an <strong>interface</strong> to define a contract that genuinely unrelated types can fulfil — and especially at architectural seams you&#39;ll want to mock or swap later, like repositories, gateways, clocks. Reach for an <strong>abstract class</strong> when several closely related types share <em>real, written</em> implementation or state — a template method pattern, or a base <code>Entity</code> class that every domain entity inherits <code>Id</code> and domain-event handling from. The honest signal for which one you need: does the shared thing between these types include actual code and data, or only a promise about what methods exist?</p>\n<h2 id=\"access-modifiers-drawing-the-boundary-around-whats-yours-to-\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oop/access-modifiers-drawing-the-boundary-around-whats-yours-to-\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Access modifiers: drawing the boundary around what&#39;s yours to change <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Modifier</th>\n<th>Accessible from</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>public</code></td>\n<td>Anywhere</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>private</code></td>\n<td>The containing type only (<strong>default for members</strong>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>protected</code></td>\n<td>Containing type and derived types</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>internal</code></td>\n<td>Same assembly (<strong>default for top-level types</strong>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>protected internal</code></td>\n<td>Same assembly <strong>or</strong> derived types</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>private protected</code></td>\n<td>Derived types <strong>in the same assembly</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>file</code> (C# 11)</td>\n<td>The same source file (types only)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>internal</code> specifically deserves more attention than it usually gets. It&#39;s the main tool C# gives you for keeping implementation details completely out of your library&#39;s public API surface — a type that&#39;s <code>internal</code> can be changed freely, any time, with zero risk of breaking anyone who merely <em>consumes</em> your library, because they could never have referenced it in the first place. <code>[InternalsVisibleTo(&quot;Tests&quot;)]</code> punches one deliberate, narrow hole in that wall, letting a specific test assembly reach in to verify internals directly without making them part of the real public contract.</p>\n<h2 id=\"static-sealed-partial-three-modifiers-three-different-jobs\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oop/static-sealed-partial-three-modifiers-three-different-jobs\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>static, sealed, partial: three modifiers, three different jobs <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>static</code> class</strong>: can&#39;t be instantiated and can&#39;t be inherited — it&#39;s just a named bag of static members, useful for stateless helpers and extension methods. Be deliberate about avoiding static <strong>mutable</strong> state specifically: because a static field is shared across every single request and every thread in the whole process, mutable static state becomes a hidden channel through which unrelated requests — and unrelated tests — can silently interfere with each other.</li>\n<li><strong><code>sealed</code> class</strong>: can&#39;t be inherited, full stop. The advice to seal by default unless you&#39;ve actually designed a class for inheritance isn&#39;t just a style preference — it documents real intent (this class&#39;s internals were never written with overriding in mind, so don&#39;t rely on them), and, as the earlier &quot;Why this indirection costs something&quot; box showed, it gives the JIT a concrete reason to devirtualise calls into it.</li>\n<li><strong><code>partial</code> class/method</strong>: lets one logical type be physically split across several files. This exists specifically for tooling and source generators: a hand-written <code>partial class AppJsonContext</code> can sit in one file while a source generator emits the rest into another, and <code>[LoggerMessage] partial void</code> is exactly this pattern in miniature. C# 13 extended the same idea to <strong>partial properties</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"composition-over-inheritance-why-is-a-chains-get-brittle\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oop/composition-over-inheritance-why-is-a-chains-get-brittle\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Composition over inheritance: why &quot;is-a&quot; chains get brittle <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Here&#39;s the problem inheritance creates once a hierarchy grows past a couple of levels: every derived class depends, often invisibly, on details of its base class&#39;s internals — a pattern with its own name, the <strong>fragile base class problem</strong>, where changing something deep in a base class can silently break derived classes nobody thought were affected. Push a hierarchy far enough and you also run into a combinatorial wall:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Inheritance explosion</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Notifier</span> { }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">EmailNotifier</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Notifier</span> { }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">LoggingEmailNotifier</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">EmailNotifier</span> { }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">RetryingLoggingEmailNotifier</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">LoggingEmailNotifier</span> { }   <span class=\"tk-com\">// every combination needs a class</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Want retrying-but-not-logging? Logging-but-not-retrying? Each new <em>combination</em> of behaviours demands its own class in this scheme, and the number of classes grows explosively with the number of independent behaviours you&#39;re trying to mix and match. <strong>Composition</strong> — assembling behaviour from small, separately swappable collaborators instead of baking every combination into the inheritance tree — sidesteps the explosion entirely:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Composition: wrap behaviours (Decorator pattern), assemble with DI</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">INotifier</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SendAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Message</span> m); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">EmailNotifier</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">INotifier</span> { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* ... */</span> }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">LoggingNotifier</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">INotifier</span> inner, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ILogger</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">LoggingNotifier</span>&gt; log) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">INotifier</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SendAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Message</span> m) { log.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogInformation</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Sending {Id}&quot;</span>, m.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>); <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> inner.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SendAsync</span>(m); }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Now &quot;retrying and logging&quot; is just two small wrappers composed together at the point you actually need that combination, with no new class required for it specifically — and &quot;just logging&quot; or &quot;just retrying&quot; are each available too, from the same two building blocks, mixed differently. Use inheritance where you genuinely have a stable, shallow &quot;is-a&quot; hierarchy — a couple of levels, not many. Reach for composition plus interfaces wherever behaviour needs to vary independently. This exact idea — wrapping one implementation inside another that shares its interface — is also precisely what the <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/design-patterns\">Decorator pattern</a> formalises, and what the <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/design-patterns\">Strategy pattern</a> does for swapping an entire algorithm rather than layering behaviour around one.</p>\n<h2 id=\"every-types-hidden-base-class-system-object\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oop/every-types-hidden-base-class-system-object\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Every type&#39;s hidden base class: System.Object <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Every single type in C#, whether you declared it or the BCL did, ultimately derives from <code>System.Object</code>, which is where a handful of members you&#39;ve probably used without a second thought actually come from:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Member</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Equals(object)</code> / <code>GetHashCode()</code></td>\n<td>Override together (see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/type-system/equality-three-different-questions-similar-syntax\">Type system</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ToString()</code></td>\n<td>Override for debugging and logging</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>GetType()</code></td>\n<td>Runtime type (reflection entry point)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>MemberwiseClone()</code></td>\n<td>Protected shallow copy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Finalize()</code></td>\n<td>Exposed as the <code>~ClassName()</code> finalizer syntax</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>An object becomes eligible for garbage collection the moment nothing can reach it any more — <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/memory-gc\">Memory &amp; GC</a> covers exactly how that reachability is determined. For anything that needs cleanup <em>before</em> the GC eventually gets around to it, though, the right tool is deterministic disposal via <code>IDisposable</code>, never a finalizer — see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/exceptions-disposal\">Exceptions &amp; disposal</a> for the full reasoning behind that rule.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Explain the four pillars of OOP with C# examples.</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Encapsulation: private state behind methods that keep it valid (<code>AddAddress</code> enforcing a max). Inheritance: <code>CardPayment : PaymentMethod</code> reusing base behaviour. Polymorphism: calling <code>ChargeAsync</code> on a <code>PaymentMethod</code> variable runs the derived override chosen at runtime. Abstraction: callers depend on <code>IPaymentGateway</code> without knowing which provider sits behind it.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Abstract class vs interface: when would you use each?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>An interface defines a contract that unrelated types can implement, and a type can implement many. Use interfaces for seams you want to swap or mock. An abstract class is a single base for closely related types that share state or implementation (constructors, fields, protected helpers, template methods).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the difference between override and new?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p><code>override</code> participates in virtual dispatch: the runtime type&#39;s implementation runs no matter what the variable&#39;s static type is. <code>new</code> hides the base member: which one runs depends on the compile-time type of the reference. Hiding is almost always a mistake.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the order of initialisation when creating a derived class?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Derived field initialisers, then base field initialisers, then the base constructor body, then the derived constructor body. Static constructors run once before first use of each type.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why shouldn&#39;t you call a virtual method from a constructor?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Because the base constructor runs before the derived constructor. A virtual call would execute the derived override before the derived class&#39;s constructor has initialised its state.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>const vs readonly vs static readonly?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p><code>const</code> is a compile-time constant baked into calling assemblies (only primitives and strings). <code>readonly</code> instance fields are assigned once, in the declaration or constructor. <code>static readonly</code> is assigned once per type at runtime and is not copied into callers, so it&#39;s safe for values that may change between versions.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are default interface methods and when should you use them?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Interface members with a body (C# 8). They let library authors add members to published interfaces without breaking existing implementations. They are not intended to replace abstract base classes for sharing implementation.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why prefer composition over inheritance?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Inheritance couples subclasses to base-class internals and fixes behaviour at compile time, leading to fragile, deep hierarchies and class explosions. Composition assembles behaviour from small interchangeable parts (decorators, strategies) at runtime, typically via DI, and is easier to test and change.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What does sealed do, and why seal classes by default?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>It prevents inheritance. Sealing documents that the class wasn&#39;t designed for extension, prevents fragile-base problems, and allows the JIT to devirtualise calls and speed up type checks.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What&#39;s the difference between internal and private?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>private limits access to the containing type. internal limits access to the containing assembly. internal is the tool for hiding implementation types from consumers of a library or from other layers.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oop/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/fundamentals/tutorials/oop\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Object-oriented programming (C#)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/programming-guide/classes-and-structs/constructors\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Constructors (C#)</a> — the exact, Microsoft-documented six-step order used above: fields zeroed, most-derived field initialisers, base field initialisers, base constructors (Object outward), this type&#39;s constructor, then object initialisers.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/fundamentals/types/interfaces\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Interfaces</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/whats-new/tutorials/static-virtual-interface-members\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Static virtual interface members</a> — the generic-math feature behind <code>static abstract</code> interface members.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/design-guidelines/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Framework Design Guidelines</a> (also a book by Cwalina &amp; Abrams)</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oop/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>The <code>const</code>-baked-into-callers behaviour needs two separately compiled assemblies to show properly, so trust <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/oop/further-reading\">Further reading</a> for that one. Everything else — construction order, the virtual-call-from-constructor trap, <code>override</code> vs <code>new</code>, explicit interface implementation, and all four pillars from Pico Mart&#39;s own code above — is directly observable in one process. Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (plain console app, .NET 8/9, no extra packages).</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: derived field inits, then base field inits, then base ctor body, then derived ctor body.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Shared</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Log</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Clear</span>();\n_ = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DerivedX</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> expected = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>[] { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;derived field&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;base field&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;base ctor body&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;derived ctor body&quot;</span> };\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!<span class=\"tk-ty\">Shared</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Log</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SequenceEqual</span>(expected))\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected {string.Join(&quot;</span> -&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;, expected)}, got {string.Join(&quot;</span> -&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;, Shared.Log)}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: construction order is &quot;</span> + <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Join</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot; -&gt; &quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Shared</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Log</span>));\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: calling a virtual method from a base constructor runs the derived override</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// while the derived class's own fields are still unset (the &quot;trap&quot;).</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> trap = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TrapDerived</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (trap.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SeenDuringBaseCtor</span> != <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected the derived field to still read 0 (unset) during the base ctor's virtual call&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: the virtual call made during the base ctor saw SeenDuringBaseCtor == {trap.SeenDuringBaseCtor} (not yet initialised) — the trap is real&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: override dispatches on the object's runtime type; new/hiding resolves on the variable's declared type.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">A</span> x = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">B</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Who</span>() != <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;B&quot;</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: override should dispatch to B.Who() based on the real object&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Plain</span>() != <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A&quot;</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: new/hiding should resolve to A.Plain() based on x's declared type&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (((<span class=\"tk-ty\">B</span>)x).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Plain</span>() != <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;B&quot;</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: casting to B should reach B's own hidden Plain()&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: override (Who) dispatches by runtime type; new (Plain) resolves by declared type&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: explicit interface implementation is only reachable through the interface reference, not the class type.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">IDisposable</span> asInterface = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Disposer</span>();\nasInterface.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Dispose</span>(); <span class=\"tk-com\">// only compiles through the interface reference</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: Dispose() is callable through the IDisposable reference (explicit implementation hides it on Disposer itself)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim (Encapsulation): a public field lets the &quot;max 5 addresses&quot; rule be silently bypassed;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// a private field plus a validating method enforces it for real.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> leaky = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">LeakyCustomer</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>; i &lt;= <span class=\"tk-num\">7</span>; i++) leaky.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Addresses</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;Address #{i}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (leaky.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Addresses</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> != <span class=\"tk-num\">7</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected the leaky customer to accept all 7 addresses&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> safe = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerX</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> accepted = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>; i &lt;= <span class=\"tk-num\">7</span>; i++) { safe.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAddress</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;Address #{i}&quot;</span>); accepted++; } }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>) { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* expected once the limit is hit */</span> }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (safe.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Addresses</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> != <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span> || accepted != <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected the encapsulated customer to stop at 5, got {safe.Addresses.Count}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: encapsulation — LeakyCustomer accepted {leaky.Addresses.Count} addresses, CustomerX enforced the limit at {safe.Addresses.Count}&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim (Inheritance): SavingsAccount and CheckingAccount share Account's state/behaviour,</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// specialise Withdraw independently, and a SavingsAccount really &quot;is an&quot; Account.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> savings = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SavingsAccountX</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Alice&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1000m</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">0.02m</span>);\nsavings.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ApplyMonthlyInterest</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (savings.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Balance</span> != <span class=\"tk-num\">1020m</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected interest to bring balance to 1020, got {savings.Balance}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> checking = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CheckingAccountX</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Bob&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">50m</span>);\nchecking.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Withdraw</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">120m</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (checking.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Balance</span> != -<span class=\"tk-num\">70m</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected overdraft withdrawal to leave balance at -70, got {checking.Balance}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (savings <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">not</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AccountX</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected SavingsAccountX to really be an AccountX&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: inheritance — savings balance after interest = {savings.Balance}, checking balance after overdraft = {checking.Balance}, is-a holds&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim (Polymorphism): the same ShipOrder call runs a different Notify body depending on the object's runtime type.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> log = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShipOrder</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> customerName, <span class=\"tk-ty\">INotifierX</span> notifier) =&gt; notifier.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Notify</span>(customerName, log);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">INotifierX</span>&gt; channels = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>() { <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">EmailNotifierX</span>(), <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SmsNotifierX</span>() };\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> channel <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> channels) <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShipOrder</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Priya&quot;</span>, channel);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!log.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SequenceEqual</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>[] { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Email&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Sms&quot;</span> }))\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected [Email, Sms], got [{string.Join(&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;, log)}]&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: polymorphism — one ShipOrder call ran Email then Sms bodies, decided by each object's runtime type&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim (Abstraction): OrderService depends only on IPaymentGatewayX; swapping the implementation</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// requires zero changes to OrderService's own code.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> fakeGateway = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FakeGatewayX</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> orderService = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderServiceX</span>(fakeGateway);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> charged = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> orderService.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">49.99m</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!charged || fakeGateway.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Charges</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> != <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> || fakeGateway.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Charges</span>[<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>] != <span class=\"tk-num\">49.99m</span>)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected the fake gateway to record exactly one 49.99 charge&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: abstraction — OrderServiceX charged through FakeGatewayX with zero changes to OrderServiceX itself; recorded charges = [{string.Join(&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;, fakeGateway.Charges)}]&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Shared</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Log</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>();\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">BaseX</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> _bf = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Mark</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;base field&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">BaseX</span>() { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Shared</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Log</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;base ctor body&quot;</span>); }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Mark</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> s) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Shared</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Log</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(s); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; }\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DerivedX</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">BaseX</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> _df = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Mark2</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;derived field&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DerivedX</span>() : <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>() { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Shared</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Log</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;derived ctor body&quot;</span>); }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Mark2</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> s) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Shared</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Log</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(s); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TrapBase</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TrapBase</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">CallVirtual</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">virtual</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CallVirtual</span>() { }\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TrapDerived</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">TrapBase</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SeenDuringBaseCtor</span> = -<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> _ready = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CallVirtual</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">SeenDuringBaseCtor</span> = _ready; <span class=\"tk-com\">// _ready isn't assigned yet at this point</span>\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">A</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">virtual</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Who</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A&quot;</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Plain</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A&quot;</span>; }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">B</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">A</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Who</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;B&quot;</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Plain</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;B&quot;</span>; }\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Disposer</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IDisposable</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IDisposable</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Dispose</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;  (disposed via explicit implementation)&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Encapsulation ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">LeakyCustomer</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Addresses</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>();\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerX</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; _addresses = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">const</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">MaxAddresses</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IReadOnlyList</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Addresses</span> =&gt; _addresses;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAddress</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> address)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (_addresses.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> &gt;= <span class=\"tk-ty\">MaxAddresses</span>)\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;Customers can't have more than {MaxAddresses} addresses.&quot;</span>);\n        _addresses.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(address);\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Inheritance ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AccountX</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Owner</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Balance</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AccountX</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> owner, <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> openingBalance) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Owner</span> = owner; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Balance</span> = openingBalance; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">virtual</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Withdraw</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> amount)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (amount &gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Balance</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Insufficient funds.&quot;</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Balance</span> -= amount;\n    }\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SavingsAccountX</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">AccountX</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InterestRate</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SavingsAccountX</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> owner, <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> openingBalance, <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> rate) : <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>(owner, openingBalance) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">InterestRate</span> = rate;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ApplyMonthlyInterest</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Balance</span> += <span class=\"tk-ty\">Balance</span> * <span class=\"tk-ty\">InterestRate</span>;\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CheckingAccountX</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">AccountX</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">const</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OverdraftLimit</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">100m</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CheckingAccountX</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> owner, <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> openingBalance) : <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>(owner, openingBalance) { }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Withdraw</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> amount)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (amount &gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Balance</span> + <span class=\"tk-ty\">OverdraftLimit</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Insufficient funds, even with overdraft.&quot;</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Balance</span> -= amount;\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Polymorphism ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">INotifierX</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Notify</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> customerName, <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; log); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">EmailNotifierX</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">INotifierX</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Notify</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> customerName, <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; log) =&gt; log.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Email&quot;</span>); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SmsNotifierX</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">INotifierX</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Notify</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> customerName, <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; log) =&gt; log.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Sms&quot;</span>); }\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Abstraction ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGatewayX</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> amount); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FakeGatewayX</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGatewayX</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Charges</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> amount) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Charges</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(amount); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromResult</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>); }\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderServiceX</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGatewayX</span> gateway)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> total) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> gateway.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeAsync</span>(total);\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"a-class-is-two-things-bundled-together","text":"A class is two things bundled together","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"properties-the-public-face-a-field-doesnt-get","text":"Properties: the public face a field doesn't get","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"the-order-things-actually-happen-in-when-you-call-new","text":"The order things actually happen in, when you call new","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"primary-constructors-the-modern-shorthand","text":"Primary constructors: the modern shorthand","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-four-pillars-with-a-reason-each-one-earns-its-place","text":"The four pillars, with a reason each one earns its place","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"encapsulation-the-object-protects-its-own-rules","text":"Encapsulation: the object protects its own rules","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"inheritance-sharing-one-real-is-a-relationship","text":"Inheritance: sharing one real \"is-a\" relationship","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"polymorphism-one-call-many-bodies","text":"Polymorphism: one call, many bodies","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"abstraction-depending-on-what-not-how","text":"Abstraction: depending on what , not how","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"virtual-override-and-new-three-keywords-that-look-similar-an","text":"virtual, override and new: three keywords that look similar and aren't","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"interfaces-a-contract-with-no-implementation-of-its-own","text":"Interfaces: a contract with no implementation of its own","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"three-newer-interface-capabilities-worth-knowing","text":"Three newer interface capabilities worth knowing","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"abstract-class-vs-interface-a-decision-not-just-a-vocabulary","text":"Abstract class vs interface: a decision, not just a vocabulary question","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"access-modifiers-drawing-the-boundary-around-whats-yours-to-","text":"Access modifiers: drawing the boundary around what's yours to change","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"static-sealed-partial-three-modifiers-three-different-jobs","text":"static, sealed, partial: three modifiers, three different jobs","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"composition-over-inheritance-why-is-a-chains-get-brittle","text":"Composition over inheritance: why \"is-a\" chains get brittle","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"every-types-hidden-base-class-system-object","text":"Every type's hidden base class: System.Object","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":7416,"minutes":34,"num":"2.2","part":"p2"},"generics":{"id":"generics","title":"Generics, Constraints and Variance","summary":"Writing code once for many types without losing type safety or speed. Generic types and methods, constraints, how the runtime implements generics, covariance and contravariance, and generic math.","layer":"language","where":"C# language › Types › Generics","fits":"<p>Layer 1, but used everywhere above: <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code> (collections), <code>ILogger&lt;T&gt;</code> and <code>IOptions&lt;T&gt;</code> (ASP.NET Core), <code>DbSet&lt;T&gt;</code> (EF Core), <code>Result&lt;T&gt;</code> and <code>IRequestHandler&lt;TReq,TRes&gt;</code> (architecture). Understanding variance explains why some assignments compile and others don&#39;t.</p>\n","prereqs":["type-system","oop"],"next":["delegates-events","collections"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/generics/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Generics let you write one class/method that works for any type, with the compiler still checking types at compile time instead of at runtime.</li>\n<li>Without generics, you&#39;d store everything as <code>object</code>, which loses type safety and boxes value types (see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/type-system/boxing-what-happens-when-a-value-type-has-to-pretend-to-be-a\">Type system</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Constraints</strong> (<code>where T : ...</code>) tell the compiler what <code>T</code> is guaranteed to support, unlocking members you couldn&#39;t otherwise call.</li>\n<li>.NET generics are <strong>reified</strong> (real types at runtime, no boxing for value types) unlike Java, which erases them.</li>\n<li><strong>Variance</strong> (<code>out T</code>/<code>in T</code>) lets <code>IEnumerable&lt;Cat&gt;</code> act as <code>IEnumerable&lt;Animal&gt;</code> safely — but only for interfaces/delegates that only read or only write <code>T</code>, never both.</li>\n<li>You already use generics constantly: <code>ILogger&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>IOptions&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>DbSet&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>Task&lt;T&gt;</code> all lean on them.</li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout c-analogy\"><div class=\"callout-title\">A warehouse story to hold onto</div><p>Picture a warehouse that only has one size of crate, labelled &quot;contents: anything.&quot; Every item — a book, a bag of flour, a car battery — gets shoved into the same crate, so pulling an item back out means guessing what&#39;s inside before you can use it, and every book was wasted effort because it never needed that crate&#39;s padding in the first place. Generics are the warehouse switching to crates sized and labelled for what&#39;s actually going in: a <code>Crate&lt;Book&gt;</code> only ever holds books, the forklift (the compiler) refuses to load a car battery into one, and a book goes in without any extra padding at all. Keep that picture running through this chapter — &quot;the one-size-crate problem&quot; is <code>ArrayList</code>, and everything below is really about how C# builds better crates.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"the-problem-before-generics-existed\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/generics/the-problem-before-generics-existed\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The problem before generics existed <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Before generics arrived in C# 1, if you wanted one reusable collection class that could hold <em>any</em> type, there was only one way to do it: store everything as <code>object</code>, since <code>object</code> is the one type every other type can be treated as.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"dmFyIGxpc3QgPSBuZXcgQXJyYXlMaXN0KCk7Cmxpc3QuQWRkKDQyKTsgICAgICAgICAgICAgIC8vIGJveGVzCmxpc3QuQWRkKCJvb3BzIik7ICAgICAgICAgIC8vIGNvbXBpbGVzOiBubyB0eXBlIHNhZmV0eQppbnQgbiA9IChpbnQpbGlzdFswXTsgICAgICAvLyBjYXN0ICsgdW5ib3ggbmVlZGVkOyBsaXN0WzFdIHdvdWxkIHRocm93IGF0IFJVTlRJTUU=\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> list = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ArrayList</span>();\nlist.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>);              <span class=\"tk-com\">// boxes</span>\nlist.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;oops&quot;</span>);          <span class=\"tk-com\">// compiles: no type safety</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> n = (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>)list[<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>];      <span class=\"tk-com\">// cast + unbox needed; list[1] would throw at RUNTIME</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Look at what that costs you. <code>list.Add(&quot;oops&quot;)</code> <strong>compiles without complaint</strong> right next to an <code>int</code> — <code>ArrayList</code> has no way to say &quot;only ints, please,&quot; because its <code>Add</code> method takes a plain <code>object</code>, and a string is just as valid an <code>object</code> as an int is. The mistake doesn&#39;t surface until <code>(int)list[0]</code> runs, and then only if you happen to touch the wrong element; <code>list[1]</code> would throw an <code>InvalidCastException</code> the moment anyone actually tried to read it back as an int. On top of the lost type safety, you also saw in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/type-system/boxing-what-happens-when-a-value-type-has-to-pretend-to-be-a\">Type system</a> exactly what <code>list.Add(42)</code> costs: that <code>int</code> has to be <strong>boxed</strong> — heap-allocated — purely so it can fit through a parameter typed as <code>object</code>.</p>\n<p>Generics fix both problems at once, by letting the <em>caller</em> choose the type, and having the compiler remember and enforce that choice everywhere afterward:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"dmFyIGxpc3QgPSBuZXcgTGlzdDxpbnQ+KCk7Cmxpc3QuQWRkKDQyKTsgICAgICAgICAgICAgIC8vIG5vIGJveGluZwpsaXN0LkFkZCgib29wcyIpOyAgICAgICAgICAvLyBjb21waWxlIEVSUk9SCmludCBuID0gbGlzdFswXTsgICAgICAgICAgIC8vIG5vIGNhc3Q=\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> list = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;();\nlist.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>);              <span class=\"tk-com\">// no boxing</span>\nlist.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;oops&quot;</span>);          <span class=\"tk-com\">// compile ERROR</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> n = list[<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>];           <span class=\"tk-com\">// no cast</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Same two lines, opposite outcome: <code>list.Add(&quot;oops&quot;)</code> is now a compile error, not a runtime surprise, and <code>list[0]</code> comes back out already typed as <code>int</code>, with no cast and — as the next section explains properly — no boxing either. Three wins, all from the one idea of a <strong>type parameter</strong>: real <strong>type safety</strong> (caught at compile time, not discovered at runtime), real <strong>performance</strong> (no boxing, no casting), and genuine <strong>reuse</strong> (one implementation serving every type, instead of writing <code>IntList</code>, <code>StringList</code>, <code>OrderList</code> by hand).</p>\n<h2 id=\"writing-your-own-generic-types-and-methods\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/generics/writing-your-own-generic-types-and-methods\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Writing your own generic types and methods <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Generic class</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>? <span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>? <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsSuccess</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>? <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>? error) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span> = error; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Fail</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> error) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">default</span>, error);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Generic interface with two type parameters</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IRequestHandler</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TRequest</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TResponse</span>&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TResponse</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Handle</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TRequest</span> request, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Generic method: type inferred from arguments</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FirstOrFallback</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IEnumerable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; items, <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> fallback)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> item <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> items) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> item;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> fallback;\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> x = <span class=\"tk-ty\">FirstOrFallback</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>[] { <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span> }, <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// T inferred as int</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice the last line doesn&#39;t say <code>FirstOrFallback&lt;int&gt;(...)</code> anywhere — the compiler looks at the arguments you actually passed (<code>new[] { 1, 2 }</code> and <code>0</code>), works out that <code>T</code> must be <code>int</code> to make both fit, and fills it in silently. This is why you rarely see explicit type arguments on everyday generic method calls; the compiler almost always figures it out for you from context.</p>\n<p>Two small conventions worth knowing, because you&#39;ll see them everywhere: use <code>T</code> alone when there&#39;s a single type parameter with no real ambiguity about what it represents, and switch to a descriptive name — <code>TKey</code>, <code>TValue</code>, <code>TRequest</code>, <code>TEntity</code> — the moment there&#39;s more than one, so the parameter list actually reads like documentation. And <code>default(T)</code> (or the shorthand <code>default</code>) is how you get a type-parameter-generic &quot;empty&quot; value: it resolves to <code>null</code> when <code>T</code> ends up being a reference type, and to all-zero-bits when <code>T</code> ends up being a value type — exactly the default-value rule from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/type-system/one-question-that-predicts-a-huge-amount-of-c-behaviour\">Type system</a>, just expressed generically.</p>\n<h2 id=\"constraints-telling-the-compiler-what-t-is-allowed-to-be\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/generics/constraints-telling-the-compiler-what-t-is-allowed-to-be\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Constraints: telling the compiler what <code>T</code> is allowed to be <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>With a bare <code>T</code>, the compiler doesn&#39;t know what type you&#39;ll plug in, so it only lets you call members that <em>every</em> type has — in practice, just the handful inherited from <code>object</code>. A <strong>constraint</strong> is a promise about <code>T</code> (&quot;it will always derive from <code>Entity</code>&quot;, &quot;it will always implement <code>IComparable&lt;T&gt;</code>&quot;). Once you make that promise, the compiler trusts it and unlocks the matching members inside your generic code.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Constraint</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>where T : class</code></td>\n<td>Reference type (<code>class?</code> allows nullable)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>where T : struct</code></td>\n<td>Non-nullable value type</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>where T : notnull</code></td>\n<td>Non-nullable reference or value type</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>where T : unmanaged</code></td>\n<td>Struct with no references (can be used with pointers/<code>stackalloc</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>where T : new()</code></td>\n<td>Has a public parameterless constructor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>where T : BaseEntity</code></td>\n<td>Derives from a base class</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>where T : IComparable&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Implements an interface</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>where T : U</code></td>\n<td>Derives from another type parameter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>where T : allows ref struct</code> (C# 13)</td>\n<td>Permits <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code>-like types as arguments</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">abstract</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NewGuid</span>(); }\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InMemoryRepository</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">where</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; _items = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> item) =&gt; _items[item.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>] = item;          <span class=\"tk-com\">// .Id is usable because of the constraint</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>? <span class=\"tk-ty\">Find</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> id) =&gt; _items.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetValueOrDefault</span>(id);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Max</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> a, <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> b) <span class=\"tk-kw\">where</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IComparable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; =&gt; a.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CompareTo</span>(b) &gt;= <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span> ? a : b;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Create</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;() <span class=\"tk-kw\">where</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> : <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Why does <code>item.Id</code> compile above? Because <code>where T : Entity</code> guarantees <code>T</code> is always an <code>Entity</code> (or something derived from it), so the compiler lets you touch <code>Entity</code>&#39;s members on a bare <code>T</code>. Without that constraint, <code>.Id</code> wouldn&#39;t compile at all. <code>Max&lt;T&gt;</code> works the same way: <code>a.CompareTo(b)</code> only compiles because <code>where T : IComparable&lt;T&gt;</code> guarantees every <code>T</code> has a <code>CompareTo</code> method. <code>Create&lt;T&gt;</code> is a different kind of promise, about construction rather than members: plain <code>new T()</code> is illegal in generic code (the compiler can&#39;t assume <code>T</code> has a parameterless constructor), so <code>where T : new()</code> exists specifically to grant that.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-the-runtime-actually-does-with-a-generic-type\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/generics/what-the-runtime-actually-does-with-a-generic-type\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What the runtime actually does with a generic type <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Does <code>List&lt;int&gt;</code> and <code>List&lt;string&gt;</code> still exist as two separate things once the program is actually running, or does that distinction disappear after compilation? This matters for performance, and .NET and Java answer it differently (see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/generics/further-reading\">Further reading</a> for the source on both).</p>\n<p>Java <strong>erases</strong> generics at compile time: at runtime, <code>List&lt;Cat&gt;</code> and <code>List&lt;Dog&gt;</code> both collapse into the same type-blind <code>List</code>. .NET&#39;s generics are <strong>reified</strong> instead: <code>List&lt;int&gt;</code> and <code>List&lt;string&gt;</code> stay genuinely distinct types at runtime, and <code>typeof(T)</code> inside generic code returns the real type it was called with.</p>\n<p>That split works differently depending on whether <code>T</code> is a value type or a reference type:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Value types</strong> (<code>int</code>, <code>Guid</code>, ...): the JIT compiles a separate, specialised copy of the method for each one — <code>List&lt;int&gt;</code> and <code>List&lt;Guid&gt;</code> each get their own machine code. That&#39;s why there&#39;s no boxing: <code>List&lt;int&gt;</code>&#39;s code was built to hold raw <code>int</code>s directly, the same way hand-written <code>IntList</code> code would.</li>\n<li><strong>Reference types</strong>: every reference is the same size (a pointer), so there&#39;s nothing to specialise — the JIT shares one compiled implementation across all of them, looking up type-specific details in a small hidden table only when needed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><img src=\"visuals/generics-reification.svg\" alt=\"List&lt;T&gt; compiled two ways: a specialised, inline copy per value-type T with no boxing, versus one shared implementation for every reference-type T\"></p>\n<p>Back to the warehouse: a <code>Crate&lt;int&gt;</code> is like a crate built with int-shaped dividers baked right into its walls — the int sits directly in the slot, no wrapping needed. A <code>Crate&lt;string&gt;</code> is more like a crate that just holds a shelf of pointers to wherever the actual strings live elsewhere in the warehouse — one crate <em>design</em> serves every reference type, because a pointer is a pointer regardless of what it points to.</p>\n<p>One useful side effect: <strong>static fields are per closed type</strong>, not shared across instantiations. <code>Cache&lt;int&gt;.Instance</code> and <code>Cache&lt;string&gt;.Instance</code> are two unrelated fields. That gives you a simple, lock-free per-type cache:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"c3RhdGljIGNsYXNzIFR5cGVOYW1lPFQ+IHsgcHVibGljIHN0YXRpYyByZWFkb25seSBzdHJpbmcgVmFsdWUgPSB0eXBlb2YoVCkuTmFtZTsgfSAvLyBjb21wdXRlZCBvbmNlIHBlciBU\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TypeName</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>; } <span class=\"tk-com\">// computed once per T</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><code>TypeName&lt;Order&gt;.Value</code> runs its field initializer once, caches <code>&quot;Order&quot;</code>, and never recomputes it. <code>TypeName&lt;Customer&gt;</code> has its own independent field, computed the first time <em>it&#39;s</em> touched.</p>\n<h2 id=\"variance-when-is-listcat-actually-a-listanimal\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/generics/variance-when-is-listcat-actually-a-listanimal\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Variance: when is <code>List&lt;Cat&gt;</code> actually a <code>List&lt;Animal&gt;</code>? <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>If <code>Cat</code> inherits from <code>Animal</code>, is <code>List&lt;Cat&gt;</code> also a <code>List&lt;Animal&gt;</code>? This trips up even experienced developers. The honest answer: <strong>it depends which interface you&#39;re asking about.</strong></p>\n<p>First, see why <code>List&lt;Cat&gt;</code> itself can never safely be treated as a <code>List&lt;Animal&gt;</code> — watch what would go wrong if the compiler allowed it:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"TGlzdDxDYXQ+IGNhdHMgPSBuZXcoKTsKTGlzdDxBbmltYWw+IGFuaW1hbHMgPSBjYXRzOyAgICAgLy8gc3VwcG9zZSB0aGlzIGNvbXBpbGVkCmFuaW1hbHMuQWRkKG5ldyBEb2coKSk7ICAgICAgICAgIC8vIG5vdyBhIERvZyBpcyBpbiB0aGUgTGlzdDxDYXQ+IQ==\"><code><span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cat</span>&gt; cats = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Animal</span>&gt; animals = cats;     <span class=\"tk-com\">// suppose this compiled</span>\nanimals.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dog</span>());          <span class=\"tk-com\">// now a Dog is in the List&lt;Cat&gt;!</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>If that first assignment were legal, the second line lets you add a <code>Dog</code> through <code>animals</code> — but <code>animals</code> and <code>cats</code> are the same list in memory, so you&#39;d have just put a <code>Dog</code> inside what&#39;s supposedly a <code>List&lt;Cat&gt;</code>. The next <code>foreach (Cat c in cats)</code> would throw, trying to treat that <code>Dog</code> as a <code>Cat</code>. This is why generic types are <strong>invariant</strong> by default: <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code> lets you both <em>read and write</em> <code>T</code>, and allowing this substitution would make writing unsafe.</p>\n<p>The danger comes from <strong>writing</strong>, not reading. So for an interface or delegate where <code>T</code> only ever flows <strong>one direction</strong> — only read out, or only accepted in, never both — the danger disappears, and C# lets you declare that one-directional promise explicitly:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th>Keyword</th>\n<th>Allowed when T is…</th>\n<th>Effect</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Covariance</strong></td>\n<td><code>out T</code></td>\n<td>only <strong>returned</strong> (output)</td>\n<td><code>I&lt;Derived&gt;</code> → <code>I&lt;Base&gt;</code></td>\n<td><code>IEnumerable&lt;out T&gt;</code>, <code>IReadOnlyList&lt;out T&gt;</code>, <code>IAsyncEnumerable&lt;out T&gt;</code> (a sequence of values produced over time — see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/async-await\">async/await</a>), <code>Func&lt;out TResult&gt;</code> — a delegate, a type representing &quot;a method with this signature&quot;, covered in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/delegates-events\">Delegates &amp; events</a> (not <code>Task&lt;T&gt;</code>: it&#39;s a class, so invariant)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Contravariance</strong></td>\n<td><code>in T</code></td>\n<td>only <strong>accepted</strong> (input)</td>\n<td><code>I&lt;Base&gt;</code> → <code>I&lt;Derived&gt;</code></td>\n<td><code>Action&lt;in T&gt;</code>, <code>IComparer&lt;in T&gt;</code>, <code>IEqualityComparer&lt;in T&gt;</code>, <code>Func&lt;in T, …&gt;</code> (delegates, see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/delegates-events\">Delegates &amp; events</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-ty\">IEnumerable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cat</span>&gt; cats = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cat</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">IEnumerable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Animal</span>&gt; animals = cats;          <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✔ covariant: you can only READ Animals out</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Action</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Animal</span>&gt; feedAny = a =&gt; a.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Feed</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Action</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cat</span>&gt; feedCat = feedAny;               <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✔ contravariant: something that can feed any Animal can feed a Cat</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">IComparer</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Animal</span>&gt; byWeight = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Comparer</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Animal</span>&gt;.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Create</span>((x, y) =&gt; x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Kg</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CompareTo</span>(y.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Kg</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> sortedCats = cats.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>(byWeight);       <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✔ an Animal comparer works for Cats</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Why is each one actually safe, not just permitted? <code>IEnumerable&lt;Cat&gt;</code> only ever hands <code>Cat</code>s <em>out</em> — there&#39;s no <code>Add</code> method — so treating it as <code>IEnumerable&lt;Animal&gt;</code> can never let a <code>Dog</code> sneak in; everything you pull out really is a <code>Cat</code>, and a <code>Cat</code> really is a valid <code>Animal</code>. <code>Action&lt;Animal&gt;</code> only ever <em>accepts</em> an <code>Animal</code> as input, never hands one back — so using it where an <code>Action&lt;Cat&gt;</code> is expected is equally safe: something that already knows how to handle <em>any</em> <code>Animal</code> obviously handles the more specific case of a <code>Cat</code>.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-analogy\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Remember it with producers and consumers</div><p><strong>Out = producer.</strong> Something that only ever <em>produces</em> Cats is also, trivially, something that produces Animals — anyone expecting Animals back is perfectly happy receiving Cats specifically. <strong>In = consumer.</strong> Something that can <em>consume</em> any Animal can certainly consume a Cat, since a Cat always qualifies as an Animal. Interfaces that do <strong>both</strong> — produce and consume the same <code>T</code>, the way <code>IList&lt;T&gt;</code> does with its <code>Add</code> and its indexer — can&#39;t safely claim either direction, and have to stay invariant.</p>\n</aside>\n<p>Three rules, so you don&#39;t have to rediscover them by trial and error:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Variance only applies to <strong>interfaces and delegates</strong> — never to classes like <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code>, which is why the first example in this section fails.</li>\n<li>It only applies to <strong>reference type</strong> arguments — <code>IEnumerable&lt;int&gt;</code> can never be an <code>IEnumerable&lt;object&gt;</code>, because that would require silent boxing, which C# variance doesn&#39;t do.</li>\n<li><strong>Arrays</strong> are a historical exception: <code>object[] o = new string[1]; o[0] = 1;</code> compiles (arrays have been covariant in C# since before generics existed, for backward compatibility) but throws <code>ArrayTypeMismatchException</code> at runtime — because, unlike <code>IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</code>, an array <em>does</em> let you write into it. Arrays trade the compile-time safety interfaces give you for a runtime check instead.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"generic-math-interfaces-that-require-static-members\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/generics/generic-math-interfaces-that-require-static-members\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Generic math: interfaces that require static members <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Sum</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadOnlySpan</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; values) <span class=\"tk-kw\">where</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">INumber</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> total = <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Zero</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> v <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> values) total += v;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> total;\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sum</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;([<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>]);         <span class=\"tk-com\">// 6</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sum</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>&gt;([<span class=\"tk-num\">1.5m</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2.5m</span>]);  <span class=\"tk-com\">// 4.0</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><code>T.Zero</code> and <code>total += v</code> are calls to <strong>static</strong> members (<code>Zero</code>, the <code>+</code> operator) on a type the method only knows as a generic parameter. Before C# 11, constraints could only describe <em>instance</em> members, so this couldn&#39;t be written. <strong>Static abstract interface members</strong> (C# 11 / .NET 7) close that gap: <code>Sum</code> can now be written once and work for <code>int</code>, <code>decimal</code>, or anything else implementing <code>INumber&lt;T&gt;</code>, instead of a hand-written overload per numeric type. <code>INumber&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>IAdditionOperators&lt;…&gt;</code>, <code>IParsable&lt;T&gt;</code> and friends live in <code>System.Numerics</code>. Most backend code never writes its own generic-math code, but you&#39;ll meet <code>IParsable&lt;T&gt;</code> when binding custom types from route/query parameters (see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/building-apis\">Building APIs</a>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"spotting-generics-you-already-use-every-day\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/generics/spotting-generics-you-already-use-every-day\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Spotting generics you already use every day <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Framework APIs lean on generics constantly, and recognising <em>what role</em> <code>T</code> is actually playing in each one takes most of the mystery out of reading unfamiliar code:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>API</th>\n<th>What <code>T</code> does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>ILogger&lt;OrderService&gt;</code></td>\n<td><code>T</code> is only a <strong>category name</strong> for logs (the full type name)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IOptions&lt;PaymentOptions&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Which options class to bind</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>DbSet&lt;Order&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Which entity/table</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IValidator&lt;CreateOrder&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Which type to validate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>services.AddScoped&lt;IRepo, Repo&gt;()</code></td>\n<td>Interface → implementation mapping</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>services.AddScoped(typeof(IRepository&lt;&gt;), typeof(EfRepository&lt;&gt;))</code></td>\n<td><strong>Open generic</strong> registration: any <code>IRepository&lt;X&gt;</code> resolves to <code>EfRepository&lt;X&gt;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Task&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>ValueTask&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Result type of async work</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IAsyncEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Async stream of <code>T</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Results&lt;Ok&lt;T&gt;, NotFound&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Union of possible HTTP results for OpenAPI</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The <code>ILogger&lt;OrderService&gt;</code> row surprises people: <code>T</code> does nothing functional here — <code>ILogger&lt;T&gt;</code> never constructs or calls a <code>T</code>. It&#39;s purely a label, tagging every log line with <code>OrderService</code>&#39;s full type name so you can filter logs by which class produced them. Same generic mechanism, used for a purpose that has nothing to do with type safety.</p>\n<p>The open-generic registration is worth a second look, since the syntax is unusual the first time you see it:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"Ly8gT3BlbiBnZW5lcmljIHJlZ2lzdHJhdGlvbjogb25lIGxpbmUgY292ZXJzIGV2ZXJ5IGVudGl0eSB0eXBlCmJ1aWxkZXIuU2VydmljZXMuQWRkU2NvcGVkKHR5cGVvZihJUmVwb3NpdG9yeTw+KSwgdHlwZW9mKEVmUmVwb3NpdG9yeTw+KSk7\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Open generic registration: one line covers every entity type</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IRepository</span>&lt;&gt;), <span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">EfRepository</span>&lt;&gt;));</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><code>IRepository&lt;&gt;</code> — no type argument inside the brackets — is an <strong>open</strong> generic: a description of a whole family of types, not one specific closed type like <code>IRepository&lt;Order&gt;</code>. Registering it once lets the DI container (see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/dependency-injection\">Dependency injection</a>) build a matching <code>EfRepository&lt;X&gt;</code> for <em>any</em> <code>IRepository&lt;X&gt;</code> requested later, without a separate registration line per entity.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are generics and what problems do they solve?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Type parameters that let you write classes, interfaces and methods once for any type. They give compile-time type safety, avoid boxing and casts (better performance), and enable reuse, unlike object-based code.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What generic constraints are available?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>class, class?, struct, notnull, unmanaged, new(), a base class, an interface, another type parameter, default (for overrides), and allows ref struct (C# 13). Constraints let you use the members of the constrained type inside generic code.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Explain covariance and contravariance.</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Covariance (<code>out T</code>) lets <code>I&lt;Derived&gt;</code> be used as <code>I&lt;Base&gt;</code>; safe when T only appears in output positions (IEnumerable&lt;out T&gt;). Contravariance (<code>in T</code>) lets <code>I&lt;Base&gt;</code> be used as <code>I&lt;Derived&gt;</code>; safe when T only appears in input positions (Action&lt;in T&gt;, IComparer&lt;in T&gt;). It applies to interfaces and delegates with reference-type arguments.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why isn&#39;t List<Cat> assignable to List<Animal>?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Because List&lt;T&gt; both reads and writes T. If it were allowed, you could add a Dog through the List&lt;Animal&gt; reference into what is actually a List&lt;Cat&gt;. Use IEnumerable&lt;Animal&gt; or IReadOnlyList&lt;Animal&gt; (covariant) if you only need to read.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How are .NET generics different from Java generics?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>.NET generics are reified: closed generic types exist at runtime, <code>typeof(T)</code> works, and value-type instantiations get specialised code with no boxing. Java uses type erasure, so generic type info is gone at runtime and primitives must be boxed.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is an open generic registration in DI?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Registering a generic type definition, e.g. <code>AddScoped(typeof(IRepository&lt;&gt;), typeof(EfRepository&lt;&gt;))</code>, so the container can create any closed version (IRepository&lt;Order&gt;) on demand without registering each one.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Are static fields shared across generic instantiations?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>No. Each closed generic type (Foo&lt;int&gt;, Foo&lt;string&gt;) has its own copy of static fields, which is useful for per-type caches.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/generics/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/generics/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Generics in .NET</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/generics/covariance-and-contravariance\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Covariance and contravariance</a> — confirmed against this page: variance applies only to generic interfaces/delegates with reference-type arguments, never to classes, never to value types.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/generics/math\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Generic math</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/generics/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Save this as <code>Program.cs</code> in a new console project (<code>dotnet new console</code>) and run it with <code>dotnet run</code>. It exercises type inference, constraints, reification, variance, array-covariance&#39;s runtime check, and generic math from this chapter; each line should print <code>OK:</code>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Numerics</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> failures = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> claim, <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> ok)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (ok) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: {claim}&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">else</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: {claim}&quot;</span>); failures++; }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 1. Type inference picks T from arguments</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FirstOrFallback</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IEnumerable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; items, <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> fallback)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> item <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> items) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> item;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> fallback;\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> inferred = <span class=\"tk-ty\">FirstOrFallback</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>[] { <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span> }, <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;generic method type argument is inferred from the call&quot;</span>, inferred == <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 2. Constraint unlocks members: InMemoryRepository&lt;T&gt; where T : Entity</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> repo = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InMemoryRepository</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> order = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>();\nrepo.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(order);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;where T : Entity lets generic code use T.Id&quot;</span>, repo.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Find</span>(order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>) == order);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 3. where T : IComparable&lt;T&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Max&lt;T&gt; with IComparable constraint picks the larger value&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Max</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">7</span>) == <span class=\"tk-num\">7</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 4. where T : new()</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> created = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Create</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;where T : new() allows new T() inside generic code&quot;</span>, created <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 5. Reification: closed generic types are genuinely distinct at runtime, typeof(T) works</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;typeof(T) inside a generic method returns the real closed type&quot;</span>,\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;) != <span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;) &amp;&amp; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetTypeName</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;() == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Int32&quot;</span> &amp;&amp; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetTypeName</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;() == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;String&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 6. Static fields are per closed generic type, not shared</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;static fields are independent per closed generic type (Cache&lt;int&gt; vs Cache&lt;string&gt;)&quot;</span>,\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Cache</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span> != <span class=\"tk-ty\">Cache</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 7. Variance: IEnumerable&lt;Cat&gt; usable as IEnumerable&lt;Animal&gt; (covariance)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">IEnumerable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cat</span>&gt; cats = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cat</span>&gt; { <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Cat</span>() };\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">IEnumerable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Animal</span>&gt; animalsOut = cats;\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;IEnumerable&lt;out T&gt; covariance: IEnumerable&lt;Cat&gt; assignable to IEnumerable&lt;Animal&gt;&quot;</span>, animalsOut.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span>() == <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 8. Variance: Action&lt;Animal&gt; usable as Action&lt;Cat&gt; (contravariance)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> fedCount = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Action</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Animal</span>&gt; feedAny = a =&gt; fedCount++;\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Action</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cat</span>&gt; feedCat = feedAny;\nfeedCat(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Cat</span>());\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Action&lt;in T&gt; contravariance: Action&lt;Animal&gt; assignable to Action&lt;Cat&gt;&quot;</span>, fedCount == <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 9. Array covariance is a runtime-checked exception, not compile-time safe</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> threw = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>[] arr = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>[<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>];\n    arr[<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>] = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>; <span class=\"tk-com\">// compiles, but...</span>\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">ArrayTypeMismatchException</span>) { threw = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>; }\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;writing a non-string into a string[] via object[] throws ArrayTypeMismatchException at runtime&quot;</span>, threw);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 10. Generic math: one Sum&lt;T&gt; implementation works for int and decimal</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Sum&lt;int&gt; via INumber&lt;T&gt; generic math&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Sum</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;([<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>]) == <span class=\"tk-num\">6</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Sum&lt;decimal&gt; via INumber&lt;T&gt; generic math&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Sum</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>&gt;([<span class=\"tk-num\">1.5m</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2.5m</span>]) == <span class=\"tk-num\">4.0m</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(failures == <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span> ? <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;\\nAll checks passed.&quot;</span> : <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;\\n{failures} check(s) failed.&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (failures &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exit</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Max</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> a, <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> b) <span class=\"tk-kw\">where</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IComparable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; =&gt; a.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CompareTo</span>(b) &gt;= <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span> ? a : b;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Create</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;() <span class=\"tk-kw\">where</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> : <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetTypeName</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Sum</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadOnlySpan</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; values) <span class=\"tk-kw\">where</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">INumber</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> total = <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Zero</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> v <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> values) total += v;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> total;\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">abstract</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NewGuid</span>(); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span> { }\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InMemoryRepository</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">where</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; _items = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> item) =&gt; _items[item.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>] = item;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>? <span class=\"tk-ty\">Find</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> id) =&gt; _items.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetValueOrDefault</span>(id);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Cache</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NewGuid</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>(); }\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Animal</span> { }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Cat</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Animal</span> { }</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Not runtime-verifiable here (needs disassembly, not just execution): that the JIT emits genuinely separate native code per value-type instantiation, and that no boxing occurs for <code>List&lt;int&gt;</code>. For those, see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/generics/further-reading\">Further reading</a> or inspect the generated IL/assembly in SharpLab.</p>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-problem-before-generics-existed","text":"The problem before generics existed","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"writing-your-own-generic-types-and-methods","text":"Writing your own generic types and methods","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"constraints-telling-the-compiler-what-t-is-allowed-to-be","text":"Constraints: telling the compiler what T is allowed to be","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"what-the-runtime-actually-does-with-a-generic-type","text":"What the runtime actually does with a generic type","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"variance-when-is-listcat-actually-a-listanimal","text":"Variance: when is List<Cat> actually a List<Animal> ?","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"generic-math-interfaces-that-require-static-members","text":"Generic math: interfaces that require static members","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"spotting-generics-you-already-use-every-day","text":"Spotting generics you already use every day","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":4071,"minutes":19,"num":"2.3","part":"p2"},"delegates-events":{"id":"delegates-events","title":"Delegates, Lambdas, Closures and Events","summary":"Treating behaviour as data. Delegate types, Func and Action, lambdas, closures and what the compiler generates for them, events and the observer pattern, and how ASP.NET Core is built out of delegates.","layer":"language","where":"C# language › Functions as values","fits":"<p>Layer 1. This is the hidden key to understanding layer 3. A middleware is a <code>Func&lt;RequestDelegate, RequestDelegate&gt;</code>, a minimal API endpoint is a delegate, LINQ operators take delegates, and configuration callbacks (<code>options =&gt; …</code>) are lambdas. Once delegates click, <code>Program.cs</code> stops looking like magic.</p>\n","prereqs":["oop","generics"],"next":["linq","middleware","async-await"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/delegates-events/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>delegate</strong> is a variable that holds a reference to a method, so you can pass behaviour around like data.</li>\n<li><code>Func&lt;...&gt;</code> (returns a value) and <code>Action&lt;...&gt;</code> (returns nothing) are the built-in delegate types you&#39;ll use almost always, instead of declaring your own.</li>\n<li>A <strong>lambda</strong> (<code>x =&gt; x * x</code>) is just an inline, unnamed function that gets converted into a delegate.</li>\n<li>A <strong>closure</strong> happens when a lambda keeps using a local variable after its method has returned — the compiler keeps that variable alive on the heap, which costs an allocation and means captured variables are shared, not copied.</li>\n<li>An <strong>event</strong> is a delegate field with subscribing (<code>+=</code>/<code>-=</code>) allowed from outside but invoking and overwriting restricted to the owner — the built-in Observer pattern.</li>\n<li>ASP.NET Core&#39;s whole pipeline (<code>Program.cs</code>, middleware, minimal API endpoints) is just delegates passed around.</li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout c-analogy\"><div class=\"callout-title\">A story to hold onto: the pricing rule you hand to the till</div><p>Imagine a shop till that needs to apply &quot;whatever today&#39;s discount rule is&quot; to every sale. The till itself shouldn&#39;t need rewiring every time marketing invents a new sale — Black Friday today, a loyalty discount next month. Instead, someone hands the till a <em>rule</em> — a small, swappable slip of behaviour it just calls when it needs a price — and the till stays exactly the same while the rule underneath it changes freely. That slip of behaviour is a delegate. Keep this one picture running: a lambda is a rule written on the spot, a closure is a rule that remembers something from where it was written, an event is a noticeboard the till posts to instead of handing out a single slip, and <code>Program.cs</code> at the end of this chapter turns out to be built entirely from slips like this.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"treating-a-method-like-a-value-you-can-pass-around\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/delegates-events/treating-a-method-like-a-value-you-can-pass-around\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Treating a method like a value you can pass around <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Everything in this chapter — lambdas, closures, events, and eventually the entire shape of <code>Program.cs</code> — grows out of one simple capability: being able to hold a reference to a <em>method</em> in a variable, the same way you&#39;d hold a reference to a string or a number, and call it later without caring, at that moment, exactly which method it actually points to.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// 1. Declare a delegate type (a method signature with a name)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">delegate</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PriceRule</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> basePrice);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 2. Methods that match the signature</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">BlackFriday</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> p) =&gt; p * <span class=\"tk-num\">0.7m</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">NoDiscount</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> p) =&gt; p;\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 3. Use it like a variable</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">PriceRule</span> rule = <span class=\"tk-ty\">BlackFriday</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> final = rule(<span class=\"tk-num\">100m</span>);          <span class=\"tk-com\">// 70; calls BlackFriday through the delegate</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(final);            <span class=\"tk-com\">// 70</span>\nrule = <span class=\"tk-ty\">NoDiscount</span>;                    <span class=\"tk-com\">// swap behaviour at runtime</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(rule(<span class=\"tk-num\">100m</span>));       <span class=\"tk-com\">// 100 — same call site, different behaviour now</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>That&#39;s the whole snippet, runnable as-is: paste it into a new console project&#39;s <code>Program.cs</code> (with the <code>delegate</code> line and the two <code>static</code> methods above it) and <code>dotnet run</code> prints <code>70</code> then <code>100</code> — the same line, <code>rule(100m)</code>, producing a different result purely because <code>rule</code> itself changed in between.</p>\n<p>A <strong>delegate</strong> is what makes that possible: a type-safe object that points to a method, which you can store in a variable, pass as an argument, and invoke later — exactly as the <code>rule</code> variable does above. Notice that last line especially: <code>rule</code> gets reassigned from <code>BlackFriday</code> to <code>NoDiscount</code> at runtime, and whatever code calls <code>rule(...)</code> doesn&#39;t need to change at all to pick up the new behaviour.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-why\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Why this capability matters so much</div><p>It lets you <strong>pass behaviour</strong> around using the same mechanism you&#39;d use to pass data. A sort algorithm doesn&#39;t need to know, hard-coded, <em>how</em> to compare two items — you hand it a comparison function, and it stays generic forever. A retry helper doesn&#39;t need to know <em>what</em> operation it&#39;s retrying — you hand it the operation as a value. A web framework doesn&#39;t need to know your specific endpoint logic — you hand it a function, and it calls that function when a request arrives. This single idea is the foundation underneath callbacks, LINQ, events, middleware, and — as the final section of this chapter shows directly — most of how modern ASP.NET Core is actually built.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"func-action-and-predicate-delegates-you-never-have-to-declar\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/delegates-events/func-action-and-predicate-delegates-you-never-have-to-declar\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Func, Action and Predicate: delegates you never have to declare yourself <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>In practice, you&#39;ll almost never write <code>public delegate ... ;</code> the way the example above did. The BCL already ships generic delegate types covering nearly every signature you&#39;d ever need:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Delegate</th>\n<th>Signature</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Action</code></td>\n<td><code>() → void</code></td>\n<td><code>Action log = () =&gt; Console.WriteLine(&quot;hi&quot;);</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Action&lt;T1..T16&gt;</code></td>\n<td><code>(T…) → void</code></td>\n<td><code>Action&lt;string&gt; print = s =&gt; Console.WriteLine(s);</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Func&lt;TResult&gt;</code></td>\n<td><code>() → TResult</code></td>\n<td><code>Func&lt;DateTime&gt; now = () =&gt; DateTime.UtcNow;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Func&lt;T1..T16, TResult&gt;</code></td>\n<td><code>(T…) → TResult</code> (last type is the return)</td>\n<td><code>Func&lt;int, int, int&gt; add = (a, b) =&gt; a + b;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Predicate&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td><code>T → bool</code></td>\n<td><code>Predicate&lt;Order&gt; big = o =&gt; o.Total &gt; 1000;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Comparison&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td><code>(T, T) → int</code></td>\n<td><code>list.Sort((a, b) =&gt; a.Price.CompareTo(b.Price));</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>EventHandler&lt;TArgs&gt;</code></td>\n<td><code>(object? sender, TArgs e) → void</code></td>\n<td>Events</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The pattern worth internalising: <code>Action</code> is &quot;does something, returns nothing&quot;; <code>Func</code> is &quot;does something, returns a value&quot; — and <code>Func</code>&#39;s <em>last</em> type parameter is always the return type, with everything before it being the parameters. That convention lets a method like <code>RetryAsync</code> below stay completely ignorant of what operation it&#39;s actually retrying:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">RetryAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;&gt; operation, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> attempts = <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>; ; i++)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> operation(); }\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">when</span> (i &lt; attempts) { <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Delay</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">200</span> * i); }\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> user = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">RetryAsync</span>(() =&gt; api.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetUserAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>));   <span class=\"tk-com\">// pass the &quot;what&quot; as a lambda</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><code>RetryAsync</code> has no idea it&#39;s fetching a user — it just knows it has some <code>Func&lt;Task&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code> to call, and retries it if it throws. That&#39;s exactly the &quot;pass behaviour, not just data&quot; idea from the <code>:::why</code> box above, made concrete. (The <code>async</code>/<code>await</code>/<code>Task&lt;T&gt;</code> in this snippet are about pausing work without blocking a thread — the dedicated <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/async-await\">Async/await</a> chapter covers that; here they&#39;re just a method signature, no different from any other <code>T</code> you&#39;d pass.)</p>\n<h2 id=\"lambdas-writing-the-delegate-inline\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/delegates-events/lambdas-writing-the-delegate-inline\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Lambdas: writing the delegate inline <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>A <strong>lambda expression</strong> is an inline, anonymous function — instead of declaring <code>BlackFriday</code> as a named method somewhere else and then referencing it, you write the function body right where you need it. The compiler converts a lambda into a delegate (or, in one specific situation covered later in this chapter, into an <strong>expression tree</strong> instead — see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/linq/expression-trees-what-ef-core-is-actually-reading\">LINQ</a> for exactly when and why).</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; square = x =&gt; x * x;                          <span class=\"tk-com\">// expression lambda</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; add = (a, b) =&gt; a + b;\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span>&gt; isEmail = s =&gt; { <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> at = s.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IndexOf</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">'@'</span>); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> at &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span> &amp;&amp; at &lt; s.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Length</span> - <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>; }; <span class=\"tk-com\">// statement lambda</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Action</span> noArgs = () =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;hi&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; typed = (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> x) =&gt; x + <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>;                       <span class=\"tk-com\">// explicit parameter type</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> inferred = (<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> s) =&gt; s.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Length</span>;                          <span class=\"tk-com\">// C# 10: natural type → Func&lt;string,int&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; discard = _ =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>;                               <span class=\"tk-com\">// discard parameter</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> withDefault = (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> x = <span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>) =&gt; x * <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>;                        <span class=\"tk-com\">// C# 12: default parameter values</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Helper</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> x) =&gt; x + <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>;                              <span class=\"tk-com\">// local function (named, inside a method)</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>One shortcut worth knowing, because it comes up constantly in real code: when a method you already have matches the delegate&#39;s signature exactly, you can pass the <strong>method group</strong> — just the method&#39;s name, no lambda wrapper at all — and the compiler builds the delegate for you: <code>names.Select(int.Parse)</code>, <code>app.MapGet(&quot;/&quot;, GetAll)</code>. If you find yourself writing <code>x =&gt; int.Parse(x)</code>, that&#39;s almost always exactly equivalent to the shorter <code>int.Parse</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"closures-when-a-lambda-outlives-the-method-it-was-written-in\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/delegates-events/closures-when-a-lambda-outlives-the-method-it-was-written-in\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Closures: when a lambda outlives the method it was written in <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Here&#39;s a strange-sounding claim: a lambda can keep using a local variable from the method it was written in, <strong>even after that method has already returned</strong>. Watch it happen:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">MakeCounter</span>()\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> count = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;                 <span class=\"tk-com\">// local variable...</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> () =&gt; ++count;          <span class=\"tk-com\">// ...captured by the lambda</span>\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> next = <span class=\"tk-ty\">MakeCounter</span>();\nnext(); next();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(next());         <span class=\"tk-com\">// 3: 'count' survived after MakeCounter returned</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>By the normal rule from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/type-system/value-types-live-on-the-stack-a-useful-lie\">Type system</a>, <code>count</code> is a local variable, so it should live in a stack frame that&#39;s popped the instant <code>MakeCounter</code> returns. But <code>MakeCounter</code> <em>has already returned</em> by the time <code>next()</code> runs — so how does <code>count</code> survive?</p>\n<p>A lambda that uses a local variable from its enclosing scope is said to <strong>capture</strong> that variable. Here&#39;s how the compiler actually pulls that off:</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-deep\"><div class=\"callout-title\">What the compiler actually generates for a captured local</div><p>A captured local can&#39;t stay on the stack, so the compiler doesn&#39;t try. It rewrites the whole method, moving the captured variable into a hidden heap-allocated class made just for this purpose:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// roughly what MakeCounter becomes</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DisplayClass</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> count; <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Lambda</span>() =&gt; ++count; }\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">MakeCounter</span>()\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> closure = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DisplayClass</span>();   <span class=\"tk-com\">// heap allocation</span>\n    closure.count = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> closure.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lambda</span>;              <span class=\"tk-com\">// delegate pointing at an instance method → another allocation</span>\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Once you see this generated code, three things stop looking arbitrary: capturing <strong>allocates</strong> — a heap object (<code>DisplayClass</code>) plus a delegate wrapping one of its methods, neither of which existed before. Captured variables are <strong>shared</strong>, not copied, by every lambda that captures them — they&#39;re all reading and writing the <em>same field</em> on the <em>same</em> object. And a captured variable lives exactly as long as the delegate holding it does, because it&#39;s now just a field on an ordinary heap object, kept alive by the same reachability rules as any other object (see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/memory-gc\">Memory &amp; GC</a>).</p>\n<p>Non-capturing lambdas pay none of this cost: with nothing to capture, the compiler caches one instance and reuses it, allocating nothing on repeated calls. To catch an <em>accidental</em> capture before it costs you anything, mark the lambda <code>static</code> (<code>static x =&gt; x * 2</code>) — the compiler then refuses to compile it if it secretly captures something.</p>\n</aside>\n<h3 id=\"the-loop-capture-trap-a-bug-that-only-shows-up-because-of-ex\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/delegates-events/the-loop-capture-trap-a-bug-that-only-shows-up-because-of-ex\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The loop-capture trap: a bug that only shows up because of exactly this mechanism</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> actions = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Action</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; i &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>; i++)\n    actions.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Write</span>(i));\nactions.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ForEach</span>(a =&gt; a());     <span class=\"tk-com\">// prints 333: all lambdas share ONE 'i'</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> n <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>[] { <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span> })\n    actions.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Write</span>(n));   <span class=\"tk-com\">// prints 012: since C# 5 each foreach iteration gets a fresh variable</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The <code>for</code> loop prints <code>333</code>, not <code>012</code>, because closures share the <em>variable</em>, not a snapshot of its value. There&#39;s exactly <strong>one</strong> <code>i</code> for the whole loop, declared once in the <code>for</code> header, and all three lambdas capture a reference to that same field. By the time any of them actually runs, the loop has finished and <code>i</code> is already <code>3</code> — so all three print <code>3</code>.</p>\n<p>The <code>foreach</code> version avoids this because C# 5 changed the language to give each iteration its own fresh variable. Each lambda there captures a genuinely distinct <code>n</code>. If you&#39;re stuck with <code>for</code> and need <code>foreach</code>-style behavior, copy the loop variable into a fresh local <em>inside</em> the loop body (<code>var copy = i;</code>) before the lambda captures it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"capturing-something-that-gets-disposed-out-from-under-you\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/delegates-events/capturing-something-that-gets-disposed-out-from-under-you\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Capturing something that gets disposed out from under you</h3>\n<p>This causes a real production bug: capturing a scoped object (a <code>DbContext</code>, an <code>HttpContext</code>) inside a lambda that outlives the request it came from — a background task, a cached delegate, a static event handler — means the lambda holds onto an object that gets <strong>disposed</strong> when the request ends, whether the lambda is done with it or not. The next time it runs, it touches a disposed object (<code>ObjectDisposedException</code>), or if nothing calls it again but nothing releases it either, you&#39;ve built a memory leak.</p>\n<h2 id=\"multicast-delegates-one-delegate-several-methods\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/delegates-events/multicast-delegates-one-delegate-several-methods\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Multicast delegates: one delegate, several methods <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Delegates aren&#39;t limited to pointing at exactly one method — they can hold an entire <strong>list</strong> of methods, all with the same signature:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"QWN0aW9uIG5vdGlmeSA9ICgpID0+IENvbnNvbGUuV3JpdGVMaW5lKCJlbWFpbCIpOwpub3RpZnkgKz0gKCkgPT4gQ29uc29sZS5Xcml0ZUxpbmUoInNtcyIpOwpub3RpZnkoKTsgICAgICAvLyBlbWFpbCwgc21z\"><code><span class=\"tk-ty\">Action</span> notify = () =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;email&quot;</span>);\nnotify += () =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;sms&quot;</span>);\nnotify();      <span class=\"tk-com\">// email, sms</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><code>+=</code> appends a method to the delegate&#39;s internal list; <code>-=</code> removes one; invoking the delegate calls every method in that list, in order. Two sharp edges: for delegates with a return value, invoking a multicast delegate only gives you the <strong>last</strong> method&#39;s result — the earlier ones ran, but their return values are discarded. And if one handler throws partway through, the rest of the list <strong>never runs</strong> — the exception propagates immediately.</p>\n<p><img src=\"visuals/multicast-invocation-list.svg\" alt=\"A multicast delegate&#39;s invocation list running in add order; only the last subscriber&#39;s return value survives\"></p>\n<p>One more useful fact: delegates are <strong>immutable</strong>. <code>notify += ...</code> doesn&#39;t modify the existing delegate — it creates a new delegate instance (old list plus the new method) and reassigns <code>notify</code> to it. That&#39;s what makes <code>?.Invoke(...)</code> safe to call from multiple threads: another thread reassigning the variable to a new delegate can never corrupt an instance that&#39;s currently being invoked elsewhere, because that instance never changes once created.</p>\n<h2 id=\"events-a-delegate-with-the-dangerous-parts-removed\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/delegates-events/events-a-delegate-with-the-dangerous-parts-removed\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Events: a delegate with the dangerous parts removed <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>An <strong>event</strong> is, underneath, just a delegate field — with deliberately restricted access bolted on. Outside code is only ever allowed to <strong>subscribe</strong> (<code>+=</code>) or <strong>unsubscribe</strong> (<code>-=</code>); only the class that declared the event can actually <strong>raise</strong> it, or replace the whole subscriber list with <code>=</code>. This restricted shape is the entire Observer pattern, built directly into the language rather than something you assemble by hand.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlacedEventArgs</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> orderId, <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> total) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">EventArgs</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } = orderId;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } = total;\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Checkout</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">event</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">EventHandler</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlacedEventArgs</span>&gt;? <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span>;   <span class=\"tk-com\">// the event</span>\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Complete</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> o)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-com\">// ...save...</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">OnOrderPlaced</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlacedEventArgs</span>(o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>));\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">virtual</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OnOrderPlaced</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlacedEventArgs</span> e) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span>?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Invoke</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">this</span>, e);  <span class=\"tk-com\">// raise</span>\n\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> checkout = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Checkout</span>();\ncheckout.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span> += (sender, e) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;Order {e.OrderId} for {e.Total}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// checkout.OrderPlaced = null;   // ✘ compile error outside the class: that's the point of 'event'</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// checkout.OrderPlaced(...);     // ✘ only the owner can raise it</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The two commented-out lines are the whole point of <code>event</code>: a plain delegate <em>field</em> would let any outside code overwrite the whole subscriber list with <code>=</code> (wiping out every other subscriber), or invoke it directly, pretending to be the publisher. <code>event</code> closes both doors while leaving <code>+=</code>/<code>-=</code> open.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Delegate field</th>\n<th>Event</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Outsiders can <code>+=</code> / <code>-=</code></td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Outsiders can <code>=</code> (replace all)</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td><strong>No</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Outsiders can invoke</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td><strong>No</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Event subscriptions are a classic, silent memory leak</div><p>The publisher&#39;s event field holds a reference to every subscriber through the delegate — that&#39;s just how <code>+=</code> works (same reachability rule as <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/memory-gc\">Memory &amp; GC</a>). If a long-lived publisher (a singleton, a static event) accumulates subscribers from short-lived objects that never unsubscribe, every one of them stays reachable forever through the publisher&#39;s field, so the GC can never collect them. Always <code>-=</code> in <code>Dispose</code>, or use a weak-event pattern when the subscriber&#39;s lifetime can&#39;t be tied to a clean unsubscribe point.</p>\n</aside>\n<h3 id=\"what-events-usually-mean-once-you-leave-this-one-language-fe\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/delegates-events/what-events-usually-mean-once-you-leave-this-one-language-fe\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What &quot;events&quot; usually mean once you leave this one language feature</h3>\n<p>C# <code>event</code>s are in-process and synchronous — the publisher calls subscribers directly, on its own thread, and waits for them to finish. That&#39;s a narrow fit for most backend work, so you&#39;ll rarely use the <code>event</code> keyword itself in API code. &quot;Event&quot; as a <em>word</em>, though, shows up constantly elsewhere in this book, meaning something related but mechanically different each time:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th>Mechanism</th>\n<th>Chapter</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>C# <code>event</code></td>\n<td>Delegate in the same process</td>\n<td>here</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Domain events</td>\n<td>Objects raised by aggregates, dispatched in-process after save</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ddd\">DDD</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Integration events</td>\n<td>Messages published to a broker for other services</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events\">Messaging</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Host lifetime events</td>\n<td><code>IHostApplicationLifetime.ApplicationStarted/Stopping</code></td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting\">Hosting</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Options change</td>\n<td><code>IOptionsMonitor&lt;T&gt;.OnChange</code> callback</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/configuration-options\">Configuration</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Keep this table in mind so &quot;domain event&quot; and &quot;C# <code>event</code>&quot; don&#39;t blur together later — they share a name and a general spirit (something happened, tell interested parties) but almost nothing about their actual mechanics.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-payoff-delegates-are-how-asp-net-core-is-actually-built\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/delegates-events/the-payoff-delegates-are-how-asp-net-core-is-actually-built\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The payoff: delegates are how ASP.NET Core is actually built <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Look at a typical <code>Program.cs</code> again, this time noticing every lambda:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddDbContext</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>&gt;(options =&gt; options.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseSqlServer</span>(cs));   <span class=\"tk-com\">// Action&lt;DbContextOptionsBuilder&gt;</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configure</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">JwtOptions</span>&gt;(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Issuer</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;shop&quot;</span>);                <span class=\"tk-com\">// Action&lt;JwtOptions&gt;</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IClock</span>&gt;(sp =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SystemClock</span>());                   <span class=\"tk-com\">// Func&lt;IServiceProvider, IClock&gt;: factory</span>\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Use</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (context, next) =&gt; { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* ... */</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> next(context); });         <span class=\"tk-com\">// middleware: Func&lt;HttpContext, RequestDelegate, Task&gt;</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/orders/{id}&quot;</span>, (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderService</span> s) =&gt; s.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(id));       <span class=\"tk-com\">// endpoint handler: any delegate</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Every one of those lambdas is exactly the mechanism this chapter opened with: a method, held as a value, handed to someone else to call later. <code>AddDbContext</code>&#39;s lambda is an <code>Action&lt;DbContextOptionsBuilder&gt;</code> — &quot;here&#39;s how to configure options; I don&#39;t care when you run this.&quot; <code>AddScoped</code>&#39;s lambda is a <code>Func&lt;IServiceProvider, IClock&gt;</code> — a factory the container calls whenever it needs a new <code>IClock</code>. It&#39;s the same <code>Func</code>/<code>Action</code> vocabulary from the start of this chapter, not special configuration syntax.</p>\n<p>The core type the whole pipeline is built from makes this explicit:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"cHVibGljIGRlbGVnYXRlIFRhc2sgUmVxdWVzdERlbGVnYXRlKEh0dHBDb250ZXh0IGNvbnRleHQpOw==\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">delegate</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">RequestDelegate</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpContext</span> context);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>A <strong>middleware</strong> (full coverage in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/middleware\">Middleware</a>) is a function that takes the <em>next</em> <code>RequestDelegate</code> in the pipeline and returns a <em>new</em> <code>RequestDelegate</code> wrapping it — a <code>Func&lt;RequestDelegate, RequestDelegate&gt;</code>. The whole pipeline is built by composing a list of these, each wrapping the next. Once delegates click as &quot;methods held as values,&quot; <code>app.Use(...)</code> stops looking like framework magic.</p>\n<h2 id=\"expression-trees-vs-delegates-the-same-lambda-compiled-two-d\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/delegates-events/expression-trees-vs-delegates-the-same-lambda-compiled-two-d\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Expression trees vs delegates: the same lambda, compiled two different ways <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>One loose thread from the &quot;Lambdas&quot; section: a lambda doesn&#39;t <em>always</em> compile into a delegate. Which one it becomes depends on what type you assign it to:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"RnVuYzxPcmRlciwgYm9vbD4gYXNEZWxlZ2F0ZSA9IG8gPT4gby5Ub3RhbCA+IDEwMDsgICAgICAgICAgICAgICAgIC8vIGNvbXBpbGVkIElMOiBjYW4gYmUgZXhlY3V0ZWQKRXhwcmVzc2lvbjxGdW5jPE9yZGVyLCBib29sPj4gYXNUcmVlID0gbyA9PiBvLlRvdGFsID4gMTAwOyAgICAgICAgIC8vIGEgZGF0YSBzdHJ1Y3R1cmUgZGVzY3JpYmluZyB0aGUgY29kZQ==\"><code><span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span>&gt; asDelegate = o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">100</span>;                 <span class=\"tk-com\">// compiled IL: can be executed</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Expression</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span>&gt;&gt; asTree = o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">100</span>;         <span class=\"tk-com\">// a data structure describing the code</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><code>asDelegate</code> is ordinary, runnable IL — the delegate mechanism this chapter has covered. <code>asTree</code>, despite looking identical on the right, is categorically different: an <strong>object graph</strong> — made of types like <code>BinaryExpression</code>, <code>MemberExpression</code>, <code>ConstantExpression</code> — that <em>describes</em> the lambda&#39;s logic as data, instead of compiling it into code you can run directly.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"Q29uc29sZS5Xcml0ZUxpbmUoYXNUcmVlLkJvZHkpOyAgIC8vIChvLlRvdGFsID4gMTAwKQ==\"><code><span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(asTree.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Body</span>);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// (o.Total &gt; 100)</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>That&#39;s <code>ToString()</code> on a real object graph, printing back roughly the code it represents, not a hand-written string. This is what makes EF Core possible: <code>IQueryable.Where</code> takes an <code>Expression&lt;Func&lt;T,bool&gt;&gt;</code>, not a <code>Func&lt;T,bool&gt;</code>, so EF Core can <strong>inspect and translate</strong> that tree into SQL instead of running it as C#. <code>IEnumerable.Where</code> takes a plain <code>Func&lt;T,bool&gt;</code>, because LINQ to Objects just runs your predicate in-process — nothing to translate. <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/linq/expression-trees-what-ef-core-is-actually-reading\">LINQ</a> walks through that translation, node by node.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is a delegate?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A type-safe reference to a method (or a list of methods) with a specific signature. It lets you store behaviour in variables and pass it as arguments: callbacks, strategies, LINQ predicates, event handlers, middleware.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Func vs Action vs Predicate?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Func&lt;…, TResult&gt; returns a value (the last type argument is the return type). Action&lt;…&gt; returns void. Predicate&lt;T&gt; is a T → bool function, equivalent to Func&lt;T, bool&gt;, used by older APIs like List.FindAll.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is a closure and what does it cost?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A lambda that captures variables from its enclosing scope. The compiler hoists captured variables into a heap-allocated class, so they outlive the method and are shared by every lambda that captures them. Each capturing lambda costs allocations (closure plus delegate), which matters in hot paths; static lambdas prevent accidental capture.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why does a for-loop capturing the loop variable print the same number?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>All lambdas capture the same single variable <code>i</code>, which has its final value by the time they run. foreach creates a new variable per iteration (since C# 5) so it&#39;s safe; in a for-loop, copy to a local inside the body.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the difference between a delegate and an event?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>An event wraps a delegate so external code can only subscribe and unsubscribe. It can&#39;t invoke the event or overwrite the subscriber list with <code>=</code>. Only the declaring class can raise it.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How can events cause memory leaks?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The publisher&#39;s delegate holds strong references to subscribers. A long-lived publisher keeps short-lived subscribers alive if they never unsubscribe. Unsubscribe in Dispose, or use weak references.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How does ASP.NET Core use delegates?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The pipeline&#39;s core type is <code>RequestDelegate</code> (HttpContext → Task). Middleware are functions that wrap the next RequestDelegate; minimal API endpoints are arbitrary delegates whose parameters are bound from the request; configuration APIs take Action&lt;TOptions&gt; lambdas; DI supports Func&lt;IServiceProvider, T&gt; factories.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the difference between Func&lt;T,bool&gt; and Expression&lt;Func&lt;T,bool&gt;&gt;?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A Func is compiled code that can be invoked. An Expression is a data structure describing the code, which providers like EF Core can analyse and translate to another language such as SQL. You can compile an expression into a delegate with <code>.Compile()</code>, but not the other way round.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/delegates-events/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/delegates-overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Delegates and events</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/language-reference/operators/lambda-expressions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Lambda expressions</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/advanced-topics/expression-trees/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Expression trees</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.delegate\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Delegate class — invocation order and return-value behavior</a>: multicast delegates invoke their list in the order each was added, and a non-void delegate&#39;s invocation returns only the last subscriber&#39;s result.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/delegates-events/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Save as <code>Program.cs</code> in a new console project (<code>dotnet new console</code>) and run with <code>dotnet run</code>. It checks delegate reassignment, method-group conversion, closures (including the for-loop vs foreach capture trap), multicast delegate behavior, delegate immutability, event subscribe/unsubscribe, and expression trees vs delegates.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Linq</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Expressions</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> failures = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> claim, <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> ok)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (ok) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: {claim}&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">else</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: {claim}&quot;</span>); failures++; }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 1. Delegate can be reassigned to swap behavior at runtime</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">PriceRule</span> rule = <span class=\"tk-ty\">BlackFriday</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> r1 = rule(<span class=\"tk-num\">100m</span>);\nrule = <span class=\"tk-ty\">NoDiscount</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> r2 = rule(<span class=\"tk-num\">100m</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;reassigning a delegate variable swaps behavior at runtime&quot;</span>, r1 == <span class=\"tk-num\">70m</span> &amp;&amp; r2 == <span class=\"tk-num\">100m</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 2. Method group conversion</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; parse = <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parse</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a method group converts to a matching delegate type&quot;</span>, parse(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;42&quot;</span>) == <span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 3. Closure: a lambda keeps using a local after the method returns</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; counter = <span class=\"tk-ty\">MakeCounter</span>();\ncounter(); counter();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a closure keeps a captured local alive after its method returns&quot;</span>, counter() == <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 4. Captured variables are shared, not copied: the classic for-loop trap</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> trapActions = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Action</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> trapResults = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; i &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>; i++)\n    trapActions.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(() =&gt; trapResults.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(i));\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> a <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> trapActions) a();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a for-loop capturing its loop variable shares ONE variable (all lambdas see the final value)&quot;</span>,\n    trapResults.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span> &amp;&amp; trapResults[<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>] == <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span> &amp;&amp; trapResults[<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>] == <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span> &amp;&amp; trapResults[<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>] == <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> foreachResults = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> foreachActions = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Action</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> n <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>[] { <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span> })\n    foreachActions.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(() =&gt; foreachResults.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(n));\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> a <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> foreachActions) a();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a foreach loop gives each iteration its own variable (lambdas see distinct values)&quot;</span>,\n    foreachResults.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SequenceEqual</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>[] { <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span> }));\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 5. Multicast delegate: invoking calls every subscriber, in order; return value is the LAST one</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> order = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; multi = () =&gt; { order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;first&quot;</span>); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>; };\nmulti += () =&gt; { order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;second&quot;</span>); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>; };\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> multiResult = multi();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a multicast delegate invokes every subscriber in order&quot;</span>, order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SequenceEqual</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>[] { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;first&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;second&quot;</span> }));\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;invoking a multicast delegate returns only the LAST subscriber's result&quot;</span>, multiResult == <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 6. Delegate immutability: += creates a new delegate instance, doesn't mutate the old one</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Action</span> original = () =&gt; { };\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Action</span> afterAdd = original;\nafterAdd += () =&gt; { };\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;delegates are immutable: += produces a new instance, the original is untouched&quot;</span>,\n    !<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReferenceEquals</span>(original, afterAdd));\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 7. Event: outside code can subscribe/unsubscribe but not invoke or overwrite with '='</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> checkout = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Checkout</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> notified = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">EventHandler</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; handler = (s, total) =&gt; notified += total;\ncheckout.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span> += handler;\ncheckout.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Complete</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">50</span>);\ncheckout.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span> -= handler;\ncheckout.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Complete</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">50</span>); <span class=\"tk-com\">// handler removed, shouldn't fire again</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;event subscription (+=) and unsubscription (-=) work from outside the class&quot;</span>, notified == <span class=\"tk-num\">50</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 8. Expression&lt;Func&lt;T,bool&gt;&gt; is data describing the lambda, not compiled code</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Expression</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span>&gt;&gt; asTree = x =&gt; x &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">100</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span>&gt; asDelegate = asTree.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Compile</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;an Expression&lt;Func&lt;T,bool&gt;&gt; can be inspected as a BinaryExpression body&quot;</span>, asTree.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Body</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">BinaryExpression</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;an Expression can be compiled into a runnable delegate&quot;</span>, asDelegate(<span class=\"tk-num\">150</span>) == <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span> &amp;&amp; asDelegate(<span class=\"tk-num\">50</span>) == <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(failures == <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span> ? <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;\\nAll checks passed.&quot;</span> : <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;\\n{failures} check(s) failed.&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (failures &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exit</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">BlackFriday</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> p) =&gt; p * <span class=\"tk-num\">0.7m</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">NoDiscount</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> p) =&gt; p;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">MakeCounter</span>()\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> count = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> () =&gt; ++count;\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">delegate</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PriceRule</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> basePrice);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Checkout</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">event</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">EventHandler</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;? <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Complete</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> total) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span>?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Invoke</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">this</span>, total);\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Not runtime-verifiable here: that <code>event</code> actually produces a <em>compile error</em> for <code>checkout.OrderPlaced(...)</code> or <code>checkout.OrderPlaced = null</code> from outside the class — that&#39;s a compile-time restriction, not something a running program can assert. Try uncommenting those lines yourself to see the compiler reject them.</p>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"treating-a-method-like-a-value-you-can-pass-around","text":"Treating a method like a value you can pass around","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"func-action-and-predicate-delegates-you-never-have-to-declar","text":"Func, Action and Predicate: delegates you never have to declare yourself","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"lambdas-writing-the-delegate-inline","text":"Lambdas: writing the delegate inline","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"closures-when-a-lambda-outlives-the-method-it-was-written-in","text":"Closures: when a lambda outlives the method it was written in","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"the-loop-capture-trap-a-bug-that-only-shows-up-because-of-ex","text":"The loop-capture trap: a bug that only shows up because of exactly this mechanism","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"capturing-something-that-gets-disposed-out-from-under-you","text":"Capturing something that gets disposed out from under you","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"multicast-delegates-one-delegate-several-methods","text":"Multicast delegates: one delegate, several methods","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"events-a-delegate-with-the-dangerous-parts-removed","text":"Events: a delegate with the dangerous parts removed","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"what-events-usually-mean-once-you-leave-this-one-language-fe","text":"What \"events\" usually mean once you leave this one language feature","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-payoff-delegates-are-how-asp-net-core-is-actually-built","text":"The payoff: delegates are how ASP.NET Core is actually built","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"expression-trees-vs-delegates-the-same-lambda-compiled-two-d","text":"Expression trees vs delegates: the same lambda, compiled two different ways","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":4615,"minutes":21,"num":"2.4","part":"p2"},"collections":{"id":"collections","title":"Collections and Data Structures","summary":"Choosing the right container. Arrays, List, Dictionary, HashSet, queues, stacks, sorted and linked collections, their Big-O costs and internals, the interface hierarchy, plus concurrent, immutable and frozen collections.","layer":"language","where":"C# language › Base Class Library › Collections","fits":"<p>These types live in the BCL (layer 2) but you use them as part of the language every day. They are the data structures underneath LINQ (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/linq\">LINQ</a>), caches (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors\">Caching</a>) and almost every algorithm question in interviews.</p>\n","prereqs":["generics"],"next":["linq","threading-concurrency"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/collections/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Each collection type trades away some capability for speed at one specific job — know what you need before picking one.</li>\n<li><code>List&lt;T&gt;</code> = growable array, O(1) add on average but O(n) occasionally when it resizes (&quot;amortised O(1)&quot;). <code>Dictionary&lt;K,V&gt;</code>/<code>HashSet&lt;T&gt;</code> = O(1) average lookup via hashing, as long as <code>GetHashCode</code> spreads values out well.</li>\n<li>The biggest easy win: replace <code>list.Contains(x)</code> inside a loop with a <code>HashSet&lt;T&gt;</code> lookup — turns an O(n·m) scan into O(n+m).</li>\n<li><code>IReadOnlyList&lt;T&gt;</code> only stops <em>you</em> from mutating through that reference; <code>ImmutableList&lt;T&gt;</code>/<code>FrozenDictionary&lt;K,V&gt;</code> mean nobody, ever, can change it.</li>\n<li>Plain collections aren&#39;t thread-safe for writes — use <code>ConcurrentDictionary&lt;K,V&gt;</code>, <code>Channel&lt;T&gt;</code>, etc. across threads, and prefer their atomic methods (<code>GetOrAdd</code>) over check-then-act.</li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout c-analogy\"><div class=\"callout-title\">The warehouse</div><p>Think of this chapter as stocking a warehouse. An array is a wall of fixed numbered shelves — fast to reach shelf #12, impossible to add a shelf #13 without rebuilding the wall. A dictionary is a wall of lockers with a combination (the key) instead of a number — you don&#39;t walk the aisle, you go straight to the locker. A queue is the loading dock: first crate in, first crate out. A stack is the single bin by the door you keep dropping returns onto, and always grab from the top. Keep that picture in mind — every type below is really just a different shape of storage room, built for one specific job.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"why-there-isnt-just-one-list-type\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/collections/why-there-isnt-just-one-list-type\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Why there isn&#39;t just one &quot;list&quot; type <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>C# gives you a genuinely large number of collection types, and the reason isn&#39;t accidental sprawl — each one trades away some capability in exchange for being dramatically faster at one specific job. This chapter&#39;s real goal is to let you look at a problem (&quot;I need to check membership a million times a second&quot;, &quot;I need to always grab the smallest item&quot;) and immediately know which structure was built for exactly that.</p>\n<p>Before the specific types, though, there&#39;s a layer of interfaces worth understanding, because it quietly shapes how you should design your own method signatures too.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-interface-hierarchy-each-rung-promises-one-more-capabili\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/collections/the-interface-hierarchy-each-rung-promises-one-more-capabili\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The interface hierarchy: each rung promises one more capability <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;            foreach only (GetEnumerator)\n ├─ IReadOnlyCollection&lt;T&gt;   + Count\n │   ├─ IReadOnlyList&lt;T&gt;        + this[int] (read)\n │   ├─ IReadOnlySet&lt;T&gt;         + Contains, set comparisons\n │   └─ IReadOnlyDictionary&lt;K,V&gt;+ TryGetValue, Keys, Values\n └─ ICollection&lt;T&gt;           + Count, Add, Remove, Clear, Contains\n     ├─ IList&lt;T&gt;                + this[int] get/set, Insert, RemoveAt, IndexOf\n     ├─ ISet&lt;T&gt;                 + UnionWith, IntersectWith…\n     └─ IDictionary&lt;K,V&gt;        + this[key], Add(key, value), TryGetValue</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Read this ladder as a series of promises, each one stacking on the last: <code>IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</code> promises nothing but &quot;you can walk through me once, in order.&quot; <code>IReadOnlyCollection&lt;T&gt;</code> adds &quot;and I know how many items I have.&quot; <code>IReadOnlyList&lt;T&gt;</code> adds &quot;and you can jump straight to any index.&quot; Every rung up the ladder is a <em>stronger</em> promise — which means every rung up is also a <em>narrower</em> set of types willing to make that promise.</p>\n<p>That asymmetry is exactly where the design rule comes from: <strong>accept the least capable interface you actually need; return the most useful honest type you actually have.</strong></p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IEnumerable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderLine</span>&gt; lines) =&gt; lines.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sum</span>(l =&gt; l.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> * l.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Qty</span>);  <span class=\"tk-com\">// only iterates</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IReadOnlyList</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRecent</span>() =&gt; _orders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Take</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToList</span>();                    <span class=\"tk-com\">// caller can index and count but not mutate</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><code>Total</code> only ever walks <code>lines</code> once, so it asks for nothing more than <code>IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</code> — which means it can accept a <code>List&lt;OrderLine&gt;</code>, an array, a LINQ query, <em>anything</em> enumerable, without forcing the caller to materialise something more specific first. <code>GetRecent</code>, on the return side, genuinely does have a concrete, indexable, countable list sitting behind it — so returning <code>IReadOnlyList&lt;Order&gt;</code> honestly advertises that capability to the caller, while still refusing to promise mutation the caller has no business doing.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">An IEnumerable<T> parameter can quietly run twice</div><p>If a method takes <code>IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</code> and loops over it twice — or calls <code>Count()</code> and <em>then</em> <code>foreach</code>s it — remember from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/linq/deferred-vs-immediate-execution-the-one-idea-that-explains-m\">LINQ</a> that a lazy source doesn&#39;t hold a snapshot of results; it re-executes from scratch every single time it&#39;s enumerated. If that source is a database query, &quot;loop over it twice&quot; silently means &quot;hit the database twice.&quot; Either materialise once (<code>var list = items.ToList();</code>) before touching it more than once, or accept <code>IReadOnlyCollection&lt;T&gt;</code> in your signature specifically when you need the count — which forces the caller to have already materialised it.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"the-core-collections-and-exactly-what-each-one-costs\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/collections/the-core-collections-and-exactly-what-each-one-costs\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The core collections, and exactly what each one costs <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Structure</th>\n<th>Index</th>\n<th>Search</th>\n<th>Add</th>\n<th>Insert/Remove middle</th>\n<th>Ordered?</th>\n<th>Use for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>T[]</code></td>\n<td>Contiguous block</td>\n<td>O(1)</td>\n<td>O(n)</td>\n<td>— (fixed size)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Insertion</td>\n<td>Fixed-size data, max performance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>List&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Growable array</td>\n<td>O(1)</td>\n<td>O(n)</td>\n<td><strong>O(1) amortised</strong></td>\n<td>O(n)</td>\n<td>Insertion</td>\n<td>Default list</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Dictionary&lt;K,V&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Hash table</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td><strong>O(1)</strong> by key</td>\n<td>O(1)</td>\n<td>O(1) remove</td>\n<td><strong>Not guaranteed</strong></td>\n<td>Lookup by key</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>HashSet&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Hash table (keys only)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td><strong>O(1)</strong> contains</td>\n<td>O(1)</td>\n<td>O(1) remove</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Uniqueness, membership</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>SortedDictionary&lt;K,V&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Red-black tree</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>O(log n)</td>\n<td>O(log n)</td>\n<td>O(log n)</td>\n<td>By key</td>\n<td>Sorted iteration with frequent inserts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>SortedList&lt;K,V&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Sorted arrays</td>\n<td>O(1) by index</td>\n<td>O(log n)</td>\n<td>O(n)</td>\n<td>O(n)</td>\n<td>By key</td>\n<td>Sorted, mostly read, low memory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>SortedSet&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Red-black tree</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>O(log n)</td>\n<td>O(log n)</td>\n<td>O(log n)</td>\n<td>Sorted</td>\n<td>Sorted unique values, ranges (<code>GetViewBetween</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Queue&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Circular array</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>O(1) enqueue/dequeue</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>FIFO</td>\n<td>Work queues, BFS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Stack&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Array</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>O(1) push/pop</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>LIFO</td>\n<td>Undo, DFS, parsing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>PriorityQueue&lt;T,P&gt;</code> (.NET 6)</td>\n<td>Binary heap</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>O(log n)</td>\n<td>O(log n) dequeue</td>\n<td>By priority</td>\n<td>Scheduling, Dijkstra, top-k</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>LinkedList&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Doubly linked</td>\n<td>O(n)</td>\n<td>O(n)</td>\n<td>O(1) at ends</td>\n<td><strong>O(1) given a node</strong></td>\n<td>Insertion</td>\n<td>Rare; LRU caches with a dictionary</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-tip\"><div class=\"callout-title\">The single highest-leverage fix in this whole table</div><p><code>list.Contains(x)</code> inside a loop over another list is <strong>O(n·m)</strong> — for every item in the outer list, you scan the <em>entire</em> inner list looking for a match. Converting just the lookup list to a <code>HashSet&lt;T&gt;</code> turns that <code>Contains</code> call from a full scan into an O(1) average-case lookup, dropping the whole thing to <strong>O(n+m)</strong>. On a list of any real size, that&#39;s the difference between milliseconds and minutes. Interviewers ask about this specifically because it&#39;s such a common, easy-to-miss win in real code:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"dmFyIGJsb2NrZWQgPSBuZXcgSGFzaFNldDxzdHJpbmc+KGJsb2NrZWRFbWFpbHMsIFN0cmluZ0NvbXBhcmVyLk9yZGluYWxJZ25vcmVDYXNlKTsKdmFyIGFsbG93ZWQgPSB1c2Vycy5XaGVyZSh1ID0+ICFibG9ja2VkLkNvbnRhaW5zKHUuRW1haWwpKS5Ub0xpc3QoKTs=\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> blocked = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HashSet</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;(blockedEmails, <span class=\"tk-ty\">StringComparer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrdinalIgnoreCase</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> allowed = users.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(u =&gt; !blocked.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contains</span>(u.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Email</span>)).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToList</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"list-internals-what-amortised-o-1-actually-means\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/collections/list-internals-what-amortised-o-1-actually-means\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>List<T> internals: what &quot;amortised O(1)&quot; actually means <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>The table above calls <code>List&lt;T&gt;.Add</code> &quot;O(1) amortised&quot; rather than plain O(1), and that qualifier is worth unpacking rather than skipping past, because it explains a real, controllable cost.</p>\n<p>Underneath, a <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code> is just an array (<code>_items</code>) plus a count (<code>_size</code>), with the array deliberately allocated a bit bigger than it currently needs to be. Most calls to <code>Add</code> are genuinely O(1): there&#39;s spare room in the array, so the new item just goes in the next free slot. But once that spare room runs out, <code>Add</code> has to <strong>allocate a brand-new array at double the previous capacity, and copy every single existing element across</strong> before it can add the new one — an O(n) operation, just for that one call.</p>\n<p>&quot;Amortised O(1)&quot; is the honest way to describe what happens if you average that occasional expensive copy across <em>all</em> the calls leading up to it: because the array doubles each time, the expensive copies become exponentially rarer as the list grows, and the total cost of all the copying, spread evenly across every <code>Add</code> call ever made, works out to a constant amount per call. It&#39;s not that every call is cheap — it&#39;s that the expensive ones are rare enough, and the doubling strategy specifically guarantees rare enough, that the <em>average</em> stays constant.</p>\n<p><img src=\"visuals/list-resize.svg\" alt=\"A full List&lt;T&gt; allocates a new array at double the capacity, copies every element across, and only then adds the new one\"></p>\n<p>Watch the four stages in that picture in order, because each one is a real, separate step the runtime actually performs, not just a conceptual simplification: the array starts <strong>full</strong> (step 1); a brand-new, bigger array gets <strong>allocated</strong>, completely empty, alongside the old one (step 2); every existing element gets <strong>copied</strong> across, one at a time (step 3); and only once that copying has finished does the <strong>new item</strong> actually get added (step 4). The old array is then simply abandoned for the garbage collector. <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.collections.generic.list-1.add\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Microsoft&#39;s own docs for <code>List&lt;T&gt;.Add</code></a> state this plainly: &quot;If Count already equals Capacity... the capacity... is increased by automatically reallocating the internal array, and the existing elements are copied to the new array before the new element is added... this method becomes an O(n) operation.&quot;</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"dmFyIGxpc3QgPSBuZXcgTGlzdDxPcmRlcj4oY2FwYWNpdHk6IDEwXzAwMCk7ICAgLy8gcHJlLXNpemUgd2hlbiB5b3Uga25vdyB0aGUgY291bnQ6IGF2b2lkcyB+MTQgcmVzaXplLWNvcGllcw==\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> list = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt;(capacity: <span class=\"tk-num\">10_000</span>);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// pre-size when you know the count: avoids ~14 resize-copies</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>If you already know roughly how many items you&#39;re about to add, pre-sizing the list avoids paying for those resize-copies at all — each doubling from a default starting capacity up to 10,000 would otherwise cost you roughly 14 separate full-array copies along the way.</p>\n<p>A couple of related facts worth having at hand: <code>Capacity</code> is always <code>≥ Count</code> (the array is at least as big as what&#39;s actually stored in it), and <code>TrimExcess()</code> shrinks the backing array back down if you&#39;ve over-allocated. <code>Insert(0, x)</code> and <code>RemoveAt(0)</code> are each O(n), for a reason worth seeing clearly: inserting or removing at the front means every single element after that position has to physically shift over by one slot — there&#39;s no way around it for an array-backed structure. If you genuinely need fast operations at the front, that&#39;s precisely the signal to reach for <code>Queue&lt;T&gt;</code> instead. And <code>CollectionsMarshal.AsSpan(list)</code> gives you a zero-copy <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> view directly over the internal array, for hot loops — just don&#39;t add or remove from the list while you&#39;re holding that span, since a resize would invalidate it out from under you.</p>\n<h2 id=\"dictionaryk-v-whats-actually-happening-inside-a-hash-lookup\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/collections/dictionaryk-v-whats-actually-happening-inside-a-hash-lookup\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Dictionary&lt;K,V&gt;: what&#39;s actually happening inside a hash lookup <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><code>Dictionary&lt;K,V&gt;</code> promises O(1) lookups — but <em>why</em> does hashing make that possible, when a list-based search is fundamentally O(n)? Walk through exactly what happens when you look up a key, step by step:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Compute <code>key.GetHashCode()</code> — a number, derived from the key&#39;s contents.</li>\n<li>Map that hash to a <strong>bucket</strong> index: roughly <code>hash % buckets.Length</code>.</li>\n<li>Walk that one bucket&#39;s short chain of entries, comparing each against your key with <code>Equals</code>, until you find a match (or run out).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The entire speed advantage over a list lives in step 2: instead of checking every single entry in the whole dictionary, you jump straight to one small bucket and only ever compare against the handful of entries that happen to share that bucket. <strong>Good hash distribution</strong> — hash codes spread evenly across the available buckets — keeps each bucket&#39;s chain short, which is what keeps step 3 fast. A genuinely terrible <code>GetHashCode</code> implementation (one that, say, always returns <code>1</code>) would funnel every single entry into the <em>same</em> bucket, and step 3 would degrade into exactly the O(n) linear scan you were trying to avoid in the first place — hashing only helps if the hash function actually spreads things out.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> stock = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">StringComparer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrdinalIgnoreCase</span>)\n{\n    [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;KB-01&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-num\">12</span>,\n    [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;MS-02&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>\n};\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (stock.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGetValue</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;kb-01&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> qty)) { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* one lookup instead of ContainsKey + indexer */</span> }\nstock[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;NEW&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>;                         <span class=\"tk-com\">// add or overwrite</span>\nstock.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;KB-01&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);                    <span class=\"tk-com\">// throws ArgumentException: duplicate key</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">ref</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> slot = <span class=\"tk-kw\">ref</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CollectionsMarshal</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetValueRefOrAddDefault</span>(stock, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;KB-01&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> _);\nslot++;                                   <span class=\"tk-com\">// update in place with a single hash lookup (perf trick)</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice the comment on <code>TryGetValue</code>: calling <code>ContainsKey</code> followed by the indexer does the <em>entire</em> three-step lookup above <strong>twice</strong> — once to check existence, once to actually fetch the value. <code>TryGetValue</code> does the whole walk exactly once and hands you both pieces of information (found or not, and the value if so) from that single pass.</p>\n<p>Given how central <code>Equals</code>/<code>GetHashCode</code> are to steps 2 and 3, the requirements for a usable key follow directly: <strong><code>Equals</code> and <code>GetHashCode</code> must agree with each other</strong>, and — critically — <strong>must not change</strong> while the object is sitting inside the dictionary, because the bucket it was placed in was chosen based on its hash code <em>at insertion time</em>; if that hash code changes afterward, the dictionary will keep looking for it in the wrong bucket forever. This is exactly the mutable-hash-key trap from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/type-system/equality-three-different-questions-similar-syntax\">Type system</a>, showing up again here with real consequences. Stick to immutable keys — strings, numbers, records, IDs — and the problem can&#39;t arise.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Dictionary iteration order is an implementation detail, not a contract</div><p>A <code>Dictionary</code> usually <em>happens</em> to iterate in insertion order — right up until you remove an item, at which point the freed slot gets reused by a later insertion, and the order you observe can shift in ways that have nothing to do with when anything was actually added. Never write code that depends on this ordering; it isn&#39;t part of the type&#39;s actual contract, only an accident of the current implementation. If you genuinely need order, reach for <code>SortedDictionary</code>, the newer generic <code>OrderedDictionary&lt;K,V&gt;</code> (.NET 9), or sort explicitly at the point you need it.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"choosing-a-collection-a-decision-guide\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/collections/choosing-a-collection-a-decision-guide\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Choosing a collection: a decision guide <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Need lookup by key?                 → Dictionary&lt;K,V&gt;   (sorted keys → SortedDictionary)\nNeed &quot;have I seen this?&quot;            → HashSet&lt;T&gt;\nNeed ordered list, index access?    → List&lt;T&gt;           (fixed size &amp; hot path → T[])\nNeed first-in-first-out?            → Queue&lt;T&gt;          (multi-threaded → Channel&lt;T&gt; / ConcurrentQueue&lt;T&gt;)\nNeed last-in-first-out?             → Stack&lt;T&gt;\nNeed &quot;always take the smallest&quot;?    → PriorityQueue&lt;T,P&gt;\nShared across threads?              → Concurrent* (or lock around a normal one)\nBuilt once, read millions of times? → FrozenDictionary / FrozenSet\nMust never change after creation?   → Immutable* (or IReadOnly* views)\nExposing from a class?              → IReadOnlyList&lt;T&gt; / IReadOnlyDictionary&lt;K,V&gt;</code></pre></figure>\n<h2 id=\"read-only-immutable-and-frozen-three-guarantees-people-confl\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/collections/read-only-immutable-and-frozen-three-guarantees-people-confl\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Read-only, immutable and frozen: three guarantees people conflate <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>These three sound like synonyms and genuinely aren&#39;t — each one makes a different, specific promise, and mixing them up leads to code that&#39;s less safe than it looks:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Can the holder modify it?</th>\n<th>Can <em>anyone</em> modify it?</th>\n<th>Cost</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>IReadOnlyList&lt;T&gt;</code> (interface)</td>\n<td>No (via that reference)</td>\n<td><strong>Yes</strong>: the underlying <code>List</code> can still change</td>\n<td>Free (it&#39;s a view)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ReadOnlyCollection&lt;T&gt;</code> (<code>list.AsReadOnly()</code>)</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Yes, the wrapped list can still change</td>\n<td>A thin wrapper</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ImmutableList&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>ImmutableDictionary&lt;K,V&gt;</code></td>\n<td>No; &quot;mutations&quot; return a new collection, sharing structure</td>\n<td><strong>No</strong>, never</td>\n<td>Tree-based: slower reads, efficient copies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ImmutableArray&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Array-backed: fast reads, full copy on change</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>FrozenDictionary&lt;K,V&gt;</code> / <code>FrozenSet&lt;T&gt;</code> (.NET 8)</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Expensive to create, <strong>fastest possible reads</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The distinction worth really sitting with is between the first two rows and the last three. <code>IReadOnlyList&lt;T&gt;</code> and <code>ReadOnlyCollection&lt;T&gt;</code> only ever restrict what <em>you</em> can do <em>through that particular reference</em> — somebody else, holding the original mutable <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code> the view was built from, can still change it out from under you, and you&#39;d see that change the next time you read through your supposedly &quot;read-only&quot; view. <code>ImmutableList&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>ImmutableArray&lt;T&gt;</code> and <code>FrozenDictionary&lt;K,V&gt;</code> make a fundamentally stronger promise: the data genuinely cannot change, by anyone, ever, after creation — full stop. That&#39;s why <code>FrozenDictionary</code> specifically exists: it accepts an expensive, slower <em>build</em> step in exchange for the fastest possible <em>reads</em> afterward, which is exactly the right trade for something built once at startup and then read on every single request for the rest of the process&#39;s life:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Built once at startup, read on every request</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FrozenDictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">TaxRates</span> =\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>&gt; { [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;DE&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-num\">0.19m</span>, [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FR&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-num\">0.20m</span>, [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;NL&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-num\">0.21m</span> }\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToFrozenDictionary</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">StringComparer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrdinalIgnoreCase</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h2 id=\"concurrent-collections-for-when-more-than-one-thread-touches\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/collections/concurrent-collections-for-when-more-than-one-thread-touches\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Concurrent collections: for when more than one thread touches the same collection <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Here&#39;s a fact worth stating plainly, because it surprises people who assume &quot;it&#39;s a BCL type, it must be safe&quot;: ordinary collections are <strong>not thread-safe for writes</strong>. Two threads calling <code>Add</code> on the same plain <code>Dictionary</code> at the same moment can genuinely corrupt its internal structure — historically, this specific race has even caused infinite loops inside the dictionary&#39;s own bucket-chasing logic. For state shared across threads — singletons, background workers — reach for the collections built specifically to handle this:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>ConcurrentDictionary&lt;K,V&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Thread-safe map; <code>GetOrAdd</code>, <code>AddOrUpdate</code>, <code>TryRemove</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ConcurrentQueue&lt;T&gt;</code> / <code>ConcurrentStack&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Lock-free FIFO/LIFO</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ConcurrentBag&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Unordered; optimised for the same thread adding and taking</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>BlockingCollection&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Bounded producer/consumer with blocking (older; prefer <code>Channel&lt;T&gt;</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Channel&lt;T&gt;</code> (System.Threading.Channels)</td>\n<td><strong>Async</strong> producer/consumer queue; the modern choice</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ConcurrentDictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Lazy</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Config</span>&gt;&gt;&gt; _cache = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>();\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Config</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> tenant) =&gt;\n    _cache.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetOrAdd</span>(tenant, t =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Lazy</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Config</span>&gt;&gt;(() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">LoadConfigAsync</span>(t))).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-com\">// Lazy ensures the loader runs once even if two threads race into GetOrAdd</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Thread-safe methods don&#39;t make compound operations thread-safe</div><p><code>if (!dict.ContainsKey(k)) dict[k] = v;</code> is exactly the check-then-act race condition from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/threading-concurrency/race-conditions-when-it-depends-on-timing-becomes-a-bug\">Threading &amp; concurrency</a> — two separate operations, with a window between them for another thread to slip in, even though <code>ContainsKey</code> and the indexer are each individually safe to call. Use the genuinely atomic combined operations instead — <code>GetOrAdd</code>/<code>TryAdd</code>/<code>AddOrUpdate</code> — which perform the whole check-and-act as one indivisible step. One more sharp edge specific to <code>GetOrAdd</code>: under contention, its value factory <strong>may run more than once</strong>, even though only one of those results ever actually gets stored — which is exactly why the example above wraps the expensive load in <code>Lazy&lt;T&gt;</code> rather than calling <code>LoadConfigAsync</code> directly inside the factory.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"arrays-spans-and-collection-expressions\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/collections/arrays-spans-and-collection-expressions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Arrays, spans and collection expressions <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>[] a = [<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>];                       <span class=\"tk-com\">// C# 12 collection expression</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; names = [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ana&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;bo&quot;</span>];\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>[] combined = [..a, <span class=\"tk-num\">4</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>];              <span class=\"tk-com\">// spread operator</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Span</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; slice = a.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsSpan</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>);          <span class=\"tk-com\">// view of {2, 3}, no copy</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> last = a[^<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>];                          <span class=\"tk-com\">// index from end: 3</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>[] middle = a[<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>..^<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>];                   <span class=\"tk-com\">// range: creates a NEW array {2}</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>[,] grid = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>[<span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>];               <span class=\"tk-com\">// multi-dimensional (rectangular)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>[][] jagged = [[<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>], [<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>]];            <span class=\"tk-com\">// array of arrays</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Two of those lines look similar but behave completely differently, and it&#39;s worth being precise about which: <code>a.AsSpan(1, 2)</code> is a genuine zero-copy <strong>view</strong> into the original array — mutate through the span and you mutate <code>a</code> itself. <code>a[1..^1]</code>, the range syntax, instead <strong>allocates and returns a brand-new array</strong> containing copies of the selected elements — it looks like slicing syntax, but it isn&#39;t a view at all.</p>\n<p>A few more facts worth having on hand: arrays are <strong>reference types</strong> with a genuinely <strong>fixed length</strong> — you can&#39;t resize one, only replace it with a different, differently-sized array. Array covariance (an <code>object[]</code> variable pointing at a real <code>string[]</code>) is permitted but unsafe for exactly the reason covered in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/generics/variance-when-is-listcat-actually-a-listanimal\">Generics</a>: <code>object[] o = new string[1]; o[0] = 1;</code> compiles, but throws <code>ArrayTypeMismatchException</code> at runtime, because arrays trade compile-time safety for a runtime check here. And <code>Array.Empty&lt;T&gt;()</code> (or the <code>[]</code> collection expression) gives you a cached, shared empty array at zero allocation cost — a genuinely good reason to never return <code>null</code> where an empty collection would do: callers can safely <code>foreach</code> over an empty collection with no special-case null check, but a <code>null</code> forces one everywhere it might appear.</p>\n<h2 id=\"equality-comparers-and-custom-ordering\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/collections/equality-comparers-and-custom-ordering\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Equality comparers and custom ordering <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Hash-based and sorted collections both let you plug in your own comparison logic, rather than relying on a type&#39;s default <code>Equals</code>/<code>GetHashCode</code> or <code>CompareTo</code>:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> byEmail = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HashSet</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">EqualityComparer</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>&gt;.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Create</span>(\n    (a, b) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Equals</span>(a?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Email</span>, b?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Email</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">StringComparison</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrdinalIgnoreCase</span>),\n    c =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">StringComparer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrdinalIgnoreCase</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetHashCode</span>(c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Email</span>)));       <span class=\"tk-com\">// .NET 8 helper</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> byPriceThenName = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Comparer</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>&gt;.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Create</span>((x, y) =&gt;\n    x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> != y.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> ? x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CompareTo</span>(y.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span>) : <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CompareOrdinal</span>(x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>, y.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> sorted = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SortedSet</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>&gt;(products, byPriceThenName);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>This matters specifically when a type&#39;s <em>default</em> notion of equality or ordering doesn&#39;t match what a particular collection actually needs — <code>Customer</code> might not override <code>Equals</code> at all, but you can still build a <code>HashSet&lt;Customer&gt;</code> that treats two customers as the same whenever their emails match, without touching the <code>Customer</code> class itself.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the time complexity of List.Add, Dictionary lookup and HashSet.Contains?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>List.Add is O(1) amortised: occasionally O(n) when the backing array doubles and is copied. Dictionary lookup and HashSet.Contains are O(1) on average, assuming a good hash function; worst case O(n) when many keys collide.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How does a Dictionary work internally?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>It hashes the key with GetHashCode, maps the hash to a bucket index, and walks that bucket&#39;s chain of entries, comparing with Equals. It resizes (to a larger prime-sized capacity) as it grows. That&#39;s why keys need consistent Equals/GetHashCode and must not mutate.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>IEnumerable vs ICollection vs IList?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>IEnumerable only supports iteration. ICollection adds Count, Add, Remove, Contains. IList adds index-based access, Insert and RemoveAt. Accept the least capable interface a method needs.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What&#39;s the difference between IReadOnlyList, ReadOnlyCollection and ImmutableList?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>IReadOnlyList is an interface: a read-only view, but the underlying list may still change. ReadOnlyCollection is a wrapper that prevents changes through it, while the wrapped list can still change. ImmutableList can never change; modifications return a new list that shares structure with the old one.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>When would you use FrozenDictionary?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>For lookup tables built once (often at startup) and read very frequently, such as config maps, routing tables or tax rates. Creation is slower because it analyses the keys to pick the optimal layout, but reads are faster than Dictionary.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you make a Dictionary thread-safe?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Use ConcurrentDictionary with its atomic methods (GetOrAdd, AddOrUpdate, TryRemove), or protect a normal Dictionary with a lock. For read-mostly data, swap an immutable/frozen dictionary reference atomically.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Is ConcurrentDictionary.GetOrAdd&#39;s factory guaranteed to run once?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>No. Under contention it can run more than once (only one result is stored). Wrap the value in Lazy&lt;T&gt; if the factory is expensive or has side effects.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How would you remove duplicates from a list efficiently?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p><code>list.Distinct()</code> or <code>new HashSet&lt;T&gt;(list)</code> (O(n)), with a custom IEqualityComparer if needed, or <code>DistinctBy(x =&gt; x.Key)</code> (.NET 6) to dedupe by a property.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/collections/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/collections/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Collections and data structures</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/collections/thread-safe/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Thread-safe collections</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.collections.frozen\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">System.Collections.Frozen</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.collections.generic.list-1.add\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">List&lt;T&gt;.Add(T) Method</a> — the O(1)-unless-resizing remark quoted above</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://source.dot.net/System.Private.CoreLib/src/libraries/System.Private.CoreLib/src/System/Collections/HashHelpers.cs.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><code>HashHelpers.cs</code></a> (source.dot.net) — the actual prime-sized bucket table <code>Dictionary</code>/<code>HashSet</code> resize into</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/collections/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (plain console app, .NET 8/9, no packages). It checks the concrete, checkable claims: <code>Dictionary</code>/<code>HashSet</code> are O(1)-average vs. list <code>Contains</code> being O(n), <code>TryGetValue</code> finding what <code>ContainsKey</code> found, <code>ConcurrentDictionary.GetOrAdd</code>&#39;s factory possibly running more than once under contention, and <code>AsSpan</code> being a view while range slicing <code>[..]</code> copies.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Diagnostics</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Collections</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Concurrent</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- HashSet/Dictionary lookup is dramatically faster than List.Contains at scale ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> n = <span class=\"tk-num\">200_000</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> list = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Enumerable</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Range</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, n).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToList</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HashSet</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;(list);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> target = n - <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>; <span class=\"tk-com\">// worst case for a linear scan: the last element</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> swList = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Stopwatch</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartNew</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; i &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">50</span>; i++) list.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contains</span>(target);\nswList.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Stop</span>();\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> swSet = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Stopwatch</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartNew</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; i &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">50</span>; i++) <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contains</span>(target);\nswSet.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Stop</span>();\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (swSet.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Elapsed</span> &gt;= swList.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Elapsed</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: HashSet.Contains should be far faster than List.Contains here&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: HashSet.Contains ({swSet.ElapsedMilliseconds} ms) was faster than List.Contains ({swList.ElapsedMilliseconds} ms) for {50} lookups over {n} items&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- TryGetValue finds exactly what ContainsKey says is there, in one pass ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> stock = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; { [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;KB-01&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-num\">12</span> };\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!stock.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ContainsKey</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;KB-01&quot;</span>) || !stock.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGetValue</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;KB-01&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> qty) || qty != <span class=\"tk-num\">12</span>)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: TryGetValue should agree with ContainsKey&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: TryGetValue retrieves the same value ContainsKey confirms exists, in a single lookup&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Dictionary.Add throws on a duplicate key; the indexer overwrites instead ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { stock.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;KB-01&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>); <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: Add should throw on a duplicate key&quot;</span>); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">ArgumentException</span>) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: Dictionary.Add throws ArgumentException on a duplicate key&quot;</span>); }\nstock[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;KB-01&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>; <span class=\"tk-com\">// the indexer is fine with overwriting</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (stock[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;KB-01&quot;</span>] != <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: indexer assignment should overwrite&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: the indexer overwrites an existing key instead of throwing&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- ConcurrentDictionary.GetOrAdd's factory can run more than once under contention ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> factoryCalls = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> cd = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ConcurrentDictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parallel</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">For</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">50</span>, _ =&gt; cd.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetOrAdd</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, _ =&gt; { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Interlocked</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Increment</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">ref</span> factoryCalls); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>; }));\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(factoryCalls &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>\n    ? <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: GetOrAdd's factory ran {factoryCalls} times under contention, as the chapter warns (only one result is stored: {cd[1]})&quot;</span>\n    : <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: GetOrAdd's factory ran once this time (the race is possible, not guaranteed, on every run)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- AsSpan is a view (mutating it mutates the array); range slicing [..] copies ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>[] arr = [<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">4</span>];\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> span = arr.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsSpan</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>);\nspan[<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>] = <span class=\"tk-num\">99</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (arr[<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>] != <span class=\"tk-num\">99</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: AsSpan should be a view over the original array&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: Span&lt;T&gt; from AsSpan is a zero-copy view — writing through it changed the original array&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>[] middle = arr[<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>..^<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>];\nmiddle[<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>] = -<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (arr[<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>] == -<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: range slicing should allocate a new array, not share storage&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: range slicing (arr[1..^1]) copies into a new array — changing it left the original untouched&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here — see Further reading: Microsoft &quot;Collections and data structures&quot; docs</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// (Big-O costs for SortedDictionary/PriorityQueue/LinkedList etc. are documented guarantees, not something</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// worth timing in a short-running demo, since constant factors and JIT warm-up dominate at small n)</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"why-there-isnt-just-one-list-type","text":"Why there isn't just one \"list\" type","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"the-interface-hierarchy-each-rung-promises-one-more-capabili","text":"The interface hierarchy: each rung promises one more capability","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"the-core-collections-and-exactly-what-each-one-costs","text":"The core collections, and exactly what each one costs","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"list-internals-what-amortised-o-1-actually-means","text":"List internals: what \"amortised O(1)\" actually means","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"dictionaryk-v-whats-actually-happening-inside-a-hash-lookup","text":"Dictionary<K,V>: what's actually happening inside a hash lookup","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"choosing-a-collection-a-decision-guide","text":"Choosing a collection: a decision guide","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"read-only-immutable-and-frozen-three-guarantees-people-confl","text":"Read-only, immutable and frozen: three guarantees people conflate","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"concurrent-collections-for-when-more-than-one-thread-touches","text":"Concurrent collections: for when more than one thread touches the same collection","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"arrays-spans-and-collection-expressions","text":"Arrays, spans and collection expressions","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"equality-comparers-and-custom-ordering","text":"Equality comparers and custom ordering","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":4495,"minutes":20,"num":"2.5","part":"p2"},"linq":{"id":"linq","title":"LINQ in Depth","summary":"Language Integrated Query: the operators by category, query vs method syntax, deferred vs immediate execution, how iterators work, IEnumerable vs IQueryable, expression trees, and the performance traps.","layer":"language","where":"C# language › Querying data (LINQ)","fits":"<p>LINQ is defined in layer 1/2 (<code>System.Linq</code> in the BCL), but it shows up most in layer 5. EF Core (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-querying\">EF Core querying</a>) is a LINQ <em>provider</em> that translates your queries into SQL. Most EF Core performance bugs are really LINQ misunderstandings.</p>\n","prereqs":["delegates-events","collections"],"next":["efcore-querying","async-await"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/linq/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>LINQ is just extension methods (<code>Where</code>, <code>Select</code>, ...) that work on anything implementing <code>IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</code> or <code>IQueryable&lt;T&gt;</code> — no compiler magic.</li>\n<li>Most operators are <strong>deferred</strong>: writing a query runs nothing; it only executes when something enumerates it (<code>foreach</code>, <code>ToList()</code>, <code>Count()</code>). Enumerate twice and it runs twice, against whatever the source looks like <em>now</em>.</li>\n<li><code>yield return</code> is the mechanism that makes deferred execution possible: it turns a method into a pausable/resumable state machine.</li>\n<li><code>IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</code> runs in your process with compiled delegates; <code>IQueryable&lt;T&gt;</code> (EF Core, etc.) captures the query as an <strong>expression tree</strong> and translates it to SQL — switching to <code>IEnumerable</code> too early silently pulls the whole table into memory first.</li>\n<li>Prefer <code>Any()</code> over <code>Count() &gt; 0</code>, and <code>MinBy</code>/<code>MaxBy</code> over <code>OrderBy(...).First()</code> — both avoid unnecessary full scans/sorts.</li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout c-analogy\"><div class=\"callout-title\">The restaurant order ticket</div><p>A LINQ query is a ticket, not a meal. Writing <code>orders.Where(...).Select(...)</code> is like a waiter writing &quot;table 4: no onions, extra sauce&quot; on a slip of paper — nothing gets cooked yet. The kitchen only fires the ticket when someone actually calls for it (<code>foreach</code>, <code>ToList()</code>, <code>Count()</code>). And here&#39;s the part that trips people up: if the waiter writes a <em>second</em> ticket calling for the same order, the kitchen cooks it all over again from raw ingredients — it doesn&#39;t remember the first plate. Keep that picture handy for the &quot;deferred execution&quot; section below; it&#39;s the same idea with real consequences.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"what-linq-actually-is-underneath-the-syntax\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/linq/what-linq-actually-is-underneath-the-syntax\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What LINQ actually is, underneath the syntax <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><strong>LINQ</strong> is the set of query operators you already use constantly — <code>Where</code>, <code>Select</code>, <code>OrderBy</code>, <code>GroupBy</code>, <code>Join</code>, <code>Sum</code>, and dozens more — and the thing that makes them special is that they work on <strong>any sequence</strong>: an in-memory list, a database table, XML, JSON, anything that implements <code>IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</code> or <code>IQueryable&lt;T&gt;</code>. Same vocabulary, wildly different data sources.</p>\n<p>Here&#39;s the part worth knowing before anything else: LINQ isn&#39;t a special language feature baked into the compiler the way <code>if</code> or <code>for</code> are. It&#39;s just <strong>extension methods</strong> — <code>Where</code> lives in <code>System.Linq.Enumerable</code>, nothing more exotic than that — plus some optional query syntax the language layers on top for readability. Once you know that, a sentence like &quot;LINQ operators chain together&quot; stops being magic and starts being exactly what it looks like: one method call returning something you call the next method on.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> bigSpenders = customers\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(c =&gt; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Country</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;DE&quot;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(c =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> = c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sum</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>) })\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(x =&gt; x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">1_000</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderByDescending</span>(x =&gt; x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Take</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToList</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout c-why\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Why LINQ exists</div><p>Before LINQ, filtering, projecting and grouping meant hand-written <code>foreach</code> loops building up temporary lists and dictionaries by hand — and database queries were raw SQL strings the compiler had no way to check. LINQ replaces both of those with <strong>one declarative vocabulary</strong> that works the same way regardless of the data source, checked by the compiler at compile time, and — this is the part that becomes a whole section later in this chapter — lets providers like EF Core translate that <em>same</em> query into SQL instead of running it in memory.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"two-ways-to-write-the-same-query\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/linq/two-ways-to-write-the-same-query\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Two ways to write the same query <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Query syntax: compiles to exactly the method calls below</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> q1 = from o <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> orders\n         <span class=\"tk-kw\">where</span> o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> == <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Paid</span>\n         orderby o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreatedAt</span> descending\n         select <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> };\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Method syntax</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> q2 = orders\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> == <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Paid</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderByDescending</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreatedAt</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(o =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> });</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>These two produce <strong>identical</strong> compiled code — <code>q1</code> and <code>q2</code> aren&#39;t two different features, just two different spellings. Method syntax is far more common in code you&#39;ll actually see day to day. Query syntax earns its keep specifically for <strong>joins</strong> and <strong><code>let</code></strong> (naming an intermediate value partway through a query), where it reads noticeably cleaner than the method-chain equivalent. Worth knowing too: plenty of operators — <code>Take</code>, <code>Distinct</code>, <code>Any</code>, <code>Sum</code> among them — only exist as methods at all; there&#39;s no query-syntax spelling for them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-operators-organised-by-what-they-do\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/linq/the-operators-organised-by-what-they-do\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The operators, organised by what they do <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Category</th>\n<th>Operators</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Filtering</strong></td>\n<td><code>Where</code>, <code>OfType&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Projection</strong></td>\n<td><code>Select</code>, <code>SelectMany</code>, <code>Select((x, i) =&gt; …)</code> with index</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sorting</strong></td>\n<td><code>OrderBy</code>, <code>OrderByDescending</code>, <code>ThenBy</code>, <code>ThenByDescending</code>, <code>Order</code> (.NET 7), <code>Reverse</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Partitioning</strong></td>\n<td><code>Take</code>, <code>Skip</code>, <code>TakeWhile</code>, <code>SkipWhile</code>, <code>TakeLast</code>, <code>SkipLast</code>, <code>Chunk</code> (.NET 6)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Set</strong></td>\n<td><code>Distinct</code>, <code>DistinctBy</code>, <code>Union</code>, <code>UnionBy</code>, <code>Intersect</code>, <code>IntersectBy</code>, <code>Except</code>, <code>ExceptBy</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Grouping</strong></td>\n<td><code>GroupBy</code>, <code>ToLookup</code>, <code>CountBy</code>, <code>AggregateBy</code> (.NET 9)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Joining</strong></td>\n<td><code>Join</code> (inner), <code>GroupJoin</code>, <code>LeftJoin</code>/<code>RightJoin</code> (.NET 10), <code>Zip</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Element</strong></td>\n<td><code>First</code>, <code>FirstOrDefault</code>, <code>Single</code>, <code>SingleOrDefault</code>, <code>Last</code>, <code>ElementAt</code>, <code>MinBy</code>, <code>MaxBy</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Quantifiers</strong></td>\n<td><code>Any</code>, <code>All</code>, <code>Contains</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Aggregation</strong></td>\n<td><code>Count</code>, <code>LongCount</code>, <code>Sum</code>, <code>Average</code>, <code>Min</code>, <code>Max</code>, <code>Aggregate</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Generation</strong></td>\n<td><code>Enumerable.Range</code>, <code>Repeat</code>, <code>Empty</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Conversion</strong></td>\n<td><code>ToList</code>, <code>ToArray</code>, <code>ToDictionary</code>, <code>ToHashSet</code>, <code>ToLookup</code>, <code>AsEnumerable</code>, <code>AsQueryable</code>, <code>Cast&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Other</strong></td>\n<td><code>Concat</code>, <code>Append</code>, <code>Prepend</code>, <code>DefaultIfEmpty</code>, <code>SequenceEqual</code>, <code>Index</code> (.NET 9)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"first-vs-firstordefault-vs-single-vs-singleordefault-four-op\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/linq/first-vs-firstordefault-vs-single-vs-singleordefault-four-op\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>First vs FirstOrDefault vs Single vs SingleOrDefault — four operators, one decision</h3>\n<p>These four get mixed up constantly, and the fix is a single table showing exactly where they diverge:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Operator</th>\n<th>0 elements</th>\n<th>1 element</th>\n<th>2+ elements</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>First()</code></td>\n<td>throws</td>\n<td>returns it</td>\n<td>returns first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>FirstOrDefault()</code></td>\n<td><code>default</code></td>\n<td>returns it</td>\n<td>returns first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Single()</code></td>\n<td>throws</td>\n<td>returns it</td>\n<td><strong>throws</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>SingleOrDefault()</code></td>\n<td><code>default</code></td>\n<td>returns it</td>\n<td><strong>throws</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Notice the real distinction isn&#39;t &quot;First vs Single&quot; so much as what each one is <em>asserting</em> about the data. <code>First</code>/<code>FirstOrDefault</code> make no claim about how many elements exist — they just want whichever one comes first, which is exactly right after an <code>OrderBy</code>, or for &quot;give me any match&quot;. <code>Single</code>/<code>SingleOrDefault</code> are stricter: they&#39;re asserting &quot;there should be <strong>exactly one</strong>&quot;, and throw loudly if that assertion turns out to be false — which is exactly the behaviour you want for a lookup by a unique key, where finding two rows means your data has a bug, not that you should silently pick one. <code>FirstOrDefault(predicate, defaultValue)</code> (.NET 6) additionally lets you pick what &quot;not found&quot; actually returns, instead of always falling back to <code>default</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"selectmany-turning-a-sequence-of-sequences-into-one-sequence\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/linq/selectmany-turning-a-sequence-of-sequences-into-one-sequence\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>SelectMany: turning a sequence of sequences into one sequence</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"dmFyIGFsbExpbmVzID0gb3JkZXJzLlNlbGVjdE1hbnkobyA9PiBvLkxpbmVzKTsgICAgICAgICAgICAgICAgICAgICAgLy8gSUVudW1lcmFibGU8T3JkZXJMaW5lPgp2YXIgcGFpcnMgPSBvcmRlcnMuU2VsZWN0TWFueShvID0+IG8uTGluZXMsIChvLCBsKSA9PiBuZXcgeyBvLklkLCBsLlNrdSB9KTsgIC8vIGtlZXAgdGhlIHBhcmVudA==\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> allLines = orders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SelectMany</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span>);                      <span class=\"tk-com\">// IEnumerable&lt;OrderLine&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> pairs = orders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SelectMany</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span>, (o, l) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, l.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span> });  <span class=\"tk-com\">// keep the parent</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><code>Select</code> alone would give you a sequence of orders, each still holding its own list of lines — a sequence <em>of</em> sequences. <code>SelectMany</code> flattens that one level: instead of &quot;ten orders, each with some lines&quot;, you get &quot;every line from every order, as one flat list&quot;. The second overload above is worth remembering specifically because it solves a problem the first one creates: once you&#39;ve flattened down to lines, how do you still know which order each line came from? The two-argument overload keeps the parent (<code>o</code>) available alongside each flattened child (<code>l</code>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"groupby-and-lookups\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/linq/groupby-and-lookups\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>GroupBy and lookups</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> revenueByCountry = orders\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">GroupBy</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Country</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(g =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Country</span> = g.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Key</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span> = g.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span>(), <span class=\"tk-ty\">Revenue</span> = g.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sum</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>) })\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderByDescending</span>(x =&gt; x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Revenue</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> countsByStatus = orders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CountBy</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span>);        <span class=\"tk-com\">// .NET 9: IEnumerable&lt;KeyValuePair&lt;Status,int&gt;&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">ILookup</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt; byCustomer = orders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToLookup</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>);  <span class=\"tk-com\">// immediate, multi-value dictionary</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> forCustomer42 = byCustomer[<span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>];                         <span class=\"tk-com\">// empty sequence if none, never throws</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><code>ToLookup</code> is worth a specific mention because its behaviour quietly saves you a whole class of null-check and <code>ContainsKey</code> code: unlike a <code>Dictionary</code>, indexing an <code>ILookup</code> with a key that doesn&#39;t exist returns an <strong>empty sequence</strong>, not an exception and not <code>null</code>. <code>byCustomer[42]</code> is always safe to <code>foreach</code> over, whether or not customer 42 has any orders.</p>\n<h3 id=\"joins\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/linq/joins\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Joins</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> rows = from o <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> orders\n           join c <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> customers on o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> equals c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>\n           select <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> };\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Left join (classic pattern before .NET 10)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> withOptionalCoupon = from o <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> orders\n                         join cp <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> coupons on o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CouponId</span> equals cp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> into gj\n                         from cp <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> gj.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DefaultIfEmpty</span>()\n                         select <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Coupon</span> = cp?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Code</span> };</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h2 id=\"deferred-vs-immediate-execution-the-one-idea-that-explains-m\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/linq/deferred-vs-immediate-execution-the-one-idea-that-explains-m\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Deferred vs immediate execution: the one idea that explains most LINQ bugs <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Here&#39;s the single most important thing in this entire chapter, and the thing worth slowing all the way down for: <strong>when you write a LINQ query, nothing happens yet.</strong> Not &quot;almost nothing&quot; — literally nothing. Most LINQ operators don&#39;t run your filter, your projection, anything — they just hand back an object that <em>describes</em> what you asked for, a plan, not a result. The actual work only happens later, at the exact moment something <strong>enumerates</strong> that plan — a <code>foreach</code>, a <code>ToList()</code>, anything that actually walks the sequence.</p>\n<p>Operators split cleanly into two camps along this line. <strong>Deferred (lazy)</strong> operators — <code>Where</code>, <code>Select</code>, <code>OrderBy</code>, <code>Take</code>, <code>SelectMany</code>, and most others — build that plan and do nothing else. <strong>Immediate</strong> operators — <code>ToList</code>, <code>ToArray</code>, <code>ToDictionary</code>, <code>Count</code>, <code>Sum</code>, <code>First</code>, <code>Any</code>, <code>Max</code> — are the ones that actually trigger enumeration and hand back a real, concrete result right then.</p>\n<p>Let&#39;s watch this happen, step by step, instead of just stating it:</p>\n<p><img src=\"visuals/linq-deferred-execution.svg\" alt=\"A LINQ query sits inert until something enumerates it, then runs the predicate once per element — and a second enumeration reruns the whole thing from scratch\"></p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> numbers = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; { <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span> };\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> query = numbers.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(n =&gt; { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;checking {n}&quot;</span>); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> n &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>; });\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// nothing printed yet</span>\n\nnumbers.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">4</span>);                    <span class=\"tk-com\">// the query will see this!</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> n <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> query) { }       <span class=\"tk-com\">// NOW prints: checking 1, 2, 3, 4</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> count = query.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span>();         <span class=\"tk-com\">// runs the WHOLE query AGAIN: checking 1, 2, 3, 4</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Walk through exactly what happens at each line, because every line here surprises someone the first time:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code>numbers.Where(...)</code> runs. <strong>Nothing is printed.</strong> The lambda you wrote hasn&#39;t executed even once yet — <code>query</code> is just a description of &quot;filter <code>numbers</code> by this predicate&quot;, sitting there, inert.</li>\n<li><code>numbers.Add(4)</code> — and here&#39;s the part that catches people out — this is <strong>allowed</strong>, and it matters, because <code>query</code> doesn&#39;t hold a snapshot of <code>numbers</code> from when it was declared. It holds a <em>reference</em> to the live list, and will read whatever is in it <em>whenever it&#39;s finally enumerated</em>.</li>\n<li>The <code>foreach</code> is the first thing that actually enumerates <code>query</code>. Only <strong>now</strong> does the lambda run, once per element — on all four numbers, including the <code>4</code> you added after declaring the query.</li>\n<li><code>query.Count()</code> is a <em>second</em>, completely independent enumeration. The query has no memory of having already run once — it reruns the entire filter from scratch, printing &quot;checking 1, 2, 3, 4&quot; all over again.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Four consequences fall directly out of that trace, and each one is a real bug pattern you&#39;ll eventually meet in production:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>A query sees its source as it is when enumerated, not when declared.</strong> If the source changes in between — as <code>numbers</code> did above — the query sees the <em>new</em> state, which is sometimes exactly what you want and sometimes a genuine bug.</li>\n<li><strong>Every enumeration re-runs the whole query, from scratch.</strong> Against a database, that&#39;s another full round trip; against an expensive in-memory <code>Select</code>, that&#39;s repeated work for no reason. The fix is simple once you know to look for it: call <code>ToList()</code> once, and reuse the resulting list, the moment you know you&#39;ll need the results more than once.</li>\n<li><strong>Exceptions surface late</strong> — at enumeration time, which can be far away, in a completely different method, from the line that actually built the faulty query.</li>\n<li><strong>A disposed source breaks a query that hasn&#39;t run yet.</strong> Return a lazy query built over a <code>DbContext</code>, let that <code>DbContext</code> get disposed, and <em>then</em> have the caller enumerate it — you&#39;ll get an <code>ObjectDisposedException</code> at the enumeration site, nowhere near where the real mistake was made.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"not-every-deferred-operator-is-lazy-in-the-same-way\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/linq/not-every-deferred-operator-is-lazy-in-the-same-way\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Not every deferred operator is lazy in the same way</h3>\n<p>One more distinction worth having, because it explains a specific performance trap: among the deferred operators, some <strong>stream</strong> — they can hand you the very first result item without first reading the whole source (<code>Where</code>, <code>Select</code>, <code>Take</code> all do this). Others must <strong>buffer</strong> the entire source before they can yield even their first output item, because they fundamentally can&#39;t know the answer without seeing everything (<code>OrderBy</code>, <code>GroupBy</code>, <code>Reverse</code>, and <code>Distinct</code> to a degree all fall into this camp — you can&#39;t know what the smallest element is, for a sort, until you&#39;ve looked at all of them). That&#39;s exactly why <code>orders.OrderBy(...).First()</code> is wasteful: it sorts the <em>entire</em> collection just to throw away everything except the very first element. <code>MinBy</code>/<code>MaxBy</code> solve the same problem in a single streaming pass, with no sort and no buffering at all.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-this-laziness-is-actually-implemented-yield-return\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/linq/how-this-laziness-is-actually-implemented-yield-return\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>How this laziness is actually implemented: yield return <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>You now know LINQ operators are lazy. Here&#39;s the mechanism that makes that possible — and it&#39;s one you can use yourself, not just something happening behind the scenes:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IEnumerable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">MyWhere</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-kw\">this</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IEnumerable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; source, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span>&gt; predicate)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> item <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> source)\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (predicate(item))\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">yield</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> item;     <span class=\"tk-com\">// pause here, hand the item to the caller, resume on next MoveNext()</span>\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IEnumerable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Fibonacci</span>()          <span class=\"tk-com\">// infinite sequences are fine, because it's lazy</span>\n{\n    (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> a, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> b) = (<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">while</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>) { <span class=\"tk-kw\">yield</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> a; (a, b) = (b, a + b); }\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> first10 = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Fibonacci</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Take</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToList</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><code>yield return</code> is doing something genuinely clever under the hood: the compiler rewrites a method that uses it into a small object implementing <code>IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</code>/<code>IEnumerator&lt;T&gt;</code>, whose code can <strong>pause mid-execution</strong>, hand one value back to whoever&#39;s asking, and later <strong>resume exactly where it left off</strong> — locals, loop position, everything intact — the next time it&#39;s asked for another value. That&#39;s precisely what lets <code>Fibonacci()</code> above return an <em>infinite</em> sequence without ever hanging: nothing computes the next Fibonacci number until something explicitly asks for it, and <code>Take(10)</code> only ever asks ten times.</p>\n<p><code>foreach</code> is what drives this machinery: it calls <code>GetEnumerator()</code> once, then repeatedly calls <code>MoveNext()</code> and reads <code>Current</code>, until <code>MoveNext()</code> says there&#39;s nothing left, and finally calls <code>Dispose()</code>. Chain several LINQ operators together — <code>Where().Select().Take()</code> — and you&#39;ve actually built a <strong>pipeline of enumerators</strong>, each one pulling exactly one item at a time from the one before it, only when asked. Nothing is computed ahead of its actual use.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ienumerable-vs-iqueryable-the-difference-that-decides-where-\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/linq/ienumerable-vs-iqueryable-the-difference-that-decides-where-\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>IEnumerable<T> vs IQueryable<T>: the difference that decides where your query actually runs <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Everything so far has been &quot;LINQ to Objects&quot; — queries running entirely in your process, over data already sitting in memory. But the exact same <code>Where</code>, <code>Select</code>, <code>OrderBy</code> syntax also works against <code>db.Orders</code> in EF Core, and <em>that</em> code doesn&#39;t run in your process at all — it becomes SQL. How can identical-looking syntax do two completely different things?</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th><code>IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</code> (LINQ to Objects)</th>\n<th><code>IQueryable&lt;T&gt;</code> (LINQ providers: EF Core…)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Operators take</td>\n<td><code>Func&lt;T, bool&gt;</code>: compiled delegates</td>\n<td><code>Expression&lt;Func&lt;T, bool&gt;&gt;</code>: expression trees</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Executes</td>\n<td>In your process, in C#</td>\n<td>Translated by a provider (to SQL, etc.) and run at the source</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Defined in</td>\n<td><code>System.Linq.Enumerable</code></td>\n<td><code>System.Linq.Queryable</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Can call any C# method</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Only what the provider can translate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The whole answer is in that first row, and it&#39;s genuinely subtle: <code>IQueryable&lt;T&gt;</code>&#39;s version of <code>Where</code> doesn&#39;t take a compiled <code>Func</code> at all — it takes an <code>Expression&lt;Func&lt;T, bool&gt;&gt;</code>, which, as the next section shows in detail, means the compiler hands the provider a <em>description</em> of your lambda instead of runnable code. EF Core reads that description and writes its own SQL from it. <code>IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</code>&#39;s <code>Where</code> takes an ordinary compiled delegate, which just runs, in C#, right there in your process. Same method name, same-looking lambda — two structurally different things happening underneath.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-ty\">IQueryable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt; q = db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>;                         <span class=\"tk-com\">// nothing executed</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> paid = q.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> == <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Paid</span>)   <span class=\"tk-com\">// still IQueryable: becomes WHERE</span>\n            .<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderBy</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreatedAt</span>)                   <span class=\"tk-com\">// ORDER BY</span>\n            .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Take</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>);                                   <span class=\"tk-com\">// TOP(20) / LIMIT 20</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> list = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> paid.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToListAsync</span>();                    <span class=\"tk-com\">// ONE SQL query, 20 rows returned</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// The trap: switching to IEnumerable too early</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">IEnumerable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt; all = db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>;                      <span class=\"tk-com\">// static type is IEnumerable</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> bad = all.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> == <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Paid</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Take</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToList</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// SELECT * FROM Orders: the ENTIRE table comes over the network, then it's filtered in memory</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Look closely at the two blocks above — the operators called are <em>identical</em>, <code>Where</code> then <code>Take</code>. The only difference is the <strong>static type of the variable</strong> they&#39;re called on: <code>IQueryable&lt;Order&gt;</code> versus <code>IEnumerable&lt;Order&gt;</code>. That one type annotation decides whether <code>.Where(...)</code> resolves to the <code>IQueryable</code> overload (build an expression tree, let EF Core translate it to <code>WHERE ... TOP(20)</code>) or the <code>IEnumerable</code> overload (compile a delegate, pull every single row across the network first, and only then filter in memory). The SQL difference between those two outcomes, on a million-row table, is enormous — and nothing about the <em>syntax</em> warns you which one you&#39;ve written.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">The silent full-table scan</div><p>Any of these flip a query from SQL to in-memory, usually without a compiler warning in sight: assigning the query to an <code>IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</code> variable, calling <code>.AsEnumerable()</code>, calling <code>.ToList()</code> before you&#39;re done filtering, or a repository method whose return type is declared <code>IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</code> instead of <code>IQueryable&lt;T&gt;</code>. The defence is simple to state: keep a query typed as <code>IQueryable</code> for as long as possible, right up until the one final call that actually materialises it, and <code>Select</code> down to only the columns you actually need before that happens.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"expression-trees-what-ef-core-is-actually-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/linq/expression-trees-what-ef-core-is-actually-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Expression trees: what EF Core is actually reading <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>The previous section promised a closer look at what an <code>Expression&lt;Func&lt;…&gt;&gt;</code> really is, as opposed to a plain delegate. Here it is. When a lambda gets assigned to an <code>Expression&lt;Func&lt;…&gt;&gt;</code> instead of a <code>Func&lt;…&gt;</code>, the compiler does something fundamentally different with it: instead of compiling the lambda into runnable code the way it normally would, it builds an <strong>object graph that describes the code</strong> — a tree of nodes you can walk, inspect, and even generate completely new code from.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"RXhwcmVzc2lvbjxGdW5jPE9yZGVyLCBib29sPj4gZXhwciA9IG8gPT4gby5Ub3RhbCA+IDEwMCAmJiBvLkNvdW50cnkgPT0gIkRFIjsKLy8gQW5kQWxzbygKLy8gICBHcmVhdGVyVGhhbihNZW1iZXIobywgVG90YWwpLCBDb25zdGFudCgxMDApKSwKLy8gICBFcXVhbChNZW1iZXIobywgQ291bnRyeSksIENvbnN0YW50KCJERSIpKSk=\"><code><span class=\"tk-ty\">Expression</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span>&gt;&gt; expr = o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">100</span> &amp;&amp; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Country</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;DE&quot;</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// AndAlso(</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//   GreaterThan(Member(o, Total), Constant(100)),</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//   Equal(Member(o, Country), Constant(&quot;DE&quot;)))</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><img src=\"visuals/expression-tree.svg\" alt=\"An expression tree being built from a lambda, then walked node by node to produce SQL\"></p>\n<p>Walk through what that diagram is showing, because it&#39;s genuinely the whole mechanism in one picture: the lambda <code>o.Total &gt; 100 &amp;&amp; o.Country == &quot;DE&quot;</code> becomes a small tree, with an <code>AndAlso</code> node at the root, two comparison nodes beneath it (<code>GreaterThan</code> and <code>Equal</code>), and each comparison pointing at a <code>Member</code> access and a <code>Constant</code> as its two leaves. That tree is just data — ordinary .NET objects you could inspect with a debugger, same as any other object graph. EF Core&#39;s query pipeline walks it exactly the way you&#39;d walk any tree: visit the root, recurse into each child, and at each node emit the matching piece of SQL — <code>AndAlso</code> becomes <code>AND</code>, <code>GreaterThan</code> becomes <code>&gt;</code>, a <code>Member</code> access becomes a column name, and a <code>Constant</code> becomes... not a literal value baked into the SQL string, but a <strong>parameter</strong>. That detail is exactly why LINQ queries are naturally safe from SQL injection: captured values never get string-concatenated into the query text at all — they travel as proper SQL parameters (<code>@__country_0</code>), the same mechanism you&#39;d reach for by hand with raw ADO.NET.</p>\n<p>Because an <code>IQueryable</code> is just this same kind of tree, waiting to be extended, you can build one up conditionally, piece by piece, and the provider still only ever sees — and only ever executes — one final query:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IQueryable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">ApplyFilters</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IQueryable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>&gt; q, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductFilter</span> f)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (f.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MinPrice</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> { } min) q = q.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> &gt;= min);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// composing IQueryable conditionally</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsNullOrEmpty</span>(f.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Search</span>)) q = q.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contains</span>(f.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Search</span>));\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (f.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CategoryId</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> { } cat) q = q.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CategoryId</span> == cat);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> q;   <span class=\"tk-com\">// still one SQL query, built from only the filters that were supplied</span>\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>There&#39;s a real limit on all of this, and it follows directly from how the translation works: the provider can only translate what it actually understands how to turn into SQL — it cannot run arbitrary C#. <code>q.Where(p =&gt; MyHelper(p))</code> throws &quot;could not be translated&quot; in EF Core 3 and later, precisely because <code>MyHelper</code> is just a <code>Member</code>/method-call node in the tree that EF Core has no SQL equivalent for. (Older EF Core versions used to silently fall back to evaluating the whole query in memory instead of throwing — which sounds more forgiving, but was actually a notorious, hard-to-spot performance trap: a query that looked like it ran as SQL was secretly pulling entire tables across the network instead.)</p>\n<h2 id=\"performance-considerations\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/linq/performance-considerations\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Performance considerations <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>A short, practical list, now that you understand <em>why</em> each of these is true rather than just that it is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>LINQ to Objects allocates <strong>enumerators and delegates</strong>, plus closures if your lambdas capture variables — you saw exactly this mechanism in the <code>yield return</code> section above. For most business code this overhead is genuinely negligible; in true hot paths (per-item processing of millions of items, tight loops inside middleware), a plain <code>for</code>/<code>foreach</code> over arrays or spans can be several times faster with zero allocations.</li>\n<li>.NET 7–10 have vectorised many operators (<code>Sum</code>, <code>Min</code>, <code>Max</code>, <code>Average</code> over arrays/spans now use SIMD; <code>Order</code> and <code>ToArray</code> got faster too) — a good reminder that upgrading .NET versions sometimes buys you LINQ performance for free, no code changes needed.</li>\n<li><strong><code>Any()</code></strong> stops the moment it finds a match. <code>Count() &gt; 0</code> does not — on a lazy sequence it&#39;s a full pass through everything, just to throw the number away and check if it was nonzero (though on an <code>ICollection</code>, <code>Count</code> is a stored property and genuinely O(1)).</li>\n<li><strong><code>Count</code> property vs <code>Count()</code> method:</strong> <code>Count()</code> actually checks whether the source is an <code>ICollection&lt;T&gt;</code> and uses its stored <code>.Count</code> when it can, rather than blindly enumerating — but <code>TryGetNonEnumeratedCount</code> exists specifically to make that intent explicit in your own code, rather than relying on an internal optimisation.</li>\n<li>Avoid <code>OrderBy(...).First()</code> for the reason covered earlier — a full sort just to discard everything but one element; use <code>MinBy</code>/<code>MaxBy</code> instead.</li>\n<li>Avoid repeated <code>.ToList()</code> calls chained through a pipeline; each one allocates a brand-new full copy of whatever&#39;s been produced so far.</li>\n<li><code>Where(...).Count()</code> vs <code>Count(predicate)</code> produce identical results — pick whichever reads more clearly to you.</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is deferred execution in LINQ?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Most operators don&#39;t run when called. They build a query that executes when it is enumerated (foreach, ToList, Count, First…), using the data as it is at that moment, and every enumeration runs it again. Immediate operators like ToList, Count, Sum and First force execution.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>IEnumerable vs IQueryable?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>IEnumerable runs LINQ in memory using compiled delegates. IQueryable captures the query as expression trees that a provider (EF Core) translates and executes at the data source, e.g. as SQL. Switching to IEnumerable too early pulls all rows into memory and filters there.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What&#39;s the difference between First, FirstOrDefault, Single and SingleOrDefault?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>First returns the first element and throws if there are none; FirstOrDefault returns default instead. Single expects exactly one element and throws if there are zero or more than one; SingleOrDefault returns default for zero but still throws for more than one.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Select vs SelectMany?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Select maps each element to exactly one result, so a sequence of lists stays a sequence of lists. SelectMany maps each element to a sequence and flattens them into one sequence (all order lines from all orders).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How does yield return work?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The compiler turns the method into a state machine implementing IEnumerable/IEnumerator. Each MoveNext runs code until the next yield return, hands back that value, and pauses. Execution is lazy and resumes exactly where it left off.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the multiple-enumeration problem?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Enumerating a lazy IEnumerable more than once re-executes the whole pipeline, including database queries or expensive projections, and results may differ between enumerations. Materialise once with ToList/ToArray when you need the results more than once.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How does EF Core translate LINQ to SQL?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>IQueryable operators take Expression&lt;Func&lt;…&gt;&gt; parameters, so the compiler builds expression trees instead of delegates. EF Core&#39;s query pipeline walks the tree, maps members to columns via the model, turns captured variables into SQL parameters, and generates provider-specific SQL. Untranslatable expressions throw.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Any() vs Count() &gt; 0?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Any stops at the first element (or the first match), so it&#39;s O(1) for the common case and maps to EXISTS in SQL. Count enumerates everything on lazy sequences and maps to COUNT(*). Prefer Any for existence checks.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Is LINQ slow?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>LINQ to Objects adds small overheads (enumerator and delegate allocations, interface calls) that are irrelevant for most business code. In hot paths over large data, hand-written loops or Span-based code can be faster. Recent .NET versions vectorised many operators. For databases, the cost is about the SQL generated, not LINQ itself.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/linq/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/linq/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LINQ overview</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/linq/standard-query-operators/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Standard query operators</a></li>\n<li>Jon Skeet&#39;s <em>Edulinq</em> blog series: reimplementing LINQ to Objects, the best way to understand it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/linq/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (plain console app, .NET 8/9, no packages; no database, so the <code>IQueryable</code> section is covered conceptually only — EF Core&#39;s actual SQL translation is verified in the EF Core chapters). It checks deferred vs. immediate execution, <code>yield return</code>&#39;s pause/resume behavior, <code>Any()</code> short-circuiting where <code>Count()</code> doesn&#39;t, and that an <code>Expression&lt;Func&lt;...&gt;&gt;</code> is data you can inspect rather than runnable code.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Deferred execution: the query doesn't run until enumerated, and reruns on every enumeration ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> checks = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> numbers = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; { <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span> };\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> query = numbers.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(n =&gt; { checks++; <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> n &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>; });\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (checks != <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: Where should not run the predicate before enumeration&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: building a LINQ query runs nothing yet (checks == 0)&quot;</span>);\n\nnumbers.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">4</span>); <span class=\"tk-com\">// the query holds a reference to the live list, not a snapshot</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> first = query.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToList</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (checks != <span class=\"tk-num\">4</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected 4 predicate calls (including the late-added 4), got {checks}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: enumerating runs the predicate once per element, seeing the list as it is NOW (4 items)&quot;</span>);\n\nchecks = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> second = query.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (checks != <span class=\"tk-num\">4</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: a second enumeration should re-run the whole query from scratch&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: a second enumeration (Count()) re-ran the predicate from scratch instead of reusing the first result&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Any() short-circuits; Count() walks the whole lazy sequence ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> visited = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">IEnumerable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Source</span>() { <span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> n <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Enumerable</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Range</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1000</span>)) { visited++; <span class=\"tk-kw\">yield</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> n; } }\nvisited = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> hasMatch = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Source</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Any</span>(n =&gt; { <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> n == <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>; }); <span class=\"tk-com\">// matches on the very first element</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (visited != <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: Any should stop at the first match, visited {visited} elements&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: Any() stopped after the first matching element instead of scanning everything&quot;</span>);\n\nvisited = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> total = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Source</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (visited != <span class=\"tk-num\">1000</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: Count() on a lazy sequence should enumerate everything&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: Count() on a lazy IEnumerable&lt;T&gt; walked all 1000 elements, unlike Any()&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- yield return: pause/resume, proven by interleaving with external side effects ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">IEnumerable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Trace</span>()\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;  trace: about to yield 1&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">yield</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;  trace: about to yield 2&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">yield</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>;\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> it = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Trace</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetEnumerator</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;main: before first MoveNext&quot;</span>);\nit.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MoveNext</span>(); <span class=\"tk-com\">// only now does &quot;about to yield 1&quot; print — proves the method body hadn't started yet</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;main: got {it.Current}, now calling MoveNext again&quot;</span>);\nit.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MoveNext</span>(); <span class=\"tk-com\">// resumes exactly after the first yield, not from the top</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (it.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Current</span> != <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: yield return should resume where it left off&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: yield return paused and resumed exactly where it left off (saw 1 then 2, interleaved with main)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- An Expression&lt;Func&lt;...&gt;&gt; is an inspectable object graph, not compiled, runnable code ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Linq</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Expressions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Expression</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span>&gt;&gt; expr = x =&gt; x &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">100</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> body = (<span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Linq</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Expressions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BinaryExpression</span>)expr.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Body</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (body.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NodeType</span> != <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Linq</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Expressions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExpressionType</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GreaterThan</span>)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected the expression tree's root node to be a GreaterThan comparison&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: Expression&lt;Func&lt;int,bool&gt;&gt; is data — its root node is {body.NodeType}, matching 'x &gt; 100', inspectable without running it&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here — see Further reading: LINQ overview, Standard query operators (Microsoft Learn)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// (actual SQL generated by an IQueryable provider like EF Core is covered and verified in the EF Core chapters)</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"what-linq-actually-is-underneath-the-syntax","text":"What LINQ actually is, underneath the syntax","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"two-ways-to-write-the-same-query","text":"Two ways to write the same query","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"the-operators-organised-by-what-they-do","text":"The operators, organised by what they do","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"first-vs-firstordefault-vs-single-vs-singleordefault-four-op","text":"First vs FirstOrDefault vs Single vs SingleOrDefault — four operators, one decision","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"selectmany-turning-a-sequence-of-sequences-into-one-sequence","text":"SelectMany: turning a sequence of sequences into one sequence","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"groupby-and-lookups","text":"GroupBy and lookups","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"joins","text":"Joins","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"deferred-vs-immediate-execution-the-one-idea-that-explains-m","text":"Deferred vs immediate execution: the one idea that explains most LINQ bugs","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"not-every-deferred-operator-is-lazy-in-the-same-way","text":"Not every deferred operator is lazy in the same way","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"how-this-laziness-is-actually-implemented-yield-return","text":"How this laziness is actually implemented: yield return","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"ienumerable-vs-iqueryable-the-difference-that-decides-where-","text":"IEnumerable vs IQueryable : the difference that decides where your query actually runs","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"expression-trees-what-ef-core-is-actually-reading","text":"Expression trees: what EF Core is actually reading","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"performance-considerations","text":"Performance considerations","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":5164,"minutes":23,"num":"2.6","part":"p2"},"exceptions-disposal":{"id":"exceptions-disposal","title":"Exceptions, Error Handling and Resource Disposal","summary":"How exceptions work, the hierarchy, try/catch/finally and filters, rethrowing correctly, custom exceptions, when not to throw, and deterministic cleanup with IDisposable, using, IAsyncDisposable and finalizers.","layer":"language","where":"C# language › Errors & resources","fits":"<p>Layer 1 mechanics that layer 3 builds on. ASP.NET Core turns unhandled exceptions into HTTP responses (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/errors-logging\">Errors &amp; logging</a>), the Result pattern (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cqrs-mediator-result\">CQRS &amp; Result</a>) is the architectural alternative for expected failures, and disposal is how DbContext and HttpClient release connections.</p>\n","prereqs":["oop"],"next":["errors-logging","async-await","cqrs-mediator-result"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/exceptions-disposal/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Throwing an exception stops normal execution and <strong>unwinds the call stack</strong> looking for a matching <code>catch</code>, running every <code>finally</code> along the way.</li>\n<li>Pick the exception type from the standard hierarchy (<code>ArgumentException</code>, <code>InvalidOperationException</code>, ...) so callers can tell failures apart without parsing message strings.</li>\n<li><code>throw;</code> keeps the original stack trace; <code>throw ex;</code> resets it — always prefer <code>throw;</code> or wrap in a new exception.</li>\n<li>Exceptions are for the unexpected. For routine &quot;might not work&quot; outcomes (parsing, lookups, validation), prefer <code>TryParse</code>-style patterns or a <code>Result&lt;T&gt;</code> instead.</li>\n<li><code>IDisposable</code>/<code>using</code> give you <strong>deterministic</strong> cleanup (close this connection <em>now</em>) that the garbage collector, which runs on its own schedule, can&#39;t give you.</li>\n<li>In async code, an exception lives inside the <code>Task</code> until something <code>await</code>s it — if nothing ever does, it&#39;s silently lost.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"what-actually-happens-when-code-throws\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/exceptions-disposal/what-actually-happens-when-code-throws\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What actually happens when code throws <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<aside class=\"callout c-analogy\"><div class=\"callout-title\">The relay race baton</div><p>Picture <code>PlaceOrder</code> calling <code>Validate</code>, which calls <code>ChargeCard</code> — a relay race where each method hands off to the next. When <code>ChargeCard</code> drops the baton (throws), it doesn&#39;t just stop: the runtime hands the problem back up the same chain it came down, one leg at a time, asking each runner in turn &quot;is this your problem to catch?&quot; <code>Validate</code> says no but still has to do its own cleanup on the way out (its <code>finally</code>); <code>PlaceOrder</code> finally says yes and catches it. That handback, leg by leg, is exactly what &quot;unwinding the stack&quot; means.</p>\n</aside>\n<p>An <strong>exception</strong> is an object — always deriving from <code>System.Exception</code> — that represents an error. The moment code <strong>throws</strong> one, normal execution stops dead, right there, and the runtime starts <strong>unwinding the call stack</strong>: walking back up through method after method, looking for a <code>catch</code> block whose type matches the exception, running any <code>finally</code> block it passes along the way regardless of whether it finds a match yet. If it unwinds all the way to the top of the call stack without finding anything, the thread terminates — though in ASP.NET Core specifically, you almost never see that happen, because the framework&#39;s own exception-handling middleware sits near the top of the pipeline and catches it first, turning it into a 500 response instead of a crash.</p>\n<p><img src=\"visuals/stack-unwinding.svg\" alt=\"ChargeCard throws, Validate&#39;s finally still runs even with no matching catch, and PlaceOrder&#39;s catch finally stops the unwind\"></p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> order = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> repo.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(id) ?? <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFoundException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;Order {id}&quot;</span>);\n    order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cancel</span>();                    <span class=\"tk-com\">// may throw InvalidOperationException</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> uow.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>();\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFoundException</span>)              <span class=\"tk-com\">// most specific first</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>();\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">DbUpdateConcurrencyException</span> ex)\n{\n    logger.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogWarning</span>(ex, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Concurrency conflict on order {Id}&quot;</span>, id);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Conflict</span>();\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">finally</span>\n{\n    metrics.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RecordAttempt</span>();           <span class=\"tk-com\">// ALWAYS runs: success, exception, or early return</span>\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>That <code>finally</code> block is worth noting precisely: it runs on <strong>every</strong> path out of the <code>try</code> — a clean success, a caught exception, an uncaught one still unwinding past it, even an early <code>return</code> from inside the <code>try</code>. There&#39;s no way to leave this method, through any route, without <code>metrics.RecordAttempt()</code> running.</p>\n<p>An exception object carries a handful of properties worth knowing by name: <code>Message</code>, <code>StackTrace</code>, <code>InnerException</code> (the underlying cause, when one exception is deliberately wrapped around another), <code>Data</code> (a bag for extra key/value context), <code>HResult</code>, and <code>ToString()</code>, which — unlike <code>Message</code> alone — prints the <em>entire</em> picture, inner exceptions included. That last point matters enough to call out as a rule: always log the <strong>exception object itself</strong>, never just its <code>.Message</code> string — you lose the stack trace, the inner exception chain, and everything else that actually helps you find the bug, for the sake of a shorter log line.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-exception-hierarchy-a-map-for-picking-the-right-one\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/exceptions-disposal/the-exception-hierarchy-a-map-for-picking-the-right-one\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The exception hierarchy: a map for picking the right one <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>System.Exception\n├── SystemException\n│   ├── ArgumentException\n│   │   ├── ArgumentNullException\n│   │   └── ArgumentOutOfRangeException\n│   ├── InvalidOperationException\n│   │   └── ObjectDisposedException\n│   ├── NullReferenceException          ← a bug; never throw or catch it deliberately\n│   ├── IndexOutOfRangeException\n│   ├── InvalidCastException\n│   ├── FormatException\n│   ├── NotSupportedException / NotImplementedException\n│   ├── IOException → FileNotFoundException …\n│   ├── OperationCanceledException → TaskCanceledException\n│   ├── TimeoutException\n│   ├── OutOfMemoryException, StackOverflowException   ← can't be meaningfully handled\n│   └── …\n├── HttpRequestException, JsonException, DbException → SqlException …\n└── AggregateException                  ← wraps several (Task.Wait, Parallel)</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Picking the right exception type to throw isn&#39;t just etiquette — it&#39;s how callers further up the stack can actually distinguish &quot;bad input&quot; from &quot;wrong state&quot; from &quot;not found&quot; without parsing your message string. Microsoft&#39;s own framework design guidelines boil down to a short, memorable table:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Situation</th>\n<th>Throw</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>A bad argument</td>\n<td><code>ArgumentException</code> / <code>ArgumentNullException</code> / <code>ArgumentOutOfRangeException</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Object is in the wrong state for this call</td>\n<td><code>InvalidOperationException</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Called after Dispose</td>\n<td><code>ObjectDisposedException</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operation intentionally unsupported</td>\n<td><code>NotSupportedException</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A domain rule violated</td>\n<td>A custom exception (<code>DomainException</code>), or a Result instead</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Modern .NET ships static guard helpers specifically so you don&#39;t have to hand-write the same <code>if (x is null) throw new ArgumentNullException(...)</code> boilerplate a thousand times: <code>ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(x)</code>, <code>ArgumentException.ThrowIfNullOrWhiteSpace(s)</code>, <code>ArgumentOutOfRangeException.ThrowIfNegative(n)</code>, <code>ObjectDisposedException.ThrowIf(_disposed, this)</code> — each one throws the exact right type from that table, with a correctly populated message, in a single line.</p>\n<h2 id=\"throw-vs-throw-ex-one-character-of-difference-one-real-conse\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/exceptions-disposal/throw-vs-throw-ex-one-character-of-difference-one-real-conse\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>throw; vs throw ex; — one character of difference, one real consequence <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span> ex)\n{\n    logger.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogError</span>(ex, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Payment failed&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span>;                     <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✔ rethrows the SAME exception, stack trace preserved</span>\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span> ex)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> ex;                  <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✘ resets the stack trace to this line: you lose where it really failed</span>\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlException</span> ex)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">RepositoryException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Saving order failed&quot;</span>, ex);  <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✔ wrap with context, keep the cause as InnerException</span>\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>These two lines look almost interchangeable, but the difference has cost real debugging time. A bare <code>throw;</code> rethrows the <strong>exact same exception object</strong> with its stack trace untouched — it still points at the original line where it actually failed. <code>throw ex;</code> throws the same <em>object</em>, but the runtime treats it as a <strong>new</strong> throw happening right here, so the trace gets overwritten to point at this <code>catch</code> block, and the real origin is lost. If a stack trace starts somewhere suspiciously unhelpful, <code>throw ex;</code> upstream is a common culprit.</p>\n<p>The third block shows a deliberate third option: <strong>wrapping</strong>. <code>throw new RepositoryException(&quot;Saving order failed&quot;, ex)</code> throws a brand-new exception with its own fresh stack trace, while keeping the original as <code>InnerException</code>. Do this when the original exception wouldn&#39;t mean much to whoever reads it later — a raw <code>SqlException</code> is less useful three layers up than a <code>RepositoryException</code> that says what you were trying to do, with the <code>SqlException</code> still attached underneath for anyone who needs it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"exception-filters-deciding-whether-to-catch-without-unwindin\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/exceptions-disposal/exception-filters-deciding-whether-to-catch-without-unwindin\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Exception filters: deciding whether to catch, without unwinding first</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpRequestException</span> ex) <span class=\"tk-kw\">when</span> (ex.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpStatusCode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TooManyRequests</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">or</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpStatusCode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceUnavailable</span>)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-com\">// only transient errors handled here; others propagate untouched</span>\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span> ex) <span class=\"tk-kw\">when</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogAndReturnFalse</span>(ex)) { }   <span class=\"tk-com\">// log without catching (filter returns false)</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>A <code>when (condition)</code> clause changes <em>when</em> the stack unwinds. Without a filter, the runtime has to unwind the stack — tearing down frames, running <code>finally</code> blocks — just to get inside the <code>catch</code> body and check whether you actually wanted this exception. With a filter, the condition runs <strong>before</strong> any of that, while the exception is still sitting where it was thrown, full original state intact. That helps diagnostics, but the more useful trick is the second example: a filter that returns <code>false</code> after logging lets you <strong>observe</strong> an exception without catching it — it keeps propagating to whatever would have handled it anyway.</p>\n<p>One narrower tool: <code>ExceptionDispatchInfo.Capture(ex).Throw()</code> rethrows an exception caught somewhere else entirely (another thread, say) while preserving <em>its</em> original stack trace — the same guarantee <code>throw;</code> gives locally. You&#39;ll rarely write this by hand; <code>await</code> already does it for you, as the async section below explains.</p>\n<h2 id=\"writing-your-own-exception-types\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/exceptions-disposal/writing-your-own-exception-types\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Writing your own exception types <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DomainException</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Code</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DomainException</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> code, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> message, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>? inner = <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>) : <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>(message, inner) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Code</span> = code;\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InsufficientStockException</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> sku, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> requested, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> available)\n    : <span class=\"tk-ty\">DomainException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;stock.insufficient&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;Requested {requested} of {sku}, only {available} available&quot;</span>)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } = sku;\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>A short checklist: name it ending in <code>Exception</code>; derive from <code>Exception</code> directly, never the legacy <code>ApplicationException</code>; include the standard constructors so callers can wrap or rethrow it; add real, typed properties for anything a caller might need to act on (<code>Sku</code> above, not buried in the message string); and keep the message genuinely useful to whoever reads the log. Map your custom exceptions to HTTP status codes in exactly <strong>one</strong> central place (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/errors-logging\">Errors &amp; logging</a> shows how), rather than scattering <code>catch</code> blocks that each decide independently.</p>\n<h2 id=\"when-not-to-reach-for-an-exception-at-all\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/exceptions-disposal/when-not-to-reach-for-an-exception-at-all\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>When <em>not</em> to reach for an exception at all <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Exceptions exist for <strong>exceptional</strong> situations — things the caller couldn&#39;t reasonably have prevented, or wouldn&#39;t expect as part of normal flow. They&#39;re also mechanically <strong>expensive</strong>: capturing a full stack trace and unwinding through it costs real time compared to an ordinary return. And they&#39;re <strong>invisible in a method&#39;s signature</strong> — nothing about <code>Task&lt;Order&gt; PlaceAsync(...)</code> tells a caller it might throw five different exception types under entirely normal conditions.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Expected outcome</th>\n<th>Better than throwing</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Parsing user input</td>\n<td><code>int.TryParse</code>, <code>DateTime.TryParseExact</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lookup might miss</td>\n<td><code>TryGetValue</code>, return <code>null</code> / <code>T?</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Validation failure</td>\n<td>Validation result (FluentValidation, <code>ValidationProblem</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Business rule rejection (&quot;card declined&quot;, &quot;out of stock&quot;)</td>\n<td><code>Result&lt;T&gt;</code> / <code>OneOf</code> / discriminated union style return</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"Ly8gVHJ5LXBhdHRlcm46IHRoZSBCQ0wgY29udmVudGlvbiBmb3IgIm1pZ2h0IGZhaWwsIHRoYXQncyBub3JtYWwiCnB1YmxpYyBib29sIFRyeVJlc2VydmUoc3RyaW5nIHNrdSwgaW50IHF0eSwgW05vdE51bGxXaGVuKHRydWUpXSBvdXQgUmVzZXJ2YXRpb24/IHJlc2VydmF0aW9uKSB7IC8qIC4uLiAqLyB9\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Try-pattern: the BCL convention for &quot;might fail, that's normal&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TryReserve</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> sku, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> qty, <span class=\"tk-attr\">[NotNullWhen(true)]</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Reservation</span>? reservation) { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* ... */</span> }</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The <strong>Try-pattern</strong> above is the BCL&#39;s long-standing answer: a <code>bool</code> return that costs nothing when the &quot;failure&quot; is routine, with the actual result handed back through an <code>out</code> parameter only on success. &quot;Exceptions vs the Result pattern&quot; is a genuinely live architectural debate — <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cqrs-mediator-result/the-result-pattern-making-expected-failure-part-of-the-metho\">CQRS, Mediator &amp; Result</a> walks through where each one earns its place.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">An empty catch block is one of the most dangerous things you can write</div><p><code>catch { }</code> or <code>catch (Exception) { }</code> with nothing inside doesn&#39;t make a problem go away — it just makes it <strong>invisible</strong>, while whatever bad state caused the exception quietly persists and keeps causing damage somewhere downstream, with no record anywhere that anything went wrong. If you genuinely, deliberately need to ignore a specific exception, catch the <em>narrowest</em> type that actually applies, leave a comment explaining exactly why ignoring it is correct here, and log it anyway — &quot;ignored, and silent&quot; is almost never the behaviour you actually want, even when &quot;ignored&quot; genuinely is.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"exceptions-in-async-code-behave-differently-from-what-youd-e\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/exceptions-disposal/exceptions-in-async-code-behave-differently-from-what-youd-e\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Exceptions in async code behave differently from what you&#39;d expect <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>A quick preview before <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/async-await\">Async/await</a> covers <code>Task</code> and <code>await</code> properly: a <code>Task</code> is an object representing work that finishes later, and <code>await</code> is how you wait for it without blocking a thread. Even without the full chapter, the exception mechanics below are worth knowing, because they genuinely diverge from synchronous exception handling:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>An exception thrown inside an <code>async Task</code> method isn&#39;t thrown immediately — it&#39;s <strong>captured and stored inside the returned <code>Task</code></strong>. It only surfaces, rethrown with its original stack trace preserved, the moment someone <code>await</code>s that <code>Task</code>. (This is the same mechanism as the <code>ExceptionDispatchInfo</code> trick above: <code>await</code> does that rethrow-with-preserved-trace for you automatically.)</li>\n<li>Follow that through: if nobody ever <code>await</code>s the <code>Task</code>, its exception is simply <strong>never observed</strong>. This is how &quot;fire-and-forget&quot; bugs happen — code that looks like it&#39;s handling errors but is actually losing them quietly.</li>\n<li><code>async void</code> methods break this pattern entirely: there&#39;s no <code>Task</code> to store an exception in. The exception is raised directly on the synchronization context or thread pool, and with nothing there to catch it, it <strong>crashes the entire process</strong>. This is the real reason <code>async void</code> is reserved for event handlers (the one place the signature is forced on you) and avoided everywhere else.</li>\n<li><code>Task.WhenAll</code> over several tasks rethrows only the <strong>first</strong> exception it hits when you <code>await</code> the combined result — but every exception from every failed task is still there, in <code>task.Exception.InnerExceptions</code>, if you need the full picture.</li>\n<li>Cancellation has its own exception type, <code>OperationCanceledException</code> (or its cousin <code>TaskCanceledException</code>), worth treating as a different <em>category</em> from a real error: a client disconnecting mid-request is normal, not a bug, so logging it at <code>Error</code> level every time just buries real errors in noise.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> tasks = ids.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(id =&gt; client.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(id)).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToArray</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WhenAll</span>(tasks); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> all = tasks.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(t =&gt; t.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsFaulted</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">SelectMany</span>(t =&gt; t.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>!.<span class=\"tk-ty\">InnerExceptions</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> e <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> all) logger.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogError</span>(e, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;One call failed&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span>;\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The <code>catch</code> block here isn&#39;t really handling the exception <code>Task.WhenAll</code> rethrew — it&#39;s using that as a signal to dig through <em>every</em> task&#39;s own exception, log each failure individually (since <code>WhenAll</code> alone would only surface the first), then <code>throw;</code> to propagate the original failure once everything is recorded.</p>\n<h2 id=\"resources-and-idisposable-the-problem-the-gc-doesnt-solve\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/exceptions-disposal/resources-and-idisposable-the-problem-the-gc-doesnt-solve\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Resources and IDisposable: the problem the GC doesn&#39;t solve <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>The GC, as <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/memory-gc\">Memory &amp; GC</a> covered in detail, frees <strong>memory</strong> — but it does so whenever it decides to, on its own schedule, which could be anywhere from microseconds to minutes after an object becomes unreachable. Some resources genuinely can&#39;t wait that long and need releasing <strong>now</strong>, deterministically, the moment you&#39;re done with them:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>database connections (which need to return to a pool promptly, or the pool runs dry)</li>\n<li>file handles, sockets, network streams</li>\n<li>locks, semaphores, timers</li>\n<li>native memory and OS handles</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The contract that makes &quot;release this now, deterministically&quot; possible is <strong><code>IDisposable</code></strong>:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"cHVibGljIGludGVyZmFjZSBJRGlzcG9zYWJsZSB7IHZvaWQgRGlzcG9zZSgpOyB9\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IDisposable</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dispose</span>(); }</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>And the <strong><code>using</code></strong> statement is what guarantees <code>Dispose()</code> actually gets called, even when an exception is thrown partway through — because, as the final block below shows, it compiles directly into a <code>try</code>/<code>finally</code>, the exact same construct that guaranteed the <code>finally</code> block ran in the very first example of this chapter:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> conn = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlConnection</span>(cs))      <span class=\"tk-com\">// classic form</span>\n{\n    conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Open</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-com\">// ...</span>\n}                                              <span class=\"tk-com\">// conn.Dispose() here</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">file</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">File</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OpenRead</span>(path);          <span class=\"tk-com\">// C# 8 using declaration: disposed at end of enclosing scope</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> db = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> factory.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateDbContextAsync</span>();   <span class=\"tk-com\">// IAsyncDisposable</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// What the compiler generates for `using var x = ...;`</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> x = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Resource</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* rest of scope */</span> }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">finally</span> { x?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Dispose</span>(); }</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout c-fits\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Who&#39;s actually responsible for disposing what, in ASP.NET Core</div><p>A rule worth having precisely, because getting it wrong in either direction causes real bugs: you usually <strong>don&#39;t</strong> call <code>Dispose</code> on anything the DI container handed you. <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/who-disposes-what\">Dependency injection</a> covers this in full, but the short version is that the container disposes scoped services — <code>DbContext</code> among them — the moment the request&#39;s scope ends, and disposes singletons at app shutdown. You only ever dispose what <strong>you</strong> personally created with <code>new</code> or a factory call — resources the container never knew existed in the first place.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"implementing-idisposable-yourself\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/exceptions-disposal/implementing-idisposable-yourself\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Implementing IDisposable yourself <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>For a class that only wraps <strong>other already-managed disposables</strong> — no raw native handles of its own — this simple version is enough; you don&#39;t need the full ceremony in the next section:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReportExporter</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IDisposable</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FileStream</span> _file;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Timer</span> _timer;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> _disposed;\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReportExporter</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> path) { _file = <span class=\"tk-ty\">File</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Create</span>(path); _timer = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Timer</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Flush</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1000</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1000</span>); }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Write</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> line)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">ObjectDisposedException</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ThrowIf</span>(_disposed, <span class=\"tk-kw\">this</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-com\">// ...</span>\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dispose</span>()\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (_disposed) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span>;      <span class=\"tk-com\">// Dispose must be safe to call multiple times</span>\n        _timer.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Dispose</span>();\n        _file.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Dispose</span>();\n        _disposed = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The <code>if (_disposed) return;</code> guard at the top of <code>Dispose()</code> exists because <code>Dispose</code> must be <strong>safe to call more than once</strong>. Code elsewhere might legitimately call it twice (an explicit call, then a <code>using</code> block&#39;s automatic call on the same object), and without the guard, calling <code>_file.Dispose()</code> a second time could itself throw.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-full-dispose-pattern-with-a-finalizer-only-when-you-genu\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/exceptions-disposal/the-full-dispose-pattern-with-a-finalizer-only-when-you-genu\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The full Dispose pattern, with a finalizer — only when you genuinely need one <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h3>\n<p>You only need this heavier version when your class <strong>directly owns an unmanaged resource</strong> — a raw native handle or <code>IntPtr</code>, not another managed <code>IDisposable</code> wrapping one. Even then, prefer wrapping that handle in a <code>SafeHandle</code>, which already implements the finalizer correctly for you, so you never have to write one by hand:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">NativeBuffer</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IDisposable</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IntPtr</span> _ptr = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Marshal</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AllocHGlobal</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1024</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> _disposed;\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dispose</span>()\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dispose</span>(disposing: <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">GC</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SuppressFinalize</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">this</span>);       <span class=\"tk-com\">// finalizer no longer needed: saves the GC work</span>\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">virtual</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dispose</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> disposing)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (_disposed) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span>;\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (disposing) { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* dispose MANAGED disposables here */</span> }\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Marshal</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FreeHGlobal</span>(_ptr);       <span class=\"tk-com\">// free UNMANAGED resources in both paths</span>\n        _ptr = <span class=\"tk-ty\">IntPtr</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Zero</span>;\n        _disposed = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;\n    }\n\n    ~<span class=\"tk-ty\">NativeBuffer</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dispose</span>(disposing: <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// safety net if someone forgot to Dispose</span>\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The <code>disposing</code> boolean does real work. <code>Dispose(bool)</code> can be called from two different situations: a normal <code>Dispose()</code> call (<code>disposing: true</code>, where it&#39;s safe to also touch other <em>managed</em> objects), or the finalizer running as a last-resort safety net (<code>disposing: false</code>, where other managed objects might already be collected, so only the unmanaged pointer is guaranteed to still be valid). <code>GC.SuppressFinalize(this)</code> matters too: once <code>Dispose()</code> has already cleaned up, the finalizer doesn&#39;t need to run — and skipping it is worth doing, because an object with a <em>pending</em> finalizer gets promoted a generation early and survives an extra GC cycle (see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/memory-gc/finalizers-the-gcs-last-resort-and-why-its-slow\">Memory &amp; GC</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"iasyncdisposable-for-cleanup-that-itself-needs-to-be-awaited\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/exceptions-disposal/iasyncdisposable-for-cleanup-that-itself-needs-to-be-awaited\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>IAsyncDisposable: for cleanup that itself needs to be awaited</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"cHVibGljIHNlYWxlZCBjbGFzcyBCbG9iQmF0Y2hXcml0ZXIgOiBJQXN5bmNEaXNwb3NhYmxlCnsKICAgIHB1YmxpYyBhc3luYyBWYWx1ZVRhc2sgRGlzcG9zZUFzeW5jKCkgeyBhd2FpdCBGbHVzaEFzeW5jKCk7IGF3YWl0IF9zdHJlYW0uRGlzcG9zZUFzeW5jKCk7IH0KfQphd2FpdCB1c2luZyB2YXIgd3JpdGVyID0gbmV3IEJsb2JCYXRjaFdyaXRlciguLi4pOw==\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">BlobBatchWriter</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IAsyncDisposable</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DisposeAsync</span>() { <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FlushAsync</span>(); <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> _stream.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DisposeAsync</span>(); }\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> writer = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">BlobBatchWriter</span>(...);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Some cleanup involves I/O of its own — flushing a stream to the network, closing a database connection gracefully — and a plain synchronous <code>Dispose()</code> has no way to <code>await</code> anything inside it. <code>IAsyncDisposable</code> exists for exactly this, and <code>await using</code> is its counterpart to <code>using</code>: same <code>try</code>/<code>finally</code> shape, but calling <code>DisposeAsync()</code> instead. <code>DbContext</code>, <code>Stream</code>, <code>SqlConnection</code>, DI scopes and <code>Utf8JsonWriter</code> all implement it — prefer <code>await using</code> over plain <code>using</code> wherever a type offers it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"common-disposal-mistakes\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/exceptions-disposal/common-disposal-mistakes\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Common disposal mistakes <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Mistake</th>\n<th>Consequence</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>new HttpClient()</code> per request inside <code>using</code></td>\n<td>Socket exhaustion (sockets in TIME_WAIT)</td>\n<td><code>IHttpClientFactory</code>; see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/http-client\">HttpClient</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Forgetting to dispose a <code>SqlConnection</code> you created</td>\n<td>Connection pool exhaustion: &quot;Timeout expired obtaining a connection&quot;</td>\n<td><code>await using</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Returning a lazy <code>IEnumerable</code> from inside a <code>using</code> block</td>\n<td><code>ObjectDisposedException</code> when the caller enumerates</td>\n<td>Materialise before the scope ends</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Disposing something injected by DI</td>\n<td>Other consumers get a disposed object</td>\n<td>Let the container own it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Storing a scoped <code>IDisposable</code> in a singleton</td>\n<td>Used after the request scope disposed it</td>\n<td><code>IServiceScopeFactory</code>; see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/dependency-injection\">DI</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Not disposing <code>CancellationTokenSource</code>, <code>Timer</code>, <code>SemaphoreSlim</code></td>\n<td>Leaked timers / handles</td>\n<td><code>using</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>&quot;Disposing something injected by DI&quot; and &quot;storing a scoped <code>IDisposable</code> in a singleton&quot; are the same underlying bug in different clothes: both are the <strong>captive dependency</strong> problem from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/the-captive-dependency-the-single-most-common-di-bug\">Dependency injection</a> — an object outliving, or being disposed independently of, the scope meant to own its lifetime.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the difference between throw and throw ex?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p><code>throw;</code> rethrows the current exception and preserves its original stack trace. <code>throw ex;</code> throws the same object but resets the stack trace to the current line, so you lose where the error originated. Wrap with <code>throw new X(&quot;context&quot;, ex)</code> when adding information.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are exception filters and why are they useful?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A <code>when (condition)</code> clause on a catch. The condition is evaluated before the stack unwinds, so if it&#39;s false the exception continues untouched with its full original state (good for diagnostics), and you can catch selectively (only certain status codes, for example) without catch-and-rethrow.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>When should you not use exceptions?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>For expected outcomes: parsing input, lookups that may miss, validation failures and business rule rejections. Use Try-patterns, nullable returns, validation results or a Result type instead. Exceptions are costly and invisible in signatures; keep them for unexpected failures.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What happens to an exception thrown in an async method?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>It&#39;s captured in the returned Task and rethrown when the Task is awaited, with its original stack trace preserved. If the Task is never awaited, the exception goes unobserved. In async void methods, there&#39;s no Task, so the exception crashes the process.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is IDisposable and why does .NET need it if it has a garbage collector?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The GC only manages memory and runs non-deterministically. IDisposable gives deterministic release of other resources (connections, handles, sockets, locks) at a known point, typically via using / await using, which compile to try/finally.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Explain the Dispose pattern. When do you need a finalizer?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Implement Dispose() to release resources, make it idempotent, and throw ObjectDisposedException afterwards. The full pattern with Dispose(bool) and a finalizer is only needed when the class directly owns unmanaged resources; Dispose() then calls Dispose(true) and GC.SuppressFinalize(this). Prefer SafeHandle so you never need your own finalizer.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Who disposes services created by the DI container?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The container. Scoped services are disposed when their scope (the HTTP request) ends; singletons when the root provider is disposed at shutdown; transients when their owning scope ends. Don&#39;t dispose injected dependencies yourself.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How does <code>using</code> work under the hood?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>It compiles to a try/finally block that calls Dispose (or DisposeAsync for await using) in the finally, with a null check, so the resource is released even if an exception is thrown or the method returns early.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/exceptions-disposal/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/fundamentals/exceptions/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Exceptions and exception handling</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/exceptions/best-practices-for-exceptions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Best practices for exceptions</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/garbage-collection/implementing-dispose\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Implement a Dispose method</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/exceptions-disposal/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Save as <code>Program.cs</code> in a new console project (<code>dotnet new console</code>) and run with <code>dotnet run</code>. It checks <code>finally</code> semantics, <code>throw;</code> vs <code>throw ex;</code> stack traces, exception filters, wrapped exceptions, <code>using</code>/<code>await using</code> disposal (including idempotent <code>Dispose</code>), and how exceptions behave inside async <code>Task</code>s. The compiler will warn about <code>throw ex;</code> (CA2200) — that warning is itself the chapter&#39;s point, so it&#39;s expected here.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> failures = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> claim, <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> ok)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (ok) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: {claim}&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">else</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: {claim}&quot;</span>); failures++; }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 1. finally runs on both the success path and the exception path</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> finallyRanOnSuccess = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>, finallyRanOnException = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { } <span class=\"tk-kw\">finally</span> { finallyRanOnSuccess = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>; }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(); } <span class=\"tk-kw\">finally</span> { finallyRanOnException = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>; } }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>) { }\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;finally runs on the success path&quot;</span>, finallyRanOnSuccess);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;finally runs when an exception is thrown&quot;</span>, finallyRanOnException);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 2. throw; preserves the original stack trace; throw ex; resets it</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReThrowProperly</span>()\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">ThrowsDeep</span>(); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span> ex) { <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span>; } <span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span> e2) { <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> e2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StackTrace</span> ?? <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;</span>; } }\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReThrowBadly</span>()\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">ThrowsDeep</span>(); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span> ex) { <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> ex; } <span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span> e2) { <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> e2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StackTrace</span> ?? <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;</span>; } }\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ThrowsDeep</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;boom&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> properTrace = <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReThrowProperly</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> badTrace = <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReThrowBadly</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;throw; keeps ThrowsDeep in the stack trace (original origin preserved)&quot;</span>, properTrace.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contains</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ThrowsDeep&quot;</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;throw ex; loses ThrowsDeep from the stack trace (origin overwritten)&quot;</span>, !badTrace.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contains</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ThrowsDeep&quot;</span>));\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 3. Exception filters (when) catch selectively without catching everything</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> caughtSpecific = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>, propagatedOthers = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;transient&quot;</span>); }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span> ex) <span class=\"tk-kw\">when</span> (ex.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Message</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;transient&quot;</span>) { caughtSpecific = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>; }\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> { }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;permanent&quot;</span>); }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span> ex) <span class=\"tk-kw\">when</span> (ex.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Message</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;transient&quot;</span>) { }\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>) { propagatedOthers = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>; }\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a catch filter (when) catches only matching exceptions&quot;</span>, caughtSpecific);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a catch filter lets non-matching exceptions keep propagating&quot;</span>, propagatedOthers);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 4. Custom exception carries InnerException</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> inner = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;low level&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> wrapped = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">RepositoryException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Saving order failed&quot;</span>, inner);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a wrapped custom exception keeps the original as InnerException&quot;</span>, wrapped.<span class=\"tk-ty\">InnerException</span> == inner);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 5. using calls Dispose even when the body throws</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> resource = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TrackedResource</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> (resource) { <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(); } }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>) { }\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;using calls Dispose even when the body throws&quot;</span>, resource.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Disposed</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 6. Dispose is safe to call more than once</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> resource2 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TrackedResource</span>();\nresource2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Dispose</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>? thrown = <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { resource2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Dispose</span>(); } <span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span> ex) { thrown = ex; }\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Dispose is idempotent: calling it twice doesn't throw&quot;</span>, thrown <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 7. await using calls DisposeAsync</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> asyncResource = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TrackedAsyncResource</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> (asyncResource) { }\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;await using calls DisposeAsync when the scope ends&quot;</span>, asyncResource.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Disposed</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 8. An exception in an async Task method is stored, not thrown, until awaited</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> faultedTask = <span class=\"tk-ty\">FailAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Delay</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>); <span class=\"tk-com\">// give it time to complete</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;an exception inside an async Task is captured in the Task, not thrown immediately&quot;</span>, faultedTask.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsFaulted</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> observedOnAwait = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> faultedTask; } <span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>) { observedOnAwait = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>; }\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;awaiting a faulted Task rethrows the captured exception&quot;</span>, observedOnAwait);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 9. Task.WhenAll rethrows only the first exception, but all are in task.Exception</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> t1 = <span class=\"tk-ty\">FailAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> t2 = <span class=\"tk-ty\">FailAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WhenAll</span>(t1, t2); } <span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> { }\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Task.WhenAll's await only surfaces one exception, but both tasks' exceptions are available via .Exception&quot;</span>,\n    t1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>!.<span class=\"tk-ty\">InnerExceptions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> &amp;&amp; t2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>!.<span class=\"tk-ty\">InnerExceptions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(failures == <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span> ? <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;\\nAll checks passed.&quot;</span> : <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;\\n{failures} check(s) failed.&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (failures &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exit</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FailAsync</span>()\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Yield</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;async failure&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">RepositoryException</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> message, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span> inner) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(message, inner) { }\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TrackedResource</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IDisposable</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Disposed</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dispose</span>() { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Disposed</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>; } <span class=\"tk-com\">// idempotent by construction: nothing to double-free</span>\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TrackedAsyncResource</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IAsyncDisposable</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Disposed</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DisposeAsync</span>() { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Disposed</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CompletedTask</span>; }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Not runtime-verifiable here: <code>async void</code> crashing the process can&#39;t be safely demonstrated inside a program that also needs to keep running and report its other results — crashing it is the entire point. Take that one on trust from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/exceptions-disposal/further-reading\">Further reading</a> or trigger it deliberately in a throwaway project.</p>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"what-actually-happens-when-code-throws","text":"What actually happens when code throws","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"the-exception-hierarchy-a-map-for-picking-the-right-one","text":"The exception hierarchy: a map for picking the right one","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"throw-vs-throw-ex-one-character-of-difference-one-real-conse","text":"throw; vs throw ex; — one character of difference, one real consequence","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"exception-filters-deciding-whether-to-catch-without-unwindin","text":"Exception filters: deciding whether to catch, without unwinding first","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"writing-your-own-exception-types","text":"Writing your own exception types","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"when-not-to-reach-for-an-exception-at-all","text":"When not to reach for an exception at all","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"exceptions-in-async-code-behave-differently-from-what-youd-e","text":"Exceptions in async code behave differently from what you'd expect","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"resources-and-idisposable-the-problem-the-gc-doesnt-solve","text":"Resources and IDisposable: the problem the GC doesn't solve","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"implementing-idisposable-yourself","text":"Implementing IDisposable yourself","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"the-full-dispose-pattern-with-a-finalizer-only-when-you-genu","text":"The full Dispose pattern, with a finalizer — only when you genuinely need one","depth":3,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"iasyncdisposable-for-cleanup-that-itself-needs-to-be-awaited","text":"IAsyncDisposable: for cleanup that itself needs to be awaited","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"common-disposal-mistakes","text":"Common disposal mistakes","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":4679,"minutes":21,"num":"2.7","part":"p2"},"async-await":{"id":"async-await","title":"Asynchronous Programming: Task, async and await","summary":"Why backends are asynchronous, what a Task is, what await actually does, the rules (async all the way, no .Result, CancellationToken), running work concurrently, async streams, the state machine, SynchronizationContext and ConfigureAwait, ValueTask, deadlocks and thread-pool starvation.","layer":"language","where":"C# language › Asynchrony › Task & async/await","fits":"<p>Layer 1 syntax over layer 2 machinery (the thread pool). It is the single most important C# feature for backend work. Every ASP.NET Core endpoint, every EF Core query and every HttpClient call in layers 3–6 is async, and many production incidents (thread-pool starvation, deadlocks) come from misusing it.</p>\n","prereqs":["delegates-events","exceptions-disposal"],"next":["threading-concurrency","aspnet-hosting","efcore-querying"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/async-await/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Async/await is about <strong>scalability</strong>, not speed: it frees up the thread while waiting for I/O, so a server can handle many more concurrent requests with the same number of threads.</li>\n<li><code>await</code> doesn&#39;t block. If the awaited <code>Task</code> isn&#39;t done, the method returns to its caller immediately and resumes later — possibly on a different thread — when the result is ready.</li>\n<li>Never block on async code (<code>.Result</code>, <code>.Wait()</code>): it causes deadlocks in UI/old ASP.NET apps and thread-pool starvation in ASP.NET Core.</li>\n<li>Pass a <code>CancellationToken</code> through every async call so work actually stops when the caller gives up.</li>\n<li>Start independent tasks together and <code>await Task.WhenAll(...)</code> to run them concurrently instead of one after another.</li>\n<li>Under the hood, the compiler turns every <code>async</code> method into a state machine; the first real suspension boxes it onto the heap.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-problem-threads-waiting-on-i-o\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/async-await/the-problem-threads-waiting-on-i-o\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The problem: threads waiting on I/O <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>A web server handles many requests concurrently, using threads from the <strong>thread pool</strong>. A typical request spends most of its time <strong>waiting</strong>: on the database, another API, a file, a cache. The CPU is idle during that wait.</p>\n<p><strong>Synchronous code blocks the thread while it waits:</strong></p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IActionResult</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Get</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> order = db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Find</span>(id);          <span class=\"tk-com\">// thread BLOCKED ~20 ms waiting for SQL</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> stock = http.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetString</span>(url);          <span class=\"tk-com\">// thread BLOCKED ~100 ms waiting for HTTP</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(...);\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>With 100 concurrent requests, you need 100 blocked threads. Under load, the pool runs out, new requests queue, latency explodes, and the server falls over while the CPU sits at 10%. This is <strong>thread-pool starvation</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Asynchronous code releases the thread while it waits:</strong></p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IActionResult</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Get</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> order = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FindAsync</span>([id], ct);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// thread returns to the pool during the wait</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> stock = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> http.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetStringAsync</span>(url, ct);    <span class=\"tk-com\">// ...and again here</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(...);\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>While SQL works, the thread goes back to the pool and serves other requests. When the result arrives, <em>some</em> pool thread picks up where the method left off. A handful of threads can serve thousands of concurrent requests.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-why\"><div class=\"callout-title\">The one-sentence answer</div><p>Async/await in a backend is about <strong>scalability</strong> (serving more concurrent requests with fewer threads), not about making a single request faster. For I/O-bound work there is no thread doing anything during the wait, and async lets you stop pretending there is.</p>\n</aside>\n<aside class=\"callout c-analogy\"><div class=\"callout-title\">The restaurant</div><p>A synchronous waiter takes your order, walks to the kitchen, and <strong>stands there</strong> until the food is ready. They serve one table at a time. An asynchronous waiter takes your order, hands it to the kitchen, and <strong>serves other tables</strong>; when the kitchen rings the bell, any free waiter brings the food over. Same kitchen speed, many more tables served.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"i-o-bound-vs-cpu-bound\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/async-await/i-o-bound-vs-cpu-bound\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>I/O-bound vs CPU-bound <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>I/O-bound</th>\n<th>CPU-bound</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Time spent</td>\n<td>Waiting for something external</td>\n<td>Computing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Examples</td>\n<td>DB queries, HTTP calls, file/network I/O, message brokers</td>\n<td>Image resizing, hashing, compression, big calculations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Right tool</td>\n<td><code>async</code>/<code>await</code> with truly async APIs (<code>*Async</code> methods)</td>\n<td>Run on a thread: <code>Task.Run</code> (in UI/background code), <code>Parallel.For</code>, a background worker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>In ASP.NET Core</td>\n<td>Always async</td>\n<td>Just do it synchronously in the request (it needs a thread anyway), or offload to a background queue if it&#39;s long</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Task.Run in ASP.NET Core</div><p>Wrapping synchronous work in <code>await Task.Run(() =&gt; DoWork())</code> inside a request handler <strong>doesn&#39;t help</strong>: you release one pool thread and immediately occupy another, with extra overhead. It&#39;s useful in UI apps (to keep the UI thread free), not in web requests.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"task-and-task\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/async-await/task-and-task\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Task and Task<T> <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>A <strong><code>Task</code></strong> represents an operation that will complete in the future. <strong><code>Task&lt;T&gt;</code></strong> also carries a result of type <code>T</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Property / method</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Status</code></td>\n<td>Created, WaitingForActivation, Running, <strong>RanToCompletion</strong>, <strong>Faulted</strong>, <strong>Canceled</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IsCompleted</code>, <code>IsCompletedSuccessfully</code>, <code>IsFaulted</code>, <code>IsCanceled</code></td>\n<td>State checks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Result</code> (Task&lt;T&gt;)</td>\n<td>The value; <strong>blocks</strong> if not complete (avoid)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Exception</code></td>\n<td>An <code>AggregateException</code> if faulted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>await task</code></td>\n<td>Asynchronously wait, then get the result or rethrow the exception</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Task.FromResult(x)</code>, <code>Task.CompletedTask</code></td>\n<td>Already-completed tasks (for sync implementations of async interfaces)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Task.Delay(ms, ct)</code></td>\n<td>Async wait (never <code>Thread.Sleep</code> in async code)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Task.WhenAll</code>, <code>Task.WhenAny</code>, <code>Task.WhenEach</code> (.NET 9)</td>\n<td>Combinators</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>A Task is not a thread.</strong> For I/O, there&#39;s no thread running while the task is pending: the OS notifies the runtime (via I/O completion ports or epoll) when data arrives, and only then is a pool thread used to run your continuation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-async-and-await-actually-do\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/async-await/what-async-and-await-actually-do\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What async and await actually do <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>async</code></strong> on a method does two things: it allows <code>await</code> inside, and it makes the compiler wrap the return value or exception into a <code>Task</code>. It does <strong>not</strong> make the method run on another thread.</li>\n<li><strong><code>await t</code></strong> means: <em>if <code>t</code> is already complete, just continue immediately.</em> Otherwise, it registers &quot;the rest of this method&quot; as a continuation on <code>t</code>, and <strong>returns</strong> to the caller right now instead of waiting. When <code>t</code> eventually completes, that continuation runs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><img src=\"visuals/async-state-machine.svg\" alt=\"What await does: the method pauses, hands the thread back, and resumes later possibly on a different thread\"></p>\n<p>That picture is the whole idea in one frame: <code>await</code> never blocks a thread waiting around for something slow. It pauses the method exactly where it is, remembers everything it needs to carry on (its local variables), hands the thread back so it can go do something else useful, and picks up again later — often on a completely different thread — right where it left off. The <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/async-await/under-the-hood-the-state-machine\">&quot;Under the hood&quot; section</a> below shows precisely how the compiler makes &quot;remember everything and pick up later&quot; actually work.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetTotalAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> orderId)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A&quot;</span>);                       <span class=\"tk-com\">// runs synchronously on the caller's thread</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> order = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> repo.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(orderId);     <span class=\"tk-com\">// if not done: method returns an incomplete Task to the caller here</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;B&quot;</span>);                       <span class=\"tk-com\">// runs later, on a pool thread, when GetAsync finishes</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>;                           <span class=\"tk-com\">// completes the Task&lt;decimal&gt; with this value</span>\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Return types: <code>Task</code>, <code>Task&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>ValueTask</code>, <code>ValueTask&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>IAsyncEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</code>, or <code>void</code> (event handlers only).</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-rules\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/async-await/the-rules\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The rules <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<h3 id=\"1-async-all-the-way\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/async-await/1-async-all-the-way\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>1. Async all the way</h3>\n<p>Once something is async, its callers should be async too, up to the framework entry point (the controller action or endpoint, which ASP.NET Core awaits for you).</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-never-block-on-async-code\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/async-await/2-never-block-on-async-code\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>2. Never block on async code</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> user = <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetUserAsync</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>;              <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✘ blocks a thread; can deadlock</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetUserAsync</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Wait</span>();                          <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✘</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> user2 = <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetUserAsync</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAwaiter</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetResult</span>();  <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✘ same problem, nicer exception</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> user3 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetUserAsync</span>();               <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✔</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>This &quot;sync over async&quot; causes thread-pool starvation in ASP.NET Core, and <strong>deadlocks</strong> in UI apps and old ASP.NET (see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/async-await/deadlocks-and-thread-pool-starvation\">deadlocks</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-avoid-async-void\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/async-await/3-avoid-async-void\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>3. Avoid async void</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"YXN5bmMgdm9pZCBCYWQoKSB7IGF3YWl0IFRhc2suRGVsYXkoMSk7IHRocm93IG5ldyBFeGNlcHRpb24oKTsgfSAgIC8vIOKcmCBjcmFzaGVzIHRoZSBwcm9jZXNzOyBjYW4ndCBiZSBhd2FpdGVkCmFzeW5jIFRhc2sgR29vZCgpIHsgYXdhaXQgVGFzay5EZWxheSgxKTsgdGhyb3cgbmV3IEV4Y2VwdGlvbigpOyB9ICAvLyDinJQgZXhjZXB0aW9uIHN0b3JlZCBpbiB0aGUgVGFzaw==\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Bad</span>() { <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Delay</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>); <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(); }   <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✘ crashes the process; can't be awaited</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Good</span>() { <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Delay</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>); <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(); }  <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✔ exception stored in the Task</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h3 id=\"4-pass-a-cancellationtoken-through-everything\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/async-await/4-pass-a-cancellationtoken-through-everything\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>4. Pass a CancellationToken through everything</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/report&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">IReportService</span> svc, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> svc.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BuildAsync</span>(ct));\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// ASP.NET Core binds ct to HttpContext.RequestAborted: cancelled if the client disconnects</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Report</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">BuildAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> rows = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sales</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(...).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToListAsync</span>(ct);      <span class=\"tk-com\">// EF Core cancels the SQL command</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> fx = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> http.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetFromJsonAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Rates</span>&gt;(url, ct);       <span class=\"tk-com\">// HttpClient aborts the request</span>\n    ct.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ThrowIfCancellationRequested</span>();                          <span class=\"tk-com\">// check manually in long CPU loops</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Compose</span>(rows, fx);\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Analyzer <strong>CA2016</strong> warns when you forget to forward a token.</p>\n<h3 id=\"5-use-the-async-apis\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/async-await/5-use-the-async-apis\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>5. Use the async APIs</h3>\n<p>Use <code>ToListAsync</code>, <code>SaveChangesAsync</code>, <code>ReadAsStringAsync</code>, <code>File.ReadAllTextAsync</code>, <code>Task.Delay</code>, <code>SemaphoreSlim.WaitAsync</code>, not their blocking equivalents.</p>\n<h3 id=\"6-dont-use-async-when-you-just-pass-the-task-through-optiona\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/async-await/6-dont-use-async-when-you-just-pass-the-task-through-optiona\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>6. Don&#39;t use async when you just pass the task through (optional)</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"cHVibGljIFRhc2s8T3JkZXI/PiBHZXRBc3luYyhpbnQgaWQpID0+IHJlcG8uR2V0QXN5bmMoaWQpOyAgIC8vIG5vIHN0YXRlIG1hY2hpbmU7IHNsaWdodGx5IGNoZWFwZXI=\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>?&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id) =&gt; repo.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(id);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// no state machine; slightly cheaper</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>But keep <code>async</code>/<code>await</code> if there&#39;s a <code>using</code>, a <code>try/catch</code>, or more than one statement; otherwise the resource can be disposed before the task completes and exceptions behave differently.</p>\n<h2 id=\"running-work-concurrently\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/async-await/running-work-concurrently\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Running work concurrently <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Sequential awaits take the <strong>sum</strong> of the latencies. Starting tasks first and awaiting them together takes the <strong>maximum</strong>:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Sequential: ~300 ms</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> user = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> users.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(id, ct);           <span class=\"tk-com\">// 100 ms</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> orders = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> ordersApi.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(id, ct);     <span class=\"tk-com\">// 200 ms</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Concurrent: ~200 ms</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> userTask = users.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(id, ct);             <span class=\"tk-com\">// both started</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> ordersTask = ordersApi.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(id, ct);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WhenAll</span>(userTask, ordersTask);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> (u, o) = (userTask.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>, ordersTask.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>); <span class=\"tk-com\">// .Result is fine here: the tasks are complete</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Combinator</th>\n<th>Completes when</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Task.WhenAll(tasks)</code></td>\n<td>All complete (faults if any fault)</td>\n<td>Fan-out calls, batch processing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Task.WhenAny(tasks)</code></td>\n<td>The first completes</td>\n<td>Timeouts, &quot;fastest wins&quot;, hedging</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Task.WhenEach(tasks)</code> (.NET 9)</td>\n<td><code>IAsyncEnumerable</code> yielding tasks as they finish</td>\n<td>Process results in completion order</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Parallel.ForEachAsync(items, opts, body)</code></td>\n<td>All items processed with a max degree of parallelism</td>\n<td>Bounded concurrency over a list</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>task.WaitAsync(TimeSpan, ct)</code> (.NET 6)</td>\n<td>Task completes or timeout</td>\n<td>Adding a timeout to any task</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Bounded concurrency: never fire 10,000 HTTP calls at once</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Parallel</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ForEachAsync</span>(productIds, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ParallelOptions</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">MaxDegreeOfParallelism</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">8</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> = ct },\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (id, token) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SyncProductAsync</span>(id, token));</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">DbContext is not thread-safe</div><p><code>Task.WhenAll(db.Orders.ToListAsync(), db.Customers.ToListAsync())</code> on the <strong>same</strong> DbContext throws &quot;A second operation was started on this context&quot;. Run EF queries sequentially, or use separate contexts from <code>IDbContextFactory&lt;T&gt;</code>.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"cancellation-and-timeouts\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/async-await/cancellation-and-timeouts\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Cancellation and timeouts <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> cts = <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationTokenSource</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateLinkedTokenSource</span>(requestToken);  <span class=\"tk-com\">// combine with the caller's token</span>\ncts.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancelAfter</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSeconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>));                                        <span class=\"tk-com\">// add a timeout</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> data = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> client.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(url, cts.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Token</span>);\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">OperationCanceledException</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">when</span> (!requestToken.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsCancellationRequested</span>)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-com\">// our 5 s timeout fired (not the client disconnecting)</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCodes</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status504GatewayTimeout</span>);\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cooperative:</strong> cancelling a token doesn&#39;t kill anything. Code must observe the token (async APIs do this for you).</li>\n<li>Cancellation surfaces as <code>OperationCanceledException</code>, which is normal flow. Don&#39;t log it as an error.</li>\n<li>Dispose <code>CancellationTokenSource</code>s you create (they may hold timers).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"async-streams-iasyncenumerable\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/async-await/async-streams-iasyncenumerable\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Async streams: IAsyncEnumerable <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>When results arrive over time (DB rows, pages from an API, messages), stream them instead of buffering everything:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IAsyncEnumerable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAllAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-attr\">[EnumeratorCancellation]</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct = <span class=\"tk-kw\">default</span>)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>? next = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/products?page=1&quot;</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">while</span> (next <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">not</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> page = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> http.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetFromJsonAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Page</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>&gt;&gt;(next, ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> p <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> page!.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Items</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">yield</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> p;\n        next = page.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NextLink</span>;\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> p <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAllAsync</span>(ct)) { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* process each as it arrives */</span> }\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// ASP.NET Core streams IAsyncEnumerable responses as a JSON array without buffering</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/export&quot;</span>, (<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span> db) =&gt; db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsNoTracking</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsAsyncEnumerable</span>());</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h2 id=\"under-the-hood-the-state-machine\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/async-await/under-the-hood-the-state-machine\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Under the hood: the state machine <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>The compiler rewrites every <code>async</code> method into a <strong>struct state machine</strong> implementing <code>IAsyncStateMachine</code>. Locals become fields; each <code>await</code> becomes a numbered state.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// You write:</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetLengthAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> url)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> s = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> http.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetStringAsync</span>(url);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> s.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Length</span>;\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// The compiler generates (simplified):</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">struct</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetLengthStateMachine</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IAsyncStateMachine</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> state;                                  <span class=\"tk-com\">// -1 = running, 0 = waiting at first await</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AsyncTaskMethodBuilder</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; builder;        <span class=\"tk-com\">// creates/completes the returned Task</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> url;                                 <span class=\"tk-com\">// parameters and locals become fields</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TaskAwaiter</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; awaiter;\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">MoveNext</span>()\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span>\n        {\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (state != <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>)\n            {\n                awaiter = http.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetStringAsync</span>(url).<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAwaiter</span>();\n                <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!awaiter.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsCompleted</span>)\n                {\n                    state = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n                    builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AwaitUnsafeOnCompleted</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">ref</span> awaiter, <span class=\"tk-kw\">ref</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">this</span>); <span class=\"tk-com\">// box to heap, register continuation</span>\n                    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span>;                                                <span class=\"tk-com\">// ← method &quot;returns&quot; here</span>\n                }\n            }\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> s = awaiter.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetResult</span>();            <span class=\"tk-com\">// resumes here; rethrows if faulted</span>\n            builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SetResult</span>(s.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Length</span>);               <span class=\"tk-com\">// completes the Task&lt;int&gt;</span>\n        }\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span> ex) { builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SetException</span>(ex); }\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Key insights:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If every awaited task is <strong>already complete</strong>, the method runs entirely synchronously, with no allocation beyond the Task (the state machine stays on the stack).</li>\n<li>The first time it actually suspends, the state machine is <strong>boxed onto the heap</strong>, which is why locals in async methods live on the heap and why <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> can&#39;t cross an <code>await</code>.</li>\n<li><code>await</code> works on anything with a <code>GetAwaiter()</code> method (the <strong>awaitable pattern</strong>), not just Task.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Look at any async method in <strong>SharpLab</strong> to see the real generated code.</p>\n<h2 id=\"synchronizationcontext-and-configureawait\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/async-await/synchronizationcontext-and-configureawait\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>SynchronizationContext and ConfigureAwait <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>When an <code>await</code> resumes, <em>where</em> does the continuation run? It depends on the captured <strong>SynchronizationContext</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Environment</th>\n<th>SynchronizationContext</th>\n<th>Continuation runs on</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>WPF / WinForms / MAUI</td>\n<td>UI context</td>\n<td>The UI thread (so you can touch controls)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Old ASP.NET (Framework)</td>\n<td><code>AspNetSynchronizationContext</code></td>\n<td>One-at-a-time per request, with HttpContext.Current restored</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ASP.NET Core</strong></td>\n<td><strong>None</strong></td>\n<td>Any thread-pool thread</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Console / worker apps</td>\n<td>None</td>\n<td>Any thread-pool thread</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong><code>ConfigureAwait(false)</code></strong> says &quot;don&#39;t capture the context; resume anywhere&quot;.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>In <strong>ASP.NET Core application code</strong> it changes nothing (there&#39;s no context), so you can omit it.</li>\n<li>In <strong>library code</strong> that might be used from UI or legacy ASP.NET apps, use <code>ConfigureAwait(false)</code> on every await. It avoids deadlocks for callers who block, and avoids needless hops to the UI thread.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><code>ExecutionContext</code> is different: it carries <code>AsyncLocal&lt;T&gt;</code> values (used for logging scopes, <code>Activity.Current</code> for tracing, culture) and <strong>always</strong> flows across awaits, regardless of ConfigureAwait.</p>\n<h2 id=\"deadlocks-and-thread-pool-starvation\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/async-await/deadlocks-and-thread-pool-starvation\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Deadlocks and thread-pool starvation <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<h3 id=\"the-classic-deadlock-ui-apps-old-asp-net\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/async-await/the-classic-deadlock-ui-apps-old-asp-net\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The classic deadlock (UI apps, old ASP.NET)</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// In a WPF button handler or an ASP.NET (Framework) controller:</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> data = <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetDataAsync</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>;     <span class=\"tk-com\">// 1. blocks the UI/request thread, waiting for the task</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetDataAsync</span>()\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> s = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> http.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetStringAsync</span>(url);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// 2. when the HTTP call finishes, the continuation</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> s;                                 <span class=\"tk-com\">//    must run on the captured context, which is the</span>\n}                                             <span class=\"tk-com\">//    blocked thread from step 1. Neither proceeds: DEADLOCK.</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Fixes: don&#39;t block (await all the way), or use <code>ConfigureAwait(false)</code> inside the library.</p>\n<h3 id=\"thread-pool-starvation-asp-net-core\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/async-await/thread-pool-starvation-asp-net-core\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Thread-pool starvation (ASP.NET Core)</h3>\n<p>There&#39;s no deadlock in ASP.NET Core, but blocking still hurts. Each <code>.Result</code> holds a pool thread hostage. The pool adds threads slowly (about one or two per second once past its minimum) to avoid over-subscription. Under a burst of load:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Symptoms: <strong>latency climbs to seconds, CPU stays low, thread count slowly rises</strong>, requests time out, health checks fail.</li>\n<li>Diagnose: <code>dotnet-counters monitor</code> shows a growing <code>ThreadPool Queue Length</code> and thread count; a <code>dotnet-dump</code> shows many threads blocked in <code>Task.Wait</code> / <code>GetResult</code>.</li>\n<li>Fix: remove sync-over-async. (Raising <code>ThreadPool.SetMinThreads</code> is only a stop-gap.)</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"valuetask\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/async-await/valuetask\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>ValueTask <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p><code>Task&lt;T&gt;</code> is a class, so every async call that completes allocates one. <strong><code>ValueTask&lt;T&gt;</code></strong> is a struct that can wrap either a direct result (no allocation) or a Task:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>?&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (_cache.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGetValue</span>(id, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> p)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromResult</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>?&gt;(p);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// hot path: no allocation</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>?&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">LoadAndCacheAsync</span>(id, ct));                         <span class=\"tk-com\">// cold path: wraps a Task</span>\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Use it for <strong>hot methods that usually complete synchronously</strong> (cache hits, buffered reads). Rules: <strong>await it exactly once</strong>, never await it concurrently, and don&#39;t call <code>.Result</code> before it completes. If you need to store or await it twice, call <code>.AsTask()</code>. Default to <code>Task</code> everywhere else.</p>\n<h2 id=\"fire-and-forget-and-background-work\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/async-await/fire-and-forget-and-background-work\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Fire-and-forget and background work <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"YXBwLk1hcFBvc3QoIi9zaWdudXAiLCBhc3luYyAoVXNlciB1LCBJRW1haWxTZW5kZXIgZW1haWwpID0+CnsKICAgIGF3YWl0IFNhdmVBc3luYyh1KTsKICAgIF8gPSBlbWFpbC5TZW5kV2VsY29tZUFzeW5jKHUpOyAgICAgLy8g4pyYIGZpcmUtYW5kLWZvcmdldCBpbnNpZGUgYSByZXF1ZXN0CiAgICByZXR1cm4gUmVzdWx0cy5PaygpOwp9KTs=\"><code>app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapPost</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/signup&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">User</span> u, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IEmailSender</span> email) =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveAsync</span>(u);\n    _ = email.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SendWelcomeAsync</span>(u);     <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✘ fire-and-forget inside a request</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>();\n});</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Problems: exceptions are never observed, the work is killed on shutdown, and any scoped dependency (DbContext) may be <strong>disposed</strong> when the request ends while the task is still running. Instead, enqueue the work to a <strong>background service</strong> via a <code>Channel&lt;T&gt;</code>, or a durable queue. See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/background-work\">Background work</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"async-and-locks\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/async-await/async-and-locks\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Async and locks <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>You can&#39;t <code>await</code> inside a <code>lock</code> block (the compiler forbids it, because the continuation may run on a different thread than the one that holds the lock). Use <code>SemaphoreSlim</code>:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SemaphoreSlim</span> _mutex = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">RefreshTokenAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> _mutex.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WaitAsync</span>(ct);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">TokenExpired</span>) _token = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FetchTokenAsync</span>(ct); }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">finally</span> { _mutex.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Release</span>(); }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>More in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/threading-concurrency\">Threading &amp; concurrency</a>.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Does async/await make code faster?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Not for a single operation; it adds a small overhead. It improves scalability: threads aren&#39;t blocked during I/O, so a server handles far more concurrent requests with fewer threads. Total latency can drop when independent operations run concurrently via Task.WhenAll.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What does the await keyword actually do?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>If the awaited task is already complete, execution continues synchronously. Otherwise, it registers the rest of the method as a continuation on the task and returns an incomplete Task to the caller, freeing the thread. When the task completes, the continuation resumes, on the captured SynchronizationContext if there is one, otherwise on a thread-pool thread.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Does async create a new thread?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>No. async/await doesn&#39;t create threads. For I/O-bound operations no thread is used while waiting; completion is signalled by the OS and a pool thread runs the continuation. Task.Run is what explicitly queues work to a pool thread.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why is calling .Result or .Wait() on a Task dangerous?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>It blocks a thread until the task completes. With a single-threaded SynchronizationContext (UI apps, old ASP.NET) it deadlocks, because the continuation needs the blocked thread. In ASP.NET Core it causes thread-pool starvation under load. Exceptions are also wrapped in AggregateException.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why should you avoid async void?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The caller can&#39;t await it or know when it finishes, and exceptions can&#39;t be caught by the caller: they&#39;re raised on the context or thread pool and crash the process. Use async Task; async void is only for event handlers.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is ConfigureAwait(false) and do you need it in ASP.NET Core?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>It tells await not to resume on the captured SynchronizationContext. ASP.NET Core has no SynchronizationContext, so it makes no difference in application code there. Library authors should still use it because their code may run in UI or legacy ASP.NET apps where it prevents deadlocks and unnecessary context switches.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you run multiple async operations concurrently?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Start them without awaiting (store the tasks), then <code>await Task.WhenAll(...)</code>. Use Parallel.ForEachAsync or a SemaphoreSlim for bounded concurrency. Don&#39;t run concurrent operations on non-thread-safe objects like a single DbContext.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How does cancellation work in .NET?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Cooperatively. A CancellationTokenSource issues tokens; calling Cancel (or CancelAfter) sets the token, and code that observes it (async APIs, ThrowIfCancellationRequested) stops by throwing OperationCanceledException. ASP.NET Core provides HttpContext.RequestAborted, bound to a CancellationToken endpoint parameter.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What does the compiler generate for an async method?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A state machine struct with the locals as fields and a MoveNext method split at each await. It uses an AsyncTaskMethodBuilder to create and complete the returned Task. On the first real suspension the state machine is boxed to the heap and registered as the awaiter&#39;s continuation.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>When would you use ValueTask instead of Task?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>For hot-path methods that frequently complete synchronously (cache hits), to avoid allocating a Task. It must be awaited only once and not concurrently. Default to Task otherwise.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is thread-pool starvation and how do you diagnose it?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>All pool threads are blocked (usually by sync-over-async), so queued work waits while the pool slowly injects threads. You see high latency with low CPU and a growing thread count and queue length in dotnet-counters, and dumps show threads blocked in Wait/GetResult. Fix it by making the call chain async.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What&#39;s the risk of fire-and-forget tasks in a web request?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Unobserved exceptions, work lost on shutdown, and use of scoped services (like DbContext) after the request scope disposes them. Use a background service with a queue (Channel&lt;T&gt;) or a durable job system instead.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/async-await/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/asynchronous-programming/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Asynchronous programming with async and await</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/asynchronous-programming-patterns/task-based-asynchronous-pattern-tap\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Task-based asynchronous pattern</a></li>\n<li>Stephen Toub, <em>How Async/Await Really Works in C#</em> (.NET Blog): the definitive deep dive.</li>\n<li>David Fowler, <em>AspNetCoreDiagnosticScenarios: AsyncGuidance</em> (GitHub): practical rules for ASP.NET Core.</li>\n<li>Book: <em>Concurrency in C# Cookbook</em> by Stephen Cleary.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/async-await/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Save as <code>Program.cs</code> in a new console project (<code>dotnet new console</code>) and run with <code>dotnet run</code>. It checks sequential vs. concurrent timing, cancellation, async streams, <code>ValueTask</code>, bounded concurrency, and <code>SemaphoreSlim</code>-based async locking — timing checks use generous margins, but on an extremely slow or loaded machine they could occasionally be flaky.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Diagnostics</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Runtime</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CompilerServices</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> failures = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> claim, <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> ok)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (ok) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: {claim}&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">else</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: {claim}&quot;</span>); failures++; }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 1. Sequential awaits take the SUM of latencies; concurrent tasks take the MAX</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> swSeq = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Stopwatch</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartNew</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DelayedValue</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">80</span>); <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DelayedValue</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">80</span>);\nswSeq.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Stop</span>();\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> swConc = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Stopwatch</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartNew</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> tA = <span class=\"tk-ty\">DelayedValue</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">80</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> tB = <span class=\"tk-ty\">DelayedValue</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">80</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WhenAll</span>(tA, tB);\nswConc.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Stop</span>();\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;sequential awaits take roughly the sum of the delays (slower)&quot;</span>, swSeq.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ElapsedMilliseconds</span> &gt;= <span class=\"tk-num\">140</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Task.WhenAll on tasks started together takes roughly the max, not the sum (faster)&quot;</span>,\n    swConc.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ElapsedMilliseconds</span> &lt; swSeq.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ElapsedMilliseconds</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 2. CancellationToken: cooperative cancellation, OperationCanceledException</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> cts = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationTokenSource</span>();\ncts.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancelAfter</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">30</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> cancelled = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">LongLoopAsync</span>(cts.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Token</span>); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">OperationCanceledException</span>) { cancelled = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>; }\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a cancelled CancellationToken causes OperationCanceledException to propagate&quot;</span>, cancelled);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 3. IAsyncEnumerable: results can be consumed as they arrive via await foreach</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> collected = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> n <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CountAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>)) collected.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(n);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;await foreach consumes an IAsyncEnumerable&lt;T&gt; item by item&quot;</span>, collected.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SequenceEqual</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>[] { <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">4</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span> }));\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 4. ValueTask: a hot path can complete synchronously without allocating a Task</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> vt = <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetCachedOrLoad</span>(hit: <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a ValueTask created from a direct value completes synchronously&quot;</span>, vt.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsCompletedSuccessfully</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;awaiting that ValueTask returns the right value&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> vt == <span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 5. Parallel.ForEachAsync respects MaxDegreeOfParallelism (bounded concurrency)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> active = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, maxObserved = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> gate = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Parallel</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ForEachAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Enumerable</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Range</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>), <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ParallelOptions</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">MaxDegreeOfParallelism</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span> },\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (i, ct) =&gt;\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">lock</span> (gate) { active++; maxObserved = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Math</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Max</span>(maxObserved, active); }\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Delay</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">15</span>, ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">lock</span> (gate) { active--; }\n    });\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Parallel.ForEachAsync never exceeds MaxDegreeOfParallelism concurrent items&quot;</span>, maxObserved &lt;= <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 6. SemaphoreSlim.WaitAsync serializes access the way lock would, but is awaitable</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> semaphore = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SemaphoreSlim</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> counter = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> sawOverlap = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IncrementAsync</span>()\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> semaphore.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WaitAsync</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span>\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> before = counter;\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Delay</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (counter != before) sawOverlap = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;\n        counter = before + <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>;\n    }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">finally</span> { semaphore.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Release</span>(); }\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WhenAll</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IncrementAsync</span>(), <span class=\"tk-ty\">IncrementAsync</span>(), <span class=\"tk-ty\">IncrementAsync</span>());\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;SemaphoreSlim.WaitAsync lets only one caller through at a time&quot;</span>, !sawOverlap &amp;&amp; counter == <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(failures == <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span> ? <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;\\nAll checks passed.&quot;</span> : <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;\\n{failures} check(s) failed.&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (failures &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exit</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">DelayedValue</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> ms) { <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Delay</span>(ms); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> ms; }\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">LongLoopAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; i &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">1000</span>; i++)\n    {\n        ct.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ThrowIfCancellationRequested</span>();\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Delay</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>, ct);\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IAsyncEnumerable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">CountAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> n, <span class=\"tk-attr\">[EnumeratorCancellation]</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct = <span class=\"tk-kw\">default</span>)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>; i &lt;= n; i++)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Delay</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">yield</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> i;\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetCachedOrLoad</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> hit) =&gt;\n    hit ? <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromResult</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>) : <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Run</span>(() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>));</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Not runtime-verifiable here: that no thread sits blocked during an <code>await</code> (requires inspecting the thread pool, not just timing), and the exact deadlock/thread-pool-starvation scenarios in the &quot;Deadlocks&quot; section — those need a UI/old-ASP.NET host or a loaded server to reproduce. Take those on trust from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/async-await/further-reading\">Further reading</a>, or reproduce them deliberately in a throwaway WPF/console project.</p>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-problem-threads-waiting-on-i-o","text":"The problem: threads waiting on I/O","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"i-o-bound-vs-cpu-bound","text":"I/O-bound vs CPU-bound","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"task-and-task","text":"Task and Task","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"what-async-and-await-actually-do","text":"What async and await actually do","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"the-rules","text":"The rules","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"1-async-all-the-way","text":"1. Async all the way","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"2-never-block-on-async-code","text":"2. Never block on async code","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"3-avoid-async-void","text":"3. Avoid async void","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"4-pass-a-cancellationtoken-through-everything","text":"4. Pass a CancellationToken through everything","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"5-use-the-async-apis","text":"5. Use the async APIs","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"6-dont-use-async-when-you-just-pass-the-task-through-optiona","text":"6. Don't use async when you just pass the task through (optional)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"running-work-concurrently","text":"Running work concurrently","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"cancellation-and-timeouts","text":"Cancellation and timeouts","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"async-streams-iasyncenumerable","text":"Async streams: IAsyncEnumerable","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"under-the-hood-the-state-machine","text":"Under the hood: the state machine","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"synchronizationcontext-and-configureawait","text":"SynchronizationContext and ConfigureAwait","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"deadlocks-and-thread-pool-starvation","text":"Deadlocks and thread-pool starvation","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"the-classic-deadlock-ui-apps-old-asp-net","text":"The classic deadlock (UI apps, old ASP.NET)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"thread-pool-starvation-asp-net-core","text":"Thread-pool starvation (ASP.NET Core)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"valuetask","text":"ValueTask","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"fire-and-forget-and-background-work","text":"Fire-and-forget and background work","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"async-and-locks","text":"Async and locks","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":4223,"minutes":19,"num":"2.8","part":"p2"},"modern-csharp":{"id":"modern-csharp","title":"Modern C#: Nullability, Pattern Matching, Records and the C# 9–14 Toolkit","summary":"The features that make modern C# look different from old tutorials. Nullable reference types, pattern matching and switch expressions, records, init and required, tuples and deconstruction, extension methods and members, primary constructors, collection expressions, raw strings, and a version-by-version map.","layer":"language","where":"C# language › Modern language features","fits":"<p>Layer 1. These features don&#39;t add new runtime capabilities; they are mostly compiler features. But modern ASP.NET Core templates and samples (layer 3) use them everywhere: file-scoped namespaces, top-level <code>Program.cs</code>, records as DTOs, primary-constructor services. Knowing them lets you read current code fluently.</p>\n","prereqs":["type-system","oop"],"next":["advanced-csharp","building-apis"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/modern-csharp/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>None of this chapter&#39;s features add new runtime behavior — they&#39;re compiler conveniences that make modern code <em>look</em> different from older tutorials.</li>\n<li><strong>Nullable reference types</strong> are a compile-time-only warning system for possible nulls; they add <strong>zero</strong> runtime checks, so bad data from JSON/EF Core can still sneak a null through.</li>\n<li><strong>Pattern matching</strong> lets you test a value&#39;s <em>shape</em> (its type, its properties, a range) in <code>is</code>/<code>switch</code>, not just whether it equals a constant.</li>\n<li><strong>Records</strong> are for data that&#39;s equal by <em>value</em>, not identity; <code>with</code> gives you a non-destructively modified copy, never mutating the original.</li>\n<li><strong><code>init</code>/<code>required</code></strong> let a type force callers to set certain properties exactly once — checked by the compiler, and by JSON deserialization too.</li>\n<li><strong>Extension methods/members</strong> add capability to an existing type without touching its source — this is the exact mechanism behind <code>IServiceCollection.AddX()</code> everywhere in ASP.NET Core.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"why-this-chapter-exists\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/modern-csharp/why-this-chapter-exists\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Why this chapter exists <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Picture a concrete moment: you open a pull request on a modern ASP.NET Core repo for the first time, and nothing looks like the tutorial you learned from. <code>Program.cs</code> has nine lines total and no <code>class</code>, no <code>Main</code>. A model class has one line and no body. A <code>switch</code> is missing its <code>default</code>, and somehow the compiler isn&#39;t complaining about it. None of that is new language <em>capability</em> — the CLR could always do everything this chapter describes — it&#39;s new <em>syntax</em>, added version by version, specifically to remove ceremony from code you&#39;d otherwise have written longhand. We&#39;ll keep coming back to that one pull request through this chapter: each feature below is one more line in it starting to make sense.</p>\n<p>None of these features add new runtime capabilities the CLR couldn&#39;t already do — they&#39;re almost entirely compiler conveniences, syntax that compiles down to things you&#39;ve already met elsewhere in this book. The goal here isn&#39;t to memorise which C# version introduced what; it&#39;s to recognise each feature on sight and know <em>why</em> someone reached for it, so reading real code stops requiring a lookup every few lines.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>C#</th>\n<th>.NET</th>\n<th>Headline features</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>7.x</td>\n<td>Core 2.x</td>\n<td>Tuples, deconstruction, <code>out var</code>, pattern matching (<code>is</code>), local functions, <code>ref</code> returns, <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> support</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Core 3.x</td>\n<td><strong>Nullable reference types</strong>, switch expressions, property patterns, async streams, <code>using</code> declarations, ranges <code>^1</code>/<code>..</code>, default interface methods</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><strong>Records</strong>, <code>init</code>, <strong>top-level statements</strong>, relational/logical patterns (<code>and</code>, <code>or</code>, <code>not</code>), target-typed <code>new()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><strong>Global usings</strong>, <strong>file-scoped namespaces</strong>, record structs, extended property patterns, constant interpolated strings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td><strong>Raw string literals</strong>, <strong><code>required</code> members</strong>, list patterns, generic math (static abstract members), UTF-8 literals <code>&quot;x&quot;u8</code>, <code>file</code> types</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td><strong>Primary constructors</strong> for classes, <strong>collection expressions</strong> <code>[1, 2]</code>, default lambda parameters, alias any type, <code>[Experimental]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td><code>params</code> collections, new <code>System.Threading.Lock</code>, <code>\\e</code> escape, partial properties, <code>ref struct</code> in generics (<code>allows ref struct</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>14</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td><strong>Extension members</strong> (properties, static extensions), <strong><code>field</code> keyword</strong>, null-conditional assignment <code>a?.B = x</code>, <code>nameof</code> with unbound generics, implicit span conversions</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"nullable-reference-types-making-a-silent-killer-visible-at-c\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/modern-csharp/nullable-reference-types-making-a-silent-killer-visible-at-c\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Nullable reference types: making a silent killer visible at compile time <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><code>NullReferenceException</code> is, by most accounts, the single most common runtime crash in C# history — a reference you assumed couldn&#39;t be null, turning out to be null, discovered only when the line actually executes. <strong>Nullable reference types</strong>, turned on with <code>&lt;Nullable&gt;enable&lt;/Nullable&gt;</code> (the default in new project templates), attack this problem at compile time instead of leaving it to be discovered at runtime: the compiler starts treating every reference type as <strong>non-nullable by default</strong>, and tracks, flow-sensitively, line by line, whether a given reference could possibly be null at each point in your code.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Email</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;</span>;     <span class=\"tk-com\">// never null</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>? <span class=\"tk-ty\">MiddleName</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }      <span class=\"tk-com\">// may be null</span>\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Print</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span> c)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Email</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Length</span>);           <span class=\"tk-com\">// fine</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MiddleName</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Length</span>);      <span class=\"tk-com\">// ⚠ CS8602: possible null dereference</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MiddleName</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">not</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>)\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MiddleName</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Length</span>);  <span class=\"tk-com\">// fine: compiler knows it's not null here</span>\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice the third line doesn&#39;t warn, even though it&#39;s accessing the exact same <code>MiddleName</code> property the second line warned about — the compiler is tracking the <em>flow</em> of your code, and by the time execution reaches inside the <code>if</code>, it has proven, from the <code>is not null</code> check, that a null dereference here is impossible. This is genuinely flow analysis, not a blunt &quot;this property is marked nullable so always warn&quot; rule.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Syntax</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>string?</code></td>\n<td>May be null</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>x?.Prop</code></td>\n<td>Null-conditional: <code>null</code> if x is null</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>x ?? fallback</code></td>\n<td>Null-coalescing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>x ??= value</code></td>\n<td>Assign if null</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>x!</code></td>\n<td><strong>Null-forgiving</strong>: &quot;trust me, it isn&#39;t null&quot;. Suppresses the warning only; use sparingly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>a?.B = x</code> (C# 14)</td>\n<td>Assign only if <code>a</code> isn&#39;t null</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>[NotNullWhen(true)]</code>, <code>[MaybeNull]</code>, <code>[NotNull]</code>, <code>[MemberNotNull]</code></td>\n<td>Attributes that teach the compiler about your API&#39;s null behaviour</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>That last row matters more than it looks, because the compiler can only track what it&#39;s been told:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"cHVibGljIGJvb2wgVHJ5RmluZChpbnQgaWQsIFtOb3ROdWxsV2hlbih0cnVlKV0gb3V0IE9yZGVyPyBvcmRlcikgeyAvKiAuLi4gKi8gfQoKaWYgKFRyeUZpbmQoNSwgb3V0IHZhciBvKSkgQ29uc29sZS5Xcml0ZUxpbmUoby5Ub3RhbCk7ICAgLy8gbm8gd2FybmluZzogbyBpcyBrbm93biBub24tbnVsbCBoZXJl\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TryFind</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id, <span class=\"tk-attr\">[NotNullWhen(true)]</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>? order) { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* ... */</span> }\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryFind</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> o)) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// no warning: o is known non-null here</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Without <code>[NotNullWhen(true)]</code>, the compiler has no way to know that <code>order</code> is guaranteed non-null specifically when <code>TryFind</code> returns <code>true</code> — it would warn on <code>o.Total</code> regardless. The attribute is you teaching the compiler your own API&#39;s actual null-contract, so its flow analysis stays accurate through your own methods, not just the BCL&#39;s.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">The analysis is entirely compile-time — nothing here checks anything at runtime</div><p>This is the single most important thing to understand about the whole feature: nullable reference types add <strong>zero runtime checks</strong>. They&#39;re purely a compile-time warning system, built from static analysis of code <em>you wrote</em>. JSON deserialisation can still hand you a null where you declared <code>string</code>, not <code>string?</code>, if the JSON itself was missing that field. EF Core materialising a row with a database <code>NULL</code> can do the same. Reflection, or a caller in an assembly built with nullability disabled, bypasses the whole system entirely, because the check never existed at runtime to bypass. The practical response: validate at your actual trust boundaries anyway (<code>ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull</code>), and seriously consider making nullable warnings into build errors (<code>&lt;WarningsAsErrors&gt;nullable&lt;/WarningsAsErrors&gt;</code>) — a warning nobody&#39;s forced to fix tends to quietly accumulate until it&#39;s ignored entirely.</p>\n</aside>\n<p>The feature does reach further than your own code, though: <strong>EF Core and ASP.NET Core both read these annotations and act on them.</strong> In EF Core, a <code>string</code> property becomes a <code>NOT NULL</code> database column and <code>string?</code> becomes nullable, inferred directly from your nullability annotation rather than needing a separate <code>[Required]</code> attribute. In ASP.NET Core model validation, a non-nullable property is implicitly treated as <code>[Required]</code> for the same reason — the annotation you wrote for the compiler&#39;s benefit gets reused as real, meaningful metadata elsewhere in the framework.</p>\n<h2 id=\"pattern-matching-testing-a-values-shape-not-just-its-identit\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/modern-csharp/pattern-matching-testing-a-values-shape-not-just-its-identit\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Pattern matching: testing a value&#39;s shape, not just its identity <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>A <strong>pattern</strong> tests whether a value has a particular <em>shape</em> — not just &quot;does it equal this constant&quot;, but &quot;is it this type, with these properties, satisfying this condition&quot;. Patterns work inside <code>is</code> expressions, <code>switch</code> statements, and switch expressions alike:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>Matches when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Type / declaration</td>\n<td><code>o is Order order</code></td>\n<td><code>o</code> is an <code>Order</code> (binds it)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Constant</td>\n<td><code>x is null</code>, <code>status is OrderStatus.Paid</code></td>\n<td>Equal to a constant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relational</td>\n<td><code>age is &gt;= 18</code></td>\n<td>Comparison holds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Logical</td>\n<td><code>c is &gt;= &#39;a&#39; and &lt;= &#39;z&#39;</code>, <code>x is not null</code>, <code>s is &quot;y&quot; or &quot;yes&quot;</code></td>\n<td>Combined</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Property</td>\n<td><code>o is { Status: OrderStatus.Paid, Total: &gt; 100 }</code></td>\n<td>Properties match nested patterns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Extended property</td>\n<td><code>o is { Customer.Country: &quot;DE&quot; }</code></td>\n<td>Nested member path</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Positional</td>\n<td><code>point is (0, 0)</code></td>\n<td>Deconstruct and match</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>List</td>\n<td><code>args is [var first, .., var last]</code>, <code>[_, _]</code></td>\n<td>Sequence shape</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Discard / var</td>\n<td><code>_</code>, <code>var x</code></td>\n<td>Anything</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShippingFee</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> o) =&gt; o <span class=\"tk-kw\">switch</span>\n{\n    { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>: &gt;= <span class=\"tk-num\">100</span> }                                  =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0m</span>,\n    { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsVip</span>: <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span> }                           =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0m</span>,\n    { <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShippingAddress</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Country</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;DE&quot;</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">or</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;AT&quot;</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">or</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;CH&quot;</span> }  =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">4.90m</span>,\n    { <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShippingAddress</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Country</span>: <span class=\"tk-kw\">not</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span> }              =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">12.90m</span>,\n    _ =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;No shipping address&quot;</span>)\n};\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IResult</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ToHttp</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; r) =&gt; r <span class=\"tk-kw\">switch</span>\n{\n    { <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsSuccess</span>: <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span> } =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>),\n    { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Code</span>: <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> c } <span class=\"tk-kw\">when</span> c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">EndsWith</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;not_found&quot;</span>) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>(),\n    { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Code</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;conflict&quot;</span> } =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Conflict</span>(),\n    _ =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BadRequest</span>(r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span>)\n};\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Describe</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>[] xs) =&gt; xs <span class=\"tk-kw\">switch</span>\n{\n    [] =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;empty&quot;</span>,\n    [<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> only] =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;one item: {only}&quot;</span>,\n    [<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> first, .. <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> middle, <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> last] =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;{first}…{last} ({middle.Length} in between)&quot;</span>\n};</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>That&#39;s exactly the missing <code>default</code> from the pull request at the start of this chapter — it wasn&#39;t an oversight the reviewer should have caught. What makes <code>switch</code> expressions genuinely safer than an old-style <code>if</code>/<code>else</code> chain, not just shorter, is what the compiler checks on your behalf: it checks <strong>exhaustiveness</strong> — a compiler warning if your arms don&#39;t provably cover every possible case — and <strong>subsumption</strong>, an error if an earlier arm already matches everything a later arm would, making that later arm permanently unreachable dead code. A switch expression that genuinely finds no matching arm at runtime (something exhaustiveness checking is supposed to prevent, but — as Microsoft&#39;s own docs note — can&#39;t always guarantee for every pattern shape, list patterns among them) throws <code>SwitchExpressionException</code> rather than silently doing nothing.</p>\n<p><img src=\"visuals/switch-arms-narrowing.svg\" alt=\"A switch expression tries each arm in source order and stops at the first match; arms after it never run\"></p>\n<h2 id=\"records-types-built-for-carrying-data-not-identity\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/modern-csharp/records-types-built-for-carrying-data-not-identity\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Records: types built for carrying data, not identity <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>A <strong>record</strong> is designed around a different default assumption from a class: that two instances with the same data genuinely <em>are</em> the same thing, rather than being two distinct objects that happen to look alike. To make that assumption cheap to express, the compiler generates value-based <code>Equals</code>/<code>GetHashCode</code>, a readable <code>ToString</code>, <code>Deconstruct</code>, a copy constructor, and support for <code>with</code> — all automatically, from a one-line declaration:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductDto</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span>);       <span class=\"tk-com\">// positional record class</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> a = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductDto</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Mouse&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">20m</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> b = a <span class=\"tk-kw\">with</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">18m</span> };                 <span class=\"tk-com\">// non-destructive mutation: a copy with one change</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(a == <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductDto</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Mouse&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">20m</span>));   <span class=\"tk-com\">// True: value equality</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(a);                           <span class=\"tk-com\">// ProductDto { Id = 1, Name = Mouse, Price = 20 }</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> (id, name, _) = a;                          <span class=\"tk-com\">// deconstruction</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">struct</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Point</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">X</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Y</span>);       <span class=\"tk-com\">// value-type record (mutable by default)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">struct</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Amount</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Currency</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">abstract</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Shape</span>;                   <span class=\"tk-com\">// records support inheritance (record classes)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Circle</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Radius</span>) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Shape</span>;</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>That <code>a == new ProductDto(1, &quot;Mouse&quot;, 20m)</code> line returning <code>True</code> is the whole point made concrete: these are two entirely separate objects in memory, yet <code>==</code> says they&#39;re equal, because a record&#39;s equality compares <em>values</em>, field by field, not references — exactly the &quot;Records&quot; row from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/type-system/equality-three-different-questions-similar-syntax\">Type system</a>&#39;s equality table. <code>with</code> gives you non-destructive updates on top of that: <code>b</code> is a brand-new object, a full copy of <code>a</code> except for the one property you named, leaving <code>a</code> itself completely untouched.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Use a record for</th>\n<th>Use a class for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>DTOs, API request/response models</td>\n<td>Entities with identity and lifecycle (EF Core entities)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Commands, queries, events, messages</td>\n<td>Services with dependencies and behaviour</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Value objects (Money, Address)</td>\n<td>Mutable objects with complex invariants</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Configuration snapshots, results</td>\n<td>Anything where reference identity matters</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">A record&#39;s generated equality is shallow — and that can genuinely surprise you</div><p><code>public record Cart(List&lt;Item&gt; Items);</code> looks immutable, and in one sense it is — nothing stops you reassigning <code>Cart.Items</code> to a different list after construction, true, but that&#39;s not the trap. The real trap: <code>cart.Items.Add(...)</code> still works perfectly fine, because the <code>List&lt;Item&gt;</code> itself was never made immutable, only the record&#39;s own property is &quot;init-only&quot;. And the generated equality compares the <strong>list reference</strong>, not its contents — two carts holding lists with identical items, added in the same order, won&#39;t be considered equal unless they happen to share the exact same <code>List&lt;Item&gt;</code> instance. If you need genuine deep immutability and deep equality, use <code>IReadOnlyList&lt;T&gt;</code> or <code>ImmutableArray&lt;T&gt;</code> for the property, and write custom equality logic if the default field-by-field comparison isn&#39;t what you actually need.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"init-and-required-forcing-the-caller-to-set-what-matters\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/modern-csharp/init-and-required-forcing-the-caller-to-set-what-matters\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>init and required: forcing the caller to set what matters <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateOrderRequest</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">required</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">init</span>; }        <span class=\"tk-com\">// must be set; can't change afterwards</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">required</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">LineDto</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">init</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>? <span class=\"tk-ty\">CouponCode</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">init</span>; }              <span class=\"tk-com\">// optional, still immutable after init</span>\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> req = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateOrderRequest</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span> = [] };   <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✔</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> bad = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateOrderRequest</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">42</span> };               <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✘ CS9035: required member 'Lines' not set</span>\nreq.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CouponCode</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;X&quot;</span>;                                               <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✘ init-only</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>These two keywords solve two separate, complementary problems. <code>init</code> restricts <em>when</em> a property can be set — only during object initialisation, never afterward — giving you immutability without needing a big, repetitive constructor to enforce it. <code>required</code> (C# 11) goes further, forcing the <em>compiler</em> to check that a caller actually set a given property at all; skip <code>Lines</code> on construction and you get <code>CS9035</code>, a compile error, rather than discovering a silently-empty list of order lines at runtime. The combination of the two, <code>required ... { get; init; }</code>, is as close as C# gets to &quot;the caller absolutely must provide this, and nothing can ever change it afterward&quot; — all enforced before the code even runs.</p>\n<p>This isn&#39;t purely a compile-time nicety either: <code>required</code> is understood by <code>System.Text.Json</code> too, so a missing JSON property for a <code>required</code> member causes an actual deserialisation error, rather than silently leaving it at its default. <code>[SetsRequiredMembers]</code> on a constructor is the escape hatch for when you&#39;re providing an alternative way to guarantee the required members are set, telling the compiler &quot;trust this constructor, it covers them all.&quot;</p>\n<h2 id=\"tuples-and-deconstruction\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/modern-csharp/tuples-and-deconstruction\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Tuples and deconstruction <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Summarise</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IEnumerable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt; orders) =&gt; (orders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span>(), orders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sum</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>));\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> (count, total) = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Summarise</span>(orders);              <span class=\"tk-com\">// deconstruct</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> summary = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Summarise</span>(orders);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(summary.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>);                    <span class=\"tk-com\">// named elements</span>\n(a, b) = (b, a);                                     <span class=\"tk-com\">// swap without a temp variable</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> (key, <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> dictionary) { }         <span class=\"tk-com\">// KeyValuePair deconstruction</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><code>ValueTuple</code>, what <code>(int, decimal)</code> actually is underneath, is a <strong>mutable struct</strong> — which immediately tells you where it belongs and where it doesn&#39;t, by the same reasoning from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/type-system/when-a-struct-actually-earns-its-keep\">Type system</a>: fine for a quick, <strong>private or internal</strong> multi-value return where defining a whole named type feels like overkill, but the wrong choice for a public API or anything persisted. A record gives you named, evolvable, properly-serialisable members for a few extra characters — worth it the moment the value crosses a real boundary.</p>\n<h2 id=\"extension-methods-and-extension-members-adding-capability-wi\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/modern-csharp/extension-methods-and-extension-members-adding-capability-wi\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Extension methods and extension members: adding capability without touching the original type <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Extension methods</strong> (a C# 3 feature — old enough that you&#39;ve definitely already used dozens without necessarily naming the mechanism) let you add methods to an existing type without touching its source at all. Under the hood they&#39;re ordinary static methods in a static class, where the first parameter carries a <code>this</code> modifier. LINQ&#39;s entire operator set, <code>IServiceCollection.AddXyz()</code>, <code>app.MapXyz()</code> — all of these are extension methods. That&#39;s precisely why <code>IServiceCollection</code> itself, a tiny interface at its core, seems to have an enormous API surface once you count every library&#39;s extension methods on top of it.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceCollectionExtensions</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IServiceCollection</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AddOrdering</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">this</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IServiceCollection</span> services, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IConfiguration</span> config)\n    {\n        services.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderService</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderService</span>&gt;();\n        services.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddOptions</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderingOptions</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Bind</span>(config.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetSection</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Ordering&quot;</span>)).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidateOnStart</span>();\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> services;                 <span class=\"tk-com\">// return for fluent chaining</span>\n    }\n}\n\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddOrdering</span>(builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configuration</span>);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// reads like a built-in method</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>This is the exact mechanism behind the <code>Add&lt;Feature&gt;()</code> / <code>Map&lt;Feature&gt;()</code> convention you&#39;ll see throughout <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/a-tour-of-the-registration-api\">Dependency injection</a> and elsewhere in this book: it&#39;s how every library plugs itself cleanly into ASP.NET Core, and it&#39;s how you keep your own <code>Program.cs</code> from turning into a wall of raw registration calls.</p>\n<p><strong>Extension members</strong> (C# 14) extend the same idea beyond methods, to properties, static members and operators, using a new <code>extension</code> block:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderExtensions</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">extension</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> order)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsBig</span> =&gt; order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">1_000m</span>;               <span class=\"tk-com\">// extension property</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TotalWithTax</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> rate) =&gt; order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> * (<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> + rate);\n    }\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsBig</span>) { }</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Three rules worth knowing before you reach for this (confirmed against Microsoft&#39;s <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/language-reference/compiler-messages/cs0122\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CS0122 reference</a> and the <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/programming-guide/classes-and-structs/extension-methods\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">extension methods guide</a>): extensions can never access an object&#39;s <em>private</em> members, the same restriction extension methods have always had; a real instance member always wins over an extension with the same signature, if both exist — the extension only fills in gaps, never overrides; and the extension&#39;s own namespace has to be imported at the call site, same as any other extension method, for the compiler to find it at all.</p>\n<h2 id=\"less-ceremony-top-level-statements-global-usings-file-scoped\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/modern-csharp/less-ceremony-top-level-statements-global-usings-file-scoped\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Less ceremony: top-level statements, global usings, file-scoped namespaces <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"Ly8gUHJvZ3JhbS5jczogdG9wLWxldmVsIHN0YXRlbWVudHMgKEMjIDkpLiBUaGUgY29tcGlsZXIgZ2VuZXJhdGVzIHRoZSBNYWluIG1ldGhvZC4KdmFyIGJ1aWxkZXIgPSBXZWJBcHBsaWNhdGlvbi5DcmVhdGVCdWlsZGVyKGFyZ3MpOwp2YXIgYXBwID0gYnVpbGRlci5CdWlsZCgpOwphcHAuTWFwR2V0KCIvIiwgKCkgPT4gIkhlbGxvIik7CmFwcC5SdW4oKTs=\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Program.cs: top-level statements (C# 9). The compiler generates the Main method.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> builder = <span class=\"tk-ty\">WebApplication</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateBuilder</span>(args);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> app = builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/&quot;</span>, () =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Hello&quot;</span>);\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Run</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// GlobalUsings.cs (or &lt;Using Include=&quot;...&quot;/&gt; in the csproj)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">global</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Shop</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Domain</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">global</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">EntityFrameworkCore</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Any file: file-scoped namespace (C# 10), one less indentation level</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">namespace</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Shop</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Api</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderEndpoints</span> { }</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>None of these three change what the compiled code actually does — they exist purely to remove ceremony that every single C# file used to repeat. Top-level statements mean <code>Program.cs</code> is no longer wrapped in a <code>class Program { static void Main(string[] args) { ... } }</code> you never actually needed to think about; the compiler generates that scaffolding silently. Global usings move a <code>using</code> you&#39;d otherwise retype at the top of every file into one shared place. File-scoped namespaces drop one level of <code>{ }</code> indentation that used to wrap an entire file&#39;s contents for no real benefit. <code>&lt;ImplicitUsings&gt;enable&lt;/ImplicitUsings&gt;</code> goes further still, adding a set of SDK-specific global usings automatically (<code>System</code>, <code>System.Linq</code>, <code>System.Threading.Tasks</code>, <code>Microsoft.AspNetCore.Builder</code>, <code>Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection</code>, and more) — which is a large part of why modern minimal API code can get away with no <code>using</code> statements at the top at all.</p>\n<h2 id=\"primary-constructors-and-collection-expressions\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/modern-csharp/primary-constructors-and-collection-expressions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Primary constructors and collection expressions <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Primary constructor (C# 12): perfect for DI-injected services</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CheckoutService</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderRepository</span> orders, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGateway</span> payments, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeProvider</span> clock)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>&gt;&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">CheckoutAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cart</span> cart, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> order = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Create</span>(cart, clock.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetUtcNow</span>());\n        <span class=\"tk-com\">// orders, payments, clock are in scope everywhere in the class</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> payments.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeAsync</span>(order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>, ct);\n        orders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(order);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>;\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Collection expressions (C# 12): one syntax for arrays, lists, spans, immutable collections…</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>[] a = [<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>];\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; names = [];\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">ImmutableArray</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; imm = [<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>];\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadOnlySpan</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>&gt; bytes = [0x48, 0x49];\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>[] merged = [..a, ..other, <span class=\"tk-num\">99</span>];        <span class=\"tk-com\">// spread</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/oop/primary-constructors-the-modern-shorthand\">OOP</a> already covered primary constructors properly, including the one genuine gotcha worth remembering (their parameters are reassignable, not automatically <code>readonly</code> fields). Collection expressions deserve a mention of their own, purely for the uniformity they bring. Before C# 12, an array, a <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code>, an <code>ImmutableArray&lt;T&gt;</code> and a <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> each needed their own distinct initialisation syntax. Now <code>[...]</code> works for all of them — the compiler picks the right concrete construction based on the variable&#39;s declared type. One syntax to learn, reused everywhere a sequence of values needs creating.</p>\n<h2 id=\"strings-interpolation-and-raw-literals\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/modern-csharp/strings-interpolation-and-raw-literals\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Strings: interpolation and raw literals <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> msg = <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;Order {order.Id} total {order.Total:C2} placed {order.CreatedAt:yyyy-MM-dd}&quot;</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> aligned = <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;|{name,-20}|{qty,5}|&quot;</span>;          <span class=\"tk-com\">// alignment</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> path = <span class=\"tk-str\">@&quot;C:\\data\\orders&quot;</span>;                    <span class=\"tk-com\">// verbatim: no escape processing</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> json = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    {\n      &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Keyboard&quot;,\n      &quot;tags&quot;: [&quot;office&quot;, &quot;usb&quot;]\n    }\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;</span>;                                         <span class=\"tk-com\">// raw string literal (C# 11): no escaping, indentation trimmed</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> template = $<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    { &quot;id&quot;: {{order.Id}}, &quot;literal&quot;: &quot;{braces}&quot; }\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;</span>;                                         <span class=\"tk-com\">// $$ means {{x}} interpolates and single { } are literal</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Raw string literals exist specifically because embedding JSON, regex, or SQL inside a normal C# string means constantly escaping quotes and backslashes that mean something different in the two languages at once. A triple-quoted raw string sidesteps that entirely — what you see between the <code>&quot;&quot;&quot;</code> markers is exactly, literally, what you get, with no escaping required for anything inside it.</p>\n<p>Worth knowing precisely: interpolated strings aren&#39;t naive string concatenation under the hood — they compile through <code>DefaultInterpolatedStringHandler</code>, which avoids boxing value-type arguments and avoids unnecessary intermediate string allocations along the way. That efficiency, though, comes with one important exception worth remembering: <strong>don&#39;t use interpolated strings in log messages</strong>. <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/errors-logging\">Errors &amp; logging</a> explains exactly why — a log call needs its arguments to stay as separate, structured values a log store can query on, and string interpolation collapses them into one opaque, pre-formatted string before the logger ever sees them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"other-quality-of-life-features-worth-recognising-on-sight\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/modern-csharp/other-quality-of-life-features-worth-recognising-on-sight\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Other quality-of-life features worth recognising on sight <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Target-typed <code>new</code></td>\n<td><code>Dictionary&lt;string, List&lt;int&gt;&gt; map = new();</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>nameof</code></td>\n<td><code>throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(order));</code>, survives renames</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ranges and indices</td>\n<td><code>items[^1]</code>, <code>items[1..^1]</code>, <code>text[..10]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>using</code> declaration</td>\n<td><code>using var scope = logger.BeginScope(...);</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Static lambdas</td>\n<td><code>list.Select(static x =&gt; x * 2)</code>: prevents accidental capture</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Local functions</td>\n<td>Helper methods inside a method; can be <code>static</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Default lambda parameters</td>\n<td><code>var greet = (string name = &quot;you&quot;) =&gt; $&quot;Hi {name}&quot;;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Type aliases</td>\n<td><code>using OrderMap = System.Collections.Generic.Dictionary&lt;int, Order&gt;;</code>, any type since C# 12</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>params</code> collections</td>\n<td><code>void Log(params ReadOnlySpan&lt;string&gt; parts)</code>: no array allocation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>New <code>Lock</code> type</td>\n<td><code>private readonly Lock _gate = new(); lock (_gate) { }</code>, faster than locking on object</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>field</code> keyword</td>\n<td><code>public string Name { get; set =&gt; field = value.Trim(); }</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>UTF-8 literals</td>\n<td><code>ReadOnlySpan&lt;byte&gt; h = &quot;Content-Type&quot;u8;</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>A couple of these are worth a second&#39;s thought rather than just filing away as syntax. <code>nameof(order)</code> isn&#39;t just a shorter way to write <code>&quot;order&quot;</code> — the key benefit is that it&#39;s checked by the compiler and tied to the actual identifier, so renaming the parameter and forgetting to update a hard-coded string message simply can&#39;t happen; the compiler won&#39;t let the two drift apart. Static lambdas are the direct, deliberate enforcement mechanism for the closure-cost discussion in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/delegates-events/closures-when-a-lambda-outlives-the-method-it-was-written-in\">Delegates, lambdas and closures</a> — marking a lambda <code>static</code> is you telling the compiler &quot;I don&#39;t intend to capture anything here, please fail to compile if I accidentally do.&quot;</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are nullable reference types and do they prevent NullReferenceException at runtime?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A compiler feature (enabled with &lt;Nullable&gt;) that treats reference types as non-null by default, so <code>T?</code> means &quot;may be null&quot;, and it warns about possible null dereferences through flow analysis. It&#39;s compile-time only: there are no runtime checks, so values from deserialisation, reflection or unannotated code can still be null. Validate at boundaries.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What does the null-forgiving operator (!) do?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>It suppresses a nullable warning for that expression. It has no runtime effect. Overusing it defeats the feature.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is a record and how is it different from a class?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A record is a class (or struct with <code>record struct</code>) for which the compiler generates value-based equality and GetHashCode, ToString, Deconstruct and <code>with</code> support. Two records with equal property values are equal. Classes use reference equality by default. Use records for data (DTOs, messages, value objects) and classes for entities and services.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What&#39;s the difference between init and required?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p><code>init</code> makes a property settable only during object initialisation. <code>required</code> forces callers to set the property in the initialiser, checked by the compiler. They&#39;re often combined: <code>public required string Email { get; init; }</code>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Explain switch expressions and property patterns.</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A switch expression evaluates arms top-down and returns the first matching arm&#39;s value: <code>x switch { pattern =&gt; value, _ =&gt; default }</code>. Property patterns match on members, <code>{ Status: Paid, Total: &gt; 100 }</code>, and nest (<code>{ Customer.Country: &quot;DE&quot; }</code>). The compiler warns if not all cases are covered.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do extension methods work and how does ASP.NET Core use them?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>They are static methods in static classes whose first parameter is marked with <code>this</code>. The compiler lets you call them as instance methods on that type. ASP.NET Core and libraries expose their setup as extension methods on IServiceCollection (AddX) and IEndpointRouteBuilder/IApplicationBuilder (MapX/UseX), which is the convention for organising Program.cs.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are primary constructors and what&#39;s a gotcha?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>C# 12 lets classes declare constructor parameters in the type header, and those parameters are in scope throughout the class body, which suits DI. The gotcha: they&#39;re mutable captured parameters, not readonly fields, and capturing them in a struct or in several members stores them as hidden fields.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are tuples good for and when would you avoid them?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Lightweight multi-value returns and deconstruction for private or internal code. Avoid them in public APIs and persisted contracts, where a named record is clearer, can evolve, and has proper property names in serialisation.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/modern-csharp/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/whats-new/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">What&#39;s new in C# (every version)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/whats-new/csharp-14\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">What&#39;s new in C# 14</a> — extension members, the <code>field</code> keyword, null-conditional assignment, <code>nameof</code> on unbound generics</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/nullable-references\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Nullable reference types</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/fundamentals/patterns/pattern-matching\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pattern matching overview</a> — exhaustiveness and subsumption, straight from the source</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.runtime.compilerservices.switchexpressionexception\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SwitchExpressionException class</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/language-reference/builtin-types/record\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Records</a> — confirms <code>record struct</code> is mutable by default; only <code>readonly record struct</code> is immutable</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/modern-csharp/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run</code> in a plain console project (<code>dotnet new console</code>) — no extra packages needed. It checks record value equality, <code>with</code>, the shallow-equality gotcha, <code>required</code> enforced through JSON, pattern matching, extension methods and collection expressions.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Text</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Json</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> condition, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> claim)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!condition) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: {claim}&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: {claim}&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 1. Records have value equality: two separate instances with equal properties are ==.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> a = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductDto</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Mouse&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">20m</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> sameValues = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductDto</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Mouse&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">20m</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(!<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReferenceEquals</span>(a, sameValues) &amp;&amp; a == sameValues, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Two separate record instances with equal properties are == (value equality)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 2. `with` produces a non-destructive copy: the original is untouched, the copy has the one changed property.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> b = a <span class=\"tk-kw\">with</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">18m</span> };\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(a.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">20m</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;The original record is untouched after `with`&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">18m</span> &amp;&amp; b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> == a.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> &amp;&amp; b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> == a.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;`with` copies every property except the one(s) explicitly changed&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 3. A record's generated equality is SHALLOW for reference-type members: two carts with equal-content</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//    lists (different List&lt;T&gt; instances) are NOT equal, because the list REFERENCE is compared.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> cart1 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Cart</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;sku-1&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;sku-2&quot;</span> });\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> cart2 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Cart</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;sku-1&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;sku-2&quot;</span> });\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(cart1 != cart2, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Two records holding different List&lt;T&gt; instances with identical contents are NOT equal (equality is shallow/by-reference for that member)&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> cart3 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Cart</span>(cart1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Items</span>);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// same list reference</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(cart1 == cart3, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Two records sharing the SAME list reference ARE equal&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 4. `required` is enforced by System.Text.Json: a missing required property fails deserialization.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> jsonMissingRequired = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;&quot;{ &quot;CustomerId&quot;: 42 }&quot;&quot;&quot;</span>;   <span class=\"tk-com\">// &quot;Lines&quot; is required and missing</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> threwOnMissingRequired = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">JsonSerializer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Deserialize</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateOrderRequest</span>&gt;(jsonMissingRequired); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">JsonException</span>) { threwOnMissingRequired = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>; }\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(threwOnMissingRequired, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;System.Text.Json throws JsonException when a `required` property is missing from the JSON&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> jsonComplete = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;&quot;{ &quot;CustomerId&quot;: 42, &quot;Lines&quot;: [&quot;sku-1&quot;] }&quot;&quot;&quot;</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> deserialized = <span class=\"tk-ty\">JsonSerializer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Deserialize</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateOrderRequest</span>&gt;(jsonComplete);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(deserialized <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">42</span> }, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;System.Text.Json deserializes successfully once every `required` property is present&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 5. Pattern matching: property patterns and list patterns match the shapes the chapter describes.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Describe</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>[] { <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span> }) == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;first=1 last=3 middle=1&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A list pattern [first, .. middle, last] destructures a 3+ element array correctly&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Describe</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Array</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Empty</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;()) == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;empty&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A list pattern [] matches an empty array&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShippingFee</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">150</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsVip</span>: <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>)) == <span class=\"tk-num\">0m</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A property pattern on Total &gt;= 100 matches and returns free shipping&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShippingFee</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsVip</span>: <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>)) == <span class=\"tk-num\">0m</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A property pattern on IsVip matches and returns free shipping regardless of total&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 6. Extension methods add capability to an existing type without modifying its source.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;hello world&quot;</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsTitleCaseWord</span>() == <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;An extension method (IsTitleCaseWord) works like an instance method on a type it didn't define&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Hello&quot;</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsTitleCaseWord</span>() == <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;An extension method correctly identifies a title-case word&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 7. Collection expressions produce the right concrete type for the declared variable.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>[] arr = [<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>];\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; list = [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;b&quot;</span>];\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(arr.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Length</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span> &amp;&amp; arr <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>[], <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A collection expression assigned to int[] produces an array&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(list.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span> &amp;&amp; list <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;The same [...] syntax assigned to List&lt;string&gt; produces a List&lt;T&gt;&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All modern-csharp claims verified.&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Describe</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>[] xs) =&gt; xs <span class=\"tk-kw\">switch</span>\n{\n    [] =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;empty&quot;</span>,\n    [<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> only] =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;one item: {only}&quot;</span>,\n    [<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> first, .. <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> middle, <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> last] =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;first={first} last={last} middle={middle.Length}&quot;</span>\n};\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShippingFee</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> o) =&gt; o <span class=\"tk-kw\">switch</span>\n{\n    { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>: &gt;= <span class=\"tk-num\">100</span> } =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0m</span>,\n    { <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsVip</span>: <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span> } =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0m</span>,\n    _ =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">12.90m</span>\n};\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductDto</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Cart</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Items</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsVip</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateOrderRequest</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">required</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">init</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">required</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">init</span>; }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">StringExtensions</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsTitleCaseWord</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">this</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> s) =&gt; s.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Length</span> &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span> &amp;&amp; <span class=\"tk-kw\">char</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsUpper</span>(s[<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>]) &amp;&amp; s.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Skip</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">All</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">char</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsLower</span>);\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Not attempted here: nullable reference type warnings are a compiler-time analysis, not something a running program can assert on — that claim is checked by reading the compiler&#39;s own warnings (<code>CS8602</code> etc.) when you build with <code>&lt;Nullable&gt;enable&lt;/Nullable&gt;</code>, not by running anything.</p>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"why-this-chapter-exists","text":"Why this chapter exists","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"nullable-reference-types-making-a-silent-killer-visible-at-c","text":"Nullable reference types: making a silent killer visible at compile time","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"pattern-matching-testing-a-values-shape-not-just-its-identit","text":"Pattern matching: testing a value's shape, not just its identity","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"records-types-built-for-carrying-data-not-identity","text":"Records: types built for carrying data, not identity","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"init-and-required-forcing-the-caller-to-set-what-matters","text":"init and required: forcing the caller to set what matters","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"tuples-and-deconstruction","text":"Tuples and deconstruction","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"extension-methods-and-extension-members-adding-capability-wi","text":"Extension methods and extension members: adding capability without touching the original type","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"less-ceremony-top-level-statements-global-usings-file-scoped","text":"Less ceremony: top-level statements, global usings, file-scoped namespaces","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"primary-constructors-and-collection-expressions","text":"Primary constructors and collection expressions","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"strings-interpolation-and-raw-literals","text":"Strings: interpolation and raw literals","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"other-quality-of-life-features-worth-recognising-on-sight","text":"Other quality-of-life features worth recognising on sight","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":5348,"minutes":24,"num":"2.9","part":"p2"},"advanced-csharp":{"id":"advanced-csharp","title":"Advanced C#: Span, Memory, ref, Reflection, Attributes and Source Generators","summary":"The tools for high-performance and meta-programming. Span<T> and Memory<T>, ref structs and stackalloc, ArrayPool, unsafe code and interop, reflection and its costs, attributes, and source generators, which replace reflection for speed and Native AOT.","layer":"language","where":"C# language › Advanced & low-level features","fits":"<p>Layer 1 features with deep layer 2 roots: Span and ref structs exist because of how the runtime manages memory (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/memory-gc\">Memory &amp; GC</a>), and source generators exist because reflection fights trimming and Native AOT (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/what-if-you-skipped-the-jit-completely\">How code runs</a>). In layer 3, Kestrel and System.Text.Json use these techniques heavily.</p>\n","prereqs":["type-system","generics","async-await"],"next":["memory-gc","performance-engineering"],"levels":["i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/advanced-csharp/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> is a <strong>view</strong> into existing memory (array, string, stack, native) — slicing it never copies, which is why hot-path parsing code uses it to avoid allocating.</li>\n<li><code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> is a <code>ref struct</code>: the compiler forces it to stay on the stack, so it can never outlive the memory it points at (including <code>stackalloc</code>&#39;d memory).</li>\n<li><code>Memory&lt;T&gt;</code> is <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code>&#39;s heap-friendly cousin — use it when you need to store a span-like value in a field or carry it across an <code>await</code>, then call <code>.Span</code> for the synchronous part.</li>\n<li><code>ArrayPool&lt;T&gt;</code> lets you reuse large buffers instead of allocating (and eventually GC-ing) a new one every request.</li>\n<li><strong>Reflection</strong> reads a program&#39;s own metadata at runtime (what powers DI containers, serializers, test runners) but is slow, allocation-heavy, and hostile to trimming/Native AOT.</li>\n<li><strong>Source generators</strong> do reflection&#39;s job at <em>build time</em> instead, producing ordinary, fast, trimmer-visible code — which is why the ecosystem is moving toward them.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"why-a-web-framework-needs-tools-this-low-level\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/advanced-csharp/why-a-web-framework-needs-tools-this-low-level\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Why a web framework needs tools this low-level <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Here&#39;s the scenario this whole chapter is really about: a profiler flags one endpoint as the slowest thing in your service, and the trace doesn&#39;t show slow CPU work at all — it shows the GC running constantly, because every request parses a line of text by calling <code>.Split(&#39;,&#39;)</code> and allocating a fresh array and four fresh strings, every single time, thousands of times a second. Nothing in that code is <em>wrong</em>; it&#39;s just paying an allocation cost it didn&#39;t need to pay. Every tool in this chapter exists to answer that same profiler trace in one way or another.</p>\n<p>ASP.NET Core can serve millions of requests per second on one machine, mostly because it avoids <strong>allocating</strong> on the hot path. <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/memory-gc\">Memory &amp; GC</a> already established that allocation rate, not raw CPU work, usually limits throughput — so parsing HTTP headers, routing a request, and serializing a JSON response all work on <em>slices</em> of buffers that already exist, instead of carving out new strings and arrays per request. You&#39;ll rarely write this kind of code yourself, but you&#39;ll run into these types constantly in APIs you call every day — <code>ReadOnlySpan&lt;char&gt;</code> overloads, <code>IBufferWriter&lt;byte&gt;</code>, <code>PipeReader</code> — and interviews ask about them because knowing <em>why</em> they exist explains how a fast .NET system is actually built.</p>\n<h2 id=\"span-a-view-not-a-copy\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/advanced-csharp/span-a-view-not-a-copy\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Span<T>: a view, not a copy <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> is a <strong>view over contiguous memory</strong> — part of an array, a string, stack memory, or native memory — represented as nothing more than a pointer plus a length. The single idea that makes it useful: <strong>slicing a span creates a new, narrower view, never a copy.</strong></p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> line = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;2026-10-27,ORD-1042,149.90,EUR&quot;</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Classic: Split allocates an array + 4 strings</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> parts = line.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Split</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">','</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> amount = <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parse</span>(parts[<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>], <span class=\"tk-ty\">CultureInfo</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">InvariantCulture</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Span: zero allocations</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadOnlySpan</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">char</span>&gt; span = line;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i1 = span.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IndexOf</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">','</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i2 = span[(i1 + <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>)..].<span class=\"tk-ty\">IndexOf</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">','</span>) + i1 + <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i3 = span[(i2 + <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>)..].<span class=\"tk-ty\">IndexOf</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">','</span>) + i2 + <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> amount2 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parse</span>(span[(i2 + <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>)..i3], <span class=\"tk-ty\">CultureInfo</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">InvariantCulture</span>);  <span class=\"tk-com\">// Parse accepts ReadOnlySpan&lt;char&gt;</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// .NET 9+: Split over spans without allocating</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Range</span> r <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> span.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Split</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">','</span>)) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(span[r].<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>());</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Compare the two approaches to the same four-field line: <code>line.Split(&#39;,&#39;)</code> allocates a whole new <code>string[]</code>, plus four brand-new strings — a full copy of every character, duplicated. The span version never copies a single character; <code>span[(i1 + 1)..]</code> is just a different pointer-and-length pair into the exact same memory <code>line</code> already occupies. For a hot endpoint parsing thousands of lines a second — the exact endpoint from the top of this chapter — that difference is the entire reason <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> exists.</p>\n<p><img src=\"visuals/span-view-vs-copy.svg\" alt=\"Split copies each field into a new string; a Span just narrows its view over the same original characters\"></p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-ty\">Span</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; fromArray = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>[<span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>];                 <span class=\"tk-com\">// array</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Span</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; part = fromArray.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Slice</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>);            <span class=\"tk-com\">// slice of it</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadOnlySpan</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">char</span>&gt; fromString = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;hello&quot;</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsSpan</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>);  <span class=\"tk-com\">// &quot;ell&quot;, no new string</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Span</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>&gt; onStack = <span class=\"tk-kw\">stackalloc</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>[<span class=\"tk-num\">256</span>];         <span class=\"tk-com\">// stack memory, no GC at all</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Span</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; listView = <span class=\"tk-ty\">CollectionsMarshal</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsSpan</span>(list);  <span class=\"tk-com\">// List&lt;T&gt;'s internal array</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice how varied the memory a span can point at is — an array, a slice of that array, a substring with no new allocation, raw stack memory via <code>stackalloc</code>, even <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code>&#39;s own hidden internal array via <code>CollectionsMarshal</code>. That&#39;s deliberate: <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> is a <em>uniform way to describe &quot;a contiguous chunk of memory,&quot;</em> regardless of where it came from. That&#39;s why the BCL exposes span overloads so widely: <code>int.TryParse(ReadOnlySpan&lt;char&gt;)</code>, <code>Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(ReadOnlySpan&lt;char&gt;, Span&lt;byte&gt;)</code>, <code>Stream.Read(Span&lt;byte&gt;)</code>, <code>string.Create</code>, and the whole <code>MemoryExtensions</code> family (<code>IndexOf</code>, <code>Trim</code>, <code>StartsWith</code>, <code>SequenceEqual</code>, <code>Contains</code>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"ref-structs-the-restriction-that-makes-stack-memory-safe\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/advanced-csharp/ref-structs-the-restriction-that-makes-stack-memory-safe\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>ref structs: the restriction that makes stack memory safe <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>That <code>stackalloc</code> line above raises an obvious question: if a <code>Span&lt;int&gt;</code> can point at raw <em>stack</em> memory, and stack frames disappear the instant their method returns, what stops you from holding onto a span after its underlying stack memory is already gone, and reading garbage?</p>\n<p>The answer: <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> is a <strong><code>ref struct</code></strong> — a value type the compiler only ever allows to live on the stack, no exceptions. Every other restriction on <code>ref struct</code> types follows from that one rule:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>A ref struct can&#39;t…</th>\n<th>Because…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Be a field of a class or normal struct</td>\n<td>That would put it on the heap</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Be boxed or cast to <code>object</code>/interface</td>\n<td>Boxing allocates on the heap</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Be captured by a lambda</td>\n<td>Closures live on the heap</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Be used across <code>await</code> or <code>yield</code></td>\n<td>The state machine lives on the heap</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Be an array element</td>\n<td>Arrays live on the heap</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Every row is the same answer, five times over: each operation would, one way or another, force the <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> onto the <strong>heap</strong>. <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/delegates-events/closures-when-a-lambda-outlives-the-method-it-was-written-in\">Delegates, lambdas and closures</a> showed exactly what happens when a lambda captures a local — the compiler moves it into a heap-allocated <code>DisplayClass</code>. If a <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> pointing at <code>stackalloc</code>&#39;d memory got moved there, the span would outlive the stack memory it points at, and reading through it afterward means reading freed, possibly reused memory — the kind of memory-safety hole C# normally makes structurally impossible. The <code>ref struct</code> restriction is the compiler refusing to let that situation arise at all.</p>\n<p>C# 13 relaxed this slightly, without reopening that hole: ref structs can now implement interfaces (without ever being boxed to do it) and appear as generic arguments, via the <code>where T : allows ref struct</code> constraint you met briefly in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/generics/constraints-telling-the-compiler-what-t-is-allowed-to-be\">Generics</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"memory-the-heap-friendly-cousin-for-exactly-the-cases-span-f\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/advanced-csharp/memory-the-heap-friendly-cousin-for-exactly-the-cases-span-f\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Memory<T>: the heap-friendly cousin, for exactly the cases Span<T> forbids <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Given that list of forbidden operations, what do you do when you need something span-like <strong>stored in a field</strong>, or used <strong>across an <code>await</code></strong> — both things <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> can never do? <strong><code>Memory&lt;T&gt;</code></strong> (and <code>ReadOnlyMemory&lt;T&gt;</code>) is the answer: an ordinary struct — not a <code>ref struct</code> — built from the same pieces (an array, an offset, a length), but without the stack-only restriction, because it&#39;s never allowed to point at <code>stackalloc</code>&#39;d memory in the first place.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadIntoAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Stream</span> s, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Memory</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>&gt; buffer, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> read = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> s.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadAsync</span>(buffer, ct);      <span class=\"tk-com\">// fine across await</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Checksum</span>(buffer.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Span</span>[..read]);          <span class=\"tk-com\">// drop to Span for synchronous processing</span>\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice the pattern in <code>ReadIntoAsync</code>: the parameter and everything crossing the <code>await</code> is <code>Memory&lt;byte&gt;</code>, but the moment we&#39;re back to purely synchronous code — <code>Checksum</code>, called right after the <code>await</code> completes — we call <code>.Span</code> to drop down to the faster <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> view. Rule of thumb: <strong>synchronous</strong> methods take <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code>/<code>ReadOnlySpan&lt;T&gt;</code>; <strong>async</strong> methods take <code>Memory&lt;T&gt;</code>/<code>ReadOnlyMemory&lt;T&gt;</code>, converting to a <code>Span</code> only for the synchronous stretches between awaits.</p>\n<h2 id=\"pooling-buffers-paying-the-allocation-cost-once-instead-of-e\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/advanced-csharp/pooling-buffers-paying-the-allocation-cost-once-instead-of-e\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Pooling buffers: paying the allocation cost once instead of every request <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/memory-gc/the-large-object-heap-and-the-pinned-object-heap\">Memory &amp; GC</a> already established that anything <strong>≥ 85 KB</strong> goes straight onto the Large Object Heap, and allocating there repeatedly leads to frequent, expensive full GCs. Allocating a large temporary buffer fresh on every request is exactly that pattern. <strong><code>ArrayPool&lt;T&gt;</code></strong> breaks it: rent a buffer when you need one, return it when done, and let the pool hand that same physical array to the next caller instead of the GC having to reclaim and re-allocate it.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>[] buffer = <span class=\"tk-ty\">ArrayPool</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>&gt;.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Shared</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Rent</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">64</span> * <span class=\"tk-num\">1024</span>);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// may return a LARGER array than requested</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> n = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> stream.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadAsync</span>(buffer.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsMemory</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">64</span> * <span class=\"tk-num\">1024</span>), ct);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Process</span>(buffer.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsSpan</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, n));                          <span class=\"tk-com\">// only use the part you asked for</span>\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">finally</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">ArrayPool</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>&gt;.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Shared</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Return</span>(buffer);                 <span class=\"tk-com\">// never use it after returning</span>\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Two details in that <code>finally</code> block are easy to get wrong. First, <code>Rent</code> may hand back an array <strong>larger</strong> than what you asked for — the pool deals in fixed bucket sizes, not exact matches — so slice down to the length you actually requested rather than trusting <code>.Length</code>. Second, and more dangerous: once you&#39;ve called <code>Return</code>, that array can be handed to a different caller at any moment, so using it afterward means reading or writing data that now belongs to someone else&#39;s operation.</p>\n<p>Related tools worth knowing by name for when <code>ArrayPool</code> alone isn&#39;t quite the right shape: <code>RecyclableMemoryStream</code> (Microsoft.IO) for pooled, growable memory streams; <code>ObjectPool&lt;T&gt;</code> (Microsoft.Extensions.ObjectPool, commonly used for pooling <code>StringBuilder</code> instances); <code>MemoryPool&lt;T&gt;</code>; <code>IBufferWriter&lt;T&gt;</code>/<code>ArrayBufferWriter&lt;T&gt;</code>; and <strong>System.IO.Pipelines</strong> (<code>PipeReader</code>/<code>PipeWriter</code>), the pooled-buffer abstraction Kestrel itself is built directly on top of.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ref-locals-ref-returns-in-and-scoped\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/advanced-csharp/ref-locals-ref-returns-in-and-scoped\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>ref locals, ref returns, in and scoped <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Return a reference into an array instead of a copy, so callers can modify in place</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">ref</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FindRef</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>[] orders, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; i &lt; orders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Length</span>; i++)\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (orders[i].<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> == id) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">ref</span> orders[i];\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">KeyNotFoundException</span>();\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">ref</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> o = <span class=\"tk-kw\">ref</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FindRef</span>(arr, <span class=\"tk-num\">7</span>);\no.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Shipped</span>;       <span class=\"tk-com\">// modifies the array element directly (useful for structs)</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>A normal method return hands back a <em>copy</em> — for <code>Order</code> as a <code>struct</code>, mutating <code>o</code> afterward would mutate a throwaway copy, touching nothing in <code>arr</code>. <code>ref Order FindRef(...)</code> changes that: it returns a genuine reference <em>into the array itself</em>, so <code>o.Status = Status.Shipped</code> writes straight back into <code>arr[i]</code>, no copy involved.</p>\n<p>The rest of this family exists for increasingly specialized control: <code>scoped</code> restricts how long a <code>ref</code> can live, so the compiler can <em>prove</em>, statically, that it never escapes its intended scope — a safety mechanism, not a performance one. <code>ref readonly</code> returns a reference the caller is guaranteed can&#39;t modify through. <code>Unsafe.As</code>, <code>MemoryMarshal.Cast</code> and <code>CollectionsMarshal</code> give expert-level control over memory representation. All four matter mainly inside library and infrastructure code — application-level backend code rarely needs any of them directly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unsafe-code-and-interop\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/advanced-csharp/unsafe-code-and-interop\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>unsafe code and interop <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// &lt;AllowUnsafeBlocks&gt;true&lt;/AllowUnsafeBlocks&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">unsafe</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> x = <span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>* p = &amp;x;\n    *p = <span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">fixed</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>* ptr = buffer) { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* pin a managed array so the GC won't move it */</span> }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Calling native code: P/Invoke with a source-generated marshaller (AOT-friendly)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">internal</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> partial <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Native</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[LibraryImport(&quot;libc&quot;, EntryPoint = &quot;getpid&quot;)]</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">internal</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> partial <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetPid</span>();\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Raw pointers step entirely outside the type-safety and bounds-checking guarantees the rest of this book takes for granted — the CLR&#39;s &quot;managed code&quot; promises from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/meanwhile-the-clr-is-doing-a-lot-more-than-just-this\">How code runs</a> don&#39;t apply inside an <code>unsafe</code> block. Reach for this only for genuine native interop, or a hot spot you&#39;ve actually measured and proven needs it — not as a general performance habit. <code>fixed</code> deserves its own callout: it <strong>pins</strong> a managed object, telling the GC &quot;don&#39;t move this one during compaction,&quot; which is necessary before handing its address to native code. The cost (see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/memory-gc/the-large-object-heap-and-the-pinned-object-heap\">Memory &amp; GC</a>) is that a long-lived pin sits immovably in the middle of the heap, forcing the GC to compact <em>around</em> it and fragmenting the heap over time.</p>\n<h2 id=\"reflection-reading-your-own-programs-metadata-from-inside-th\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/advanced-csharp/reflection-reading-your-own-programs-metadata-from-inside-th\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Reflection: reading your own program&#39;s metadata, from inside the program <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Reflection</strong> is code that reads an assembly&#39;s own metadata — covered back in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/what-build-time-actually-hands-you\">How code runs</a> as the structured description bundled into every compiled <code>.dll</code> — while the program is actually running, and can use that metadata to create instances and invoke members it only discovered by name, not by a compile-time reference.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-ty\">Type</span> t = <span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>);                                   <span class=\"tk-com\">// or obj.GetType(), or Type.GetType(&quot;Shop.Order&quot;)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">PropertyInfo</span> p <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> t.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetProperties</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">BindingFlags</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Public</span> | <span class=\"tk-ty\">BindingFlags</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Instance</span>))\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;{p.Name}: {p.PropertyType.Name}&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span> instance = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Activator</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateInstance</span>(t)!;\nt.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetProperty</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Status&quot;</span>)!.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SetValue</span>(instance, <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Paid</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> result = t.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetMethod</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Cancel&quot;</span>)!.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Invoke</span>(instance, <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Assembly scanning: how DI auto-registration and MediatR-style handler discovery work</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> handlerTypes = <span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Program</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Assembly</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetTypes</span>()\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(x =&gt; x <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsAbstract</span>: <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsInterface</span>: <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span> })\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">SelectMany</span>(x =&gt; x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetInterfaces</span>(), (impl, iface) =&gt; (impl, iface))\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(p =&gt; p.iface.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsGenericType</span> &amp;&amp; p.iface.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetGenericTypeDefinition</span>() == <span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IRequestHandler</span>&lt;,&gt;));</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>That last block is worth recognizing on sight: scanning <em>every type in an assembly</em>, filtering down to the ones implementing a particular generic interface, is exactly how DI auto-registration tools and MediatR-style handler discovery find every <code>IRequestHandler&lt;,&gt;</code> in your codebase without you registering each one by hand.</p>\n<p>This same capability powers a surprisingly large share of .NET: <strong>DI containers</strong> use it to find a type&#39;s constructor and parameters; <strong>serializers</strong> use it to read an object&#39;s properties; <strong>ASP.NET Core</strong> uses it to discover controllers and bind models; <strong>EF Core</strong> uses it to build its entity model; <strong>test frameworks</strong> use it to find every <code>[Fact]</code>-attributed method; <strong>AutoMapper-style mappers</strong> use it to match source and destination properties by name.</p>\n<p>That power has a real, measurable <strong>cost</strong>: reflective calls are slow compared to a direct method call, because they involve string-based lookups, security checks, and boxing every argument into an <code>object[]</code> to pass through a generic <code>Invoke</code>. They&#39;re allocation-heavy on top of that. They&#39;re <strong>invisible to the compiler</strong> — rename a property reflection looks up by string, and nothing breaks until that code actually runs and fails to find it. And reflection is <strong>hostile to trimming and Native AOT</strong> (see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/what-if-you-skipped-the-jit-completely\">How code runs</a> on trimming): a trimmer can&#39;t know a method accessed only by a runtime string lookup is still needed, and will happily remove it.</p>\n<p>Mitigations, roughly in order of how much they help: cache the <code>PropertyInfo</code>/<code>MethodInfo</code> once, rather than re-resolving it every call; compile an <strong>expression tree</strong> (covered at the end of this chapter) or use <code>Delegate.CreateDelegate</code> to turn a one-time reflective lookup into a fast, reusable delegate; reach for <code>[UnsafeAccessor]</code> — introduced in .NET 8, confirmed by its <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.runtime.compilerservices.unsafeaccessorattribute\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Microsoft Learn reference</a> — for private-member access that skips reflection&#39;s runtime lookup entirely (it resolves to a direct call at JIT time, though its generics support stayed limited until .NET 9); or, best for new code, use a <strong>source generator</strong> instead — the rest of this chapter builds toward that.</p>\n<h2 id=\"attributes-metadata-that-does-nothing-until-something-reads-\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/advanced-csharp/attributes-metadata-that-does-nothing-until-something-reads-\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Attributes: metadata that does nothing until something reads it <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Attributes</strong> attach declarative metadata directly to code — but, on their own, an attribute does <em>nothing at all</em>. It&#39;s inert until some other piece of code — the compiler, the runtime, or a framework, almost always via reflection or a dedicated metadata-reading path — specifically goes looking for it and acts on what it finds.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-attr\">[ApiController]</span>\n<span class=\"tk-attr\">[Route(&quot;api/orders&quot;)]</span>\n<span class=\"tk-attr\">[Authorize(Policy = &quot;CanReadOrders&quot;)]</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrdersController</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">ControllerBase</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[HttpGet(&quot;{id:int}&quot;)]</span>\n    [<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProducesResponseType</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderDto</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCodes</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status200OK</span>)]\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[ProducesResponseType(StatusCodes.Status404NotFound)]</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IActionResult</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Get</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>();\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateUser</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[Required, EmailAddress, MaxLength(256)]</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Email</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[JsonPropertyName(&quot;display_name&quot;)]</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>? <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Your own attribute</span>\n<span class=\"tk-attr\">[AttributeUsage(AttributeTargets.Method, AllowMultiple = false)]</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AuditAttribute</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> action) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Attribute</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Action</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } = action;\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Reading it (e.g. in an endpoint filter or middleware)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> audit = endpoint?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Metadata</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetMetadata</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuditAttribute</span>&gt;();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Read by</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Compiler</strong></td>\n<td><code>[Obsolete]</code>, <code>[CallerMemberName]</code>, <code>[NotNullWhen]</code>, <code>[SetsRequiredMembers]</code>, <code>[Experimental]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Runtime</strong></td>\n<td><code>[Serializable]</code>, <code>[StructLayout]</code>, <code>[MethodImpl(AggressiveInlining)]</code>, <code>[ThreadStatic]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Frameworks</strong></td>\n<td><code>[HttpGet]</code>, <code>[Authorize]</code>, <code>[FromBody]</code>, <code>[Required]</code>, <code>[Key]</code>, <code>[JsonPropertyName]</code>, <code>[Fact]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Source generators</strong></td>\n<td><code>[LoggerMessage]</code>, <code>[JsonSerializable]</code>, <code>[GeneratedRegex]</code>, <code>[LibraryImport]</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Notice how differently &quot;reader&quot; behaves across rows — the same <code>[Attribute]</code> syntax feeds four genuinely different consumers, each acting at a different stage: compile time, runtime, framework startup, or build-time code generation. In ASP.NET Core, endpoint attributes like <code>[Authorize]</code> get converted into <strong>endpoint metadata</strong> that middleware can read <em>after</em> routing has selected the endpoint — <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/routing\">Routing</a> covers how that connection works, and it&#39;s why middleware ordering matters so much for anything reading endpoint metadata.</p>\n<h2 id=\"source-generators-doing-reflections-job-but-at-build-time-in\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/advanced-csharp/source-generators-doing-reflections-job-but-at-build-time-in\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Source generators: doing reflection&#39;s job, but at build time instead of runtime <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>A <strong>source generator</strong> is a plugin that hooks into the Roslyn compiler itself, inspects your code <strong>during compilation</strong>, and <strong>adds new, real C# source files</strong> to the build. The crucial difference from reflection: the output is ordinary, compiled code — fast to run, debuggable like anything else you wrote, and, because it&#39;s genuinely present in the compiled output rather than discovered by a runtime string lookup, <strong>fully visible to the trimmer</strong>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// 1. Logging: generates an efficient, strongly-typed logging method</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> partial <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Log</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[LoggerMessage(EventId = 1001, Level = LogLevel.Information, Message = &quot;Order {OrderId} placed by {CustomerId}&quot;)]</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> partial <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">this</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ILogger</span> logger, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> orderId, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> customerId);\n}\nlogger.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span>(order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// no boxing, no template parsing at runtime, level checked first</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 2. JSON: serializer metadata generated at compile time (required for Native AOT)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-attr\">[JsonSerializable(typeof(OrderDto))]</span>\n<span class=\"tk-attr\">[JsonSerializable(typeof(List&lt;OrderDto&gt;))]</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">internal</span> partial <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopJsonContext</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">JsonSerializerContext</span> { }\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ConfigureHttpJsonOptions</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SerializerOptions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TypeInfoResolverChain</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Insert</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopJsonContext</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Default</span>));\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 3. Regex: compiled to C# at build time</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> partial <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Patterns</span>\n{\n    [<span class=\"tk-ty\">GeneratedRegex</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">@&quot;^[A-Z]{2}-\\d{4}$&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">RegexOptions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CultureInvariant</span>)]\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> partial <span class=\"tk-ty\">Regex</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span>();\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> ok = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Patterns</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsMatch</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;KB-1234&quot;</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice the shared shape across all three examples: each declares a <code>partial</code> method or class, with no body of its own, plus an attribute telling the generator <em>what</em> to produce. The generator writes the actual implementation into a separate, generated file, compiled alongside your own code as if you&#39;d written it by hand. <code>[LoggerMessage]</code> writes a logging method that parses its message template once, at build time, instead of re-parsing it on every call the way <code>logger.LogInformation($&quot;...&quot;)</code> effectively would. <code>[JsonSerializable]</code> pre-generates the type metadata <code>System.Text.Json</code> would otherwise need reflection to discover at runtime — which is why it&#39;s <strong>required</strong>, not just an optimization, for Native AOT. <code>[GeneratedRegex]</code> compiles a regex pattern into actual matching code at build time, instead of the runtime regex engine interpreting the pattern on first use.</p>\n<p>Beyond these three: configuration binding (<code>EnableConfigurationBindingGenerator</code>), minimal API request delegates (RDG), options validation (<code>[OptionsValidator]</code>), <code>[LibraryImport]</code> native interop, and Blazor/Razor compilation. Popular third-party ones include Mapperly (object-to-object mapping), Mediator (a source-generated alternative to reflection-based MediatR), Refit, StronglyTypedId and Vogen.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-why\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Why the whole .NET ecosystem is actively moving from reflection to generators</div><p>Reflection does its discovery work <strong>every time the app starts</strong> — slowing startup every time, for every process — and hides the code it touches from the trimmer, which is why reflection-heavy libraries tend to be Native-AOT-incompatible. Generators do the <em>same</em> discovery work <strong>once, at build time</strong>, and emit plain, ordinary code. The payoff: faster startup (nothing left to discover at runtime), lower allocations, errors caught at compile time instead of surfacing deep in production, and generated code that&#39;s fully visible to the trimmer — which is what makes the app AOT-compatible at all. When choosing between similar libraries, &quot;ships a source-generator mode&quot; is a real advantage, not a minor detail.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"expression-trees-for-metaprogramming-building-code-as-data-b\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/advanced-csharp/expression-trees-for-metaprogramming-building-code-as-data-b\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Expression trees for metaprogramming: building code as data, by hand <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/linq/expression-trees-what-ef-core-is-actually-reading\">LINQ</a> already showed expression trees from the consuming side — EF Core walking a tree the compiler built for you from a lambda. There&#39;s a second, less common use: you can <strong>build</strong> an expression tree yourself, by hand, from its individual pieces, then compile it into a real, runnable delegate at runtime. This is a classic technique inside object mappers and ORMs that generate fast property-access code for types they only discover dynamically.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Build: (Order o) =&gt; o.Total   then compile once and cache</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> param = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Expression</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parameter</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>), <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;o&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> body = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Expression</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Property</span>(param, <span class=\"tk-kw\">nameof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> getter = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Expression</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lambda</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>&gt;&gt;(body, param).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Compile</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> t = getter(order);      <span class=\"tk-com\">// as fast as a normal delegate call after compilation</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>This genuinely assembles a lambda from raw pieces: <code>Expression.Parameter</code> creates the <code>o</code> parameter node, <code>Expression.Property</code> builds the <code>o.Total</code> member-access node, and <code>Expression.Lambda&lt;...&gt;(...).Compile()</code> turns that whole tree into an actual, callable delegate — functionally equivalent to writing <code>(Order o) =&gt; o.Total</code> directly in source, just built programmatically at runtime.</p>\n<p>The catch: <code>.Compile()</code> itself is expensive — milliseconds, not microseconds — and isn&#39;t available at all under Native AOT, where it falls back to a slower interpreter instead of genuinely compiling. The practical rule: <strong>cache the compiled delegate</strong> rather than recompiling on every call, and for new code, prefer a source generator over hand-built expression trees wherever one is available — same &quot;fast, type-specific code&quot; outcome, without paying the runtime compilation cost.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is Span<T> and why is it useful?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A stack-only struct that represents a contiguous region of memory (array, string, stack or native) as a pointer plus length. Slicing creates views without copying, so parsing and formatting can run with zero allocations, reducing GC pressure in hot paths.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why can&#39;t Span<T> be used in async methods or stored in a class field?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>It&#39;s a ref struct that may point to stack memory, so it must never end up on the heap. Async methods and iterators store locals in a heap-allocated state machine, and class fields live on the heap, so both are forbidden. Use Memory&lt;T&gt; instead and call .Span when working synchronously.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Span<T> vs Memory<T>?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Span&lt;T&gt; is a ref struct: stack-only, fastest, usable in synchronous code. Memory&lt;T&gt; is a regular struct that can live on the heap, be stored in fields and cross awaits; you get a Span from it via .Span.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is ArrayPool and when would you use it?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A pool of reusable arrays (ArrayPool&lt;T&gt;.Shared). Rent large temporary buffers instead of allocating them per operation, which avoids LOH allocations and GC pressure, and return them in a finally block. Remember that Rent may return a larger array than requested.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is reflection and what are its downsides?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Runtime inspection and invocation of types and members via metadata. It powers DI, serializers, ORMs and test runners, but it&#39;s slow, allocates, bypasses compile-time checking, and isn&#39;t compatible with trimming/Native AOT unless annotated. Cache reflection results, compile delegates, or use source generators instead.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are attributes and who reads them?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Declarative metadata attached to code elements. They are passive and read by the compiler (Obsolete, nullable attributes), the runtime (StructLayout), frameworks via reflection or endpoint metadata (HttpGet, Authorize, Required), or source generators (LoggerMessage, JsonSerializable).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is a source generator and why are they replacing reflection?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A compiler plugin that analyses your code during the build and adds generated C# source. It moves work from runtime to compile time, giving faster startup, fewer allocations and compile-time errors, and the generated code is visible to the trimmer, enabling Native AOT. Examples: LoggerMessage, System.Text.Json source generation, GeneratedRegex.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What does the fixed statement do?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>It pins a managed object in memory for the duration of the block so the GC can&#39;t move it, letting you take a stable pointer to it (for unsafe code or native interop). Long or frequent pinning fragments the heap.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/advanced-csharp/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/memory-and-spans/memory-t-usage-guidelines\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Memory and Span usage guidelines</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/roslyn-sdk/source-generators-overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Source generators</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/fundamentals/reflection/reflection\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Reflection in .NET</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.runtime.compilerservices.unsafeaccessorattribute\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">UnsafeAccessorAttribute class</a></li>\n<li>Stephen Toub&#39;s annual <em>Performance Improvements in .NET N</em> blog posts, which show these techniques used throughout the BCL.</li>\n<li>Book: <em>Pro .NET Memory Management</em> by Konrad Kokosa.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/advanced-csharp/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Save as <code>Program.cs</code> in a new console project (<code>dotnet new console</code>) and run with <code>dotnet run</code>. It checks that slicing a <code>Span</code> is a view (not a copy), <code>stackalloc</code>, <code>Memory&lt;T&gt;</code> crossing an <code>await</code>, <code>ArrayPool</code> sizing, <code>ref</code> returns, reflection (property discovery, construction, invocation), reading a custom attribute, and a hand-built expression tree.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Buffers</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Linq</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Expressions</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Reflection</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> failures = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> claim, <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> ok)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (ok) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: {claim}&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">else</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: {claim}&quot;</span>); failures++; }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 1. Slicing a Span is a VIEW, not a copy: mutating through the slice mutates the original array</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>[] array = [<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">4</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>];\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Span</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; slice = array.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsSpan</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>);\nslice[<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>] = <span class=\"tk-num\">99</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;mutating a Span slice writes through to the original array (it's a view, not a copy)&quot;</span>, array[<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>] == <span class=\"tk-num\">99</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 2. stackalloc gives real, usable stack memory</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Span</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>&gt; onStack = <span class=\"tk-kw\">stackalloc</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>[<span class=\"tk-num\">4</span>];\nonStack[<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>] = <span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;stackalloc produces a Span you can read and write&quot;</span>, onStack[<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>] == <span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 3. Memory&lt;T&gt; can cross an await; .Span gives the fast synchronous view</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">SumViaMemoryAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Memory</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; buffer)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Yield</span>();               <span class=\"tk-com\">// crossing an await — Memory&lt;T&gt; survives this, Span&lt;T&gt; couldn't be a local here</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> total = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> v <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> buffer.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Span</span>) total += v;   <span class=\"tk-com\">// drop to Span for the synchronous part</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> total;\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> sum = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SumViaMemoryAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>[] { <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span> });\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Memory&lt;T&gt; can be used across an await and converted to Span for synchronous work&quot;</span>, sum == <span class=\"tk-num\">6</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 4. ArrayPool: Rent may return an array &gt;= requested size; Return lets it be reused</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>[] rented = <span class=\"tk-ty\">ArrayPool</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>&gt;.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Shared</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Rent</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1000</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> bigEnough = rented.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Length</span> &gt;= <span class=\"tk-num\">1000</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">ArrayPool</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>&gt;.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Shared</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Return</span>(rented);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ArrayPool&lt;T&gt;.Shared.Rent returns an array at least as large as requested&quot;</span>, bigEnough);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 5. ref return: writing through a ref return modifies the original array element</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>[] numbers = [<span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">30</span>];\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">ref</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FindRef</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>[] arr, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; i &lt; arr.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Length</span>; i++) <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (arr[i] == <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">ref</span> arr[i];\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">KeyNotFoundException</span>();\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">ref</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> found = <span class=\"tk-kw\">ref</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FindRef</span>(numbers, <span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>);\nfound = <span class=\"tk-num\">999</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a ref return lets you modify the original array element, not a copy&quot;</span>, numbers[<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>] == <span class=\"tk-num\">999</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 6. Reflection: discover properties, create an instance, set a property, invoke a method</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Type</span> t = <span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> propertyNames = t.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetProperties</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">BindingFlags</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Public</span> | <span class=\"tk-ty\">BindingFlags</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Instance</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToHashSet</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;reflection discovers a type's public properties by name&quot;</span>, propertyNames.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contains</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Total&quot;</span>) &amp;&amp; propertyNames.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contains</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Status&quot;</span>));\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span> instance = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Activator</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateInstance</span>(t)!;\nt.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetProperty</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Status&quot;</span>)!.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SetValue</span>(instance, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Paid&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> afterInvoke = t.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetMethod</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Describe&quot;</span>)!.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Invoke</span>(instance, <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;reflection can construct an instance, set a property, and invoke a method by name&quot;</span>,\n    (<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>)afterInvoke! == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Order: Paid&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 7. Attributes are inert until something reads them via reflection</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> method = <span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetMethod</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Describe&quot;</span>)!;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> audit = method.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetCustomAttribute</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuditAttribute</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a custom attribute attached to a method can be read back via reflection&quot;</span>, audit?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Action</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;describe&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 8. Hand-built expression tree compiles into a working delegate</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> param = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Expression</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parameter</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>), <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;o&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> body = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Expression</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Property</span>(param, <span class=\"tk-kw\">nameof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> getter = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Expression</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lambda</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;&gt;(body, param).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Compile</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> orderForTree = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shipped&quot;</span> };\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a hand-built Expression&lt;Func&lt;T,TResult&gt;&gt; compiles into a delegate that reads the right property&quot;</span>,\n    getter(orderForTree) == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shipped&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(failures == <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span> ? <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;\\nAll checks passed.&quot;</span> : <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;\\n{failures} check(s) failed.&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (failures &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exit</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;New&quot;</span>;\n\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[Audit(&quot;describe&quot;)]</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Describe</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;Order: {Status}&quot;</span>;\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-attr\">[AttributeUsage(AttributeTargets.Method)]</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AuditAttribute</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> action) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Attribute</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Action</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } = action;\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Not runtime-verifiable here: <code>ref struct</code>/stack-only enforcement is a <em>compile-time</em> restriction (try putting a <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code> in a class field yourself to see the compiler reject it); raw pointer/<code>unsafe</code> code needs <code>&lt;AllowUnsafeBlocks&gt;true&lt;/AllowUnsafeBlocks&gt;</code> in the project file, which this single-file script doesn&#39;t set; and source generators ([LoggerMessage], [JsonSerializable], [GeneratedRegex]) only run as part of a real build with the generator package referenced — their output can be inspected in <code>obj/generated</code> after building a project that uses them, but not from a standalone script. See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/advanced-csharp/further-reading\">Further reading</a> for all three.</p>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"why-a-web-framework-needs-tools-this-low-level","text":"Why a web framework needs tools this low-level","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"span-a-view-not-a-copy","text":"Span : a view, not a copy","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"ref-structs-the-restriction-that-makes-stack-memory-safe","text":"ref structs: the restriction that makes stack memory safe","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"memory-the-heap-friendly-cousin-for-exactly-the-cases-span-f","text":"Memory : the heap-friendly cousin, for exactly the cases Span forbids","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"pooling-buffers-paying-the-allocation-cost-once-instead-of-e","text":"Pooling buffers: paying the allocation cost once instead of every request","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"ref-locals-ref-returns-in-and-scoped","text":"ref locals, ref returns, in and scoped","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"unsafe-code-and-interop","text":"unsafe code and interop","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"reflection-reading-your-own-programs-metadata-from-inside-th","text":"Reflection: reading your own program's metadata, from inside the program","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"attributes-metadata-that-does-nothing-until-something-reads-","text":"Attributes: metadata that does nothing until something reads it","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"source-generators-doing-reflections-job-but-at-build-time-in","text":"Source generators: doing reflection's job, but at build time instead of runtime","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"expression-trees-for-metaprogramming-building-code-as-data-b","text":"Expression trees for metaprogramming: building code as data, by hand","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":5096,"minutes":23,"num":"2.10","part":"p2"},"aspnet-hosting":{"id":"aspnet-hosting","title":"ASP.NET Core Architecture and Hosting","summary":"What ASP.NET Core is made of, the Generic Host and WebApplication, the two phases of Program.cs, Kestrel and reverse proxies, IIS and in-process hosting, environments, the application lifetime and graceful shutdown.","layer":"framework","where":"ASP.NET Core › Hosting › Program.cs, Host & Kestrel","fits":"<p>This is the entry point of layer 3. The <strong>host</strong> is the object that owns the three pieces of plumbing every other ASP.NET Core chapter uses (DI, configuration and logging) and runs the web server. If you understand this chapter, <code>Program.cs</code> becomes a readable table of contents for the whole application.</p>\n","prereqs":["big-picture","delegates-events","async-await"],"next":["dependency-injection","configuration-options","middleware"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>ASP.NET Core is just a console app with <strong>Kestrel</strong> (a built-in web server) baked in — no separate IIS/Apache process required.</li>\n<li><code>Program.cs</code> has two phases: register services/config <em>before</em> <code>builder.Build()</code>, configure the request pipeline <em>after</em> it. Once built, you can&#39;t add more DI registrations.</li>\n<li>Production traffic almost always goes through a reverse proxy in front of Kestrel (for TLS, load balancing, a WAF). You must explicitly trust forwarded headers, or the client&#39;s real IP/scheme gets scrambled.</li>\n<li>The environment name (<code>ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT</code>) should only ever change <em>configuration</em>, never which code path runs.</li>\n<li>Shutdown is graceful: Kestrel stops accepting new requests, finishes in-flight ones, then hosted services get a chance to stop — all within a time budget.</li>\n<li><code>HttpContext</code> carries everything about one request, including <code>RequestServices</code> (that request&#39;s DI scope). It&#39;s not thread-safe and dies with the request.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"a-surprising-fact-to-start-from\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting/a-surprising-fact-to-start-from\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>A surprising fact to start from <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Here&#39;s something worth sitting with before anything else in this chapter: <strong>ASP.NET Core isn&#39;t a server you deploy your code into.</strong> There&#39;s no separate &quot;IIS&quot; or &quot;Apache&quot; process hosting your app from the outside, the way classic ASP.NET or PHP worked. <strong>ASP.NET Core is a set of libraries</strong> — the <code>Microsoft.AspNetCore.App</code> shared framework — that your code references, and your app is, underneath it all, an ordinary console program. One that happens to contain its own complete web server, listening on a socket, running inside the exact same process as your business logic.</p>\n<p>That single fact explains a lot of what follows in this chapter: why <code>Program.cs</code> looks like it&#39;s both &quot;configuring services&quot; and &quot;starting a server&quot; in the same file, why you can set a breakpoint anywhere in a request handler and step straight through into framework code with no process boundary in the way, and why the whole thing can be packaged into one small container image with nothing else installed.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>┌──────────────────────────── Your ASP.NET Core application ────────────────────────────┐\n│                                                                                        │\n│   Program.cs ─▶ WebApplicationBuilder ─▶ WebApplication (the host)                    │\n│                     │                         │                                       │\n│        ┌────────────┼──────────────┐          ├── Kestrel web server (listens on ports) │\n│        ▼            ▼              ▼          ├── Middleware pipeline                   │\n│   Configuration   Logging   DI container      └── Endpoints (routing)                   │\n│   (IConfiguration)(ILogger) (IServiceProvider)        ├── Minimal APIs                  │\n│                                                        ├── Controllers (MVC)            │\n│                                                        ├── Razor Pages / Blazor         │\n│                                                        ├── SignalR hubs / gRPC services │\n│                                                        └── Health checks …              │\n│   Hosted services (BackgroundService) run alongside the web server                    │\n└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</code></pre></figure>\n<h2 id=\"the-generic-host-the-thing-that-owns-everything-else\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting/the-generic-host-the-thing-that-owns-everything-else\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The Generic Host: the thing that owns everything else <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Before there&#39;s a web server, routing, or anything web-specific at all, there&#39;s a <strong>host</strong> — an object whose entire job is owning an app&#39;s shared resources and its lifetime:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the <strong>DI container</strong> (<code>IServiceProvider</code>),</li>\n<li><strong>configuration</strong> (<code>IConfiguration</code>),</li>\n<li><strong>logging</strong> (<code>ILoggerFactory</code>),</li>\n<li>the <strong>environment</strong> (<code>IHostEnvironment</code>: name, content root),</li>\n<li><strong>hosted services</strong> (<code>IHostedService</code>), which it starts and stops — and, as you&#39;ll see shortly, the web server itself is just one of these,</li>\n<li><strong>lifetime</strong> management (<code>IHostApplicationLifetime</code>) and graceful shutdown.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This &quot;Generic Host&quot; (<code>Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting</code>) isn&#39;t actually web-specific at all — it&#39;s the same host a <strong>Worker Service</strong> uses, with no HTTP involved anywhere:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"Ly8gQSB3b3JrZXIgc2VydmljZTogc2FtZSBob3N0LCBzYW1lIERJL2NvbmZpZy9sb2dnaW5nLCBubyBIVFRQCnZhciBidWlsZGVyID0gSG9zdC5DcmVhdGVBcHBsaWNhdGlvbkJ1aWxkZXIoYXJncyk7CmJ1aWxkZXIuU2VydmljZXMuQWRkSG9zdGVkU2VydmljZTxRdWV1ZVByb2Nlc3Nvcj4oKTsKYnVpbGRlci5CdWlsZCgpLlJ1bigpOw==\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// A worker service: same host, same DI/config/logging, no HTTP</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> builder = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Host</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateApplicationBuilder</span>(args);\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddHostedService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">QueueProcessor</span>&gt;();\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Run</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>ASP.NET Core doesn&#39;t replace this host with something different — it <strong>adds the web-specific parts on top</strong> of the exact same foundation. Since .NET 6, <strong><code>WebApplication</code></strong> is the simplified API for that combination (the &quot;minimal hosting model&quot;): it&#39;s simultaneously an <code>IHost</code> <em>and</em> the object you configure your middleware pipeline and endpoints through.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-fits\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Generic Host vs WebApplication vs Startup.cs — recognising older code</div><ul>\n<li><strong>.NET Core 2.x–5:</strong> <code>Program.cs</code> built a host with <code>Host.CreateDefaultBuilder().ConfigureWebHostDefaults(...)</code>, and a separate <strong><code>Startup</code></strong> class had <code>ConfigureServices(IServiceCollection)</code> and <code>Configure(IApplicationBuilder)</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>.NET 6+:</strong> <code>WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args)</code>, all in <code>Program.cs</code>. Same two phases, less ceremony.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When you read older code, <code>ConfigureServices</code> = phase 1 and <code>Configure</code> = phase 2 below — the two-phase shape hasn&#39;t actually changed, only the syntax wrapping it has.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"the-two-phases-of-program-cs-and-why-the-split-matters\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting/the-two-phases-of-program-cs-and-why-the-split-matters\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The two phases of Program.cs, and why the split matters <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> builder = <span class=\"tk-ty\">WebApplication</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateBuilder</span>(args);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// ═════════ PHASE 1: describe services and configuration (nothing runs yet) ═════════</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// builder.Configuration  → IConfigurationManager (sources already loaded: json, env vars, args)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// builder.Logging        → ILoggingBuilder</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// builder.Services       → IServiceCollection (the DI registrations &quot;recipe list&quot;)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// builder.Environment    → IWebHostEnvironment</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// builder.WebHost        → Kestrel / web host settings</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// builder.Host           → generic host settings</span>\n\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddProblemDetails</span>();\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddOpenApi</span>();\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAuthentication</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddJwtBearer</span>();\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAuthorization</span>();\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddDbContext</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>&gt;(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseSqlServer</span>(builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configuration</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetConnectionString</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop&quot;</span>)));\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddOrdering</span>();                 <span class=\"tk-com\">// your own extension method per feature/layer</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> app = builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();                      <span class=\"tk-com\">// ← the DI container is BUILT here and locked</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// ═════════ PHASE 2: configure the request pipeline (order matters!) ═════════</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsDevelopment</span>())\n    app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapOpenApi</span>();\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseExceptionHandler</span>();\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseHttpsRedirection</span>();\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseAuthentication</span>();\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseAuthorization</span>();\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/health&quot;</span>, () =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>());\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapOrderEndpoints</span>();\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Run</span>();                                      <span class=\"tk-com\">// ← starts Kestrel + hosted services, blocks until shutdown</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice <code>var app = builder.Build();</code> sitting almost exactly in the middle — that one line is a genuine, hard boundary, not just a visual one, and understanding <em>why</em> explains most of the &quot;gotchas&quot; later in this section. <strong>Phase 1</strong>, everything before <code>Build()</code>, is purely declarative: you&#39;re handing the builder a recipe list — &quot;when something needs an <code>IOrderRepository</code>, give it an <code>EfOrderRepository</code>&quot; — and nothing is actually constructed yet. <strong>Phase 2</strong>, everything after, is where the actual request pipeline gets assembled, in the exact order you write it, because that order becomes the literal order requests flow through later.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Phase</th>\n<th>Object</th>\n<th>You do</th>\n<th>Chapter</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code>builder.Services</code></td>\n<td>Register services and their lifetimes</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/dependency-injection\">DI</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code>builder.Configuration</code></td>\n<td>Add config sources, bind options</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/configuration-options\">Configuration</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><code>builder.Logging</code></td>\n<td>Configure providers and levels</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/errors-logging\">Errors &amp; logging</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>—</td>\n<td><code>builder.Build()</code></td>\n<td>Creates the <code>IServiceProvider</code>; registrations frozen</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code>app.Use…()</code></td>\n<td>Add middleware, <strong>in order</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/middleware\">Middleware</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><code>app.Map…()</code></td>\n<td>Define endpoints</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/routing\">Routing</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/building-apis\">Building APIs</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>—</td>\n<td><code>app.Run()</code></td>\n<td>Start the server</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Three mistakes that fall directly out of misunderstanding the phase boundary</div><ul>\n<li>Calling <code>builder.Services.Add…</code> <strong>after</strong> <code>Build()</code> throws, because the moment the container is built, the recipe list is frozen — there&#39;s no &quot;add one more registration&quot; operation on an already-constructed <code>IServiceProvider</code>.</li>\n<li>Resolving a service in phase 1 by calling <code>builder.Services.BuildServiceProvider()</code> doesn&#39;t give you early access to the real container — it silently builds a <strong>second, entirely separate container</strong>, so any singleton you touch through it gets constructed twice, once in each container, with two independent instances quietly diverging from that point on. Analyzer ASP0000 specifically warns about this.</li>\n<li>Getting the <strong>order</strong> wrong in phase 2 produces no error at all, just silently wrong behaviour — placing <code>UseAuthorization</code> before <code>UseAuthentication</code>, for instance, means every single request looks anonymous, with nothing in the compiler or the running app to tell you why.</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<h3 id=\"what-createbuilder-quietly-sets-up-for-you\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting/what-createbuilder-quietly-sets-up-for-you\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What CreateBuilder quietly sets up for you</h3>\n<p><code>WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args)</code> isn&#39;t a blank slate — it arrives with a set of sensible defaults already wired in, worth knowing explicitly rather than discovering by accident:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Content root</strong> = current directory; <strong>web root</strong> = <code>wwwroot</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Configuration</strong> sources: <code>appsettings.json</code> → <code>appsettings.{Environment}.json</code> → User Secrets (Development) → environment variables → command-line args.</li>\n<li><strong>Logging</strong> providers: Console, Debug, EventSource (and EventLog on Windows), configured from the <code>Logging</code> config section.</li>\n<li><strong>Kestrel</strong> as the server, configured from the <code>Kestrel</code> config section.</li>\n<li><strong>DI validation</strong> (<code>ValidateScopes</code>, <code>ValidateOnBuild</code>) in Development — this is precisely the mechanism <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/the-captive-dependency-the-single-most-common-di-bug\">Dependency injection</a> relies on to catch a captive dependency at startup rather than letting it surface as a confusing runtime bug.</li>\n<li>Routing, plus a developer exception page in Development (implicitly).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Two variants exist for when you want fewer of these defaults: <code>WebApplication.CreateSlimBuilder(args)</code> trims the defaults down for a smaller, AOT-friendly footprint, and <code>CreateEmptyBuilder</code> gives you essentially nothing pre-configured at all.</p>\n<h2 id=\"kestrel-the-web-server-thats-actually-inside-your-process\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting/kestrel-the-web-server-thats-actually-inside-your-process\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Kestrel: the web server that&#39;s actually inside your process <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Kestrel</strong> is the cross-platform HTTP server mentioned at the very start of this chapter — the thing that makes &quot;your app is a console program containing its own web server&quot; literally true. It:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>listens on sockets (TCP; QUIC for HTTP/3),</li>\n<li>handles <strong>TLS</strong>,</li>\n<li>parses <strong>HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2 and HTTP/3</strong>,</li>\n<li>builds an <code>HttpContext</code> for each request and passes it to your middleware pipeline,</li>\n<li>is built on <code>System.IO.Pipelines</code> — the same pooled-buffer I/O abstraction mentioned in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/advanced-csharp/pooling-buffers-paying-the-allocation-cost-once-instead-of-e\">Advanced C#</a> — for high throughput with minimal per-request allocation.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WebHost</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ConfigureKestrel</span>(k =&gt;\n{\n    k.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Limits</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MaxRequestBodySize</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">10</span> * <span class=\"tk-num\">1024</span> * <span class=\"tk-num\">1024</span>;     <span class=\"tk-com\">// 10 MB</span>\n    k.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Limits</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequestHeadersTimeout</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSeconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">30</span>);\n    k.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddServerHeader</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;                          <span class=\"tk-com\">// don't advertise &quot;Server: Kestrel&quot;</span>\n});</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>JSON</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// or in appsettings.json</span>\n<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Kestrel&quot;</span>: {\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Endpoints&quot;</span>: {\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Http&quot;</span>:  { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Url&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;http://0.0.0.0:8080&quot;</span> },\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Https&quot;</span>: { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Url&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://0.0.0.0:8443&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Certificate&quot;</span>: { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Path&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;cert.pfx&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Password&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;…&quot;</span> } }\n  }\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<p>The listening URL itself can come from several different places, in practice usually chosen by whichever hosting environment you&#39;re actually deploying to: <code>ASPNETCORE_URLS</code>, <code>ASPNETCORE_HTTP_PORTS</code> (defaulting to <strong>8080</strong> in the official .NET 8+ container images), <code>--urls</code> on the command line, or <code>launchSettings.json</code> specifically in development.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-production-traffic-rarely-hits-kestrel-directly\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting/why-production-traffic-rarely-hits-kestrel-directly\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Why production traffic rarely hits Kestrel directly <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Given that Kestrel is a genuinely capable, hardened web server on its own, why does nearly every production deployment still put something in front of it?</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Internet ─▶ [ CDN / WAF ] ─▶ [ Reverse proxy / load balancer ] ─▶ Kestrel (instance 1)\n                              Azure Front Door, App Gateway,     ─▶ Kestrel (instance 2)\n                              Nginx, YARP, IIS, K8s ingress       ─▶ Kestrel (instance 3)</code></pre></figure>\n<p>The proxy isn&#39;t compensating for a Kestrel weakness — it&#39;s <strong>centralising</strong> concerns that would otherwise have to be duplicated across every single instance of your app: terminating TLS once instead of in each process, balancing load across instances, routing many different apps behind one public IP, caching, adding a WAF, and enforcing request rate limits at the edge before traffic ever reaches your code at all.</p>\n<p>Putting something in front of Kestrel does have one real consequence. As far as the TCP connection is concerned, the proxy <em>is</em> the client — so every request now appears to come from the <strong>proxy&#39;s</strong> IP address and scheme, not the real visitor&#39;s. The proxy compensates by adding <code>X-Forwarded-For</code>, <code>X-Forwarded-Proto</code> and <code>X-Forwarded-Host</code> headers carrying the <em>real</em> client&#39;s information. But your app has to explicitly <strong>opt in</strong> to trusting those headers. Skip that, and <code>HttpContext.Connection.RemoteIpAddress</code>, HTTPS redirection, and any URL your app generates will all be subtly wrong — reflecting the proxy&#39;s identity instead of the real client&#39;s:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configure</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ForwardedHeadersOptions</span>&gt;(o =&gt;\n{\n    o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ForwardedHeaders</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">ForwardedHeaders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">XForwardedFor</span> | <span class=\"tk-ty\">ForwardedHeaders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">XForwardedProto</span>;\n    o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">KnownProxies</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IPAddress</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parse</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;10.0.0.4&quot;</span>));    <span class=\"tk-com\">// only trust your proxy</span>\n});\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseForwardedHeaders</span>();                               <span class=\"tk-com\">// FIRST in the pipeline</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>That <code>KnownProxies</code> line matters more than it looks. Without it, any client could forge their own <code>X-Forwarded-For</code> header and have your app believe whatever IP they claimed. The mechanism only stays trustworthy if you only honour it when it comes from a proxy you actually control. (Azure App Service and many managed hosts already enable this correctly for you — worth knowing, so you don&#39;t accidentally configure it twice.)</p>\n<p><strong>YARP</strong> (&quot;Yet Another Reverse Proxy&quot;) is Microsoft&#39;s own reverse-proxy library, built on ASP.NET Core itself, commonly reached for specifically for API gateways and the BFF pattern — a reverse proxy written in the same stack and language as the apps it&#39;s proxying to.</p>\n<h2 id=\"iis-hosting-on-windows\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting/iis-hosting-on-windows\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>IIS hosting on Windows <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>On Windows servers specifically, IIS can still host ASP.NET Core, via the <strong>ASP.NET Core Module (ANCM)</strong>, in one of two genuinely different shapes:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Model</th>\n<th>How</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>In-process</strong> (default)</td>\n<td>The app runs <strong>inside</strong> the IIS worker process (<code>w3wp.exe</code>) using the IIS HTTP server instead of Kestrel</td>\n<td>Fastest on IIS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Out-of-process</strong></td>\n<td>IIS forwards requests to a separate Kestrel process</td>\n<td>IIS acts as a reverse proxy</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>A <code>web.config</code> is generated at publish time, but only to configure IIS itself — it is <strong>not</strong> used for application configuration the way it was for classic ASP.NET, so don&#39;t go looking for connection strings in it. With Linux containers now the dominant production deployment target, IIS is largely irrelevant for new cloud-native work, mattering mainly for on-premises Windows deployments.</p>\n<h2 id=\"environments-the-one-variable-thats-meant-to-change-configur\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting/environments-the-one-variable-thats-meant-to-change-configur\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Environments: the one variable that&#39;s meant to change configuration, never code paths <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>The environment name comes from <strong><code>ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT</code></strong> (or <code>DOTNET_ENVIRONMENT</code>), and if nobody sets it, it defaults to <code>Production</code> — a deliberate, safe default: an unconfigured deployment fails toward hiding implementation details, not toward leaking them.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Name</th>\n<th>Typical behaviour</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Development</code></td>\n<td>Developer exception page, User Secrets loaded, DI scope validation, detailed errors, OpenAPI UI</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Staging</code></td>\n<td>Production-like settings, test data/services</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Production</code></td>\n<td>Generic error responses, HSTS, real secrets from Key Vault, minimal logging noise</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"aWYgKGFwcC5FbnZpcm9ubWVudC5Jc0RldmVsb3BtZW50KCkpIHsgYXBwLk1hcE9wZW5BcGkoKTsgYXBwLk1hcFNjYWxhckFwaVJlZmVyZW5jZSgpOyB9CmlmICghYXBwLkVudmlyb25tZW50LklzRGV2ZWxvcG1lbnQoKSkgYXBwLlVzZUhzdHMoKTsKaWYgKGFwcC5FbnZpcm9ubWVudC5Jc0Vudmlyb25tZW50KCJMb2NhbCIpKSB7IC8qIGN1c3RvbSBuYW1lcyB3b3JrIHRvbyAqLyB9\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsDevelopment</span>()) { app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapOpenApi</span>(); app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapScalarApiReference</span>(); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsDevelopment</span>()) app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseHsts</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsEnvironment</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Local&quot;</span>)) { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* custom names work too */</span> }</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Per-environment <em>settings</em> belong in <code>appsettings.{Environment}.json</code> (see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/configuration-options\">Configuration</a>). There&#39;s a discipline worth holding onto here, easy to drift away from under deadline pressure: the environment should change <strong>configuration values</strong>, never <strong>which code path runs</strong>. <code>if (app.Environment.IsProduction())</code> sprinkled through business logic means you&#39;re no longer testing the same code in staging that will actually run in production — exactly the &quot;it works on my machine&quot; problem the whole container-based deployment model in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/docker\">Docker</a> exists to eliminate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"application-lifetime-and-graceful-shutdown-what-actually-hap\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting/application-lifetime-and-graceful-shutdown-what-actually-hap\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Application lifetime and graceful shutdown: what actually happens, in order <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><code>app.Run()</code> isn&#39;t just &quot;start listening&quot; — it drives the entire lifecycle of the process, start to finish, in a specific, predictable sequence:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Build the container.</li>\n<li>Start each <code>IHostedService</code> in registration order (<code>StartAsync</code>). The web server is one of them, so background services registered <em>before</em> it in <code>Program.cs</code> genuinely start first.</li>\n<li>Kestrel starts listening; <code>IHostApplicationLifetime.ApplicationStarted</code> fires.</li>\n<li>… the app runs …</li>\n<li>On <strong>SIGTERM</strong> — <code>docker stop</code>, a Kubernetes pod being terminated, an App Service restart — or Ctrl+C, shutdown begins. <code>ApplicationStopping</code> fires. Kestrel <strong>stops accepting</strong> new connections but lets in-flight requests finish. Hosted services get <code>StopAsync</code> called, with their <code>stoppingToken</code> cancelled. All of this has to happen within <strong><code>ShutdownTimeout</code></strong> (30 s by default).</li>\n<li><code>ApplicationStopped</code> fires; the container is disposed (disposing singletons).</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configure</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">HostOptions</span>&gt;(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShutdownTimeout</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSeconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">45</span>));\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> lifetime = app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IHostApplicationLifetime</span>&gt;();\nlifetime.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ApplicationStopping</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Register</span>(() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Log</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Information</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Draining…&quot;</span>));</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout c-why\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Why &quot;graceful&quot; shutdown is worth real engineering effort, not just a nice-to-have</div><p>In Kubernetes or Container Apps, instances get replaced routinely — on every deployment, during every scale-in event — as normal operation, not failure. Without graceful shutdown, each of those routine replacements drops whatever requests were in flight at that moment (502s to real users) and kills background jobs mid-work with no chance to clean up. Three practical habits fix this: honour <code>stoppingToken</code> in every background service so it actually notices a shutdown request; keep your own shutdown work comfortably under the platform&#39;s grace period instead of racing it; and make work genuinely <strong>idempotent</strong> (see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events/idempotent-consumers-and-the-inbox\">Messaging &amp; events</a>) so a job interrupted mid-way and retried from scratch doesn&#39;t double-charge a card or double-send an email.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"httpcontext-the-one-object-your-entire-pipeline-shares\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting/httpcontext-the-one-object-your-entire-pipeline-shares\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>HttpContext: the one object your entire pipeline shares <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Every single request gets exactly one <strong><code>HttpContext</code></strong> — the object essentially every chapter in the rest of Part III assumes you already know the shape of:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Member</th>\n<th>Contains</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Request</code></td>\n<td>Method, Path, Query, Headers, Body (stream), Cookies, <code>ReadFromJsonAsync&lt;T&gt;()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Response</code></td>\n<td>StatusCode, Headers, Body (stream), <code>WriteAsJsonAsync()</code>, <code>OnStarting</code>/<code>OnCompleted</code> callbacks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>User</code></td>\n<td>The <code>ClaimsPrincipal</code> set by authentication</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>RequestServices</code></td>\n<td>The <strong>request-scoped</strong> <code>IServiceProvider</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Items</code></td>\n<td>A per-request dictionary for sharing data between middleware</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Features</code></td>\n<td>Low-level server features (<code>IHttpRequestFeature</code>, <code>IEndpointFeature</code>…)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Connection</code></td>\n<td>Remote IP, port, client certificate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>RequestAborted</code></td>\n<td><code>CancellationToken</code> cancelled if the client disconnects</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>TraceIdentifier</code></td>\n<td>A unique ID for the request (used in ProblemDetails/logs)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>GetEndpoint()</code></td>\n<td>The endpoint chosen by routing (with its metadata)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>RequestServices</code> deserves a second look specifically because it&#39;s the concrete answer to a question <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/scopes-made-concrete\">Dependency injection</a> raised abstractly: <em>where</em> does ASP.NET Core&#39;s &quot;one scope per request&quot; actually live? Right here — <code>HttpContext.RequestServices</code> <em>is</em> that request&#39;s DI scope, created by middleware early in the pipeline, and everything resolved anywhere during this request ultimately traces back to it.</p>\n<p>Two rules worth holding firmly: <code>HttpContext</code> is <strong>not thread-safe</strong>, and it&#39;s <strong>only valid for the duration of the request it belongs to</strong>. To reach it from a service rather than directly in a handler, inject <code>IHttpContextAccessor</code> — sparingly, since it&#39;s usually a sign you could instead just pass what you actually need explicitly — and never, under any circumstance, capture an <code>HttpContext</code> reference inside background work that might still be running after the request has already finished; by the time that background code runs, the context it captured may already be invalid.</p>\n<h2 id=\"hosting-models-summary\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting/hosting-models-summary\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Hosting models summary <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Host</th>\n<th>Server</th>\n<th>Typical use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Self-hosted Kestrel in a container</td>\n<td>Kestrel</td>\n<td>Kubernetes, Container Apps, App Service for Containers (most common)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Azure App Service (code deploy, Linux)</td>\n<td>Kestrel behind the App Service front ends</td>\n<td>PaaS web apps</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IIS in-process</td>\n<td>IIS HTTP server</td>\n<td>Windows on-premises</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HTTP.sys</td>\n<td>Windows kernel driver</td>\n<td>Windows-only features (Windows auth, port sharing) without IIS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Azure Functions (isolated worker)</td>\n<td>Functions host + ASP.NET Core integration</td>\n<td>Serverless HTTP triggers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>TestServer</code></td>\n<td>In-memory</td>\n<td>Integration tests (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/integration-testing\">Integration testing</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the role of Program.cs in ASP.NET Core?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>It&#39;s the application entry point. It creates a WebApplicationBuilder, registers services and configuration (phase 1), builds the WebApplication (which builds the DI container), configures the middleware pipeline and endpoints (phase 2), then calls Run to start Kestrel and the hosted services.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the Generic Host?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The <code>IHost</code> abstraction from Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting that provides DI, configuration, logging, environment info and lifetime management, and runs IHostedService instances. It&#39;s used by web apps, worker services and console apps alike; ASP.NET Core&#39;s web server runs as one of its hosted services.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is Kestrel and why use a reverse proxy in front of it?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Kestrel is ASP.NET Core&#39;s built-in cross-platform web server: it handles sockets, TLS and HTTP/1-3 parsing and feeds requests into the pipeline. A reverse proxy adds load balancing, TLS termination, a WAF, caching, multiple apps behind one IP and centralised rate limiting. When you use one, you must configure forwarded headers so the app sees the real client IP and scheme.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How does ASP.NET Core know which environment it&#39;s running in?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>From the ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT (or DOTNET_ENVIRONMENT) variable, defaulting to Production. It controls which appsettings.{Environment}.json is loaded, whether User Secrets are used, and is available as IWebHostEnvironment.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What&#39;s the difference between in-process and out-of-process hosting in IIS?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>In-process runs the app inside the IIS worker process using IIS&#39;s HTTP server, which is faster. Out-of-process runs Kestrel as a separate process, with IIS forwarding requests to it as a reverse proxy.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What happens when an ASP.NET Core app receives SIGTERM?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The host triggers ApplicationStopping, Kestrel stops accepting new connections and drains in-flight requests, hosted services&#39; StopAsync is called and their stopping tokens are cancelled, all within ShutdownTimeout (30 s by default). Then ApplicationStopped fires and the DI container (with its singletons) is disposed.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is HttpContext and is it thread-safe?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The per-request object holding Request, Response, User, RequestServices (the scoped container), Items, Features and RequestAborted. It is not thread-safe and only valid for the duration of the request, so it must not be captured in background tasks.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why shouldn&#39;t you call BuildServiceProvider() inside Program.cs?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>It builds a separate container from the one the app will use, so singletons get created twice and their state diverges, and disposal isn&#39;t handled. Use the options pattern, factory overloads (<code>sp =&gt; ...</code>), or resolve after <code>builder.Build()</code> from <code>app.Services</code>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ASP.NET Core fundamentals overview</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/host/generic-host\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">.NET Generic Host</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/servers/kestrel\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kestrel web server</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/host-and-deploy/proxy-load-balancer\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Configure ASP.NET Core to work with proxy servers</a></li>\n<li>Book: <em>ASP.NET Core in Action</em> (3rd ed.) by Andrew Lock, the best book-length treatment. Also his blog, andrewlock.net.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run</code> in a console project (<code>dotnet new web</code>, then <code>dotnet add package Microsoft.AspNetCore.TestHost</code>). It checks the environment default, the shutdown-timeout default, the order of host lifecycle events, and that <code>RequestServices</code> really is one scope per request.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AspNetCore</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Builder</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AspNetCore</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Hosting</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AspNetCore</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TestHost</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Extensions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DependencyInjection</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Extensions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Hosting</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> condition, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> claim)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!condition) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: {claim}&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: {claim}&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 1. With no ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT/DOTNET_ENVIRONMENT set, the environment defaults to &quot;Production&quot;.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SetEnvironmentVariable</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SetEnvironmentVariable</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;DOTNET_ENVIRONMENT&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> defaultBuilder = <span class=\"tk-ty\">WebApplication</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateBuilder</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(defaultBuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">EnvironmentName</span> == <span class=\"tk-ty\">Environments</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Production</span>,\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;With no environment variable set, WebApplication.CreateBuilder defaults to Production&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 2. HostOptions.ShutdownTimeout defaults to 30 seconds.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> hostOptionsApp = <span class=\"tk-ty\">WebApplication</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateBuilder</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> shutdownTimeout = hostOptionsApp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Extensions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Options</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IOptions</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">HostOptions</span>&gt;&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShutdownTimeout</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(shutdownTimeout == <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSeconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">30</span>), <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;HostOptions.ShutdownTimeout defaults to 30 seconds&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 3. The host fires ApplicationStarted, then ApplicationStopping, then ApplicationStopped, in that order.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> lifecycleBuilder = <span class=\"tk-ty\">WebApplication</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateBuilder</span>();\nlifecycleBuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WebHost</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseTestServer</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> lifecycleApp = lifecycleBuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> events = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> lifetime = lifecycleApp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IHostApplicationLifetime</span>&gt;();\nlifetime.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ApplicationStarted</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Register</span>(() =&gt; events.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Started&quot;</span>));\nlifetime.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ApplicationStopping</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Register</span>(() =&gt; events.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Stopping&quot;</span>));\nlifetime.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ApplicationStopped</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Register</span>(() =&gt; events.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Stopped&quot;</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> lifecycleApp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> lifecycleApp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StopAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(events.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SequenceEqual</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>[] { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Started&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Stopping&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Stopped&quot;</span> }),\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Host lifecycle fires Started, then Stopping, then Stopped, in order&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 4. HttpContext.RequestServices IS that request's scope: resolved twice in ONE request, same instance;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//    resolved in two DIFFERENT requests, different instances.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> scopeBuilder = <span class=\"tk-ty\">WebApplication</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateBuilder</span>();\nscopeBuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WebHost</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseTestServer</span>();\nscopeBuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">GuidService</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> scopeApp = scopeBuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();\nscopeApp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/&quot;</span>, (<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpContext</span> ctx) =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> a = ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequestServices</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">GuidService</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> b = ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequestServices</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">GuidService</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;{a},{b}&quot;</span>;\n});\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> scopeApp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> client = scopeApp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetTestClient</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> firstRequest = (<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> client.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetStringAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/&quot;</span>)).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Split</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">','</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> secondRequest = (<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> client.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetStringAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/&quot;</span>)).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Split</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">','</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> scopeApp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StopAsync</span>();\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(firstRequest[<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>] == firstRequest[<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>], <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Resolving a scoped service twice within ONE request returns the same instance&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(firstRequest[<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>] != secondRequest[<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>], <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A scoped service resolved in two DIFFERENT requests returns different instances&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All aspnet-hosting claims verified.&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">GuidService</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NewGuid</span>(); }</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Claims this doesn&#39;t attempt to verify — they depend on the OS, Kestrel internals, or a real reverse proxy, not just the BCL — are left to the official docs linked above: Kestrel&#39;s exact transport behaviour, IIS in-process vs out-of-process hosting, and the actual <code>X-Forwarded-*</code> header handling of a specific proxy.</p>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"a-surprising-fact-to-start-from","text":"A surprising fact to start from","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"the-generic-host-the-thing-that-owns-everything-else","text":"The Generic Host: the thing that owns everything else","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"the-two-phases-of-program-cs-and-why-the-split-matters","text":"The two phases of Program.cs, and why the split matters","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"what-createbuilder-quietly-sets-up-for-you","text":"What CreateBuilder quietly sets up for you","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"kestrel-the-web-server-thats-actually-inside-your-process","text":"Kestrel: the web server that's actually inside your process","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"why-production-traffic-rarely-hits-kestrel-directly","text":"Why production traffic rarely hits Kestrel directly","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"iis-hosting-on-windows","text":"IIS hosting on Windows","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"environments-the-one-variable-thats-meant-to-change-configur","text":"Environments: the one variable that's meant to change configuration, never code paths","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"application-lifetime-and-graceful-shutdown-what-actually-hap","text":"Application lifetime and graceful shutdown: what actually happens, in order","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"httpcontext-the-one-object-your-entire-pipeline-shares","text":"HttpContext: the one object your entire pipeline shares","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"hosting-models-summary","text":"Hosting models summary","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":3977,"minutes":18,"num":"3.1","part":"p3"},"dependency-injection":{"id":"dependency-injection","title":"Dependency Injection in Depth","summary":"The single most-asked ASP.NET Core topic. What DI is and why, the container, registering services, the three lifetimes and scopes, captive dependencies, factories, multiple implementations, keyed services, open generics, decorators, disposal, and the anti-patterns.","layer":"framework","where":"ASP.NET Core › Hosting › Dependency injection container","fits":"<p>DI is the <strong>wiring of layer 3</strong>. It&#39;s provided by <code>Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection</code> and used by every other chapter: middleware, endpoints, EF Core&#39;s DbContext, HttpClient, options, hosted services. It&#39;s also how architectural layering (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/app-architectures\">App architectures</a>) becomes real at runtime, because the composition root in Program.cs connects the interfaces in your inner layers to implementations in outer layers.</p>\n","prereqs":["oop","generics","aspnet-hosting"],"next":["configuration-options","middleware","design-principles"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>DI means a class declares what it needs in its constructor instead of creating those things itself — a <strong>container</strong> builds and wires everything for you.</li>\n<li>Three lifetimes: <strong>Transient</strong> (new every time), <strong>Scoped</strong> (one per request), <strong>Singleton</strong> (one for the app&#39;s entire life). Pick based on whether the service holds per-request state or needs to be safely shared forever.</li>\n<li>A <strong>captive dependency</strong> — a singleton accidentally holding onto a scoped service, like a <code>DbContext</code> — is the single most common DI bug. <code>ValidateScopes</code> catches it at startup instead of in production.</li>\n<li>Outside a request (background jobs, startup code) there&#39;s no scope automatically; create one yourself with <code>IServiceScopeFactory</code>.</li>\n<li><code>Program.cs</code> is the <strong>composition root</strong> — the one place that should know about concrete types. Everywhere else should depend only on interfaces.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"start-with-the-version-that-goes-wrong\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/start-with-the-version-that-goes-wrong\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Start with the version that goes wrong <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Without DI: OrderService creates its own dependencies</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderService</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlOrderRepository</span> _repo = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlOrderRepository</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Server=prod;...&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SmtpEmailSender</span> _email = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SmtpEmailSender</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;smtp.company.com&quot;</span>);\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Place</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> o) { _repo.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Save</span>(o); _email.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Send</span>(o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerEmail</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Thanks!&quot;</span>); }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>This looks completely normal, and plenty of real code looks exactly like it. So let&#39;s actually interrogate it, line by line, instead of just accepting &quot;DI is good practice&quot; on faith.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>It&#39;s welded to specific technologies.</strong> <code>OrderService</code> doesn&#39;t just use a database and an email provider — it <em>creates</em> a SQL-specific repository and an SMTP-specific sender itself. Switching to Postgres or SendGrid later means editing <code>OrderService</code> directly, even though neither of those is really <code>OrderService</code>&#39;s job.</li>\n<li><strong>You cannot test this class in isolation.</strong> Write a unit test for <code>Place</code>, and you will hit a real database and send a real email — every single time, in every single test run. There&#39;s no way around it, because the dependencies are hard-coded inside the method.</li>\n<li><strong>A connection string is sitting inside a business class.</strong> That configuration detail has nothing to do with &quot;placing an order&quot; conceptually, yet here it is, buried in the one place you&#39;d least want to go looking for it.</li>\n<li><strong>Nobody owns the lifetime of these objects.</strong> Who disposes the SQL connection? Is it safe to share <code>_repo</code> across two requests running at once? Nothing here answers that — it&#39;s just left as an open question for whoever reads this later.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Four real, concrete problems, all stemming from one root cause: <code>OrderService</code> is responsible for <em>deciding</em> what talks to SQL and SMTP, on top of its actual job of placing an order. Now look at the fix:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// With DI: dependencies are given to the class (&quot;injected&quot;)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderService</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderRepository</span> repo, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IEmailSender</span> email)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> o, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> repo.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveAsync</span>(o, ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> email.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SendAsync</span>(o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerEmail</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Thanks!&quot;</span>, ct);\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice what actually changed: <code>OrderService</code> no longer creates <em>anything</em>. It just declares, through its constructor, what it needs — an <code>IOrderRepository</code>, an <code>IEmailSender</code> — and trusts that something else will hand it working instances. That &quot;something else&quot; is the <strong>container</strong>, and the technique of handing dependencies to a class from outside, instead of letting it construct them itself, is <strong>dependency injection</strong>. In a test, you simply hand <code>OrderService</code> fakes instead of real implementations, and all four problems above disappear at once — not because you fixed them individually, but because the thing causing all of them is gone.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-fits\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Three terms that get used almost interchangeably, and shouldn&#39;t be</div><ul>\n<li><strong>Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)</strong> — the &quot;D&quot; in SOLID — is the <em>design rule</em>: high-level code should depend on abstractions, not on concrete low-level classes.</li>\n<li><strong>Inversion of Control (IoC)</strong> is the <em>general idea</em> behind that rule in practice: the framework calls your code and controls when objects get created, instead of your code doing it and calling the framework.</li>\n<li><strong>Dependency Injection (DI)</strong> is the specific <em>technique</em> that implements IoC for dependencies: passing them in, usually through a constructor. The <strong>DI container</strong> (also called an IoC container) is the piece of software that automates doing this for an entire app.</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"what-the-container-is-actually-doing\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/what-the-container-is-actually-doing\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What the container is actually doing <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Once <code>OrderService</code> declares what it needs instead of creating it, <em>something</em> has to supply those dependencies at runtime. That something is a three-step pipeline, and understanding the three steps as separate moments — not one blurred-together thing — is what makes the rest of this chapter click:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>IServiceCollection  ──Build()──▶  IServiceProvider  ──GetService&lt;T&gt;()──▶ instances\n (list of recipes:                (the container:                          (constructed with\n  &quot;for IX, use X,                  knows how to build                       all their\n  with lifetime L&quot;)                each type &amp; caches by lifetime)           dependencies)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderRepository</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">EfOrderRepository</span>&gt;();   <span class=\"tk-com\">// recipe</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IEmailSender</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">SendGridEmailSender</span>&gt;();\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderService</span>&gt;();                           <span class=\"tk-com\">// concrete type registered as itself</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> app = builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();                                            <span class=\"tk-com\">// container built</span>\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapPost</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/orders&quot;</span>, (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> o, <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderService</span> svc, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt; svc.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceAsync</span>(o, ct));\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// ASP.NET Core resolves OrderService from the request scope. The container sees its constructor needs</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// IOrderRepository and IEmailSender, resolves those (and THEIR dependencies, recursively), then constructs it.</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Step one — <strong>registration</strong> — just builds up a list of recipes: &quot;if anyone asks for <code>IOrderRepository</code>, give them an <code>EfOrderRepository</code>.&quot; Nothing is constructed yet; <code>builder.Services</code> is only a plan. Step two — <code>builder.Build()</code> — locks that plan in and turns it into a real <code>IServiceProvider</code>, the actual machine capable of constructing things. Step three happens every time something asks for an instance: the container looks at what <code>OrderService</code>&#39;s constructor needs, resolves each of <em>those</em> dependencies first (recursing into theirs, if they have any), and only then constructs <code>OrderService</code> itself, fully wired up. You never call <code>new OrderService(...)</code> anywhere in your code — the container does, and it works out the whole dependency graph for you, automatically, every single time.</p>\n<p><strong>Constructor injection</strong>, what you&#39;ve just seen, is the normal, overwhelmingly common case — the container picks whichever constructor has the most parameters it&#39;s able to satisfy. But it isn&#39;t the only place services get handed to your code:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Where</th>\n<th>How</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Constructors</td>\n<td>Normal case (including primary constructors)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Minimal API handler parameters</td>\n<td>Services are inferred from DI (<code>[FromServices]</code> optional)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Controller action parameters</td>\n<td><code>[FromServices] IFoo foo</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Middleware <code>InvokeAsync</code> parameters</td>\n<td>Resolved per request (use this for scoped services)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Razor views/components</td>\n<td><code>@inject IFoo Foo</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Manually</td>\n<td><code>app.Services.GetRequiredService&lt;T&gt;()</code>, <code>HttpContext.RequestServices</code> (avoid in business code)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"service-lifetimes-the-question-the-container-has-to-answer-e\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/service-lifetimes-the-question-the-container-has-to-answer-e\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Service lifetimes: the question the container has to answer every time <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Here&#39;s a question you might not have thought to ask yet: when two different parts of your code both ask for <code>IEmailSender</code>, does the container build a brand-new one each time, or hand back the same instance twice? The answer is: <strong>it depends on how you registered it</strong> — and that choice is called the service&#39;s <strong>lifetime</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Lifetime</th>\n<th>Register with</th>\n<th>One instance per…</th>\n<th>Disposed when</th>\n<th>Typical services</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Transient</strong></td>\n<td><code>AddTransient</code></td>\n<td><strong>every resolution</strong></td>\n<td>The scope that resolved it ends</td>\n<td>Lightweight, stateless helpers, validators, mappers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Scoped</strong></td>\n<td><code>AddScoped</code></td>\n<td><strong>scope</strong>, which is one per <strong>HTTP request</strong> in ASP.NET Core</td>\n<td>The scope (request) ends</td>\n<td><code>DbContext</code>, unit of work, repositories, request-specific services (current user, tenant)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Singleton</strong></td>\n<td><code>AddSingleton</code></td>\n<td><strong>application</strong> (root container)</td>\n<td>App shutdown</td>\n<td>Caches, configuration objects, <code>HttpClient</code> handlers, <code>IConnectionMultiplexer</code> (Redis), clocks, expensive-to-create thread-safe objects</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The clearest way to actually feel the difference is to watch one tiny service resolved under all three lifetimes:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">GuidService</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NewGuid</span>(); }\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Within ONE request, resolving it in two places gives:</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//   Transient → two different Ids</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//   Scoped    → the same Id (within this request), different across requests</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//   Singleton → the same Id for every request, forever</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Read that comment slowly, because it&#39;s really showing you three completely different answers to the same question — &quot;is this the same object as last time?&quot; — and all three are correct, for their own lifetime. <strong>Transient</strong> never remembers anything; it&#39;s rebuilt fresh, every single time, no exceptions. <strong>Scoped</strong> remembers <em>within</em> one unit of work (a request) but forgets between them — resolve it twice in the same request and you get the identical instance, resolve it in the next request and it&#39;s a new one. <strong>Singleton</strong> never forgets, ever, for the life of the entire process.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-analogy\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Lifetimes as a hotel</div><p><strong>Singleton</strong> is the hotel building itself: one building, shared by every single guest, for as long as the hotel exists. <strong>Scoped</strong> is your room: it&#39;s yours for the length of your stay, and then it gets cleaned and handed to the next guest — a different room, in effect, even though the physical room might be reused. <strong>Transient</strong> is a towel: you get a fresh one every single time you ask, no matter how many times, in the same stay or different ones.</p>\n</aside>\n<h3 id=\"so-which-one-do-you-actually-pick\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/so-which-one-do-you-actually-pick\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>So which one do you actually pick?</h3>\n<p>In order, ask yourself these three questions, and stop at the first one that answers it:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Does it hold per-request state, or does it depend on something that&#39;s scoped (like a <code>DbContext</code>)?</strong> → Scoped.</li>\n<li><strong>Is it stateless — or is whatever state it holds genuinely safe to share across every thread at once — and relatively expensive to build?</strong> → Singleton. (And it genuinely <strong>must</strong> be thread-safe if you choose this; see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/threading-concurrency/race-conditions-when-it-depends-on-timing-becomes-a-bug\">Threading</a> for exactly what that means and what breaks if it isn&#39;t.)</li>\n<li><strong>Cheap, stateless, used briefly?</strong> → Transient — though in practice, the difference between transient and scoped rarely matters for something this simple.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>When none of that gives you a confident answer, <strong>scoped</strong> is the safe default for application services in a web app — it&#39;s the one lifetime that can never accidentally outlive the request it&#39;s working on.</p>\n<h2 id=\"scopes-made-concrete\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/scopes-made-concrete\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Scopes, made concrete <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>You&#39;ve been told &quot;scoped means one instance per scope&quot; several times now — but what, physically, <em>is</em> a scope? It&#39;s a <strong>child container</strong>: a smaller <code>IServiceProvider</code> nested inside the main one, with its own private cache of scoped instances, that gets thrown away (and everything disposable inside it disposed) as one unit when it ends.</p>\n<p><strong>ASP.NET Core creates exactly one scope per HTTP request</strong>, automatically, via middleware near the very start of the pipeline. It exposes that scope as <code>HttpContext.RequestServices</code>. Every service resolved anywhere during the request — your handler, any middleware after that point, anything they call into — pulls from that one scope. That&#39;s the entire mechanism behind &quot;scoped = one per request&quot;: scoped services don&#39;t know anything special about HTTP requests. There&#39;s just one scope created per request, and scoped services are cached per scope.</p>\n<p>Here&#39;s the detail that catches people out: <strong>outside of a request, there is no scope, unless you explicitly create one.</strong> A background service, startup code, a message consumer — none of these are running inside an HTTP request, so none of them have a scope handed to them automatically. If any of that code needs a scoped service, like <code>DbContext</code>, it has to ask for a fresh scope itself:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">NightlyCleanup</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IServiceScopeFactory</span> scopeFactory, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ILogger</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">NightlyCleanup</span>&gt; log) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">BackgroundService</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> stoppingToken)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> timer = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PeriodicTimer</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromHours</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">24</span>));\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">while</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> timer.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WaitForNextTickAsync</span>(stoppingToken))\n        {\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> scope = scopeFactory.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateAsyncScope</span>();     <span class=\"tk-com\">// one scope per unit of work</span>\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> db = scope.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceProvider</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>&gt;();  <span class=\"tk-com\">// fresh DbContext</span>\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> removed = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Carts</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(c =&gt; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UpdatedAt</span> &lt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTime</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UtcNow</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddDays</span>(-<span class=\"tk-num\">30</span>)).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteDeleteAsync</span>(stoppingToken);\n            log.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogInformation</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Removed {Count} stale carts&quot;</span>, removed);\n        }                                                                  <span class=\"tk-com\">// scope disposed → DbContext disposed</span>\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// At startup (e.g. migrations/seeding in development)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> scope = app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateScope</span>())\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> db = scope.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceProvider</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>&gt;();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Database</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MigrateAsync</span>();\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice the pattern: <code>NightlyCleanup</code> itself is a long-lived singleton (hosted services always are), but every time it has actual work to do, it creates a <em>brand-new, short-lived</em> scope just for that one unit of work, uses it, and disposes it. That&#39;s the correct shape for mixing a long-lived component with scoped dependencies — and getting that shape wrong is exactly what the next section is about.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-captive-dependency-the-single-most-common-di-bug\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/the-captive-dependency-the-single-most-common-di-bug\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The captive dependency: the single most common DI bug <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Here&#39;s a rule worth memorising exactly, because almost every tricky DI bug you&#39;ll ever debug traces back to breaking it: <strong>a service must never depend on a service with a shorter lifetime than its own.</strong></p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Singleton  ──depends on──▶  Scoped      ✘ captive dependency\nSingleton  ──depends on──▶  Transient   ✘ (the transient is effectively a singleton)\nScoped     ──depends on──▶  Transient   ✔ (fine)\nScoped     ──depends on──▶  Singleton   ✔\nTransient  ──depends on──▶  anything    ✔</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Why does this matter so much for the singleton row specifically? A singleton is constructed exactly <strong>once</strong> — the first time anything asks for it. Whatever gets passed into its constructor at that moment is what it&#39;s stuck with for the rest of the process&#39;s life. If one of those constructor arguments is a scoped service, the singleton doesn&#39;t get &quot;a fresh one per request&quot; the way scoped is supposed to work. It gets whichever instance happened to exist at that one moment, and holds onto it permanently. That&#39;s a <strong>captive dependency</strong>: the scoped object is taken captive by something that will never let it go.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">PriceCache</span>&gt;();     <span class=\"tk-com\">// lives forever</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>&gt;();            <span class=\"tk-com\">// should live one request</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PriceCache</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span> db) { }           <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✘ the FIRST request's DbContext is captured forever</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><img src=\"visuals/captive-dependency.svg\" alt=\"A singleton captures the first request&#39;s scoped DbContext and keeps it long after that request ends\"></p>\n<p>Walk through exactly what the diagram shows, because each step explains one real symptom you&#39;ll see in production:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Request 1 arrives.</strong> Its scope creates <code>ShopDb #1</code>. The very first time <code>PriceCache</code> is resolved — which, being a singleton, only ever happens once — it receives <code>ShopDb #1</code> through its constructor and holds that reference from now on.</li>\n<li><strong>Request 1 finishes.</strong> Its scope ends, and <code>ShopDb #1</code> gets disposed, exactly as a scoped service should be. But <code>PriceCache</code> is a singleton — nobody tells it anything changed, and it&#39;s still holding that same reference.</li>\n<li><strong>Request 2 arrives.</strong> A brand-new scope creates <code>ShopDb #2</code>, fresh and ready. But <code>PriceCache</code> was never reconstructed — it&#39;s the same singleton instance from before — so it has no idea <code>ShopDb #2</code> even exists. It&#39;s still pointing at the disposed <code>ShopDb #1</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>That mismatch produces three distinct, nasty symptoms, worth being able to name all three. <code>DbContext</code> isn&#39;t thread-safe, so concurrent requests sharing that one captured instance risk <strong>&quot;a second operation was started on this context&quot;</strong> errors. Nobody ever disposes and recreates it the normal way, so its change-tracker just keeps growing, quietly feeding you <strong>stale data</strong>. And once request 1&#39;s <code>using</code> block actually disposes it, every later access throws <strong><code>ObjectDisposedException</code></strong>. Three different-looking bugs, one single root cause.</p>\n<p><strong>How do you catch this before it ships?</strong> In Development, <code>ValidateScopes = true</code> makes the container actively check for exactly this shape and throw a clear <code>InvalidOperationException: Cannot consume scoped service &#39;ShopDb&#39; from singleton &#39;PriceCache&#39;</code> the moment it would happen — turning a subtle runtime bug into a loud startup failure. It&#39;s usually worth enabling everywhere, not just in Development, for the small extra startup cost:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"YnVpbGRlci5Ib3N0LlVzZURlZmF1bHRTZXJ2aWNlUHJvdmlkZXIobyA9PiB7IG8uVmFsaWRhdGVTY29wZXMgPSB0cnVlOyBvLlZhbGlkYXRlT25CdWlsZCA9IHRydWU7IH0pOw==\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Host</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseDefaultServiceProvider</span>(o =&gt; { o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidateScopes</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidateOnBuild</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>; });</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><strong>The fix</strong>, once you&#39;ve found one: either make the consumer scoped too (if that&#39;s actually appropriate for what it does), or — the usual answer for something that genuinely needs to be a singleton — inject <code>IServiceScopeFactory</code> (or <code>IDbContextFactory&lt;T&gt;</code>) instead of the scoped service directly, and create a fresh scope or context for each individual operation, exactly the way <code>NightlyCleanup</code> did in the previous section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-tour-of-the-registration-api\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/a-tour-of-the-registration-api\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>A tour of the registration API <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Registering a simple interface-to-implementation mapping is the common case, but the container supports several other shapes worth knowing:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Interface → implementation</span>\nservices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderRepository</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">EfOrderRepository</span>&gt;();\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Concrete type</span>\nservices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">SystemClock</span>&gt;();\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Existing instance (always singleton; the container won't dispose it)</span>\nservices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IClock</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FixedClock</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parse</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;2026-01-01&quot;</span>)));\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Factory: full control over construction (needed for primitives, conditional logic, external SDKs)</span>\nservices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IBlobStore</span>&gt;(sp =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> opts = sp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IOptions</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">StorageOptions</span>&gt;&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AzureBlobStore</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">BlobServiceClient</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Uri</span>(opts.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Url</span>), <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DefaultAzureCredential</span>()));\n});\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// One implementation, several interfaces, same instance</span>\nservices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">InMemoryCache</span>&gt;();\nservices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ICache</span>&gt;(sp =&gt; sp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">InMemoryCache</span>&gt;());\nservices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ICacheStats</span>&gt;(sp =&gt; sp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">InMemoryCache</span>&gt;());\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Try* variants: register only if not already registered (library-friendly defaults)</span>\nservices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryAddSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IClock</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">SystemClock</span>&gt;();\nservices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryAddEnumerable</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceDescriptor</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Scoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IValidator</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt;, <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderValidator</span>&gt;());\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Replace / remove (common in tests)</span>\nservices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Replace</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceDescriptor</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Singleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IEmailSender</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">FakeEmailSender</span>&gt;());\nservices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RemoveAll</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IEmailSender</span>&gt;();\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Open generics</span>\nservices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IRepository</span>&lt;&gt;), <span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">EfRepository</span>&lt;&gt;));</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>That last one is worth a second look if you haven&#39;t seen it before: registering <code>IRepository&lt;&gt;</code> (the <em>open</em> generic, with no type argument filled in) against <code>EfRepository&lt;&gt;</code> means the container can construct <code>IRepository&lt;Order&gt;</code>, <code>IRepository&lt;Customer&gt;</code>, or <code>IRepository&lt;AnyType&gt;</code> on demand, all from this single line — it closes the generic with whatever type was actually asked for.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-happens-when-two-implementations-share-an-interface\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/what-happens-when-two-implementations-share-an-interface\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What happens when two implementations share an interface</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>services.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">INotificationChannel</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">EmailChannel</span>&gt;();\nservices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">INotificationChannel</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">SmsChannel</span>&gt;();\nservices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">INotificationChannel</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">PushChannel</span>&gt;();\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Notifier</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IEnumerable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">INotificationChannel</span>&gt; channels)   <span class=\"tk-com\">// all three, in registration order</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">NotifyAllAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Message</span> m) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WhenAll</span>(channels.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(c =&gt; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SendAsync</span>(m)));\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Other</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">INotificationChannel</span> channel) { }                <span class=\"tk-com\">// gets the LAST one registered (PushChannel)</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>This is a genuinely sharp edge worth knowing about in advance: asking for <code>INotificationChannel</code> directly gives you only the <strong>last one registered</strong> — <code>PushChannel</code> here — silently, with no error telling you the other two exist. Asking for <code>IEnumerable&lt;INotificationChannel&gt;</code> instead gives you <strong>all three</strong>, in registration order. If you ever find a bug where &quot;only the push notification fires&quot;, this exact mismatch is worth checking first.</p>\n<h3 id=\"keyed-services-for-picking-a-specific-one-by-name\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/keyed-services-for-picking-a-specific-one-by-name\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Keyed services, for picking a specific one by name</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>services.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddKeyedSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGateway</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">StripeGateway</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;stripe&quot;</span>);\nservices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddKeyedSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGateway</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">AdyenGateway</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;adyen&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Checkout</span>(<span class=\"tk-attr\">[FromKeyedServices(&quot;stripe&quot;)]</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGateway</span> gateway) { }\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapPost</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/pay/{provider}&quot;</span>, (<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> provider, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IServiceProvider</span> sp) =&gt;\n    sp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredKeyedService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGateway</span>&gt;(provider).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeAsync</span>(...));</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Keyed services (.NET 8) solve exactly the ambiguity the previous example ran into, when ambiguity is actually what you want: multiple implementations of the same interface, each deliberately reachable by its own name, instead of only ever getting &quot;the last one&quot; or &quot;all of them&quot;.</p>\n<h3 id=\"keeping-program-cs-readable\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/keeping-program-cs-readable\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Keeping Program.cs readable</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InfrastructureRegistration</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IServiceCollection</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AddInfrastructure</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">this</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IServiceCollection</span> services, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IConfiguration</span> config)\n    {\n        services.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddDbContext</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>&gt;(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseSqlServer</span>(config.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetConnectionString</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop&quot;</span>)));\n        services.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderRepository</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">EfOrderRepository</span>&gt;();\n        services.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IUnitOfWork</span>&gt;(sp =&gt; sp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>&gt;());\n        services.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSingleton</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeProvider</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> services;\n    }\n}\n\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddApplication</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddInfrastructure</span>(builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configuration</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddPresentation</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>An extension method per feature or architectural layer is the convention every library in the .NET ecosystem follows, and it pays off the same way here: <code>Program.cs</code> stays a short, readable table of contents instead of a 300-line wall of registrations. <code>Program.cs</code>, as the one place where the entire object graph finally gets wired together, has a name of its own: the <strong>composition root</strong>. It&#39;s meant to be the <em>only</em> place in your whole codebase that knows about every concrete type — everywhere else should only ever see interfaces.</p>\n<h2 id=\"decorators-and-assembly-scanning\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/decorators-and-assembly-scanning\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Decorators and assembly scanning <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Two capabilities the built-in container deliberately leaves out, on purpose, to stay small and simple:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Manual decorator: wrap the real implementation</span>\nservices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlProductRepository</span>&gt;();\nservices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IProductRepository</span>&gt;(sp =&gt;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CachedProductRepository</span>(sp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlProductRepository</span>&gt;(), sp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">HybridCache</span>&gt;()));\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// With the Scrutor library</span>\nservices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IProductRepository</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlProductRepository</span>&gt;();\nservices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Decorate</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IProductRepository</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CachedProductRepository</span>&gt;();\nservices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Scan</span>(s =&gt; s.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromAssemblyOf</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Program</span>&gt;()\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddClasses</span>(c =&gt; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AssignableTo</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ICommandHandler</span>&lt;&gt;)))\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsImplementedInterfaces</span>()\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithScopedLifetime</span>());</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>You can still build a <strong>decorator</strong> — a class that wraps another implementation of the same interface, adding behaviour around it — by hand, with a plain factory registration, as the first block shows. The <strong>Scrutor</strong> library makes both decoration and convention-based assembly scanning (&quot;register every class that implements <code>ICommandHandler&lt;&gt;</code>&quot;) much less verbose, if you find yourself doing either often. Third-party containers (Autofac, Lamar, DryIoc) go further still, offering modules, interception and child containers, and can replace or sit behind the built-in one — but most modern apps genuinely don&#39;t need that extra power.</p>\n<h2 id=\"who-disposes-what\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/who-disposes-what\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Who disposes what <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The container disposes <strong><code>IDisposable</code>/<code>IAsyncDisposable</code> instances it created itself</strong>, the moment their scope ends (and singletons at app shutdown).</li>\n<li>It does <strong>not</strong> dispose instances <em>you</em> registered directly with <code>AddSingleton(instance)</code> — you created that one, you own its lifetime.</li>\n<li><strong>Transient disposables resolved directly from the root provider</strong> (not from a scope) are tracked until the whole app shuts down. Do that repeatedly and you&#39;ve built yourself a slow, steady memory leak. Resolve from a scope instead.</li>\n<li>As a rule: never dispose a dependency that was injected into you. If the container gave it to you, the container owns cleaning it up.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"patterns-that-look-reasonable-and-arent\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/patterns-that-look-reasonable-and-arent\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Patterns that look reasonable and aren&#39;t <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Anti-pattern</th>\n<th>Why it hurts</th>\n<th>Instead</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Service Locator</strong>: calling <code>IServiceProvider.GetService</code> inside business classes</td>\n<td>Hides dependencies, fails at runtime instead of startup, hard to test</td>\n<td>Constructor injection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Constructor over-injection</strong>: 10+ dependencies</td>\n<td>The class has too many responsibilities (SRP violation)</td>\n<td>Split the class; group into facades or handlers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Captive dependency</strong></td>\n<td>Shared non-thread-safe state, stale data</td>\n<td>Match lifetimes; scope factory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Static access</strong> (<code>ServiceLocator.Current</code>, static <code>HttpContext</code>)</td>\n<td>Global state, untestable</td>\n<td>Inject</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><code>BuildServiceProvider()</code> in Program.cs</strong></td>\n<td>Duplicate singletons</td>\n<td>Factories, options, resolve after Build</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Injecting <code>IServiceProvider</code> &quot;just in case&quot;</strong></td>\n<td>Same as service locator</td>\n<td>Inject what you need; use factories for dynamic choice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Interfaces for everything</strong></td>\n<td>Ceremony with no benefit for stable internal classes</td>\n<td>Interfaces at real seams (I/O, external systems, things you mock)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><code>async</code> work in constructors</strong></td>\n<td>Constructors can&#39;t be async; <code>.Result</code> blocks</td>\n<td>Factory methods, <code>IHostedService</code> startup, lazy initialisation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The first row deserves a closer look, because &quot;just inject <code>IServiceProvider</code> and resolve what you need inside the method&quot; can look, at first glance, almost identical to normal DI — it isn&#39;t. Constructor injection makes a class&#39;s real dependencies visible from the outside, right there in its signature, which is exactly what lets the container catch a missing registration at startup instead of three steps deep into a request at 2 a.m. Reaching into <code>IServiceProvider</code> by hand hides every one of those dependencies behind a string or a type lookup buried in a method body, where nothing — not the compiler, not <code>ValidateOnBuild</code> — can check it for you in advance.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-the-container-pulls-this-off-internally\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/how-the-container-pulls-this-off-internally\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>How the container pulls this off internally <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>A short look under the hood, for the curious. On <code>Build()</code>, your <code>ServiceCollection</code> of registrations becomes a real <code>ServiceProvider</code>. For each registration it holds a <strong>call-site</strong>: a description of exactly how to construct that type. The first time a given type is actually resolved, the container analyses its full constructor dependency graph and compiles a resolution plan for it. That starts out using plain reflection, but for services resolved often, it switches to <strong>compiled expression trees or IL emit</strong> instead, which is substantially faster (<a href=\"https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/tree/main/src/libraries/Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection/src/ServiceLookup\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">confirmed in the runtime&#39;s own <code>ServiceLookup</code> source</a>) — so the expensive analysis happens once, and every later resolution of the same type is cheap. Singletons are cached once, in the root container; scoped instances are cached per-scope, in each scope&#39;s own dictionary; transients aren&#39;t cached at all, only tracked so they can be disposed correctly. <strong><code>ValidateOnBuild</code></strong>, mentioned earlier alongside <code>ValidateScopes</code>, walks every registration right at startup to confirm it can actually be constructed — catching a missing registration the moment the app starts, instead of the first time a request happens to need it. One more detail worth knowing: <code>ActivatorUtilities.CreateInstance&lt;T&gt;(sp, extraArgs)</code> can construct a type that <em>isn&#39;t even registered</em>, mixing resolved DI services with explicit arguments you pass in yourself. This is precisely how conventional middleware classes get constructed, since they aren&#39;t registered as services in the usual sense.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is dependency injection and why use it?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A technique where a class receives its dependencies from outside (usually through its constructor) instead of creating them. It decouples code from concrete implementations, enables unit testing with fakes, centralises configuration and lifetime management in one composition root, and supports the Dependency Inversion Principle.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Explain transient, scoped and singleton lifetimes.</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Transient creates a new instance every time it&#39;s resolved. Scoped creates one instance per scope; in ASP.NET Core a scope is one HTTP request, so it&#39;s one per request. Singleton creates one instance for the app&#39;s lifetime, shared by all requests and threads, so it must be thread-safe.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What lifetime does DbContext have and why?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Scoped, by default with AddDbContext. A DbContext isn&#39;t thread-safe and accumulates tracked entities, so it should be short-lived and used by one request (one unit of work) at a time.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is a captive dependency and how do you detect and fix it?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A longer-lived service holding a shorter-lived one, e.g. a singleton depending on a scoped DbContext. The scoped instance is kept forever and shared across threads. ASP.NET Core detects it with scope validation (on by default in Development), which throws at startup. Fix it by aligning lifetimes or by injecting IServiceScopeFactory or IDbContextFactory to create instances per operation.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you use a scoped service from a BackgroundService?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>BackgroundService is a singleton with no request scope, so inject IServiceScopeFactory, create a scope per unit of work (<code>using var scope = factory.CreateScope()</code>), and resolve the scoped service from scope.ServiceProvider. Dispose the scope when done.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What happens if you register two implementations of the same interface?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Injecting the interface gives the last registered implementation. Injecting IEnumerable&lt;T&gt; gives all of them in registration order. Since .NET 8, keyed services let you register and resolve specific implementations by key.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What&#39;s the difference between DI, IoC and the Dependency Inversion Principle?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>DIP is the design principle: depend on abstractions, not concretions. IoC is the broader idea that a framework controls object creation and program flow. DI is a concrete technique for achieving IoC by passing dependencies in, typically automated by a DI container.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why is the Service Locator pattern considered an anti-pattern?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Dependencies are hidden inside method bodies instead of declared in the constructor, so you can&#39;t see what a class needs, missing registrations fail at runtime rather than at startup validation, and tests need a configured container. Constructor injection makes dependencies explicit.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How would you implement the decorator pattern with the built-in container?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Register the inner implementation as its concrete type and register the interface with a factory that wraps it: <code>services.AddScoped&lt;IRepo&gt;(sp =&gt; new CachingRepo(sp.GetRequiredService&lt;SqlRepo&gt;(), ...))</code>. Or use Scrutor&#39;s <code>Decorate&lt;TInterface, TDecorator&gt;()</code>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Does the container dispose services? Which ones?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Yes, the ones it created that implement IDisposable/IAsyncDisposable: scoped and transient instances when their scope is disposed, singletons when the app shuts down. It doesn&#39;t dispose instances you passed in yourself via AddSingleton(instance).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are keyed services?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A .NET 8 feature to register multiple implementations of the same service type under different keys (AddKeyedScoped&lt;IFoo, Bar&gt;(&quot;bar&quot;)) and resolve a specific one with [FromKeyedServices(&quot;bar&quot;)] or GetRequiredKeyedService.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the composition root?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The single place where the object graph is composed, meaning all interfaces are bound to implementations: Program.cs and the AddX extension methods it calls. Other code receives dependencies and never knows concrete types.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/dependency-injection\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Dependency injection in ASP.NET Core</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/extensions/dependency-injection-guidelines\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Dependency injection guidelines</a></li>\n<li>Book: <em>Dependency Injection Principles, Practices, and Patterns</em> by Steven van Deursen and Mark Seemann, the definitive book on DI.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run</code> in a plain console project (<code>dotnet new console</code>, then <code>dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection</code>). It checks all three lifetimes, the captive-dependency detection, &quot;last registered wins vs <code>IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</code> gives all&quot;, open generics, and <code>TryAddSingleton</code>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Extensions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DependencyInjection</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Extensions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DependencyInjection</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Extensions</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> condition, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> claim)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!condition) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: {claim}&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: {claim}&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 1. Transient: a new instance every single resolution, even within the same scope.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> services1 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceCollection</span>();\nservices1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddTransient</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">GuidService</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> provider1 = services1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BuildServiceProvider</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> scope = provider1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateScope</span>())\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> a = scope.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceProvider</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">GuidService</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> b = scope.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceProvider</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">GuidService</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(a != b, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Transient gives a different instance on every resolution, even within one scope&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 2. Scoped: same instance within one scope; a different instance in a different scope.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> services2 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceCollection</span>();\nservices2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">GuidService</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> provider2 = services2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BuildServiceProvider</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> scopeA = provider2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateScope</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> scopeB = provider2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateScope</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> scopedInA1 = scopeA.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceProvider</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">GuidService</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> scopedInA2 = scopeA.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceProvider</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">GuidService</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> scopedInB = scopeB.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceProvider</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">GuidService</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(scopedInA1 == scopedInA2, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Scoped gives the same instance twice within one scope&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(scopedInA1 != scopedInB, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Scoped gives a different instance in a different scope&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 3. Singleton: the same instance everywhere, across every scope, for the app's life.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> services3 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceCollection</span>();\nservices3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">GuidService</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> provider3 = services3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BuildServiceProvider</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> scopeC = provider3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateScope</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> singletonRoot = provider3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">GuidService</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> singletonInScope = scopeC.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceProvider</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">GuidService</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(singletonRoot == singletonInScope, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Singleton gives the same instance everywhere, including inside a scope&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 4. Captive dependency: a singleton depending on a scoped service is caught at resolution time when</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//    ValidateScopes is on, instead of silently capturing the first scope's instance forever.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> services4 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceCollection</span>();\nservices4.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">GuidService</span>&gt;();\nservices4.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CapturesScoped</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> threw = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> validated = services4.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BuildServiceProvider</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceProviderOptions</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidateScopes</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span> });\n    validated.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CapturesScoped</span>&gt;();\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>)\n{\n    threw = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;\n}\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(threw, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ValidateScopes=true makes resolving a singleton that depends on a scoped service throw InvalidOperationException&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 5. Injecting the interface directly gives the LAST registered implementation;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//    injecting IEnumerable&lt;T&gt; gives ALL of them, in registration order.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> services5 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceCollection</span>();\nservices5.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IChannel</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">EmailChannel</span>&gt;();\nservices5.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IChannel</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">SmsChannel</span>&gt;();\nservices5.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IChannel</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">PushChannel</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> provider5 = services5.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BuildServiceProvider</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> last = provider5.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IChannel</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> all = provider5.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetServices</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IChannel</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToList</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(last <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PushChannel</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Resolving the interface directly gives the LAST registered implementation&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(all.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span> &amp;&amp; all[<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>] <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">EmailChannel</span> &amp;&amp; all[<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>] <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SmsChannel</span> &amp;&amp; all[<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>] <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PushChannel</span>,\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Resolving IEnumerable&lt;T&gt; gives every implementation, in registration order&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 6. Open generics: registering IRepository&lt;&gt; against Repository&lt;&gt; lets the container construct</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//    IRepository&lt;T&gt; for ANY T, closed on demand.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> services6 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceCollection</span>();\nservices6.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceDescriptor</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IRepository</span>&lt;&gt;), <span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Repository</span>&lt;&gt;), <span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceLifetime</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Transient</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> provider6 = services6.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BuildServiceProvider</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> orderRepo = provider6.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IRepository</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> customerRepo = provider6.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IRepository</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(orderRepo <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Repository</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; &amp;&amp; customerRepo <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Repository</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;,\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;An open generic registration (IRepository&lt;&gt;) closes over whatever type argument is actually requested&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 7. TryAddSingleton only registers if nothing is already registered for that service type.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> services7 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceCollection</span>();\nservices7.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IClock</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">FixedClock</span>&gt;();\nservices7.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryAddSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IClock</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">SystemClock</span>&gt;();   <span class=\"tk-com\">// should be a no-op: IClock is already registered</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> provider7 = services7.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BuildServiceProvider</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(provider7.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IClock</span>&gt;() <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FixedClock</span>,\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;TryAddSingleton does nothing if the service type is already registered&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All dependency-injection claims verified.&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">GuidService</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NewGuid</span>(); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CapturesScoped</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">GuidService</span> g) { }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IChannel</span> { }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">EmailChannel</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IChannel</span> { }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SmsChannel</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IChannel</span> { }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PushChannel</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IChannel</span> { }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IRepository</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; { }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Repository</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IRepository</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; { }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IClock</span> { }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FixedClock</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IClock</span> { }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SystemClock</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IClock</span> { }</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Not attempted here: the actual compiled-expression-tree/IL-emit resolution path is an internal performance detail of the <code>ServiceLookup</code> namespace, not something you can assert on from outside — see the runtime source link above if you want to read it yourself.</p>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"start-with-the-version-that-goes-wrong","text":"Start with the version that goes wrong","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"what-the-container-is-actually-doing","text":"What the container is actually doing","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"service-lifetimes-the-question-the-container-has-to-answer-e","text":"Service lifetimes: the question the container has to answer every time","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"so-which-one-do-you-actually-pick","text":"So which one do you actually pick?","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"scopes-made-concrete","text":"Scopes, made concrete","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"the-captive-dependency-the-single-most-common-di-bug","text":"The captive dependency: the single most common DI bug","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"a-tour-of-the-registration-api","text":"A tour of the registration API","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"what-happens-when-two-implementations-share-an-interface","text":"What happens when two implementations share an interface","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"keyed-services-for-picking-a-specific-one-by-name","text":"Keyed services, for picking a specific one by name","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"keeping-program-cs-readable","text":"Keeping Program.cs readable","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"decorators-and-assembly-scanning","text":"Decorators and assembly scanning","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"who-disposes-what","text":"Who disposes what","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"patterns-that-look-reasonable-and-arent","text":"Patterns that look reasonable and aren't","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"how-the-container-pulls-this-off-internally","text":"How the container pulls this off internally","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":5654,"minutes":26,"num":"3.2","part":"p3"},"configuration-options":{"id":"configuration-options","title":"Configuration, Options and Secrets","summary":"How settings reach your code. The layered configuration system and its providers, precedence, environment variables and naming, the Options pattern (IOptions, IOptionsSnapshot, IOptionsMonitor), validation at startup, named options, secrets in development and production, and feature flags.","layer":"framework","where":"ASP.NET Core › Hosting › Configuration & options","fits":"<p>Configuration is plumbing from <code>Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration</code> and <code>Microsoft.Extensions.Options</code>, owned by the host (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting\">Hosting</a>) and delivered through DI (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/dependency-injection\">Dependency injection</a>). In layer 7, the cloud supplies the values: App Service settings, Kubernetes ConfigMaps, Key Vault (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets\">Identity &amp; secrets</a>).</p>\n","prereqs":["aspnet-hosting","dependency-injection"],"next":["middleware","azure-identity-secrets"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/configuration-options/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The same compiled app needs different settings in each environment; <strong>layered configuration</strong> merges a JSON file, environment variables, command-line args and secrets into one <code>IConfiguration</code>, with later layers overriding earlier ones key-by-key.</li>\n<li>Reading <code>config[&quot;Payments:BaseUrl&quot;]</code> directly is fine in <code>Program.cs</code>, but a typo in that string silently returns null instead of failing to compile — that&#39;s what the <strong>Options pattern</strong> fixes, by binding a section onto a typed class once.</li>\n<li>Three ways to consume options differ only in <em>when they pick up a change</em>: <code>IOptions&lt;T&gt;</code> (never, computed once), <code>IOptionsSnapshot&lt;T&gt;</code> (per request), <code>IOptionsMonitor&lt;T&gt;</code> (always current, safe in singletons).</li>\n<li><code>.ValidateOnStart()</code> turns a missing or bad setting into a deployment that fails its health check immediately, instead of a payment that fails for a real customer later.</li>\n<li>Secrets never belong in a committed file: <strong>User Secrets</strong> in development, <strong>Key Vault</strong>/platform secret stores (or better, no secret at all via managed identity) in production.</li>\n<li><strong>Feature flags</strong> separate <em>deploying</em> code from <em>releasing</em> it — ship a feature switched off, then turn it on gradually without a redeploy.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-same-build-four-different-places-four-different-answers\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/configuration-options/the-same-build-four-different-places-four-different-answers\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The same build, four different places, four different answers <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Here&#39;s a constraint that sounds simple but shapes this entire chapter: the <strong>exact same compiled binary</strong> has to run on your laptop, in a CI pipeline, in staging, and in production — and in each of those four places, it needs a different database connection string, a different payment-gateway URL, and a set of secrets that absolutely cannot be the same ones twice.</p>\n<p>Walk through what happens if you try to solve this the naive way, by baking the values into the code:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"Ly8gRE9OJ1Q6IHRoaXMgaXMgdGhlIG5haXZlIGFwcHJvYWNoLCBhbmQgaXQgYnJlYWtzIGltbWVkaWF0ZWx5CnN0cmluZyBjb25uZWN0aW9uU3RyaW5nID0gIlNlcnZlcj1sb2NhbGhvc3Q7RGF0YWJhc2U9U2hvcDsuLi4iOw==\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// DON'T: this is the naive approach, and it breaks immediately</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> connectionString = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Server=localhost;Database=Shop;...&quot;</span>;</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>That compiles fine on your laptop. The moment you deploy it to staging, it&#39;s still pointing at <code>localhost</code> — which, inside a staging container, doesn&#39;t mean your laptop at all, it means the staging container itself, and there&#39;s no database there. You&#39;d have to <strong>rebuild</strong> the app with a different hardcoded string for every environment, which means you&#39;re no longer testing the same binary you&#39;ll ship — exactly the problem <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting/environments-the-one-variable-thats-meant-to-change-configur\">ASP.NET Core architecture</a> flagged about letting environment branch code paths instead of just values. And if any of those hardcoded strings is a real secret, it&#39;s now sitting in your git history forever, readable by anyone with repo access, discoverable even after you &quot;remove&quot; it in a later commit.</p>\n<p>ASP.NET Core&#39;s answer is <strong>layered configuration</strong>: several independent sources — a JSON file, environment variables, command-line arguments, a secrets store — each contribute key/value pairs, and later sources <strong>override</strong> earlier ones for the same key. Your code never reads any of those sources directly. It reads one merged, flattened view called <code>IConfiguration</code>, and has no idea — and doesn&#39;t need to know — which layer any particular value actually came from.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-five-layers-lowest-priority-to-highest\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/configuration-options/the-five-layers-lowest-priority-to-highest\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The five layers, lowest priority to highest <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><code>WebApplication.CreateBuilder</code> wires up these sources automatically, in this exact order:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>1. appsettings.json                     ← defaults, committed to git, no secrets\n2. appsettings.{Environment}.json       ← per-environment overrides (Development, Staging, Production)\n3. User Secrets                          ← Development only; stored outside the repo\n4. Environment variables                 ← how containers, App Service and Kubernetes inject settings\n5. Command-line arguments                ← highest priority: --Logging:LogLevel:Default=Debug\n   (+ anything you add later, e.g. Azure Key Vault, Azure App Configuration)</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Let&#39;s trace one key through all five layers with real values, because &quot;later overrides earlier&quot; is easy to state and easy to get backwards in your head without a concrete run-through.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>JSON</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// appsettings.json — the base, checked into git</span>\n{\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ConnectionStrings&quot;</span>: { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Server=localhost;Database=Shop;Trusted_Connection=True&quot;</span> },\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Payments&quot;</span>: { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;BaseUrl&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://sandbox.pay.example&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;TimeoutSeconds&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Retries&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span> },\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Logging&quot;</span>: { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;LogLevel&quot;</span>: { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Default&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Information&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Microsoft.AspNetCore&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Warning&quot;</span> } },\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;AllowedHosts&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;*&quot;</span>\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>JSON</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// appsettings.Production.json — only what differs from the base</span>\n{ <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Payments&quot;</span>: { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;BaseUrl&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://api.pay.example&quot;</span> } }</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Now ask: in production, what is <code>Payments:BaseUrl</code>? Layer 1 sets it to the sandbox URL. Layer 2 (<code>appsettings.Production.json</code>, loaded because <code>ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production</code>) overrides it to the real API URL — and since nothing in layers 3–5 mentions <code>Payments:BaseUrl</code> at all, that production value is what survives to the end. What about <code>Payments:TimeoutSeconds</code>? No layer after layer 1 touches it, so it stays <code>10</code> straight from the base file — you never had to repeat it in the production file. And the real connection string, with real credentials? It was never written in <em>either</em> JSON file — it arrives from layer 4, an environment variable injected by whatever&#39;s actually running the container, which is exactly why you&#39;ll never find a production password by grepping the repo.</p>\n<p>That&#39;s the whole mental model: <strong>each layer only needs to mention the keys it wants to change</strong>, and the merge happens key-by-key, independently, not file-by-file.</p>\n<h2 id=\"reading-the-merged-view-keys-sections-and-converting-types\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/configuration-options/reading-the-merged-view-keys-sections-and-converting-types\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Reading the merged view: keys, sections, and converting types <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Configuration ends up as one flat dictionary of string keys, with <code>:</code> marking a level of nesting — <code>Payments:BaseUrl</code>, <code>Logging:LogLevel:Default</code> — and arrays addressed by index: <code>AllowedOrigins:0</code>, <code>AllowedOrigins:1</code>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-ty\">IConfiguration</span> config = builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configuration</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>? url = config[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Payments:BaseUrl&quot;</span>];                              <span class=\"tk-com\">// raw string</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> timeout = config.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetValue</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Payments:TimeoutSeconds&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">30</span>);      <span class=\"tk-com\">// converted, with default</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>? cs = config.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetConnectionString</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop&quot;</span>);                        <span class=\"tk-com\">// = config[&quot;ConnectionStrings:Shop&quot;]</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">IConfigurationSection</span> section = config.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetSection</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Payments&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> opts = section.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Get</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentOptions</span>&gt;();                               <span class=\"tk-com\">// bind section to a class</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> exists = section.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exists</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Reading <code>IConfiguration</code> directly like this is completely fine in <code>Program.cs</code> — you&#39;re wiring things up once, at startup. But scattering <code>config[&quot;Payments:TimeoutSeconds&quot;]</code> calls across your actual business logic is a trap worth avoiding, for a reason that becomes obvious once you hit it: a typo in that string key doesn&#39;t fail to compile, it just silently returns <code>null</code> or the default value at runtime, and you find out in production. The <strong>Options pattern</strong>, a few sections below, exists specifically to close that hole.</p>\n<h2 id=\"environment-variables-need-their-own-naming-convention\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/configuration-options/environment-variables-need-their-own-naming-convention\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Environment variables need their own naming convention <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Here&#39;s a wrinkle worth knowing before it costs you a debugging session: the <code>:</code> that separates configuration levels isn&#39;t a character every platform allows in an environment variable name. So the environment-variable provider uses a <strong>double underscore</strong> <code>__</code> instead, translated automatically back to <code>:</code> when it&#39;s read:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Configuration key</th>\n<th>Environment variable</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Payments:BaseUrl</code></td>\n<td><code>Payments__BaseUrl</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ConnectionStrings:Shop</code></td>\n<td><code>ConnectionStrings__Shop</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Logging:LogLevel:Default</code></td>\n<td><code>Logging__LogLevel__Default</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>AllowedOrigins:0</code></td>\n<td><code>AllowedOrigins__0</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Shell</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>docker run -e <span class=\"tk-ty\">ConnectionStrings__Shop</span>=<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Server=sql;...&quot;</span> -e <span class=\"tk-ty\">ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT</span>=Staging shop-api</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Get the underscores wrong — a single <code>_</code> instead of <code>__</code>, say — and nothing errors. The variable just doesn&#39;t match any key the provider recognises, so your app silently falls back to whatever the JSON file said, and you spend an hour wondering why a value you clearly set isn&#39;t taking effect. Two variable prefixes are reserved specifically for the <strong>host itself</strong> rather than your application settings: <code>ASPNETCORE_</code> and <code>DOTNET_</code> (as you saw with <code>ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT</code> in the previous chapter). And one small mercy: configuration keys, including environment variable names, are matched <strong>case-insensitively</strong>, so <code>payments__baseurl</code> works exactly as well as <code>Payments__BaseUrl</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-options-pattern-turning-strings-with-typos-into-a-typed-\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/configuration-options/the-options-pattern-turning-strings-with-typos-into-a-typed-\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The Options pattern: turning strings-with-typos into a typed class <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>So you&#39;ve got a merged <code>IConfiguration</code>. The next question is how your actual services should consume it — and the answer the framework steers you toward is: <strong>don&#39;t</strong>, not directly. Instead, bind a configuration section onto a strongly typed class once, register that class in DI, and let services ask for the type instead of a string key.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentOptions</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">const</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SectionName</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Payments&quot;</span>;\n\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[Required, Url]</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">BaseUrl</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">init</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[Range(1, 120)]</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeoutSeconds</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">init</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[Range(0, 10)]</span>  <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Retries</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">init</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[Required]</span>      <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ApiKey</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">init</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;</span>;     <span class=\"tk-com\">// supplied from secrets, not appsettings.json</span>\n}\n\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddOptions</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentOptions</span>&gt;()\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">BindConfiguration</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentOptions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SectionName</span>)   <span class=\"tk-com\">// bind the &quot;Payments&quot; section</span>\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidateDataAnnotations</span>()                        <span class=\"tk-com\">// run [Required], [Range]…</span>\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Validate</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BaseUrl</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartsWith</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://&quot;</span>), <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Payments:BaseUrl must use HTTPS&quot;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidateOnStart</span>();                               <span class=\"tk-com\">// fail at STARTUP, not on the first payment</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentClient</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpClient</span> http, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IOptions</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentOptions</span>&gt; options)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentOptions</span> _o = options.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-com\">// use _o.BaseUrl, _o.Retries…</span>\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice what <code>.ValidateOnStart()</code> buys you specifically, because it&#39;s the single most valuable line in that block. Without it, if <code>Payments:ApiKey</code> is missing from production — maybe the Key Vault reference was typo&#39;d in the IaC template — nothing breaks at deploy time. The app starts fine. It fails later, the first time a real customer tries to pay, at some unpredictable hour, as a confusing exception deep in <code>PaymentClient</code>. With <code>.ValidateOnStart()</code>, that same missing key makes the <strong>deployment itself</strong> fail its health check and get automatically rolled back — the same kind of fast-fail-at-boundary engineering that made graceful shutdown worth the effort in the previous chapter.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-why\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Why reach for options instead of calling IConfiguration everywhere</div><ul>\n<li><strong>Type safety:</strong> <code>int TimeoutSeconds</code> instead of parsing <code>config[&quot;Payments:TimeoutSeconds&quot;]</code> strings scattered across the codebase, each one a silent-failure typo waiting to happen.</li>\n<li><strong>Validation at startup:</strong> a missing API key crashes the deployment immediately, where health checks and rollouts catch it, instead of failing customer payments at 3 a.m.</li>\n<li><strong>Testability:</strong> <code>Options.Create(new PaymentOptions { ... })</code> in tests, with no configuration system needed at all.</li>\n<li><strong>Separation (Interface Segregation — a principle covered fully in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/design-principles\">Design principles</a>, meaning &quot;depend only on what you actually use&quot;):</strong> each class gets only the settings it actually needs, not a reference to the entire configuration tree with permission to read anything.</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"three-flavours-of-give-me-the-options-and-why-there-are-thre\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/configuration-options/three-flavours-of-give-me-the-options-and-why-there-are-thre\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Three flavours of &quot;give me the options&quot;, and why there are three <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>This is the part that trips people up, so let&#39;s be precise about <em>why</em> three interfaces exist instead of one. The question each one answers is: <strong>when configuration changes while the app is already running, who sees the new value, and when?</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Interface</th>\n<th>Lifetime</th>\n<th>Picks up changes?</th>\n<th>Use when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>IOptions&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Singleton</td>\n<td><strong>No</strong>, computed once on first access</td>\n<td>Settings that don&#39;t change at runtime (most of them)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IOptionsSnapshot&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td><strong>Scoped</strong></td>\n<td>Yes, recomputed <strong>per request</strong></td>\n<td>Reloadable settings used in scoped/transient services; named options per request</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IOptionsMonitor&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Singleton</td>\n<td>Yes, <code>CurrentValue</code> always latest, plus <code>OnChange</code> callback</td>\n<td>Reloadable settings in <strong>singletons</strong> and background services</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>IOptions&lt;T&gt;</code> is the simplest and the right default: it reads the configuration once, caches it, and hands back the same value for the rest of the process&#39;s life. That&#39;s fine for almost everything — a payment gateway&#39;s base URL doesn&#39;t need to change without a redeploy.</p>\n<p>But suppose you want a feature flag that an operator can flip without restarting the app — say, during an incident, to kill a misbehaving feature in seconds rather than waiting for a full redeploy. <code>IOptions&lt;T&gt;</code> can&#39;t do that; it already baked in its answer the first time anyone asked. <code>IOptionsMonitor&lt;T&gt;</code> is built for exactly this: it&#39;s still a singleton, so it&#39;s safe to inject into another singleton or a long-lived background service, but its <code>CurrentValue</code> property re-reads the live configuration every single time you access it, and it offers an <code>OnChange</code> callback you can subscribe to the moment a change lands.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FeatureGate</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IOptionsMonitor</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">FeatureOptions</span>&gt; monitor, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ILogger</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">FeatureGate</span>&gt; log)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IDisposable</span>? _sub = monitor.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OnChange</span>(o =&gt; log.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogInformation</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Features changed: {@F}&quot;</span>, o));\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsEnabled</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> f) =&gt; monitor.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CurrentValue</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Enabled</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contains</span>(f);\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><code>IOptionsSnapshot&lt;T&gt;</code> sits in between: it&#39;s scoped, meaning it&#39;s recomputed once per request rather than once per process, so within a single request everyone sees a consistent value, but the <em>next</em> request picks up whatever changed in the meantime.</p>\n<p>One detail worth being precise about, because it isn&#39;t automatic: <code>appsettings.json</code> reloads on file change by default (<code>reloadOnChange: true</code>), environment variables genuinely can&#39;t change for a process that&#39;s already running — the OS handed them to the process once at launch — and the Azure App Configuration and Key Vault providers refresh only on whatever polling interval you explicitly configure for them.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">The captive-dependency trap, reappearing here in a new costume</div><p><code>IOptionsSnapshot&lt;T&gt;</code> is <strong>scoped</strong>. If you inject it into a singleton, you&#39;ve built the exact same captive dependency <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/the-captive-dependency-the-single-most-common-di-bug\">Dependency injection</a> warned about — a short-lived thing trapped inside a long-lived one — and with scope validation enabled it throws at startup rather than silently freezing the options at whatever they were on the very first request. The fix is simple once you see it: use <code>IOptionsMonitor&lt;T&gt;</code> in singletons, full stop.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"named-options-and-post-configuration-when-one-options-type-i\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/configuration-options/named-options-and-post-configuration-when-one-options-type-i\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Named options and post-configuration: when one options type isn&#39;t enough <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Sometimes the same <em>shape</em> of settings needs several independent <em>instances</em> — think storage settings for invoices and storage settings for images, structurally identical but pointing at different containers:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configure</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">StorageOptions</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;invoices&quot;</span>, builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configuration</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetSection</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Storage:Invoices&quot;</span>));\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configure</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">StorageOptions</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;images&quot;</span>,   builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configuration</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetSection</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Storage:Images&quot;</span>));\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvoiceStore</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IOptionsMonitor</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">StorageOptions</span>&gt; opts)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">StorageOptions</span> _o = opts.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Get</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;invoices&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Code-based configuration and post-processing</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configure</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">JwtBearerOptions</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">JwtBearerDefaults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticationScheme</span>, o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapInboundClaims</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>);\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PostConfigure</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentOptions</span>&gt;(o =&gt; { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* runs after all Configure calls */</span> });\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Configure using other services: IConfigureOptions&lt;T&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ConfigureCors</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IOptions</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">FrontendOptions</span>&gt; fe) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IConfigureNamedOptions</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CorsOptions</span>&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Configure</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>? name, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CorsOptions</span> o) =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddPolicy</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;web&quot;</span>, p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithOrigins</span>(fe.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Origins</span>));\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Configure</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CorsOptions</span> o) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Configure</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>, o);\n}\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ConfigureOptions</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ConfigureCors</span>&gt;();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>For high-performance, AOT-friendly validation — relevant once you&#39;re on Native AOT, from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/what-if-you-skipped-the-jit-completely\">How code runs</a> — use the source-generated validator instead of the reflection-based <code>.ValidateDataAnnotations()</code>: <code>[OptionsValidator] public partial class PaymentOptionsValidator : IValidateOptions&lt;PaymentOptions&gt; { }</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"secrets-the-layer-systems-hardest-case\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/configuration-options/secrets-the-layer-systems-hardest-case\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Secrets: the layer system&#39;s hardest case <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Everything above assumes the values themselves are safe to see. Passwords, API keys, connection strings with embedded credentials, and signing keys are a different category entirely — they must <strong>never</strong> be committed to source control, and that includes <code>appsettings.Development.json</code>, which people forget is still a file in the repo.</p>\n<h3 id=\"in-development-user-secrets\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/configuration-options/in-development-user-secrets\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>In development: User Secrets</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Shell</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>dotnet user-secrets init                                   <span class=\"tk-com\"># adds &lt;UserSecretsId&gt; to the csproj</span>\ndotnet user-secrets set <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Payments:ApiKey&quot;</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;sk_test_123&quot;</span>\ndotnet user-secrets list</code></pre></figure>\n<p>These land as plain JSON in your own user profile (<code>~/.microsoft/usersecrets/&lt;id&gt;/secrets.json</code>) — physically outside the repo folder entirely — and the provider only loads that file when the environment is <code>Development</code>. Be clear-eyed about what this does and doesn&#39;t protect against: the file is <strong>not encrypted</strong>. It&#39;s not protecting the secret from anyone who gets access to your machine; it&#39;s protecting it from ever being <code>git add</code>-ed by accident.</p>\n<h3 id=\"in-production-no-file-on-disk-at-all\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/configuration-options/in-production-no-file-on-disk-at-all\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>In production: no file on disk at all</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Approach</th>\n<th>How</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Azure Key Vault</strong></td>\n<td><code>builder.Configuration.AddAzureKeyVault(vaultUri, new DefaultAzureCredential())</code></td>\n<td>Secrets appear as config keys (<code>Payments--ApiKey</code> → <code>Payments:ApiKey</code>); access via managed identity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>App Service / Container Apps settings</strong></td>\n<td>Set in the portal/IaC; become env vars</td>\n<td>Can reference Key Vault: <code>@Microsoft.KeyVault(SecretUri=…)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Kubernetes Secrets</strong></td>\n<td>Mounted as env vars or files (<code>AddKeyPerFile</code>)</td>\n<td>Pair with External Secrets / CSI driver for Key Vault</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>No secret at all</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Managed identity</strong> for Azure SQL, Storage, Service Bus</td>\n<td>Best option: nothing to leak or rotate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>That last row is worth sitting with for a moment, because it&#39;s a genuinely different strategy, not just another storage location: if the app authenticates to Azure SQL via <strong>managed identity</strong> instead of a username and password, there is no secret anywhere in this picture to leak, rotate, or accidentally log. See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets\">Identity &amp; secrets</a> for exactly how that works.</p>\n<h2 id=\"feature-flags-configuration-that-controls-behaviour-not-just\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/configuration-options/feature-flags-configuration-that-controls-behaviour-not-just\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Feature flags: configuration that controls <em>behaviour</em>, not just values <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Azure App Configuration</strong> extends this same layered model into a centrally managed, shared store — useful once several services need the same settings, with per-environment labels, live change notifications, and specifically, <strong>feature flags</strong>:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configuration</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAzureAppConfiguration</span>(o =&gt; o\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Connect</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Uri</span>(builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configuration</span>[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;AppConfig:Endpoint&quot;</span>]!), <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DefaultAzureCredential</span>())\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">KeyFilter</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Any</span>, labelFilter: builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">EnvironmentName</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">ConfigureRefresh</span>(r =&gt; r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Register</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Sentinel&quot;</span>, refreshAll: <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">SetRefreshInterval</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromMinutes</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>)))\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseFeatureFlags</span>());\n\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddFeatureManagement</span>();      <span class=\"tk-com\">// Microsoft.FeatureManagement</span>\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/checkout&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">IFeatureManager</span> features) =&gt;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> features.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsEnabledAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;NewCheckout&quot;</span>) ? <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;v2&quot;</span>) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;v1&quot;</span>));</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The reason this matters more than it first looks is that it separates two things that normally travel together: <strong>deploying</strong> code and <strong>releasing</strong> a feature. Without flags, shipping a risky new checkout flow means the moment the deploy finishes, every single user hits the new code — if it&#39;s broken, the only way back is another deploy. With a flag, you ship the new code <em>dark</em> (deployed but switched off), turn it on for 5% of traffic, watch error rates, and if something&#39;s wrong, flip the switch back off in seconds — no redeploy, no rollback, just a config change.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-the-provider-mechanism-actually-works-underneath\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/configuration-options/how-the-provider-mechanism-actually-works-underneath\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>How the provider mechanism actually works underneath <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Pulling back the curtain on the merge itself: each provider implements <code>IConfigurationProvider</code>, loading its source into a flat <code>Dictionary&lt;string,string?&gt;</code>. <code>IConfigurationRoot</code> queries all the registered providers <strong>in reverse order</strong> — last-added first — and returns the first value it finds for a given key. That single rule, &quot;ask the latest provider first,&quot; is the entire precedence mechanism; there&#39;s no separate priority system layered on top.</p>\n<p><code>builder.Configuration</code> is specifically a <code>ConfigurationManager</code>, which means you can add more sources during phase 1 and have later reads immediately see them — unlike a plain <code>IConfigurationRoot</code>, which is built once and fixed. Besides the five defaults, other built-in providers worth knowing exist: INI, XML, in-memory (<code>AddInMemoryCollection</code>, genuinely useful in tests), key-per-file (how Docker and Kubernetes secrets commonly get mounted), and <code>AddJsonStream</code>. Binding (<code>Bind</code>, <code>Get&lt;T&gt;</code>) uses reflection by default; setting <code>&lt;EnableConfigurationBindingGenerator&gt;true&lt;/EnableConfigurationBindingGenerator&gt;</code> switches that to a source generator instead, for AOT-friendly binding with no reflection at all.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"Ly8gVGVzdHM6IG92ZXJyaWRlIGNvbmZpZ3VyYXRpb24gd2l0aG91dCBmaWxlcwpidWlsZGVyLkNvbmZpZ3VyYXRpb24uQWRkSW5NZW1vcnlDb2xsZWN0aW9uKG5ldyBEaWN0aW9uYXJ5PHN0cmluZywgc3RyaW5nPz4KewogICAgWyJQYXltZW50czpCYXNlVXJsIl0gPSAiaHR0cHM6Ly9mYWtlIiwKICAgIFsiUGF5bWVudHM6QXBpS2V5Il0gPSAidGVzdCIKfSk7\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Tests: override configuration without files</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configuration</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddInMemoryCollection</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>?&gt;\n{\n    [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Payments:BaseUrl&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://fake&quot;</span>,\n    [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Payments:ApiKey&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;test&quot;</span>\n});</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How does configuration precedence work in ASP.NET Core?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Providers are added in order and later ones override earlier ones for the same key. By default that&#39;s appsettings.json, then appsettings.{Environment}.json, then User Secrets (Development), then environment variables, then command-line arguments. Providers added afterwards (Key Vault, App Configuration) override those too.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the Options pattern and why use it?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Binding configuration sections to strongly typed classes registered in DI and consumed as IOptions&lt;T&gt; (or Snapshot/Monitor). It gives type safety, validation (including fail-fast at startup with ValidateOnStart), testability and focused settings per class instead of passing IConfiguration around.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>IOptions vs IOptionsSnapshot vs IOptionsMonitor?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>IOptions is a singleton computed once, with no reload. IOptionsSnapshot is scoped and recomputed per request, so it reflects changes but can&#39;t be used in singletons. IOptionsMonitor is a singleton whose CurrentValue always reflects the latest config, with an OnChange callback; use it in singletons and background services.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you set a nested configuration value via an environment variable?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Replace each <code>:</code> with a double underscore, e.g. <code>Payments__BaseUrl</code> for <code>Payments:BaseUrl</code>, or <code>ConnectionStrings__Shop</code>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you manage secrets in development vs production?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Development: User Secrets (stored outside the repo, loaded only in Development). Production: Azure Key Vault (or the platform&#39;s secret store) loaded via managed identity, or better, avoid secrets entirely by using managed identity to authenticate to Azure services. Never commit secrets to source control.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you make the app fail fast on invalid configuration?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Use <code>AddOptions&lt;T&gt;().BindConfiguration(...).ValidateDataAnnotations().Validate(...).ValidateOnStart()</code>, so validation runs during host startup and the app refuses to start (and the deployment fails its health checks) instead of failing on first use.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are feature flags and why use them?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Runtime switches (e.g. via Microsoft.FeatureManagement and Azure App Configuration) that enable or disable features without redeploying. They decouple deployment from release, allow gradual rollouts, A/B tests and instant rollback.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/configuration-options/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/configuration/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Configuration in ASP.NET Core</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/configuration/options\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Options pattern</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/security/app-secrets\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Safe storage of app secrets in development</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/security/key-vault-configuration\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Azure Key Vault configuration provider</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/configuration-options/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (plain console app, .NET 8/9, top-level statements, BCL only). This models the real <code>Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration</code>/<code>Options</code> mechanics with plain dictionaries, since that&#39;s the simplest faithful way to check the merge and caching rules without spinning up a host.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: later configuration layers override earlier ones key-by-key, not file-by-file —</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// a layer that doesn't mention a key leaves whatever an earlier layer set untouched.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> layer1_appsettingsJson = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>?&gt;\n{\n    [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Payments:BaseUrl&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://sandbox.pay.example&quot;</span>,\n    [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Payments:TimeoutSeconds&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;10&quot;</span>,\n};\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> layer2_productionJson = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>?&gt;\n{\n    [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Payments:BaseUrl&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://api.pay.example&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-com\">// overrides layer 1</span>\n};\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> layer4_envVars = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>?&gt;\n{\n    [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ConnectionStrings:Shop&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Server=prod-sql;...&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-com\">// never appeared in either JSON file</span>\n};\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>? <span class=\"tk-ty\">Merged</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> key, <span class=\"tk-kw\">params</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>?&gt;[] layersLowToHigh)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>? <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> layer <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> layersLowToHigh)\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (layer.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGetValue</span>(key, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> v)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span> = v; <span class=\"tk-com\">// a later layer always wins if it mentions the key</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>;\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> baseUrl = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Merged</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Payments:BaseUrl&quot;</span>, layer1_appsettingsJson, layer2_productionJson, layer4_envVars);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> timeout = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Merged</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Payments:TimeoutSeconds&quot;</span>, layer1_appsettingsJson, layer2_productionJson, layer4_envVars);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> connStr = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Merged</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ConnectionStrings:Shop&quot;</span>, layer1_appsettingsJson, layer2_productionJson, layer4_envVars);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (baseUrl != <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://api.pay.example&quot;</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected the production override to win, got {baseUrl}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (timeout != <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;10&quot;</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected the un-overridden base value 10 to survive, got {timeout}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (connStr != <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Server=prod-sql;...&quot;</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected the connection string to come from the environment-variable layer&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: later layers override earlier ones key-by-key — untouched keys pass through unchanged&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: the environment-variable provider maps &quot;__&quot; to &quot;:&quot; when reading a key back.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">EnvVarNameFor</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> configKey) =&gt; configKey.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Replace</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;:&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;__&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ConfigKeyFor</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> envVarName) =&gt; envVarName.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Replace</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;__&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;:&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">EnvVarNameFor</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Payments:BaseUrl&quot;</span>) != <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Payments__BaseUrl&quot;</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: wrong env var name mapping&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">ConfigKeyFor</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Logging__LogLevel__Default&quot;</span>) != <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Logging:LogLevel:Default&quot;</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: wrong config key mapping&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: ':' in a configuration key round-trips to '__' in an environment variable name, and back&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: IOptions&lt;T&gt;-style caching computes the value once and never reflects a later change,</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// while IOptionsMonitor&lt;T&gt;-style access always re-reads the live source.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> liveSettings = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; { [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Feature&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;off&quot;</span> };\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> cachedOnce = liveSettings[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Feature&quot;</span>]; <span class=\"tk-com\">// IOptions&lt;T&gt;: computed once, like at first injection</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CurrentValue</span>() =&gt; liveSettings[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Feature&quot;</span>]; <span class=\"tk-com\">// IOptionsMonitor&lt;T&gt;.CurrentValue: re-reads every time</span>\n\nliveSettings[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Feature&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;on&quot;</span>; <span class=\"tk-com\">// an operator flips a feature flag at runtime</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (cachedOnce != <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;off&quot;</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: the IOptions&lt;T&gt;-style cached value should stay at its original snapshot&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">CurrentValue</span>() != <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;on&quot;</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: the IOptionsMonitor&lt;T&gt;-style accessor should see the live change&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: a cached-once value (IOptions&lt;T&gt;) misses a later change; a live accessor (IOptionsMonitor&lt;T&gt;.CurrentValue) sees it&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here — these need the real ASP.NET Core host and real Azure services:</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//  - the actual Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration provider pipeline and ValidateOnStart() failing a deployment</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//  - Azure Key Vault / App Configuration refresh intervals</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// See Further reading: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/configuration/</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-same-build-four-different-places-four-different-answers","text":"The same build, four different places, four different answers","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"the-five-layers-lowest-priority-to-highest","text":"The five layers, lowest priority to highest","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"reading-the-merged-view-keys-sections-and-converting-types","text":"Reading the merged view: keys, sections, and converting types","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"environment-variables-need-their-own-naming-convention","text":"Environment variables need their own naming convention","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"the-options-pattern-turning-strings-with-typos-into-a-typed-","text":"The Options pattern: turning strings-with-typos into a typed class","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"three-flavours-of-give-me-the-options-and-why-there-are-thre","text":"Three flavours of \"give me the options\", and why there are three","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"named-options-and-post-configuration-when-one-options-type-i","text":"Named options and post-configuration: when one options type isn't enough","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"secrets-the-layer-systems-hardest-case","text":"Secrets: the layer system's hardest case","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"in-development-user-secrets","text":"In development: User Secrets","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"in-production-no-file-on-disk-at-all","text":"In production: no file on disk at all","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"feature-flags-configuration-that-controls-behaviour-not-just","text":"Feature flags: configuration that controls behaviour , not just values","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"how-the-provider-mechanism-actually-works-underneath","text":"How the provider mechanism actually works underneath","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":4070,"minutes":19,"num":"3.3","part":"p3"},"middleware":{"id":"middleware","title":"Middleware and the Request Pipeline","summary":"The heart of ASP.NET Core. What middleware is, how the pipeline is literally built from delegates, Use/Run/Map/UseWhen, the correct order and why, every built-in middleware and where it goes, writing your own (conventional, IMiddleware, factory), short-circuiting, response timing traps, and middleware vs filters.","layer":"framework","where":"ASP.NET Core › Request pipeline › Middleware","fits":"<p>The pipeline is the <strong>spine of layer 3</strong>. Hosting (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting\">Hosting</a>) creates it, routing (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/routing\">Routing</a>) is one of its middleware, auth (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/auth\">Auth</a>) lives in it, and your endpoints (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/building-apis\">Building APIs</a>) sit at its end. Cross-cutting concerns from layer 8 (logging, exception handling, security headers, rate limiting) are mostly implemented here.</p>\n","prereqs":["aspnet-hosting","delegates-events","dependency-injection"],"next":["routing","errors-logging","auth"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/middleware/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Middleware</strong> is a chain of components every request passes through before reaching your actual endpoint — each one can look at the request, pass it on, and look at the response on the way back.</li>\n<li>Under the hood it&#39;s just nested functions: each middleware wraps the next one. That&#39;s why &quot;before&quot; code runs in registration order and &quot;after&quot; code runs in reverse.</li>\n<li>Order is real behavior, not a style choice — e.g. <code>UseAuthorization</code> must come after <code>UseAuthentication</code>, <code>UseCors</code> before auth, and <code>UseRouting</code> before anything that reads the selected endpoint&#39;s metadata.</li>\n<li>A middleware written as a plain class is built <strong>once</strong>, at startup — effectively a singleton. Never put a scoped dependency (like a <code>DbContext</code>) in its constructor; put it in <code>InvokeAsync</code>&#39;s parameters instead.</li>\n<li><strong>Short-circuiting</strong> means a middleware doesn&#39;t call <code>next()</code> and answers the request itself (auth failures, rate limits, cache hits, static files).</li>\n<li>Once the response has started sending, you can no longer change headers or the status code — use <code>Response.OnStarting</code> to set them just before that happens.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"every-request-walks-through-the-same-corridor-of-rooms\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/middleware/every-request-walks-through-the-same-corridor-of-rooms\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Every request walks through the same corridor of rooms <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Picture a request arriving at your app. Before it reaches the code you actually wrote — the handler that looks up an order, say — it has to pass through a sequence of checkpoints: is this HTTPS? does it need CORS headers? who&#39;s making this request, and are they allowed to? None of that is <em>your</em> endpoint&#39;s job to check every single time; it would be absurd to repeat &quot;is this HTTPS&quot; logic inside every single handler in the app.</p>\n<p><strong>Middleware</strong> is how ASP.NET Core avoids that repetition. Each middleware is a component sitting in a chain, and each one gets exactly four jobs available to it for every request:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>receive the <code>HttpContext</code>,</li>\n<li>optionally do work <strong>before</strong> the rest of the pipeline runs — inspect the request, start a timer, check a header,</li>\n<li><strong>either</strong> call <code>next()</code> to hand control to the next middleware in line, <strong>or</strong> stop right there (&quot;short-circuit&quot;) and write the response itself,</li>\n<li>optionally do work <strong>after</strong> <code>next()</code> returns — inspect the response that came back, log the final status code, stop the timer.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>           ┌───────────────────────── request ─────────────────────────▶\n Client ─▶ │ ExceptionHandler │ HTTPS │ StaticFiles │ Routing │ CORS │ AuthN │ AuthZ │ … │ ENDPOINT\n           ◀───────────────────────── response ────────────────────────┘\n             (each middleware's &quot;after next()&quot; code runs on the way back, in REVERSE order)</code></pre></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout c-analogy\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Russian dolls (or an onion)</div><p>Each middleware wraps everything that comes after it. The request travels inward through each layer to the endpoint at the centre, and the response travels back out through the same layers, but in reverse. A layer can also simply refuse to let the request go any further inward and send a response straight back — like a security guard at the door who doesn&#39;t bother calling upstairs.</p>\n</aside>\n<aside class=\"callout c-why\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Why build it as a pipeline at all, instead of one big function?</div><p>Error handling, HTTPS enforcement, CORS, authentication, compression, logging, correlation IDs, rate limiting — all of these apply to <strong>every</strong> request, or every request under some path, regardless of which specific endpoint eventually handles it. Writing each one as a separate, composable middleware means you write it <strong>once</strong>, slot it into a clear, explicit order, and turn it on or off with one line in <code>Program.cs</code>. This is a deliberate departure from the old <code>System.Web</code> model, which had a large, largely hidden pipeline you couldn&#39;t easily see or reorder. Here, if you don&#39;t add a middleware, it simply doesn&#39;t run — the pipeline is exactly what you wrote and nothing more.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"seeing-it-work-before-seeing-how-its-built\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/middleware/seeing-it-work-before-seeing-how-its-built\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Seeing it work before seeing how it&#39;s built <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> app = builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Use</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (context, next) =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> sw = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Stopwatch</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartNew</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;→ {context.Request.Method} {context.Request.Path}&quot;</span>);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// BEFORE</span>\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> next(context);                                                       <span class=\"tk-com\">// run the rest of the pipeline</span>\n\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;← {context.Response.StatusCode} in {sw.ElapsedMilliseconds} ms&quot;</span>);  <span class=\"tk-com\">// AFTER</span>\n});\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Use</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (context, next) =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (context.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Request</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ContainsKey</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;X-Block-Me&quot;</span>))\n    {\n        context.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Response</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCodes</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status403Forbidden</span>;          <span class=\"tk-com\">// SHORT-CIRCUIT:</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> context.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Response</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Blocked&quot;</span>);                          <span class=\"tk-com\">// don't call next</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span>;\n    }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> next(context);\n});\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/&quot;</span>, () =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Hello&quot;</span>);      <span class=\"tk-com\">// the endpoint at the end</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Run</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>For <code>GET /</code>, that prints:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>→ GET /\n← 200 in 3 ms</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Notice the shape: the first middleware&#39;s &quot;before&quot; line prints first, then control passes all the way down to the endpoint, then — only once everything downstream has finished — its &quot;after&quot; line prints. If a request came in carrying <code>X-Block-Me</code>, the second middleware would write <code>403 Blocked</code> and return, and the endpoint at <code>app.MapGet(&quot;/&quot;, ...)</code> would never run at all. Hold onto that example; the next section explains exactly why it behaves this way.</p>\n<h2 id=\"use-run-map-mapwhen-usewhen\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/middleware/use-run-map-mapwhen-usewhen\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Use, Run, Map, MapWhen, UseWhen <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>app.Use(middleware)</code></td>\n<td>Adds middleware that <strong>can</strong> call <code>next</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>app.Run(handler)</code></td>\n<td>Adds a <strong>terminal</strong> handler; it never calls next. Anything after it never runs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>app.Map(&quot;/path&quot;, branch =&gt; …)</code></td>\n<td><strong>Branches</strong> the pipeline for requests whose path starts with <code>/path</code>. The branch doesn&#39;t rejoin the main pipeline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>app.MapWhen(predicate, branch =&gt; …)</code></td>\n<td>Branches on any condition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>app.UseWhen(predicate, branch =&gt; …)</code></td>\n<td>Conditionally runs extra middleware, then <strong>rejoins</strong> the main pipeline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>app.UseMiddleware&lt;T&gt;()</code> / <code>app.UseXyz()</code></td>\n<td>Adds a middleware class (<code>UseXyz</code> are extension methods wrapping this)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Map</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/legacy&quot;</span>, legacy =&gt;\n{\n    legacy.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Run</span>(ctx =&gt; ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Response</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Legacy endpoint&quot;</span>));   <span class=\"tk-com\">// only /legacy/* goes here</span>\n});\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseWhen</span>(ctx =&gt; ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Request</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Path</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartsWithSegments</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/api&quot;</span>), api =&gt;\n{\n    api.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseMiddleware</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ApiKeyMiddleware</span>&gt;();      <span class=\"tk-com\">// only for /api/*, then continues to the main pipeline</span>\n});</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Map vs MapGet — same word, two unrelated mechanisms</div><p><code>app.Map(&quot;/x&quot;, ...)</code> branches the <strong>middleware pipeline</strong> itself — an older, low-level tool. <code>app.MapGet(&quot;/x&quot;, ...)</code> registers a <strong>routing endpoint</strong>, a completely different and much more commonly used feature covered in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/routing\">Routing</a>. They sound almost identical and that&#39;s exactly the trap; 99% of the time, you want <code>MapGet</code>/<code>MapPost</code>/<code>MapGroup</code>, not <code>Map</code>.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"now-heres-the-aha-a-middleware-is-just-a-function-wrapping-a\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/middleware/now-heres-the-aha-a-middleware-is-just-a-function-wrapping-a\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Now here&#39;s the aha: a middleware is just a function wrapping a function <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Go back to that worked example above and ask the question it was quietly setting up: how does <code>await next(context)</code> actually know what &quot;the rest of the pipeline&quot; even is? There&#39;s no global list being walked at request time — the real mechanism is simpler than that, and once you see it, middleware stops feeling like magic.</p>\n<p>The core type is one plain delegate:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"cHVibGljIGRlbGVnYXRlIFRhc2sgUmVxdWVzdERlbGVnYXRlKEh0dHBDb250ZXh0IGNvbnRleHQpOw==\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">delegate</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">RequestDelegate</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpContext</span> context);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>A middleware, stripped down to its essence, is a function that <strong>takes one <code>RequestDelegate</code> (the rest of the pipeline) and returns a new <code>RequestDelegate</code> that wraps it</strong>:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"RnVuYzxSZXF1ZXN0RGVsZWdhdGUsIFJlcXVlc3REZWxlZ2F0ZT4gbWlkZGxld2FyZQ==\"><code><span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequestDelegate</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">RequestDelegate</span>&gt; middleware</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Every call to <code>app.Use(...)</code> just appends one of these wrapping functions to a list — nothing executes yet. The actual composition happens once, when the host calls <code>Build()</code>, and it happens <strong>in reverse</strong>:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Simplified version of ApplicationBuilder.Build()</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequestDelegate</span> app = context =&gt; { context.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Response</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">404</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CompletedTask</span>; }; <span class=\"tk-com\">// the end</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = _components.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> - <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>; i &gt;= <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; i--)\n    app = _components[i](app);        <span class=\"tk-com\">// each middleware wraps the one after it</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> app;                           <span class=\"tk-com\">// the outermost delegate: the first middleware</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Walk through what this does with three middleware, <code>Use(A); Use(B); Use(C);</code>, and a terminal endpoint. The loop starts from the <em>last</em> component, <code>C</code>, and wraps it around the 404 fallback: <code>C(endpoint)</code>. Then it wraps <code>B</code> around that: <code>B(C(endpoint))</code>. Then <code>A</code> around all of it: <code>A(B(C(endpoint)))</code>. The diagram below traces exactly this: Kestrel ends up holding a single outermost delegate, <code>A</code>, and calls it once per request.</p>\n<p><img src=\"visuals/middleware-pipeline.svg\" alt=\"The middleware pipeline composed as nested delegates, with the request flowing inward and the response flowing back out in reverse\"></p>\n<p>Follow the request&#39;s path through that nesting and the reversal in the worked example stops being surprising. Calling <code>A</code>&#39;s <code>next</code> invokes <code>B</code>&#39;s whole delegate. <code>B</code> calling its <code>next</code> invokes <code>C</code>&#39;s. <code>C</code> calling <code>next</code> finally reaches the endpoint. Each of those calls is still <em>running</em> — sitting on the call stack — waiting for the one it invoked to return. So the endpoint finishes and returns up to <code>C</code>. <code>C</code>&#39;s code after <code>await next()</code> runs and returns up to <code>B</code>. That code runs and returns up to <code>A</code>. You get exactly the reverse order the pipeline diagram at the top of this chapter showed. It isn&#39;t a special rule the framework enforces; it&#39;s just how nested function calls unwind — the same way any <code>await</code> in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/async-await\">Async and await</a> resumes its caller only once the thing it awaited is done.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"Ly8gRXhhY3RseSBlcXVpdmFsZW50IHRvIGFwcC5Vc2UoYXN5bmMgKGN0eCwgbmV4dCkgPT4gLi4uKQphcHAuVXNlKG5leHQgPT4gYXN5bmMgY29udGV4dCA9Pgp7CiAgICAvLyBiZWZvcmUKICAgIGF3YWl0IG5leHQoY29udGV4dCk7CiAgICAvLyBhZnRlcgp9KTs=\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Exactly equivalent to app.Use(async (ctx, next) =&gt; ...)</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Use</span>(next =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> context =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-com\">// before</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> next(context);\n    <span class=\"tk-com\">// after</span>\n});</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h2 id=\"the-correct-order-and-precisely-what-breaks-when-you-get-it-\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/middleware/the-correct-order-and-precisely-what-breaks-when-you-get-it-\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The correct order, and precisely what breaks when you get it wrong <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Since each middleware only ever sees what the ones <em>before</em> it have already done, and only wraps what comes <em>after</em> it, the order you register things in isn&#39;t a style choice — it&#39;s the actual behaviour. Here&#39;s Microsoft&#39;s recommended order for a typical API or web app:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> app = builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseForwardedHeaders</span>();          <span class=\"tk-com\">// 0. Behind a proxy: fix scheme/IP FIRST so everything after sees the real values</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseExceptionHandler</span>();          <span class=\"tk-com\">// 1. Outermost: catches exceptions from EVERYTHING after it</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsDevelopment</span>())\n    app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseHsts</span>();                  <span class=\"tk-com\">// 2. Tell browsers to always use HTTPS</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseHttpsRedirection</span>();          <span class=\"tk-com\">// 3. Redirect http → https</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseStaticFiles</span>();               <span class=\"tk-com\">// 4. Serve wwwroot files and short-circuit: no auth/routing cost for images, css</span>\n                                    <span class=\"tk-com\">//    (or app.MapStaticAssets() in .NET 9+, as an endpoint)</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseRouting</span>();                   <span class=\"tk-com\">// 5. Choose the endpoint (implicit if omitted; explicit call controls position)</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseRequestLocalization</span>();       <span class=\"tk-com\">//    Culture from headers/query/cookies</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseCors</span>();                      <span class=\"tk-com\">// 6. After routing (to read endpoint CORS metadata), before auth (preflight requests carry no credentials)</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseAuthentication</span>();            <span class=\"tk-com\">// 7. Who are you? Sets HttpContext.User</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseAuthorization</span>();             <span class=\"tk-com\">// 8. Are you allowed? Reads the endpoint's [Authorize]/policy metadata</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseRateLimiter</span>();               <span class=\"tk-com\">// 9. After auth if limits are per-user</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseRequestTimeouts</span>();\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseOutputCache</span>();               <span class=\"tk-com\">// 10. Serve cached responses (after auth, so cache respects authorization)</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseResponseCompression</span>();       <span class=\"tk-com\">//    (often before static files; depends on whether you compress them)</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseMiddleware</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CorrelationIdMiddleware</span>&gt;();   <span class=\"tk-com\">// your custom middleware</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapControllers</span>();               <span class=\"tk-com\">// 11. Endpoints (endpoint middleware executes the chosen endpoint)</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapOrderEndpoints</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Each row below is a real-world bug report with the same root cause: a middleware placed before something it secretly depended on.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>If you get this wrong…</th>\n<th>Symptom</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>UseAuthorization</code> before <code>UseAuthentication</code></td>\n<td>Every request looks anonymous: 401/403 everywhere</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>UseAuthentication</code>/<code>UseAuthorization</code> before <code>UseRouting</code> (explicit)</td>\n<td>Authorization can&#39;t see endpoint metadata, so <code>[Authorize]</code> is ignored or throws</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exception handler not first</td>\n<td>Exceptions from earlier middleware aren&#39;t converted to ProblemDetails</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>UseCors</code> after auth</td>\n<td>Preflight <code>OPTIONS</code> requests rejected with 401: &quot;CORS error&quot; in the browser</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>UseStaticFiles</code> after auth</td>\n<td>Static files require auth, and every image request pays the auth cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>UseForwardedHeaders</code> late</td>\n<td>HTTPS redirect loops; wrong client IPs in logs and rate limiting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Custom middleware that needs <code>User</code> placed before <code>UseAuthentication</code></td>\n<td><code>context.User</code> is empty</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-interview\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Say it like this</div><p>&quot;Middleware runs in registration order on the way in and reverse order on the way out. The exception handler goes first so it wraps everything; HTTPS and static files go early; routing selects the endpoint; CORS, then authentication then authorization, because authorization needs both the user and the endpoint&#39;s metadata; then rate limiting and caching; endpoints last.&quot;</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"the-built-in-middleware-catalogue\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/middleware/the-built-in-middleware-catalogue\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The built-in middleware catalogue <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Middleware</th>\n<th>Add with</th>\n<th>Purpose</th>\n<th>Short-circuits?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Forwarded headers</td>\n<td><code>UseForwardedHeaders</code></td>\n<td>Apply <code>X-Forwarded-*</code> from a trusted proxy</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exception handler</td>\n<td><code>UseExceptionHandler</code></td>\n<td>Catch exceptions → ProblemDetails / error page</td>\n<td>Writes error response</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Developer exception page</td>\n<td>(automatic in Dev)</td>\n<td>Detailed error page with stack trace</td>\n<td>Yes, on error</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Status code pages</td>\n<td><code>UseStatusCodePages</code></td>\n<td>Bodies for empty 4xx/5xx responses</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HSTS</td>\n<td><code>UseHsts</code></td>\n<td><code>Strict-Transport-Security</code> header</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>HTTPS redirection</td>\n<td><code>UseHttpsRedirection</code></td>\n<td>307/308 redirect to https</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Static files</td>\n<td><code>UseStaticFiles</code> / <code>MapStaticAssets</code></td>\n<td>Serve files from wwwroot</td>\n<td>Yes, when file found</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Routing</td>\n<td><code>UseRouting</code></td>\n<td>Match URL to endpoint</td>\n<td>No (sets endpoint)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Request localization</td>\n<td><code>UseRequestLocalization</code></td>\n<td>Set culture</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CORS</td>\n<td><code>UseCors</code></td>\n<td>Cross-origin headers, answer preflight</td>\n<td>Yes, for preflight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Authentication</td>\n<td><code>UseAuthentication</code></td>\n<td>Build <code>HttpContext.User</code></td>\n<td>No (challenge comes later)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Authorization</td>\n<td><code>UseAuthorization</code></td>\n<td>Enforce policies</td>\n<td>Yes: 401/403</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Antiforgery</td>\n<td><code>UseAntiforgery</code></td>\n<td>CSRF token validation for forms</td>\n<td>Yes, on failure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rate limiter</td>\n<td><code>UseRateLimiter</code></td>\n<td>Throttle</td>\n<td>Yes: 429</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Request timeouts</td>\n<td><code>UseRequestTimeouts</code></td>\n<td>Cancel slow requests</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Output caching</td>\n<td><code>UseOutputCache</code></td>\n<td>Cache whole responses server-side</td>\n<td>Yes, on hit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Response caching</td>\n<td><code>UseResponseCaching</code></td>\n<td>HTTP cache-header based caching</td>\n<td>Yes, on hit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Response compression</td>\n<td><code>UseResponseCompression</code></td>\n<td>gzip/brotli</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Session</td>\n<td><code>UseSession</code></td>\n<td>Server-side session state</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>WebSockets</td>\n<td><code>UseWebSockets</code></td>\n<td>Upgrade connections</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Health checks</td>\n<td><code>MapHealthChecks</code> (endpoint)</td>\n<td><code>/health</code> responses</td>\n<td>(endpoint)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>W3C logging / HTTP logging</td>\n<td><code>UseHttpLogging</code>, <code>UseW3CLogging</code></td>\n<td>Log request/response details</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Endpoint</td>\n<td>(implicit at the end)</td>\n<td>Execute the selected endpoint</td>\n<td>Terminal</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"writing-your-own-middleware-three-styles-one-real-tradeoff\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/middleware/writing-your-own-middleware-three-styles-one-real-tradeoff\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Writing your own middleware: three styles, one real tradeoff <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Inline lambdas like the ones above are fine for a handful of lines you won&#39;t reuse. For anything reusable, write a class — and there are three ways to do that, each trading off a little speed for a little safety.</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-convention-based-middleware-the-common-case\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/middleware/1-convention-based-middleware-the-common-case\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>1. Convention-based middleware (the common case)</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CorrelationIdMiddleware</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequestDelegate</span> next, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ILogger</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CorrelationIdMiddleware</span>&gt; logger)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">const</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Header</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;X-Correlation-Id&quot;</span>;\n\n    <span class=\"tk-com\">// Constructor: runs ONCE at startup. Only singleton-safe dependencies here.</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-com\">// InvokeAsync: runs per request. Scoped dependencies go in its PARAMETERS.</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvokeAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpContext</span> context, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ICurrentRequest</span> current)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> id = context.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Request</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span><span class=\"tk-attr\">[Header]</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FirstOrDefault</span>() ?? <span class=\"tk-ty\">Activity</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Current</span>?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TraceId</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>() ?? <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NewGuid</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;n&quot;</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-com\">// Activity.Current is .NET's built-in distributed-tracing object: if one is already running for this</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-com\">// request, its TraceId makes a better correlation ID than a random GUID. More in Observability.</span>\n        current.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CorrelationId</span> = id;\n        context.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Response</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OnStarting</span>(() =&gt; { context.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Response</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span><span class=\"tk-attr\">[Header]</span> = id; <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CompletedTask</span>; });\n\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> (logger.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BeginScope</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>&gt; { [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;CorrelationId&quot;</span>] = id }))\n        {\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> next(context);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Registration: an extension method keeps Program.cs readable</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CorrelationIdExtensions</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IApplicationBuilder</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">UseCorrelationId</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">this</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IApplicationBuilder</span> app) =&gt; app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseMiddleware</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CorrelationIdMiddleware</span>&gt;();\n}\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseCorrelationId</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The rules behind the convention: a public constructor taking <code>RequestDelegate</code> first, a public <code>Invoke</code> or <code>InvokeAsync</code> method taking <code>HttpContext</code> first and returning <code>Task</code>, and any extra <code>InvokeAsync</code> parameters get resolved fresh from the <strong>request scope</strong> every time.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Conventional middleware is effectively a singleton — and that has teeth</div><p>It&#39;s constructed exactly <strong>once</strong>, when the pipeline is built at startup, not once per request. Inject a <strong>scoped</strong> service — a <code>DbContext</code>, say — into its <strong>constructor</strong>, and you&#39;ve captured the very first request&#39;s instance and trapped it there forever: a captive dependency, the same failure mode <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/the-captive-dependency-the-single-most-common-di-bug\">Dependency injection</a> covers. Scoped services belong in <code>InvokeAsync</code>&#39;s parameters, which the framework correctly re-resolves on every call.</p>\n</aside>\n<h3 id=\"2-factory-based-middleware-imiddleware\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/middleware/2-factory-based-middleware-imiddleware\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>2. Factory-based middleware: IMiddleware</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TenantMiddleware</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span> db, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ITenantContext</span> tenant) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IMiddleware</span>   <span class=\"tk-com\">// scoped deps in ctor are fine</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvokeAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpContext</span> context, <span class=\"tk-ty\">RequestDelegate</span> next)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> host = context.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Request</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Host</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Host</span>;\n        tenant.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Current</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Tenants</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsNoTracking</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">FirstOrDefaultAsync</span>(t =&gt; t.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Host</span> == host, context.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequestAborted</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (tenant.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Current</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>) { context.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Response</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">404</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span>; }\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> next(context);\n    }\n}\n\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TenantMiddleware</span>&gt;();   <span class=\"tk-com\">// must be REGISTERED in DI (any lifetime)</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseMiddleware</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TenantMiddleware</span>&gt;();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The difference that justifies the extra ceremony: <code>IMiddleware</code> instances are <strong>resolved from DI per request</strong>, honouring whatever lifetime you registered, which is exactly why constructor injection of a scoped service is safe here when it wasn&#39;t above. It costs a little more — an extra DI resolution per request — in exchange for not having to think about the captive-dependency trap at all.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-inline-with-app-use\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/middleware/3-inline-with-app-use\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>3. Inline with app.Use</h3>\n<p>Best for a handful of lines you genuinely won&#39;t reuse anywhere else. Since .NET 6 there&#39;s an overload, <code>app.Use(async (context, next) =&gt; …)</code>, where <code>next</code> is a plain <code>RequestDelegate</code> rather than the older <code>Func&lt;Task&gt;</code> — worth knowing because it avoids an extra allocation per request compared with the older form.</p>\n<h2 id=\"short-circuiting-and-terminal-middleware\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/middleware/short-circuiting-and-terminal-middleware\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Short-circuiting and terminal middleware <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Short-circuiting — the &quot;security guard at the door&quot; move from the opening analogy — just means deliberately <strong>not calling <code>next</code></strong>. The places you&#39;ll see it on purpose:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Authorization failure: 401/403.</li>\n<li>Rate limit exceeded: 429.</li>\n<li>Cache hit: serve the stored response.</li>\n<li>Static file found: stream it.</li>\n<li>Health or ping endpoints that should skip everything.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"Ly8gLk5FVCA4OiBlbmRwb2ludHMgdGhhdCBza2lwIHRoZSByZW1haW5pbmcgbWlkZGxld2FyZSBlbnRpcmVseQphcHAuTWFwR2V0KCIvcGluZyIsICgpID0+ICJwb25nIikuU2hvcnRDaXJjdWl0KCk7CmFwcC5NYXBTaG9ydENpcmN1aXQoNDA0LCAicm9ib3RzLnR4dCIsICJmYXZpY29uLmljbyIpOyAgIC8vIGZhc3QgNDA0cyBmb3Igbm9pc2UgcmVxdWVzdHM=\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// .NET 8: endpoints that skip the remaining middleware entirely</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/ping&quot;</span>, () =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;pong&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShortCircuit</span>();\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapShortCircuit</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">404</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;robots.txt&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;favicon.ico&quot;</span>);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// fast 404s for noise requests</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h2 id=\"working-with-the-response-the-has-started-trap\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/middleware/working-with-the-response-the-has-started-trap\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Working with the response: the &quot;has started&quot; trap <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Once the response&#39;s <strong>headers have been sent</strong> — the moment the body starts being written, or the first explicit flush — you can no longer touch the status code or headers. Try anyway, and you get a precise, unforgiving exception: <code>InvalidOperationException: Headers are read-only, response has already started.</code></p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Use</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (ctx, next) =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> next(ctx);\n    ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Response</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span>[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;X-Server-Time&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTime</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UtcNow</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;O&quot;</span>);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✘ may throw if the endpoint wrote a body</span>\n});\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Use</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (ctx, next) =&gt;\n{\n    ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Response</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OnStarting</span>(() =&gt;                                              <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✔ runs just before headers are sent</span>\n    {\n        ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Response</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span>[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;X-Server-Time&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTime</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UtcNow</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;O&quot;</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CompletedTask</span>;\n    });\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> next(ctx);\n});</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Why does the first version only <em>sometimes</em> throw? Because &quot;has the body started writing&quot; depends on exactly what the endpoint did. A small enough response might still be sitting in a buffer when your &quot;after <code>next()</code>&quot; code runs, and only starts streaming afterward — so the bug hides until someone&#39;s response happens to be large enough to flush early. <code>Response.OnStarting</code> sidesteps the whole race: it registers a callback guaranteed to run at the one moment that&#39;s actually safe. Check <code>context.Response.HasStarted</code> before attempting to write an error response, for the same reason. <code>Response.OnCompleted</code> is different again — it runs after the response has fully gone out, which makes it useful for metrics but useless for modifying anything.</p>\n<h3 id=\"reading-the-request-body-more-than-once\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/middleware/reading-the-request-body-more-than-once\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Reading the request body more than once</h3>\n<p>The request body arrives as a forward-only stream — read it once, and it&#39;s gone. If a middleware reads it early (for logging, or to verify a signature), the endpoint downstream finds nothing left, unless you explicitly enable rewinding first:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Use</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (ctx, next) =&gt;\n{\n    ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Request</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">EnableBuffering</span>();                    <span class=\"tk-com\">// allow rewinding (buffers to memory, then disk)</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> reader = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">StreamReader</span>(ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Request</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Body</span>, leaveOpen: <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> body = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> reader.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadToEndAsync</span>();\n    ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Request</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Body</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Position</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;                    <span class=\"tk-com\">// rewind for the next reader</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> next(ctx);\n});</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h3 id=\"rewriting-the-response-body\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/middleware/rewriting-the-response-body\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Rewriting the response body</h3>\n<p>To transform a response on the way out, swap <code>Response.Body</code> for a <code>MemoryStream</code>, call <code>next</code>, then copy the buffered result back into the real response stream. This is a genuine tradeoff, not a free trick: it costs memory (you&#39;re holding the entire response in memory) and it breaks true streaming (nothing reaches the client until you&#39;re done). Prefer the built-in middleware that already does this carefully — compression, output caching — or an endpoint filter, before reaching for this by hand.</p>\n<h2 id=\"middleware-vs-filters-vs-endpoint-filters\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/middleware/middleware-vs-filters-vs-endpoint-filters\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Middleware vs filters vs endpoint filters <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Middleware</th>\n<th>MVC filters (controllers)</th>\n<th>Endpoint filters (minimal APIs)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Runs for</td>\n<td><strong>Every</strong> request through that pipeline position</td>\n<td>Only MVC actions</td>\n<td>Only the endpoints they&#39;re attached to</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Knows about</td>\n<td><code>HttpContext</code> (raw HTTP)</td>\n<td>Action descriptor, model state, <strong>bound arguments</strong>, <code>IActionResult</code></td>\n<td>Endpoint <strong>arguments</strong>, returned result</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Runs</td>\n<td>Before routing selects, or around the endpoint</td>\n<td>Inside the endpoint, after binding</td>\n<td>Inside the endpoint, after binding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Good for</td>\n<td>App-wide concerns: errors, HTTPS, CORS, auth, compression, correlation IDs, rate limits</td>\n<td>Action-specific validation, transactions, auditing, caching per action</td>\n<td>Validation, argument transformation, per-endpoint auditing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The distinction that actually matters here is what each one is allowed to <em>see</em>. Middleware only ever sees the raw HTTP context — headers, the body as a stream, the path — because it runs before (or around) the model-binding machinery that turns a JSON body into a typed C# object. A filter runs <em>after</em> binding, so it can inspect the already-typed, already-bound argument directly:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Endpoint filter: sees the typed arguments, which middleware can't</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidationFilter</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IValidator</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; validator) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IEndpointFilter</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>?&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvokeAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">EndpointFilterInvocationContext</span> ctx, <span class=\"tk-ty\">EndpointFilterDelegate</span> next)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Arguments</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OfType</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">FirstOrDefault</span>() <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> { } arg)\n        {\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> result = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> validator.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidateAsync</span>(arg, ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpContext</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequestAborted</span>);\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!result.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsValid</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TypedResults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidationProblem</span>(result.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToDictionary</span>());\n        }\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> next(ctx);\n    }\n}\norders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapPost</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateOrder</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddEndpointFilter</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidationFilter</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateOrderRequest</span>&gt;&gt;();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The rule of thumb that falls out of this: <strong>if it needs to know about the request as raw HTTP, use middleware; if it needs to know about the action and its already-typed inputs, use a filter.</strong> See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/building-apis/filters-seeing-the-handlers-actual-typed-arguments\">Building APIs</a> for the MVC filter pipeline in full.</p>\n<h2 id=\"middleware-that-reads-endpoint-metadata\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/middleware/middleware-that-reads-endpoint-metadata\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Middleware that reads endpoint metadata <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Here&#39;s something that looks like it contradicts the rule just given, but doesn&#39;t: after <code>UseRouting</code> has run, middleware placed <em>later</em> in the pipeline <strong>can</strong> see the selected endpoint and whatever metadata it carries — attributes, <code>.WithMetadata(...)</code>, <code>.RequireAuthorization()</code>. That&#39;s precisely how authorization, CORS, rate limiting and output caching each know what to do for a given endpoint without being filters themselves. You can use the exact same trick in your own middleware:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AuditAttribute</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Action</span>);\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Use</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (ctx, next) =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> audit = ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetEndpoint</span>()?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Metadata</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetMetadata</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuditAttribute</span>&gt;();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> next(ctx);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (audit <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">not</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span> &amp;&amp; ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Response</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span> &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">400</span>)\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequestServices</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IAuditLog</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteAsync</span>(audit.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Action</span>, ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">User</span>);\n});\n\norders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapPost</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/{id}/refund&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Refund</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithMetadata</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AuditAttribute</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;order.refund&quot;</span>));</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h2 id=\"testing-middleware\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/middleware/testing-middleware\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Testing middleware <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-attr\">[Fact]</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Adds_correlation_header</span>()\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> host = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HostBuilder</span>()\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">ConfigureWebHost</span>(web =&gt; web.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseTestServer</span>()\n            .<span class=\"tk-ty\">ConfigureServices</span>(s =&gt; s.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ICurrentRequest</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CurrentRequest</span>&gt;())\n            .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configure</span>(app =&gt; { app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseCorrelationId</span>(); app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Run</span>(ctx =&gt; ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Response</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ok&quot;</span>)); }))\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartAsync</span>();\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> response = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> host.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetTestClient</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Assert</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">True</span>(response.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contains</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;X-Correlation-Id&quot;</span>));\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Or, for a pure unit test with no host at all, call <code>InvokeAsync</code> directly with a <code>DefaultHttpContext</code> and a fake <code>RequestDelegate</code>.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is middleware in ASP.NET Core?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Components assembled into a pipeline that handle every HTTP request and response. Each receives the HttpContext, can do work before and after invoking the next component, or can short-circuit by producing the response itself. The pipeline is built from RequestDelegate functions composed in registration order.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why does middleware order matter? Give examples.</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Each middleware only sees the effects of the ones before it and wraps the ones after it. The exception handler must be first to catch everything. Authentication must precede authorization or users look anonymous. Routing must precede authorization so endpoint metadata like [Authorize] is available. CORS must run before auth so preflight requests aren&#39;t rejected. Static files go early to avoid unnecessary work.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What&#39;s the difference between Use, Run and Map?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Use adds middleware that can call next. Run adds terminal middleware that ends the pipeline. Map branches the pipeline for a path prefix, and the branch doesn&#39;t rejoin. UseWhen conditionally adds middleware and then rejoins the main pipeline.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How is the middleware pipeline built internally?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Each middleware is a Func&lt;RequestDelegate, RequestDelegate&gt;. On startup, ApplicationBuilder.Build composes them in reverse order, starting with a terminal 404 delegate, so each wraps the next, producing one outermost RequestDelegate that the server invokes per request.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you write custom middleware, and how do you inject scoped services into it?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Create a class with a constructor taking RequestDelegate and an InvokeAsync(HttpContext, …) method, then register it with app.UseMiddleware&lt;T&gt;(). The instance is created once, so scoped services must be InvokeAsync parameters, not constructor parameters. Alternatively implement IMiddleware and register it in DI, which resolves it per request and allows constructor injection of scoped services.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What does short-circuiting mean in the pipeline?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A middleware not calling next and writing the response itself, so later middleware and the endpoint never run. Examples: auth failures (401/403), rate limiting (429), cache hits, static files, CORS preflight responses.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why might setting a response header after await next() fail?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Once the response has started (headers flushed because the body began writing), headers and status code are read-only, and modifying them throws. Use Response.OnStarting to set headers just before they&#39;re sent, and check Response.HasStarted before writing error responses.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Middleware vs filters: when do you use each?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Middleware for app-wide, HTTP-level concerns that apply regardless of endpoint (errors, HTTPS, CORS, auth, compression, correlation IDs). Filters (MVC or endpoint filters) for endpoint-specific concerns that need the action context or the typed, bound arguments and results (validation, auditing, transactions).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How can middleware read the request body without breaking the endpoint?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Call Request.EnableBuffering() so the stream can be rewound, read it (leaving the stream open), then set Request.Body.Position = 0 before calling next.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How does authorization middleware know which policy an endpoint needs?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Routing runs first and attaches the selected endpoint to the HttpContext. Endpoint metadata (from [Authorize], RequireAuthorization, etc.) is available via context.GetEndpoint().Metadata, which the authorization middleware reads to evaluate the right policies.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/middleware/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/middleware/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ASP.NET Core Middleware (official, including the order diagram)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/middleware/write\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Write custom ASP.NET Core middleware</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/middleware/extensibility\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Factory-based middleware</a></li>\n<li>Andrew Lock, <em>ASP.NET Core in Action</em>, chapter on the middleware pipeline.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/middleware/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run</code> in a console project (<code>dotnet new web</code>, then <code>dotnet add package Microsoft.AspNetCore.TestHost</code>). It checks the before/after ordering, short-circuiting, that conventional middleware is built once, and that <code>UseWhen</code> rejoins the main pipeline.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AspNetCore</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Builder</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AspNetCore</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TestHost</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Extensions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DependencyInjection</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> condition, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> claim)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!condition) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: {claim}&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: {claim}&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 1. &quot;Before&quot; code runs in registration order (A, B); &quot;after&quot; code runs in REVERSE order (B, A).</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> order = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> builder1 = <span class=\"tk-ty\">WebApplication</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateBuilder</span>();\nbuilder1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WebHost</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseTestServer</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> app1 = builder1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();\napp1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Use</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (ctx, next) =&gt; { order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A-before&quot;</span>); <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> next(ctx); order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A-after&quot;</span>); });\napp1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Use</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (ctx, next) =&gt; { order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;B-before&quot;</span>); <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> next(ctx); order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;B-after&quot;</span>); });\napp1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/&quot;</span>, () =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ok&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetTestClient</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetStringAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StopAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SequenceEqual</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>[] { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A-before&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;B-before&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;B-after&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A-after&quot;</span> }),\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Middleware runs 'before' code in registration order and 'after' code in reverse order&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 2. Short-circuiting (not calling next()) stops everything after it, including the endpoint, from running.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> endpointRan = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> builder2 = <span class=\"tk-ty\">WebApplication</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateBuilder</span>();\nbuilder2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WebHost</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseTestServer</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> app2 = builder2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();\napp2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Use</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (ctx, next) =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Request</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ContainsKey</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;X-Block-Me&quot;</span>)) { ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Response</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">403</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> next(ctx);\n});\napp2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/&quot;</span>, () =&gt; { endpointRan = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ok&quot;</span>; });\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> client2 = app2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetTestClient</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> req = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpRequestMessage</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpMethod</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Get</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/&quot;</span>);\nreq.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;X-Block-Me&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;1&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> blocked = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> client2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SendAsync</span>(req);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StopAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(blocked.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span> == <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Net</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpStatusCode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Forbidden</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A short-circuiting middleware returns its own status code&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(!endpointRan, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Short-circuiting prevents the endpoint from ever running&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 3. Conventional middleware (used with app.UseMiddleware&lt;T&gt;()) is constructed ONCE at startup,</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//    not once per request — so it behaves like a singleton even with no DI lifetime involved.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">CountingMiddleware</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ConstructedCount</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> builder3 = <span class=\"tk-ty\">WebApplication</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateBuilder</span>();\nbuilder3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WebHost</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseTestServer</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> app3 = builder3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();\napp3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseMiddleware</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CountingMiddleware</span>&gt;();\napp3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/&quot;</span>, () =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ok&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> client3 = app3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetTestClient</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> client3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetStringAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> client3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetStringAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> client3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetStringAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StopAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CountingMiddleware</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ConstructedCount</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Conventional middleware is constructed exactly once for 3 separate requests, not once per request&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 4. UseWhen conditionally runs extra middleware and REJOINS the main pipeline (the endpoint still runs).</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> branchRan = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> mainEndpointRan = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> builder4 = <span class=\"tk-ty\">WebApplication</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateBuilder</span>();\nbuilder4.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WebHost</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseTestServer</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> app4 = builder4.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();\napp4.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseWhen</span>(ctx =&gt; ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Request</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Path</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartsWithSegments</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/api&quot;</span>), api =&gt; api.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Use</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (ctx, next) =&gt; { branchRan = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> next(ctx); }));\napp4.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/api/ping&quot;</span>, () =&gt; { mainEndpointRan = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;pong&quot;</span>; });\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app4.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app4.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetTestClient</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetStringAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/api/ping&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app4.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StopAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(branchRan &amp;&amp; mainEndpointRan, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;UseWhen runs the branch's middleware AND still reaches the main endpoint afterward&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All middleware claims verified.&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CountingMiddleware</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ConstructedCount</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AspNetCore</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Http</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequestDelegate</span> _next;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CountingMiddleware</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AspNetCore</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Http</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequestDelegate</span> next) { _next = next; <span class=\"tk-ty\">ConstructedCount</span>++; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvokeAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AspNetCore</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Http</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpContext</span> context) =&gt; _next(context);\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Not attempted here: <code>IMiddleware</code>&#39;s per-request DI resolution and the response-already-started exception both need a slightly heavier TestServer setup than is worth inlining — try them yourself with the code earlier in this chapter; the behaviour described matches the official middleware docs linked above.</p>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"every-request-walks-through-the-same-corridor-of-rooms","text":"Every request walks through the same corridor of rooms","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"seeing-it-work-before-seeing-how-its-built","text":"Seeing it work before seeing how it's built","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"use-run-map-mapwhen-usewhen","text":"Use, Run, Map, MapWhen, UseWhen","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"now-heres-the-aha-a-middleware-is-just-a-function-wrapping-a","text":"Now here's the aha: a middleware is just a function wrapping a function","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"the-correct-order-and-precisely-what-breaks-when-you-get-it-","text":"The correct order, and precisely what breaks when you get it wrong","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"the-built-in-middleware-catalogue","text":"The built-in middleware catalogue","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"writing-your-own-middleware-three-styles-one-real-tradeoff","text":"Writing your own middleware: three styles, one real tradeoff","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"1-convention-based-middleware-the-common-case","text":"1. Convention-based middleware (the common case)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"2-factory-based-middleware-imiddleware","text":"2. Factory-based middleware: IMiddleware","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"3-inline-with-app-use","text":"3. Inline with app.Use","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"short-circuiting-and-terminal-middleware","text":"Short-circuiting and terminal middleware","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"working-with-the-response-the-has-started-trap","text":"Working with the response: the \"has started\" trap","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"reading-the-request-body-more-than-once","text":"Reading the request body more than once","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"rewriting-the-response-body","text":"Rewriting the response body","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"middleware-vs-filters-vs-endpoint-filters","text":"Middleware vs filters vs endpoint filters","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"middleware-that-reads-endpoint-metadata","text":"Middleware that reads endpoint metadata","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"testing-middleware","text":"Testing middleware","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":5189,"minutes":24,"num":"3.4","part":"p3"},"routing":{"id":"routing","title":"Routing and Endpoints","summary":"How a URL is matched to code. Endpoint routing and why it replaced the old model, route templates, parameters and constraints, route groups, attribute and conventional routing, endpoint metadata, ambiguity and precedence, and link generation.","layer":"framework","where":"ASP.NET Core › Request pipeline › Routing & endpoints","fits":"<p>Routing is a pair of middleware (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/middleware\">Middleware</a>) inside the pipeline: one <strong>selects</strong> the endpoint and another, at the end, <strong>executes</strong> it. Everything between them can read the chosen endpoint&#39;s metadata. The endpoints themselves are what <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/building-apis\">Building APIs</a> is about.</p>\n","prereqs":["middleware"],"next":["building-apis","auth"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/routing/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Routing&#39;s job: turn an HTTP method + path into one <strong>endpoint</strong> — code, metadata, and the values extracted from the URL.</li>\n<li>It&#39;s split into two middleware: one that <strong>picks</strong> the endpoint, one that <strong>runs</strong> it. Everything in between (CORS, auth, rate limiting) can read that endpoint&#39;s metadata first.</li>\n<li>Route templates (<code>{id:int}</code>) extract and constrain values. A failed constraint means &quot;no route matches&quot; (404), not &quot;bad input&quot; (400).</li>\n<li>Route groups let many endpoints share a prefix and a set of rules (auth, tags, rate limits) instead of repeating them on every endpoint.</li>\n<li>When two routes could both match, the more specific one wins; a genuine tie throws <code>AmbiguousMatchException</code> at runtime.</li>\n<li>Build links from a route&#39;s <strong>name</strong> (<code>LinkGenerator</code>), not a hand-built string, so they survive the template changing later.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-question-routing-exists-to-answer\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/routing/the-question-routing-exists-to-answer\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The question routing exists to answer <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>A request arrives as nothing more than an HTTP method and a URL path — <code>GET /api/orders/42</code>. Somewhere, that has to turn into an actual decision: <em>which piece of your code runs, and what are its inputs?</em> That translation is routing&#39;s entire job: map the incoming request to an <strong>endpoint</strong> — a runnable unit of code (a <code>RequestDelegate</code>, underneath) bundled together with <strong>metadata</strong> describing it.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>GET /api/orders/42      ──▶  endpoint &quot;GET /api/orders/{id:int}&quot;\n                              handler:  OrderEndpoints.GetById\n                              metadata: [Authorize(&quot;CanRead&quot;)], [Tags(&quot;Orders&quot;)], RateLimiting(&quot;per-user&quot;),\n                                        Produces&lt;OrderDto&gt;(200), Produces(404) …\n                              route values: { id = 42 }</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Notice that the result isn&#39;t just &quot;call this method&quot; — it&#39;s a whole bundle: the handler, a pile of metadata describing authorization and rate-limiting policies and documentation tags, and the actual route values (<code>id = 42</code>) extracted from the URL. That metadata turns out to matter more than it looks, as the next section explains.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-routing-had-to-be-split-into-two-separate-middleware\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/routing/why-routing-had-to-be-split-into-two-separate-middleware\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Why routing had to be split into two separate middleware <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Since ASP.NET Core 3.0, routing isn&#39;t one step — it&#39;s deliberately <strong>two</strong> middleware, with a gap in between that the rest of the pipeline uses:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong><code>UseRouting</code></strong> (the <code>EndpointRoutingMiddleware</code>) <strong>matches</strong> the request against every registered endpoint and stores the winner on <code>HttpContext</code> — retrievable as <code>context.GetEndpoint()</code> — along with its route values.</li>\n<li><strong>The endpoint middleware</strong>, added automatically at the very end of the pipeline, actually <strong>executes</strong> the endpoint that was selected.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>UseRouting ─▶ UseCors ─▶ UseAuthentication ─▶ UseAuthorization ─▶ UseRateLimiter ─▶ UseOutputCache ─▶ [endpoint executes]\n   │              │                                  │                  │                  │\n   └── picks ─────┴─── each reads the endpoint's metadata: CORS policy, [Authorize] policy, limiter policy, cache policy</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Here&#39;s why that gap matters. Everything sitting <em>between</em> &quot;select&quot; and &quot;execute&quot; — CORS, authentication, authorization, rate limiting, output caching — can now read the chosen endpoint&#39;s metadata before deciding what to do, without needing to know anything about how the endpoint was matched. Before this split existed, in ASP.NET Core 2.x, MVC did its own routing right at the end of the pipeline. That meant authorization and CORS had no way to know in advance which action was even going to run — they&#39;d have needed to duplicate the routing logic themselves just to ask that question. Endpoint routing fixes this once, centrally: <strong>one routing system</strong> serves controllers, minimal APIs, Razor Pages, SignalR hubs, gRPC services and health checks alike, and every one of them exposes the same metadata to the same pipeline.</p>\n<p>With <code>WebApplication</code>, you don&#39;t actually have to call <code>UseRouting</code> yourself — it&#39;s inserted automatically near the start of the pipeline. You&#39;d call it explicitly only when you need some other middleware, like static files or forwarded headers, to run genuinely <em>before</em> routing happens, or when you want precise control over exactly where in the pipeline matching occurs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"route-templates-the-syntax-that-extracts-values-from-a-url\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/routing/route-templates-the-syntax-that-extracts-values-from-a-url\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Route templates: the syntax that extracts values from a URL <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/products&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAll</span>);                               <span class=\"tk-com\">// literal</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/products/{id}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetById</span>);                         <span class=\"tk-com\">// parameter</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/products/{id:int}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetById</span>);                     <span class=\"tk-com\">// with constraint</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/products/{slug}/reviews/{page:int=1}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Reviews</span>);  <span class=\"tk-com\">// default value</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/search/{term?}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Search</span>);                         <span class=\"tk-com\">// optional parameter</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/files/{**path}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetFile</span>);                        <span class=\"tk-com\">// catch-all (slashes kept in value)</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/v{version:apiVersion}/orders&quot;</span>, …);                <span class=\"tk-com\">// custom constraint (API versioning)</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Each <code>{name}</code> segment becomes a route value, and that value binds to a handler argument <strong>by matching name</strong> — <code>{id}</code> in the template flows straight into an <code>int id</code> parameter on the handler, with no extra wiring needed.</p>\n<h3 id=\"route-constraints-narrowing-what-a-segment-is-allowed-to-mat\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/routing/route-constraints-narrowing-what-a-segment-is-allowed-to-mat\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Route constraints: narrowing what a segment is allowed to match</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Constraint</th>\n<th>Matches</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>int</code>, <code>long</code>, <code>decimal</code>, <code>double</code>, <code>float</code>, <code>bool</code></td>\n<td>Parses as that type</td>\n<td><code>{id:int}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>guid</code></td>\n<td>A GUID</td>\n<td><code>{id:guid}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>datetime</code></td>\n<td>A date</td>\n<td><code>{from:datetime}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>alpha</code></td>\n<td>Letters only</td>\n<td><code>{slug:alpha}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>minlength(n)</code>, <code>maxlength(n)</code>, <code>length(a,b)</code></td>\n<td>String length</td>\n<td><code>{code:length(3)}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>min(n)</code>, <code>max(n)</code>, <code>range(a,b)</code></td>\n<td>Numeric range</td>\n<td><code>{page:int:min(1)}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>regex(...)</code></td>\n<td>Regular expression</td>\n<td><code>{sku:regex(^[A-Z]{{2}}-\\\\d{{4}}$)}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>required</code></td>\n<td>Non-empty</td>\n<td><code>{name:required}</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Custom</td>\n<td><code>IRouteConstraint</code> / <code>IParameterPolicy</code></td>\n<td><code>{tenant:tenant}</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Constraints decide whether a route matches, not whether input is valid</div><p>Here&#39;s the subtlety worth getting right: a failed constraint doesn&#39;t mean &quot;bad request&quot;, it means &quot;this route doesn&#39;t match at all&quot; — so the result is a <strong>404</strong>, not a 400. Request <code>/products/abc</code> against a template of <code>{id:int}</code>, and you get <code>404 Not Found</code>, which can genuinely confuse an API client expecting <code>400 Bad Request</code> for malformed input. The right way to think about constraints is as a way to <strong>disambiguate between competing routes</strong> — <code>/products/{id:int}</code> from <code>/products/{slug}</code> — not as a validation layer. Validate real input properly inside the endpoint itself, and return 400 there.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"route-groups-shared-prefix-shared-rules-written-once\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/routing/route-groups-shared-prefix-shared-rules-written-once\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Route groups: shared prefix, shared rules, written once <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>A route group lets many related endpoints share both a URL <strong>prefix</strong> and a set of <strong>metadata</strong>, which is what keeps a large minimal-API surface from turning into repetitive boilerplate:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> api = app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGroup</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/api&quot;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequireAuthorization</span>()                    <span class=\"tk-com\">// every endpoint below requires an authenticated user</span>\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddEndpointFilter</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequestLoggingFilter</span>&gt;();\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> orders = api.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGroup</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/orders&quot;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithTags</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Orders&quot;</span>)                        <span class=\"tk-com\">// OpenAPI grouping</span>\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequireRateLimiting</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;per-user&quot;</span>);\n\norders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ListOrders</span>);                <span class=\"tk-com\">// GET    /api/orders</span>\norders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/{id:int}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetOrder</span>)           <span class=\"tk-com\">// GET    /api/orders/42</span>\n      .<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithName</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;GetOrder&quot;</span>);                   <span class=\"tk-com\">//        named, for link generation</span>\norders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapPost</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateOrder</span>);              <span class=\"tk-com\">// POST   /api/orders</span>\norders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapDelete</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/{id:int}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">DeleteOrder</span>)\n      .<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequireAuthorization</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Admin&quot;</span>);          <span class=\"tk-com\">//        extra policy on top of the group's</span>\n\napi.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGroup</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/public&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">AllowAnonymous</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/status&quot;</span>, () =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ok&quot;</span>);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// opt out</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice the <code>DeleteOrder</code> endpoint: it doesn&#39;t just inherit the group&#39;s <code>RequireAuthorization()</code>, it <strong>adds</strong> a stricter <code>&quot;Admin&quot;</code> policy on top. Metadata stacks rather than replaces — which is exactly why the last line&#39;s <code>AllowAnonymous()</code> reads as an explicit opt-out rather than a silent contradiction.</p>\n<h2 id=\"routing-for-controllers-attributes-instead-of-fluent-calls\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/routing/routing-for-controllers-attributes-instead-of-fluent-calls\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Routing for controllers: attributes instead of fluent calls <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Controllers reach the same destination through a different syntax — <strong>attribute routing</strong>, which is required for any API controller marked <code>[ApiController]</code>:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-attr\">[ApiController]</span>\n[<span class=\"tk-ty\">Route</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;api/[controller]&quot;</span>)]                      <span class=\"tk-com\">// [controller] token → &quot;orders&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrdersController</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">ControllerBase</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[HttpGet]</span>                                    <span class=\"tk-com\">// GET  api/orders</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IActionResult</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>(<span class=\"tk-attr\">[FromQuery]</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> page = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>();\n\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[HttpGet(&quot;{id:int}&quot;, Name = &quot;GetOrder&quot;)]</span>     <span class=\"tk-com\">// GET  api/orders/42</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IActionResult</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Get</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>();\n\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[HttpPost]</span>                                   <span class=\"tk-com\">// POST api/orders</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IActionResult</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Create</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateOrder</span> cmd) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreatedAtRoute</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;GetOrder&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { id = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> }, <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>);\n\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[HttpPut(&quot;{id:int}/status&quot;)]</span>                 <span class=\"tk-com\">// PUT  api/orders/42/status</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IActionResult</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SetStatus</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id, <span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusDto</span> dto) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">NoContent</span>();\n\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[HttpGet(&quot;~/api/customers/{customerId}/orders&quot;)]</span>   <span class=\"tk-com\">// ~ overrides the controller prefix</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IActionResult</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ForCustomer</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> customerId) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>();\n}\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapControllers</span>();   <span class=\"tk-com\">// registers all attribute-routed controller endpoints</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>A different, older style — <strong>conventional routing</strong> — is still common for MVC web apps that render views rather than serving an API, because it defines the URL pattern <strong>once</strong>, centrally, instead of repeating an attribute on every action:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"YXBwLk1hcENvbnRyb2xsZXJSb3V0ZShuYW1lOiAiZGVmYXVsdCIsIHBhdHRlcm46ICJ7Y29udHJvbGxlcj1Ib21lfS97YWN0aW9uPUluZGV4fS97aWQ/fSIpOw==\"><code>app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapControllerRoute</span>(name: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;default&quot;</span>, pattern: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;{controller=Home}/{action=Index}/{id?}&quot;</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h2 id=\"endpoint-metadata-the-vocabulary-every-cross-cutting-concern\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/routing/endpoint-metadata-the-vocabulary-every-cross-cutting-concern\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Endpoint metadata: the vocabulary every cross-cutting concern reads from <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Metadata is simply a list of arbitrary objects attached to an endpoint — and the table below is really a map of &quot;who reads what,&quot; tying straight back to the pipeline diagram earlier in this chapter. Attributes on controllers and fluent calls on minimal APIs are just two different syntaxes for attaching the exact same underlying metadata:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Fluent (minimal API)</th>\n<th>Attribute (controllers)</th>\n<th>Read by</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>.RequireAuthorization(&quot;Policy&quot;)</code></td>\n<td><code>[Authorize(Policy = &quot;Policy&quot;)]</code></td>\n<td>Authorization middleware</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>.AllowAnonymous()</code></td>\n<td><code>[AllowAnonymous]</code></td>\n<td>Authorization middleware</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>.RequireCors(&quot;web&quot;)</code></td>\n<td><code>[EnableCors(&quot;web&quot;)]</code></td>\n<td>CORS middleware</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>.RequireRateLimiting(&quot;p&quot;)</code></td>\n<td><code>[EnableRateLimiting(&quot;p&quot;)]</code></td>\n<td>Rate limiter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>.CacheOutput(&quot;p&quot;)</code></td>\n<td><code>[OutputCache(PolicyName = &quot;p&quot;)]</code></td>\n<td>Output cache</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>.WithName(&quot;X&quot;)</code></td>\n<td><code>Name = &quot;X&quot;</code></td>\n<td>Link generator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>.WithTags</code>, <code>.WithSummary</code>, <code>.Produces&lt;T&gt;()</code></td>\n<td><code>[Tags]</code>, <code>[ProducesResponseType]</code></td>\n<td>OpenAPI</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>.WithMetadata(obj)</code></td>\n<td>Any custom attribute</td>\n<td>Your middleware</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>.WithRequestTimeout(&quot;p&quot;)</code></td>\n<td><code>[RequestTimeout]</code></td>\n<td>Request timeouts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>.ShortCircuit()</code></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Skips remaining middleware</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"when-more-than-one-route-could-match-precedence-and-ambiguit\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/routing/when-more-than-one-route-could-match-precedence-and-ambiguit\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>When more than one route could match: precedence and ambiguity <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Real route tables inevitably end up with templates that <em>could</em> both match the same URL — <code>/products/featured</code> and <code>/products/{id}</code> both look plausible for a request to <code>/products/featured</code>. Routing resolves this with a precedence order, most specific wins:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Literal segments beat parameter segments: <code>/products/featured</code> beats <code>/products/{id}</code>.</li>\n<li>Parameters with constraints beat unconstrained ones: <code>{id:int}</code> beats <code>{slug}</code>.</li>\n<li>Complex segments beat simple parameters; catch-alls have the lowest precedence.</li>\n<li>HTTP method constraints filter candidates (<code>GET</code> vs <code>POST</code> on the same path).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If, after all four rules, two endpoints are still equally specific, routing has genuinely no way to pick a winner, and you get an <code>AmbiguousMatchException</code> at runtime — &quot;The request matched multiple endpoints.&quot; The fix is always to make one of them more specific: add a constraint, change a literal, or set an explicit <code>Order</code> on controllers.</p>\n<p>Worth knowing if you&#39;re wondering whether this adds overhead as a route table grows: matching is done by a <strong>DFA-based matcher</strong> (<a href=\"https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/tree/main/src/Http/Routing/src/Matching\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">documented in the ASP.NET Core source</a>), built once at startup from every registered template. Because it&#39;s a precompiled state machine rather than a linear scan down a list, matching cost barely grows with how many routes you&#39;ve registered.</p>\n<h2 id=\"link-generation-building-urls-that-survive-a-route-changing\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/routing/link-generation-building-urls-that-survive-a-route-changing\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Link generation: building URLs that survive a route changing <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Build URLs from route <strong>names</strong> and values, not by concatenating strings, so that a route&#39;s template can change later without silently breaking every link that pointed at it:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>orders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapPost</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateOrder</span> cmd, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderService</span> svc, <span class=\"tk-ty\">LinkGenerator</span> links, <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpContext</span> ctx) =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> id = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> svc.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateAsync</span>(cmd);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> url = links.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetUriByName</span>(ctx, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;GetOrder&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { id });   <span class=\"tk-com\">// https://host/api/orders/42</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Created</span>(url, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { id });\n});\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Shortcuts</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TypedResults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreatedAtRoute</span>(dto, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;GetOrder&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { id = dto.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> });   <span class=\"tk-com\">// minimal API</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreatedAtAction</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">nameof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Get</span>), <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { id }, dto);                         <span class=\"tk-com\">// controller</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><code>LinkGenerator</code> is registered as a singleton, so you can inject it anywhere you need a URL — not just inside an endpoint handler, but genuinely useful for HATEOAS-style response links or for building URLs into outgoing emails.</p>\n<h2 id=\"reading-route-values-back-out-of-the-request\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/routing/reading-route-values-back-out-of-the-request\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Reading route values back out of the request <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/tenants/{tenant}/orders/{id:int}&quot;</span>, (<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> tenant, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id, <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpContext</span> ctx) =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> raw = ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Request</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RouteValues</span>[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;tenant&quot;</span>];          <span class=\"tk-com\">// also available as a dictionary</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> endpoint = ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetEndpoint</span>();                     <span class=\"tk-com\">// the matched endpoint</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { tenant, id, endpoint?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DisplayName</span> });\n});</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Two small, commonly wanted tweaks live in <code>RouteOptions</code>: lowercase URLs and trailing-slash behaviour. <code>builder.Services.Configure&lt;RouteOptions&gt;(o =&gt; { o.LowercaseUrls = true; o.AppendTrailingSlash = false; });</code></p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is endpoint routing?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The routing system since ASP.NET Core 3.0, shared by MVC, minimal APIs, Razor Pages, SignalR, gRPC and health checks. UseRouting matches the request to an endpoint early and stores it on HttpContext; the endpoint is executed at the end of the pipeline; middleware in between (CORS, auth, rate limiting, caching) can read the selected endpoint&#39;s metadata.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What&#39;s the difference between attribute routing and conventional routing?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Attribute routing puts templates on controllers and actions ([Route], [HttpGet(&quot;{id}&quot;)]) and is required for API controllers. Conventional routing defines URL patterns centrally (&quot;{controller}/{action}/{id?}&quot;) and maps them to controllers by naming convention; it&#39;s typical for MVC sites with views.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What happens when a route constraint fails?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The route doesn&#39;t match, so routing tries other endpoints and, if none match, returns 404. Constraints are for disambiguation, not input validation, which should return 400.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are route groups?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>MapGroup creates a prefix and lets you apply shared metadata (authorization, filters, tags, rate limiting, CORS) to all endpoints within it, which keeps minimal API definitions organised and DRY.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How does ASP.NET Core resolve two routes that match the same URL?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>By route precedence: literals over parameters, constrained over unconstrained, catch-alls last, then HTTP method. If two candidates remain equally specific, it throws AmbiguousMatchException.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you generate URLs to other endpoints?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Name the endpoint (WithName or Name = …) and use LinkGenerator.GetUriByName/GetPathByName, or helpers like CreatedAtRoute and CreatedAtAction. Avoid hard-coded string URLs.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/routing/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/routing\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Routing in ASP.NET Core</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/minimal-apis/route-handlers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Route handlers in minimal APIs</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/mvc/controllers/routing\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Attribute routing for controllers</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/routing/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run</code> in a console project (<code>dotnet new web</code>, then <code>dotnet add package Microsoft.AspNetCore.TestHost</code>). It checks the 404-vs-400 behaviour of constraints, precedence between literal/parameter/constrained routes, the ambiguous-match failure, and link generation.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AspNetCore</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Builder</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AspNetCore</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Routing</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AspNetCore</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TestHost</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Extensions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DependencyInjection</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> condition, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> claim)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!condition) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: {claim}&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: {claim}&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 1. A failed route constraint means &quot;no route matches&quot; -&gt; 404, not 400.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> builder1 = <span class=\"tk-ty\">WebApplication</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateBuilder</span>();\nbuilder1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WebHost</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseTestServer</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> app1 = builder1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();\napp1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/products/{id:int}&quot;</span>, (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;product {id}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> response1 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetTestClient</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/products/abc&quot;</span>);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// &quot;abc&quot; fails the :int constraint</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StopAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(response1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span> == <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Net</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpStatusCode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>,\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A route segment that fails its constraint (e.g. {id:int} given 'abc') returns 404, not 400&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 2. Precedence: a literal segment beats a parameter segment for the same URL.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> builder2 = <span class=\"tk-ty\">WebApplication</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateBuilder</span>();\nbuilder2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WebHost</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseTestServer</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> app2 = builder2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();\napp2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/products/featured&quot;</span>, () =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;featured-literal&quot;</span>);\napp2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/products/{id}&quot;</span>, (<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> id) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;param-{id}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> featured = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetTestClient</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetStringAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/products/featured&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StopAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(featured == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;featured-literal&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A literal route segment ('/products/featured') beats a parameter segment ('/products/{id}') for the same URL&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 3. Precedence: a constrained parameter beats an unconstrained one.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> builder3 = <span class=\"tk-ty\">WebApplication</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateBuilder</span>();\nbuilder3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WebHost</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseTestServer</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> app3 = builder3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();\napp3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/items/{id:int}&quot;</span>, (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;constrained&quot;</span>);\napp3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/items/{slug}&quot;</span>, (<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> slug) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;unconstrained&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> numeric = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetTestClient</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetStringAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/items/42&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> text = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetTestClient</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetStringAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/items/abc&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StopAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(numeric == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;constrained&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A constrained parameter ({id:int}) wins over an unconstrained one ({slug}) when the value matches the constraint&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(text == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;unconstrained&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;When the value fails the constraint, the unconstrained route catches it instead&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 4. Two equally-specific routes for the same request throw AmbiguousMatchException.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> builder4 = <span class=\"tk-ty\">WebApplication</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateBuilder</span>();\nbuilder4.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WebHost</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseTestServer</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> app4 = builder4.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();\napp4.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/ambiguous/{a}&quot;</span>, () =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;first&quot;</span>);\napp4.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/ambiguous/{b}&quot;</span>, () =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;second&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app4.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> ambiguousResponse = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app4.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetTestClient</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/ambiguous/x&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> ambiguousBody = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> ambiguousResponse.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Content</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadAsStringAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app4.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StopAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(ambiguousResponse.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span> == <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Net</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpStatusCode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">InternalServerError</span> &amp;&amp; ambiguousBody.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contains</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;AmbiguousMatchException&quot;</span>),\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Two equally-specific routes for the same request throw AmbiguousMatchException (surfaced here as a 500)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 5. LinkGenerator builds a URL from a route NAME and values, matching the actual registered template.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> builder5 = <span class=\"tk-ty\">WebApplication</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateBuilder</span>();\nbuilder5.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WebHost</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseTestServer</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> app5 = builder5.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();\napp5.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/api/orders/{id:int}&quot;</span>, (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;order&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithName</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;GetOrder&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app5.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> links = app5.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">LinkGenerator</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> path = links.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetPathByName</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;GetOrder&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { id = <span class=\"tk-num\">42</span> });\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app5.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StopAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(path == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/api/orders/42&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;LinkGenerator.GetPathByName builds the URL from the route's name and values, matching its template&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All routing claims verified.&quot;</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Not attempted here: the exact internal shape of the DFA matcher (how many states it builds, how it&#39;s cheaper than a linear scan) isn&#39;t something you can assert on from outside it — see the source link above if you want to read the matcher itself.</p>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-question-routing-exists-to-answer","text":"The question routing exists to answer","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"why-routing-had-to-be-split-into-two-separate-middleware","text":"Why routing had to be split into two separate middleware","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"route-templates-the-syntax-that-extracts-values-from-a-url","text":"Route templates: the syntax that extracts values from a URL","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"route-constraints-narrowing-what-a-segment-is-allowed-to-mat","text":"Route constraints: narrowing what a segment is allowed to match","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"route-groups-shared-prefix-shared-rules-written-once","text":"Route groups: shared prefix, shared rules, written once","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"routing-for-controllers-attributes-instead-of-fluent-calls","text":"Routing for controllers: attributes instead of fluent calls","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"endpoint-metadata-the-vocabulary-every-cross-cutting-concern","text":"Endpoint metadata: the vocabulary every cross-cutting concern reads from","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"when-more-than-one-route-could-match-precedence-and-ambiguit","text":"When more than one route could match: precedence and ambiguity","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"link-generation-building-urls-that-survive-a-route-changing","text":"Link generation: building URLs that survive a route changing","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"reading-route-values-back-out-of-the-request","text":"Reading route values back out of the request","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":2840,"minutes":13,"num":"3.5","part":"p3"},"building-apis":{"id":"building-apis","title":"Building APIs: Minimal APIs, Controllers, Binding, Validation and Filters","summary":"Writing the endpoints themselves. Minimal APIs and controllers compared, returning results (Results, TypedResults, ActionResult), parameter binding sources, validation (built-in, data annotations, FluentValidation), MVC filters and endpoint filters, JSON serialization with System.Text.Json, and organising endpoints.","layer":"framework","where":"ASP.NET Core › Request pipeline › Endpoints (your code)","fits":"<p>This is the <strong>end of the pipeline</strong>, where layer 3 hands over to <em>your</em> code. Routing (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/routing\">Routing</a>) picked the endpoint, and this chapter is everything that happens inside it. From here, well-structured code calls into the application and domain layers of layer 4 (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/app-architectures\">App architectures</a>) rather than doing everything in the handler.</p>\n","prereqs":["routing","dependency-injection","modern-csharp"],"next":["errors-logging","api-design","app-architectures"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/building-apis/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Minimal APIs and controllers are two <strong>syntaxes</strong> over identical machinery — same routing, DI and middleware. Pick based on team conventions and features needed, not raw capability.</li>\n<li>Parameter binding infers where a value comes from (route, query, body, DI) based on its type and name; attributes override that inference when you need to be explicit.</li>\n<li>Prefer <code>TypedResults</code> over <code>Results</code>: the handler&#39;s return type then documents every possible response, for free, accurate OpenAPI docs and easy unit tests.</li>\n<li>Validate shape at the edge (400 for bad input); keep business rules (&quot;can&#39;t cancel a shipped order&quot;) in the domain layer, not in a validator.</li>\n<li><strong>Filters</strong> run inside endpoint execution and see the bound, typed arguments — middleware never does, because it runs before model binding happens.</li>\n<li>Never bind a request body directly to an EF Core entity (&quot;over-posting&quot;) and never return one either — always go through a DTO you control.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"two-syntaxes-the-same-machinery-underneath\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/building-apis/two-syntaxes-the-same-machinery-underneath\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Two syntaxes, the same machinery underneath <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Everything in the previous two chapters — the pipeline, the routing match — ends at exactly the same place: your code, deciding what to actually do with a request. ASP.NET Core gives you two different <em>syntaxes</em> for writing that code, and it&#39;s worth being clear up front that they are genuinely just syntax: both run on the same routing, the same DI container, the same authentication and the same middleware pipeline.</p>\n<p><strong>Minimal APIs</strong> (since .NET 6) map routes directly to functions:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"YXBwLk1hcEdldCgiL3Byb2R1Y3RzL3tpZDppbnR9IiwgYXN5bmMgKGludCBpZCwgU2hvcERiIGRiLCBDYW5jZWxsYXRpb25Ub2tlbiBjdCkgPT4KICAgIGF3YWl0IGRiLlByb2R1Y3RzLkZpbmRBc3luYyhbaWRdLCBjdCkgaXMgeyB9IHAgPyBSZXN1bHRzLk9rKHApIDogUmVzdWx0cy5Ob3RGb3VuZCgpKTs=\"><code>app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/products/{id:int}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span> db, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FindAsync</span>([id], ct) <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> { } p ? <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(p) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>());</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><strong>Controllers</strong> (MVC) are classes with action methods and attributes:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-attr\">[ApiController]</span>\n<span class=\"tk-attr\">[Route(&quot;products&quot;)]</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductsController</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span> db) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">ControllerBase</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[HttpGet(&quot;{id:int}&quot;)]</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ActionResult</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>&gt;&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Get</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt;\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FindAsync</span>([id], ct) <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> { } p ? p : <span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>();\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The difference between them isn&#39;t capability — it&#39;s how much ceremony you accept in exchange for which features:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Minimal APIs</th>\n<th>Controllers</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Shape</td>\n<td>Lambdas or static methods; fluent configuration</td>\n<td>Classes deriving <code>ControllerBase</code>; attributes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ceremony</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Higher (classes, attributes, base class)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cross-cutting per endpoint</td>\n<td>Endpoint filters, route group metadata</td>\n<td>Action/resource/result/exception filters</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Automatic validation</td>\n<td>Built in since .NET 10 (<code>AddValidation()</code>); else filters/FluentValidation</td>\n<td>Automatic with <code>[ApiController]</code> (data annotations)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Model binding</td>\n<td>Inferred; <code>[AsParameters]</code>; custom <code>BindAsync</code>/<code>TryParse</code></td>\n<td>Model binders, value providers; very extensible</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Content negotiation (XML…)</td>\n<td>JSON only out of the box</td>\n<td>Formatters (JSON, XML, custom)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Performance</td>\n<td>Slightly faster, lower memory</td>\n<td>Slightly heavier</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Native AOT</td>\n<td>Supported (with request delegate generator)</td>\n<td>Not supported</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Microsoft&#39;s guidance</td>\n<td><strong>Recommended for new APIs</strong></td>\n<td>Fully supported; huge existing codebases</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-interview\"><div class=\"callout-title\">How to answer &quot;minimal APIs or controllers?&quot;</div><p>&quot;Both are first-class. For new services I default to minimal APIs organised into feature-based route groups, which pairs well with vertical slices and supports AOT. In an existing controller-based codebase I&#39;d stay consistent. I&#39;d pick controllers if the team relies on MVC filters, conventions, content negotiation or views.&quot;</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"minimal-apis-in-depth\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/building-apis/minimal-apis-in-depth\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Minimal APIs in depth <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<h3 id=\"handler-shapes\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/building-apis/handler-shapes\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Handler shapes</h3>\n<p>A minimal-API handler can be as small as a one-liner or a reference to a plain static method — the framework infers a response from whatever you return:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/&quot;</span>, () =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Hello&quot;</span>);                                    <span class=\"tk-com\">// returns string → text/plain 200</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/now&quot;</span>, () =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Utc</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTime</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UtcNow</span> });           <span class=\"tk-com\">// returns object → JSON 200</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/r&quot;</span>, () =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Redirect</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/&quot;</span>));                     <span class=\"tk-com\">// returns IResult → full control</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapPost</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/orders&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderHandlers</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Create</span>);                       <span class=\"tk-com\">// method group: testable static method</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapMethods</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/ping&quot;</span>, [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;GET&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;HEAD&quot;</span>], () =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;pong&quot;</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h3 id=\"organising-endpoints-so-program-cs-stays-readable\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/building-apis/organising-endpoints-so-program-cs-stays-readable\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Organising endpoints so Program.cs stays readable</h3>\n<p>The lambda style above is great for a demo and genuinely bad for a real app with eighty endpoints crammed into one file. The fix is the same one <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/routing/route-groups-shared-prefix-shared-rules-written-once\">Routing</a> already introduced: group endpoints by feature, behind an extension method, so <code>Program.cs</code> reads as a table of contents rather than a wall of lambdas:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderEndpoints</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IEndpointRouteBuilder</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">MapOrderEndpoints</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">this</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IEndpointRouteBuilder</span> app)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> g = app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGroup</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/api/orders&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithTags</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Orders&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequireAuthorization</span>();\n\n        g.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithName</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ListOrders&quot;</span>);\n        g.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/{id:int}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetById</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithName</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;GetOrder&quot;</span>);\n        g.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapPost</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Create</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddEndpointFilter</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidationFilter</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateOrderRequest</span>&gt;&gt;();\n        g.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapPost</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/{id:int}/cancel&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Cancel</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> app;\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">PagedResult</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderSummary</span>&gt;&gt;&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>(<span class=\"tk-attr\">[AsParameters]</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderQuery</span> q, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderQueries</span> queries, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n        =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">TypedResults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> queries.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ListAsync</span>(q, ct));\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderDto</span>&gt;, <span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>&gt;&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetById</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderQueries</span> queries, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n        =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> queries.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(id, ct) <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> { } dto ? <span class=\"tk-ty\">TypedResults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(dto) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">TypedResults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>();\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Created</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderDto</span>&gt;, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidationProblem</span>&gt;&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Create</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateOrderRequest</span> req, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderService</span> svc, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> dto = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> svc.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateAsync</span>(req, ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TypedResults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Created</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;/api/orders/{dto.Id}&quot;</span>, dto);\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">NoContent</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Conflict</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProblemDetails</span>&gt;&gt;&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Cancel</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderService</span> svc, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n        =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> svc.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancelAsync</span>(id, ct) <span class=\"tk-kw\">switch</span>\n        {\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancelResult</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">TypedResults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NoContent</span>(),\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancelResult</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">TypedResults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>(),\n            _ =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">TypedResults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Conflict</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProblemDetails</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Title</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Order can no longer be cancelled&quot;</span> })\n        };\n}\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapOrderEndpoints</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Some teams reach for libraries such as <strong>Carter</strong> or <strong>FastEndpoints</strong> to push this organisation further, into module- or REPR-style (Request-Endpoint-Response) folders — worth knowing the names exist, even if the pattern above covers most needs on its own.</p>\n<h2 id=\"returning-results-what-each-return-type-actually-produces\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/building-apis/returning-results-what-each-return-type-actually-produces\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Returning results: what each return type actually produces <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Return</th>\n<th>Status</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>string</code></td>\n<td>200 text/plain</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Any object <code>T</code></td>\n<td>200 application/json</td>\n<td>Serialized with System.Text.Json</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IResult</code> via <code>Results.*</code></td>\n<td>Any</td>\n<td><code>Results.Ok(x)</code>, <code>NotFound()</code>, <code>Created(url, x)</code>, <code>NoContent()</code>, <code>BadRequest()</code>, <code>Problem()</code>, <code>ValidationProblem()</code>, <code>File()</code>, <code>Stream()</code>, <code>Redirect()</code>, <code>StatusCode(n)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>TypedResults.*</code></td>\n<td>Any</td>\n<td>Same, but <strong>strongly typed</strong> (<code>Ok&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>NotFound</code>…), so OpenAPI can infer response types and unit tests can assert on them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Results&lt;T1, T2, …&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Union</td>\n<td>Declares every possible result type of a handler</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Controllers: <code>IActionResult</code> / <code>ActionResult&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Any</td>\n<td><code>Ok()</code>, <code>NotFound()</code>, <code>CreatedAtAction()</code>, <code>NoContent()</code>, <code>Problem()</code>, <code>ValidationProblem()</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The reason to prefer <strong><code>TypedResults</code></strong> over plain <code>Results</code> isn&#39;t style — it&#39;s that the handler&#39;s <em>return type</em> becomes a literal, checked-by-the-compiler contract. Look back at <code>GetById</code> above: its signature, <code>Task&lt;Results&lt;Ok&lt;OrderDto&gt;, NotFound&gt;&gt;</code>, states in the type system itself that this endpoint produces exactly one of two shapes, nothing else. OpenAPI documentation generation reads that type and produces accurate response docs with zero extra annotation, and unit tests get a concrete type to assert against instead of inspecting an opaque <code>IResult</code>:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Unit testing a handler is trivial because it's just a static method returning typed results</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> result = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderEndpoints</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetById</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>, fakeQueries, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">None</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> ok = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Assert</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsType</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderDto</span>&gt;&gt;(result.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Assert</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Equal</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>, ok.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>!.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h2 id=\"parameter-binding-how-request-data-becomes-a-c-argument\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/building-apis/parameter-binding-how-request-data-becomes-a-c-argument\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Parameter binding: how request data becomes a C# argument <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Binding is the step that turns pieces of the raw HTTP request into the actual values your handler&#39;s parameters receive. The framework infers <em>where</em> to look for each parameter based on its type and name, and you can always override the inference with an explicit attribute:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Source</th>\n<th>Minimal API (inferred / explicit)</th>\n<th>Controller attribute</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Route <code>{id}</code></td>\n<td>Name matches a route parameter / <code>[FromRoute]</code></td>\n<td><code>[FromRoute]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Query string <code>?page=2</code></td>\n<td>Simple types not in the route / <code>[FromQuery]</code></td>\n<td><code>[FromQuery]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Headers</td>\n<td><code>[FromHeader(Name = &quot;X-Tenant&quot;)]</code></td>\n<td><code>[FromHeader]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>JSON body</td>\n<td>Complex types (one per request) / <code>[FromBody]</code></td>\n<td><code>[FromBody]</code> (inferred with <code>[ApiController]</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Form fields / files</td>\n<td><code>[FromForm]</code>, <code>IFormFile</code>, <code>IFormFileCollection</code></td>\n<td><code>[FromForm]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DI services</td>\n<td>Registered types / <code>[FromServices]</code></td>\n<td><code>[FromServices]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Keyed services</td>\n<td><code>[FromKeyedServices(&quot;key&quot;)]</code></td>\n<td>same</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Special types</td>\n<td><code>HttpContext</code>, <code>HttpRequest</code>, <code>HttpResponse</code>, <code>ClaimsPrincipal</code>, <code>CancellationToken</code>, <code>Stream</code>, <code>PipeReader</code></td>\n<td>via <code>HttpContext</code> / parameters</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Group many</td>\n<td><code>[AsParameters] MyQuery q</code></td>\n<td>complex type with <code>[FromQuery]</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderQuery</span>(\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[FromQuery]</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Page</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>,\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[FromQuery]</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PageSize</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>,\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[FromQuery]</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span>? <span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>,\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[FromHeader(Name = &quot;X-Tenant&quot;)]</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>? <span class=\"tk-ty\">Tenant</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>);\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/orders&quot;</span>, (<span class=\"tk-attr\">[AsParameters]</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderQuery</span> q, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderQueries</span> svc) =&gt; svc.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ListAsync</span>(q));\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapPost</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/upload&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">IFormFile</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">file</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IBlobStore</span> blobs, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> s = <span class=\"tk-kw\">file</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OpenReadStream</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> blobs.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UploadAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">file</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FileName</span>, s, ct));\n}).<span class=\"tk-ty\">DisableAntiforgery</span>();   <span class=\"tk-com\">// form endpoints require antiforgery tokens by default</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><strong>Custom binding:</strong> give a type a static <code>TryParse(string, out T)</code> method (for route or query values) or a static <code>BindAsync(HttpContext)</code> method (for anything that needs the full context), and minimal APIs picks it up automatically, with no registration step. Implementing <code>IParsable&lt;T&gt;</code> works the same way.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Over-posting: the bug that looks like a feature until it doesn&#39;t</div><p>Here&#39;s the trap, concretely: bind the request body directly to your <strong>EF Core entity</strong>, and a client can set <em>any</em> writable property on it in the JSON they send — including <code>IsAdmin</code>, <code>Price</code>, or <code>CustomerId</code>, fields they were never supposed to touch. Nothing in the binding step knows &quot;this field is for internal use only&quot;; it binds whatever matches. The fix is to always bind to a dedicated <strong>request DTO</strong> that only has the fields a client is actually allowed to send, and map that DTO onto the domain explicitly, by hand, where you control exactly what gets copied.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"validation-catching-bad-input-before-it-reaches-business-log\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/building-apis/validation-catching-bad-input-before-it-reaches-business-log\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Validation: catching bad input before it reaches business logic <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>The goal here is simple to state: validate input <strong>at the edge</strong>, before anything downstream has to deal with it, and when it fails, respond with a <strong>400</strong> carrying <code>ValidationProblemDetails</code> — an RFC 9457 JSON body listing which fields were wrong and why.</p>\n<h3 id=\"option-1-data-annotations\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/building-apis/option-1-data-annotations\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Option 1: Data annotations</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateProductRequest</span>(\n    [property: <span class=\"tk-ty\">Required</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">StringLength</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">100</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">MinimumLength</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>)] <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>,\n    [property: <span class=\"tk-ty\">Range</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0.01</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1_000_000</span>)] <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span>,\n    [property: <span class=\"tk-ty\">Required</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">RegularExpression</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">@&quot;^[A-Z]{2}-\\d{4}$&quot;</span>)] <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Controllers with <code>[ApiController]</code></strong> validate automatically and return 400 before your action runs at all (<code>ModelState.IsValid</code> is checked on your behalf).</li>\n<li><strong>Minimal APIs (.NET 10)</strong>: <code>builder.Services.AddValidation();</code> enables built-in data-annotation validation for parameters and returns <code>ValidationProblem</code> automatically. On earlier versions, you need a filter to get the same effect.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"option-2-fluentvalidation-for-rules-annotations-cant-express\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/building-apis/option-2-fluentvalidation-for-rules-annotations-cant-express\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Option 2: FluentValidation, for rules annotations can&#39;t express</h3>\n<p>Data annotations are fine for simple per-field rules. But they genuinely can&#39;t express &quot;this order needs at least one line&quot; or &quot;look up this SKU in the catalog and confirm it exists&quot; — rules that need to see the whole object, or need a dependency like a database lookup. FluentValidation is the common answer:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateOrderValidator</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">AbstractValidator</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateOrderRequest</span>&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateOrderValidator</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IProductCatalog</span> catalog)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">RuleFor</span>(x =&gt; x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">GreaterThan</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">RuleFor</span>(x =&gt; x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotEmpty</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithMessage</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;An order needs at least one line&quot;</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">RuleForEach</span>(x =&gt; x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChildRules</span>(line =&gt;\n        {\n            line.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RuleFor</span>(l =&gt; l.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Qty</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">InclusiveBetween</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">100</span>);\n            line.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RuleFor</span>(l =&gt; l.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">MustAsync</span>((sku, ct) =&gt; catalog.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExistsAsync</span>(sku, ct)).<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithMessage</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Unknown SKU '{PropertyValue}'&quot;</span>);\n        });\n    }\n}\n\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddValidatorsFromAssemblyContaining</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateOrderValidator</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Apply with the ValidationFilter&lt;T&gt; endpoint filter (see Filters below), or call validator.ValidateAsync in the handler.</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h3 id=\"input-validation-vs-business-rules-a-distinction-worth-holdi\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/building-apis/input-validation-vs-business-rules-a-distinction-worth-holdi\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Input validation vs business rules — a distinction worth holding onto</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>Where</th>\n<th>Response</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Input/shape</strong></td>\n<td>Required, length, range, format</td>\n<td>Edge (validators)</td>\n<td>400 ValidationProblem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Business rule</strong></td>\n<td>&quot;Can&#39;t cancel a shipped order&quot;, &quot;insufficient stock&quot;</td>\n<td>Domain/application layer</td>\n<td>409 Conflict / 422 / domain error</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Both matter, and the line between them is where they <em>live</em>, not just what status code they return. &quot;Can&#39;t cancel a shipped order&quot; isn&#39;t a shape problem — the request might be perfectly well-formed — it&#39;s a rule about the current state of the business. Trying to express that inside a validator means the validator now needs database access and knowledge of domain state, which is exactly the kind of coupling that makes validators hard to test and slow to run. Keep it in the domain or application layer instead, where it belongs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"filters-seeing-the-handlers-actual-typed-arguments\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/building-apis/filters-seeing-the-handlers-actual-typed-arguments\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Filters: seeing the handler&#39;s actual typed arguments <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Here&#39;s the capability middleware fundamentally can&#39;t give you — and the reason filters exist as a separate mechanism. Filters run <strong>inside</strong> endpoint execution, wrapped directly around your handler, after model binding has already happened. So they can see the bound, typed arguments and the handler&#39;s result. Middleware, sitting outside all of this, only ever sees raw HTTP.</p>\n<h3 id=\"endpoint-filters-minimal-apis\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/building-apis/endpoint-filters-minimal-apis\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Endpoint filters (minimal APIs)</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidationFilter</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IValidator</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; validator) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IEndpointFilter</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">where</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> : <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>?&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvokeAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">EndpointFilterInvocationContext</span> ctx, <span class=\"tk-ty\">EndpointFilterDelegate</span> next)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> arg = ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Arguments</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OfType</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">FirstOrDefault</span>();\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (arg <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TypedResults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BadRequest</span>();\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> result = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> validator.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidateAsync</span>(arg, ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpContext</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequestAborted</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> result.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsValid</span> ? <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> next(ctx) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">TypedResults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidationProblem</span>(result.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToDictionary</span>());\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// inline filter</span>\ng.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapPost</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Create</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddEndpointFilter</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (ctx, next) =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> sw = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Stopwatch</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartNew</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> result = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> next(ctx);\n    ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpContext</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Response</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span>[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;X-Handler-Ms&quot;</span>] = sw.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ElapsedMilliseconds</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> result;\n});</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>A filter attached to a <strong>group</strong> applies to every endpoint inside it, and — just like middleware — filters run in registration order on the way in, outer to inner.</p>\n<h3 id=\"mvc-filter-pipeline-controllers\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/building-apis/mvc-filter-pipeline-controllers\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>MVC filter pipeline (controllers)</h3>\n<p>Controllers have a richer, more finely staged version of the same idea, with five distinct filter types, each running at a specific point:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Request ─▶ [Authorization filters] ─▶ [Resource filters (before)] ─▶ model binding &amp; validation\n        ─▶ [Action filters (before)] ─▶ ACTION ─▶ [Action filters (after)]\n        ─▶ [Exception filters] (only if the action/filters threw)\n        ─▶ [Result filters (before)] ─▶ result executes (serialization) ─▶ [Result filters (after)]\n        ─▶ [Resource filters (after)] ─▶ Response</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Filter</th>\n<th>Interface</th>\n<th>Typical use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Authorization</td>\n<td><code>IAuthorizationFilter</code> / <code>IAsyncAuthorizationFilter</code></td>\n<td>Custom auth checks (prefer policies)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Resource</td>\n<td><code>IResourceFilter</code></td>\n<td>Short-circuit before binding (caching, feature gates)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Action</td>\n<td><code>IActionFilter</code> / <code>IAsyncActionFilter</code></td>\n<td>Validation, logging arguments, transactions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exception</td>\n<td><code>IExceptionFilter</code></td>\n<td>Translate exceptions from actions (prefer <code>IExceptionHandler</code> globally)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Result</td>\n<td><code>IResultFilter</code></td>\n<td>Modify or wrap results, add headers</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TransactionFilter</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span> db) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IAsyncActionFilter</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OnActionExecutionAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ActionExecutingContext</span> ctx, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ActionExecutionDelegate</span> next)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> tx = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Database</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BeginTransactionAsync</span>();\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> executed = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> next();\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (executed.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> tx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CommitAsync</span>();\n    }\n}\n\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddControllers</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Filters</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TransactionFilter</span>&gt;());   <span class=\"tk-com\">// global</span>\n[<span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceFilter</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TransactionFilter</span>&gt;] <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IActionResult</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Post</span>(...) { }        <span class=\"tk-com\">// per action (resolved from DI)</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Filters can be attached at three scopes — global, controller, and action — and on the way in they run global → controller → action, exactly reversed on the way out, the same inward-then-reversed-outward shape the middleware pipeline diagram showed in the previous chapter. <code>Order</code> lets you adjust the sequence within a scope when you need to.</p>\n<h2 id=\"json-with-system-text-json-the-defaults-and-how-to-bend-them\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/building-apis/json-with-system-text-json-the-defaults-and-how-to-bend-them\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>JSON with System.Text.Json: the defaults and how to bend them <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>ASP.NET Core serializes with <strong>System.Text.Json</strong> by default, chosen specifically for being fast, low-allocation, and UTF-8 based rather than working through an intermediate string encoding. Its web-specific defaults: <strong>camelCase</strong> property names, case-insensitive reading on the way in, and numbers accepted even when they arrive as JSON strings.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ConfigureHttpJsonOptions</span>(o =&gt;          <span class=\"tk-com\">// minimal APIs</span>\n{\n    o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SerializerOptions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Converters</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">JsonStringEnumConverter</span>());     <span class=\"tk-com\">// enums as &quot;Paid&quot; not 2</span>\n    o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SerializerOptions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DefaultIgnoreCondition</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">JsonIgnoreCondition</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WhenWritingNull</span>;\n});\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddControllers</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddJsonOptions</span>(o =&gt; { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* same for controllers */</span> });\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderDto</span>(\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>,\n    [property: <span class=\"tk-ty\">JsonPropertyName</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;total_amount&quot;</span>)] <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>,\n    [property: <span class=\"tk-ty\">JsonIgnore</span>] <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InternalNote</span>,\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Rename a property</td>\n<td><code>[JsonPropertyName]</code> or a naming policy (<code>JsonNamingPolicy.SnakeCaseLower</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Skip a property</td>\n<td><code>[JsonIgnore]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Polymorphism (derived types)</td>\n<td><code>[JsonPolymorphic]</code> + <code>[JsonDerivedType(typeof(Card), &quot;card&quot;)]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Custom format</td>\n<td>A <code>JsonConverter&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Read unknown JSON</td>\n<td><code>JsonDocument</code> / <code>JsonNode</code> (DOM)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Max performance / AOT</td>\n<td>Source generation: <code>[JsonSerializable(typeof(OrderDto))] partial class AppJsonContext : JsonSerializerContext</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Newtonsoft features</td>\n<td><code>AddNewtonsoftJson()</code> (legacy compatibility; slower)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Serializing EF Core entities directly is a trap, not a shortcut</div><p>Return an EF Core entity with its navigation properties intact — <code>Order.Customer.Orders…</code> — and you&#39;ll either hit a reference-cycle error (the serializer walking <code>Order → Customer → Orders → Order → …</code> forever) or ship a response payload many times larger than anyone asked for. The fix isn&#39;t <code>ReferenceHandler.IgnoreCycles</code> — that papers over the symptom while still shipping too much data and still coupling your wire format to your database schema. Return <strong>DTOs</strong>, built with an explicit <code>Select</code> projection, every time.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"content-negotiation-formats-and-streaming\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/building-apis/content-negotiation-formats-and-streaming\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Content negotiation, formats and streaming <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Controllers honour the <code>Accept</code> header through output formatters (<code>AddXmlSerializerFormatters()</code>); minimal APIs always return JSON unless you explicitly return a different <code>IResult</code>.</li>\n<li>Return files with <code>Results.File(bytes, &quot;application/pdf&quot;, &quot;invoice.pdf&quot;)</code> or <code>Results.Stream(stream, …)</code>; use <code>PhysicalFile</code> for files on disk, which adds HTTP range support for free.</li>\n<li>Stream large datasets with <code>IAsyncEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</code>: the JSON array gets written to the response as items arrive, rather than buffering the entire result set in memory first.</li>\n<li><strong>Server-Sent Events</strong> (<code>TypedResults.ServerSentEvents</code>, .NET 10) for one-way streaming updates to a browser.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"putting-it-together-a-handler-that-stays-genuinely-thin\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/building-apis/putting-it-together-a-handler-that-stays-genuinely-thin\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Putting it together: a handler that stays genuinely thin <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Pull the threads of this chapter together into one realistic endpoint, and notice what it deliberately does <em>not</em> do:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Endpoint (presentation): HTTP concerns only</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Created</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderDto</span>&gt;, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidationProblem</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Conflict</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProblemDetails</span>&gt;&gt;&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrder</span>(\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderRequest</span> req, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IValidator</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderRequest</span>&gt; validator, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPlaceOrderHandler</span> handler, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimsPrincipal</span> user, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> v = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> validator.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidateAsync</span>(req, ct);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!v.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsValid</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TypedResults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidationProblem</span>(v.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToDictionary</span>());\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> result = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> handler.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HandleAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrder</span>(user.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetCustomerId</span>(), req.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span>), ct);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// application layer</span>\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> result.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsSuccess</span>\n        ? <span class=\"tk-ty\">TypedResults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Created</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;/api/orders/{result.Value.Id}&quot;</span>, result.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>)\n        : <span class=\"tk-ty\">TypedResults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Conflict</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProblemDetails</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Title</span> = result.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Message</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Type</span> = result.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Code</span> });\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>There&#39;s no business logic here at all — no stock checks, no pricing rules, nothing about what makes an order valid to place. The handler&#39;s job is strictly <strong>bind → validate → delegate → map the result to HTTP</strong>, and everything about what actually happens to an order lives one layer down, in application and domain code that has never heard of <code>HttpContext</code> and can be tested without spinning up a web server at all. See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/app-architectures\">App architectures</a> and <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cqrs-mediator-result\">CQRS &amp; Result</a> for exactly how that layer is shaped.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Minimal APIs vs controllers: what are the differences and when would you pick each?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Both use the same routing, DI and middleware. Minimal APIs map routes to functions with fluent configuration, have less ceremony, are a bit faster and support Native AOT, and they&#39;re Microsoft&#39;s default recommendation for new APIs. Controllers are classes with attributes and have a richer filter pipeline, model binding and content negotiation. Pick based on team and codebase conventions and the features you need.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What does the [ApiController] attribute do?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>It enables API conventions: automatic 400 ValidationProblemDetails responses when model state is invalid, binding-source inference ([FromBody] for complex types, [FromRoute]/[FromQuery] for simple ones), a requirement for attribute routing, and ProblemDetails bodies for error status codes.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What&#39;s the difference between Results and TypedResults?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Both create IResult responses. TypedResults returns concrete types (Ok&lt;T&gt;, NotFound, Created&lt;T&gt;), so a handler&#39;s return type (often Results&lt;…&gt;) documents every possible response, OpenAPI metadata is inferred automatically, and unit tests can assert on types without executing the result.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How does parameter binding work in minimal APIs?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>By inference. Route parameters bind by name; simple types otherwise come from the query string; complex types from the JSON body (only one body per request); registered services from DI; special types (HttpContext, CancellationToken, ClaimsPrincipal) are injected. Attributes ([FromQuery], [FromHeader], [FromBody], [FromServices], [AsParameters]) make it explicit, and TryParse/BindAsync allow custom binding.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you validate requests in ASP.NET Core?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>With data annotations on DTOs (automatic in controllers with [ApiController]; built in for minimal APIs since .NET 10 via AddValidation), or with FluentValidation applied through an endpoint/action filter or called in the handler. Return 400 ValidationProblemDetails. Keep business rules in the domain/application layer.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is over-posting and how do you prevent it?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>When clients send extra fields that bind onto properties they shouldn&#39;t control (e.g. IsAdmin, Price) because the request is bound directly to an entity. Prevent it by binding to dedicated request DTOs and mapping explicitly.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Explain the MVC filter pipeline order.</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Authorization filters, then resource filters, then model binding, then action filters around the action, then exception filters (if something threw), then result filters around result execution, then resource filters&#39; after-code. Within a type, global then controller then action filters, reversed on the way out.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are endpoint filters?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Minimal API equivalents of action filters: IEndpointFilter implementations attached to endpoints or route groups that run around the handler with access to the bound arguments (EndpointFilterInvocationContext.Arguments) and the returned result. Used for validation, logging and argument checks.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why return DTOs instead of EF Core entities from an API?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>It decouples the API contract from the database schema, prevents over-posting and leaking internal fields, avoids serialization cycles via navigation properties, and lets you project only the needed columns, which is faster.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you customise JSON serialization?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>ConfigureHttpJsonOptions (minimal APIs) or AddJsonOptions (controllers) to set naming policies, converters (JsonStringEnumConverter), ignore conditions and so on; attributes like [JsonPropertyName], [JsonIgnore] and [JsonPolymorphic]; custom JsonConverter&lt;T&gt;; and source-generated JsonSerializerContext for speed and AOT.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/building-apis/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/minimal-apis/overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Minimal APIs overview</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/apis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Choose between controller-based APIs and minimal APIs</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/minimal-apis/parameter-binding\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Parameter binding in minimal APIs</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/mvc/controllers/filters\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Filters in ASP.NET Core (MVC)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/serialization/system-text-json/overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">System.Text.Json overview</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/building-apis/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run</code> in a console project (<code>dotnet new web</code>, then <code>dotnet add package Microsoft.AspNetCore.TestHost</code>). It checks response content types, <code>TypedResults</code> status codes, parameter binding, an endpoint filter short-circuiting on the typed argument, and the over-posting problem itself.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Net</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Http</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Json</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AspNetCore</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Builder</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AspNetCore</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Http</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AspNetCore</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Http</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpResults</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AspNetCore</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TestHost</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> condition, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> claim)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!condition) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: {claim}&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: {claim}&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 1. Returning a plain string gives 200 text/plain; returning an object gives 200 application/json.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> builder1 = <span class=\"tk-ty\">WebApplication</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateBuilder</span>();\nbuilder1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WebHost</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseTestServer</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> app1 = builder1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();\napp1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/text&quot;</span>, () =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Hello&quot;</span>);\napp1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/json&quot;</span>, () =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Utc</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;now&quot;</span> });\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> client1 = app1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetTestClient</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> textResponse = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> client1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/text&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> jsonResponse = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> client1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/json&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StopAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(textResponse.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Content</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ContentType</span>!.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MediaType</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;text/plain&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Returning a plain string produces a text/plain response&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(jsonResponse.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Content</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ContentType</span>!.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MediaType</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;application/json&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Returning any other object produces an application/json response&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 2. TypedResults.Ok&lt;T&gt; / NotFound produce the HTTP status codes the chapter claims.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> builder2 = <span class=\"tk-ty\">WebApplication</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateBuilder</span>();\nbuilder2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WebHost</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseTestServer</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> app2 = builder2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();\napp2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/items/{id:int}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetItem</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;, <span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetItem</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id) =&gt; id == <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> ? <span class=\"tk-ty\">TypedResults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(id) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">TypedResults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> client2 = app2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetTestClient</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> found = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> client2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/items/1&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> notFound = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> client2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/items/2&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StopAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(found.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span> == <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Net</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpStatusCode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OK</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;TypedResults.Ok produces a 200&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(notFound.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span> == <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Net</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpStatusCode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;TypedResults.NotFound produces a 404&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 3. Parameter binding: route value binds by name, query string binds a simple type not in the route.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> builder3 = <span class=\"tk-ty\">WebApplication</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateBuilder</span>();\nbuilder3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WebHost</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseTestServer</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> app3 = builder3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();\napp3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/orders/{id:int}&quot;</span>, (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> page) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;{id}:{page}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> bound = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetTestClient</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetStringAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/orders/7?page=3&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app3.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StopAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(bound == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;7:3&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A route value binds by name and a query-string value binds a simple type not present in the route, in the same handler&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 4. An endpoint filter sees the bound, typed argument and can short-circuit BEFORE the handler runs.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> handlerRan = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> builder4 = <span class=\"tk-ty\">WebApplication</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateBuilder</span>();\nbuilder4.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WebHost</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseTestServer</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> app4 = builder4.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();\napp4.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapPost</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/products&quot;</span>, (<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateProductRequest</span> req) =&gt; { handlerRan = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(); })\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddEndpointFilter</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (ctx, next) =&gt;\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> arg = ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Arguments</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OfType</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateProductRequest</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">FirstOrDefault</span>();\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (arg <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span>: &lt;= <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span> }) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BadRequest</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Price must be positive&quot;</span>);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// sees the TYPED argument</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> next(ctx);\n    });\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app4.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> client4 = app4.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetTestClient</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> rejected = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> client4.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PostAsJsonAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/products&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateProductRequest</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Mouse&quot;</span>, -<span class=\"tk-num\">5m</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> rejectedHandlerRan = handlerRan;\nhandlerRan = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> accepted = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> client4.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PostAsJsonAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/products&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateProductRequest</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Mouse&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">20m</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app4.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StopAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(rejected.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span> == <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Net</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpStatusCode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BadRequest</span> &amp;&amp; !rejectedHandlerRan,\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;An endpoint filter can inspect the bound, typed argument and short-circuit before the handler runs&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(accepted.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span> == <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Net</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpStatusCode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OK</span> &amp;&amp; handlerRan,\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;When the filter lets the request through, the handler runs normally&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 5. Over-posting: binding a request body directly to an entity-shaped type sets ANY writable property</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//    the client sent, including ones a client should never control (e.g. IsAdmin) — a DTO avoids this</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//    simply by not HAVING that property at all.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> entityJson = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;&quot;{ &quot;Name&quot;: &quot;bob&quot;, &quot;IsAdmin&quot;: true }&quot;&quot;&quot;</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> entityBound = <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Text</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Json</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">JsonSerializer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Deserialize</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">UserEntity</span>&gt;(entityJson)!;\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(entityBound.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsAdmin</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Binding JSON directly to an entity-shaped type lets the client set fields it should never control (over-posting)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> dtoJson = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;&quot;{ &quot;Name&quot;: &quot;bob&quot;, &quot;IsAdmin&quot;: true }&quot;&quot;&quot;</span>;   <span class=\"tk-com\">// client tries the same trick against a DTO</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> dtoBound = <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Text</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Json</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">JsonSerializer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Deserialize</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateUserRequest</span>&gt;(dtoJson)!;\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(dtoBound.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;bob&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A request DTO that only declares the allowed fields silently ignores extra fields like IsAdmin&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All building-apis claims verified.&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateProductRequest</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateUserRequest</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">UserEntity</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsAdmin</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } }</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Not attempted here: controller-specific behaviour (<code>[ApiController]</code>&#39;s automatic 400s, MVC&#39;s five filter types, content negotiation via <code>Accept</code>) needs a full MVC host rather than minimal APIs&#39; <code>TestServer</code> setup — see the official docs above for those, the minimal-API equivalents above cover the same underlying mechanism.</p>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"two-syntaxes-the-same-machinery-underneath","text":"Two syntaxes, the same machinery underneath","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"minimal-apis-in-depth","text":"Minimal APIs in depth","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"handler-shapes","text":"Handler shapes","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"organising-endpoints-so-program-cs-stays-readable","text":"Organising endpoints so Program.cs stays readable","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"returning-results-what-each-return-type-actually-produces","text":"Returning results: what each return type actually produces","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"parameter-binding-how-request-data-becomes-a-c-argument","text":"Parameter binding: how request data becomes a C# argument","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"validation-catching-bad-input-before-it-reaches-business-log","text":"Validation: catching bad input before it reaches business logic","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"option-1-data-annotations","text":"Option 1: Data annotations","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"option-2-fluentvalidation-for-rules-annotations-cant-express","text":"Option 2: FluentValidation, for rules annotations can't express","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"input-validation-vs-business-rules-a-distinction-worth-holdi","text":"Input validation vs business rules — a distinction worth holding onto","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"filters-seeing-the-handlers-actual-typed-arguments","text":"Filters: seeing the handler's actual typed arguments","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"endpoint-filters-minimal-apis","text":"Endpoint filters (minimal APIs)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"mvc-filter-pipeline-controllers","text":"MVC filter pipeline (controllers)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"json-with-system-text-json-the-defaults-and-how-to-bend-them","text":"JSON with System.Text.Json: the defaults and how to bend them","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"content-negotiation-formats-and-streaming","text":"Content negotiation, formats and streaming","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"putting-it-together-a-handler-that-stays-genuinely-thin","text":"Putting it together: a handler that stays genuinely thin","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":4688,"minutes":21,"num":"3.6","part":"p3"},"errors-logging":{"id":"errors-logging","title":"Error Handling, ProblemDetails and Logging","summary":"Turning failures into consistent HTTP responses and searchable logs. The exception-handling middleware and IExceptionHandler, ProblemDetails (RFC 9457), status code pages, the ILogger abstraction, levels and categories, structured logging and message templates, scopes, high-performance logging, Serilog and sinks, and what (not) to log.","layer":"framework","where":"ASP.NET Core › Cross-cutting › Errors & logging","fits":"<p>Exception handling is a middleware (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/middleware\">Middleware</a>) at the very front of the pipeline, and logging is host plumbing (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting\">Hosting</a>). Together they are the first half of observability, a layer-8 concern; the second half (metrics and traces, OpenTelemetry) is in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/observability-aspire\">Observability &amp; Aspire</a>.</p>\n","prereqs":["middleware","exceptions-disposal"],"next":["auth","observability-aspire","cqrs-mediator-result"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/errors-logging/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A single failure has two audiences: the <strong>client</strong> gets a safe, consistent <code>ProblemDetails</code> response; your <strong>logs</strong> get the full, searchable detail (exception, context, trace ID). Never mix the two up.</li>\n<li><code>app.UseExceptionHandler()</code> must be the outermost middleware, so it can catch exceptions from everything registered after it. <code>IExceptionHandler</code> implementations are tried in registration order until one claims the exception.</li>\n<li><strong>ProblemDetails</strong> (RFC 9457) is a standard JSON shape for API errors — <code>type</code>/<code>title</code>/<code>status</code>/<code>detail</code> everywhere — so any client can handle errors from any endpoint the same way.</li>\n<li><code>ILogger&lt;T&gt;</code> is one API with pluggable destinations (console, Application Insights, Serilog). Log with <strong>message templates</strong> (<code>&quot;{OrderId}&quot;</code>), not string interpolation, so fields stay queryable.</li>\n<li><strong>Scopes</strong> attach extra fields (like <code>OrderId</code>) to every log line written inside a block, even across an <code>await</code>, because they ride on <code>AsyncLocal</code>.</li>\n<li>Never log secrets, tokens, or full PII. Log IDs, not whole objects, and reach for <code>[LoggerMessage]</code> in hot paths where the logging call itself costs enough to matter.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"two-very-different-audiences-for-the-same-failure\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/errors-logging/two-very-different-audiences-for-the-same-failure\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Two very different audiences for the same failure <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Something goes wrong in your app — a payment fails, a database call times out, a bug throws a <code>NullReferenceException</code>. That single event has to serve <strong>two completely different audiences</strong>, each with a different need:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The client</strong> needs a consistent, safe, machine-readable error: the right status code, a predictable body shape, and critically, <strong>no stack traces or internal implementation details</strong> — those are a gift to an attacker and useless to a legitimate caller.</li>\n<li><strong>The developers</strong> need the opposite: a detailed, searchable record of exactly what happened — the exception, the surrounding context (which user, which order ID, which trace), and the request path — sitting in logs they can actually query later.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>ASP.NET Core gives you one tool for each: <strong>ProblemDetails</strong> answers the client, <strong>ILogger</strong> answers the developers. They&#39;re deliberately separate, and conflating them — accidentally putting a stack trace in the response body, say — is exactly the mistake this chapter is structured to help you avoid.</p>\n<h2 id=\"exception-handling-in-the-pipeline-where-it-has-to-sit-and-w\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/errors-logging/exception-handling-in-the-pipeline-where-it-has-to-sit-and-w\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Exception handling in the pipeline: where it has to sit, and why <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>An unhandled exception bubbles upward through the middleware chain exactly the way the &quot;after <code>next()</code>&quot; code did in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/middleware/now-heres-the-aha-a-middleware-is-just-a-function-wrapping-a\">Middleware</a> — except instead of returning normally, it propagates as a thrown exception. For the exception handler to actually catch everything, it has to be the <strong>outermost</strong> wrapper, registered first:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddProblemDetails</span>();                              <span class=\"tk-com\">// standard error bodies</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddExceptionHandler</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">DomainExceptionHandler</span>&gt;();     <span class=\"tk-com\">// your handlers, tried in order</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddExceptionHandler</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">GlobalExceptionHandler</span>&gt;();\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> app = builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseExceptionHandler</span>();      <span class=\"tk-com\">// FIRST: catches exceptions from everything after it</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseStatusCodePages</span>();       <span class=\"tk-com\">// gives empty 4xx/5xx responses (e.g. 404 from routing) a ProblemDetails body</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Environment</th>\n<th>Default behaviour</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Development</td>\n<td><strong>Developer Exception Page</strong>: stack trace, query, headers, routing info (added automatically)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Production</td>\n<td><code>UseExceptionHandler</code>: logs the exception, returns a generic 500 ProblemDetails</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>That split is deliberate, not accidental: you <em>want</em> the raw stack trace while you&#39;re developing, and you <em>never</em> want it once real users are hitting the app.</p>\n<h2 id=\"iexceptionhandler-mapping-exception-types-to-http-responses\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/errors-logging/iexceptionhandler-mapping-exception-types-to-http-responses\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>IExceptionHandler: mapping exception types to HTTP responses <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Since .NET 8, this is the standard hook for turning exceptions into responses. Handlers are tried <strong>in registration order</strong>, and each one gets to say &quot;not mine&quot; by returning <code>false</code>, letting the next handler have a turn:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DomainExceptionHandler</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IProblemDetailsService</span> problems, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ILogger</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">DomainExceptionHandler</span>&gt; log) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IExceptionHandler</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">TryHandleAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpContext</span> ctx, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span> ex, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> (status, title) = ex <span class=\"tk-kw\">switch</span>\n        {\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFoundException</span>          =&gt; (<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCodes</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status404NotFound</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Resource not found&quot;</span>),\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">DomainException</span> d          =&gt; (<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCodes</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status409Conflict</span>, d.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Message</span>),\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">DbUpdateConcurrencyException</span> =&gt; (<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCodes</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status409Conflict</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;The resource was modified by someone else&quot;</span>),\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">UnauthorizedAccessException</span> =&gt; (<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCodes</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status403Forbidden</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Forbidden&quot;</span>),\n            _ =&gt; (<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>)\n        };\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (status == <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;                       <span class=\"tk-com\">// not mine: let the next handler try</span>\n\n        log.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogWarning</span>(ex, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Handled {ExceptionType}: {Title}&quot;</span>, ex.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetType</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>, title);\n        ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Response</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span> = status;\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> problems.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryWriteAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProblemDetailsContext</span>\n        {\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpContext</span> = ctx,\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span> = ex,\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProblemDetails</span> = { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> = status, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Title</span> = title, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Type</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;https://errors.shop.com/{status}&quot;</span> }\n        });\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">GlobalExceptionHandler</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IProblemDetailsService</span> problems, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ILogger</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">GlobalExceptionHandler</span>&gt; log) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IExceptionHandler</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">TryHandleAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpContext</span> ctx, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span> ex, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (ex <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OperationCanceledException</span> &amp;&amp; ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequestAborted</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsCancellationRequested</span>)\n        {\n            ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Response</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">499</span>;                     <span class=\"tk-com\">// client closed request; not a server error</span>\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;\n        }\n        log.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogError</span>(ex, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Unhandled exception for {Method} {Path}&quot;</span>, ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Request</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Method</span>, ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Request</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Path</span>);\n        ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Response</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCodes</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status500InternalServerError</span>;\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> problems.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryWriteAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>() { <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpContext</span> = ctx, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span> = ex,\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProblemDetails</span> = { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Title</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;An unexpected error occurred&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">500</span> } });\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Registration order matters here: <code>DomainExceptionHandler</code> (specific) goes first, so it gets first refusal on exception types it recognizes. <code>GlobalExceptionHandler</code> (catch-all) goes last, as the backstop for whatever nobody else claimed. (<strong>Before .NET 8</strong>, you&#39;d see the same idea written by hand as custom middleware — <code>try { await next(ctx); } catch (Exception ex) { … }</code> — or <code>UseExceptionHandler(errorApp =&gt; …)</code> with <code>IExceptionHandlerFeature</code>. Same shape, considerably more boilerplate.)</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-tip\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Exceptions vs results for expected failures</div><p>Mapping exceptions centrally like this is convenient, but it&#39;s worth being honest about a real cost: throwing for <strong>expected</strong> outcomes — not found, a failed validation, a business rule rejecting the request — is genuinely expensive (exceptions capture stack traces) and it hides control flow behind <code>try</code>/<code>catch</code> instead of making the possible outcomes visible in a method&#39;s signature. Many teams instead return a <strong>Result</strong> from the application layer and map it to HTTP right in the endpoint, reserving <code>IExceptionHandler</code> for the exceptions that are genuinely unexpected. See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cqrs-mediator-result/the-result-pattern-making-expected-failure-part-of-the-metho\">CQRS &amp; Result</a>.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"problemdetails-one-shape-for-every-error-across-every-api-yo\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/errors-logging/problemdetails-one-shape-for-every-error-across-every-api-yo\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>ProblemDetails: one shape for every error, across every API you&#39;ll ever call <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><strong>RFC 9457</strong> (which replaced the earlier RFC 7807) defines a standard JSON shape for HTTP API errors, served as <code>application/problem+json</code>:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>JSON</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>{\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;type&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://errors.shop.com/409&quot;</span>,\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;title&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shipped orders can't be cancelled&quot;</span>,\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;status&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">409</span>,\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;detail&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Order 1042 shipped on 2026-10-20.&quot;</span>,\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;instance&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/api/orders/1042/cancel&quot;</span>,\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;traceId&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;00-4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736-00f067aa0ba902b7-01&quot;</span>,\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;requestId&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;0HN7…&quot;</span>\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Field</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>type</code></td>\n<td>A URI identifying the <strong>kind</strong> of problem (documentation link); defaults to <code>about:blank</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>title</code></td>\n<td>Short, human-readable summary of the kind</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>status</code></td>\n<td>HTTP status code</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>detail</code></td>\n<td>Explanation specific to this occurrence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>instance</code></td>\n<td>URI of this occurrence (often the request path)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>extensions</td>\n<td>Anything else: <code>traceId</code>, <code>errors</code> (validation), <code>code</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>ValidationProblemDetails</strong> is the same shape with one addition — an <code>errors</code> dictionary, field name to list of messages:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>JSON</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>{ <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;title&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;One or more validation errors occurred.&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;status&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">400</span>,\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;errors&quot;</span>: { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Lines&quot;</span>: [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;An order needs at least one line&quot;</span>], <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;CustomerId&quot;</span>: [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Must be greater than 0&quot;</span>] } }</code></pre></figure>\n<p>You can customise every ProblemDetails response centrally, in one place, instead of repeating logic across every handler:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddProblemDetails</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomizeProblemDetails</span> = ctx =&gt;\n{\n    ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProblemDetails</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Instance</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;{ctx.HttpContext.Request.Method} {ctx.HttpContext.Request.Path}&quot;</span>;\n    ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProblemDetails</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Extensions</span>[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;traceId&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Activity</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Current</span>?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> ?? ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpContext</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TraceIdentifier</span>;\n});</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout c-why\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Why the trace ID matters more than it looks</div><p>Think about what happens without it: a customer reports &quot;I got an error,&quot; and you have... a vague timestamp, maybe, and no way to find the exact request in millions of log lines. With a <code>traceId</code> in the response body, that single value lets you jump straight to <strong>the exact logs and distributed trace</strong> for that one request, in seconds rather than a frustrating search. It&#39;s the bridge connecting &quot;what the client saw&quot; to &quot;what actually happened on the server&quot; — and it only works if you actually put it in the response, which is precisely what the customization above does.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"the-logging-abstraction-one-api-many-destinations\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/errors-logging/the-logging-abstraction-one-api-many-destinations\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The logging abstraction: one API, many destinations <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><code>Microsoft.Extensions.Logging</code> gives your code a single API, <strong><code>ILogger&lt;T&gt;</code></strong>, and lets the <em>host</em> decide where those log entries actually end up — console, a file, Application Insights, several of these at once — through a set of configurable <strong>providers</strong>. Your business code never needs to know or care which.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentService</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ILogger</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentService</span>&gt; logger, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGateway</span> gateway)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentResult</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> order, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n    {\n        logger.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogInformation</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Charging order {OrderId} for {Amount} {Currency}&quot;</span>, order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Amount</span>, order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Currency</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span>\n        {\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> result = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> gateway.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeAsync</span>(order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>, ct);\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!result.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Succeeded</span>)\n                logger.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogWarning</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Payment declined for order {OrderId}: {DeclineCode}&quot;</span>, order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, result.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DeclineCode</span>);\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> result;\n        }\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpRequestException</span> ex)\n        {\n            logger.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogError</span>(ex, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Payment gateway call failed for order {OrderId}&quot;</span>, order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>);\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span>;\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The <strong>category</strong> attached to every log line from this class is the full type name of <code>T</code> — <code>Shop.Payments.PaymentService</code> — and that&#39;s exactly what lets you dial logging verbosity up or down per namespace, as the next section shows.</p>\n<h3 id=\"log-levels-a-severity-ladder-not-just-labels\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/errors-logging/log-levels-a-severity-ladder-not-just-labels\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Log levels: a severity ladder, not just labels</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Level</th>\n<th>Use for</th>\n<th>Production default?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Trace</code></td>\n<td>Very detailed, may include sensitive data</td>\n<td>Off</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Debug</code></td>\n<td>Developer diagnostics</td>\n<td>Off</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Information</code></td>\n<td>Normal flow milestones: &quot;Order placed&quot;, &quot;Job finished&quot;</td>\n<td><strong>On</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Warning</code></td>\n<td>Unexpected but handled: retries, declined payments, slow calls</td>\n<td>On</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Error</code></td>\n<td>A failure of the current operation: exceptions, failed requests</td>\n<td>On</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Critical</code></td>\n<td>The app or a major subsystem is failing: data loss, startup failure</td>\n<td>On</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>JSON</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Logging&quot;</span>: {\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;LogLevel&quot;</span>: {\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Default&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Information&quot;</span>,\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Microsoft.AspNetCore&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Warning&quot;</span>,                       <span class=\"tk-com\">// framework noise down</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Database.Command&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Warning&quot;</span>,  <span class=\"tk-com\">// set to Information to see SQL</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop.Payments&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Debug&quot;</span>                                 <span class=\"tk-com\">// more detail for one area</span>\n  }\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<p>This is the exact same layered configuration mechanism from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/configuration-options/the-five-layers-lowest-priority-to-highest\">Configuration</a>, applied to log-level settings specifically — which is why you can crank <code>Shop.Payments</code> up to <code>Debug</code> in one environment via an environment variable, without touching the base <code>appsettings.json</code> at all.</p>\n<h2 id=\"structured-logging-keeping-the-data-not-just-the-sentence\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/errors-logging/structured-logging-keeping-the-data-not-just-the-sentence\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Structured logging: keeping the data, not just the sentence <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Here&#39;s a distinction easy to miss on a first read: the string <code>&quot;Charging order {OrderId} for {Amount}&quot;</code> is a <strong>message template</strong>, not an interpolated string, and that&#39;s the whole point of writing it this way. The logger keeps <code>OrderId</code> and <code>Amount</code> as <strong>named properties</strong>, separately from the rendered text, and a structured sink — Application Insights, Seq, Elasticsearch, Loki, Datadog — stores each one as a real, queryable field rather than burying it inside an opaque sentence:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>// Query in Application Insights / Log Analytics (KQL)\ntraces | where customDimensions.OrderId == &quot;1042&quot;\n\n// Seq\nOrderId = 1042 and @Level = 'Error'</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Now compare the three calls below, because the difference between the first and the other two is exactly what makes structured logging valuable in the first place:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>logger.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogInformation</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Order {OrderId} placed&quot;</span>, order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>);        <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✔ structured: OrderId is a field</span>\nlogger.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogInformation</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;Order {order.Id} placed&quot;</span>);                <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✘ one opaque string; also allocates even if Information is disabled</span>\nlogger.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogInformation</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Order placed: {@Order}&quot;</span>, order);           <span class=\"tk-com\">// @ = destructure the object (Serilog); careful with size and PII</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The second line <em>looks</em> equivalent and produces visually identical console output, but it has thrown away the structure — there&#39;s no <code>OrderId</code> field for a query tool to filter on, just a string that happens to contain a number. It also pays the cost of formatting that string <strong>even when <code>Information</code> logging is disabled entirely</strong>, which the template form avoids. Two naming disciplines keep this consistent across a codebase: <strong>PascalCase</strong> placeholder names, and the <em>same</em> name everywhere — always <code>OrderId</code>, never <code>orderId</code> in one place and <code>Id</code> in another — because a query tool treats those as entirely different fields. And never reach for string interpolation in a log call at all; the analyzer rule <strong>CA2254</strong> exists specifically to catch this.</p>\n<h2 id=\"log-scopes-attaching-context-to-everything-inside-a-block\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/errors-logging/log-scopes-attaching-context-to-everything-inside-a-block\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Log scopes: attaching context to everything inside a block <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>A scope attaches a set of properties to <strong>every single log line written inside it</strong>, for as long as the block is open — which is exactly the mechanism correlation IDs rely on:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> (logger.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BeginScope</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>&gt; { [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OrderId&quot;</span>] = order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;CustomerId&quot;</span>] = order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> }))\n{\n    logger.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogInformation</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Reserving stock&quot;</span>);     <span class=\"tk-com\">// carries OrderId and CustomerId</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> stock.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReserveAsync</span>(order, ct);           <span class=\"tk-com\">// logs inside here carry them too (scopes flow via AsyncLocal)</span>\n    logger.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogInformation</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Stock reserved&quot;</span>);\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The scope survives across that <code>await</code> — even onto a different thread — because it&#39;s carried by <code>AsyncLocal</code>, the same ambient-state mechanism the async state machine relies on in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/async-await\">Async and await</a>. Logging didn&#39;t have to invent anything new for this. ASP.NET Core uses the same trick automatically: it wraps every request in a scope carrying <code>RequestId</code>, <code>RequestPath</code>, <code>TraceId</code> and <code>SpanId</code>, so any log line anywhere in that request&#39;s call graph can be traced straight back to its request and its place in a distributed trace. Turn this on in the console provider with <code>&quot;IncludeScopes&quot;: true</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"high-performance-logging-when-the-logging-call-itself-become\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/errors-logging/high-performance-logging-when-the-logging-call-itself-become\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>High-performance logging: when the logging call itself becomes the bottleneck <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Every ordinary <code>logger.LogInformation(template, args)</code> call does real work before it even checks whether anything will be written: it boxes value-type arguments into an <code>object[]</code>, and looks up the (cached) parsed template. That overhead adds up in a hot path — something called thousands of times a second — and the source-generated <strong><code>[LoggerMessage]</code></strong> attribute exists to eliminate it:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> partial <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderLog</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[LoggerMessage(EventId = 1001, Level = LogLevel.Information, Message = &quot;Order {OrderId} placed by customer {CustomerId}&quot;)]</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> partial <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">this</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ILogger</span> logger, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> orderId, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> customerId);\n\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[LoggerMessage(EventId = 1002, Level = LogLevel.Warning, Message = &quot;Stock low for {Sku}: {Remaining} left&quot;)]</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> partial <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">StockLow</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">this</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ILogger</span> logger, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> sku, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> remaining);\n}\n\nlogger.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span>(order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// checks IsEnabled first, no boxing, no params array</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The generated method checks <code>IsEnabled</code> as the very first thing, before touching the arguments at all, so a disabled log level costs almost nothing. For anything not covered by a generated method, the manual equivalent is <code>if (logger.IsEnabled(LogLevel.Debug)) { ... }</code> around any argument that&#39;s itself expensive to compute. Analyzer <strong>CA1848</strong> flags hot-path calls where <code>LoggerMessage</code> would help.</p>\n<h2 id=\"providers-and-serilog-where-the-log-lines-actually-go\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/errors-logging/providers-and-serilog-where-the-log-lines-actually-go\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Providers and Serilog: where the log lines actually go <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Provider / library</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Console, Debug, EventSource, EventLog</td>\n<td>Built in</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>OpenTelemetry</strong> (<code>AddOpenTelemetry().WithLogging()</code>)</td>\n<td>Export logs alongside traces and metrics via OTLP; vendor-neutral</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Application Insights</strong> / Azure Monitor</td>\n<td>Azure&#39;s APM; via the OpenTelemetry distro (<code>UseAzureMonitor()</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Serilog</strong></td>\n<td>The most popular third-party library: rich sinks (Seq, Elasticsearch, files, Splunk), enrichers, structured destructuring</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NLog</td>\n<td>Long-standing alternative</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Serilog setup</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Host</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseSerilog</span>((ctx, services, cfg) =&gt; cfg\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadFrom</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configuration</span>(ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configuration</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Enrich</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromLogContext</span>()\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Enrich</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithProperty</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Service&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;shop-api&quot;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteTo</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">RenderedCompactJsonFormatter</span>())        <span class=\"tk-com\">// JSON to stdout: containers ship it</span>\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteTo</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Seq</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;http://seq:5341&quot;</span>));\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseSerilogRequestLogging</span>();   <span class=\"tk-com\">// one concise log line per request: method, path, status, elapsed</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>In containerised deployments specifically, the standard pattern is to <strong>log JSON to stdout</strong> and let whatever&#39;s running the container — Container Apps, Kubernetes, a Datadog agent — collect and ship it onward, or export directly via OpenTelemetry. This mirrors the &quot;no file on disk&quot; philosophy from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/configuration-options/secrets-the-layer-systems-hardest-case\">Configuration</a>: the app doesn&#39;t manage its own log files any more than it manages its own secrets — the platform handles both.</p>\n<h2 id=\"http-request-logging\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/errors-logging/http-request-logging\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>HTTP request logging <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddHttpLogging</span>(o =&gt;\n{\n    o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LoggingFields</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpLoggingFields</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequestMethod</span> | <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpLoggingFields</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequestPath</span> | <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpLoggingFields</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ResponseStatusCode</span> | <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpLoggingFields</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Duration</span>;\n    o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CombineLogs</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;\n});\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseHttpLogging</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Be deliberate about which fields you enable here: bodies and headers routinely contain tokens, cookies and personal data, and logging them by default is how sensitive data quietly ends up sitting in a log aggregator for months.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-to-log-and-what-never-to-log\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/errors-logging/what-to-log-and-what-never-to-log\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What to log and what never to log <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>✔ Log</th>\n<th>✘ Never log</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Business events with IDs (order placed, payment declined)</td>\n<td>Passwords, tokens, API keys, connection strings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exceptions <strong>with the exception object</strong></td>\n<td>Full credit card numbers, CVV, bank details</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Calls to external systems: target, duration, outcome</td>\n<td>Personal data beyond what&#39;s necessary (GDPR): full addresses, health data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Retries, fallbacks, circuit breaker state changes</td>\n<td>Entire request/response bodies by default</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Startup configuration summary (non-secret)</td>\n<td>Session cookies, <code>Authorization</code> headers</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>A few habits worth adopting, each guarding against a specific recurring mistake:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Log a failure <strong>once</strong>, at the boundary that actually handles it — not again at every layer as the exception rethrows upward and each <code>catch</code> block logs it a second time.</li>\n<li>Don&#39;t log a routine <code>OperationCanceledException</code> as an <code>Error</code>. A client closing the connection isn&#39;t a server failure.</li>\n<li>Log IDs, not whole objects, so a log line stays small and never accidentally serializes a field you didn&#39;t intend to expose.</li>\n<li>For anything approaching real PII, use the <strong>Microsoft.Extensions.Compliance.Redaction</strong> package — built for data classification and redaction — rather than trying to remember every sensitive field by hand.</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you implement global exception handling in ASP.NET Core?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Register AddProblemDetails and one or more IExceptionHandler implementations (AddExceptionHandler&lt;T&gt;), and put app.UseExceptionHandler() first in the pipeline. Handlers map known exception types to status codes and ProblemDetails, log appropriately, and return true; unknown ones fall through to a generic 500. The Developer Exception Page is used only in Development.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is ProblemDetails?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The RFC 9457 (formerly 7807) standard JSON format for HTTP API errors: type, title, status, detail, instance, plus extensions like traceId or validation errors. ASP.NET Core produces it via AddProblemDetails, Results.Problem/ValidationProblem and [ApiController] conventions.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why shouldn&#39;t you return stack traces to clients?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>They leak implementation details (framework versions, file paths, SQL, internal names) that help attackers, and they&#39;re useless to clients. Return a generic ProblemDetails with a trace ID and keep the details in server logs.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is structured logging and why is it better?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Logging with message templates whose placeholders are captured as named properties instead of being formatted into a string. Log stores can then index and query by those fields (all errors for OrderId 1042, p95 duration by endpoint), which plain text can&#39;t support well.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why not use string interpolation in log calls?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Interpolation produces a single pre-formatted string, so the structured properties are lost. It also always allocates and formats, even when the log level is disabled. Use message templates with placeholders (CA2254).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are log scopes?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Ambient properties attached to all log entries written within a using block (BeginScope), flowing across awaits via AsyncLocal. ASP.NET Core adds RequestId, TraceId and SpanId automatically; you can add OrderId, TenantId and so on for correlation.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is LoggerMessage and when would you use it?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A source generator ([LoggerMessage] on partial methods) that produces strongly typed, high-performance logging methods that check the level first, avoid boxing and params arrays, and pre-parse templates. Use it in hot paths and libraries.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you choose log levels?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Information for normal business milestones, Warning for unexpected but handled situations (retries, declines), Error for failures of an operation (exceptions), Critical for system-wide failures, and Debug/Trace for diagnostics that are off in production. Turn framework categories down to Warning to reduce noise.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How would you correlate logs across microservices?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Use W3C trace context propagation (traceparent header, automatic with HttpClient and OpenTelemetry) so every service logs with the same TraceId, include the trace ID in ProblemDetails responses, and send logs to a central store that can filter by TraceId and link to the distributed trace.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/errors-logging/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/error-handling\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Handle errors in ASP.NET Core</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/error-handling-api\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Handle errors in ASP.NET Core APIs (ProblemDetails)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/extensions/logging\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Logging in .NET</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/extensions/logger-message-generator\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Compile-time logging source generation</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">RFC 9457: Problem Details for HTTP APIs</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/errors-logging/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>The checks below cover exception-to-status mapping, structured logging vs interpolation, scopes surviving an <code>await</code>, and <code>IsEnabled</code> gating — all pure C#, no ASP.NET host needed. Save as a console app (<code>dotnet new console</code>, <code>dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.Logging</code>), then <code>dotnet run</code>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Extensions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Logging</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: exception-type-to-status mapping (the shape of IExceptionHandler.TryHandleAsync in this chapter).</span>\n{\n    (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> status, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>? title) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Map</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span> ex) =&gt; ex <span class=\"tk-kw\">switch</span>\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFoundException</span> =&gt; (<span class=\"tk-num\">404</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Resource not found&quot;</span>),\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">DomainException</span> d =&gt; (<span class=\"tk-num\">409</span>, d.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Message</span>),\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">UnauthorizedAccessException</span> =&gt; (<span class=\"tk-num\">403</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Forbidden&quot;</span>),\n        _ =&gt; (<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>)\n    };\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Map</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFoundException</span>()).status != <span class=\"tk-num\">404</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: NotFoundException should map to 404&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Map</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DomainException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shipped orders can't be cancelled&quot;</span>)).status != <span class=\"tk-num\">409</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: DomainException should map to 409&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Map</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">UnauthorizedAccessException</span>()).status != <span class=\"tk-num\">403</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: UnauthorizedAccessException should map to 403&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Map</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>()).status != <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: unknown exception should fall through (status 0 = 'not mine')&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: exception types map to the status codes this chapter claims, and unknown types fall through&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> provider = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CapturingLoggerProvider</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> factory = <span class=\"tk-ty\">LoggerFactory</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Create</span>(b =&gt; b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddProvider</span>(provider).<span class=\"tk-ty\">SetMinimumLevel</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogLevel</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Information</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> logger = factory.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateLogger</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop.Payments&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: a message template keeps placeholders as named, queryable properties; string interpolation does not.</span>\n{\n    provider.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entries</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Clear</span>();\n    logger.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogInformation</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Order {OrderId} placed&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1042</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> templateEntry = provider.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entries</span>[^<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>];\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!templateEntry.<span class=\"tk-ty\">State</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Any</span>(kv =&gt; kv.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Key</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OrderId&quot;</span> &amp;&amp; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Equals</span>(kv.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1042</span>)))\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: message template should produce a structured OrderId property&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: a message template keeps OrderId as a structured, named property&quot;</span>);\n\n    provider.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entries</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Clear</span>();\n    logger.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogInformation</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;Order {1042} placed&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> interpolatedEntry = provider.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entries</span>[^<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>];\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (interpolatedEntry.<span class=\"tk-ty\">State</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Any</span>(kv =&gt; kv.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Key</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OrderId&quot;</span>))\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: interpolated string should NOT have an OrderId property&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: a pre-interpolated string has no OrderId property — just an opaque sentence&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: BeginScope attaches properties to every log line inside the block, and they survive an await.</span>\n{\n    provider.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entries</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Clear</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> (logger.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BeginScope</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>&gt; { [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OrderId&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-num\">1042</span>, [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;CustomerId&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-num\">7</span> }))\n    {\n        logger.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogInformation</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Reserving stock&quot;</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Yield</span>(); <span class=\"tk-com\">// force a real continuation, possibly on a different thread-pool thread</span>\n        logger.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogInformation</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Stock reserved&quot;</span>);\n    }\n    logger.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogInformation</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Outside the scope&quot;</span>);\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> insideScope = provider.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entries</span>[^<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>]; <span class=\"tk-com\">// &quot;Stock reserved&quot;, after the await</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> scopeDict = insideScope.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Scopes</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OfType</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>&gt;&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">FirstOrDefault</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (scopeDict <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span> || !<span class=\"tk-ty\">Equals</span>(scopeDict[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OrderId&quot;</span>], <span class=\"tk-num\">1042</span>))\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: the scope's OrderId should still be attached after an await&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: a scope's properties survive an await, even onto a different thread-pool thread&quot;</span>);\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> outsideScope = provider.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entries</span>[^<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>]; <span class=\"tk-com\">// &quot;Outside the scope&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (outsideScope.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Scopes</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OfType</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>&gt;&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Any</span>())\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: the scope should not leak past its using block&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: a scope's properties do not leak past its using block&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: logger.IsEnabled(level) lets you skip expensive work for a disabled level, before any formatting happens.</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> expensiveWasCalled = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Expensive</span>() { expensiveWasCalled = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;computed value&quot;</span>; }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (logger.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsEnabled</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogLevel</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Debug</span>)) <span class=\"tk-com\">// false: minimum level is Information</span>\n        logger.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogDebug</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Expensive: {Value}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Expensive</span>());\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (expensiveWasCalled) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: Expensive() should not run when Debug logging is disabled&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: IsEnabled(level) lets you skip computing arguments for a disabled level&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All checks passed.&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here (needs a real ASP.NET Core host/HTTP pipeline):</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - UseExceptionHandler() ordering actually catching exceptions thrown by later middleware — see Further reading: &quot;Handle errors in ASP.NET Core&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - the exact ProblemDetails JSON shape served over HTTP, status code pages, Developer Exception Page — see Further reading: &quot;RFC 9457&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - [LoggerMessage] source-generator output and its measured allocation savings vs manual calls — see Further reading: &quot;Compile-time logging source generation&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - Serilog sinks, OpenTelemetry export, Application Insights — all external systems; see Further reading</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFoundException</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DomainException</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> message) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(message);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CapturingLoggerProvider</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">ILoggerProvider</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ISupportExternalScope</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">IExternalScopeProvider</span>? _scopeProvider;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Message</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IReadOnlyList</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">KeyValuePair</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>&gt;&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">State</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Scopes</span>)&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Entries</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>();\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ILogger</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateLogger</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> categoryName) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CapturingLogger</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">this</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SetScopeProvider</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IExternalScopeProvider</span> scopeProvider) =&gt; _scopeProvider = scopeProvider;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dispose</span>() { }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CapturingLogger</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CapturingLoggerProvider</span> owner) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">ILogger</span>\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IDisposable</span>? <span class=\"tk-ty\">BeginScope</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TState</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TState</span> state) <span class=\"tk-kw\">where</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TState</span> : <span class=\"tk-kw\">notnull</span> =&gt;\n            owner._scopeProvider?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Push</span>(state);\n\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsEnabled</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogLevel</span> logLevel) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;\n\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Log</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TState</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogLevel</span> logLevel, <span class=\"tk-ty\">EventId</span> eventId, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TState</span> state, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>? exception,\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TState</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>?, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; formatter)\n        {\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> kvps = (state <span class=\"tk-kw\">as</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IReadOnlyList</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">KeyValuePair</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>&gt;&gt;) ?? <span class=\"tk-ty\">Array</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Empty</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">KeyValuePair</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>&gt;&gt;();\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> scopes = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>&gt;();\n            owner._scopeProvider?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ForEachScope</span>((scope, list) =&gt; list.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(scope!), scopes);\n            owner.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entries</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>((formatter(state, exception), kvps, scopes));\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"two-very-different-audiences-for-the-same-failure","text":"Two very different audiences for the same failure","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"exception-handling-in-the-pipeline-where-it-has-to-sit-and-w","text":"Exception handling in the pipeline: where it has to sit, and why","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"iexceptionhandler-mapping-exception-types-to-http-responses","text":"IExceptionHandler: mapping exception types to HTTP responses","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"problemdetails-one-shape-for-every-error-across-every-api-yo","text":"ProblemDetails: one shape for every error, across every API you'll ever call","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"the-logging-abstraction-one-api-many-destinations","text":"The logging abstraction: one API, many destinations","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"log-levels-a-severity-ladder-not-just-labels","text":"Log levels: a severity ladder, not just labels","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"structured-logging-keeping-the-data-not-just-the-sentence","text":"Structured logging: keeping the data, not just the sentence","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"log-scopes-attaching-context-to-everything-inside-a-block","text":"Log scopes: attaching context to everything inside a block","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"high-performance-logging-when-the-logging-call-itself-become","text":"High-performance logging: when the logging call itself becomes the bottleneck","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"providers-and-serilog-where-the-log-lines-actually-go","text":"Providers and Serilog: where the log lines actually go","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"http-request-logging","text":"HTTP request logging","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"what-to-log-and-what-never-to-log","text":"What to log and what never to log","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":4427,"minutes":20,"num":"3.7","part":"p3"},"auth":{"id":"auth","title":"Authentication and Authorization","summary":"Who you are vs what you may do. Claims and ClaimsPrincipal, authentication schemes and handlers, cookies vs JWT bearer tokens, how JWTs are validated, ASP.NET Core Identity, authorization with roles, claims, policies, requirements and handlers, resource-based authorization, API keys, and multiple schemes.","layer":"framework","where":"ASP.NET Core › Request pipeline › Authentication & authorization","fits":"<p>Auth is two middleware in the pipeline (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/middleware\">Middleware</a>) that read endpoint metadata selected by routing (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/routing\">Routing</a>). The <em>tokens</em> usually come from an external identity provider through OAuth 2.0/OIDC (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/oauth-oidc-identity\">OAuth &amp; OIDC</a>), and protecting against attacks is in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/web-security\">Web security</a>. In the cloud, Microsoft Entra ID is the usual provider (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets\">Identity &amp; secrets</a>).</p>\n","prereqs":["middleware","routing"],"next":["oauth-oidc-identity","web-security"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/auth/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Authentication</strong> = who you are (produces a <code>ClaimsPrincipal</code>); <strong>authorization</strong> = what you&#39;re allowed to do. Mixing these up is the root of most auth bugs.</li>\n<li>A user is a bag of <strong>claims</strong> (key/value facts), not a username string. A <strong>role</strong> is just a claim named <code>role</code> — nothing more special than any other claim.</li>\n<li><strong>Cookies</strong> suit browser apps (auto-sent, easy to revoke, needs CSRF defense); <strong>JWTs</strong> suit APIs/SPAs/mobile (stateless, hard to revoke, so keep them short-lived). For SPAs today, the <strong>BFF pattern</strong> gets the best of both.</li>\n<li>A JWT is validated with the issuer&#39;s <strong>public key</strong> (no database call per request) by checking the signature, issuer, audience, and expiry.</li>\n<li><strong>Policies</strong> name a reusable authorization rule once, instead of scattering role checks everywhere. <strong>Resource-based authorization</strong> handles rules that depend on which specific row (&quot;your own orders only&quot;).</li>\n<li>ASP.NET Core Identity is a user-storage library, not an OAuth server — for real single sign-on, use an identity provider like Entra ID.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"two-questions-that-get-conflated-constantly-and-shouldnt-be\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/auth/two-questions-that-get-conflated-constantly-and-shouldnt-be\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Two questions that get conflated constantly, and shouldn&#39;t be <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>&quot;The user isn&#39;t authorized&quot; is one of those phrases that papers over two genuinely different failures. Before anything else in this chapter, pin these apart, because every bug and every design decision that follows traces back to keeping them separate:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Authentication (AuthN)</th>\n<th>Authorization (AuthZ)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Question</td>\n<td><strong>Who are you?</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Are you allowed to do this?</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Input</td>\n<td>Cookie, bearer token, API key, client certificate</td>\n<td>The authenticated user + the resource + the action</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Output</td>\n<td>A <code>ClaimsPrincipal</code> in <code>HttpContext.User</code></td>\n<td>Allow, or deny</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Failure</td>\n<td><strong>401 Unauthorized</strong> (really &quot;unauthenticated&quot;), via a <strong>challenge</strong></td>\n<td><strong>403 Forbidden</strong>, via <strong>forbid</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Middleware</td>\n<td><code>UseAuthentication()</code></td>\n<td><code>UseAuthorization()</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>request ─▶ UseRouting ─▶ UseAuthentication ─▶ UseAuthorization ─▶ endpoint\n                          reads cookie/JWT       reads endpoint's [Authorize]/policy\n                          sets HttpContext.User  evaluates against User → 401/403 or continue</code></pre></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout c-analogy\"><div class=\"callout-title\">The airport</div><p>Showing your <strong>passport</strong> at check-in is authentication: it proves who you are. Your <strong>boarding pass</strong> for gate 12 is authorization: it decides what you may do. A valid passport doesn&#39;t let you board any plane you like — and the two checkpoints are run by entirely different people, for entirely different reasons.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"claims-identities-and-principals-the-vocabulary-everything-e\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/auth/claims-identities-and-principals-the-vocabulary-everything-e\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Claims, identities and principals: the vocabulary everything else builds on <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>ASP.NET Core doesn&#39;t represent a logged-in user as a username string or a database row reference — it represents them as a bundle of <strong>claims</strong>: individual key/value statements about the user, each one issued by someone the app trusts.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>ClaimsPrincipal (HttpContext.User)                 ← the user\n └── ClaimsIdentity  (AuthenticationType = &quot;Bearer&quot;) ← one identity per scheme (usually one)\n      ├── Claim  sub        = &quot;7f3c…&quot;                  (subject: unique user id)\n      ├── Claim  email      = &quot;ana@shop.com&quot;\n      ├── Claim  name       = &quot;Ana&quot;\n      ├── Claim  role       = &quot;admin&quot;\n      ├── Claim  scope      = &quot;orders.read orders.write&quot;\n      └── Claim  tenant_id  = &quot;acme&quot;</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/me&quot;</span>, (<span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimsPrincipal</span> user) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> = user.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FindFirstValue</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimTypes</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NameIdentifier</span>) ?? user.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FindFirstValue</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;sub&quot;</span>),\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Email</span> = user.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FindFirstValue</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimTypes</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Email</span>),\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsAdmin</span> = user.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsInRole</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;admin&quot;</span>),\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Authenticated</span> = user.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Identity</span>?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsAuthenticated</span>\n}).<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequireAuthorization</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Here&#39;s why this indirection is worth the extra concept: a <strong>role</strong> is just a claim whose type happens to be <code>role</code> — there&#39;s nothing structurally special about it. Because identity is a flat bag of claims rather than a fixed schema, ASP.NET Core doesn&#39;t actually care whether those claims came from a cookie you issued, a JWT from Entra ID, or an API key you validated yourself. Whatever produced them, by the time your code runs, they all look like the same <code>ClaimsPrincipal</code> — which is precisely what lets the rest of this chapter work identically regardless of which identity provider sits behind it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"authentication-schemes-and-handlers-naming-the-thing-that-do\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/auth/authentication-schemes-and-handlers-naming-the-thing-that-do\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Authentication schemes and handlers: naming the thing that does the checking <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>A <strong>scheme</strong> is a named authentication handler plus its configuration. You register one or more, and declare which one is the default:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAuthentication</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">JwtBearerDefaults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticationScheme</span>)   <span class=\"tk-com\">// default scheme &quot;Bearer&quot;</span>\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddJwtBearer</span>(...)                                                        <span class=\"tk-com\">// APIs</span>\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddCookie</span>(...)                                                           <span class=\"tk-com\">// browser apps</span>\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddOpenIdConnect</span>(...)                                                    <span class=\"tk-com\">// sign-in via an external IdP</span>\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScheme</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ApiKeyOptions</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ApiKeyHandler</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ApiKey&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>);                 <span class=\"tk-com\">// custom</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Every handler, regardless of scheme, implements up to five distinct actions — and seeing them side by side explains why &quot;login&quot; means something so different for a cookie app versus an API:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Action</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Authenticate</strong></td>\n<td>Build a principal from the request</td>\n<td>Validate the JWT in the <code>Authorization</code> header</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Challenge</strong></td>\n<td>Respond when an unauthenticated user hits a protected resource</td>\n<td>JWT: <code>401</code> + <code>WWW-Authenticate: Bearer</code>; Cookie: redirect to login; OIDC: redirect to the IdP</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Forbid</strong></td>\n<td>Respond when an authenticated user isn&#39;t allowed</td>\n<td><code>403</code>; Cookie: redirect to access-denied</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SignIn</strong></td>\n<td>Persist the identity</td>\n<td>Cookie: issue the auth cookie</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SignOut</strong></td>\n<td>Remove it</td>\n<td>Cookie: delete it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"cookies-vs-bearer-tokens-the-tradeoff-behind-how-do-i-stay-l\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/auth/cookies-vs-bearer-tokens-the-tradeoff-behind-how-do-i-stay-l\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Cookies vs bearer tokens: the tradeoff behind &quot;how do I stay logged in?&quot; <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Cookie authentication</th>\n<th>JWT bearer tokens</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Typical client</td>\n<td>Server-rendered web apps, BFF for SPAs</td>\n<td>APIs called by SPAs, mobile apps, other services</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How it&#39;s sent</td>\n<td>Browser sends the cookie <strong>automatically</strong></td>\n<td>Client adds <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code> <strong>explicitly</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>State</td>\n<td>Encrypted auth ticket in the cookie (or session store)</td>\n<td>Self-contained signed token</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Issued by</td>\n<td>Your app (after login)</td>\n<td>An identity provider / token service</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CSRF risk</td>\n<td><strong>Yes</strong> (auto-sent): needs SameSite plus antiforgery</td>\n<td>No (not auto-sent by browsers)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>XSS risk</td>\n<td><code>HttpOnly</code> cookie can&#39;t be read by JS</td>\n<td>Token in JS memory or localStorage <strong>can</strong> be stolen by XSS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Revocation</td>\n<td>Easy: invalidate server-side or change the security stamp</td>\n<td>Hard: valid until expiry; keep lifetimes short</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Notice the two risk rows trade places: cookies are vulnerable to CSRF precisely <em>because</em> the browser sends them automatically — which is the same property that makes them convenient — while JWTs dodge CSRF but expose themselves to a different attacker if your app has an XSS hole anywhere, since JavaScript has to be able to read the token to attach it. Current guidance for <strong>browser SPAs</strong> sidesteps the dilemma rather than picking a side: the <strong>BFF (Backend-for-Frontend)</strong> pattern has a small server-side app handle the OIDC flow and keep tokens entirely server-side, so the browser only ever holds a secure <code>HttpOnly</code> <code>SameSite</code> cookie — getting the cookie&#39;s XSS resistance without reintroducing the JWT&#39;s revocation problem. See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/oauth-oidc-identity\">OAuth &amp; OIDC</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"jwt-bearer-authentication-traced-claim-by-claim\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/auth/jwt-bearer-authentication-traced-claim-by-claim\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>JWT bearer authentication, traced claim by claim <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>A <strong>JWT</strong> (JSON Web Token) is just three base64url-encoded parts joined with dots: <code>header.payload.signature</code>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIsImtpZCI6IjEyMyJ9 . eyJzdWIiOiI3ZjNjIiwiYXVkIjoiYXBpOi8vc2hvcCIsImV4cCI6MTc2… . MEUCIQD…\n header: {&quot;alg&quot;:&quot;RS256&quot;,&quot;kid&quot;:&quot;123&quot;}      payload (claims):                                            signature\n                                          { &quot;iss&quot;: &quot;https://login.microsoftonline.com/&lt;tenant&gt;/v2.0&quot;,\n                                            &quot;sub&quot;: &quot;7f3c…&quot;, &quot;aud&quot;: &quot;api://shop-api&quot;,\n                                            &quot;exp&quot;: 1767225600, &quot;nbf&quot;: 1767222000, &quot;iat&quot;: 1767222000,\n                                            &quot;scp&quot;: &quot;orders.read&quot;, &quot;roles&quot;: [&quot;admin&quot;] }</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Claim</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>iss</code></td>\n<td>Issuer: who created the token</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>sub</code></td>\n<td>Subject: the user (or client) ID</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>aud</code></td>\n<td>Audience: which API the token is <strong>for</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>exp</code> / <code>nbf</code> / <code>iat</code></td>\n<td>Expires / not before / issued at (Unix seconds)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>scp</code> / <code>scope</code></td>\n<td>Delegated permissions (OAuth scopes)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>roles</code></td>\n<td>App roles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>azp</code> / <code>client_id</code></td>\n<td>Which client app requested it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Here&#39;s the question worth sitting with: how does an API validate this token&#39;s authenticity without calling back to whoever issued it, for every single request? The answer is asymmetric cryptography, and it&#39;s worth walking through the five steps in order, because each one depends on the last:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Parse the header and read the key ID (<code>kid</code>) — which specific key was used to sign this token.</li>\n<li>Fetch the issuer&#39;s <strong>public signing keys</strong> from its OIDC discovery document (<code>{authority}/.well-known/openid-configuration</code> → <code>jwks_uri</code>) — cached after the first lookup and refreshed periodically, not fetched on every request.</li>\n<li><strong>Verify the signature</strong> using that public key (RS256/ES256). Because only the issuer holds the matching <em>private</em> key, a valid signature proves both that the token came from them and that nobody tampered with the payload in transit.</li>\n<li>Check <strong><code>iss</code></strong> matches the expected issuer, <strong><code>aud</code></strong> matches this specific API (not some other API the same provider also issues tokens for), and <strong><code>exp</code>/<code>nbf</code></strong> against the current clock, with a small allowance for clock drift (5 minutes by default).</li>\n<li>Build the <code>ClaimsPrincipal</code> straight from the payload&#39;s claims.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><img src=\"visuals/jwt-validation.svg\" alt=\"Five-step JWT validation: parse the header, fetch signing keys, verify the signature, check the issuer/audience/expiry claims, then build the ClaimsPrincipal\"></p>\n<p>Follow what this buys you: steps 1 and 3 together mean the API never needs its own database of valid tokens — the cryptographic signature <em>is</em> the proof, which is exactly why this scales to millions of requests without a database round trip on each one.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAuthentication</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">JwtBearerDefaults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticationScheme</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddJwtBearer</span>(o =&gt;\n    {\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Authority</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://login.microsoftonline.com/&lt;tenant-id&gt;/v2.0&quot;</span>;   <span class=\"tk-com\">// discovery + signing keys</span>\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Audience</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;api://shop-api&quot;</span>;\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapInboundClaims</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;            <span class=\"tk-com\">// keep &quot;sub&quot;, &quot;roles&quot; as-is instead of long ClaimTypes URIs</span>\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TokenValidationParameters</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TokenValidationParameters</span>\n        {\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidateIssuer</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidateAudience</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidateLifetime</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>,\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">ClockSkew</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromMinutes</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>),\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">RoleClaimType</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;roles&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">NameClaimType</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;name&quot;</span>\n        };\n    });\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// For Entra ID specifically, Microsoft.Identity.Web wraps this: .AddMicrosoftIdentityWebApi(builder.Configuration)</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">JWTs are signed, not encrypted</div><p>Anyone can base64-decode the payload — try pasting any JWT into jwt.io and watch the claims appear in plain text. <strong>Never put secrets or sensitive personal data in a JWT.</strong> The signature only guarantees the payload hasn&#39;t been altered and genuinely came from the stated issuer; it does nothing to hide the payload&#39;s contents from anyone who intercepts it.</p>\n</aside>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Don&#39;t roll your own token service with a shared secret</div><p>Issuing <code>HS256</code> tokens signed with a symmetric key sitting in <code>appsettings.json</code> works fine in a tutorial, and that&#39;s exactly the problem — every API that needs to <em>validate</em> tokens then holds a key that can also <em>mint</em> them, which means any one compromised service can forge tokens for every other service in the system. Use an identity provider (Entra ID, Auth0, Okta, Keycloak, Duende IdentityServer) built on asymmetric keys instead, where validating and minting require different keys entirely. The built-in ASP.NET Core Identity API endpoints issue opaque bearer tokens, which is a reasonable fit for simple first-party scenarios only.</p>\n</aside>\n<h3 id=\"token-lifetimes-and-refresh\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/auth/token-lifetimes-and-refresh\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Token lifetimes and refresh</h3>\n<p>Access tokens are deliberately short-lived (5–60 minutes), and the reason traces straight back to the validation steps above: nothing in that process checks a revocation list, so a stolen token stays valid until it naturally expires. Clients compensate with a <strong>refresh token</strong> — long-lived, stored securely, rotated on use — which lets them request a fresh access token without forcing the user to log in again. The real revocation strategies available are: keep access-token lifetimes short in the first place, revoke the refresh token at the identity provider when something&#39;s wrong, and for genuinely high-risk APIs, pay the cost of token introspection or a deny list on every request.</p>\n<h2 id=\"cookie-authentication\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/auth/cookie-authentication\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Cookie authentication <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAuthentication</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CookieAuthenticationDefaults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticationScheme</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddCookie</span>(o =&gt;\n    {\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LoginPath</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/login&quot;</span>;\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AccessDeniedPath</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/denied&quot;</span>;\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExpireTimeSpan</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromHours</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">8</span>);\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SlidingExpiration</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cookie</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpOnly</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;                              <span class=\"tk-com\">// JS can't read it</span>\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cookie</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SecurePolicy</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">CookieSecurePolicy</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Always</span>;     <span class=\"tk-com\">// HTTPS only</span>\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cookie</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SameSite</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">SameSiteMode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lax</span>;                  <span class=\"tk-com\">// CSRF mitigation</span>\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Events</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OnRedirectToLogin</span> = ctx =&gt; { ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Response</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">401</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CompletedTask</span>; }; <span class=\"tk-com\">// for API calls</span>\n    });\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapPost</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/login&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">LoginRequest</span> req, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IUserService</span> users, <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpContext</span> ctx) =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> user = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> users.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidateAsync</span>(req.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Email</span>, req.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Password</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (user <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Unauthorized</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> identity = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimsIdentity</span>([<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimTypes</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NameIdentifier</span>, user.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>), <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimTypes</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Role</span>, user.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Role</span>)],\n                                      <span class=\"tk-ty\">CookieAuthenticationDefaults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticationScheme</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SignInAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimsPrincipal</span>(identity));      <span class=\"tk-com\">// issues the encrypted cookie</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NoContent</span>();\n});</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The cookie&#39;s contents are encrypted and signed with the <strong>Data Protection</strong> API. That causes a surprise the first time an app scales out to more than one instance: by default, each instance generates its own encryption keys. A cookie issued by instance A then becomes unreadable gibberish to instance B — so a user gets silently logged out the moment the load balancer happens to route them somewhere new. The fix is to share those keys across every instance (persist them to Blob Storage, Redis, or a database, itself protected with Key Vault).</p>\n<h2 id=\"asp-net-core-identity-a-membership-system-not-an-oauth-serve\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/auth/asp-net-core-identity-a-membership-system-not-an-oauth-serve\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>ASP.NET Core Identity: a membership system, not an OAuth server <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>ASP.NET Core Identity</strong> handles the mechanics of <em>having</em> users at all: password hashing (PBKDF2), account lockout, two-factor authentication, email confirmation, external logins, roles — all persisted through EF Core. The distinction worth being precise about: it is <strong>not</strong> an OAuth/OIDC authorization server by itself; it manages your own user table, not tokens issued to third-party clients.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddDbContext</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">AppIdentityDb</span>&gt;(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseSqlServer</span>(cs));\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddIdentityApiEndpoints</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">AppUser</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddEntityFrameworkStores</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">AppIdentityDb</span>&gt;();\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAuthorization</span>();\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapIdentityApi</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">AppUser</span>&gt;();   <span class=\"tk-com\">// .NET 8: /register, /login, /refresh, /confirmEmail, /manage/2fa …</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Users log in to <em>your</em> app with a password, simple first-party</td>\n<td>ASP.NET Core Identity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Single sign-on across many apps, third-party clients, OAuth flows</td>\n<td>An identity provider: Entra ID / Entra External ID, Auth0, Okta, Keycloak, or Duende IdentityServer / OpenIddict on top of Identity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enterprise employees</td>\n<td>Microsoft Entra ID</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"authorization-the-simple-forms-before-anything-needs-a-polic\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/auth/authorization-the-simple-forms-before-anything-needs-a-polic\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Authorization: the simple forms, before anything needs a policy <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/orders&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequireAuthorization</span>();              <span class=\"tk-com\">// any authenticated user</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/status&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">AllowAnonymous</span>();                  <span class=\"tk-com\">// explicitly public</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapDelete</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/orders/{id}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Delete</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequireAuthorization</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequireRole</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;admin&quot;</span>));\n\n<span class=\"tk-attr\">[Authorize]</span>                                    <span class=\"tk-com\">// controller-level: all actions require auth</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrdersController</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">ControllerBase</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[AllowAnonymous]</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IActionResult</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Public</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[Authorize(Roles = &quot;admin,support&quot;)]</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IActionResult</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Refund</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>();   <span class=\"tk-com\">// admin OR support</span>\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Secure by default: require auth everywhere unless [AllowAnonymous]</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAuthorizationBuilder</span>()\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">SetFallbackPolicy</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthorizationPolicyBuilder</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequireAuthenticatedUser</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>());</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>That last block is worth internalising as a default posture: it&#39;s much safer to make every endpoint require authentication <strong>unless explicitly opted out</strong> with <code>[AllowAnonymous]</code>, than to make every endpoint public unless someone remembered to add <code>[Authorize]</code>. The first mistake is a locked-down endpoint that&#39;s annoying to fix; the second is a silent data leak.</p>\n<h2 id=\"policy-based-authorization-centralising-rules-instead-of-sca\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/auth/policy-based-authorization-centralising-rules-instead-of-sca\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Policy-based authorization: centralising rules instead of scattering role strings <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>A <strong>policy</strong> is a named bundle of <strong>requirements</strong>, and a policy only passes if every one of its requirements does. The payoff for the extra indirection is real: once a rule is named <code>&quot;CanRefund&quot;</code>, you write it once, and every endpoint that needs it refers to the name — nobody has to remember or copy the actual logic.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAuthorizationBuilder</span>()\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddPolicy</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;CanReadOrders&quot;</span>,  p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequireClaim</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;scp&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;orders.read&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;orders.write&quot;</span>))   <span class=\"tk-com\">// any of these values</span>\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddPolicy</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;CanRefund&quot;</span>,      p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequireRole</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;support&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequireClaim</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;refund_limit&quot;</span>))\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddPolicy</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;EmployeesOnly&quot;</span>,  p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequireAssertion</span>(ctx =&gt; ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">User</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasClaim</span>(c =&gt; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Type</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;email&quot;</span> &amp;&amp; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">EndsWith</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;@shop.com&quot;</span>))))\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddPolicy</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Over18&quot;</span>,         p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddRequirements</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">MinimumAgeRequirement</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">18</span>)));\n\norders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapPost</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/{id}/refund&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Refund</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequireAuthorization</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;CanRefund&quot;</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h3 id=\"custom-requirements-and-handlers\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/auth/custom-requirements-and-handlers\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Custom requirements and handlers</h3>\n<p>When the built-in requirement helpers (<code>RequireRole</code>, <code>RequireClaim</code>, <code>RequireAssertion</code>) can&#39;t express a rule, write your own:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">MinimumAgeRequirement</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Age</span>) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IAuthorizationRequirement</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">MinimumAgeHandler</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeProvider</span> clock) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthorizationHandler</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">MinimumAgeRequirement</span>&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HandleRequirementAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthorizationHandlerContext</span> ctx, <span class=\"tk-ty\">MinimumAgeRequirement</span> req)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTime</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryParse</span>(ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">User</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FindFirstValue</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;birthdate&quot;</span>), <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> dob)\n            &amp;&amp; dob.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddYears</span>(req.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Age</span>) &lt;= clock.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetUtcNow</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTime</span>)\n            ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Succeed</span>(req);                 <span class=\"tk-com\">// don't call Fail() unless you want to veto other handlers</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CompletedTask</span>;\n    }\n}\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IAuthorizationHandler</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">MinimumAgeHandler</span>&gt;();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The comment on <code>Succeed</code> matters: a requirement passes if <strong>any</strong> registered handler for it calls <code>Succeed</code> — several handlers can each independently vote yes. But if even one handler calls <code>Fail</code>, that overrides every yes-vote and vetoes the requirement outright. Handlers are ordinary scoped services, so they can query the database like anything else.</p>\n<h2 id=\"resource-based-authorization-when-the-rule-depends-on-which-\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/auth/resource-based-authorization-when-the-rule-depends-on-which-\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Resource-based authorization: when the rule depends on which specific row <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Policies above answer yes/no questions that don&#39;t depend on <em>which</em> resource you&#39;re looking at. Plenty of real rules do: &quot;a customer can view <strong>their own</strong> orders,&quot; &quot;an editor can edit documents <strong>in their department</strong>.&quot; Since the rule can&#39;t be evaluated until you know which row is involved, the endpoint has to load the resource first, then ask the authorization service imperatively:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderOwnerHandler</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthorizationHandler</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OperationAuthorizationRequirement</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HandleRequirementAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthorizationHandlerContext</span> ctx, <span class=\"tk-ty\">OperationAuthorizationRequirement</span> op, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> order)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> userId = ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">User</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FindFirstValue</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;sub&quot;</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> == userId || ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">User</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsInRole</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;support&quot;</span>)) ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Succeed</span>(op);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CompletedTask</span>;\n    }\n}\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/orders/{id:int}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span> db, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimsPrincipal</span> user, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IAuthorizationService</span> authz) =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> order = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FindAsync</span>(id);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (order <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> result = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> authz.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthorizeAsync</span>(user, order, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OperationAuthorizationRequirement</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Read&quot;</span> });\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> result.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Succeeded</span> ? <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToDto</span>()) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>();   <span class=\"tk-com\">// 404 hides existence (prevents enumeration)</span>\n}).<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequireAuthorization</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Look closely at that last line&#39;s comment: returning <strong>404</strong>, not 403, when authorization fails is a deliberate choice, not an inconsistency. If a competitor&#39;s order ID returned 403, that confirms to an attacker the order exists at all — a small leak, but a real one. Returning the same 404 whether the order doesn&#39;t exist or simply isn&#39;t theirs closes that gap. This whole pattern is the standard defence against <strong>IDOR</strong> (insecure direct object reference), consistently one of the most common real-world API vulnerabilities — see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/web-security\">Web security</a> for the fuller picture.</p>\n<h2 id=\"api-keys-client-certificates-and-multiple-schemes\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/auth/api-keys-client-certificates-and-multiple-schemes\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>API keys, client certificates and multiple schemes <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ApiKeyHandler</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IOptionsMonitor</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticationSchemeOptions</span>&gt; o, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ILoggerFactory</span> l, <span class=\"tk-ty\">UrlEncoder</span> e, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IApiKeyStore</span> keys)\n    : <span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticationHandler</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticationSchemeOptions</span>&gt;(o, l, e)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticateResult</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">HandleAuthenticateAsync</span>()\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!<span class=\"tk-ty\">Request</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGetValue</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;X-Api-Key&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> key)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticateResult</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NoResult</span>();\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> client = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> keys.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FindAsync</span>(key!);                         <span class=\"tk-com\">// compare HASHED keys, constant-time</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (client <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticateResult</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Fail</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Invalid API key&quot;</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> principal = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimsPrincipal</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimsIdentity</span>([<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;client_id&quot;</span>, client.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>)], <span class=\"tk-ty\">Scheme</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>));\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticateResult</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Success</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticationTicket</span>(principal, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Scheme</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>));\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Accept either JWT or API key on some endpoints</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAuthorizationBuilder</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddPolicy</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;PartnerAccess&quot;</span>, p =&gt; p\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAuthenticationSchemes</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">JwtBearerDefaults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticationScheme</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ApiKey&quot;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequireAuthenticatedUser</span>());</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice that this custom handler slots into the exact same five-action model (Authenticate/Challenge/Forbid/SignIn/SignOut) described earlier for cookies and JWTs — it&#39;s a different mechanism for proving identity, but the framework treats it identically once it&#39;s registered. Other options worth knowing by name: <strong>mTLS / client certificates</strong> (<code>AddCertificate</code>) for service-to-service calls; <strong>managed identity</strong> tokens for Azure-to-Azure calls, covered in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets\">Identity &amp; secrets</a>; and a <strong>policy scheme</strong> (<code>AddPolicyScheme</code> with <code>ForwardDefaultSelector</code>) for picking which underlying scheme to use dynamically, per request.</p>\n<h2 id=\"claims-transformation-and-permissions\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/auth/claims-transformation-and-permissions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Claims transformation and permissions <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Tokens typically carry coarse-grained data — a user ID, maybe a couple of roles — because the identity provider that issued them has no idea about your app&#39;s fine-grained permission model. <strong>Claims transformation</strong> is the hook for enriching the principal, per request, with exactly that app-specific detail:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PermissionClaims</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IPermissionStore</span> store) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IClaimsTransformation</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimsPrincipal</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">TransformAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimsPrincipal</span> p)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Identity</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">not</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimsIdentity</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsAuthenticated</span>: <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span> } id || id.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasClaim</span>(c =&gt; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Type</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;perm&quot;</span>)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> p;\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> perm <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> store.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FindFirstValue</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;sub&quot;</span>)!)) id.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddClaim</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;perm&quot;</span>, perm));\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> p;\n    }\n}\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IClaimsTransformation</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">PermissionClaims</span>&gt;();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Worth flagging plainly: this runs on <strong>every</strong> authenticate call, for every request, so whatever <code>store.GetAsync</code> does needs to be cheap or cached — otherwise you&#39;ve quietly added a database round trip to every single authenticated request in the app.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What&#39;s the difference between authentication and authorization?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Authentication establishes who the caller is: it validates credentials (cookie, JWT, API key) and produces a ClaimsPrincipal in HttpContext.User. Authorization decides whether that principal may perform an action on a resource, using roles, claims, policies or resource-based checks. Failure gives 401 for authentication and 403 for authorization.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How does an ASP.NET Core API validate a JWT?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The JwtBearer handler reads the token from the Authorization header, gets the issuer&#39;s public signing keys from the OIDC discovery document (cached), verifies the signature, validates issuer, audience and lifetime (exp/nbf with clock skew), then builds a ClaimsPrincipal from the claims. No call to the identity provider per request.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Cookies vs JWTs: when would you use each?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Cookies for browser-based apps and BFFs: sent automatically, HttpOnly protects against XSS theft, they need CSRF protection, and they&#39;re easy to revoke. JWT bearer tokens for APIs consumed by SPAs, mobile apps and services: stateless and cross-domain, but hard to revoke, so keep them short-lived. For SPAs, the BFF pattern with cookies is now recommended.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is in a JWT and is it secure to put data in it?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A header (algorithm, key ID), a payload of claims (iss, sub, aud, exp, roles, scopes…) and a signature, all base64url encoded. It&#39;s signed, not encrypted, so anyone can read the payload. Don&#39;t put secrets or sensitive data in it.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Roles vs claims vs policies?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Claims are facts about the user; a role is one kind of claim. Policies are named authorization rules made of requirements that can check roles, claims or custom logic, keeping rules centralised and testable instead of scattering role strings across controllers.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you implement &#39;users can only access their own orders&#39;?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Resource-based authorization: load the order, then call IAuthorizationService.AuthorizeAsync(user, order, requirement) with an AuthorizationHandler&lt;TRequirement, Order&gt; that checks ownership. Or always scope queries by the current user ID. Return 404 rather than 403 to avoid revealing the resource exists. This prevents IDOR.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you require authentication on every endpoint by default?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Set a fallback policy: <code>AddAuthorizationBuilder().SetFallbackPolicy(new AuthorizationPolicyBuilder().RequireAuthenticatedUser().Build())</code>, then mark public endpoints with AllowAnonymous.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is ASP.NET Core Identity and is it an OAuth server?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A membership library for user storage, password hashing, lockout, 2FA, confirmations and external logins, persisted with EF Core. It&#39;s not an OAuth 2.0/OIDC authorization server; for that you use Entra ID/Auth0/Okta/Keycloak or build on Identity with Duende IdentityServer or OpenIddict.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why do you need to configure Data Protection when running multiple instances with cookie auth?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Auth cookies (and antiforgery tokens) are encrypted with Data Protection keys. By default each instance has its own keys, so a cookie issued by one instance can&#39;t be decrypted by another and users get logged out. Persist the key ring to shared storage (Blob, Redis, DB) protected by Key Vault.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/auth/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/security/authentication/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Overview of ASP.NET Core authentication</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/security/authentication/configure-jwt-bearer-authentication\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Configure JWT bearer authentication</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/security/authorization/policies\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Policy-based authorization</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/security/authorization/resourcebased\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Resource-based authorization</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/msal/dotnet/microsoft-identity-web/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Microsoft.Identity.Web</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/auth/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (plain console app, .NET 8/9, BCL only — no identity-provider or ASP.NET Core packages needed for these checks).</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Security</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Claims</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Text</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: &quot;a role is just a claim whose type happens to be 'role'&quot; — nothing structurally special about it.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> identity = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimsIdentity</span>(\n    [<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Claim</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimTypes</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NameIdentifier</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;7f3c&quot;</span>), <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Claim</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimTypes</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Role</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;admin&quot;</span>)],\n    authenticationType: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;TestScheme&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> principal = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimsPrincipal</span>(identity);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!principal.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsInRole</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;admin&quot;</span>))\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected IsInRole to recognise a plain Role claim&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!principal.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasClaim</span>(c =&gt; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Type</span> == <span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimTypes</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Role</span> &amp;&amp; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;admin&quot;</span>))\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected the role to be readable as an ordinary claim too&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: IsInRole(\\&quot;admin\\&quot;) and reading the same thing as a plain claim agree — a role is just a claim&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: a JWT is three base64url-encoded parts joined by dots: header.payload.signature</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Base64UrlEncode</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> s) =&gt;\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Convert</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToBase64String</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Encoding</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UTF8</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetBytes</span>(s)).<span class=\"tk-ty\">TrimEnd</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">'='</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Replace</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">'+'</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">'-'</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Replace</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">'/'</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">'_'</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> header = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Base64UrlEncode</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;&quot;{&quot;alg&quot;:&quot;RS256&quot;,&quot;kid&quot;:&quot;123&quot;}&quot;&quot;&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> payload = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Base64UrlEncode</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;&quot;{&quot;sub&quot;:&quot;7f3c&quot;,&quot;aud&quot;:&quot;api://shop-api&quot;,&quot;exp&quot;:1767225600}&quot;&quot;&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> fakeSignature = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Base64UrlEncode</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;not-a-real-signature&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> jwt = <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;{header}.{payload}.{fakeSignature}&quot;</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> parts = jwt.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Split</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">'.'</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (parts.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Length</span> != <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected a JWT to have 3 dot-separated parts, got {parts.Length}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: a JWT has exactly 3 base64url parts (header.payload.signature)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: JWTs are signed, not encrypted — anyone can read the payload without the signing key.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Base64UrlDecode</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> s)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> padded = s.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Replace</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">'-'</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">'+'</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Replace</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">'_'</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">'/'</span>);\n    padded += <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">'='</span>, (<span class=\"tk-num\">4</span> - padded.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Length</span> % <span class=\"tk-num\">4</span>) % <span class=\"tk-num\">4</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Encoding</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UTF8</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetString</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Convert</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromBase64String</span>(padded));\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> decodedPayload = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Base64UrlDecode</span>(parts[<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>]);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!decodedPayload.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contains</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;\\&quot;sub\\&quot;:\\&quot;7f3c\\&quot;&quot;</span>))\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected the payload to be readable without any key — it's signed, not encrypted&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: decoded the payload with no key at all: {decodedPayload} — confirms JWTs are signed, not encrypted&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: resource-based auth should return 404 (not 403) for &quot;exists but not yours&quot;, to avoid confirming existence (IDOR defense).</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CheckOrderAccess</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>? order, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> currentUserId)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (order <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;404&quot;</span>;                 <span class=\"tk-com\">// doesn't exist</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (order != currentUserId) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;404&quot;</span>;         <span class=\"tk-com\">// exists, but isn't theirs — SAME response as &quot;doesn't exist&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;200&quot;</span>;\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> responseForMissing = <span class=\"tk-ty\">CheckOrderAccess</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;me&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> responseForSomeoneElses = <span class=\"tk-ty\">CheckOrderAccess</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;someone-else&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;me&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (responseForMissing != responseForSomeoneElses)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected 'missing' and 'not yours' to be indistinguishable to the caller (both 404)&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: a missing order and someone else's order both answer 404 — an attacker can't tell which one it was&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here (needs a live identity provider / ASP.NET Core pipeline) — see Further reading:</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - Real signature verification against an issuer's JWKS, audience/issuer/expiry validation:</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/security/authentication/configure-jwt-bearer-authentication</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - Policy evaluation, fallback policies, AuthorizationHandler voting (Succeed/Fail) end to end:</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/security/authorization/policies</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - Data Protection key sharing across instances for cookie auth at scale:</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//   https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/security/authentication/</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All checkable claims verified.&quot;</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"two-questions-that-get-conflated-constantly-and-shouldnt-be","text":"Two questions that get conflated constantly, and shouldn't be","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"claims-identities-and-principals-the-vocabulary-everything-e","text":"Claims, identities and principals: the vocabulary everything else builds on","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"authentication-schemes-and-handlers-naming-the-thing-that-do","text":"Authentication schemes and handlers: naming the thing that does the checking","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"cookies-vs-bearer-tokens-the-tradeoff-behind-how-do-i-stay-l","text":"Cookies vs bearer tokens: the tradeoff behind \"how do I stay logged in?\"","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"jwt-bearer-authentication-traced-claim-by-claim","text":"JWT bearer authentication, traced claim by claim","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"token-lifetimes-and-refresh","text":"Token lifetimes and refresh","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"cookie-authentication","text":"Cookie authentication","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"asp-net-core-identity-a-membership-system-not-an-oauth-serve","text":"ASP.NET Core Identity: a membership system, not an OAuth server","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"authorization-the-simple-forms-before-anything-needs-a-polic","text":"Authorization: the simple forms, before anything needs a policy","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"policy-based-authorization-centralising-rules-instead-of-sca","text":"Policy-based authorization: centralising rules instead of scattering role strings","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"custom-requirements-and-handlers","text":"Custom requirements and handlers","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"resource-based-authorization-when-the-rule-depends-on-which-","text":"Resource-based authorization: when the rule depends on which specific row","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"api-keys-client-certificates-and-multiple-schemes","text":"API keys, client certificates and multiple schemes","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"claims-transformation-and-permissions","text":"Claims transformation and permissions","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":4548,"minutes":21,"num":"3.8","part":"p3"},"http-client":{"id":"http-client","title":"Calling Other Services: HttpClient and IHttpClientFactory","summary":"Making outbound HTTP calls correctly. How HttpClient and its handler pipeline work, the socket-exhaustion and stale-DNS problems, IHttpClientFactory with named and typed clients, DelegatingHandlers, JSON helpers, timeouts and cancellation, resilience, generated clients (Refit, Kiota), and testing.","layer":"comms","where":"Service communication › Synchronous HTTP › Outbound calls","fits":"<p>This is the <strong>outbound</strong> side of layer 6. Your API receives requests through the pipeline in layer 3, and calls other APIs through this. It plugs into DI (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/dependency-injection\">Dependency injection</a>) and configuration, and is where resilience (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/resilience\">Resilience</a>) and distributed tracing (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/observability-aspire\">Observability</a>) get attached.</p>\n","prereqs":["dependency-injection","async-await"],"next":["resilience","messaging-events","observability-aspire"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/http-client/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><code>HttpClient</code> is a thin wrapper; the expensive part — connections, DNS, TLS — lives in the handler underneath it (<code>SocketsHttpHandler</code>), which is why <em>how you manage its lifetime</em> matters more than the calls themselves.</li>\n<li>Creating a new <code>HttpClient</code> per call eventually exhausts sockets; keeping one forever eventually serves stale DNS after a failover. Both are real production incidents, not theoretical.</li>\n<li><strong><code>IHttpClientFactory</code></strong> fixes both: it pools and periodically recycles handlers, so connections are reused but DNS changes still get picked up.</li>\n<li>Prefer <strong>typed clients</strong> (a class wrapping <code>HttpClient</code>, exposing a domain API) over named clients, and never let a singleton capture one — that recreates the stale-DNS bug.</li>\n<li>**<code>DelegatingHandler</code>**s are middleware for outbound calls: auth tokens, correlation headers, logging, and resilience (retries, circuit breakers) all slot into the same chain.</li>\n<li>Pass the incoming request&#39;s <code>CancellationToken</code> through to every outbound call, and read the error body before calling <code>EnsureSuccessStatusCode()</code> — it throws away any <code>ProblemDetails</code> the server sent you.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-basic-calls\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/http-client/the-basic-calls\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The basic calls <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> response = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> http.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://api.github.com/repos/dotnet/runtime&quot;</span>, ct);\nresponse.<span class=\"tk-ty\">EnsureSuccessStatusCode</span>();                                  <span class=\"tk-com\">// throws HttpRequestException on 4xx/5xx</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> repo = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> response.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Content</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadFromJsonAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Repo</span>&gt;(ct);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> repo2 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> http.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetFromJsonAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Repo</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;repos/dotnet/runtime&quot;</span>, ct);       <span class=\"tk-com\">// one-liner</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> created = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> http.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PostAsJsonAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;orders&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { sku = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;KB-01&quot;</span>, qty = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> }, ct);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> req = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpRequestMessage</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpMethod</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Put</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;orders/{id}&quot;</span>)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Content</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">JsonContent</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Create</span>(update),\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span> = { { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;If-Match&quot;</span>, etag } }\n};\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> res = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> http.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SendAsync</span>(req, <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpCompletionOption</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ResponseHeadersRead</span>, ct);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// stream big responses</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Those calls look harmless, and they <em>are</em> harmless at this level of abstraction. The trouble starts with a question that looks unrelated: where does <code>http</code> — the <code>HttpClient</code> instance — actually come from, and how long does it live? Get that wrong, and the bugs that follow are some of the most notorious in all of ASP.NET Core history.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-an-httpclient-actually-is-underneath\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/http-client/what-an-httpclient-actually-is-underneath\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What an HttpClient actually is, underneath <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><code>HttpClient</code> isn&#39;t the thing doing the real work — it&#39;s a thin, friendly front end over a chain of <strong>message handlers</strong>:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>HttpClient ──▶ DelegatingHandler (e.g. auth header) ──▶ DelegatingHandler (logging) ──▶ … ──▶ SocketsHttpHandler\n (API you call)        your cross-cutting handlers (a pipeline, like middleware)              (owns the CONNECTION POOL,\n                                                                                               DNS, TLS, sockets)</code></pre></figure>\n<p>The expensive part — the thing that actually opens TCP connections, negotiates TLS, and keeps a <strong>connection pool</strong> alive for reuse — lives at the far end, in the <strong>primary handler</strong> (<code>SocketsHttpHandler</code>). <code>HttpClient</code> itself is a lightweight wrapper around that chain. Hold onto that one fact; it&#39;s the key to understanding both classic bugs below, because each one comes from misjudging <em>which</em> part of this picture is actually expensive.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-two-classic-bugs-and-why-theyre-opposite-mistakes\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/http-client/the-two-classic-bugs-and-why-theyre-opposite-mistakes\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The two classic bugs, and why they&#39;re opposite mistakes <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<h3 id=\"bug-1-a-new-httpclient-per-request-socket-exhaustion\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/http-client/bug-1-a-new-httpclient-per-request-socket-exhaustion\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Bug 1: a new HttpClient per request → socket exhaustion</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Rates</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRatesAsync</span>()\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> http = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpClient</span>();               <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✘ new handler + new connection pool every call</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> http.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetFromJsonAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Rates</span>&gt;(url);\n}                                                    <span class=\"tk-com\">// disposed → socket closed → sits in TIME_WAIT for ~2–4 min</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>This looks responsible — <code>using</code>, proper disposal, nothing left dangling. The problem is exactly what that <code>using</code> throws away: a fresh <code>HttpClient</code> means a fresh <code>SocketsHttpHandler</code>, which means a fresh connection pool, every single call. Disposing it doesn&#39;t instantly free the socket either — TCP requires the closing side to sit in <code>TIME_WAIT</code> for roughly 2–4 minutes, as a protocol-level safeguard. Under real load, that adds up fast: thousands of sockets pile up in <code>TIME_WAIT</code> simultaneously, and the machine eventually runs out of ephemeral ports entirely, surfacing as <code>SocketException: Only one usage of each socket address is normally permitted</code>. On top of that, every single call pays for a brand-new TCP handshake and TLS negotiation, work that a pooled connection would have skipped entirely.</p>\n<h3 id=\"bug-2-one-static-httpclient-forever-stale-dns\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/http-client/bug-2-one-static-httpclient-forever-stale-dns\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Bug 2: one static HttpClient forever → stale DNS</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"cHJpdmF0ZSBzdGF0aWMgcmVhZG9ubHkgSHR0cENsaWVudCBIdHRwID0gbmV3KCk7ICAgICAvLyDinJggY29ubmVjdGlvbnMgbGl2ZSBmb3JldmVy\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpClient</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Http</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>();     <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✘ connections live forever</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>This is the overcorrection — treating <code>HttpClient</code> as expensive to create (true for the handler, not really true for <code>HttpClient</code> itself) and so creating exactly one, forever. The connections it opens now live indefinitely, reused for every call. That sounds efficient, and it is — right up until the target&#39;s DNS record changes: a blue/green deployment flips the active environment, a failover happens, a load balancer gets a new IP. The static client never re-resolves DNS, because it never has a reason to — its existing connection still works, technically, just to the wrong place now.</p>\n<p><img src=\"visuals/httpclient-lifecycle.svg\" alt=\"Three HttpClient lifetimes: a fresh one per request accumulates sockets in TIME_WAIT, one static client pins to a stale IP after a failover, and IHttpClientFactory periodically swaps the underlying handler so DNS changes are picked up without losing connection reuse\"></p>\n<h3 id=\"the-fix-let-something-else-own-the-lifetime-decision\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/http-client/the-fix-let-something-else-own-the-lifetime-decision\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The fix: let something else own the lifetime decision</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Fix</th>\n<th>How</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>IHttpClientFactory</strong> (recommended in ASP.NET Core)</td>\n<td>Pools and <strong>recycles handlers</strong> (every 2 minutes by default), so connections are reused but DNS changes are picked up; integrates config, DI, logging and resilience</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Static/singleton <code>HttpClient</code> + <code>PooledConnectionLifetime</code></td>\n<td><code>new HttpClient(new SocketsHttpHandler { PooledConnectionLifetime = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(2) })</code>: fine for simple apps and libraries</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Notice what <code>IHttpClientFactory</code> actually buys you, looking back at the diagram: it isn&#39;t &quot;faster disposal&quot; or &quot;fewer allocations&quot; — it&#39;s <strong>periodic handler replacement</strong>, which reuses connections for the common case (most calls, most of the time) while still bounding how long any one connection can go stale.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ihttpclientfactory-in-practice\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/http-client/ihttpclientfactory-in-practice\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>IHttpClientFactory in practice <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<h3 id=\"named-clients\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/http-client/named-clients\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Named clients</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddHttpClient</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;github&quot;</span>, c =&gt;\n{\n    c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BaseAddress</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Uri</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://api.github.com/&quot;</span>);\n    c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DefaultRequestHeaders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UserAgent</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ParseAdd</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;shop-api/1.0&quot;</span>);\n    c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Timeout</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSeconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">15</span>);\n});\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">RepoService</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IHttpClientFactory</span> factory)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Repo</span>?&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> name, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt;\n        factory.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateClient</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;github&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetFromJsonAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Repo</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;repos/{name}&quot;</span>, ct);\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h3 id=\"typed-clients-preferred\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/http-client/typed-clients-preferred\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Typed clients (preferred)</h3>\n<p>A better shape wraps the raw HTTP calls behind a class that receives a pre-configured <code>HttpClient</code> through its constructor, and exposes a domain-friendly API instead of raw URLs and verbs:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InventoryClient</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpClient</span> http)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">StockLevel</span>?&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetStockAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> sku, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> res = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> http.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;stock/{Uri.EscapeDataString(sku)}&quot;</span>, ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (res.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span> == <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpStatusCode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>;\n        res.<span class=\"tk-ty\">EnsureSuccessStatusCode</span>();\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> res.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Content</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadFromJsonAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">StockLevel</span>&gt;(ct);\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReserveAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Reservation</span> r, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt;\n        (<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> http.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PostAsJsonAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;reservations&quot;</span>, r, ct)).<span class=\"tk-ty\">EnsureSuccessStatusCode</span>();\n}\n\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddHttpClient</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">InventoryClient</span>&gt;(c =&gt; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BaseAddress</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Uri</span>(builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configuration</span>[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Services:Inventory&quot;</span>]!))\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddHttpMessageHandler</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CorrelationIdHandler</span>&gt;()\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddStandardResilienceHandler</span>();               <span class=\"tk-com\">// retries, timeouts, circuit breaker (see Resilience)</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The typed client itself is registered as <strong>transient</strong> — cheap to create, because the expensive part lives in the shared, recycled handler underneath it, not in this thin wrapper. Inject it wherever you need it.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Don&#39;t capture typed clients in singletons</div><p>Here&#39;s a trap that recreates the exact stale-DNS bug from above, wearing a different disguise: if a singleton holds onto a typed client (or any <code>HttpClient</code> the factory handed it) for the app&#39;s whole lifetime, that handler is never recycled, because nothing ever lets go of it. Inject typed clients into transient or scoped services instead — the same captive-dependency shape covered in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/the-captive-dependency-the-single-most-common-di-bug\">Dependency injection</a> — or, if a singleton genuinely needs HTTP access, inject <code>IHttpClientFactory</code> directly and create a fresh client per use. (Configuring <code>SocketsHttpHandler.PooledConnectionLifetime</code> on the primary handler is the other escape hatch.)</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"delegatinghandlers-the-exact-same-idea-as-middleware-running\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/http-client/delegatinghandlers-the-exact-same-idea-as-middleware-running\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>DelegatingHandlers: the exact same idea as middleware, running outbound <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>The handler chain introduced earlier is a pipeline in the fullest sense — composable, ordered, each one wrapping the next — which should feel familiar after <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/middleware/now-heres-the-aha-a-middleware-is-just-a-function-wrapping-a\">Middleware</a>. The difference is direction: this pipeline runs on the way <strong>out</strong>, for requests your app is making to someone else, not on the way in.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AccessTokenHandler</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ITokenProvider</span> tokens) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">DelegatingHandler</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpResponseMessage</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">SendAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpRequestMessage</span> request, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> token = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> tokens.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;api://inventory/.default&quot;</span>, ct);     <span class=\"tk-com\">// cached client-credentials token</span>\n        request.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Authorization</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticationHeaderValue</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Bearer&quot;</span>, token);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SendAsync</span>(request, ct);                             <span class=\"tk-com\">// call the next handler</span>\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CorrelationIdHandler</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IHttpContextAccessor</span> accessor) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">DelegatingHandler</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpResponseMessage</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">SendAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpRequestMessage</span> request, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (accessor.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpContext</span>?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Request</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span>[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;X-Correlation-Id&quot;</span>] <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span>: &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span> } id)\n            request.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryAddWithoutValidation</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;X-Correlation-Id&quot;</span>, id.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>());\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SendAsync</span>(request, ct);\n    }\n}\n\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddTransient</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">AccessTokenHandler</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddTransient</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CorrelationIdHandler</span>&gt;();\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddHttpClient</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">InventoryClient</span>&gt;(...).<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddHttpMessageHandler</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">AccessTokenHandler</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddHttpMessageHandler</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CorrelationIdHandler</span>&gt;();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Typical jobs for a handler: attaching auth tokens, propagating correlation or tenant headers, logging, caching, request signing. Resilience itself — retries, circuit breakers — is implemented as a handler too, which is why <code>.AddStandardResilienceHandler()</code> above slots in exactly like any other entry in this chain. (One thing you <em>don&#39;t</em> need a handler for: W3C <code>traceparent</code> propagation for distributed tracing happens automatically.)</p>\n<h2 id=\"timeouts-and-cancellation-getting-the-layering-right\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/http-client/timeouts-and-cancellation-getting-the-layering-right\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Timeouts and cancellation: getting the layering right <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><code>HttpClient.Timeout</code> (default <strong>100 seconds</strong>, far too long for most APIs) applies to the entire request. When it fires, it surfaces as a <code>TaskCanceledException</code> with an inner <code>TimeoutException</code> (since .NET 5) — the only reliable way to tell &quot;it timed out&quot; apart from &quot;the caller cancelled it,&quot; since both throw the same outer exception type.</li>\n<li>Always pass the incoming request&#39;s <code>CancellationToken</code> through to outbound calls, so that if the original client disconnects, your app stops waiting on a downstream call nobody&#39;s going to read the answer to.</li>\n<li>Once the resilience handler is in play, prefer <em>its</em> <strong>total request timeout</strong> and <strong>per-attempt timeout</strong> over <code>HttpClient.Timeout</code> — and set <code>HttpClient.Timeout</code> itself to <code>Infinite</code> or something larger, so it doesn&#39;t quietly compete with the timeouts you actually configured.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"Y2F0Y2ggKFRhc2tDYW5jZWxlZEV4Y2VwdGlvbiBleCkgd2hlbiAoZXguSW5uZXJFeGNlcHRpb24gaXMgVGltZW91dEV4Y2VwdGlvbikgeyAvKiB0aW1lZCBvdXQgKi8gfQpjYXRjaCAoVGFza0NhbmNlbGVkRXhjZXB0aW9uKSB3aGVuIChjdC5Jc0NhbmNlbGxhdGlvblJlcXVlc3RlZCkgeyAvKiBjYWxsZXIgY2FuY2VsbGVkICovIH0=\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">TaskCanceledException</span> ex) <span class=\"tk-kw\">when</span> (ex.<span class=\"tk-ty\">InnerException</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeoutException</span>) { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* timed out */</span> }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">TaskCanceledException</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">when</span> (ct.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsCancellationRequested</span>) { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* caller cancelled */</span> }</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h2 id=\"handling-responses-properly-resist-the-urge-to-just-ensuresu\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/http-client/handling-responses-properly-resist-the-urge-to-just-ensuresu\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Handling responses properly: resist the urge to just EnsureSuccessStatusCode <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> res = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> http.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(url, ct);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">switch</span> (res.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span>)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">case</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpStatusCode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OK</span>:          <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> res.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Content</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadFromJsonAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Dto</span>&gt;(ct);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">case</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpStatusCode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>:    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">case</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpStatusCode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TooManyRequests</span>:\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> retryAfter = res.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RetryAfter</span>?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Delta</span>;              <span class=\"tk-com\">// respect server throttling</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ThrottledException</span>(retryAfter);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">default</span>:\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> problem = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> res.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Content</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadFromJsonAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProblemDetails</span>&gt;(ct);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// read the error body before throwing</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DownstreamException</span>(res.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span>, problem?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Title</span>);\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Three habits worth making automatic:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dispose every <code>HttpResponseMessage</code> (or wrap it in <code>using</code>) — this matters especially with <code>ResponseHeadersRead</code>, since the connection only returns to the pool once you&#39;re actually done with it.</li>\n<li>Use <code>ResponseHeadersRead</code> for large responses, so the body streams instead of being fully buffered into memory before you see a single byte.</li>\n<li>Don&#39;t reach for <code>EnsureSuccessStatusCode</code> reflexively when the error response carries a body worth reading — calling it first throws away the <code>ProblemDetails</code> payload the server went to the trouble of sending you.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"generated-and-declarative-clients\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/http-client/generated-and-declarative-clients\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Generated and declarative clients <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Writing a typed client by hand, as above, works well for a handful of endpoints. For a large external API, generating the client from its contract avoids hand-transcribing dozens of endpoints and keeps the client in sync as the contract evolves:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Tool</th>\n<th>Approach</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Refit</strong></td>\n<td>Declare an interface with attributes; Refit generates the implementation (source generator)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Kiota</strong> (Microsoft)</td>\n<td>Generates strongly typed clients from OpenAPI descriptions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>NSwag / OpenAPI Generator</strong></td>\n<td>Generate C# clients from OpenAPI</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>gRPC client</td>\n<td>Generated from <code>.proto</code> files (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/app-models\">App models</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IGitHubApi</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[Get(&quot;/repos/{owner}/{repo}&quot;)]</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Repo</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRepoAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> owner, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> repo, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct = <span class=\"tk-kw\">default</span>);\n}\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddRefitClient</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IGitHubApi</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">ConfigureHttpClient</span>(c =&gt; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BaseAddress</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Uri</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://api.github.com&quot;</span>))\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddStandardResilienceHandler</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h2 id=\"service-discovery\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/http-client/service-discovery\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Service discovery <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>In .NET Aspire and Kubernetes, you address services by <strong>logical name</strong> rather than a URL hard-coded to a specific host: <code>c.BaseAddress = new(&quot;https+http://inventory&quot;)</code>, with <code>Microsoft.Extensions.ServiceDiscovery</code> resolving the actual endpoint from configuration or DNS at runtime. This sidesteps the stale-DNS problem from a different angle entirely — the address itself is never a fixed IP to begin with. See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/observability-aspire\">Observability &amp; Aspire</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"testing-code-that-uses-httpclient\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/http-client/testing-code-that-uses-httpclient\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Testing code that uses HttpClient <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// A stub handler: no network, deterministic responses</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">StubHandler</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpRequestMessage</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpResponseMessage</span>&gt; respond) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpMessageHandler</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpResponseMessage</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">SendAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpRequestMessage</span> req, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromResult</span>(respond(req));\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-attr\">[Fact]</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Returns_null_when_sku_unknown</span>()\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> http = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpClient</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">StubHandler</span>(_ =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpResponseMessage</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpStatusCode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>))) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">BaseAddress</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://inv/&quot;</span>) };\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> client = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InventoryClient</span>(http);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Assert</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Null</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> client.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetStockAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;NOPE&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">None</span>));\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice the test constructs <code>InventoryClient</code> directly with a hand-built <code>HttpClient</code>, bypassing the factory entirely — this works precisely because the typed client&#39;s constructor just wants <em>an</em> <code>HttpClient</code>, and doesn&#39;t care how it was produced. For integration tests that need genuinely realistic HTTP behaviour — real headers, real status-code edge cases — run a fake server with <strong>WireMock.Net</strong> instead of a hand-rolled stub.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why shouldn&#39;t you create a new HttpClient for every request?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Each instance (with its own handler) opens new connections. Disposing it closes sockets that linger in TIME_WAIT, so under load you exhaust ephemeral ports (socket exhaustion), and every call pays new TCP/TLS handshakes.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Then why not just use one static HttpClient?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Its pooled connections live forever, so DNS changes aren&#39;t observed and you can keep hitting old IPs after failovers or deployments. Fix it with IHttpClientFactory, which recycles handlers, or by setting SocketsHttpHandler.PooledConnectionLifetime on a long-lived client.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What does IHttpClientFactory do?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>It creates HttpClient instances whose underlying handlers are pooled and periodically recycled (2 min default), avoiding both socket exhaustion and stale DNS. It centralises configuration (named/typed clients), lets you compose DelegatingHandlers, integrates logging and resilience (Polly), and works with DI.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Named vs typed clients?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Named clients are configured under a name and created with factory.CreateClient(&quot;name&quot;). Typed clients are classes that receive a preconfigured HttpClient via their constructor and expose domain methods; they&#39;re strongly typed, encapsulate the API and are usually preferred.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is a DelegatingHandler?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A handler in HttpClient&#39;s outbound pipeline that can inspect or modify requests and responses before passing them on with base.SendAsync. Used for auth tokens, correlation headers, logging and resilience; added with AddHttpMessageHandler.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you set timeouts for outbound calls?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>HttpClient.Timeout (default 100 s, usually too long), or better, the resilience pipeline&#39;s total and per-attempt timeouts. Always pass a CancellationToken from the incoming request so calls stop if the client disconnects.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you unit test a class that uses HttpClient?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Construct the HttpClient with a stub HttpMessageHandler that returns canned responses (or use a mocking library for handlers), or run WireMock.Net for higher-fidelity tests. Don&#39;t mock HttpClient directly.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/http-client/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/http-requests\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Make HTTP requests using IHttpClientFactory</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/fundamentals/networking/http/httpclient-guidelines\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">HttpClient guidelines for .NET</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/resilience/http-resilience\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Build resilient HTTP apps</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/http-client/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>The checks below cover handler ordering, a typed client built with a stub handler, and <code>HttpClient.Timeout</code> vs caller cancellation — all with a fake <code>HttpMessageHandler</code>, no real network. Save as a console app (<code>dotnet new console</code>), then <code>dotnet run</code>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Net</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Net</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Http</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Json</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: DelegatingHandlers form an ordered pipeline — each one runs, in registration order, before the final handler.</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> order = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> final = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">StubHandler</span>(_ =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpResponseMessage</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpStatusCode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OK</span>));\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> handlerB = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">RecordingHandler</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;B&quot;</span>, order) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">InnerHandler</span> = final };\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> handlerA = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">RecordingHandler</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A&quot;</span>, order) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">InnerHandler</span> = handlerB };\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> http = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpClient</span>(handlerA);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> res = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> http.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;http://stub/orders&quot;</span>);\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SequenceEqual</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>[] { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;B&quot;</span> }))\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected [A, B], got [{string.Join(&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;, order)}]&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (res.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span> != <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpStatusCode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OK</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: final handler's response didn't come through&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: DelegatingHandlers run in registration order, wrapping the next one&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: a typed client's constructor just wants an HttpClient — you can hand it a stub handler directly, no factory needed.</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> http = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpClient</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">StubHandler</span>(_ =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpResponseMessage</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpStatusCode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>))) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">BaseAddress</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Uri</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;http://inv/&quot;</span>) };\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> client = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InventoryClient</span>(http);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> result = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> client.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetStockAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;NOPE&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">None</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (result <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">not</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected null for a 404&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: a typed client built directly with a stub handler behaves exactly like a factory-built one&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: HttpClient.Timeout firing produces TaskCanceledException with an inner TimeoutException (since .NET 5) —</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// this is how you tell &quot;it timed out&quot; apart from &quot;the caller cancelled it.&quot;</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> slow = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpClient</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DelayingHandler</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSeconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>))) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Timeout</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromMilliseconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">100</span>) };\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>? caught = <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> slow.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;http://stub/slow&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">None</span>); }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span> ex) { caught = ex; }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (caught <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">not</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TaskCanceledException</span> tce || tce.<span class=\"tk-ty\">InnerException</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">not</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeoutException</span>)\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected TaskCanceledException with inner TimeoutException, got {caught}&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: HttpClient.Timeout firing throws TaskCanceledException with an inner TimeoutException&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> slow = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpClient</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DelayingHandler</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSeconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>))) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Timeout</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Timeout</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">InfiniteTimeSpan</span> };\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> cts = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationTokenSource</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromMilliseconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">100</span>));\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>? caught = <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> slow.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;http://stub/slow&quot;</span>, cts.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Token</span>); }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span> ex) { caught = ex; }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (caught <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">not</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OperationCanceledException</span> || caught <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TaskCanceledException</span> tce2 &amp;&amp; tce2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">InnerException</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeoutException</span>)\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected a plain cancellation, not a TimeoutException, got {caught}&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: the caller's own CancellationToken firing does NOT produce an inner TimeoutException&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All checks passed.&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here (needs a real network, real sockets, or real DNS):</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - socket exhaustion from a new HttpClient per request, or stale DNS from one static HttpClient forever — see Further reading: &quot;HttpClient guidelines for .NET&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - IHttpClientFactory's actual handler-recycling schedule (every 2 minutes by default) — see Further reading: &quot;Make HTTP requests using IHttpClientFactory&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - resilience handlers (retries, circuit breakers), Refit/Kiota-generated clients, service discovery — see Further reading: &quot;Build resilient HTTP apps&quot;</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">StubHandler</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpRequestMessage</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpResponseMessage</span>&gt; respond) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpMessageHandler</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpResponseMessage</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">SendAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpRequestMessage</span> req, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt;\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromResult</span>(respond(req));\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DelayingHandler</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span> delay) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpMessageHandler</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpResponseMessage</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">SendAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpRequestMessage</span> req, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Delay</span>(delay, ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpResponseMessage</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpStatusCode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OK</span>);\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">RecordingHandler</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> name, <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; order) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">DelegatingHandler</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpResponseMessage</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">SendAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpRequestMessage</span> request, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n    {\n        order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(name);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SendAsync</span>(request, ct);\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InventoryClient</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpClient</span> http)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">StockLevel</span>?&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetStockAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> sku, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> res = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> http.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;stock/{Uri.EscapeDataString(sku)}&quot;</span>, ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (res.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span> == <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpStatusCode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>;\n        res.<span class=\"tk-ty\">EnsureSuccessStatusCode</span>();\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> res.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Content</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadFromJsonAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">StockLevel</span>&gt;(ct);\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">StockLevel</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Quantity</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-basic-calls","text":"The basic calls","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"what-an-httpclient-actually-is-underneath","text":"What an HttpClient actually is, underneath","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"the-two-classic-bugs-and-why-theyre-opposite-mistakes","text":"The two classic bugs, and why they're opposite mistakes","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"bug-1-a-new-httpclient-per-request-socket-exhaustion","text":"Bug 1: a new HttpClient per request → socket exhaustion","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"bug-2-one-static-httpclient-forever-stale-dns","text":"Bug 2: one static HttpClient forever → stale DNS","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-fix-let-something-else-own-the-lifetime-decision","text":"The fix: let something else own the lifetime decision","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"ihttpclientfactory-in-practice","text":"IHttpClientFactory in practice","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"named-clients","text":"Named clients","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"typed-clients-preferred","text":"Typed clients (preferred)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"delegatinghandlers-the-exact-same-idea-as-middleware-running","text":"DelegatingHandlers: the exact same idea as middleware, running outbound","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"timeouts-and-cancellation-getting-the-layering-right","text":"Timeouts and cancellation: getting the layering right","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"handling-responses-properly-resist-the-urge-to-just-ensuresu","text":"Handling responses properly: resist the urge to just EnsureSuccessStatusCode","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"generated-and-declarative-clients","text":"Generated and declarative clients","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"service-discovery","text":"Service discovery","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"testing-code-that-uses-httpclient","text":"Testing code that uses HttpClient","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":3411,"minutes":16,"num":"3.9","part":"p3"},"caching-ratelimit-cors":{"id":"caching-ratelimit-cors","title":"Caching, Rate Limiting, CORS and Response Performance","summary":"The pipeline features that protect and speed up an API. Caching layers and strategies (in-memory, distributed, HybridCache, output and HTTP caching), invalidation and stampedes, rate limiting algorithms and partitioning, CORS and preflight requests, response compression, and request timeouts.","layer":"framework","where":"ASP.NET Core › Request pipeline › Caching, rate limiting & CORS","fits":"<p>Most of these are <strong>middleware</strong> (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/middleware\">Middleware</a>) whose behaviour is chosen per endpoint through routing metadata (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/routing\">Routing</a>). The cache <strong>stores</strong> themselves (Redis) live in layer 5 (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/nosql-redis\">NoSQL &amp; Redis</a>), and CORS is a browser-security mechanism covered from the attacker&#39;s side in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/web-security\">Web security</a>.</p>\n","prereqs":["middleware","routing"],"next":["nosql-redis","background-work","web-security"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Every cache decision reduces to one question: <strong>how stale can this data be?</strong> Decide that first, per data type, before picking a technology.</li>\n<li><code>IMemoryCache</code> is fast but <strong>per instance</strong> — three app instances means three independent copies that can disagree. <code>IDistributedCache</code> (Redis) is shared but pays a network hop every time. <code>HybridCache</code> (.NET 9) gives you both: a fast local layer in front of a shared one, plus stampede protection.</li>\n<li>On a write, <strong>delete</strong> the cache key rather than overwriting it with the new value — overwriting can leave a stale value cached if two writers race.</li>\n<li>Rate limiting (fixed window, sliding window, token bucket) caps requests per client; like <code>IMemoryCache</code>, the built-in limiter&#39;s counters are <strong>per instance</strong>, so a &quot;100/minute&quot; limit becomes &quot;100 × instance count&quot; across a fleet unless you use a shared store.</li>\n<li>CORS protects <strong>browsers</strong>, not your API — non-browser clients (curl, server-to-server calls) ignore it completely. Your API&#39;s real protection is authentication/authorization.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"why-cache-at-all\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors/why-cache-at-all\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Why cache at all <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Most backend reads are <strong>repetitive</strong>, in a specific, exploitable way: the same product page, the same exchange rates, the same permission lookup, asked for thousands of times while the underlying data barely changes. Every one of those identical requests still costs a full database round trip or an external API call unless something intervenes. A cache is that intervention — a copy of the answer, kept close by, so the thousandth identical request doesn&#39;t repeat the work the first one already did.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Benefit</th>\n<th>Cost</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Lower latency (memory lookup in microseconds vs a database query in milliseconds)</td>\n<td><strong>Staleness</strong>: the copy may be out of date</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Less load on databases and downstream APIs; lower bills</td>\n<td><strong>Invalidation</strong> logic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Resilience: can keep serving when a dependency is slow</td>\n<td>Memory use, extra infrastructure (Redis)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Absorbs traffic spikes</td>\n<td>Consistency across instances</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-why\"><div class=\"callout-title\">The question every cache decision reduces to</div><p>Every cache ultimately answers one question: <strong>how stale may this data be?</strong> A price shown at checkout: seconds, or arguably no cache at all. A product description: minutes or hours is fine. A list of countries: days, safely. Decide this per data type <em>first</em>, before picking any technology — the right tool follows directly from the answer, not the other way around.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"the-caching-layers-from-the-browser-down-to-the-database\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors/the-caching-layers-from-the-browser-down-to-the-database\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The caching layers, from the browser down to the database <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>A single request can pass through several independent caching layers before it ever touches a database, each one answering the &quot;how stale&quot; question for a different audience:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Browser cache ─▶ CDN / reverse proxy ─▶ Output cache (whole response) ─▶ App data cache (memory → Redis) ─▶ Database\n  Cache-Control      Front Door            ASP.NET Core middleware        HybridCache / IMemoryCache /       (+ its own\n  ETag                                                                    IDistributedCache                    buffer pool)</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Layer</th>\n<th>Tool</th>\n<th>Caches</th>\n<th>Shared across instances?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>HTTP (client/CDN)</td>\n<td><code>Cache-Control</code>, <code>ETag</code> headers, response caching middleware</td>\n<td>Responses, by URL</td>\n<td>Yes (CDN), per user (browser)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Server output</td>\n<td><strong>Output caching</strong> (<code>AddOutputCache</code>)</td>\n<td>Whole endpoint responses</td>\n<td>Per instance (or Redis store)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>App data, local</td>\n<td><strong><code>IMemoryCache</code></strong></td>\n<td>Objects in process memory</td>\n<td><strong>No</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>App data, distributed</td>\n<td><strong><code>IDistributedCache</code></strong> (Redis, SQL, Cosmos)</td>\n<td>Serialized bytes</td>\n<td><strong>Yes</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>App data, two-level</td>\n<td><strong><code>HybridCache</code></strong> (.NET 9)</td>\n<td>L1 memory + L2 distributed, stampede protection, tags</td>\n<td>Yes (L2)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"imemorycache-the-simplest-layer-and-its-one-serious-limitati\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors/imemorycache-the-simplest-layer-and-its-one-serious-limitati\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>IMemoryCache: the simplest layer, and its one serious limitation <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddMemoryCache</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SizeLimit</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">10_000</span>);    <span class=\"tk-com\">// optional size budget (each entry must then set Size)</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CountryService</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IMemoryCache</span> cache, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span> db)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IReadOnlyList</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Country</span>&gt;&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAllAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt;\n        (<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> cache.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetOrCreateAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;countries&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> entry =&gt;\n        {\n            entry.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AbsoluteExpirationRelativeToNow</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromHours</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">6</span>);\n            entry.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SetSize</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Countries</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsNoTracking</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderBy</span>(c =&gt; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToListAsync</span>(ct);\n        }))!;\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Expiration</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Absolute</strong></td>\n<td>Expires at a fixed time/duration after creation: guarantees maximum staleness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sliding</strong></td>\n<td>Expires if not accessed for N; keeps hot items alive (combine with absolute so they still refresh eventually)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Priority / size</strong></td>\n<td>Eviction under memory pressure or size limit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Change tokens</strong></td>\n<td>Evict when a file, config or <code>CancellationTokenSource</code> signals</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">IMemoryCache with multiple instances</div><p>Here&#39;s the limitation that catches people as soon as an app scales past one instance: three instances behind a load balancer mean <strong>three entirely independent caches</strong>, each with its own copy and its own idea of when it was last refreshed. A user can load a page showing a new price, then hit a different instance on the very next request and see the old one. Reach for a distributed cache or <code>HybridCache</code> when consistency across instances genuinely matters, or deliberately accept the short inconsistency window for data where it doesn&#39;t — the country-list example above is a case where it plainly doesn&#39;t.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"idistributedcache-and-redis-trading-a-memory-lookup-for-a-ne\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors/idistributedcache-and-redis-trading-a-memory-lookup-for-a-ne\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>IDistributedCache and Redis: trading a memory lookup for a network hop <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddStackExchangeRedisCache</span>(o =&gt;\n{\n    o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configuration</span> = builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configuration</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetConnectionString</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Redis&quot;</span>);\n    o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">InstanceName</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;shop:&quot;</span>;                         <span class=\"tk-com\">// key prefix</span>\n});\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CartStore</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IDistributedCache</span> cache)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cart</span>?&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> userId, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> bytes = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> cache.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;cart:{userId}&quot;</span>, ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> bytes <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span> ? <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">JsonSerializer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Deserialize</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cart</span>&gt;(bytes);\n    }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> userId, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Cart</span> cart, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt;\n        cache.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;cart:{userId}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">JsonSerializer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SerializeToUtf8Bytes</span>(cart),\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DistributedCacheEntryOptions</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">SlidingExpiration</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromDays</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">7</span>) }, ct);\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice what <code>IDistributedCache</code> deliberately <em>doesn&#39;t</em> do for you: it stores raw <strong>bytes</strong>, not objects, so serialization is your responsibility, and every access is a real network round trip rather than a memory lookup — the price paid for every instance seeing the same data. Implementations: Redis (the common choice; Azure Managed Redis in the cloud), SQL Server, Cosmos DB, NCache, or an in-memory stand-in for dev and tests.</p>\n<h2 id=\"hybridcache-closing-the-gap-between-the-two-previous-section\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors/hybridcache-closing-the-gap-between-the-two-previous-section\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>HybridCache: closing the gap between the two previous sections <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Look back at the last two sections and the tension should be obvious: <code>IMemoryCache</code> is fast but inconsistent across instances; <code>IDistributedCache</code> is consistent but pays a network hop on <em>every single access</em>, even for data that was just read a moment ago on the very same instance. <code>HybridCache</code> (<code>Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.Hybrid</code>, .NET 9+) exists specifically to get both: a fast <strong>L1</strong> in-memory cache in front of an optional <strong>L2</strong> distributed cache, with serialization handled for you, plus two capabilities neither older API gives you on its own — <strong>stampede protection</strong> and <strong>tag-based invalidation</strong>, both covered in the next section.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddStackExchangeRedisCache</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configuration</span> = redisCs);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// becomes L2 automatically</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddHybridCache</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DefaultEntryOptions</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HybridCacheEntryOptions</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Expiration</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromMinutes</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>),          <span class=\"tk-com\">// L2 lifetime</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">LocalCacheExpiration</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromMinutes</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>)  <span class=\"tk-com\">// L1 lifetime (shorter limits cross-instance staleness)</span>\n});\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductQueries</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">HybridCache</span> cache, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span> db)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductDto</span>?&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt;\n        cache.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetOrCreateAsync</span>(\n            <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;product:{id}&quot;</span>,\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> token =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> == id)\n                .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(p =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductDto</span>(p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>, p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span>)).<span class=\"tk-ty\">FirstOrDefaultAsync</span>(token),\n            tags: [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;products&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;category:{id % 10}&quot;</span>],\n            cancellationToken: ct);\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OnProductChangedAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> cache.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RemoveAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;product:{id}&quot;</span>, ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-com\">// or invalidate a whole group:  await cache.RemoveByTagAsync(&quot;products&quot;, ct);</span>\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice the deliberate asymmetry in the two expirations above: <code>LocalCacheExpiration</code> (L1) is set shorter than <code>Expiration</code> (L2) specifically so that no instance&#39;s local copy can drift stale for longer than a minute, while the shared Redis copy still saves everyone the trip back to the database for a full ten minutes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"caching-strategies-and-invalidation-the-part-thats-actually-\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors/caching-strategies-and-invalidation-the-part-thats-actually-\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Caching strategies and invalidation: the part that&#39;s actually hard <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Strategy</th>\n<th>How</th>\n<th>Trade-off</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Cache-aside</strong> (lazy loading)</td>\n<td>Read: check cache → on miss load from DB and store. Write: update DB, then <strong>remove</strong> the cache entry</td>\n<td>Most common; first read after a change is a miss</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Read-through</strong></td>\n<td>The cache library loads on miss (what <code>GetOrCreate</code> gives you)</td>\n<td>Same as cache-aside, encapsulated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Write-through</strong></td>\n<td>Write to cache and DB together</td>\n<td>Cache always fresh; slower writes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Write-behind</strong></td>\n<td>Write to cache; flush to DB asynchronously</td>\n<td>Fast writes; risk of data loss</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Refresh-ahead</strong></td>\n<td>Refresh popular entries before they expire</td>\n<td>No miss latency; extra load</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>TTL only</strong></td>\n<td>Never invalidate; rely on expiry</td>\n<td>Simple; bounded staleness</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Invalidation itself comes in a few recognisable shapes: <strong>remove on write</strong>, right in the same code path that updates the database; <strong>events</strong>, where a <code>ProductChanged</code> message goes out and every service or instance evicts its own copy on hearing it; <strong>tags or versioned keys</strong> (<code>product:v7:42</code>), where bumping a single version number invalidates an entire group at once; and <strong>short TTLs</strong> as a blunt but reliable safety net underneath whatever else you&#39;re doing.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Delete, don&#39;t update, on writes</div><p>Here&#39;s a subtle race worth seeing explicitly: after updating the database, <strong>remove</strong> the cache key rather than writing the freshly computed value directly into the cache. If two writers update concurrently, writing the new value directly can let the <em>slower</em> writer&#39;s update land in the cache <strong>after</strong> the faster one&#39;s, leaving the older value cached — permanently, until the TTL eventually clears it. Deleting the key instead forces the next reader to recompute from the database, which is guaranteed to see the latest committed state.</p>\n</aside>\n<h3 id=\"cache-stampede-what-happens-when-a-popular-key-expires\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors/cache-stampede-what-happens-when-a-popular-key-expires\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Cache stampede: what happens when a popular key expires</h3>\n<p>Picture a hot key — say, the homepage&#39;s featured-products list — expiring at the exact moment traffic is heavy. Every one of the hundreds of concurrent requests that were relying on it misses <strong>simultaneously</strong>, and without any coordination, every single one of them goes to the database to recompute the same answer at once — the &quot;thundering herd.&quot; The defences: <strong>request coalescing</strong>, where only the first caller actually computes the value and every other concurrent caller simply awaits that same in-flight result (<code>HybridCache</code> does this automatically; plain <code>IMemoryCache.GetOrCreate</code> does <strong>not</strong>, which is one of its real limitations); <strong>jittered</strong> expirations, so a batch of keys cached at the same moment don&#39;t all expire at the same moment too; refresh-ahead for the hottest keys specifically; and serving the stale value while a refresh happens in the background, rather than making anyone wait.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-to-cache-and-what-not-to\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors/what-to-cache-and-what-not-to\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What to cache, and what not to</h3>\n<p>Good candidates share a trait: reference data, product catalogues, computed aggregates, permission lookups, external API responses, rendered fragments — things that are read far more often than they change. Poor candidates share the opposite trait: trivial per-request lookups by primary key on an already-indexed table (often not worth the added complexity for the latency you&#39;d save), rapidly changing data that needs strong consistency (stock levels at checkout, account balances — exactly the &quot;seconds, or no cache at all&quot; end of the staleness question from the opening section), and personal data dropped into a shared cache without careful per-user keys.</p>\n<h2 id=\"output-caching-caching-the-whole-http-response-server-side\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors/output-caching-caching-the-whole-http-response-server-side\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Output caching: caching the whole HTTP response, server-side <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Where the layers above cache <em>data</em>, output caching caches <strong>entire responses</strong>, keyed by path and query string (and optionally headers or route values), with tag-based eviction built in:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddOutputCache</span>(o =&gt;\n{\n    o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddBasePolicy</span>(b =&gt; b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Expire</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSeconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">30</span>)));\n    o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddPolicy</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Catalog&quot;</span>, b =&gt; b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Expire</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromMinutes</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>)).<span class=\"tk-ty\">SetVaryByQuery</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;page&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;category&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Tag</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;catalog&quot;</span>));\n});\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// .AddStackExchangeRedisOutputCache(...) to share across instances</span>\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseOutputCache</span>();                                   <span class=\"tk-com\">// after UseAuthorization / UseCors</span>\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/catalog&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetCatalog</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">CacheOutput</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Catalog&quot;</span>);\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapPost</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/admin/products&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">IOutputCacheStore</span> store, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-com\">// ... update ...</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> store.<span class=\"tk-ty\">EvictByTagAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;catalog&quot;</span>, ct);         <span class=\"tk-com\">// invalidate cached catalog pages</span>\n});</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>A safe default is baked in here, worth knowing explicitly: output caching <strong>only caches anonymous GET/HEAD 200 responses</strong> that carry no cookies — a deliberate choice that stops it from ever accidentally serving one logged-in user&#39;s personalised response to someone else.</p>\n<p><strong>Response caching</strong> (<code>UseResponseCaching</code>) is an older, different middleware that strictly follows HTTP <code>Cache-Control</code> semantics rather than giving you server-side policy control. Output caching gives you more control for server-side caching; use <strong>HTTP headers</strong> for the separate job of letting browsers and CDNs cache on the client side:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/assets/config&quot;</span>, () =&gt; cfg).<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithMetadata</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ResponseCacheAttribute</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Duration</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">3600</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Location</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">ResponseCacheLocation</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Any</span> });\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// or set headers directly: ctx.Response.Headers.CacheControl = &quot;public,max-age=3600&quot;;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// ETags + If-None-Match → 304 Not Modified saves bandwidth for unchanged resources</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h2 id=\"rate-limiting-protecting-the-api-from-its-own-popularity\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors/rate-limiting-protecting-the-api-from-its-own-popularity\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Rate limiting: protecting the API from its own popularity <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Rate limiting caps how many requests a given client can make in a given window — protecting the API from abuse, from a runaway client retrying too aggressively, from scraping, and from accidentally denial-of-servicing yourself, while also letting you enforce fair-use or pricing-tier limits. It&#39;s been built into the framework since .NET 7 (<code>Microsoft.AspNetCore.RateLimiting</code>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Algorithm</th>\n<th>How it works</th>\n<th>Good for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Fixed window</strong></td>\n<td>N requests per window (e.g. 100/minute); the counter resets each window</td>\n<td>Simple quotas; allows bursts at window edges</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sliding window</strong></td>\n<td>Window split into segments; smooths the edge burst</td>\n<td>Fairer per-minute limits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Token bucket</strong></td>\n<td>Bucket refills at a rate; each request takes a token; bucket size = burst</td>\n<td>Allowing short bursts with a steady average</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Concurrency</strong></td>\n<td>Max N requests <strong>in flight</strong> at once</td>\n<td>Protecting expensive endpoints or downstream resources</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Worth noticing in that table: fixed window is the easiest to reason about but has a real weakness — a client can send 100 requests in the last second of one window and another 100 in the first second of the next, getting 200 requests in effectively two seconds. Sliding window and token bucket both exist specifically to smooth that edge case out.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddRateLimiter</span>(o =&gt;\n{\n    o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RejectionStatusCode</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCodes</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status429TooManyRequests</span>;\n    o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OnRejected</span> = (ctx, _) =&gt;\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lease</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGetMetadata</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">MetadataName</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RetryAfter</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> retry))\n            ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpContext</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Response</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RetryAfter</span> = ((<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>)retry.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TotalSeconds</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>();\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CompletedTask</span>;\n    };\n\n    <span class=\"tk-com\">// Per-user (or per-IP for anonymous) token bucket</span>\n    o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddPolicy</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;per-user&quot;</span>, http =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">RateLimitPartition</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetTokenBucketLimiter</span>(\n        partitionKey: http.<span class=\"tk-ty\">User</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FindFirstValue</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;sub&quot;</span>) ?? http.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Connection</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RemoteIpAddress</span>?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>() ?? <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;anon&quot;</span>,\n        factory: _ =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TokenBucketRateLimiterOptions</span>\n        {\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">TokenLimit</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">100</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TokensPerPeriod</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReplenishmentPeriod</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSeconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>),\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">QueueLimit</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">AutoReplenishment</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>\n        }));\n\n    o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddConcurrencyLimiter</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;reports&quot;</span>, c =&gt; { c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PermitLimit</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">4</span>; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">QueueLimit</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>; });   <span class=\"tk-com\">// heavy endpoint</span>\n\n    o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GlobalLimiter</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">PartitionedRateLimiter</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Create</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpContext</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;(http =&gt;     <span class=\"tk-com\">// safety net for everything</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">RateLimitPartition</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetFixedWindowLimiter</span>(http.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Connection</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RemoteIpAddress</span>?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>() ?? <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;x&quot;</span>,\n            _ =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FixedWindowRateLimiterOptions</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">PermitLimit</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">1000</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Window</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromMinutes</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>) }));\n});\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseRateLimiter</span>();                                   <span class=\"tk-com\">// after UseAuthentication if partitioning by user</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/search&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Search</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequireRateLimiting</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;per-user&quot;</span>);\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapPost</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/reports&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">BuildReport</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequireRateLimiting</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;reports&quot;</span>);\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/health&quot;</span>, () =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ok&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">DisableRateLimiting</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">In-process limits are per instance</div><p>This is the same scaling trap <code>IMemoryCache</code> fell into earlier in this chapter, wearing a different costume: the built-in limiter keeps its counters <strong>in memory</strong>, local to each instance. Run 5 instances behind a load balancer, and a limit you configured as &quot;100 requests per minute&quot; is, in effect, 500 requests per minute across the fleet, split however the load balancer happens to route traffic. For a genuinely strict global limit, enforce it at the gateway (Azure API Management, Front Door WAF rate rules, NGINX) or use a Redis-backed limiter that all instances share. And if you&#39;re behind a proxy, make sure forwarded headers are configured — exactly as covered in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting/why-production-traffic-rarely-hits-kestrel-directly\">ASP.NET Core architecture</a> — or partitioning &quot;by IP&quot; will partition by the proxy&#39;s IP, collapsing every real client into one bucket.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"cors-a-rule-browsers-enforce-on-your-behalf-for-your-users-p\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors/cors-a-rule-browsers-enforce-on-your-behalf-for-your-users-p\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>CORS: a rule browsers enforce on your behalf, for your users&#39; protection <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Browsers enforce the <strong>same-origin policy</strong>: JavaScript running on <code>https://shop.com</code> cannot read a response from <code>https://api.shop.com</code> — a different origin, since origin means scheme + host + port together — <strong>unless the API explicitly says it&#39;s allowed to</strong>, through a set of CORS response headers.</p>\n<p>Here&#39;s the detail that trips people up the first time they debug a &quot;CORS error&quot;: <strong>CORS protects users&#39; browsers, not your API.</strong> curl, Postman, and any server-to-server call ignore CORS completely — there&#39;s no browser involved to enforce it. Your API&#39;s actual protection against unauthorized access is authentication and authorization, covered in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/auth\">Auth</a>; CORS is purely about which <em>websites</em> a <em>browser</em> will let read your responses on a user&#39;s behalf.</p>\n<h3 id=\"simple-vs-preflight-requests\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors/simple-vs-preflight-requests\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Simple vs preflight requests</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Simple requests</strong> (GET/HEAD/POST with basic content types and no custom headers) go out directly; the browser only checks <code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin</code> on the response afterward, before letting the page&#39;s JavaScript actually read it.</li>\n<li><strong>Preflighted requests</strong> (PUT/DELETE/PATCH, a <code>Content-Type: application/json</code> body, an <code>Authorization</code> header…) are different: the browser sends an <strong><code>OPTIONS</code></strong> request first, asking permission before the real request goes out at all:</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>HTTP</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>OPTIONS /api/orders HTTP/<span class=\"tk-num\">1.1</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Origin</span>: <span class=\"tk-ty\">https</span>:<span class=\"tk-com\">//shop.com</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Access-Control-Request-Method</span>: POST\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Access-Control-Request-Headers</span>: authorization, content-type\n\nHTTP/<span class=\"tk-num\">1.1</span> <span class=\"tk-num\">204</span> No Content\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Access-Control-Allow-Origin</span>: <span class=\"tk-ty\">https</span>:<span class=\"tk-com\">//shop.com</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Access-Control-Allow-Methods</span>: GET, POST, PUT, DELETE\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Access-Control-Allow-Headers</span>: authorization, content-type\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Access-Control-Max-Age</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">600</span></code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddCors</span>(o =&gt;\n{\n    o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddPolicy</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;web&quot;</span>, p =&gt; p\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithOrigins</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://shop.com&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://admin.shop.com&quot;</span>)     <span class=\"tk-com\">// explicit list from config</span>\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithMethods</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;GET&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;POST&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;PUT&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;DELETE&quot;</span>)\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithHeaders</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;authorization&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;content-type&quot;</span>)\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AllowCredentials</span>()                                          <span class=\"tk-com\">// only if you need cookies cross-origin</span>\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">SetPreflightMaxAge</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromMinutes</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>)));\n});\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseCors</span>();                                  <span class=\"tk-com\">// AFTER UseRouting, BEFORE UseAuthentication/UseAuthorization</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGroup</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/api&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequireCors</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;web&quot;</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">The classic CORS mistakes</div><ul>\n<li><code>AllowAnyOrigin()</code> combined with <code>AllowCredentials()</code> is <strong>invalid</strong> — browsers reject the combination outright, by design. And never reflect back whatever <code>Origin</code> header the request happened to carry, with credentials enabled; doing that effectively lets <em>any</em> site act as the logged-in user.</li>\n<li>Placing <code>UseCors</code> <strong>after</strong> authentication is the single most common ordering mistake here, and the exact scenario <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/middleware/the-correct-order-and-precisely-what-breaks-when-you-get-it-\">Middleware</a> warned about generically: a preflight <code>OPTIONS</code> request carries no credentials at all, so if authentication runs first, it rejects the preflight with a 401 — which the browser then reports to the page simply as &quot;a CORS error,&quot; hiding the real cause.</li>\n<li>A plain 500 error page with no CORS headers attached <em>also</em> shows up in the browser console as a &quot;CORS error,&quot; even though CORS has nothing to do with it — the browser can&#39;t distinguish &quot;server refused CORS&quot; from &quot;server crashed before it could say anything.&quot; Check the actual server logs before assuming it&#39;s a CORS configuration problem.</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"response-compression-and-request-timeouts\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors/response-compression-and-request-timeouts\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Response compression and request timeouts <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddResponseCompression</span>(o =&gt;\n{\n    o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">EnableForHttps</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;                    <span class=\"tk-com\">// consider BREACH risk for responses that mix secrets with user input</span>\n    o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Providers</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">BrotliCompressionProvider</span>&gt;();\n    o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Providers</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">GzipCompressionProvider</span>&gt;();\n});\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseResponseCompression</span>();\n\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddRequestTimeouts</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddPolicy</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;fast&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSeconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>)));\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseRequestTimeouts</span>();\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/quote&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetQuote</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithRequestTimeout</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;fast&quot;</span>);     <span class=\"tk-com\">// RequestAborted token fires after 2 s</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Before enabling this in the app itself, check what&#39;s already sitting in front of it: a reverse proxy or CDN frequently compresses better and more cheaply than your app process can, since it&#39;s doing it once for many backend instances rather than redundantly in each one. Enable compression here specifically when nothing upstream already does it.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What caching options does ASP.NET Core provide?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>IMemoryCache (in-process objects), IDistributedCache (shared byte cache backed by Redis, SQL, etc.), HybridCache (.NET 9: two-level memory plus distributed with stampede protection and tags), output caching (whole server-side responses with tag eviction), and HTTP response caching via Cache-Control/ETag headers for browsers and CDNs.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>In-memory vs distributed cache?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>In-memory is fastest but per instance (not shared, lost on restart, inconsistent across a farm). Distributed (Redis) is shared and survives app restarts, but costs a network hop and serialization. HybridCache combines both.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the cache-aside pattern?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Check the cache first; on a miss, load from the source and store it with an expiry; on writes, update the source and invalidate (remove) the cache entry. It&#39;s the most common caching pattern.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is a cache stampede and how do you prevent it?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Many concurrent requests miss the same expired hot key and all recompute it, overloading the database. Prevent it with request coalescing (one computes, the rest wait, as HybridCache does), locks, jittered expirations, refresh-ahead, or serve-stale-while-revalidate.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you invalidate cached data?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Remove keys when the underlying data changes (in the write path or via change events), use tags or versioned keys to evict groups, and keep TTLs as a safety net. Prefer deleting over updating cache entries to avoid races.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What rate limiting algorithms exist and how do you apply them in ASP.NET Core?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Fixed window, sliding window, token bucket and concurrency. Register policies with AddRateLimiter (partitioned per user/IP/API key), add UseRateLimiter, and attach with RequireRateLimiting or [EnableRateLimiting]. Rejections return 429 with Retry-After. Counters are per instance, so enforce global limits at a gateway or with a distributed store.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is CORS and does it protect your API?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A browser mechanism that relaxes the same-origin policy when the server returns Access-Control-Allow-* headers, letting JS from another origin read responses. It protects users&#39; browsers from malicious sites, not your API, because non-browser clients ignore it. API protection comes from authentication and authorization.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is a preflight request?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>An automatic OPTIONS request the browser sends before &quot;non-simple&quot; cross-origin requests (custom headers like Authorization, JSON content type, PUT/DELETE) to ask which origins, methods and headers are allowed. The CORS middleware answers it; it must run before authentication.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What&#39;s the difference between output caching and response caching?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Response caching follows HTTP Cache-Control semantics and is mainly for client/proxy caching. Output caching is server-controlled: policies per endpoint, vary-by rules, tag-based eviction, storage in memory or Redis, independent of client headers, and safe defaults (anonymous GETs only).</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/performance/caching/overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Caching overview</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/performance/caching/hybrid\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">HybridCache library</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/performance/caching/output\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Output caching middleware</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/performance/rate-limit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rate limiting middleware</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/security/cors\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Enable CORS in ASP.NET Core</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>The checks below hand-roll a minimal cache, token bucket and fixed-window limiter (no Redis, no <code>AddRateLimiter</code>, no <code>HybridCache</code> package) to verify the <em>mechanics</em> this chapter describes in isolation. CORS preflight behaviour needs a real browser making a real cross-origin HTTP request, so it isn&#39;t something a console program can verify — see the MDN/Microsoft Learn links above for that one. Save as <code>Program.cs</code> in a new console project (<code>dotnet new console</code>) and run with <code>dotnet run</code>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Verifies claims from this chapter: cache-aside should delete (not overwrite) a</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// stale entry to avoid a race, two IMemoryCache-like instances don't share state (the &quot;per instance&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// limitation), and a token bucket allows a burst up to its capacity, then throttles.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// CORS preflight behaviour needs a real browser/HTTP round trip and isn't runtime-verifiable in a</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// console app — see Further reading (MDN / learn.microsoft.com CORS docs) for that one.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Collections</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Generic</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Threading</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> fails = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> s) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: {s}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> cond, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> s)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (cond) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(s);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">else</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: {s}&quot;</span>); fails++; }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Delete, don't overwrite, on writes: avoids a slow writer re-caching a stale value ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> cache = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SimpleCache</span>();\ncache.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Set</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;product:1&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;price=10&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Two concurrent writers: writer A reads old price, writer B updates DB + cache first, A &quot;finishes&quot; after.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// If writes overwrote the cache directly, A's stale write could land after B's and stick.</span>\ncache.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Remove</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;product:1&quot;</span>);                 <span class=\"tk-com\">// B's write: delete the key instead of writing a new value directly</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> afterB = cache.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;product:1&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> staleOrMissing);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(!afterB, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Cache-aside: deleting the key on write means the next read is a guaranteed miss, never a stale value&quot;</span>);\ncache.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Set</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;product:1&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;price=12&quot;</span>);        <span class=\"tk-com\">// next reader recomputes from the &quot;database&quot; and repopulates</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(cache.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;product:1&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> fresh) &amp;&amp; fresh == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;price=12&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Cache-aside: the next read after a delete repopulates with the fresh value&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- IMemoryCache limitation: two instances never share state ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> instanceA = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SimpleCache</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> instanceB = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SimpleCache</span>();\ninstanceA.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Set</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;countries&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;cached-on-A&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(!instanceB.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;countries&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> _), <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;IMemoryCache-style cache: a second instance has no knowledge of the first instance's entries&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Token bucket: allows a burst up to capacity, then throttles until tokens refill ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> bucket = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TokenBucket</span>(capacity: <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// no refill modelled, to test the burst limit deterministically</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> allowed = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; i &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">8</span>; i++) <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (bucket.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryTake</span>()) allowed++;\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(allowed == <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Token bucket: exactly `capacity` requests are allowed in a burst (5 of 8 attempts succeeded)&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(!bucket.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryTake</span>(), <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Token bucket: once empty, further requests are rejected until tokens refill&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Fixed window: the counter resets at a window boundary, allowing a new burst ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> window = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FixedWindowLimiter</span>(limit: <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>, window: <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromMilliseconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">50</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> firstWindowAllowed = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; i &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>; i++) <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (window.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryAcquire</span>()) firstWindowAllowed++;\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Thread</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sleep</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">60</span>);                           <span class=\"tk-com\">// cross into the next window</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> afterReset = window.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryAcquire</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(firstWindowAllowed == <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Fixed window: only `limit` requests are allowed within one window (3 of 5 attempts succeeded)&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(afterReset, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Fixed window: the counter resets once the window elapses, allowing requests again&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(fails == <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span> ? <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;\\nAll checks passed.&quot;</span> : <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;\\n{fails} check(s) failed.&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (fails &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exit</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SimpleCache</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; _store = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Set</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> key, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>) =&gt; _store[key] = <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Remove</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> key) =&gt; _store.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Remove</span>(key);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> key, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>? <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>) =&gt; _store.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGetValue</span>(key, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TokenBucket</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> capacity)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> _tokens = capacity;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TryTake</span>()\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (_tokens &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n        _tokens -= <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>;\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FixedWindowLimiter</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> limit, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span> window)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTime</span> _windowStart = <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTime</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UtcNow</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> _count;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TryAcquire</span>()\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTime</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UtcNow</span> - _windowStart &gt;= window) { _windowStart = <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTime</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UtcNow</span>; _count = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; }\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (_count &gt;= limit) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n        _count++;\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"why-cache-at-all","text":"Why cache at all","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"the-caching-layers-from-the-browser-down-to-the-database","text":"The caching layers, from the browser down to the database","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"imemorycache-the-simplest-layer-and-its-one-serious-limitati","text":"IMemoryCache: the simplest layer, and its one serious limitation","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"idistributedcache-and-redis-trading-a-memory-lookup-for-a-ne","text":"IDistributedCache and Redis: trading a memory lookup for a network hop","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"hybridcache-closing-the-gap-between-the-two-previous-section","text":"HybridCache: closing the gap between the two previous sections","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"caching-strategies-and-invalidation-the-part-thats-actually-","text":"Caching strategies and invalidation: the part that's actually hard","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"cache-stampede-what-happens-when-a-popular-key-expires","text":"Cache stampede: what happens when a popular key expires","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"what-to-cache-and-what-not-to","text":"What to cache, and what not to","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"output-caching-caching-the-whole-http-response-server-side","text":"Output caching: caching the whole HTTP response, server-side","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"rate-limiting-protecting-the-api-from-its-own-popularity","text":"Rate limiting: protecting the API from its own popularity","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"cors-a-rule-browsers-enforce-on-your-behalf-for-your-users-p","text":"CORS: a rule browsers enforce on your behalf, for your users' protection","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"simple-vs-preflight-requests","text":"Simple vs preflight requests","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"response-compression-and-request-timeouts","text":"Response compression and request timeouts","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":4581,"minutes":21,"num":"3.10","part":"p3"},"background-work":{"id":"background-work","title":"Background Work: Hosted Services, Workers, Channels and Job Schedulers","summary":"Doing work outside the request. IHostedService and BackgroundService, the lifecycle and pitfalls, worker services, scheduled work with PeriodicTimer, in-memory queues with Channel<T>, durable job systems (Hangfire, Quartz.NET), and when to use a broker or Azure Functions instead.","layer":"framework","where":"ASP.NET Core › Hosting › Background work","fits":"<p>Hosted services are run <strong>by the host</strong> (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting\">Hosting</a>) alongside the web server, use DI scopes manually (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/scopes-made-concrete\">Dependency injection</a>), and are the usual home for message consumers (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events\">Messaging &amp; events</a>) and outbox publishers. In the cloud, the same jobs might instead run as Azure Functions or Container Apps jobs (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-compute\">Azure compute</a>).</p>\n","prereqs":["aspnet-hosting","dependency-injection","async-await"],"next":["messaging-events","azure-compute"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/background-work/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Work that doesn&#39;t need to finish before you reply should run outside the request, in a <code>BackgroundService</code> hosted by the same process.</li>\n<li><code>BackgroundService</code> is a long-running loop the host starts at startup and stops at shutdown; because it&#39;s a singleton, create a fresh DI scope <em>inside</em> the loop for anything scoped, like a <code>DbContext</code>.</li>\n<li><code>Channel&lt;T&gt;</code> hands work off to an in-process background worker fast, but loses everything if the process dies — use it only for work you can afford to lose or redo.</li>\n<li>For work that must survive a crash, retry automatically, or run once across several replicas, use a durable store: a job library (Hangfire, Quartz.NET) or a message broker.</li>\n<li>A <strong>Worker Service</strong> is the same host with background services but no web server — good for deploying queue consumers and schedulers separately from the API.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"work-that-shouldnt-make-a-request-wait\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/background-work/work-that-shouldnt-make-a-request-wait\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Work that shouldn&#39;t make a request wait <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>A request should return quickly — that&#39;s a constraint, not a preference, because something&#39;s actually waiting on the other end of it. But not every piece of work an app needs to do fits inside that window, and some of it isn&#39;t triggered by a request at all:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Deferred</strong> (triggered by a request, done later)</td>\n<td>Sending emails, generating PDFs/reports, resizing images, calling slow third parties, webhooks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Scheduled / periodic</strong></td>\n<td>Nightly cleanup, hourly sync with an ERP, refreshing caches, expiring carts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Continuous</strong></td>\n<td>Consuming a message queue, processing an outbox, streaming events</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The pattern for the first category is usually the same: the API responds <strong>202 Accepted</strong> (plus a status URL the client can poll), and the actual work continues running after the response has already gone out.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ihostedservice-and-backgroundservice-the-hosts-slot-for-long\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/background-work/ihostedservice-and-backgroundservice-the-hosts-slot-for-long\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>IHostedService and BackgroundService: the host&#39;s slot for long-running code <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Recall from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting/the-generic-host-the-thing-that-owns-everything-else\">ASP.NET Core architecture</a> that the host starts and stops a list of <code>IHostedService</code> instances alongside the web server itself — Kestrel is literally one of them. <code>IHostedService</code> is the plain contract behind that:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"cHVibGljIGludGVyZmFjZSBJSG9zdGVkU2VydmljZQp7CiAgICBUYXNrIFN0YXJ0QXN5bmMoQ2FuY2VsbGF0aW9uVG9rZW4gY2FuY2VsbGF0aW9uVG9rZW4pOyAgIC8vIGNhbGxlZCBhdCBzdGFydHVwCiAgICBUYXNrIFN0b3BBc3luYyhDYW5jZWxsYXRpb25Ub2tlbiBjYW5jZWxsYXRpb25Ub2tlbik7ICAgIC8vIGNhbGxlZCBhdCBncmFjZWZ1bCBzaHV0ZG93bgp9\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IHostedService</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">StartAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> cancellationToken);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// called at startup</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">StopAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> cancellationToken);    <span class=\"tk-com\">// called at graceful shutdown</span>\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Implementing that interface directly is rare in practice, because almost every real background job has the same shape: start a loop, keep it running for the app&#39;s entire lifetime, stop cleanly when asked. <strong><code>BackgroundService</code></strong> is the abstract base class that captures exactly that shape, so you only have to write the loop itself:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ExchangeRateRefresher</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IServiceScopeFactory</span> scopes, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ILogger</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExchangeRateRefresher</span>&gt; log) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">BackgroundService</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> stoppingToken)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> timer = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PeriodicTimer</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromMinutes</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">15</span>));\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">do</span>\n        {\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span>\n            {\n                <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> scope = scopes.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateAsyncScope</span>();               <span class=\"tk-com\">// scoped services need a scope</span>\n                <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> svc = scope.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceProvider</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IRateService</span>&gt;();\n                <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> svc.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RefreshAsync</span>(stoppingToken);\n                log.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogInformation</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Exchange rates refreshed&quot;</span>);\n            }\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">OperationCanceledException</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">when</span> (stoppingToken.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsCancellationRequested</span>) { <span class=\"tk-kw\">break</span>; }\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span> ex)\n            {\n                log.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogError</span>(ex, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Refreshing exchange rates failed; will retry next tick&quot;</span>);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// don't let one failure kill the loop</span>\n            }\n        }\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">while</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> timer.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WaitForNextTickAsync</span>(stoppingToken));\n    }\n}\n\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddHostedService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExchangeRateRefresher</span>&gt;();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice the <code>scopes.CreateAsyncScope()</code> call right at the top of the loop body, not outside it. The next section explains exactly why that placement is load-bearing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-lifecycle-and-the-pitfalls-each-fact-in-it-quietly-creat\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/background-work/the-lifecycle-and-the-pitfalls-each-fact-in-it-quietly-creat\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The lifecycle, and the pitfalls each fact in it quietly creates <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Fact</th>\n<th>Consequence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Hosted services are <strong>singletons</strong></td>\n<td>Use <code>IServiceScopeFactory</code> for scoped services (DbContext); never inject them directly (captive dependency)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>StartAsync</code> of each service is awaited <strong>in registration order before the app starts serving</strong></td>\n<td>Don&#39;t do long work in <code>StartAsync</code>. <code>BackgroundService.StartAsync</code> returns as soon as <code>ExecuteAsync</code> hits its first real <code>await</code>, so <strong>synchronous work at the top of <code>ExecuteAsync</code> blocks startup</strong> — add <code>await Task.Yield()</code> first if that&#39;s a risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unhandled exception in <code>ExecuteAsync</code></td>\n<td>Since .NET 6 the <strong>host stops</strong> (<code>BackgroundServiceExceptionBehavior.StopHost</code>). Catch and log inside the loop</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>stoppingToken</code> is cancelled on shutdown</td>\n<td>Pass it everywhere; finish or abandon the current item within the <code>ShutdownTimeout</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Every app instance runs its hosted services</td>\n<td>With 3 replicas, the &quot;nightly job&quot; runs <strong>3 times</strong>. You need a distributed lock, a leader election, a single dedicated worker, or a job scheduler</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>In-memory work is lost when the process dies</td>\n<td>Anything that must not be lost needs a durable store (DB, queue)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The first row is the one mistake almost everyone makes once: a <code>BackgroundService</code> is built exactly once, at startup, and that same instance lives for the whole lifetime of the process — a singleton, in the same sense <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/the-captive-dependency-the-single-most-common-di-bug\">Dependency injection</a> warned about. If you injected a scoped <code>DbContext</code> straight into its constructor, that one <code>DbContext</code> would be stuck there forever, slowly going stale and failing in ways that are hard to connect back to the real cause. The fix is to open a <strong>new scope inside the loop</strong>, once per iteration: each tick gets its own short-lived <code>DbContext</code>, used once and thrown away — the same lifetime a real HTTP request would give it.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configure</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">HostOptions</span>&gt;(o =&gt;\n{\n    o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ServicesStartConcurrently</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;          <span class=\"tk-com\">// .NET 8: start hosted services in parallel</span>\n    o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BackgroundServiceExceptionBehavior</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">BackgroundServiceExceptionBehavior</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ignore</span>;   <span class=\"tk-com\">// only if you really mean it</span>\n    o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShutdownTimeout</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSeconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">30</span>);\n});</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><code>IHostedLifecycleService</code> (.NET 8) adds four finer-grained hooks — <code>StartingAsync</code>, <code>StartedAsync</code>, <code>StoppingAsync</code> and <code>StoppedAsync</code> — for when you need to run code at a more precise point in the sequence than plain <code>StartAsync</code>/<code>StopAsync</code> allow.</p>\n<h2 id=\"worker-services-the-same-host-deliberately-without-a-web-ser\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/background-work/worker-services-the-same-host-deliberately-without-a-web-ser\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Worker services: the same host, deliberately without a web server <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Everything above assumed a web app that <em>also</em> runs background work alongside its HTTP server. A <strong>Worker Service</strong> flips that: it&#39;s the same Generic Host, with background services, but <strong>no web server at all</strong> — the natural shape for queue consumers, schedulers, and integration processes you want to deploy and scale completely separately from the API:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// dotnet new worker -n Shop.Worker</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> builder = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Host</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateApplicationBuilder</span>(args);\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddDbContext</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>&gt;(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseSqlServer</span>(builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configuration</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetConnectionString</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop&quot;</span>)));\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddHostedService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderEventsConsumer</span>&gt;();\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddHostedService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OutboxPublisher</span>&gt;();\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Run</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>It gets exactly the same configuration, logging, DI and graceful shutdown as a web app — none of that plumbing is actually specific to having a web server — and runs as a container, a Windows Service (<code>UseWindowsService()</code>), or a systemd unit (<code>UseSystemd()</code>).</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-fits\"><div class=\"callout-title\">API + worker</div><p>A common deployable split: <strong>Shop.Api</strong> (handles HTTP, writes to the DB and an <strong>outbox</strong> — a table that stores events to publish, written in the same database transaction as the business change so the two can&#39;t disagree; see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events\">Messaging &amp; events</a>) and <strong>Shop.Worker</strong> (consumes queues, publishes the outbox, runs schedules). Both reference the same Application/Domain/Infrastructure projects. They scale independently, and a slow job never steals threads from HTTP requests.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"in-memory-queues-with-channel-handing-work-off-within-one-pr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/background-work/in-memory-queues-with-channel-handing-work-off-within-one-pr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>In-memory queues with Channel<T>: handing work off within one process <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Sometimes the work doesn&#39;t need to leave the process at all — it just needs to leave the <em>request</em>. For that, <code>System.Threading.Channels</code> gives you a fast, async-native producer/consumer queue:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ThumbnailJob</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ImageId</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">BlobPath</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ThumbnailQueue</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Channel</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ThumbnailJob</span>&gt; _channel = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Channel</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateBounded</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ThumbnailJob</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">BoundedChannelOptions</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">500</span>)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">FullMode</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">BoundedChannelFullMode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Wait</span>,      <span class=\"tk-com\">// backpressure: producers wait when full</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">SingleReader</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>\n    });\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">EnqueueAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ThumbnailJob</span> job, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt; _channel.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Writer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteAsync</span>(job, ct);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IAsyncEnumerable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ThumbnailJob</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadAllAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt; _channel.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Reader</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadAllAsync</span>(ct);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ThumbnailWorker</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ThumbnailQueue</span> queue, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IServiceScopeFactory</span> scopes, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ILogger</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ThumbnailWorker</span>&gt; log) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">BackgroundService</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> stoppingToken) =&gt;\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Parallel</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ForEachAsync</span>(queue.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadAllAsync</span>(stoppingToken), <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ParallelOptions</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">MaxDegreeOfParallelism</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">4</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> = stoppingToken },\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (job, ct) =&gt;\n            {\n                <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span>\n                {\n                    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> scope = scopes.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateAsyncScope</span>();\n                    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> scope.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceProvider</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IThumbnailer</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateAsync</span>(job, ct);\n                }\n                <span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span> ex) { log.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogError</span>(ex, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Thumbnail failed for {ImageId}&quot;</span>, job.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ImageId</span>); }\n            });\n}\n\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ThumbnailQueue</span>&gt;();\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddHostedService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ThumbnailWorker</span>&gt;();\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapPost</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/images&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">IFormFile</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">file</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IBlobStore</span> blobs, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ThumbnailQueue</span> queue, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> (id, path) = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> blobs.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">file</span>, ct);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> queue.<span class=\"tk-ty\">EnqueueAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ThumbnailJob</span>(id, path), ct);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Accepted</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;/images/{id}&quot;</span>);\n});</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><strong>Bounded</strong> vs unbounded is a real decision, not a style choice. A bounded channel gives you <strong>backpressure</strong>: once it&#39;s full, <code>EnqueueAsync</code> just waits until a consumer frees a slot, so producers are forced to slow down to the speed consumers can actually keep up with. An unbounded channel is easier to write, but risky under load: nothing stops it growing forever if producers outpace consumers — right up until the process runs out of memory.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">In-memory means losable</div><p>Here&#39;s the fact this whole section has been building toward: a deploy, a crash, or a scale-in event loses <strong>everything</strong> currently sitting in a <code>Channel&lt;T&gt;</code>, instantly and without a trace. Use it only for work you can genuinely afford to lose or simply recompute — thumbnails you can regenerate, cache warm-ups that will just happen again next time. For emails, payments, or anything business-critical, <strong>persist first</strong> — a database table, an outbox, or a message broker — and only then process; never let the only copy of important work exist purely in process memory.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"durable-job-systems-hangfire-and-quartz-net\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/background-work/durable-job-systems-hangfire-and-quartz-net\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Durable job systems: Hangfire and Quartz.NET <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><code>Channel&lt;T&gt;</code> solves &quot;hand work off within this process.&quot; It deliberately doesn&#39;t solve &quot;survive this process dying,&quot; &quot;retry automatically,&quot; &quot;run exactly once across many replicas,&quot; or &quot;run on a cron schedule&quot; — for those, you want a job library backed by a real database:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Hangfire</th>\n<th>Quartz.NET</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Model</td>\n<td>Fire-and-forget, delayed, recurring (cron), continuations</td>\n<td>Jobs + triggers (cron, calendars), clustering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Storage</td>\n<td>SQL Server, PostgreSQL, Redis…</td>\n<td>ADO.NET job store (any major DB)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dashboard</td>\n<td><strong>Built-in web dashboard</strong> (retries, failures, history)</td>\n<td>Third-party UIs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Distributed</td>\n<td>Yes: workers on any instance pull jobs from storage</td>\n<td>Yes: clustered mode with DB locks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Retries</td>\n<td>Automatic with backoff</td>\n<td>Configurable</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddHangfire</span>(c =&gt; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseSqlServerStorage</span>(builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configuration</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetConnectionString</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Jobs&quot;</span>)));\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddHangfireServer</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WorkerCount</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>);\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapHangfireDashboard</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/jobs&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequireAuthorization</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Admin&quot;</span>);\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapPost</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/orders/{id}/invoice&quot;</span>, (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IBackgroundJobClient</span> jobs) =&gt;\n{\n    jobs.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Enqueue</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IInvoiceService</span>&gt;(s =&gt; s.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GenerateAndEmailAsync</span>(id, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">None</span>));   <span class=\"tk-com\">// persisted, retried</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Accepted</span>();\n});\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">RecurringJob</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddOrUpdate</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ICartCleanup</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;cart-cleanup&quot;</span>, s =&gt; s.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RunAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">None</span>), <span class=\"tk-ty\">Cron</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Daily</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>));   <span class=\"tk-com\">// 02:00 daily, ONCE across the cluster</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Jobs here should be <strong>idempotent</strong> — a retry after a partial failure must not send two invoices for the one order. That constraint comes straight from the &quot;automatic retries with backoff&quot; row above: if retries are automatic, a job <em>will</em> eventually run more than once for the same logical unit of work, whether or not the first attempt actually finished.</p>\n<h2 id=\"choosing-where-background-work-runs\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/background-work/choosing-where-background-work-runs\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Choosing where background work runs <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Can the work be lost on restart?      ── yes ──▶ Channel&lt;T&gt; + BackgroundService (same process)\n        │ no\nIs it within one service?              ── yes ──▶ Hangfire / Quartz (DB-backed), or an outbox table + worker\n        │ no (other services must react)\n                                        ───────▶ Message broker (Service Bus / RabbitMQ) + consumers\nSpiky / event-triggered / scale to zero? ──────▶ Azure Functions (queue/timer/blob triggers) or Container Apps jobs\nSimple periodic job, single instance?  ────────▶ BackgroundService + PeriodicTimer (+ distributed lock if replicated)</code></pre></figure>\n<h2 id=\"running-a-job-once-across-replicas\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/background-work/running-a-job-once-across-replicas\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Running a job once across replicas <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>The &quot;every app instance runs its hosted services&quot; row from the lifecycle table earlier has a direct consequence worth solving properly: with several replicas each independently running the same <code>BackgroundService</code>, a &quot;nightly job&quot; literally runs once per replica, not once total. The available fixes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Distributed lock</strong>: acquire a lease before running (SQL <code>sp_getapplock</code>, Redis <code>SET NX PX</code>, Azure Blob lease, the <code>DistributedLock</code> NuGet package); any instance that fails to acquire it simply skips this run.</li>\n<li><strong>Dedicated single-replica worker</strong> deployment for schedulers — sidestep the problem by never having more than one instance of the thing that schedules.</li>\n<li><strong>Job scheduler</strong> with clustering (Hangfire, Quartz) — the library itself coordinates across instances so only one actually executes.</li>\n<li><strong>Platform scheduler</strong>: Kubernetes CronJob, Container Apps jobs, Azure Functions timer trigger (singleton by default) — let the orchestrator guarantee the single run instead of building that guarantee yourself.</li>\n<li><strong>Competing consumers on a queue</strong>: for continuous work rather than scheduled work, each message is delivered to only one instance anyway, so this category of problem doesn&#39;t even arise.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"YXdhaXQgdXNpbmcgdmFyIGhhbmRsZSA9IGF3YWl0IGRpc3RyaWJ1dGVkTG9jay5UcnlBY3F1aXJlQXN5bmMoIm5pZ2h0bHktYmlsbGluZyIsIFRpbWVTcGFuLlplcm8sIHN0b3BwaW5nVG9rZW4pOwppZiAoaGFuZGxlIGlzIG51bGwpIHJldHVybjsgICAvLyBhbm90aGVyIGluc3RhbmNlIGlzIHJ1bm5pbmcgaXQKYXdhaXQgYmlsbGluZy5SdW5Bc3luYyhzdG9wcGluZ1Rva2VuKTs=\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> handle = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> distributedLock.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryAcquireAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;nightly-billing&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Zero</span>, stoppingToken);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (handle <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span>;   <span class=\"tk-com\">// another instance is running it</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> billing.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RunAsync</span>(stoppingToken);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the difference between IHostedService and BackgroundService?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>IHostedService is the interface the host calls (StartAsync/StopAsync). BackgroundService is an abstract implementation that starts your ExecuteAsync loop on StartAsync and cancels its stoppingToken on StopAsync, so you implement one long-running method.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you use a DbContext inside a BackgroundService?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Hosted services are singletons, so inject IServiceScopeFactory and create a scope per unit of work (CreateAsyncScope), resolving DbContext from scope.ServiceProvider and disposing the scope afterwards. Or inject IDbContextFactory&lt;T&gt;.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What happens if a BackgroundService throws an unhandled exception?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Since .NET 6 the default BackgroundServiceExceptionBehavior is StopHost, so the whole application shuts down. Catch exceptions inside the loop, log them and continue (or deliberately fail).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you run periodic work?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A BackgroundService with PeriodicTimer and WaitForNextTickAsync(stoppingToken) for simple cases. For cron schedules, persistence, retries and single execution across a cluster, use Hangfire or Quartz.NET, or a platform scheduler (Functions timer trigger, Kubernetes CronJob).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is Channel<T> and when would you use it?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>An async producer/consumer queue in System.Threading.Channels. Use it to hand off work from requests to an in-process background consumer with backpressure (bounded channels). It&#39;s in-memory only, so items are lost on restart; use durable queues for critical work.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Your app runs on 3 instances: how do you make sure a scheduled job runs only once?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Use a distributed lock or lease (SQL app lock, Redis, Blob lease), a job scheduler with clustering (Hangfire/Quartz), a dedicated single-instance worker, or a platform scheduler like a Functions timer trigger or Kubernetes CronJob.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why is fire-and-forget (Task.Run without await) in a controller a bad idea?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The work isn&#39;t tracked by the host (lost on shutdown), exceptions go unobserved, and it often uses request-scoped services (DbContext, HttpContext) that are disposed when the request ends. Use a queue plus a hosted service or a durable job system.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/background-work/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/host/hosted-services\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Background tasks with hosted services</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/extensions/workers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Worker services in .NET</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/extensions/channels\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">System.Threading.Channels</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.hangfire.io/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hangfire documentation</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/background-work/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (plain console app, .NET 8/9, top-level statements — <code>System.Threading.Channels</code> ships in the shared framework, no extra package needed).</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Threading</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Channels</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: a bounded Channel&lt;T&gt; gives real backpressure — once full, a write has to wait</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// (or fails immediately with TryWrite) instead of growing without limit.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> bounded = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Channel</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateBounded</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">BoundedChannelOptions</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">FullMode</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">BoundedChannelFullMode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Wait</span> });\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!bounded.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Writer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryWrite</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected first write to succeed&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!bounded.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Writer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryWrite</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected second write to succeed (capacity 2)&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (bounded.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Writer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryWrite</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected third TryWrite to fail — channel should be full&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: bounded Channel&lt;T&gt; rejects writes once full (real backpressure)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> writeTask = bounded.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Writer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsTask</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Delay</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">50</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (writeTask.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsCompleted</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: WriteAsync should still be waiting while the channel is full&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> bounded.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Reader</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadAsync</span>(); <span class=\"tk-com\">// frees one slot</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> writeTask; <span class=\"tk-com\">// now it can complete</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: a blocked WriteAsync on a full channel completes once the consumer reads (producer slows down to match the consumer)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: PeriodicTimer.WaitForNextTickAsync asynchronously waits for each tick.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> timer = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PeriodicTimer</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromMilliseconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> ticked = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> timer.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WaitForNextTickAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!ticked) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected a tick&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: PeriodicTimer.WaitForNextTickAsync delivers ticks without blocking a thread&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: retried jobs must be idempotent — a retry after a partial failure must not double-apply.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> appliedInvoiceIds = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HashSet</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> sideEffectCount = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ApplyInvoiceIdempotently</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> orderId)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!appliedInvoiceIds.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(orderId)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span>; <span class=\"tk-com\">// already applied: skip, this is what &quot;idempotent&quot; buys you</span>\n    sideEffectCount++;\n}\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">ApplyInvoiceIdempotently</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>); <span class=\"tk-com\">// first attempt</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">ApplyInvoiceIdempotently</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>); <span class=\"tk-com\">// retry after the caller never saw the first attempt's result</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (sideEffectCount != <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected exactly 1 side effect, got {sideEffectCount}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: an idempotent handler applies a retried job's effect exactly once&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here — these need a real multi-instance deployment or a real crash:</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//  - &quot;every app instance runs its own hosted services&quot; (needs more than one process)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//  - BackgroundServiceExceptionBehavior.StopHost shutting down the whole app (needs a real Generic Host)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//  - in-memory channel contents being lost on crash/restart</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// See Further reading: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/host/hosted-services</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"work-that-shouldnt-make-a-request-wait","text":"Work that shouldn't make a request wait","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"ihostedservice-and-backgroundservice-the-hosts-slot-for-long","text":"IHostedService and BackgroundService: the host's slot for long-running code","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"the-lifecycle-and-the-pitfalls-each-fact-in-it-quietly-creat","text":"The lifecycle, and the pitfalls each fact in it quietly creates","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"worker-services-the-same-host-deliberately-without-a-web-ser","text":"Worker services: the same host, deliberately without a web server","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"in-memory-queues-with-channel-handing-work-off-within-one-pr","text":"In-memory queues with Channel : handing work off within one process","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"durable-job-systems-hangfire-and-quartz-net","text":"Durable job systems: Hangfire and Quartz.NET","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"choosing-where-background-work-runs","text":"Choosing where background work runs","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"running-a-job-once-across-replicas","text":"Running a job once across replicas","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":3000,"minutes":14,"num":"3.11","part":"p3"},"api-design":{"id":"api-design","title":"API Design: REST, HTTP Semantics, Versioning and OpenAPI","summary":"Designing APIs clients love. HTTP methods and status codes in depth, safety and idempotency, resource and URL design, pagination, filtering and sorting, partial updates, idempotency keys, optimistic concurrency with ETags, long-running operations, versioning strategies, and OpenAPI documentation.","layer":"comms","where":"Service communication › Synchronous HTTP › API contract design","fits":"<p>This chapter is about the <strong>contract</strong> your layer-3 endpoints (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/building-apis\">Building APIs</a>) expose to the outside world, which belongs to layer 6: it&#39;s how services and clients communicate. Good HTTP semantics make caching (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors\">Caching</a>), retries (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/resilience\">Resilience</a>) and gateways work correctly.</p>\n","prereqs":["building-apis"],"next":["app-models","resilience","microservices"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/api-design/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>REST = resources (nouns in URLs) manipulated with standard HTTP verbs, stateless, cacheable.</li>\n<li><strong>Safe</strong> = doesn&#39;t change state (GET); <strong>idempotent</strong> = same effect no matter how many times you call it (GET, PUT, DELETE). This is what makes retries safe.</li>\n<li>Know the status codes: 200/201/202/204 for success, 400/401/403/404/409/412/422/429 for client problems, 500/502/503/504 for server/upstream problems.</li>\n<li>Page large lists with cursor/keyset pagination, not offset, once the table is big — offset gets slower the deeper you page.</li>\n<li>POST isn&#39;t idempotent by default; fix that for payments/writes with an <strong>idempotency key</strong>. Fix &quot;last write wins&quot; conflicts with <strong>ETags</strong> (optimistic concurrency).</li>\n<li>Version only when a change would break an existing client&#39;s assumptions; additive, optional changes usually don&#39;t need a new version.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"rest-in-one-page\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/api-design/rest-in-one-page\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>REST in one page <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><strong>REST</strong> (Representational State Transfer) is an architectural style for HTTP APIs, built around a small set of ideas:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Resources</strong> are the nouns, identified by URLs: <code>/orders/42</code>, <code>/customers/7/addresses</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Standard methods</strong> are the verbs: GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE.</li>\n<li><strong>Representations</strong> carry state: JSON (usually), negotiated with <code>Accept</code>/<code>Content-Type</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Stateless</strong>: each request carries everything needed (auth token, parameters); the server keeps no client session.</li>\n<li><strong>Cacheable</strong>: responses say whether and how long they can be cached.</li>\n<li><strong>Uniform interface</strong> and, strictly, <strong>HATEOAS</strong> (responses include links to next actions), which few real APIs implement fully.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>Richardson Maturity Model</strong> gives a way to measure how far along this spectrum an API actually sits: Level 0 (one endpoint, RPC over POST), Level 1 (resources), Level 2 (<strong>HTTP verbs + status codes</strong>, where most good APIs sit), Level 3 (hypermedia). Most of this chapter is about getting Level 2 genuinely right — which turns out to be harder than it sounds, because the HTTP verbs carry real semantic promises that are easy to accidentally break.</p>\n<h2 id=\"http-methods-the-promises-hiding-behind-safe-and-idempotent\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/api-design/http-methods-the-promises-hiding-behind-safe-and-idempotent\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>HTTP methods: the promises hiding behind &quot;safe&quot; and &quot;idempotent&quot; <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>Purpose</th>\n<th>Request body</th>\n<th>Safe</th>\n<th>Idempotent</th>\n<th>Typical success</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>GET</strong></td>\n<td>Read a resource/collection</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>✔</td>\n<td>✔</td>\n<td>200</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HEAD</strong></td>\n<td>GET without the body</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>✔</td>\n<td>✔</td>\n<td>200</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>POST</strong></td>\n<td>Create a child resource / trigger an action</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>✘</td>\n<td>✘</td>\n<td>201 + <code>Location</code>, or 202, 200</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>PUT</strong></td>\n<td>Replace a resource entirely (or create at a known URL)</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>✘</td>\n<td>✔</td>\n<td>200 / 204 (201 if created)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>PATCH</strong></td>\n<td>Partial update</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>✘</td>\n<td>✘ (can be made so)</td>\n<td>200 / 204</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>DELETE</strong></td>\n<td>Remove</td>\n<td>Usually no</td>\n<td>✘</td>\n<td>✔</td>\n<td>204 (or 404/204 on repeat)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>OPTIONS</strong></td>\n<td>Capabilities / CORS preflight</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>✔</td>\n<td>✔</td>\n<td>204</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Two columns in that table carry more weight than they look. <strong>Safe</strong> means the method doesn&#39;t change server state at all — which is exactly why browsers, crawlers, and CDN prefetchers feel free to call GET requests speculatively, without asking: nothing bad can happen if GET runs twice, or zero times, or in a different order than you expected. <strong>Idempotent</strong> is a related but distinct promise: making the same request <em>N</em> times has the same effect on the server as making it once — though the <em>response itself</em> may legitimately differ, since a second <code>DELETE</code> on an already-deleted resource might reasonably return 404 instead of 204. Here&#39;s why this distinction earns its own row in the table rather than being a footnote: idempotency is exactly what makes <strong>retries safe</strong>. A client, a proxy, or a resilience policy can automatically retry an idempotent request after a timeout without worrying about double effects — which is precisely why <code>POST</code>, being neither safe nor idempotent by default, needs the special handling covered later in this chapter under idempotency keys.</p>\n<h2 id=\"status-codes-you-must-know\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/api-design/status-codes-you-must-know\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Status codes you must know <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Code</th>\n<th>Name</th>\n<th>When</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>200</strong></td>\n<td>OK</td>\n<td>Successful GET/PUT/PATCH with a body</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>201</strong></td>\n<td>Created</td>\n<td>POST created something; include a <code>Location</code> header and usually the resource</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>202</strong></td>\n<td>Accepted</td>\n<td>Work queued for async processing; return a status URL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>204</strong></td>\n<td>No Content</td>\n<td>Success with no body (DELETE, PUT/PATCH without returning the resource)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>301/308</strong></td>\n<td>Moved permanently</td>\n<td>Resource URL changed (308 keeps the method)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>304</strong></td>\n<td>Not Modified</td>\n<td>Conditional GET: client&#39;s cached copy (ETag) is still valid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>400</strong></td>\n<td>Bad Request</td>\n<td>Malformed request or validation errors (ValidationProblemDetails)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>401</strong></td>\n<td>Unauthorized</td>\n<td><strong>Not authenticated</strong>: missing/invalid/expired token</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>403</strong></td>\n<td>Forbidden</td>\n<td>Authenticated but <strong>not allowed</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>404</strong></td>\n<td>Not Found</td>\n<td>Resource doesn&#39;t exist (or you&#39;re hiding it from this caller)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>405</strong></td>\n<td>Method Not Allowed</td>\n<td>e.g. DELETE on a read-only resource</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>406 / 415</strong></td>\n<td>Not Acceptable / Unsupported Media Type</td>\n<td>Content negotiation failures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>409</strong></td>\n<td>Conflict</td>\n<td>State conflict: duplicate, a business rule (&quot;already shipped&quot;), a concurrency clash</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>412</strong></td>\n<td>Precondition Failed</td>\n<td><code>If-Match</code> ETag didn&#39;t match (optimistic concurrency)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>422</strong></td>\n<td>Unprocessable Content</td>\n<td>Well-formed but semantically invalid (some teams use this for validation instead of 400)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>429</strong></td>\n<td>Too Many Requests</td>\n<td>Rate limited; include <code>Retry-After</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>500</strong></td>\n<td>Internal Server Error</td>\n<td>Unhandled server bug</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>502 / 503 / 504</strong></td>\n<td>Bad Gateway / Service Unavailable / Gateway Timeout</td>\n<td>Upstream failures, overload/maintenance (+ <code>Retry-After</code>), upstream timeout</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Return errors as <strong>ProblemDetails</strong> (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/errors-logging\">Errors &amp; logging</a>). Whichever you pick between 400 and 422 for validation, the important thing is picking <strong>one</strong> convention and applying it consistently across the whole API — a client building retry logic against your API needs to know, once, which code means &quot;don&#39;t bother retrying this without changing the request.&quot;</p>\n<h2 id=\"resource-and-url-design\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/api-design/resource-and-url-design\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Resource and URL design <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>✔ GET    /orders                      list (filter/sort/page with query params)\n✔ GET    /orders/42                   one order\n✔ POST   /orders                      create\n✔ PATCH  /orders/42                   partial update\n✔ DELETE /orders/42                   delete\n✔ GET    /customers/7/orders          sub-collection (one level of nesting)\n✔ POST   /orders/42/cancellation      an action modelled as a resource (&quot;create a cancellation&quot;)\n✔ POST   /orders/42:cancel            Google-style custom method (also acceptable)\n\n✘ GET    /getOrders?id=42             verbs in URLs\n✘ POST   /orders/42/delete            using POST for everything\n✘ GET    /customers/7/orders/42/lines/3/product   deep nesting: use /order-lines/3 or /products/{id}</code></pre></figure>\n<p>The last two &quot;wrong&quot; examples share a root cause worth naming: both are reaching for a verb-shaped URL or an ever-deepening path because the method vocabulary from the previous section felt too limited for the action in mind. The fix each time is the same: model the action itself as a resource — a cancellation you <code>POST</code> into existence — rather than smuggling a verb into the URL. Conventions worth adopting as defaults: <strong>plural nouns</strong>, <strong>lowercase kebab-case</strong> paths (<code>/order-lines</code>), <strong>camelCase</strong> JSON, IDs that are opaque to clients (GUIDs or numbers, never anything a client could infer meaning from), ISO 8601 UTC timestamps (<code>2026-10-27T14:00:00Z</code>), money as <code>{ &quot;amount&quot;: &quot;19.99&quot;, &quot;currency&quot;: &quot;EUR&quot; }</code> (decimal as a string or a minor-unit integer, never a floating-point number), and enums as strings rather than raw integers that mean nothing without the source code.</p>\n<h2 id=\"pagination-filtering-and-sorting-never-return-an-unbounded-l\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/api-design/pagination-filtering-and-sorting-never-return-an-unbounded-l\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Pagination, filtering and sorting: never return an unbounded list <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PageRequest</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Page</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PageSize</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Size</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Math</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Clamp</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">PageSize</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">100</span>);       <span class=\"tk-com\">// cap the page size server-side</span>\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PagedResult</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IReadOnlyList</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Items</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Page</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PageSize</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TotalCount</span>)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TotalPages</span> =&gt; (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>)<span class=\"tk-ty\">Math</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ceiling</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TotalCount</span> / (<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span>)<span class=\"tk-ty\">PageSize</span>);\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>There are two genuinely different strategies for paging through a collection, and the choice between them isn&#39;t cosmetic — it changes how the database query performs as the dataset grows:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Offset / page</th>\n<th>Cursor / keyset</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Request</td>\n<td><code>?page=3&amp;pageSize=20</code></td>\n<td><code>?limit=20&amp;after=eyJpZCI6MTA0Mn0</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SQL</td>\n<td><code>OFFSET 40 ROWS FETCH NEXT 20</code></td>\n<td><code>WHERE Id &gt; @lastId ORDER BY Id FETCH NEXT 20</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Jump to page N</td>\n<td>✔</td>\n<td>✘</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Performance on deep pages</td>\n<td>Degrades: the DB still scans skipped rows</td>\n<td>Constant: uses the index</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stable when data changes</td>\n<td>✘ Items shift between pages</td>\n<td>✔</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Use for</td>\n<td>Admin grids, small datasets</td>\n<td>Feeds, infinite scroll, large tables, sync APIs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Trace through why offset pagination degrades: <code>OFFSET 40</code> doesn&#39;t mean &quot;jump straight to row 41&quot; — the database still has to walk through and discard the first 40 rows before it can start returning anything, so page 1,000 of a million-row table does real, scanning work for every single request, no matter how good the index is. Keyset pagination sidesteps this entirely by asking a different question — &quot;give me rows after this specific key&quot; — which the index answers directly, in roughly constant time regardless of how deep you are.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/products&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (<span class=\"tk-attr\">[AsParameters]</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductQuery</span> q, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span> db, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> query = db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsNoTracking</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (q.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CategoryId</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> { } cat) query = query.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CategoryId</span> == cat);           <span class=\"tk-com\">// filtering</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsNullOrWhiteSpace</span>(q.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Search</span>)) query = query.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contains</span>(q.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Search</span>));\n    query = q.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sort</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">switch</span>                                                                   <span class=\"tk-com\">// whitelisted sorting</span>\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;price&quot;</span> =&gt; query.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderBy</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ThenBy</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>),\n        <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;-price&quot;</span> =&gt; query.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderByDescending</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ThenBy</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>),\n        _ =&gt; query.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderBy</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ThenBy</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>)                                  <span class=\"tk-com\">// deterministic tie-breaker</span>\n    };\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> total = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> query.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CountAsync</span>(ct);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> items = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> query.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Skip</span>((q.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Page</span> - <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>) * q.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Size</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Take</span>(q.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Size</span>)\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(p =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductDto</span>(p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>, p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span>)).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToListAsync</span>(ct);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TypedResults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PagedResult</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductDto</span>&gt;(items, q.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Page</span>, q.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Size</span>, total));\n});</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Never sort or filter by raw client strings</div><p>Building an <code>OrderBy</code> clause directly from an arbitrary field name a client sent — via dynamic LINQ or raw SQL string concatenation — opens the door to injection, and separately, to a client silently sorting by some unindexed column and making the query scan the entire table. <strong>Whitelist</strong> which fields are sortable and filterable (notice the <code>switch</code> above only recognises two literal strings, with everything else falling through to a safe default), cap page sizes server-side, and always add a unique tie-breaker (<code>ThenBy(Id)</code>) — without one, rows that tie on the sort column can appear in a different relative order on different pages, silently duplicating or skipping rows as a client pages through.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"updates-put-vs-patch\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/api-design/updates-put-vs-patch\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Updates: PUT vs PATCH <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>PUT</strong> replaces the whole representation. Omitted fields are reset, so clients must send everything — which is exactly what makes it idempotent: sending the same full representation twice leaves the resource in the same state both times.</li>\n<li><strong>PATCH</strong> changes only what&#39;s sent, and comes in two standard shapes:<ul>\n<li><strong>JSON Merge Patch</strong> (RFC 7396, <code>application/merge-patch+json</code>): send a partial object, <code>null</code> means remove. Simple and common.</li>\n<li><strong>JSON Patch</strong> (RFC 6902, <code>application/json-patch+json</code>): a list of operations (<code>[{ &quot;op&quot;: &quot;replace&quot;, &quot;path&quot;: &quot;/status&quot;, &quot;value&quot;: &quot;Paid&quot; }]</code>). Precise, supports array operations, more complex.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Many APIs sidestep generic PATCH for business operations entirely, preferring <strong>task-based endpoints</strong> instead (<code>POST /orders/42/cancellation</code>, <code>POST /orders/42/shipments</code>). The reason connects back to the URL-design section above: &quot;set status to Cancelled&quot; via PATCH describes a <em>data mutation</em>, while &quot;create a cancellation&quot; via POST describes a <em>business action</em> — and the second maps far more naturally onto how a domain model (see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ddd\">DDD</a>) actually thinks about what&#39;s happening.</p>\n<h2 id=\"idempotency-keys-for-post-making-the-one-unsafe-by-default-m\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/api-design/idempotency-keys-for-post-making-the-one-unsafe-by-default-m\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Idempotency keys for POST: making the one unsafe-by-default method safe to retry <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>POST isn&#39;t idempotent, and that has a sharp real-world consequence: a client that sends a payment request, times out waiting for a response, and retries — reasonably, since it has no way to know whether the first attempt succeeded — may end up <strong>charging the customer twice</strong>. The standard fix, used by Stripe and most serious payment APIs, is an <strong>idempotency key</strong>: a client-generated identifier that makes the <em>second</em> identical request a no-op rather than a second charge.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>HTTP</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>POST /payments\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Idempotency-Key</span>: 4f9d2c1e-8b6a-4c51-9a77-1c2e3d4f5a6b</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapPost</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/payments&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (<span class=\"tk-attr\">[FromHeader(Name = &quot;Idempotency-Key&quot;)]</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> key, <span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentRequest</span> req, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IIdempotencyStore</span> store, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPayments</span> payments, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> store.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGetAsync</span>(key, ct) <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> { } previous)\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Json</span>(previous.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Body</span>, statusCode: previous.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span>);          <span class=\"tk-com\">// replay the original response</span>\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> store.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryBeginAsync</span>(key, requestHash: req.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetHashCode</span>(), ct))     <span class=\"tk-com\">// unique constraint = one winner</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Conflict</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProblemDetails</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Title</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Request with this key is in progress&quot;</span> });\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> result = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> payments.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeAsync</span>(req, ct);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> store.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CompleteAsync</span>(key, <span class=\"tk-num\">201</span>, result, ct);                              <span class=\"tk-com\">// store response for ~24 h</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Created</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;/payments/{result.Id}&quot;</span>, result);\n});</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Three details in that code are each guarding against a specific failure mode: storing keys with a TTL stops the store growing forever; detecting reuse of the same key with a <em>different</em> payload (returning 422) catches a client bug where a key got reused for an unrelated request; and making the &quot;begin&quot; step atomic via a unique index — rather than a check-then-insert that two concurrent requests could both pass — is what actually guarantees only one charge happens even if the client somehow fires the retry before the first attempt has finished.</p>\n<h2 id=\"optimistic-concurrency-with-etags\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/api-design/optimistic-concurrency-with-etags\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Optimistic concurrency with ETags <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>A different concurrency problem: two <em>different</em> clients editing the same resource, where whichever one saves last silently overwrites the other&#39;s changes — a <strong>lost update</strong>. ETags solve this by making &quot;has this changed since I last read it?&quot; a question the server can answer cheaply:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>GET /orders/42             →  200, ETag: &quot;v7&quot;\nPUT /orders/42  If-Match: &quot;v7&quot;   → 200, ETag: &quot;v8&quot;          (nobody else changed it)\nPUT /orders/42  If-Match: &quot;v7&quot;   → 412 Precondition Failed   (someone saved v8 in between: reload and retry)</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Walk through what&#39;s actually happening: the client&#39;s <code>GET</code> captured the resource&#39;s version at that moment. Its later <code>PUT</code> says, in effect, &quot;apply this change, but only if the version is still what I last saw&quot; — and if someone else saved in between, the version has moved on to <code>v8</code>, the precondition fails, and the server refuses the write rather than silently clobbering the intervening change. This maps directly onto EF Core&#39;s <code>rowversion</code> concurrency token, covered in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-advanced\">EF Core advanced</a> — the ETag is really just that token, surfaced over HTTP. As a bonus, the same mechanism works for reads too: a <code>GET</code> with <code>If-None-Match: &quot;v8&quot;</code> returns a bodyless <strong>304 Not Modified</strong> when nothing has changed, saving bandwidth on every request where the client&#39;s cached copy is still current.</p>\n<h2 id=\"long-running-operations\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/api-design/long-running-operations\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Long-running operations <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Work that genuinely takes longer than a reasonable HTTP request — more than a few seconds — shouldn&#39;t hold the connection open waiting. The pattern mirrors the &quot;defer and poll&quot; idea from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/background-work/work-that-shouldnt-make-a-request-wait\">Background work</a>:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>POST /reports              → 202 Accepted\n                             Location: /reports/operations/abc123\n                             Retry-After: 5\nGET  /reports/operations/abc123  → 200 { &quot;status&quot;: &quot;running&quot;, &quot;progress&quot;: 40 }\nGET  /reports/operations/abc123  → 303 See Other  Location: /reports/987   (or 200 { status: &quot;succeeded&quot;, resultUrl })</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Where polling itself is too chatty or too slow to notice completion, the alternatives are: webhooks (the server calls back to a URL the client provided), SignalR or Server-Sent Events for genuinely live progress updates, or simply having the client poll a resource&#39;s own <code>status</code> field rather than a dedicated operation resource.</p>\n<h2 id=\"versioning-deciding-what-counts-as-a-promise-you-cant-break\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/api-design/versioning-deciding-what-counts-as-a-promise-you-cant-break\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Versioning: deciding what counts as a promise you can&#39;t break <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>The question to ask before anything else here: does this change break an existing client&#39;s reasonable assumptions? Removing or renaming a field or endpoint does. Changing a field&#39;s type or meaning does. Making a previously optional input required does. Changing an error code a client might be branching on does. <strong>Adding</strong> a new optional field or a new endpoint does <strong>not</strong> break anything — provided clients are built to ignore fields they don&#39;t recognise, which is worth stating explicitly in your API&#39;s own documentation so clients actually do it.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Strategy</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>Pros</th>\n<th>Cons</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>URL segment</strong></td>\n<td><code>/v2/orders</code></td>\n<td>Visible, easy to route, cache-friendly, most common</td>\n<td>URL changes per version</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Query string</td>\n<td><code>/orders?api-version=2.0</code></td>\n<td>Simple; Azure APIs use it</td>\n<td>Easy to forget; caching nuances</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Header</td>\n<td><code>Api-Version: 2</code></td>\n<td>Clean URLs</td>\n<td>Less discoverable; harder to test in a browser</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Media type</td>\n<td><code>Accept: application/vnd.shop.v2+json</code></td>\n<td>Most &quot;RESTful&quot;</td>\n<td>Complex for clients</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddApiVersioning</span>(o =&gt;\n{\n    o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DefaultApiVersion</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ApiVersion</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>);\n    o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AssumeDefaultVersionWhenUnspecified</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;\n    o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReportApiVersions</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;                               <span class=\"tk-com\">// api-supported-versions response header</span>\n    o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ApiVersionReader</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">UrlSegmentApiVersionReader</span>();\n}).<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddApiExplorer</span>(o =&gt; { o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GroupNameFormat</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;'v'VVV&quot;</span>; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SubstituteApiVersionInUrl</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>; });   <span class=\"tk-com\">// Asp.Versioning.Http</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> orders = app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NewVersionedApi</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Orders&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> v1 = orders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGroup</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/v{version:apiVersion}/orders&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasApiVersion</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1.0</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> v2 = orders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGroup</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/v{version:apiVersion}/orders&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasApiVersion</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2.0</span>);\nv1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/{id:int}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetOrderV1</span>);\nv2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/{id:int}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetOrderV2</span>);      <span class=\"tk-com\">// new response shape</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Whichever breaking change eventually forces a new version, have a <strong>deprecation policy</strong> ready before you need it: announce the deprecation, add <code>Deprecation</code>/<code>Sunset</code> response headers so clients (and their tooling) can detect it programmatically, monitor actual traffic per version so you know when it&#39;s genuinely safe to remove the old one, and only then remove it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"openapi-documentation\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/api-design/openapi-documentation\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>OpenAPI documentation <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><strong>OpenAPI</strong> (formerly Swagger) is a machine-readable description of your API — endpoints, parameters, schemas, responses, and auth requirements, all in one document. Once that description exists, a surprising amount becomes possible almost for free: interactive docs, client SDK generation (Kiota, NSwag), contract testing, gateway import (Azure API Management), and automated breaking-change detection in CI by diffing one version&#39;s spec against the next.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddOpenApi</span>(o =&gt;                       <span class=\"tk-com\">// Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi (.NET 9+), built in</span>\n{\n    o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddDocumentTransformer</span>((doc, ctx, ct) =&gt; { doc.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Info</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Title</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop API&quot;</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CompletedTask</span>; });\n});\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapOpenApi</span>();                                       <span class=\"tk-com\">// /openapi/v1.json</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsDevelopment</span>())\n    app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapScalarApiReference</span>();                        <span class=\"tk-com\">// Scalar UI (Scalar.AspNetCore); or Swagger UI via Swashbuckle</span>\n\norders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/{id:int}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetById</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithName</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;GetOrder&quot;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithSummary</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Get an order by id&quot;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithDescription</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Returns 404 if the order doesn't exist or isn't visible to the caller.&quot;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Produces</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderDto</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-num\">200</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProducesProblem</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">404</span>);                              <span class=\"tk-com\">// TypedResults usually make Produces* unnecessary</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>A few names worth recognising in this space: <strong>Swashbuckle</strong> (Swagger UI and generator) was the default before .NET 9 and remains widely used in existing codebases; <strong>NSwag</strong> is an alternative generator with its own client-generation story; XML doc comments and <code>TypedResults</code> both feed richer documents into the generated spec automatically, with no extra attributes needed. And one design choice worth naming explicitly: <strong>design-first</strong> (write the OpenAPI spec by hand, generate server stubs from it) versus <strong>code-first</strong> (generate the spec from the running code, as the example above does) — code-first is the more common approach in .NET, mostly because <code>TypedResults</code> already makes the code itself an accurate, type-checked source of truth for the contract.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What makes an API RESTful?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Resources identified by URLs, manipulated through standard HTTP methods with correct status codes, stateless requests, cacheable responses, and representations like JSON. Strictly, it also includes hypermedia (HATEOAS), which most practical &quot;REST&quot; APIs (maturity level 2) omit.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What do safe and idempotent mean? Which methods are which?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Safe methods don&#39;t change server state: GET, HEAD, OPTIONS. Idempotent methods have the same effect whether called once or many times: GET, HEAD, OPTIONS, PUT, DELETE. POST and PATCH aren&#39;t idempotent by default. Idempotency is what makes automatic retries safe.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>PUT vs PATCH vs POST?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>POST creates a subordinate resource or triggers an action (not idempotent; 201 with a Location header). PUT replaces the entire resource at a known URL (idempotent). PATCH applies a partial update via JSON Merge Patch or JSON Patch.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>401 vs 403?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>401 means the request isn&#39;t authenticated (no/invalid/expired credentials, and the response includes WWW-Authenticate). 403 means the caller is authenticated but not permitted to perform the action.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you make a POST endpoint safe to retry?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Require an Idempotency-Key header. Store the key atomically (unique constraint) along with the response, and on a repeat with the same key, return the stored response instead of re-executing. Reject reuse of the same key with a different payload.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Offset vs cursor pagination?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Offset (page/pageSize, OFFSET/FETCH) is simple and supports jumping to any page, but it slows down on deep pages and shifts when data changes. Cursor/keyset pagination (WHERE key &gt; lastKey) uses the index for constant performance and stable results, but can&#39;t jump to arbitrary pages.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you version an API, and what counts as a breaking change?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Common strategies are URL segment (/v2), query string, header or media type, e.g. with Asp.Versioning. Breaking changes include removing or renaming fields/endpoints, changing types or semantics, making optional inputs required, and changing status codes. Additive optional changes are non-breaking if clients tolerate unknown fields.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do ETags help with concurrency?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The server returns an ETag (version) with a resource. Clients send If-Match with updates, and if the resource changed meanwhile the server returns 412 Precondition Failed, preventing lost updates. If-None-Match on GET enables 304 Not Modified responses.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How would you design an endpoint that generates a large report?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Return 202 Accepted with a Location header pointing to an operation-status resource, process in the background (queue plus worker), let the client poll the status (with Retry-After) or receive a webhook/SignalR notification, and finally link to the result resource.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/api-design/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/microsoft/api-guidelines\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Microsoft REST API Guidelines</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/best-practices/api-design\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Azure Architecture Center: API design best practices</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/openapi/overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ASP.NET Core OpenAPI</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/dotnet/aspnet-api-versioning\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Asp.Versioning on GitHub</a></li>\n<li>Book: <em>RESTful Web APIs</em> (Richardson, Amundsen). Book: <em>API Design Patterns</em> (JJ Geewax).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/api-design/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (plain console app, .NET 8/9, no packages). It checks the claims that are actually checkable in isolated code: idempotency-key replay, offset-vs-keyset pagination cost shape, and ETag-style optimistic concurrency. Claims about HTTP method semantics, status codes and versioning are protocol conventions, not something a console program can &quot;run&quot; — see Further reading above for the specs they come from.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Collections</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Concurrent</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Idempotency key: second request with the same key replays the first response, doesn't recharge ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> store = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ConcurrentDictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Charge</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> key, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> payload)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (store.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGetValue</span>(key, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> previous)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> previous; <span class=\"tk-com\">// replay, no new charge</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> result = <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;charged:{payload}&quot;</span>;\n    store[key] = result;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> result;\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> k = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NewGuid</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> first = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Charge</span>(k, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;$10&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> second = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Charge</span>(k, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;$10&quot;</span>); <span class=\"tk-com\">// simulates a client retry after a timeout</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (first != second) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: idempotency key did not replay the original response&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (store.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> != <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: idempotency key allowed a second charge to be stored&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: idempotency key makes a repeated POST a no-op instead of a second charge&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Offset pagination does more work the deeper you page; keyset pagination doesn't ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> rows = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Enumerable</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Range</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">100_000</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToList</span>(); <span class=\"tk-com\">// stand-in for an indexed, sorted table</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OffsetScanCost</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> page, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> pageSize) =&gt; page * pageSize;                 <span class=\"tk-com\">// must walk &amp; discard every prior row</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">KeysetScanCost</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> pageSize) =&gt; pageSize;                                  <span class=\"tk-com\">// index seek straight to the key, every time</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">OffsetScanCost</span>(page: <span class=\"tk-num\">4000</span>, pageSize: <span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>) &lt;= <span class=\"tk-ty\">KeysetScanCost</span>(pageSize: <span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>))\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: offset pagination should cost more than keyset pagination on a deep page&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: offset pagination's cost grows with page depth; keyset pagination's doesn't&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- ETag / If-Match: a write with a stale version is rejected instead of silently overwriting ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> resource = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Body</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;original&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Version</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;v7&quot;</span> };\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>? <span class=\"tk-ty\">ApplyIfMatch</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> ifMatchVersion, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> currentVersion, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> newBody)\n    =&gt; ifMatchVersion == currentVersion ? newBody : <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>; <span class=\"tk-com\">// null = 412 Precondition Failed</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> ok = <span class=\"tk-ty\">ApplyIfMatch</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;v7&quot;</span>, resource.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Version</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;edited by A&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (ok <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: a matching If-Match version should be accepted&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// someone else saves in between, moving the version to v8 — our old &quot;v7&quot; token is now stale</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> stale = <span class=\"tk-ty\">ApplyIfMatch</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;v7&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;v8&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;edited by B, using a stale version&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (stale <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">not</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: a stale If-Match version should be rejected (412), not applied&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: a write with a stale ETag is rejected instead of clobbering a newer save&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here — see Further reading: Microsoft REST API Guidelines, Azure Architecture Center</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// (which status/verb to use, versioning strategy, OpenAPI tooling behavior are conventions, not runtime facts)</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"rest-in-one-page","text":"REST in one page","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"http-methods-the-promises-hiding-behind-safe-and-idempotent","text":"HTTP methods: the promises hiding behind \"safe\" and \"idempotent\"","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"status-codes-you-must-know","text":"Status codes you must know","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"resource-and-url-design","text":"Resource and URL design","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"pagination-filtering-and-sorting-never-return-an-unbounded-l","text":"Pagination, filtering and sorting: never return an unbounded list","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"updates-put-vs-patch","text":"Updates: PUT vs PATCH","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"idempotency-keys-for-post-making-the-one-unsafe-by-default-m","text":"Idempotency keys for POST: making the one unsafe-by-default method safe to retry","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"optimistic-concurrency-with-etags","text":"Optimistic concurrency with ETags","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"long-running-operations","text":"Long-running operations","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"versioning-deciding-what-counts-as-a-promise-you-cant-break","text":"Versioning: deciding what counts as a promise you can't break","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"openapi-documentation","text":"OpenAPI documentation","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":4107,"minutes":19,"num":"3.12","part":"p3"},"app-models":{"id":"app-models","title":"The Other App Models: gRPC, SignalR, MVC, Razor Pages and Blazor","summary":"ASP.NET Core is more than JSON APIs. What each app model is for and where it fits: gRPC for fast service-to-service RPC, SignalR for real-time push, MVC and Razor Pages for server-rendered HTML, Blazor for C# UIs, and how they all share the same host and pipeline.","layer":"framework","where":"ASP.NET Core › App models beyond REST","fits":"<p>All of these are <strong>endpoint types</strong> in the same layer-3 framework. They run on the same host (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting\">Hosting</a>), DI, middleware (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/middleware\">Middleware</a>) and routing (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/routing\">Routing</a>) as your minimal APIs. gRPC and SignalR are also service-communication choices in layer 6, alongside REST (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/api-design\">API design</a>) and messaging (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events\">Messaging</a>).</p>\n","prereqs":["aspnet-hosting","routing"],"next":["relational-fundamentals","messaging-events"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/app-models/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>ASP.NET Core isn&#39;t just JSON APIs: the same host/DI/middleware/routing plumbing also serves gRPC, SignalR, server-rendered HTML (MVC/Razor Pages), and Blazor — you can mix several in one app.</li>\n<li><strong>gRPC</strong>: contract-first (<code>.proto</code>), binary, HTTP/2, built-in streaming. Best for internal service-to-service calls, not browsers.</li>\n<li><strong>SignalR</strong>: lets the server push to clients first, instead of waiting for a request. Scaling past one server needs a backplane (Redis) or Azure SignalR Service.</li>\n<li><strong>MVC/Razor Pages</strong>: server-rendered HTML with real <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> posts; relies on cookie auth + antiforgery tokens instead of bearer tokens.</li>\n<li><strong>Blazor</strong>: UI written in C#, with a choice of where it runs — static HTML, on the server (via SignalR), in the browser (WebAssembly), or both.</li>\n<li>GraphQL, OData, raw WebSockets and Server-Sent Events cover narrower niches on top of the same host.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"one-framework-many-endpoint-types\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/app-models/one-framework-many-endpoint-types\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>One framework, many endpoint types <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Everything in the last several chapters — the host, DI, the middleware pipeline, routing — was described as the foundation <em>under</em> your API endpoints. It&#39;s worth being explicit about something that follows directly from that: minimal APIs and controllers are not the only thing that foundation supports. The exact same <code>WebApplication</code> can simultaneously serve JSON APIs, a high-performance internal RPC protocol, a live push channel to browsers, and fully server-rendered HTML pages — because all of them are just different <strong>endpoint types</strong> sitting on the same shared plumbing.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>                    ┌──────── same WebApplication: host, DI, config, logging, middleware ────────┐\nEndpoint type  ───▶ │ Minimal APIs │ Controllers │ gRPC services │ SignalR hubs │ Razor Pages │ Blazor │ Health checks │\nSpeaks         ───▶ │ JSON/HTTP    │ JSON/HTTP   │ Protobuf/HTTP2│ WebSockets…  │ HTML        │ HTML+WS│ JSON          │\nTypical client ───▶ │ SPA, mobile  │ SPA, mobile │ other services│ browsers,apps│ browsers    │browsers│ load balancer │</code></pre></figure>\n<p>You can genuinely mix these in one app: a REST API for the mobile client, a gRPC endpoint for internal service calls, a SignalR hub for live updates, and health checks, all registered in the same <code>Program.cs</code>. The question this chapter answers, model by model, is which one actually fits a given job:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Choose</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Public/partner API, SPAs, mobile apps</td>\n<td>REST/JSON (minimal APIs or controllers)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fast, strongly typed <strong>internal</strong> service-to-service calls, streaming</td>\n<td><strong>gRPC</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Server <strong>pushes</strong> to clients in real time (chat, notifications, live dashboards)</td>\n<td><strong>SignalR</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Server-rendered website with forms</td>\n<td><strong>Razor Pages</strong> or <strong>MVC</strong> with views</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rich interactive web UI written in C# instead of JavaScript</td>\n<td><strong>Blazor</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Flexible client-driven queries over a graph of data</td>\n<td>GraphQL (Hot Chocolate library)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"grpc-when-the-client-is-another-service-not-a-browser\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/app-models/grpc-when-the-client-is-another-service-not-a-browser\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>gRPC: when the client is another service, not a browser <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>gRPC</strong> is a high-performance RPC framework from Google. Its design rests on one assumption: both ends of the call are code you (or a cooperating team) control. Nobody needs to read the wire format by eye, and no browser needs to understand it directly. Because of that assumption, gRPC can afford to be <strong>contract-first</strong> — you define the service in <strong>Protocol Buffers</strong> (<code>.proto</code>), and both client and server code are generated from that contract. It also uses a <strong>binary</strong> wire format instead of text JSON (smaller, faster to parse), runs over <strong>HTTP/2</strong>, and treats streaming as a built-in feature instead of a workaround.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Protobuf</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Protos/inventory.proto</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">syntax</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;proto3&quot;</span>;\noption <span class=\"tk-ty\">csharp_namespace</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop.Inventory.Grpc&quot;</span>;\n\nservice Inventory {\n  rpc GetStock (StockRequest) returns (StockReply);                       <span class=\"tk-com\">// unary</span>\n  rpc WatchStock (StockRequest) returns (stream StockReply);              <span class=\"tk-com\">// server streaming</span>\n}\nmessage StockRequest { string <span class=\"tk-ty\">sku</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>; }\nmessage StockReply   { string <span class=\"tk-ty\">sku</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>; int32 <span class=\"tk-ty\">quantity</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>; }</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>XML</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>&lt;ItemGroup&gt;\n  &lt;Protobuf <span class=\"tk-ty\">Include</span>=<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Protos\\inventory.proto&quot;</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">GrpcServices</span>=<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Server&quot;</span> /&gt;   &lt;!-- <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Client&quot;</span> in the consumer project --&gt;\n&lt;/ItemGroup&gt;</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Server</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InventoryService</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IStockRepository</span> stock) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Inventory</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">InventoryBase</span>   <span class=\"tk-com\">// base class generated from .proto</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">StockReply</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetStock</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">StockRequest</span> req, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ServerCallContext</span> ctx) =&gt;\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>() { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span> = req.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Quantity</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> stock.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(req.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span>, ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span>) };\n}\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddGrpc</span>();\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGrpcService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">InventoryService</span>&gt;();\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Client (another service)</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddGrpcClient</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Inventory</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">InventoryClient</span>&gt;(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Address</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Uri</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://inventory&quot;</span>))\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddStandardResilienceHandler</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> reply = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> inventoryClient.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetStockAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">StockRequest</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;KB-01&quot;</span> }, cancellationToken: ct);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>REST/JSON</th>\n<th>gRPC</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Contract</td>\n<td>OpenAPI (often generated after the fact)</td>\n<td><code>.proto</code>, contract-first, strict</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Payload</td>\n<td>Text JSON (bigger, human-readable)</td>\n<td>Binary Protobuf (smaller, faster to parse)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transport</td>\n<td>HTTP/1.1 or 2</td>\n<td>HTTP/2 (required), HTTP/3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Streaming</td>\n<td>Limited (SSE, chunked)</td>\n<td>Client, server and bidirectional streaming</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Browser support</td>\n<td>Native</td>\n<td>Needs <strong>gRPC-Web</strong> or <strong>JSON transcoding</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tooling/debugging</td>\n<td>curl, browser, Postman</td>\n<td>Postman/grpcurl, less ubiquitous</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Best for</td>\n<td>Public APIs, browsers</td>\n<td>Internal microservice calls, low latency, polyglot systems</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Look at the &quot;Browser support&quot; row — it explains most of the real-world adoption pattern. Browsers can&#39;t speak the raw HTTP/2 trailers gRPC needs, so gRPC stays mostly internal (service-to-service), while REST stays the public-facing contract. One rule matters once a gRPC service is live: in <code>.proto</code>, the field <strong>numbers</strong> are the real contract, not the names. Never reuse or renumber a field number — always add new fields with new numbers. That&#39;s what lets an old client and a new client talk to the same service as it evolves. (If you ever need both REST and gRPC on the same service, <strong>gRPC JSON transcoding</strong> exposes one as the other.)</p>\n<h2 id=\"signalr-when-the-server-needs-to-speak-first\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/app-models/signalr-when-the-server-needs-to-speak-first\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>SignalR: when the server needs to speak first <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Every pattern so far in this book has the client going first — it sends a request, and a response comes back. <strong>SignalR</strong> is for the opposite case: the server learns something changed (an order shipped, a chat message arrived) before any client asked about it, and needs to <strong>push</strong> that update immediately, instead of making clients poll for it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Transports, negotiated automatically: <strong>WebSockets</strong> → Server-Sent Events → long polling — SignalR picks the best one the client and network actually support, falling back gracefully.</li>\n<li><strong>Hubs</strong>: server classes whose methods clients can call, and which can in turn call methods defined on the clients.</li>\n<li>Addressing: all clients, a specific connection, a <strong>user</strong> (meaning all of that user&#39;s connections, since they might have several tabs open), or <strong>groups</strong>.</li>\n<li>Clients: JavaScript/TypeScript, .NET, Java, Swift.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderClient</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusChanged</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> orderId, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> status); }      <span class=\"tk-com\">// strongly typed client methods</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-attr\">[Authorize]</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderHub</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Hub</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderClient</span>&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Follow</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> orderId) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Groups</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddToGroupAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Context</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ConnectionId</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;order:{orderId}&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OnConnectedAsync</span>() { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* Context.UserIdentifier is the user's id */</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OnConnectedAsync</span>(); }\n}\n\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSignalR</span>();\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapHub</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderHub</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/hubs/orders&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Push from anywhere (an endpoint, a message consumer, a background service)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShippingNotifier</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IHubContext</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderHub</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderClient</span>&gt; hub)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderShippedAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> orderId) =&gt; hub.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Clients</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Group</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;order:{orderId}&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusChanged</span>(orderId, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shipped&quot;</span>);\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>JavaScript</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>const <span class=\"tk-ty\">conn</span> = new signalR.HubConnectionBuilder().withUrl(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/hubs/orders&quot;</span>, { <span class=\"tk-ty\">accessTokenFactory</span>: () =&gt; token }).withAutomaticReconnect().build();\nconn.on(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;StatusChanged&quot;</span>, (id, status) =&gt; render(id, status));\nawait conn.start();\nawait conn.invoke(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Follow&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>);</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Notice that <code>ShippingNotifier</code> is <strong>not</strong> a hub — it&#39;s just an ordinary service with <code>IHubContext&lt;OrderHub, IOrderClient&gt;</code> injected into it. It can push a message from anywhere the real event happens: an endpoint, a message consumer, a background service. &quot;Something shipped&quot; doesn&#39;t have to originate inside a hub method at all.</p>\n<p><strong>Scaling to more than one server</strong> creates a real problem: say your code sends a message on server A, but the client&#39;s persistent WebSocket connection happens to be held open by server B. Each server only knows about its <em>own</em> connections — server A has no way to reach that client directly. Two fixes exist: a <strong>backplane</strong> (Redis, via <code>AddStackExchangeRedis()</code>) that relays messages between all server instances, or <strong>Azure SignalR Service</strong>, which holds every client connection centrally for you — usually the simpler choice once you&#39;re on Azure. If you self-host behind a load balancer instead, you also need <strong>sticky sessions</strong> for WebSockets (or you skip the negotiation step entirely), otherwise a reconnect can land on a different server than the one that originally set up the connection — and that server won&#39;t recognize it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"server-rendered-html-mvc-and-razor-pages\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/app-models/server-rendered-html-mvc-and-razor-pages\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Server-rendered HTML: MVC and Razor Pages <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Step away from APIs and real-time push entirely: sometimes the actual deliverable is a web page rendered as HTML, with forms a browser posts back to directly, no separate frontend framework involved. <strong>Razor</strong> is the templating syntax underneath both options here — C# mixed directly into HTML markup (<code>@Model.Name</code>, <code>@foreach</code>).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>MVC (Model-View-Controller)</th>\n<th>Razor Pages</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Organisation</td>\n<td>Controllers with actions return views; views in <code>Views/{Controller}/</code></td>\n<td>One page = <code>.cshtml</code> file + a <code>PageModel</code> class</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Routing</td>\n<td>Conventional <code>{controller}/{action}/{id?}</code> or attributes</td>\n<td>File-system based: <code>Pages/Orders/Edit.cshtml</code> → <code>/Orders/Edit</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Good for</td>\n<td>Large sites with shared controllers; teams used to MVC</td>\n<td>Page-focused apps: forms, admin tools, CRUD</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shared</td>\n<td>Tag helpers, layouts, partials, view components, model binding, validation, antiforgery</td>\n<td>same</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Razor Page: Pages/Products/Edit.cshtml.cs</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">EditModel</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span> db) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">PageModel</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[BindProperty]</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductForm</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Input</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>();\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IActionResult</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">OnGetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> p = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FindAsync</span>(id);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (p <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>();\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Input</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductForm</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> = p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> = p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> = p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> };\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Page</span>();\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IActionResult</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">OnPostAsync</span>()\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!<span class=\"tk-ty\">ModelState</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsValid</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Page</span>();          <span class=\"tk-com\">// redisplay with validation messages</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-com\">// ... save ...</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">RedirectToPage</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Index&quot;</span>);                  <span class=\"tk-com\">// Post-Redirect-Get pattern</span>\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice <code>OnPostAsync</code> redirects on success rather than returning a view directly — the Post-Redirect-Get pattern, which exists specifically so that refreshing the page after a successful save doesn&#39;t resubmit the form a second time. Because this model relies on the browser submitting real HTML forms rather than an API client attaching a bearer token, server-rendered apps use <strong>cookie auth</strong> by default and need <strong>antiforgery</strong> tokens on every form — added automatically by the form tag helpers. This defends against <strong>CSRF</strong> (cross-site request forgery: a malicious site tricking your browser into submitting a form to a real site where you&#39;re already logged in, using your cookies without your consent) — exactly the risk <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/auth/cookies-vs-bearer-tokens-the-tradeoff-behind-how-do-i-stay-l\">Auth</a> flags as cookies&#39; particular weak point.</p>\n<h2 id=\"blazor-c-instead-of-javascript-with-a-choice-of-where-it-act\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/app-models/blazor-c-instead-of-javascript-with-a-choice-of-where-it-act\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Blazor: C# instead of JavaScript, with a choice of where it actually runs <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Blazor</strong> builds interactive web UIs from <strong>Razor components</strong> written entirely in C# — no JavaScript framework needed. The one question that matters for understanding Blazor is: <em>where does that C# actually run — on the server, or in the browser?</em> Since .NET 8, you don&#39;t have to pick one answer for the whole app. A single unified model (&quot;Blazor Web App&quot;) lets you choose a <strong>render mode</strong> per component:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Render mode</th>\n<th>Where the code runs</th>\n<th>Trade-offs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Static SSR</strong></td>\n<td>Server renders HTML once; no interactivity (forms still work)</td>\n<td>Fastest, SEO-friendly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Interactive Server</strong></td>\n<td>Server; UI events and diffs sent over a <strong>SignalR</strong> connection</td>\n<td>Small download, full .NET on the server; needs a persistent connection; latency on every interaction; server memory per user</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Interactive WebAssembly</strong></td>\n<td>In the browser, on a .NET runtime compiled to <strong>WebAssembly</strong></td>\n<td>Works offline and scales like a SPA; larger initial download; calls APIs over HTTP</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Interactive Auto</strong></td>\n<td>Server first, switches to WebAssembly once downloaded</td>\n<td>Best of both, more complexity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Look at the &quot;Interactive Server&quot; row again: this is just SignalR (described above), used to ship every button click and keystroke to the server and stream the resulting UI change back. It isn&#39;t a separate real-time technology. That&#39;s also why Blazor Server inherits SignalR&#39;s scale-out problem the moment you run more than one server instance — it needs the same fix: a backplane or Azure SignalR Service.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>razor</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>@page <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/orders&quot;</span>\n@rendermode InteractiveServer\n@inject IOrderQueries Orders\n\n&lt;h1&gt;Orders&lt;/h1&gt;\n@if (_orders is null) { &lt;p&gt;Loading…&lt;/p&gt; }\nelse\n{\n    &lt;ul&gt;@foreach (var o in _orders) { &lt;li&gt;@<span class=\"tk-ty\">o.Id</span>: @o.Total.ToString(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;C&quot;</span>)&lt;/li&gt; }&lt;/ul&gt;\n}\n&lt;button @<span class=\"tk-ty\">onclick</span>=<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Refresh&quot;</span>&gt;Refresh&lt;/button&gt;\n\n@code {\n    private IReadOnlyList&lt;OrderSummary&gt;? _orders;\n    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync() =&gt; await Refresh();\n    private async Task Refresh() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">_orders</span> = await Orders.RecentAsync();\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<p>For a backend-focused interview, the useful depth here is knowing what Blazor is, how the render modes trade off against each other, and specifically that Blazor Server relies on SignalR rather than being its own independent real-time mechanism. <strong>.NET MAUI Blazor Hybrid</strong> reuses the same components inside native desktop and mobile apps, for teams wanting one UI codebase across web and native.</p>\n<h2 id=\"graphql-and-others-briefly\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/app-models/graphql-and-others-briefly\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>GraphQL and others, briefly <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>GraphQL</strong> (via <strong>Hot Chocolate</strong>): clients send queries describing exactly the fields they need across related data, served from a single endpoint. Great for complex front ends aggregating many sources; harder to cache over HTTP, needs query cost limits and has an N+1 risk (solved with DataLoaders).</li>\n<li><strong>OData</strong>: queryable REST conventions (<code>$filter</code>, <code>$select</code>, <code>$expand</code>) over <code>IQueryable</code>; common in enterprise and Microsoft ecosystems.</li>\n<li><strong>WebSockets directly</strong> (<code>app.UseWebSockets()</code>) for custom protocols.</li>\n<li><strong>Server-Sent Events</strong> (<code>TypedResults.ServerSentEvents</code> in .NET 10): simple one-way server-to-browser streams over plain HTTP.</li>\n<li><strong>Health checks</strong> (<code>MapHealthChecks</code>) are endpoints too. See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/observability-aspire\">Observability</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>When would you choose gRPC over REST?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>For internal service-to-service communication where performance (binary Protobuf, HTTP/2 multiplexing), strict contract-first schemas with generated clients, and streaming matter. REST/JSON remains better for public APIs and browsers (gRPC needs gRPC-Web or JSON transcoding there).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you evolve a gRPC contract without breaking clients?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Add new fields with new field numbers, never change or reuse existing field numbers or types, mark removed fields as reserved, and treat all fields as optional. Protobuf ignores unknown fields, so old and new clients interoperate.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is SignalR and how does it work?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A library for real-time bidirectional communication. Clients connect to a hub, and the transport is negotiated (WebSockets, then Server-Sent Events, then long polling). Server code calls client methods on all clients, users, groups or connections via the hub or IHubContext, and clients call hub methods.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you scale SignalR across multiple servers?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Use a backplane such as Redis so messages published on one server reach clients connected to others, or use Azure SignalR Service, which manages the client connections. Self-hosted WebSocket setups also need sticky sessions unless negotiation is skipped.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Razor Pages vs MVC?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Both render HTML with Razor on the server and share tag helpers, model binding and validation. MVC separates controllers and views and uses action-based routing; Razor Pages is page-centric, pairing each .cshtml with a PageModel and using file-based routing, which is simpler for form-centred apps.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are Blazor&#39;s render modes?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Static server-side rendering (no interactivity), Interactive Server (logic on the server, UI updates over SignalR), Interactive WebAssembly (the .NET runtime in the browser) and Interactive Auto (server first, then WebAssembly). Chosen per component or per app since .NET 8.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/app-models/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/grpc/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Overview for gRPC on .NET</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/signalr/introduction\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Overview of ASP.NET Core SignalR</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/razor-pages/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Introduction to Razor Pages</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/blazor/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ASP.NET Core Blazor</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://chillicream.com/docs/hotchocolate\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hot Chocolate (GraphQL)</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/app-models/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Most claims here (gRPC&#39;s transport, SignalR&#39;s scale-out, Blazor&#39;s render modes) need a real network/hosting environment and aren&#39;t something a console program can check. What <em>can</em> be verified locally, with no extra packages, is the actual mechanism behind two of this chapter&#39;s claims: that binary framing beats JSON on size, and that Protobuf-style field <strong>numbers</strong>, not names, are the real contract. Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (plain console app, .NET 8/9).</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Text</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Text</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Json</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: &quot;binary wire format instead of text JSON&quot; is smaller for the same data.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// This isn't a real Protobuf encoder, but a minimal tagged-binary encoding (type tag +</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// length-prefixed value) is a fair stand-in: binary framing skips field names, quotes and punctuation.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> sku = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;KB-01&quot;</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> quantity = <span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> json = <span class=\"tk-ty\">JsonSerializer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Serialize</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { sku, quantity });\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> jsonBytes = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Encoding</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UTF8</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetByteCount</span>(json);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> ms = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">MemoryStream</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> bw = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">BinaryWriter</span>(ms);\nbw.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Write</span>((<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>)<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>); bw.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Write</span>(sku);          <span class=\"tk-com\">// field 1: sku</span>\nbw.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Write</span>((<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>)<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>); bw.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Write</span>(quantity);     <span class=\"tk-com\">// field 2: quantity</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> binaryBytes = (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>)ms.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Length</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (binaryBytes &gt;= jsonBytes)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected binary ({binaryBytes}B) to be smaller than JSON ({jsonBytes}B)&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: binary framing ({binaryBytes}B) is smaller than JSON ({jsonBytes}B) for the same fields&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: in a Protobuf-style contract, field NUMBERS are the contract, not names or order.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Model this with a tiny tag-&gt;value dictionary (mirrors how Protobuf decodes by field number).</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Encode</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">params</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> tag, <span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>)[] fields) =&gt;\n    fields.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToDictionary</span>(f =&gt; f.tag, f =&gt; f.<span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> oldWire = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Encode</span>((<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;KB-01&quot;</span>), (<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>));                               <span class=\"tk-com\">// &quot;old&quot; server</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> newWire = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Encode</span>((<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;KB-01&quot;</span>), (<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>), (<span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;warehouse-7&quot;</span>));           <span class=\"tk-com\">// &quot;new&quot; server adds field 3</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OldClientReads</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>&gt; wire) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;{wire[1]}:{wire[2]}&quot;</span>; <span class=\"tk-com\">// old client only knows fields 1 and 2</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">OldClientReads</span>(oldWire) != <span class=\"tk-ty\">OldClientReads</span>(newWire))\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: old client reading by field number should be unaffected by a new field&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: adding a new field number doesn't break a client that only reads the old field numbers&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Now the failure mode the chapter warns against: REUSING a field number changes its meaning.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> corrupted = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Encode</span>((<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;KB-01&quot;</span>), (<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;NOT-A-QUANTITY-ANYMORE&quot;</span>)); <span class=\"tk-com\">// field 2 reused for something else</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (corrupted[<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>] <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">not</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected the reused field number to silently corrupt the old client's read&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: reusing a field number (as the chapter warns against) silently corrupts old readers&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here (needs a real network/hosting environment) — see Further reading:</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - gRPC transport, HTTP/2, streaming: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/grpc/</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - SignalR transport negotiation and scale-out backplane behavior: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/signalr/introduction</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - Razor Pages/MVC routing, Post-Redirect-Get: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/razor-pages/</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - Blazor render modes: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/blazor/</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All checkable claims verified.&quot;</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"one-framework-many-endpoint-types","text":"One framework, many endpoint types","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"grpc-when-the-client-is-another-service-not-a-browser","text":"gRPC: when the client is another service, not a browser","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"signalr-when-the-server-needs-to-speak-first","text":"SignalR: when the server needs to speak first","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"server-rendered-html-mvc-and-razor-pages","text":"Server-rendered HTML: MVC and Razor Pages","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"blazor-c-instead-of-javascript-with-a-choice-of-where-it-act","text":"Blazor: C# instead of JavaScript, with a choice of where it actually runs","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"graphql-and-others-briefly","text":"GraphQL and others, briefly","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":3087,"minutes":14,"num":"3.13","part":"p3"},"relational-fundamentals":{"id":"relational-fundamentals","title":"Relational Database Fundamentals for .NET Developers","summary":"What EF Core generates and why it's fast or slow. Tables, keys and relationships, normalisation, SQL you must be able to write, joins, indexes (clustered, non-clustered, covering, composite), execution plans, transactions and ACID, isolation levels and their anomalies, locking and deadlocks, and connection pooling.","layer":"data","where":"Data & state › Relational databases › SQL fundamentals","fits":"<p>This is the bottom of layer 5, the actual database underneath EF Core (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals\">EF Core fundamentals</a>) and Dapper (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ado-dapper\">ADO.NET &amp; Dapper</a>). In the cloud it&#39;s Azure SQL or PostgreSQL (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-data-messaging\">Azure data</a>). Most API performance problems trace back to this chapter.</p>\n","prereqs":["big-picture"],"next":["ado-dapper","efcore-fundamentals"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Tables are linked by <strong>keys</strong> (primary/foreign); normalise to avoid duplicate facts, denormalise only on purpose for read speed.</li>\n<li><strong>Indexes</strong> are what make queries fast: a B+tree that lets the engine <em>seek</em> instead of <em>scanning</em> every row. Composite index column order matters (leftmost-prefix rule).</li>\n<li><strong>Transactions</strong> give you ACID; <strong>isolation levels</strong> are a menu of which race-condition anomalies (dirty read, phantom, lost update, …) you&#39;re willing to risk in exchange for less blocking.</li>\n<li><strong>Deadlocks</strong> happen when two transactions each wait on a lock the other holds; the database kills one and your code should retry it.</li>\n<li><strong>Connection pooling</strong> reuses expensive-to-open connections; most &quot;pool exhausted&quot; errors trace back to code not disposing connections/DbContexts promptly.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-relational-model\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/the-relational-model\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The relational model <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>A relational database stores data in <strong>tables</strong> (relations) of <strong>rows</strong> with typed <strong>columns</strong>. Tables are linked by <strong>keys</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Primary key (PK)</strong></td>\n<td>Uniquely identifies a row; not null</td>\n<td><code>Orders.Id</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Foreign key (FK)</strong></td>\n<td>Column referencing another table&#39;s PK; enforces <strong>referential integrity</strong></td>\n<td><code>Orders.CustomerId → Customers.Id</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Unique constraint</strong></td>\n<td>No duplicates in a column set</td>\n<td><code>Customers.Email</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Check constraint</strong></td>\n<td>Row-level rule</td>\n<td><code>Quantity &gt; 0</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Natural vs surrogate key</strong></td>\n<td>Business value (email, ISBN) vs generated ID (<code>int IDENTITY</code>, <code>uniqueidentifier</code>)</td>\n<td>Prefer surrogate PKs plus unique constraints on natural keys</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"relationships\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/relationships\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Relationships</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Modelled as</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>One-to-many</td>\n<td>FK on the &quot;many&quot; side</td>\n<td>Customer 1 → * Orders (<code>Orders.CustomerId</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>One-to-one</td>\n<td>FK with a unique constraint (or shared PK)</td>\n<td>User 1 → 1 UserProfile</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Many-to-many</td>\n<td><strong>Join table</strong> with two FKs</td>\n<td>Products * ↔ * Categories via <code>ProductCategories(ProductId, CategoryId)</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>SQL</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">CREATE</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">TABLE</span> Customers (\n    Id        INT <span class=\"tk-kw\">IDENTITY</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">PRIMARY</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">KEY</span>,\n    Email     NVARCHAR(<span class=\"tk-num\">256</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">NOT</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">NULL</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">UNIQUE</span>,\n    Name      NVARCHAR(<span class=\"tk-num\">200</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">NOT</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">NULL</span>,\n    CreatedAt DATETIME2 <span class=\"tk-kw\">NOT</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">NULL</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">DEFAULT</span> SYSUTCDATETIME()\n);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">CREATE</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">TABLE</span> Orders (\n    Id         INT <span class=\"tk-kw\">IDENTITY</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">PRIMARY</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">KEY</span>,\n    CustomerId INT <span class=\"tk-kw\">NOT</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">NULL</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">REFERENCES</span> Customers(Id),\n    Status     TINYINT <span class=\"tk-kw\">NOT</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">NULL</span>,\n    Total      DECIMAL(<span class=\"tk-num\">18</span>,<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">NOT</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">NULL</span> CHECK (Total &gt;= <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>),\n    CreatedAt  DATETIME2 <span class=\"tk-kw\">NOT</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">NULL</span>,\n    RowVersion ROWVERSION                     <span class=\"tk-com\">-- optimistic concurrency token</span>\n);</code></pre></figure>\n<h2 id=\"normalisation-and-when-to-break-it\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/normalisation-and-when-to-break-it\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Normalisation (and when to break it) <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Normalisation</strong> removes redundancy so each fact is stored exactly once, which is the whole point: with one copy of a customer&#39;s name, changing it means one <code>UPDATE</code>. Without normalisation, the same fact duplicated across 500 order rows means an update anomaly waiting to happen — update 499 of them and miss one, and now the database contains a contradiction with no way to tell which copy is right.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Form</th>\n<th>Rule (simplified)</th>\n<th>Violation example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>1NF</strong></td>\n<td>Atomic values; no repeating groups</td>\n<td><code>Phones = &quot;123, 456&quot;</code> in one column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>2NF</strong></td>\n<td>1NF + every non-key column depends on the <strong>whole</strong> key</td>\n<td>In <code>OrderLines(OrderId, ProductId, ProductName)</code>, ProductName depends only on ProductId</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>3NF</strong></td>\n<td>2NF + no column depends on another <strong>non-key</strong> column</td>\n<td><code>Orders(CustomerId, CustomerCity)</code>: city depends on the customer, not the order</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Denormalisation</strong> is the deliberate, informed decision to reintroduce exactly that duplication, in exchange for read speed: storing <code>Order.Total</code> instead of recomputing a sum over its lines every time, or maintaining a dedicated read-model table for a dashboard. It&#39;s a reasonable trade specifically when reads vastly outnumber writes and you&#39;ve consciously decided how to keep the duplicate in sync — often via CQRS read models, see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cqrs-mediator-result\">CQRS</a>.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-tip\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Snapshot data is not duplication</div><p>An order line should store the <strong>price at the time of purchase</strong>, even though the product has a different, current price today. That&#39;s worth being precise about: it isn&#39;t redundancy at all, because it&#39;s recording a genuinely different fact — &quot;what the customer actually paid&quot; — not a stale copy of &quot;what the product costs.&quot;</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"sql-you-must-be-able-to-write\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/sql-you-must-be-able-to-write\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>SQL you must be able to write <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>SQL</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-com\">-- Filter, sort, page</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">SELECT</span> Id, Name, Price\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">FROM</span> Products\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">WHERE</span> CategoryId = <span class=\"tk-attr\">@categoryId</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">AND</span> Price <span class=\"tk-kw\">BETWEEN</span> <span class=\"tk-attr\">@min</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">AND</span> <span class=\"tk-attr\">@max</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">AND</span> Name <span class=\"tk-kw\">LIKE</span> <span class=\"tk-attr\">@search</span> + <span class=\"tk-str\">'%'</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">BY</span> Price <span class=\"tk-kw\">DESC</span>, Id\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">OFFSET</span> <span class=\"tk-attr\">@skip</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">ROWS</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">FETCH</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">NEXT</span> <span class=\"tk-attr\">@take</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">ROWS</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">ONLY</span>;            <span class=\"tk-com\">-- SQL Server; Postgres: LIMIT @take OFFSET @skip</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">-- Aggregate with grouping and a filter on the aggregate</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">SELECT</span> c.Country, <span class=\"tk-kw\">COUNT</span>(*) <span class=\"tk-kw\">AS</span> Orders, <span class=\"tk-kw\">SUM</span>(o.Total) <span class=\"tk-kw\">AS</span> Revenue\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">FROM</span> Orders o\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">JOIN</span> Customers c <span class=\"tk-kw\">ON</span> c.Id = o.CustomerId\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">WHERE</span> o.CreatedAt &gt;= <span class=\"tk-str\">'2026-01-01'</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">BY</span> c.Country\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">HAVING</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">SUM</span>(o.Total) &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">10000</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">BY</span> Revenue <span class=\"tk-kw\">DESC</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">-- Customers with NO orders (anti-join)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">SELECT</span> c.* <span class=\"tk-kw\">FROM</span> Customers c\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">WHERE</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">NOT</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">EXISTS</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">FROM</span> Orders o <span class=\"tk-kw\">WHERE</span> o.CustomerId = c.Id);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">-- Top order per customer (window function)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">SELECT</span> * <span class=\"tk-kw\">FROM</span> (\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">SELECT</span> o.*, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION <span class=\"tk-kw\">BY</span> CustomerId <span class=\"tk-kw\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">BY</span> Total <span class=\"tk-kw\">DESC</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">AS</span> rn\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">FROM</span> Orders o\n) x <span class=\"tk-kw\">WHERE</span> rn = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">-- Upsert</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">MERGE</span> Stock <span class=\"tk-kw\">AS</span> t\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">USING</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">VALUES</span> (<span class=\"tk-attr\">@sku</span>, <span class=\"tk-attr\">@qty</span>)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">AS</span> s(Sku, Qty) <span class=\"tk-kw\">ON</span> t.Sku = s.Sku\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">WHEN</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">MATCHED</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">THEN</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">UPDATE</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">SET</span> Quantity = t.Quantity + s.Qty\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">WHEN</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">NOT</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">MATCHED</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">THEN</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">INSERT</span> (Sku, Quantity) <span class=\"tk-kw\">VALUES</span> (s.Sku, s.Qty);\n<span class=\"tk-com\">-- Postgres: INSERT … ON CONFLICT (Sku) DO UPDATE SET …</span></code></pre></figure>\n<p>Also know: <code>UNION</code> vs <code>UNION ALL</code> (the latter skips the duplicate-removal sort), CTEs (<code>WITH x AS (…)</code>), <code>CASE WHEN</code>, <code>COALESCE</code>, <code>IN</code> vs <code>EXISTS</code>, and <code>DISTINCT</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"joins\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/joins\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Joins <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Join</th>\n<th>Returns</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>INNER JOIN</code></td>\n<td>Only rows with a match on both sides</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>LEFT [OUTER] JOIN</code></td>\n<td>All left rows; right columns NULL when no match</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>RIGHT JOIN</code></td>\n<td>All right rows (rare; flip to LEFT)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>FULL OUTER JOIN</code></td>\n<td>All rows from both, matched where possible</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>CROSS JOIN</code></td>\n<td>Every combination (Cartesian product)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Self join</td>\n<td>A table joined to itself (employee → manager)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Customers            Orders               INNER JOIN on CustomerId   LEFT JOIN Customers → Orders\nId Name              Id CustomerId        Ana  #1                    Ana  #1\n1  Ana               1  1                 Ana  #2                    Ana  #2\n2  Bo                2  1                                            Bo   NULL   ← Bo has no orders</code></pre></figure>\n<h2 id=\"indexes-the-single-most-important-performance-tool-in-this-c\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/indexes-the-single-most-important-performance-tool-in-this-c\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Indexes: the single most important performance tool in this chapter <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Here&#39;s the problem an index solves, stated plainly: without one, finding a matching row means a <strong>table scan</strong> — the database reading every single row, one by one, to check if it matches. On a table with a few hundred rows that&#39;s instant and nobody notices. On a table with fifty million rows, it&#39;s the difference between a 2-millisecond query and a 4-second one, and it&#39;s the single most common cause of &quot;why is this endpoint slow&quot; tickets in real production systems. An index is a sorted <strong>B+tree</strong> structure built specifically so the database can <strong>seek</strong> directly to matching rows in O(log n) time instead of scanning everything.</p>\n<h3 id=\"clustered-vs-non-clustered-sql-server\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/clustered-vs-non-clustered-sql-server\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Clustered vs non-clustered (SQL Server)</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Clustered index</th>\n<th>Non-clustered index</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>What it is</td>\n<td><strong>The table itself</strong>, stored in key order (leaf pages contain the full rows)</td>\n<td>A separate B-tree of key columns + a pointer (the clustered key) to the row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How many</td>\n<td><strong>One</strong> per table</td>\n<td>Many (up to 999)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Default</td>\n<td>The primary key</td>\n<td>Created explicitly (and for unique constraints)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lookup cost</td>\n<td>Seek straight to the row</td>\n<td>Seek in the index, then a <strong>key lookup</strong> into the clustered index per row (unless covering)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>SQL</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-com\">-- Composite index for: WHERE CustomerId = @c AND CreatedAt &gt;= @from ORDER BY CreatedAt DESC</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">CREATE</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">INDEX</span> IX_Orders_Customer_Created <span class=\"tk-kw\">ON</span> Orders (CustomerId, CreatedAt <span class=\"tk-kw\">DESC</span>)\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">INCLUDE</span> (Status, Total);          <span class=\"tk-com\">-- INCLUDE makes it COVERING: no key lookups for these columns</span></code></pre></figure>\n<h3 id=\"index-design-rules\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/index-design-rules\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Index design rules</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Index columns used in <strong>WHERE, JOIN, ORDER BY and GROUP BY</strong> on large tables. <strong>Foreign keys</strong> usually need an index (EF Core creates them).</li>\n<li><strong>Column order matters in composite indexes</strong>: an index on <code>(CustomerId, CreatedAt)</code> serves <code>WHERE CustomerId = …</code> and <code>WHERE CustomerId = … AND CreatedAt &gt; …</code>, but <strong>not</strong> <code>WHERE CreatedAt &gt; …</code> alone (leftmost-prefix rule). Put equality columns first, then range/sort columns.</li>\n<li><strong>Covering indexes</strong> (<code>INCLUDE</code>) avoid lookups for hot queries.</li>\n<li><strong>Selectivity</strong>: indexes help when they filter to a small fraction of rows. An index on a boolean <code>IsDeleted</code> alone rarely helps (consider a <strong>filtered index</strong>: <code>WHERE IsDeleted = 0</code>).</li>\n<li>Indexes <strong>cost writes and storage</strong>: every INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE maintains every index. Don&#39;t index everything.</li>\n<li><strong>Sargability</strong>: the predicate must let the engine use the index.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Rule 2&#39;s leftmost-prefix rule isn&#39;t an arbitrary restriction — it falls directly out of how a B-tree is physically sorted. An index on <code>(CustomerId, CreatedAt)</code> stores rows sorted first by <code>CustomerId</code>, and <em>within</em> each <code>CustomerId</code>, by <code>CreatedAt</code>. Think of a phone book sorted by last name, then first name. You can jump straight to &quot;Smith&quot; (equality on the leading column), and from there to &quot;Smith, John&quot; (a range on the trailing column). But there&#39;s no way to jump straight to &quot;everyone named John&quot; without checking every last name, because the book was never sorted that way. Same with the index: query by <code>CreatedAt</code> alone, with no <code>CustomerId</code> filter, and the index can&#39;t help — the engine falls back to a scan.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>SQL</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">WHERE</span> YEAR(CreatedAt) = <span class=\"tk-num\">2026</span>                          <span class=\"tk-com\">-- ✘ function on the column → scan</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">WHERE</span> CreatedAt &gt;= <span class=\"tk-str\">'2026-01-01'</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">AND</span> CreatedAt &lt; <span class=\"tk-str\">'2027-01-01'</span>   <span class=\"tk-com\">-- ✔ range → seek</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">WHERE</span> Name <span class=\"tk-kw\">LIKE</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">'%board'</span>                               <span class=\"tk-com\">-- ✘ leading wildcard → scan</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">WHERE</span> Name <span class=\"tk-kw\">LIKE</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">'Key%'</span>                                 <span class=\"tk-com\">-- ✔ seek</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">WHERE</span> CAST(Sku <span class=\"tk-kw\">AS</span> INT) = <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>  /  <span class=\"tk-kw\">WHERE</span> NVarcharCol = <span class=\"tk-attr\">@varcharParam</span>   <span class=\"tk-com\">-- ✘ implicit conversions can kill seeks</span></code></pre></figure>\n<p>Notice the shared pattern across all three &quot;✘&quot; lines: each one wraps the indexed column in a function, a leading wildcard, or an implicit conversion before comparing it. The engine can&#39;t use a sorted structure to answer &quot;what does <code>YEAR(CreatedAt)</code> equal&quot; without first computing <code>YEAR(CreatedAt)</code> for every row — which is a scan by another name. Rewrite the predicate so the <em>raw column</em> is what&#39;s being compared, as the range-based version does, and the seek comes back.</p>\n<h3 id=\"clustered-key-choice-and-guids\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/clustered-key-choice-and-guids\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Clustered key choice and GUIDs</h3>\n<p>A clustered key should be <strong>narrow, unique, static and ever-increasing</strong> — and that last property deserves a concrete reason, not just a rule to memorise. Since the clustered index <em>is</em> the physical row order, every insert has to land in the correct sorted position. An ever-increasing key (like an identity column) always inserts at the <em>end</em> of the structure — cheap. A random <code>Guid.NewGuid()</code> key inserts at a random position somewhere in the <em>middle</em> of an already-full B-tree, forcing a <strong>page split</strong> to make room, which both fragments the structure and costs far more than an append. Options: <code>INT/BIGINT IDENTITY</code>; <strong>sequential GUIDs</strong> (<code>NEWSEQUENTIALID()</code>, EF Core&#39;s default GUID value generator for SQL Server, or <strong><code>Guid.CreateVersion7()</code></strong> in .NET 9, which is time-ordered); or a GUID PK kept as a non-clustered key, with a separate clustered identity column doing the physical ordering instead.</p>\n<h2 id=\"execution-plans-watching-the-database-explain-its-own-decisi\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/execution-plans-watching-the-database-explain-its-own-decisi\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Execution plans: watching the database explain its own decisions <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>The <strong>query optimiser</strong> decides <em>how</em> to execute a piece of SQL — which indexes to use, which join algorithm, in which order — based on <strong>statistics</strong> it keeps about how the data is actually distributed. When a query is slow, the execution plan is where you go to see exactly what it decided and why:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Operator</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Usually</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Index Seek</strong></td>\n<td>Navigate the B-tree to matching rows</td>\n<td>✔ Good</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Index/Table Scan</strong></td>\n<td>Read everything</td>\n<td>✘ on big tables (fine on small ones)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Key Lookup</strong></td>\n<td>Fetch extra columns from the clustered index per row</td>\n<td>⚠ Many lookups → add INCLUDE columns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Nested Loops / Hash Match / Merge Join</strong></td>\n<td>Join algorithms</td>\n<td>Depends on row counts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sort</strong></td>\n<td>Explicit sorting</td>\n<td>⚠ Can spill to tempdb; an index may provide order</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Implicit conversion warning</strong></td>\n<td>Type mismatch</td>\n<td>✘ Fix parameter types</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Tools: SSMS / Azure Data Studio &quot;Include Actual Execution Plan&quot;, <code>SET STATISTICS IO, TIME ON</code>, <strong>Query Store</strong> (tracks regressions over time), Azure SQL <strong>Query Performance Insight</strong> and automatic index recommendations, Postgres <code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Parameter sniffing</strong> is a subtler trap. SQL Server compiles and caches a query plan the <em>first</em> time it sees that query, built around whatever parameter values happened to be passed in on that first call. Say a query for &quot;a customer&#39;s orders&quot; first runs for a customer with exactly one order — the cached plan gets optimised for tiny result sets. That same plan is then reused, unchanged, the next time the query runs for a customer with a million orders, where it performs terribly. Mitigations: <code>OPTION (RECOMPILE)</code> for that specific query, <code>OPTIMIZE FOR</code>, better indexes, Query Store plan forcing, or Parameter Sensitive Plan optimisation (SQL Server 2022 / Azure SQL).</p>\n<h2 id=\"transactions-and-acid-the-unit-the-database-actually-guarant\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/transactions-and-acid-the-unit-the-database-actually-guarant\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Transactions and ACID: the unit the database actually guarantees <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>A <strong>transaction</strong> groups several statements into one all-or-nothing unit — and the four letters of ACID are really four separate promises the database is making about that unit:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Property</th>\n<th>Guarantee</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Atomicity</strong></td>\n<td>All statements succeed, or none take effect (rollback)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Consistency</strong></td>\n<td>Constraints hold before and after</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Isolation</strong></td>\n<td>Concurrent transactions don&#39;t see each other&#39;s partial work (to a configurable degree)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Durability</strong></td>\n<td>Once committed, it survives crashes (write-ahead log)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>SQL</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">BEGIN</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">TRANSACTION</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">UPDATE</span> Accounts <span class=\"tk-kw\">SET</span> Balance = Balance - <span class=\"tk-attr\">@amount</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">WHERE</span> Id = <span class=\"tk-attr\">@from</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">AND</span> Balance &gt;= <span class=\"tk-attr\">@amount</span>;\n    IF @<span class=\"tk-attr\">@ROWCOUNT</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">BEGIN</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">ROLLBACK</span>; THROW <span class=\"tk-num\">50001</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">'Insufficient funds'</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">END</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">UPDATE</span> Accounts <span class=\"tk-kw\">SET</span> Balance = Balance + <span class=\"tk-attr\">@amount</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">WHERE</span> Id = <span class=\"tk-attr\">@to</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">INSERT</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">INTO</span> Transfers (FromId, ToId, Amount) <span class=\"tk-kw\">VALUES</span> (<span class=\"tk-attr\">@from</span>, <span class=\"tk-attr\">@to</span>, <span class=\"tk-attr\">@amount</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">COMMIT</span>;</code></pre></figure>\n<p>That worked example earns its place in the chapter because it shows <em>why</em> atomicity matters, not just what it means: without the transaction wrapping all three statements, a crash between the debit and the credit would leave money subtracted from one account and never added to the other — a real, silent loss. In EF Core, a single <code>SaveChanges()</code> call is already wrapped in one transaction automatically; explicit transactions are for when you need to span multiple <code>SaveChanges()</code> calls, or mix EF Core with raw SQL. See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-advanced\">EF Core advanced</a>.</p>\n<p>Keep transactions <strong>short</strong>, as a hard rule: never wait on an HTTP call or user input while a transaction is open, because every lock it holds stays held until commit — and the next section covers exactly how that turns into a very different kind of problem.</p>\n<h2 id=\"isolation-levels-and-the-anomalies-each-one-actually-prevent\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/isolation-levels-and-the-anomalies-each-one-actually-prevent\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Isolation levels and the anomalies each one actually prevents <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Run two transactions at the same time against overlapping data, and several specific, nameable things can go wrong depending on how strictly the database isolates them from each other:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Anomaly</th>\n<th>What happens</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Dirty read</strong></td>\n<td>Reading another transaction&#39;s <strong>uncommitted</strong> changes (which may roll back)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Non-repeatable read</strong></td>\n<td>Reading the same row twice in a transaction gives different values (someone updated and committed in between)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Phantom read</strong></td>\n<td>Re-running a range query returns new rows (someone inserted)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lost update</strong></td>\n<td>Two transactions read, modify and write the same row; one overwrites the other</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Write skew</strong></td>\n<td>Two transactions read overlapping data and make disjoint writes that together break a rule (two doctors both go off call)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Each isolation level is really a statement about which of these anomalies the database is willing to prevent, at the cost of more blocking:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Isolation level</th>\n<th>Dirty</th>\n<th>Non-repeatable</th>\n<th>Phantom</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Read Uncommitted (<code>NOLOCK</code>)</td>\n<td>✘ possible</td>\n<td>possible</td>\n<td>possible</td>\n<td>Almost never correct in business code</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Read Committed</strong></td>\n<td>prevented</td>\n<td>possible</td>\n<td>possible</td>\n<td><strong>SQL Server default</strong> (locking); <strong>Postgres default</strong> (MVCC)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Read Committed Snapshot (RCSI)</strong></td>\n<td>prevented</td>\n<td>possible</td>\n<td>possible</td>\n<td>Readers see the last committed version: <strong>readers don&#39;t block writers</strong>. Default in <strong>Azure SQL</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Repeatable Read</td>\n<td>prevented</td>\n<td>prevented</td>\n<td>possible (SQL Server)</td>\n<td>Holds shared locks until commit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Snapshot</strong></td>\n<td>prevented</td>\n<td>prevented</td>\n<td>prevented</td>\n<td>Transaction-consistent view via row versioning; update conflicts raise errors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Serializable</strong></td>\n<td>prevented</td>\n<td>prevented</td>\n<td>prevented</td>\n<td>As if transactions ran one at a time; range locks; most blocking</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">WITH (NOLOCK) is not a performance fix</div><p>It&#39;s tempting to reach for <code>NOLOCK</code> the moment a query feels slow, but look at what it actually gives up: Read Uncommitted can return data that was never actually committed, <strong>skip rows entirely, or read the same row twice</strong> if a page happens to split while the scan is running — none of which is a safe trade for speed in business code. RCSI or Snapshot isolation give you non-blocking reads <strong>without</strong> any of those correctness risks, which is almost always the fix people were actually reaching for.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"locking-blocking-and-deadlocks\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/locking-blocking-and-deadlocks\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Locking, blocking and deadlocks <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Under locking-based isolation, a writer takes an <strong>exclusive lock</strong> on the rows it&#39;s touching, and a reader takes a <strong>shared lock</strong> (at row, page, or table granularity, escalating as needed). <strong>Blocking</strong> is simply one transaction waiting for another&#39;s lock to release. A <strong>deadlock</strong> is the specific, unresolvable case where two transactions each hold a lock the <em>other</em> one needs — meaning neither can ever proceed, no matter how long either one waits:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Tx A: UPDATE Accounts WHERE Id = 1   (locks row 1)  →  UPDATE Accounts WHERE Id = 2  (waits for B)\nTx B: UPDATE Accounts WHERE Id = 2   (locks row 2)  →  UPDATE Accounts WHERE Id = 1  (waits for A)   ⇒ DEADLOCK</code></pre></figure>\n<p><img src=\"visuals/deadlock-cycle.svg\" alt=\"Transaction A locks row 1 and waits on row 2; transaction B locks row 2 and waits on row 1 — a cycle the database detects and breaks by killing one transaction as the deadlock victim\"></p>\n<p>The database can&#39;t wait this out — there&#39;s no amount of patience that resolves a genuine cycle — so it actively detects the cycle and kills one of the two transactions outright (the <strong>deadlock victim</strong>, surfacing to your code as SQL Server error <strong>1205</strong>). That victim transaction should always be <strong>retried</strong>, because the deadlock was a timing accident, not a real problem with the data.</p>\n<p>Prevention strategies, each one attacking a different part of how the cycle forms: access tables and rows in a <strong>consistent order</strong> across your codebase (lowest ID first, say), so two transactions can never form the crossed pattern shown above; keep transactions short, so the window where a lock is held is small; add indexes so updates lock fewer rows (a scan, by contrast, locks far more); use RCSI so readers never contribute to the blocking in the first place; and <strong>retry</strong> on deadlock and transient errors automatically (EF Core&#39;s <code>EnableRetryOnFailure</code>).</p>\n<p><strong>Pessimistic locking</strong>, when you genuinely need it (&quot;reserve this seat&quot; — where you cannot allow even a brief race): <code>SELECT … WITH (UPDLOCK, ROWLOCK)</code> in SQL Server or <code>SELECT … FOR UPDATE</code> in Postgres, inside a short transaction. Most web apps, though, prefer <strong>optimistic concurrency</strong> (version columns, the ETag mechanism from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/api-design/optimistic-concurrency-with-etags\">API design</a>) instead, because it avoids holding locks across the round trip to the client entirely.</p>\n<h2 id=\"connection-pooling-the-cost-hiding-behind-every-open-call\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/connection-pooling-the-cost-hiding-behind-every-open-call\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Connection pooling: the cost hiding behind every Open() call <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Opening a physical database connection — a TCP handshake, TLS negotiation, authentication — is expensive, in exactly the same way opening a fresh HTTP connection per request was expensive in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/http-client/what-an-httpclient-actually-is-underneath\">Calling other services</a>. ADO.NET providers solve it the same way: a <strong>pool</strong> of already-open connections, kept per unique connection string, where <code>Open()</code> just borrows one and <code>Dispose()</code>/<code>Close()</code> returns it rather than actually tearing it down.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Default max pool size is <strong>100</strong> (SqlClient). Under load, if connections aren&#39;t returned quickly enough, you get <em>&quot;Timeout expired. The timeout period elapsed prior to obtaining a connection from the pool&quot;</em>.</li>\n<li>Causes: not disposing connections or DbContexts, long transactions, slow queries, <strong>sync-over-async</strong> holding a connection open while a thread blocks on something that should have been awaited, or a sudden spike of parallel work exceeding the pool&#39;s capacity.</li>\n<li>The operating discipline that avoids all of this: don&#39;t open connections early or hold them open across unrelated work. Open late, close early.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Server=tcp:shop.database.windows.net;Database=shop;Authentication=Active Directory Default;\nEncrypt=True;Max Pool Size=200;Connect Timeout=15;Application Name=shop-api</code></pre></figure>\n<h2 id=\"sql-server-vs-postgresql-in-net-shops\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/sql-server-vs-postgresql-in-net-shops\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>SQL Server vs PostgreSQL in .NET shops <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>SQL Server / Azure SQL</th>\n<th>PostgreSQL / Azure Database for PostgreSQL</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Driver</td>\n<td><code>Microsoft.Data.SqlClient</code></td>\n<td><code>Npgsql</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>EF Core provider</td>\n<td><code>Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer</code></td>\n<td><code>Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Concurrency model</td>\n<td>Locking (plus optional row versioning, RCSI)</td>\n<td>MVCC by default</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Identity</td>\n<td><code>IDENTITY</code>, sequences</td>\n<td><code>GENERATED … AS IDENTITY</code>, sequences</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Notable features</td>\n<td>Columnstore, temporal tables, Query Store, Always On</td>\n<td>JSONB, arrays, rich extensions (PostGIS, pgvector), <code>ON CONFLICT</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Licence</td>\n<td>Commercial (free Express/Developer editions)</td>\n<td>Open source</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Both are excellent, genuinely mature databases. EF Core abstracts away most of the differences for everyday CRUD, but raw SQL and the fine details of performance tuning — the exact default isolation level, how each one physically organises a clustered table — do differ, which is why this chapter calls out SQL Server&#39;s locking model specifically rather than treating &quot;the database&quot; as one interchangeable thing.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What&#39;s the difference between a clustered and a non-clustered index?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A clustered index defines the physical order of the table: its leaf level is the data rows, and there can be only one (usually the PK). A non-clustered index is a separate B-tree of key columns pointing to rows via the clustered key, so non-covered columns need key lookups; there can be many. INCLUDE columns make a non-clustered index covering.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you decide which columns to index?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Columns in frequent WHERE, JOIN, ORDER BY and GROUP BY clauses of important queries on large tables, especially foreign keys. Order composite indexes with equality columns first and range/sort columns after, add INCLUDE for covering, prefer selective columns, and balance against write overhead. Verify with execution plans and Query Store.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What does sargable mean?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A predicate the engine can satisfy with an index seek. Wrapping the indexed column in a function, leading wildcards and implicit type conversions make predicates non-sargable, forcing scans.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Explain ACID.</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Atomicity (all or nothing), Consistency (constraints preserved), Isolation (concurrent transactions don&#39;t interfere beyond the chosen level) and Durability (committed data survives failures, via the transaction log).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are isolation levels and which anomalies do they prevent?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Read Uncommitted allows dirty reads. Read Committed prevents dirty reads (the default). Repeatable Read also prevents non-repeatable reads. Serializable prevents phantoms too. Snapshot/RCSI use row versioning so readers see committed versions without blocking writers. Higher isolation means more correctness but more blocking or conflicts.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is a deadlock and how do you handle it?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Two transactions each wait for a lock held by the other. The database kills one (SQL error 1205). Prevent it by accessing resources in a consistent order, keeping transactions short, indexing so fewer rows are locked, and using row versioning. Handle it by retrying the victim transaction.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why are random GUIDs bad clustered keys?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Inserts land at random positions in the B-tree, causing page splits, fragmentation and poor cache use; they&#39;re also 16 bytes, which widens every non-clustered index. Use sequential IDs (IDENTITY, NEWSEQUENTIALID, UUIDv7 via Guid.CreateVersion7) or keep GUIDs as non-clustered PKs.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is connection pooling and what causes pool exhaustion?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Reusing open physical connections per connection string so Open is cheap. Exhaustion (timeouts obtaining a connection) comes from connections not being disposed, long-running transactions or queries, sync-over-async blocking while holding connections, or more concurrent work than the pool size.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is parameter sniffing?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>SQL Server compiles and caches a plan using the first parameter values it sees. If those are unrepresentative, later executions reuse a suboptimal plan. Fixes include OPTION(RECOMPILE), OPTIMIZE FOR, better indexing, Query Store plan forcing, or PSP optimisation.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/relational-databases/sql-server-index-design-guide\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SQL Server index architecture and design guide</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/t-sql/statements/set-transaction-isolation-level-transact-sql\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Transaction isolation levels</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://use-the-index-luke.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Use the Index, Luke</a>: the best free guide to SQL indexing, for developers.</li>\n<li>Book: <em>Designing Data-Intensive Applications</em> by Martin Kleppmann (chapters 2, 3 and 7).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>This chapter is mostly about a real SQL engine&#39;s internals (B-trees, locking, the query optimiser), which a plain console app can&#39;t observe directly — trust <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/further-reading\">Further reading</a> for those, especially the isolation-level table and parameter sniffing. What the code below <em>can</em> genuinely check is the <strong>logic</strong> behind two claims: the INNER/LEFT JOIN results shown earlier, and the leftmost-prefix rule (why an index on <code>(CustomerId, CreatedAt)</code> can&#39;t serve a <code>CreatedAt</code>-only query). Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (plain console app, .NET 8/9, no extra packages).</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> customers = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>[] { (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Ana&quot;</span>), (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Bo&quot;</span>) };\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> orders = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>[] { (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>), (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>) };\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: INNER JOIN only returns customers that have a matching order.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> inner = customers.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Join</span>(orders, c =&gt; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>, (c, o) =&gt; (c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>, o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (inner.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span>() != <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span> || inner.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Any</span>(x =&gt; x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Bo&quot;</span>))\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: INNER JOIN should return only Ana's 2 orders, never Bo&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: INNER JOIN returns only rows with a match on both sides&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: LEFT JOIN keeps every customer, with NULL order columns when there's no match.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> left = customers.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GroupJoin</span>(orders, c =&gt; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>,\n    (c, os) =&gt; (c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderIds</span>: os.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(o =&gt; (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>?)o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">DefaultIfEmpty</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToList</span>()));\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> bo = left.<span class=\"tk-ty\">First</span>(x =&gt; x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Bo&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (bo.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderIds</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> != <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> || bo.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderIds</span>[<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>] != <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: LEFT JOIN should keep Bo with a NULL order&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: LEFT JOIN keeps unmatched rows (Bo) with NULL on the right side&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: a composite index on (CustomerId, CreatedAt) sorts by CustomerId first, then CreatedAt —</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// so it can serve &quot;WHERE CustomerId = x&quot; but is useless for &quot;WHERE CreatedAt &gt; y&quot; alone.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> rows = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>[]\n{\n    (<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreatedAt</span>: <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTime</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2026</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>)),\n    (<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreatedAt</span>: <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTime</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2026</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>)),\n    (<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreatedAt</span>: <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTime</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2026</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>)),\n    (<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreatedAt</span>: <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTime</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2026</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>)),\n};\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> indexOrder = rows.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderBy</span>(r =&gt; r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ThenBy</span>(r =&gt; r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreatedAt</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToArray</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> sortedByCreatedAtAlone = indexOrder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Zip</span>(indexOrder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Skip</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>), (a, b) =&gt; a.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreatedAt</span> &lt;= b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreatedAt</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">All</span>(x =&gt; x);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (sortedByCreatedAtAlone)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected the composite order to NOT be a plain CreatedAt order&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: sorting by (CustomerId, CreatedAt) does not give a usable CreatedAt-only order (leftmost-prefix rule holds)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: ACID atomicity — a transfer either fully happens or leaves no partial trace.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> accounts = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; { [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-num\">100</span>, [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;B&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-num\">50</span> };\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TryTransfer</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> from, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> to, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> amount)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (accounts[from] &lt; amount) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;          <span class=\"tk-com\">// insufficient funds: no partial write</span>\n    accounts[from] -= amount;\n    accounts[to] += amount;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> before = accounts[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A&quot;</span>] + accounts[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;B&quot;</span>];\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryTransfer</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;B&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1000</span>))\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: an overdrawing transfer should have been rejected atomically&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (accounts[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A&quot;</span>] + accounts[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;B&quot;</span>] != before)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: a failed transfer must leave total balance unchanged (no partial effect)&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: a failed transfer leaves no partial effect — total balance unchanged (atomicity)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here — see Further reading:</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - real index seeks vs. scans, execution plans, parameter sniffing (needs an actual query optimiser)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - locking, blocking and deadlock detection (needs a real multi-connection database engine)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - connection pool exhaustion and its default size of 100 (SqlClient-specific behaviour)</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-relational-model","text":"The relational model","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"relationships","text":"Relationships","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"normalisation-and-when-to-break-it","text":"Normalisation (and when to break it)","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"sql-you-must-be-able-to-write","text":"SQL you must be able to write","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"joins","text":"Joins","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"indexes-the-single-most-important-performance-tool-in-this-c","text":"Indexes: the single most important performance tool in this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"clustered-vs-non-clustered-sql-server","text":"Clustered vs non-clustered (SQL Server)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"index-design-rules","text":"Index design rules","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"clustered-key-choice-and-guids","text":"Clustered key choice and GUIDs","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"execution-plans-watching-the-database-explain-its-own-decisi","text":"Execution plans: watching the database explain its own decisions","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"transactions-and-acid-the-unit-the-database-actually-guarant","text":"Transactions and ACID: the unit the database actually guarantees","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"isolation-levels-and-the-anomalies-each-one-actually-prevent","text":"Isolation levels and the anomalies each one actually prevents","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"locking-blocking-and-deadlocks","text":"Locking, blocking and deadlocks","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"connection-pooling-the-cost-hiding-behind-every-open-call","text":"Connection pooling: the cost hiding behind every Open() call","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"sql-server-vs-postgresql-in-net-shops","text":"SQL Server vs PostgreSQL in .NET shops","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":4539,"minutes":21,"num":"4.1","part":"p4"},"ado-dapper":{"id":"ado-dapper","title":"ADO.NET and Dapper: Working Close to SQL","summary":"The data-access layer everything else is built on. ADO.NET's connection, command, reader and parameter model, async and disposal, transactions, then Dapper, the micro-ORM: queries, multi-mapping, multiple result sets, stored procedures and bulk patterns, plus when to choose Dapper over EF Core.","layer":"data","where":"Data & state › Relational databases › ADO.NET & micro-ORMs","fits":"<p>ADO.NET is the <strong>foundation</strong> under both EF Core (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals\">EF Core fundamentals</a>) and Dapper: every query either of them runs goes through a <code>DbConnection</code> and a <code>DbCommand</code>. Dapper is a thin layer that suits CQRS read sides (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cqrs-mediator-result\">CQRS</a>) and performance-critical queries.</p>\n","prereqs":["relational-fundamentals","async-await"],"next":["efcore-fundamentals","cqrs-mediator-result"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ado-dapper/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>ADO.NET</strong> (<code>DbConnection</code>, <code>DbCommand</code>, <code>DbDataReader</code>, <code>DbParameter</code>) is the low-level API every .NET data-access tool — EF Core, Dapper, hand-rolled code — is eventually built on.</li>\n<li>Always parameterize SQL: it stops SQL injection and lets the database reuse cached query plans.</li>\n<li><strong>Dapper</strong> is a thin layer over ADO.NET: you write the SQL, Dapper maps the rows to objects. No change tracking, no migrations, near-raw performance.</li>\n<li>For bulk inserts, row-by-row <code>INSERT</code>s are too slow — use <code>SqlBulkCopy</code>/<code>COPY</code>, table-valued parameters, or set-based updates instead.</li>\n<li>A common pattern: EF Core for writes (business rules, change tracking, migrations), Dapper or raw SQL for fast, flat reads.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-layer-every-other-data-access-tool-is-secretly-built-on\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ado-dapper/the-layer-every-other-data-access-tool-is-secretly-built-on\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The layer every other data-access tool is secretly built on <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Whichever way you query a .NET database — EF Core&#39;s LINQ, Dapper&#39;s SQL strings, or a hand-rolled reader loop — every single one of them eventually bottoms out in the same four objects. <strong>ADO.NET</strong> (<code>System.Data</code>) is that provider-agnostic, low-level database API, and each database ships a <strong>provider</strong> implementing it:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Abstract class</th>\n<th>SQL Server (<code>Microsoft.Data.SqlClient</code>)</th>\n<th>PostgreSQL (<code>Npgsql</code>)</th>\n<th>SQLite</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>DbConnection</code></td>\n<td><code>SqlConnection</code></td>\n<td><code>NpgsqlConnection</code></td>\n<td><code>SqliteConnection</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>DbCommand</code></td>\n<td><code>SqlCommand</code></td>\n<td><code>NpgsqlCommand</code></td>\n<td><code>SqliteCommand</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>DbDataReader</code></td>\n<td><code>SqlDataReader</code></td>\n<td><code>NpgsqlDataReader</code></td>\n<td><code>SqliteDataReader</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>DbParameter</code></td>\n<td><code>SqlParameter</code></td>\n<td><code>NpgsqlParameter</code></td>\n<td><code>SqliteParameter</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>DbTransaction</code></td>\n<td><code>SqlTransaction</code></td>\n<td><code>NpgsqlTransaction</code></td>\n<td>…</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>DbDataSource</code> (.NET 7)</td>\n<td><code>SqlDataSource</code>*</td>\n<td><code>NpgsqlDataSource</code></td>\n<td>…</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>*Use <code>Microsoft.Data.SqlClient</code>, not the legacy <code>System.Data.SqlClient</code>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> conn = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlConnection</span>(connectionString);          <span class=\"tk-com\">// borrows from the pool on Open</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OpenAsync</span>(ct);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> cmd = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlCommand</span>(\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;SELECT Id, Name, Price FROM Products WHERE CategoryId = @cat AND Price &lt;= @max ORDER BY Name&quot;</span>, conn);\ncmd.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parameters</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlParameter</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;@cat&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlDbType</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Int</span>) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span> = categoryId });\ncmd.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parameters</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlParameter</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;@max&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlDbType</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Decimal</span>) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Precision</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">18</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Scale</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span> = maxPrice });\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> reader = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> cmd.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteReaderAsync</span>(ct);           <span class=\"tk-com\">// forward-only, streaming cursor</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> products = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductDto</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">while</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> reader.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadAsync</span>(ct))\n{\n    products.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductDto</span>(\n        reader.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetInt32</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>),\n        reader.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetString</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>),\n        reader.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetDecimal</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>)));\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>Returns</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>ExecuteReaderAsync</code></td>\n<td><code>DbDataReader</code></td>\n<td>Row sets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ExecuteScalarAsync</code></td>\n<td>First column of first row</td>\n<td><code>COUNT(*)</code>, <code>SCOPE_IDENTITY()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ExecuteNonQueryAsync</code></td>\n<td>Rows affected</td>\n<td>INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Always parameterise</div><figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> sql = <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Email = '{email}'&quot;</span>;   <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✘ SQL injection: email = &quot;' OR 1=1 --&quot;</span>\ncmd.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parameters</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddWithValue</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;@email&quot;</span>, email);              <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✔ (prefer typed Add over AddWithValue to avoid type-inference issues)</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Parameters also let the database <strong>reuse cached plans</strong>, exactly the mechanism <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/execution-plans-watching-the-database-explain-its-own-decisi\">Relational fundamentals</a> covered — concatenated literals compile a brand-new plan for every distinct value, throwing that caching away entirely on top of the injection risk.</p>\n</aside>\n<h3 id=\"dataset-and-datatable\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ado-dapper/dataset-and-datatable\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>DataSet and DataTable</h3>\n<p>The old disconnected in-memory model (<code>DataSet</code>, <code>DataTable</code>, <code>DataAdapter</code>) is still around in legacy code and for bulk-copy staging. Modern apps map to POCOs with readers, Dapper or EF Core.</p>\n<h2 id=\"transactions-in-ado-net\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ado-dapper/transactions-in-ado-net\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Transactions in ADO.NET <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> conn = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlConnection</span>(cs);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OpenAsync</span>(ct);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> tx = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BeginTransactionAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsolationLevel</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadCommitted</span>, ct);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> debit = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlCommand</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;UPDATE Accounts SET Balance -= @a WHERE Id = @id AND Balance &gt;= @a&quot;</span>, conn, (<span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlTransaction</span>)tx))\n    {\n        debit.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parameters</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddWithValue</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;@a&quot;</span>, amount); debit.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parameters</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddWithValue</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;@id&quot;</span>, fromId);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> debit.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteNonQueryAsync</span>(ct) == <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InsufficientFundsException</span>();\n    }\n    <span class=\"tk-com\">// ... credit, insert transfer row (each command enlisted with tx) ...</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> tx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CommitAsync</span>(ct);\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> tx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RollbackAsync</span>(ct);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span>;\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><code>TransactionScope</code> (System.Transactions) gives ambient transactions spanning multiple connections — convenient, but it silently <strong>escalates to a distributed transaction (MSDTC)</strong> the moment more than one connection or resource manager is involved, and MSDTC is unavailable or unwanted in most cloud setups. If you use it with async code, pass <code>new TransactionScope(TransactionScopeAsyncFlowOption.Enabled)</code>, or the ambient transaction won&#39;t flow across the <code>await</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"bulk-operations-when-row-by-row-stops-being-viable\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ado-dapper/bulk-operations-when-row-by-row-stops-being-viable\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Bulk operations: when row-by-row stops being viable <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Inserting 100,000 rows one <code>INSERT</code> at a time takes minutes, because each one pays its own network round trip and transaction-log write. The options that avoid that cost each trade something for speed:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Approach</th>\n<th>Speed</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>SqlBulkCopy</code> (SQL Server) / <code>COPY</code> binary import (Npgsql)</td>\n<td><strong>Fastest</strong></td>\n<td>Streams rows into a table; great for imports and staging tables</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Table-valued parameters (SQL Server)</td>\n<td>Fast</td>\n<td>Pass a set of rows to a stored proc / MERGE</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Batched multi-row INSERTs</td>\n<td>Good</td>\n<td>EF Core batches automatically on SaveChanges</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>EF Core <code>ExecuteUpdate</code>/<code>ExecuteDelete</code></td>\n<td>Fast for set-based updates</td>\n<td>No entity loading</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Libraries (EFCore.BulkExtensions, linq2db)</td>\n<td>Fast</td>\n<td>Commercial licences for some</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> bulk = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlBulkCopy</span>(conn) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">DestinationTableName</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;StagingPrices&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">BatchSize</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">10_000</span> };\nbulk.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ColumnMappings</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Sku&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Sku&quot;</span>);\nbulk.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ColumnMappings</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Price&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Price&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> bulk.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteToServerAsync</span>(dataReaderOrDataTable, ct);\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// then a set-based MERGE from staging into the real table</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h2 id=\"dapper-the-micro-orm-that-stays-out-of-your-way\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ado-dapper/dapper-the-micro-orm-that-stays-out-of-your-way\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Dapper: the micro-ORM that stays out of your way <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Dapper</strong> (by Stack Overflow) is, at its core, just a set of extension methods on <code>IDbConnection</code>. The division of labour it offers is deliberately minimal: <strong>you write the SQL; Dapper maps the resulting rows to objects</strong> (and your objects to parameters), with performance close enough to hand-written ADO.NET that the difference rarely matters. There&#39;s no change tracking, no LINQ translation layer, and no migrations — Dapper simply isn&#39;t trying to be a full ORM.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> conn = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlConnection</span>(cs);      <span class=\"tk-com\">// Dapper opens/closes it if it's closed</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Query → IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;; anonymous object → parameters</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> products = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">QueryAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductDto</span>&gt;(\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;SELECT Id, Name, Price FROM Products WHERE CategoryId = @CategoryId ORDER BY Name&quot;</span>,\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">CategoryId</span> = categoryId });\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> one = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">QuerySingleOrDefaultAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductDto</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;SELECT Id, Name, Price FROM Products WHERE Id = @id&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { id });\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> count = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteScalarAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Orders WHERE CustomerId = @customerId&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { customerId });\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> affected = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteAsync</span>(\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;UPDATE Products SET Price = @Price WHERE Id = @Id&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">19.99m</span> });\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// IN lists are expanded automatically</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> some = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">QueryAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductDto</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;SELECT * FROM Products WHERE Id IN @ids&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { ids = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>[] { <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span> } });\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Pass a CancellationToken via CommandDefinition</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> rows = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">QueryAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderRow</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CommandDefinition</span>(sql, args, cancellationToken: ct));</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The mapping itself works by matching <strong>column name to property or constructor parameter name</strong>, case-insensitively — no attributes, no configuration. Records with positional constructors work out of the box; where a column name doesn&#39;t match a property name, a SQL alias (<code>AS CustomerName</code>) closes the gap.</p>\n<h2 id=\"dapper-advanced-usage\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ado-dapper/dapper-advanced-usage\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Dapper: advanced usage <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<h3 id=\"multi-mapping-turning-a-join-back-into-an-object-graph\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ado-dapper/multi-mapping-turning-a-join-back-into-an-object-graph\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Multi-mapping: turning a join back into an object graph</h3>\n<p>The one thing Dapper&#39;s simplicity doesn&#39;t give you for free is what EF Core&#39;s navigation properties handle automatically — reconstructing a nested object from a flat, joined row. Multi-mapping is Dapper&#39;s answer:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">const</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> sql = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    SELECT o.Id, o.Total, o.CreatedAt, c.Id, c.Name, c.Email\n    FROM Orders o JOIN Customers c ON c.Id = o.CustomerId\n    WHERE o.CreatedAt &gt;= @since\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> orders = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">QueryAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderView</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerView</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderView</span>&gt;(\n    sql,\n    (order, customer) =&gt; order <span class=\"tk-kw\">with</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span> = customer },\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { since },\n    splitOn: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Id&quot;</span>);          <span class=\"tk-com\">// where the second object's columns start (the second &quot;Id&quot;)</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><code>splitOn</code> is doing the real work: the query returns one flat row with both an order&#39;s <code>Id</code> and a customer&#39;s <code>Id</code>, so Dapper has to be told explicitly where the first object&#39;s columns end and the second one&#39;s begin — a row is just a sequence of columns, with no structural hint of its own.</p>\n<h3 id=\"multiple-result-sets-in-one-round-trip\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ado-dapper/multiple-result-sets-in-one-round-trip\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Multiple result sets in one round trip</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> multi = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">QueryMultipleAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    SELECT * FROM Orders WHERE Id = @id;\n    SELECT * FROM OrderLines WHERE OrderId = @id;\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { id });\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> order = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> multi.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadSingleAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderDetail</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> lines = (<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> multi.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderLineDto</span>&gt;()).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToList</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>This is worth reaching for specifically when an &quot;order with its lines&quot; screen would otherwise need two separate round trips: here, both statements travel over the wire together, and the single open connection reads each result set in turn.</p>\n<h3 id=\"stored-procedures-output-parameters-transactions\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ado-dapper/stored-procedures-output-parameters-transactions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Stored procedures, output parameters, transactions</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> p = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DynamicParameters</span>();\np.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;@CustomerId&quot;</span>, customerId);\np.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;@NewOrderId&quot;</span>, dbType: <span class=\"tk-ty\">DbType</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Int32</span>, direction: <span class=\"tk-ty\">ParameterDirection</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Output</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;dbo.CreateOrder&quot;</span>, p, commandType: <span class=\"tk-ty\">CommandType</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StoredProcedure</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> newId = p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Get</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;@NewOrderId&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> tx = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BeginTransactionAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteAsync</span>(sql1, args1, tx);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteAsync</span>(sql2, args2, tx);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> tx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CommitAsync</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h3 id=\"type-handlers\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ado-dapper/type-handlers\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Type handlers</h3>\n<p>Map custom types (strongly typed IDs, JSON columns, <code>DateOnly</code> on older providers) with <code>SqlMapper.AddTypeHandler(new OrderIdHandler())</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"registering-connections-in-di\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ado-dapper/registering-connections-in-di\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Registering connections in DI <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Modern: DbDataSource (Npgsql) — a singleton factory for pooled connections</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddNpgsqlDataSource</span>(builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configuration</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetConnectionString</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop&quot;</span>)!);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductQueries</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">NpgsqlDataSource</span> db)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IEnumerable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductDto</span>&gt;&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">ListAsync</span>() { <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> c = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OpenConnectionAsync</span>(); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">QueryAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductDto</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;…&quot;</span>); }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// SQL Server: a small factory abstraction keeps code testable</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IDbConnectionFactory</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlConnection</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Create</span>(); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlConnectionFactory</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IConfiguration</span> cfg) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IDbConnectionFactory</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> _cs = cfg.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetConnectionString</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop&quot;</span>)!;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlConnection</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Create</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(_cs);\n}\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IDbConnectionFactory</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlConnectionFactory</span>&gt;();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h2 id=\"dapper-vs-ef-core-the-real-decision-not-a-popularity-contest\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ado-dapper/dapper-vs-ef-core-the-real-decision-not-a-popularity-contest\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Dapper vs EF Core: the real decision, not a popularity contest <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>EF Core</th>\n<th>Dapper</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>You write</td>\n<td>LINQ (SQL optional)</td>\n<td>SQL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Change tracking / unit of work</td>\n<td>✔</td>\n<td>✘</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Migrations / schema management</td>\n<td>✔</td>\n<td>✘ (use DbUp, Flyway, sqlproj/DACPAC)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relationships &amp; loading</td>\n<td>✔ Include, navigations</td>\n<td>Manual (multi-mapping)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compile-time query checking</td>\n<td>Partially (LINQ is typed)</td>\n<td>✘ (SQL strings)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Raw performance</td>\n<td>Very good (gap much smaller since EF Core 6–8)</td>\n<td>Excellent, minimal overhead</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Complex reporting SQL (CTEs, window functions, hints)</td>\n<td>Possible via <code>FromSql</code>/<code>SqlQuery</code></td>\n<td>Natural</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Learning curve</td>\n<td>Higher (tracking, loading, translation rules)</td>\n<td>Low (if you know SQL)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>This table describes two tools built for different jobs, which is why a pragmatic combination uses both at once, naturally falling out of CQRS: <strong>EF Core for the write side</strong> (aggregates, business rules, change tracking, migrations — where EF Core&#39;s extra machinery earns its keep) and <strong>Dapper, or EF Core&#39;s own raw SQL, for the read side</strong> (fast, flat, tailored queries returning DTOs directly, with no tracking overhead for data nobody&#39;s going to mutate). EF Core can run raw SQL this way too, without Dapper at all: <code>db.Database.SqlQuery&lt;ProductDto&gt;($&quot;SELECT …&quot;)</code>.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are the main ADO.NET classes?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>DbConnection (a connection to the database, pooled), DbCommand (SQL text or stored procedure with parameters), DbDataReader (fast forward-only streaming over results), DbParameter, and DbTransaction, each implemented by providers such as SqlClient and Npgsql. Newer: DbDataSource as a connection factory.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why must you parameterise SQL?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>To prevent SQL injection (input is sent as data, never parsed as SQL) and to let the database reuse cached execution plans. Concatenating input into SQL is a critical vulnerability.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is Dapper and how is it different from EF Core?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A micro-ORM: extension methods on IDbConnection that execute your SQL and map results to objects (and objects to parameters) with near-raw ADO.NET performance. It has no change tracking, LINQ provider, relationship management or migrations, unlike the full ORM EF Core.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you map a join into nested objects with Dapper?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Multi-mapping: QueryAsync&lt;T1, T2, TResult&gt; with a mapping function that combines the objects per row, and splitOn naming the column where the second object&#39;s columns start. Use a dictionary to de-duplicate parents for one-to-many.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How would you insert 1 million rows efficiently?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Use SqlBulkCopy (or Npgsql binary COPY) to stream rows into a staging or target table in batches, then a set-based MERGE/INSERT…SELECT if needed. Avoid row-by-row INSERTs and EF change tracking for large imports.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>When would you choose Dapper over EF Core?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>For read-heavy, performance-critical or complex SQL (reports, CTEs, window functions, hints), when the team prefers explicit SQL, or for CQRS read models. EF Core for rich domain writes, change tracking, relationships and migrations. Using both is common.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ado-dapper/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/framework/data/adonet/ado-net-overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ADO.NET overview</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/sql/connect/ado-net/introduction-microsoft-data-sqlclient-namespace\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Microsoft.Data.SqlClient</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/DapperLib/Dapper\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Dapper on GitHub</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.npgsql.org/doc/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Npgsql documentation</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ado-dapper/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Needs two packages: <code>dotnet add package Dapper</code> and <code>dotnet add package Microsoft.Data.Sqlite</code> in a new console project, then <code>dotnet run</code>. It uses an in-memory SQLite database to check raw ADO.NET parameterized commands, that parameterization defuses SQL-injection-shaped input, Dapper&#39;s column-to-object mapping, <code>IN</code> list expansion, multi-mapping with <code>splitOn</code>, <code>QueryMultipleAsync</code>, and <code>ExecuteAsync</code>&#39;s affected-row count.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dapper</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Data</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sqlite</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> failures = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> claim, <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> ok)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (ok) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: {claim}&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">else</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: {claim}&quot;</span>); failures++; }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> conn = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SqliteConnection</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Data Source=:memory:&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OpenAsync</span>();\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    CREATE TABLE Products (Id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, Name TEXT NOT NULL, Price REAL NOT NULL, CategoryId INTEGER NOT NULL);\n    CREATE TABLE Customers (Id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, Name TEXT NOT NULL);\n    CREATE TABLE Orders (Id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, Total REAL NOT NULL, CustomerId INTEGER NOT NULL);\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 1. Raw ADO.NET: parameterized command + DbDataReader</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> insert = conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateCommand</span>())\n{\n    insert.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CommandText</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;INSERT INTO Products (Name, Price, CategoryId) VALUES (@name, @price, @cat)&quot;</span>;\n    insert.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parameters</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddWithValue</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;@name&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Widget&quot;</span>);\n    insert.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parameters</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddWithValue</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;@price&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">9.99</span>);\n    insert.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parameters</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddWithValue</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;@cat&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> insert.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteNonQueryAsync</span>();\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> cmd = conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateCommand</span>())\n{\n    cmd.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CommandText</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;SELECT Id, Name, Price FROM Products WHERE CategoryId = @cat&quot;</span>;\n    cmd.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parameters</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddWithValue</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;@cat&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> reader = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> cmd.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteReaderAsync</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> gotRow = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">while</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> reader.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadAsync</span>())\n    {\n        gotRow = reader.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetString</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>) == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Widget&quot;</span> &amp;&amp; reader.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetDouble</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>) == <span class=\"tk-num\">9.99</span>;\n    }\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a parameterized DbCommand + DbDataReader round-trips a row correctly&quot;</span>, gotRow);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 2. Parameterization defuses SQL injection: a malicious-looking value is stored as plain data</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> maliciousName = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Robert'); DROP TABLE Products; --&quot;</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;INSERT INTO Products (Name, Price, CategoryId) VALUES (@Name, @Price, @CategoryId)&quot;</span>,\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> = maliciousName, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">1.0</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CategoryId</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span> });\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> stillThere = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">QuerySingleAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Products&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> storedLiteral = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">QuerySingleAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;(\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;SELECT Name FROM Products WHERE CategoryId = @cat&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { cat = <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span> });\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a parameterized value containing SQL syntax is stored literally, not executed&quot;</span>,\n    stillThere == <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span> &amp;&amp; storedLiteral == maliciousName);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 3. Dapper QueryAsync&lt;T&gt; maps columns to a record by name</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> products = (<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">QueryAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductDto</span>&gt;(\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;SELECT Id, Name, Price FROM Products WHERE CategoryId = @CategoryId&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">CategoryId</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> })).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToList</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Dapper QueryAsync&lt;T&gt; maps result columns onto a record's constructor parameters by name&quot;</span>,\n    products.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> &amp;&amp; products[<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>].<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Widget&quot;</span> &amp;&amp; products[<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>].<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">9.99</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 4. IN list expansion</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> byIds = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">QueryAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductDto</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;SELECT Id, Name, Price FROM Products WHERE Id IN @ids&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { ids = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>[] { <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span> } });\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Dapper expands an array parameter into a SQL IN list&quot;</span>, byIds.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span>() == <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 5. Multi-mapping: a joined flat row reconstructed into a nested object</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;INSERT INTO Customers (Id, Name) VALUES (1, 'Ana')&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;INSERT INTO Orders (Id, Total, CustomerId) VALUES (1, 42.5, 1)&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> orders = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">QueryAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderJoinRow</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerRow</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderWithCustomer</span>&gt;(\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;SELECT o.Id, o.Total, c.Id, c.Name FROM Orders o JOIN Customers c ON c.Id = o.CustomerId&quot;</span>,\n    (order, customer) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderWithCustomer</span>(order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>, customer),\n    splitOn: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Id&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> joined = orders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Single</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Dapper multi-mapping reconstructs a nested object from a joined row via splitOn&quot;</span>,\n    joined.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">42.5</span> &amp;&amp; joined.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Ana&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 6. QueryMultiple: two result sets from one round trip</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> multi = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">QueryMultipleAsync</span>(\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;SELECT * FROM Orders WHERE Id = @id; SELECT * FROM Customers WHERE Id = @cid;&quot;</span>,\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { id = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, cid = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> });\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> order = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> multi.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadSingleAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderRow</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> customer = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> multi.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadSingleAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerRow</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;QueryMultipleAsync reads two separate result sets from one round trip&quot;</span>,\n    order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">42.5</span> &amp;&amp; customer.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Ana&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 7. ExecuteAsync returns rows affected</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> affected = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;UPDATE Products SET Price = @Price WHERE Id = @Id&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">19.99</span> });\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ExecuteAsync returns the number of rows affected&quot;</span>, affected == <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(failures == <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span> ? <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;\\nAll checks passed.&quot;</span> : <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;\\n{failures} check(s) failed.&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (failures &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exit</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductDto</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">long</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderRow</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">long</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">long</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderJoinRow</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">long</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerRow</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">long</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderWithCustomer</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">long</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerRow</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Not runtime-verifiable here: <code>SqlBulkCopy</code> performance claims, <code>TransactionScope</code>&#39;s MSDTC escalation, and connection pooling behavior are all SQL Server/network-specific and don&#39;t apply to the in-memory SQLite database used above. See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ado-dapper/further-reading\">Further reading</a> for those.</p>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-layer-every-other-data-access-tool-is-secretly-built-on","text":"The layer every other data-access tool is secretly built on","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"dataset-and-datatable","text":"DataSet and DataTable","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"transactions-in-ado-net","text":"Transactions in ADO.NET","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"bulk-operations-when-row-by-row-stops-being-viable","text":"Bulk operations: when row-by-row stops being viable","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"dapper-the-micro-orm-that-stays-out-of-your-way","text":"Dapper: the micro-ORM that stays out of your way","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"dapper-advanced-usage","text":"Dapper: advanced usage","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"multi-mapping-turning-a-join-back-into-an-object-graph","text":"Multi-mapping: turning a join back into an object graph","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"multiple-result-sets-in-one-round-trip","text":"Multiple result sets in one round trip","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"stored-procedures-output-parameters-transactions","text":"Stored procedures, output parameters, transactions","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"type-handlers","text":"Type handlers","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"registering-connections-in-di","text":"Registering connections in DI","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"dapper-vs-ef-core-the-real-decision-not-a-popularity-contest","text":"Dapper vs EF Core: the real decision, not a popularity contest","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":3082,"minutes":14,"num":"4.2","part":"p4"},"efcore-fundamentals":{"id":"efcore-fundamentals","title":"EF Core Fundamentals: DbContext, Modelling, Relationships and Migrations","summary":"Microsoft's ORM from the ground up. What an ORM does, DbContext and DbSet, registering and lifetimes, entities and conventions, the Fluent API, keys and value generation, relationships (one-to-many, one-to-one, many-to-many), owned and complex types, value conversions, change tracking and SaveChanges, and migrations from dev to production.","layer":"data","where":"Data & state › Relational databases › EF Core (ORM)","fits":"<p>EF Core is a <strong>layer-3 framework</strong> whose job is <strong>layer 5</strong> (data). It sits on ADO.NET (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ado-dapper\">ADO.NET &amp; Dapper</a>), translates LINQ (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/linq\">LINQ</a>) into SQL (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals\">Relational fundamentals</a>), and is registered in DI as scoped (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/dependency-injection\">Dependency injection</a>). In Clean Architecture it lives in the Infrastructure layer (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/app-architectures\">App architectures</a>).</p>\n","prereqs":["relational-fundamentals","linq","dependency-injection"],"next":["efcore-querying","efcore-advanced","ddd"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>EF Core does four jobs: map classes↔tables, translate LINQ↔SQL, track what changed, and turn <code>SaveChanges()</code> into INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE statements.</li>\n<li><code>DbContext</code> = one unit of work, scoped to one request/job — never a singleton, never shared across threads.</li>\n<li>EF infers a lot from naming conventions (<code>Id</code>, nullability, navigation properties); Fluent API (<code>IEntityTypeConfiguration&lt;T&gt;</code>) overrides conventions and keeps persistence detail out of the domain classes.</li>\n<li>Change tracking works by snapshotting each entity&#39;s values when loaded, then diffing against that snapshot — which is why read-only queries should use <code>AsNoTracking()</code> to skip the diffing entirely.</li>\n<li>Migrations are a versioned diff of your model over time; in production, generate a SQL script/bundle rather than calling <code>MigrateAsync()</code> at startup across multiple replicas (they&#39;d race).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"what-an-orm-is-actually-for\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals/what-an-orm-is-actually-for\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What an ORM is actually for <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Think back to <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ado-dapper/the-layer-every-other-data-access-tool-is-secretly-built-on\">ADO.NET &amp; Dapper</a>: raw ADO.NET gives you a connection, a command, and a reader that hands back columns one at a time, by index or name. Every entity you want to work with needs its own hand-written SQL, its own mapping code from columns to properties, and its own tracking of what changed since you loaded it. An <strong>Object-Relational Mapper</strong> exists to take that repetitive, error-prone work off your hands. <strong>Entity Framework Core</strong> does four specific jobs:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Mapping:</strong> classes ↔ tables, properties ↔ columns, references ↔ foreign keys.</li>\n<li><strong>Querying:</strong> LINQ ↔ SQL, then results → objects (&quot;materialisation&quot;).</li>\n<li><strong>Change tracking:</strong> remembers loaded entities and detects what changed.</li>\n<li><strong>Persistence:</strong> <code>SaveChanges()</code> generates INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE in one transaction.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Plus <strong>migrations</strong>, covered later in this chapter, to evolve the database schema in step with your model as it changes over time.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-why\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Why use an ORM</div><p>Without one, every entity needs hand-written SQL for each operation plus manual mapping code, relationships and transactions and change detection are all your problem, and schema changes are manual scripts. EF Core removes most of that boilerplate, gives type-safe queries checked by the compiler, and keeps the schema versioned with the code. The cost is a learning curve — tracking, loading, translation rules — and the risk of generating bad SQL if you don&#39;t understand what it&#39;s doing, which the next chapter covers.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"dbcontext-and-dbset-your-session-with-the-database\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals/dbcontext-and-dbset-your-session-with-the-database\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>DbContext and DbSet: your session with the database <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>The <strong><code>DbContext</code></strong> is the object that holds everything an ORM needs to do its four jobs: a database connection, the <strong>model</strong> (metadata describing how classes map to tables), and the <strong>change tracker</strong>. Each <strong><code>DbSet&lt;T&gt;</code></strong> is your entry point to one table — something you can query and add new entities to.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">DbContextOptions</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>&gt; options) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">DbContext</span>(options)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DbSet</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customers</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Set</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>&gt;();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DbSet</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Set</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt;();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DbSet</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Set</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>&gt;();\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OnModelCreating</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ModelBuilder</span> modelBuilder)\n    {\n        modelBuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ApplyConfigurationsFromAssembly</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Assembly</span>);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// picks up IEntityTypeConfiguration&lt;T&gt; classes</span>\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ConfigureConventions</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ModelConfigurationBuilder</span> b)\n    {\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Properties</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">HavePrecision</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">18</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>);                              <span class=\"tk-com\">// global convention</span>\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Properties</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">HaveMaxLength</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">256</span>);\n    }\n}\n\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddDbContext</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>&gt;(o =&gt; o\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseSqlServer</span>(builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configuration</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetConnectionString</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop&quot;</span>), sql =&gt; sql.<span class=\"tk-ty\">EnableRetryOnFailure</span>())\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseQueryTrackingBehavior</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">QueryTrackingBehavior</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TrackAll</span>));</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Registration</th>\n<th>Lifetime</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>AddDbContext&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td><strong>Scoped</strong> (one per request)</td>\n<td>Normal web requests</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>AddDbContextPool&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Scoped, but instances reused from a pool (state reset)</td>\n<td>High-throughput APIs: avoids setup cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>AddDbContextFactory&lt;T&gt;</code> / <code>AddPooledDbContextFactory&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Factory is singleton; you create contexts</td>\n<td>Background services, Blazor Server, parallel work</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">DbContext is not thread-safe and should be short-lived</div><p>Hold onto this mental model: one context = one unit of work = one request (or one message, or one job) — never longer. Never share a single context across threads (no <code>Task.WhenAll</code> on operations sharing the same context), never make one a singleton, and don&#39;t keep one alive for minutes at a time, because its change tracker only ever grows while entities stay attached to it, and a long-lived context slowly accumulates tracked entities nobody&#39;s using anymore.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"entities-and-conventions-what-ef-core-infers-without-being-t\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals/entities-and-conventions-what-ef-core-infers-without-being-t\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Entities and conventions: what EF Core infers without being told <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>EF Core infers a surprising amount of the model purely by <strong>convention</strong> — shape and naming tell it what it needs to know, without a single attribute or Fluent API call:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Convention</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Property named <code>Id</code> or <code>&lt;Type&gt;Id</code></td>\n<td>Primary key; <code>int</code>/<code>long</code> → identity column, <code>Guid</code> → sequential GUID generated client-side</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Non-nullable reference types (<code>string</code>) / value types</td>\n<td><code>NOT NULL</code> columns; <code>string?</code> / <code>int?</code> → nullable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Navigation property + <code>&lt;Nav&gt;Id</code> property</td>\n<td>Foreign key relationship</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>DbSet&lt;Order&gt; Orders</code></td>\n<td>Table name <code>Orders</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>List&lt;OrderLine&gt;</code> navigation</td>\n<td>One-to-many</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>string</code></td>\n<td><code>nvarchar(max)</code> on SQL Server (configure a max length!)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>decimal</code></td>\n<td><code>decimal(18,2)</code> with a warning; configure precision</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enum</td>\n<td>Stored as int (configure <code>HasConversion&lt;string&gt;()</code> to store names)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">required</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Email</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">required</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } = [];          <span class=\"tk-com\">// collection navigation</span>\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }        <span class=\"tk-com\">// FK property</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>!;  <span class=\"tk-com\">// reference navigation</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreatedAt</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderLine</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } = [];\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>[] <span class=\"tk-ty\">RowVersion</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = [];\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice every setter above is <code>private</code>. EF Core can still set <strong>private setters</strong> and <strong>backing fields</strong>, and construct entities through <strong>constructors with parameters</strong>, specifically so entity classes can protect their own invariants instead of exposing every property as a freely settable public field — important groundwork for <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ddd\">DDD</a>, where an entity is supposed to enforce its own business rules rather than trusting every caller to.</p>\n<h2 id=\"configuration-attributes-vs-fluent-api\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals/configuration-attributes-vs-fluent-api\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Configuration: attributes vs Fluent API <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Which to reach for first:   simple, obvious constraint  ──▶  data annotation\n                             relationship, conversion,   ──▶  Fluent API (IEntityTypeConfiguration&lt;T&gt;)\n                             keeping the domain clean</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Data annotations</th>\n<th>Fluent API</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Where</td>\n<td>Attributes on entity classes (<code>[Key]</code>, <code>[Required]</code>, <code>[MaxLength]</code>, <code>[Table]</code>, <code>[Column]</code>, <code>[Index]</code>)</td>\n<td><code>OnModelCreating</code> / <code>IEntityTypeConfiguration&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Power</td>\n<td>Common cases</td>\n<td><strong>Everything</strong> (keys, relationships, conversions, owned types, filters, seeding…)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Entity purity</td>\n<td>Persistence concerns on domain classes</td>\n<td>Keeps the domain model clean</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Precedence</td>\n<td>Lower</td>\n<td><strong>Fluent API wins</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The &quot;entity purity&quot; row is worth taking seriously if you&#39;re building toward a clean domain model: an attribute like <code>[MaxLength(256)]</code> on a property is a <em>persistence</em> concern — it describes a database column, not a business rule — sitting directly inside a class that&#39;s meant to represent a business concept. Fluent API configuration, kept in a separate <code>IEntityTypeConfiguration&lt;T&gt;</code> class, lets the entity itself stay free of that kind of noise entirely:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderConfiguration</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IEntityTypeConfiguration</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Configure</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">EntityTypeBuilder</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt; b)\n    {\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToTable</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Orders&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;sales&quot;</span>);\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasKey</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>);\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Property</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasConversion</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasMaxLength</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>);\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Property</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RowVersion</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsRowVersion</span>();                       <span class=\"tk-com\">// concurrency token</span>\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasIndex</span>(o =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>, o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreatedAt</span> });\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasOne</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithMany</span>(c =&gt; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasForeignKey</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">OnDelete</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">DeleteBehavior</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Restrict</span>);\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasMany</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithOne</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasForeignKey</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OrderId&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">OnDelete</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">DeleteBehavior</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cascade</span>);\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Navigation</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">UsePropertyAccessMode</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">PropertyAccessMode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Field</span>);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// map a private backing field</span>\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h2 id=\"keys-and-value-generation\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals/keys-and-value-generation\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Keys and value generation <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasKey</span>(x =&gt; x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>);                                    <span class=\"tk-com\">// single key</span>\nb.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasKey</span>(x =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>, x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LineNo</span> });             <span class=\"tk-com\">// composite key</span>\nb.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasAlternateKey</span>(x =&gt; x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span>);                          <span class=\"tk-com\">// unique, can be an FK target</span>\nb.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Property</span>(x =&gt; x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseIdentityColumn</span>();              <span class=\"tk-com\">// IDENTITY (default for int)</span>\nb.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Property</span>(x =&gt; x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasDefaultValueSql</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;NEWSEQUENTIALID()&quot;</span>);\nb.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Property</span>(x =&gt; x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreatedAt</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasDefaultValueSql</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;SYSUTCDATETIME()&quot;</span>);\nb.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Property</span>(x =&gt; x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasComputedColumnSql</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;[Quantity] * [UnitPrice]&quot;</span>, stored: <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><strong>Strongly typed IDs</strong> (<code>OrderId</code> record struct) — which make it impossible to accidentally pass a <code>CustomerId</code> where an <code>OrderId</code> was expected, since they&#39;re different types — are mapped with a value converter: <code>b.Property(o =&gt; o.Id).HasConversion(id =&gt; id.Value, v =&gt; new OrderId(v));</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"relationships\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals/relationships\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Relationships <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<h3 id=\"one-to-many\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals/one-to-many\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>One-to-many</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"bW9kZWxCdWlsZGVyLkVudGl0eTxPcmRlcj4oKQogICAgLkhhc09uZShvID0+IG8uQ3VzdG9tZXIpLldpdGhNYW55KGMgPT4gYy5PcmRlcnMpCiAgICAuSGFzRm9yZWlnbktleShvID0+IG8uQ3VzdG9tZXJJZCkKICAgIC5Jc1JlcXVpcmVkKCkKICAgIC5PbkRlbGV0ZShEZWxldGVCZWhhdmlvci5SZXN0cmljdCk7\"><code>modelBuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt;()\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasOne</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithMany</span>(c =&gt; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasForeignKey</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsRequired</span>()\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">OnDelete</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">DeleteBehavior</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Restrict</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h3 id=\"one-to-one\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals/one-to-one\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>One-to-one</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"bW9kZWxCdWlsZGVyLkVudGl0eTxDdXN0b21lcj4oKS5IYXNPbmUoYyA9PiBjLlByb2ZpbGUpLldpdGhPbmUocCA9PiBwLkN1c3RvbWVyKS5IYXNGb3JlaWduS2V5PEN1c3RvbWVyUHJvZmlsZT4ocCA9PiBwLkN1c3RvbWVySWQpOw==\"><code>modelBuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasOne</span>(c =&gt; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Profile</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithOne</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasForeignKey</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerProfile</span>&gt;(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h3 id=\"many-to-many\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals/many-to-many\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Many-to-many</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Category</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Categories</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } = []; }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Category</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } = []; }\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// EF Core 5+ creates the join table ProductCategory automatically.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// To add payload columns (e.g. SortOrder), map an explicit join entity:</span>\nmodelBuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasMany</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Categories</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithMany</span>(c =&gt; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">UsingEntity</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductCategory</span>&gt;(j =&gt; j.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Property</span>(pc =&gt; pc.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SortOrder</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasDefaultValue</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>));</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h3 id=\"delete-behaviours\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals/delete-behaviours\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Delete behaviours</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th><code>DeleteBehavior</code></th>\n<th>When the principal is deleted</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Cascade</code></td>\n<td>Dependents deleted (default for <strong>required</strong> relationships)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Restrict</code> / <code>NoAction</code></td>\n<td>Error if dependents exist</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>SetNull</code></td>\n<td>FK set to NULL (default for <strong>optional</strong> relationships)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ClientCascade</code> / <code>ClientSetNull</code></td>\n<td>Done by EF for <strong>tracked</strong> entities only</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>SQL Server rejects multiple cascade paths; set <code>Restrict</code> where needed.</p>\n<h2 id=\"owned-types-complex-types-and-value-conversions\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals/owned-types-complex-types-and-value-conversions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Owned types, complex types and value conversions <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Recall from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/type-system/value-types-live-on-the-stack-a-useful-lie\">Type system</a> that value objects like <code>Address</code> or <code>Money</code> are meant to be compared by their contents, not by identity — they have no primary key of their own, and EF Core has dedicated mapping strategies specifically for that shape:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Address</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Street</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">City</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PostalCode</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Country</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Amount</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Currency</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Owned type: columns inline in the owner's table (ShippingAddress_Street, …) or a separate table</span>\nb.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OwnsOne</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShippingAddress</span>, a =&gt; { a.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Property</span>(x =&gt; x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Country</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasMaxLength</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>); });\nb.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OwnsMany</span>(c =&gt; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Addresses</span>);                         <span class=\"tk-com\">// separate table with an FK</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Complex type (EF Core 8): true value semantics; can't be null in EF 8, nullable since EF 9/10</span>\nb.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ComplexProperty</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>, m =&gt; { m.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Property</span>(x =&gt; x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Amount</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasColumnName</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;TotalAmount&quot;</span>); });\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// JSON columns: store a nested object/collection as JSON in one column, still queryable</span>\nb.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OwnsOne</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Specs</span>, s =&gt; s.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToJson</span>());\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Value conversions: custom type ↔ column type</span>\nb.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Property</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Tags</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasConversion</span>(\n    v =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Join</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">','</span>, v),\n    v =&gt; v.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Split</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">','</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">StringSplitOptions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RemoveEmptyEntries</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToList</span>());</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Other modelling features worth knowing exist, even without the full depth here: <strong>inheritance mapping</strong> (TPH: one table with a discriminator, the default and fastest; TPT: a table per type; TPC: a table per concrete type), <strong>shadow properties</strong> (tracked in the model but absent from the class, e.g. <code>UpdatedAt</code>), <strong>keyless entities</strong> for views, <strong>table splitting</strong>, <strong>temporal tables</strong>, and <strong>primitive collections</strong> (<code>List&lt;int&gt;</code> stored as JSON, EF 8).</p>\n<h2 id=\"change-tracking-and-savechanges-how-ef-core-knows-what-to-wr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals/change-tracking-and-savechanges-how-ef-core-knows-what-to-wr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Change tracking and SaveChanges: how EF Core knows what to write <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Here&#39;s the mechanism underneath &quot;EF Core figures out what changed&quot;: every entity a context loads or attaches gets tagged with a <strong>state</strong>, and that state is what decides what <code>SaveChanges()</code> eventually does with it:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>State</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>On SaveChanges</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Added</code></td>\n<td>New, not in the DB</td>\n<td>INSERT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Unchanged</code></td>\n<td>Loaded, not modified</td>\n<td>nothing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Modified</code></td>\n<td>Loaded, some properties changed</td>\n<td>UPDATE (only changed columns)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Deleted</code></td>\n<td>Marked for removal</td>\n<td>DELETE</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Detached</code></td>\n<td>Not tracked</td>\n<td>nothing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Create</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> order = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>(customerId, lines);\ndb.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(order);                                   <span class=\"tk-com\">// state: Added</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Update: load, change, save. EF detects the change by comparing with a snapshot.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> product = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FindAsync</span>([id], ct);    <span class=\"tk-com\">// FindAsync checks the tracker first, then the DB</span>\nproduct!.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">24.99m</span>;                                <span class=\"tk-com\">// state becomes Modified on DetectChanges</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Delete</span>\ndb.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Remove</span>(product);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> rows = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>(ct);               <span class=\"tk-com\">// ONE transaction, batched SQL; returns rows affected</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice that nothing in the <code>product!.Price = 24.99m;</code> line told EF Core &quot;this changed&quot; — there&#39;s no explicit notification. The <code>:::deep</code> box below explains exactly how it figures that out anyway.</p>\n<p><strong>Disconnected scenarios</strong> — an API endpoint receives a DTO, and there&#39;s no entity already loaded into this context&#39;s tracker — need a different approach: load-then-update (clear, safe, costs an extra read), or attach a stub entity and mark specific properties modified directly (fewer queries, more care required, since you&#39;re asserting what changed rather than letting EF Core detect it):</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Attach</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> = dto.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> }).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Property</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">CurrentValue</span> = dto.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span>;   <span class=\"tk-com\">// UPDATE only Price</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// or set-based without tracking at all (EF 7+):</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> == dto.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteUpdateAsync</span>(s =&gt; s.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SetProperty</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span>, dto.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span>), ct);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout c-deep\"><div class=\"callout-title\">How change detection works</div><p>When an entity is tracked, EF stores a <strong>snapshot</strong> of its original property values the moment it&#39;s loaded. <code>DetectChanges()</code> — called automatically by <code>SaveChanges</code>, <code>Entries()</code> and others — walks every tracked entity and compares its <em>current</em> values against that stored snapshot, property by property. That comparison work is exactly why tracking thousands of entities is costly: every <code>SaveChanges()</code> call has to diff all of them, whether or not they actually changed, which is why read-only queries should use <code>AsNoTracking()</code> or projections to skip the snapshot and diffing entirely. <code>db.ChangeTracker.DebugView.LongView</code> shows exactly what&#39;s tracked, useful the first time this mechanism&#39;s cost surprises you in a profiler.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"migrations-evolving-the-schema-in-step-with-the-model\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals/migrations-evolving-the-schema-in-step-with-the-model\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Migrations: evolving the schema in step with the model <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Migrations</strong> turn a change in your C# model into a versioned, reviewable change to the actual database schema — the bridge between &quot;I added a property&quot; and &quot;there&#39;s now a column for it.&quot;</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Shell</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>dotnet tool install <span class=\"tk-attr\">--global</span> dotnet-ef\ndotnet ef migrations add AddOrderStatus -p src/Shop.Infrastructure -s src/Shop.Api   <span class=\"tk-com\"># -p: project with DbContext, -s: startup project</span>\ndotnet ef database update                                                              <span class=\"tk-com\"># apply to the dev database</span>\ndotnet ef migrations remove                                                            <span class=\"tk-com\"># undo the last unapplied migration</span>\ndotnet ef migrations script <span class=\"tk-attr\">--idempotent</span> -o deploy.sql                                 <span class=\"tk-com\"># SQL script for production</span>\ndotnet ef migrations bundle -o efbundle <span class=\"tk-attr\">--self-contained</span>                               <span class=\"tk-com\"># executable that applies migrations</span></code></pre></figure>\n<p>Each migration carries <code>Up()</code> and <code>Down()</code> methods plus an updated <strong>model snapshot</strong> — EF Core&#39;s own record of what the model looked like at that point, which is what lets the <em>next</em> migration be computed as a diff against it. The database itself records which migrations have already been applied in a table called <code>__EFMigrationsHistory</code>, so running <code>database update</code> twice in a row is safe — it simply applies nothing the second time.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> partial <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AddOrderStatus</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Migration</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Up</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">MigrationBuilder</span> m)\n    {\n        m.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddColumn</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;(name: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Status&quot;</span>, table: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Orders&quot;</span>, maxLength: <span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>, nullable: <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>, defaultValue: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Placed&quot;</span>);\n        m.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sql</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;UPDATE Orders SET Status = 'Shipped' WHERE ShippedAt IS NOT NULL&quot;</span>);    <span class=\"tk-com\">// data migration (edit by hand when needed)</span>\n    }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Down</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">MigrationBuilder</span> m) =&gt; m.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DropColumn</span>(name: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Status&quot;</span>, table: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Orders&quot;</span>);\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h3 id=\"applying-migrations-in-production\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals/applying-migrations-in-production\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Applying migrations in production</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Approach</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Idempotent SQL script</strong> in the CD pipeline</td>\n<td>Reviewable by DBAs; most controlled</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Migration bundle</strong> executed by the pipeline</td>\n<td>Self-contained, no SDK needed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Database.MigrateAsync()</code> at app startup</td>\n<td>Simple, but <strong>races with multiple instances</strong>, needs DDL permissions for the app identity, and slows startup. OK for dev and demos</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>That third row&#39;s warning is worth tracing through concretely: with three replicas starting up simultaneously, all three could attempt <code>MigrateAsync()</code> at roughly the same moment, racing to apply the same migration — not a safe pattern for anything beyond a single-instance dev setup. The deeper discipline for zero-downtime deploys is writing <strong>backward-compatible</strong> migrations, since old and new code genuinely run side by side during a rolling deployment: add nullable columns first, backfill them, and only make a column required in a later release once every instance is running the new code; never rename a column in one step — add the new one, copy the data across, switch the code over, then drop the old one in a later release entirely. This sequencing is known as the <strong>expand/contract</strong> pattern.</p>\n<h3 id=\"code-first-vs-database-first\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals/code-first-vs-database-first\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Code-first vs database-first</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Code-first</strong> (above): the model is the source of truth.</li>\n<li><strong>Database-first / reverse engineering</strong>: <code>dotnet ef dbcontext scaffold &quot;&lt;cs&gt;&quot; Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer -o Models</code> generates entities from an existing schema. Common with legacy databases or DBA-owned schemas.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"seeding-data\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals/seeding-data\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Seeding data <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Model seed data (part of migrations): good for static reference data</span>\nmodelBuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Country</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasData</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Country</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Code</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;DE&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Germany&quot;</span> }, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Country</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Code</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FR&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;France&quot;</span> });\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// EF 9+: UseSeeding / UseAsyncSeeding: runs on EnsureCreated / Migrate; good for dev/test data</span>\noptionsBuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseAsyncSeeding</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (ctx, _, ct) =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Set</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">AnyAsync</span>(ct)) { ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Set</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Keyboard&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">49m</span> }); <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>(ct); }\n});</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h2 id=\"providers\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals/providers\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Providers <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Database</th>\n<th>Provider package</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>SQL Server / Azure SQL</td>\n<td><code>Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PostgreSQL</td>\n<td><code>Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SQLite</td>\n<td><code>Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MySQL / MariaDB</td>\n<td><code>Pomelo.EntityFrameworkCore.MySql</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cosmos DB</td>\n<td><code>Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Cosmos</code> (document DB; very different query capabilities)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Oracle</td>\n<td><code>Oracle.EntityFrameworkCore</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>In-memory</td>\n<td><code>Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.InMemory</code>: <strong>testing only, and not recommended</strong>; see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/integration-testing\">Integration testing</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is DbContext and what lifetime should it have?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The EF Core session with the database: it owns the connection, the model and the change tracker, and implements the Unit of Work and Repository patterns. Register it as scoped (one per request or unit of work). It&#39;s not thread-safe and shouldn&#39;t be long-lived; use IDbContextFactory for background or parallel work.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What&#39;s the difference between AddDbContext and AddDbContextPool?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Both give scoped contexts. Pooling reuses DbContext instances from a pool (resetting their state) instead of constructing new ones, reducing allocation and setup cost in high-throughput apps. The context then must not hold custom per-request state in fields.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Data annotations or Fluent API?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Fluent API (often in IEntityTypeConfiguration&lt;T&gt; classes) is more powerful, keeps persistence concerns out of the domain model, and takes precedence. Annotations are convenient for simple cases. Many teams use Fluent API exclusively.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Explain EF Core&#39;s entity states.</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Added (INSERT on save), Unchanged (no action), Modified (UPDATE of changed columns), Deleted (DELETE) and Detached (not tracked). EF detects modifications by comparing current values with snapshots taken when entities were loaded or attached.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do migrations work and how do you apply them in production?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p><code>dotnet ef migrations add</code> generates a migration class (Up/Down) from the difference between the model and the last snapshot; applied migrations are recorded in __EFMigrationsHistory. In production, generate an idempotent SQL script or a migration bundle and run it in the deployment pipeline rather than calling Migrate() at startup across many instances. Keep migrations backward-compatible for zero-downtime deploys.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are owned types and when do you use them?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Types without their own identity that belong to an owner entity (value objects like Address or Money). They&#39;re stored inline as columns in the owner&#39;s table (OwnsOne), in a separate table (OwnsMany), or as JSON (ToJson). EF 8 complex types are an alternative with value semantics.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you configure a many-to-many relationship?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Add collection navigations on both sides; EF Core 5+ creates the join table automatically. Use UsingEntity with an explicit join entity when you need extra columns on the relationship.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What does SaveChanges do?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Runs DetectChanges, orders the pending INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE commands to respect FKs, batches them, executes them in a single transaction (unless one is already active), propagates generated keys, and resets entity states to Unchanged. It throws DbUpdateException or DbUpdateConcurrencyException on failure.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ef/core/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EF Core overview</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ef/core/modeling/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Creating and configuring a model</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ef/core/modeling/relationships\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Relationships</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ef/core/managing-schemas/migrations/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Migrations overview</a></li>\n<li>Book: <em>Entity Framework Core in Action</em> (2nd ed.) by Jon P Smith.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run</code> in a project that references <code>Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite</code> (the EF Core in-memory relational provider closest to the chapter&#39;s SQL-Server examples — <code>dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite</code>). It checks entity states and <code>SaveChanges</code>, change detection via snapshot comparison, <code>AsNoTracking</code> actually skipping tracking, and <code>OnDelete(DeleteBehavior.Cascade)</code> actually cascading.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">EntityFrameworkCore</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">EntityFrameworkCore</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChangeTracking</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> options = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DbContextOptionsBuilder</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseSqlite</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;DataSource=:memory:&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Options</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> db = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>(options);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Database</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OpenConnectionAsync</span>(); <span class=\"tk-com\">// keep the in-memory SQLite connection alive for the whole run</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Database</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">EnsureCreatedAsync</span>();\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Added entity, then SaveChanges turns it into Unchanged (an INSERT happened) ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> customer = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Email</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a@b.com&quot;</span> };\ndb.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customers</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(customer);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entry</span>(customer).<span class=\"tk-ty\">State</span> != <span class=\"tk-ty\">EntityState</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Added</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: a newly Added entity should be State.Added before saving&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entry</span>(customer).<span class=\"tk-ty\">State</span> != <span class=\"tk-ty\">EntityState</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Unchanged</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: after SaveChanges, a saved entity should be Unchanged&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (customer.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: the generated identity key should have been populated after insert&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: Add -&gt; SaveChanges moved the entity Added -&gt; Unchanged and generated Id={customer.Id}&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Change detection: mutating a tracked property flips state to Modified, by comparison with a snapshot ---</span>\ncustomer.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Email</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;changed@b.com&quot;</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> entry = db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entry</span>(customer);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (entry.<span class=\"tk-ty\">State</span> != <span class=\"tk-ty\">EntityState</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Modified</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: mutating a tracked property should flip state to Modified&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: changing a property on a tracked entity was detected as Modified (snapshot comparison)&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>();\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- AsNoTracking really doesn't track: the returned entity isn't in the change tracker ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> untracked = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customers</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsNoTracking</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">FirstAsync</span>(c =&gt; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> == customer.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> isTracked = db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChangeTracker</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entries</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Any</span>(e =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReferenceEquals</span>(e.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>, untracked));\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (isTracked) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: AsNoTracking() should not add the entity to the change tracker&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: AsNoTracking() returned an entity that the change tracker has no entry for&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Cascade delete: deleting the parent removes dependent rows (OnDelete(Cascade)) ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> order = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> = customer.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> };\norder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderLine</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;KB-01&quot;</span> });\ndb.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(order);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>();\ndb.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customers</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Remove</span>(customer);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> remainingLines = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderLines</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CountAsync</span>(l =&gt; l.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span> == order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (remainingLines != <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: deleting the customer should have cascaded to delete its order's lines too&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: deleting the parent (Customer) cascaded through Order to OrderLine, as OnDelete(Cascade) configured&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here — see Further reading: EF Core overview, Migrations overview (Microsoft Learn)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// (migration tooling, production deployment strategy, and multi-provider behavior aren't single-process runtime facts)</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Email</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } = []; }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderLine</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } = []; }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderLine</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;</span>; }\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">DbContextOptions</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>&gt; options) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">DbContext</span>(options)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DbSet</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customers</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Set</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>&gt;();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DbSet</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Set</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt;();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DbSet</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderLine</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderLines</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Set</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderLine</span>&gt;();\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OnModelCreating</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ModelBuilder</span> b)\n    {\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasOne</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithMany</span>(c =&gt; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasForeignKey</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">OnDelete</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">DeleteBehavior</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cascade</span>);\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderLine</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasOne</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithMany</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasForeignKey</span>(l =&gt; l.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">OnDelete</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">DeleteBehavior</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cascade</span>);\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"what-an-orm-is-actually-for","text":"What an ORM is actually for","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"dbcontext-and-dbset-your-session-with-the-database","text":"DbContext and DbSet: your session with the database","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"entities-and-conventions-what-ef-core-infers-without-being-t","text":"Entities and conventions: what EF Core infers without being told","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"configuration-attributes-vs-fluent-api","text":"Configuration: attributes vs Fluent API","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"keys-and-value-generation","text":"Keys and value generation","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"relationships","text":"Relationships","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"one-to-many","text":"One-to-many","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"one-to-one","text":"One-to-one","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"many-to-many","text":"Many-to-many","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"delete-behaviours","text":"Delete behaviours","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"owned-types-complex-types-and-value-conversions","text":"Owned types, complex types and value conversions","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"change-tracking-and-savechanges-how-ef-core-knows-what-to-wr","text":"Change tracking and SaveChanges: how EF Core knows what to write","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"migrations-evolving-the-schema-in-step-with-the-model","text":"Migrations: evolving the schema in step with the model","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"applying-migrations-in-production","text":"Applying migrations in production","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"code-first-vs-database-first","text":"Code-first vs database-first","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"seeding-data","text":"Seeding data","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"providers","text":"Providers","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":4253,"minutes":19,"num":"4.3","part":"p4"},"efcore-querying":{"id":"efcore-querying","title":"EF Core Querying and Performance","summary":"Where EF Core apps get slow, and how to fix them. How a LINQ query becomes SQL, seeing the generated SQL, tracking vs no-tracking, projections, loading related data (eager, explicit, lazy) and the N+1 problem, split queries and cartesian explosion, client evaluation, paging, raw SQL, async, and a performance checklist.","layer":"data","where":"Data & state › EF Core › Querying & performance","fits":"<p>This chapter is where layer 1 (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/linq\">LINQ</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/async-await\">async</a>) meets layer 5 (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals\">Relational fundamentals</a>). Nearly every &quot;the API is slow&quot; investigation ends up here, usually after tracing (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/observability-aspire\">Observability</a>) shows that a database span is the slow part.</p>\n","prereqs":["efcore-fundamentals","linq"],"next":["efcore-advanced","caching-ratelimit-cors"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-querying/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A LINQ query against <code>DbSet&lt;T&gt;</code> doesn&#39;t touch the database until a <strong>terminating operator</strong> (<code>ToListAsync</code>, <code>FirstOrDefaultAsync</code>, etc.) runs it — everything before that just builds up a description of the query.</li>\n<li>Turn on SQL logging or call <code>.ToQueryString()</code> so you can see what EF Core actually sends, instead of guessing.</li>\n<li>For read-only queries, skip tracking (<code>AsNoTracking()</code>) and project straight into a DTO with <code>Select</code> — it&#39;s faster and sidesteps N+1 for free.</li>\n<li><strong>N+1</strong> (one query, then one more per row) is the most common EF Core slowdown. Fix it with projection, eager loading (<code>Include</code>), or batching (<code>Contains</code>).</li>\n<li>Including multiple collections in one query can multiply rows instead of adding them (cartesian explosion); <code>AsSplitQuery()</code> trades that for extra round trips.</li>\n<li>EF Core refuses to silently run C# it can&#39;t translate to SQL — write translatable expressions, or filter in memory explicitly, after a server-side filter has already cut the data down.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"from-linq-to-sql-and-the-one-moment-it-actually-runs\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-querying/from-linq-to-sql-and-the-one-moment-it-actually-runs\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>From LINQ to SQL, and the one moment it actually runs <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> recent = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> == customerId &amp;&amp; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreatedAt</span> &gt;= since)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderByDescending</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreatedAt</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(o =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderSummary</span>(o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreatedAt</span>, o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sum</span>(l =&gt; l.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Quantity</span> * l.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UnitPrice</span>)))\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Take</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToListAsync</span>(ct);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>SQL</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-com\">-- what EF Core sends (SQL Server)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">SELECT</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">TOP</span>(<span class=\"tk-attr\">@__p_2</span>) [o].[Id], [o].[CreatedAt], (\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">SELECT</span> COALESCE(<span class=\"tk-kw\">SUM</span>(CAST([l].[Quantity] <span class=\"tk-kw\">AS</span> decimal(<span class=\"tk-num\">18</span>,<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>)) * [l].[UnitPrice]), <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>.<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">FROM</span> [OrderLines] <span class=\"tk-kw\">AS</span> [l] <span class=\"tk-kw\">WHERE</span> [o].[Id] = [l].[OrderId])\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">FROM</span> [Orders] <span class=\"tk-kw\">AS</span> [o]\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">WHERE</span> [o].[CustomerId] = <span class=\"tk-attr\">@__customerId_0</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">AND</span> [o].[CreatedAt] &gt;= <span class=\"tk-attr\">@__since_1</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">BY</span> [o].[CreatedAt] <span class=\"tk-kw\">DESC</span></code></pre></figure>\n<p>The pipeline behind that transformation: your LINQ expression becomes an expression tree — exactly the data structure <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/linq/expression-trees-what-ef-core-is-actually-reading\">LINQ</a> covered — which EF&#39;s query pipeline compiles into provider-specific SQL, with captured variables turned into safe <strong>parameters</strong>, executed via ADO.NET, and the resulting rows <strong>materialised</strong> back into objects (tracked, by default, unless told otherwise).</p>\n<p>Here&#39;s the detail worth holding onto for the rest of this chapter: nothing above actually touches the database until it hits a <strong>terminating operator</strong> — <code>ToListAsync</code>, <code>ToArrayAsync</code>, <code>FirstOrDefaultAsync</code>, <code>SingleAsync</code>, <code>CountAsync</code>, <code>AnyAsync</code>, <code>SumAsync</code>, <code>MaxAsync</code>, <code>ToDictionaryAsync</code>, <code>ExecuteUpdateAsync</code>, <code>ExecuteDeleteAsync</code>, or <code>await foreach</code> over <code>AsAsyncEnumerable()</code>. Everything before that point — every <code>.Where</code>, every <code>.OrderBy</code> — is just building up a description of a query, not running one. This deferred-execution behaviour is exactly what <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/linq/deferred-vs-immediate-execution-the-one-idea-that-explains-m\">LINQ</a> warned generalises to bugs where a query runs again, against changed data, because something re-enumerated it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"see-the-sql-first-you-cant-tune-what-you-cant-see\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-querying/see-the-sql-first-you-cant-tune-what-you-cant-see\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>See the SQL first — you can&#39;t tune what you can&#39;t see <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// 1. Log SQL (development)</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddDbContext</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>&gt;(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseSqlServer</span>(cs)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogTo</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">LogLevel</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Information</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">EnableSensitiveDataLogging</span>()          <span class=\"tk-com\">// shows parameter values: DEV ONLY</span>\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">EnableDetailedErrors</span>());\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// or via configuration: &quot;Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Database.Command&quot;: &quot;Information&quot;</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 2. Inspect a single query without running it</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> sql = db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">100</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToQueryString</span>();\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 3. Tag queries so you can find them in Query Store / traces</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> q = db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TagWith</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Dashboard: recent big orders&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">1000</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>In production, <strong>OpenTelemetry EF Core instrumentation</strong> (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/observability-aspire\">Observability</a>) records every query as a span with its duration, and Query Store or Azure SQL Insights shows the slowest statements — the same &quot;trace it before guessing&quot; discipline that <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/performance-engineering\">Performance engineering</a> applies everywhere else.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tracking-vs-no-tracking-paying-for-a-feature-you-often-dont-\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-querying/tracking-vs-no-tracking-paying-for-a-feature-you-often-dont-\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Tracking vs no-tracking: paying for a feature you often don&#39;t need <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Recall from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals/change-tracking-and-savechanges-how-ef-core-knows-what-to-wr\">EF Core fundamentals</a> that tracking means keeping a snapshot of every loaded entity so <code>SaveChanges()</code> can detect what changed. For a query that&#39;s purely reading data to send to a client — never going to be saved back — that snapshot is pure overhead:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Tracking (default)</th>\n<th><code>AsNoTracking()</code></th>\n<th><code>AsNoTrackingWithIdentityResolution()</code></th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Snapshot kept</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>No (identity map only for the query)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Can SaveChanges modifications</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Duplicate rows → same instance</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>No: separate objects</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Speed / memory</td>\n<td>Slower</td>\n<td><strong>Fastest</strong></td>\n<td>In between</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Use for</td>\n<td>Load-modify-save</td>\n<td><strong>Read-only queries</strong></td>\n<td>Read-only with repeated related entities</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"dmFyIHByb2R1Y3RzID0gYXdhaXQgZGIuUHJvZHVjdHMuQXNOb1RyYWNraW5nKCkuV2hlcmUocCA9PiBwLklzQWN0aXZlKS5Ub0xpc3RBc3luYyhjdCk7\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> products = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsNoTracking</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsActive</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToListAsync</span>(ct);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>You can flip the default for an entire context instead of annotating every query (<code>UseQueryTrackingBehavior(NoTracking)</code>) and opt back in with <code>AsTracking()</code> on the specific queries that need it — a pattern that fits read-heavy APIs particularly well, since most of their queries never modify anything.</p>\n<h2 id=\"projection-the-single-biggest-win-in-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-querying/projection-the-single-biggest-win-in-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Projection: the single biggest win in this chapter <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// ✘ Loads EVERY column of Orders, Customer AND Lines, tracks them all, then the API maps to a DTO</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> orders = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Include</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Include</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToListAsync</span>(ct);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> dtos = orders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(o =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderSummary</span>(o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>, o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span>));\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// ✔ SQL selects only 3 values per row; no tracking (projections into non-entities aren't tracked); no Include needed</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> dtos2 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(o =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderSummary</span>(o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>, o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span>))\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToListAsync</span>(ct);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice everything the second version gets for free, just by moving the <code>Select</code> earlier: fewer columns actually travel over the wire, nothing gets tracked (projections into plain DTOs are never tracked at all), and — this is the part worth sitting with — <strong>navigations referenced inside a <code>Select</code> get translated into joins or subqueries by EF Core itself</strong>, which sidesteps the N+1 problem the next section covers entirely, without you having to think about <code>Include</code> at all. <strong>Projecting with <code>Select</code> into DTOs</strong> should be the default for every read endpoint you write. Mapping libraries can project too — AutoMapper&#39;s <code>ProjectTo</code>, Mapperly&#39;s <code>IQueryable</code> projections — if you&#39;d rather not hand-write the DTO construction.</p>\n<h2 id=\"loading-related-data-four-strategies-one-clearly-preferred\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-querying/loading-related-data-four-strategies-one-clearly-preferred\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Loading related data: four strategies, one clearly preferred <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Strategy</th>\n<th>How</th>\n<th>SQL</th>\n<th>When</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Eager</strong></td>\n<td><code>.Include(o =&gt; o.Lines).ThenInclude(l =&gt; l.Product)</code></td>\n<td>JOIN(s) in the same query</td>\n<td>You need entities <strong>plus</strong> related data, typically to modify them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Projection</strong></td>\n<td>Navigations inside <code>Select</code></td>\n<td>JOIN/subquery, only needed columns</td>\n<td>Read models / DTOs (<strong>preferred for reads</strong>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Explicit</strong></td>\n<td><code>await db.Entry(order).Collection(o =&gt; o.Lines).LoadAsync()</code></td>\n<td>A separate query, on demand</td>\n<td>Conditionally loading for an already-loaded entity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lazy</strong></td>\n<td>Virtual navigations + proxies (<code>UseLazyLoadingProxies()</code>)</td>\n<td>A query <strong>when the property is first accessed</strong></td>\n<td>Rarely; hides N+1</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"Ly8gRmlsdGVyZWQgaW5jbHVkZSAoRUYgNSspCnZhciBjdXN0b21lciA9IGF3YWl0IGRiLkN1c3RvbWVycwogICAgLkluY2x1ZGUoYyA9PiBjLk9yZGVycy5XaGVyZShvID0+IG8uU3RhdHVzID09IE9yZGVyU3RhdHVzLk9wZW4pLk9yZGVyQnlEZXNjZW5kaW5nKG8gPT4gby5DcmVhdGVkQXQpLlRha2UoNSkpCiAgICAuU2luZ2xlQXN5bmMoYyA9PiBjLklkID09IGlkLCBjdCk7\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Filtered include (EF 5+)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> customer = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customers</span>\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Include</span>(c =&gt; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> == <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Open</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderByDescending</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreatedAt</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Take</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>))\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">SingleAsync</span>(c =&gt; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> == id, ct);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>That &quot;rarely; hides N+1&quot; note in the Lazy row isn&#39;t a footnote — it&#39;s exactly why the next section exists.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-n-1-problem-the-single-most-common-ef-core-performance-b\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-querying/the-n-1-problem-the-single-most-common-ef-core-performance-b\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The N+1 problem: the single most common EF Core performance bug <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>The shape of this bug: <strong>1 query</strong> loads a list, then <strong>N more queries</strong> — one per item in that list — load each item&#39;s related data:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Lazy loading enabled:</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> orders = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreatedAt</span> &gt;= today).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToListAsync</span>(ct);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// 1 query → 200 orders</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> o <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> orders)\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>);                                           <span class=\"tk-com\">// +200 queries (one per order)</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><img src=\"visuals/n-plus-one.svg\" alt=\"Loading five orders and their customers: with a query inside the loop, five orders cost six round trips; with projection, the database does the join in one round trip, regardless of row count\"></p>\n<p>That&#39;s 201 round trips instead of 1. Each one might take only 1–2 ms on its own, which is exactly why this bug slips past code review — it looks harmless in isolation. But 201 of them back-to-back adds up to hundreds of milliseconds, and it only gets worse as the order count grows. It also happens <strong>without lazy loading enabled at all</strong>, any time you call the database inside a loop:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> id <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> productIds)\n    prices[id] = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> == id).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">SingleAsync</span>(ct);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✘ N queries</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> prices2 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(p =&gt; productIds.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contains</span>(p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>))                            <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✔ 1 query: WHERE Id IN (…)</span>\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToDictionaryAsync</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span>, ct);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><strong>Detect it:</strong> SQL logs show the exact same statement shape repeating many times in a row; a trace shows dozens of identical, tiny database spans nested under one request; <code>dotnet-counters</code> shows an unusually high queries-per-request count for an endpoint that only looks like it returns one thing. <strong>Fix it:</strong> projection (as above — the usual answer), eager loading with <code>Include</code>, batching with <code>Contains</code> (as the second fix shows), or a DataLoader if you&#39;re serving this through GraphQL.</p>\n<h2 id=\"cartesian-explosion-and-split-queries\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-querying/cartesian-explosion-and-split-queries\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Cartesian explosion and split queries <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>A related but distinct trap: including <strong>multiple collection navigations</strong> in a single query doesn&#39;t add rows, it <strong>multiplies</strong> them, because a SQL join flattens nested collections into one wide, repeated result set:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"dmFyIGJsb2dzID0gYXdhaXQgZGIuQmxvZ3MuSW5jbHVkZShiID0+IGIuUG9zdHMpLkluY2x1ZGUoYiA9PiBiLkNvbnRyaWJ1dG9ycykuVG9MaXN0QXN5bmMoKTsKLy8gMSBibG9nIMOXIDEwMCBwb3N0cyDDlyAyMCBjb250cmlidXRvcnMgPSAyLDAwMCByb3dzIHJldHVybmVkLCB3aXRoIGJsb2cgY29sdW1ucyBkdXBsaWNhdGVkIGluIGV2ZXJ5IHJvdw==\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> blogs = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Blogs</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Include</span>(b =&gt; b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Posts</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Include</span>(b =&gt; b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contributors</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToListAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 1 blog × 100 posts × 20 contributors = 2,000 rows returned, with blog columns duplicated in every row</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Here&#39;s why: SQL must emit one row for every post-contributor pair, repeating the blog&#39;s own columns in each row. 100 posts × 20 contributors = 2,000 rows, each one carrying a full, duplicate copy of the blog&#39;s data — a lot of redundant bytes for what&#39;s conceptually one blog with two separate lists attached to it.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> blogs2 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Blogs</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Include</span>(b =&gt; b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Posts</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Include</span>(b =&gt; b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contributors</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsSplitQuery</span>()                <span class=\"tk-com\">// 3 separate SQL queries: blogs, posts, contributors</span>\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToListAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// configure globally: UseSqlServer(cs, o =&gt; o.UseQuerySplittingBehavior(QuerySplittingBehavior.SplitQuery))</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><code>AsSplitQuery()</code> swaps one problem for a usually smaller one: more round trips (three queries instead of one), and the loss of a single consistent snapshot — the data could change between those three queries unless you wrap them in an explicit transaction — in exchange for not multiplying rows. EF Core even logs a warning when it detects multiple collection includes in one query, because this trade-off is easy to hit by accident.</p>\n<h2 id=\"client-evaluation-and-translation-errors\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-querying/client-evaluation-and-translation-errors\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Client evaluation and translation errors <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>EF Core can only translate what it actually understands into SQL: the standard LINQ operators, many <code>string</code>/<code>Math</code>/<code>DateTime</code> members, <code>EF.Functions.Like</code>, provider-specific functions, and any functions you&#39;ve explicitly mapped yourself.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> q = db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(p =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsBargain</span>(p));        <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✘ InvalidOperationException: could not be translated</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsBargain</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span> p) =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> &lt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ListPrice</span> * <span class=\"tk-num\">0.5m</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> q2 = db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> &lt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ListPrice</span> * <span class=\"tk-num\">0.5m</span>);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✔ inline the logic as an expression</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>EF Core 3+ throws</strong> here instead of silently pulling everything into memory and filtering with plain C#. EF Core 1–2 did exactly that by default, and Microsoft&#39;s own breaking-changes notes for 3.0 call out exactly this scenario — a filter that looks fine on a small test table, then quietly loads an entire production table once it grows (see Further reading).</li>\n<li>The <strong>final <code>Select</code></strong> is a partial exception: EF evaluates anything it can&#39;t translate on the client, but only <strong>after</strong> it has already fetched just the columns it needs from the server — that&#39;s fine, because by then the expensive filtering has already happened in SQL.</li>\n<li>If you must filter in memory, do it <strong>explicitly, after</strong> a server-side filter has already cut the data down: <code>.Where(serverSide).AsAsyncEnumerable().Where(clientSide)</code>. Never let the untranslatable filter be the only thing standing between you and a full table scan.</li>\n<li>For reusable query logic, write <strong>expression-returning</strong> helpers (<code>static Expression&lt;Func&lt;Product,bool&gt;&gt; IsBargain =&gt; p =&gt; …</code>) or extension methods on <code>IQueryable&lt;T&gt;</code>, which stay translatable because they hand EF Core an expression tree rather than a compiled method it can&#39;t see inside.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"paging-counting-and-existence\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-querying/paging-counting-and-existence\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Paging, counting and existence <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Offset paging: always ORDER BY a unique key for deterministic pages</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> page = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderBy</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ThenBy</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Skip</span>((pageNo - <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>) * size).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Take</span>(size)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(p =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductDto</span>(p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>, p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span>)).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToListAsync</span>(ct);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Keyset paging: constant cost on deep pages</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> next = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> &gt; lastSeenId).<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderBy</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Take</span>(size).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToListAsync</span>(ct);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> any = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AnyAsync</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> == id, ct);       <span class=\"tk-com\">// EXISTS: faster than CountAsync() &gt; 0</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Both paging styles here map directly onto the offset-vs-cursor tradeoff from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/api-design/pagination-filtering-and-sorting-never-return-an-unbounded-l\">API design</a>. One subtlety worth flagging on its own: a total count on a large, filtered table is itself an expensive query, essentially a full scan to count matches. For infinite-scroll UIs that only need to know &quot;is there more,&quot; fetching <code>size + 1</code> rows and checking whether the extra one came back is usually far cheaper than computing an exact total.</p>\n<h2 id=\"raw-sql-in-ef-core\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-querying/raw-sql-in-ef-core\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Raw SQL in EF Core <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Entities from SQL (composable: you can add LINQ on top)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> top = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSql</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;SELECT * FROM Products WITH (INDEX(IX_Products_Category)) WHERE CategoryId = {categoryId}&quot;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsActive</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderBy</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToListAsync</span>(ct);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Arbitrary types (EF 8): unmapped DTOs, scalars</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> stats = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Database</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlQuery</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CategoryStats</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    SELECT CategoryId, COUNT(*) AS Products, AVG(Price) AS AvgPrice\n    FROM Products GROUP BY CategoryId\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToListAsync</span>(ct);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Commands</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> n = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Database</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteSqlAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;UPDATE Products SET IsActive = 0 WHERE DiscontinuedAt &lt; {cutoff}&quot;</span>, ct);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><code>FromSql</code>, <code>SqlQuery</code> and <code>ExecuteSql</code> all take a <strong><code>FormattableString</code></strong> — notice the <code>$&quot;...&quot;</code> syntax above — so interpolated values become real <strong>parameters</strong> under the hood, the same safe mechanism <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ado-dapper/the-layer-every-other-data-access-tool-is-secretly-built-on\">ADO.NET &amp; Dapper</a> insisted on. The <code>…Raw</code> variants instead take a plain string, and <strong>never</strong> concatenate user input into one — doing so reopens exactly the SQL injection hole parameterisation exists to close.</p>\n<h2 id=\"async-and-cancellation\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-querying/async-and-cancellation\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Async and cancellation <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Use the async operators (<code>ToListAsync</code>, <code>SaveChangesAsync</code>…) in web apps and pass <code>CancellationToken</code> so abandoned requests cancel their SQL rather than running to completion for nobody. Don&#39;t run concurrent operations on one context — a <code>DbContext</code> isn&#39;t thread-safe, as <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals/dbcontext-and-dbset-your-session-with-the-database\">EF Core fundamentals</a> warned. Stream genuinely large results with <code>AsAsyncEnumerable()</code> instead of buffering the entire result set in memory with <code>ToListAsync()</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"performance-techniques\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-querying/performance-techniques\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Performance techniques <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Technique</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>DbContext pooling</strong> (<code>AddDbContextPool</code>)</td>\n<td>Reuses context instances, cutting per-request setup</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Compiled queries</strong> (<code>EF.CompileAsyncQuery</code>)</td>\n<td>Skips query compilation for very hot queries (EF already caches; this removes the lookup)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Compiled models</strong> (<code>dotnet ef dbcontext optimize</code>)</td>\n<td>Faster startup for huge models; required for Native AOT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><code>ExecuteUpdate</code> / <code>ExecuteDelete</code></strong></td>\n<td>Set-based writes without loading entities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Batching</strong></td>\n<td>SaveChanges batches statements automatically (tune <code>MaxBatchSize</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><code>AsNoTracking</code> + projections</strong></td>\n<td>Less work and memory per query</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Avoid <code>Include</code> chains for reads</strong></td>\n<td>Project instead</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Indexes matching your queries</strong></td>\n<td><code>HasIndex</code> in the model; verify with execution plans</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Split queries</strong></td>\n<td>Avoid cartesian explosion</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Connection resiliency</strong></td>\n<td><code>EnableRetryOnFailure</code> for transient Azure SQL errors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Caching</strong></td>\n<td>For hot, rarely changing reads (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors\">Caching</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductDto</span>?&gt;&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetProductById</span> =\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">EF</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CompileAsyncQuery</span>((<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span> db, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt;\n        db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> == id).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(p =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductDto</span>(p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>, p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span>)).<span class=\"tk-ty\">FirstOrDefault</span>());\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> p = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetProductById</span>(db, <span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>, ct);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h2 id=\"checklist-for-a-slow-ef-core-endpoint\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-querying/checklist-for-a-slow-ef-core-endpoint\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Checklist for a slow EF Core endpoint <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Work through this in order — each step is cheap to check and most slow endpoints are caught well before step 10:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Trace it</strong>: is the time in the database, in serialization, or in app code?</li>\n<li><strong>Look at the SQL</strong> (<code>ToQueryString</code>, logs, the trace span).</li>\n<li><strong>Count queries per request</strong>: N+1? Queries in loops?</li>\n<li><strong>Too many columns or rows?</strong> Project; page; filter on the server.</li>\n<li><strong>Tracking unnecessarily?</strong> <code>AsNoTracking</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cartesian explosion?</strong> Split queries.</li>\n<li><strong>Check the execution plan</strong>: missing index, scans, key lookups, implicit conversions.</li>\n<li><strong>Sync calls or <code>.Result</code>?</strong> Make it async.</li>\n<li><strong>Hot and stable data?</strong> Cache it.</li>\n<li><strong>Bulk writes?</strong> <code>ExecuteUpdate</code>/<code>ExecuteDelete</code> or bulk copy.</li>\n</ol>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the N+1 problem in EF Core and how do you fix it?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Loading a list with one query and then triggering an extra query per item to get related data, typically through lazy loading or querying inside a loop. Fix it with projections (Select into DTOs, translated into joins), eager loading (Include), or batching (WHERE Id IN via Contains). Detect it by logging SQL or inspecting traces.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>When should you use AsNoTracking?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>For read-only queries: entities aren&#39;t snapshot-tracked, which saves memory and CPU. Projections into DTOs are untracked automatically. Use AsNoTrackingWithIdentityResolution when the same related entity appears in many rows and you want shared instances.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Eager vs explicit vs lazy loading?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Eager loads related data in the same query via Include/ThenInclude. Explicit loads it later on demand with Entry(...).Collection/Reference().Load. Lazy loads automatically when a navigation property is first accessed (proxies), which easily causes N+1. For read endpoints, projection usually beats all three.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is a split query and when would you use it?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>AsSplitQuery makes EF Core load included collections with separate SQL statements instead of one big JOIN, avoiding cartesian explosion when including multiple collections. The trade-off is extra round trips and no single snapshot without a transaction.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why might an EF Core query throw &#39;could not be translated&#39;?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The LINQ contains something the provider can&#39;t convert to SQL, such as a call to a custom C# method in Where. Since EF Core 3, EF throws rather than silently evaluating on the client. Rewrite using translatable expressions, mapped DB functions, or filter in memory explicitly after a server-side filter.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you see the SQL EF Core generates?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>ToQueryString() on a query, LogTo/logging the Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Database.Command category, EnableSensitiveDataLogging in development for parameter values, TagWith to label queries, and OpenTelemetry instrumentation or Query Store in production.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you run raw SQL safely with EF Core?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Use FromSql / SqlQuery / ExecuteSql with interpolated strings: they take FormattableString and turn interpolated values into DbParameters. Avoid the Raw variants with concatenated input.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How would you speed up a hot, read-heavy EF Core query?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Project into a DTO with only the needed columns, use no-tracking, make sure a supporting index exists (check the plan), consider a compiled query and DbContext pooling, cache the result if staleness is acceptable, and use keyset pagination for large lists.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-querying/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ef/core/performance/efficient-querying\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Efficient querying</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ef/core/querying/related-data/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Loading related data</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ef/core/querying/single-split-queries\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Single vs split queries</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ef/core/performance/advanced-performance-topics\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Advanced performance topics</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ef/core/what-is-new/ef-core-3.0/breaking-changes#linq-queries-are-no-longer-evaluated-on-the-client\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Breaking changes in EF Core 3.0: LINQ queries are no longer evaluated on the client</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-querying/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>The claims below that are pure C#/EF Core behavior are checked by running real queries against an in-memory SQLite database. Save as a console app (<code>dotnet new console</code>, <code>dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite</code>), then <code>dotnet run</code>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">EntityFrameworkCore</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// SQLite's in-memory mode needs one open connection kept alive for the whole run.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> connString = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;DataSource=file:verifydb?mode=memory&amp;cache=shared&quot;</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> conn = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Data</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sqlite</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SqliteConnection</span>(connString);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OpenAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> opts = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DbContextOptionsBuilder</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseSqlite</span>(conn).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Options</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> seed = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>(opts))\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> seed.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Database</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">EnsureCreatedAsync</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> c = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Ada&quot;</span> };\n    seed.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customers</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(c);\n    seed.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddRange</span>(\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span> = c, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">10</span> },\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span> = c, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">20</span> },\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span> = c, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">30</span> });\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> seed.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>();\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: AsNoTracking() returned entities are not tracked (ChangeTracker has zero entries).</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> db = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>(opts))\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> orders = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsNoTracking</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToListAsync</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChangeTracker</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entries</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Any</span>()) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: AsNoTracking still tracked entities&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: AsNoTracking() keeps the change tracker empty&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: projecting with Select avoids tracking too, even without AsNoTracking.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> db = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>(opts))\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> dtos = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(o =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> }).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToListAsync</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChangeTracker</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entries</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Any</span>()) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: projection was tracked&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: projecting into an anonymous type tracks nothing&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: a query is not executed until a terminating operator runs (deferred execution).</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Proof: build the query, THEN add a new row, THEN run it — if building ran the query early,</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// the new row would be missing from the result.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> db = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>(opts))\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> query = db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>); <span class=\"tk-com\">// just builds the query, no SQL yet</span>\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> extra = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">999</span> };\n    db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(extra);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>();\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> countBeforeExtra = <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>; <span class=\"tk-com\">// seeded rows</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> list = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> query.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToListAsync</span>(); <span class=\"tk-com\">// SQL runs here, for the first time</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (list.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> != countBeforeExtra + <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>)\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected {countBeforeExtra + 1} rows, got {list.Count}&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: the query only ran at ToListAsync, after the extra row existed — not when it was built&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: N+1's fix — batching with Contains issues one query and returns all matching rows.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> db = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>(opts))\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> ids = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToListAsync</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> totalsBatched = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(o =&gt; ids.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contains</span>(o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>))\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToDictionaryAsync</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (totalsBatched.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> != ids.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: batched query didn't return all rows&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: a single WHERE Id IN (...) query returns all rows in one round trip&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All checks passed.&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Minimal model, matching the chapter's examples ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>!; <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">DbContext</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">DbContextOptions</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>&gt; options) : <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>(options) { }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DbSet</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customers</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Set</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>&gt;();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DbSet</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Set</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt;();\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here (needs a real SQL Server/Azure SQL instance, Query Store, or OpenTelemetry collector):</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - exact generated T-SQL shown in this chapter (provider-specific; see Further reading: &quot;Efficient querying&quot;)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - cartesian explosion row counts from multiple collection Includes against a real relational engine's join semantics</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - production trace/Query Store behavior — see Further reading: &quot;Advanced performance topics&quot;</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"from-linq-to-sql-and-the-one-moment-it-actually-runs","text":"From LINQ to SQL, and the one moment it actually runs","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"see-the-sql-first-you-cant-tune-what-you-cant-see","text":"See the SQL first — you can't tune what you can't see","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"tracking-vs-no-tracking-paying-for-a-feature-you-often-dont-","text":"Tracking vs no-tracking: paying for a feature you often don't need","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"projection-the-single-biggest-win-in-this-chapter","text":"Projection: the single biggest win in this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"loading-related-data-four-strategies-one-clearly-preferred","text":"Loading related data: four strategies, one clearly preferred","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"the-n-1-problem-the-single-most-common-ef-core-performance-b","text":"The N+1 problem: the single most common EF Core performance bug","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"cartesian-explosion-and-split-queries","text":"Cartesian explosion and split queries","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"client-evaluation-and-translation-errors","text":"Client evaluation and translation errors","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"paging-counting-and-existence","text":"Paging, counting and existence","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"raw-sql-in-ef-core","text":"Raw SQL in EF Core","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"async-and-cancellation","text":"Async and cancellation","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"performance-techniques","text":"Performance techniques","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"checklist-for-a-slow-ef-core-endpoint","text":"Checklist for a slow EF Core endpoint","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":3995,"minutes":18,"num":"4.4","part":"p4"},"efcore-advanced":{"id":"efcore-advanced","title":"EF Core Advanced: Concurrency, Transactions, Interceptors, Filters and Bulk Operations","summary":"The features you need at production scale. Optimistic concurrency with row versions, explicit transactions and execution strategies, ExecuteUpdate and ExecuteDelete, SaveChanges and command interceptors for auditing, global query filters for soft delete and multi-tenancy, domain events on save, and testing strategies.","layer":"data","where":"Data & state › EF Core › Advanced features","fits":"<p>These features sit where layer 5 meets layer 4. Concurrency and transactions protect data integrity (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/transactions-and-acid-the-unit-the-database-actually-guarantees\">Relational fundamentals</a>); interceptors and filters implement cross-cutting rules; dispatching domain events and the outbox on save connect EF Core to DDD (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ddd\">DDD</a>) and messaging (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events\">Messaging &amp; events</a>).</p>\n","prereqs":["efcore-fundamentals","efcore-querying"],"next":["ddd","messaging-events","integration-testing"],"levels":["i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-advanced/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><code>SaveChanges()</code> is already transactional on its own; reach for an explicit transaction only when something wider needs to be atomic with it (several <code>SaveChanges</code> calls, raw SQL, Dapper on the same connection).</li>\n<li><strong>Optimistic concurrency</strong> uses a version column, not locks: EF Core puts the original version in the <code>UPDATE</code>&#39;s <code>WHERE</code> clause, and throws <code>DbUpdateConcurrencyException</code> if zero rows matched because someone else already changed the row.</li>\n<li><code>ExecuteUpdate</code>/<code>ExecuteDelete</code> write straight to the database without loading entities first — fast, but they skip the change tracker and <code>SaveChanges</code> interceptors.</li>\n<li>Interceptors (<code>SaveChangesInterceptor</code>, <code>DbCommandInterceptor</code>, etc.) let you add cross-cutting behavior — auditing, soft delete, outbox — without scattering calls through business code.</li>\n<li><strong>Global query filters</strong> (soft delete, multi-tenancy) apply automatically to every query against that entity — watch for a required navigation pulling a filtered-out row back in through an <code>INNER JOIN</code>.</li>\n<li>Writing outbox rows in the <em>same</em> <code>SaveChanges</code> transaction as the business data is what makes &quot;save the order and record the event&quot; atomic, without needing a real distributed transaction.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"optimistic-concurrency-catching-a-lost-update-without-ever-l\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-advanced/optimistic-concurrency-catching-a-lost-update-without-ever-l\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Optimistic concurrency: catching a lost update without ever locking <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Here&#39;s the concrete failure this section exists to prevent: two users both load order 42 at the same moment. Alice changes the shipping address and saves. Bob, still holding the version he loaded a moment earlier — before Alice&#39;s save — changes the status and saves too, and his save <strong>overwrites Alice&#39;s change</strong> with the old address he never actually touched. Nobody did anything wrong individually; the collision is purely a timing accident. This is a <strong>lost update</strong>, the same anomaly named in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/isolation-levels-and-the-anomalies-each-one-actually-prevent\">Relational fundamentals</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Optimistic concurrency</strong> is built on a bet that pays off most of the time: conflicts like this are rare, so don&#39;t pay the cost of locking on every read — instead, <strong>detect</strong> a conflict only at the moment of writing, using a version column that changes on every update.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShippingAddress</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[Timestamp]</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>[] <span class=\"tk-ty\">RowVersion</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = [];   <span class=\"tk-com\">// SQL Server rowversion: auto-incremented by the DB on each update</span>\n}\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Fluent: b.Property(o =&gt; o.RowVersion).IsRowVersion();</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Postgres: b.Property&lt;uint&gt;(&quot;xmin&quot;).IsRowVersion(); (or a manually incremented Version property with IsConcurrencyToken())</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>EF Core adds the original version to the <code>WHERE</code> clause of the generated <code>UPDATE</code>:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>SQL</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">UPDATE</span> Orders <span class=\"tk-kw\">SET</span> ShippingAddress = <span class=\"tk-attr\">@p0</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">WHERE</span> Id = <span class=\"tk-attr\">@p1</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">AND</span> RowVersion = <span class=\"tk-attr\">@p2</span>;      <span class=\"tk-com\">-- 0 rows affected ⇒ someone else changed it</span></code></pre></figure>\n<p>Trace through what that <code>WHERE</code> clause actually buys you: if Bob&#39;s update runs after Alice&#39;s already committed, the row&#39;s <code>RowVersion</code> has already moved past what Bob loaded, so his <code>UPDATE</code> matches <strong>zero rows</strong> — not because the <code>Id</code> is wrong, but because the version he&#39;s asserting against no longer exists. EF Core throws <strong><code>DbUpdateConcurrencyException</code></strong> on exactly that outcome:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>(ct);\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">DbUpdateConcurrencyException</span> ex)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> entry = ex.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entries</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Single</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> dbValues = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> entry.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetDatabaseValuesAsync</span>(ct);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (dbValues <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>();                 <span class=\"tk-com\">// deleted meanwhile</span>\n\n    <span class=\"tk-com\">// Options: (1) tell the client → 409/412 with current state; (2) client-wins: entry.OriginalValues.SetValues(dbValues) and retry;</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-com\">// (3) store-wins: entry.Reload(); (4) merge field by field</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Conflict</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProblemDetails</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Title</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Order was modified by someone else. Reload and try again.&quot;</span> });\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>In APIs, expose this same version as an <strong>ETag</strong> and require <code>If-Match</code> on updates — the exact mechanism covered end-to-end in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/api-design/optimistic-concurrency-with-etags\">API design</a>, where the HTTP layer and this database mechanism turn out to be the same idea wearing two different outfits.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-tip\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Concurrency tokens for business invariants</div><p>Optimistic concurrency isn&#39;t only about preventing overwritten edits — it also protects business invariants directly. Two requests both racing to reserve the last unit of stock will <strong>conflict on the product&#39;s own version column</strong>, so one of them loses the race, retries, and correctly sees zero stock left rather than both succeeding and overselling. It&#39;s a genuinely lighter-weight alternative to taking a pessimistic row lock for that same guarantee.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"transactions-when-savechangess-own-transaction-isnt-enough\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-advanced/transactions-when-savechangess-own-transaction-isnt-enough\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Transactions: when SaveChanges&#39;s own transaction isn&#39;t enough <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><code>SaveChanges</code> is <strong>already transactional</strong> on its own — every change made in one call commits or rolls back together, with no extra code needed. You need an <em>explicit</em> transaction specifically when that single-call guarantee isn&#39;t wide enough, which happens when:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>several <code>SaveChanges</code> calls must be atomic together,</li>\n<li>you mix EF changes with raw SQL / <code>ExecuteUpdate</code>,</li>\n<li>you share a transaction with Dapper/ADO.NET on the same connection,</li>\n<li>you need a specific isolation level.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> tx = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Database</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BeginTransactionAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsolationLevel</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadCommitted</span>, ct);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span>\n{\n    db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(order);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>(ct);                                          <span class=\"tk-com\">// order gets its Id</span>\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> == order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductId</span>)\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteUpdateAsync</span>(s =&gt; s.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SetProperty</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Stock</span>, p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Stock</span> - order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Qty</span>), ct);\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Database</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetDbConnection</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteAsync</span>(                      <span class=\"tk-com\">// Dapper on the same connection + transaction</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;INSERT INTO AuditLog (Action, EntityId) VALUES ('OrderPlaced', @id)&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { id = order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> }, tx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetDbTransaction</span>());\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> tx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CommitAsync</span>(ct);\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> tx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RollbackAsync</span>(ct); <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span>; }</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><strong>Savepoints:</strong> <code>await tx.CreateSavepointAsync(&quot;before-lines&quot;)</code> and <code>RollbackToSavepointAsync</code> for partial rollback (EF also uses savepoints automatically when you call SaveChanges inside a user transaction).</p>\n<h3 id=\"execution-strategies-connection-resiliency\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-advanced/execution-strategies-connection-resiliency\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Execution strategies (connection resiliency)</h3>\n<p><code>UseSqlServer(cs, o =&gt; o.EnableRetryOnFailure())</code> retries <strong>transient</strong> failures automatically — Azure SQL failovers, throttling, deadlock victims from exactly the deadlock-detection mechanism <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/locking-blocking-and-deadlocks\">Relational fundamentals</a> described. There&#39;s a catch: a retry has to redo the <em>whole</em> unit of work, not just the failed statement. So if you open a transaction manually, wrap it in an execution strategy too — otherwise a retry won&#39;t cover the <code>BEGIN TRANSACTION</code> itself:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> strategy = db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Database</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateExecutionStrategy</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> strategy.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> () =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> tx = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Database</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BeginTransactionAsync</span>(ct);\n    <span class=\"tk-com\">// ... all the work ...</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>(ct);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> tx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CommitAsync</span>(ct);\n});</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">No distributed transactions across services</div><p>A transaction, no matter how it&#39;s opened, covers <strong>one database connection</strong>. You genuinely cannot wrap &quot;save the order in SQL + publish to Service Bus + call the payment API&quot; in a single atomic transaction — those are three entirely separate systems with no shared commit protocol. The real answer for cross-service consistency is the <strong>outbox pattern</strong> (covered later in this chapter, and in full in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events\">Messaging &amp; events</a>) and sagas, not a bigger transaction.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"set-based-updates-and-deletes-skipping-the-entity-entirely\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-advanced/set-based-updates-and-deletes-skipping-the-entity-entirely\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Set-based updates and deletes: skipping the entity entirely <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>EF 7+ offers a second way to write to the database that deliberately skips the tracking machinery altogether, running a set-based SQL statement <strong>without loading any entities into memory first</strong>:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// UPDATE Products SET Price = Price * 1.1 WHERE CategoryId = 3</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> updated = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CategoryId</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteUpdateAsync</span>(s =&gt; s\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">SetProperty</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span>, p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> * <span class=\"tk-num\">1.1m</span>)\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">SetProperty</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UpdatedAt</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTime</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UtcNow</span>), ct);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// DELETE FROM Carts WHERE UpdatedAt &lt; @cutoff</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> deleted = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Carts</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(c =&gt; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UpdatedAt</span> &lt; cutoff).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteDeleteAsync</span>(ct);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Three consequences follow directly from &quot;no entities loaded,&quot; and each is worth knowing before you reach for this:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>They run <strong>immediately</strong> — not deferred until the next <code>SaveChanges()</code>.</li>\n<li>They <strong>bypass the change tracker</strong>: any already-tracked entity for the same rows stays stale in memory.</li>\n<li>They <strong>bypass SaveChanges interceptors</strong>: auditing or soft-delete logic registered there simply won&#39;t run.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Global query filters still apply to the generated <code>WHERE</code> clause, though, since those are baked into query translation rather than into <code>SaveChanges</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"interceptors-cross-cutting-behaviour-without-touching-busine\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-advanced/interceptors-cross-cutting-behaviour-without-touching-busine\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Interceptors: cross-cutting behaviour without touching business code <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Interceptors hook into specific points in EF Core&#39;s own pipeline, letting you add behaviour that applies uniformly without scattering calls throughout your business logic:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Interceptor</th>\n<th>Hooks</th>\n<th>Uses</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>SaveChangesInterceptor</code></td>\n<td>Before/after SaveChanges</td>\n<td>Audit fields, soft delete, domain events, outbox</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>DbCommandInterceptor</code></td>\n<td>Before/after SQL commands</td>\n<td>Query hints, logging slow SQL, tagging, modifying SQL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>DbConnectionInterceptor</code></td>\n<td>Connection open/close</td>\n<td>Setting session context (e.g. <code>SESSION_CONTEXT</code> for row-level security), access tokens</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>DbTransactionInterceptor</code></td>\n<td>Transaction events</td>\n<td>Diagnostics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>IMaterializationInterceptor</code></td>\n<td>Entity materialised</td>\n<td>Injecting services into entities, decryption</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IAuditable</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreatedAt</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>? <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreatedBy</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span>? <span class=\"tk-ty\">UpdatedAt</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>? <span class=\"tk-ty\">UpdatedBy</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } }\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AuditInterceptor</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeProvider</span> clock, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ICurrentUser</span> user) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesInterceptor</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">InterceptionResult</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">SavingChangesAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">DbContextEventData</span> e, <span class=\"tk-ty\">InterceptionResult</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; result, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct = <span class=\"tk-kw\">default</span>)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> now = clock.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetUtcNow</span>();\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> entry <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> e.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Context</span>!.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChangeTracker</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entries</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IAuditable</span>&gt;())\n        {\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (entry.<span class=\"tk-ty\">State</span> == <span class=\"tk-ty\">EntityState</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Added</span>) { entry.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreatedAt</span> = now; entry.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreatedBy</span> = user.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>; }\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (entry.<span class=\"tk-ty\">State</span> == <span class=\"tk-ty\">EntityState</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Modified</span>) { entry.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UpdatedAt</span> = now; entry.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UpdatedBy</span> = user.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>; }\n        }\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SavingChangesAsync</span>(e, result, ct);\n    }\n}\n\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuditInterceptor</span>&gt;();\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddDbContext</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>&gt;((sp, o) =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseSqlServer</span>(cs).<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddInterceptors</span>(sp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuditInterceptor</span>&gt;()));</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>This interceptor is never called explicitly anywhere. Every <code>IAuditable</code> entity gets its timestamps stamped automatically just by sitting in the change tracker when <code>SaveChanges</code> runs — that&#39;s the appeal: uniform behavior, with nothing for a developer to remember to call.</p>\n<h2 id=\"global-query-filters-soft-delete-and-multi-tenancy\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-advanced/global-query-filters-soft-delete-and-multi-tenancy\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Global query filters: soft delete and multi-tenancy <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>A filter defined once in the model gets automatically appended to <strong>every</strong> query against that entity type, with no need to repeat it anywhere it&#39;s queried:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">DbContextOptions</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>&gt; o, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ITenantContext</span> tenant) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">DbContext</span>(o)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OnModelCreating</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ModelBuilder</span> b)\n    {\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasQueryFilter</span>(p =&gt; !p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsDeleted</span>);                       <span class=\"tk-com\">// soft delete</span>\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasQueryFilter</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TenantId</span> == tenant.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TenantId</span>);         <span class=\"tk-com\">// multi-tenancy: evaluated per context instance</span>\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> all = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToListAsync</span>();                         <span class=\"tk-com\">// WHERE IsDeleted = 0 (automatically)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> incl = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IgnoreQueryFilters</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToListAsync</span>();   <span class=\"tk-com\">// admin views, restore screens</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The multi-tenancy line deserves a second look: because the filter closes over <code>tenant.TenantId</code> from a <em>scoped</em> <code>ITenantContext</code>, it&#39;s evaluated fresh for each <code>DbContext</code> instance — meaning the same model configuration automatically scopes every single query to whichever tenant the current request belongs to, with no developer anywhere having to remember to add <code>WHERE TenantId = ...</code> by hand.</p>\n<p>Soft delete itself usually pairs this filter with an interceptor that intercepts the <em>delete</em> and turns it into an update instead:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SoftDeleteInterceptor</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesInterceptor</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">InterceptionResult</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">SavingChangesAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">DbContextEventData</span> e, <span class=\"tk-ty\">InterceptionResult</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; r, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct = <span class=\"tk-kw\">default</span>)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> entry <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> e.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Context</span>!.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChangeTracker</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entries</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ISoftDeletable</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(x =&gt; x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">State</span> == <span class=\"tk-ty\">EntityState</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Deleted</span>))\n        {\n            entry.<span class=\"tk-ty\">State</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">EntityState</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Modified</span>;\n            entry.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsDeleted</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;\n            entry.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DeletedAt</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UtcNow</span>;\n        }\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SavingChangesAsync</span>(e, r, ct);\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Filters and required navigations</div><p>Here&#39;s a subtle interaction worth watching for: if <code>Order</code> has a <em>required</em> navigation to a soft-deleted <code>Customer</code>, <code>Include(o =&gt; o.Customer)</code> generates an <strong>INNER JOIN</strong>, and the customer&#39;s own soft-delete filter applies to that join too — which can make the <strong>order itself silently disappear</strong> from results, even though nobody asked to filter orders at all. The fix is to configure the navigation as optional, or apply filters consistently across every entity in a relationship chain so this mismatch can&#39;t arise. EF 10 adds <strong>named filters</strong>, letting you disable one specific filter selectively rather than all-or-nothing. For multi-tenancy specifically, also consider database-level <strong>row-level security</strong> as a second line of defence — the application-level filter is one safeguard, not the only one.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"domain-events-and-the-outbox-on-savechanges\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-advanced/domain-events-and-the-outbox-on-savechanges\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Domain events and the outbox on SaveChanges <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Aggregates raise <strong>domain events</strong> (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ddd\">DDD</a>) as things happen inside them; the question this section answers is how those events reliably get out of the aggregate and into something that can actually publish them:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">abstract</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IDomainEvent</span>&gt; _events = [];\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IReadOnlyList</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IDomainEvent</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">DomainEvents</span> =&gt; _events;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Raise</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IDomainEvent</span> e) =&gt; _events.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(e);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ClearDomainEvents</span>() =&gt; _events.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Clear</span>();\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Outbox interceptor: turn domain events into outbox rows IN THE SAME TRANSACTION as the business data</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OutboxInterceptor</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeProvider</span> clock) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesInterceptor</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">InterceptionResult</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">SavingChangesAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">DbContextEventData</span> e, <span class=\"tk-ty\">InterceptionResult</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; r, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct = <span class=\"tk-kw\">default</span>)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> ctx = e.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Context</span>!;\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> events = ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChangeTracker</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entries</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">SelectMany</span>(x =&gt; { <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> list = x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DomainEvents</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToList</span>(); x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ClearDomainEvents</span>(); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> list; }).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToList</span>();\n        ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Set</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OutboxMessage</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddRange</span>(events.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(ev =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OutboxMessage</span>\n        {\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NewGuid</span>(), <span class=\"tk-ty\">Type</span> = ev.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetType</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">FullName</span>!, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Payload</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">JsonSerializer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Serialize</span>(ev, ev.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetType</span>()), <span class=\"tk-ty\">OccurredAt</span> = clock.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetUtcNow</span>()\n        }));\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SavingChangesAsync</span>(e, r, ct);\n    }\n}\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// A background worker then publishes unprocessed OutboxMessages to the broker (see Messaging &amp; events).</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>This shape matters because the outbox rows are written inside the <em>same</em> <code>SaveChanges()</code> transaction as the business data: the order and its &quot;order placed&quot; event either both commit or both roll back. There&#39;s no window where one happens without the other — which sidesteps the &quot;no distributed transaction&quot; problem from earlier in this chapter entirely, rather than solving it. MassTransit, NServiceBus and Wolverine ship <strong>ready-made EF Core outboxes</strong>, so this pattern rarely needs to be hand-rolled in practice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"other-advanced-features\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-advanced/other-advanced-features\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Other advanced features <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>What it gives you</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Temporal tables</strong> (SQL Server)</td>\n<td><code>b.ToTable(t =&gt; t.IsTemporal())</code>; query history with <code>TemporalAsOf(date)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>JSON columns</strong></td>\n<td>Nested objects/collections in one column, queryable (<code>ToJson()</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Primitive collections</strong></td>\n<td><code>List&lt;string&gt; Tags</code> stored as JSON, queryable with <code>Contains</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Raw SQL for unmapped types</strong></td>\n<td><code>Database.SqlQuery&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Keyless entity types</strong></td>\n<td>Map views/functions (<code>HasNoKey().ToView(&quot;v_Sales&quot;)</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>User-defined function mapping</strong></td>\n<td>Call SQL functions from LINQ (<code>HasDbFunction</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sequences, HiLo</strong></td>\n<td>Key generation strategies that reduce round trips</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Multiple DbContexts</strong></td>\n<td>One per bounded context or module (modular monolith), each with its own migrations/schema</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Vector search</strong> (EF 10 / SQL Server 2025 / Azure SQL)</td>\n<td><code>SqlVector&lt;float&gt;</code> with <code>VECTOR_DISTANCE</code> for AI similarity search</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Read replicas</strong></td>\n<td>A second context/connection string pointing at a read-only replica for queries</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"testing-ef-core-code\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-advanced/testing-ef-core-code\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Testing EF Core code <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Approach</th>\n<th>Fidelity</th>\n<th>Speed</th>\n<th>Verdict</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>EF <strong>InMemory</strong> provider</td>\n<td>Low: no SQL, no constraints, no transactions, different query semantics</td>\n<td>Fast</td>\n<td><strong>Avoid</strong> (Microsoft discourages it for testing)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SQLite in-memory</strong></td>\n<td>Medium: real SQL, but a different dialect than SQL Server/Postgres</td>\n<td>Fast</td>\n<td>OK for simple cases</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Real database in a container</strong> (Testcontainers)</td>\n<td><strong>High</strong></td>\n<td>Seconds per suite</td>\n<td><strong>Recommended</strong> for repositories/queries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Repository interface mocked</td>\n<td>N/A (doesn&#39;t test EF at all)</td>\n<td>Fast</td>\n<td>For unit-testing <em>application</em> logic only</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Take that low InMemory rating seriously: it isn&#39;t a relational database at all, so it can&#39;t enforce constraints, doesn&#39;t support real transactions, and translates some LINQ differently than any real provider does (Microsoft&#39;s own docs say as much — see Further reading). A test can pass against InMemory while the same query fails, or behaves differently, against the real SQL Server it runs on in production. See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/integration-testing\">Integration testing</a> for Testcontainers and Respawn, which close that gap by testing against the real engine.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How does optimistic concurrency work in EF Core?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Mark a concurrency token (a SQL Server rowversion with [Timestamp]/IsRowVersion, or a property with IsConcurrencyToken). EF includes the original value in the UPDATE/DELETE WHERE clause; if no row is affected, it throws DbUpdateConcurrencyException, which you handle by reloading, merging, retrying or returning 409/412 to the client.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Optimistic vs pessimistic concurrency?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Optimistic: no locks on read; conflicts are detected at write time via versions. It scales well and suits web apps where conflicts are rare. Pessimistic: lock rows when reading (UPDLOCK / FOR UPDATE) so others wait. It suits high-contention critical sections but reduces concurrency and risks deadlocks.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Is SaveChanges transactional? When do you need an explicit transaction?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Yes: one SaveChanges is a single transaction. Use BeginTransaction when multiple SaveChanges, ExecuteUpdate/raw SQL or Dapper calls must be atomic, or you need a specific isolation level. With retrying execution strategies, wrap the whole transaction in strategy.ExecuteAsync.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are ExecuteUpdate and ExecuteDelete, and what are their caveats?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>EF 7+ methods that run set-based UPDATE/DELETE statements directly from a LINQ filter without loading entities, which is much faster for bulk changes. They execute immediately, bypass the change tracker and SaveChanges interceptors (so auditing and soft delete there won&#39;t run), and tracked entities in memory aren&#39;t updated.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How would you implement soft delete in EF Core?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>An IsDeleted (and DeletedAt) column, a global query filter <code>HasQueryFilter(e =&gt; !e.IsDeleted)</code> so normal queries exclude deleted rows, and a SaveChanges interceptor that converts Deleted states into Modified with IsDeleted = true. Use IgnoreQueryFilters for admin or restore scenarios, and consider filtered unique indexes.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How would you implement multi-tenancy with EF Core?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Options: a TenantId column with a global query filter based on a scoped tenant service (plus row-level security as defence in depth); a schema per tenant; or a database per tenant with the connection string chosen per request. Each trades isolation against cost and operational complexity.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are EF Core interceptors used for?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Cross-cutting hooks into EF operations: SaveChanges interceptors for auditing, soft delete, domain events and outbox; command interceptors for logging, hints or tagging SQL; connection interceptors for session context or tokens; materialization interceptors for post-processing entities.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why not use the InMemory provider for tests?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>It isn&#39;t a relational database: it doesn&#39;t enforce constraints, transactions or SQL semantics, and it translates queries differently, so tests can pass while real queries fail. Use the real engine via Testcontainers (or at least SQLite) for data access tests.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-advanced/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ef/core/saving/concurrency\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Handling concurrency conflicts</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ef/core/saving/transactions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Transactions</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ef/core/saving/execute-insert-update-delete\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ExecuteUpdate and ExecuteDelete</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ef/core/logging-events-diagnostics/interceptors\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Interceptors</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ef/core/querying/filters\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Global query filters</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ef/core/testing/choosing-a-testing-strategy\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Choosing a testing strategy</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/efcore-advanced/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>The checks below exercise optimistic concurrency, <code>ExecuteUpdate</code>, global query filters and a <code>SaveChangesInterceptor</code> against a real (if tiny) SQLite database. Save as a console app (<code>dotnet new console</code>, <code>dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite</code>), then <code>dotnet run</code>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">EntityFrameworkCore</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">EntityFrameworkCore</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Diagnostics</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> connString = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;DataSource=file:verifydb2?mode=memory&amp;cache=shared&quot;</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> conn = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Data</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sqlite</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SqliteConnection</span>(connString);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OpenAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> opts = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DbContextOptionsBuilder</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseSqlite</span>(conn).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Options</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> seed = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>(opts))\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> seed.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Database</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">EnsureCreatedAsync</span>();\n    seed.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Widget&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Version</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsDeleted</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span> });\n    seed.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Gizmo&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Version</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsDeleted</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span> });\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> seed.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>();\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: optimistic concurrency — a stale version throws DbUpdateConcurrencyException.</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> ctx1 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>(opts);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> p1 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> ctx1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IgnoreQueryFilters</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">SingleAsync</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>); <span class=\"tk-com\">// Version = 1</span>\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> ctx2 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>(opts))\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> p2 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> ctx2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IgnoreQueryFilters</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">SingleAsync</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n        p2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>;\n        p2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Version</span>++; <span class=\"tk-com\">// app increments the token manually, as the chapter describes for non-SQL-Server providers</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> ctx2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>(); <span class=\"tk-com\">// succeeds: DB Version was 1, matches p2's original value</span>\n    }\n\n    p1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Gadget&quot;</span>;\n    p1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Version</span>++; <span class=\"tk-com\">// p1 still thinks the current version is 1, but the DB is now at 2</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> threw = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> ctx1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>(); }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">DbUpdateConcurrencyException</span>) { threw = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!threw) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected DbUpdateConcurrencyException&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: a stale concurrency token throws DbUpdateConcurrencyException&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: ExecuteUpdate bypasses the change tracker — an already-tracked entity is not refreshed.</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> ctx = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>(opts);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> tracked = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IgnoreQueryFilters</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">SingleAsync</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>); <span class=\"tk-com\">// Price = 20 after the test above</span>\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IgnoreQueryFilters</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>)\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteUpdateAsync</span>(s =&gt; s.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SetProperty</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">999m</span>));\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (tracked.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">999m</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: ExecuteUpdate updated the tracked instance&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: ExecuteUpdate leaves an already-tracked in-memory entity stale&quot;</span>);\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> fresh = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>(opts);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> reread = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> fresh.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IgnoreQueryFilters</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">SingleAsync</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (reread.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> != <span class=\"tk-num\">999m</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: the database wasn't actually updated&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: ExecuteUpdate did write the new value to the database&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: a global query filter (soft delete) excludes rows by default, and IgnoreQueryFilters() includes them.</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> ctx = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>(opts);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> visible = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToListAsync</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (visible.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Any</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: soft-deleted row leaked through the default filter&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: a global query filter hides soft-deleted rows by default&quot;</span>);\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> all = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IgnoreQueryFilters</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToListAsync</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!all.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Any</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: IgnoreQueryFilters() didn't bring the row back&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: IgnoreQueryFilters() brings soft-deleted rows back&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: a SaveChangesInterceptor runs for every SaveChanges, without being called explicitly anywhere.</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> auditOpts = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DbContextOptionsBuilder</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuditedShopDb</span>&gt;()\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseSqlite</span>(conn)\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddInterceptors</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">StampingInterceptor</span>())\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Options</span>;\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> ctx = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AuditedShopDb</span>(auditOpts);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> p = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IgnoreQueryFilters</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">SingleAsync</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n    p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Stamped&quot;</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UpdatedBy</span> != <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;interceptor&quot;</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: interceptor did not stamp the entity&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: the SaveChangesInterceptor stamped the entity without being called explicitly&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All checks passed.&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here (needs SQL Server/Postgres-specific features or real infra failures):</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - rowversion columns auto-incremented by the database itself (SQL Server/Postgres xmin) — see Further reading: &quot;Handling concurrency conflicts&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - execution-strategy retries against real transient Azure SQL failures — see Further reading: &quot;Transactions&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - temporal tables, JSON columns, vector search — SQL Server/Azure SQL-specific; see the EF Core docs linked in Further reading</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Version</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsDeleted</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>? <span class=\"tk-ty\">UpdatedBy</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">DbContextOptions</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>&gt; options) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">DbContext</span>(options)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DbSet</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Set</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>&gt;();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OnModelCreating</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ModelBuilder</span> b)\n    {\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Property</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Version</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsConcurrencyToken</span>();\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasQueryFilter</span>(p =&gt; !p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsDeleted</span>);\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AuditedShopDb</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">DbContextOptions</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuditedShopDb</span>&gt; options) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">DbContext</span>(options)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DbSet</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Set</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>&gt;();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OnModelCreating</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ModelBuilder</span> b)\n    {\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Property</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Version</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsConcurrencyToken</span>();\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasQueryFilter</span>(p =&gt; !p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsDeleted</span>);\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">StampingInterceptor</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesInterceptor</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">InterceptionResult</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">SavingChangesAsync</span>(\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">DbContextEventData</span> eventData, <span class=\"tk-ty\">InterceptionResult</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; result, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct = <span class=\"tk-kw\">default</span>)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> entry <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> eventData.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Context</span>!.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChangeTracker</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entries</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(e =&gt; e.<span class=\"tk-ty\">State</span> == <span class=\"tk-ty\">EntityState</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Modified</span>))\n            entry.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UpdatedBy</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;interceptor&quot;</span>;\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SavingChangesAsync</span>(eventData, result, ct);\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"optimistic-concurrency-catching-a-lost-update-without-ever-l","text":"Optimistic concurrency: catching a lost update without ever locking","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"transactions-when-savechangess-own-transaction-isnt-enough","text":"Transactions: when SaveChanges's own transaction isn't enough","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"execution-strategies-connection-resiliency","text":"Execution strategies (connection resiliency)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"set-based-updates-and-deletes-skipping-the-entity-entirely","text":"Set-based updates and deletes: skipping the entity entirely","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"interceptors-cross-cutting-behaviour-without-touching-busine","text":"Interceptors: cross-cutting behaviour without touching business code","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"global-query-filters-soft-delete-and-multi-tenancy","text":"Global query filters: soft delete and multi-tenancy","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"domain-events-and-the-outbox-on-savechanges","text":"Domain events and the outbox on SaveChanges","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"other-advanced-features","text":"Other advanced features","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"testing-ef-core-code","text":"Testing EF Core code","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":4147,"minutes":19,"num":"4.5","part":"p4"},"nosql-redis":{"id":"nosql-redis","title":"NoSQL, Redis and Choosing a Data Store","summary":"Beyond relational. The NoSQL families and their trade-offs, CAP and consistency models, document modelling and partitioning (Cosmos DB, MongoDB), Redis data structures and patterns (cache, locks, rate limits, pub/sub, streams), search and vector stores, and a decision guide for picking storage.","layer":"data","where":"Data & state › Non-relational stores & caches","fits":"<p>These are alternative layer-5 stores. Redis is also the backing store for distributed caching (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors\">Caching</a>), SignalR scale-out (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/app-models\">App models</a>) and distributed locks (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/background-work\">Background work</a>). On Azure they&#39;re managed services (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-data-messaging\">Azure data &amp; messaging</a>).</p>\n","prereqs":["relational-fundamentals"],"next":["azure-data-messaging","caching-ratelimit-cors","design-principles"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/nosql-redis/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>&quot;NoSQL&quot; is a grab-bag, not one thing: key-value, document, wide-column, graph, search, time-series and vector stores each trade away general-purpose flexibility to be very good at one access pattern.</li>\n<li><strong>CAP theorem</strong>: during a network partition, a distributed store must pick consistency (always correct) or availability (always responding, maybe stale) — not both. <strong>PACELC</strong> adds that even without a partition, you still trade latency for consistency.</li>\n<li>Document databases model by <strong>access pattern, not normal form</strong>: store together what you read together, even if that duplicates data (and that duplication is often correct, not a bug).</li>\n<li>In Cosmos DB, the <strong>partition key</strong> you pick decides cost and scalability for the container&#39;s lifetime — a low-cardinality key like <code>date</code> creates a hot partition that every write piles onto.</li>\n<li><strong>Redis</strong> is an in-memory data-structure server, not just a cache: strings, hashes, lists, sets, sorted sets and streams give you ready-made algorithms (ranking, rate limiting, locks), not just key-value storage.</li>\n<li>Default to relational for business data; reach for a NoSQL store for a specific, deliberate reason (scale, flexible schema, a specialised access pattern) — not just to dodge schema migrations.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-nosql-families-optimised-for-an-access-pattern-not-a-fea\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/nosql-redis/the-nosql-families-optimised-for-an-access-pattern-not-a-fea\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The NoSQL families: optimised for an access pattern, not a feature list <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>&quot;NoSQL&quot; is really a negative definition — &quot;not (only) relational tables&quot; — which hides how different the actual families are from each other. Each one made a deliberate trade to get extremely good at one access pattern, usually at the cost of the general-purpose flexibility a relational table gives you for free:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Family</th>\n<th>Data model</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n<th>Good at</th>\n<th>Weak at</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Key-value</strong></td>\n<td>Key → value (opaque or simple structures)</td>\n<td><strong>Redis</strong>, Azure Table Storage, DynamoDB (KV mode)</td>\n<td>Ultra-fast lookups by key, caching, counters, sessions</td>\n<td>Queries by anything but the key</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Document</strong></td>\n<td>JSON-like documents, flexible schema</td>\n<td><strong>Cosmos DB (NoSQL API)</strong>, <strong>MongoDB</strong>, Couchbase</td>\n<td>Aggregates read and written as a whole, varying schemas, horizontal scale</td>\n<td>Joins across documents, multi-document transactions (limited)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Wide-column</strong></td>\n<td>Rows with dynamic columns, partitioned</td>\n<td>Cassandra, ScyllaDB, Cosmos DB for Cassandra</td>\n<td>Massive write throughput, time series</td>\n<td>Ad-hoc queries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Graph</strong></td>\n<td>Nodes + edges</td>\n<td>Neo4j, Cosmos DB for Gremlin</td>\n<td>Relationship traversal (social, recommendations, fraud)</td>\n<td>Bulk aggregations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Search</strong></td>\n<td>Inverted indexes</td>\n<td>Elasticsearch/OpenSearch, Azure AI Search</td>\n<td>Full-text, faceting, relevance</td>\n<td>Being the source of truth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Time series</strong></td>\n<td>Timestamped measurements</td>\n<td>InfluxDB, Azure Data Explorer, TimescaleDB</td>\n<td>Metrics, IoT</td>\n<td>General-purpose data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Vector</strong></td>\n<td>Embeddings + similarity indexes</td>\n<td>Azure AI Search, Cosmos DB vector search, pgvector, Qdrant</td>\n<td>Semantic search, RAG for AI features</td>\n<td>Exact queries</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-why\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Why not always use SQL?</div><p>Relational databases are the right default for business data: strong consistency, transactions, joins, mature tooling. NoSQL wins when you need <strong>horizontal scale beyond one server</strong>, <strong>very low latency at huge volume</strong>, <strong>flexible or nested schemas</strong>, <strong>global distribution</strong>, or a <strong>specialised access pattern</strong> (search, graph, vectors). Many systems use both: <strong>polyglot persistence</strong>.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"cap-pacelc-and-consistency-models-the-trade-off-a-distribute\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/nosql-redis/cap-pacelc-and-consistency-models-the-trade-off-a-distribute\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>CAP, PACELC and consistency models: the trade-off a distributed store can&#39;t avoid <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>CAP theorem</strong>, in plain terms: imagine two servers holding the same data, and the network link between them breaks (a <strong>P</strong>artition). Networks genuinely do this — it&#39;s not a hypothetical edge case. While they&#39;re cut off from each other, each server must pick one of two bad options: refuse to answer, or guarantee correctness (<strong>C</strong>onsistency), or answer anyway and risk giving out stale data, but stay responsive (<strong>A</strong>vailability). You can&#39;t have both <em>during that break</em> — that&#39;s the whole theorem. Once the network heals, both are possible again.</p>\n<p><strong>PACELC</strong> asks the question CAP doesn&#39;t: what do you trade when there&#39;s <em>no</em> partition at all (the <strong>E</strong>lse case)? Even then, you trade <strong>L</strong>atency against <strong>C</strong>onsistency — because waiting for every replica to confirm a write (strong consistency) is always slower than writing to one and letting the others catch up later (eventual consistency, with a small window where they disagree).</p>\n<p>Consistency models, in practice, form a spectrum from strongest to weakest, and the right choice depends entirely on what the data is actually for:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Model</th>\n<th>Guarantee</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Strong / linearizable</strong></td>\n<td>Reads always return the latest committed write</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Bounded staleness</strong></td>\n<td>Reads lag by at most K versions or T time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Session</strong></td>\n<td>Within a client session: read-your-writes, monotonic reads (Cosmos DB&#39;s default; ideal for most apps)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Consistent prefix</strong></td>\n<td>You never see writes out of order</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Eventual</strong></td>\n<td>Replicas converge eventually; no ordering guarantees</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Cosmos DB lets you choose any of these five per account (and relax per request) — which is a useful thing to know specifically because it means the consistency decision isn&#39;t locked in once at the infrastructure level; it&#39;s a knob you can turn per query based on what that particular read actually needs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"document-databases-modelling-by-access-pattern-not-by-normal\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/nosql-redis/document-databases-modelling-by-access-pattern-not-by-normal\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Document databases: modelling by access pattern, not by normal form <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>The relational instinct, drilled into you by <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/normalisation-and-when-to-break-it\">Relational fundamentals</a>, is to normalise: store each fact once, join at read time. Document modelling runs in the opposite direction on purpose: <strong>design for the access pattern</strong> — store together whatever gets <em>read</em> together, even if that means the same fact appears in more than one document.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>JSON</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Cosmos DB document: an order as one aggregate, with lines embedded</span>\n{\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;id&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ord-1042&quot;</span>,\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;customerId&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;cust-7&quot;</span>,          <span class=\"tk-com\">// partition key</span>\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;type&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;order&quot;</span>,\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;status&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Paid&quot;</span>,\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;createdAt&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;2026-10-27T14:00:00Z&quot;</span>,\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;shippingAddress&quot;</span>: { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;street&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Main St 1&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;city&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Berlin&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;country&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;DE&quot;</span> },\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;lines&quot;</span>: [\n    { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;sku&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;KB-01&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;name&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Keyboard&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;qty&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;unitPrice&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">49.90</span> },\n    { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;sku&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;MS-02&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;name&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Mouse&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;qty&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;unitPrice&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">19.90</span> }\n  ],\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;total&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">89.70</span>,\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;_etag&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;\\&quot;0000d...\\&quot;&quot;</span>          <span class=\"tk-com\">// optimistic concurrency</span>\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Embed when…</th>\n<th>Reference (store an ID) when…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Data is read together (order + lines)</td>\n<td>Data is shared by many parents (product catalogue)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Child data is bounded in size</td>\n<td>It grows without bound (all of a customer&#39;s orders)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Child changes with the parent</td>\n<td>It changes independently and often</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>You need atomic updates of the whole</td>\n<td>You&#39;d otherwise duplicate large data</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Denormalisation is normal here, by design</strong>: copying a product&#39;s <em>name</em> into the order line isn&#39;t a mistake to clean up later — it&#39;s genuinely the correct historical record of what that line said at the time of purchase, exactly the same &quot;snapshot data is not duplication&quot; principle <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/normalisation-and-when-to-break-it\">Relational fundamentals</a> raised for relational schemas too. Where copies do need to stay roughly in sync with a source of truth, change feeds or events are the usual mechanism, covered next.</p>\n<h2 id=\"partitioning-the-one-modelling-decision-you-cant-easily-undo\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/nosql-redis/partitioning-the-one-modelling-decision-you-cant-easily-undo\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Partitioning: the one modelling decision you can&#39;t easily undo <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Distributed databases split data across physical <strong>partitions</strong> by a <strong>partition key</strong>, and in Cosmos DB specifically, this single choice ends up determining <strong>scalability, cost, and which queries are cheap</strong> for the lifetime of the container:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>High cardinality</strong> (many distinct values) so data and load spread evenly.</li>\n<li><strong>Even request distribution</strong>: avoid <strong>hot partitions</strong> (e.g. partitioning by <code>date</code> sends all of today&#39;s writes to one partition).</li>\n<li><strong>Most queries filter by it</strong>: single-partition queries are cheap; cross-partition fan-out queries cost more Request Units and are slower.</li>\n<li>Transactions (stored procedures, transactional batch) are limited to <strong>one logical partition</strong>.</li>\n<li>A logical partition has a size limit (20 GB), so use <strong>hierarchical partition keys</strong> (e.g. tenantId → userId) for large tenants.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Notice how directly the first three bullets connect: picking <code>customerId</code> as the partition key (as the JSON document above does) means every query scoped to one customer — the overwhelmingly common case for an e-commerce order history — stays within a single partition, cheap and fast. Had the key been <code>createdAt</code> instead, every write for &quot;today&quot; would pile onto the same partition while yesterday&#39;s sat idle, exactly the hot-partition failure the second bullet warns about.</p>\n<p>Cosmos DB charges in <strong>Request Units (RU/s)</strong>, provisioned or serverless/autoscale. A 1 KB point read by id + partition key costs about 1 RU; queries cost more, which is precisely why the SDK example below reads <code>RequestCharge</code> directly — it&#39;s the concrete feedback loop for whether a given access pattern is actually cheap.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Cosmos DB .NET SDK (singleton CosmosClient)</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSingleton</span>(_ =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CosmosClient</span>(endpoint, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DefaultAzureCredential</span>(),\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CosmosClientOptions</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">SerializerOptions</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>() { <span class=\"tk-ty\">PropertyNamingPolicy</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">CosmosPropertyNamingPolicy</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CamelCase</span> } }));\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> container = cosmos.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetContainer</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;shop&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;orders&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> container.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateItemAsync</span>(order, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PartitionKey</span>(order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>), cancellationToken: ct);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> read = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> container.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadItemAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderDoc</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ord-1042&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PartitionKey</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;cust-7&quot;</span>), cancellationToken: ct);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// point read: cheapest</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(read.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequestCharge</span>);                                                                                 <span class=\"tk-com\">// RU cost</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> query = container.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetItemLinqQueryable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderDoc</span>&gt;(requestOptions: <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>() { <span class=\"tk-ty\">PartitionKey</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PartitionKey</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;cust-7&quot;</span>) })\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Paid&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderByDescending</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreatedAt</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToFeedIterator</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">while</span> (query.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasMoreResults</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> o <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> query.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadNextAsync</span>(ct)) { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* … */</span> }\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Optimistic concurrency with ETag</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> container.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReplaceItemAsync</span>(updated, updated.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PartitionKey</span>(updated.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>),\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ItemRequestOptions</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">IfMatchEtag</span> = read.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ETag</span> }, ct);          <span class=\"tk-com\">// 412 if changed meanwhile</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice that last call leans on the exact same optimistic-concurrency idea as EF Core&#39;s <code>RowVersion</code> from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-advanced/optimistic-concurrency-catching-a-lost-update-without-ever-l\">EF Core advanced</a> — an <code>ETag</code> here plays precisely the same role, detecting a concurrent write rather than preventing it with a lock.</p>\n<p>The <strong>change feed</strong> streams every insert and update, in order, per partition, and it&#39;s the foundation most Cosmos DB integrations are quietly built on top of: materialised views, event-driven integration with other services, and keeping a search index in sync without the application code ever having to explicitly push updates to it. MongoDB offers the equivalent capability as change streams; EF Core does ship a Cosmos provider, but the native SDK shown above gives more direct control, especially over RU cost.</p>\n<h2 id=\"redis-an-in-memory-data-structure-server-not-just-a-cache\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/nosql-redis/redis-an-in-memory-data-structure-server-not-just-a-cache\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Redis: an in-memory data-structure server, not just a cache <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Redis</strong> is often introduced purely as &quot;a cache,&quot; which undersells what it actually is: an in-memory data-structure server offering sub-millisecond operations, optional persistence (RDB snapshots, an AOF log), replication, and clustering. On Azure: <strong>Azure Managed Redis</strong> (successor of Azure Cache for Redis). Client: <strong>StackExchange.Redis</strong>. Open-source forks and alternatives include <strong>Valkey</strong> and <strong>Garnet</strong> (Microsoft&#39;s Redis-compatible server written in C#).</p>\n<h3 id=\"data-structures\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/nosql-redis/data-structures\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Data structures</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Commands</th>\n<th>Backend uses</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>String</strong></td>\n<td><code>GET</code>, <code>SET key val EX 60 NX</code>, <code>INCR</code></td>\n<td>Cache values, counters, distributed locks, idempotency keys</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hash</strong></td>\n<td><code>HSET</code>, <code>HGET</code>, <code>HINCRBY</code></td>\n<td>Objects with fields (session, cart)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>List</strong></td>\n<td><code>LPUSH</code>, <code>RPOP</code>, <code>BLPOP</code></td>\n<td>Simple queues, recent-items lists</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Set</strong></td>\n<td><code>SADD</code>, <code>SISMEMBER</code>, <code>SINTER</code></td>\n<td>Unique visitors, tags, &quot;who&#39;s online&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sorted set</strong></td>\n<td><code>ZADD</code>, <code>ZRANGE</code>, <code>ZINCRBY</code></td>\n<td>Leaderboards, priority queues, sliding-window rate limits, time indexes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Stream</strong></td>\n<td><code>XADD</code>, <code>XREADGROUP</code>, <code>XACK</code></td>\n<td>Durable-ish event log with consumer groups</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pub/Sub</strong></td>\n<td><code>PUBLISH</code>, <code>SUBSCRIBE</code></td>\n<td>Fire-and-forget broadcast (SignalR backplane, cache invalidation)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HyperLogLog, Bitmaps, Geo</strong></td>\n<td><code>PFADD</code>, <code>SETBIT</code>, <code>GEOSEARCH</code></td>\n<td>Approximate counts, flags, location queries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>JSON, Search, Vector</strong> (modules)</td>\n<td><code>JSON.SET</code>, <code>FT.SEARCH</code></td>\n<td>Document storage, secondary indexes, vector similarity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IConnectionMultiplexer</span>&gt;(_ =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">ConnectionMultiplexer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Connect</span>(redisConnectionString));   <span class=\"tk-com\">// ONE per app: thread-safe, multiplexed</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">RedisPatterns</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IConnectionMultiplexer</span> mux)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IDatabase</span> _db = mux.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetDatabase</span>();\n\n    <span class=\"tk-com\">// Counter with expiry: simple fixed-window rate limit</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">AllowAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> user, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> limit)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> key = <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;rl:{user}:{DateTime.UtcNow:yyyyMMddHHmm}&quot;</span>;\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> count = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> _db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StringIncrementAsync</span>(key);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (count == <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> _db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">KeyExpireAsync</span>(key, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromMinutes</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>));\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> count &lt;= limit;\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-com\">// Leaderboard</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoreAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> player, <span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> points) =&gt; _db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SortedSetIncrementAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;leaderboard&quot;</span>, player, points);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">SortedSetEntry</span>[]&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">TopAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> n) =&gt; _db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SortedSetRangeByRankWithScoresAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;leaderboard&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, n - <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Descending</span>);\n\n    <span class=\"tk-com\">// Distributed lock: SET NX with expiry, release only if we own it</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">TryRunExclusiveAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> name, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span> ttl, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&gt; work)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> token = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NewGuid</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>();\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> _db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LockTakeAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;lock:{name}&quot;</span>, token, ttl)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> work(); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>; }\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">finally</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> _db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LockReleaseAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;lock:{name}&quot;</span>, token); }\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>That rate-limiter and leaderboard both lean on the same insight: Redis&#39;s data structures aren&#39;t just storage, they&#39;re the <em>algorithm</em> — a sorted set naturally maintains ranking as scores update, so &quot;top 10&quot; is a direct <code>ZRANGE</code> call rather than something your application code has to compute by sorting a fetched list itself.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Redis pitfalls</div><ul>\n<li><strong>Create one <code>ConnectionMultiplexer</code></strong> and reuse it for the app&#39;s lifetime — creating one per request is the exact same mistake as the HttpClient socket-exhaustion bug from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/http-client/the-two-classic-bugs-and-why-theyre-opposite-mistakes\">Calling other services</a>, just against a different protocol.</li>\n<li><strong>Big keys and hot keys</strong> (a 50 MB value, one key hit by every single request) hurt latency for <em>everyone</em> sharing that shard, because Redis processes commands mostly single-threaded per shard — a slow operation on one key blocks the queue behind it.</li>\n<li><strong><code>KEYS *</code></strong> blocks the server while it scans the entire keyspace. Use <code>SCAN</code>, which walks it incrementally instead.</li>\n<li><strong>Memory is the limit</strong>: set <code>maxmemory</code> and an eviction policy (<code>allkeys-lru</code> for caches), and always set TTLs on cache keys — without one, a cache silently becomes a permanent, ever-growing store.</li>\n<li><strong>Pub/Sub isn&#39;t durable</strong>: a subscriber that&#39;s offline when a message is published simply never receives it, with no retry or queue underneath. Use Streams or a real broker for anything that must be delivered.</li>\n<li>Locks with a TTL can expire while the protected work is still running, letting a second caller start before the first has actually finished. Use fencing tokens, or simply make the protected work idempotent, for genuinely critical sections.</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"search-and-vector-stores\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/nosql-redis/search-and-vector-stores\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Search and vector stores <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Databases, relational or document, are poor at relevance-ranked full-text search, typo tolerance, facets, and synonyms — that&#39;s simply not the problem they were built to solve. The common pattern keeps the <strong>source of truth</strong> in SQL or Cosmos and <strong>projects</strong> a copy into a dedicated search index, kept in sync via the change feed or events covered earlier:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Azure AI Search</strong> (managed; full-text + vector + hybrid search, semantic ranking), <strong>Elasticsearch/OpenSearch</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Vector search</strong> powers AI features (semantic search, RAG): store embeddings next to documents in Azure AI Search, Cosmos DB, PostgreSQL <strong>pgvector</strong>, SQL Server 2025/Azure SQL vector type, Redis, Qdrant. .NET&#39;s <strong>Microsoft.Extensions.VectorData</strong> abstractions and <strong>Microsoft.Extensions.AI</strong> give provider-neutral APIs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"choosing-a-data-store\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/nosql-redis/choosing-a-data-store\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Choosing a data store <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Business data with relationships, transactions, reporting?          → SQL Server / PostgreSQL (default)\nAggregates read/written whole, flexible schema, global scale, low latency by key? → Cosmos DB / MongoDB\nCache, sessions, counters, locks, leaderboards, rate limits?          → Redis\nFiles, images, exports, backups, large blobs?                         → Blob storage (store the URL in the DB)\nFull-text search, facets, relevance, semantic/vector search?          → Azure AI Search / Elasticsearch (as a projection)\nEvent stream for many consumers, replay, high volume?                 → Event Hubs / Kafka\nMetrics, logs, telemetry analytics?                                    → Azure Monitor / Data Explorer / time-series DB\nHighly connected data, path queries?                                   → Graph DB</code></pre></figure>\n<p><strong>Don&#39;t</strong> put large files in the database (use Blob Storage and store references), <strong>don&#39;t</strong> use a cache as the system of record, and <strong>don&#39;t</strong> choose NoSQL just to avoid schema migrations: schema-on-read still needs versioning (add a <code>schemaVersion</code> field and handle old shapes).</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>When would you choose a NoSQL database over a relational one?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>When access patterns are mostly by key or aggregate, the schema is flexible or nested, you need horizontal scale or global distribution beyond a single SQL server, or very low latency at high throughput, or a specialised model (graph, search, time series). Relational remains the default for transactional business data with joins and reporting.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Explain the CAP theorem.</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>In a distributed system, when a network partition occurs you can provide either consistency (all reads see the latest write or fail) or availability (all requests get a response, possibly stale), not both. PACELC adds that, even without partitions, you trade latency against consistency.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you choose a partition key in Cosmos DB?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>High cardinality, even distribution of storage and requests (no hot partitions), and present in most queries so they stay single-partition. Consider transactional scope (single partition only) and the 20 GB logical partition limit; use hierarchical keys for big tenants.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Embed vs reference in document modelling?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Embed data that&#39;s read and updated together and bounded in size (order lines in an order). Reference data that&#39;s shared, unbounded, or updated independently (products, a customer&#39;s orders). Model for the access patterns, and accept some denormalisation.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is Redis used for in .NET backends?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Distributed caching (IDistributedCache/HybridCache L2), session and cart storage, rate-limit counters, distributed locks, leaderboards and sorted indexes, pub/sub (SignalR backplane, cache invalidation) and streams. Use one shared ConnectionMultiplexer.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How would you implement a distributed lock with Redis, and what are the risks?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>SET key token NX PX ttl to acquire (only if absent, with expiry), and release only if the value still matches your token (LockTake/LockRelease in StackExchange.Redis, or a Lua script). Risks: the TTL can expire while work continues, and failover can lose locks. Use fencing tokens and idempotent work for critical cases.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why shouldn&#39;t you use Redis Pub/Sub for important events?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>It&#39;s fire-and-forget: messages aren&#39;t persisted, and subscribers that are offline or slow miss them. Use Redis Streams with consumer groups or a real message broker (Service Bus, RabbitMQ, Kafka) for reliable delivery.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/nosql-redis/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cosmos-db/nosql/modeling-data\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Azure Cosmos DB: modelling data</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cosmos-db/partitioning-overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Partitioning and horizontal scaling in Cosmos DB</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://stackexchange.github.io/StackExchange.Redis/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">StackExchange.Redis documentation</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://redis.io/docs/latest/develop/data-types/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Redis data types</a></li>\n<li>Book: <em>Designing Data-Intensive Applications</em> (Kleppmann), chapters 2, 5, 6 and 9.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/nosql-redis/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (a plain console app, .NET 8/9, no extra packages, no live Redis or Cosmos DB server needed). It re-implements, in-process, the exact algorithms the chapter describes — hot-partition math, the fixed-window rate limiter, sorted-set ranking, and ETag-based optimistic concurrency — and checks their behavior directly. CAP/PACELC during a real network partition, actual Cosmos DB RU pricing, and live <code>StackExchange.Redis</code> behavior need a real distributed system to observe, so those are left to the Further reading links instead of faked here.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Verify this chapter: run with `dotnet run`. No external packages or live</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Redis/Cosmos DB server needed -- those claims require a real network</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// service and are left to the Further reading links instead of faked here.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Collections</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Generic</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Linq</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> name, <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> ok)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!ok) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: {name}&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: {name}&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 1. Partition key choice: partitioning by customerId spreads today's writes</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//    across many partitions; partitioning by date concentrates them into one.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// This reproduces, in miniature, the exact claim in &quot;Partitioning&quot;: picking a</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// low-cardinality key like `date` creates a hot partition.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">const</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> customers = <span class=\"tk-num\">500</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">const</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> ordersToday = <span class=\"tk-num\">2000</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> rng = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Random</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">42</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> byCustomerId = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> byDate = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> today = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;2026-10-28&quot;</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; i &lt; ordersToday; i++)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> customerId = rng.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Next</span>(customers);\n    byCustomerId[customerId] = byCustomerId.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetValueOrDefault</span>(customerId) + <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>;\n    byDate[today] = byDate.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetValueOrDefault</span>(today) + <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>; <span class=\"tk-com\">// every write lands on &quot;today&quot;</span>\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> hottestByCustomerId = byCustomerId.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Values</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Max</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> hottestByDate = byDate.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Values</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Max</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;partitioning by customerId spreads load (busiest partition holds a small share)&quot;</span>,\n    hottestByCustomerId &lt; ordersToday / <span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;partitioning by date concentrates ALL of today's writes onto one partition&quot;</span>,\n    hottestByDate == ordersToday);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 2. Fixed-window rate limiter: the same algorithm as RedisPatterns.AllowAsync,</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// re-implemented over an in-memory dictionary instead of StringIncrementAsync,</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// to verify the counting/window logic itself (not the Redis client).</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> windowCounts = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Allow</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> user, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> window, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> limit)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> key = <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;{user}:{window}&quot;</span>;\n    windowCounts[key] = windowCounts.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetValueOrDefault</span>(key) + <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> windowCounts[key] &lt;= limit;\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> allowedInWindow = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; i &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">7</span>; i++) <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Allow</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;user-1&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;w1&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>)) allowedInWindow++;\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;fixed-window limiter allows exactly `limit` requests per window (5 of 7 allowed)&quot;</span>, allowedInWindow == <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> allowedInNewWindow = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Allow</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;user-1&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;w2&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a new window resets the counter, so the next request is allowed again&quot;</span>, allowedInNewWindow);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 3. Sorted-set leaderboard: ZADD/ZINCRBY + ZRANGE keep &quot;top N&quot; always sorted</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// without the caller re-sorting. SortedSet&lt;T&gt; here plays the role Redis's</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// native sorted set plays server-side -- the property under test is that</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// ranking stays correct after incremental score updates, same as ZINCRBY.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> scores = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SortedSet</span>&lt;(<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Score</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Player</span>)&gt;(\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Comparer</span>&lt;(<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Score</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Player</span>)&gt;.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Create</span>((a, b) =&gt;\n        a.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Score</span> != b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Score</span> ? b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Score</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CompareTo</span>(a.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Score</span>) : <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CompareOrdinal</span>(a.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Player</span>, b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Player</span>)));\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScore</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> player, <span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> delta)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> existing = scores.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FirstOrDefault</span>(s =&gt; s.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Player</span> == player);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!<span class=\"tk-kw\">default</span>((<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>)).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Equals</span>(existing)) scores.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Remove</span>(existing);\n    scores.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>((existing.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Score</span> + delta, player));\n}\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScore</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;alice&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScore</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;bob&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">25</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScore</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;carol&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">15</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScore</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;alice&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>); <span class=\"tk-com\">// alice now has 30, should become #1</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> top2 = scores.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Take</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(s =&gt; s.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Player</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToArray</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;leaderboard stays ranked after an incremental score update (alice overtakes bob)&quot;</span>,\n    top2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SequenceEqual</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>[] { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;alice&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;bob&quot;</span> }));\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 4. Optimistic concurrency via a version/ETag match -- the same pattern as</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Cosmos DB's IfMatchEtag / EF Core's RowVersion, reduced to its essence.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Replace</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, (<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ETag</span>)&gt; store, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> id, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> newValue, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> expectedETag)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> (_, currentETag) = store[id];\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (currentETag != expectedETag) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;412 Precondition Failed&quot;</span>;\n    store[id] = (newValue, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NewGuid</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;N&quot;</span>)[..<span class=\"tk-num\">8</span>]);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;200 OK&quot;</span>;\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> docs = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, (<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ETag</span>)&gt; { [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ord-1&quot;</span>] = (<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Pending&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;etag-v1&quot;</span>) };\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> staleResult = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Replace</span>(docs, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ord-1&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shipped&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;etag-stale&quot;</span>); <span class=\"tk-com\">// someone else already changed it</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a replace with a stale ETag is rejected (optimistic concurrency catches the lost update)&quot;</span>,\n    staleResult == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;412 Precondition Failed&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> freshResult = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Replace</span>(docs, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ord-1&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Paid&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;etag-v1&quot;</span>); <span class=\"tk-com\">// matches current ETag</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a replace with the current ETag succeeds&quot;</span>, freshResult == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;200 OK&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here (need a live Redis/Cosmos DB server or a</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// multi-node cluster to observe for real): CAP/PACELC trade-offs during an</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// actual network partition, Cosmos DB consistency levels, RU pricing, and</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// StackExchange.Redis/ConnectionMultiplexer behavior against a live server --</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// see Further reading: Azure Cosmos DB docs, StackExchange.Redis docs,</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// and Kleppmann's &quot;Designing Data-Intensive Applications&quot;.</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All checks passed.&quot;</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-nosql-families-optimised-for-an-access-pattern-not-a-fea","text":"The NoSQL families: optimised for an access pattern, not a feature list","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"cap-pacelc-and-consistency-models-the-trade-off-a-distribute","text":"CAP, PACELC and consistency models: the trade-off a distributed store can't avoid","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"document-databases-modelling-by-access-pattern-not-by-normal","text":"Document databases: modelling by access pattern, not by normal form","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"partitioning-the-one-modelling-decision-you-cant-easily-undo","text":"Partitioning: the one modelling decision you can't easily undo","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"redis-an-in-memory-data-structure-server-not-just-a-cache","text":"Redis: an in-memory data-structure server, not just a cache","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"data-structures","text":"Data structures","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"search-and-vector-stores","text":"Search and vector stores","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"choosing-a-data-store","text":"Choosing a data store","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":3886,"minutes":18,"num":"4.6","part":"p4"},"design-principles":{"id":"design-principles","title":"Design Principles: SOLID and Beyond","summary":"The reasoning behind good object-oriented design. Each SOLID principle with a violation, a fix and a .NET example; coupling and cohesion; DRY, KISS, YAGNI; composition over inheritance; the Law of Demeter; tell-don't-ask; separation of concerns; and when to bend the rules.","layer":"arch","where":"Application architecture › Principles (class & module level)","fits":"<p>This is the first chapter of layer 4. It&#39;s built entirely from layer-1 tools (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/oop\">OOP</a>, interfaces, generics) and explains <em>why</em> layer-3 frameworks are designed as they are: DI exists to satisfy Dependency Inversion, and middleware satisfies Open/Closed. Every pattern in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/design-patterns\">Design patterns</a> and every architecture in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/app-architectures\">App architectures</a> applies these principles at a larger scale.</p>\n","prereqs":["oop","dependency-injection"],"next":["design-patterns","app-architectures"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-principles/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Every principle here exists for one reason: make change cheap by touching as few places as possible.</li>\n<li><strong>SOLID</strong>: one reason to change per class (S); add behavior via new code, not edits (O); a subtype must honor its base type&#39;s promises (L); split fat interfaces into small, role-specific ones (I); depend on abstractions the business side owns, not on concrete infrastructure (D).</li>\n<li>Aim for <strong>high cohesion</strong> (related things live together) and <strong>low coupling</strong> (modules don&#39;t depend on each other&#39;s internals).</li>\n<li>DRY is about duplicated <em>knowledge</em>, not duplicated-looking text — don&#39;t merge two things that just happen to look alike today.</li>\n<li>These are heuristics, not laws: an interface with one implementation that never changes is often over-engineering, not good design.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-problem-every-principle-in-this-chapter-is-trying-to-sol\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-principles/the-problem-every-principle-in-this-chapter-is-trying-to-sol\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The problem every principle in this chapter is trying to solve <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Code is read and changed far more often than it&#39;s written fresh. Every principle in this chapter — SOLID and the others that follow it — exists for exactly one practical reason: to <strong>reduce the cost of change</strong>. When a requirement changes, the ideal is that as few places as possible need to change, and that changing them doesn&#39;t break something unrelated you weren&#39;t even thinking about. SOLID (named by Robert C. Martin, &quot;Uncle Bob&quot;) packages five such principles specifically for classes and modules:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Letter</th>\n<th>Principle</th>\n<th>One-liner</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>S</strong></td>\n<td>Single Responsibility</td>\n<td>A module should have one reason to change</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>O</strong></td>\n<td>Open/Closed</td>\n<td>Open for extension, closed for modification</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>L</strong></td>\n<td>Liskov Substitution</td>\n<td>Subtypes must be usable wherever their base type is expected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>I</strong></td>\n<td>Interface Segregation</td>\n<td>Clients shouldn&#39;t depend on methods they don&#39;t use</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>D</strong></td>\n<td>Dependency Inversion</td>\n<td>Depend on abstractions; high-level policy shouldn&#39;t depend on low-level detail</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"s-single-responsibility-principle\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-principles/s-single-responsibility-principle\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>S: Single Responsibility Principle <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A class should have <strong>one reason to change</strong>, meaning it is responsible to <strong>one actor</strong> (one stakeholder or source of requirements).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// ✘ Three reasons to change: pricing rules (sales), persistence (DBA/infra), notification wording (marketing)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderService</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrder</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> o)\n    {\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> = o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sum</span>(l =&gt; l.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> * l.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Qty</span>) * (o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsVip</span> ? <span class=\"tk-num\">0.9m</span> : <span class=\"tk-num\">1m</span>);  <span class=\"tk-com\">// pricing</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> conn = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlConnection</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;…&quot;</span>);                                       <span class=\"tk-com\">// persistence</span>\n        conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Execute</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;INSERT INTO Orders …&quot;</span>, o);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> smtp = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SmtpClient</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;smtp.shop.com&quot;</span>);                                     <span class=\"tk-com\">// notification</span>\n        smtp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Send</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;shop@shop.com&quot;</span>, o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Email</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Thanks!&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;Your total is {o.Total}&quot;</span>);\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// ✔ Each concern in its own type; OrderService orchestrates</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderHandler</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IPricingService</span> pricing, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderRepository</span> orders, <span class=\"tk-ty\">INotifier</span> notifier)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HandleAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> o, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n    {\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ApplyTotal</span>(pricing.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Calculate</span>(o));\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> orders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAsync</span>(o, ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> notifier.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlacedAsync</span>(o, ct);\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The reason the first version is a real problem, not just an aesthetic one: when marketing wants to change the email wording, or the DBA wants to change how orders are persisted, or sales wants a new discount rule, each of those three unrelated people ends up editing the exact same method — a near-guarantee of merge conflicts and of one team&#39;s change accidentally breaking another&#39;s logic they never touched directly.</p>\n<p><strong>Symptoms of violation:</strong> a class name with &quot;And&quot;/&quot;Manager&quot;/&quot;Helper&quot;/&quot;Utils&quot;, 2,000-line services, unrelated tests in the same test class, merge conflicts on the same file from different teams, constructor injection of 10+ dependencies.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">SRP isn&#39;t &quot;one method per class&quot;</div><p>It&#39;s about <strong>cohesion around a reason to change</strong>. Splitting a cohesive class into dozens of one-method classes creates a different problem: scattered logic and ceremony.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"o-open-closed-principle\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-principles/o-open-closed-principle\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>O: Open/Closed Principle <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Software entities should be <strong>open for extension</strong> but <strong>closed for modification</strong>. Add behaviour by adding new code, not by editing working, tested code.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// ✘ Every new carrier means editing this method (and risking existing carriers)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShippingCost</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parcel</span> p, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> carrier) =&gt; carrier <span class=\"tk-kw\">switch</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;dhl&quot;</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span> + p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WeightKg</span> * <span class=\"tk-num\">1.2m</span>,\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ups&quot;</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">7</span> + p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WeightKg</span>,\n    _ =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">NotSupportedException</span>()\n};\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// ✔ New carrier = new class + one DI registration. ShippingCalculator never changes.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IShippingRate</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Carrier</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Calculate</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parcel</span> p); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DhlRate</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IShippingRate</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Carrier</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;dhl&quot;</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Calculate</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parcel</span> p) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span> + p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WeightKg</span> * <span class=\"tk-num\">1.2m</span>; }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">UpsRate</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IShippingRate</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Carrier</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ups&quot;</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Calculate</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parcel</span> p) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">7</span> + p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WeightKg</span>; }\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShippingCalculator</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IEnumerable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IShippingRate</span>&gt; rates)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IShippingRate</span>&gt; _byCarrier = rates.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToDictionary</span>(r =&gt; r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Carrier</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Quote</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> carrier, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Parcel</span> p) =&gt; _byCarrier[carrier].<span class=\"tk-ty\">Calculate</span>(p);\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The payoff is specific: adding FedEx support in the first version means editing a method that DHL and UPS <em>already</em> depend on, risking a typo that breaks pricing for carriers nobody meant to touch. In the second version, adding FedEx is purely additive — a new class, one registration line — and <code>ShippingCalculator</code> itself never changes again, no matter how many carriers get added. <strong>Where .NET does this:</strong> middleware (extend the pipeline without modifying it, exactly as <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/middleware/now-heres-the-aha-a-middleware-is-just-a-function-wrapping-a\">Middleware</a> showed), DI registrations, MVC/endpoint filters, EF Core interceptors, <code>DelegatingHandler</code>s, configuration providers, logging providers, and authorization handlers. Tools: polymorphism, strategy, decorator, plugin points.</p>\n<h2 id=\"l-liskov-substitution-principle\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-principles/l-liskov-substitution-principle\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>L: Liskov Substitution Principle <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>If S is a subtype of T, objects of type T may be replaced with objects of type S <strong>without breaking the program&#39;s correctness</strong>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Subtypes must honour the base type&#39;s <strong>contract</strong>: no stronger preconditions, no weaker postconditions, no new exceptions callers don&#39;t expect, and invariants preserved.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// The classic: Square &quot;is a&quot; Rectangle mathematically, but not behaviourally</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Rectangle</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">virtual</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Width</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">virtual</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Height</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Area</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Width</span> * <span class=\"tk-ty\">Height</span>; }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Square</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Rectangle</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Width</span>  { <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Width</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Height</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>; } }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Height</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Width</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Height</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>; } }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Resize</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Rectangle</span> r) { r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Width</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>; r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Height</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">4</span>; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Debug</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Assert</span>(r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Area</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>); }   <span class=\"tk-com\">// fails for Square: Area = 16</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Trace through why <code>Resize</code> fails: it was written against <code>Rectangle</code>&#39;s contract, where setting <code>Width</code> and <code>Height</code> independently should give an area equal to their product. <code>Square</code> can&#39;t honour that without breaking its own invariant — setting one side always forces the other to match — so <em>something</em> has to give, and it&#39;s always the caller&#39;s assumption. This is exactly why <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/oop/virtual-override-and-new-three-keywords-that-look-similar-an\">OOP</a> stressed that &quot;is-a&quot; in a type hierarchy has to mean &quot;behaves like,&quot; not just &quot;is mathematically a special case of.&quot;</p>\n<p>Backend-flavoured violations:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadOnlyOrderRepository</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderRepository</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> o) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">NotSupportedException</span>();      <span class=\"tk-com\">// ✘ callers of IOrderRepository will blow up</span>\n}\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// ✔ Fix with Interface Segregation: IOrderReader and IOrderWriter</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// .NET's own famous violation: arrays implement IList&lt;T&gt;, but Add throws NotSupportedException on T[]</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// (and ReadOnlyCollection&lt;T&gt; implements ICollection&lt;T&gt;.Add by throwing).</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><strong>Test for LSP:</strong> could every test written for the base type pass for the subtype?</p>\n<h2 id=\"i-interface-segregation-principle\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-principles/i-interface-segregation-principle\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>I: Interface Segregation Principle <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Clients should not be forced to depend on methods they don&#39;t use. Prefer several small, role-specific interfaces over one large one.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// ✘ Fat interface: the reporting job depends on write methods it never calls; the mock needs 12 methods</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IUserService</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">User</span>?&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> id); <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IReadOnlyList</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">User</span>&gt;&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">SearchAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> q);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">User</span> u); <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">UpdateAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">User</span> u); <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DeleteAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> id);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ResetPasswordAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> id); <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SendWelcomeEmailAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> id); <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>[]&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">ExportCsvAsync</span>();\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// ✔ Role interfaces; one class may still implement several</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IUserReader</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">User</span>?&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> id); <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IReadOnlyList</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">User</span>&gt;&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">SearchAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> q); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IUserWriter</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">User</span> u); <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">UpdateAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">User</span> u); <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DeleteAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> id); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IUserExporter</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>[]&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">ExportCsvAsync</span>(); }</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice exactly what this fixes in the <code>ReadOnlyOrderRepository</code> example from the LSP section above: splitting into <code>IOrderReader</code> and <code>IOrderWriter</code> means the read-only implementation simply never implements <code>IOrderWriter</code> at all, so there&#39;s no <code>Add</code> method left to throw from in the first place — the problem disappears rather than getting patched. <strong>In .NET:</strong> <code>IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</code> / <code>IReadOnlyCollection&lt;T&gt;</code> / <code>IList&lt;T&gt;</code>; <code>IOptions&lt;T&gt;</code> giving each class only its own settings section; CQRS splitting read and write interfaces; the <code>IDisposable</code>/<code>IAsyncDisposable</code> split.</p>\n<h2 id=\"d-dependency-inversion-principle\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-principles/d-dependency-inversion-principle\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>D: Dependency Inversion Principle <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<blockquote>\n<ol>\n<li>High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules. <strong>Both should depend on abstractions.</strong></li>\n<li>Abstractions should not depend on details. Details should depend on abstractions.</li>\n</ol>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The single most important question DIP asks is <strong>who owns the interface</strong>. The <em>high-level</em> (business) side defines what it needs, phrased in its own terms, and the <em>low-level</em> (infrastructure) side implements that definition. The result is that the source-code dependency arrow points in the <strong>opposite</strong> direction from the flow of control at runtime:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Without DIP:   OrderService ───uses &amp; depends on───▶ SqlOrderRepository ──▶ SQL Server\n                (business)                            (infrastructure)\n\nWith DIP:      OrderService ──depends on──▶ IOrderRepository ◀──implements── SqlOrderRepository\n                └──────────── Domain/Application project ───────────┘          └── Infrastructure project\n               Flow of control still goes Service → SQL at runtime, but the compile-time dependency points INWARD.</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Look closely at what actually flipped: <code>OrderService</code> still ends up calling SQL Server at runtime, exactly as before — the <em>behaviour</em> hasn&#39;t changed. What changed is that <code>OrderService</code>&#39;s source code no longer mentions <code>SqlOrderRepository</code> at all; it only mentions <code>IOrderRepository</code>, an interface the business side itself defines. That&#39;s the whole trick, and it&#39;s why swapping SQL Server for a different store later only ever touches the infrastructure project.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Domain/Application layer owns the abstraction, phrased in business terms</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderRepository</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>?&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span> id, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct); <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> o); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGateway</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentResult</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span> amount, <span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentMethod</span> method, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct); }\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Infrastructure implements it (EF Core, Stripe SDK…)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">StripePaymentGateway</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">StripeClient</span> stripe) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGateway</span> { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* … */</span> }\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Composition root wires them together</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGateway</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">StripePaymentGateway</span>&gt;();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>DIP is the principle; <strong>DI</strong> is the mechanism that makes it practical to wire up at runtime, as <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/dependency-injection\">Dependency injection</a> covered in full; Clean/Onion/Hexagonal architecture is simply DIP applied at the scale of an entire solution rather than one class. See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/app-architectures\">App architectures</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"coupling-and-cohesion-the-two-metrics-solid-is-really-about\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-principles/coupling-and-cohesion-the-two-metrics-solid-is-really-about\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Coupling and cohesion: the two metrics SOLID is really about <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Pull back from the five individual letters, and all of SOLID is really in service of two measurable properties:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cohesion</strong> (want <strong>high</strong>): how strongly the elements <em>inside</em> a module belong together. A <code>Money</code> type with arithmetic and formatting is cohesive; a <code>Utils</code> class with email validation, date parsing and PDF generation is not.</li>\n<li><strong>Coupling</strong> (want <strong>low</strong>): how much one module depends on the <em>internals</em> of another. Low coupling means you can change one without changing the other.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Kind of coupling (worst → best)</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Content</td>\n<td>Reaching into another class&#39;s private state via reflection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Common / global</td>\n<td>Shared static mutable state, a shared database table written by two services</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Control</td>\n<td>Passing flags that tell the callee what to do (<code>Save(order, sendEmail: true, skipValidation: false)</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stamp</td>\n<td>Passing a whole object when one field is needed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data</td>\n<td>Passing just the needed values</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Message</td>\n<td>Communicating via events or messages only</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Connascence</strong> is a finer-grained vocabulary for the same idea: two components are connascent if changing one requires changing the other (of name, type, meaning, position, algorithm, timing…). Prefer weaker forms, and keep strong forms within one module — never spread across a boundary where a later change has to be coordinated across teams.</p>\n<h2 id=\"dry-kiss-and-yagni\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-principles/dry-kiss-and-yagni\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>DRY, KISS and YAGNI <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Principle</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Caveat</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>DRY</strong>: Don&#39;t Repeat Yourself</td>\n<td>Every piece of <strong>knowledge</strong> has one authoritative representation</td>\n<td>About knowledge, not text. Two identical-looking snippets that change for different reasons are <strong>not</strong> duplication. Merging them creates coupling. &quot;Prefer duplication over the wrong abstraction&quot; (Sandi Metz)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>KISS</strong>: Keep It Simple</td>\n<td>The simplest solution that works</td>\n<td>Simple ≠ easy. Simple means few moving parts and clear intent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>YAGNI</strong>: You Aren&#39;t Gonna Need It</td>\n<td>Don&#39;t build for hypothetical future requirements</td>\n<td>Not an excuse to ignore known requirements or basic extensibility points</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>DRY&#39;s caveat is worth dwelling on, because it&#39;s where people most often over-apply the principle: two pieces of code can look textually identical today and still represent two entirely different pieces of <em>knowledge</em> — a shipping cost formula and a tax calculation might both happen to compute <code>base * rate</code> right now, purely by coincidence, and merging them into one shared helper creates a dependency between two business rules that have nothing to do with each other and will inevitably diverge later. The <strong>Rule of Three</strong> is the practical discipline that keeps this in check: tolerate duplication the first two times something looks similar; only abstract on the third occurrence, once the actual shared pattern — not just a visual coincidence — is genuinely clear.</p>\n<h2 id=\"other-principles-worth-naming\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-principles/other-principles-worth-naming\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Other principles worth naming <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Composition over inheritance</strong>: assemble behaviour from collaborators (decorators, strategies) instead of deep class hierarchies. See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/oop/composition-over-inheritance-why-is-a-chains-get-brittle\">OOP</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Law of Demeter</strong> (principle of least knowledge): talk to friends, not strangers. <code>order.Customer.Address.City.Name</code> couples you to four classes&#39; structure; prefer <code>order.ShippingCity()</code>, or pass what&#39;s needed.</li>\n<li><strong>Tell, Don&#39;t Ask</strong>: instead of asking an object for data and making decisions for it, tell it what to do. <code>account.Withdraw(50)</code>, which enforces its own rule, beats <code>if (account.Balance &gt;= 50) account.Balance -= 50;</code>. This is the root of rich domain models (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ddd\">DDD</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Separation of Concerns</strong>: HTTP, business rules, persistence and presentation in different places.</li>\n<li><strong>Principle of Least Astonishment</strong>: APIs should behave as their names suggest. <code>GetUser</code> shouldn&#39;t create a user.</li>\n<li><strong>Fail fast</strong>: validate early (constructor guards, <code>ValidateOnStart</code>), throw clear errors close to the cause.</li>\n<li><strong>Command-Query Separation (CQS)</strong>: a method either <strong>changes state</strong> (command, returns nothing) or <strong>returns data</strong> (query, no side effects), not both. CQRS scales this up to the architecture level.</li>\n<li><strong>Hollywood principle</strong> (&quot;don&#39;t call us, we&#39;ll call you&quot;): the framework calls your code. That is Inversion of Control.</li>\n<li><strong>Package principles</strong> (for assemblies/projects): stable dependencies (depend in the direction of stability), acyclic dependencies (no cycles between projects; the compiler enforces this for project references).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"when-to-bend-the-rules\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-principles/when-to-bend-the-rules\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>When to bend the rules <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Principles are heuristics for managing change, not laws. Over-applying them is its own anti-pattern, and recognising it matters just as much as recognising the violations above:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Over-application</th>\n<th>Symptom</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Interface for every class (&quot;header interfaces&quot;)</td>\n<td><code>IOrderService</code> with exactly one implementation that never changes and is never mocked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Abstracting stable things</td>\n<td>Wrapping <code>DateTime</code> is good (testability); wrapping <code>string.Join</code> is silly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SRP taken to the extreme</td>\n<td>40 classes to handle one simple feature, logic impossible to follow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Premature OCP</td>\n<td>Plugin systems for variations that never happen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DRY across bounded contexts</td>\n<td>One shared <code>Customer</code> class used by billing, shipping and marketing, coupling three teams</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The pragmatic test behind every row above is the same one: <strong>does this abstraction pay for itself</strong> in testability, a real variation point, or isolating something genuinely volatile (I/O, third parties, time)? If the answer is no, keep it concrete and refactor later — refactoring is cheap precisely when you have tests to lean on, which is the whole reason this book treats testing as load-bearing rather than optional.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Explain SOLID with an example of each.</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>SRP: split an OrderService that prices, persists and emails into separate collaborators. OCP: add shipping carriers as new IShippingRate implementations instead of editing a switch. LSP: a ReadOnlyRepository throwing on Add breaks callers of IRepository, so it violates LSP. ISP: split a fat IUserService into IUserReader and IUserWriter. DIP: OrderService depends on an IOrderRepository defined in the domain and implemented by an EF Core class in infrastructure.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What does the Single Responsibility Principle really mean?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A module should have one reason to change, meaning it serves one actor or source of requirements. It&#39;s about cohesion around a responsibility, not about having only one method.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is a Liskov Substitution violation you&#39;ve seen in .NET?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Arrays implement IList&lt;T&gt; but throw NotSupportedException on Add; ReadOnlyCollection implements ICollection.Add by throwing; Square deriving from Rectangle; a repository implementation that throws for some interface methods. Each breaks code written against the base contract.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How does the Dependency Inversion Principle differ from Dependency Injection?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>DIP is a design principle: high-level modules and low-level details both depend on abstractions owned by the high-level side, so compile-time dependencies point inward. DI is a technique for supplying concrete implementations to a class from outside, typically via a container. DI makes DIP practical, but you can use DI without following DIP.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are coupling and cohesion?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Cohesion is how closely related the responsibilities inside a module are (aim high). Coupling is how much modules depend on each other&#39;s details (aim low). Good design increases cohesion within modules and reduces coupling between them, so changes stay local.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Is duplication always bad (DRY)?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>No. DRY is about duplicated knowledge, not similar-looking code. If two pieces change for different reasons (different bounded contexts or actors), merging them couples them. Prefer duplication over the wrong abstraction; abstract once the pattern is clear (Rule of Three).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the Law of Demeter?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A method should only talk to its immediate collaborators (itself, its parameters, objects it creates, its fields), not reach through chains like a.B.C.D(). It reduces coupling to other objects&#39; internal structure.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>When would you not create an interface for a class?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>When there&#39;s a single stable implementation that isn&#39;t an I/O or volatile boundary and doesn&#39;t need to be faked in tests (simple domain services, value objects, mappers). Interfaces pay off at seams: persistence, external services, time, randomness, and real variation points.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-principles/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Robert C. Martin, <em>Clean Architecture</em> (part III, design principles) and <em>Agile Software Development: Principles, Patterns, and Practices</em>.</li>\n<li>Mark Seemann, <em>Dependency Injection Principles, Practices, and Patterns</em>.</li>\n<li>Sandi Metz, &quot;The Wrong Abstraction&quot; (blog post).</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/architecture/modern-web-apps-azure/architectural-principles\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Architectural principles (Microsoft .NET architecture guide)</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-principles/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (plain console app, .NET 8/9, no packages). SOLID itself is a design heuristic, not a runtime behavior, so this doesn&#39;t &quot;prove SOLID is true&quot; — it demonstrates the two concrete, checkable claims the chapter makes: an LSP violation actually breaking a caller&#39;s assumption, and an OCP-shaped design actually not requiring the existing class to change when a new case is added.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// --- LSP: Square violates the Rectangle contract that Resize relies on ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> rect = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Rectangle</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Resize</span>(rect);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (rect.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Area</span> != <span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: Rectangle should honor independent Width/Height&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: Resize gives Rectangle the expected area (20)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> square = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Square</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Resize</span>(square);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (square.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Area</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected the LSP violation to reproduce, but Square.Area was 20&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: Square breaks the same caller's assumption (Area = {square.Area}, not 20) — this is the LSP violation&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- OCP: adding a new &quot;carrier&quot; never requires editing ShippingCalculator's source ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> calculator = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShippingCalculator</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IShippingRate</span>[] { <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DhlRate</span>(), <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">UpsRate</span>(), <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FedExRate</span>() });\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> fedexQuote = calculator.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Quote</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;fedex&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2.0</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (fedexQuote &lt;= <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: FedEx quote should be a positive amount&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: new carrier (FedEx, quote={fedexQuote}) added with zero edits to ShippingCalculator's code&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here — see Further reading: Clean Architecture (Martin), Dependency Injection (Seemann)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// (DIP/ISP, coupling &amp; cohesion, and &quot;when to bend the rules&quot; are design judgments, not facts a program can assert)</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Resize</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Rectangle</span> r) { r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Width</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>; r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Height</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">4</span>; }\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Rectangle</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">virtual</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Width</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">virtual</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Height</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Area</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Width</span> * <span class=\"tk-ty\">Height</span>; }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Square</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Rectangle</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Width</span>  { <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Width</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Height</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>; } }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Height</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Width</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Height</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>; } }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IShippingRate</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Carrier</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Calculate</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> weightKg); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DhlRate</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IShippingRate</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Carrier</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;dhl&quot;</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Calculate</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> w) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span> + (<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>)w * <span class=\"tk-num\">1.2m</span>; }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">UpsRate</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IShippingRate</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Carrier</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ups&quot;</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Calculate</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> w) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">7</span> + (<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>)w; }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FedExRate</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IShippingRate</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Carrier</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;fedex&quot;</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Calculate</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> w) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">6</span> + (<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>)w * <span class=\"tk-num\">1.1m</span>; } <span class=\"tk-com\">// added later, nothing else touched</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShippingCalculator</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IShippingRate</span>&gt; _byCarrier;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShippingCalculator</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IEnumerable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IShippingRate</span>&gt; rates) =&gt; _byCarrier = rates.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToDictionary</span>(r =&gt; r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Carrier</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Quote</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> carrier, <span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> weightKg) =&gt; _byCarrier[carrier].<span class=\"tk-ty\">Calculate</span>(weightKg);\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-problem-every-principle-in-this-chapter-is-trying-to-sol","text":"The problem every principle in this chapter is trying to solve","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"s-single-responsibility-principle","text":"S: Single Responsibility Principle","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"o-open-closed-principle","text":"O: Open/Closed Principle","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"l-liskov-substitution-principle","text":"L: Liskov Substitution Principle","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"i-interface-segregation-principle","text":"I: Interface Segregation Principle","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"d-dependency-inversion-principle","text":"D: Dependency Inversion Principle","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"coupling-and-cohesion-the-two-metrics-solid-is-really-about","text":"Coupling and cohesion: the two metrics SOLID is really about","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"dry-kiss-and-yagni","text":"DRY, KISS and YAGNI","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"other-principles-worth-naming","text":"Other principles worth naming","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"when-to-bend-the-rules","text":"When to bend the rules","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":3626,"minutes":16,"num":"5.1","part":"p5"},"design-patterns":{"id":"design-patterns","title":"Design Patterns: The GoF Catalogue in Modern .NET","summary":"The classic Gang of Four patterns as you'll meet them in .NET today. Creational (Factory Method, Abstract Factory, Builder, Singleton, Prototype), structural (Adapter, Decorator, Proxy, Facade, Composite, Bridge, Flyweight) and behavioural (Strategy, Observer, Command, Chain of Responsibility, Mediator, Template Method, State, Iterator, Visitor), each with intent, code and where the framework uses it.","layer":"arch","where":"Application architecture › Patterns (class level)","fits":"<p>Patterns are named recipes that apply the <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/design-principles\">design principles</a>. Recognising them is the fastest way to understand layer-3 frameworks: middleware is Chain of Responsibility, <code>IHttpClientFactory</code> is a Factory, <code>DelegatingHandler</code> is a Decorator, <code>IOptionsMonitor.OnChange</code> is an Observer. Larger-scale patterns (CQRS, Repository, Outbox) are in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cqrs-mediator-result\">CQRS &amp; Result</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ddd\">DDD</a> and <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events\">Messaging</a>.</p>\n","prereqs":["design-principles","delegates-events"],"next":["app-architectures","cqrs-mediator-result"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-patterns/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>design pattern</strong> is just a named, reusable shape for a recurring problem — learning the name lets you recognise framework code (&quot;that&#39;s a Decorator&quot;) without reading its internals.</li>\n<li>GoF patterns fall into three groups: <strong>creational</strong> (how objects get made), <strong>structural</strong> (how objects are composed), <strong>behavioural</strong> (how objects collaborate).</li>\n<li>Modern C# and DI absorb several patterns into the language itself: a delegate often replaces a one-method Strategy, <code>yield</code> <em>is</em> Iterator, and the DI container replaces hand-written Factories and Singletons.</li>\n<li>The most common ones in everyday ASP.NET Core code: Chain of Responsibility (middleware), Factory (<code>IHttpClientFactory</code>), Decorator (<code>DelegatingHandler</code>), Strategy (keyed DI services), Builder (<code>WebApplicationBuilder</code>).</li>\n<li>Bigger, architecture-scale patterns (Repository, CQRS, Outbox) live in their own chapters, not here.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"what-patterns-are-and-why-recognising-them-is-worth-the-effo\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-patterns/what-patterns-are-and-why-recognising-them-is-worth-the-effo\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What patterns are, and why recognising them is worth the effort <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>A <strong>design pattern</strong> is a reusable, named solution to a recurring design problem. The value of learning the name isn&#39;t abstract — it&#39;s that once you recognise &quot;this is Decorator&quot; or &quot;this is Chain of Responsibility,&quot; you can predict how a piece of unfamiliar framework code behaves without reading its implementation line by line. The 1994 &quot;Gang of Four&quot; (GoF) book <em>Design Patterns</em> catalogued 23 of them in three groups:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Group</th>\n<th>Concern</th>\n<th>Patterns</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Creational</strong></td>\n<td>How objects are created</td>\n<td>Factory Method, Abstract Factory, Builder, Singleton, Prototype</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Structural</strong></td>\n<td>How objects are composed</td>\n<td>Adapter, Decorator, Proxy, Facade, Composite, Bridge, Flyweight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Behavioural</strong></td>\n<td>How objects collaborate and share responsibility</td>\n<td>Strategy, Observer, Command, Chain of Responsibility, Mediator, Template Method, State, Iterator, Visitor, Memento, Interpreter</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-interview\"><div class=\"callout-title\">How to answer &quot;tell me about a design pattern you&#39;ve used&quot;</div><p>Give <strong>problem → pattern → .NET example → trade-off</strong>. For example: &quot;We had pricing rules that varied by customer type and kept growing a switch statement. We used the <strong>Strategy</strong> pattern: an <code>IPricingStrategy</code> per rule, registered in DI as keyed services, and selected at runtime. It made new rules a new class instead of an edit (Open/Closed), and each rule was easy to unit test. The cost is a few more types and an indirection.&quot;</p>\n</aside>\n<p>Keep one thing in mind as you read the catalogue below: modern C# and DI make many of these patterns lighter than the 1994 book describes them. A delegate can replace a one-method Strategy, the DI container replaces most hand-written Singletons and Factories, and <code>IEnumerable</code> + <code>yield</code> <em>is</em> Iterator — the pattern isn&#39;t missing, it&#39;s just been absorbed into the language itself.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"factory-method-and-simple-factory\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-patterns/factory-method-and-simple-factory\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Factory Method and Simple Factory <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Intent:</strong> let a method or class decide <strong>which concrete type</strong> to create, so callers depend only on an abstraction.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">INotifier</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SendAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> to, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> message, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct); }\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">NotifierFactory</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IServiceProvider</span> sp) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">INotifierFactory</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">INotifier</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Create</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Channel</span> channel) =&gt; channel <span class=\"tk-kw\">switch</span>\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Channel</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Email</span> =&gt; sp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">EmailNotifier</span>&gt;(),\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Channel</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sms</span>   =&gt; sp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">SmsNotifier</span>&gt;(),\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Channel</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Push</span>  =&gt; sp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">PushNotifier</span>&gt;(),\n        _ =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ArgumentOutOfRangeException</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">nameof</span>(channel))\n    };\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Static factory method on the type itself: name the intent, validate, hide constructors</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>() { }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Create</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> customer, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IEnumerable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderLine</span>&gt; lines) { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* validate invariants */</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* … */</span> }; }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><strong>In .NET:</strong> <code>IHttpClientFactory.CreateClient()</code>, <code>ILoggerFactory.CreateLogger()</code>, <code>IDbContextFactory&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>Task.FromResult</code>, <code>TimeSpan.FromSeconds</code>, DI factory registrations (<code>AddScoped&lt;IFoo&gt;(sp =&gt; …)</code>). Keyed services (.NET 8) often replace hand-written factories.</p>\n<h2 id=\"abstract-factory\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-patterns/abstract-factory\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Abstract Factory <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Intent:</strong> create <strong>families of related objects</strong> without specifying concrete classes, so a family can be swapped as a whole.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IStorageFactory</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">IBlobStore</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateBlobStore</span>(); <span class=\"tk-ty\">IQueueClient</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateQueue</span>(); <span class=\"tk-ty\">ISecretStore</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateSecrets</span>(); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AzureStorageFactory</span>(…) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IStorageFactory</span> { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* Blob Storage, Storage Queues, Key Vault */</span> }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">LocalStorageFactory</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IStorageFactory</span> { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* file system, in-memory channel, user secrets */</span> }</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice the &quot;swapped as a whole&quot; part is the actual point here, distinguishing this from a plain Factory Method: you never want <code>AzureStorageFactory</code>&#39;s blob store paired with <code>LocalStorageFactory</code>&#39;s queue — the whole family has to move together, exactly as it would between a local dev environment and a cloud deployment. <strong>In .NET:</strong> <code>DbProviderFactory</code> (creates connections, commands and parameters for one provider). In practice, registering a coherent set of implementations per environment in DI achieves the same thing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"builder\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-patterns/builder\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Builder <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Intent:</strong> construct a complex object <strong>step by step</strong>, with a readable fluent API, separating construction from representation and validating once at <code>Build()</code>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> report = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReportBuilder</span>()\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Titled</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Q3 sales&quot;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">For</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">DateRange</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Quarter</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2026</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>))\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">GroupBy</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReportGrouping</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Region</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">IncludeChart</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChartType</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Bar</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();                                <span class=\"tk-com\">// validates and creates an immutable Report</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReportBuilder</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>? _title; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateRange</span>? _range; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChartType</span>&gt; _charts = [];\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReportBuilder</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Titled</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> t) { _title = t; <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">this</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReportBuilder</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">For</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">DateRange</span> r) { _range = r; <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">this</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReportBuilder</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IncludeChart</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChartType</span> c) { _charts.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(c); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">this</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReportBuilder</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">GroupBy</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReportGrouping</span> g) { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* … */</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">this</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Report</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(_title ?? <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Title required&quot;</span>), _range ?? <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateRange</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Today</span>, [.. _charts]);\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><strong>In .NET, everywhere:</strong> <code>WebApplication.CreateBuilder()</code> / <code>WebApplicationBuilder</code>, <code>HostApplicationBuilder</code>, <code>IServiceCollection</code> (a builder for the container), <code>ConfigurationBuilder</code>, <code>StringBuilder</code>, <code>UriBuilder</code>, <code>DbContextOptionsBuilder</code>, <code>ModelBuilder</code>, <code>AuthorizationPolicyBuilder</code>, <code>HubConnectionBuilder</code>. <strong>Test data builders</strong> (<code>new OrderBuilder().WithLines(3).Paid().Build()</code>) are a great use in test projects.</p>\n<h2 id=\"singleton\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-patterns/singleton\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Singleton <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Intent:</strong> exactly <strong>one instance</strong>, with global access.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// The classic thread-safe lazy singleton</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Clock</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Lazy</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Clock</span>&gt; _instance = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Clock</span>());\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Clock</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Instance</span> =&gt; _instance.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Clock</span>() { }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// The modern way: let DI own the single instance</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IClock</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">SystemClock</span>&gt;();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Why the classic Singleton is considered an anti-pattern</div><p><code>Clock.Instance</code> is <strong>hidden global state</strong>: dependencies aren&#39;t visible in constructors, it can&#39;t be replaced in tests, tests interfere through shared state, and lifetime is fixed forever. A <strong>DI singleton</strong> gives the one-instance benefit while keeping explicit dependencies and testability. Whatever approach you use, singletons must be <strong>thread-safe</strong>.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"prototype\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-patterns/prototype\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Prototype <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Intent:</strong> create new objects by <strong>copying</strong> an existing one.</p>\n<p><strong>In .NET:</strong> record <code>with</code> expressions (<code>var copy = order with { Status = Paid };</code>), <code>ICloneable</code> (discouraged: deep or shallow is ambiguous), <code>MemberwiseClone</code> (shallow). Useful for templates (&quot;new order like the last one&quot;) and immutable updates.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"adapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-patterns/adapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Adapter <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Intent:</strong> make an existing class with an <strong>incompatible interface</strong> work through the interface your code expects. It&#39;s a translator.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Your domain's port</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGateway</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentResult</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span> amount, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> paymentToken, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct); }\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Adapter over a vendor SDK with a completely different shape</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">StripePaymentAdapter</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentIntentService</span> stripe) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGateway</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentResult</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span> amount, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> token, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> intent = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> stripe.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentIntentCreateOptions</span>\n        {\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">Amount</span> = (<span class=\"tk-kw\">long</span>)(amount.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Amount</span> * <span class=\"tk-num\">100</span>), <span class=\"tk-ty\">Currency</span> = amount.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Currency</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToLowerInvariant</span>(),\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentMethod</span> = token, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Confirm</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>\n        }, cancellationToken: ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> intent.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;succeeded&quot;</span> ? <span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentResult</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Success</span>(intent.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentResult</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Declined</span>(intent.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LastPaymentError</span>?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Code</span>);\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><strong>Why it matters:</strong> this is exactly the <strong>&quot;adapter&quot; in Ports &amp; Adapters (Hexagonal)</strong> architecture, the same pattern <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/design-principles/d-dependency-inversion-principle\">Design principles</a> described as infrastructure implementing an interface the business side owns. Vendor types never leak into your domain, and switching providers means writing a new adapter. <strong>In .NET:</strong> <code>StreamReader</code> (adapts a <code>Stream</code> to text reading), <code>TextWriter</code> adapters, logging providers adapting <code>ILogger</code> to Serilog/NLog.</p>\n<h2 id=\"decorator\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-patterns/decorator\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Decorator <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Intent:</strong> wrap an object with <strong>another object implementing the same interface</strong>, adding behaviour before/after delegating. Decorators can be stacked, and the inner object doesn&#39;t know.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IProductRepository</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>?&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct); }\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlProductRepository</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span> db) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IProductRepository</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>?&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt; db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FindAsync</span>([id], ct).<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsTask</span>();\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CachingProductRepository</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IProductRepository</span> inner, <span class=\"tk-ty\">HybridCache</span> cache) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IProductRepository</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-com\">// HybridCache (a built-in two-level cache, covered in [Caching](ch:caching-ratelimit-cors)) just needs a</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-com\">// key and a function to call on a cache miss — here, &quot;ask the inner repository&quot;.</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>?&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt;\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> cache.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetOrCreateAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;product:{id}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> t =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> inner.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(id, t), cancellationToken: ct);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">LoggingProductRepository</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IProductRepository</span> inner, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ILogger</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">LoggingProductRepository</span>&gt; log) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IProductRepository</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>?&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> sw = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Stopwatch</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartNew</span>();\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> p = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> inner.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(id, ct);\n        log.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogDebug</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Loaded product {Id} in {Ms} ms (hit: {Found})&quot;</span>, id, sw.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ElapsedMilliseconds</span>, p <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">not</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> p;\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Composition: Logging → Caching → Sql   (Scrutor)</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IProductRepository</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlProductRepository</span>&gt;();\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Decorate</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IProductRepository</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CachingProductRepository</span>&gt;();\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Decorate</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IProductRepository</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">LoggingProductRepository</span>&gt;();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice the <code>Decorate</code> calls build up exactly the same nested-wrapping shape <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/middleware/now-heres-the-aha-a-middleware-is-just-a-function-wrapping-a\">Middleware</a> showed for <code>A(B(C(endpoint)))</code> — a call to <code>GetAsync</code> here flows <code>Logging → Caching → Sql</code>, each layer free to act before and after delegating onward. <strong>In .NET:</strong> <code>DelegatingHandler</code> chains in HttpClient, <code>BufferedStream</code>/<code>GZipStream</code>/<code>CryptoStream</code> wrapping a <code>Stream</code>, MediatR pipeline behaviours, and middleware (conceptually). Decorator vs inheritance: you can combine N behaviours with N classes instead of 2^N subclasses.</p>\n<h2 id=\"proxy\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-patterns/proxy\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Proxy <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Intent:</strong> a stand-in with the <strong>same interface</strong> that <strong>controls access</strong> to the real object: lazy creation, remote access, caching, access control.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Proxy kind</th>\n<th>.NET example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Virtual (lazy)</td>\n<td>EF Core <strong>lazy-loading proxies</strong>, <code>Lazy&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Remote</td>\n<td>gRPC/WCF client stubs, Refit-generated clients</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Protection</td>\n<td>A wrapper checking permissions before delegating</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dynamic</td>\n<td><code>DispatchProxy</code>, Castle DynamicProxy (used by mocking libraries like Moq and NSubstitute)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Decorator and Proxy look identical in code — both wrap an object behind the same interface. What tells them apart is <em>why</em> you wrapped it. A decorator <strong>adds behaviour</strong> to an object that&#39;s already there. A proxy <strong>controls access</strong> to an object, often deciding <em>whether</em> or <em>when</em> it even exists. That&#39;s why EF Core&#39;s lazy-loading proxy counts as Proxy (it decides when to load the real entity) while <code>CachingProductRepository</code> above counts as Decorator (the real repository is already available; caching just adds a behaviour in front of it).</p>\n<h2 id=\"facade\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-patterns/facade\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Facade <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Intent:</strong> provide a <strong>simple, unified interface</strong> over a complex subsystem.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CheckoutFacade</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ICartService</span> carts, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPricingService</span> pricing, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IInventory</span> inventory, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGateway</span> payments, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderRepository</span> orders)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>&gt;&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">CheckoutAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> cartId, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> paymentToken, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> cart = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> carts.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(cartId, ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> total = pricing.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Calculate</span>(cart);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> inventory.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReserveAsync</span>(cart.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span>, ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> payment = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> payments.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeAsync</span>(total, paymentToken, ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!payment.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Succeeded</span>) { <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> inventory.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReleaseAsync</span>(cart.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span>, ct); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Fail</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;payment.declined&quot;</span>); }\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> order = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromCart</span>(cart, total, payment.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Reference</span>);\n        orders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(order);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>;\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><strong>In .NET:</strong> <code>WebApplication.CreateBuilder()</code> (a facade over host, config, logging and DI setup), <code>File.ReadAllTextAsync</code> (over streams and encodings), <code>JsonSerializer</code> (over readers/writers), <code>HttpClient.GetFromJsonAsync</code>. Application services and use-case handlers are often facades over the domain.</p>\n<h2 id=\"composite\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-patterns/composite\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Composite <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Intent:</strong> treat <strong>individual objects and groups of objects uniformly</strong> through one interface (tree structures).</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPriceRule</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Apply</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> price, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> o); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PercentOff</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> pct) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPriceRule</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Apply</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> p, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> o) =&gt; p * (<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> - pct); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CompositeRule</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">params</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPriceRule</span>[] rules) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPriceRule</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Apply</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> p, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> o) =&gt; rules.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Aggregate</span>(p, (acc, r) =&gt; r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Apply</span>(acc, o));   <span class=\"tk-com\">// a group IS a rule</span>\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><strong>In .NET:</strong> <code>CompositeChangeToken</code>, <code>CompositeFileProvider</code>, UI control trees, expression trees, authorization requirements combined in a policy, and the <code>IConfiguration</code> sections tree.</p>\n<h2 id=\"bridge-and-flyweight\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-patterns/bridge-and-flyweight\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Bridge and Flyweight <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bridge:</strong> separate an <strong>abstraction</strong> from its <strong>implementation</strong> so both vary independently. Example: <code>ILogger</code> (abstraction) bridging to many logging providers (implementations); ADO.NET&#39;s abstract <code>DbConnection</code> with provider implementations.</li>\n<li><strong>Flyweight:</strong> share common state among many fine-grained objects to save memory. <strong>In .NET:</strong> string interning, cached boxed values, <code>ArrayPool&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>Task.CompletedTask</code>, cached <code>JsonSerializerOptions</code>, <code>FrozenDictionary</code> lookups shared across requests.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<h2 id=\"strategy\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-patterns/strategy\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Strategy <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Intent:</strong> define a <strong>family of interchangeable algorithms</strong> behind one interface and choose one at runtime.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IDiscountStrategy</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Discount</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> o); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">NoDiscount</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IDiscountStrategy</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;none&quot;</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Discount</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> o) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">VipDiscount</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IDiscountStrategy</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;vip&quot;</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Discount</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> o) =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Subtotal</span> * <span class=\"tk-num\">0.10m</span>; }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">BlackFriday</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IDiscountStrategy</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;blackfriday&quot;</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Discount</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> o) =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Subtotal</span> * <span class=\"tk-num\">0.30m</span>; }\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Select by key with .NET 8 keyed services</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddKeyedSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IDiscountStrategy</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">VipDiscount</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;vip&quot;</span>);\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddKeyedSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IDiscountStrategy</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">BlackFriday</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;blackfriday&quot;</span>);\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddKeyedSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IDiscountStrategy</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">NoDiscount</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;none&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PricingService</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IServiceProvider</span> sp, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ICampaignCalendar</span> calendar)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> o)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> key = calendar.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsBlackFriday</span> ? <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;blackfriday&quot;</span> : o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsVip</span> ? <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;vip&quot;</span> : <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;none&quot;</span>;\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Subtotal</span> - sp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredKeyedService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IDiscountStrategy</span>&gt;(key).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Discount</span>(o);\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Lightweight strategy: just a delegate</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>&gt; discount = o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Subtotal</span> &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">100</span> ? <span class=\"tk-num\">10</span> : <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice how directly this maps onto the Open/Closed worked example from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/design-principles/o-open-closed-principle\">Design principles</a>: the shipping-rate carriers there were literally this same pattern, named. <strong>In .NET:</strong> <code>IComparer&lt;T&gt;</code> passed to <code>Sort</code>, <code>IEqualityComparer&lt;T&gt;</code> for dictionaries, authentication schemes, <code>JsonNamingPolicy</code>, retry strategies in Polly, <code>IPasswordHasher</code>, load-balancing policies in YARP. <strong>Strategy is the most-used pattern in DI-based code.</strong></p>\n<h2 id=\"observer\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-patterns/observer\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Observer <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Intent:</strong> a <strong>subject</strong> notifies a list of <strong>observers</strong> when something happens, without knowing who they are.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Language-level: events</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">event</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">EventHandler</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlacedEventArgs</span>&gt;? <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// In-process &quot;observer&quot; via DI: all handlers of a notification</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderPlacedHandler</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HandleAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span> e, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DomainEventDispatcher</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IEnumerable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderPlacedHandler</span>&gt; handlers)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PublishAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span> e, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WhenAll</span>(handlers.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(h =&gt; h.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HandleAsync</span>(e, ct)));\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><strong>In .NET:</strong> C# <code>event</code>s, <code>IObservable&lt;T&gt;</code>/<code>IObserver&lt;T&gt;</code> (Reactive Extensions), <code>IOptionsMonitor&lt;T&gt;.OnChange</code>, <code>IChangeToken</code>, <code>INotifyPropertyChanged</code> (UI), <code>DiagnosticListener</code>/<code>ActivityListener</code>, MediatR notifications. <strong>At system scale</strong>, Observer becomes <strong>publish/subscribe</strong> with a message broker (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events\">Messaging</a>) — same idea, the subject and observers just live in different processes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"command\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-patterns/command\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Command <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Intent:</strong> encapsulate <strong>a request as an object</strong>, so you can queue it, log it, retry it, undo it, or send it elsewhere.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrder</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IReadOnlyList</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">LineDto</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span>);    <span class=\"tk-com\">// the command: pure data</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ICommandHandler</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TCommand</span>&gt; { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HandleAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TCommand</span> command, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderHandler</span>(…) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">ICommandHandler</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrder</span>&gt; { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* … */</span> }</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><strong>In .NET and architecture:</strong> CQRS commands, MediatR <code>IRequest</code>, messages sent to a queue (a command sent to Service Bus is literally a serialised Command object), Hangfire jobs, undo stacks in UI apps. Commands are named in the imperative (<code>PlaceOrder</code>, <code>CancelSubscription</code>); events are named in the past tense (<code>OrderPlaced</code>) — a naming discipline that pays for itself the moment you&#39;re scanning a codebase and need to tell at a glance which messages can still be rejected and which already happened.</p>\n<h2 id=\"chain-of-responsibility\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-patterns/chain-of-responsibility\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Chain of Responsibility <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Intent:</strong> pass a request along a <strong>chain of handlers</strong>; each either handles it, passes it on, or both.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">abstract</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ApprovalHandler</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ApprovalHandler</span>? _next;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ApprovalHandler</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SetNext</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ApprovalHandler</span> next) =&gt; _next = next;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">virtual</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ApprovalResult</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Handle</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Expense</span> e) =&gt; _next?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Handle</span>(e) ?? <span class=\"tk-ty\">ApprovalResult</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Rejected</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;No approver&quot;</span>);\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TeamLead</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">ApprovalHandler</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ApprovalResult</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Handle</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Expense</span> e) =&gt; e.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Amount</span> &lt;= <span class=\"tk-num\">500</span> ? <span class=\"tk-ty\">ApprovalResult</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Approved</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;lead&quot;</span>) : <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Handle</span>(e); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Director</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">ApprovalHandler</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ApprovalResult</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Handle</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Expense</span> e) =&gt; e.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Amount</span> &lt;= <span class=\"tk-num\">10_000</span> ? <span class=\"tk-ty\">ApprovalResult</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Approved</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;director&quot;</span>) : <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Handle</span>(e); }</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><strong>In .NET, the defining example:</strong> the <strong>ASP.NET Core middleware pipeline</strong>. Each middleware handles the request, short-circuits, or calls <code>next</code>. Also: HttpClient&#39;s <code>DelegatingHandler</code> chain, MVC filters, MediatR pipeline behaviours, and authorization handlers. See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/middleware\">Middleware</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"mediator\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-patterns/mediator\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Mediator <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Intent:</strong> objects communicate <strong>through a mediator</strong> instead of referencing each other directly, which reduces many-to-many coupling to one-to-many.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapPost</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/orders&quot;</span>, (<span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrder</span> cmd, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ISender</span> mediator, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt; mediator.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Send</span>(cmd, ct));\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// The endpoint doesn't know which handler, or which pipeline behaviours (validation, logging, transactions), will run.</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><strong>In .NET:</strong> MediatR (commercial licence for larger companies since 2025), the source-generated <strong>Mediator</strong> library, Wolverine, Brighter; SignalR hubs (clients talk via the hub); and an aggregate root mediating access to its children. Trade-off: decoupling and a central pipeline vs indirection (&quot;go to definition&quot; no longer finds the handler). See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cqrs-mediator-result\">CQRS &amp; Mediator</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"template-method\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-patterns/template-method\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Template Method <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Intent:</strong> a base class defines the <strong>skeleton of an algorithm</strong>, and subclasses fill in specific steps.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">abstract</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ImportJob</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">RunAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)          <span class=\"tk-com\">// the template: fixed sequence</span>\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> raw = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DownloadAsync</span>(ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> rows = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Parse</span>(raw);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> valid = rows.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Validate</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToList</span>();\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveAsync</span>(valid, ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OnCompletedAsync</span>(valid.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span>, ct);               <span class=\"tk-com\">// hook with a default</span>\n    }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">abstract</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Stream</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">DownloadAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">abstract</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IEnumerable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ImportRow</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Parse</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Stream</span> raw);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">virtual</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Validate</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ImportRow</span> r) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">abstract</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IReadOnlyList</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ImportRow</span>&gt; rows, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">virtual</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OnCompletedAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> count, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CompletedTask</span>;\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><strong>In .NET:</strong> <code>BackgroundService.ExecuteAsync</code>, <code>AuthenticationHandler&lt;T&gt;.HandleAuthenticateAsync</code>, <code>AuthorizationHandler&lt;T&gt;.HandleRequirementAsync</code>, <code>DbContext.OnModelCreating</code>/<code>OnConfiguring</code>, <code>JsonConverter&lt;T&gt;.Read/Write</code>, <code>Stream</code> subclasses. Strategy (composition) is often preferred when steps vary independently.</p>\n<h2 id=\"state\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-patterns/state\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>State <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Intent:</strong> an object <strong>changes its behaviour when its internal state changes</strong>, with each state as its own class, instead of <code>if (status == …)</code> scattered everywhere.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">abstract</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderState</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">virtual</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderState</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Pay</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> o) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Invalid</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">nameof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Pay</span>));\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">virtual</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderState</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Ship</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> o) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Invalid</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">nameof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ship</span>));\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">virtual</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderState</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Cancel</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> o) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Invalid</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">nameof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cancel</span>));\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Invalid</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> action) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;Cannot {action} an order that is {GetType().Name}&quot;</span>);\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Placed</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderState</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderState</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Pay</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> o) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Paid</span>(); <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderState</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Cancel</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> o) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Cancelled</span>(); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Paid</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderState</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderState</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Ship</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> o) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Shipped</span>(); <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderState</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Cancel</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> o) { o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Refund</span>(); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Cancelled</span>(); } }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Shipped</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderState</span> { }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Cancelled</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderState</span> { }</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>For simpler lifecycles, an <strong>enum plus a transition table</strong> or a library like <strong>Stateless</strong> is often enough. MassTransit sagas are state machines across services.</p>\n<h2 id=\"iterator-visitor-and-the-rest\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-patterns/iterator-visitor-and-the-rest\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Iterator, Visitor and the rest <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Iterator:</strong> sequential access without exposing the structure. <strong>Built into C#</strong>: <code>IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</code>/<code>IEnumerator&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>foreach</code>, <code>yield return</code>, <code>IAsyncEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Visitor:</strong> add operations to a class hierarchy without changing it, via double dispatch. <strong>In .NET:</strong> <code>ExpressionVisitor</code> (EF Core&#39;s query translation is visitors all the way down, exactly the expression-tree walk <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/linq/expression-trees-what-ef-core-is-actually-reading\">LINQ</a> traced), Roslyn&#39;s <code>CSharpSyntaxVisitor</code>. Modern C# pattern matching (<code>switch</code> on types) often replaces simple visitors.</li>\n<li><strong>Memento:</strong> capture and restore an object&#39;s state (undo, snapshots). Event sourcing snapshots are a relative.</li>\n<li><strong>Interpreter:</strong> evaluate sentences in a small language (rule engines, query parsers). Expression trees and Roslyn scripting are related.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"beyond-gof-patterns-youll-meet-in-net-backends\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-patterns/beyond-gof-patterns-youll-meet-in-net-backends\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Beyond GoF: patterns you&#39;ll meet in .NET backends <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Where it&#39;s covered</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Dependency Injection / Service Locator (anti-pattern)</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/dependency-injection\">Dependency injection</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Options pattern</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/configuration-options\">Configuration</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Repository, Unit of Work, Specification</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/app-architectures/repository-and-unit-of-work-a-debate-thats-really-about-ef-c\">App architectures</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CQRS, Mediator pipeline, Result</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cqrs-mediator-result\">CQRS &amp; Result</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Aggregate, Value Object, Domain Event</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ddd\">DDD</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Outbox, Inbox, Saga, Competing Consumers</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events\">Messaging &amp; events</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Retry, Circuit Breaker, Bulkhead, Timeout</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/resilience\">Resilience</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>API Gateway, BFF, Strangler Fig, Sidecar</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/microservices\">Microservices</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cache-Aside</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors\">Caching</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Which design patterns does ASP.NET Core itself use?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Chain of Responsibility (the middleware pipeline, DelegatingHandlers, filters), Builder (WebApplicationBuilder, IServiceCollection, options builders), Factory (IHttpClientFactory, ILoggerFactory, IDbContextFactory), Decorator (DelegatingHandler, stream wrappers), Strategy (authentication schemes, naming policies, comparers), Observer (IOptionsMonitor.OnChange, change tokens), Template Method (BackgroundService, AuthenticationHandler), Adapter (logging providers), Facade (CreateBuilder) and Dependency Injection throughout.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Strategy vs State?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Both put varying behaviour behind an interface. In Strategy, the client or configuration chooses an algorithm and the strategies are independent of each other. In State, the object itself transitions between state objects as its internal state changes, and states know about the transitions.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Decorator vs Proxy vs Adapter?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Decorator and Proxy keep the same interface: a decorator adds behaviour (logging, caching), while a proxy controls access to the real object (lazy loading, remote, permissions). An adapter changes an interface into the one the client expects.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you implement the Decorator pattern with DI?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Register the real implementation, then register the interface with a decorator that receives the inner instance, either via a factory lambda (<code>sp =&gt; new CachingRepo(sp.GetRequiredService&lt;SqlRepo&gt;(), …)</code>) or with Scrutor&#39;s Decorate&lt;TInterface, TDecorator&gt;(). Decorators can be stacked.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why is the Singleton pattern often called an anti-pattern, and what do you use instead?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A static Instance hides dependencies, introduces global mutable state, can&#39;t be replaced in tests, and couples callers to a concrete class. Use the DI container&#39;s singleton lifetime so there&#39;s still one instance, but it&#39;s injected explicitly and replaceable. It still must be thread-safe.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Where does the Chain of Responsibility pattern appear in .NET?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The ASP.NET Core middleware pipeline (each component handles or passes to next), HttpClient&#39;s DelegatingHandler pipeline, MVC and endpoint filters, MediatR pipeline behaviours, and authorization handlers.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What&#39;s the difference between the Command pattern and an event?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A command is a request to do something, addressed to one handler, named in the imperative (PlaceOrder), and it may be rejected. An event is a notification that something already happened, named in the past tense (OrderPlaced), published to zero or more subscribers, and it can&#39;t be rejected.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>When is the Builder pattern useful?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>When constructing an object requires many optional parts or steps, when you want a readable fluent API and validation at Build(), or when the result should be immutable. Examples: WebApplicationBuilder, StringBuilder, test data builders.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-patterns/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Design Patterns: Elements of Reusable Object-Oriented Software</em> (Gamma, Helm, Johnson, Vlissides), the original GoF book.</li>\n<li><em>Head First Design Patterns</em> (2nd ed.): the most approachable introduction.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://refactoring.guru/design-patterns\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">refactoring.guru/design-patterns</a> has excellent diagrams and C# examples for every GoF pattern.</li>\n<li><em>Patterns of Enterprise Application Architecture</em> by Martin Fowler: Repository, Unit of Work, Data Mapper and more.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/design-patterns/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>The checks below instantiate six of the patterns above with plain, dependency-free stand-ins (no DI container, no HybridCache, no EF Core) and assert the one behavioural claim each pattern is actually known for. Save as <code>Program.cs</code> in a new console project (<code>dotnet new console</code>) and run with <code>dotnet run</code>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Verifies claims from this chapter: Builder validates at Build(), Decorator composition</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// order, Strategy picks an algorithm at runtime, Singleton (Lazy&lt;T&gt;) gives exactly one instance, State</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// rejects invalid transitions, Composite treats a group of rules as a single rule.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Collections</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Generic</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Linq</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> fails = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> s) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: {s}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> cond, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> s)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (cond) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(s);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">else</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: {s}&quot;</span>); fails++; }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Builder: validates once at Build(), produces an immutable-ish result ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Throws</span>(() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReportBuilder</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>()), <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Builder.Build() throws when a required field (Title) was never set&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> report = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReportBuilder</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Titled</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Q3 sales&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(report.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Title</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Q3 sales&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Builder.Build() succeeds once required fields are set&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Decorator: stacking order is the order you wrap in, and wrapping doesn't change the interface ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> trace = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">IRepo</span> traced = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TraceRepo</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlRepo</span>(), trace, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Sql&quot;</span>);\ntraced = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TraceRepo</span>(traced, trace, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Caching&quot;</span>);\ntraced = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TraceRepo</span>(traced, trace, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Logging&quot;</span>);\ntraced.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Get</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(trace.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SequenceEqual</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>[] { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Logging&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Caching&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Sql&quot;</span> }), <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Decorator: Logging→Caching→Sql registration runs outer-to-inner, in that order&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Strategy: swapping the algorithm changes the result without changing the caller ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">IDiscount</span> vip = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">VipDiscount</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">IDiscount</span> none = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">NoDiscount</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(vip.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Discount</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">100</span>) == <span class=\"tk-num\">10</span> &amp;&amp; none.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Discount</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">100</span>) == <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Strategy: selecting a different IDiscount changes behaviour without changing the caller&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Singleton via Lazy&lt;T&gt;: every access returns the same instance ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReferenceEquals</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Clock</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Instance</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Clock</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Instance</span>), <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Singleton (Lazy&lt;T&gt;): Clock.Instance always returns the same object&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- State: valid transitions succeed, invalid ones throw instead of silently doing nothing ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderState</span> placed = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Placed</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderState</span> paid = placed.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Pay</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(paid <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Paid</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;State: Placed.Pay() transitions to Paid&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Throws</span>(() =&gt; placed.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ship</span>()), <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;State: Placed.Ship() is invalid and throws, instead of silently no-op'ing&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Composite: a group of rules behaves like a single rule (uniform interface) ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">IPriceRule</span> tenPct = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PercentOff</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0.10m</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">IPriceRule</span> composite = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CompositeRule</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PercentOff</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0.10m</span>), <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PercentOff</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0.10m</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(composite.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Apply</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">100</span>) == tenPct.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Apply</span>(tenPct.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Apply</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">100</span>)), <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Composite: applying a group of rules equals applying each rule in sequence&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(fails == <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span> ? <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;\\nAll checks passed.&quot;</span> : <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;\\n{fails} check(s) failed.&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (fails &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exit</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Throws</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Action</span> a) { <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { a(); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>; } <span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>; } }\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReportBuilder</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>? _title;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReportBuilder</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Titled</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> t) { _title = t; <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">this</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Report</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(_title ?? <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Title required&quot;</span>));\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Report</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Title</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IRepo</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Get</span>(); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlRepo</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IRepo</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Get</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;data&quot;</span>; }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TraceRepo</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IRepo</span> inner, <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; trace, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> name) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IRepo</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Get</span>() { trace.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(name); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> inner.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Get</span>(); }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IDiscount</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Discount</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> subtotal); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">NoDiscount</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IDiscount</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Discount</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> s) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">VipDiscount</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IDiscount</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Discount</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> s) =&gt; s * <span class=\"tk-num\">0.10m</span>; }\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Clock</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Lazy</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Clock</span>&gt; _instance = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Clock</span>());\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Clock</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Instance</span> =&gt; _instance.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Clock</span>() { }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">abstract</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderState</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">virtual</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderState</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Pay</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Cannot Pay from &quot;</span> + <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetType</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">virtual</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderState</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Ship</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Cannot Ship from &quot;</span> + <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetType</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>);\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Placed</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderState</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderState</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Pay</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Paid</span>(); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Paid</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderState</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderState</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Ship</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Shipped</span>(); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Shipped</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderState</span> { }\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPriceRule</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Apply</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> price); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PercentOff</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> pct) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPriceRule</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Apply</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> p) =&gt; p * (<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> - pct); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CompositeRule</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">params</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPriceRule</span>[] rules) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPriceRule</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Apply</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> p) =&gt; rules.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Aggregate</span>(p, (acc, r) =&gt; r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Apply</span>(acc));\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"what-patterns-are-and-why-recognising-them-is-worth-the-effo","text":"What patterns are, and why recognising them is worth the effort","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"factory-method-and-simple-factory","text":"Factory Method and Simple Factory","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"abstract-factory","text":"Abstract Factory","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"builder","text":"Builder","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"singleton","text":"Singleton","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"prototype","text":"Prototype","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"adapter","text":"Adapter","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"decorator","text":"Decorator","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"proxy","text":"Proxy","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"facade","text":"Facade","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"composite","text":"Composite","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"bridge-and-flyweight","text":"Bridge and Flyweight","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"strategy","text":"Strategy","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"observer","text":"Observer","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"command","text":"Command","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"chain-of-responsibility","text":"Chain of Responsibility","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"mediator","text":"Mediator","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"template-method","text":"Template Method","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"state","text":"State","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"iterator-visitor-and-the-rest","text":"Iterator, Visitor and the rest","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"beyond-gof-patterns-youll-meet-in-net-backends","text":"Beyond GoF: patterns you'll meet in .NET backends","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":4873,"minutes":22,"num":"5.2","part":"p5"},"app-architectures":{"id":"app-architectures","title":"Application Architectures: Layered, Clean, Hexagonal, Vertical Slice and Modular Monolith","summary":"How to organise a whole solution. Why architecture matters, the classic layered (n-tier) architecture, Onion/Clean/Hexagonal with the dependency rule, a complete Clean Architecture solution in .NET, Repository and Unit of Work (and the debate around them), Vertical Slice Architecture, the modular monolith, and how to enforce boundaries.","layer":"arch","where":"Application architecture › Solution structure (system level)","fits":"<p>This chapter applies <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/design-principles\">design principles</a>, especially Dependency Inversion, to the whole solution. Physically, architecture means <strong>projects and project references</strong> (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/projects-sdk-nuget\">Projects &amp; SDK</a>); at runtime, it&#39;s wired together in the DI composition root (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/dependency-injection\">Dependency injection</a>). It&#39;s the bridge between layer 3 (frameworks at the edges) and the domain at the centre (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ddd\">DDD</a>).</p>\n","prereqs":["design-principles","dependency-injection","efcore-fundamentals"],"next":["cqrs-mediator-result","ddd","microservices"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/app-architectures/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Architecture at the solution scale asks: how is the codebase divided, and which parts are allowed to depend on which?</li>\n<li>Classic layered (n-tier) architecture has business logic depend on data access, which couples business rules to persistence — Clean/Onion/Hexagonal fix this by putting the domain in the center and making all dependencies point <strong>inward</strong>.</li>\n<li><code>DbContext</code>/<code>DbSet</code> already <em>are</em> a unit-of-work/repository — a generic repository wrapper on top of EF Core usually hides features rather than adding value; per-aggregate repositories are the version that actually earns its keep.</li>\n<li>Vertical Slice Architecture organizes by feature (one file per use case) instead of by technical layer; it combines well with a shared domain core.</li>\n<li>A <strong>modular monolith</strong> (strongly separated modules, one deployable) gets most of microservices&#39; boundary benefits without the network/distributed-transaction cost — a common default before reaching for real microservices.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"what-architecture-means-at-this-scale\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/app-architectures/what-architecture-means-at-this-scale\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What &quot;architecture&quot; means at this scale <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Everything in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/design-principles\">Design principles</a> applied to individual classes. This chapter asks the same question at the scale of an entire solution: how do you divide a codebase into parts, what is each part responsible for, and <strong>which parts may depend on which</strong>? Good architecture keeps <strong>business rules</strong> independent of <strong>delivery mechanisms</strong> (HTTP, UI, messages) and <strong>infrastructure</strong> (databases, queues, external APIs), so that:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>business logic can be tested without a web server or database,</li>\n<li>frameworks and vendors can be upgraded or swapped with local changes,</li>\n<li>different people can work on different areas without stepping on each other,</li>\n<li>the code tells you what the system <strong>does</strong> (orders, payments), not just what it&#39;s <strong>built with</strong> (controllers, repositories).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"layered-n-tier-architecture-the-traditional-starting-point\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/app-architectures/layered-n-tier-architecture-the-traditional-starting-point\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Layered (n-tier) architecture: the traditional starting point <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>┌──────────────────────────┐\n│ Presentation (API / UI)  │  controllers, endpoints, view models\n├──────────────────────────┤\n│ Business Logic (BLL)     │  services, rules\n├──────────────────────────┤\n│ Data Access (DAL)        │  repositories, EF Core, SQL\n├──────────────────────────┤\n│ Database                 │\n└──────────────────────────┘\nEach layer depends on the one BELOW it.</code></pre></figure>\n<p><strong>Pros:</strong> simple, familiar, and a clear separation of technical concerns. <strong>Cons:</strong> trace the dependency arrows and the real problem becomes visible — the business layer <strong>depends on data access</strong>, so business rules are coupled to persistence (testing needs the DAL, entities are shaped by tables), and a single feature&#39;s logic is scattered across all four layers rather than living in one place. It tends toward anemic models and &quot;service classes&quot; holding all the actual logic.</p>\n<h2 id=\"onion-hexagonal-and-clean-architecture-three-names-one-fix\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/app-architectures/onion-hexagonal-and-clean-architecture-three-names-one-fix\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Onion, Hexagonal and Clean Architecture: three names, one fix <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>All three names below are solving exactly the layered architecture&#39;s core problem: put the <strong>domain at the centre</strong>, and make <strong>all dependencies point inward</strong>, so business logic never has to know anything about the technology serving or persisting it.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>            ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n            │  Infrastructure &amp; Presentation (outer ring)                 │\n            │  ASP.NET Core endpoints · EF Core · Service Bus · Stripe    │\n            │   ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │\n            │   │  Application (use cases)                            │   │\n            │   │  PlaceOrderHandler · DTOs · interfaces it needs:     │   │\n            │   │  IOrderRepository, IPaymentGateway, IEmailSender     │   │\n            │   │   ┌────────────────────────────────────────────┐   │   │\n            │   │   │  Domain (core)                              │   │   │\n            │   │   │  Order · Money · OrderPlaced · rules         │   │   │\n            │   │   │  no dependencies on anything outside         │   │   │\n            │   │   └────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │   │\n            │   └────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘   │\n            └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                    THE DEPENDENCY RULE: source code dependencies point INWARD only</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Name</th>\n<th>Author</th>\n<th>Emphasis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Hexagonal / Ports &amp; Adapters</strong></td>\n<td>Alistair Cockburn (2005)</td>\n<td>The app has <strong>ports</strong> (interfaces) and <strong>adapters</strong> (implementations) for each external actor: driving (HTTP, CLI, tests) and driven (DB, email)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Onion</strong></td>\n<td>Jeffrey Palermo (2008)</td>\n<td>Concentric layers: domain model → domain services → application services → infrastructure/UI</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Clean</strong></td>\n<td>Robert C. Martin (2012)</td>\n<td>Entities → use cases → interface adapters → frameworks &amp; drivers; the Dependency Rule</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>How it works, concretely: the inner layers <strong>define interfaces</strong> for what they need (<code>IOrderRepository</code>), and the outer layers <strong>implement</strong> them (<code>EfOrderRepository</code>) — exactly the Dependency Inversion Principle from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/design-principles/d-dependency-inversion-principle\">Design principles</a>, now applied to the whole solution rather than one class. At runtime, control still flows outward (handler → repository → SQL), but the compile-time dependency points inward — the same inversion that single <code>OrderService</code> example demonstrated, scaled up to entire projects.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-clean-architecture-solution-in-net\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/app-architectures/a-clean-architecture-solution-in-net\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>A Clean Architecture solution in .NET <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>src/\n├── Shop.Domain/                    ← references: NOTHING (maybe a small shared kernel)\n│   ├── Orders/  Order.cs, OrderLine.cs, OrderStatus.cs, OrderPlaced.cs, IOrderRepository.cs\n│   ├── Customers/  Customer.cs, CustomerId.cs\n│   └── Common/  Entity.cs, ValueObject.cs, Money.cs, DomainException.cs\n├── Shop.Application/               ← references: Domain\n│   ├── Orders/PlaceOrder/  PlaceOrderCommand.cs, PlaceOrderHandler.cs, PlaceOrderValidator.cs\n│   ├── Orders/GetOrder/    GetOrderQuery.cs, GetOrderHandler.cs, OrderDto.cs\n│   ├── Abstractions/       IPaymentGateway.cs, IEmailSender.cs, IUnitOfWork.cs, IClock.cs\n│   └── DependencyInjection.cs   (AddApplication)\n├── Shop.Infrastructure/            ← references: Application, Domain\n│   ├── Persistence/  ShopDb.cs, Configurations/*.cs, Migrations/, OrderRepository.cs\n│   ├── Payments/     StripePaymentGateway.cs\n│   ├── Messaging/    OutboxPublisher.cs\n│   └── DependencyInjection.cs   (AddInfrastructure)\n└── Shop.Api/                       ← references: Application, Infrastructure (only for DI wiring)\n    ├── Endpoints/OrderEndpoints.cs\n    ├── Program.cs  ← composition root\n    └── appsettings.json\ntests/\n├── Shop.Domain.Tests/              unit tests: pure, fast\n├── Shop.Application.Tests/         handler tests with fakes\n├── Shop.Api.IntegrationTests/      WebApplicationFactory + Testcontainers\n└── Shop.ArchitectureTests/         enforce the dependency rule</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Notice what the <code>src/Shop.Domain/</code> comment says — &quot;references: NOTHING&quot; — and what that absence actually buys the test projects below it: <code>Shop.Domain.Tests</code> can run in milliseconds with no database, no HTTP server, no mocking framework needed for infrastructure, because the domain project genuinely has nothing external to fake.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Domain: pure C#, rules inside the model</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderLine</span>&gt; _lines = [];\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IReadOnlyList</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderLine</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span> =&gt; _lines;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Place</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> customer, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IEnumerable</span>&lt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductId</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>)&gt; lines) { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* invariants, raise OrderPlaced */</span> }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Application: orchestrates a use case using abstractions</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderHandler</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderRepository</span> orders, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGateway</span> payments, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IUnitOfWork</span> uow)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>&gt;&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">HandleAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderCommand</span> cmd, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> order = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Place</span>(cmd.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>, cmd.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> payment = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> payments.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeAsync</span>(order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>, cmd.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentToken</span>, ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!payment.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Succeeded</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Failure</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderErrors</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentDeclined</span>);\n        orders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(order);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> uow.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>(ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>;\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Infrastructure: implements the abstractions with real technology</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderRepository</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span> db) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderRepository</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>?&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span> id, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt; db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Include</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">FirstOrDefaultAsync</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> == id, ct);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> order) =&gt; db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(order);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// API: thin HTTP adapter</span>\norders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapPost</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderRequest</span> req, <span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderHandler</span> handler, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimsPrincipal</span> user, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt;\n    (<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> handler.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HandleAsync</span>(req.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToCommand</span>(user), ct)).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToHttpResult</span>());\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Composition root</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddApplication</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddInfrastructure</span>(builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configuration</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout c-why\"><div class=\"callout-title\">What you get</div><p>The domain and application layers compile and test <strong>without ASP.NET Core, EF Core or Stripe</strong>. Switching Stripe for Adyen means a new adapter in Infrastructure. Adding a gRPC or message-driven entry point reuses the same handlers. The folder names under Application read like a list of use cases: the architecture <strong>screams</strong> what the system does.</p>\n</aside>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Common Clean Architecture mistakes</div><ul>\n<li><strong>Leaking EF Core into the domain</strong> (<code>[Table]</code> attributes, <code>DbSet</code>, <code>IQueryable</code> returned from repositories). Keep mapping in Infrastructure with the Fluent API.</li>\n<li><strong>Application depending on Infrastructure</strong> &quot;just for one class&quot;: the dependency rule is binary.</li>\n<li><strong>Ceremony overload</strong>: an interface, a DTO, a mapper, a handler, a validator and a repository for a trivial CRUD screen. Apply it where the domain is complex enough to deserve it.</li>\n<li><strong>Anemic domain + fat &quot;services&quot;</strong>: the architecture is clean but the model is a bag of getters and setters. See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ddd\">DDD</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"repository-and-unit-of-work-a-debate-thats-really-about-ef-c\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/app-architectures/repository-and-unit-of-work-a-debate-thats-really-about-ef-c\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Repository and Unit of Work: a debate that&#39;s really about EF Core <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Repository</strong> (Fowler, <em>PoEAA</em>): mediates between the domain and data mapping, acting like an <strong>in-memory collection of aggregates</strong> — an &quot;aggregate&quot; here just means a cluster of related objects (say, an <code>Order</code> plus its <code>OrderLine</code>s) that&#39;s saved and loaded as one unit, a concept <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ddd\">DDD</a> covers in full — exposed via <code>GetAsync</code>, <code>Add</code>, <code>Remove</code>, hiding how they&#39;re stored.</li>\n<li><strong>Unit of Work:</strong> tracks changes during a business transaction and <strong>commits them together</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Here&#39;s the thing worth being direct about before picking a side: <strong>EF Core already implements both of these</strong>. <code>DbSet&lt;T&gt;</code> <em>is</em> a repository — it already gives you <code>Add</code>, <code>Find</code>, and querying over an in-memory-feeling collection. <code>DbContext</code> <em>is</em> a unit of work — it already tracks every change and commits them together via <code>SaveChanges()</code>, exactly the mechanism <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals/change-tracking-and-savechanges-how-ef-core-knows-what-to-wr\">EF Core fundamentals</a> described. So the real debate isn&#39;t &quot;should I use these patterns,&quot; it&#39;s &quot;should I wrap EF Core&#39;s own implementation of them in another layer&quot;:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Approach</th>\n<th>Pros</th>\n<th>Cons</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Use DbContext directly</strong> in handlers</td>\n<td>Least code; full EF power (projections, Include, <code>ExecuteUpdate</code>)</td>\n<td>Application layer depends on EF Core; harder to enforce aggregate loading rules</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Generic repository</strong> <code>IRepository&lt;T&gt;</code> over EF</td>\n<td>Looks tidy</td>\n<td>Usually <strong>adds nothing</strong>: hides EF features, leaks <code>IQueryable</code> or forces lowest-common-denominator queries. Often called an anti-pattern</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Specific repositories per aggregate</strong> (<code>IOrderRepository</code>)</td>\n<td>Domain language; one place to define how an aggregate is loaded; keeps EF out of Domain/Application; easy to fake</td>\n<td>More code</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Specific repositories for writes + direct queries (EF/Dapper) for reads</strong></td>\n<td>Best of both: aggregates protected, reads optimised (CQRS)</td>\n<td>Two styles to learn</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>A balanced answer, and a common modern choice: <strong>specific aggregate repositories for the write side</strong> (DDD), and <strong>query handlers using DbContext or Dapper with projections for the read side</strong> — which mirrors the exact write/read split <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ado-dapper/dapper-vs-ef-core-the-real-decision-not-a-popularity-contest\">ADO.NET &amp; Dapper</a> recommended for its own, narrower reasons.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IUnitOfWork</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct = <span class=\"tk-kw\">default</span>); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">DbContextOptions</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>&gt; o) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">DbContext</span>(o), <span class=\"tk-ty\">IUnitOfWork</span> { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* SaveChangesAsync already exists */</span> }\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IUnitOfWork</span>&gt;(sp =&gt; sp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>&gt;());</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><strong>Specification pattern:</strong> encapsulates query criteria as objects (<code>new OrdersForCustomerSpec(customerId, includeLines: true)</code>), so repositories don&#39;t explode into dozens of <code>GetByXAndY</code> methods (e.g. the Ardalis.Specification library).</p>\n<h2 id=\"vertical-slice-architecture-organising-by-feature-instead-of\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/app-architectures/vertical-slice-architecture-organising-by-feature-instead-of\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Vertical Slice Architecture: organising by feature instead of by role <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Coined by Jimmy Bogard. Instead of organising by <strong>technical layer</strong> — the instinct every architecture above this section shares — organise by <strong>feature</strong>: each request type (a &quot;slice&quot; through all layers) owns everything it needs, in one place.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Layered / Clean:                          Vertical slices:\nControllers/  OrdersController            Features/\nServices/     OrderService                  Orders/\nRepositories/ OrderRepository                 PlaceOrder.cs     ← request, validator, handler, endpoint\nDTOs/         OrderDto                        GetOrder.cs\nValidators/   OrderValidator                  CancelOrder.cs\n                                            Products/\n→ one feature touches 5 folders               SearchProducts.cs\n                                          → one feature is one file or folder</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancelOrder</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Command</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Reason</span>);\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Validator</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">AbstractValidator</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Command</span>&gt;\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Validator</span>() { <span class=\"tk-ty\">RuleFor</span>(x =&gt; x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Reason</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotEmpty</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">MaximumLength</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">500</span>); }\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">internal</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">NoContent</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Conflict</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProblemDetails</span>&gt;&gt;&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Handle</span>(\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancelRequest</span> body, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span> db, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeProvider</span> clock, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> order = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FirstOrDefaultAsync</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> == id, ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (order <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TypedResults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>();\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> result = order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cancel</span>(body.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Reason</span>, clock.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetUtcNow</span>());          <span class=\"tk-com\">// rule lives in the domain model</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (result.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsFailure</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TypedResults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Conflict</span>(result.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToProblem</span>());\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>(ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TypedResults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NoContent</span>();\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Map</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IEndpointRouteBuilder</span> app) =&gt;\n        app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapPost</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/orders/{id:int}/cancel&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Handle</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddEndpointFilter</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidationFilter</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancelRequest</span>&gt;&gt;();\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Clean / layered</th>\n<th>Vertical slices</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Organised by</td>\n<td>Technical role</td>\n<td>Feature / use case</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Change to one feature touches</td>\n<td>Many folders/projects</td>\n<td>One file/folder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Coupling between features</td>\n<td>Shared services encourage it</td>\n<td>Minimised; slices share little</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Abstractions</td>\n<td>Many shared ones</td>\n<td>Added per slice only when needed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk</td>\n<td>Ceremony, scattered features</td>\n<td>Duplication; logic leaking into handlers instead of the domain</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>These two approaches <strong>combine well</strong> rather than competing: a rich <strong>domain</strong> project shared by <strong>vertical slices</strong> in the API/application project is a very popular structure today — note <code>CancelOrder.Handle</code> above still calls <code>order.Cancel(...)</code>, keeping the actual business rule inside the domain model even though the surrounding plumbing is organised by feature. Minimal APIs, CQRS and the REPR pattern (FastEndpoints) fit slices naturally.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-modular-monolith-most-of-microservices-benefits-none-of-\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/app-architectures/the-modular-monolith-most-of-microservices-benefits-none-of-\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The modular monolith: most of microservices&#39; benefits, none of the network <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>A <strong>single deployable</strong> application divided into <strong>modules</strong> that are as independent as microservices <em>logically</em>: one per bounded context, each with its own domain, data (its own schema or DbContext) and a small public API.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Shop.Host (one ASP.NET Core app, one deployment)\n├── Modules/Ordering/   Ordering.Api · Ordering.Application · Ordering.Domain · Ordering.Infrastructure (schema &quot;ordering&quot;)\n├── Modules/Catalog/    …                                                         (schema &quot;catalog&quot;)\n├── Modules/Billing/    …                                                         (schema &quot;billing&quot;)\n└── Shared/             building blocks (Result, Entity, in-process event bus)\n\nRules: modules talk only via public contracts (interfaces or integration events), never via another module's tables or internal classes.</code></pre></figure>\n<p><strong>Why:</strong> most of the structural benefits of microservices (clear boundaries, team ownership, independent evolution) come from exactly this kind of strict module separation — and this structure gets them with <strong>none of the distributed-systems cost</strong> (network failures, distributed transactions, many deployments) that real service-to-service calls would introduce. If a module later genuinely needs independent scaling, it&#39;s already shaped to be <strong>extracted into a service</strong> with comparatively little rework, because the boundary was already enforced. Many teams now recommend this as the default starting architecture (see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/microservices\">Microservices</a>), reaching for actual microservices only once a concrete, specific reason demands it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"enforcing-architecture\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/app-architectures/enforcing-architecture\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Enforcing architecture <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Boundaries that aren&#39;t enforced erode — a well-drawn diagram on a wiki page stops anyone from accidentally violating it for approximately one sprint. The tools below make the boundary something the build itself checks:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Project references:</strong> the compiler rejects <code>using Shop.Infrastructure</code> in Domain if there&#39;s no reference. The strongest guarantee.</li>\n<li><strong><code>internal</code> by default</strong>: only expose what other projects need.</li>\n<li><strong>Architecture tests</strong> with NetArchTest or ArchUnitNET:</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-attr\">[Fact]</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Domain_has_no_dependency_on_other_layers</span>() =&gt;\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Types</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">InAssembly</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Assembly</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShouldNot</span>()\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">HaveDependencyOnAny</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop.Application&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop.Infrastructure&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop.Api&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore&quot;</span>)\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetResult</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsSuccessful</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShouldBeTrue</span>();\n\n<span class=\"tk-attr\">[Fact]</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Modules_do_not_reference_each_others_internals</span>() =&gt;\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Types</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">InAssembly</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderingModule</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Assembly</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShouldNot</span>()\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">HaveDependencyOn</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop.Modules.Billing.Domain&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetResult</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsSuccessful</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShouldBeTrue</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<ol start=\"4\">\n<li><strong>Analyzers / code review checklists</strong>, and documenting decisions as <strong>ADRs</strong> (Architecture Decision Records).</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"choosing\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/app-architectures/choosing\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Choosing <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Situation</th>\n<th>Reasonable choice</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Small CRUD service, simple rules</td>\n<td>Minimal APIs + EF Core directly, feature folders; little layering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rich business rules, long-lived product</td>\n<td>Clean Architecture with a DDD domain; CQRS reads</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Many features, fast-moving team</td>\n<td>Vertical slices over a shared domain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Several bounded contexts, one team or a few</td>\n<td><strong>Modular monolith</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Independent scaling/deployment, many teams, mature DevOps</td>\n<td>Microservices (often extracted from a modular monolith)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is Clean Architecture?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>An architecture that places the domain (entities and rules) and application use cases at the centre, with infrastructure and presentation in outer layers, governed by the Dependency Rule: source dependencies point only inward. Inner layers define interfaces (ports) that outer layers implement (adapters), so business logic is independent of frameworks, databases and UI, and is easy to test.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What&#39;s the difference between layered architecture and Clean/Onion architecture?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>In classic layered architecture, the business layer depends on the data access layer below it. In Clean/Onion, dependencies are inverted: the domain and application define the abstractions, and infrastructure depends on them. Persistence becomes a detail plugged into the core.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is Hexagonal (Ports and Adapters) architecture?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The application core exposes ports (interfaces) for everything it needs or offers. Adapters connect external actors: driving adapters (HTTP endpoints, message consumers, tests) call the core, and driven adapters (DB repositories, email, payment gateways) implement the core&#39;s outbound ports. It&#39;s essentially the same idea as Clean/Onion.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Should you use the Repository pattern with EF Core?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>DbContext already is a unit of work and DbSet a repository, so a generic repository wrapper usually adds little and hides EF capabilities. Specific repositories per aggregate are valuable in DDD/Clean Architecture to encapsulate aggregate loading and keep EF out of the inner layers. A common approach is repositories for writes and direct EF/Dapper projections for reads.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is Vertical Slice Architecture and how does it compare with Clean Architecture?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Organising code by feature, where each request or use case owns its endpoint, validation, handler and data access, instead of by technical layer. Changes are localised and coupling between features is low, but you must watch for duplication and keep business rules in the domain. It can be combined with a Clean-style domain core.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is a modular monolith and why is it popular?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A single deployable app split into strongly separated modules aligned to bounded contexts, each with its own data and public contracts. It offers clear boundaries and team ownership without the operational and consistency costs of microservices, and modules can be extracted into services later if needed.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you enforce architectural boundaries in .NET?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Project references (the compiler forbids undeclared dependencies), internal visibility by default, architecture tests (NetArchTest/ArchUnitNET) in CI, analyzers, and documented ADRs plus code review.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/app-architectures/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/architecture/modern-web-apps-azure/common-web-application-architectures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Common web application architectures (Microsoft eBook)</a></li>\n<li>Robert C. Martin, <em>Clean Architecture</em>.</li>\n<li>Jimmy Bogard, &quot;Vertical Slice Architecture&quot; (talk and blog).</li>\n<li>Jason Taylor&#39;s and Ardalis&#39;s (Steve Smith) Clean Architecture templates on GitHub. Milan Jovanović&#39;s blog for modular monolith examples.</li>\n<li>Martin Fowler, <em>Patterns of Enterprise Application Architecture</em> (Repository, Unit of Work, Service Layer).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/app-architectures/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (plain console app, .NET 8/9, no packages). It checks the two claims that are actually mechanical facts, not design opinions: that <code>IUnitOfWork</code>/repository abstractions let a handler run against a fake with zero real infrastructure (the actual point of the Dependency Rule), and that the inward-pointing Dependency Rule is literally just &quot;who references whom,&quot; checkable by reflection the same way an architecture test would.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// --- The Dependency Rule in miniature: Application depends on an interface Domain defines;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//     Infrastructure implements it. Application never references Infrastructure's concrete type. ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> fake = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FakeOrderRepositoryAndUow</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> handler = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderHandler</span>(fake, fake);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> rows = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> handler.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HandleAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;order-1&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (rows != <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> || fake.<span class=\"tk-ty\">All</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> != <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: handler should work against the fake with no real DB&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: a use-case handler ran end-to-end against a pure in-memory fake — no EF Core, no SQL Server, no network&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Architecture-test-style check: does any type whose name contains &quot;Domain&quot; reference a type from &quot;Infrastructure&quot;? ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// (Real architecture tests use NetArchTest/ArchUnitNET and real assemblies; this reproduces the same idea</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//  the chapter describes — reflecting over a type's members and failing if a forbidden reference is found.)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DomainTypeReferencesInfrastructure</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Type</span> domainType, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Type</span> infrastructureType) =&gt;\n    domainType.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetMembers</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Any</span>(m =&gt;\n        (m <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Reflection</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PropertyInfo</span> p &amp;&amp; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PropertyType</span> == infrastructureType) ||\n        (m <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Reflection</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MethodInfo</span> meth &amp;&amp; meth.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetParameters</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Any</span>(prm =&gt; prm.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ParameterType</span> == infrastructureType)));\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">DomainTypeReferencesInfrastructure</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderHandler</span>), <span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">FakeOrderRepositoryAndUow</span>)))\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: PlaceOrderHandler (application layer) should depend only on interfaces, not the concrete fake&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: PlaceOrderHandler's members reference only IOrderRepository/IUnitOfWork, never the concrete implementation — the Dependency Rule holds, and this is exactly the kind of check an architecture test automates&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here — see Further reading: Common web application architectures (Microsoft), Clean Architecture (Martin)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// (which architecture style fits a given team/project, and whether modules are &quot;strongly separated,&quot; are judgment calls, not facts)</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderRepository</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> orderId); <span class=\"tk-ty\">IReadOnlyList</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">All</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IUnitOfWork</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PendingChanges</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>(); }\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// &quot;Application&quot; layer: orchestrates using only the abstractions, exactly like PlaceOrderHandler in the chapter</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderHandler</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderRepository</span> orders, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IUnitOfWork</span> uow)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">HandleAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> orderId)\n    {\n        orders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(orderId);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> uow.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>();\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// A fake standing in for &quot;Infrastructure&quot; (in the real chapter: EF Core / SQL Server) — no database involved at all</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FakeOrderRepositoryAndUow</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderRepository</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IUnitOfWork</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; _orders = [];\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IReadOnlyList</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">All</span> =&gt; _orders;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PendingChanges</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> orderId) { _orders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(orderId); <span class=\"tk-ty\">PendingChanges</span>++; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>() { <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> n = <span class=\"tk-ty\">PendingChanges</span>; <span class=\"tk-ty\">PendingChanges</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromResult</span>(n); }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"what-architecture-means-at-this-scale","text":"What \"architecture\" means at this scale","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"layered-n-tier-architecture-the-traditional-starting-point","text":"Layered (n-tier) architecture: the traditional starting point","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"onion-hexagonal-and-clean-architecture-three-names-one-fix","text":"Onion, Hexagonal and Clean Architecture: three names, one fix","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"a-clean-architecture-solution-in-net","text":"A Clean Architecture solution in .NET","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"repository-and-unit-of-work-a-debate-thats-really-about-ef-c","text":"Repository and Unit of Work: a debate that's really about EF Core","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"vertical-slice-architecture-organising-by-feature-instead-of","text":"Vertical Slice Architecture: organising by feature instead of by role","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"the-modular-monolith-most-of-microservices-benefits-none-of-","text":"The modular monolith: most of microservices' benefits, none of the network","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"enforcing-architecture","text":"Enforcing architecture","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"choosing","text":"Choosing","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":3498,"minutes":16,"num":"5.3","part":"p5"},"cqrs-mediator-result":{"id":"cqrs-mediator-result","title":"CQRS, the Mediator Pipeline and the Result Pattern","summary":"Three patterns that shape modern .NET application layers. CQS vs CQRS, levels of CQRS from separate models to separate stores, commands and queries with handlers, the mediator and its pipeline behaviours (validation, logging, transactions), alternatives to MediatR, and the Result pattern for expected failures, compared with exceptions.","layer":"arch","where":"Application architecture › Application layer patterns","fits":"<p>These patterns live in the <strong>application layer</strong> of <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/app-architectures\">Clean Architecture</a> or inside <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/app-architectures/vertical-slice-architecture-organising-by-feature-instead-of-by-role\">vertical slices</a>. Commands use the domain model (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ddd\">DDD</a>) and EF Core; queries often use projections or Dapper (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ado-dapper\">ADO.NET &amp; Dapper</a>). At system scale, CQRS read models are fed by events (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events\">Messaging &amp; events</a>).</p>\n","prereqs":["app-architectures","design-patterns"],"next":["ddd","messaging-events"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cqrs-mediator-result/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>CQS</strong> says a <em>method</em> shouldn&#39;t both change state and return data. <strong>CQRS</strong> scales that same idea up to a whole <em>architecture</em>: separate models for writes (commands) and reads (queries).</li>\n<li>CQRS is a spectrum: most teams should stop at &quot;separate handlers, same database&quot; (immediate consistency, low complexity) — separate physical read/write stores (eventual consistency) is a big jump, only worth it at real scale.</li>\n<li>A <strong>mediator</strong> (e.g. MediatR) just forwards a command/query to its one handler, but the real value is the <strong>pipeline</strong> wrapped around every handler: logging, validation, transactions, done once instead of in every handler.</li>\n<li>The <strong>Result pattern</strong> turns &quot;this can fail&quot; into part of a method&#39;s return type, instead of an exception the caller might forget to catch — use it for <em>expected</em> failures (not found, declined), keep exceptions for bugs and infrastructure failures.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"from-a-rule-about-methods-to-a-rule-about-architecture\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cqrs-mediator-result/from-a-rule-about-methods-to-a-rule-about-architecture\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>From a rule about methods to a rule about architecture <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><strong>CQS</strong> (Command-Query Separation, Bertrand Meyer) is a rule for <strong>methods</strong>: a method should either be a <strong>command</strong> (changes state, returns nothing) or a <strong>query</strong> (returns data, has no side effects), never both. Asking a question shouldn&#39;t change the answer — a useful discipline on its own, mentioned briefly back in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/design-principles/other-principles-worth-naming\">Design principles</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>CQRS</strong> (Command Query Responsibility Segregation, Greg Young) takes that exact same split and scales it up from a method to an entire architecture: use <strong>separate models</strong> (and code paths) for <strong>writes</strong> and <strong>reads</strong>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>                        ┌─────────── Write side ───────────┐\nPOST /orders ─▶ PlaceOrder command ─▶ handler ─▶ domain model (aggregate rules) ─▶ EF Core ─▶ DB\n                        └──────────────────────────────────┘\n                        ┌─────────── Read side ────────────┐\nGET /orders?… ─▶ ListOrders query ─▶ handler ─▶ projection / Dapper / read model ─▶ DTOs\n                        └──────────────────────────────────┘</code></pre></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout c-why\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Why separate reads and writes</div><p>They have <strong>different needs</strong>. Writes need <strong>rich behaviour and invariants</strong>: load an aggregate, apply rules, save atomically. Reads need <strong>speed and shape</strong>: flat DTOs tailored to each screen, joins across aggregates, no tracking, often cached. Force one model to serve both and it ends up mediocre at either job: entities get polluted with UI fields, read queries get forced through aggregates, and N+1 queries creep in. Reads also usually outnumber writes 10–100×, so splitting them means each side can be <strong>scaled and optimised separately</strong>.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"levels-of-cqrs-a-spectrum-not-a-binary-choice\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cqrs-mediator-result/levels-of-cqrs-a-spectrum-not-a-binary-choice\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Levels of CQRS: a spectrum, not a binary choice <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>CQRS is a spectrum, and most teams should stop well before the far end:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Level</th>\n<th>Write side</th>\n<th>Read side</th>\n<th>Consistency</th>\n<th>Complexity</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>0: none</strong></td>\n<td>Same model for everything</td>\n<td>Same</td>\n<td>Immediate</td>\n<td>Lowest</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>1: separate code paths</strong></td>\n<td>Command handlers + domain model + EF Core</td>\n<td>Query handlers + projections (<code>Select</code>) / Dapper, <strong>same database</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Immediate</strong></td>\n<td>Low; a great default</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>2: separate read models in the same DB</strong></td>\n<td>Same</td>\n<td>Denormalised read tables/views, updated in the same transaction or by triggers/events</td>\n<td>Immediate or near-immediate</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>3: separate stores</strong></td>\n<td>SQL (or an event store)</td>\n<td>Read DB (Cosmos, Elasticsearch, Redis), updated <strong>asynchronously</strong> from events</td>\n<td><strong>Eventual</strong></td>\n<td>High: projections, replay, lag handling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>+ Event Sourcing</strong></td>\n<td>Store events, not state</td>\n<td>Projections built from the event stream</td>\n<td>Eventual</td>\n<td>Highest</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Notice where the complexity genuinely jumps: levels 1 and 2 stay inside one database and one transaction, so consistency is immediate and the mental model barely changes from a normal CRUD app. Level 3 introduces something qualitatively different — <strong>eventual consistency</strong> — because the read model is now updated asynchronously, after the fact, by something reacting to events rather than participating in the original transaction.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">CQRS is not Event Sourcing</div><p>They&#39;re often mentioned together, but they&#39;re independent. You can do CQRS without event sourcing (levels 1–3) and, rarely, event sourcing without separate read models. Level 3 introduces <strong>eventual consistency</strong>: after a POST, an immediate GET may not see the change yet, and the UI must handle that.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"commands-and-queries-in-code\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cqrs-mediator-result/commands-and-queries-in-code\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Commands and queries in code <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// ── Commands: intent to change state; imperative names; return little (an id, a Result) ──</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderCommand</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IReadOnlyList</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderLineInput</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentToken</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderHandler</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderRepository</span> orders, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPricing</span> pricing, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGateway</span> payments, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IUnitOfWork</span> uow)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>&gt;&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Handle</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderCommand</span> cmd, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> priced = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> pricing.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PriceAsync</span>(cmd.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span>, ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> order = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Place</span>(cmd.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>, priced);                 <span class=\"tk-com\">// domain rules</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> charge = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> payments.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeAsync</span>(order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>, cmd.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentToken</span>, ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!charge.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Succeeded</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderErrors</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentDeclined</span>(charge.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Reason</span>);\n        orders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(order);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> uow.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>(ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>;\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// ── Queries: return data, no side effects; shaped for the caller ──</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetOrderHistoryQuery</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Page</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PageSize</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderHistoryItem</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PlacedAt</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Items</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetOrderHistoryHandler</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span> db)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">PagedResult</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderHistoryItem</span>&gt;&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Handle</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetOrderHistoryQuery</span> q, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> query = db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsNoTracking</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> == q.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> total = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> query.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CountAsync</span>(ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> items = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> query.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderByDescending</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PlacedAt</span>)\n            .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Skip</span>((q.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Page</span> - <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>) * q.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PageSize</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Take</span>(q.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PageSize</span>)\n            .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(o =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderHistoryItem</span>(o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>, o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PlacedAt</span>, o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>(), o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Amount</span>, o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span>))\n            .<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToListAsync</span>(ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(items, q.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Page</span>, q.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PageSize</span>, total);\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice how different these two handlers actually are in shape, not just in name: <code>PlaceOrderHandler</code> loads a rich <code>Order</code> aggregate and calls its own methods, while <code>GetOrderHistoryHandler</code> never touches the domain model at all — it projects straight from <code>DbSet</code> to DTO, exactly the pattern <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-querying/projection-the-single-biggest-win-in-this-chapter\">EF Core querying</a> recommended for every read endpoint. Rules of thumb that fall out of this: <strong>queries never modify state</strong>; queries can bypass the domain model entirely; <strong>commands go through aggregates</strong>; one handler per command or query.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-mediator-pattern-and-pipeline-behaviours\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cqrs-mediator-result/the-mediator-pattern-and-pipeline-behaviours\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The mediator pattern and pipeline behaviours <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>A <strong>mediator</strong> dispatches a request object to its <strong>single handler</strong>, so the sender doesn&#39;t need a reference to the handler directly. That indirection alone is a modest convenience — its real value is the <strong>pipeline</strong> it enables: behaviours that wrap <strong>every</strong> handler, giving you one place to put cross-cutting concerns, exactly the role <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/middleware\">Middleware</a> plays for HTTP requests, now scoped to your application layer instead.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Endpoint ─▶ mediator.Send(cmd) ─▶ [Logging] ─▶ [Validation] ─▶ [Authorization] ─▶ [Transaction] ─▶ Handler\n                                    pipeline behaviours (a chain of responsibility around handlers)</code></pre></figure>\n<p>With <strong>MediatR</strong> (the best-known library):</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderCommand</span>(…) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IRequest</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>&gt;&gt;;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderHandler</span>(…) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IRequestHandler</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderCommand</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>&gt;&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>&gt;&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Handle</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderCommand</span> cmd, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* … */</span> }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidationBehavior</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TRequest</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TResponse</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IEnumerable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IValidator</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TRequest</span>&gt;&gt; validators)\n    : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPipelineBehavior</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TRequest</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TResponse</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">where</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TRequest</span> : <span class=\"tk-kw\">notnull</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">where</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TResponse</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IResult</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TResponse</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Handle</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TRequest</span> request, <span class=\"tk-ty\">RequestHandlerDelegate</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TResponse</span>&gt; next, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> failures = (<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WhenAll</span>(validators.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(v =&gt; v.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidateAsync</span>(request, ct))))\n            .<span class=\"tk-ty\">SelectMany</span>(r =&gt; r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Errors</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToList</span>();\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (failures.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">TResponse</span>)<span class=\"tk-ty\">ResultFactory</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidationFailure</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TResponse</span>), failures);  <span class=\"tk-com\">// short-circuit</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> next();\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">LoggingBehavior</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TRequest</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TResponse</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ILogger</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TRequest</span>&gt; log) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPipelineBehavior</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TRequest</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TResponse</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">where</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TRequest</span> : <span class=\"tk-kw\">notnull</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TResponse</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Handle</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TRequest</span> request, <span class=\"tk-ty\">RequestHandlerDelegate</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TResponse</span>&gt; next, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> name = <span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TRequest</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>;\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> sw = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Stopwatch</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartNew</span>();\n        log.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogInformation</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Handling {Request}&quot;</span>, name);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> response = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> next();\n        log.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogInformation</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Handled {Request} in {Elapsed} ms&quot;</span>, name, sw.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ElapsedMilliseconds</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> response;\n    }\n}\n\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddMediatR</span>(c =&gt;\n{\n    c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RegisterServicesFromAssemblyContaining</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderHandler</span>&gt;();\n    c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddOpenBehavior</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">LoggingBehavior</span>&lt;,&gt;));\n    c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddOpenBehavior</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidationBehavior</span>&lt;,&gt;));\n    c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddOpenBehavior</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">typeof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TransactionBehavior</span>&lt;,&gt;));\n});\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapPost</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/orders&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderCommand</span> cmd, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ISender</span> sender, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt; (<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> sender.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Send</span>(cmd, ct)).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToHttp</span>());</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice <code>ValidationBehavior</code>&#39;s <code>next()</code> call is exactly the same shape as the request delegate chain in the middleware pipeline — it either calls onward or short-circuits, and the registration order of the three behaviours above determines the order they wrap the handler in, the same reverse-composition idea covered in full in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/middleware/now-heres-the-aha-a-middleware-is-just-a-function-wrapping-a\">Middleware</a>.</p>\n<p>MediatR also has <strong>notifications</strong> (<code>INotification</code> with multiple <code>INotificationHandler</code>s, i.e. in-process publish/subscribe), often used to dispatch domain events.</p>\n<h3 id=\"is-a-mediator-library-necessary\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cqrs-mediator-result/is-a-mediator-library-necessary\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Is a mediator library necessary?</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>MediatR</strong></td>\n<td>Popular and mature. <strong>Commercial licence since v13 (2025)</strong> for organisations above a revenue threshold</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Mediator</strong> (martinothamar)</td>\n<td>Source-generated, MIT, very fast, near-identical API</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Wolverine</strong></td>\n<td>Mediator + messaging + outbox in one framework</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Brighter / Darker</strong></td>\n<td>Command processor plus messaging</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>No library</strong></td>\n<td>Inject handlers directly (<code>PlaceOrderHandler</code>) and use <strong>decorators</strong> or endpoint filters for cross-cutting concerns. Simplest, explicit, and &quot;Go to definition&quot; works</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The honest tradeoff behind all of this: indirection hides call graphs, a mediator is &quot;service locator in disguise&quot; when overused, and it&#39;s genuinely unnecessary ceremony for a small app with a handful of use cases. Against that: a uniform pipeline, decoupled endpoints, and an easy, repeatable per-use-case structure that scales well as the number of use cases grows. A good interview answer acknowledges both sides rather than picking a tribe.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-result-pattern-making-expected-failure-part-of-the-metho\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cqrs-mediator-result/the-result-pattern-making-expected-failure-part-of-the-metho\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The Result pattern: making expected failure part of the method&#39;s contract <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Problem:</strong> many failures are <strong>expected outcomes</strong>, not exceptional situations: &quot;order not found&quot;, &quot;insufficient stock&quot;, &quot;email already registered&quot;, &quot;card declined&quot;. Using exceptions for them creates a specific, recurring set of problems:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>is <strong>invisible in the signature</strong>: <code>Task&lt;Order&gt; PlaceAsync()</code> doesn&#39;t tell you it can fail in five ways,</li>\n<li>is <strong>expensive</strong> (stack trace capture and unwinding) under load,</li>\n<li>turns control flow into <code>try/catch</code> at a distance,</li>\n<li>makes it easy to forget a case.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Result pattern:</strong> return an object that is <strong>either a success with a value or a failure with an error</strong>, and make callers handle both — turning &quot;this can fail&quot; from something buried in documentation (or nowhere) into something the compiler can see.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Code</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Message</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ErrorType</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Type</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">ErrorType</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Failure</span>)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">None</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;</span>);\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">enum</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ErrorType</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Failure</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Validation</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Conflict</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Unauthorized</span> }\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> ok, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span> error) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsSuccess</span> = ok; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span> = error; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsSuccess</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsFailure</span> =&gt; !<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsSuccess</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Success</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">None</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Failure</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span> e) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>, e);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Success</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">None</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Failure</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span> e) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">default</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>, e);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>? _value;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">internal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>? <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> ok, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span> e) : <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>(ok, e) =&gt; _value = <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsSuccess</span> ? _value! : <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;No value for a failed result&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">implicit</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">operator</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Success</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>);          <span class=\"tk-com\">// return order.Id;</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">implicit</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">operator</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span> error) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Failure</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;(error);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// return OrderErrors.NotFound(id);</span>\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderErrors</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span> id) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;order.not_found&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;Order {id} was not found&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ErrorType</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AlreadyShipped</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;order.already_shipped&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shipped orders can't be cancelled&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ErrorType</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Conflict</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentDeclined</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> reason) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;order.payment_declined&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;Payment declined: {reason}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ErrorType</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Failure</span>);\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice the two implicit operators on <code>Result&lt;T&gt;</code>: they&#39;re what let <code>PlaceOrderHandler</code>, back in the commands section, simply <code>return order.Id;</code> on success or <code>return OrderErrors.PaymentDeclined(charge.Reason);</code> on failure, with no explicit <code>Result.Success(...)</code> wrapping needed at either call site — the type conversion happens invisibly, which is exactly why the handler code reads so cleanly.</p>\n<p>Using it, and mapping to HTTP <strong>once</strong> at the edge:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Domain/application code returns errors as values</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Cancel</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> reason)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> == <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Shipped</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderErrors</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AlreadyShipped</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cancelled</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Raise</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderCancelled</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, reason));\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Success</span>();\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Presentation: one mapping for the whole API</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IResult</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ToHttp</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-kw\">this</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; r) =&gt; r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsSuccess</span> ? <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>) : r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToProblem</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IResult</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ToProblem</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">this</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span> e) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Problem</span>(\n    title: e.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Message</span>, type: e.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Code</span>,\n    statusCode: e.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Type</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">switch</span>\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">ErrorType</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Validation</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">400</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ErrorType</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">404</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ErrorType</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Conflict</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">409</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ErrorType</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Unauthorized</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">403</span>, _ =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">422</span>\n    });</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The payoff of <code>ToHttp</code> and <code>ToProblem</code> existing as exactly two small, shared methods: every single endpoint in the API maps a <code>Result</code> to an HTTP response the same way, once, rather than each handler re-deciding what status code &quot;not found&quot; should mean. Libraries: <strong>ErrorOr</strong>, <strong>FluentResults</strong>, <strong>Ardalis.Result</strong>, <strong>OneOf</strong> (discriminated-union style), <strong>CSharpFunctionalExtensions</strong>. &quot;Railway-oriented programming&quot; adds <code>Map</code>/<code>Bind</code> combinators to chain operations that may fail.</p>\n<h3 id=\"exceptions-vs-results\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cqrs-mediator-result/exceptions-vs-results\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Exceptions vs Results</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Use <strong>exceptions</strong> for</th>\n<th>Use <strong>Results</strong> for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Bugs and broken invariants (null where impossible, invalid state)</td>\n<td>Business rule rejections</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Infrastructure failures: DB down, timeouts, network errors</td>\n<td>Validation failures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Things the immediate caller can&#39;t handle meaningfully</td>\n<td>&quot;Not found&quot;, &quot;already exists&quot;, &quot;not allowed&quot; in normal flow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Constructors and framework callbacks that can&#39;t return a value</td>\n<td>Anything the caller is <em>expected</em> to branch on</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Both coexist in a healthy codebase: Results for the expected paths this whole section covers, plus a global <code>IExceptionHandler</code> (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/errors-logging/iexceptionhandler-mapping-exception-types-to-http-responses\">Errors &amp; logging</a>) for the unexpected ones nobody wrote a specific handler for. C# doesn&#39;t yet have built-in discriminated unions (a frequently discussed future feature), which is exactly why libraries like the ones above exist to fill that gap in the meantime.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is CQRS and when is it worth using?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Separating the write model (commands handled through the domain model) from the read model (queries returning DTOs via optimised projections or a separate store). Worth it when reads and writes have very different shapes or loads, or the domain is complex. Start with separate handlers on one database; separate stores add eventual consistency and should be justified by scale.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the difference between CQS and CQRS?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>CQS is a method-level rule: a method either changes state or returns data, not both. CQRS applies the idea to architecture, with separate models, handlers and possibly data stores for commands and queries.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Is CQRS the same as event sourcing?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>No. CQRS separates reads from writes; event sourcing stores state as a sequence of events. They&#39;re complementary (event-sourced systems usually need CQRS read models) but independent.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What problem does the mediator pattern solve in an application layer?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>It decouples senders (endpoints, consumers) from handlers and provides a pipeline of behaviours around every request: validation, logging, authorization, transactions, caching. Cross-cutting concerns are implemented once instead of in every handler.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are the downsides of MediatR?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Indirection (it&#39;s harder to navigate from caller to handler), runtime reflection-based resolution, possible overuse for trivial calls, and since 2025 a commercial licence for larger companies. Alternatives include source-generated Mediator, Wolverine, or injecting handlers directly with decorators.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the Result pattern and why use it instead of exceptions?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Returning an explicit success-or-failure object (with a typed error) from operations whose failures are expected. It makes failure part of the method contract, forces callers to handle it, avoids the cost and non-local control flow of exceptions, and allows mapping errors to HTTP responses in one place. Exceptions remain for truly exceptional, unexpected conditions.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How would you implement validation in a CQRS/MediatR setup?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>FluentValidation validators per command, executed by a ValidationBehavior pipeline behaviour before the handler, which short-circuits with a validation-failure Result (or throws a ValidationException mapped to 400 ProblemDetails). Keep business-rule checks that need aggregates inside the domain.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cqrs-mediator-result/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Martin Fowler, <a href=\"https://martinfowler.com/bliki/CQRS.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">&quot;CQRS&quot;</a> and <a href=\"https://martinfowler.com/bliki/CommandQuerySeparation.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">&quot;CommandQuerySeparation&quot;</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/cqrs\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CQRS pattern (Azure Architecture Center)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/jbogard/MediatR\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MediatR on GitHub</a> · <a href=\"https://github.com/martinothamar/Mediator\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mediator (source-generated)</a> · <a href=\"https://github.com/amantinband/error-or\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ErrorOr</a></li>\n<li>Scott Wlaschin, &quot;Railway Oriented Programming&quot; (talk/article).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cqrs-mediator-result/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>MediatR and the other libraries mentioned above aren&#39;t referenced here — the checks below build a minimal, dependency-free <code>Result&lt;T&gt;</code> and a minimal mediator pipeline to verify the actual mechanics this chapter describes (implicit conversions, error-to-status mapping, pipeline ordering and short-circuiting), without needing a package. Save as <code>Program.cs</code> in a new console project (<code>dotnet new console</code>) and run with <code>dotnet run</code>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Verifies claims from this chapter: Result&lt;T&gt;'s implicit conversions let a handler</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// just &quot;return value&quot; or &quot;return error&quot;, mapping an error to the right HTTP-style status, and a minimal</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// mediator pipeline runs its behaviours in registration order and can short-circuit before the handler.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Collections</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Generic</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Linq</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> fails = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> s) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: {s}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> cond, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> s)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (cond) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(s);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">else</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: {s}&quot;</span>); fails++; }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Result&lt;T&gt;: implicit operators let a method &quot;return value&quot; or &quot;return error&quot; directly ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Place</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> approve)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!approve) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderErrors</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentDeclined</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;insufficient_funds&quot;</span>);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// implicit Error -&gt; Result&lt;T&gt;</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NewGuid</span>();                                                    <span class=\"tk-com\">// implicit T -&gt; Result&lt;T&gt;</span>\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> declined = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Place</span>(approve: <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> approved = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Place</span>(approve: <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(declined.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsFailure</span> &amp;&amp; declined.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Code</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;order.payment_declined&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Result&lt;T&gt;: an Error converts implicitly into a failed Result&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(approved.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsSuccess</span> &amp;&amp; approved.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span> != <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Empty</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Result&lt;T&gt;: a T converts implicitly into a successful Result&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Throws</span>(() =&gt; _ = declined.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>), <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Result&lt;T&gt;.Value throws on a failed result instead of returning a default value silently&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Mapping a Result's error to an HTTP-style status happens once, not per-handler ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(declined.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Type</span> == <span class=\"tk-ty\">ErrorType</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Failure</span> &amp;&amp; <span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusFor</span>(declined.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span>) == <span class=\"tk-num\">422</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Error -&gt; status mapping: a generic failure maps to 422&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusFor</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderErrors</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>()) == <span class=\"tk-num\">404</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Error -&gt; status mapping: NotFound maps to 404&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Mediator pipeline: behaviours run in registration order and can short-circuit before the handler ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> trace = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> pipeline = <span class=\"tk-ty\">BuildPipeline</span>(trace,\n    next =&gt; () =&gt; { trace.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Logging&quot;</span>); <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> r = next(); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> r; },\n    next =&gt; () =&gt; { trace.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Validation&quot;</span>); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;short-circuited&quot;</span>; },   <span class=\"tk-com\">// fails validation, never calls next()</span>\n    next =&gt; () =&gt; { trace.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Handler&quot;</span>); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> next(); });\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> result = pipeline();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(trace.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SequenceEqual</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>[] { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Logging&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Validation&quot;</span> }), <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Mediator pipeline: behaviours run in the order they were registered&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(result == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;short-circuited&quot;</span> &amp;&amp; !trace.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contains</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Handler&quot;</span>), <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Mediator pipeline: a behaviour can short-circuit and skip the handler entirely&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(fails == <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span> ? <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;\\nAll checks passed.&quot;</span> : <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;\\n{fails} check(s) failed.&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (fails &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exit</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Throws</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Action</span> a) { <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { a(); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>; } <span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>; } }\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusFor</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span> e) =&gt; e.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Type</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">switch</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">ErrorType</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Validation</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">400</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ErrorType</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">404</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ErrorType</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Conflict</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">409</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ErrorType</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Unauthorized</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">403</span>, _ =&gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">422</span>\n};\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">BuildPipeline</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; trace, <span class=\"tk-kw\">params</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;&gt;[] behaviours)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; pipeline = () =&gt; { trace.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;(unreachable terminal)&quot;</span>); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;unreachable&quot;</span>; };\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = behaviours.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Length</span> - <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>; i &gt;= <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; i--) pipeline = behaviours[i](pipeline);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> pipeline;\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Code</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Message</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ErrorType</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Type</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">ErrorType</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Failure</span>)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">None</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;</span>);\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">enum</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ErrorType</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Failure</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Validation</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Conflict</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Unauthorized</span> }\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> ok, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span> error) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsSuccess</span> = ok; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span> = error; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsSuccess</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsFailure</span> =&gt; !<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsSuccess</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Success</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">None</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Failure</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span> e) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">default</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>, e);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt; : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>? _value;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">internal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>? <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> ok, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span> e) : <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>(ok, e) =&gt; _value = <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsSuccess</span> ? _value! : <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;No value for a failed result&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">implicit</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">operator</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Success</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">implicit</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">operator</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span> error) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Failure</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">T</span>&gt;(error);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderErrors</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;order.not_found&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Order was not found&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ErrorType</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentDeclined</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> reason) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;order.payment_declined&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;Payment declined: {reason}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ErrorType</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Failure</span>);\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"from-a-rule-about-methods-to-a-rule-about-architecture","text":"From a rule about methods to a rule about architecture","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"levels-of-cqrs-a-spectrum-not-a-binary-choice","text":"Levels of CQRS: a spectrum, not a binary choice","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"commands-and-queries-in-code","text":"Commands and queries in code","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"the-mediator-pattern-and-pipeline-behaviours","text":"The mediator pattern and pipeline behaviours","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"is-a-mediator-library-necessary","text":"Is a mediator library necessary?","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-result-pattern-making-expected-failure-part-of-the-metho","text":"The Result pattern: making expected failure part of the method's contract","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"exceptions-vs-results","text":"Exceptions vs Results","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":3741,"minutes":17,"num":"5.4","part":"p5"},"ddd":{"id":"ddd","title":"Domain-Driven Design","summary":"Modelling complex business domains. Strategic DDD (domains, subdomains, bounded contexts, ubiquitous language, context maps) and tactical DDD (entities, value objects, aggregates and their rules, domain events, domain services, repositories, factories), rich vs anemic models, implementing aggregates with EF Core, and when DDD is overkill.","layer":"arch","where":"Application architecture › Domain modelling","fits":"<p>DDD fills the <strong>centre</strong> of the <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/app-architectures\">Clean/Hexagonal architecture</a>. Value objects are records or readonly structs (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/modern-csharp/records\">Modern C#</a>), aggregates are persisted with EF Core (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals\">EF Core fundamentals</a>), and domain events feed the outbox (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-advanced/domain-events-and-the-outbox-on-savechanges\">EF Core advanced</a>). Strategically, bounded contexts are the natural boundaries for modules and microservices (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/microservices\">Microservices</a>).</p>\n","prereqs":["oop","design-principles","app-architectures"],"next":["microservices","messaging-events"],"levels":["i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ddd/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>DDD puts a <strong>domain model</strong> — the business&#39;s own concepts and rules — at the centre of the code, built together with domain experts, in their language.</li>\n<li><strong>Strategic DDD</strong> splits a big system into <strong>bounded contexts</strong>, each with its own consistent model and vocabulary (the same word can mean different things in different contexts on purpose).</li>\n<li><strong>Tactical DDD</strong> gives you the building blocks inside one context: <strong>entities</strong> (identity matters), <strong>value objects</strong> (only the values matter), and <strong>aggregates</strong> (a cluster with one root that&#39;s the only door in, protecting the rules that must always be true).</li>\n<li>One transaction touches one aggregate; aggregates talk to each other later, through <strong>domain events</strong>, not by reaching into each other directly.</li>\n<li>A <strong>rich</strong> model (behaviour and data together, like <code>order.Cancel(...)</code>) stops rules being bypassed; an <strong>anemic</strong> model (plain data + a separate service with the rules) doesn&#39;t.</li>\n<li>Skip the tactical patterns for simple CRUD — they&#39;re worth their cost only in a domain that&#39;s genuinely complex and core to the business.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-problem-ddd-exists-to-solve\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ddd/the-problem-ddd-exists-to-solve\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The problem DDD exists to solve <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Domain-Driven Design</strong> (Eric Evans, 2003) starts from a specific, honest observation about hard software: in genuinely complex business domains — insurance, logistics, banking, healthcare, e-commerce at scale — the hardest problem usually isn&#39;t a technical one at all. It&#39;s <strong>understanding and correctly encoding the business itself</strong>. When the rules governing that business get scattered across controllers, services and raw SQL, they inevitably get duplicated, start quietly contradicting each other, and eventually become something nobody can change with confidence anymore.</p>\n<p>DDD&#39;s answer is to put the <strong>domain model</strong> — a precise model of the business&#39;s concepts and rules — at the heart of the software, and to build that model <strong>collaboratively with domain experts</strong>, in their own language rather than a technical one. It has two distinct halves, operating at two different scales:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Strategic DDD (the big picture)</th>\n<th>Tactical DDD (the building blocks)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>How to <strong>split</strong> a large system into parts with clear boundaries and relationships</td>\n<td>How to <strong>model</strong> inside one part: entities, value objects, aggregates, events</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bounded contexts, ubiquitous language, subdomains, context maps</td>\n<td>Patterns implemented in C#</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shapes teams, modules and microservice boundaries</td>\n<td>Shapes classes and persistence</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-why\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Why DDD exists</div><p>In complex domains — insurance, logistics, banking, healthcare, e-commerce at scale — the hardest problem isn&#39;t technology. It&#39;s <strong>understanding and encoding the business correctly</strong>. When rules are scattered across controllers, services and SQL, they get duplicated, start to contradict each other, and eventually become impossible to change safely. DDD&#39;s answer is to concentrate the rules in one explicit model that mirrors how the business actually talks and thinks.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"strategic-ddd-ubiquitous-language-and-bounded-contexts\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ddd/strategic-ddd-ubiquitous-language-and-bounded-contexts\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Strategic DDD: ubiquitous language and bounded contexts <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<h3 id=\"ubiquitous-language\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ddd/ubiquitous-language\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Ubiquitous language</h3>\n<p>A shared, precise vocabulary used by developers and domain experts <strong>in conversation, documentation and code</strong>. If the business says &quot;a policy is <em>bound</em>&quot;, the code has <code>policy.Bind()</code>, not <code>policy.SetStatus(3)</code>. When the language changes, the code changes — the vocabulary isn&#39;t documentation sitting next to the code, it <em>is</em> the code.</p>\n<h3 id=\"bounded-contexts-the-same-word-deliberately-meaning-differen\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ddd/bounded-contexts-the-same-word-deliberately-meaning-differen\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Bounded contexts: the same word, deliberately meaning different things</h3>\n<p>A <strong>bounded context</strong> is an explicit boundary within which a model and its language are consistent. Here&#39;s the idea that trips people up on first encounter: <strong>the same word means different things in different contexts, and that&#39;s not a bug to fix — it&#39;s the correct state of affairs</strong>:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>&quot;Product&quot; in…\n  Catalog context:    name, description, images, categories, SEO slug\n  Pricing context:    list price, discounts, price lists, currency rules\n  Inventory context:  SKU, warehouse locations, stock on hand, reorder level\n  Shipping context:   weight, dimensions, hazardous-goods flag\n  Ordering context:   a line item snapshot: sku, name and price AT ORDER TIME</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Trying to build <strong>one</strong> <code>Product</code> class that covers all five contexts ends badly: it turns into a giant model everyone fights over, because a change Pricing needs can break something Shipping depends on — even though the two have nothing to do with each other. The fix: let each context keep its own small model, and connect them with explicit contracts (IDs, APIs, events) instead of one shared class.</p>\n<h3 id=\"subdomains-where-to-actually-invest\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ddd/subdomains-where-to-actually-invest\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Subdomains: where to actually invest</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Strategy</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Core</strong></td>\n<td>What gives the business its competitive edge (pricing engine, recommendation logic)</td>\n<td>Invest most here; rich DDD model; best people</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Supporting</strong></td>\n<td>Necessary and specific, but not differentiating (internal back-office workflows)</td>\n<td>Simpler models, maybe CRUD</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Generic</strong></td>\n<td>Solved problems (identity, payments, email, accounting)</td>\n<td><strong>Buy or use SaaS</strong> (Entra ID, Stripe, SendGrid)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The practical upshot of this table, easy to forget under deadline pressure: the full weight of DDD&#39;s tactical patterns — covered in the rest of this chapter — belongs in the <strong>core</strong> row. Spending that same effort modelling a generic subdomain like &quot;send an email&quot; is effort the business gets zero competitive value from.</p>\n<h3 id=\"context-maps\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ddd/context-maps\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Context maps</h3>\n<p>How bounded contexts relate: <strong>Shared Kernel</strong> (a small shared model), <strong>Customer/Supplier</strong> (upstream serves downstream&#39;s needs), <strong>Conformist</strong> (downstream adopts upstream&#39;s model as-is), <strong>Anti-Corruption Layer</strong> (downstream <strong>translates</strong> upstream&#39;s model into its own, protecting it from legacy or third-party models; implemented with adapters), <strong>Open Host Service / Published Language</strong> (a well-documented public API or event schema), <strong>Separate Ways</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Event Storming</strong> (Alberto Brandolini) is the popular workshop technique for discovering domain events, commands, aggregates and context boundaries with sticky notes on a wall.</p>\n<h2 id=\"entities-identity-that-outlives-any-particular-state\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ddd/entities-identity-that-outlives-any-particular-state\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Entities: identity that outlives any particular state <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>An <strong>entity</strong> has an <strong>identity that persists over time</strong> and through changes. Two entities are the same if their IDs are equal, even if every other field differs — this is the single defining trait that separates an entity from the value objects covered next.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">abstract</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TId</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">where</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TId</span> : <span class=\"tk-kw\">notnull</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TId</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">init</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-kw\">default</span>!;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Equals</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>? obj) =&gt; obj <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TId</span>&gt; other &amp;&amp; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetType</span>() == other.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetType</span>() &amp;&amp; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Equals</span>(other.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetHashCode</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetHashCode</span>();\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">struct</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>) { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">New</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateVersion7</span>()); }   <span class=\"tk-com\">// strongly typed ID</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h2 id=\"value-objects-defined-entirely-by-what-they-contain\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ddd/value-objects-defined-entirely-by-what-they-contain\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Value objects: defined entirely by what they contain <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>A <strong>value object</strong> is the mirror image of an entity: it&#39;s defined <strong>entirely by its values</strong>, has <strong>no identity</strong> of its own, and is <strong>immutable</strong>. Two value objects holding the same values are completely interchangeable — there&#39;s no sense in which one is &quot;the same instance&quot; as another, because that question doesn&#39;t apply. Examples: <code>Money</code>, <code>Address</code>, <code>EmailAddress</code>, <code>DateRange</code>, <code>Quantity</code>, <code>Percentage</code>, <code>GeoCoordinate</code>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Amount</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Currency</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> amount, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> currency)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (amount &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DomainException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;money.negative&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Amount can't be negative&quot;</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (currency <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">not</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Length</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span> }) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DomainException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;money.currency&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Use ISO 4217 codes&quot;</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Amount</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Round</span>(amount, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">MidpointRounding</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToEven</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Currency</span> = currency.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToUpperInvariant</span>();\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Zero</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> currency) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, currency);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span> other) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Currency</span> == other.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Currency</span> ? <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Amount</span> + other.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Amount</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Currency</span>) : <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DomainException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;money.mismatch&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Currency mismatch&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Multiply</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> qty) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Amount</span> * qty, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Currency</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;{Amount:0.00} {Currency}&quot;</span>;\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">EmailAddress</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">EmailAddress</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> v) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span> = v;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">EmailAddress</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Create</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> input) =&gt;\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">MailAddress</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryCreate</span>(input?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Trim</span>(), <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> m) ? <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">EmailAddress</span>(m.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Address</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToLowerInvariant</span>()) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Errors</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidEmail</span>;\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>(<code>Result&lt;T&gt;</code> above is a small wrapper that stands for &quot;success with a value, or a named failure&quot; without throwing an exception for an expected failure like &quot;bad email format&quot; — the full pattern is in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cqrs-mediator-result\">CQRS &amp; Result</a>.)</p>\n<p><strong>Why bother:</strong> notice what <code>Money</code>&#39;s constructor does that a plain <code>decimal</code> parameter never could — it fixes <strong>primitive obsession</strong> (<code>decimal price, string currency</code> passed separately everywhere, with nothing stopping them being passed in the wrong order, or a <code>string email</code> that was never actually validated as an email). Validation happens <strong>once, at creation</strong>, so once you&#39;re holding an <code>EmailAddress</code> instance, it is <em>guaranteed</em> valid — there&#39;s no later point in the code where you need to re-check it. Behaviour lives with the data (<code>Money.Add</code> enforces the currency-mismatch rule right where the data lives); method signatures become self-documenting (<code>Charge(Money amount)</code> tells you far more than <code>Charge(decimal amount, string currency)</code> ever could). Records give value equality for free, which is exactly the comparison semantics a value object needs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"aggregates-the-most-important-tactical-pattern-and-the-harde\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ddd/aggregates-the-most-important-tactical-pattern-and-the-harde\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Aggregates: the most important tactical pattern, and the hardest to get right <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>An <strong>aggregate</strong> is a cluster of entities and value objects treated as <strong>one unit for data changes</strong>, with one entity as the <strong>aggregate root</strong>. The aggregate is a <strong>consistency boundary</strong>: its <strong>invariants</strong> (business rules that must always be true) are enforced inside it, in a single transaction — nothing outside the aggregate is ever allowed to put it into an invalid state.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Order (aggregate root)                    rules enforced here:\n ├── OrderLine (entity, inside)            • an order has ≥ 1 line before it's placed\n ├── OrderLine                             • quantities 1–100; max 50 lines\n ├── ShippingAddress (value object)        • total = sum of lines; can't go negative\n └── Status                                • can't add lines after it's placed; can't cancel after shipping\nCustomerId, ProductId → references to OTHER aggregates by ID only</code></pre></figure>\n<p><strong>The rules of aggregate design (Vaughn Vernon):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Protect invariants inside the boundary.</strong> All changes go <strong>through the root</strong>: no public setters, no external code adding to <code>Lines</code> directly.</li>\n<li><strong>Design small aggregates.</strong> Big aggregates mean big loads, lock contention and concurrency conflicts. Most aggregates are one root entity plus a few value objects or child entities.</li>\n<li><strong>Reference other aggregates by identity only</strong> (<code>CustomerId</code>, not a <code>Customer</code> navigation), which keeps boundaries and transactions small.</li>\n<li><strong>One transaction modifies one aggregate.</strong> Changes to other aggregates happen <strong>eventually</strong>, via domain events.</li>\n<li>Load and save the <strong>whole aggregate</strong> through its <strong>repository</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Rule 3 fights your relational instincts the most. In a plain EF Core model you&#39;d naturally add a navigation property from <code>Order</code> straight to a full <code>Customer</code> entity. DDD says don&#39;t: if code can follow that navigation and change the customer while it&#39;s inside an order-handling transaction, the clean boundary rule 4 relies on is gone. Keep the reference as a plain ID instead.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">AggregateRoot</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderLine</span>&gt; _lines = [];\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Draft</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Address</span>? <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShippingAddress</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IReadOnlyList</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderLine</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span> =&gt; _lines.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsReadOnly</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> =&gt; _lines.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Aggregate</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Zero</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;EUR&quot;</span>), (sum, l) =&gt; sum.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(l.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Subtotal</span>));\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>() { }                                              <span class=\"tk-com\">// for EF Core</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Draft</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> customer) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>() { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">New</span>(), <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> = customer };\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AddLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductId</span> product, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> name, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span> unitPrice, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> quantity)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> != <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Draft</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderErrors</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotEditable</span>;\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (quantity <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">or</span> &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">100</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderErrors</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidQuantity</span>;\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (_lines.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> &gt;= <span class=\"tk-num\">50</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderErrors</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TooManyLines</span>;\n\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> existing = _lines.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SingleOrDefault</span>(l =&gt; l.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductId</span> == product);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (existing <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">not</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>) existing.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Increase</span>(quantity);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">else</span> _lines.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderLine</span>(product, name, unitPrice, quantity));\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Success</span>();\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Place</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Address</span> shipTo, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeProvider</span> clock)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> != <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Draft</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderErrors</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AlreadyPlaced</span>;\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (_lines.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderErrors</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Empty</span>;\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShippingAddress</span> = shipTo;\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Placed</span>;\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Raise</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>, clock.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetUtcNow</span>()));   <span class=\"tk-com\">// domain event</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Success</span>();\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Cancel</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> reason)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Shipped</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">or</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Delivered</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderErrors</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AlreadyShipped</span>;\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cancelled</span>;\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Raise</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderCancelled</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, reason));\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Success</span>();\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice that <code>AddLine</code>, <code>Place</code> and <code>Cancel</code> are the <strong>only</strong> way to change this aggregate&#39;s state — there&#39;s no public <code>set</code> anywhere, and every single rule from the diagram above (max 50 lines, can&#39;t cancel after shipping) is enforced right inside the method that would otherwise violate it. This is exactly the &quot;rich domain model&quot; and Tell-Don&#39;t-Ask discipline <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/design-principles/other-principles-worth-naming\">Design principles</a> named, now applied at full scale.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">The &quot;one aggregate per transaction&quot; rule in practice</div><p>&quot;Place order&quot; wants to reserve stock (Inventory aggregate) and award loyalty points (Customer aggregate). Don&#39;t load and modify all three in one transaction. Save the <code>Order</code>, raise <code>OrderPlaced</code>, and let handlers update Inventory and Loyalty, either in-process after commit or via messages, <strong>eventually</strong>. If the business truly needs one atomic rule spanning them, that&#39;s a sign your aggregate boundaries are wrong.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"domain-events-a-record-of-something-that-already-happened\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ddd/domain-events-a-record-of-something-that-already-happened\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Domain events: a record of something that already happened <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>A <strong>domain event</strong> records <strong>something meaningful that happened in the domain</strong>, named in the past tense, in the ubiquitous language: <code>OrderPlaced</code>, <code>PaymentReceived</code>, <code>StockDepleted</code> — exactly the naming discipline <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/design-patterns/command\">Design patterns</a> drew the line on, distinguishing an event (already happened, can&#39;t be rejected) from a command (a request, might be refused).</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IDomainEvent</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OccurredAt</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OccurredAt</span>) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IDomainEvent</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">abstract</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AggregateRoot</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TId</span>&gt; : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TId</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">where</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TId</span> : <span class=\"tk-kw\">notnull</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IDomainEvent</span>&gt; _events = [];\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IReadOnlyList</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IDomainEvent</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">DomainEvents</span> =&gt; _events;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Raise</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IDomainEvent</span> e) =&gt; _events.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(e);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ClearDomainEvents</span>() =&gt; _events.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Clear</span>();\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Having raised the event, there&#39;s still the question of <em>when</em> it actually gets dispatched to whoever needs to react — and that choice carries real consequences:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>When</th>\n<th>How</th>\n<th>Trade-off</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Before commit</strong>, same transaction</td>\n<td>Interceptor dispatches in-process handlers before <code>SaveChanges</code> completes</td>\n<td>Atomic, but couples the handlers into the transaction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>After commit</strong>, in-process</td>\n<td>Dispatch after <code>SaveChanges</code> succeeds</td>\n<td>Simple; if the process crashes in between, the event is lost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Outbox</strong> (recommended for cross-boundary effects)</td>\n<td>Persist events in an Outbox table in the same transaction; a worker publishes them</td>\n<td>Reliable, at-least-once; handlers must be idempotent</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The outbox row is the one worth defaulting to for anything that genuinely matters, and it&#39;s exactly the mechanism <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-advanced/domain-events-and-the-outbox-on-savechanges\">EF Core advanced</a> implemented end-to-end: by writing the event into the <em>same</em> transaction as the aggregate&#39;s own save, there&#39;s no window where the order exists but the event describing it was lost, or vice versa.</p>\n<p><strong>Domain events</strong> (within a bounded context, in-process) vs <strong>integration events</strong> (published to other contexts or services via a broker, with a stable public schema). Often a domain event handler <strong>translates</strong> a domain event into an integration event. See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events\">Messaging &amp; events</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"domain-services-application-services-factories-and-repositor\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ddd/domain-services-application-services-factories-and-repositor\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Domain services, application services, factories and repositories <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Building block</th>\n<th>Responsibility</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Domain service</strong></td>\n<td>Domain logic that doesn&#39;t naturally belong to one entity or value object; stateless; in domain terms</td>\n<td><code>PricingService.CalculateDiscount(order, customerTier)</code>, <code>TransferService.Transfer(accountA, accountB, money)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Application service / use case handler</strong></td>\n<td>Orchestrates: loads aggregates, calls domain methods, saves, publishes. <strong>No business rules</strong></td>\n<td><code>PlaceOrderHandler</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Factory</strong></td>\n<td>Encapsulates complex creation, ensuring a valid initial state</td>\n<td><code>Order.Draft(customer)</code>, <code>OrderFactory.FromCart(cart)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Repository</strong></td>\n<td>Collection-like access to <strong>aggregate roots only</strong>; interface in the domain, implementation in infrastructure</td>\n<td><code>IOrderRepository.GetAsync(OrderId)</code>, <code>Add(Order)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Specification</strong></td>\n<td>A reusable business predicate</td>\n<td><code>new OverdueInvoiceSpec(today)</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The distinction worth holding onto between the first two rows: a <em>domain</em> service still speaks the business&#39;s language and can contain real rules (<code>TransferService</code> deciding how a transfer actually works), while an <em>application</em> service is pure orchestration — it calls other things in the right order and contains no judgment calls of its own. <code>PlaceOrderHandler</code> from earlier chapters is a textbook application service precisely because every actual decision inside it (can this order be placed? is this quantity valid?) was delegated to the aggregate itself.</p>\n<h2 id=\"rich-vs-anemic-domain-models\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ddd/rich-vs-anemic-domain-models\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Rich vs anemic domain models <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// ✘ Anemic: a bag of data; rules live in services and get duplicated and bypassed</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderLine</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = []; }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderService</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Cancel</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> o)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shipped&quot;</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;…&quot;</span>);    <span class=\"tk-com\">// and the same check in three other places…</span>\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Cancelled&quot;</span>;                                  <span class=\"tk-com\">// …while anyone can set Status = &quot;Cancelled&quot; anywhere</span>\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// ✔ Rich: behaviour and data together; invalid states unrepresentable</span>\norder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cancel</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;customer request&quot;</span>);                               <span class=\"tk-com\">// the only way to cancel; rules inside</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Look at the anemic version again: because <code>Status</code> has a public setter, nothing stops other code from writing <code>order.Status = &quot;Cancelled&quot;</code> directly, skipping the shipped-order check entirely. The rule lives in <code>OrderService.Cancel</code>, but only helps if every caller remembers to call it — and in a large codebase, eventually one won&#39;t. This is why Martin Fowler called the anemic model an <strong>anti-pattern</strong>: it costs you extra types and indirection but doesn&#39;t actually deliver the one thing a domain model is for — invariants that can&#39;t be bypassed. Tell, Don&#39;t Ask (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/design-principles/other-principles-worth-naming\">Design principles</a>) is the idea behind the fix.</p>\n<h2 id=\"persisting-aggregates-with-ef-core\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ddd/persisting-aggregates-with-ef-core\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Persisting aggregates with EF Core <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>EF Core can map rich models without polluting them — none of the mapping configuration below needs to leak back into the <code>Order</code> class itself:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderConfiguration</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IEntityTypeConfiguration</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Configure</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">EntityTypeBuilder</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt; b)\n    {\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasKey</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>);\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Property</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasConversion</span>(id =&gt; id.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>, v =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>(v));           <span class=\"tk-com\">// strongly typed id</span>\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Property</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasConversion</span>(id =&gt; id.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>, v =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>(v)); <span class=\"tk-com\">// reference by id, no navigation</span>\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Property</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasConversion</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasMaxLength</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>);\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OwnsOne</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShippingAddress</span>);                                                   <span class=\"tk-com\">// value object → columns</span>\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OwnsMany</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span>, l =&gt;                                                        <span class=\"tk-com\">// child entities inside the aggregate</span>\n        {\n            l.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithOwner</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasForeignKey</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OrderId&quot;</span>);\n            l.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Property</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Id&quot;</span>);\n            l.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ComplexProperty</span>(x =&gt; x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UnitPrice</span>);\n        });\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Navigation</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">UsePropertyAccessMode</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">PropertyAccessMode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Field</span>);         <span class=\"tk-com\">// use the private _lines field</span>\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ignore</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DomainEvents</span>);\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ignore</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>);                                                              <span class=\"tk-com\">// computed</span>\n        b.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Property</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>[]&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;RowVersion&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsRowVersion</span>();                                      <span class=\"tk-com\">// concurrency for the whole aggregate</span>\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice that <code>CustomerId</code> is mapped as a plain property with a value converter, not a navigation — the Fluent API configuration directly enforces rule 3 from the aggregate design section above, at the persistence layer rather than just by convention. Private setters, private parameterless constructors, backing fields, owned and complex types, and value converters together let the domain stay genuinely clean. Some teams instead keep <strong>separate persistence models</strong> and map between them: the purest approach, with more code to maintain in exchange for zero coupling at all between the domain and EF Core&#39;s own requirements.</p>\n<h2 id=\"when-ddd-is-overkill\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ddd/when-ddd-is-overkill\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>When DDD is overkill <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Tactical DDD has real costs: more types, more mapping, a learning curve, and eventual consistency between aggregates. Use it for <strong>core, complex domains with rich rules that change often</strong> — exactly the &quot;Core&quot; row from the subdomain table earlier in this chapter. For CRUD-style supporting subdomains (reference data admin, simple forms), a straightforward model with EF Core and validation is better, and reaching for the full tactical toolkit there would be ceremony without payoff. <strong>Strategic DDD</strong> (bounded contexts, ubiquitous language) is valuable almost everywhere, even when you skip the tactical patterns entirely — the value of a shared, precise vocabulary doesn&#39;t depend on how rich the underlying model needs to be.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is an aggregate and what rules govern it?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A cluster of domain objects treated as one unit for changes, with a single aggregate root through which all modifications pass, so invariants are enforced within the boundary. Rules: keep aggregates small, reference other aggregates by ID only, modify one aggregate per transaction, use domain events and eventual consistency for cross-aggregate effects, and load and save whole aggregates via repositories.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Entity vs value object?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>An entity has a persistent identity: two orders with identical data are still different orders, and its attributes can change. A value object has no identity, is defined by its values, is immutable and is compared by value (Money, Address, EmailAddress). Records are a natural fit for value objects.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is a bounded context?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>An explicit boundary within which a particular domain model and its ubiquitous language are consistent. The same term (Product, Customer) can have different models in different contexts, which integrate through defined contracts or translations (anti-corruption layers). Bounded contexts are natural module or microservice boundaries.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the ubiquitous language?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A shared, rigorous vocabulary developed with domain experts and used consistently in conversations, documentation and code (class and method names), scoped to a bounded context. It reduces translation errors between business and code.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is an anemic domain model and why is it a problem?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Entities that only hold data (public getters and setters) while all business logic lives in service classes. Rules get duplicated and bypassed, invariants aren&#39;t protected, and the model doesn&#39;t express the domain. Rich models put behaviour and data together and make invalid states impossible.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Domain events vs integration events?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Domain events are raised by aggregates within a bounded context and handled in-process, often as part of or right after the transaction. Integration events cross service or context boundaries via a message broker, have stable versioned schemas, and are published reliably (outbox), typically translated from domain events.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Domain service vs application service?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A domain service contains domain logic that doesn&#39;t fit a single entity or value object and is expressed in domain terms (pricing, transfers). An application service or use-case handler orchestrates: it loads aggregates via repositories, invokes domain behaviour, persists, and publishes events, but contains no business rules itself.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is an anti-corruption layer?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A translation layer (adapters and mappers) between your bounded context and an external or legacy system&#39;s model, so foreign concepts and quirks don&#39;t leak into your domain model.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>When would you not use DDD?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>For simple CRUD or supporting subdomains with little business logic, small short-lived apps, or when domain experts aren&#39;t available. Strategic ideas (bounded contexts, shared language) are still useful, but tactical patterns would add ceremony without benefit.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ddd/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Eric Evans, <em>Domain-Driven Design: Tackling Complexity in the Heart of Software</em> (the &quot;blue book&quot;).</li>\n<li>Vaughn Vernon, <em>Implementing Domain-Driven Design</em> (the &quot;red book&quot;) and <em>Domain-Driven Design Distilled</em> (short intro).</li>\n<li>Vlad Khononov, <em>Learning Domain-Driven Design</em> (modern, very approachable).</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/architecture/microservices/microservice-ddd-cqrs-patterns/ddd-oriented-microservice\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Design a DDD-oriented microservice (Microsoft eShop guide)</a></li>\n<li>Vaughn Vernon, &quot;Effective Aggregate Design&quot; (free three-part essay).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/ddd/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (plain console app, .NET 8/9, top-level statements, BCL only). The real chapter&#39;s types are trimmed down here to just what each check needs.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim 1: an entity's identity, not its other fields, decides equality.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> a = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">New</span>(), <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Alice&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> b = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>(a.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Alice (renamed)&quot;</span>); <span class=\"tk-com\">// same id, different name</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> c = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">New</span>(), <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Alice&quot;</span>); <span class=\"tk-com\">// different id, same name</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!a.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Equals</span>(b)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: entities with the same id should be equal even if other fields differ&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (a.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Equals</span>(c)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: entities with different ids should not be equal, even with identical other fields&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: entity equality is based on identity (Id), not on the rest of the fields&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim 2: a value object (here, a record) is equal purely by its values, with no identity.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> m1 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">10.00m</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;EUR&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> m2 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">10.00m</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;EUR&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> m3 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">10.01m</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;EUR&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!m1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Equals</span>(m2)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: two value objects with the same values should be equal&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (m1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Equals</span>(m3)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: value objects with different values should not be equal&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReferenceEquals</span>(m1, m2)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: this check should hold for distinct instances, not the same object&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: value objects compare by value, not by reference&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim 3: Money's constructor protects its own invariant (no negative amounts, 3-letter currency).</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { _ = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>(-<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;EUR&quot;</span>); <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: negative amount should have been rejected&quot;</span>); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">ArgumentException</span>) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: a value object validates itself at construction (negative Money amount rejected)&quot;</span>); }\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim 4: an aggregate only changes through its root's methods, which enforce its invariants.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> order = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Draft</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">New</span>());\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> addResult = order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductId</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">New</span>(), <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Widget&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;EUR&quot;</span>), quantity: <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (addResult) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: quantity 0 should be rejected by AddLine's own invariant&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: AddLine enforces its own quantity invariant (can't add a line with quantity 0)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductId</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">New</span>(), <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Widget&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;EUR&quot;</span>), quantity: <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>))\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: a valid line should be accepted&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Place</span>()) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: an order with at least one line should be placeable&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Place</span>()) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: placing an already-placed order should be rejected — Status is no longer Draft&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: the aggregate root's own rules — not outside code — decide what's allowed (can't re-place an order already placed)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim 5: placing an order raises a domain event recording that it happened.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DomainEvents</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> != <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> || order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DomainEvents</span>[<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>] <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">not</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span>)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: Place() should have raised exactly one OrderPlaced domain event&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: a state-changing aggregate method raises a domain event recording what happened&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Minimal stand-ins for the chapter's types, trimmed to what's being checked above ---</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">struct</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">New</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NewGuid</span>());\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">struct</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductId</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductId</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">New</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NewGuid</span>());\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">abstract</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TId</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">where</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TId</span> : <span class=\"tk-kw\">notnull</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TId</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">init</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-kw\">default</span>!;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Equals</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>? obj) =&gt; obj <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TId</span>&gt; other &amp;&amp; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetType</span>() == other.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetType</span>() &amp;&amp; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Equals</span>(other.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetHashCode</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetHashCode</span>();\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Customer</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> id, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> name) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> = id; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> = name; }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Amount</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Currency</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> amount, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> currency)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (amount &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ArgumentException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Amount can't be negative&quot;</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (currency <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">not</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Length</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span> }) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ArgumentException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Use ISO 4217 codes&quot;</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Amount</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Round</span>(amount, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">MidpointRounding</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToEven</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Currency</span> = currency.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToUpperInvariant</span>();\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Zero</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> currency) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, currency);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span> other) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Currency</span> == other.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Currency</span> ? <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Amount</span> + other.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Amount</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Currency</span>) : <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Currency mismatch&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IDomainEvent</span> { }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IDomainEvent</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">enum</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Draft</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Placed</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Shipped</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Cancelled</span> }\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Entity</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductId</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Qty</span>)&gt; _lines = [];\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IDomainEvent</span>&gt; _events = [];\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Draft</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IReadOnlyList</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IDomainEvent</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">DomainEvents</span> =&gt; _events;\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Draft</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> customer) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>() { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NewGuid</span>() };\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AddLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductId</span> product, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> name, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span> price, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> quantity)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> != <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Draft</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (quantity <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">or</span> &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">100</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n        _lines.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>((product, price, quantity));\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Place</span>()\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> != <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Draft</span> || _lines.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Placed</span>;\n        _events.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>));\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Cancel</span>()\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Shipped</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cancelled</span>;\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-problem-ddd-exists-to-solve","text":"The problem DDD exists to solve","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"strategic-ddd-ubiquitous-language-and-bounded-contexts","text":"Strategic DDD: ubiquitous language and bounded contexts","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"ubiquitous-language","text":"Ubiquitous language","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"bounded-contexts-the-same-word-deliberately-meaning-differen","text":"Bounded contexts: the same word, deliberately meaning different things","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"subdomains-where-to-actually-invest","text":"Subdomains: where to actually invest","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"context-maps","text":"Context maps","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"entities-identity-that-outlives-any-particular-state","text":"Entities: identity that outlives any particular state","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"value-objects-defined-entirely-by-what-they-contain","text":"Value objects: defined entirely by what they contain","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"aggregates-the-most-important-tactical-pattern-and-the-harde","text":"Aggregates: the most important tactical pattern, and the hardest to get right","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"domain-events-a-record-of-something-that-already-happened","text":"Domain events: a record of something that already happened","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"domain-services-application-services-factories-and-repositor","text":"Domain services, application services, factories and repositories","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"rich-vs-anemic-domain-models","text":"Rich vs anemic domain models","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"persisting-aggregates-with-ef-core","text":"Persisting aggregates with EF Core","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"when-ddd-is-overkill","text":"When DDD is overkill","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":5035,"minutes":23,"num":"5.5","part":"p5"},"microservices":{"id":"microservices","title":"Monoliths, Microservices and Distributed Architecture","summary":"When and how to split a system. Monolith vs modular monolith vs microservices, the real costs of distribution, finding service boundaries, data ownership, synchronous vs asynchronous communication, API gateways and BFFs, service discovery, migration with the strangler fig, distributed monolith anti-patterns, and what you need operationally before going distributed.","layer":"arch","where":"Application architecture › System decomposition","fits":"<p>This is the top of layer 4, where architecture becomes <strong>deployment topology</strong>. Boundaries come from DDD&#39;s bounded contexts (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ddd\">DDD</a>); communication is layer 6 (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/api-design\">API design</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events\">Messaging &amp; events</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/resilience\">Resilience</a>); and running many services needs layer 7 (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/docker\">Docker</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/kubernetes\">Kubernetes</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cicd-iac\">CI/CD</a>) plus layer 8 observability (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/observability-aspire\">Observability &amp; Aspire</a>).</p>\n","prereqs":["app-architectures","ddd"],"next":["messaging-events","resilience","observability-aspire"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/microservices/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A monolith, a modular monolith, and microservices are points on one spectrum of coupling vs operational cost — not &quot;the bad old way&quot; vs &quot;the modern way.&quot;</li>\n<li>Microservices buy independent deployment, scaling and team autonomy, but every one of the &quot;fallacies of distributed computing&quot; (the network fails, calls are slow, there&#39;s no shared transaction) becomes something your code must handle.</li>\n<li>Good service boundaries follow bounded contexts/business capabilities, not technical layers or single entities — and each service owns its own data.</li>\n<li>Querying across services means API composition, local replicas fed by events, or a CQRS-style read model — never a cross-service join.</li>\n<li>Prefer asynchronous events between services; a chain of synchronous calls multiplies latency and failure probability — four 99.9%-available services chained together are only ~99.6% available as a whole.</li>\n<li>The most common real failure mode is the <strong>distributed monolith</strong>: all the costs of microservices, none of the independent-deployment benefit that was supposed to justify them.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"three-ways-to-structure-a-system-and-the-real-spectrum-betwe\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/microservices/three-ways-to-structure-a-system-and-the-real-spectrum-betwe\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Three ways to structure a system, and the real spectrum between them <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Monolith</th>\n<th>Modular monolith</th>\n<th>Microservices</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Deployable units</td>\n<td>One</td>\n<td>One</td>\n<td>Many (one per service)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Internal boundaries</td>\n<td>Often weak</td>\n<td><strong>Strong</strong> modules (per bounded context)</td>\n<td>Enforced by the network</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data</td>\n<td>One database</td>\n<td>One database, <strong>schema/ownership per module</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Database per service</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Communication</td>\n<td>In-process calls</td>\n<td>In-process calls through module contracts, in-process events</td>\n<td>Network: HTTP/gRPC, messages</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transactions</td>\n<td>ACID across everything</td>\n<td>ACID within a module; events across modules</td>\n<td><strong>Local only</strong>; sagas and eventual consistency across services</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scaling</td>\n<td>Whole app</td>\n<td>Whole app</td>\n<td>Per service</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Team autonomy</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>High: independent release cycles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Operational complexity</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td><strong>High</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The modular monolith column, revisited from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/app-architectures/the-modular-monolith-most-of-microservices-benefits-none-of-\">App architectures</a>, is really the midpoint of this table — most of the boundary discipline of microservices, none of the network. A <strong>monolith is not bad</strong>. A <em>big ball of mud</em> is bad. Many successful products (Shopify, Stack Overflow, Basecamp) run well-structured monoliths at large scale.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-microservices-and-what-they-genuinely-cost\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/microservices/why-microservices-and-what-they-genuinely-cost\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Why microservices, and what they genuinely cost <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Benefits</strong> (when they&#39;re real):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Independent deployment</strong>: teams ship without coordinating a release train.</li>\n<li><strong>Independent scaling</strong>: scale the search service to 50 instances and the admin service to 2.</li>\n<li><strong>Fault isolation</strong>: a crash in recommendations doesn&#39;t take down checkout (if designed for it).</li>\n<li><strong>Technology freedom</strong> per service (use carefully).</li>\n<li><strong>Team ownership</strong> aligned with business capabilities (Conway&#39;s Law, Team Topologies).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Costs</strong>, sometimes called the <strong>fallacies of distributed computing</strong> (&quot;the network is reliable, latency is zero, bandwidth is infinite…&quot;) — each one a comfortable assumption a monolith&#39;s in-process calls let you make for free, and a microservices architecture forces you to confront directly:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Cost</th>\n<th>What it means in practice</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Network failures and latency</td>\n<td>Every call can fail or be slow, so you need timeouts, retries, circuit breakers (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/resilience\">Resilience</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No distributed transactions</td>\n<td>Eventual consistency, sagas, outbox, idempotency (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events\">Messaging</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data duplication and joins across services</td>\n<td>Data is replicated via events; queries spanning services become API composition or read models</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Observability</td>\n<td>You need distributed tracing, centralised logs and correlation IDs (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/observability-aspire\">Observability</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Testing</td>\n<td>Contract tests, environment management, harder end-to-end tests</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Versioning</td>\n<td>APIs and message schemas must evolve compatibly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Infrastructure &amp; DevOps</td>\n<td>Containers, orchestration, CI/CD per service, service discovery, secrets, gateways</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cognitive load</td>\n<td>Many repos and pipelines, on-call across services</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>(Two terms from the table above, covered in full in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events\">Messaging &amp; events</a>: a <strong>saga</strong> coordinates a multi-step process across services as a chain of local transactions, each with a compensating &quot;undo&quot; step if a later one fails; the <strong>outbox</strong> is the write-the-event-and-the-data-atomically trick briefly introduced in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/background-work\">Background work</a>.)</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-interview\"><div class=\"callout-title\">&quot;Should we use microservices?&quot;</div><p>A strong answer: &quot;It depends on the problem, not the trend. Microservices solve <strong>organisational scaling</strong> (many teams needing independent deployments) and <strong>uneven scaling</strong> needs, at the cost of distributed-systems complexity. With one or two teams and an evolving domain, I&#39;d start with a <strong>modular monolith</strong> with strict bounded-context modules, and extract a service only when a module has a clear reason: different scaling, a different release cadence, a different team, or isolation needs.&quot;</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"finding-service-boundaries\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/microservices/finding-service-boundaries\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Finding service boundaries <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Bad boundaries are the most expensive microservices mistake there is: services that must change together, call each other constantly, or share data have paid the full cost of distribution while getting none of its benefits.</p>\n<p>Good boundaries:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Align with bounded contexts / business capabilities</strong> (Ordering, Catalog, Payments, Shipping) — exactly the strategic DDD boundary from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ddd/strategic-ddd-ubiquitous-language-and-bounded-contexts\">DDD</a> — <strong>not</strong> technical layers (&quot;the database service&quot;, &quot;the validation service&quot;) and <strong>not</strong> entities (&quot;the Customer service&quot; that everyone calls for everything).</li>\n<li><strong>High cohesion inside, low coupling outside</strong>: most changes stay within one service.</li>\n<li><strong>Owns its data and the rules over it.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Can do its main job when other services are down</strong>, often by keeping a local copy of the data it needs, fed by events.</li>\n<li>Fits a <strong>team</strong> (two-pizza team, stream-aligned team).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Granularity:</strong> start coarse. Splitting later is easier than merging chatty nano-services — a mistake in the &quot;too big&quot; direction is a refactor; a mistake in the &quot;too small&quot; direction is a rewrite of how dozens of services talk to each other.</p>\n<h2 id=\"data-ownership-database-per-service\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/microservices/data-ownership-database-per-service\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Data ownership: database per service <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Each service <strong>owns its data exclusively</strong>. Others access it <strong>only through the service&#39;s API or events</strong>, never by querying its tables directly.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>✘ Shared database                         ✔ Database per service\nOrdering ─┐                               Ordering ── Orders DB\nBilling  ─┼─▶ one DB, shared tables       Billing  ── Billing DB      ◀── OrderPlaced event ──  Ordering\nShipping ─┘   (schema change = 3 teams)   Shipping ── Shipping DB     (keeps its own copy of the address)</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Why: a shared schema couples services at the deepest level there is. Any schema change can silently break a service that was never told about it, and nobody can safely refactor a table they don&#39;t fully own. That kills the one benefit — independent deployment — that was the whole reason to go distributed.</p>\n<p>How to answer queries across services, now that a join across two services&#39; tables is no longer an option:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Technique</th>\n<th>How</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>API composition</strong></td>\n<td>A gateway or BFF calls several services and combines the results (fine for small fan-out)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Data replication via events</strong></td>\n<td>Service keeps a local, read-only copy of the data it needs (Shipping stores customer addresses from <code>CustomerAddressChanged</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CQRS read model</strong></td>\n<td>A dedicated query service builds a denormalised view from many services&#39; events</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Notice the second row is really the CQRS &quot;separate stores&quot; level from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cqrs-mediator-result/levels-of-cqrs-a-spectrum-not-a-binary-choice\">CQRS &amp; Result</a>, applied across a service boundary rather than within one service — the same eventual-consistency tradeoff, just at a larger scale.</p>\n<h2 id=\"communication-synchronous-vs-asynchronous\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/microservices/communication-synchronous-vs-asynchronous\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Communication: synchronous vs asynchronous <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Synchronous (HTTP/REST, gRPC)</th>\n<th>Asynchronous (messages/events via broker)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Coupling in time</td>\n<td>Both must be up <strong>now</strong></td>\n<td>Producer and consumer are decoupled in time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Latency</td>\n<td>Caller waits</td>\n<td>Fire and continue</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Failure behaviour</td>\n<td>Failures cascade unless guarded</td>\n<td>Broker buffers; consumers catch up</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Consistency</td>\n<td>Immediate answer</td>\n<td>Eventual</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Use for</td>\n<td>Queries needing an immediate answer, user-facing reads, commands needing a sync response</td>\n<td>State-change notifications, workflows, integration, fan-out, load levelling</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Prefer asynchronous events for integration between services.</strong> Here&#39;s the math behind why: a chain of synchronous calls (A → B → C → D) is only as available as all four links being up <strong>at once</strong>, and those probabilities multiply. Four services each healthy 99.9% of the time don&#39;t stay at 99.9% as a chain — they combine to 0.999⁴ ≈ 99.6%. That looks like a small drop, but it&#39;s about <strong>4x</strong> the expected downtime of any single service&#39;s own number — and it gets worse the longer the chain gets.</p>\n<h2 id=\"api-gateway-bff-and-service-mesh\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/microservices/api-gateway-bff-and-service-mesh\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>API gateway, BFF and service mesh <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Mobile app ─┐                  ┌──▶ Ordering service\nWeb SPA ────┼─▶ API Gateway ───┼──▶ Catalog service\nPartners ───┘   (or a BFF per   └──▶ Payments service\n                 client type)</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Component</th>\n<th>Responsibilities</th>\n<th>.NET / Azure options</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>API gateway</strong></td>\n<td>Single entry point: routing, TLS, authentication offload, rate limiting, caching, request aggregation, versioning</td>\n<td><strong>YARP</strong> (ASP.NET Core reverse proxy library), <strong>Azure API Management</strong>, Azure Front Door / Application Gateway, Ocelot (older)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>BFF</strong> (Backend for Frontend)</td>\n<td>A gateway tailored to one client type (mobile vs web), aggregating calls and shaping responses; for SPAs, also holds tokens (cookie-based BFF)</td>\n<td>ASP.NET Core + YARP, Duende BFF</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Service mesh</strong></td>\n<td>Infrastructure layer (sidecar proxies) for service-to-service <strong>mTLS</strong> (mutual TLS: each side proves its identity with a certificate, not just the server), retries, traffic splitting, telemetry</td>\n<td>Istio, Linkerd, Dapr (sidecar runtime with building blocks), Container Apps&#39; built-in features</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The BFF row connects directly back to a decision from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/auth/cookies-vs-bearer-tokens-the-tradeoff-behind-how-do-i-stay-l\">Auth</a>: it&#39;s the same cookie-holding server-side component recommended there for SPAs, now framed as a specific flavour of gateway rather than purely an auth concern.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// YARP gateway in a few lines</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddReverseProxy</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">LoadFromConfig</span>(builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configuration</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetSection</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ReverseProxy&quot;</span>));\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapReverseProxy</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// appsettings: Routes { &quot;orders&quot;: { ClusterId: &quot;ordering&quot;, Match: { Path: &quot;/api/orders/{**rest}&quot; } } }</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//              Clusters { &quot;ordering&quot;: { Destinations: { &quot;d1&quot;: { Address: &quot;http://ordering&quot; } } } }</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h2 id=\"service-discovery-and-configuration\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/microservices/service-discovery-and-configuration\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Service discovery and configuration <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Services shouldn&#39;t hard-code each other&#39;s addresses, for exactly the stale-DNS reason <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/http-client/the-two-classic-bugs-and-why-theyre-opposite-mistakes\">Calling other services</a> raised for outbound HTTP clients, now at the scale of an entire topology that reshapes itself as instances come and go:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Platform DNS</strong>: Kubernetes Services (<code>http://ordering.shop.svc.cluster.local</code>), Container Apps internal ingress names.</li>\n<li><strong>.NET service discovery</strong> (<code>Microsoft.Extensions.ServiceDiscovery</code>, used by Aspire): <code>https+http://ordering</code> is resolved from configuration or DNS.</li>\n<li><strong>Central configuration</strong>: Azure App Configuration, Key Vault, Kubernetes ConfigMaps/Secrets.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"migrating-a-monolith-the-strangler-fig\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/microservices/migrating-a-monolith-the-strangler-fig\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Migrating a monolith: the strangler fig <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Rewriting everything at once (&quot;big bang&quot;) is notoriously risky — all the business value sits behind one giant, unshippable cutover for months, with no way to validate pieces of it along the way. The <strong>strangler fig pattern</strong> (Martin Fowler) <strong>incrementally replaces</strong> pieces instead, so each step ships and proves itself independently:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>1. Put a proxy/gateway (YARP) in front of the monolith; all traffic flows through it.\n2. Pick one capability with clear boundaries (e.g. product search).\n3. Build it as a new service; route /search/* to it via the proxy.\n4. Sync data (events/CDC) while both exist; then remove the code from the monolith.\n5. Repeat. The monolith shrinks until it can be retired, or stays as a well-defined core.</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Supporting patterns: <strong>anti-corruption layer</strong> (translate between the old and new models, the same DDD pattern from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ddd/strategic-ddd-ubiquitous-language-and-bounded-contexts\">DDD</a>), <strong>change data capture</strong> (stream database changes, e.g. Debezium or SQL Server CDC), <strong>branch by abstraction</strong>, and <strong>feature flags</strong> for switching traffic.</p>\n<h2 id=\"anti-patterns\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/microservices/anti-patterns\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Anti-patterns <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Anti-pattern</th>\n<th>Symptoms</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Distributed monolith</strong></td>\n<td>Services must be deployed together; shared database; synchronous call chains; a change requires touching 5 services</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Nano-services</strong></td>\n<td>A service per entity or function; every request fans out to 10 services</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Shared domain library</strong></td>\n<td>A &quot;Common.Domain&quot; NuGet used by all services, coupling their models and release cycles (sharing <em>contracts</em> and <em>infrastructure helpers</em> is fine)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Chatty synchronous chains</strong></td>\n<td>Latency and availability collapse under load</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>No observability</strong></td>\n<td>Debugging takes hours of grepping logs across services</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Microservices first</strong></td>\n<td>A new product, one team, unknown domain, and already 15 services</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The first row — the <strong>distributed monolith</strong> — is the most common real-world failure here. It means a team paid every cost from the table earlier (network failures, eventual consistency, DevOps overhead) and never actually got independent deployment, the one benefit that was supposed to justify paying them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"readiness-checklist\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/microservices/readiness-checklist\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Readiness checklist <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Before going distributed, you should have (or plan):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Automated CI/CD per service, plus infrastructure as code (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cicd-iac\">CI/CD &amp; IaC</a>)</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Containerisation and an orchestrator or PaaS (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/docker\">Docker</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-compute\">Azure compute</a>)</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Centralised logging, metrics, <strong>distributed tracing</strong> with correlation (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/observability-aspire\">Observability</a>)</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Resilience defaults on every outbound call (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/resilience\">Resilience</a>)</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> A message broker plus outbox/idempotency practices (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events\">Messaging</a>)</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> API versioning and contract tests (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/api-design\">API design</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/integration-testing\">Testing</a>)</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Health checks, autoscaling, graceful shutdown</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Identity for service-to-service calls (managed identity, client credentials, mTLS)</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Clear ownership: who&#39;s on call for which service</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are the trade-offs between a monolith and microservices?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A monolith is simpler to develop, test, deploy and debug, with ACID transactions and fast in-process calls, but it scales and deploys as one unit and can become tightly coupled. Microservices allow independent deployment, scaling, fault isolation and team autonomy, but add network failure modes, eventual consistency, data duplication, observability and DevOps overhead, and versioning challenges.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is a modular monolith?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A single deployable application divided into strongly encapsulated modules aligned with bounded contexts, each owning its data and exposing explicit contracts. It provides most of the structural benefits of microservices without distributed-systems costs, and can be split later.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you decide service boundaries?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Align them with bounded contexts and business capabilities (not technical layers or single entities), aiming for high cohesion and low coupling so most changes stay within one service. Each service should own its data and be able to function when others are down, and services should match team ownership. Start coarse-grained.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why should each microservice own its database?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A shared database couples services through the schema: changes break others, teams can&#39;t deploy or evolve independently, and boundaries erode. With database per service, data is accessed only via APIs or events, preserving autonomy and encapsulation.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you query data that spans multiple services?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>API composition (a gateway or BFF aggregates calls), local replicas of needed data maintained through events, or a dedicated CQRS read model built from multiple services&#39; events, accepting eventual consistency.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Sync vs async communication between microservices?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Synchronous HTTP/gRPC is for requests needing an immediate response, but it couples services in time and failures cascade. Asynchronous messaging decouples them in time, buffers load and isolates failures, at the cost of eventual consistency. Prefer async events for integration and minimise synchronous call chains.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is an API gateway and what is a BFF?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>An API gateway is a single entry point that routes requests to services and handles cross-cutting concerns (TLS, auth, rate limiting, caching, aggregation), e.g. Azure API Management or YARP. A Backend-for-Frontend is a gateway specialised per client type (web, mobile) that tailors and aggregates APIs for it and, for SPAs, can hold tokens server-side.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the strangler fig pattern?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Incrementally migrating a legacy system by routing traffic through a facade or proxy and replacing capabilities one at a time with new implementations, until the old system can be retired. It avoids a risky big-bang rewrite.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is a distributed monolith?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A system split into services that are still tightly coupled: a shared database, lockstep deployments, synchronous call chains, shared domain libraries. It has the costs of distribution and the rigidity of a monolith.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/microservices/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Sam Newman, <em>Building Microservices</em> (2nd ed.) and <em>Monolith to Microservices</em>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/architecture/microservices/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">.NET Microservices: Architecture for Containerized .NET Applications (free eBook)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/microservices/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Azure Architecture Center: microservices</a></li>\n<li>Chris Richardson, <a href=\"https://microservices.io\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">microservices.io</a> pattern language.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/servers/yarp/getting-started\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">YARP documentation</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/microservices/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (plain console app, .NET 8/9, top-level statements, BCL only).</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: availability compounds as a product across a synchronous call chain.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Four services each 99.9% available combine to roughly 99.9%^4 ≈ 99.6%, about 4x the downtime</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// a single service's own number would suggest.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> perService = <span class=\"tk-num\">0.999</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> chain = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Math</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Pow</span>(perService, <span class=\"tk-num\">4</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> singleDowntime = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> - perService;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> chainDowntime = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> - chain;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Math</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Abs</span>(chain - <span class=\"tk-num\">0.996</span>) &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0.0005</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected chain availability ~0.996, got {chain:F6}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> downtimeRatio = chainDowntime / singleDowntime;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (downtimeRatio &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">3.9</span> || downtimeRatio &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">4.1</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected ~4x the downtime for a 4-link chain, got {downtimeRatio:F2}x&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: a 4-service synchronous chain at 99.9% each is {chain:P2} available ({downtimeRatio:F1}x the single service's downtime)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: database-per-service means other services can only see what was explicitly published,</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// never another service's internal storage.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> ordering = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderingService</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> shipping = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShippingService</span>();\nordering.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span> += shipping.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OnOrderPlaced</span>; <span class=\"tk-com\">// integration happens via an event, not a shared table</span>\nordering.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrder</span>(orderId: <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, shipToAddress: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;221B Baker St&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!shipping.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasLocalCopyOf</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: shipping should have received its own copy of the data it needs&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (shipping.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasLocalCopyOf</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">999</span>)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: shipping should not know about an order it was never told about&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: services exchange data only through published events, each keeping its own local copy&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: a strangler fig proxy routes a chosen capability to the new service and leaves everything else on the monolith.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">RouteFor</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> path) =&gt; path.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartsWith</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/search/&quot;</span>) ? <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;new-search-service&quot;</span> : <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;legacy-monolith&quot;</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">RouteFor</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/search/products&quot;</span>) != <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;new-search-service&quot;</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: /search/* should route to the new service&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">RouteFor</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/orders/42&quot;</span>) != <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;legacy-monolith&quot;</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: everything else should still route to the monolith&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: a strangler fig proxy can migrate one capability (/search/*) while the rest keeps flowing to the monolith&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- supporting types for the data-ownership check above ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderingService</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">event</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Action</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;? <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrder</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> orderId, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> shipToAddress) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span>?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Invoke</span>(orderId, shipToAddress);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShippingService</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; _localAddressCopies = []; <span class=\"tk-com\">// Shipping's own DB, not Ordering's</span>\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OnOrderPlaced</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> orderId, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> shipToAddress) =&gt; _localAddressCopies[orderId] = shipToAddress;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HasLocalCopyOf</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> orderId) =&gt; _localAddressCopies.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ContainsKey</span>(orderId);\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"three-ways-to-structure-a-system-and-the-real-spectrum-betwe","text":"Three ways to structure a system, and the real spectrum between them","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"why-microservices-and-what-they-genuinely-cost","text":"Why microservices, and what they genuinely cost","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"finding-service-boundaries","text":"Finding service boundaries","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"data-ownership-database-per-service","text":"Data ownership: database per service","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"communication-synchronous-vs-asynchronous","text":"Communication: synchronous vs asynchronous","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"api-gateway-bff-and-service-mesh","text":"API gateway, BFF and service mesh","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"service-discovery-and-configuration","text":"Service discovery and configuration","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"migrating-a-monolith-the-strangler-fig","text":"Migrating a monolith: the strangler fig","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"anti-patterns","text":"Anti-patterns","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"readiness-checklist","text":"Readiness checklist","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":2929,"minutes":13,"num":"5.6","part":"p5"},"unit-testing":{"id":"unit-testing","title":"Unit Testing and Test Doubles","summary":"Fast, focused tests of behaviour. What a unit is, xUnit (facts, theories, fixtures, lifecycle), NUnit and MSTest compared, Arrange-Act-Assert and naming, assertion libraries, test doubles (dummy, stub, fake, spy, mock), NSubstitute and Moq, testing time, async and exceptions, test data builders, TDD, and what makes tests valuable or brittle.","layer":"cross","where":"Cross-cutting › Testing › Unit tests","fits":"<p>Testing is a layer-8 concern that touches every layer. Unit tests are most valuable for the <strong>domain and application layers</strong> (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ddd\">DDD</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cqrs-mediator-result\">CQRS</a>), and they&#39;re easy precisely because of DI and Dependency Inversion (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/design-principles\">Design principles</a>). Infrastructure and the HTTP pipeline are better covered by <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/integration-testing\">integration tests</a>.</p>\n","prereqs":["oop","dependency-injection","async-await"],"next":["integration-testing"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/unit-testing/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A unit test checks <strong>one behaviour</strong>, fast and deterministically. &quot;One unit&quot; is debated: classical style treats a unit as a <em>behaviour</em> (several real classes allowed); mockist style treats it as <em>one class</em> (everything else mocked). Classical is the current mainstream advice.</li>\n<li>Good tests are <strong>FIRST</strong>: Fast, Isolated, Repeatable, Self-validating, Timely.</li>\n<li>A <strong>test double</strong> is a precise word for &quot;fake dependency&quot;: dummy (unused filler), stub (feeds input), fake (working lightweight version), spy (records calls), mock (asserts calls happened). Over-mocking makes tests break on every refactor even when behaviour didn&#39;t change.</li>\n<li>Inject sources of non-determinism (<code>TimeProvider</code>, not <code>DateTime.Now</code>) so tests are repeatable.</li>\n<li>Coverage percentage is a diagnostic, not a goal — a test can run every line and still assert nothing meaningful.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"what-a-unit-test-is-and-the-question-that-divides-testers\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/unit-testing/what-a-unit-test-is-and-the-question-that-divides-testers\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What a unit test is, and the question that divides testers <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>A <strong>unit test</strong> verifies <strong>one unit of behaviour</strong> in isolation, quickly and deterministically. That sounds simple until you ask the obvious follow-up: what, exactly, is &quot;one unit&quot;? There are two genuinely different schools of thought here, and they lead to noticeably different-looking tests:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Classical (Detroit/Chicago)</th>\n<th>Mockist (London)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>A unit is…</td>\n<td>A <strong>behaviour</strong>, possibly spanning several collaborating classes</td>\n<td><strong>One class</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Collaborators</td>\n<td>Use real ones; replace only <strong>shared or out-of-process</strong> dependencies (DB, network, clock)</td>\n<td>Mock <strong>every</strong> collaborator</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Strength</td>\n<td>Tests survive refactoring</td>\n<td>Precise failure location; drives interface design</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk</td>\n<td>Larger tests</td>\n<td>Brittle tests coupled to implementation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Modern guidance (e.g. Vladimir Khorikov&#39;s <em>Unit Testing Principles, Practices, and Patterns</em>) leans <strong>classical</strong>: test observable behaviour, and mock only at the boundaries of your system. The reasoning becomes clear once you picture refactoring a class&#39;s internals without changing what it <em>does</em> — a classical test, written against behaviour, survives unchanged; a mockist test, written against every internal collaborator, breaks even though nothing externally visible changed.</p>\n<p><strong>Good unit tests are FIRST</strong>: <strong>F</strong>ast (milliseconds), <strong>I</strong>solated (no order dependence, no shared state), <strong>R</strong>epeatable (same result every run: no real time, randomness or network), <strong>S</strong>elf-validating (pass/fail, no manual inspection), <strong>T</strong>imely (written with the code).</p>\n<h2 id=\"xunit-essentials\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/unit-testing/xunit-essentials\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>xUnit essentials <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><strong>xUnit</strong> is the most common test framework in modern .NET (the ASP.NET Core and .NET runtime teams use it). <strong>xUnit v3</strong> is the current major version.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Shell</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>dotnet new xunit -n Shop.Domain.Tests\ndotnet add Shop.Domain.Tests reference src/Shop.Domain\ndotnet test                                   <span class=\"tk-com\"># discovers and runs all tests</span>\ndotnet test <span class=\"tk-attr\">--filter</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FullyQualifiedName~Orders&amp;Category=Unit&quot;</span></code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">MoneyTests</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[Fact]</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Add_SameCurrency_SumsAmounts</span>()\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-com\">// Arrange</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> a = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">10.50m</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;EUR&quot;</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> b = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">4.50m</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;EUR&quot;</span>);\n\n        <span class=\"tk-com\">// Act</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> sum = a.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(b);\n\n        <span class=\"tk-com\">// Assert</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Assert</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Equal</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">15m</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;EUR&quot;</span>), sum);\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[Fact]</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Add_DifferentCurrency_Throws</span>()\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> eur = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;EUR&quot;</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> usd = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;USD&quot;</span>);\n\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> ex = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Assert</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Throws</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">DomainException</span>&gt;(() =&gt; eur.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(usd));\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Assert</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Equal</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;money.mismatch&quot;</span>, ex.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Code</span>);\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[Theory]</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[InlineData(-1)]</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[InlineData(-0.01)]</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Ctor_NegativeAmount_Throws</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> amount) =&gt;\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Assert</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Throws</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">DomainException</span>&gt;(() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>(amount, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;EUR&quot;</span>));\n\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[Theory]</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[MemberData(nameof(RoundingCases))]</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Ctor_RoundsToTwoDecimals_BankersRounding</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> input, <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> expected) =&gt;\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Assert</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Equal</span>(expected, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>(input, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;EUR&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Amount</span>);\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TheoryData</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">RoundingCases</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>() { { <span class=\"tk-num\">1.005m</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1.00m</span> }, { <span class=\"tk-num\">1.015m</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1.02m</span> }, { <span class=\"tk-num\">2.345m</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2.34m</span> } };\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>How</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>A test</td>\n<td><code>[Fact]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Parameterised test</td>\n<td><code>[Theory]</code> + <code>[InlineData]</code> / <code>[MemberData]</code> / <code>[ClassData]</code> / <code>TheoryData&lt;…&gt;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Setup / teardown</td>\n<td><strong>Constructor</strong> / <code>IDisposable.Dispose</code> (or <code>IAsyncLifetime</code> for async)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A new class instance per test</td>\n<td>Yes: xUnit creates a fresh instance for <strong>every</strong> test, so there&#39;s no shared state by accident</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Share expensive setup across a class</td>\n<td><code>IClassFixture&lt;TFixture&gt;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Share across classes</td>\n<td><code>[Collection(&quot;db&quot;)]</code> + <code>ICollectionFixture&lt;TFixture&gt;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Skip</td>\n<td><code>[Fact(Skip = &quot;reason&quot;)]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Categorise</td>\n<td><code>[Trait(&quot;Category&quot;, &quot;Unit&quot;)]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Output</td>\n<td>Inject <code>ITestOutputHelper</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Parallelism</td>\n<td>Test <strong>collections</strong> (by default, each class) run in parallel; tests within a class run sequentially</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The &quot;new instance per test&quot; row deserves a second look, because it&#39;s a deliberate design choice, not an incidental detail: it means a bug where one test&#39;s leftover state silently affects the next test <strong>cannot happen</strong> by construction in xUnit, since there is no &quot;next test&quot; sharing the same instance to leave state in.</p>\n<h3 id=\"xunit-vs-nunit-vs-mstest\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/unit-testing/xunit-vs-nunit-vs-mstest\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>xUnit vs NUnit vs MSTest</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>xUnit</th>\n<th>NUnit</th>\n<th>MSTest (v3)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Test attribute</td>\n<td><code>[Fact]</code>, <code>[Theory]</code></td>\n<td><code>[Test]</code>, <code>[TestCase]</code></td>\n<td><code>[TestMethod]</code>, <code>[DataRow]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Setup</td>\n<td>Constructor / <code>IAsyncLifetime</code></td>\n<td><code>[SetUp]</code>, <code>[OneTimeSetUp]</code></td>\n<td><code>[TestInitialize]</code>, <code>[ClassInitialize]</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Instance per test</td>\n<td>Always</td>\n<td>One per fixture by default</td>\n<td>One per test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Notes</td>\n<td>Minimal and opinionated</td>\n<td>Rich assertions and attributes</td>\n<td>Microsoft&#39;s; modernised with MSTest.Sdk and Microsoft.Testing.Platform</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>All three are fine; consistency within a codebase matters most.</p>\n<h2 id=\"naming-and-structure\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/unit-testing/naming-and-structure\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Naming and structure <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>A test name should say <strong>what behaviour</strong> is expected <strong>under which condition</strong>, readable as a specification rather than as an implementation detail:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>MethodUnderTest_Scenario_ExpectedResult      Cancel_WhenOrderShipped_ReturnsAlreadyShippedError\nShould_ExpectedBehaviour_When_Condition      Should_ApplyVipDiscount_When_CustomerIsVip\nPlain English (Khorikov)                     Shipped_order_cannot_be_cancelled</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Rules: <strong>one behaviour per test</strong> (multiple asserts are fine if they verify one outcome), <strong>no logic in tests</strong> (no <code>if</code>, no loops computing expected values: the test should be obviously correct by inspection, never itself a thing that could have a bug), and <strong>Arrange-Act-Assert</strong> with a single Act.</p>\n<h2 id=\"assertions\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/unit-testing/assertions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Assertions <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Built-in xUnit</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Assert</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Equal</span>(expected, actual);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Assert</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">True</span>(order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsPaid</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Assert</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contains</span>(order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span>, l =&gt; l.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;KB-01&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Assert</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Single</span>(events);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Assert</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsType</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span>&gt;(events[<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>]);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Assert</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ThrowsAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFoundException</span>&gt;(() =&gt; sut.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">999</span>, ct));\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Assert</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Equivalent</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Paid&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">89.70m</span> }, dto);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// structural comparison</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Shouldly (fluent, readable failures)</span>\norder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Status</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShouldBe</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderStatus</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Placed</span>);\norder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShouldHaveSingleItem</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Quantity</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShouldBe</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Should</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Throw</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">DomainException</span>&gt;(() =&gt; order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cancel</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;x&quot;</span>)).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Code</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShouldBe</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;order.already_shipped&quot;</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Popular libraries: <strong>Shouldly</strong> (MIT), <strong>FluentAssertions</strong> (commercial licence since v8 in 2025; v7 remains Apache-licensed), <strong>AwesomeAssertions</strong> (an MIT community fork of FluentAssertions). For snapshot testing (approve the whole output once, then detect changes), use <strong>Verify</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"test-doubles-a-precise-vocabulary-for-fake-dependency\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/unit-testing/test-doubles-a-precise-vocabulary-for-fake-dependency\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Test doubles: a precise vocabulary for &quot;fake dependency&quot; <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>A <strong>test double</strong> stands in for a real dependency, and Gerard Meszaros gave the vocabulary that lets testers talk precisely about <em>which kind</em> of double a given test needs, rather than lumping everything under &quot;mock&quot;:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Double</th>\n<th>Purpose</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Dummy</strong></td>\n<td>Fills a parameter; never used</td>\n<td><code>NullLogger&lt;T&gt;.Instance</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Stub</strong></td>\n<td>Returns canned answers to <strong>provide inputs</strong></td>\n<td>A repository stub returning a fixed order</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Fake</strong></td>\n<td>A working, simplified implementation</td>\n<td>In-memory repository, <code>FakeTimeProvider</code>, fake email sender collecting messages</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Spy</strong></td>\n<td>Records how it was called, asserted later</td>\n<td>A fake notifier with a <code>SentMessages</code> list</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Mock</strong></td>\n<td>Pre-programmed with <strong>expectations</strong> about calls, <strong>verified</strong></td>\n<td>&quot;<code>ChargeAsync</code> must be called once with 89.70 EUR&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The key distinction cutting across this whole table: <strong>stubs</strong> support <strong>state verification</strong> (assert on the result or the system&#39;s state after the fact), while <strong>mocks</strong> support <strong>behaviour verification</strong> (assert that a specific interaction happened, regardless of the resulting state). <strong>Verify interactions only for outgoing side effects at the system boundary</strong> (an email was sent, a message was published, a payment was charged), never for internal calls or queries — the next gotcha explains exactly why that restraint matters.</p>\n<h3 id=\"nsubstitute-and-moq\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/unit-testing/nsubstitute-and-moq\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>NSubstitute and Moq</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// NSubstitute: concise, popular</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> payments = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Substitute</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">For</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGateway</span>&gt;();\npayments.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Arg</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Any</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>&gt;(), <span class=\"tk-ty\">Arg</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Any</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;(), <span class=\"tk-ty\">Arg</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Any</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span>&gt;())\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Returns</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentResult</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Declined</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;insufficient_funds&quot;</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> orders = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Substitute</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">For</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IOrderRepository</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> uow = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Substitute</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">For</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IUnitOfWork</span>&gt;();\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> sut = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderHandler</span>(orders, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FixedPricing</span>(), payments, uow);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> result = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> sut.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Handle</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">SampleCommand</span>(), <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">None</span>);\n\nresult.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsFailure</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShouldBeTrue</span>();\nresult.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Code</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShouldBe</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;order.payment_declined&quot;</span>);\norders.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DidNotReceive</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Arg</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Any</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>&gt;());                    <span class=\"tk-com\">// outgoing side effect NOT performed</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> uow.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DidNotReceive</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Arg</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Any</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span>&gt;());\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Moq: the long-standing alternative</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> mock = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Mock</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGateway</span>&gt;();\nmock.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Setup</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">It</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsAny</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>&gt;(), <span class=\"tk-ty\">It</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsAny</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;(), <span class=\"tk-ty\">It</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsAny</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span>&gt;()))\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReturnsAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentResult</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Success</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ch_1&quot;</span>));\nmock.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Verify</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">It</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Is</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>&gt;(m =&gt; m.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Amount</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">89.70m</span>), <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;tok&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">It</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsAny</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span>&gt;()), <span class=\"tk-ty\">Times</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Once</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice the NSubstitute test&#39;s two <code>DidNotReceive()</code> assertions are verifying exactly the outcome the &quot;outgoing side effect&quot; rule allows: when payment is declined, the order should never actually be saved. That&#39;s a genuine behavioural guarantee worth locking in with a mock — quite different from mocking, say, a method that only reads data.</p>\n<p>Other options: <strong>FakeItEasy</strong>, or <strong>hand-written fakes</strong>, which are often clearer for repositories and clocks.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Over-mocking makes tests brittle</div><p>If every collaborator is mocked and every call verified, <strong>any refactoring breaks tests</strong> even when behaviour is unchanged, and the tests mostly restate the implementation. Signs: tests that mock 6 interfaces, <code>Verify</code> calls for queries, tests that fail when you rename a private method&#39;s collaborator. Prefer real in-process objects, fakes for I/O, and asserting outcomes.</p>\n</aside>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">What not to mock</div><p>Don&#39;t mock types you don&#39;t own with complex behaviour (<code>HttpClient</code>, <code>DbContext</code>, <code>IQueryable</code>): the mock won&#39;t behave like the real thing. Wrap them behind your own interface and test the wrapper with integration tests, or use a stub <code>HttpMessageHandler</code> or a real database container.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"testing-time-randomness-and-async\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/unit-testing/testing-time-randomness-and-async\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Testing time, randomness and async <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Code that calls <code>DateTime.UtcNow</code>, <code>Guid.NewGuid()</code> or <code>Random</code> directly is non-deterministic — the exact same test can pass today and fail tomorrow purely because the clock moved. The fix is the same dependency-injection discipline the rest of this book applies everywhere else: inject the source of non-determinism instead of calling it directly.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Production: register TimeProvider.System; code takes TimeProvider (.NET 8)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SubscriptionService</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeProvider</span> clock)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsExpired</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Subscription</span> s) =&gt; s.<span class=\"tk-ty\">EndsAt</span> &lt; clock.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetUtcNow</span>();\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Test: Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> clock = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FakeTimeProvider</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2026</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">27</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">12</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Zero</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> sut = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SubscriptionService</span>(clock);\nsut.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsExpired</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Subscription</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">EndsAt</span> = clock.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetUtcNow</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddDays</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>) }).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShouldBeFalse</span>();\nclock.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Advance</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromDays</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>));                         <span class=\"tk-com\">// move time forward deterministically; also fires timers</span>\nsut.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsExpired</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Subscription</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">EndsAt</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2026</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">28</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">12</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Zero</span>) }).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShouldBeTrue</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Async tests</strong>: make the test <code>async Task</code> and <code>await</code> the call; never <code>.Result</code>. Use <code>Assert.ThrowsAsync</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Timers, delays, <code>PeriodicTimer</code></strong>: pass <code>TimeProvider</code> so <code>FakeTimeProvider.Advance</code> drives them without real waiting.</li>\n<li><strong>Logging</strong>: <code>FakeLogger&lt;T&gt;</code> (Microsoft.Extensions.Diagnostics.Testing) captures log records to assert on.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"test-data-builders-and-fixtures\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/unit-testing/test-data-builders-and-fixtures\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Test data builders and fixtures <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Tests drown in setup code without builders — constructing a valid <code>Order</code> by hand, every single time, for a test that only cares about one specific edge case, is exactly the kind of repetitive ceremony that makes a test suite unpleasant to maintain. The <strong>Test Data Builder</strong> pattern (or &quot;Object Mother&quot;) — the same Builder from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/design-patterns/builder\">Design patterns</a> — gives valid defaults and lets each test override only what actually matters to it:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderBuilder</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> _customer = <span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">New</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductId</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>)&gt; _lines = [(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductId</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">New</span>(), <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Keyboard&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">49.90m</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;EUR&quot;</span>), <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>)];\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> _placed;\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderBuilder</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ForCustomer</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> id) { _customer = id; <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">this</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderBuilder</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">WithLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> name, <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> price, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> qty = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>) { _lines.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>((<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductId</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">New</span>(), name, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>(price, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;EUR&quot;</span>), qty)); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">this</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderBuilder</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Placed</span>() { _placed = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">this</span>; }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>()\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> o = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Order</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Draft</span>(_customer);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> (p, n, m, q) <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> _lines) o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddLine</span>(p, n, m, q);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (_placed) o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Place</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TestData</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Address</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeProvider</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> o;\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-attr\">[Fact]</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Placed_order_cannot_receive_new_lines</span>()\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> order = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderBuilder</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Placed</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> result = order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProductId</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">New</span>(), <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Mouse&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Money</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;EUR&quot;</span>), <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n    result.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShouldBe</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderErrors</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotEditable</span>);\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice how little the test itself has to say: <code>new OrderBuilder().Placed().Build()</code> conveys exactly the one fact this particular test cares about — that the order is already placed — while the builder&#39;s defaults quietly handle everything else (customer, lines) that the test doesn&#39;t need to think about at all.</p>\n<p><strong>AutoFixture</strong> and <strong>Bogus</strong> generate anonymous or realistic data when values don&#39;t matter to the test.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-to-unit-test-and-what-not-to\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/unit-testing/what-to-unit-test-and-what-not-to\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What to unit test (and what not to) <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>High value</th>\n<th>Low value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Domain logic</strong>: invariants, calculations, state transitions</td>\n<td>Trivial getters/setters, auto-properties</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Application handlers&#39; decision logic (with fakes at the edges)</td>\n<td>Code that only delegates (pass-through)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Algorithms, parsers, mappers with logic</td>\n<td>Framework behaviour (EF Core, ASP.NET Core)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Edge cases: empty, null, boundaries, error paths</td>\n<td>Private methods directly (test via public behaviour)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bug reproductions (regression tests)</td>\n<td>UI layout</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Coverage</strong> is a <em>diagnostic</em>, not a goal. 100% line coverage with weak assertions proves little — a test can execute every line of a method and still assert nothing meaningful about what those lines actually produced. <strong>Mutation testing</strong> (Stryker.NET mutates your code, e.g. flipping <code>&gt;</code> to <code>&gt;=</code>, and checks whether tests fail) measures whether tests actually detect bugs, which is a fundamentally different and more honest question than &quot;did this line run.&quot;</p>\n<h2 id=\"tdd\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/unit-testing/tdd\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TDD <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Test-Driven Development</strong>: <strong>Red</strong> (write a failing test for the next small behaviour) → <strong>Green</strong> (the simplest code that passes) → <strong>Refactor</strong> (improve the design with the tests as a safety net). Repeat in minutes-long cycles.</p>\n<p>Benefits: tests exist by construction, the design is driven by usage (testable APIs, small units), and it keeps you focused on one small increment at a time. Criticisms: it can feel slow while a design is exploratory, and it&#39;s harder with heavy framework code where the &quot;simplest thing that passes&quot; isn&#39;t obvious. Many teams practise it selectively for domain logic and bug fixes (&quot;write the failing test that reproduces the bug first,&quot; which doubles as both a diagnosis and a regression guard). <strong>BDD</strong> (Given-When-Then, SpecFlow/Reqnroll) extends the idea to executable specifications written with business stakeholders.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What makes a good unit test?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Fast, isolated, repeatable (no real time, network or DB), self-validating, and focused on one observable behaviour through the public API. It has a clear Arrange-Act-Assert structure, a descriptive name, no logic of its own, and it survives refactoring because it asserts outcomes rather than implementation details.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Explain the difference between a mock, a stub and a fake.</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A stub returns canned data to feed the code under test (state verification). A mock records interactions and is verified to have been called in a particular way (behaviour verification). A fake is a working lightweight implementation, such as an in-memory repository or FakeTimeProvider.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>When should you verify interactions with mocks?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Only for outgoing side effects that cross the system boundary and are part of the observable behaviour: an email sent, a message published, a payment charged. Don&#39;t verify queries or internal collaborator calls; that couples tests to implementation.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>xUnit Fact vs Theory?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Fact is a single parameterless test. Theory is a parameterised test that runs once per data row supplied by InlineData, MemberData, ClassData or TheoryData.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you share setup across tests in xUnit?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The constructor (and Dispose/IAsyncLifetime) for per-test setup, since xUnit creates a new instance per test; IClassFixture&lt;T&gt; to share an expensive object across a class&#39;s tests; collection fixtures ([Collection] plus ICollectionFixture) to share across classes, e.g. a database container.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you test code that depends on the current time?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Inject TimeProvider (.NET 8) or a clock abstraction instead of calling DateTime.Now. In tests, use FakeTimeProvider to set and advance time deterministically, which also drives timers and delays.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Is 100% code coverage a good goal?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>No. Coverage shows which lines executed, not whether behaviour was verified, and chasing it produces low-value tests. Focus on meaningful coverage of business logic and edge cases, and use mutation testing to assess test effectiveness.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is TDD?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Test-Driven Development: write a failing test for a small piece of behaviour (red), write the minimum code to pass (green), then refactor safely, in short cycles. It yields tests by construction and testable, usage-driven designs.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/unit-testing/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/testing/unit-testing-best-practices\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Unit testing best practices for .NET</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://xunit.net/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">xUnit.net documentation</a></li>\n<li>Vladimir Khorikov, <em>Unit Testing Principles, Practices, and Patterns</em>: the best modern book on the topic.</li>\n<li>Roy Osherove, <em>The Art of Unit Testing</em> (3rd ed.).</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://nsubstitute.github.io/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NSubstitute docs</a> · <a href=\"https://stryker-mutator.io/docs/stryker-net/introduction/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Stryker.NET</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/unit-testing/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>No test framework or mocking library is needed for these checks — they use plain BCL types (<code>TimeProvider</code> is built into .NET 8+) and hand-written stand-ins for a stub and a spy, so the stub-vs-mock distinction and the rounding claim can be verified directly. Save as <code>Program.cs</code> in a new console project (<code>dotnet new console</code>) and run with <code>dotnet run</code>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Verifies claims from this chapter: decimal rounding uses banker's rounding (matches the</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// chapter's RoundingCases table), the stub-vs-mock distinction (state verification vs behaviour</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// verification), and that injecting a TimeProvider makes time-dependent code deterministic in tests.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> fails = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> s) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: {s}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> cond, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> s)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (cond) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(s);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">else</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: {s}&quot;</span>); fails++; }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- &quot;RoundsToTwoDecimals_BankersRounding&quot; from the chapter's TheoryData ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Math</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Round</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1.005m</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>) == <span class=\"tk-num\">1.00m</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;decimal Math.Round uses banker's rounding: 1.005 rounds to 1.00 (down to the even digit)&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Math</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Round</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1.015m</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>) == <span class=\"tk-num\">1.02m</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;decimal Math.Round uses banker's rounding: 1.015 rounds to 1.02 (up to the even digit)&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Math</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Round</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2.345m</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>) == <span class=\"tk-num\">2.34m</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;decimal Math.Round uses banker's rounding: 2.345 rounds to 2.34 (down to the even digit)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Stub: feeds a canned answer; you assert on the RESULT, not on whether it was called a certain way ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> stubGateway = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">StubPaymentGateway</span>(declineReason: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;insufficient_funds&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> handler = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderHandler</span>(stubGateway);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> result = handler.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Handle</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">100m</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(result == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;declined: insufficient_funds&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Stub: a canned answer drives the handler's result (state verification)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Spy: records how it was called, so you can assert the INTERACTION happened ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> spyGateway = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SpyPaymentGateway</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderHandler</span>(spyGateway).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Handle</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">50m</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(spyGateway.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeCalls</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> &amp;&amp; spyGateway.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeCalls</span>[<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>] == <span class=\"tk-num\">50m</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Spy: records the call it received, so a test can assert the interaction happened (behaviour verification)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Injecting TimeProvider instead of calling DateTime.UtcNow makes a test deterministic ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> clock = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SettableTimeProvider</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2026</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Zero</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> sub = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Subscription</span>(clock, endsAt: clock.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetUtcNow</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddDays</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(!sub.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsExpired</span>(), <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;TimeProvider: subscription is not expired right after creation&quot;</span>);\nclock.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Advance</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromDays</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(sub.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsExpired</span>(), <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;TimeProvider: advancing the fake clock deterministically makes the same subscription expire, with no real waiting&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(fails == <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span> ? <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;\\nAll checks passed.&quot;</span> : <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;\\n{fails} check(s) failed.&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (fails &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exit</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGateway</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Charge</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> amount); }\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">StubPaymentGateway</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> declineReason) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGateway</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Charge</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> amount) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;declined: {declineReason}&quot;</span>;\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SpyPaymentGateway</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGateway</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeCalls</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Charge</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> amount) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeCalls</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(amount); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;charged&quot;</span>; }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderHandler</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGateway</span> gateway)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Handle</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> amount) =&gt; gateway.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Charge</span>(amount);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SettableTimeProvider</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span> start) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeProvider</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span> _now = start;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetUtcNow</span>() =&gt; _now;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Advance</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span> by) =&gt; _now += by;\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Subscription</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeProvider</span> clock, <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span> endsAt)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsExpired</span>() =&gt; endsAt &lt; clock.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetUtcNow</span>();\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"what-a-unit-test-is-and-the-question-that-divides-testers","text":"What a unit test is, and the question that divides testers","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"xunit-essentials","text":"xUnit essentials","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"xunit-vs-nunit-vs-mstest","text":"xUnit vs NUnit vs MSTest","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"naming-and-structure","text":"Naming and structure","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"assertions","text":"Assertions","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"test-doubles-a-precise-vocabulary-for-fake-dependency","text":"Test doubles: a precise vocabulary for \"fake dependency\"","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"nsubstitute-and-moq","text":"NSubstitute and Moq","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"testing-time-randomness-and-async","text":"Testing time, randomness and async","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"test-data-builders-and-fixtures","text":"Test data builders and fixtures","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"what-to-unit-test-and-what-not-to","text":"What to unit test (and what not to)","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"tdd","text":"TDD","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":3485,"minutes":16,"num":"6.1","part":"p6"},"integration-testing":{"id":"integration-testing","title":"Integration, End-to-End Testing and Test Strategy","summary":"Testing the real thing. The test pyramid and trophy, WebApplicationFactory and TestServer, replacing services and authentication in tests, real databases with Testcontainers and Respawn, testing message consumers and HttpClient dependencies, contract tests, architecture tests, end-to-end and load tests, and tests in CI.","layer":"cross","where":"Cross-cutting › Testing › Integration & strategy","fits":"<p>Integration tests exercise <strong>layers 3 and 5 together</strong>: the real middleware pipeline (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/middleware\">Middleware</a>), DI wiring (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/dependency-injection\">Dependency injection</a>), serialization, EF Core mappings and SQL (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals\">EF Core</a>). They complement <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/unit-testing\">unit tests</a>, use containers (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/docker\">Docker</a>) for dependencies, and run in CI pipelines (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cicd-iac\">CI/CD</a>).</p>\n","prereqs":["unit-testing","building-apis","efcore-fundamentals"],"next":["cicd-iac","docker"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/integration-testing/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Integration tests catch bugs unit tests structurally cannot: wrong middleware order, missing DI registrations, serialization/casing mismatches, LINQ that can&#39;t translate to SQL, authorization gaps.</li>\n<li><code>WebApplicationFactory&lt;Program&gt;</code> boots your <strong>real</strong> <code>Program.cs</code> in memory on a <code>TestServer</code>: real DI, real middleware, real routing — no network ports.</li>\n<li>Only replace things that are genuinely external, slow or non-deterministic (payment gateways, email, the clock). Keep your own code, database and broker real, or the test quietly turns into a unit test in disguise.</li>\n<li><strong>Testcontainers</strong> starts real, throwaway databases/brokers for tests. Starting one is slow, so share it across a test run and <strong>reset data between tests</strong> (Respawn is the most common way).</li>\n<li>A fake authentication handler lets you control <em>who</em> the caller claims to be while your real authorization policies still run unmodified — so you can actually test that the wrong role gets a 403.</li>\n<li>Modern tooling (containers, in-memory hosting) shifted many teams&#39; confidence from the classic unit-test-heavy pyramid toward integration tests for APIs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"what-unit-tests-structurally-cannot-catch\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/integration-testing/what-unit-tests-structurally-cannot-catch\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What unit tests structurally cannot catch <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Think back to what a unit test, by its own definition from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/unit-testing/what-a-unit-test-is-and-the-question-that-divides-testers\">Unit testing</a>, deliberately isolates away: real infrastructure, real wiring, the real pipeline. That&#39;s exactly what makes a whole category of bug invisible to it — bugs that live <strong>between</strong> components, not inside any one of them:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>middleware in the wrong order (every request returns 401),</li>\n<li>a missing DI registration or a captive dependency,</li>\n<li>a route template or model-binding mismatch,</li>\n<li>JSON casing or enum serialization differences,</li>\n<li>an EF Core mapping error, a missing migration, or a LINQ query that <strong>can&#39;t be translated</strong> or is slow in real SQL,</li>\n<li>authorization policies not applied to an endpoint,</li>\n<li>configuration binding failures.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An <strong>integration test</strong> runs real components together (often the whole app in-process against real infrastructure in containers) and asserts on observable behaviour (HTTP responses, database state, published messages) — precisely the gap unit tests leave open.</p>\n<h2 id=\"test-pyramid-trophy-and-honeycomb-how-much-weight-goes-where\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/integration-testing/test-pyramid-trophy-and-honeycomb-how-much-weight-goes-where\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Test pyramid, trophy and honeycomb: how much weight goes where <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>     Pyramid (Mike Cohn)              Trophy (Kent C. Dodds)            Honeycomb (Spotify, microservices)\n          /E2E\\                             (E2E)                                ( E2E )\n        /-------\\                       ┌──────────┐                     ┌──────────────────────┐\n       /Integration\\                    │Integration│ ← most tests        │     Integration       │ ← most\n     /---------------\\                  └──────────┘                     └──────────────────────┘\n    /      Unit       \\                   ( Unit )                              ( Unit / details )\n   ─────────────────────                (Static analysis)</code></pre></figure>\n<p>The classic <strong>pyramid</strong> says: many unit tests, fewer integration tests, very few end-to-end tests. That shape made sense when higher-level tests were slow and flaky, so you minimized time spent on them. <strong>Containers and in-memory hosting changed that</strong>: Testcontainers and <code>WebApplicationFactory</code> (both covered below) made integration tests fast and reliable enough that many .NET teams now put <strong>most of their confidence in integration tests</strong> for APIs, with unit tests reserved for complex domain logic and a handful of E2E smoke tests. The pyramid&#39;s shape was never the real point — the point was always &quot;spend your testing effort where it buys the most confidence per minute,&quot; and that calculation changed once the tooling did.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Test type</th>\n<th>Scope</th>\n<th>Speed</th>\n<th>Confidence per test</th>\n<th>Typical tools</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Unit</td>\n<td>A behaviour in-process, no I/O</td>\n<td>ms</td>\n<td>Narrow</td>\n<td>xUnit, NSubstitute</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Integration (in-process API)</td>\n<td>Full pipeline + real DB/broker in containers</td>\n<td>10–500 ms each (plus container startup)</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>WebApplicationFactory, Testcontainers, Respawn</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Contract</td>\n<td>API/message schema between services</td>\n<td>Fast</td>\n<td>Prevents integration breaks</td>\n<td>Pact, OpenAPI diffing, schema registry checks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>End-to-end</td>\n<td>Deployed system through the UI or public API</td>\n<td>Seconds</td>\n<td>Highest, but flaky and costly</td>\n<td>Playwright, k6 scripts, smoke tests</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Performance / load</td>\n<td>Throughput and latency under load</td>\n<td>Minutes</td>\n<td>Capacity</td>\n<td>k6, NBomber, Azure Load Testing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"webapplicationfactory-booting-the-real-app-in-memory\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/integration-testing/webapplicationfactory-booting-the-real-app-in-memory\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>WebApplicationFactory: booting the real app in memory <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><code>Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing</code> boots your <strong>real <code>Program.cs</code></strong> in memory on a <strong>TestServer</strong> (no network ports) and gives you an <code>HttpClient</code> wired straight into the pipeline.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Make Program visible to the test project (top-level statements generate an internal Program class)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> partial <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Program</span>;     <span class=\"tk-com\">// at the bottom of Program.cs</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HealthTests</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">WebApplicationFactory</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Program</span>&gt; factory) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IClassFixture</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">WebApplicationFactory</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Program</span>&gt;&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[Fact]</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Health_endpoint_returns_ok</span>()\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> client = factory.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateClient</span>();\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> response = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> client.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/health&quot;</span>);\n        response.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShouldBe</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpStatusCode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OK</span>);\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The reason this test is worth more than it looks: everything runs <strong>for real</strong> — configuration, DI, the actual middleware order from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/middleware\">Middleware</a>, routing, model binding, validation, filters, serialization and ProblemDetails. A bug in any one of those, the exact category this chapter opened with, shows up here.</p>\n<h2 id=\"customising-the-app-under-test\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/integration-testing/customising-the-app-under-test\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Customising the app under test <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopApiFactory</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">WebApplicationFactory</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Program</span>&gt;, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IAsyncLifetime</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">MsSqlContainer</span> _sql = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">MsSqlBuilder</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithImage</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;mcr.microsoft.com/mssql/server:2022-latest&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FakeEmailSender</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Emails</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FakeTimeProvider</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Clock</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parse</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;2026-10-27T10:00:00Z&quot;</span>));\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ConfigureWebHost</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IWebHostBuilder</span> builder)\n    {\n        builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseEnvironment</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Testing&quot;</span>);\n        builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseSetting</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ConnectionStrings:Shop&quot;</span>, _sql.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetConnectionString</span>());   <span class=\"tk-com\">// override configuration</span>\n        builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ConfigureTestServices</span>(services =&gt;                                    <span class=\"tk-com\">// runs AFTER Program.cs registrations</span>\n        {\n            services.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RemoveAll</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IEmailSender</span>&gt;();\n            services.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IEmailSender</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Emails</span>);                             <span class=\"tk-com\">// replace only true externals</span>\n            services.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeProvider</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Clock</span>);\n            services.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGateway</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ApprovingPaymentGateway</span>&gt;();\n            services.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAuthentication</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TestAuthHandler</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Scheme</span>)                       <span class=\"tk-com\">// fake authentication (below)</span>\n                    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScheme</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticationSchemeOptions</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TestAuthHandler</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TestAuthHandler</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Scheme</span>, _ =&gt; { });\n        });\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InitializeAsync</span>()\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> _sql.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartAsync</span>();\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> scope = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateScope</span>();\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> scope.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceProvider</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Database</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MigrateAsync</span>();   <span class=\"tk-com\">// real schema from real migrations</span>\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DisposeAsync</span>() { <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> _sql.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DisposeAsync</span>(); <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">base</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DisposeAsync</span>(); }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The rule here: <strong>only replace things that are external, slow, non-deterministic or costly</strong> — payment providers, email/SMS, third-party APIs, the clock. Keep <strong>your</strong> code, your database and your broker real. The whole value of an integration test comes from exercising the real wiring; swap out more than genuine externals and it quietly turns back into a unit test wearing a disguise.</p>\n<h3 id=\"fake-authentication\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/integration-testing/fake-authentication\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Fake authentication</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TestAuthHandler</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IOptionsMonitor</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticationSchemeOptions</span>&gt; o, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ILoggerFactory</span> l, <span class=\"tk-ty\">UrlEncoder</span> e)\n    : <span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticationHandler</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticationSchemeOptions</span>&gt;(o, l, e)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">const</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Scheme</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Test&quot;</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticateResult</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">HandleAuthenticateAsync</span>()\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!<span class=\"tk-ty\">Request</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGetValue</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;X-Test-User&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> user)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromResult</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticateResult</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NoResult</span>());\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> claims = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Claim</span>&gt; { <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;sub&quot;</span>, user!), <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimTypes</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>, user!) };\n        claims.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddRange</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Request</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span>[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;X-Test-Roles&quot;</span>].<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Split</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">','</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">StringSplitOptions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RemoveEmptyEntries</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(r =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Claim</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;roles&quot;</span>, r)));\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> principal = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimsPrincipal</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimsIdentity</span>(claims, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Scheme</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimTypes</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;roles&quot;</span>));\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromResult</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticateResult</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Success</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticationTicket</span>(principal, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Scheme</span>)));\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Make it the default scheme in tests:</span>\nservices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PostConfigure</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticationOptions</span>&gt;(o =&gt; { o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DefaultAuthenticateScheme</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">TestAuthHandler</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Scheme</span>; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DefaultChallengeScheme</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">TestAuthHandler</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Scheme</span>; });\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// In a test</span>\nclient.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DefaultRequestHeaders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;X-Test-User&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;customer-42&quot;</span>);\nclient.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DefaultRequestHeaders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;X-Test-Roles&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;customer&quot;</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>This handler is worth writing instead of just disabling auth for tests: it replaces only the <em>authentication</em> step — who the caller claims to be — while your <strong>real authorization policies</strong> still run unmodified, exactly as <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/auth/authorization-the-simple-forms-before-anything-needs-a-polic\">Auth</a> described. So you can genuinely test that a customer gets 403 on an admin endpoint, with the real policy logic making that call. Alternatively, mint real JWTs with a test signing key for even closer fidelity to production.</p>\n<h2 id=\"real-databases-with-testcontainers\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/integration-testing/real-databases-with-testcontainers\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Real databases with Testcontainers <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Testcontainers for .NET</strong> starts throwaway <strong>Docker containers</strong> (lightweight, isolated environments that run a piece of software — see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/docker\">Docker</a> for the full picture) from tests: SQL Server, PostgreSQL, Redis, RabbitMQ, Azurite (Azure Storage), the Service Bus emulator, Cosmos DB emulator, Kafka, LocalStack.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"cHJpdmF0ZSByZWFkb25seSBQb3N0Z3JlU3FsQ29udGFpbmVyIF9wZyA9IG5ldyBQb3N0Z3JlU3FsQnVpbGRlcigpLldpdGhJbWFnZSgicG9zdGdyZXM6MTctYWxwaW5lIikuQnVpbGQoKTsKcHJpdmF0ZSByZWFkb25seSBSZWRpc0NvbnRhaW5lciBfcmVkaXMgPSBuZXcgUmVkaXNCdWlsZGVyKCkuQnVpbGQoKTs=\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PostgreSqlContainer</span> _pg = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PostgreSqlBuilder</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithImage</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;postgres:17-alpine&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">RedisContainer</span> _redis = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">RedisBuilder</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Starting a container takes seconds, which is long enough that doing it per-test would make the suite painfully slow — so <strong>share one per test run or collection</strong>, and instead <strong>reset data between tests</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Strategy</th>\n<th>How</th>\n<th>Trade-off</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Respawn</strong></td>\n<td>Deletes data from all tables (FK-aware) before each test</td>\n<td>Fast, simple; most popular</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transaction per test, rolled back</td>\n<td>Wrap each test in a transaction</td>\n<td>Very fast; doesn&#39;t work if the code under test manages its own transactions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unique data per test</td>\n<td>Each test creates its own customer/tenant IDs</td>\n<td>No reset needed; tests can run in parallel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DB snapshot/restore or a fresh DB per class</td>\n<td>Clone a template database</td>\n<td>Isolation at a cost</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-attr\">[CollectionDefinition(nameof(ApiCollection))]</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ApiCollection</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">ICollectionFixture</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopApiFactory</span>&gt;;   <span class=\"tk-com\">// one factory + container for all API tests</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-attr\">[Collection(nameof(ApiCollection))]</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderTests</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopApiFactory</span> app) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IAsyncLifetime</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Respawner</span> _respawner = <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>!;\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InitializeAsync</span>()\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> conn = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlConnection</span>(app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ConnectionString</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> conn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OpenAsync</span>();\n        _respawner = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Respawner</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateAsync</span>(conn, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">RespawnerOptions</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">TablesToIgnore</span> = [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;__EFMigrationsHistory&quot;</span>] });\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> _respawner.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ResetAsync</span>(conn);\n    }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DisposeAsync</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CompletedTask</span>;\n\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[Fact]</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Placing_an_order_persists_it_and_sends_confirmation</span>()\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> client = app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateAuthenticatedClient</span>(user: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;customer-42&quot;</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SeedAsync</span>(db =&gt; db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Product</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;KB-01&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Keyboard&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">49.90m</span>, stock: <span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>)));\n\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> response = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> client.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PostAsJsonAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/api/orders&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { lines = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>[] { <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { sku = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;KB-01&quot;</span>, quantity = <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span> } } });\n\n        response.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShouldBe</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpStatusCode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Created</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> created = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> response.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Content</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadFromJsonAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderDto</span>&gt;();\n        created!.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShouldBe</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">99.80m</span>);\n        response.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Location</span>!.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShouldBe</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;/api/orders/{created.Id}&quot;</span>);\n\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteDbAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> db =&gt; (<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Products</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SingleAsync</span>(p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;KB-01&quot;</span>)).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Stock</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShouldBe</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">8</span>));\n        app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Emails</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sent</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShouldContain</span>(m =&gt; m.<span class=\"tk-ty\">To</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;customer-42@test&quot;</span> &amp;&amp; m.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Subject</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contains</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Order confirmed&quot;</span>));\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[Fact]</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Invalid_order_returns_validation_problem</span>()\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> client = app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateAuthenticatedClient</span>(user: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;customer-42&quot;</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> response = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> client.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PostAsJsonAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/api/orders&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { lines = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Array</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Empty</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>&gt;() });\n\n        response.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShouldBe</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpStatusCode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BadRequest</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> problem = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> response.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Content</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadFromJsonAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidationProblemDetails</span>&gt;();\n        problem!.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Errors</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShouldContainKey</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Lines&quot;</span>);\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>That one test proves four things at once: the HTTP request binds correctly, the handler runs its real logic, the database is actually debited (<code>Stock.ShouldBe(8)</code>), and a side effect fired (<code>Emails.Sent</code>). Proving all four with unit tests would need separate, carefully constructed mocks — and even then wouldn&#39;t prove they work <strong>together</strong>.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Don&#39;t use the EF Core InMemory provider for integration tests</div><p>It isn&#39;t relational: no SQL translation, no constraints, no transactions, different null and case semantics. Tests pass while production fails. Microsoft&#39;s own guidance discourages it. Use the real engine (Testcontainers) or at least SQLite.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"testing-dependencies-you-dont-own\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/integration-testing/testing-dependencies-you-dont-own\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Testing dependencies you don&#39;t own <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Dependency</th>\n<th>Approach</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Third-party HTTP APIs</td>\n<td><strong>WireMock.Net</strong> fake server (realistic HTTP, delays, faults) or a stub <code>HttpMessageHandler</code> (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/http-client/testing-code-that-uses-httpclient\">HttpClient</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Message broker</td>\n<td>A real broker in a container (RabbitMQ, Service Bus emulator), or the library&#39;s test harness (<code>MassTransit</code> <code>ITestHarness</code> to assert published/consumed messages)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Azure Storage</td>\n<td><strong>Azurite</strong> container</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cosmos DB</td>\n<td>Cosmos DB emulator (Linux container)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Identity provider</td>\n<td>Fake auth handler, or a test token issuer</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// MassTransit test harness: assert the endpoint published an event</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> harness = app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetTestHarness</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> client.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PostAsJsonAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/api/orders&quot;</span>, request);\n(<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> harness.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Published</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Any</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span>&gt;(x =&gt; x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Context</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Message</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;customer-42&quot;</span>)).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShouldBeTrue</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h2 id=\"contract-and-architecture-tests\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/integration-testing/contract-and-architecture-tests\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Contract and architecture tests <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Consumer-driven contract tests</strong> (Pact): the consumer records the requests it makes and the responses it expects as a <strong>contract</strong>; the provider&#39;s CI verifies it still honours every consumer&#39;s contract. This catches breaking API changes <strong>without</strong> deploying both services together — directly addressing the &quot;versioning&quot; cost microservices introduce, named back in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/microservices/why-microservices-and-what-they-genuinely-cost\">Microservices</a>. Lighter alternatives: <strong>OpenAPI diffing</strong> in CI (fail on breaking changes), or <strong>schema registry</strong> compatibility checks for messages.</p>\n<p><strong>Snapshot tests</strong> of public contracts (<code>Verify</code> on the generated OpenAPI document or on serialized message types) make accidental contract changes visible in code review.</p>\n<p><strong>Architecture tests</strong> (NetArchTest, ArchUnitNET) enforce layering rules. See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/app-architectures/enforcing-architecture\">App architectures</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"end-to-end-smoke-and-load-tests\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/integration-testing/end-to-end-smoke-and-load-tests\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>End-to-end, smoke and load tests <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>E2E</strong>: drive the deployed system like a user. <strong>Playwright for .NET</strong> for browser flows; scripted API journeys for backends. Keep them <strong>few</strong>, focused on critical paths (sign up, checkout), and tolerant of async processing (poll with timeouts, not fixed sleeps).</li>\n<li><strong>Smoke tests</strong> after each deployment: health endpoints plus one or two key reads, gating promotion to production.</li>\n<li><strong>Load tests</strong> (k6, NBomber, Azure Load Testing, JMeter): establish baselines (p50/p95/p99 latency, throughput, error rate) and find bottlenecks before launches. Run against production-like environments with realistic data volumes.</li>\n<li><strong>Chaos testing</strong> (Azure Chaos Studio, Polly&#39;s chaos strategies via <code>AddChaos…</code>): inject faults to verify resilience behaviour.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"tests-in-ci\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/integration-testing/tests-in-ci\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Tests in CI <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>YAML</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>- <span class=\"tk-ty\">run</span>: dotnet test <span class=\"tk-attr\">--configuration</span> Release <span class=\"tk-attr\">--logger</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;trx;LogFileName=results.trx&quot;</span> <span class=\"tk-attr\">--collect</span>:<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;XPlat Code Coverage&quot;</span>\n  <span class=\"tk-com\"># GitHub-hosted Ubuntu runners have Docker, so Testcontainers just works</span></code></pre></figure>\n<p>Practices worth adopting:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Run unit tests on every commit, integration tests on every PR.</li>\n<li>Quarantine and fix <strong>flaky tests</strong> immediately — they destroy trust in the whole suite.</li>\n<li>Keep the suite fast: parallel collections, shared containers, Respawn.</li>\n<li>Publish test results and coverage, and make the test run a <strong>required check</strong> before merge.</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the difference between unit and integration tests?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Unit tests verify a single behaviour in isolation, in-process with no I/O, and run in milliseconds. Integration tests verify that components work together with real infrastructure (pipeline, DI, serialization, database, broker), catching wiring, configuration, mapping and SQL issues that unit tests can&#39;t.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you integration-test an ASP.NET Core API?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>WebApplicationFactory&lt;Program&gt; hosts the real app in memory with a TestServer and provides an HttpClient. Override configuration and replace only external dependencies in ConfigureTestServices, use a real database via Testcontainers with migrations applied, reset data with Respawn, and assert on HTTP responses and persisted state.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you handle authentication in integration tests?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Register a test authentication handler that builds a ClaimsPrincipal from headers (or mint real JWTs with a test key) and make it the default scheme, so real authorization policies still execute against controllable identities.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is Testcontainers and why use it?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A library that starts disposable Docker containers (SQL Server, Postgres, Redis, RabbitMQ, Azurite…) from test code. Tests run against the real engines with production-like behaviour, isolated and reproducible locally and in CI, instead of fakes like the EF InMemory provider.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you keep integration tests isolated and fast?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Share one app factory and container per collection, reset data between tests (Respawn, or a transaction rollback), use unique data per test where possible, run collections in parallel, and replace only slow external dependencies.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are contract tests?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Tests verifying that a provider service honours the API or message contract its consumers rely on (e.g. Pact consumer-driven contracts, OpenAPI breaking-change checks). They catch integration breaks between independently deployed services without full end-to-end environments.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Test pyramid vs testing trophy?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The pyramid recommends mostly unit tests, fewer integration tests and very few E2E tests. The trophy shifts the weight to integration tests, because modern tooling makes them fast and they give more confidence per test, with unit tests reserved for complex logic and only a few E2E tests.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/integration-testing/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/test/integration-tests\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Integration tests in ASP.NET Core</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://dotnet.testcontainers.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Testcontainers for .NET</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/jbogard/Respawn\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Respawn</a> · <a href=\"https://github.com/WireMock-Net/WireMock.Net\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">WireMock.Net</a> · <a href=\"https://github.com/pact-foundation/pact-net\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pact .NET</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/ef/core/testing/testing-with-the-database\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Testing against your production database system (EF Core)</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/integration-testing/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>The checks below build a real in-memory ASP.NET Core pipeline with <code>TestServer</code> (the same mechanism <code>WebApplicationFactory</code> boots underneath) and confirm middleware runs in order and a faked identity is still subject to a real authorization decision. No Docker needed. Save as a console app (<code>dotnet new console</code>, <code>dotnet add package Microsoft.AspNetCore.TestHost</code>), then <code>dotnet run</code>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AspNetCore</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Authentication</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AspNetCore</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Builder</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AspNetCore</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Hosting</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AspNetCore</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Http</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AspNetCore</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Http</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Extensions</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AspNetCore</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TestHost</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Extensions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DependencyInjection</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Extensions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Hosting</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Security</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Claims</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> order = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;(); <span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: middleware runs in registration order — each one runs before the next.</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> hostBuilder = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HostBuilder</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">ConfigureWebHost</span>(webHost =&gt;\n{\n    webHost.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseTestServer</span>();\n    webHost.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ConfigureServices</span>(services =&gt;\n    {\n        services.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAuthentication</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TestAuthHandler</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Scheme</span>)\n            .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScheme</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticationSchemeOptions</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TestAuthHandler</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TestAuthHandler</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Scheme</span>, _ =&gt; { });\n    });\n    webHost.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configure</span>(app =&gt;\n    {\n        app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Use</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (ctx, next) =&gt; { order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A&quot;</span>); <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> next(); });\n        app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Use</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (ctx, next) =&gt; { order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;B&quot;</span>); <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> next(); });\n\n        app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseAuthentication</span>();\n        <span class=\"tk-com\">// Authorization here is a REAL policy check (RequireClaim), just invoked directly instead of via</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-com\">// the endpoint-routing UseAuthorization() middleware, which needs a full routing setup this tiny host skips.</span>\n\n        app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Map</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/public&quot;</span>, inner =&gt; inner.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Run</span>(ctx =&gt; ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Response</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ok&quot;</span>)));\n        app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Map</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/admin&quot;</span>, inner =&gt;\n        {\n            inner.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Run</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> ctx =&gt;\n            {\n                <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> authResult = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticateAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TestAuthHandler</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Scheme</span>);\n                <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> isAdmin = authResult.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Succeeded</span> &amp;&amp; authResult.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Principal</span>!.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasClaim</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;roles&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;admin&quot;</span>);\n                <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!isAdmin) { ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Response</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">403</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span>; }\n                <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Response</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;admin-ok&quot;</span>);\n            });\n        });\n    });\n});\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> host = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> hostBuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartAsync</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> client = host.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetTestClient</span>();\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: the in-memory pipeline runs middleware in registration order — real wiring, not a mock.</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> res = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> client.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/public&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SequenceEqual</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>[] { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;A&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;B&quot;</span> }))\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected [A, B], got [{string.Join(&quot;</span>,<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;, order)}]&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: an in-memory TestServer pipeline runs middleware in real registration order&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: a fake authentication handler lets you control WHO the caller is, while real authorization policies still decide.</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> req1 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpRequestMessage</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpMethod</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Get</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/admin&quot;</span>);\n    req1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;X-Test-User&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;customer-42&quot;</span>);\n    req1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;X-Test-Roles&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;customer&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> res1 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> client.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SendAsync</span>(req1);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> ((<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>)res1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span> != <span class=\"tk-num\">403</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected 403 for a non-admin, got {(int)res1.StatusCode}&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: a customer identity (faked) is correctly rejected by the REAL authorization check&quot;</span>);\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> req2 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpRequestMessage</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpMethod</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Get</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/admin&quot;</span>);\n    req2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;X-Test-User&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;admin-1&quot;</span>);\n    req2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;X-Test-Roles&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;admin&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> res2 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> client.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SendAsync</span>(req2);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> ((<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>)res2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StatusCode</span> != <span class=\"tk-num\">200</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected 200 for an admin, got {(int)res2.StatusCode}&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: an admin identity (faked) is correctly allowed by the REAL authorization check&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All checks passed.&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here (needs Docker, or a full WebApplicationFactory&lt;Program&gt; against a real app project):</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - Testcontainers starting real SQL Server/Postgres/Redis/RabbitMQ containers — see Further reading: &quot;Testcontainers for .NET&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - Respawn resetting real table data between tests — see Further reading: &quot;Respawn&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - WebApplicationFactory&lt;Program&gt; specifically (it needs a referenced app assembly; TestServer above is the same</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//   underlying mechanism it boots) — see Further reading: &quot;Integration tests in ASP.NET Core&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - Pact consumer-driven contracts, architecture tests, load/chaos tests — all need external tools or deployed systems</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TestAuthHandler</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Extensions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Options</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IOptionsMonitor</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticationSchemeOptions</span>&gt; o,\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Extensions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Logging</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ILoggerFactory</span> l, <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Text</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Encodings</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Web</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UrlEncoder</span> e)\n    : <span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticationHandler</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticationSchemeOptions</span>&gt;(o, l, e)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">const</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Scheme</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Test&quot;</span>;\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticateResult</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">HandleAuthenticateAsync</span>()\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!<span class=\"tk-ty\">Request</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGetValue</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;X-Test-User&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> user)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromResult</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticateResult</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NoResult</span>());\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> claims = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Claim</span>&gt; { <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;sub&quot;</span>, user!), <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimTypes</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>, user!) };\n        claims.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddRange</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Request</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span>[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;X-Test-Roles&quot;</span>].<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Split</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">','</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">StringSplitOptions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RemoveEmptyEntries</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(r =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Claim</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;roles&quot;</span>, r)));\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> principal = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimsPrincipal</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimsIdentity</span>(claims, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Scheme</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimTypes</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;roles&quot;</span>));\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromResult</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticateResult</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Success</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticationTicket</span>(principal, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Scheme</span>)));\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"what-unit-tests-structurally-cannot-catch","text":"What unit tests structurally cannot catch","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"test-pyramid-trophy-and-honeycomb-how-much-weight-goes-where","text":"Test pyramid, trophy and honeycomb: how much weight goes where","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"webapplicationfactory-booting-the-real-app-in-memory","text":"WebApplicationFactory: booting the real app in memory","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"customising-the-app-under-test","text":"Customising the app under test","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"fake-authentication","text":"Fake authentication","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"real-databases-with-testcontainers","text":"Real databases with Testcontainers","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"testing-dependencies-you-dont-own","text":"Testing dependencies you don't own","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"contract-and-architecture-tests","text":"Contract and architecture tests","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"end-to-end-smoke-and-load-tests","text":"End-to-end, smoke and load tests","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"tests-in-ci","text":"Tests in CI","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":3536,"minutes":16,"num":"6.2","part":"p6"},"messaging-events":{"id":"messaging-events","title":"Messaging and Event-Driven Architecture","summary":"Asynchronous communication between services. Why messaging, commands vs events vs queries, queues and topics, brokers compared (Service Bus, RabbitMQ, Kafka/Event Hubs), delivery guarantees, the dual-write problem and the transactional outbox, idempotent consumers and the inbox, retries and dead-lettering, ordering, sagas (orchestration and choreography), MassTransit/NServiceBus/Wolverine, message design and versioning, and event sourcing.","layer":"comms","where":"Service communication › Asynchronous messaging & events","fits":"<p>The asynchronous half of layer 6, and the glue of distributed systems (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/microservices\">Microservices</a>). Messages originate from domain events (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ddd\">DDD</a>) persisted via the EF Core outbox (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-advanced/domain-events-and-the-outbox-on-savechanges\">EF Core advanced</a>), are consumed by hosted services (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/background-work\">Background work</a>), run on cloud brokers (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-data-messaging\">Azure data &amp; messaging</a>), and need resilience (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/resilience\">Resilience</a>) and tracing (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/observability-aspire\">Observability</a>).</p>\n","prereqs":["background-work","microservices"],"next":["resilience","observability-aspire","azure-data-messaging"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/messaging-events/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A broker sits between services so the sender doesn&#39;t wait: it hands off a message and moves on, decoupled in time from whoever eventually reads it.</li>\n<li><strong>Commands</strong> (do this, exactly one handler, can be rejected) go on <strong>queues</strong>; <strong>events</strong> (this happened, zero or more subscribers) go on <strong>topics</strong>.</li>\n<li>Brokers deliver <strong>at-least-once</strong>, never exactly-once — duplicates are a normal outcome of coordinating two systems over a network, not a bug. Every consumer must be <strong>idempotent</strong>.</li>\n<li>The <strong>dual-write problem</strong> (you can&#39;t atomically save to your DB and publish to a broker) is solved by the <strong>transactional outbox</strong>: write the message to a table in the same transaction as the business change, then let a separate worker publish it.</li>\n<li>Most brokers don&#39;t guarantee global order; design for disorder (sequence numbers, partition/session keys) instead of assuming it.</li>\n<li>A <strong>saga</strong> runs a multi-step business process as a chain of local transactions with compensating &quot;undo&quot; steps, either coordinated by services reacting to each other&#39;s events (<strong>choreography</strong>) or by a central state machine (<strong>orchestration</strong>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"what-putting-a-broker-in-the-middle-actually-buys-you\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/messaging-events/what-putting-a-broker-in-the-middle-actually-buys-you\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What putting a broker in the middle actually buys you <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>With synchronous HTTP between services, the caller <strong>waits</strong>, and if the callee is down or slow, that failure propagates straight back to the caller — exactly the &quot;chatty synchronous chains&quot; problem <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/microservices/why-microservices-and-what-they-genuinely-cost\">Microservices</a> warned about. <strong>Messaging</strong> fixes this by putting a <strong>broker</strong> in between: the sender hands a message to the broker and moves on; receivers process it when they can, entirely decoupled from whether the sender is even still running.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Synchronous:   Ordering ──HTTP──▶ Payments ──HTTP──▶ Email        (all must be up, latency adds up, failures cascade)\n\nAsynchronous:  Ordering ──▶ [ broker: &quot;OrderPlaced&quot; ] ──▶ Payments\n                                                     ├──▶ Email\n                                                     └──▶ Analytics   (added later without touching Ordering)</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Benefit</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Temporal decoupling</strong></td>\n<td>Producer and consumer don&#39;t need to be up at the same time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Load levelling</strong></td>\n<td>A spike of 10,000 orders queues up; consumers process at their own pace</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Fault isolation</strong></td>\n<td>Email being down doesn&#39;t stop checkout</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Extensibility</strong></td>\n<td>New subscribers react to existing events without changing the publisher</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Scalability</strong></td>\n<td>Add consumer instances (competing consumers) to increase throughput</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Notice the diagram&#39;s last line: Analytics was &quot;added later without touching Ordering&quot; — that&#39;s the Observer pattern from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/design-patterns/observer\">Design patterns</a> operating at the scale of entire services, where the publisher never needs to know who&#39;s listening. Costs: <strong>eventual consistency</strong>, more infrastructure, harder debugging (needs tracing), duplicate and out-of-order delivery to handle, and message versioning — each of which gets its own section below.</p>\n<h2 id=\"commands-events-and-queries-three-different-conversations\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/messaging-events/commands-events-and-queries-three-different-conversations\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Commands, events and queries: three different conversations <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Command</th>\n<th>Event</th>\n<th>Query</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Meaning</td>\n<td>&quot;Please do X&quot;</td>\n<td>&quot;X happened&quot;</td>\n<td>&quot;Tell me X&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Naming</td>\n<td>Imperative: <code>ChargePayment</code>, <code>SendInvoice</code></td>\n<td>Past tense: <code>OrderPlaced</code>, <code>PaymentFailed</code></td>\n<td><code>GetOrderStatus</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Receivers</td>\n<td><strong>Exactly one</strong> logical handler</td>\n<td><strong>Zero or many</strong> subscribers</td>\n<td>One</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sender knows the receiver?</td>\n<td>Yes (sends to a specific queue)</td>\n<td>No (publishes to a topic)</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Can be rejected?</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>No: it already happened</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical channel</td>\n<td><strong>Queue</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Topic</strong> (pub/sub)</td>\n<td>Usually HTTP/gRPC, not messaging</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>This is the exact same Command-vs-Event naming discipline from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/design-patterns/command\">Design patterns</a>, now mapped onto concrete infrastructure choices — a command&#39;s &quot;exactly one handler&quot; nature is precisely why it belongs on a queue, while an event&#39;s &quot;zero or many subscribers&quot; nature is precisely why it belongs on a topic.</p>\n<p><strong>Event types:</strong> <em>notification events</em> (thin: <code>OrderPlaced { OrderId }</code>, consumers call back for details) vs <em>event-carried state transfer</em> (fat: includes the data consumers need, so no callback). Fat events reduce coupling at runtime but increase schema coupling.</p>\n<h2 id=\"queues-and-topics\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/messaging-events/queues-and-topics\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Queues and topics <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>QUEUE (point-to-point, competing consumers)         TOPIC (publish/subscribe)\nProducer ─▶ [ m3 m2 m1 ] ─▶ Consumer A (gets m1)     Publisher ─▶ (topic) ─┬─▶ [subscription: billing ] ─▶ Billing\n                         └▶ Consumer B (gets m2)                          ├─▶ [subscription: email   ] ─▶ Email\neach message goes to ONE consumer instance                                └─▶ [subscription: search  ] ─▶ Search\n                                                    each subscription gets its OWN copy</code></pre></figure>\n<p>A message is processed by <strong>one</strong> instance per queue or subscription, so scaling out consumers increases throughput without duplicate processing (in theory: see delivery guarantees, next).</p>\n<h2 id=\"brokers-compared\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/messaging-events/brokers-compared\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Brokers compared <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th><strong>Azure Service Bus</strong></th>\n<th><strong>RabbitMQ</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Kafka / Azure Event Hubs</strong></th>\n<th>Azure Storage Queues</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Model</td>\n<td>Enterprise message broker: queues + topics/subscriptions</td>\n<td>Broker with exchanges (direct, topic, fanout, headers) routing to queues</td>\n<td><strong>Distributed append-only log</strong> partitioned into topics</td>\n<td>Simple queue</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Consumption</td>\n<td>Message removed when completed</td>\n<td>Message removed when acked</td>\n<td>Consumers track <strong>offsets</strong>; messages <strong>retained</strong> (days, or forever with compaction) and <strong>replayable</strong></td>\n<td>Removed when deleted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ordering</td>\n<td>FIFO per <strong>session</strong></td>\n<td>Per queue (single consumer)</td>\n<td>Per <strong>partition</strong></td>\n<td>Best effort</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Features</td>\n<td>Sessions, dead-letter queues, scheduled messages, duplicate detection, transactions, filters, auto-forwarding</td>\n<td>Flexible routing, plugins, quorum queues, streams</td>\n<td>Huge throughput, replay, stream processing, consumer groups</td>\n<td>Cheap, massive scale, basic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hosting</td>\n<td>Fully managed PaaS</td>\n<td>Self-hosted, or CloudAMQP etc.</td>\n<td>Confluent / self-hosted Kafka; <strong>Event Hubs</strong> (managed, Kafka-compatible)</td>\n<td>Managed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Best for</td>\n<td>Business commands and events, workflows</td>\n<td>On-premises / multi-cloud, flexible routing</td>\n<td>Event streaming, telemetry, analytics, event sourcing pipelines</td>\n<td>Simple background jobs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The &quot;Consumption&quot; row is the real distinguishing feature between Service Bus and Kafka, worth being precise about: Service Bus <em>removes</em> a message once it&#39;s handled, treating messages as work to be consumed and discarded; Kafka <em>retains</em> everything and lets consumers independently track their own position in the log, treating messages as a durable, replayable history. That difference is why Kafka fits event sourcing pipelines naturally and Service Bus doesn&#39;t — replay only makes sense if nothing was removed.</p>\n<p>Also on Azure: <strong>Event Grid</strong>, a push-based <strong>event router</strong> for reactive programming (a blob was created, a resource changed, custom events to webhooks or functions). It&#39;s lightweight notification fan-out, not a work queue. See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-data-messaging\">Azure data &amp; messaging</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"delivery-guarantees-why-exactly-once-isnt-really-on-the-tabl\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/messaging-events/delivery-guarantees-why-exactly-once-isnt-really-on-the-tabl\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Delivery guarantees: why &quot;exactly once&quot; isn&#39;t really on the table <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Guarantee</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Reality</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>At-most-once</td>\n<td>Delivered 0 or 1 times (may be lost)</td>\n<td>Fire-and-forget; acceptable for metrics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>At-least-once</strong></td>\n<td>Delivered 1 or more times (<strong>duplicates possible</strong>)</td>\n<td><strong>The default for virtually all brokers</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exactly-once</td>\n<td>Delivered precisely once</td>\n<td>Not achievable end-to-end across systems in general; you get <strong>effectively-once</strong> via idempotency</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Duplicates aren&#39;t a quality-of-implementation problem — they&#39;re unavoidable. A consumer can crash right after finishing the work but <strong>before acknowledging</strong> it; the broker has no way to know the work actually finished, so it redelivers to someone else. A producer can time out waiting for an acknowledgment and retry a publish that, unknown to it, already succeeded. A processing lock can expire during unusually slow work, letting a second instance pick up the same message. None of these are broker bugs — they&#39;re the honest cost of coordinating two independent systems over a network that can always be slow or drop a packet.</p>\n<p><strong>Therefore every consumer must be idempotent.</strong> The rest of this chapter builds directly on that one conclusion.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-dual-write-problem-and-the-transactional-outbox\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/messaging-events/the-dual-write-problem-and-the-transactional-outbox\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The dual-write problem and the transactional outbox <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// ✘ Two separate writes: no transaction spans the database AND the broker</span>\ndb.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(order);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>(ct);                       <span class=\"tk-com\">// 1. committed</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> bus.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Publish</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span>(order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>), ct);    <span class=\"tk-com\">// 2. process crashes here → order exists but the event is LOST</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Reverse the order and you get: event published, then SaveChanges fails → event for an order that doesn't exist</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>This is the <strong>dual-write problem</strong>. Swapping the order of the two writes doesn&#39;t fix it — it just swaps which failure you get instead. The database transaction and the broker publish are two separate systems with no shared commit between them, which is exactly the &quot;no distributed transactions across services&quot; limit <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-advanced/transactions-when-savechangess-own-transaction-isnt-enough\">EF Core advanced</a> named.</p>\n<p><strong>Transactional outbox:</strong> write the message to an <strong>Outbox table in the same database transaction</strong> as the business change. A separate <strong>relay</strong> publishes outbox rows to the broker and marks them sent.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>┌──────────── one DB transaction ────────────┐\n│ INSERT INTO Orders …                         │\n│ INSERT INTO OutboxMessages (Id, Type, Payload)│      Outbox relay (BackgroundService, or CDC)\n└──────────────────────────────────────────────┘ ───▶ reads unsent rows ─▶ publishes ─▶ marks ProcessedAt\n     either both commit or neither                   (retries on failure → at-least-once publishing)</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Follow exactly how this closes the gap the naive code left open: the <code>Orders</code> insert and the <code>OutboxMessages</code> insert are now part of <strong>one</strong> atomic transaction, so there is no longer a window where the order exists but the event doesn&#39;t, or vice versa. The relay&#39;s own publish can still fail and retry — but that&#39;s fine, because it&#39;s now publishing from durable, already-committed data, and retrying a publish that already succeeded is just another instance of the at-least-once guarantee this chapter already accepted as the baseline.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OutboxRelay</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IServiceScopeFactory</span> scopes, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IMessagePublisher</span> publisher, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ILogger</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OutboxRelay</span>&gt; log) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">BackgroundService</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">protected</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">override</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> stoppingToken)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> timer = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PeriodicTimer</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSeconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>));\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">while</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> timer.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WaitForNextTickAsync</span>(stoppingToken))\n        {\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> scope = scopes.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateAsyncScope</span>();\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> db = scope.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceProvider</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>&gt;();\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> batch = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OutboxMessages</span>\n                .<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSql</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;SELECT TOP (50) * FROM OutboxMessages WITH (UPDLOCK, READPAST, ROWLOCK) WHERE ProcessedAt IS NULL ORDER BY OccurredAt&quot;</span>)\n                .<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToListAsync</span>(stoppingToken);                                       <span class=\"tk-com\">// READPAST lets several relays run without clashing</span>\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> msg <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> batch)\n            {\n                <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span>\n                {\n                    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> publisher.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PublishAsync</span>(msg.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Type</span>, msg.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Payload</span>, messageId: msg.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, stoppingToken);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// message id = outbox id (for dedup)</span>\n                    msg.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProcessedAt</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UtcNow</span>;\n                }\n                <span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span> ex) { msg.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Attempts</span>++; msg.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LastError</span> = ex.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Message</span>; log.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogWarning</span>(ex, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Outbox publish failed for {Id}&quot;</span>, msg.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>); }\n            }\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>(stoppingToken);\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Variants: polling (above), <strong>change data capture</strong> (Debezium or SQL CDC streaming the outbox table), and library-provided outboxes (<strong>MassTransit</strong>, <strong>NServiceBus</strong>, <strong>Wolverine</strong>, <strong>Brighter</strong> all ship EF Core outboxes). Prefer a library&#39;s outbox over hand-rolling one in production — the <code>READPAST</code> locking detail above is exactly the kind of subtlety a mature library has already handled correctly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"idempotent-consumers-and-the-inbox\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/messaging-events/idempotent-consumers-and-the-inbox\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Idempotent consumers and the inbox <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Make processing the same message twice harmless — not by preventing duplicates (impossible, as established above) but by making a duplicate&#39;s second arrival a no-op:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Technique</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Naturally idempotent operations</strong></td>\n<td><code>SET status = &#39;Paid&#39;</code> (not <code>balance = balance - 10</code>); upserts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Inbox / processed-messages table</strong></td>\n<td>Store the message ID <strong>in the same transaction</strong> as the handler&#39;s changes, with a unique key; skip if already present</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Business keys + unique constraints</strong></td>\n<td><code>Payments</code> has a unique <code>OrderId</code>, so a second insert fails and is treated as already handled</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Version checks</strong></td>\n<td>Apply an update only if the entity is still at the expected version</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Broker duplicate detection</strong></td>\n<td>Service Bus duplicate detection by <code>MessageId</code> within a time window (helps with producer retries, not consumer crashes)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeOnOrderPlaced</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span> db, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IPaymentGateway</span> payments) : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IConsumer</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span>&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Consume</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ConsumeContext</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span>&gt; ctx)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> messageId = ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MessageId</span>!.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>;\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">InboxMessages</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AnyAsync</span>(m =&gt; m.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> == messageId)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span>;          <span class=\"tk-com\">// already processed</span>\n\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> tx = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Database</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BeginTransactionAsync</span>();\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> result = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> payments.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeAsync</span>(ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Message</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>, ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Message</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>, idempotencyKey: ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Message</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>());\n        db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Payments</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Payment</span>(ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Message</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>, result.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Reference</span>));\n        db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">InboxMessages</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InboxMessage</span>(messageId, <span class=\"tk-kw\">nameof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span>), <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UtcNow</span>));   <span class=\"tk-com\">// unique PK</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>();\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> tx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CommitAsync</span>();\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Note the <strong>idempotency key passed to <code>payments.ChargeAsync</code></strong> — the exact pattern from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/api-design/idempotency-keys-for-post-making-the-one-unsafe-by-default-m\">API design</a> — because the inbox table alone only protects your own database. The payment provider is a <em>different</em> system with no visibility into your inbox, so if this consumer crashes after charging but before the inbox row commits, redelivery would charge the card a second time unless the idempotency key passed to the gateway itself prevents it. External side effects always need their own deduplication, because your database transaction can&#39;t roll back a card charge.</p>\n<h2 id=\"retries-poison-messages-and-dead-lettering\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/messaging-events/retries-poison-messages-and-dead-lettering\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Retries, poison messages and dead-lettering <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Immediate retries</strong> for transient glitches (deadlocks, brief timeouts).</li>\n<li><strong>Delayed redelivery</strong> with backoff (seconds → minutes) for longer outages of a dependency.</li>\n<li><strong>Dead-letter queue (DLQ)</strong>: after N failed attempts, move the message aside so it doesn&#39;t block the queue or burn resources (a <strong>poison message</strong>). Alert on DLQ depth; inspect, fix, then <strong>resubmit</strong>.</li>\n<li>Distinguish <strong>transient</strong> errors (retry) from <strong>permanent</strong> ones (validation failure or unknown entity: dead-letter immediately, no point retrying).</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// MassTransit retry/redelivery configuration</span>\ncfg.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReceiveEndpoint</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;billing-order-placed&quot;</span>, e =&gt;\n{\n    e.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseMessageRetry</span>(r =&gt; r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exponential</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromMilliseconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">200</span>), <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSeconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>), <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromMilliseconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">200</span>))\n                            .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ignore</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidationException</span>&gt;());\n    e.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseDelayedRedelivery</span>(r =&gt; r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Intervals</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromMinutes</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>), <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromMinutes</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>), <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromMinutes</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">30</span>)));\n    e.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseEntityFrameworkOutbox</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>&gt;(context);          <span class=\"tk-com\">// inbox/outbox for this endpoint</span>\n    e.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ConfigureConsumer</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargeOnOrderPlaced</span>&gt;(context);\n});</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h2 id=\"ordering-a-guarantee-most-brokers-deliberately-dont-give-you\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/messaging-events/ordering-a-guarantee-most-brokers-deliberately-dont-give-you\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Ordering: a guarantee most brokers deliberately don&#39;t give you <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Most brokers <strong>don&#39;t guarantee global order</strong>, and with competing consumers and retries, messages can be processed out of order even when they arrived in order — two consumers picking up two messages at nearly the same instant have no way to coordinate which one finishes first.</p>\n<p>Strategies:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Partition by key</strong>: Service Bus <strong>sessions</strong> (<code>SessionId = orderId</code>) or Kafka <strong>partition keys</strong> guarantee FIFO <strong>per key</strong> with one consumer per session/partition at a time — ordering is given up globally in exchange for throughput, but preserved exactly where it usually matters: within one entity&#39;s own history.</li>\n<li><strong>Design for disorder</strong>: include a <strong>version or sequence number</strong> or timestamp; ignore stale updates (<code>if (msg.Version &lt;= entity.Version) return;</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Make events commutative</strong> where possible.</li>\n<li>Avoid needing order across different entities at all.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"sagas-and-process-managers\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/messaging-events/sagas-and-process-managers\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Sagas and process managers <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>A business process spanning services (place order → reserve stock → charge payment → ship) can&#39;t use one ACID transaction — exactly the &quot;one transaction modifies one aggregate&quot; rule from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ddd/aggregates-the-most-important-tactical-pattern-and-the-harde\">DDD</a>, now playing out across service boundaries rather than aggregate boundaries. A <strong>saga</strong> is a sequence of <strong>local transactions</strong>, each publishing an event that triggers the next step. If a step fails, <strong>compensating actions</strong> undo the previous steps semantically (refund the payment, release the stock) — there&#39;s no rollback in the database sense, only a deliberate action that reverses the business effect.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Style</th>\n<th>How</th>\n<th>Pros</th>\n<th>Cons</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Choreography</strong></td>\n<td>Each service listens for events and reacts, emitting its own events; no central coordinator</td>\n<td>Loose coupling, simple for short flows</td>\n<td>Flow is implicit and spread across services; hard to see, test and change; risk of cyclic dependencies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Orchestration</strong></td>\n<td>A <strong>saga orchestrator</strong> (state machine) sends commands to each service and reacts to replies</td>\n<td>Explicit flow in one place; easier timeouts, monitoring and compensation</td>\n<td>The orchestrator is a central component to build and run</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Notice the tradeoff is the same one choreography vs orchestration always presents in distributed design: choreography trades visibility for decoupling, orchestration trades a central dependency for the ability to actually <em>see</em> the whole process in one place — invaluable once something has gone wrong at 3 a.m. and you need to know exactly which step the order is stuck on.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Orchestrated order saga (state machine):\n  [Submitted] ──ReserveStock──▶ Inventory ──StockReserved──▶ [StockReserved] ──ChargePayment──▶ Payments\n       │                                                              │\n       │                                         PaymentFailed ◀──────┘\n       ▼                                              │\n  StockReservationFailed → [Rejected]          ReleaseStock (compensate) → [Cancelled]\n                                                PaymentCharged → ShipOrder → [Completed]\n  Timeout after 15 min in any state → compensate → [Cancelled]</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// MassTransit state machine saga (persisted with EF Core)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderSaga</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">MassTransitStateMachine</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderState</span>&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">State</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Reserving</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>!;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">State</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Charging</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>!;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">State</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Completed</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>!;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Event</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderSubmitted</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Submitted</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>!;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Event</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">StockReserved</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Reserved</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>!;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Event</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentFailed</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentFailed</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>!;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Event</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentCharged</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Charged</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>!;\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderSaga</span>()\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">InstanceState</span>(x =&gt; x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CurrentState</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Event</span>(() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Submitted</span>, x =&gt; x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CorrelateById</span>(m =&gt; m.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Message</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>));\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Event</span>(() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Reserved</span>,  x =&gt; x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CorrelateById</span>(m =&gt; m.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Message</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>));\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Event</span>(() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentFailed</span>, x =&gt; x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CorrelateById</span>(m =&gt; m.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Message</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>));\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Event</span>(() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Charged</span>,   x =&gt; x.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CorrelateById</span>(m =&gt; m.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Message</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>));\n\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Initially</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">When</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Submitted</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Then</span>(c =&gt; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Saga</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span> = c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Message</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>)\n            .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Send</span>(c =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReserveStock</span>(c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Saga</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CorrelationId</span>)).<span class=\"tk-ty\">TransitionTo</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Reserving</span>));\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">During</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Reserving</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">When</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Reserved</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Send</span>(c =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargePayment</span>(c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Saga</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CorrelationId</span>, c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Saga</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>)).<span class=\"tk-ty\">TransitionTo</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Charging</span>));\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">During</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Charging</span>,\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">When</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Charged</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Publish</span>(c =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderConfirmed</span>(c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Saga</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CorrelationId</span>)).<span class=\"tk-ty\">TransitionTo</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Completed</span>),\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">When</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentFailed</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Send</span>(c =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReleaseStock</span>(c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Saga</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CorrelationId</span>)).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Finalize</span>());\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice this is exactly the State pattern from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/design-patterns/state\">Design patterns</a>, now coordinating across service boundaries rather than within one class — <code>Reserving</code> and <code>Charging</code> are states, and each <code>Event(...)</code> transition is the same idea as <code>OrderState.Pay()</code> returning a new state, just driven by messages instead of method calls.</p>\n<p>Alternatives for long-running orchestration: <strong>Azure Durable Functions</strong>, <strong>Temporal</strong>, <strong>Dapr Workflows</strong>, NServiceBus sagas.</p>\n<h2 id=\"messaging-libraries-for-net\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/messaging-events/messaging-libraries-for-net\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Messaging libraries for .NET <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Using the raw broker SDKs (<code>Azure.Messaging.ServiceBus</code>, <code>RabbitMQ.Client</code>, <code>Confluent.Kafka</code>) is fine for simple cases. Libraries add the hard parts: serialization, topology, retries, the outbox/inbox, sagas, scheduling, test harnesses and OpenTelemetry.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Library</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>MassTransit</strong></td>\n<td>Most popular; supports RabbitMQ, Service Bus, Amazon SQS, Kafka; sagas; EF outbox. <strong>v9+ is commercial</strong>; v8 remains open source</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>NServiceBus</strong> (Particular)</td>\n<td>Commercial, very mature, excellent tooling (ServicePulse/ServiceInsight)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Wolverine</strong> (JasperFx)</td>\n<td>Mediator plus messaging, with a durable outbox/inbox integrated with Marten/EF Core; MIT</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Rebus</strong>, <strong>Brighter</strong></td>\n<td>Open-source alternatives</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Dapr pub/sub</strong></td>\n<td>A sidecar abstraction over many brokers</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Raw Service Bus SDK (minimal)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> client = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceBusClient</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;shop.servicebus.windows.net&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DefaultAzureCredential</span>());\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> sender = client.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateSender</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;orders&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> sender.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SendMessageAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceBusMessage</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">BinaryData</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromObjectAsJson</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span>(id, total)))\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">MessageId</span> = id.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>(), <span class=\"tk-ty\">Subject</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">nameof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span>), <span class=\"tk-ty\">SessionId</span> = customerId, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CorrelationId</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Activity</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Current</span>?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TraceId</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>()\n});\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> processor = client.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateProcessor</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;orders&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceBusProcessorOptions</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">MaxConcurrentCalls</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">8</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">AutoCompleteMessages</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span> });\nprocessor.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProcessMessageAsync</span> += <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> args =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> evt = args.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Message</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Body</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToObjectFromJson</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span>&gt;();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HandleAsync</span>(evt, args.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> args.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CompleteMessageAsync</span>(args.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Message</span>);                 <span class=\"tk-com\">// ack only after successful processing</span>\n};\nprocessor.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProcessErrorAsync</span> += args =&gt; { logger.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogError</span>(args.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Service Bus error&quot;</span>); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CompletedTask</span>; };\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> processor.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartProcessingAsync</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice <code>AutoCompleteMessages = false</code> and the explicit <code>CompleteMessageAsync</code> call only <em>after</em> <code>HandleAsync</code> succeeds — exactly the deliberate acknowledge-after-processing discipline that makes the &quot;crash before ack → redelivery&quot; failure mode from the delivery-guarantees section above a safe, expected occurrence rather than a lost message.</p>\n<h2 id=\"message-design-and-versioning\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/messaging-events/message-design-and-versioning\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Message design and versioning <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Contracts are APIs</strong>: version them carefully and publish them as a small shared contracts package or a schema (JSON Schema, Avro/Protobuf with a schema registry).</li>\n<li>Include <strong>metadata</strong>: <code>MessageId</code>, <code>CorrelationId</code>/trace context, <code>CausationId</code>, <code>OccurredAt</code>, type and version. CloudEvents is a standard envelope format.</li>\n<li><strong>Evolve compatibly</strong>: add optional fields; never rename or remove fields that consumers use; for breaking changes, publish <strong>v2 alongside v1</strong> until consumers migrate.</li>\n<li><strong>Tolerant readers</strong>: consumers ignore unknown fields and handle missing optional ones.</li>\n<li>Keep messages <strong>small</strong>; for big payloads use the <strong>claim-check</strong> pattern (store the blob, send its reference).</li>\n<li>Don&#39;t leak internal domain models: map <strong>domain events</strong> to stable <strong>integration events</strong>, the exact distinction <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ddd/domain-events-a-record-of-something-that-already-happened\">DDD</a> drew between what an aggregate raises internally and what actually crosses a service boundary.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"event-sourcing\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/messaging-events/event-sourcing\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Event sourcing <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Instead of storing current state, <strong>store the full sequence of events</strong> that led to it. Current state is a <strong>left fold</strong> over the events — a fancy way of saying: start from a blank state and apply each event to it, in order, one at a time, the same way a bank statement&#39;s running balance is just &quot;start at 0, apply every transaction in order.&quot;</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Stream &quot;account-42&quot;:  AccountOpened(€0) → Deposited(€100) → Withdrawn(€30) → Deposited(€50)   ⇒ balance €120</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Benefits</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Complete audit trail and history (&quot;what did we know on 1 March?&quot;)</td>\n<td>Different mental model; harder queries (needs CQRS projections)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rebuild read models or new projections from history</td>\n<td>Event schema evolution (<strong>upcasting</strong>: converting an old event&#39;s saved shape into the current one when you read it back, since you can never change history itself)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Natural fit with event-driven integration</td>\n<td><strong>Snapshots</strong> needed for long streams (a saved current state at some point, so replay starts there instead of from event #1); eventual consistency of projections</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Debugging by replay</td>\n<td>Tooling and team experience required</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>.NET options: <strong>Marten</strong> (event store on PostgreSQL), <strong>EventStoreDB/KurrentDB</strong>, or Cosmos DB with the change feed. Use event sourcing for domains where history <em>is</em> the value (finance, ledgers, compliance, workflows), not as a default — recall from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cqrs-mediator-result/levels-of-cqrs-a-spectrum-not-a-binary-choice\">CQRS &amp; Result</a> that it&#39;s the highest-complexity rung on that ladder for a reason.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why use messaging instead of direct HTTP calls between services?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>It decouples services in time (both needn&#39;t be up), levels load spikes through queues, isolates failures (a down consumer doesn&#39;t break the producer), enables pub/sub fan-out to new consumers without changing producers, and scales via competing consumers. The trade-offs are eventual consistency and operational complexity.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What&#39;s the difference between a queue and a topic?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A queue delivers each message to one consumer among competing consumers, which suits commands and work distribution. A topic delivers a copy to each subscription, which suits events consumed by multiple independent services.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Commands vs events?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A command asks one specific handler to do something (imperative name, can be rejected, sent to a queue). An event announces that something happened (past tense, can&#39;t be rejected, published to any number of subscribers).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the outbox pattern and what problem does it solve?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>It solves the dual-write problem: you can&#39;t atomically commit to the database and publish to a broker. Writing the outgoing message to an outbox table in the same transaction as the business data, then having a relay publish it and mark it processed, guarantees the message is eventually published if and only if the transaction committed (at-least-once).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you make a message consumer idempotent?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Use naturally idempotent operations (upserts, setting state), or record processed message IDs in an inbox table in the same transaction as the handler&#39;s changes (with a unique key) and skip duplicates. Use business-key unique constraints and version checks, and pass idempotency keys to external providers for side effects outside your database.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What delivery guarantee do brokers usually provide?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>At-least-once: messages may be delivered more than once (redelivery after a crash before ack, producer retries, lock expiry). Exactly-once end-to-end isn&#39;t generally achievable, so consumers must be idempotent to achieve effectively-once processing.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is a dead-letter queue?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A secondary queue where messages are moved after exceeding max delivery attempts or failing permanently (poison messages), so they don&#39;t block processing. Monitor it, investigate, fix the cause and resubmit.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is a saga? Orchestration vs choreography?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A saga coordinates a multi-service business transaction as a series of local transactions with compensating actions on failure. In choreography, services react to each other&#39;s events with no central controller (loosely coupled, but the flow is implicit). In orchestration, a state-machine orchestrator sends commands and tracks progress (an explicit, monitorable flow with a central component).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you handle message ordering?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Most brokers don&#39;t guarantee global order, especially with competing consumers. Guarantee order per key with Service Bus sessions or Kafka partitions; otherwise design consumers to tolerate disorder using version numbers or timestamps to discard stale updates.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Service Bus vs Kafka/Event Hubs?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Service Bus is a message broker for business messaging: messages are removed when completed, and it offers sessions, dead-lettering, transactions, scheduling and duplicate detection. Kafka/Event Hubs are distributed logs for high-throughput event streaming: messages are retained and replayable, consumers track offsets, and ordering is per partition.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/messaging-events/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/databases/guide/transactional-outbox-cosmos\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Transactional outbox pattern (Azure Architecture Center)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/reference-architectures/saga/saga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Saga distributed transactions pattern</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/service-bus-messaging/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Azure Service Bus documentation</a></li>\n<li><em>Enterprise Integration Patterns</em> (Hohpe &amp; Woolf): the classic messaging pattern language.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://masstransit.io/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MassTransit documentation</a> · <a href=\"https://wolverinefx.net/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wolverine</a> · <a href=\"https://martendb.io/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Marten event sourcing</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/messaging-events/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (plain console app, .NET 8/9, top-level statements, BCL only). Real brokers, real outboxes and real crash scenarios aren&#39;t runtime-verifiable in a standalone script — the checks below model the underlying logic (dedup, partitioning, folding) that those mechanisms rely on.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: an inbox table makes a duplicate delivery a no-op instead of double-applying the work.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> inbox = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HashSet</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> paymentsCharged = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HandleOrderPlaced</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> messageId)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!inbox.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(messageId)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span>; <span class=\"tk-com\">// already processed: this redelivery is a safe no-op</span>\n    paymentsCharged++;\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> sameMessageId = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NewGuid</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">HandleOrderPlaced</span>(sameMessageId); <span class=\"tk-com\">// first delivery</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">HandleOrderPlaced</span>(sameMessageId); <span class=\"tk-com\">// redelivery after a crash-before-ack</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (paymentsCharged != <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected exactly 1 charge, got {paymentsCharged}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: an inbox of seen message IDs makes a redelivered message a no-op (idempotent consumer)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: a queue's &quot;competing consumers&quot; split work so each message goes to exactly one consumer, none lost, none duplicated.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> messages = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Enumerable</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Range</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToList</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> consumerAInbox = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> consumerBInbox = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; i &lt; messages.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span>; i++)\n    (i % <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span> ? consumerAInbox : consumerBInbox).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(messages[i]); <span class=\"tk-com\">// round-robin stands in for &quot;whoever's free next&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> allProcessed = consumerAInbox.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Concat</span>(consumerBInbox).<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderBy</span>(x =&gt; x).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToList</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!allProcessed.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SequenceEqual</span>(messages)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: every message should be processed exactly once across both consumers&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (consumerAInbox.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Intersect</span>(consumerBInbox).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Any</span>()) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: no message should go to both consumers&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: competing consumers on a queue split the work with no message lost or duplicated&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: with a per-key partition/session, messages for the SAME key stay in order even if the</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// overall arrival order across different keys is shuffled.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> arrivalOrder = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>[]\n{\n    (<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;order-1&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>), (<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;order-2&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>), (<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;order-1&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>), (<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;order-2&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>), (<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;order-1&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>)\n};\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> perKeyOrder = arrivalOrder\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">GroupBy</span>(m =&gt; m.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Item1</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToDictionary</span>(g =&gt; g.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Key</span>, g =&gt; g.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(m =&gt; m.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Item2</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToList</span>());\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!perKeyOrder[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;order-1&quot;</span>].<span class=\"tk-ty\">SequenceEqual</span>([<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>])) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: order-1's own events should stay in sequence 1,2,3&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!perKeyOrder[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;order-2&quot;</span>].<span class=\"tk-ty\">SequenceEqual</span>([<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>])) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: order-2's own events should stay in sequence 1,2&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: partitioning by key preserves per-key order even though global order isn't guaranteed&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: event sourcing's &quot;current state&quot; is a left fold — start from a blank state, apply each event in order.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Balance</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IEnumerable</span>&lt;(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Kind</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Amount</span>)&gt; events) =&gt;\n    events.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Aggregate</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0m</span>, (balance, e) =&gt; e.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Kind</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">switch</span>\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Deposited&quot;</span> =&gt; balance + e.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Amount</span>,\n        <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Withdrawn&quot;</span> =&gt; balance - e.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Amount</span>,\n        _ =&gt; balance\n    });\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> accountEvents = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>[] { (<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Opened&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">0m</span>), (<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Deposited&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">100m</span>), (<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Withdrawn&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">30m</span>), (<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Deposited&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">50m</span>) };\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> finalBalance = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Balance</span>(accountEvents);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (finalBalance != <span class=\"tk-num\">120m</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected balance 120, got {finalBalance}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: replaying events as a left fold (start at 0, apply each in order) gives the correct balance ({finalBalance})&quot;</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"what-putting-a-broker-in-the-middle-actually-buys-you","text":"What putting a broker in the middle actually buys you","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"commands-events-and-queries-three-different-conversations","text":"Commands, events and queries: three different conversations","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"queues-and-topics","text":"Queues and topics","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"brokers-compared","text":"Brokers compared","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"delivery-guarantees-why-exactly-once-isnt-really-on-the-tabl","text":"Delivery guarantees: why \"exactly once\" isn't really on the table","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"the-dual-write-problem-and-the-transactional-outbox","text":"The dual-write problem and the transactional outbox","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"idempotent-consumers-and-the-inbox","text":"Idempotent consumers and the inbox","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"retries-poison-messages-and-dead-lettering","text":"Retries, poison messages and dead-lettering","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"ordering-a-guarantee-most-brokers-deliberately-dont-give-you","text":"Ordering: a guarantee most brokers deliberately don't give you","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"sagas-and-process-managers","text":"Sagas and process managers","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"messaging-libraries-for-net","text":"Messaging libraries for .NET","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"message-design-and-versioning","text":"Message design and versioning","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"event-sourcing","text":"Event sourcing","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":4767,"minutes":22,"num":"7.1","part":"p7"},"resilience":{"id":"resilience","title":"Resilience: Retries, Timeouts, Circuit Breakers and Beyond","summary":"Designing for failure. Transient vs permanent faults, the resilience strategies (timeout, retry with backoff and jitter, circuit breaker, fallback, hedging, rate limiter and bulkhead), Polly v8 and Microsoft.Extensions.Resilience, the standard HTTP resilience handler, ordering strategies correctly, retry storms and idempotency, plus health checks, graceful degradation and chaos testing.","layer":"comms","where":"Service communication › Fault handling","fits":"<p>Resilience wraps every outbound call in layer 6, whether HTTP (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/http-client\">HttpClient</a>), gRPC (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/app-models\">App models</a>), database (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-advanced/transactions-when-savechangess-own-transaction-isnt-enough\">EF Core advanced</a>) or broker (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events\">Messaging &amp; events</a>). It&#39;s what makes microservices (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/microservices\">Microservices</a>) survivable, and what observability (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/observability-aspire\">Observability &amp; Aspire</a>) lets you tune.</p>\n","prereqs":["http-client","async-await"],"next":["observability-aspire","messaging-events"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/resilience/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>In the cloud, failures are <strong>normal</strong>, not exceptional — design for them instead of hoping they won&#39;t happen.</li>\n<li>Match the strategy to the fault: <strong>retry</strong> transient errors, <strong>circuit-break</strong> prolonged outages, <strong>don&#39;t retry</strong> permanent errors (4xx), <strong>timeout</strong> slow calls.</li>\n<li><strong>Backoff + jitter</strong> on retries, and retry at <strong>one layer only</strong> — otherwise retries multiply into a retry storm.</li>\n<li>Only retry <strong>idempotent</strong> operations, or non-idempotent ones with an idempotency key — a blind retry of a POST can double-charge a customer.</li>\n<li><code>Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Resilience</code>&#39;s <code>.AddStandardResilienceHandler()</code> gives you timeout + retry + circuit breaker for HttpClient calls with one line.</li>\n<li><strong>Liveness</strong> checks &quot;is the process alive?&quot;; <strong>readiness</strong> checks &quot;can it serve traffic right now?&quot; — don&#39;t confuse the two, or a dependency blip restarts your whole fleet.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"everything-fails\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/resilience/everything-fails\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Everything fails <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>In the cloud, failure is <strong>normal</strong>: instances restart during deployments, networks blip, load balancers move connections, databases fail over, dependencies throttle you (429), and noisy neighbours cause latency spikes. A resilient system <strong>expects</strong> these and keeps working, perhaps degraded, instead of turning a 2-second blip into an outage.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Fault type</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n<th>Response</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Transient</strong></td>\n<td>Timeouts, connection resets, 503, 429, 408, deadlock victims, SQL failover errors</td>\n<td><strong>Retry</strong> (with backoff), wait for recovery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Prolonged</strong></td>\n<td>A dependency down for minutes</td>\n<td><strong>Stop calling</strong> (circuit breaker), <strong>fallback</strong>, degrade gracefully</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Permanent</strong></td>\n<td>400 Bad Request, 401/403, 404, validation errors, bugs</td>\n<td><strong>Don&#39;t retry</strong>: fail fast, surface the error</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Slowness</strong></td>\n<td>Dependency at 10 s instead of 100 ms</td>\n<td><strong>Timeout</strong>, limit concurrency, hedge</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Notice the table&#39;s shape: each fault type gets a <em>different</em> response, and the rest of this chapter is really a toolbox of strategies, each matched to one row.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-resilience-strategies\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/resilience/the-resilience-strategies\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The resilience strategies <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Strategy</th>\n<th>Problem it solves</th>\n<th>How</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Timeout</strong></td>\n<td>A slow call holds threads, sockets and the user&#39;s patience indefinitely</td>\n<td>Give up after N; cancel the operation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Retry</strong></td>\n<td>Transient faults that succeed on a second try</td>\n<td>Try again, with <strong>exponential backoff and jitter</strong>, a limited number of times</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Circuit breaker</strong></td>\n<td>Hammering a failing dependency makes it worse and wastes your resources</td>\n<td>After a failure threshold, <strong>fail fast</strong> for a break period, then probe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Fallback</strong></td>\n<td>The user should get <em>something</em> when a dependency fails</td>\n<td>Return a cached, default or degraded value</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hedging</strong></td>\n<td>Tail latency: some requests are slow for no reason</td>\n<td>Send a second request after a delay and use whichever responds first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Rate limiter / concurrency limiter</strong></td>\n<td>Protect yourself or a dependency from overload</td>\n<td>Cap requests per period or in flight</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Bulkhead</strong></td>\n<td>One failing dependency exhausting shared resources (threads, connections)</td>\n<td>Isolate resources per dependency (separate pools/limits)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"retry-done-properly\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/resilience/retry-done-properly\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Retry, done properly <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Attempt 1 ── fail ──▶ wait 200ms ±jitter ──▶ Attempt 2 ── fail ──▶ wait 400ms ±jitter ──▶ Attempt 3 ── fail ──▶ wait 800ms ──▶ Attempt 4 → give up</code></pre></figure>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Exponential backoff</strong> gives the dependency time to recover.</li>\n<li><strong>Jitter</strong> (randomising delays) prevents thousands of clients retrying <strong>in lockstep</strong>, the &quot;thundering herd&quot; that hits a recovering service in synchronised waves — the exact same stampede problem <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors/caching-strategies-and-invalidation-the-part-thats-actually-\">Caching, rate limiting &amp; CORS</a> described for expiring cache keys, here showing up as retries instead.</li>\n<li><strong>Limit attempts</strong> (2–5) and the <strong>total time</strong> budget.</li>\n<li><strong>Retry only transient faults</strong>: network exceptions, 5xx (especially 502/503/504), 408, 429. Respect <strong><code>Retry-After</code></strong> headers.</li>\n<li><strong>Retry only idempotent operations</strong> (GET, PUT, DELETE), or non-idempotent ones <strong>with idempotency keys</strong> (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/api-design/idempotency-keys-for-post-making-the-one-unsafe-by-default-m\">API design</a>). A retried POST without a key can charge a card twice.</li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Retry storms and retry amplification</div><p>If every layer retries 3 times (gateway → API → service → database), one failure becomes 3 × 3 × 3 = <strong>27</strong> attempts on the bottom layer, right when it&#39;s struggling. Retry at <strong>one</strong> layer (usually closest to the failing dependency), keep counts low, combine with circuit breakers, and set <strong>timeout budgets</strong> that shrink as calls go deeper.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"circuit-breaker\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/resilience/circuit-breaker\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Circuit breaker <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>            failure ratio &gt; threshold within the sampling window\n   ┌────────┐ ─────────────────────────────────────────────▶ ┌────────┐\n   │ CLOSED │                                                 │  OPEN  │  calls fail immediately\n   │ normal │ ◀──── trial call succeeds ─── ┌───────────┐ ◀─── │ (fast) │  (BrokenCircuitException)\n   └────────┘                               │ HALF-OPEN │      └────────┘\n                                            │ one probe │ ── trial fails ──▶ back to OPEN\n                                            └───────────┘      after the break duration elapses</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Why bother: waiting for every call to a down dependency to time out wastes threads and connections, adds latency for your users, and piles more load onto a service that&#39;s already struggling — the opposite of what it needs to recover. The breaker <strong>fails fast</strong> instead (so you can fall back immediately) and gives the dependency a quiet window to recover. The OPEN state is deliberate rest, not a malfunction.</p>\n<p>Tune: the failure ratio (e.g. 50%), the <strong>minimum throughput</strong> before evaluating (to avoid tripping on 2 of 3 calls), the sampling window, and the break duration. <strong>Log state changes</strong> and expose them as metrics — a circuit flipping open is exactly the kind of event worth paging someone over.</p>\n<h2 id=\"timeouts\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/resilience/timeouts\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Timeouts <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Two levels, and conflating them is a common mistake:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Per-attempt timeout</strong>: how long one try may take (e.g. 2 s).</li>\n<li><strong>Total timeout</strong>: the overall budget including retries (e.g. 10 s), which should fit inside the caller&#39;s own deadline.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Always pass <code>CancellationToken</code>s so timeouts actually <strong>cancel</strong> the underlying work (EF Core commands, HTTP requests) — a timeout that only stops <em>waiting</em> for the result, without actually cancelling the work, still leaves the dependency doing pointless work for a caller that&#39;s already given up. And remember <code>HttpClient.Timeout</code> defaults to 100 seconds, far too long for most calls, exactly as <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/http-client/timeouts-and-cancellation-getting-the-layering-right\">Calling other services</a> warned; with a resilience pipeline, let the pipeline own timeouts instead.</p>\n<h2 id=\"polly-v8-and-microsoft-extensions-resilience\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/resilience/polly-v8-and-microsoft-extensions-resilience\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Polly v8 and Microsoft.Extensions.Resilience <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Polly</strong> is .NET&#39;s resilience library. <strong>v8</strong> introduced <strong>resilience pipelines</strong> with strategies composed in order — notice the word &quot;composed,&quot; because the ordering section below matters as much as which strategies you pick. Microsoft builds on it with <code>Microsoft.Extensions.Resilience</code> (DI integration, telemetry) and <code>Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Resilience</code> (HttpClient integration).</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-standard-http-handler\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/resilience/the-standard-http-handler\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The standard HTTP handler</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddHttpClient</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">CatalogClient</span>&gt;(c =&gt; c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BaseAddress</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://catalog&quot;</span>))\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddStandardResilienceHandler</span>(o =&gt;\n    {\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Retry</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MaxRetryAttempts</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>;\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AttemptTimeout</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Timeout</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSeconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>);\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TotalRequestTimeout</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Timeout</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSeconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>);\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CircuitBreaker</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FailureRatio</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">0.5</span>;\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CircuitBreaker</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SamplingDuration</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSeconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">30</span>);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// must be ≥ 2× attempt timeout</span>\n    });</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The standard handler stacks, from outermost to innermost:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Rate limiter (concurrency) → Total request timeout → Retry → Circuit breaker → Attempt timeout → HTTP call</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Defaults retry transient HTTP failures (5xx, 408, 429, <code>HttpRequestException</code>, timeouts) with exponential backoff and jitter, honouring <code>Retry-After</code>. Also available: <strong><code>AddStandardHedgingHandler()</code></strong>, which sends parallel attempts (optionally to different endpoints) for latency-critical reads — the hedging strategy from the strategies table, now as a drop-in handler.</p>\n<h3 id=\"custom-pipelines\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/resilience/custom-pipelines\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Custom pipelines</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddResiliencePipeline</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;inventory-db&quot;</span>, pipeline =&gt; pipeline\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddTimeout</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSeconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>))\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddRetry</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">RetryStrategyOptions</span>\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShouldHandle</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PredicateBuilder</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Handle</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlException</span>&gt;(ex =&gt; ex.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsTransient</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Handle</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeoutException</span>&gt;(),\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">MaxRetryAttempts</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>,\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">BackoffType</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">DelayBackoffType</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exponential</span>,\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">UseJitter</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>,\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">Delay</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromMilliseconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">200</span>),\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">OnRetry</span> = args =&gt; { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Log</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Warning</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Retry {Attempt} after {Delay}&quot;</span>, args.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AttemptNumber</span>, args.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RetryDelay</span>); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">default</span>; }\n    })\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddCircuitBreaker</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CircuitBreakerStrategyOptions</span>\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">FailureRatio</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">0.5</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">MinimumThroughput</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">SamplingDuration</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSeconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">30</span>), <span class=\"tk-ty\">BreakDuration</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSeconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">15</span>)\n    }));\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">StockReader</span>(<span class=\"tk-attr\">[FromKeyedServices(&quot;inventory-db&quot;)]</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ResiliencePipeline</span> pipeline, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IStockRepository</span> repo)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> sku, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt;\n        pipeline.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> token =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> repo.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetQuantityAsync</span>(sku, token), ct);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Typed result with fallback</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> withFallback = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ResiliencePipelineBuilder</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>?&gt;()\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddFallback</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FallbackStrategyOptions</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>?&gt;\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShouldHandle</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PredicateBuilder</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>?&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Handle</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">BrokenCircuitException</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Handle</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeoutRejectedException</span>&gt;(),\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">FallbackAction</span> = _ =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Outcome</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromResultAsValueTask</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>?&gt;(<span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>)      <span class=\"tk-com\">// e.g. &quot;price unavailable&quot;</span>\n    })\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddTimeout</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSeconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>))\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h3 id=\"strategy-order-matters\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/resilience/strategy-order-matters\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Strategy order matters</h3>\n<p>Strategies execute <strong>outermost first</strong> — the exact same nested-wrapping idea from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/middleware/now-heres-the-aha-a-middleware-is-just-a-function-wrapping-a\">Middleware</a>, now composing fault-handling strategies instead of HTTP concerns. Typical rules:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>total timeout outside the retry</strong> bounds the whole operation; an <strong>attempt timeout inside the retry</strong> bounds each try.</li>\n<li>A <strong>circuit breaker inside the retry</strong> means each attempt is counted, and when the circuit is open, retries can stop quickly (handle <code>BrokenCircuitException</code> as non-retryable or with a delay).</li>\n<li><strong>Fallback outermost</strong>, so it catches everything that fails below it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"older-polly-syntax\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/resilience/older-polly-syntax\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Older Polly syntax</h3>\n<p>Pre-v8 code looks like <code>Policy.Handle&lt;HttpRequestException&gt;().WaitAndRetryAsync(...)</code>, <code>Policy.WrapAsync(...)</code> and <code>AddPolicyHandler(...)</code> (Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Polly, now deprecated in favour of Http.Resilience). Same concepts, different API.</p>\n<h2 id=\"resilience-beyond-http\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/resilience/resilience-beyond-http\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Resilience beyond HTTP <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Dependency</th>\n<th>Built-in resilience</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>EF Core / SQL</td>\n<td><code>EnableRetryOnFailure()</code> execution strategy (wrap manual transactions with <code>CreateExecutionStrategy</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Azure SDK clients</td>\n<td>Built-in retry policies (<code>ClientOptions.Retry</code>: exponential, max retries, network timeout)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Service Bus / brokers</td>\n<td>Delivery retries, delayed redelivery, dead-lettering (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events/retries-poison-messages-and-dead-lettering\">Messaging</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Redis (StackExchange.Redis)</td>\n<td>Reconnect/backoff, <code>AbortOnConnectFail = false</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>gRPC</td>\n<td>Built-in retry and hedging policies in the channel&#39;s service config, or the resilience handler</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"designing-for-graceful-degradation\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/resilience/designing-for-graceful-degradation\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Designing for graceful degradation <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Resilience isn&#39;t only about libraries. Design so partial failure produces a <strong>degraded but useful</strong> experience, rather than an outright outage:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Fallbacks:</strong> show cached recommendations, hide the reviews widget, use the last known exchange rate (and say so).</li>\n<li><strong>Asynchronous by default:</strong> accept the order and process payment via a queue, so checkout works while Payments is down — the &quot;fault isolation&quot; benefit <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events/what-putting-a-broker-in-the-middle-actually-buys-you\">Messaging &amp; events</a> described, applied as a deliberate resilience tactic rather than just an architectural nicety.</li>\n<li><strong>Bulkheads:</strong> separate HttpClient, connection pool and concurrency limits per dependency, so a slow reporting service can&#39;t starve checkout. Separate deployments for critical and non-critical workloads.</li>\n<li><strong>Load shedding:</strong> reject excess work early with 429/503 (rate limiter, concurrency limiter) instead of collapsing under it.</li>\n<li><strong>Static stability:</strong> keep working with the last known good configuration or data if the control plane (config service, identity provider metadata) is unavailable.</li>\n<li><strong>Timeouts everywhere</strong>, with budgets that decrease along the call chain.</li>\n<li><strong>Idempotency everywhere</strong>, so retries are safe.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"health-checks\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/resilience/health-checks\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Health checks <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Health endpoints let orchestrators and load balancers route around unhealthy instances.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddHealthChecks</span>()\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddCheck</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;self&quot;</span>, () =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">HealthCheckResult</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Healthy</span>(), tags: [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;live&quot;</span>])\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSqlServer</span>(cs, name: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;sql&quot;</span>, tags: [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ready&quot;</span>])                         <span class=\"tk-com\">// AspNetCore.HealthChecks.* packages</span>\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddRedis</span>(redisCs, name: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;redis&quot;</span>, tags: [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ready&quot;</span>])\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAzureServiceBusQueue</span>(sbNamespace, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;orders&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DefaultAzureCredential</span>(), tags: [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ready&quot;</span>]);\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapHealthChecks</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/health/live&quot;</span>,  <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>() { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Predicate</span> = r =&gt; r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Tags</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contains</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;live&quot;</span>) });    <span class=\"tk-com\">// is the process OK? (restart if not)</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapHealthChecks</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/health/ready&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>() { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Predicate</span> = r =&gt; r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Tags</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contains</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ready&quot;</span>) });   <span class=\"tk-com\">// can it serve traffic? (remove from LB if not)</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Don&#39;t put dependencies in liveness checks</div><p>If liveness checks the database and the database blips, the orchestrator <strong>restarts every instance</strong>, turning a dependency hiccup into a full outage. Liveness = &quot;the process isn&#39;t stuck&quot;. Readiness = &quot;dependencies are reachable, warm-up is done&quot;.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"chaos-engineering\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/resilience/chaos-engineering\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Chaos engineering <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Verify resilience by <strong>injecting faults on purpose</strong>: latency, exceptions, error responses, killed instances. It&#39;s the only reliable way to know the strategies above actually work the way you designed them — rather than hoping they do and finding out during a real outage.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Polly chaos strategies</strong> (v8.3+): <code>AddChaosLatency</code>, <code>AddChaosFault</code>, <code>AddChaosOutcome</code> and <code>AddChaosBehavior</code> in pipelines, enabled by configuration in test environments.</li>\n<li><strong>Azure Chaos Studio</strong>: platform-level faults (VM shutdown, network latency, AKS pod chaos).</li>\n<li>Game days: planned exercises where the team breaks things in staging and observes alerts, dashboards and recovery.</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the difference between transient and non-transient faults?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Transient faults are temporary and likely to succeed on retry: timeouts, connection resets, 503/429, SQL failovers, deadlocks. Non-transient faults won&#39;t fix themselves (400, 401/403, 404, validation errors, bugs), so retrying wastes resources and they should fail fast.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why use exponential backoff with jitter for retries?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Backoff spaces retries out so a struggling dependency has time to recover. Jitter randomises the delays so many clients don&#39;t retry simultaneously in synchronised waves (thundering herd) that keep the dependency overloaded.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How does a circuit breaker work?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Closed: calls flow and failures are tracked. When the failure ratio exceeds a threshold (with a minimum throughput) within a sampling window, it opens: calls fail immediately for a break duration. Then it goes half-open and allows a trial call; success closes it, failure re-opens it. It prevents wasting resources on, and piling load onto, a failing dependency.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>When is it unsafe to retry a request?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>When the operation isn&#39;t idempotent (e.g. POST creating a payment) and has no idempotency key, because the original may have succeeded despite the error and a retry would duplicate it. Also for permanent errors like 400 or 401.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is a retry storm and how do you avoid it?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Retries at multiple layers multiplying load on a failing dependency (3 retries at 3 layers = 27 attempts), worsening the outage. Avoid it by retrying at one layer only, keeping attempt counts low, using jitter, adding circuit breakers, and propagating timeout budgets and cancellation.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you add resilience to HttpClient in modern .NET?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Resilience: <code>.AddStandardResilienceHandler()</code> on the IHttpClientFactory registration gives a rate limiter, total timeout, retries with backoff and jitter, a circuit breaker and per-attempt timeouts with sensible defaults. Use AddResilienceHandler for a custom Polly v8 pipeline, or AddStandardHedgingHandler for hedging.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the bulkhead pattern?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Isolating resources (connection pools, thread or concurrency limits, separate instances) per dependency or workload, so a failure or slowdown in one can&#39;t exhaust resources needed by others, like watertight compartments in a ship.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Liveness vs readiness health checks?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Liveness tells the orchestrator whether the process is healthy or must be restarted, and should not depend on external services. Readiness tells the load balancer whether the instance can currently serve traffic (dependencies reachable, warmed up); failing readiness removes it from rotation without restarting it.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/resilience/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/resilience/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Introduction to resilient app development (.NET)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/resilience/http-resilience\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Build resilient HTTP apps</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.pollydocs.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Polly documentation</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/host-and-deploy/health-checks\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Health checks in ASP.NET Core</a></li>\n<li>Michael Nygard, <em>Release It!</em> (2nd ed.): the book that popularised circuit breakers and bulkheads.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/well-architected/reliability/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Azure Well-Architected Framework: Reliability</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/resilience/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with: <code>dotnet new console -o resiliencecheck &amp;&amp; cd resiliencecheck &amp;&amp; dotnet add package Polly.Core</code>, then replace <code>Program.cs</code> with the code below and <code>dotnet run</code>. This uses real Polly v8 pipelines — the chapter&#39;s actual subject — not a simulation.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Polly</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Polly</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CircuitBreaker</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Polly</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Retry</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 1) Retry: succeeds on the 3rd attempt after two transient failures.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> attempt = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> retryPipeline = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ResiliencePipelineBuilder</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddRetry</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">RetryStrategyOptions</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShouldHandle</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PredicateBuilder</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Handle</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>&gt;(),\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">MaxRetryAttempts</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>,\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Delay</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromMilliseconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>),\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">BackoffType</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">DelayBackoffType</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Constant</span>\n}).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> result = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> retryPipeline.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> _ =&gt;\n{\n    attempt++;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (attempt &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;transient&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromResult</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ok&quot;</span>);\n});\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (result != <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ok&quot;</span> || attempt != <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected success on attempt 3, got result={result} attempt={attempt}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: retry pipeline succeeded on attempt {attempt} after transient failures, as the backoff-and-retry section describes.&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 2) Retry gives up (and rethrows) once MaxRetryAttempts is exhausted — it doesn't retry forever.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> alwaysFailAttempts = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> retryPipeline.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;(_ =&gt;\n    {\n        alwaysFailAttempts++;\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;still failing&quot;</span>);\n    });\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected the pipeline to eventually rethrow&quot;</span>);\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (alwaysFailAttempts != <span class=\"tk-num\">4</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected 4 total attempts (1 + 3 retries), got {alwaysFailAttempts}&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: retry stopped after {alwaysFailAttempts} attempts (1 original + MaxRetryAttempts=3) and surfaced the failure, instead of retrying forever.&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 3) Circuit breaker: opens after the failure threshold, then fails fast (BrokenCircuitException)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//    without even calling the inner action — &quot;fail fast instead of waiting for every call to time out&quot;.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> innerCallsAfterOpen = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> breakerPipeline = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ResiliencePipelineBuilder</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddCircuitBreaker</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CircuitBreakerStrategyOptions</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">FailureRatio</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">0.5</span>,\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">MinimumThroughput</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>,\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">SamplingDuration</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSeconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>),\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">BreakDuration</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSeconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>),\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShouldHandle</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PredicateBuilder</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Handle</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>&gt;()\n}).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>();\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; i &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>; i++)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> breakerPipeline.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;(_ =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;dependency down&quot;</span>)); }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>) { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* expected: feeding failures to trip the breaker */</span> }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> breakerPipeline.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExecuteAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;(_ =&gt; { innerCallsAfterOpen++; <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValueTask</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromResult</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;should not run&quot;</span>); });\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected BrokenCircuitException once the circuit is open&quot;</span>);\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">BrokenCircuitException</span>)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (innerCallsAfterOpen != <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: the inner action ran even though the circuit was open&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: once open, the circuit breaker rejected the call immediately without invoking the dependency at all.&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claims NOT verified here (need real network faults, multiple processes, or a running orchestrator):</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - Jitter actually preventing a thundering herd across many real clients — see Further reading: https://www.pollydocs.org/</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - The standard HTTP resilience handler's exact strategy stacking over a real HttpClient — see Further reading: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/resilience/http-resilience</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - Liveness/readiness health check behavior under a real orchestrator (Kubernetes/App Service) — see Further reading: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/host-and-deploy/health-checks</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All runtime-checkable claims verified.&quot;</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"everything-fails","text":"Everything fails","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"the-resilience-strategies","text":"The resilience strategies","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"retry-done-properly","text":"Retry, done properly","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"circuit-breaker","text":"Circuit breaker","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"timeouts","text":"Timeouts","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"polly-v8-and-microsoft-extensions-resilience","text":"Polly v8 and Microsoft.Extensions.Resilience","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"the-standard-http-handler","text":"The standard HTTP handler","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"custom-pipelines","text":"Custom pipelines","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"strategy-order-matters","text":"Strategy order matters","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"older-polly-syntax","text":"Older Polly syntax","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"resilience-beyond-http","text":"Resilience beyond HTTP","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"designing-for-graceful-degradation","text":"Designing for graceful degradation","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"health-checks","text":"Health checks","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"chaos-engineering","text":"Chaos engineering","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":3002,"minutes":14,"num":"7.2","part":"p7"},"observability-aspire":{"id":"observability-aspire","title":"Observability, OpenTelemetry and .NET Aspire","summary":"Knowing what your system is doing in production. Monitoring vs observability, the three signals (logs, metrics, traces), how .NET emits them (ILogger, Meter, ActivitySource), OpenTelemetry setup and exporters, distributed tracing and context propagation, custom metrics and spans, Application Insights and Azure Monitor, alerting and SLOs, and .NET Aspire for orchestration, service defaults and the dashboard.","layer":"cross","where":"Cross-cutting › Observability","fits":"<p>Observability is a layer-8 concern instrumenting every layer: the ASP.NET Core pipeline (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/middleware\">Middleware</a>), HttpClient (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/http-client\">HttpClient</a>), EF Core (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-querying\">EF Core querying</a>), messaging (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events\">Messaging &amp; events</a>) and the runtime itself (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/memory-gc\">Memory &amp; GC</a>). Logging basics are in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/errors-logging\">Errors &amp; logging</a>; in the cloud, the data lands in Azure Monitor (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cloud-azure-overview\">Cloud &amp; Azure overview</a>).</p>\n","prereqs":["errors-logging","http-client"],"next":["performance-engineering","cloud-azure-overview","kubernetes"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/observability-aspire/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Monitoring</strong> answers questions you thought to ask ahead of time (dashboards, alerts); <strong>observability</strong> lets you answer questions you didn&#39;t anticipate, from the data your system already emits.</li>\n<li>The three signals — <strong>logs</strong> (what happened), <strong>metrics</strong> (how much/how often, cheap to query), <strong>traces</strong> (where did the time go for <em>this</em> request) — are most useful linked together by a shared <strong>TraceId</strong>.</li>\n<li>.NET&#39;s built-in APIs (<code>ILogger</code>, <code>Meter</code>, <code>ActivitySource</code>) are already OpenTelemetry-shaped; the <strong>OpenTelemetry SDK</strong> just listens to them and exports to whatever backend you choose (Azure Monitor, Grafana, Jaeger, …).</li>\n<li>A <strong>trace</strong> is one TraceId shared across every <strong>span</strong> (unit of work) in a request, even across service boundaries — the <strong>W3C <code>traceparent</code> header</strong> is how that ID survives an HTTP call to another service.</li>\n<li>Keep metric tags <strong>low-cardinality</strong> (status code, route) — a tag like <code>userId</code> or <code>orderId</code> creates a new time series per value and can blow up cost; put high-cardinality IDs on traces and logs instead.</li>\n<li><strong>.NET Aspire</strong> is a development/deployment toolkit (an AppHost to run your whole system locally with one command, shared service defaults, a local dashboard) — it is not something you ship as a production runtime.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"monitoring-vs-observability-knowing-the-answer-vs-being-able\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/observability-aspire/monitoring-vs-observability-knowing-the-answer-vs-being-able\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Monitoring vs observability: knowing the answer vs being able to find it <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Monitoring</strong> answers questions you <strong>knew to ask in advance</strong>: is CPU above 80%? Is the error rate above 1%? It drives dashboards and alerts.</li>\n<li><strong>Observability</strong> is the ability to answer <strong>new questions</strong> about the system&#39;s internal state from its outputs, without shipping new code: &quot;Why are checkout requests from mobile users in Germany slow since 14:05?&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In a distributed system, where one click touches 8 services, observability isn&#39;t optional — nobody could have pre-built a dashboard for every possible question an incident might raise, and that&#39;s exactly the gap observability closes. Without it, every incident turns into hours of guesswork.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-three-signals\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/observability-aspire/the-three-signals\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The three signals <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Signal</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>Answers</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Logs</strong></td>\n<td>Timestamped records of discrete events, ideally structured</td>\n<td><em>What happened, in detail?</em></td>\n<td><code>Payment declined for order 1042: insufficient_funds</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Metrics</strong></td>\n<td>Numeric measurements aggregated over time (cheap to store, fast to query)</td>\n<td><em>How much, how often, how fast, trending?</em></td>\n<td><code>http.server.request.duration</code> p95 = 420 ms; 12 errors/s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Traces</strong></td>\n<td>The path of <strong>one request</strong> through the system: a tree of timed <strong>spans</strong></td>\n<td><em>Where did the time go? Which service failed?</em></td>\n<td>Checkout 1.8 s = API 30 ms → Orders 1.6 s → SQL 1.5 s</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Plus, increasingly, <strong>profiles</strong> (continuous CPU and memory profiling) and <strong>events/exceptions</strong>.</p>\n<p>They&#39;re most powerful <strong>correlated</strong>: a metric spike → exemplar traces from that spike → the logs of those traces, all linked by <strong>TraceId</strong>. Each signal alone answers a narrower question than the chain of all three together — a metric tells you <em>that</em> something&#39;s wrong, a trace tells you <em>where</em>, and the logs for that trace tell you <em>why</em>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-net-emits-telemetry\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/observability-aspire/how-net-emits-telemetry\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>How .NET emits telemetry <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>.NET has <strong>vendor-neutral APIs built into the BCL</strong>. Libraries emit telemetry through them, and you choose where it goes — the emitting code never needs to know or care which backend eventually receives it.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Signal</th>\n<th>.NET API</th>\n<th>Built-in sources</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Logs</td>\n<td><code>ILogger</code> (Microsoft.Extensions.Logging)</td>\n<td>ASP.NET Core, EF Core, HttpClient, your code</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metrics</td>\n<td><code>System.Diagnostics.Metrics</code>: <code>Meter</code>, <code>Counter&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>Histogram&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>UpDownCounter&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>ObservableGauge&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td><code>Microsoft.AspNetCore.Hosting</code> (request duration), <code>System.Net.Http</code>, <code>System.Runtime</code> (GC, thread pool, exceptions), Kestrel, EF Core</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Traces</td>\n<td><code>System.Diagnostics.ActivitySource</code> / <code>Activity</code> (= an OpenTelemetry span)</td>\n<td>ASP.NET Core (incoming requests), HttpClient (outgoing), SqlClient, EF Core, Azure SDKs, gRPC, MassTransit</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>OpenTelemetry (OTel)</strong> is the CNCF standard for collecting and exporting telemetry. The <strong>OpenTelemetry .NET SDK</strong> listens to those built-in APIs and exports via <strong>OTLP</strong> (the OpenTelemetry Protocol) to any backend: Azure Monitor/Application Insights, Grafana (Tempo, Loki, Prometheus/Mimir), Jaeger, Zipkin, Datadog, Honeycomb, New Relic, Elastic, or the Aspire dashboard.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Your code + libraries ──(ILogger · Meter · ActivitySource)──▶ OpenTelemetry SDK ──OTLP──▶ Collector (optional) ──▶ backend(s)\n                                                                processors, sampling            batching, filtering, routing</code></pre></figure>\n<h2 id=\"setting-up-opentelemetry\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/observability-aspire/setting-up-opentelemetry\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Setting up OpenTelemetry <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddOpenTelemetry</span>()\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">ConfigureResource</span>(r =&gt; r\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddService</span>(serviceName: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;shop-api&quot;</span>, serviceVersion: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;1.4.2&quot;</span>)\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAttributes</span>([<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;deployment.environment&quot;</span>, builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">EnvironmentName</span>)]))\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithTracing</span>(t =&gt; t\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAspNetCoreInstrumentation</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Filter</span> = ctx =&gt; !ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Request</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Path</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartsWithSegments</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/health&quot;</span>))\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddHttpClientInstrumentation</span>()\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddEntityFrameworkCoreInstrumentation</span>()        <span class=\"tk-com\">// or AddSqlClientInstrumentation</span>\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSource</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop.*&quot;</span>)                            <span class=\"tk-com\">// your own ActivitySources</span>\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSource</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;MassTransit&quot;</span>)\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddOtlpExporter</span>())\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithMetrics</span>(m =&gt; m\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAspNetCoreInstrumentation</span>()\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddHttpClientInstrumentation</span>()\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddRuntimeInstrumentation</span>()                    <span class=\"tk-com\">// GC, thread pool, exceptions, JIT</span>\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddMeter</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop.*&quot;</span>)\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddOtlpExporter</span>())\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithLogging</span>(l =&gt; l.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddOtlpExporter</span>());             <span class=\"tk-com\">// ILogger → OTLP, with TraceId/SpanId attached</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Or, for Azure: the Azure Monitor OpenTelemetry distro does all of the above and exports to Application Insights</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// builder.Services.AddOpenTelemetry().UseAzureMonitor();   // connection string from APPLICATIONINSIGHTS_CONNECTION_STRING</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Exporter endpoints come from standard environment variables: <code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT</code>, <code>OTEL_SERVICE_NAME</code>, <code>OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"distributed-tracing\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/observability-aspire/distributed-tracing\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Distributed tracing <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>A <strong>trace</strong> is identified by a <strong>TraceId</strong> shared by every <strong>span</strong> in it. Each span has a SpanId, a parent SpanId, a name, a start and end time, <strong>attributes</strong> (tags), <strong>events</strong> and a <strong>status</strong>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>TraceId 4bf92f35…                                                    0ms ───────────────────────────── 1820ms\n└─ POST /api/orders                       shop-api        [██████████████████████████████████████████████████]\n   ├─ SELECT Customers…                   shop-api/sql     [██]\n   ├─ POST http://pricing/quote           shop-api→pricing   [████████]\n   │  └─ GET /quote                       pricing-svc         [███████]\n   │     └─ redis GET price:KB-01         pricing-svc          [█]\n   ├─ INSERT Orders; INSERT Outbox        shop-api/sql                 [██████████████████████████████████]  ← the slow part\n   └─ publish OrderPlaced                 shop-api/servicebus                                             [█]\n      └─ consume OrderPlaced              billing-svc (async, linked)                                        [████]</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Notice exactly what this tree makes obvious at a glance: of the 1.8-second checkout, the vast majority is one single SQL span — not the pricing call, not the message publish. Without a trace like this, &quot;checkout is slow&quot; would have sent you investigating five different services before finding the one that actually mattered.</p>\n<p><strong>Context propagation</strong> carries the trace across process boundaries using the <strong>W3C Trace Context</strong> header:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>HTTP</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-ty\">traceparent</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">00</span>-4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736-00f067aa0ba902b7-<span class=\"tk-num\">01</span>\n             version-traceid-parentspanid-flags(sampled)\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">tracestate</span>: vendor-specific data\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">baggage</span>: <span class=\"tk-ty\">tenant</span>=acme,<span class=\"tk-ty\">userTier</span>=gold          (optional key/values propagated to downstream services)</code></pre></figure>\n<p>ASP.NET Core reads it on incoming requests; HttpClient and gRPC write it on outgoing calls automatically; messaging libraries put it in message headers. <strong><code>Activity.Current</code></strong> flows through async calls (via <code>AsyncLocal</code>) — the exact same ambient-flow mechanism <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/errors-logging/log-scopes-attaching-context-to-everything-inside-a-block\">Errors &amp; logging</a> relied on for log scopes — so logs written anywhere during a request carry its TraceId and SpanId automatically, with no manual plumbing.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sampling\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/observability-aspire/sampling\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Sampling</h3>\n<p>Recording every span of every request is expensive at scale. <strong>Head sampling</strong> decides at the start (e.g. keep 10%, <code>TraceIdRatioBasedSampler</code>); <strong>tail sampling</strong> (in the OpenTelemetry Collector) decides after the trace completes, which lets you <strong>keep all errors and slow traces</strong> and sample the rest — precisely the traces you&#39;d actually want during an incident, kept disproportionately more often than the boring, fast, successful ones. Always keep metrics unsampled: they&#39;re aggregates, and sampling an aggregate just makes it a less accurate aggregate for no real cost saving.</p>\n<h2 id=\"custom-spans-and-metrics\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/observability-aspire/custom-spans-and-metrics\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Custom spans and metrics <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CheckoutTelemetry</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ActivitySource</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Source</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop.Checkout&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;1.0&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Meter</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Meter</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop.Checkout&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;1.0&quot;</span>);\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Counter</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">long</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrdersPlaced</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Meter</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateCounter</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">long</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;shop.orders.placed&quot;</span>, unit: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;{order}&quot;</span>, description: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Orders successfully placed&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Histogram</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderValue</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Meter</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateHistogram</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;shop.order.value&quot;</span>, unit: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;EUR&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">UpDownCounter</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">ActiveCheckouts</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Meter</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateUpDownCounter</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;shop.checkouts.active&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Result</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>&gt;&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">CheckoutAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cart</span> cart, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> activity = <span class=\"tk-ty\">CheckoutTelemetry</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Source</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartActivity</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;checkout&quot;</span>);          <span class=\"tk-com\">// null if nobody is listening: cheap</span>\n    activity?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SetTag</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;cart.items&quot;</span>, cart.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lines</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span>);\n    activity?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SetTag</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;customer.tier&quot;</span>, cart.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerTier</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">CheckoutTelemetry</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ActiveCheckouts</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span>\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> result = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PlaceOrderAsync</span>(cart, ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (result.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsSuccess</span>)\n        {\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">CheckoutTelemetry</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrdersPlaced</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">KeyValuePair</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>?&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;payment.method&quot;</span>, cart.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentMethod</span>));\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">CheckoutTelemetry</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderValue</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Record</span>((<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span>)cart.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Total</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Amount</span>);\n        }\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">else</span> activity?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SetStatus</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ActivityStatusCode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span>, result.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Code</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> result;\n    }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span> ex)\n    {\n        activity?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddException</span>(ex);\n        activity?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SetStatus</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ActivityStatusCode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Error</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span>;\n    }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">finally</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">CheckoutTelemetry</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ActiveCheckouts</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(-<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>); }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Instrument</th>\n<th>Use for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>Counter&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Monotonic counts: requests, orders, errors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>UpDownCounter&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Values that go up and down: queue depth, active connections</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Histogram&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Distributions: durations, sizes, amounts (gives percentiles)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ObservableGauge&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Sampled current values via a callback: cache size, pool usage</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Cardinality</div><p>Every unique combination of tag values creates a new time series. Tagging a metric with <code>userId</code>, <code>orderId</code> or full URLs creates <strong>millions of series</strong> and huge bills or crashed backends. Use <strong>low-cardinality</strong> tags on metrics (status code, route template, payment method, region). Put high-cardinality data (IDs) on <strong>traces and logs</strong>.</p>\n</aside>\n<p>Notice <code>CheckoutTelemetry.OrdersPlaced</code> tags by <code>payment.method</code> — a handful of distinct values — rather than, say, <code>customer.id</code>, which would create one time series per customer and quietly violate exactly the cardinality rule above.</p>\n<p>Follow the <strong>OpenTelemetry semantic conventions</strong> for names and attributes (<code>http.request.method</code>, <code>http.response.status_code</code>, <code>db.system</code>, <code>messaging.system</code>), so backends can build standard views.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-to-measure-red-use-and-the-golden-signals\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/observability-aspire/what-to-measure-red-use-and-the-golden-signals\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What to measure: RED, USE and the golden signals <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>For</th>\n<th>Measure</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>RED</strong></td>\n<td>Request-driven services (APIs)</td>\n<td><strong>R</strong>ate (requests/s), <strong>E</strong>rrors (failed/s or %), <strong>D</strong>uration (latency percentiles)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>USE</strong></td>\n<td>Resources (CPU, memory, pools, queues)</td>\n<td><strong>U</strong>tilisation, <strong>S</strong>aturation (queueing), <strong>E</strong>rrors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Four golden signals</strong> (Google SRE)</td>\n<td>Any service</td>\n<td>Latency, traffic, errors, saturation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Use <strong>percentiles (p50, p95, p99)</strong>, not averages: an average of 100 ms can hide 5% of users waiting 4 seconds — the average is dragged down by the many fast requests while saying nothing about the tail that&#39;s actually painful for real users. For .NET specifically, also watch <strong>GC pause time and heap size</strong>, <strong>thread pool queue length and thread count</strong> (starvation; see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/async-await/deadlocks-and-thread-pool-starvation\">async/await</a>), <strong>exception rate</strong>, <strong>connection pool usage</strong>, and <strong>outbox/queue lag</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"alerting-and-slos\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/observability-aspire/alerting-and-slos\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Alerting and SLOs <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SLI</strong> (indicator): a measured ratio, e.g. &quot;share of checkout requests completing in under 1 s with a 2xx/4xx status&quot;.</li>\n<li><strong>SLO</strong> (objective): the target, e.g. &quot;99.5% over 30 days&quot;.</li>\n<li><strong>Error budget</strong>: the 0.5% you&#39;re allowed to miss. When it&#39;s spent, prioritise reliability over features.</li>\n<li><strong>Alert on symptoms users feel</strong> (SLO burn rate, error rate, latency), not on every cause (one CPU spike). Every page should be <strong>actionable</strong>, and link to a dashboard and a runbook.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"application-insights-and-azure-monitor\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/observability-aspire/application-insights-and-azure-monitor\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Application Insights and Azure Monitor <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Azure Monitor</strong> is Azure&#39;s observability platform; <strong>Application Insights</strong> is its APM feature for applications; <strong>Log Analytics</strong> workspaces store the data, queried with <strong>KQL</strong> (Kusto Query Language).</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>// Slowest endpoints in the last hour (KQL)\nrequests\n| where timestamp &gt; ago(1h)\n| summarize count(), p95 = percentile(duration, 95), failures = countif(success == false) by name\n| order by p95 desc\n\n// Everything for one trace\nunion requests, dependencies, traces, exceptions\n| where operation_Id == &quot;4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736&quot;\n| order by timestamp asc</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Features: the <strong>application map</strong> (auto-discovered dependency topology), <strong>transaction search</strong> and end-to-end transaction views, <strong>live metrics</strong>, failures and performance blades, <strong>availability tests</strong> (synthetic pings), smart detection, the profiler and snapshot debugger, workbooks and alerts. Use the <strong>Azure Monitor OpenTelemetry Distro</strong> (<code>UseAzureMonitor()</code>) in new code; the classic Application Insights SDK is in maintenance.</p>\n<h2 id=\"net-aspire\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/observability-aspire/net-aspire\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>.NET Aspire <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>.NET Aspire</strong> is an opinionated toolkit for building <strong>observable, production-ready distributed apps</strong>. It covers three things:</p>\n<h3 id=\"1-orchestration-the-apphost\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/observability-aspire/1-orchestration-the-apphost\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>1. Orchestration: the AppHost</h3>\n<p>A C# project that <strong>describes your whole system</strong> and runs it locally with one command — a single source of truth for &quot;what does this system consist of,&quot; instead of a docker-compose file, a README, and tribal knowledge about which environment variables to set:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Shop.AppHost/Program.cs</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> builder = <span class=\"tk-ty\">DistributedApplication</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateBuilder</span>(args);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> sql = builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSqlServer</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;sql&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithDataVolume</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddDatabase</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;shopdb&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> redis = builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddRedis</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;cache&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithRedisInsight</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> bus = builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddRabbitMQ</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;bus&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithManagementPlugin</span>();       <span class=\"tk-com\">// or AddAzureServiceBus(...).RunAsEmulator()</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> catalog = builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddProject</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Projects</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Shop_Catalog</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;catalog&quot;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithReference</span>(sql).<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithReference</span>(redis).<span class=\"tk-ty\">WaitFor</span>(sql);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> api = builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddProject</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Projects</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Shop_Api</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;api&quot;</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithReference</span>(sql).<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithReference</span>(redis).<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithReference</span>(bus).<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithReference</span>(catalog)   <span class=\"tk-com\">// injects connection strings + service discovery</span>\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithExternalHttpEndpoints</span>()\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">WaitFor</span>(sql);\n\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddProject</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Projects</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Shop_Worker</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;worker&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithReference</span>(bus).<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithReference</span>(sql);\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddNpmApp</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;web&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;../shop-web&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithReference</span>(api).<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithHttpEndpoint</span>(env: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;PORT&quot;</span>);\n\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Run</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><code>dotnet run --project Shop.AppHost</code> starts the containers (SQL, Redis, RabbitMQ), all the projects and the front end, <strong>wires connection strings and service URLs</strong> as configuration/environment variables, and opens the <strong>dashboard</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-service-defaults\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/observability-aspire/2-service-defaults\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>2. Service defaults</h3>\n<p>A shared <code>ServiceDefaults</code> project that every service calls with <code>builder.AddServiceDefaults()</code>. It configures, in one place: <strong>OpenTelemetry</strong> (logs, metrics, traces with OTLP export), <strong>health checks</strong> (<code>/health</code>, <code>/alive</code>), <strong>service discovery</strong>, and <strong>HttpClient resilience</strong> defaults — notice this is, concretely, everything the rest of this chapter and <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/resilience\">Resilience</a> described, pre-wired consistently across every service in the solution rather than configured once per project and inevitably drifting out of sync.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-integrations-formerly-components\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/observability-aspire/3-integrations-formerly-components\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>3. Integrations (formerly &quot;components&quot;)</h3>\n<p>NuGet packages that register a client <strong>with health checks, telemetry, retries and configuration</strong> already set up: <code>builder.AddSqlServerDbContext&lt;ShopDb&gt;(&quot;shopdb&quot;)</code>, <code>builder.AddRedisDistributedCache(&quot;cache&quot;)</code>, <code>builder.AddAzureServiceBusClient(&quot;bus&quot;)</code>, <code>builder.AddNpgsqlDataSource(&quot;db&quot;)</code>, and many more.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-dashboard\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/observability-aspire/the-dashboard\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The dashboard</h3>\n<p>A local (or standalone) web UI showing every resource&#39;s state, console <strong>logs</strong>, <strong>structured logs</strong>, <strong>traces</strong> and <strong>metrics</strong> via OTLP, which is distributed tracing on your laptop from day one. It&#39;s also available as a standalone container for any OTLP-emitting app.</p>\n<h3 id=\"deployment\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/observability-aspire/deployment\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Deployment</h3>\n<p>Aspire&#39;s app model can be <strong>published</strong>: <code>azd up</code> (Azure Developer CLI) provisions Azure Container Apps plus the backing services, and community and Microsoft tooling generates Kubernetes manifests, Docker Compose and Bicep. Aspire is <strong>development-time and deployment tooling</strong>, not a runtime you need in production.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-why\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Why Aspire matters</div><p>Before Aspire, running &quot;API + worker + SQL + Redis + RabbitMQ + front end&quot; locally meant docker-compose files, scripts, duplicated connection strings and no tracing. Aspire makes that the default developer experience, and bakes observability and resilience best practices into every service from the start.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"diagnosing-a-production-issue-a-workflow\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/observability-aspire/diagnosing-a-production-issue-a-workflow\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Diagnosing a production issue: a workflow <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Alert fires</strong> (an SLO burn rate or error rate on checkout).</li>\n<li><strong>Dashboard</strong>: which endpoint, since when, and does it correlate with a deployment (annotations) or traffic?</li>\n<li><strong>Exemplar traces</strong> of failing or slow requests: which span is red or long, and which service and dependency?</li>\n<li><strong>Logs for that TraceId</strong>: the exception and context.</li>\n<li><strong>Runtime metrics</strong> of the affected service: GC, thread pool starvation, CPU, connection pool.</li>\n<li><strong>Mitigate first</strong> (roll back, scale out, fail over, toggle a feature flag), then <strong>root cause</strong>, and a blameless post-mortem.</li>\n<li>Add the missing <strong>signal or alert</strong> you wished you&#39;d had.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Notice how directly this seven-step workflow walks back through the three signals, in exactly the order the &quot;correlated&quot; point at the top of this chapter promised: a metric (step 1–2) points you to traces (step 3), which point you to logs (step 4), which point you to the actual fix.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are the three pillars of observability?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Logs (detailed records of discrete events), metrics (numeric aggregates over time, cheap and good for dashboards and alerts) and traces (the end-to-end path of a single request across services as a tree of timed spans). They&#39;re most useful correlated through the trace ID.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is OpenTelemetry and how does .NET support it?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A vendor-neutral CNCF standard and SDK for generating, collecting and exporting logs, metrics and traces (via OTLP). .NET&#39;s built-in APIs (ILogger, System.Diagnostics.Metrics Meter, and ActivitySource/Activity for spans) are OpenTelemetry-compatible. The OpenTelemetry .NET SDK subscribes to them and exports to backends like Azure Monitor, Grafana, Jaeger or Datadog.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How does distributed tracing work across services?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Each request gets a trace ID; each unit of work is a span with a parent. The W3C traceparent header (and baggage) propagates context on outgoing HTTP, gRPC and messages, and receivers continue the same trace. ASP.NET Core and HttpClient handle propagation automatically, and backends reassemble the full call tree with timings.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What&#39;s the difference between metrics and logs?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Metrics are pre-aggregated numeric time series (counters, histograms): small, fast, ideal for trends, dashboards and alerting, with low-cardinality dimensions. Logs are individual event records with rich context, used for investigation, and more expensive to store and query.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is cardinality and why does it matter for metrics?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The number of unique tag-value combinations. Each one creates a separate time series, so high-cardinality tags (user IDs, order IDs, raw URLs) explode storage and cost. Keep metric tags low-cardinality and put IDs on traces and logs.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What would you monitor for a .NET web API?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>RED metrics per endpoint (request rate, error rate, p95/p99 latency), dependency latency and failures, plus .NET runtime metrics (GC pause and heap, thread pool queue length and thread count, exception rate), connection pool usage, queue/outbox lag, health checks and SLO burn-rate alerts.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is .NET Aspire?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A toolkit for building distributed .NET apps: an AppHost project to orchestrate services and containers locally with automatic configuration and service discovery, ServiceDefaults to apply OpenTelemetry, health checks and resilience consistently, integration packages for common services, and a dashboard showing logs, traces and metrics. It can also drive deployment (e.g. to Azure Container Apps with azd).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are SLIs, SLOs and error budgets?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>An SLI is a measured indicator of service level (e.g. % of requests under 300 ms). An SLO is the target for it (99.9% over 30 days). The error budget is the allowed shortfall (0.1%), used to balance releasing features against reliability work and to drive alerting on burn rate.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/observability-aspire/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/diagnostics/observability-with-otel\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">.NET observability with OpenTelemetry</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/diagnostics/distributed-tracing\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Distributed tracing in .NET</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/diagnostics/metrics-instrumentation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Creating metrics</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-monitor/app/opentelemetry-enable?tabs=aspnetcore\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Azure Monitor OpenTelemetry for .NET</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/aspire/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">.NET Aspire documentation</a></li>\n<li>Book: <em>Observability Engineering</em> (Majors, Fong-Jones, Miranda). Google&#39;s <em>Site Reliability Engineering</em> (free online).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/observability-aspire/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (a plain console app, .NET 8/9; <code>System.Diagnostics.Activity</code> and <code>System.Diagnostics.Metrics</code> are part of the BCL, no extra packages). It checks the actual .NET telemetry primitives directly: that <code>StartActivity</code> is a no-op without a listener, that nested spans form a parent/child trace tree, that a <code>Counter&lt;T&gt;</code> is observable, and that a <code>traceparent</code> header built from a real <code>Activity</code> matches the W3C format. Exporting to a real OpenTelemetry Collector, Azure Monitor or the Aspire dashboard needs a live backend, so that part is left to the Further reading links instead of faked here.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Verify this chapter: run with `dotnet run`. No external packages needed --</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// System.Diagnostics.Activity and System.Diagnostics.Metrics are in the BCL.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// This checks the .NET APIs the chapter describes directly; it does not spin</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// up a real OpenTelemetry Collector or export to a backend (see Further</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// reading for that).</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Collections</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Generic</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Diagnostics</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Diagnostics</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Metrics</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Text</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RegularExpressions</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> name, <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> ok)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!ok) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: {name}&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: {name}&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 1. &quot;null if nobody is listening: cheap&quot; -- with no ActivityListener</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// registered, StartActivity returns null instead of doing real work.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> source = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ActivitySource</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop.Checkout.Test&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;1.0&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> noListenerActivity = source.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartActivity</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;checkout&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;StartActivity returns null when no ActivityListener is subscribed (cheap no-op)&quot;</span>,\n    noListenerActivity <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 2. Once a listener subscribes, the same call produces a real Activity with</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// a TraceId -- this is what the OpenTelemetry SDK does under the hood.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">ActivitySource</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddActivityListener</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ActivityListener</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShouldListenTo</span> = s =&gt; s.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop.Checkout.Test&quot;</span>,\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Sample</span> = (<span class=\"tk-kw\">ref</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ActivityCreationOptions</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ActivityContext</span>&gt; _) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">ActivitySamplingResult</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AllDataAndRecorded</span>\n});\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> parent = source.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartActivity</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;checkout&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;once a listener is registered, StartActivity returns a real Activity&quot;</span>, parent <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">not</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a recorded Activity has a non-empty TraceId&quot;</span>, parent!.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TraceId</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Length</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">32</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 3. Nested spans form a parent/child tree -- the mechanism behind the trace</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// diagrams in this chapter: a child span's ParentSpanId matches the parent's</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// own SpanId.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> child = source.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartActivity</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;sql-query&quot;</span>))\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a span started while a parent Activity is current records that parent's SpanId&quot;</span>,\n        child <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">not</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span> &amp;&amp; child.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ParentSpanId</span> == parent.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SpanId</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;the child shares the same TraceId as its parent (one trace, many spans)&quot;</span>,\n        child!.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TraceId</span> == parent.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TraceId</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 4. Counter&lt;T&gt;: a monotonic counter that a MeterListener can observe --</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// verifies CreateCounter + Add actually produces a measurement, the</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// mechanism OrdersPlaced.Add(1, ...) relies on in the chapter's example.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> meter = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Meter</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop.Checkout.Test&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;1.0&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> counter = meter.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateCounter</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">long</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;shop.orders.placed&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> recordedValues = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">long</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> meterListener = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">MeterListener</span>();\nmeterListener.<span class=\"tk-ty\">InstrumentPublished</span> = (instrument, listener) =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (instrument.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Meter</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop.Checkout.Test&quot;</span>) listener.<span class=\"tk-ty\">EnableMeasurementEvents</span>(instrument);\n};\nmeterListener.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SetMeasurementEventCallback</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">long</span>&gt;((instrument, measurement, tags, state) =&gt; recordedValues.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(measurement));\nmeterListener.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Start</span>();\n\ncounter.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\ncounter.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\ncounter.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a Counter&lt;long&gt;.Add is observed by a MeterListener with the exact recorded values&quot;</span>,\n    recordedValues.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span> &amp;&amp; recordedValues[<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>] == <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> &amp;&amp; recordedValues[<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>] == <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 5. W3C Trace Context header format: version-traceid-parentid-flags, exactly</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// as shown in the chapter's traceparent example.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> traceparent = <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;00-{parent.TraceId}-{parent.SpanId}-01&quot;</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> matchesW3CFormat = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Regex</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsMatch</span>(traceparent, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;^[0-9a-f]{2}-[0-9a-f]{32}-[0-9a-f]{16}-[0-9a-f]{2}$&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a traceparent header built from TraceId/SpanId matches the W3C Trace Context format&quot;</span>, matchesW3CFormat);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here (needs a running OpenTelemetry Collector,</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Aspire dashboard, or Azure Monitor backend to observe for real): OTLP</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// export, the Aspire AppHost orchestrating containers, head/tail sampling</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// decisions in a Collector, and KQL queries against Log Analytics -- see</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Further reading: &quot;.NET observability with OpenTelemetry&quot; and the Aspire docs.</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All checks passed.&quot;</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"monitoring-vs-observability-knowing-the-answer-vs-being-able","text":"Monitoring vs observability: knowing the answer vs being able to find it","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"the-three-signals","text":"The three signals","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"how-net-emits-telemetry","text":"How .NET emits telemetry","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"setting-up-opentelemetry","text":"Setting up OpenTelemetry","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"distributed-tracing","text":"Distributed tracing","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"sampling","text":"Sampling","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"custom-spans-and-metrics","text":"Custom spans and metrics","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"what-to-measure-red-use-and-the-golden-signals","text":"What to measure: RED, USE and the golden signals","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"alerting-and-slos","text":"Alerting and SLOs","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"application-insights-and-azure-monitor","text":"Application Insights and Azure Monitor","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"net-aspire","text":".NET Aspire","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"1-orchestration-the-apphost","text":"1. Orchestration: the AppHost","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"2-service-defaults","text":"2. Service defaults","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"3-integrations-formerly-components","text":"3. Integrations (formerly \"components\")","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-dashboard","text":"The dashboard","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"deployment","text":"Deployment","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"diagnosing-a-production-issue-a-workflow","text":"Diagnosing a production issue: a workflow","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":3741,"minutes":17,"num":"7.3","part":"p7"},"cloud-azure-overview":{"id":"cloud-azure-overview","title":"Cloud Fundamentals and the Azure Landscape","summary":"The cloud vocabulary before the services. IaaS, PaaS, serverless and SaaS, the shared responsibility model, regions and availability zones, scaling and elasticity, designing stateless cloud-native apps (the twelve factors), Azure's resource hierarchy, the Azure service map for .NET developers, cost, and the Well-Architected Framework.","layer":"infra","where":"Hosting & infrastructure › Cloud fundamentals","fits":"<p>This opens layer 7: the land your application is built on. Every later chapter in this part picks one area of the map below: <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/docker\">Docker</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-compute\">compute</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-data-messaging\">data &amp; messaging</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets\">identity &amp; secrets</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cicd-iac\">CI/CD &amp; IaC</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/kubernetes\">Kubernetes</a>. Cloud-native design choices also reach back into layer 3 (stateless apps, configuration, graceful shutdown).</p>\n","prereqs":["aspnet-hosting"],"next":["docker","azure-compute","azure-data-messaging"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cloud-azure-overview/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Cloud = renting compute/storage/services on demand, paying for use, instead of owning hardware.</li>\n<li><strong>IaaS → PaaS → Serverless → SaaS</strong> is a sliding scale of how much the provider manages for you. For .NET backends, default to PaaS/managed services — running your own VMs is operational work you probably don&#39;t need.</li>\n<li><strong>Shared responsibility</strong>: the provider secures the cloud&#39;s infrastructure; you always own your data, identities and access policies, regardless of service model.</li>\n<li>Scaling <strong>out</strong> (more instances) needs a genuinely <strong>stateless</strong> app; scaling <strong>up</strong> (a bigger instance) doesn&#39;t, but hits a ceiling.</li>\n<li>The <strong>twelve-factor app</strong> rules (config from environment, statelessness, graceful shutdown, logs as streams, etc.) are what makes an app safe to scale out and redeploy without drama.</li>\n<li>Azure resources nest: tenant → management groups → subscriptions → resource groups → resources, with RBAC and Policy applying at any level.</li>\n<li>The <strong>Well-Architected Framework</strong>&#39;s five pillars (Reliability, Security, Cost, Operations, Performance) are the lens interviewers use for system-design questions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"what-the-cloud-means\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cloud-azure-overview/what-the-cloud-means\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What &quot;the cloud&quot; means <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Renting computing resources (servers, storage, databases, networking, managed services) <strong>on demand, over the internet, paying for what you use</strong>, instead of buying and running your own hardware.</p>\n<p>Essential characteristics (NIST): <strong>on-demand self-service</strong> (create a database in minutes via the portal, CLI or code), <strong>broad network access</strong>, <strong>resource pooling</strong> (multi-tenant), <strong>rapid elasticity</strong> (scale up and down quickly), and <strong>measured service</strong> (pay per use).</p>\n<h2 id=\"service-models-who-manages-what\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cloud-azure-overview/service-models-who-manages-what\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Service models: who manages what <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>                  On-premises    IaaS           PaaS            Serverless / FaaS   SaaS\nApplication       YOU            YOU            YOU             YOU (functions)     provider\nData              YOU            YOU            YOU             YOU                 provider*\nRuntime (.NET)    YOU            YOU            provider        provider            provider\nOS / patching     YOU            YOU            provider        provider            provider\nVirtualisation    YOU            provider       provider        provider            provider\nServers/network   YOU            provider       provider        provider            provider\nAzure examples    —              Virtual        App Service,    Azure Functions,    Microsoft 365,\n                                 Machines       Azure SQL,      Container Apps      Dynamics, GitHub\n                                                Container Apps  (scale to zero)</code></pre></figure>\n<p>*You still own your data and its access control in SaaS.</p>\n<p>Reading down any one column tells you exactly where your team&#39;s operational burden starts: in IaaS, the line is drawn right after &quot;Virtualisation&quot; — you own the OS and everything above it. In PaaS, the line moves up to &quot;Runtime&quot; — Azure patches the OS and keeps the .NET runtime current, and your job is just the app itself.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Model</th>\n<th>You manage</th>\n<th>Trade-off</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>IaaS</strong></td>\n<td>VMs, OS, runtime, app</td>\n<td>Maximum control, maximum operations work</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>PaaS</strong></td>\n<td>App code and configuration</td>\n<td>Fast delivery, built-in scaling, patching and backups; less control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Containers-as-a-service</strong></td>\n<td>Container images</td>\n<td>Portable packaging with managed orchestration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Serverless</strong></td>\n<td>Functions/containers; pay per execution; scale to zero</td>\n<td>No idle cost, event-driven; cold starts, execution limits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SaaS</strong></td>\n<td>Nothing technical</td>\n<td>Use it as a product</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Rule of thumb for .NET backends: prefer PaaS and managed services.</strong> Every VM you run is patching, monitoring, backup and security work your team owns — work that buys you very little unless you have a genuine, specific reason to need OS-level control.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-shared-responsibility-model\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cloud-azure-overview/the-shared-responsibility-model\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The shared responsibility model <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>The provider secures <strong>the cloud</strong> (datacentres, hardware, hypervisor, the services&#39; infrastructure); <strong>you</strong> secure what you put <strong>in the cloud</strong>: your code, data, identities, access policies, network rules and configuration. The split shifts with the service model, but <strong>identities, data and access are always yours</strong>. Most cloud breaches are customer misconfigurations (public storage containers, leaked keys, over-privileged identities), not provider failures — a sobering fact worth keeping in mind every time <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets\">Azure identity &amp; secrets</a> asks you to configure an access policy.</p>\n<h2 id=\"regions-availability-zones-and-resilience\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cloud-azure-overview/regions-availability-zones-and-resilience\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Regions, availability zones and resilience <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Region</strong></td>\n<td>A geographic area with one or more datacentres (West Europe, East US 2, Germany West Central)</td>\n<td>Latency to users, data residency (GDPR), service availability, price</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Availability Zone (AZ)</strong></td>\n<td>Physically separate datacentres within a region, with independent power, cooling and network</td>\n<td><strong>Zone-redundant</strong> deployments survive a datacentre failure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Region pair</strong></td>\n<td>Two regions in the same geography paired for disaster recovery and staggered updates</td>\n<td>Geo-redundant storage, DR</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Geography</strong></td>\n<td>A set of regions within a data-residency boundary</td>\n<td>Compliance</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>There&#39;s a ladder of resilience tiers, each one buying more availability at the cost of more complexity and money:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Single instance</strong> — cheapest, but any deployment or failure means downtime.</li>\n<li><strong>Multiple instances across zones</strong> — the standard baseline for production.</li>\n<li><strong>Multi-region active-passive</strong> — a second region on standby for disaster recovery; you define an RTO/RPO (below) and fail over to it.</li>\n<li><strong>Multi-region active-active</strong> — the most available and the most complex: global routing (Front Door) in front of data that&#39;s replicated across regions (Cosmos DB multi-region writes, or SQL geo-replication).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Pick the tier your workload&#39;s actual availability requirement justifies — not automatically the top of the list.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>RTO</strong> (Recovery Time Objective): how long you can be down.</li>\n<li><strong>RPO</strong> (Recovery Point Objective): how much data (time) you can lose.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"scaling-and-elasticity\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cloud-azure-overview/scaling-and-elasticity\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Scaling and elasticity <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Scale <strong>up</strong> (vertical)</th>\n<th>Scale <strong>out</strong> (horizontal)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>How</td>\n<td>A bigger machine/tier (more CPU and RAM)</td>\n<td>More instances behind a load balancer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Limit</td>\n<td>The largest SKU; usually requires a restart</td>\n<td>Effectively unlimited, if the app is stateless</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Resilience</td>\n<td>Still one instance</td>\n<td>Survives instance loss</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Requirement</td>\n<td>None</td>\n<td><strong>Stateless application</strong> + shared state stores</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The &quot;Requirement&quot; row is the important one: scaling out isn&#39;t free just because the cloud makes adding instances easy — it only works if the application genuinely doesn&#39;t care which instance handles which request. That requirement is exactly what the twelve-factor discipline below is built around.</p>\n<p><strong>Autoscaling</strong> adds or removes instances automatically, based on metrics (CPU, memory, requests per second, or queue length via <strong>KEDA</strong>, a Kubernetes add-on that scales workloads using metrics from outside the cluster, like a message queue&#39;s depth) or on a schedule. <strong>Elasticity</strong> specifically means scaling <em>down</em> too, not just up — so you stop paying for capacity nobody&#39;s using.</p>\n<h2 id=\"cloud-native-app-design-the-twelve-factors\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cloud-azure-overview/cloud-native-app-design-the-twelve-factors\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Cloud-native app design: the twelve factors <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>The <strong>Twelve-Factor App</strong> methodology (Heroku) summarises how to build apps that run well on cloud platforms, and ASP.NET Core supports each factor:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Factor</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>In .NET</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>1. Codebase</td>\n<td>One repo per app, many deploys</td>\n<td>Git, one pipeline per deployable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Dependencies</td>\n<td>Declare and isolate</td>\n<td>NuGet with lock files, container images</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. <strong>Config</strong></td>\n<td>Store config in the <strong>environment</strong></td>\n<td><code>IConfiguration</code> env vars, Key Vault, App Configuration (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/configuration-options\">Configuration</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. Backing services</td>\n<td>Treat DBs and queues as attached resources via URLs</td>\n<td>Connection strings from config; swap without code changes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5. Build, release, run</td>\n<td>Strictly separate stages</td>\n<td>CI builds an immutable image; CD releases it with config</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6. <strong>Processes</strong></td>\n<td>Run as <strong>stateless</strong> processes</td>\n<td>No in-memory session, no local file storage; use Redis or Blob</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7. Port binding</td>\n<td>Self-contained, exports HTTP via a port</td>\n<td>Kestrel on <code>ASPNETCORE_HTTP_PORTS</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8. Concurrency</td>\n<td>Scale out via processes</td>\n<td>Instances/replicas; separate web and worker processes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9. <strong>Disposability</strong></td>\n<td>Fast startup, <strong>graceful shutdown</strong></td>\n<td>Host shutdown handling, SIGTERM, <code>stoppingToken</code> (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting/application-lifetime-and-graceful-shutdown-what-actually-hap\">Hosting</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10. Dev/prod parity</td>\n<td>Keep environments similar</td>\n<td>Containers, Aspire, Testcontainers, IaC</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11. <strong>Logs</strong></td>\n<td>Treat logs as event streams (stdout)</td>\n<td>Console/OTLP logging; the platform collects them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12. Admin processes</td>\n<td>Run one-off tasks as separate processes</td>\n<td>Migration bundles, jobs, <code>dotnet ef</code> in the pipeline</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Factors 3, 6 and 9 are bolded above because they&#39;re the ones ASP.NET Core chapters earlier in this book built entire sections around — configuration-from-environment, statelessness, and graceful shutdown each got dedicated treatment precisely because getting them wrong is what breaks an app the moment it&#39;s scaled past one instance.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">The classic &quot;it works with one instance&quot; bugs</div><p>In-memory sessions or caches assumed to be shared, files written to the local disk, scheduled jobs running on every replica, Data Protection keys not shared (logged-out users), SignalR without a backplane, and sticky-session assumptions. All of them show up the first time you scale out to 2 instances. Design for N instances from day one.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"azures-resource-hierarchy\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cloud-azure-overview/azures-resource-hierarchy\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Azure&#39;s resource hierarchy <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Microsoft Entra tenant (identity boundary: users, groups, app registrations)\n└── Management groups (policy &amp; RBAC inheritance for many subscriptions)\n    └── Subscriptions (billing + quota + access boundary; often one per environment or team)\n        └── Resource groups (lifecycle container: deploy/delete together, e.g. &quot;rg-shop-prod-weu&quot;)\n            └── Resources (App Service, SQL database, Key Vault, Storage account…)</code></pre></figure>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Azure Resource Manager (ARM)</strong> is the control plane: every create/update goes through it, from the portal, CLI (<code>az</code>), PowerShell, SDKs, Bicep/Terraform.</li>\n<li><strong>RBAC</strong> role assignments at any scope (management group → resource) grant <strong>who can do what</strong> (Owner, Contributor, Reader, plus data-plane roles like <em>Storage Blob Data Reader</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Azure Policy</strong> enforces rules (&quot;only these regions&quot;, &quot;storage must not allow public access&quot;, &quot;tags required&quot;).</li>\n<li><strong>Tags</strong> (<code>env=prod</code>, <code>owner=team-orders</code>, <code>costCenter=123</code>) enable cost reporting and governance.</li>\n<li><strong>Naming conventions</strong> (Cloud Adoption Framework): <code>app-shop-api-prod-weu-001</code>, <code>kv-shop-prod-weu</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-azure-map-for-net-backend-developers\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cloud-azure-overview/the-azure-map-for-net-backend-developers\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The Azure map for .NET backend developers <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Azure service(s)</th>\n<th>Chapter</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Run web apps / APIs</td>\n<td><strong>App Service</strong>, <strong>Container Apps</strong>, AKS, Functions</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-compute\">Azure compute</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Run background / event-driven code</td>\n<td><strong>Functions</strong>, Container Apps jobs, WebJobs</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-compute\">Azure compute</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Container registry</td>\n<td><strong>Azure Container Registry (ACR)</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/docker\">Docker</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relational database</td>\n<td><strong>Azure SQL Database</strong>, SQL Managed Instance, Azure Database for PostgreSQL</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-data-messaging\">Azure data</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NoSQL / global data</td>\n<td><strong>Cosmos DB</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/nosql-redis\">NoSQL</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Files, blobs, static content</td>\n<td><strong>Blob Storage</strong> (Storage account), Azure Files</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-data-messaging\">Azure data</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cache</td>\n<td><strong>Azure Managed Redis</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors\">Caching</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Messaging</td>\n<td><strong>Service Bus</strong>, <strong>Event Grid</strong>, <strong>Event Hubs</strong>, Storage Queues</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-data-messaging\">Azure data</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Secrets, keys, certificates</td>\n<td><strong>Key Vault</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets\">Identity &amp; secrets</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Identity</td>\n<td><strong>Microsoft Entra ID</strong>, <strong>managed identities</strong>, Entra External ID (customers)</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets\">Identity &amp; secrets</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Configuration and feature flags</td>\n<td><strong>App Configuration</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/configuration-options\">Configuration</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>API gateway</td>\n<td><strong>API Management</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/microservices\">Microservices</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Global entry, CDN, WAF</td>\n<td><strong>Front Door</strong>, Application Gateway (regional L7 LB + WAF), Load Balancer (L4)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Monitoring</td>\n<td><strong>Azure Monitor</strong>, <strong>Application Insights</strong>, Log Analytics</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/observability-aspire\">Observability</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Networking</td>\n<td>Virtual Network (VNet), subnets, NSGs, <strong>Private Endpoints</strong>, Private DNS, VPN/ExpressRoute</td>\n<td>below</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AI</td>\n<td>Azure OpenAI / AI Foundry, AI Search</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IaC and deployment</td>\n<td>ARM/<strong>Bicep</strong>, Terraform, <strong>azd</strong>, GitHub Actions/Azure DevOps</td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cicd-iac\">CI/CD &amp; IaC</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"networking-essentials\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cloud-azure-overview/networking-essentials\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Networking essentials <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>VNet</strong>: your private network in Azure, divided into <strong>subnets</strong>. <strong>NSGs</strong> filter traffic per subnet/NIC.</li>\n<li><strong>Private Endpoints</strong> give a PaaS service (SQL, Storage, Key Vault, Service Bus) a <strong>private IP in your VNet</strong>, and you then <strong>disable public network access</strong>. A common production security requirement. Requires <strong>Private DNS zones</strong> so names resolve to the private IPs.</li>\n<li><strong>VNet integration</strong> lets App Service/Functions/Container Apps reach private resources.</li>\n<li><strong>Service endpoints</strong>: an older, simpler alternative to private endpoints.</li>\n<li><strong>Hub-and-spoke</strong> topology: shared services (firewall, VPN gateway) in a hub VNet, workloads in peered spokes.</li>\n<li><strong>Front Door</strong> (global, edge) → <strong>Application Gateway / API Management</strong> (regional) → apps.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"cost\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cloud-azure-overview/cost\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Cost <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Cloud costs grow quietly — nobody decides to overspend, it accumulates from dozens of individually-reasonable-looking choices. Know the levers:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Lever</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Right-sizing</td>\n<td>P3v3 App Service plan at 10% CPU → P1v3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Autoscale and scale to zero</td>\n<td>Container Apps/Functions consumption for spiky workloads</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reservations / savings plans</td>\n<td>1–3 year commitments for steady workloads (Microsoft advertises up to ~60–70% off list price — see <a href=\"https://azure.microsoft.com/en-us/pricing/reserved-vm-instances/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Reservations pricing</a> in Further reading; actual savings depend on the SKU and term)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Serverless/consumption tiers</td>\n<td>Cosmos DB serverless or autoscale, Azure SQL serverless (auto-pause)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dev/test environments</td>\n<td>Smaller SKUs, shut down at night, dev/test pricing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Storage tiers</td>\n<td>Hot → Cool → Cold → Archive lifecycle policies for blobs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data egress</td>\n<td>Traffic leaving Azure or crossing regions costs money</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Logging volume</td>\n<td>Application Insights ingestion: sampling, log levels, retention</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Tools: <strong>Azure Cost Management</strong> (budgets, alerts, cost analysis by tag), <strong>Azure Advisor</strong> (cost, reliability and security recommendations), the <strong>Pricing Calculator</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-azure-well-architected-framework\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cloud-azure-overview/the-azure-well-architected-framework\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The Azure Well-Architected Framework <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Five pillars to evaluate any workload design. They map directly onto interview &quot;system design&quot; discussions, and worth noticing: each pillar is really a lens this entire book has already applied chapter by chapter — Reliability is <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/resilience\">Resilience</a>, Security is <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/auth\">Auth</a> and <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/web-security\">Web security</a>, Performance Efficiency is <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/performance-engineering\">Performance engineering</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Pillar</th>\n<th>Key questions</th>\n<th>Techniques</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Reliability</strong></td>\n<td>Does it survive failures and recover?</td>\n<td>Zones, redundancy, retries, health probes, backups, DR, chaos testing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Security</strong></td>\n<td>Is it protected end to end?</td>\n<td>Entra ID + managed identity, least privilege, Key Vault, private endpoints, WAF, encryption, Defender for Cloud</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cost Optimization</strong></td>\n<td>Is spend aligned with value?</td>\n<td>Right-sizing, autoscale, reservations, tiering, tagging</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Operational Excellence</strong></td>\n<td>Can we deploy and run it safely?</td>\n<td>IaC, CI/CD, observability, runbooks, safe deployment (slots, canaries)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Performance Efficiency</strong></td>\n<td>Does it scale to demand?</td>\n<td>Scale-out, caching, CDN, async processing, partitioning, load testing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the difference between IaaS, PaaS and serverless?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>IaaS gives virtual machines and networking: you manage the OS, runtime and app. PaaS manages the OS and runtime and you deploy code or containers (App Service, Azure SQL). Serverless runs code on demand with automatic scaling, including to zero, and per-execution billing (Azure Functions, Container Apps consumption), with constraints like cold starts.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the shared responsibility model?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The cloud provider is responsible for security of the cloud (physical infrastructure, hardware, virtualisation, managed service internals); the customer is responsible for security in the cloud (data, identities, access control, application code, network configuration). The split depends on IaaS, PaaS or SaaS, but data and identity are always the customer&#39;s.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are availability zones and why use them?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Physically separate datacentres within an Azure region with independent power, cooling and networking. Deploying instances and zone-redundant services across zones protects against a datacentre outage while keeping low latency.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Scale up vs scale out?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Scale up (vertical) moves to a larger instance with more CPU and RAM; it&#39;s limited by the largest size and doesn&#39;t add redundancy. Scale out (horizontal) adds more instances behind a load balancer: near-unlimited and resilient, but it requires a stateless application with shared state in external stores.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What makes an application cloud-native / twelve-factor?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Config from the environment, stateless processes with backing services attached via configuration, port binding, fast startup and graceful shutdown, logs as stdout streams, strict build/release/run separation, dev/prod parity, and scale-out via processes. In .NET: IConfiguration with env vars and Key Vault, Kestrel, Redis/Blob for state, hosted-service shutdown handling, container images and IaC.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How is Azure organised (resource hierarchy)?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>An Entra tenant contains management groups, which contain subscriptions (billing, quota and access boundaries), which contain resource groups (lifecycle containers), which contain resources. RBAC and Azure Policy can be applied at any level and are inherited downward. Everything is managed through Azure Resource Manager.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are private endpoints?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Network interfaces that give an Azure PaaS service a private IP address inside your VNet, so traffic stays on the Microsoft backbone and you can disable the service&#39;s public endpoint. They need Private DNS zone configuration.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are RTO and RPO?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Recovery Time Objective: the maximum acceptable downtime after a disaster. Recovery Point Objective: the maximum acceptable data loss measured in time. Both drive backup, replication and failover design.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cloud-azure-overview/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/paths/azure-fundamentals-describe-cloud-concepts/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Azure fundamentals (Microsoft Learn path, AZ-900)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/well-architected/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Azure Well-Architected Framework</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Azure Architecture Center</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://12factor.net/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Twelve-Factor App</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/azure/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Azure for .NET developers</a></li>\n<li>Certifications that match this part: AZ-900 (fundamentals), <strong>AZ-204 (Developing Solutions for Azure)</strong>, AZ-305 (architect).</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://azure.microsoft.com/en-us/pricing/reserved-vm-instances/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Reservations pricing</a> — source for the reservation-discount figures above.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cloud-azure-overview/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Most of this chapter is about Azure services, pricing and network topology, which a console program can&#39;t exercise. What <em>can</em> be verified locally is the mechanism behind two core claims: config-from-environment (twelve-factor #3) and why scaling out needs a stateless app. Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (plain console app, .NET 8/9, BCL only).</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim (twelve-factor, factor 3): &quot;store config in the environment&quot; — .NET's config system reads env vars directly.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SetEnvironmentVariable</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;SHOP_API_KEY&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;demo-key-123&quot;</span>); <span class=\"tk-com\">// stands in for a real deployment setting this</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> apiKeyFromEnv = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetEnvironmentVariable</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;SHOP_API_KEY&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (apiKeyFromEnv != <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;demo-key-123&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected config to come from the environment, not a hardcoded value&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: config read from the environment ({apiKeyFromEnv}), exactly how IConfiguration's environment provider works&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: scaling OUT only works if the app is genuinely stateless (shares nothing per-instance that a later request needs).</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Simulate two &quot;instances&quot; with their own local, in-memory dictionary — the broken pattern the chapter warns about.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> instanceA = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> instanceB = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;();\n\ninstanceA[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;cart-42&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;2x widget&quot;</span>; <span class=\"tk-com\">// request 1 lands on instance A, which remembers the cart locally</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Request 2 for the same cart gets load-balanced to instance B instead.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> statefulInstanceBSeesIt = instanceB.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ContainsKey</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;cart-42&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (statefulInstanceBSeesIt)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected instance B's local dictionary to know nothing about instance A's state&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: with local in-memory state, a second instance has no idea about the first instance's cart — this is exactly why scale-out needs a stateless app + shared state store&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Now the fix: a shared store (stands in for Redis/SQL/Blob) that every instance reads and writes to instead of local memory.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> sharedStore = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HandleOnAnyInstance</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> key, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>) =&gt; sharedStore[key] = <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>; <span class=\"tk-com\">// every &quot;instance&quot; calls this the same way</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>? <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadFromAnyInstance</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> key) =&gt; sharedStore.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGetValue</span>(key, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> v) ? v : <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">HandleOnAnyInstance</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;cart-42&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;2x widget&quot;</span>);                 <span class=\"tk-com\">// &quot;instance A&quot; handles this</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> seenFromAnotherInstance = <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadFromAnyInstance</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;cart-42&quot;</span>); <span class=\"tk-com\">// &quot;instance B&quot; handles the next request</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (seenFromAnotherInstance != <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;2x widget&quot;</span>)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected the shared store to make the cart visible from any instance&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: with state moved to a shared store, any instance can see the same cart — this is the stateless-process pattern the twelve-factor app requires&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here (needs real Azure resources, pricing, or network topology) — see Further reading:</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - Service model boundaries (IaaS/PaaS/serverless/SaaS), shared responsibility model</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - Regions, availability zones, RTO/RPO, resource hierarchy, RBAC, private endpoints</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - Reservation/savings-plan discount percentages</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - Well-Architected Framework pillars</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All checkable claims verified.&quot;</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"what-the-cloud-means","text":"What \"the cloud\" means","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"service-models-who-manages-what","text":"Service models: who manages what","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"the-shared-responsibility-model","text":"The shared responsibility model","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"regions-availability-zones-and-resilience","text":"Regions, availability zones and resilience","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"scaling-and-elasticity","text":"Scaling and elasticity","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"cloud-native-app-design-the-twelve-factors","text":"Cloud-native app design: the twelve factors","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"azures-resource-hierarchy","text":"Azure's resource hierarchy","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"the-azure-map-for-net-backend-developers","text":"The Azure map for .NET backend developers","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"networking-essentials","text":"Networking essentials","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"cost","text":"Cost","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"the-azure-well-architected-framework","text":"The Azure Well-Architected Framework","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":2966,"minutes":13,"num":"8.1","part":"p8"},"docker":{"id":"docker","title":"Containers and Docker for .NET","summary":"Packaging .NET apps as containers. Containers vs VMs, images, layers and registries, writing a production-grade multi-stage Dockerfile, .NET's container images (SDK, ASP.NET, runtime-deps, chiseled, Alpine), building without a Dockerfile (dotnet publish), configuration and secrets, non-root users and port 8080, docker compose for local development, image size and security scanning.","layer":"infra","where":"Hosting & infrastructure › Packaging (containers)","fits":"<p>The container is the <strong>unit of deployment</strong> for layer 7. Every compute option (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-compute\">Azure compute</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/kubernetes\">Kubernetes</a>) runs it, CI/CD (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cicd-iac\">CI/CD &amp; IaC</a>) builds it, and Testcontainers (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/integration-testing\">Integration testing</a>) uses it for tests. Inside the container, your app is the same ASP.NET Core host as always (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting\">Hosting</a>).</p>\n","prereqs":["projects-sdk-nuget","aspnet-hosting"],"next":["azure-compute","kubernetes","cicd-iac"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/docker/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A container shares the host&#39;s kernel instead of running its own, so it starts in milliseconds and costs much less than a VM — at the price of weaker isolation.</li>\n<li>An <strong>image</strong> is a stack of cached, read-only layers; a <strong>container</strong> is a running instance with one writable layer on top. Many containers can share one image.</li>\n<li>A good .NET Dockerfile is <strong>multi-stage</strong>: compile with the big SDK image, then copy only the published output into a small runtime image (ideally <strong>chiseled</strong>, which has no shell and runs non-root by default).</li>\n<li>Copy <code>.csproj</code> files and restore <em>before</em> copying source, so code-only changes don&#39;t invalidate the (slow) restore layer&#39;s cache.</li>\n<li>Since .NET 8, ASP.NET Core images listen on port 8080, not 80, and run as a non-root user — both for security.</li>\n<li>Configuration comes from environment variables set by the platform, never baked into the image; secrets come from a secret store or managed identity, never <code>COPY</code>&#39;d in.</li>\n<li>.NET is container-aware: the GC and thread pool read cgroup CPU/memory limits and size themselves to the container, not the host machine.</li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout c-tip\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Plain-English starting point</div><p>A <strong>container</strong> is your app plus everything it needs to run — the .NET runtime, OS files, config — sealed into one package that behaves the same everywhere. A <strong>Docker image</strong> is that package, built once. <strong>Running</strong> the image is what creates a container.</p>\n</aside>\n<p><img src=\"visuals/container-image.svg\" alt=\"Animated build of a .NET container image from layers, then run as a container\"></p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-analogy\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Building an image is like stacking transparent sheets</div><p>Picture a few transparent sheets, each with one paragraph of a letter printed on it. Stack three sheets and you can read a whole letter through them. That stack is your <strong>image</strong>: one sheet per layer (base OS, .NET runtime, your app files), stacked in order.</p>\n<p>Photocopy that stack and you get a letter you can hand out — that&#39;s a <strong>container</strong>: a running copy with one extra, blank sheet on top for anything it scribbles while it runs. Photocopy the same stack again and you get another, independent letter. Many containers, one image.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"what-a-container-is\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/docker/what-a-container-is\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What a container is <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>A <strong>container</strong> is an isolated process (or group of processes) with <strong>its own filesystem, network and process space</strong> — packaged with everything it needs: your app, the .NET runtime, OS libraries. One detail explains both its biggest strength and its biggest limit: a container shares the <strong>host&#39;s kernel</strong> instead of running its own. That&#39;s why it starts in milliseconds and barely costs anything to run — but it&#39;s also why its isolation is weaker than a full virtual machine&#39;s.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Virtual machine</th>\n<th>Container</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Virtualises</td>\n<td>Hardware: each VM has a full guest OS and kernel</td>\n<td>The OS: processes isolated with namespaces and cgroups; shared kernel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Size</td>\n<td>GBs</td>\n<td>MBs (a .NET API image is ~100–250 MB, or less with chiseled/AOT)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Startup</td>\n<td>Minutes</td>\n<td>Seconds or less</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Density</td>\n<td>Tens per host</td>\n<td>Hundreds per host</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Isolation</td>\n<td>Strong</td>\n<td>Good (weaker than a VM; sandboxed runtimes exist)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-why\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Why containers took over</div><p>&quot;It works on my machine&quot; goes away: the <strong>same immutable image</strong> runs on your laptop, in CI, in staging and in production. Dependencies are explicit, deployments are fast and reproducible, rollbacks mean redeploying the previous image tag, and every modern platform (Kubernetes, Container Apps, App Service, Functions, ECS, Cloud Run) accepts containers. The image is the contract between dev and ops.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"images-layers-containers-and-registries\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/docker/images-layers-containers-and-registries\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Images, layers, containers and registries <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Image</strong></td>\n<td>A read-only template: a stack of <strong>layers</strong> (filesystem diffs) plus metadata (entrypoint, env, exposed ports, user)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Layer</strong></td>\n<td>Each Dockerfile instruction (<code>FROM</code>, <code>COPY</code>, <code>RUN</code>) creates a layer, <strong>cached</strong> and shared between images</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Container</strong></td>\n<td>A running (or stopped) instance of an image, with a thin writable layer on top</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Registry</strong></td>\n<td>Stores and distributes images: Docker Hub, <strong>Azure Container Registry (ACR)</strong>, GitHub Container Registry, <strong>MCR</strong> (Microsoft Container Registry, where official .NET images live)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Tag</strong></td>\n<td>A human label: <code>shop-api:1.4.2</code>, <code>:latest</code> (mutable)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Digest</strong></td>\n<td>Immutable content hash: <code>shop-api@sha256:3f1c…</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Dockerfile</strong></td>\n<td>Instructions to build an image</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Shell</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>docker build -t <span class=\"tk-ty\">shop-api</span>:<span class=\"tk-num\">1.4.2</span> .\ndocker run -d -p <span class=\"tk-num\">8080</span>:<span class=\"tk-num\">8080</span> <span class=\"tk-attr\">--name</span> api -e <span class=\"tk-ty\">ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT</span>=Staging <span class=\"tk-ty\">shop-api</span>:<span class=\"tk-num\">1.4.2</span>\ndocker ps · docker logs -f api · docker exec -it api sh · docker stop api   <span class=\"tk-com\"># stop sends SIGTERM → graceful shutdown</span>\ndocker tag <span class=\"tk-ty\">shop-api</span>:<span class=\"tk-num\">1.4.2</span> shopacr.azurecr.io/<span class=\"tk-ty\">shop-api</span>:<span class=\"tk-num\">1.4.2</span>\naz acr login -n shopacr &amp;&amp; docker push shopacr.azurecr.io/<span class=\"tk-ty\">shop-api</span>:<span class=\"tk-num\">1.4.2</span></code></pre></figure>\n<h2 id=\"net-container-images\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/docker/net-container-images\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>.NET container images <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Official images are published on <strong>mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Image</th>\n<th>Contains</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>sdk:10.0</code></td>\n<td>Full SDK (compiler, CLI, NuGet) ~ 800 MB</td>\n<td><strong>Build stage only</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>aspnet:10.0</code></td>\n<td>ASP.NET Core + .NET runtime</td>\n<td><strong>Run web apps/APIs</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>runtime:10.0</code></td>\n<td>.NET runtime only</td>\n<td>Run console/worker apps</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>runtime-deps:10.0</code></td>\n<td>Only native OS dependencies (no .NET)</td>\n<td><strong>Self-contained</strong> or <strong>Native AOT</strong> apps</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Variants: <code>-alpine</code></td>\n<td>Alpine Linux (musl): small</td>\n<td>Smaller images; check native dependency compatibility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>-noble</code>, <code>-azurelinux3.0</code></td>\n<td>Ubuntu 24.04 / Azure Linux base</td>\n<td>Default and alternative distros</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><code>-noble-chiseled</code></strong> (and <code>-extra</code>)</td>\n<td><strong>Distroless</strong> Ubuntu: no shell, no package manager, non-root by default</td>\n<td><strong>Smallest attack surface</strong>, ideal for production</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>-composite</code></td>\n<td>ASP.NET with composite R2R images</td>\n<td>Faster startup</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Since .NET 8, ASP.NET Core images <strong>listen on port 8080</strong> by default (<code>ASPNETCORE_HTTP_PORTS=8080</code>), not 80, and include a non-root <strong><code>app</code></strong> user (UID 1654).</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-production-grade-dockerfile\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/docker/a-production-grade-dockerfile\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>A production-grade Dockerfile <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Dockerfile</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-com\"># syntax=docker/dockerfile:1</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\"># ---------- build stage ----------</span>\nFROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/<span class=\"tk-ty\">sdk</span>:<span class=\"tk-num\">10.0</span> AS build\nWORKDIR /src\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\"># 1. Copy only project files and restore first → this layer is cached until dependencies change</span>\nCOPY Directory.Build.props Directory.Packages.props global.json ./\nCOPY src/Shop.Api/Shop.Api.csproj                       src/Shop.Api/\nCOPY src/Shop.Application/Shop.Application.csproj       src/Shop.Application/\nCOPY src/Shop.Domain/Shop.Domain.csproj                 src/Shop.Domain/\nCOPY src/Shop.Infrastructure/Shop.Infrastructure.csproj src/Shop.Infrastructure/\nRUN dotnet restore src/Shop.Api/Shop.Api.csproj\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\"># 2. Copy the rest and publish</span>\nCOPY src/ src/\nRUN dotnet publish src/Shop.Api/Shop.Api.csproj -c Release -o /app/publish <span class=\"tk-attr\">--no-restore</span> /<span class=\"tk-ty\">p</span>:<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseAppHost</span>=false\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\"># ---------- runtime stage ----------</span>\nFROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/<span class=\"tk-ty\">aspnet</span>:<span class=\"tk-num\">10.0</span>-noble-chiseled AS final\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY <span class=\"tk-attr\">--from</span>=build /app/publish .\nUSER app                                   <span class=\"tk-com\"># non-root (chiseled images default to it already)</span>\nEXPOSE <span class=\"tk-num\">8080</span>\nENV <span class=\"tk-ty\">ASPNETCORE_HTTP_PORTS</span>=<span class=\"tk-num\">8080</span> \\\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">DOTNET_gcServer</span>=<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>\nENTRYPOINT [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;dotnet&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop.Api.dll&quot;</span>]</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Key techniques:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Multi-stage build:</strong> compile in the big SDK image, copy only the published output into the small runtime image. The final image contains no SDK, no source code and no build tools.</li>\n<li><strong>Layer caching:</strong> copy the <code>.csproj</code> files and <strong>restore before</strong> copying source, so code changes don&#39;t invalidate the (slow) restore layer. Docker caches each layer, and only reruns a layer (plus everything after it) when that layer&#39;s own inputs change. Since the <code>.csproj</code> files rarely change, restore stays cached even when you&#39;ve only edited a <code>.cs</code> file — step 1&#39;s whole point is to put the slow, rarely-changing work before the fast, frequently-changing work.</li>\n<li><strong><code>.dockerignore</code>:</strong> exclude <code>bin/</code>, <code>obj/</code>, <code>.git</code>, <code>.vs</code>, <code>node_modules</code>, <code>*.user</code>, secrets. It speeds up builds and avoids leaking files.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-root user</strong> and a <strong>minimal base image</strong> (chiseled).</li>\n<li><strong>Exec-form <code>ENTRYPOINT</code></strong> (the JSON array syntax, not a plain shell string) runs <code>dotnet</code> directly as PID 1, instead of wrapping it in a shell. That matters because <code>docker stop</code> sends <code>SIGTERM</code> to PID 1, and only the process that actually receives that signal can start a graceful shutdown. If a shell were PID 1 instead, the shell would get the signal and <code>dotnet</code> wouldn&#39;t — see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting/application-lifetime-and-graceful-shutdown-what-actually-hap\">ASP.NET Core architecture</a> for what graceful shutdown then does.</li>\n<li>Pin base image versions (<code>10.0</code> gets patches; pin digests for full reproducibility) and <strong>rebuild regularly</strong> to pick up security patches.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code># .dockerignore\n**/bin\n**/obj\n**/.vs\n**/.git\n**/node_modules\n**/*.user\n**/appsettings.Development.json\n**/secrets*.json</code></pre></figure>\n<h2 id=\"building-images-without-a-dockerfile\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/docker/building-images-without-a-dockerfile\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Building images without a Dockerfile <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>The .NET SDK can build OCI images directly, with no Docker daemon needed for the build:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Shell</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>dotnet publish src/Shop.Api -c Release /<span class=\"tk-ty\">t</span>:PublishContainer \\\n  -p <span class=\"tk-ty\">ContainerRepository</span>=shop-api \\\n  -p <span class=\"tk-ty\">ContainerImageTags</span>=<span class=\"tk-str\">'&quot;1.4.2;latest&quot;'</span> \\\n  -p <span class=\"tk-ty\">ContainerFamily</span>=noble-chiseled \\\n  -p <span class=\"tk-ty\">ContainerRegistry</span>=shopacr.azurecr.io            <span class=\"tk-com\"># push directly to a registry</span></code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>XML</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>&lt;PropertyGroup&gt;\n  &lt;ContainerRepository&gt;shop-api&lt;/ContainerRepository&gt;\n  &lt;ContainerFamily&gt;noble-chiseled&lt;/ContainerFamily&gt;\n  &lt;ContainerPort <span class=\"tk-ty\">Include</span>=<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;8080&quot;</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Type</span>=<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;tcp&quot;</span> /&gt;\n&lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Great for standard apps and CI; use a Dockerfile when you need custom OS packages or more complex builds.</p>\n<h2 id=\"configuration-secrets-and-storage-in-containers\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/docker/configuration-secrets-and-storage-in-containers\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Configuration, secrets and storage in containers <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Configuration via environment variables</strong> (<code>ConnectionStrings__Shop</code>, <code>ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT</code>), set by the platform at runtime, <strong>never baked into the image</strong> — exactly the layered-configuration discipline <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/configuration-options/the-five-layers-lowest-priority-to-highest\">Configuration</a> covered, applied at the container level. The same image is promoted through all environments.</li>\n<li><strong>Secrets</strong>: never <code>COPY</code> them into images or pass them as build args (they persist in layers). Use the platform&#39;s secret store (Key Vault references, Kubernetes Secrets, Container Apps secrets) or <strong>managed identity</strong> so there&#39;s nothing secret to inject.</li>\n<li><strong>Filesystem is ephemeral</strong>: anything written inside a container is lost when it&#39;s replaced. Use Blob Storage or a database; mount <strong>volumes</strong> only for genuinely needed persistent paths.</li>\n<li><strong>Data Protection keys</strong> must be persisted outside the container when running multiple instances (Blob + Key Vault), or cookies and antiforgery tokens break — exactly the shared-key-ring requirement <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/auth/cookie-authentication\">Auth</a> raised for cookie auth across instances.</li>\n<li><strong>Logs to stdout/stderr</strong> (console or JSON console logging, or OTLP); the platform collects them.</li>\n<li><strong>Health endpoints</strong> for the orchestrator&#39;s probes (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/resilience/health-checks\">Resilience</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"docker-compose-for-local-development\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/docker/docker-compose-for-local-development\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>docker compose for local development <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Run the API with its dependencies locally:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>YAML</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-com\"># compose.yaml</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">services</span>:\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">api</span>:\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">build</span>: .\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">ports</span>: [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;8080:8080&quot;</span>]\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">environment</span>:\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT</span>: Development\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">ConnectionStrings__Shop</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Server=sql;Database=Shop;User Id=sa;Password=Your_strong_pw1;TrustServerCertificate=True&quot;</span>\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">ConnectionStrings__Redis</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;redis:6379&quot;</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">depends_on</span>:\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">sql</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">condition</span>: service_healthy }\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">redis</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">condition</span>: service_started }\n\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">sql</span>:\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">image</span>: mcr.microsoft.com/mssql/<span class=\"tk-ty\">server</span>:<span class=\"tk-num\">2022</span>-latest\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">environment</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">ACCEPT_EULA</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Y&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">MSSQL_SA_PASSWORD</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Your_strong_pw1&quot;</span> }\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">ports</span>: [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;1433:1433&quot;</span>]\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">volumes</span>: [<span class=\"tk-ty\">sqldata</span>:/var/opt/mssql]\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">healthcheck</span>:\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">test</span>: [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;CMD-SHELL&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/opt/mssql-tools18/bin/sqlcmd -C -S localhost -U sa -P Your_strong_pw1 -Q 'SELECT 1' || exit 1&quot;</span>]\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">interval</span>: 10s\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">retries</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>\n\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">redis</span>:\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">image</span>: <span class=\"tk-ty\">redis</span>:<span class=\"tk-num\">7</span>-alpine\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">ports</span>: [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;6379:6379&quot;</span>]\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">volumes</span>:\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">sqldata</span>:</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Shell</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>docker compose up -d <span class=\"tk-attr\">--build</span>     <span class=\"tk-com\"># start everything</span>\ndocker compose logs -f api\ndocker compose down -v           <span class=\"tk-com\"># stop and remove volumes</span></code></pre></figure>\n<p><strong>.NET Aspire</strong> (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/observability-aspire/net-aspire\">Observability &amp; Aspire</a>) is the modern .NET alternative: the same orchestration in C#, with automatic wiring and a dashboard, and it&#39;s worth noticing the <code>depends_on</code> / <code>WaitFor</code> idea here is the same dependency-ordering problem Aspire&#39;s AppHost solves with actual C# instead of YAML.</p>\n<h2 id=\"image-size-startup-and-security\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/docker/image-size-startup-and-security\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Image size, startup and security <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Technique</th>\n<th>Effect</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Chiseled / distroless base</td>\n<td>Fewer packages → fewer CVEs; no shell for attackers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alpine base</td>\n<td>Small; musl libc (check native libraries like ICU)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>InvariantGlobalization=true</code></td>\n<td>Drops ICU libraries when you don&#39;t need culture data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Trimming + self-contained on <code>runtime-deps</code></td>\n<td>Smaller output</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Native AOT</strong> on <code>runtime-deps</code> (or chiseled)</td>\n<td>Tens of MB, millisecond startup (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/what-if-you-skipped-the-jit-completely\">How code runs</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ReadyToRun / composite images</td>\n<td>Faster startup</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Security practices:</strong> scan images in CI (Trivy, Grype, Docker Scout, <strong>Microsoft Defender for Containers</strong> on ACR); run as non-root with a <strong>read-only root filesystem</strong>; drop Linux capabilities; don&#39;t run as privileged; <strong>sign</strong> images (Notation/cosign) and allow only signed images in production; produce an <strong>SBOM</strong>; rebuild regularly for base-image patches (Dependabot/Renovate can bump base images).</p>\n<h2 id=\"container-memory-and-cpu-limits-with-net\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/docker/container-memory-and-cpu-limits-with-net\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Container memory and CPU limits with .NET <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>.NET is <strong>container-aware</strong>: the GC and thread pool read the container&#39;s own cgroup CPU and memory limits, not the physical host&#39;s. In other words, the runtime adapts its behavior to the box it thinks it&#39;s running in — which is the container, not the machine underneath it. That&#39;s why <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/memory-gc/generations-why-the-gc-doesnt-scan-everything-every-time\">Memory &amp; GC</a> and <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/threading-concurrency\">Threading &amp; concurrency</a> matter directly here too.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>With a memory limit, the GC heap hard limit defaults to <strong>75%</strong> of the container limit (tunable via <code>DOTNET_GCHeapHardLimitPercent</code>) — see <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/runtime-config/garbage-collector#heap-limit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Runtime configuration knobs: GC</a> in Further reading for the exact default and how to change it.</li>\n<li><strong>Server GC</strong> is the default for ASP.NET Core; .NET 9+ enables <strong>DATAS</strong> (Dynamic Adaptation To Application Sizes), so server GC uses far less memory in small containers.</li>\n<li>Set <strong>realistic requests and limits</strong> in Kubernetes. Too-low memory limits cause <strong>OOMKilled</strong> restarts.</li>\n<li><code>DOTNET_PROCESSOR_COUNT</code> can override the detected CPU count.</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the difference between a container and a virtual machine?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A VM virtualises hardware and runs a full guest OS with its own kernel: strong isolation, but heavy and slow to start. A container isolates processes on a shared host kernel (namespaces and cgroups) and packages just the app and its dependencies: lightweight, fast to start and dense.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why use a multi-stage Dockerfile for .NET?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>To build with the large SDK image and copy only the published output into a small runtime image. The final image is smaller, has less attack surface (no SDK, sources or build tools) and pulls and starts faster.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you optimise Docker layer caching for .NET builds?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Copy the csproj files (and Directory.*.props, global.json) first and run dotnet restore before copying the rest of the source. Restore is then cached and only reruns when dependencies change. Use a .dockerignore to keep bin/obj and other noise out of the build context.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Which base image would you use for an ASP.NET Core app in production?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspnet with a pinned major version, ideally the chiseled (distroless, non-root) variant for a minimal attack surface, or runtime-deps for self-contained/Native AOT apps. Use the sdk image only in the build stage.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How should configuration and secrets be provided to a container?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Through environment variables or mounted configuration supplied by the platform at runtime, so one immutable image is promoted across environments. Secrets come from a secret store (Key Vault references, Kubernetes Secrets) or are avoided with managed identity. Never bake them into the image or build args.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why does .NET 8+ listen on port 8080 in containers?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The official images default ASPNETCORE_HTTP_PORTS to 8080 and run as a non-root user, and non-root processes can&#39;t bind to privileged ports below 1024 on Linux. Map or route external port 80/443 to 8080 at the platform.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What happens when you run docker stop on a .NET container?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Docker sends SIGTERM to PID 1. With an exec-form ENTRYPOINT, that&#39;s dotnet, so the .NET host begins graceful shutdown (stops accepting connections, drains requests, stops hosted services). After the timeout (10 s default), Docker sends SIGKILL.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/docker/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/docker/build-container\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Containerize a .NET app</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/docker/container-images\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">.NET container images</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/containers/sdk-publish\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Containerize a .NET app with dotnet publish</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.docker.com/build/building/best-practices/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Dockerfile best practices</a></li>\n<li>Microsoft&#39;s eBook <em>.NET Microservices: Architecture for Containerized .NET Applications</em>.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/runtime-config/garbage-collector#heap-limit\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Runtime configuration knobs: GC</a> — source for the 75% default GC heap limit figure above.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/docker/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Most of this chapter needs an actual Docker daemon or container runtime to exercise for real (building images, cgroup limits). What you <em>can</em> verify directly, with no Docker involved, is the exact SIGTERM mechanism that <code>docker stop</code> relies on. Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (plain console app, .NET 8/9, BCL only; Linux/macOS — it uses the <code>kill</code> command to send itself a real signal).</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Diagnostics</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Runtime</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">InteropServices</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: &quot;docker stop sends SIGTERM to PID 1, and only the process that actually receives it can start</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// a graceful shutdown&quot; — demonstrate the exact mechanism .NET uses to observe SIGTERM (PosixSignalRegistration),</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// which is what an exec-form ENTRYPOINT (dotnet as PID 1) relies on.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!<span class=\"tk-ty\">OperatingSystem</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsWindows</span>())\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> received = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TaskCompletionSource</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> registration = <span class=\"tk-ty\">PosixSignalRegistration</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Create</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">PosixSignal</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SIGTERM</span>, ctx =&gt;\n    {\n        ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cancel</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>; <span class=\"tk-com\">// tell the runtime we handled it ourselves (so the process doesn't just die)</span>\n        received.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TrySetResult</span>();\n    });\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> pid = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProcessId</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> killProcess = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Process</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Start</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProcessStartInfo</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;kill&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;-TERM {pid}&quot;</span>) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">UseShellExecute</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span> });\n    killProcess!.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WaitForExit</span>();\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> completed = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WhenAny</span>(received.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Delay</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSeconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>)));\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (completed != received.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>)\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected this process to observe its own SIGTERM within 3 seconds&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: this process received SIGTERM and ran its own handler — this is the exact signal `docker stop` sends to PID 1, which is why exec-form ENTRYPOINT (dotnet as PID 1, not a shell) matters&quot;</span>);\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">else</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;SKIPPED: SIGTERM demo requires a POSIX OS (Linux/macOS) — containers run Linux, so this matches the real scenario&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: .NET reads cgroup limits and sizes the GC/thread pool to the CONTAINER, not the host.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// We can't fake cgroups here without a real container, but ProcessorCount is the exact value .NET bases that sizing on.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: Environment.ProcessorCount reports {Environment.ProcessorCount} (this process's view — inside a real container, this reflects the cgroup CPU limit, not the host's full core count)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here (needs an actual container runtime / cgroup limits / Docker daemon) — see Further reading:</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - Image layering, caching, multi-stage builds, registries</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - .NET base images (chiseled, Alpine, runtime-deps)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - GC heap hard limit default (75%) and DOTNET_GCHeapHardLimitPercent</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All checkable claims verified.&quot;</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"what-a-container-is","text":"What a container is","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"images-layers-containers-and-registries","text":"Images, layers, containers and registries","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"net-container-images","text":".NET container images","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"a-production-grade-dockerfile","text":"A production-grade Dockerfile","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"building-images-without-a-dockerfile","text":"Building images without a Dockerfile","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"configuration-secrets-and-storage-in-containers","text":"Configuration, secrets and storage in containers","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"docker-compose-for-local-development","text":"docker compose for local development","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"image-size-startup-and-security","text":"Image size, startup and security","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"container-memory-and-cpu-limits-with-net","text":"Container memory and CPU limits with .NET","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":2823,"minutes":13,"num":"8.2","part":"p8"},"azure-compute":{"id":"azure-compute","title":"Azure Compute: Where Your .NET Code Runs","summary":"Choosing and using Azure's hosting options. App Service (plans, slots, scaling, configuration), Azure Functions (triggers, bindings, isolated worker, plans, Durable Functions), Azure Container Apps (revisions, scaling with KEDA, jobs, Dapr), AKS, VMs, and a decision guide with interview-ready comparisons.","layer":"infra","where":"Hosting & infrastructure › Compute (where code runs)","fits":"<p>Compute is where the host from layer 3 (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting\">Hosting</a>) actually runs: as code on App Service, as a container (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/docker\">Docker</a>) on Container Apps or AKS (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/kubernetes\">Kubernetes</a>), or as functions triggered by events (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events\">Messaging &amp; events</a>). It&#39;s provisioned with IaC and deployed by pipelines (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cicd-iac\">CI/CD &amp; IaC</a>).</p>\n","prereqs":["cloud-azure-overview","docker"],"next":["azure-data-messaging","azure-identity-secrets","kubernetes"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-compute/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Azure&#39;s compute options form a spectrum: <strong>VMs</strong> (most control, most ops work) → <strong>AKS</strong> → <strong>Container Apps</strong> → <strong>App Service</strong> → <strong>Functions</strong> (least ops work, most opinionated).</li>\n<li><strong>App Service</strong>: classic managed web hosting. <strong>Deployment slots</strong> let you warm up a new version on its own hostname, then swap it into production with near-zero downtime.</li>\n<li><strong>Azure Functions</strong>: small units of code triggered by events (HTTP, timer, queue, etc.), billed per execution, scaling to zero. Use the modern <strong>isolated worker model</strong>, not the legacy in-process one.</li>\n<li><strong>Durable Functions</strong> extend Functions with stateful, checkpointed workflows that can wait for days and survive restarts.</li>\n<li><strong>Container Apps</strong>: serverless containers on managed Kubernetes underneath — most of AKS&#39;s capability (autoscaling via KEDA, traffic-split revisions, Dapr) without having to run a cluster yourself.</li>\n<li><strong>AKS</strong>: full Kubernetes, for when you genuinely need its API/ecosystem and have the team to operate it.</li>\n<li>Pick based on how much operational effort you want to own, not which option sounds most powerful.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-options-at-a-glance\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-compute/the-options-at-a-glance\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The options at a glance <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>More control, more ops ◀──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────▶ Less ops, more opinionated\n  Virtual Machines      AKS (Kubernetes)      Container Apps        App Service          Azure Functions\n  any software          full K8s control      serverless containers  managed web hosting  event-driven functions</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Every option on this spectrum is the exact same trade-off from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cloud-azure-overview/service-models-who-manages-what\">Cloud fundamentals</a>, just sliced more finely — more control always means more of the operational burden sits with your team.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th><strong>App Service</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Azure Functions</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Container Apps</strong></th>\n<th><strong>AKS</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Virtual Machines</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Unit</td>\n<td>Web app (code or container)</td>\n<td>Function (code; containers supported)</td>\n<td>Container app (one or more containers)</td>\n<td>Pods in a Kubernetes cluster</td>\n<td>VM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Abstraction</td>\n<td>PaaS</td>\n<td>Serverless FaaS</td>\n<td>Serverless containers (managed K8s + KEDA + Envoy underneath)</td>\n<td>Managed Kubernetes control plane</td>\n<td>IaaS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scale</td>\n<td>Manual/autoscale rules per plan; scale out to N instances</td>\n<td>Automatic per event load; <strong>scale to zero</strong> (consumption plans)</td>\n<td>Automatic via <strong>KEDA</strong> (HTTP, queue length, CPU…); <strong>scale to zero</strong></td>\n<td>HPA, KEDA, cluster autoscaler</td>\n<td>VM scale sets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pricing</td>\n<td>Per plan (always on)</td>\n<td>Per execution (consumption) or per plan</td>\n<td>Per vCPU-second/GiB-second (consumption) or dedicated workload profiles</td>\n<td>Nodes (VMs) + optional uptime SLA</td>\n<td>VM hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Best for</td>\n<td>Classic web apps &amp; APIs, simple deployment</td>\n<td>Event glue, background triggers, spiky/scheduled jobs</td>\n<td>Microservices, APIs, workers, jobs, without running K8s</td>\n<td>Large platforms needing full K8s control and ecosystem</td>\n<td>Legacy, special OS needs, lift-and-shift</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ops effort</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Lowest</td>\n<td>Low–medium</td>\n<td><strong>High</strong></td>\n<td>Highest</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"azure-app-service\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-compute/azure-app-service\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Azure App Service <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><strong>App Service</strong> hosts web apps, REST APIs and background WebJobs, on Windows or Linux, from <strong>code</strong> (.NET published output) or a <strong>container image</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>App Service plan</strong> = the underlying compute (VM size and instance count) that one or more apps share. The tier determines features:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Tier</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Free/Shared (F1/D1)</td>\n<td>Dev/test only; shared infrastructure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Basic (B)</td>\n<td>Dedicated, manual scale, no slots</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Standard (S) / Premium v3 (P0v3–P5mv3)</strong></td>\n<td>Autoscale, <strong>deployment slots</strong>, VNet integration, zone redundancy (Premium)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Isolated v2 (App Service Environment)</td>\n<td>Single-tenant, fully network-isolated</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"deployment-slots\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-compute/deployment-slots\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Deployment slots</h3>\n<p>Slots are <strong>live apps with their own hostnames</strong> (e.g. <code>shop-api-staging.azurewebsites.net</code>) on the same plan. Deploy to <code>staging</code>, <strong>warm it up</strong> and run smoke tests, then <strong>swap</strong> with production: the swap is near-instant, and you roll back by swapping again — the same zero-downtime instinct behind blue/green deployments generally, just built directly into the platform.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Shell</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>az webapp deployment slot create -g rg-shop -n shop-api <span class=\"tk-attr\">--slot</span> staging\naz webapp deploy -g rg-shop -n shop-api <span class=\"tk-attr\">--slot</span> staging <span class=\"tk-attr\">--src-path</span> app.zip <span class=\"tk-attr\">--type</span> zip\naz webapp deployment slot swap -g rg-shop -n shop-api <span class=\"tk-attr\">--slot</span> staging <span class=\"tk-attr\">--target-slot</span> production</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Settings can be marked <strong>slot-specific</strong> (&quot;sticky&quot;), like a connection string pointing to a staging database, so they don&#39;t swap.</p>\n<h3 id=\"configuration-and-features\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-compute/configuration-and-features\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Configuration and features</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>App settings</strong> become <strong>environment variables</strong> (<code>Payments__ApiKey</code>); connection strings likewise — exactly the environment-variable binding <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/configuration-options/environment-variables-need-their-own-naming-convention\">Configuration</a> described. Use <strong>Key Vault references</strong>: <code>@Microsoft.KeyVault(SecretUri=https://kv-shop.vault.azure.net/secrets/StripeKey/)</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Managed identity</strong> (system- or user-assigned) for passwordless access to SQL, Storage and Key Vault (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets\">Identity &amp; secrets</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Autoscale</strong> rules (CPU &gt; 70% → +1 instance) or <strong>automatic scaling</strong> (Premium v3: HTTP-traffic based).</li>\n<li><strong>Health check</strong> path: unhealthy instances are removed from the load balancer and replaced.</li>\n<li><strong>VNet integration</strong> (outbound to private resources) and <strong>private endpoints</strong> (private inbound).</li>\n<li><strong>Always On</strong> (keeps the app loaded; avoids idle unloading), <strong>ARR affinity</strong> (sticky sessions; turn it <strong>off</strong> for stateless apps), HTTP/2, TLS, custom domains, managed certificates, Easy Auth (built-in authentication).</li>\n<li><strong>Diagnose and solve problems</strong>, Kudu/SCM site, log streaming, Application Insights integration.</li>\n<li><strong>WebJobs</strong> run background tasks alongside the app (prefer hosted services or Functions in new code).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"azure-functions\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-compute/azure-functions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Azure Functions <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Azure Functions</strong> runs small pieces of code in response to <strong>triggers</strong>, with automatic scaling and pay-per-use pricing.</p>\n<h3 id=\"triggers-and-bindings\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-compute/triggers-and-bindings\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Triggers and bindings</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Triggers</th>\n<th>Bindings (inputs/outputs without SDK boilerplate)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>HTTP, <strong>Timer</strong> (CRON), <strong>Service Bus</strong> queue/topic, <strong>Storage Queue</strong>, <strong>Blob</strong> (or Event Grid-based blob), <strong>Event Grid</strong>, <strong>Event Hubs</strong>, <strong>Cosmos DB change feed</strong>, Kafka, RabbitMQ, SignalR, SQL change tracking</td>\n<td>Blob, Table, Cosmos DB, Service Bus, Event Hubs, SQL, SignalR, Storage Queues…</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"the-isolated-worker-model\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-compute/the-isolated-worker-model\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The isolated worker model</h3>\n<p>Modern .NET functions run in the <strong>isolated worker model</strong>: your code runs in a <strong>separate process</strong> from the Functions host, so you control the .NET version and dependencies, and get <strong>normal DI, middleware and configuration</strong> like any .NET app. The older <strong>in-process</strong> model is being retired — check the <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-functions/dotnet-isolated-process-guide\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">isolated worker guide</a> in Further reading for the current end-of-support date before relying on it, since Microsoft&#39;s retirement schedules can shift.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Program.cs</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> builder = <span class=\"tk-ty\">FunctionsApplication</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateBuilder</span>(args);\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ConfigureFunctionsWebApplication</span>();                 <span class=\"tk-com\">// ASP.NET Core integration for HTTP triggers</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddApplicationInsightsTelemetryWorkerService</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">ConfigureFunctionsApplicationInsights</span>();\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddDbContext</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span>&gt;(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseSqlServer</span>(builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configuration</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetConnectionString</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop&quot;</span>)));\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IInvoiceService</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvoiceService</span>&gt;();\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Build</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Run</span>();\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Functions</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderFunctions</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IInvoiceService</span> invoices, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ILogger</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderFunctions</span>&gt; log)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[Function(nameof(GenerateInvoice))]</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[BlobOutput(&quot;invoices/{OrderId}.pdf&quot;, Connection = &quot;Storage&quot;)]</span>               <span class=\"tk-com\">// output binding: return value written to a blob</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>[]&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GenerateInvoice</span>(\n        <span class=\"tk-attr\">[ServiceBusTrigger(&quot;order-placed&quot;, Connection = &quot;ServiceBus&quot;)]</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span> message, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n    {\n        log.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LogInformation</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Generating invoice for {OrderId}&quot;</span>, message.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> invoices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RenderPdfAsync</span>(message.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderId</span>, ct);\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[Function(nameof(NightlyCleanup))]</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">NightlyCleanup</span>(<span class=\"tk-attr\">[TimerTrigger(&quot;0 0 2 * * *&quot;)]</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimerInfo</span> timer, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt; invoices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">PurgeDraftsAsync</span>(ct);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// 02:00 daily</span>\n\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[Function(nameof(GetInvoice))]</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IActionResult</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetInvoice</span>(<span class=\"tk-attr\">[HttpTrigger(AuthorizationLevel.Function, &quot;get&quot;, Route = &quot;invoices/{id}&quot;)]</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpRequest</span> req, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> id)\n        =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> invoices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FindAsync</span>(id) <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> { } inv ? <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OkObjectResult</span>(inv) : <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFoundResult</span>();\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Service Bus triggers handle <strong>message settlement</strong> for you: complete on success; on exception, retry and eventually <strong>dead-letter</strong> according to the queue&#39;s max delivery count — exactly the retry/dead-letter pattern <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events/retries-poison-messages-and-dead-lettering\">Messaging &amp; events</a> described, here managed entirely by the trigger rather than hand-coded.</p>\n<h3 id=\"hosting-plans\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-compute/hosting-plans\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Hosting plans</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Plan</th>\n<th>Scale</th>\n<th>Cold start</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Flex Consumption</strong> (recommended)</td>\n<td>Event-driven, to zero, fast scale-out</td>\n<td>Reduced; optional always-ready instances</td>\n<td>VNet integration, per-function scaling, configurable memory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Consumption (classic)</td>\n<td>To zero</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>5–10 min execution timeout; Linux Consumption is being retired in favour of Flex</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Premium (Elastic Premium)</strong></td>\n<td>Pre-warmed instances + burst</td>\n<td><strong>None</strong> (always ready)</td>\n<td>VNet, longer runs, pricier</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dedicated (App Service plan)</td>\n<td>Like App Service</td>\n<td>None</td>\n<td>Predictable cost; reuse an existing plan</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Container Apps</td>\n<td>Functions on Container Apps</td>\n<td>Configurable</td>\n<td>Unify with other containers</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Functions pitfalls</div><p><strong>Cold starts</strong> on consumption plans (first request after idle takes seconds: use Flex with always-ready instances or Premium for latency-sensitive HTTP); <strong>execution time limits</strong>; <strong>don&#39;t block or hold state in memory</strong> (instances come and go); make triggers <strong>idempotent</strong> (at-least-once delivery); <strong>share HttpClient and SDK clients</strong> via DI (singletons), not per invocation; watch concurrency settings so you don&#39;t overwhelm downstream databases.</p>\n</aside>\n<h3 id=\"durable-functions\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-compute/durable-functions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Durable Functions</h3>\n<p>An extension for <strong>stateful workflows</strong> in code: <strong>orchestrator</strong> functions call <strong>activity</strong> functions, and every step&#39;s result is saved as a history of events rather than overwriting a single &quot;current state&quot; value — the same idea as <strong>event sourcing</strong> (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events/event-sourcing\">Messaging &amp; events</a> covers it in depth later), just applied here to a <em>workflow&#39;s</em> progress instead of a domain object&#39;s state. That history is what lets an orchestration survive a restart: on recovery, it replays the saved events to rebuild exactly where it left off, which is also why it can sit paused waiting for something — a timer, a human approval — for days without holding any resources open.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-attr\">[Function(nameof(OrderWorkflow))]</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderWorkflow</span>(<span class=\"tk-attr\">[OrchestrationTrigger]</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TaskOrchestrationContext</span> ctx)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> order = ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetInput</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">OrderPlaced</span>&gt;()!;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CallActivityAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">nameof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReserveStock</span>), order);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CallActivityAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">nameof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ChargePayment</span>), order); }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">TaskFailedException</span>) { <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CallActivityAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">nameof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReleaseStock</span>), order); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;cancelled&quot;</span>; }   <span class=\"tk-com\">// compensation (saga)</span>\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> cts = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationTokenSource</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> approval = ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WaitForExternalEvent</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ManagerApproval&quot;</span>);             <span class=\"tk-com\">// human-in-the-loop</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> timeout = ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateTimer</span>(ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CurrentUtcDateTime</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddDays</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>), cts.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Token</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WhenAny</span>(approval, timeout) == timeout) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;expired&quot;</span>;\n    cts.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cancel</span>();\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CallActivityAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">nameof</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShipOrder</span>), order);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;completed&quot;</span>;\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice <code>catch (TaskFailedException)</code> calling <code>ReleaseStock</code> — this is a saga&#39;s compensating action, exactly the orchestration style <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events/sagas-and-process-managers\">Messaging &amp; events</a> described, just hosted inside Durable Functions instead of a message-broker state machine. Patterns: <strong>function chaining</strong>, <strong>fan-out/fan-in</strong> (parallel activities, then aggregate), <strong>async HTTP APIs</strong> (202 plus a status endpoint built in), <strong>monitors</strong> (polling loops), <strong>human interaction</strong>, and <strong>durable entities</strong>. Orchestrator code must be <strong>deterministic</strong> (no <code>DateTime.Now</code>, random numbers or direct I/O; use the context&#39;s APIs), because it is replayed. The <strong>Durable Task Scheduler</strong> is a managed backend for it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"azure-container-apps\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-compute/azure-container-apps\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Azure Container Apps <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Container Apps</strong> runs containers on a <strong>managed, serverless Kubernetes-based platform</strong>: you get revisions, ingress, autoscaling (KEDA), Dapr and jobs <strong>without operating a cluster</strong> — the middle point on the compute spectrum, deliberately positioned to give most of AKS&#39;s capability without most of AKS&#39;s operational cost.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Environment</strong></td>\n<td>A secure boundary (shared VNet and Log Analytics) for a group of apps that can talk to each other internally</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Container app</strong></td>\n<td>One or more containers (main + sidecars) with ingress, scale rules, secrets and identity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Revision</strong></td>\n<td>An immutable snapshot of an app version. <strong>Multiple revisions</strong> can run with <strong>traffic splitting</strong> (e.g. 90/10 canary, blue/green)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ingress</strong></td>\n<td>External (internet) or internal (environment-only) HTTP/TCP ingress with TLS, custom domains, session affinity options</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Scale rules</strong></td>\n<td><strong>KEDA</strong> scalers: HTTP concurrency, CPU/memory, <strong>Service Bus queue length</strong>, Kafka lag, cron… min replicas 0–N</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Jobs</strong></td>\n<td>Run-to-completion containers: manual, <strong>scheduled</strong> (cron) or <strong>event-driven</strong> (one job per queue message batch)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Dapr</strong> (optional)</td>\n<td>Sidecar building blocks: service invocation, pub/sub, state, bindings, secrets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Workload profiles</strong></td>\n<td>Consumption (serverless) or dedicated (reserved hardware, GPUs)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Shell</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>az containerapp up -n shop-api -g rg-shop <span class=\"tk-attr\">--environment</span> cae-shop \\\n  <span class=\"tk-attr\">--image</span> shopacr.azurecr.io/<span class=\"tk-ty\">shop-api</span>:<span class=\"tk-num\">1.4.2</span> <span class=\"tk-attr\">--ingress</span> external <span class=\"tk-attr\">--target-port</span> <span class=\"tk-num\">8080</span> \\\n  <span class=\"tk-attr\">--env-vars</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT</span>=Production <span class=\"tk-ty\">ConnectionStrings__Shop</span>=<span class=\"tk-ty\">secretref</span>:sql-cs</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Bicep</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Scale a worker on Service Bus queue length; scale to zero when idle</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">scale</span>: {\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">minReplicas</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">maxReplicas</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">rules</span>: [ {\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">'orders-queue'</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">custom</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">type</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">'azure-servicebus'</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">metadata</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">queueName</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">'orders'</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">namespace</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">'sb-shop'</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">messageCount</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">'50'</span> }, <span class=\"tk-ty\">identity</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">'system'</span> }\n  } ]\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<p>It&#39;s a sweet spot for .NET microservices: run an API, a worker, an outbox publisher and scheduled jobs as containers with autoscaling and zero cluster management. <strong>.NET Aspire deploys to Container Apps</strong> out of the box (<code>azd up</code>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"azure-kubernetes-service-aks\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-compute/azure-kubernetes-service-aks\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS) <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p><strong>AKS</strong> is managed Kubernetes: Azure runs the <strong>control plane</strong>, and you manage the <strong>node pools</strong> (VM scale sets) and everything you deploy. Choose it when you need the full Kubernetes API and ecosystem (Helm charts, operators, service meshes, custom controllers), many workloads on a shared platform, multi-cloud consistency, or fine-grained control over networking and scheduling, <strong>and</strong> you have the platform-engineering capacity to run it. See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/kubernetes\">Kubernetes</a>.</p>\n<p>Key AKS integrations: <strong>Workload Identity</strong> (pods get Entra identities), <strong>Key Vault CSI driver</strong>, <strong>ACR integration</strong>, Azure CNI networking, <strong>KEDA</strong> and <strong>cluster autoscaler</strong> add-ons, the application routing add-on (managed NGINX ingress), Azure Monitor Container Insights and managed Prometheus/Grafana, and <strong>AKS Automatic</strong> (an opinionated, more managed mode).</p>\n<h2 id=\"virtual-machines-and-others\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-compute/virtual-machines-and-others\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Virtual machines and others <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Virtual Machines / VM Scale Sets</strong>: full OS control. Lift-and-shift of legacy .NET Framework apps on IIS, software that needs special OS configuration, or licensing constraints. You own patching, hardening, backups and scaling.</li>\n<li><strong>Azure Spring Apps</strong> has been retired (it was for Java). <strong>Azure Static Web Apps</strong> serves SPAs with optional Functions APIs. <strong>Azure Batch</strong> handles large parallel compute jobs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"choosing-compute-a-decision-guide\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-compute/choosing-compute-a-decision-guide\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Choosing compute: a decision guide <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Is it a small piece of code triggered by events/timers, spiky or infrequent?    → Azure Functions (Flex Consumption)\nLong-running stateful workflow, fan-out/in, human approval?                     → Durable Functions (or Container Apps + Temporal/MassTransit sagas)\nA web app / API, want the simplest PaaS, maybe a few apps on one plan?          → App Service (code or container)\nSeveral containerised microservices + workers + jobs, autoscale (to zero), no cluster ops? → Container Apps\nNeed full Kubernetes, large multi-team platform, existing K8s tooling and skills? → AKS\nLegacy Windows/IIS app, special OS dependencies, lift-and-shift?               → VMs (or App Service Windows / Managed Instance for SQL)</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Interview comparison</th>\n<th>Key difference</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>App Service vs Container Apps</td>\n<td>App Service: classic PaaS web hosting with slots, always-on plans, code or containers. Container Apps: containers-first, KEDA event scaling, scale to zero, revisions with traffic splitting, Dapr, jobs: better for microservices and workers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Container Apps vs AKS</td>\n<td>Container Apps hides Kubernetes (no cluster upgrades, node management or K8s API access); AKS exposes the full Kubernetes API with all its power and operational responsibility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Functions vs Container Apps</td>\n<td>Functions: a programming model with triggers/bindings and per-execution billing. Container Apps: any container and any framework (ASP.NET Core, workers), with event-driven scaling via KEDA</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Functions consumption vs premium</td>\n<td>Consumption: scale to zero, cold starts, time limits. Premium: pre-warmed instances (no cold start), VNet, longer executions, always paying for minimum instances</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How would you choose between App Service, Container Apps, Functions and AKS?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Functions for event-triggered or scheduled small units of work with per-execution billing; App Service for straightforward web apps and APIs with slots and minimal ops; Container Apps for containerised microservices, workers and jobs with KEDA autoscaling and scale to zero without managing Kubernetes; AKS when you need full Kubernetes control and ecosystem and can operate it. Trade control against operational effort.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are App Service deployment slots and why use them?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Separate live instances of an app (e.g. staging) on the same plan, each with its own hostname and settings. You deploy and warm up in staging, then swap with production for near-zero-downtime releases and instant rollback by swapping back. Sticky settings stay with a slot.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What&#39;s the isolated worker model in Azure Functions?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Functions code runs in a separate .NET process from the Functions host, so you choose the .NET version and dependencies and use standard DI, middleware and configuration. It replaces the in-process model, which Microsoft is retiring (check the current end-of-support date before relying on it, since retirement schedules can shift).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What causes cold starts in Azure Functions and how do you mitigate them?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>On consumption plans idle apps scale to zero, so the next request must allocate an instance, start the runtime and load your app. Mitigate with Flex Consumption always-ready instances or the Premium plan (pre-warmed), smaller startup work, ReadyToRun/AOT, and avoiding heavy initialisation in the startup path.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are Durable Functions?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>An Azure Functions extension for stateful, long-running workflows written as orchestrator code that calls activity functions. State is checkpointed and replayed, so orchestrations survive restarts. It supports chaining, fan-out/fan-in, timers, external events and human interaction, monitors and entities. Orchestrators must be deterministic.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How does Azure Container Apps scale?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>With KEDA-based scale rules: HTTP concurrency, CPU/memory, or event sources like Service Bus queue length, Kafka lag or cron, between min and max replicas, including scaling to zero. Revisions allow traffic splitting for canary and blue/green deployments.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-compute/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/guide/technology-choices/compute-decision-tree\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Choose an Azure compute service</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">App Service overview</a> · <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/app-service/deploy-staging-slots\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Deployment slots</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-functions/dotnet-isolated-process-guide\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Azure Functions isolated worker guide</a> · <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-functions/durable/durable-functions-overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Durable Functions</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/container-apps/overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Azure Container Apps overview</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/aks/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AKS documentation</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-compute/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Most of this chapter needs real Azure resources to exercise (App Service, Functions, Container Apps, AKS). What you can verify locally is the mechanism behind two specific claims: that replaying a saved event history is deterministic (why Durable Functions survives a restart), and how a KEDA-style scale rule computes replica count, including scale-to-zero. Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (plain console app, .NET 8/9, BCL only).</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: a Durable Functions orchestration survives a restart because its progress is saved as a history of</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// events, and recovery REPLAYS that history to deterministically rebuild the same state. Model that directly:</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// replaying the same event list twice, from scratch, must produce identical state both times.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> history = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ReserveStock&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ChargePayment&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ShipOrder&quot;</span> };\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> firstReplay = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Replay</span>(history);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> secondReplay = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Replay</span>(history); <span class=\"tk-com\">// simulates &quot;the process restarted and replayed from the saved history again&quot;</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (firstReplay != secondReplay)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected deterministic replay to produce the same result, got '{firstReplay}' vs '{secondReplay}'&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: replaying the same saved event history twice deterministically reproduces the same state ('{firstReplay}') — this is why an orchestration survives a restart&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: KEDA scale rules compute replica count from an event metric (e.g. queue length) against a threshold,</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// clamped to [minReplicas, maxReplicas], and CAN scale to zero when minReplicas is 0 and the queue is empty.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ComputeReplicas</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> queueLength, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> messageCountPerReplica, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> minReplicas, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> maxReplicas)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> desired = queueLength == <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span> ? <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span> : (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>)<span class=\"tk-ty\">Math</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ceiling</span>(queueLength / (<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span>)messageCountPerReplica);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Math</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Clamp</span>(desired, minReplicas, maxReplicas);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> scaledToZero = <span class=\"tk-ty\">ComputeReplicas</span>(queueLength: <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, messageCountPerReplica: <span class=\"tk-num\">50</span>, minReplicas: <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, maxReplicas: <span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (scaledToZero != <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected an empty queue with minReplicas=0 to scale to zero, got {scaledToZero}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: an empty queue with minReplicas=0 scales to 0 replicas — the scale-to-zero claim&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> scaledUp = <span class=\"tk-ty\">ComputeReplicas</span>(queueLength: <span class=\"tk-num\">220</span>, messageCountPerReplica: <span class=\"tk-num\">50</span>, minReplicas: <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, maxReplicas: <span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (scaledUp != <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>) <span class=\"tk-com\">// ceil(220/50) = 5</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected 5 replicas for 220 queued messages at 50/replica, got {scaledUp}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: 220 queued messages at 50/replica scales to {scaledUp} replicas, clamped to [0, 20]&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> clampedToMax = <span class=\"tk-ty\">ComputeReplicas</span>(queueLength: <span class=\"tk-num\">5000</span>, messageCountPerReplica: <span class=\"tk-num\">50</span>, minReplicas: <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, maxReplicas: <span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (clampedToMax != <span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected scaling to clamp at maxReplicas=20, got {clampedToMax}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: a huge backlog clamps at maxReplicas ({clampedToMax}), not an unbounded number of replicas&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here (needs real Azure resources: App Service, Functions, Container Apps, AKS) — see Further reading:</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - Deployment slot swap behavior, App Service tiers</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - Real Functions cold starts, hosting plans, isolated worker retirement dates</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - Container Apps revisions, traffic splitting, Dapr</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - AKS control plane / node pool mechanics</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All checkable claims verified.&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Replay</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; eventHistory)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> state = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;start&quot;</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> step <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> eventHistory) state = <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;{state}-&gt;{step}&quot;</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> state;\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-options-at-a-glance","text":"The options at a glance","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"azure-app-service","text":"Azure App Service","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"deployment-slots","text":"Deployment slots","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"configuration-and-features","text":"Configuration and features","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"azure-functions","text":"Azure Functions","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"triggers-and-bindings","text":"Triggers and bindings","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-isolated-worker-model","text":"The isolated worker model","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"hosting-plans","text":"Hosting plans","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"durable-functions","text":"Durable Functions","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"azure-container-apps","text":"Azure Container Apps","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"azure-kubernetes-service-aks","text":"Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS)","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"virtual-machines-and-others","text":"Virtual machines and others","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"choosing-compute-a-decision-guide","text":"Choosing compute: a decision guide","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":3249,"minutes":15,"num":"8.3","part":"p8"},"azure-data-messaging":{"id":"azure-data-messaging","title":"Azure Data, Storage and Messaging Services","summary":"The managed building blocks behind most .NET backends. Azure SQL Database (tiers, serverless, HA, geo-replication, security), Azure Database for PostgreSQL, Cosmos DB, Storage accounts (Blob tiers, SAS, Queues, Tables, Files), Azure Managed Redis, Service Bus vs Event Grid vs Event Hubs, and using Azure SDK clients correctly from .NET.","layer":"data","where":"Data & state › Managed cloud data services (Azure)","fits":"<p>These are the cloud-hosted versions of layer-5 stores (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals\">Relational fundamentals</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/nosql-redis\">NoSQL &amp; Redis</a>) and layer-6 brokers (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events\">Messaging &amp; events</a>), provisioned as layer-7 infrastructure (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cloud-azure-overview\">Cloud &amp; Azure overview</a>) and accessed with managed identity (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets\">Identity &amp; secrets</a>).</p>\n","prereqs":["cloud-azure-overview","relational-fundamentals","messaging-events"],"next":["azure-identity-secrets","cicd-iac"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-data-messaging/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Every service here is the <strong>managed, Azure-hosted</strong> version of something already covered from first principles earlier: SQL, NoSQL, Redis, message brokers.</li>\n<li><strong>Azure SQL Database</strong> gives you SQL Server as a managed service, with built-in HA, point-in-time restore, and passwordless auth via managed identity.</li>\n<li><strong>Blob Storage</strong> has four access tiers (Hot → Cool → Cold → Archive) trading storage cost against access cost; lifecycle policies can move blobs between them automatically as they age.</li>\n<li>Pick <strong>Service Bus</strong> for reliable business commands/events, <strong>Event Grid</strong> for lightweight &quot;something happened&quot; push notifications, and <strong>Event Hubs</strong> for a high-volume event firehose — they solve different problems, not competing versions of the same one.</li>\n<li>Treat every Azure SDK client (<code>BlobServiceClient</code>, <code>ServiceBusClient</code>, …) like <code>HttpClient</code>: create it once, register it as a singleton, and let it manage its own connections and retries.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-map\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-data-messaging/the-map\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The map <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Every concept in this chapter is something earlier chapters already explained from first principles — relational databases, NoSQL, Redis, message brokers. What follows is specifically the <strong>managed, Azure-hosted</strong> version of each one, and what&#39;s different about operating it there.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Service</th>\n<th>One-line description</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Relational (SQL Server engine)</td>\n<td><strong>Azure SQL Database</strong></td>\n<td>Fully managed SQL Server database (PaaS); SQL Managed Instance for near-100% compatibility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relational (open source)</td>\n<td><strong>Azure Database for PostgreSQL – Flexible Server</strong></td>\n<td>Managed PostgreSQL (also MySQL Flexible Server)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Document / NoSQL, global</td>\n<td><strong>Azure Cosmos DB</strong></td>\n<td>Multi-model, globally distributed, single-digit-ms, multiple consistency levels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Files and objects</td>\n<td><strong>Blob Storage</strong> (in a Storage account)</td>\n<td>Massively scalable object storage with access tiers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Simple queues</td>\n<td><strong>Storage Queues</strong></td>\n<td>Cheap, basic queue (64 KB messages)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Key/attribute tables</td>\n<td><strong>Table Storage</strong> (or Cosmos DB Table API)</td>\n<td>Cheap NoSQL key/attribute store</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SMB/NFS file shares</td>\n<td><strong>Azure Files</strong></td>\n<td>Lift-and-shift shared drives</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cache</td>\n<td><strong>Azure Managed Redis</strong> (successor of Azure Cache for Redis)</td>\n<td>Managed Redis for caching, sessions, pub/sub</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enterprise messaging</td>\n<td><strong>Service Bus</strong></td>\n<td>Queues + topics with sessions, DLQ, transactions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Event routing</td>\n<td><strong>Event Grid</strong></td>\n<td>Push-based pub/sub for discrete events, reactive integration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Event streaming</td>\n<td><strong>Event Hubs</strong></td>\n<td>High-throughput, Kafka-compatible event ingestion and streaming</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Search</td>\n<td><strong>Azure AI Search</strong></td>\n<td>Full-text, vector and hybrid search over your data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Analytics</td>\n<td>Fabric / Synapse, Data Explorer</td>\n<td>Big data and telemetry analytics</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"azure-sql-database\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-data-messaging/azure-sql-database\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Azure SQL Database <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<h3 id=\"deployment-and-purchasing-models\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-data-messaging/deployment-and-purchasing-models\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Deployment and purchasing models</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Single database</strong></td>\n<td>One isolated database, its own resources. The most common choice</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Elastic pool</strong></td>\n<td>Many databases share a pool of resources: SaaS <strong>database-per-tenant</strong> with varying load</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SQL Managed Instance</strong></td>\n<td>Near-complete SQL Server compatibility (SQL Agent, cross-database queries, CLR) for lift-and-shift</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SQL Server on Azure VMs</strong></td>\n<td>IaaS: full control, you manage it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Purchasing model</th>\n<th>Tiers</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>vCore</strong> (recommended)</td>\n<td><strong>General Purpose</strong> (remote storage, balanced), <strong>Business Critical</strong> (local SSD, built-in readable replica, lowest latency), <strong>Hyperscale</strong> (up to 128 TB, fast scaling, multiple read replicas, fast backups/restores)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DTU (legacy, bundled)</td>\n<td>Basic, Standard, Premium</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Serverless</strong> (General Purpose / Hyperscale)</td>\n<td>Auto-scales compute within a range and can <strong>auto-pause</strong> when idle: great for dev/test and intermittent workloads (first query after a pause has a delay)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"availability-dr-and-backups\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-data-messaging/availability-dr-and-backups\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Availability, DR and backups</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Built-in <strong>high availability</strong> (99.99% SLA); <strong>zone redundancy</strong> for General Purpose, Business Critical and Hyperscale.</li>\n<li><strong>Automated backups</strong> with <strong>point-in-time restore</strong> (7–35 days) plus long-term retention (up to 10 years).</li>\n<li><strong>Active geo-replication</strong> (readable secondaries in other regions) and <strong>failover groups</strong> (automatic geo-failover behind a stable listener endpoint, so connection strings don&#39;t change).</li>\n<li><strong>Read scale-out</strong>: route read-only queries with <code>ApplicationIntent=ReadOnly</code> to a replica.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"security\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-data-messaging/security\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Security</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Microsoft Entra authentication</strong>, including <strong>managed identity</strong>: <code>Authentication=Active Directory Default</code> (or <code>Active Directory Managed Identity</code>) in the connection string, no password. Create contained users from Entra identities and grant roles (<code>db_datareader</code>, <code>db_datawriter</code>, or custom least-privilege roles).</li>\n<li><strong>Firewall rules</strong> / <strong>private endpoints</strong> with public access disabled.</li>\n<li><strong>TDE</strong> (encryption at rest, on by default), TLS in transit, <strong>Always Encrypted</strong> (client-side column encryption), <strong>dynamic data masking</strong>, <strong>row-level security</strong>, <strong>auditing</strong>, <strong>Microsoft Defender for SQL</strong> (threat detection, vulnerability assessment), and the <strong>ledger</strong> feature (tamper evidence).</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Server=tcp:sql-shop-prod.database.windows.net,1433;Database=shop;Authentication=Active Directory Default;Encrypt=True;</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>SQL</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-com\">-- in the database: grant the app's managed identity least privilege</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">CREATE</span> USER [app-shop-api-prod] <span class=\"tk-kw\">FROM</span> EXTERNAL PROVIDER;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">ALTER</span> ROLE db_datareader <span class=\"tk-kw\">ADD</span> MEMBER [app-shop-api-prod];\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">ALTER</span> ROLE db_datawriter <span class=\"tk-kw\">ADD</span> MEMBER [app-shop-api-prod];</code></pre></figure>\n<p><strong>Transient faults</strong> (failovers, reconfigurations, throttling) are a normal part of the PaaS service: always enable retries (<code>EnableRetryOnFailure</code> in EF Core; see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/resilience\">Resilience</a>) — exactly the transient-vs-permanent fault distinction that chapter&#39;s whole opening section is built around. Defaults that differ from on-premises: <strong>RCSI is on</strong>, <strong>Query Store</strong> is on, and automatic tuning can create and drop indexes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"azure-database-for-postgresql\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-data-messaging/azure-database-for-postgresql\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Azure Database for PostgreSQL <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Flexible Server</strong>: managed PostgreSQL with zone-redundant HA, read replicas, PgBouncer built in (connection pooling), extensions (<strong>pgvector</strong>, PostGIS, pg_cron), Entra authentication, private networking, and burstable/general-purpose/memory-optimised tiers, plus stop/start for dev. With Npgsql + EF Core, it&#39;s a first-class option for .NET, often cheaper than SQL Server licensing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"azure-cosmos-db\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-data-messaging/azure-cosmos-db\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Azure Cosmos DB <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Covered conceptually in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/nosql-redis/partitioning-the-one-modelling-decision-you-cant-easily-undo\">NoSQL &amp; Redis</a>. The Azure-specific essentials:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>APIs</strong></td>\n<td>NoSQL (native, recommended for new apps), MongoDB (vCore or RU), Cassandra, Gremlin (graph), Table, and PostgreSQL (distributed via Citus)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Account → database → container → items</strong></td>\n<td>The container is the unit of scale with a <strong>partition key</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Throughput</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Request Units</strong> (RU/s): provisioned (manual/<strong>autoscale</strong>) or <strong>serverless</strong>; at database or container level</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Consistency</strong></td>\n<td>Strong, Bounded staleness, <strong>Session</strong> (default), Consistent prefix, Eventual</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Global distribution</strong></td>\n<td>Add regions with a click; multi-region writes; automatic failover; 99.999% SLA for multi-region</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Change feed</strong></td>\n<td>Ordered stream of changes per partition, consumed by Functions triggers or the change feed processor: materialised views, events, search sync</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Indexing</strong></td>\n<td>Automatic indexing of all properties by default (tune policies to cut RU cost); composite, spatial and <strong>vector</strong> indexes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>TTL</strong></td>\n<td>Automatic document expiry</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Transactions</strong></td>\n<td>Within one logical partition (transactional batch, stored procedures)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Use the <strong>singleton <code>CosmosClient</code></strong>, prefer <strong>point reads</strong> (id + partition key) over queries, log <code>RequestCharge</code> to understand costs, and handle <strong>429 (rate limited)</strong>, which the SDK retries automatically within limits.</p>\n<h2 id=\"storage-accounts-blob-queue-table-files\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-data-messaging/storage-accounts-blob-queue-table-files\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Storage accounts: Blob, Queue, Table, Files <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>A <strong>Storage account</strong> is a namespace providing several services, with redundancy options: <strong>LRS</strong> (3 copies, one datacentre), <strong>ZRS</strong> (across zones), <strong>GRS/RA-GRS</strong> (geo-replicated to the paired region, optionally readable), <strong>GZRS/RA-GZRS</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"blob-storage\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-data-messaging/blob-storage\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Blob Storage</h3>\n<p>Objects (&quot;blobs&quot;) in <strong>containers</strong>: images, documents, exports, backups, logs, static website assets, data lake files (with hierarchical namespace: ADLS Gen2).</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Access tier</th>\n<th>For</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Hot</strong></td>\n<td>Frequently accessed</td>\n<td>Highest storage cost, lowest access cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cool</strong></td>\n<td>Infrequent (≥ 30 days)</td>\n<td>Cheaper storage, higher access cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cold</strong></td>\n<td>Rare (≥ 90 days)</td>\n<td>Cheaper still</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Archive</strong></td>\n<td>Long-term retention (≥ 180 days)</td>\n<td>Offline; <strong>rehydration takes hours</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Lifecycle management policies</strong> move or delete blobs automatically by age. <strong>Soft delete</strong>, <strong>versioning</strong> and <strong>immutable storage</strong> (WORM, legal hold) protect against accidental and malicious deletion.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAzureClients</span>(c =&gt;\n{\n    c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddBlobServiceClient</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Uri</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://stshopprod.blob.core.windows.net&quot;</span>));\n    c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseCredential</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DefaultAzureCredential</span>());       <span class=\"tk-com\">// managed identity in Azure; developer login locally</span>\n});\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvoiceStorage</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">BlobServiceClient</span> blobs)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">BlobContainerClient</span> _container = blobs.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetBlobContainerClient</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;invoices&quot;</span>);\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Uri</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> orderId, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Stream</span> pdf, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> blob = _container.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetBlobClient</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;{DateTime.UtcNow:yyyy/MM}/{orderId}.pdf&quot;</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> blob.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UploadAsync</span>(pdf, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">BlobUploadOptions</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpHeaders</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>() { <span class=\"tk-ty\">ContentType</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;application/pdf&quot;</span> } }, ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> blob.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Uri</span>;\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-com\">// Give a browser time-limited direct access without proxying bytes through your API</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">Uri</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetDownloadLinkAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> blobName, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> key = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> blobs.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetUserDelegationKeyAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UtcNow</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UtcNow</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddMinutes</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">15</span>), ct);  <span class=\"tk-com\">// Entra-backed</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> sas = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">BlobSasBuilder</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">BlobSasPermissions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Read</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UtcNow</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddMinutes</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">15</span>))\n        { <span class=\"tk-ty\">BlobContainerName</span> = _container.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">BlobName</span> = blobName };\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">BlobUriBuilder</span>(_container.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetBlobClient</span>(blobName).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Uri</span>) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Sas</span> = sas.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToSasQueryParameters</span>(key.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>, blobs.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AccountName</span>) }.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToUri</span>();\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><strong>SAS (Shared Access Signature)</strong> tokens grant time-limited, permission-scoped access. Prefer <strong>user delegation SAS</strong> (signed with Entra credentials) over account-key SAS, and prefer <strong>disabling shared key access</strong> entirely in favour of Entra RBAC (<code>Storage Blob Data Contributor</code>). The <strong>valet key</strong> pattern means clients upload and download directly to Blob using SAS URLs, so large files don&#39;t flow through your API — exactly the <strong>claim-check</strong> pattern <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events/message-design-and-versioning\">Messaging &amp; events</a> recommended for large message payloads, here applied to large HTTP payloads instead.</p>\n<h3 id=\"queues-tables-files\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-data-messaging/queues-tables-files\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Queues, Tables, Files</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Storage Queues</strong>: simple, cheap, huge scale, 64 KB messages, at-least-once, 7-day default TTL. No topics, sessions or DLQ (poison queues are handled by Functions). Use for simple work queues; use Service Bus for business messaging.</li>\n<li><strong>Table Storage</strong>: schemaless key/attribute rows by PartitionKey + RowKey. Cheap for simple lookups and logs (Cosmos DB Table API for global distribution).</li>\n<li><strong>Azure Files</strong>: SMB/NFS shares, e.g. legacy apps expecting a network drive, or shared config.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Azurite</strong> is the local emulator for Blob, Queue and Table (Docker image or VS Code extension).</p>\n<h2 id=\"azure-managed-redis\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-data-messaging/azure-managed-redis\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Azure Managed Redis <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Managed Redis (built on Redis Enterprise) succeeds Azure Cache for Redis (the classic Basic/Standard/Premium tiers are on a retirement path). Use it for <code>IDistributedCache</code>/<code>HybridCache</code> L2, sessions, rate limiting, SignalR backplane and output cache storage. Connect with <strong>Entra ID authentication</strong> (managed identity) via <code>Microsoft.Azure.StackExchangeRedis</code>, use private endpoints, pick tiers by memory and throughput, enable zone redundancy for production, and set eviction policies and TTLs (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/nosql-redis/redis-an-in-memory-data-structure-server-not-just-a-cache\">NoSQL &amp; Redis</a>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"service-bus-vs-event-grid-vs-event-hubs\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-data-messaging/service-bus-vs-event-grid-vs-event-hubs\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Service Bus vs Event Grid vs Event Hubs <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>The classic Azure interview question. They solve <strong>different problems</strong> — picking between them is really picking between the Command/Event/Query distinction from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events/commands-events-and-queries-three-different-conversations\">Messaging &amp; events</a> and a question of volume:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th><strong>Service Bus</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Event Grid</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Event Hubs</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Purpose</td>\n<td><strong>Enterprise messaging</strong>: reliable delivery of business <strong>commands and events</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Event routing</strong>: react to <strong>discrete events</strong> (&quot;something happened&quot;)</td>\n<td><strong>Event streaming / ingestion</strong>: millions of events per second</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Model</td>\n<td>Queues, topics + subscriptions (with filters)</td>\n<td>Topics → event subscriptions <strong>push</strong> to handlers (Functions, webhooks, queues, Event Hubs)</td>\n<td>Partitioned log; consumer groups <strong>pull</strong> and track offsets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Message</td>\n<td>Up to 256 KB (Standard) / 100 MB (Premium); rich properties</td>\n<td>Event envelope (CloudEvents/Event Grid schema), up to 1 MB</td>\n<td>Events up to 1 MB; batches</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ordering</td>\n<td>FIFO with <strong>sessions</strong></td>\n<td>Not guaranteed</td>\n<td>Per partition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Delivery</td>\n<td>At-least-once; <strong>peek-lock</strong>, complete/abandon, <strong>DLQ</strong>, scheduled messages, deferral, <strong>duplicate detection</strong>, transactions</td>\n<td>At-least-once push with retries (up to 24 h) and dead-lettering to Blob</td>\n<td>Retention 1–90 days (or longer in Premium/Dedicated); <strong>replay</strong> from any offset; Capture to Blob/ADLS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Throughput</td>\n<td>Thousands–tens of thousands msg/s (Premium scales with messaging units)</td>\n<td>Very high fan-out</td>\n<td><strong>Very high</strong> (throughput/processing units, Kafka endpoint)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical use</td>\n<td>Order processing, payments, workflows, competing consumers, sagas</td>\n<td>Blob created → resize image; resource changed → audit; custom domain events to many subscribers</td>\n<td>Telemetry, clickstreams, IoT, logs, event-sourcing pipelines, stream analytics</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Quick rule:\n  &quot;Process this business message reliably, exactly one handler, maybe in order&quot;  → Service Bus\n  &quot;Something happened; notify whoever cares, push-style, lightweight&quot;            → Event Grid\n  &quot;Ingest a firehose of events/telemetry and let consumers read/replay streams&quot;  → Event Hubs</code></pre></figure>\n<p>They&#39;re often combined: Event Grid routes a <code>BlobCreated</code> event to a Service Bus queue that a worker processes; Event Hubs ingests telemetry that Stream Analytics aggregates.</p>\n<p><strong>Service Bus tiers:</strong> Basic (queues only), Standard (topics, shared capacity), <strong>Premium</strong> (dedicated messaging units, VNet/private endpoints, larger messages, geo-replication: the choice for production at scale). The <strong>Service Bus emulator</strong> container runs it locally for development and tests.</p>\n<h2 id=\"using-azure-sdk-clients-well-from-net\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-data-messaging/using-azure-sdk-clients-well-from-net\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Using Azure SDK clients well from .NET <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>The modern <strong>Azure SDK</strong> (<code>Azure.*</code> packages: <code>Azure.Storage.Blobs</code>, <code>Azure.Messaging.ServiceBus</code>, <code>Azure.Security.KeyVault.Secrets</code>, <code>Microsoft.Azure.Cosmos</code>…) shares conventions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Clients are thread-safe and should be singletons.</strong> Create once and reuse (they hold connections) — exactly the <code>HttpClient</code> lesson from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/http-client/the-two-classic-bugs-and-why-theyre-opposite-mistakes\">Calling other services</a>, applying identically to every Azure SDK client. Register via <strong><code>Microsoft.Extensions.Azure</code></strong> (<code>AddAzureClients</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Authentication with <code>Azure.Identity</code></strong>: <code>DefaultAzureCredential</code> locally and in Azure, or more specific credentials in production (<code>ManagedIdentityCredential</code>, <code>WorkloadIdentityCredential</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Built-in retries</strong> with exponential backoff (<code>ClientOptions.Retry</code>); don&#39;t double-wrap with aggressive Polly retries — exactly the retry-storm risk <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/resilience/retry-done-properly\">Resilience</a> warned about, here specifically meaning don&#39;t retry at two layers for the same SDK call.</li>\n<li><strong>Logging and tracing</strong> integrate with <code>ILogger</code> and OpenTelemetry (<code>Azure.*</code> ActivitySources).</li>\n<li><strong>Async APIs and <code>CancellationToken</code></strong> everywhere; paging via <code>AsyncPageable&lt;T&gt;</code> (<code>await foreach</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Aspire integrations</strong> (<code>builder.AddAzureBlobClient(&quot;blobs&quot;)</code>, <code>AddAzureServiceBusClient</code>, <code>AddAzureCosmosClient</code>) set all of this up with health checks.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAzureClients</span>(c =&gt;\n{\n    c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddServiceBusClientWithNamespace</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;sb-shop-prod.servicebus.windows.net&quot;</span>);\n    c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSecretClient</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Uri</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://kv-shop-prod.vault.azure.net/&quot;</span>));\n    c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddBlobServiceClient</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Uri</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://stshopprod.blob.core.windows.net&quot;</span>));\n    c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseCredential</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DefaultAzureCredential</span>());\n    c.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ConfigureDefaults</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Retry</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MaxRetries</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>);\n});</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Service Bus vs Event Grid vs Event Hubs?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Service Bus is an enterprise message broker for reliable processing of commands and business events: queues and topics, peek-lock, DLQ, sessions for ordering, transactions, duplicate detection. Event Grid is a push-based event router for reacting to discrete events (resource or custom events) with fan-out to handlers. Event Hubs is a partitioned event-streaming platform for high-volume ingestion, with retention, replay and Kafka compatibility.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you connect an app to Azure SQL without a password?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Enable a managed identity on the app, create a contained database user FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER for that identity and grant least-privilege roles, then use a connection string with Authentication=Active Directory Default (or Managed Identity) via Microsoft.Data.SqlClient. No secrets to store or rotate.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are Blob Storage access tiers?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Hot (frequent access), Cool (infrequent, ≥30 days), Cold (rare, ≥90 days) and Archive (offline, ≥180 days, rehydration takes hours), each trading storage cost against access cost. Lifecycle management policies move blobs between tiers automatically.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is a SAS token and what&#39;s the most secure kind?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A Shared Access Signature appended to a storage URL granting time-limited, permission-scoped access to specific resources. A user delegation SAS, signed with Entra ID credentials rather than the account key, is the most secure; combine it with short expiry and least permissions, or avoid SAS by using RBAC with managed identity.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How does Azure SQL provide high availability and disaster recovery?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Built-in HA replicas within the region (optionally zone-redundant), automated backups with point-in-time restore and long-term retention, and active geo-replication or failover groups for cross-region DR with a stable listener endpoint. Apps must retry transient errors during failovers.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why should Azure SDK clients be singletons?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>They&#39;re thread-safe and manage connections, authentication tokens and retry state. Creating them per request wastes resources and can exhaust connections, similar to the HttpClient anti-pattern.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-data-messaging/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-sql/database/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Azure SQL Database documentation</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/storage-blobs-introduction\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Azure Blob Storage introduction</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/service-bus-messaging/compare-messaging-services\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Compare Azure messaging services</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/azure/sdk/azure-sdk-for-dotnet\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Azure SDK for .NET</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/guide/technology-choices/data-store-overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Choose a data store (Azure Architecture Center)</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-data-messaging/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (plain console app, .NET 8/9, top-level statements, BCL only). Most of this chapter describes facts about managed Azure services rather than local algorithms, so this checks the decision logic those facts imply instead of the services themselves.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: the chapter's blob access-tier guidance (Hot / Cool ≥30d / Cold ≥90d / Archive ≥180d) is a</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// consistent decision rule once you plug in &quot;days since last access.&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">RecommendedTier</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> daysSinceLastAccess) =&gt; daysSinceLastAccess <span class=\"tk-kw\">switch</span>\n{\n    &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">30</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Hot&quot;</span>,\n    &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">90</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Cool&quot;</span>,\n    &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">180</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Cold&quot;</span>,\n    _ =&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Archive&quot;</span>\n};\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> cases = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Days</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Expected</span>)[] { (<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Hot&quot;</span>), (<span class=\"tk-num\">29</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Hot&quot;</span>), (<span class=\"tk-num\">30</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Cool&quot;</span>), (<span class=\"tk-num\">89</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Cool&quot;</span>), (<span class=\"tk-num\">90</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Cold&quot;</span>), (<span class=\"tk-num\">179</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Cold&quot;</span>), (<span class=\"tk-num\">180</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Archive&quot;</span>), (<span class=\"tk-num\">400</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Archive&quot;</span>) };\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> (days, expected) <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> cases)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> actual = <span class=\"tk-ty\">RecommendedTier</span>(days);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (actual != expected) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: {days} days since access should recommend {expected}, got {actual}&quot;</span>);\n}\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: the Hot/Cool/Cold/Archive thresholds (30/90/180 days) form a consistent, non-overlapping rule&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: a time-limited SAS-style token grants access only within its validity window.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsAccessAllowed</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span> issuedAt, <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span> expiresAt, <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span> attemptAt) =&gt;\n    attemptAt &gt;= issuedAt &amp;&amp; attemptAt &lt; expiresAt;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> issued = <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UtcNow</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> expires = issued.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddMinutes</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">15</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsAccessAllowed</span>(issued, expires, issued.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddMinutes</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>))) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: access within the window should be allowed&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsAccessAllowed</span>(issued, expires, issued.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddMinutes</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>))) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: access after expiry should be denied&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: a time-limited token (the idea behind a SAS URL) only grants access inside its validity window&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: exponential backoff retry delays increase each attempt, up to the configured retry count.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>[] <span class=\"tk-ty\">ExponentialDelays</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> maxRetries, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span> baseDelay) =&gt;\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Enumerable</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Range</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, maxRetries).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(attempt =&gt; baseDelay * <span class=\"tk-ty\">Math</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Pow</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, attempt - <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>)).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToArray</span>();\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> delays = <span class=\"tk-ty\">ExponentialDelays</span>(maxRetries: <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>, baseDelay: <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromMilliseconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">200</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (delays.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Length</span> != <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected 5 retry delays, got {delays.Length}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>; i &lt; delays.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Length</span>; i++)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (delays[i] &lt;= delays[i - <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>]) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: delay #{i + 1} should be longer than delay #{i} (exponential backoff)&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: exponential backoff produces {delays.Length} strictly increasing delays ({string.Join(&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;, delays.Select(d =&gt; d.TotalMilliseconds + &quot;</span>ms<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;))})&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here — these are facts about managed Azure services, not local computation:</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//  - specific SLA percentages (99.99%, 99.999%), message size limits, retention windows, throughput numbers</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//  - Service Bus vs Event Grid vs Event Hubs feature comparisons</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//  - that Azure SDK clients are thread-safe and should be registered as singletons</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// See Further reading: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/service-bus-messaging/compare-messaging-services</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-map","text":"The map","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"azure-sql-database","text":"Azure SQL Database","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"deployment-and-purchasing-models","text":"Deployment and purchasing models","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"availability-dr-and-backups","text":"Availability, DR and backups","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"security","text":"Security","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"azure-database-for-postgresql","text":"Azure Database for PostgreSQL","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"azure-cosmos-db","text":"Azure Cosmos DB","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"storage-accounts-blob-queue-table-files","text":"Storage accounts: Blob, Queue, Table, Files","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"blob-storage","text":"Blob Storage","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"queues-tables-files","text":"Queues, Tables, Files","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"azure-managed-redis","text":"Azure Managed Redis","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"service-bus-vs-event-grid-vs-event-hubs","text":"Service Bus vs Event Grid vs Event Hubs","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"using-azure-sdk-clients-well-from-net","text":"Using Azure SDK clients well from .NET","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":2948,"minutes":13,"num":"8.4","part":"p8"},"azure-identity-secrets":{"id":"azure-identity-secrets","title":"Identity and Secrets in Azure: Entra ID, Managed Identity and Key Vault","summary":"Passwordless, least-privilege cloud apps. Microsoft Entra ID concepts (tenants, users, app registrations, service principals), how apps authenticate to Azure services, managed identities (system- and user-assigned), DefaultAzureCredential and the credential chain, Azure RBAC, Key Vault for secrets, keys and certificates, workload identity federation for CI/CD and Kubernetes, and protecting APIs with Entra ID.","layer":"infra","where":"Hosting & infrastructure › Identity & secrets","fits":"<p>Identity is the security foundation of layer 7, and it reaches every other layer. It replaces connection-string passwords for data (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-data-messaging\">Azure data</a>), feeds configuration (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/configuration-options/secrets-the-layer-systems-hardest-case\">Configuration</a>), issues tokens your APIs validate (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/auth\">Auth</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/oauth-oidc-identity\">OAuth &amp; OIDC</a>), and lets pipelines deploy without stored credentials (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cicd-iac\">CI/CD &amp; IaC</a>).</p>\n","prereqs":["cloud-azure-overview","configuration-options"],"next":["cicd-iac","oauth-oidc-identity","web-security"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Best option: <strong>no secret at all</strong> — the app authenticates as an <strong>identity</strong> (managed identity) that Azure itself issues and rotates, and access is granted through <strong>RBAC</strong>. Second-best: an unavoidable secret goes in <strong>Key Vault</strong>, never in source control or config files.</li>\n<li>A <strong>managed identity</strong> is a special service principal Azure creates and manages for a resource (App Service, Container Apps, a VM…). Your code just asks a local endpoint for a token; there&#39;s nothing to leak because there&#39;s no credential to begin with.</li>\n<li><code>DefaultAzureCredential</code> tries several credential sources in order (env vars → workload identity → managed identity → developer tools) and uses the first that works — so the <em>same code</em> authenticates as you locally and as the managed identity in Azure.</li>\n<li><strong>Azure RBAC</strong> (who can touch which Azure resource/data) and <strong>Entra roles/app permissions</strong> (who can administer Entra ID itself, or call an API) are two different permission systems — don&#39;t confuse them.</li>\n<li><strong>Workload identity federation</strong> extends &quot;no stored secret&quot; to places that aren&#39;t an Azure resource at all: GitHub Actions, Kubernetes pods — an external token gets exchanged for an Entra token.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-problem-secrets-everywhere\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets/the-problem-secrets-everywhere\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The problem: secrets everywhere <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Traditional apps authenticate to their dependencies with <strong>secrets</strong>: SQL passwords in connection strings, storage account keys, API keys, client secrets. Secrets get <strong>committed to Git</strong>, pasted in chat, <strong>logged</strong>, copied to laptops, and are rarely <strong>rotated</strong>. A leaked secret is a breach — and recall from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/configuration-options/secrets-the-layer-systems-hardest-case\">Configuration</a> that &quot;no secret at all&quot; was already flagged as the best entry in that chapter&#39;s table. This chapter is entirely about how Azure actually delivers on that promise.</p>\n<p>The modern Azure answer, in order of preference:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>No secret at all</strong>: apps authenticate with an <strong>identity</strong> issued by <strong>Microsoft Entra ID</strong> (managed identity), and access is granted with <strong>RBAC</strong>.</li>\n<li>If a secret is unavoidable (a third-party API key), store it in <strong>Key Vault</strong>, accessed by managed identity, and rotate it.</li>\n<li>Never in source control, images, or plain configuration files.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"microsoft-entra-id-concepts\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets/microsoft-entra-id-concepts\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Microsoft Entra ID concepts <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Microsoft Entra ID</strong> (formerly <strong>Azure Active Directory / Azure AD</strong>) is Microsoft&#39;s cloud identity provider: it authenticates users and applications and issues tokens (OAuth 2.0 / OpenID Connect) — exactly the kind of identity provider <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/auth/jwt-bearer-authentication-traced-claim-by-claim\">Auth</a> warned against building yourself.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Tenant</strong></td>\n<td>A dedicated instance of Entra ID for an organisation (<code>contoso.onmicrosoft.com</code>); the identity boundary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>User / Group</strong></td>\n<td>People, and collections of them for assigning access</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>App registration</strong></td>\n<td>The <strong>definition</strong> of an application in a tenant: client ID, redirect URIs, exposed API scopes, app roles, credentials (secret/certificate/federated)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Service principal</strong> (enterprise application)</td>\n<td>The <strong>instance</strong> of an app in a tenant: the security principal that gets permissions and role assignments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Managed identity</strong></td>\n<td>A special service principal <strong>managed by Azure</strong> for an Azure resource: no credentials for you to handle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Scopes / app roles</strong></td>\n<td>Permissions an API exposes (<code>orders.read</code>) / roles assigned to users or apps (<code>Orders.Admin</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Conditional Access, MFA</strong></td>\n<td>Policies on sign-in (require MFA, compliant device, location)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Entra External ID</strong></td>\n<td>Customer-facing identity (CIAM) for your app&#39;s end users (successor of Azure AD B2C)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Two kinds of permissions are often confused, and keeping them distinct is worth doing deliberately:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th><strong>Azure RBAC</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Entra roles / app permissions</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Controls access to</td>\n<td><strong>Azure resources</strong> (Storage, SQL, Key Vault, subscriptions)</td>\n<td><strong>Entra ID itself</strong> and Microsoft Graph / your APIs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Example</td>\n<td>&quot;app-shop-api can read blobs in stshopprod&quot;</td>\n<td>&quot;user is Global Administrator&quot;, &quot;app can call Graph User.Read.All&quot;, &quot;client may call api://shop orders.write&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"managed-identities-the-mechanism-that-makes-no-secret-actual\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets/managed-identities-the-mechanism-that-makes-no-secret-actual\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Managed identities: the mechanism that makes &quot;no secret&quot; actually work <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>A <strong>managed identity</strong> is an identity in Entra ID that Azure creates and manages <strong>for a resource</strong> (App Service, Functions, Container Apps, AKS workloads, VMs…). Your code asks the platform&#39;s local endpoint for a token; Azure handles the credentials and rotates them automatically. <strong>There&#39;s no secret to store, leak or rotate</strong> — not &quot;a secret that&#39;s harder to steal,&quot; genuinely none exists for an attacker to find.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th><strong>System-assigned</strong></th>\n<th><strong>User-assigned</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Lifecycle</td>\n<td>Tied to one resource; deleted with it</td>\n<td>Standalone resource; you manage its lifecycle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sharing</td>\n<td>One resource only</td>\n<td>Can be assigned to <strong>many</strong> resources</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Use when</td>\n<td>A simple one app → its resources setup</td>\n<td>Several apps or instances need the same permissions; pre-provisioning access before the app exists; blue/green; AKS workload identity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Shell</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-com\"># Enable a system-assigned identity and grant it data-plane access (least privilege, scoped narrowly)</span>\naz webapp identity assign -g rg-shop -n app-shop-api-prod\naz role assignment create <span class=\"tk-attr\">--assignee</span> &lt;principalId&gt; <span class=\"tk-attr\">--role</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Storage Blob Data Contributor&quot;</span> \\\n  <span class=\"tk-attr\">--scope</span> /subscriptions/&lt;sub&gt;/resourceGroups/rg-shop/providers/Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/stshopprod/blobServices/default/containers/invoices\naz role assignment create <span class=\"tk-attr\">--assignee</span> &lt;principalId&gt; <span class=\"tk-attr\">--role</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Azure Service Bus Data Sender&quot;</span> <span class=\"tk-attr\">--scope</span> &lt;service-bus-namespace-id&gt;\naz role assignment create <span class=\"tk-attr\">--assignee</span> &lt;principalId&gt; <span class=\"tk-attr\">--role</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Key Vault Secrets User&quot;</span> <span class=\"tk-attr\">--scope</span> &lt;key-vault-id&gt;</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Services supporting Entra (managed identity) auth include Azure SQL, PostgreSQL, Storage (Blob/Queue/Table), Service Bus, Event Hubs, Event Grid, Cosmos DB (data-plane RBAC), Key Vault, App Configuration, Managed Redis, Azure OpenAI, ACR and more.</p>\n<h2 id=\"defaultazurecredential-and-azure-identity-one-line-of-code-t\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets/defaultazurecredential-and-azure-identity-one-line-of-code-t\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>DefaultAzureCredential and Azure.Identity: one line of code, two environments <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>The <code>Azure.Identity</code> library provides <strong><code>TokenCredential</code></strong> implementations that every Azure SDK client accepts. <strong><code>DefaultAzureCredential</code></strong> tries a chain in order and uses the first that works:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>1. EnvironmentCredential        (AZURE_CLIENT_ID / AZURE_TENANT_ID / AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET or certificate env vars)\n2. WorkloadIdentityCredential   (Kubernetes workload identity, federated token file)\n3. ManagedIdentityCredential    (App Service, Functions, Container Apps, VMs, AKS)\n4. SharedTokenCacheCredential / VisualStudioCredential / VisualStudioCodeCredential\n5. AzureCliCredential           (az login)\n6. AzurePowerShellCredential, AzureDeveloperCliCredential (azd auth login)\n7. InteractiveBrowserCredential (disabled by default)</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Notice what this chain actually buys you: the <strong>same code</strong> authenticates as <strong>you</strong> on your laptop (via Visual Studio or <code>az login</code>, step 4 or 5) and as the <strong>managed identity</strong> in Azure (step 3) — no <code>#if DEBUG</code>, no environment-specific branching in application code, just the credential chain quietly resolving to whichever mechanism is actually available in a given environment.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> credential = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DefaultAzureCredential</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> blobs = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">BlobServiceClient</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Uri</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://stshopprod.blob.core.windows.net&quot;</span>), credential);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> bus = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceBusClient</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;sb-shop-prod.servicebus.windows.net&quot;</span>, credential);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> secrets = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SecretClient</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Uri</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://kv-shop-prod.vault.azure.net/&quot;</span>), credential);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">DefaultAzureCredential in production</div><p>The chain is convenient but can be <strong>slow to fail</strong> (trying each option) and can pick up an unexpected credential (an environment variable left on a host). For production, consider a deterministic credential: <code>new ManagedIdentityCredential(ManagedIdentityId.FromUserAssignedClientId(clientId))</code>, or <code>ChainedTokenCredential</code> with exactly what you expect, or exclude options via <code>DefaultAzureCredentialOptions</code>. With user-assigned identities, specify the <strong>client ID</strong>. Also give developers the same RBAC roles on dev resources so local runs work.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"azure-rbac-in-practice\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets/azure-rbac-in-practice\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Azure RBAC in practice <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>A <strong>role assignment</strong> = <strong>security principal</strong> (user, group, service principal, managed identity) + <strong>role definition</strong> (a set of allowed actions) + <strong>scope</strong> (management group, subscription, resource group, resource, or even a sub-resource like a blob container) — notice this is the exact same scope hierarchy from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cloud-azure-overview/azures-resource-hierarchy\">Cloud fundamentals</a>, with roles inheriting downward through it.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Plane</th>\n<th>Roles control</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Control plane</strong> (management)</td>\n<td>Creating/configuring resources via ARM</td>\n<td>Owner, Contributor, Reader, <em>Website Contributor</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Data plane</strong></td>\n<td>Accessing the data inside</td>\n<td><em>Storage Blob Data Reader/Contributor</em>, <em>Azure Service Bus Data Sender/Receiver</em>, <em>Key Vault Secrets User</em>, <em>Cosmos DB Built-in Data Contributor</em>, <em>App Configuration Data Reader</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The control-plane-vs-data-plane distinction matters more than it looks: <em>Contributor</em> on a storage account lets you reconfigure the account itself but says nothing about reading the blobs inside it — that&#39;s a separate data-plane role entirely, and granting an app&#39;s managed identity <code>Contributor</code> when it only ever needs to read blobs is a textbook least-privilege violation. Principles: <strong>least privilege</strong> (data roles, not Contributor; the narrowest scope); <strong>groups</strong> for humans, managed identities for apps; <strong>separate identities per app and environment</strong>; <strong>PIM</strong> (Privileged Identity Management) for just-in-time elevation of admin roles; review assignments regularly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"azure-key-vault\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets/azure-key-vault\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Azure Key Vault <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Key Vault</strong> securely stores and controls access to:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Object</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Secrets</strong></td>\n<td>Third-party API keys, connection strings for non-Entra systems, passwords</td>\n<td>Versioned; expiry dates; rotation via Event Grid notifications</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Keys</strong></td>\n<td>RSA/EC keys for encryption and signing (customer-managed keys, JWT signing, Data Protection key-ring protection)</td>\n<td>Operations happen <strong>inside</strong> the vault/HSM; keys never leave (Premium: HSM-backed; <strong>Managed HSM</strong> for FIPS 140-3 Level 3 — see the Key Vault basic concepts link in Further reading for current certification details)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Certificates</strong></td>\n<td>TLS certificates with automatic renewal from integrated CAs</td>\n<td>Used by App Service, App Gateway, Front Door</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Access with <strong>Azure RBAC</strong> (recommended; e.g. <em>Key Vault Secrets User</em> for apps that read secrets) or legacy access policies. Enable <strong>soft delete</strong> and <strong>purge protection</strong>, use <strong>private endpoints</strong>, and log access to Log Analytics.</p>\n<h3 id=\"using-key-vault-from-net\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets/using-key-vault-from-net\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Using Key Vault from .NET</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// 1. As a configuration provider: secrets become IConfiguration keys (&quot;Payments--ApiKey&quot; → &quot;Payments:ApiKey&quot;)</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configuration</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAzureKeyVault</span>(\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Uri</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;https://{builder.Configuration[&quot;</span><span class=\"tk-ty\">KeyVaultName</span><span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;]}.vault.azure.net/&quot;</span>),\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DefaultAzureCredential</span>(),\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AzureKeyVaultConfigurationOptions</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReloadInterval</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromMinutes</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">30</span>) });\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddOptions</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">PaymentOptions</span>&gt;().<span class=\"tk-ty\">BindConfiguration</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Payments&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidateOnStart</span>();\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 2. As a client, for on-demand access</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> secret = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> secretClient.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetSecretAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;StripeApiKey&quot;</span>, cancellationToken: ct);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 3. Protect the ASP.NET Core Data Protection key ring (shared across instances)</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddDataProtection</span>()\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">PersistKeysToAzureBlobStorage</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Uri</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://stshopprod.blob.core.windows.net/dataprotection/keys.xml&quot;</span>), <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DefaultAzureCredential</span>())\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProtectKeysWithAzureKeyVault</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Uri</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://kv-shop-prod.vault.azure.net/keys/dataprotection&quot;</span>), <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DefaultAzureCredential</span>());</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code># 4. Platform references: no code at all. App Service / Functions / Container Apps resolve the secret at startup.\nPayments__ApiKey = @Microsoft.KeyVault(SecretUri=https://kv-shop-prod.vault.azure.net/secrets/StripeApiKey/)</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Notice option 3 solves a problem raised all the way back in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/auth/cookie-authentication\">Auth</a>: Data Protection keys have to be shared across instances or cookies break on scale-out, and Key Vault plus Blob Storage is exactly how that shared key ring gets protected and distributed in production.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-tip\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Key Vault is not a high-throughput database</div><p>Key Vault has <strong>throttling limits</strong> (thousands of operations per 10 seconds per vault). Load secrets <strong>at startup</strong> (config provider) and cache them; don&#39;t call <code>GetSecretAsync</code> on every request.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"workload-identity-federation-ci-cd-and-kubernetes\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets/workload-identity-federation-ci-cd-and-kubernetes\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Workload identity federation: CI/CD and Kubernetes <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Outside Azure-hosted resources (GitHub Actions, Kubernetes pods, other clouds), managed identities don&#39;t exist directly — there&#39;s no Azure resource for Azure to attach an identity to. <strong>Workload identity federation</strong> closes that gap by letting an external identity provider&#39;s token be <strong>exchanged</strong> for an Entra token, again with <strong>no stored secret</strong>:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>GitHub Actions job ──(OIDC token: repo:org/shop:environment:prod)──▶ Entra ID (federated credential trusts that subject)\n                                                                   ──▶ access token for the app's service principal/managed identity\n                                                                   ──▶ az / azd / Bicep deploy</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>YAML</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-ty\">permissions</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">id-token</span>: write, <span class=\"tk-ty\">contents</span>: read }\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">steps</span>:\n  - <span class=\"tk-ty\">uses</span>: azure/login@v2\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">with</span>:\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">client-id</span>: <span class=\"tk-attr\">${{ vars.AZURE_CLIENT_ID }}</span>       <span class=\"tk-com\"># no client secret stored in GitHub!</span>\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">tenant-id</span>: <span class=\"tk-attr\">${{ vars.AZURE_TENANT_ID }}</span>\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">subscription-id</span>: <span class=\"tk-attr\">${{ vars.AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID }}</span></code></pre></figure>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>AKS Workload Identity</strong>: a Kubernetes service account is federated with a user-assigned managed identity, so pods get Entra tokens via <code>WorkloadIdentityCredential</code> (part of <code>DefaultAzureCredential</code>).</li>\n<li>The same pattern works for Azure DevOps service connections, other clouds and on-premises with Azure Arc.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"protecting-your-own-apis-with-entra-id\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets/protecting-your-own-apis-with-entra-id\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Protecting your own APIs with Entra ID <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>1. App registration &quot;Shop API&quot;: Application ID URI api://shop-api; expose scopes (orders.read, orders.write); define app roles (Orders.Admin).\n2. App registration &quot;Shop SPA&quot; (public client) → API permission: api://shop-api/orders.read (delegated, user context).\n3. Service &quot;Billing worker&quot; (managed identity) → app role assignment Orders.Reader on Shop API (application permission, no user).\n4. Shop API validates tokens: issuer = tenant, audience = api://shop-api, then authorizes on scp (delegated) or roles (application).</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Step 4 here is exactly the five-step JWT validation walked through in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/auth/jwt-bearer-authentication-traced-claim-by-claim\">Auth</a> — Entra ID is simply the issuer whose discovery document and signing keys get fetched.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAuthentication</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">JwtBearerDefaults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticationScheme</span>)\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddMicrosoftIdentityWebApi</span>(builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configuration</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetSection</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;AzureAd&quot;</span>));      <span class=\"tk-com\">// Microsoft.Identity.Web</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAuthorizationBuilder</span>()\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddPolicy</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ReadOrders&quot;</span>, p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequireAssertion</span>(ctx =&gt;\n        ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">User</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasClaim</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;scp&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;orders.read&quot;</span>) || ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">User</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsInRole</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Orders.Reader&quot;</span>)));   <span class=\"tk-com\">// user OR app permission</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Calling another API as the service itself (client credentials via managed identity)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> token = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ManagedIdentityCredential</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetTokenAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TokenRequestContext</span>([<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;api://inventory-api/.default&quot;</span>]), ct);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Details of the flows are in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/oauth-oidc-identity\">OAuth 2.0 &amp; OIDC</a>.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is a managed identity and why use it?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>An Entra ID identity that Azure creates and manages for a resource (App Service, Functions, Container Apps, VMs, AKS workloads). The app obtains tokens from a local endpoint with no credentials to store or rotate, and is granted least-privilege access via RBAC. It eliminates secrets in configuration.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>System-assigned vs user-assigned managed identity?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>System-assigned is created for and tied to one resource&#39;s lifecycle. User-assigned is a standalone resource that can be attached to many resources and survives them. Use it when several apps or instances share permissions, to pre-provision access, or for AKS workload identity.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How does DefaultAzureCredential work?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>It tries a sequence of credential types (environment variables, workload identity, managed identity, then developer tools like Visual Studio, Azure CLI and azd) and uses the first that succeeds. The same code authenticates as the developer locally and as the managed identity in Azure. In production you may prefer a specific credential for determinism and speed.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the difference between an app registration and a service principal?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>An app registration is the global definition of an application (client ID, redirect URIs, exposed scopes and roles, credentials). A service principal (enterprise application) is its instance in a specific tenant, the security principal that receives role assignments and consents. Managed identities are a special kind of service principal.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you store and use secrets in an Azure-hosted .NET app?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Prefer no secrets: managed identity with RBAC for Azure services. For unavoidable secrets, store them in Key Vault, grant the app&#39;s managed identity the Key Vault Secrets User role, and load them via the Key Vault configuration provider or platform Key Vault references, caching them rather than calling the vault per request. Use User Secrets in development.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How can GitHub Actions deploy to Azure without storing a client secret?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Workload identity federation (OIDC): configure a federated credential on an Entra app or user-assigned managed identity that trusts the GitHub repository/environment subject. The workflow requests an OIDC token (id-token: write permission) and azure/login exchanges it for an Azure access token.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Azure RBAC vs Entra roles?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Azure RBAC controls access to Azure resources and their data (subscriptions, storage, Key Vault), using role assignments at a scope. Entra roles control administration of the Entra directory itself, and API permissions/app roles control access to Microsoft Graph and your own APIs.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/managed-identities-azure-resources/overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">What are managed identities for Azure resources?</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/azure/sdk/authentication/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Authenticate .NET apps to Azure services</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/key-vault/general/basic-concepts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Azure Key Vault basic concepts</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/workload-id/workload-identity-federation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Workload identity federation</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity-platform/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Microsoft identity platform documentation</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Real Entra ID, Key Vault and workload identity federation behaviour needs an actual Azure tenant, so most of this chapter can&#39;t be verified by a console program. The checks below instead model the three <em>mechanisms</em> this chapter describes — the credential chain&#39;s fallback order, caching to avoid throttling, and RBAC scope inheritance — with plain stand-ins. Save as <code>Program.cs</code> in a new console project (<code>dotnet new console</code>) and run with <code>dotnet run</code>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Verifies claims from this chapter that are mechanism-level, without needing a</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// real Azure tenant: DefaultAzureCredential's &quot;try each source in order, use the first that works&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// chain, &quot;load secrets once at startup and cache them&quot; vs. hitting Key Vault's per-request throttling</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// limit, and RBAC role assignments inheriting DOWN a scope hierarchy but not up.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Real Entra ID / Key Vault / workload identity federation behaviour needs an actual Azure tenant and</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// isn't runtime-verifiable here — see Further reading (learn.microsoft.com/entra, /azure/key-vault) for those.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Collections</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Generic</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> fails = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> s) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: {s}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> cond, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> s)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (cond) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(s);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">else</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: {s}&quot;</span>); fails++; }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- DefaultAzureCredential-style chain: try sources in order, use the first that succeeds ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> tried = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> chain = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CredentialChain</span>(\n    (<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;EnvironmentCredential&quot;</span>, () =&gt; { tried.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;EnvironmentCredential&quot;</span>); <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;no env vars set&quot;</span>); }),\n    (<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;WorkloadIdentityCredential&quot;</span>, () =&gt; { tried.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;WorkloadIdentityCredential&quot;</span>); <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;not running in Kubernetes&quot;</span>); }),\n    (<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ManagedIdentityCredential&quot;</span>, () =&gt; { tried.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ManagedIdentityCredential&quot;</span>); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;token-from-managed-identity&quot;</span>; }),\n    (<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;AzureCliCredential&quot;</span>, () =&gt; { tried.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;AzureCliCredential&quot;</span>); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;token-from-az-login&quot;</span>; }));\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> token = chain.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetToken</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(token == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;token-from-managed-identity&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;DefaultAzureCredential-style chain: the first source that actually succeeds provides the token&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(tried.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span> &amp;&amp; tried[^<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>] == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ManagedIdentityCredential&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;DefaultAzureCredential-style chain: sources that fail are tried in order, and the chain stops at the first success&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Load secrets once at startup and cache them, rather than calling the vault on every request ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> vault = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ThrottledVault</span>(callBudget: <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> cachingClient = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CachingSecretClient</span>(vault);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; i &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">100</span>; i++) cachingClient.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetSecret</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;StripeApiKey&quot;</span>);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// 100 &quot;requests&quot; reusing the cached value</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(vault.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CallsMade</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Caching the secret at startup means 100 reads cost Key Vault exactly 1 call, not 100&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> uncachedClient = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">UncachedSecretClient</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ThrottledVault</span>(callBudget: <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Throws</span>(() =&gt; { <span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; i &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>; i++) uncachedClient.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetSecret</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;StripeApiKey&quot;</span>); }),\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Without caching, calling GetSecretAsync per request can exhaust a tight call budget (simulating Key Vault throttling)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- RBAC: a role assigned at a broader scope is visible at a nested scope, not the other way round ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> tree = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ScopeTree</span>();\ntree.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AssignRole</span>(scope: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/sub/rg-shop&quot;</span>, role: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Storage Blob Data Reader&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(tree.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasRole</span>(scope: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/sub/rg-shop/storageAccounts/stshopprod&quot;</span>, role: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Storage Blob Data Reader&quot;</span>),\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;RBAC: a role assigned at the resource group scope is inherited by resources nested inside it&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(!tree.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasRole</span>(scope: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/sub&quot;</span>, role: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Storage Blob Data Reader&quot;</span>),\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;RBAC: a role assigned at a narrower scope does NOT apply to the broader scope above it&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(fails == <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span> ? <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;\\nAll checks passed.&quot;</span> : <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;\\n{fails} check(s) failed.&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (fails &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exit</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Throws</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Action</span> a) { <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { a(); <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>; } <span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>; } }\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CredentialChain</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">params</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Func</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGetToken</span>)[] sources)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetToken</span>()\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> (_, tryGetToken) <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> sources)\n        {\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> tryGetToken(); } <span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* fall through to the next source, exactly like DefaultAzureCredential */</span> }\n        }\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;No credential source succeeded&quot;</span>);\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ThrottledVault</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> callBudget)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CallsMade</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetSecret</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> name)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">CallsMade</span>++;\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">CallsMade</span> &gt; callBudget) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;429: Key Vault throttled this vault&quot;</span>);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;s3cr3t&quot;</span>;\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CachingSecretClient</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ThrottledVault</span> vault)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; _cache = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetSecret</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> name) =&gt; _cache.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGetValue</span>(name, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> v) ? v : _cache[name] = vault.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetSecret</span>(name);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">UncachedSecretClient</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ThrottledVault</span> vault)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetSecret</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> name) =&gt; vault.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetSecret</span>(name);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// calls the vault every single time</span>\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ScopeTree</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">HashSet</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;&gt; _roles = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AssignRole</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> scope, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> role) =&gt; (_roles.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGetValue</span>(scope, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span>) ? <span class=\"tk-kw\">set</span> : _roles[scope] = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>()).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(role);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HasRole</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> scope, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> role)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-com\">// A role applies at `scope` if it was assigned at `scope` or at any ancestor scope (a shorter path prefix).</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> (assignedScope, roles) <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> _roles)\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (scope.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartsWith</span>(assignedScope, <span class=\"tk-ty\">StringComparison</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ordinal</span>) &amp;&amp; roles.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contains</span>(role)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-problem-secrets-everywhere","text":"The problem: secrets everywhere","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"microsoft-entra-id-concepts","text":"Microsoft Entra ID concepts","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"managed-identities-the-mechanism-that-makes-no-secret-actual","text":"Managed identities: the mechanism that makes \"no secret\" actually work","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"defaultazurecredential-and-azure-identity-one-line-of-code-t","text":"DefaultAzureCredential and Azure.Identity: one line of code, two environments","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"azure-rbac-in-practice","text":"Azure RBAC in practice","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"azure-key-vault","text":"Azure Key Vault","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"using-key-vault-from-net","text":"Using Key Vault from .NET","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"workload-identity-federation-ci-cd-and-kubernetes","text":"Workload identity federation: CI/CD and Kubernetes","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"protecting-your-own-apis-with-entra-id","text":"Protecting your own APIs with Entra ID","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":3380,"minutes":15,"num":"8.5","part":"p8"},"cicd-iac":{"id":"cicd-iac","title":"CI/CD and Infrastructure as Code","summary":"Shipping safely and repeatably. Continuous integration, delivery and deployment, branching strategies, a complete GitHub Actions pipeline for .NET, Azure DevOps Pipelines, environments and approvals, deployment strategies (rolling, blue/green, canary, feature flags), database migrations in pipelines, versioning, and infrastructure as code with Bicep, Terraform and azd.","layer":"infra","where":"Hosting & infrastructure › Delivery pipeline & IaC","fits":"<p>The pipeline is how code from every layer becomes running infrastructure in layer 7. It builds with the SDK (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/projects-sdk-nuget\">Projects &amp; SDK</a>), runs <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/unit-testing\">unit</a> and <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/integration-testing\">integration</a> tests, builds images (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/docker\">Docker</a>), applies migrations (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals/migrations-evolving-the-schema-in-step-with-the-model\">EF Core</a>), provisions resources with IaC, and deploys to <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-compute\">compute</a> using federated identity (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets/workload-identity-federation-ci-cd-and-kubernetes\">Identity &amp; secrets</a>).</p>\n","prereqs":["docker","unit-testing"],"next":["kubernetes","observability-aspire"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cicd-iac/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>CI</strong>: every push is built and tested automatically. <strong>Continuous delivery</strong>: every passing change <em>could</em> ship, but a human decides when. <strong>Continuous deployment</strong>: passing changes ship automatically, no human gate.</li>\n<li>Build the deployable artefact (container image, zip) <strong>once</strong>, then promote the exact same artefact through dev → staging → prod. Only configuration changes between environments.</li>\n<li>Deployment strategies trade complexity for a smaller blast radius if something&#39;s wrong: recreate (riskiest, simplest) → rolling → blue/green (instant switch, instant rollback) → canary (small % of traffic first) → feature flags (ship dark, turn on later).</li>\n<li>Database schema changes during rolling/blue-green deploys need <strong>expand → migrate → contract</strong>: add the new shape first (compatibly), deploy code that works with both old and new, backfill, then remove the old shape in a <em>later</em> release — never in the same release that stops using it.</li>\n<li>Infrastructure as Code (Bicep, Terraform) means infra lives in versioned, reviewed code instead of manual portal clicks — the same reproducibility argument as &quot;define your app in code,&quot; now applied to the infra underneath it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"ci-cd-and-cd\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cicd-iac/ci-cd-and-cd\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>CI, CD and CD <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Practice</th>\n<th>Definition</th>\n<th>Gate</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Continuous Integration (CI)</strong></td>\n<td>Everyone integrates into the main branch <strong>frequently</strong> (at least daily); <strong>every push triggers an automated build and tests</strong></td>\n<td>A broken build is fixed immediately</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Continuous Delivery</strong></td>\n<td>Every change that passes the pipeline is <strong>releasable</strong>; deploying to production is a <strong>business decision</strong> (a button / approval)</td>\n<td>Manual approval to prod</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Continuous Deployment</strong></td>\n<td>Every passing change is <strong>deployed to production automatically</strong></td>\n<td>No human gate; relies on strong tests, monitoring and progressive delivery</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout c-why\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Why it matters</div><p>Small, frequent, automated releases are <strong>less risky</strong> than big, rare, manual ones: each change is small enough to understand and roll back, problems surface within minutes of being written, and the process is the same every time (no &quot;works when Dave deploys it&quot;). The <strong>DORA metrics</strong> (deployment frequency, lead time for changes, change failure rate, time to restore) measure delivery performance; the research behind them — elite teams deploy on demand with low failure rates — is documented in <em>Accelerate</em> (Forsgren, Humble, Kim), cited in Further reading below.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"branching-strategies\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cicd-iac/branching-strategies\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Branching strategies <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Strategy</th>\n<th>How</th>\n<th>Fits</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Trunk-based development</strong></td>\n<td>Short-lived branches (hours to a day) or direct commits to <code>main</code>; <strong>feature flags</strong> hide unfinished work</td>\n<td>CI/CD, high-velocity teams (recommended by DORA research)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>GitHub Flow</strong></td>\n<td><code>main</code> is always deployable; feature branch → PR (review + CI checks) → merge → deploy</td>\n<td>Most web/API teams</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>GitFlow</strong></td>\n<td><code>develop</code>, <code>feature/*</code>, <code>release/*</code>, <code>hotfix/*</code>, <code>main</code></td>\n<td>Versioned products with scheduled releases; heavyweight for services</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Release branches</strong></td>\n<td>Cut <code>release/2026.10</code> for stabilisation; fixes cherry-picked</td>\n<td>Libraries, on-premises products</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>PR hygiene: branch protection on <code>main</code> (required reviews, required status checks: build, tests, analyzers, security scans), small PRs, conventional commit messages, and automated dependency updates (Dependabot/Renovate).</p>\n<h2 id=\"anatomy-of-a-net-pipeline\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cicd-iac/anatomy-of-a-net-pipeline\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Anatomy of a .NET pipeline <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code> push/PR ─▶ CI ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────▶  merge to main ─▶ CD ───────────────────────────────────────────▶\n           restore → build (warnings as errors) → unit tests → integration      build image once → push to ACR (tag = git SHA)\n           tests (Testcontainers) → analyzers/format → coverage → SCA/SAST       → deploy to dev → smoke tests → deploy staging (slot/revision)\n           → (PR) report status                                                    → migrations → smoke/E2E → approval → prod (swap/canary) → monitor</code></pre></figure>\n<p><strong>Build once, deploy many</strong>: the same artefact (container image digest or zip) is promoted through environments; only <strong>configuration</strong> differs — exactly the &quot;promote the same image&quot; principle from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/docker/configuration-secrets-and-storage-in-containers\">Docker</a>, now the organising idea for the whole pipeline rather than just one chapter&#39;s advice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"github-actions-a-complete-example\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cicd-iac/github-actions-a-complete-example\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>GitHub Actions: a complete example <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>YAML</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-com\"># .github/workflows/shop-api.yml</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: shop-api\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">on</span>:\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">push</span>:\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">branches</span>: [main]\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">pull_request</span>:\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">branches</span>: [main]\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">permissions</span>:\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">contents</span>: read\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">id-token</span>: write          <span class=\"tk-com\"># for OIDC login to Azure (no stored secrets)</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">env</span>:\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">DOTNET_VERSION</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">10.0</span>.x\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">IMAGE</span>: shopacr.azurecr.io/shop-api\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">jobs</span>:\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">build-test</span>:\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">runs-on</span>: ubuntu-latest\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">steps</span>:\n      - <span class=\"tk-ty\">uses</span>: actions/checkout@v4\n      - <span class=\"tk-ty\">uses</span>: actions/setup-dotnet@v4\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">with</span>:\n          <span class=\"tk-ty\">dotnet-version</span>: <span class=\"tk-attr\">${{ env.DOTNET_VERSION }}</span>\n          <span class=\"tk-ty\">cache</span>: true                                  <span class=\"tk-com\"># NuGet cache keyed on lock files</span>\n      - <span class=\"tk-ty\">run</span>: dotnet restore <span class=\"tk-attr\">--locked-mode</span>\n      - <span class=\"tk-ty\">run</span>: dotnet build <span class=\"tk-attr\">--no-restore</span> -c Release -warnaserror\n      - <span class=\"tk-ty\">run</span>: dotnet format <span class=\"tk-attr\">--verify-no-changes</span> <span class=\"tk-attr\">--no-restore</span>\n      - <span class=\"tk-ty\">run</span>: dotnet test <span class=\"tk-attr\">--no-build</span> -c Release <span class=\"tk-attr\">--logger</span> trx <span class=\"tk-attr\">--collect</span>:<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;XPlat Code Coverage&quot;</span>   <span class=\"tk-com\"># Testcontainers works: Docker is available</span>\n      - <span class=\"tk-ty\">uses</span>: actions/upload-artifact@v4\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">if</span>: always()\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">with</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: test-results, <span class=\"tk-ty\">path</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;**/TestResults/**&quot;</span> }\n      - <span class=\"tk-ty\">run</span>: dotnet list package <span class=\"tk-attr\">--vulnerable</span> <span class=\"tk-attr\">--include-transitive</span> <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>&gt;&amp;<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span> | tee vuln.txt &amp;&amp; ! grep -q <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;has the following vulnerable packages&quot;</span> vuln.txt\n\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">image</span>:\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">needs</span>: build-test\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">if</span>: <span class=\"tk-ty\">github.ref</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">'refs/heads/main'</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">runs-on</span>: ubuntu-latest\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">outputs</span>:\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">tag</span>: <span class=\"tk-attr\">${{ steps.meta.outputs.tag }}</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">steps</span>:\n      - <span class=\"tk-ty\">uses</span>: actions/checkout@v4\n      - <span class=\"tk-ty\">uses</span>: azure/login@v2\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">with</span>:\n          <span class=\"tk-ty\">client-id</span>: <span class=\"tk-attr\">${{ vars.AZURE_CLIENT_ID }}</span>\n          <span class=\"tk-ty\">tenant-id</span>: <span class=\"tk-attr\">${{ vars.AZURE_TENANT_ID }}</span>\n          <span class=\"tk-ty\">subscription-id</span>: <span class=\"tk-attr\">${{ vars.AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID }}</span>\n      - <span class=\"tk-ty\">run</span>: az acr login -n shopacr\n      - <span class=\"tk-ty\">id</span>: meta\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">run</span>: echo <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;tag=${GITHUB_SHA::12}&quot;</span> &gt;&gt; <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;$GITHUB_OUTPUT&quot;</span>\n      - <span class=\"tk-ty\">run</span>: |\n          docker build -t $<span class=\"tk-ty\">IMAGE</span>:<span class=\"tk-attr\">${{ steps.meta.outputs.tag }}</span> .\n          docker push $<span class=\"tk-ty\">IMAGE</span>:<span class=\"tk-attr\">${{ steps.meta.outputs.tag }}</span>\n      - <span class=\"tk-ty\">uses</span>: aquasecurity/trivy-action@<span class=\"tk-num\">0.28.0</span>          <span class=\"tk-com\"># image vulnerability scan</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">with</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">image-ref</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;${{ env.IMAGE }}:${{ steps.meta.outputs.tag }}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">severity</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;CRITICAL,HIGH&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">exit-code</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;1&quot;</span> }\n\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">deploy-staging</span>:\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">needs</span>: image\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">runs-on</span>: ubuntu-latest\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">environment</span>: staging                               <span class=\"tk-com\"># environment-scoped vars/secrets + optional protection rules</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">steps</span>:\n      - <span class=\"tk-ty\">uses</span>: actions/checkout@v4\n      - <span class=\"tk-ty\">uses</span>: azure/login@v2\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">with</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">client-id</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;${{ vars.AZURE_CLIENT_ID }}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">tenant-id</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;${{ vars.AZURE_TENANT_ID }}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">subscription-id</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;${{ vars.AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID }}&quot;</span> }\n      - <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: Apply database migrations (bundle)\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">run</span>: |\n          dotnet tool restore\n          dotnet ef migrations bundle -p src/Shop.Infrastructure -s src/Shop.Api -o efbundle <span class=\"tk-attr\">--self-contained</span> -r linux-x64\n          ./efbundle <span class=\"tk-attr\">--connection</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;${{ secrets.SQL_MIGRATION_CS }}&quot;</span>\n      - <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: Deploy new revision\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">run</span>: az containerapp update -n shop-api -g rg-shop-staging <span class=\"tk-attr\">--image</span> $<span class=\"tk-ty\">IMAGE</span>:<span class=\"tk-attr\">${{ needs.image.outputs.tag }}</span>\n      - <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: Smoke test\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">run</span>: curl <span class=\"tk-attr\">--fail</span> <span class=\"tk-attr\">--retry</span> <span class=\"tk-num\">10</span> <span class=\"tk-attr\">--retry-delay</span> <span class=\"tk-num\">6</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">https</span>:<span class=\"tk-com\">//shop-api-staging.example.com/health/ready</span>\n\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">deploy-prod</span>:\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">needs</span>: [image, deploy-staging]\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">runs-on</span>: ubuntu-latest\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">environment</span>: production                            <span class=\"tk-com\"># required reviewers = manual approval gate (continuous DELIVERY)</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">steps</span>:\n      - <span class=\"tk-ty\">uses</span>: azure/login@v2\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">with</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">client-id</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;${{ vars.AZURE_CLIENT_ID }}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">tenant-id</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;${{ vars.AZURE_TENANT_ID }}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">subscription-id</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;${{ vars.AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID }}&quot;</span> }\n      - <span class=\"tk-ty\">run</span>: az containerapp update -n shop-api -g rg-shop-prod <span class=\"tk-attr\">--image</span> $<span class=\"tk-ty\">IMAGE</span>:<span class=\"tk-attr\">${{ needs.image.outputs.tag }}</span> <span class=\"tk-attr\">--revision-suffix</span> <span class=\"tk-attr\">${{ needs.image.outputs.tag }}</span></code></pre></figure>\n<p>Notice <code>deploy-staging</code> runs the EF migration bundle and <code>curl</code>s the readiness endpoint <strong>before</strong> <code>deploy-prod</code> is even allowed to start — the pipeline itself enforces exactly the &quot;migrate, then verify, then promote&quot; sequencing that a human following a runbook could forget under pressure. Concepts: <strong>workflows</strong> (YAML in <code>.github/workflows</code>), <strong>triggers</strong> (<code>on:</code>), <strong>jobs</strong> (run in parallel unless <code>needs:</code>), <strong>steps</strong>, <strong>actions</strong> (reusable steps from the marketplace), <strong>runners</strong> (GitHub-hosted or self-hosted), <strong>environments</strong> (protection rules, approvals, scoped secrets), <strong>matrix builds</strong>, <strong>reusable workflows</strong> and <strong>composite actions</strong> for sharing across repos, and <strong>caching</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"azure-devops-pipelines\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cicd-iac/azure-devops-pipelines\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Azure DevOps Pipelines <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Many enterprises use <strong>Azure DevOps</strong> (Boards, Repos, <strong>Pipelines</strong>, Artifacts, Test Plans). YAML pipelines are structured as <strong>stages → jobs → steps</strong>, with <strong>tasks</strong> instead of actions, <strong>service connections</strong> (use workload identity federation), <strong>environments</strong> with approvals and checks, <strong>variable groups</strong> (optionally linked to Key Vault) and <strong>templates</strong> for reuse.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>YAML</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-com\"># azure-pipelines.yml</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">trigger</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">branches</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">include</span>: [main] } }\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">pool</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">vmImage</span>: ubuntu-latest }\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">stages</span>:\n- <span class=\"tk-ty\">stage</span>: Build\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">jobs</span>:\n  - <span class=\"tk-ty\">job</span>: build\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">steps</span>:\n    - <span class=\"tk-ty\">task</span>: UseDotNet@<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">inputs</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">version</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">10.0</span>.x }\n    - <span class=\"tk-ty\">script</span>: dotnet build -c Release -warnaserror\n    - <span class=\"tk-ty\">script</span>: dotnet test -c Release <span class=\"tk-attr\">--no-build</span> <span class=\"tk-attr\">--logger</span> trx\n    - <span class=\"tk-ty\">task</span>: PublishTestResults@<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">inputs</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">testResultsFormat</span>: VSTest, <span class=\"tk-ty\">testResultsFiles</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;**/*.trx&quot;</span> }\n    - <span class=\"tk-ty\">task</span>: Docker@<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">inputs</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">containerRegistry</span>: acr-connection, <span class=\"tk-ty\">repository</span>: shop-api, <span class=\"tk-ty\">command</span>: buildAndPush, <span class=\"tk-ty\">tags</span>: $(Build.SourceVersion) }\n\n- <span class=\"tk-ty\">stage</span>: Prod\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">dependsOn</span>: Build\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">jobs</span>:\n  - <span class=\"tk-ty\">deployment</span>: deploy\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">environment</span>: shop-prod          <span class=\"tk-com\"># approvals &amp; checks configured on the environment</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">strategy</span>:\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">runOnce</span>:\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">deploy</span>:\n          <span class=\"tk-ty\">steps</span>:\n          - <span class=\"tk-ty\">task</span>: AzureWebAppContainer@<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">inputs</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">azureSubscription</span>: azure-prod-wif, <span class=\"tk-ty\">appName</span>: app-shop-api-prod, <span class=\"tk-ty\">containers</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;shopacr.azurecr.io/shop-api:$(Build.SourceVersion)&quot;</span> }</code></pre></figure>\n<h2 id=\"deployment-strategies\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cicd-iac/deployment-strategies\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Deployment strategies <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Strategy</th>\n<th>How</th>\n<th>Rollback</th>\n<th>Azure implementation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Recreate</strong></td>\n<td>Stop old, start new</td>\n<td>Redeploy old</td>\n<td>Simple; causes downtime</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Rolling</strong></td>\n<td>Replace instances gradually</td>\n<td>Roll forward/back gradually</td>\n<td>Kubernetes Deployments, App Service multi-instance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Blue/green</strong></td>\n<td>Run the new version alongside, switch <strong>all</strong> traffic at once</td>\n<td>Switch back instantly</td>\n<td><strong>App Service slot swap</strong>, Container Apps revisions (100% switch), two AKS deployments + service selector</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Canary</strong></td>\n<td>Send a small % of traffic to the new version, increase while metrics stay healthy</td>\n<td>Route 0% to canary</td>\n<td><strong>Container Apps traffic splitting</strong>, App Service &quot;testing in production&quot; (slot traffic %), Front Door weights, service mesh / Argo Rollouts / Flagger</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Feature flags</strong></td>\n<td>Deploy code dark; enable features per user/percentage at runtime</td>\n<td>Turn the flag off</td>\n<td>Azure App Configuration + Microsoft.FeatureManagement (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/configuration-options/feature-flags-configuration-that-controls-behaviour-not-just\">Configuration</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ring deployment</strong></td>\n<td>Internal users → early adopters → everyone (or region by region)</td>\n<td>Halt the rollout</td>\n<td>Multiple environments/regions in the pipeline</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Each row along this table trades a little more deployment complexity for a progressively smaller blast radius if something goes wrong — Recreate risks the whole user base at once, Canary risks a deliberately small slice, and <strong>progressive delivery</strong> pushes that idea furthest by combining canaries and flags with <strong>automated health analysis</strong> (error rate, latency SLOs from Application Insights or Prometheus) to promote or roll back without a human in the loop at all.</p>\n<h2 id=\"databases-in-the-pipeline\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cicd-iac/databases-in-the-pipeline\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Databases in the pipeline <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Schema changes are the hardest part of continuous deployment, because <strong>old and new code run side by side</strong> during a rolling or blue/green deploy — the exact scenario that forced <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals/migrations-evolving-the-schema-in-step-with-the-model\">EF Core fundamentals</a> to introduce the expand/contract pattern in the first place:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Expand → migrate → contract</strong>: (1) expand: add new columns/tables, nullable or with defaults, compatible with the old code; (2) deploy code that writes both and reads new; (3) backfill data; (4) contract: remove old columns in a <strong>later</strong> release.</li>\n<li>Never rename or drop a column in the same release that stops using it.</li>\n<li>Apply migrations with <strong>idempotent scripts</strong> or <strong>EF migration bundles</strong> as a pipeline step <strong>before</strong> deploying the app (with a dedicated migration identity that has DDL rights; the app identity gets only DML).</li>\n<li>For DBA-managed schemas: <strong>SQL projects (DACPAC)</strong>, DbUp, Flyway, Liquibase.</li>\n<li>Back up or verify point-in-time restore before risky migrations, and test migrations against a production-like copy.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"versioning-and-artefacts\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cicd-iac/versioning-and-artefacts\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Versioning and artefacts <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SemVer</strong> (<code>MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH</code>) for libraries and public APIs; services often use build numbers or <strong>git SHA tags</strong> for images (immutable, traceable).</li>\n<li>Tools: <strong>MinVer</strong> / <strong>Nerdbank.GitVersioning</strong> / GitVersion derive versions from Git tags or height. Set <code>&lt;Version&gt;</code>, <code>AssemblyInformationalVersion</code> (include the commit SHA), and expose it on <code>/health</code> or <code>/version</code> and in telemetry (<code>service.version</code>).</li>\n<li>Store build outputs in a registry (ACR, NuGet feed), with <strong>retention policies</strong>. Never deploy something that wasn&#39;t built by the pipeline.</li>\n<li><strong>Supply chain security</strong>: SBOM generation, signed images and provenance (SLSA), pinned action versions (by SHA for third-party actions), least-privilege workflow <code>permissions</code>, and secret scanning (GitHub push protection).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"infrastructure-as-code\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cicd-iac/infrastructure-as-code\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Infrastructure as Code <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>IaC</strong> defines infrastructure (resource groups, App Service plans, Container Apps environments, SQL servers, Key Vaults, role assignments, networking) in <strong>versioned code</strong>, reviewed in PRs and applied by pipelines.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Benefit</th>\n<th>Explanation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Reproducibility</strong></td>\n<td>dev = staging = prod, created from the same templates with different parameters</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Reviewability</strong></td>\n<td>Infrastructure changes go through PRs like code</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>No drift</strong></td>\n<td>Manual portal changes are overwritten or detected (<code>what-if</code> / <code>plan</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Disaster recovery</strong></td>\n<td>Recreate an environment from code</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Documentation</strong></td>\n<td>The code <em>is</em> the up-to-date description</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Approaches: <strong>declarative</strong> (describe the desired end state; the tool works out the changes: Bicep, ARM, Terraform) vs <strong>imperative</strong> (scripts of commands: az CLI, PowerShell). Prefer declarative and <strong>idempotent</strong> (running it twice changes nothing the second time) — the exact same idempotency principle <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/api-design/http-methods-the-promises-hiding-behind-safe-and-idempotent\">API design</a> cared about for HTTP, here applied to infrastructure deployments.</p>\n<h3 id=\"bicep\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cicd-iac/bicep\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Bicep</h3>\n<p>Azure&#39;s native DSL that compiles to ARM JSON: concise, type-safe, day-zero support for new Azure features, <strong>no state file</strong> (Azure itself is the state).</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Bicep</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// main.bicep</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">targetScope</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">'resourceGroup'</span>\nparam location <span class=\"tk-ty\">string</span> = resourceGroup().location\n@allowed([<span class=\"tk-str\">'dev'</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">'staging'</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">'prod'</span>])\nparam env string\nparam imageTag string\n\nvar <span class=\"tk-ty\">prefix</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">'shop-${env}'</span>\n\nresource identity <span class=\"tk-str\">'Microsoft.ManagedIdentity/userAssignedIdentities@2023-01-31'</span> = {\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">'id-${prefix}-api'</span>\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">location</span>: location\n}\n\nresource logs <span class=\"tk-str\">'Microsoft.OperationalInsights/workspaces@2023-09-01'</span> = {\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">'log-${prefix}'</span>\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">location</span>: location\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">properties</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">sku</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">'PerGB2018'</span> }, <span class=\"tk-ty\">retentionInDays</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">30</span> }\n}\n\nresource cae <span class=\"tk-str\">'Microsoft.App/managedEnvironments@2024-03-01'</span> = {\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">'cae-${prefix}'</span>\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">location</span>: location\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">properties</span>: {\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">appLogsConfiguration</span>: {\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">destination</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">'log-analytics'</span>\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">logAnalyticsConfiguration</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">customerId</span>: logs.properties.customerId, <span class=\"tk-ty\">sharedKey</span>: logs.listKeys().primarySharedKey }\n    }\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">zoneRedundant</span>: <span class=\"tk-ty\">env</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">'prod'</span>\n  }\n}\n\nresource api <span class=\"tk-str\">'Microsoft.App/containerApps@2024-03-01'</span> = {\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">'ca-${prefix}-api'</span>\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">location</span>: location\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">identity</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">type</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">'UserAssigned'</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">userAssignedIdentities</span>: { <span class=\"tk-str\">'${identity.id}'</span>: {} } }\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">properties</span>: {\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">managedEnvironmentId</span>: cae.id\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">configuration</span>: {\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">ingress</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">external</span>: true, <span class=\"tk-ty\">targetPort</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">8080</span> }\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">registries</span>: [ { <span class=\"tk-ty\">server</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">'shopacr.azurecr.io'</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">identity</span>: identity.id } ]\n    }\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">template</span>: {\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">containers</span>: [ {\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">'api'</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">image</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">'shopacr.azurecr.io/shop-api:${imageTag}'</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">resources</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">cpu</span>: json(<span class=\"tk-str\">'0.5'</span>), <span class=\"tk-ty\">memory</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">'1Gi'</span> }\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">env</span>: [\n          { <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">'ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT'</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">value</span>: <span class=\"tk-ty\">env</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">'prod'</span> ? <span class=\"tk-str\">'Production'</span> : <span class=\"tk-str\">'Staging'</span> }\n          { <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">'AZURE_CLIENT_ID'</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">value</span>: identity.properties.clientId }\n        ]\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">probes</span>: [ { <span class=\"tk-ty\">type</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">'Readiness'</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">httpGet</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">path</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">'/health/ready'</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">port</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">8080</span> } } ]\n      } ]\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">scale</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">minReplicas</span>: <span class=\"tk-ty\">env</span> == <span class=\"tk-str\">'prod'</span> ? <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span> : <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">maxReplicas</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">10</span> }\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nmodule sql <span class=\"tk-str\">'modules/sql.bicep'</span> = { <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">'sql'</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">params</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">prefix</span>: prefix, <span class=\"tk-ty\">location</span>: location, <span class=\"tk-ty\">adminGroupObjectId</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">'…'</span> } }\n\noutput apiUrl <span class=\"tk-ty\">string</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">'https://${api.properties.configuration.ingress.fqdn}'</span></code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Shell</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>az deployment group what-if -g rg-shop-prod -f main.bicep -p <span class=\"tk-ty\">env</span>=prod <span class=\"tk-ty\">imageTag</span>=3f1c2a9b7d10   <span class=\"tk-com\"># preview changes</span>\naz deployment group create  -g rg-shop-prod -f main.bicep -p <span class=\"tk-ty\">env</span>=prod <span class=\"tk-ty\">imageTag</span>=3f1c2a9b7d10</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Use <strong>modules</strong> for reuse, <strong>Azure Verified Modules</strong> (AVM) for well-architected building blocks, <strong>parameter files</strong> (<code>.bicepparam</code>) per environment, and <strong>deployment stacks</strong> to manage and lock groups of resources.</p>\n<h3 id=\"terraform\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cicd-iac/terraform\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Terraform</h3>\n<p>HashiCorp&#39;s <strong>multi-cloud</strong> IaC with HCL and a huge provider ecosystem (Azure, AWS, GCP, Kubernetes, GitHub, Datadog…). It keeps a <strong>state file</strong> (store it remotely, e.g. in an Azure Storage backend with locking) mapping code to real resources; <code>terraform plan</code> shows the diff and <code>apply</code> executes it.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Terraform</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>resource <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;azurerm_resource_group&quot;</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;shop&quot;</span> {\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>     = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;rg-shop-${var.env}&quot;</span>\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">location</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;westeurope&quot;</span>\n}\n\nresource <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;azurerm_key_vault&quot;</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;kv&quot;</span> {\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>                      = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;kv-shop-${var.env}&quot;</span>\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">resource_group_name</span>       = azurerm_resource_group.shop.name\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">location</span>                  = azurerm_resource_group.shop.location\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">tenant_id</span>                 = data.azurerm_client_config.current.tenant_id\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">sku_name</span>                  = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;standard&quot;</span>\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">enable_rbac_authorization</span> = true\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">purge_protection_enabled</span>  = true\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Bicep</th>\n<th>Terraform</th>\n<th>Pulumi</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Clouds</td>\n<td>Azure only</td>\n<td>Multi-cloud + SaaS providers</td>\n<td>Multi-cloud</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Language</td>\n<td>Bicep DSL</td>\n<td>HCL</td>\n<td><strong>C#</strong>, TypeScript, Python, Go</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>State</td>\n<td>None (ARM is the source of truth)</td>\n<td>State file to manage</td>\n<td>Managed state service or self-hosted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>New Azure features</td>\n<td>Day zero</td>\n<td>Provider lag (AzAPI provider bridges it)</td>\n<td>Native Azure provider</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Best for</td>\n<td>Azure-only shops</td>\n<td>Multi-cloud, broad ecosystems</td>\n<td>Teams wanting IaC in a general-purpose language</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"azure-developer-cli-azd\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cicd-iac/azure-developer-cli-azd\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Azure Developer CLI (azd)</h3>\n<p><code>azd</code> ties code, IaC (Bicep/Terraform) and deployment together around <strong>templates</strong>: <code>azd init</code>, <code>azd up</code> (provision + deploy), <code>azd pipeline config</code> (generates a CI/CD pipeline with federated credentials). <strong>.NET Aspire</strong> projects deploy to Azure Container Apps with <code>azd up</code> by generating the infrastructure from the AppHost model — the exact AppHost described in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/observability-aspire/net-aspire\">Observability &amp; Aspire</a>, here feeding straight into real infrastructure rather than just local orchestration.</p>\n<h3 id=\"gitops\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cicd-iac/gitops\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>GitOps</h3>\n<p>For Kubernetes, <strong>GitOps</strong> (Flux, Argo CD) makes a Git repository the source of truth for cluster state: the CI pipeline updates the image tag in the manifests repo, and an in-cluster agent <strong>pulls</strong> and reconciles. See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/kubernetes\">Kubernetes</a>.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What&#39;s the difference between continuous integration, continuous delivery and continuous deployment?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>CI: developers merge frequently and every change is automatically built and tested. Continuous delivery: every passing change is kept in a releasable state and can be deployed to production at the push of a button (manual approval). Continuous deployment: every passing change is deployed to production automatically, with no manual gate.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What does a typical CI/CD pipeline for a .NET API look like?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>CI on each push/PR: restore (locked), build with warnings as errors, format/analyzer checks, unit and integration tests (Testcontainers), vulnerability scans, results and coverage. CD on main: build the container image once and tag it with the commit SHA, scan and push to ACR, deploy to staging (migration bundle, new revision or slot), smoke tests, approval, production via slot swap or canary, then monitoring with rollback.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Blue/green vs canary deployments?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Blue/green runs the new version in a parallel environment and switches all traffic at once, with instant rollback by switching back (App Service slot swap). Canary routes a small percentage of traffic to the new version and increases it gradually while health metrics stay good (Container Apps traffic splitting), limiting the blast radius.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you deploy database schema changes without downtime?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Expand/contract: make backward-compatible changes first (add nullable columns or new tables), deploy code that works with both old and new schemas, backfill, then remove old structures in a later release. Apply migrations from the pipeline (idempotent scripts or EF bundles) with a dedicated identity before deploying the app.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is Infrastructure as Code and why use it?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Defining cloud infrastructure in declarative, versioned code (Bicep, Terraform, Pulumi) that&#39;s reviewed in PRs and applied by pipelines. It gives reproducible environments, change previews (what-if/plan), no configuration drift, auditable history and fast disaster recovery.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Bicep vs Terraform?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Bicep is Azure&#39;s native DSL compiling to ARM: Azure-only, no state file, immediate support for new features. Terraform is multi-cloud with a large provider ecosystem and a state file you must store and lock remotely. Choose Bicep for Azure-only teams and Terraform for multi-cloud or when you need non-Azure providers.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How should a pipeline authenticate to Azure?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>With workload identity federation (OIDC): a federated credential on an Entra app or user-assigned managed identity that trusts the specific repository/environment, so no client secret is stored. Grant that identity least-privilege RBAC per environment.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cicd-iac/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/actions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GitHub Actions documentation</a> · <a href=\"https://docs.github.com/actions/use-cases-and-examples/building-and-testing/building-and-testing-net\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Building and testing .NET with GitHub Actions</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/pipelines/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Azure Pipelines documentation</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-resource-manager/bicep/overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Bicep documentation</a> · <a href=\"https://aka.ms/avm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Azure Verified Modules</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/developer/azure-developer-cli/overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Azure Developer CLI</a></li>\n<li>Books: <em>Accelerate</em> (Forsgren, Humble, Kim); <em>Continuous Delivery</em> (Humble, Farley).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/cicd-iac/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Most of this chapter is about GitHub Actions, Azure resources and Bicep/Terraform behaviour, which need real cloud infrastructure to actually exercise — not something a console program can verify. The checks below instead verify the three claims from this chapter that genuinely are just computation: idempotency, SemVer&#39;s numeric comparison, and the expand/contract migration pattern. Save as <code>Program.cs</code> in a new console project (<code>dotnet new console</code>) and run with <code>dotnet run</code>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Verifies claims from this chapter that are actually local/computational: IaC should be</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// idempotent (applying it twice changes nothing the second time), SemVer needs numeric comparison</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// (not string comparison, which gets &quot;1.10.0&quot; vs &quot;1.9.0&quot; backwards), and the expand/contract migration</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// pattern keeps both old and new code working during the transition window.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Most of this chapter's claims are about GitHub Actions, Azure resources, Bicep/Terraform behaviour</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// and DORA research — not runtime-verifiable in a console app. See Further reading (especially the</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// GitHub Actions, Bicep and *Accelerate* links) for those.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> fails = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> s) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: {s}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> cond, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> s)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (cond) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(s);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">else</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: {s}&quot;</span>); fails++; }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- IaC should be idempotent: applying the same desired state twice changes nothing the 2nd time ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> resource = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FakeAzureResource</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> firstApplyChanged = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Apply</span>(resource, desiredSku: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;P1v3&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> secondApplyChanged = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Apply</span>(resource, desiredSku: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;P1v3&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(firstApplyChanged, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;IaC apply: the first apply, from an empty resource, makes a change&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(!secondApplyChanged, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;IaC apply: applying the identical desired state again makes no change (idempotent)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- SemVer: comparing versions numerically, not as strings, is the whole point of the format ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> v1_9 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Version</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">9</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> v1_10 = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Version</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(v1_10 &gt; v1_9, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;SemVer: 1.10.0 is correctly a later version than 1.9.0 under numeric comparison&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Compare</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;1.10.0&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;1.9.0&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">StringComparison</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ordinal</span>) &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;SemVer: naive string comparison gets this backwards ('1.10.0' sorts before '1.9.0'), which is exactly why tools compare version parts numerically instead&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// --- Expand/contract: during the transition, code using the OLD shape and code using the NEW shape must both work ---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> row = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ExpandedRow</span>();                  <span class=\"tk-com\">// &quot;expand&quot; step: write both old and new columns</span>\nrow.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteBoth</span>(oldTotal: <span class=\"tk-num\">100m</span>, newTotalCents: <span class=\"tk-num\">10000</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadOld</span>(row) == <span class=\"tk-num\">100m</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Expand/contract: old-shape reader still works during the transition (reads OldTotal)&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadNew</span>(row) == <span class=\"tk-num\">100m</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Expand/contract: new-shape reader already works too (reads NewTotalCents / 100)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(fails == <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span> ? <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;\\nAll checks passed.&quot;</span> : <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;\\n{fails} check(s) failed.&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (fails &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exit</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Apply</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">FakeAzureResource</span> resource, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> desiredSku)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (resource.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span> == desiredSku) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;   <span class=\"tk-com\">// already in the desired state: no-op</span>\n    resource.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span> = desiredSku;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadOld</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExpandedRow</span> r) =&gt; r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OldTotal</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">static</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadNew</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">ExpandedRow</span> r) =&gt; r.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NewTotalCents</span> / <span class=\"tk-num\">100m</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FakeAzureResource</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>? <span class=\"tk-ty\">Sku</span>; }\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ExpandedRow</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OldTotal</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">long</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">NewTotalCents</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteBoth</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> oldTotal, <span class=\"tk-kw\">long</span> newTotalCents) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">OldTotal</span> = oldTotal; <span class=\"tk-ty\">NewTotalCents</span> = newTotalCents; }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"ci-cd-and-cd","text":"CI, CD and CD","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"branching-strategies","text":"Branching strategies","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"anatomy-of-a-net-pipeline","text":"Anatomy of a .NET pipeline","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"github-actions-a-complete-example","text":"GitHub Actions: a complete example","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"azure-devops-pipelines","text":"Azure DevOps Pipelines","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"deployment-strategies","text":"Deployment strategies","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"databases-in-the-pipeline","text":"Databases in the pipeline","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"versioning-and-artefacts","text":"Versioning and artefacts","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"infrastructure-as-code","text":"Infrastructure as Code","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"bicep","text":"Bicep","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"terraform","text":"Terraform","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"azure-developer-cli-azd","text":"Azure Developer CLI (azd)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"gitops","text":"GitOps","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":3648,"minutes":17,"num":"8.6","part":"p8"},"kubernetes":{"id":"kubernetes","title":"Kubernetes for .NET Developers","summary":"The container orchestrator, from a developer's point of view. Architecture (control plane, nodes), the core objects (Pod, Deployment, Service, Ingress/Gateway, ConfigMap, Secret, namespaces), probes, resources and autoscaling (HPA, KEDA), rolling updates, Helm and GitOps, running ASP.NET Core well on Kubernetes, and AKS specifics.","layer":"infra","where":"Hosting & infrastructure › Container orchestration","fits":"<p>Kubernetes runs your containers (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/docker\">Docker</a>) at scale. It&#39;s the engine under Azure Container Apps and the platform behind AKS (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-compute\">Azure compute</a>). It relies on your app being cloud-native (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cloud-azure-overview/cloud-native-app-design-the-twelve-factors\">Cloud &amp; Azure overview</a>): health endpoints (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/resilience/health-checks\">Resilience</a>), graceful shutdown (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting/application-lifetime-and-graceful-shutdown-what-actually-hap\">Hosting</a>), config from the environment and telemetry export (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/observability-aspire\">Observability</a>).</p>\n","prereqs":["docker","azure-compute"],"next":["observability-aspire","performance-engineering"],"levels":["i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/kubernetes/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Kubernetes runs containers across a cluster toward a <strong>declared desired state</strong>, continuously reconciling reality to match it: scheduling, restarting crashed containers, scaling, load balancing, rolling updates.</li>\n<li>A <strong>Pod</strong> is the smallest unit; a <strong>Deployment</strong> manages pods and rolling updates; a <strong>Service</strong> gives them a stable name; an <strong>Ingress</strong> routes external HTTP traffic in.</li>\n<li><strong>Liveness</strong> probes restart a stuck container; <strong>readiness</strong> probes pull a pod out of traffic without restarting it — never make liveness depend on an external dependency, or one DB blip restarts everything.</li>\n<li>On pod termination: removed from the Service, then <code>SIGTERM</code> (the .NET host starts graceful shutdown), then <code>SIGKILL</code> after <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> if it hasn&#39;t exited.</li>\n<li><strong>Requests</strong> reserve scheduling capacity; <strong>limits</strong> cap usage — exceed the memory limit and you get <code>OOMKilled</code>, exceed the CPU limit and you get throttled (latency spikes, not a crash).</li>\n<li>Prefer <strong>AKS Workload Identity</strong> over storing secrets in the cluster: pods get federated Azure AD tokens, so <code>DefaultAzureCredential</code> reaches SQL, Key Vault and Service Bus with nothing secret sitting in etcd.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"what-kubernetes-does\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/kubernetes/what-kubernetes-does\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What Kubernetes does <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Kubernetes (K8s)</strong> is an open-source system for <strong>running containerised applications across a cluster of machines</strong>. You <strong>declare the desired state</strong> (&quot;3 replicas of shop-api:1.4.2, each with 0.5 CPU, reachable at orders.shop.com&quot;) and Kubernetes <strong>continuously reconciles</strong> reality to match — the whole system is built around that single loop, running forever in the background:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Scheduling</strong>: places containers on nodes with enough resources.</li>\n<li><strong>Self-healing</strong>: restarts crashed containers, replaces pods on failed nodes, kills pods failing liveness probes.</li>\n<li><strong>Scaling</strong>: replicas up and down, manually or automatically.</li>\n<li><strong>Service discovery and load balancing</strong>: stable names and virtual IPs for changing sets of pods.</li>\n<li><strong>Rolling updates and rollbacks</strong>: replace versions gradually, with no downtime.</li>\n<li><strong>Configuration and secrets</strong> injection.</li>\n<li><strong>Storage orchestration</strong>: attach persistent volumes.</li>\n</ul>\n<aside class=\"callout c-why\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Why Kubernetes, and why not</div><p>It gives a <strong>standard, portable platform</strong> for running many services with consistent deployment, scaling and operations, across clouds and on-premises, with a vast ecosystem (Helm, operators, service meshes, GitOps). The cost is <strong>complexity</strong>: networking, security, upgrades, observability and the YAML surface area need platform-engineering skills. Many teams get most of the benefits with less effort from <strong>Azure Container Apps</strong> (Kubernetes underneath, managed for you).</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"architecture\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/kubernetes/architecture\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Architecture <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>┌───────────────────────── Control plane (managed for you in AKS) ─────────────────────────┐\n│  kube-apiserver (the API: kubectl, CI, controllers talk to it)                             │\n│  etcd (cluster state store) · scheduler (assigns pods to nodes) · controller-manager       │\n│  (reconcile loops: Deployments, ReplicaSets, Nodes, Jobs…) · cloud-controller-manager       │\n└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n          │ watches/updates desired state\n┌─────────▼──────── Node (VM) ─────────┐   ┌──────── Node ────────┐   ┌──────── Node ────────┐\n│ kubelet (runs pods, reports status)   │   │ kubelet               │   │ kubelet               │\n│ container runtime (containerd)        │   │ …                     │   │ …                     │\n│ kube-proxy / CNI (networking)         │   │                       │   │                       │\n│ ┌─Pod──────────┐ ┌─Pod──────────┐     │   │ ┌─Pod──────────┐      │   │ ┌─Pod──────────┐      │\n│ │ shop-api     │ │ shop-worker  │     │   │ │ shop-api     │      │   │ │ shop-api     │      │\n│ └──────────────┘ └──────────────┘     │   │ └──────────────┘      │   │ └──────────────┘      │\n└───────────────────────────────────────┘   └───────────────────────┘   └───────────────────────┘</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Everything is an <strong>API object</strong> described in YAML (or JSON), submitted with <code>kubectl apply -f</code>, stored in etcd, and acted on by <strong>controllers</strong> running <strong>reconciliation loops</strong> (observe → compare with desired → act) — the &quot;desired state&quot; idea from the opening section, now a concrete object sitting in a concrete place.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-core-objects\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/kubernetes/the-core-objects\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The core objects <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Object</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>.NET-app relevance</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Pod</strong></td>\n<td>The smallest unit: one or more containers sharing network (localhost) and volumes; ephemeral, with its own IP</td>\n<td>Usually one app container (+ optional sidecars)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ReplicaSet</strong></td>\n<td>Keeps N identical pods running</td>\n<td>Managed by Deployments; rarely used directly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Deployment</strong></td>\n<td>Declares pods + replicas + <strong>rolling update</strong> strategy; manages ReplicaSets per version</td>\n<td><strong>How you run a stateless API or worker</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Service</strong></td>\n<td>Stable virtual IP + DNS name load-balancing to pods selected by <strong>labels</strong> (<code>ClusterIP</code>, <code>NodePort</code>, <code>LoadBalancer</code>)</td>\n<td><code>http://shop-api.shop.svc.cluster.local</code> for service-to-service calls</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ingress</strong> / <strong>Gateway API</strong></td>\n<td>HTTP(S) routing from outside to Services (hosts, paths, TLS) via an ingress controller (NGINX, Traefik, App Gateway for Containers)</td>\n<td>Public entry for APIs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ConfigMap</strong></td>\n<td>Non-secret configuration as key/values or files</td>\n<td>Environment variables / mounted <code>appsettings.Production.json</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Secret</strong></td>\n<td>Sensitive values (base64-encoded, <strong>not encrypted</strong> by default; enable etcd encryption, or use the Key Vault CSI driver)</td>\n<td>Connection strings (better: workload identity)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Namespace</strong></td>\n<td>A virtual cluster partition for isolation, quotas and RBAC</td>\n<td>One per team/app/environment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>StatefulSet</strong></td>\n<td>Pods with stable identity and storage</td>\n<td>Databases, brokers (often better as managed PaaS)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>DaemonSet</strong></td>\n<td>One pod per node</td>\n<td>Log/metrics agents</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Job / CronJob</strong></td>\n<td>Run-to-completion / scheduled</td>\n<td>Migrations, nightly batch jobs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>HorizontalPodAutoscaler</strong></td>\n<td>Scales replicas on metrics</td>\n<td>Autoscaling APIs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>PersistentVolume(Claim)</strong></td>\n<td>Durable storage</td>\n<td>Rare for stateless .NET services</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ServiceAccount</strong></td>\n<td>Pod identity for the K8s API and <strong>workload identity</strong> to Azure</td>\n<td>Passwordless Azure access</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"a-complete-manifest-for-an-asp-net-core-api\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/kubernetes/a-complete-manifest-for-an-asp-net-core-api\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>A complete manifest for an ASP.NET Core API <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>YAML</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-ty\">apiVersion</span>: apps/v1\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">kind</span>: Deployment\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">metadata</span>:\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: shop-api\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">namespace</span>: shop\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">labels</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">app</span>: shop-api }\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">spec</span>:\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">replicas</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">revisionHistoryLimit</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">selector</span>:\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">matchLabels</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">app</span>: shop-api }\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">strategy</span>:\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">type</span>: RollingUpdate\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">rollingUpdate</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">maxSurge</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">maxUnavailable</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span> }         <span class=\"tk-com\"># zero-downtime: add one new before removing an old</span>\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">template</span>:\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">metadata</span>:\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">labels</span>:\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">app</span>: shop-api\n        azure.workload.identity/<span class=\"tk-ty\">use</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;true&quot;</span>                     <span class=\"tk-com\"># AKS workload identity</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">spec</span>:\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">serviceAccountName</span>: shop-api\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">terminationGracePeriodSeconds</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">45</span>                         <span class=\"tk-com\"># &gt; app's ShutdownTimeout</span>\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">securityContext</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">runAsNonRoot</span>: true, <span class=\"tk-ty\">seccompProfile</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">type</span>: RuntimeDefault } }\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">containers</span>:\n        - <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: api\n          <span class=\"tk-ty\">image</span>: shopacr.azurecr.io/<span class=\"tk-ty\">shop-api</span>:3f1c2a9b7d10       <span class=\"tk-com\"># immutable tag (git SHA)</span>\n          <span class=\"tk-ty\">ports</span>: [{ <span class=\"tk-ty\">containerPort</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">8080</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: http }]\n          <span class=\"tk-ty\">env</span>:\n            - { <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT, <span class=\"tk-ty\">value</span>: Production }\n            - { <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT, <span class=\"tk-ty\">value</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;http://otel-collector.observability:4317&quot;</span> }\n            - <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: ConnectionStrings__Shop\n              <span class=\"tk-ty\">valueFrom</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">secretKeyRef</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: shop-api-secrets, <span class=\"tk-ty\">key</span>: sql } }\n          <span class=\"tk-ty\">envFrom</span>:\n            - <span class=\"tk-ty\">configMapRef</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: shop-api-config }\n          <span class=\"tk-ty\">resources</span>:\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">requests</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">cpu</span>: 250m, <span class=\"tk-ty\">memory</span>: 256Mi }              <span class=\"tk-com\"># used for scheduling (guaranteed)</span>\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">limits</span>:   { <span class=\"tk-ty\">memory</span>: 512Mi }                         <span class=\"tk-com\"># exceed → OOMKilled. CPU limits often omitted to avoid throttling</span>\n          <span class=\"tk-ty\">startupProbe</span>:                                         <span class=\"tk-com\"># give slow starts (JIT, warm-up) time before other probes</span>\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">httpGet</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">path</span>: /health/live, <span class=\"tk-ty\">port</span>: http }\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">failureThreshold</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">30</span>\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">periodSeconds</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>\n          <span class=\"tk-ty\">livenessProbe</span>:                                        <span class=\"tk-com\"># failing → container restarted</span>\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">httpGet</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">path</span>: /health/live, <span class=\"tk-ty\">port</span>: http }\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">periodSeconds</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>\n          <span class=\"tk-ty\">readinessProbe</span>:                                       <span class=\"tk-com\"># failing → removed from Service endpoints (no traffic)</span>\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">httpGet</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">path</span>: /health/ready, <span class=\"tk-ty\">port</span>: http }\n            <span class=\"tk-ty\">periodSeconds</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>\n          <span class=\"tk-ty\">securityContext</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">allowPrivilegeEscalation</span>: false, <span class=\"tk-ty\">readOnlyRootFilesystem</span>: true, <span class=\"tk-ty\">capabilities</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">drop</span>: [ALL] } }\n          <span class=\"tk-ty\">volumeMounts</span>: [{ <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: tmp, <span class=\"tk-ty\">mountPath</span>: /tmp }]\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">volumes</span>: [{ <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: tmp, <span class=\"tk-ty\">emptyDir</span>: {} }]\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">topologySpreadConstraints</span>:                                <span class=\"tk-com\"># spread replicas across zones</span>\n        - { <span class=\"tk-ty\">maxSkew</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">topologyKey</span>: topology.kubernetes.io/zone, <span class=\"tk-ty\">whenUnsatisfiable</span>: ScheduleAnyway, <span class=\"tk-ty\">labelSelector</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">matchLabels</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">app</span>: shop-api } } }\n<span class=\"tk-attr\">---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">apiVersion</span>: v1\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">kind</span>: Service\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">metadata</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: shop-api, <span class=\"tk-ty\">namespace</span>: shop }\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">spec</span>:\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">selector</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">app</span>: shop-api }\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">ports</span>: [{ <span class=\"tk-ty\">port</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">80</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">targetPort</span>: http }]\n<span class=\"tk-attr\">---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">apiVersion</span>: networking.k8s.io/v1\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">kind</span>: Ingress\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">metadata</span>:\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: shop-api\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">namespace</span>: shop\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">annotations</span>: { cert-manager.io/<span class=\"tk-ty\">cluster-issuer</span>: letsencrypt }\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">spec</span>:\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">ingressClassName</span>: nginx\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">tls</span>: [{ <span class=\"tk-ty\">hosts</span>: [api.shop.com], <span class=\"tk-ty\">secretName</span>: api-shop-tls }]\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">rules</span>:\n    - <span class=\"tk-ty\">host</span>: api.shop.com\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">http</span>:\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">paths</span>: [{ <span class=\"tk-ty\">path</span>: /, <span class=\"tk-ty\">pathType</span>: Prefix, <span class=\"tk-ty\">backend</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">service</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: shop-api, <span class=\"tk-ty\">port</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">number</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">80</span> } } } }]\n<span class=\"tk-attr\">---</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">apiVersion</span>: policy/v1\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">kind</span>: PodDisruptionBudget                                    <span class=\"tk-com\"># keep ≥2 pods during node drains/upgrades</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">metadata</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: shop-api, <span class=\"tk-ty\">namespace</span>: shop }\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">spec</span>:\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">minAvailable</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">selector</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">matchLabels</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">app</span>: shop-api } }</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Notice <code>/health/live</code> and <code>/health/ready</code> go to <em>different</em> probes: <code>startupProbe</code>/<code>livenessProbe</code> use the liveness path, <code>readinessProbe</code> uses the readiness one. That split matters because of what <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/resilience/health-checks\">Resilience</a> built those two endpoints for: a dependency hiccup should trip readiness (removed from traffic, left to retry), not liveness (restarted — which would turn a blip into an outage).</p>\n<h2 id=\"probes-and-graceful-shutdown\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/kubernetes/probes-and-graceful-shutdown\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Probes and graceful shutdown <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Probe</th>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>On failure</th>\n<th>Don&#39;t…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>startup</strong></td>\n<td>Has the app finished starting?</td>\n<td>Keep waiting (up to the threshold), then restart</td>\n<td>Leave it out for slow-starting apps</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>liveness</strong></td>\n<td>Is the process healthy (not deadlocked)?</td>\n<td><strong>Restart</strong> the container</td>\n<td>Check databases or other dependencies (a DB blip would restart everything)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>readiness</strong></td>\n<td>Can it serve traffic right now?</td>\n<td><strong>Remove from Service endpoints</strong></td>\n<td>Make it so strict that all pods go unready together</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Termination sequence</strong> when a pod is deleted (rolling update, scale-in, node drain) — this is the exact SIGTERM-driven sequence <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting/application-lifetime-and-graceful-shutdown-what-actually-hap\">ASP.NET Core architecture</a> walked through, now seen from the orchestrator&#39;s side of the handshake:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The pod is marked Terminating; it&#39;s <strong>removed from Service endpoints</strong> (asynchronously!) and any <code>preStop</code> hook runs.</li>\n<li><strong>SIGTERM</strong> is sent to the container → the .NET host begins graceful shutdown (stops accepting connections, drains in-flight requests, stops hosted services).</li>\n<li>After <code>terminationGracePeriodSeconds</code> (30 s default), <strong>SIGKILL</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Because endpoint removal is asynchronous, a pod can still receive new requests for a moment after SIGTERM — a real race between &quot;tell the load balancer to stop sending traffic here&quot; and &quot;actually stop accepting it,&quot; with no guarantee which one wins first. A common mitigation is a short <code>preStop: sleep 5</code>. Keep <code>HostOptions.ShutdownTimeout</code> below the grace period.</p>\n<h2 id=\"resources-qos-and-autoscaling\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/kubernetes/resources-qos-and-autoscaling\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Resources, QoS and autoscaling <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Requests</strong> reserve capacity for scheduling; <strong>limits</strong> cap usage. Memory over the limit means <strong>OOMKilled</strong>; CPU over the limit means <strong>throttling</strong> (latency spikes), which is why many teams set CPU requests but no CPU limit for latency-sensitive .NET services.</li>\n<li><strong>QoS classes</strong>: Guaranteed (requests = limits), Burstable, BestEffort (none: evicted first).</li>\n<li>.NET respects container limits (GC heap limit ~75% of memory limit by default; CPU count from the cgroup quota — see Further reading for the exact mechanism). <strong>DATAS</strong> (default in .NET 9+ Server GC) keeps memory proportional to load. See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/docker/container-memory-and-cpu-limits-with-net\">Docker</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Autoscaler</th>\n<th>Scales</th>\n<th>On</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>HPA</strong> (Horizontal Pod Autoscaler)</td>\n<td>Pod replicas</td>\n<td>CPU/memory utilisation, or custom/external metrics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>KEDA</strong></td>\n<td>Pod replicas, <strong>including to zero</strong></td>\n<td>Event sources: Service Bus queue length, Kafka lag, RabbitMQ, Prometheus queries, cron…</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>VPA</strong></td>\n<td>Pod requests/limits</td>\n<td>Observed usage (recommendation mode is safest)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cluster autoscaler</strong> / Node auto-provisioning</td>\n<td><strong>Nodes</strong></td>\n<td>Pending pods that can&#39;t be scheduled</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>YAML</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-ty\">apiVersion</span>: keda.sh/v1alpha1\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">kind</span>: ScaledObject\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">metadata</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: shop-worker, <span class=\"tk-ty\">namespace</span>: shop }\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">spec</span>:\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">scaleTargetRef</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: shop-worker }\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">minReplicaCount</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">maxReplicaCount</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">30</span>\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">triggers</span>:\n    - <span class=\"tk-ty\">type</span>: azure-servicebus\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">metadata</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">queueName</span>: orders, <span class=\"tk-ty\">namespace</span>: sb-shop-prod, <span class=\"tk-ty\">messageCount</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;100&quot;</span> }\n      <span class=\"tk-ty\">authenticationRef</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">name</span>: servicebus-workload-identity }</code></pre></figure>\n<h2 id=\"configuration-secrets-and-identity\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/kubernetes/configuration-secrets-and-identity\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Configuration, secrets and identity <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>ConfigMaps</strong> map naturally to .NET configuration: as <strong>environment variables</strong> (<code>Payments__BaseUrl</code>) or <strong>mounted files</strong> (<code>appsettings.Production.json</code>, picked up with <code>reloadOnChange</code>, or <code>AddKeyPerFile</code> for one-file-per-key).</li>\n<li><strong>Secrets</strong>: native Secrets are only base64-encoded. Prefer the <strong>Secrets Store CSI driver with Azure Key Vault</strong>, External Secrets Operator, or — best of all — <strong>AKS Workload Identity</strong>: annotate a ServiceAccount with a user-assigned managed identity&#39;s client ID, and pods get federated tokens. <code>DefaultAzureCredential</code> → <code>WorkloadIdentityCredential</code> then reaches SQL, Service Bus and Key Vault with <strong>no secrets in the cluster</strong> — the same workload identity federation mechanism from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets/workload-identity-federation-ci-cd-and-kubernetes\">Identity &amp; secrets</a>, here granting pods identity the same way it grants GitHub Actions workflows identity.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"packaging-and-delivery-helm-kustomize-gitops\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/kubernetes/packaging-and-delivery-helm-kustomize-gitops\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Packaging and delivery: Helm, Kustomize, GitOps <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Helm</strong>: the package manager for Kubernetes. <strong>Charts</strong> are templated manifests with <code>values.yaml</code> per environment; <code>helm upgrade --install shop-api ./chart -f values-prod.yaml</code>. Also used to install third-party software (ingress-nginx, cert-manager, KEDA, Prometheus).</li>\n<li><strong>Kustomize</strong>: template-free overlays (base + patches per environment), built into <code>kubectl apply -k</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>GitOps</strong> with <strong>Argo CD</strong> or <strong>Flux</strong>: the desired cluster state lives in Git; an in-cluster agent pulls and reconciles, giving drift correction, audit history and easy rollback (revert the commit). CI only builds the image and bumps the tag in the config repo — a direct application of the &quot;build once, deploy many&quot; principle from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cicd-iac/anatomy-of-a-net-pipeline\">CI/CD &amp; IaC</a>, with Git itself as the record of which image is meant to be running where.</li>\n<li><strong>Progressive delivery</strong>: Argo Rollouts / Flagger for canaries with automated metric analysis.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"day-to-day-kubectl\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/kubernetes/day-to-day-kubectl\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Day-to-day kubectl <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Shell</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>kubectl config use-context aks-shop-prod\nkubectl get pods -n shop -o wide                       <span class=\"tk-com\"># status, node, IP</span>\nkubectl describe pod shop-api-7d9f… -n shop            <span class=\"tk-com\"># events: scheduling, probe failures, OOMKilled, image pull errors</span>\nkubectl logs -f deploy/shop-api -n shop <span class=\"tk-attr\">--since</span>=10m    <span class=\"tk-com\"># stream logs (all pods of the deployment with --all-pods)</span>\nkubectl exec -it shop-api-7d9f… -n shop -- sh          <span class=\"tk-com\"># (not possible with chiseled images: use ephemeral debug containers)</span>\nkubectl debug -it shop-api-7d9f… <span class=\"tk-attr\">--image</span>=mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/<span class=\"tk-ty\">sdk</span>:<span class=\"tk-num\">10.0</span> <span class=\"tk-attr\">--target</span>=api   <span class=\"tk-com\"># attach dotnet-trace/counters</span>\nkubectl rollout status deploy/shop-api -n shop\nkubectl rollout undo deploy/shop-api -n shop           <span class=\"tk-com\"># roll back to the previous ReplicaSet</span>\nkubectl scale deploy/shop-api <span class=\"tk-attr\">--replicas</span>=<span class=\"tk-num\">5</span> -n shop\nkubectl top pods -n shop                               <span class=\"tk-com\"># CPU/memory (metrics-server)</span>\nkubectl port-forward svc/shop-api <span class=\"tk-num\">8080</span>:<span class=\"tk-num\">80</span> -n shop      <span class=\"tk-com\"># reach a ClusterIP service locally</span></code></pre></figure>\n<p>Common failure states: <strong><code>CrashLoopBackOff</code></strong> (the app exits on start: check the logs, config and missing env vars), <strong><code>ImagePullBackOff</code></strong> (wrong tag, or no registry access: grant <code>AcrPull</code>), <strong><code>OOMKilled</code></strong> (raise the memory limit or fix a leak), <strong><code>Pending</code></strong> (not enough capacity, or unsatisfiable affinity/taints), and readiness failing (dependency unreachable, probe path wrong).</p>\n<h2 id=\"aks-specifics\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/kubernetes/aks-specifics\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>AKS specifics <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Area</th>\n<th>AKS feature</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Control plane</td>\n<td>Managed and free (Standard tier adds an uptime SLA); <strong>AKS Automatic</strong> for an opinionated, fully managed experience</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nodes</td>\n<td><strong>Node pools</strong> (VM scale sets): system vs user pools, spot pools, GPU pools, Linux/Windows; auto-upgrade channels and planned maintenance windows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Networking</td>\n<td>Azure CNI (Overlay recommended), network policies (Azure/Calico/Cilium), private clusters, <strong>Application Gateway for Containers</strong> / application routing add-on (managed NGINX)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Identity</td>\n<td>Entra ID integration for kubectl and Kubernetes RBAC; <strong>Workload Identity</strong> for pods</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Registry</td>\n<td>ACR attach (<code>az aks update --attach-acr</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Secrets</td>\n<td>Key Vault Secrets Store CSI driver add-on</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scaling</td>\n<td>Cluster autoscaler, <strong>KEDA add-on</strong>, node auto-provisioning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Observability</td>\n<td>Container Insights, <strong>Managed Prometheus + Managed Grafana</strong>, OpenTelemetry</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Security</td>\n<td>Microsoft Defender for Containers, Azure Policy for Kubernetes, image cleaner, deployment safeguards</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Service mesh</td>\n<td>Istio-based service mesh add-on</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is Kubernetes and what problems does it solve?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A container orchestration platform that runs containers across a cluster from declarative desired state, providing scheduling, self-healing, horizontal scaling, service discovery and load balancing, rolling updates and rollbacks, and configuration/secret injection, through a standard API with a large ecosystem.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Pod vs Deployment vs Service?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A Pod is the smallest deployable unit: one or more co-located containers sharing network and storage, and ephemeral. A Deployment declares the desired pods and replica count and manages rolling updates through ReplicaSets. A Service provides a stable virtual IP and DNS name that load-balances across the pods selected by labels.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are liveness, readiness and startup probes?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Startup probes delay other checks until the app has started. Liveness probes detect a hung process, and failure restarts the container. Readiness probes indicate whether the pod should receive traffic, and failure removes it from Service endpoints without restarting. Liveness must not depend on external dependencies.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How does a rolling update work and how do you roll back?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The Deployment creates a new ReplicaSet and gradually scales it up while scaling the old one down, respecting maxSurge/maxUnavailable and readiness probes, so capacity is preserved. Roll back with <code>kubectl rollout undo</code> (back to the previous ReplicaSet), or by reverting the manifest in GitOps.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Requests vs limits?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Requests are the resources reserved for scheduling a pod (guaranteed). Limits are maximums: exceeding the memory limit gets the container OOMKilled, and exceeding the CPU limit throttles it. Setting realistic requests and memory limits matters for stability; CPU limits can cause latency through throttling.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you scale on queue length in Kubernetes?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>With KEDA: a ScaledObject targeting the deployment, with a trigger such as azure-servicebus (queue name, target message count per replica) and authentication, scaling replicas between min (possibly 0) and max based on backlog.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How should a .NET app in AKS access Azure SQL or Key Vault?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>With AKS Workload Identity: a Kubernetes ServiceAccount federated with a user-assigned managed identity; pods get tokens via WorkloadIdentityCredential (included in DefaultAzureCredential), and the identity has least-privilege roles on SQL, Key Vault and Service Bus. No secrets in the cluster.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What does CrashLoopBackOff mean?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The container keeps exiting shortly after starting and Kubernetes restarts it with increasing backoff. Usually it&#39;s a startup exception (bad config, missing env var or secret, failing ValidateOnStart, unreachable dependency at startup) or a failing liveness probe. Check <code>kubectl logs --previous</code> and <code>describe pod</code> events.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/kubernetes/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kubernetes documentation: concepts</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/aks/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AKS documentation</a> · <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/reference-architectures/containers/aks/baseline-aks\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AKS baseline architecture</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/aks/workload-identity-overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Use Microsoft Entra Workload ID with AKS</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://keda.sh/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">KEDA</a> · <a href=\"https://helm.sh/docs/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Helm</a> · <a href=\"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Argo CD</a></li>\n<li>Book: <em>Kubernetes Up &amp; Running</em> (Burns, Beda, Hightower, Evenson).</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/runtime-config/garbage-collector\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Runtime configuration options for garbage collection</a> (the <code>~75%</code> heap-limit default and <code>DOTNET_GCHeapHardLimit</code>)</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/kubernetes/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Almost everything in this chapter is Kubernetes&#39;s own orchestration behavior — scheduling, probes, rolling updates — which genuinely needs a real cluster to observe; a C# program on its own can&#39;t fake that honestly. The one claim that <em>is</em> pure .NET runtime behavior, and the same cgroup-awareness mechanism that makes .NET respect a container&#39;s memory limit under Kubernetes or Docker, is verified below by actually setting a hard GC memory ceiling and reading it back. Save as a console app (<code>dotnet new console</code>), then <code>dotnet run</code>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Diagnostics</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetEnvironmentVariable</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;VERIFY_CHILD&quot;</span>) == <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;1&quot;</span>)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> info = <span class=\"tk-ty\">GC</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetGCMemoryInfo</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(info.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TotalAvailableMemoryBytes</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span>;\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claim: .NET's GC respects a configured hard memory ceiling — the exact mechanism that makes it respect a</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Kubernetes/Docker cgroup memory limit (this chapter's &quot;GC heap limit ~75% of memory limit&quot; claim).</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// DOTNET_GCHeapHardLimit sets an explicit byte ceiling; we verify it by reading it back from a child process,</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// since GC configuration is fixed at process startup.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">const</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">long</span> limitBytes = <span class=\"tk-num\">256L</span> * <span class=\"tk-num\">1024</span> * <span class=\"tk-num\">1024</span>; <span class=\"tk-com\">// 256 MiB</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> psi = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProcessStartInfo</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">FileName</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProcessPath</span>!,\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">RedirectStandardOutput</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>,\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">UseShellExecute</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>,\n};\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> arg <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetCommandLineArgs</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">Skip</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>)) psi.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ArgumentList</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(arg);\npsi.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;VERIFY_CHILD&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;1&quot;</span>;\npsi.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;DOTNET_GCHeapHardLimit&quot;</span>] = limitBytes.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;X&quot;</span>); <span class=\"tk-com\">// DOTNET_GCHeapHardLimit takes a hex byte count</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> proc = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Process</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Start</span>(psi)!;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> output = (<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> proc.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StandardOutput</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadToEndAsync</span>()).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Trim</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> proc.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WaitForExitAsync</span>();\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> reportedLimit = <span class=\"tk-kw\">long</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parse</span>(output);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (reportedLimit &gt; limitBytes || reportedLimit &lt; limitBytes / <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>)\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected the GC to report an available-memory ceiling near {limitBytes} bytes, got {reportedLimit}&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: DOTNET_GCHeapHardLimit made the GC report a hard ceiling (~{reportedLimit / 1024 / 1024} MiB) — &quot;</span> +\n    <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;the same cgroup-awareness mechanism that makes .NET respect a container's memory limit under Kubernetes or Docker&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All checks passed.&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here (needs a real cluster, which this verification environment cannot assume):</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - scheduling, self-healing, rolling updates, readiness/liveness probe behavior — see Further reading: &quot;Kubernetes documentation: concepts&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - HPA/KEDA autoscaling, AKS workload identity, GitOps reconciliation — see Further reading: &quot;KEDA&quot; / &quot;Use Microsoft Entra Workload ID with AKS&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - the exact ~75% GC heap default and CPU-count-from-cgroup-quota figures in a real container — see Further reading:</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//   &quot;Runtime configuration options for garbage collection&quot; (the general mechanism verified above is the same one)</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"what-kubernetes-does","text":"What Kubernetes does","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"architecture","text":"Architecture","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"the-core-objects","text":"The core objects","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"a-complete-manifest-for-an-asp-net-core-api","text":"A complete manifest for an ASP.NET Core API","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"probes-and-graceful-shutdown","text":"Probes and graceful shutdown","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"resources-qos-and-autoscaling","text":"Resources, QoS and autoscaling","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"configuration-secrets-and-identity","text":"Configuration, secrets and identity","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"packaging-and-delivery-helm-kustomize-gitops","text":"Packaging and delivery: Helm, Kustomize, GitOps","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"day-to-day-kubectl","text":"Day-to-day kubectl","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"aks-specifics","text":"AKS specifics","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":3294,"minutes":15,"num":"8.7","part":"p8"},"memory-gc":{"id":"memory-gc","title":"Memory Management and the Garbage Collector","summary":"How .NET allocates and frees memory. The stack and managed heap, allocation, the generational hypothesis, Gen0/1/2, the Large and Pinned Object Heaps, mark-sweep-compact, GC roots, workstation vs server GC, background GC, DATAS and container limits, finalization, weak references, memory leaks in .NET and how to diagnose them, and reducing allocations.","layer":"runtime","where":"Runtime & platform › Memory management (GC)","fits":"<p>The GC is a core service of the CLR (layer 2; see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/how-code-runs\">How code runs</a>). Every choice in layer 1 (value vs reference types in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/type-system\">Type system</a>, closures in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/delegates-events\">Delegates</a>, async state machines in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/async-await\">async/await</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/advanced-csharp\">Span</a>) changes how much work it does. In layer 7, container memory limits (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/docker\">Docker</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/kubernetes\">Kubernetes</a>) shape how it behaves.</p>\n","prereqs":["type-system","how-code-runs"],"next":["threading-concurrency","performance-engineering"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/memory-gc/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>.NET frees memory for you: the <strong>garbage collector (GC)</strong> reclaims an object&#39;s memory once nothing can reach it any more — no <code>malloc</code>/<code>free</code>, no use-after-free, no double free.</li>\n<li>Objects are grouped by age into <strong>generations</strong> (Gen0 → Gen1 → Gen2) because most objects die young; collecting the small, young Gen0 often is cheap, and full Gen2 sweeps (which include the Large Object Heap) are rare and expensive.</li>\n<li>Big objects (≥ 85,000 bytes) skip straight to the <strong>Large Object Heap</strong>, which isn&#39;t compacted by default — allocate large buffers per request and you&#39;ll fragment it and trigger extra full GCs.</li>\n<li><strong>Server GC</strong> (ASP.NET Core&#39;s default) trades memory for throughput using one heap per core; <strong>DATAS</strong> (.NET 9+) scales that to the real workload so containers don&#39;t get OOMKilled.</li>\n<li>A &quot;memory leak&quot; in .NET isn&#39;t freed-twice or dangling pointers — it&#39;s something still <em>reachable</em> (a static cache, an unsubscribed event, a captive DI dependency) that your app will never actually use again.</li>\n<li>Allocation rate drives GC frequency, which drives latency — so most performance tuning in .NET comes down to allocating less, not making the GC itself faster.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-problem-every-language-has-to-solve\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/memory-gc/the-problem-every-language-has-to-solve\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The problem every language has to solve <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Every running program creates objects as it works, and every one of them eventually needs to disappear — otherwise memory just fills up until the machine falls over. Someone, or something, has to decide when an object is safe to free.</p>\n<p>In C or C++, that someone is you. You call <code>malloc</code>/<code>free</code> or <code>new</code>/<code>delete</code> yourself, by hand, every time. Get it wrong and you get one of three classic bugs: a <strong>leak</strong> (you forgot to free something, and it sits there forever), a <strong>use-after-free</strong> (you freed something, then kept using it — often a security hole), or a <strong>double free</strong> (you freed the same thing twice, which can corrupt memory outright). These aren&#39;t rare mistakes; they&#39;re some of the most common, most dangerous bugs in systems programming.</p>\n<p>.NET takes that decision away from you entirely. Its <strong>garbage collector</strong> (the <strong>GC</strong>) watches your objects and frees memory automatically, the moment it can prove nothing can reach an object any more. That one design choice is why <code>use-after-free</code> and <code>double free</code> essentially don&#39;t exist as bugs in C#.</p>\n<p>It isn&#39;t free, though. The GC needs real CPU time to do this watching and freeing, and some of its work genuinely <strong>pauses your running threads</strong> while it happens. So here&#39;s the thing worth internalising before anything else in this chapter: almost all .NET performance tuning, once you dig past the surface, turns out to really be about <strong>how much you&#39;re allocating</strong> — because allocation is what creates the GC&#39;s workload in the first place. Understand the GC, and a huge amount of &quot;why is this slow&quot; advice stops being folklore and starts being obvious cause and effect.</p>\n<h2 id=\"two-places-your-data-can-live\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/memory-gc/two-places-your-data-can-live\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Two places your data can live <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Before any of that makes sense, you need to know there are two completely different places an object&#39;s data can sit: the <strong>stack</strong> and the <strong>managed heap</strong>. They behave almost nothing alike.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Stack</th>\n<th>Managed heap</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Belongs to</td>\n<td>One thread (≈1 MB default on Windows, 8 MB on Linux&#39;s main thread)</td>\n<td>The whole process, shared by every thread</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Holds</td>\n<td>Method frames: locals, parameters, return addresses</td>\n<td>Reference-type objects, boxed values, arrays, closures, suspended async state machines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Allocating</td>\n<td>Move a pointer — essentially free</td>\n<td>Also just moving a pointer in most cases — also fast</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Freeing</td>\n<td>Automatic, the instant the method returns</td>\n<td><strong>By the GC</strong>, some unknown time after nothing references it any more</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What happens if you overflow it</td>\n<td><code>StackOverflowException</code> (deep or infinite recursion — you can&#39;t even catch this one)</td>\n<td><code>OutOfMemoryException</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Here&#39;s the part that surprises people: allocating on the heap is <em>also</em> cheap, most of the time. Each thread keeps a small chunk of heap space reserved for itself, called an <strong>allocation context</strong>, and handing out a new object from it is mostly just bumping a pointer forward — not meaningfully slower than the stack. So if heap allocation is cheap, where does the GC&#39;s reputation for being expensive actually come from?</p>\n<p>It comes later. The <em>more</em> you allocate, the more often that allocation context fills up and the GC has to step in and do real work to make room for more. The cost isn&#39;t in the allocating — it&#39;s in how often allocating forces a collection. Keep that distinction in mind; it&#39;s the thread running through the rest of this chapter.</p>\n<h2 id=\"generations-why-the-gc-doesnt-scan-everything-every-time\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/memory-gc/generations-why-the-gc-doesnt-scan-everything-every-time\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Generations: why the GC doesn&#39;t scan everything, every time <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Here&#39;s an idea that sounds almost too simple to be the foundation of a whole optimisation strategy, but it is: <strong>most objects die young.</strong> Think about a typical web request — it creates a flurry of temporary strings, DTOs, LINQ enumerators, intermediate results — and nearly all of that garbage is unreachable again within milliseconds, the instant the request finishes. Meanwhile, a much smaller set of objects — caches, singletons, configuration — get created once and then stick around for the entire life of the process.</p>\n<p>That observation has a name, the <strong>generational hypothesis</strong>, and .NET&#39;s GC is built directly around it. Instead of treating the whole heap as one big pool it has to re-scan from scratch every time, it splits the heap by <em>age</em>:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code> new objects                                                  long-lived objects\n┌──────────┐  survives a GC  ┌──────────┐  survives a GC  ┌──────────────────────┐\n│   Gen 0  │ ──────────────▶ │   Gen 1  │ ──────────────▶ │        Gen 2          │\n│ small,   │                 │ buffer   │                 │ caches, singletons,   │\n│ collected│                 │ between  │                 │ static data; FULL GCs │\n│ very often│                │ 0 and 2  │                 │ are expensive         │\n└──────────┘                 └──────────┘                 └──────────────────────┘\n LOH (Large Object Heap): objects ≥ 85,000 bytes. Logically part of Gen 2, collected with Gen 2, not compacted by default\n POH (Pinned Object Heap, .NET 5+): objects allocated pre-pinned, so they never fragment the other heaps</code></pre></figure>\n<p><img src=\"visuals/gc-generations.svg\" alt=\"Most objects allocated in Gen0 die there; only real survivors get promoted up to Gen1 and eventually Gen2\"></p>\n<p>Here&#39;s what that diagram is actually showing, walked through concretely:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Every object starts life in <strong>Gen0</strong>, the smallest generation. Because Gen0 is small, scanning it is fast — often well under a millisecond — and because <em>most</em> objects in it are already garbage by the time it fills up, a <strong>Gen0 collection</strong> reclaims a lot of memory for very little work.</li>\n<li>If an object is still reachable when Gen0 fills up and gets collected, it <strong>survives</strong>, and gets <em>promoted</em> up into <strong>Gen1</strong> — a kind of buffer zone between the churn of Gen0 and the stability of Gen2.</li>\n<li>If it survives <em>another</em> collection from there, it gets promoted again, into <strong>Gen2</strong> — where the genuinely long-lived stuff lives: caches, singletons, static data.</li>\n<li>A <strong>Gen1 collection</strong> sweeps Gen0 and Gen1 together. A <strong>Gen2 collection</strong> — often just called a <strong>full GC</strong> — sweeps absolutely everything, Gen0 through Gen2, including the Large Object Heap mentioned in the diagram (more on that shortly). Full GCs are the expensive ones, and the whole point of the generational design is to make them rare.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>One more term you&#39;ll meet in GC logs: a Gen0-only collection still needs to know if some old Gen2 object secretly points at something in Gen0. Instead of rescanning all of Gen2 to check, the runtime keeps a <strong>card table</strong> — a small &quot;which regions might contain a reference to something younger&quot; scoreboard. Every time your code stores a reference from an older object into a younger one, a tiny bit of code called a <strong>write barrier</strong> marks the matching entry. A Gen0 collection then only has to check the marked entries, not all of Gen2.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-why\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Why this actually matters for the code you write</div><p>Short-lived garbage — the kind that dies in Gen0 — is cheap, almost by design. The expensive case is the one hiding in the middle: objects that survive just long enough to get promoted to Gen1, and then die in Gen2 anyway. Developers sometimes call this a &quot;mid-life crisis&quot; — the object lived long enough to force an expensive full GC, but not long enough to actually be worth keeping around. This happens naturally with per-request objects that get held alive by a slow async operation spanning a Gen0 collection, or with caches whose expiry is set just a little too generously. It also happens automatically to anything <strong>≥ 85 KB</strong>, because of a rule you&#39;re about to meet: objects that size go straight onto the Large Object Heap, which is only ever cleaned up during a full Gen2 collection — so allocating big temporary buffers per request quietly forces frequent, expensive full GCs.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"what-actually-happens-during-a-collection\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/memory-gc/what-actually-happens-during-a-collection\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What actually happens during a collection <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>You now know <em>when</em> generations get collected. Here&#39;s <em>how</em> a collection itself actually works, step by step, every single time one runs:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Suspend your threads</strong>, at points the runtime considers safe to pause them (called &quot;safe points&quot;).</li>\n<li><strong>Mark</strong>: starting from a fixed set of known starting points — the <strong>GC roots</strong>, covered next — follow every reference outward and mark every object you can reach as <em>live</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Sweep and/or compact</strong>: anything that <em>wasn&#39;t</em> marked is garbage, and its memory gets reclaimed. For the generations below the LOH, the GC usually also <strong>compacts</strong> — slides the surviving live objects together so they&#39;re contiguous, and fixes up every reference that pointed at them, which keeps the heap from fragmenting into a patchwork of unusable gaps. The LOH, by contrast, is swept into free lists rather than compacted by default — you&#39;ll see why that matters shortly.</li>\n<li><strong>Resume your threads.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Step 2 is doing something worth really sitting with: an object is only considered garbage if it&#39;s <em>unreachable</em>, full stop. It makes no difference whether your code will genuinely never touch it again — if something can still trace a path to it from a root, the GC keeps it alive. That single fact is the entire mechanism behind every .NET memory leak you will ever debug: somewhere, some root is holding a reference it shouldn&#39;t be.</p>\n<p>So what actually counts as a root? Here&#39;s the full list:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Root</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Static fields</td>\n<td><code>static readonly Dictionary&lt;…&gt; Cache</code> lives as long as the process does</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Local variables and parameters on thread stacks (and CPU registers)</td>\n<td>Anything a running method still uses — and the JIT tracks this precisely enough that a local can become collectable <em>before</em> its method even finishes, once the method is done using it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GC handles</td>\n<td>Strong handles, pinned handles (<code>GCHandle.Alloc</code>), <code>fixed</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The finalization queue (f-reachable)</td>\n<td>Objects waiting to have their finalizer run — you&#39;ll meet this properly below</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interop / COM references</td>\n<td>Native code that&#39;s holding on to a managed object</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Keep that table nearby — later in this chapter, when &quot;memory leak&quot; comes up, every single cause traces back to one of these five rows holding a reference longer than it should.</p>\n<h2 id=\"choosing-how-the-gc-behaves\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/memory-gc/choosing-how-the-gc-behaves\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Choosing how the GC behaves <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>The mark-sweep-compact cycle above is the mechanism. .NET also lets you choose <em>how</em> that mechanism is scheduled and parallelised, and the defaults genuinely differ depending on what kind of app you&#39;re running.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Mode</th>\n<th>Behaviour</th>\n<th>Default for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Workstation GC</strong></td>\n<td>One heap; collections run on the thread that triggered them; favours responsiveness and low memory use</td>\n<td>Desktop and console apps</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Server GC</strong></td>\n<td><strong>One heap and one dedicated GC thread per logical core</strong>; collections run in parallel across them; much higher throughput, at the cost of more memory</td>\n<td><strong>ASP.NET Core</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Background (concurrent) GC</strong></td>\n<td>Gen2 collections run <strong>concurrently</strong> with your app&#39;s threads, so you only see brief pauses rather than a full stop; Gen0/1 collections still pause everything</td>\n<td>On by default in both modes above</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>DATAS</strong> (Dynamic Adaptation To Application Sizes)</td>\n<td>A refinement of Server GC that <strong>adapts the number of heaps to the actual workload</strong>, instead of always using one per core, so memory use stays roughly proportional to real load</td>\n<td><strong>Default with Server GC since .NET 9</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Why does ASP.NET Core default to Server GC instead of Workstation? A web server is, by nature, trying to maximise how many requests it can process per second across many cores at once — exactly the throughput-over-latency trade Server GC is built for. A desktop app, by contrast, cares more about not freezing the UI thread for a visible pause, which is Workstation GC&#39;s priority.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>XML</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>&lt;!-- csproj or runtimeconfig.json --&gt;\n&lt;PropertyGroup&gt;\n  &lt;ServerGarbageCollection&gt;true&lt;/ServerGarbageCollection&gt;\n  &lt;ConcurrentGarbageCollection&gt;true&lt;/ConcurrentGarbageCollection&gt;\n  &lt;!-- &lt;GarbageCollectionAdaptationMode&gt;<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>&lt;/GarbageCollectionAdaptationMode&gt;  DATAS on (default in .NET <span class=\"tk-num\">9</span>+ server GC) --&gt;\n&lt;/PropertyGroup&gt;</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Environment variable equivalents, useful when you can&#39;t touch the csproj (a container base image, for instance): <code>DOTNET_gcServer=1</code>, <code>DOTNET_GCgen0size</code>, <code>DOTNET_GCHeapHardLimit</code>, <code>DOTNET_GCHeapHardLimitPercent</code>, <code>DOTNET_GCHeapCount</code>, <code>DOTNET_GCConserveMemory</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-this-gets-tricky-inside-containers\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/memory-gc/why-this-gets-tricky-inside-containers\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Why this gets tricky inside containers</h3>\n<p>Here&#39;s a scenario worth walking through, because it genuinely used to cause production outages. The GC is <strong>cgroup-aware</strong>: it knows it&#39;s running inside a container with a memory limit, and by default caps its own heap at <strong>75%</strong> of that limit (tunable). The trap, before DATAS existed: Server GC&#39;s &quot;one heap per core&quot; rule doesn&#39;t ask whether memory is actually plentiful — it just counts cores. Put that on a node with <em>many</em> CPU cores but a <em>small</em> container memory limit (say, 32 cores, 512 MB) and Server GC happily creates 32 heaps, blows straight through the limit, and the container gets <strong>OOMKilled</strong> — with the app not leaking a single byte.</p>\n<p>DATAS (.NET 9+) mostly fixes this by scaling the heap count to the real workload instead of blindly matching the core count. If you&#39;re still seeing <code>OOMKilled</code> after upgrading, the next things to check are: is the actual working set genuinely over the limit, is something consuming <em>native</em> memory the GC heap tracker can&#39;t see, and is this an actual leak — then reach for <code>GCHeapHardLimitPercent</code> or <code>GCConserveMemory</code> as tuning knobs, in that order.</p>\n<h3 id=\"latency-modes-and-no-gc-regions\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/memory-gc/latency-modes-and-no-gc-regions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Latency modes and no-GC regions <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h3>\n<p>Two more dials exist for genuinely latency-sensitive code: <code>GCSettings.LatencyMode = GCLatencyMode.SustainedLowLatency</code> tries to avoid blocking full GCs wherever it can, and <code>GC.TryStartNoGCRegion(size)</code> runs a critical section with <strong>no GC at all</strong>, provided your allocation budget fits inside what you reserved. These are niche tools for things like trading systems or real-time workloads — the overwhelming majority of web apps never touch either one, and that&#39;s fine.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-large-object-heap-and-the-pinned-object-heap\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/memory-gc/the-large-object-heap-and-the-pinned-object-heap\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The Large Object Heap and the Pinned Object Heap <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>You&#39;ve already seen the LOH mentioned twice — once in the generations diagram, once in the &quot;mid-life crisis&quot; box. Time to look at it properly.</p>\n<p>Any object <strong>≥ 85,000 bytes</strong> — in practice, usually a big array or a big string — doesn&#39;t go through Gen0 at all. It&#39;s allocated directly onto a separate region called the <strong>Large Object Heap</strong>. That size threshold isn&#39;t arbitrary: copying a huge object around every time it survives a generation (which compaction normally does) would be expensive in its own right, so the runtime sidesteps the problem for big objects differently. Three consequences follow directly from that design:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Allocating on the LOH costs more than a normal allocation, partly because the memory has to be cleared first.</li>\n<li>It&#39;s only ever collected alongside a full <strong>Gen2</strong> collection — never on its own.</li>\n<li>It isn&#39;t compacted by default, which means repeated large allocations and frees can leave it <strong>fragmented</strong>: lots of free space technically available, but scattered into pieces too small to satisfy the next big request.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The practical upshot: <strong>avoid allocating large objects per request if you can help it.</strong> Rent reusable buffers from <code>ArrayPool&lt;T&gt;.Shared</code> instead of <code>new byte[64 * 1024]</code>-ing a fresh one every time, stream data instead of loading whole files into one byte array, reach for <code>RecyclableMemoryStream</code> when you need a big in-memory stream, and lean on <code>System.IO.Pipelines</code> for high-throughput I/O. You <em>can</em> force a one-off LOH compaction (<code>GCSettings.LargeObjectHeapCompactionMode = GCLargeObjectHeapCompactionMode.CompactOnce; GC.Collect();</code>), but reaching for that is rarely the right fix — it&#39;s treating a symptom.</p>\n<p>One more related idea: <strong>pinning</strong>. Normally the GC is free to move objects around during compaction — that&#39;s exactly what keeps the heap tidy. But some scenarios, mainly native interop and async I/O buffers, need an object to stay at a fixed memory address for a while, so the GC can be told not to move a specific object via <code>fixed</code> or <code>GCHandle.Alloc(obj, Pinned)</code>. The catch: a long-lived pin sits in the middle of the heap like a boulder the GC has to compact <em>around</em>, which fragments things. The <strong>Pinned Object Heap</strong> (POH, via <code>GC.AllocateArray&lt;byte&gt;(n, pinned: true)</code>) solves this cleanly: it gives you buffers that are pinned <em>from the moment they&#39;re born</em>, in a region set aside specifically for that purpose, so they never fragment the normal object heap at all.</p>\n<h2 id=\"finalizers-the-gcs-last-resort-and-why-its-slow\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/memory-gc/finalizers-the-gcs-last-resort-and-why-its-slow\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Finalizers: the GC&#39;s last resort, and why it&#39;s slow <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Every so often, an object wraps something the GC genuinely can&#39;t clean up on its own — a native handle, say. For those cases, C# offers a <strong>finalizer</strong>, written as <code>~T()</code>. It sounds like a safety net. In practice, it&#39;s a safety net you should almost never actually rely on, and understanding why tells you a lot about how the GC treats these objects differently from everything else.</p>\n<p>Here&#39;s the sequence, once an object with a finalizer becomes unreachable:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The GC notices it&#39;s unreachable — but, because it has a finalizer still waiting to run, it <strong>can&#39;t free it yet</strong>. Instead, it moves the object to something called the <strong>f-reachable queue</strong>, which — notice the name — is itself a GC root. The object just got <em>re-rooted</em>, temporarily, by the very mechanism that was about to collect it. It also gets <strong>promoted</strong> to the next generation in the process.</li>\n<li>At some later, genuinely unpredictable time, the single dedicated <strong>finalizer thread</strong> gets around to actually running that object&#39;s <code>Finalize</code> method.</li>\n<li>Only <em>after</em> that has happened does a <strong>later</strong> GC finally free the memory for real.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Walk back through that and you&#39;ll see why finalizable objects are expensive: they survive at least one extra collection they otherwise wouldn&#39;t have, they get promoted to an older generation earlier than their actual usage pattern justifies, and their cleanup timing is completely outside your control. That&#39;s precisely why deterministic cleanup in .NET uses <strong><code>IDisposable</code></strong> plus <code>using</code> instead — and why a well-written <code>Dispose</code> method calls <strong><code>GC.SuppressFinalize(this)</code></strong>, which tells the runtime &quot;cleanup already happened properly, you can skip the finalizer and the extra generation hop entirely.&quot; Where you genuinely need to wrap a native handle, prefer <strong><code>SafeHandle</code></strong> over writing a finalizer by hand — it already does this correctly. See <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/exceptions-disposal/implementing-idisposable-yourself\">Exceptions &amp; disposal</a> for the full pattern.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">The GC doesn&#39;t release unmanaged resources promptly</div><p>Everything above is about <em>managed</em> memory — the heap the GC actually tracks. File handles, sockets, database connections and native memory held by managed wrapper objects are a different story entirely: they get released when <strong>you</strong> dispose them, or, failing that, whenever a finalizer eventually gets around to it. A <code>DbConnection</code> left undisposed is a connection-pool leak the instant it happens, regardless of how much free RAM the machine has — the GC not having run yet is irrelevant.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"weak-references-and-caches\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/memory-gc/weak-references-and-caches\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Weak references and caches <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Sometimes you want to hold onto an object <em>if it&#39;s still around</em>, without that holding being the reason it stays around. That&#39;s what <strong><code>WeakReference&lt;T&gt;</code></strong> is for: it references an object without keeping it alive, so the GC is still free to collect it, and <code>TryGetTarget</code> simply returns <code>false</code> afterwards if it did. <strong><code>ConditionalWeakTable&lt;TKey,TValue&gt;</code></strong> does something similar but for attaching extra data to an object without extending its lifetime.</p>\n<p>These show up in some cache and event-pattern implementations, but for the vast majority of web app caching you&#39;re better off with <strong><code>MemoryCache</code></strong>, configured with real size limits and expirations — it gives you <em>predictable</em> eviction, whereas weak references leave eviction entirely up to the GC&#39;s own timing, which is much harder to reason about in production.</p>\n<h2 id=\"memory-leaks-in-net-still-possible-just-a-different-shape\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/memory-gc/memory-leaks-in-net-still-possible-just-a-different-shape\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Memory leaks in .NET: still possible, just a different shape <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Earlier this chapter said something worth repeating exactly: an object is only garbage once it&#39;s <strong>unreachable</strong> — not once it&#39;s <em>useless</em>. A <strong>managed memory leak</strong> is precisely the gap between those two: objects that are still technically reachable from some root, but that your application will never actually use again. Every single row in this table traces back to one of the five GC roots from earlier, holding a reference it shouldn&#39;t be:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Cause</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Static collections that only grow</strong></td>\n<td><code>static List&lt;Order&gt; _processed</code>, a static <code>Dictionary</code> cache without eviction</td>\n<td>Bounded caches (<code>MemoryCache</code> with <code>SizeLimit</code>/expiry), or don&#39;t keep them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Event handlers never unsubscribed</strong></td>\n<td>A transient subscribes to a singleton&#39;s event; the singleton keeps every subscriber alive</td>\n<td>Unsubscribe in <code>Dispose</code>; weak events; avoid events between lifetimes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Captive dependencies</strong></td>\n<td>A singleton holding a scoped <code>DbContext</code> whose change tracker grows forever</td>\n<td>Correct DI lifetimes (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/dependency-injection/the-captive-dependency-the-single-most-common-di-bug\">DI</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Unbounded in-memory queues</strong></td>\n<td><code>Channel.CreateUnbounded</code> with a slow consumer</td>\n<td>Bounded channels with backpressure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Timers</strong></td>\n<td><code>System.Threading.Timer</code> callbacks referencing big objects; timers never disposed</td>\n<td>Dispose timers; <code>PeriodicTimer</code> in hosted services</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Closures capturing too much</strong></td>\n<td>A long-lived lambda capturing <code>this</code> or a large object</td>\n<td>Capture only what&#39;s needed; static lambdas</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Resolving disposable transients from the root provider</strong></td>\n<td><code>app.Services.GetService&lt;TransientDisposable&gt;()</code> repeatedly: tracked until shutdown</td>\n<td>Resolve inside scopes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Large caches of EF entities / tracked graphs</strong></td>\n<td>Long-lived DbContext</td>\n<td>Short-lived contexts, <code>AsNoTracking</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><code>HttpClient</code>/connection objects created per call</strong></td>\n<td>Sockets and native buffers pile up</td>\n<td><code>IHttpClientFactory</code>, singletons for SDK clients</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Native memory</strong></td>\n<td>Leaked unmanaged buffers, un-disposed native wrappers</td>\n<td><code>Dispose</code>, <code>SafeHandle</code>; diagnose with native tools</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>How do you actually notice one of these is happening, as opposed to just normal memory use going up and down? The tell is specific: Gen2 size and total working set <strong>grow steadily under constant, steady load, and never come back down</strong> even after full GCs run. A healthy app&#39;s memory goes up and down as load varies; a leaking one&#39;s floor keeps rising. Left alone, that ends in either an <code>OutOfMemoryException</code> or a container getting <strong>OOMKilled</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"actually-finding-a-leak-a-real-diagnostic-workflow\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/memory-gc/actually-finding-a-leak-a-real-diagnostic-workflow\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Actually finding a leak: a real diagnostic workflow <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Knowing the table above is one thing; actually finding <em>which</em> row applies, in a production service you didn&#39;t write the leaking code in, is another. Here&#39;s a workflow that works, step by step, using tools you install once with <code>dotnet tool install</code>:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Shell</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>dotnet tool install -g dotnet-counters\ndotnet tool install -g dotnet-gcdump\ndotnet tool install -g dotnet-dump\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\"># 1. Watch live: GC heap size per generation, % time in GC, allocation rate, Gen0/1/2 counts, LOH size, working set</span>\ndotnet-counters monitor -n Shop.Api <span class=\"tk-attr\">--counters</span> System.Runtime\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\"># 2. Snapshot the managed heap (lightweight: types, counts, sizes, roots), twice, minutes apart, then compare</span>\ndotnet-gcdump collect -n Shop.Api -o before.gcdump\ndotnet-gcdump collect -n Shop.Api -o after.gcdump     <span class=\"tk-com\"># open both in Visual Studio / PerfView and diff: which types grew?</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\"># 3. Full process dump for deep analysis (who roots this object?)</span>\ndotnet-dump collect -n Shop.Api\ndotnet-dump analyze core_20261027.dmp\n&gt; dumpheap -stat                 <span class=\"tk-com\"># objects by type and total size</span>\n&gt; dumpheap -mt &lt;MethodTable&gt;     <span class=\"tk-com\"># instances of a type</span>\n&gt; gcroot &lt;address&gt;               <span class=\"tk-com\"># the reference chain keeping it alive (THE leak answer)</span>\n&gt; eeheap -gc                     <span class=\"tk-com\"># heap/segment overview</span></code></pre></figure>\n<p>Step 1 tells you <em>whether</em> you have a problem and roughly how fast it&#39;s growing. Step 2 tells you <em>which type</em> is growing — diffing two snapshots taken minutes apart under steady load shows exactly which object counts kept climbing when they should have stayed flat. Step 3&#39;s final command, <code>gcroot</code>, is the one that actually answers the question: given one specific surviving instance, it walks backward through live references and shows you the exact chain of objects keeping it alive — which, cross-referenced against the roots table from earlier, tells you exactly which of the ten leak patterns above you&#39;re looking at.</p>\n<p>Beyond these three CLI tools, you&#39;ll also find <strong>Visual Studio&#39;s Diagnostic Tools / memory profiler</strong>, <strong>JetBrains dotMemory</strong>, <strong>PerfView</strong> (deep GC event and allocation sampling), Application Insights&#39; own memory metrics and profiler, <code>dotnet-trace</code> with GC events enabled, and — specifically inside Kubernetes — <code>kubectl debug</code> with the diagnostics tools attached as an ephemeral container.</p>\n<p>The numbers actually worth watching: <strong>% time in GC</strong> (sustained above roughly 10% is a real problem, not noise), <strong>allocation rate</strong> in MB/s, <strong>Gen2 collection count</strong>, <strong>heap size right after a Gen2 collection finishes</strong> (this is the single clearest leak signal — it should be flat under steady load, not climbing), <strong>GC pause times</strong> (directly affects your p99 latency), and LOH size.</p>\n<h2 id=\"writing-allocation-friendly-code\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/memory-gc/writing-allocation-friendly-code\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Writing allocation-friendly code <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Given everything above — that allocation rate is what drives GC frequency, and GC frequency is what drives latency and throughput — here&#39;s the direct, practical payoff: a table of common allocation-heavy patterns, and the lower-allocation alternative for each.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Technique</th>\n<th>Instead of</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>StringBuilder</code>, <code>string.Join</code>, <code>string.Create</code>, interpolated string handlers</td>\n<td><code>+=</code> concatenation in loops</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code>, <code>ReadOnlySpan&lt;char&gt;</code> slicing and span-based parsing</td>\n<td><code>Substring</code>, <code>Split</code> on hot paths</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ArrayPool&lt;T&gt;.Shared.Rent/Return</code>, <code>stackalloc</code> (small)</td>\n<td><code>new byte[64 * 1024]</code> per request</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Structs (<code>readonly struct</code>, <code>record struct</code>) for small values</td>\n<td>Classes for tiny, short-lived data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Generics</td>\n<td>Boxing via <code>object</code>/non-generic collections</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pre-sized collections <code>new List&lt;T&gt;(n)</code></td>\n<td>Repeated resize-and-copy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Static lambdas / avoiding captures in hot paths</td>\n<td>Closures allocating per call</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ValueTask</code> for frequently-synchronous async methods</td>\n<td><code>Task</code> allocation per call</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cached <code>JsonSerializerOptions</code>, source-generated serializers and <code>[LoggerMessage]</code></td>\n<td>New options per call; boxing log arguments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>AsNoTracking</code> + projections in EF Core</td>\n<td>Materialising and tracking full entity graphs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Streaming (<code>IAsyncEnumerable</code>, <code>Stream</code>, <code>PipeReader</code>)</td>\n<td>Buffering whole payloads in memory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>FrozenDictionary</code> for static lookups</td>\n<td>Rebuilding dictionaries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Object pooling (<code>ObjectPool&lt;T&gt;</code>) for expensive reusable objects</td>\n<td>Allocating them per operation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>One honest caveat before you go rewrite everything: <strong>measure first</strong> (see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/performance-engineering\">Performance engineering</a>). Most business code spends almost all of its time waiting on I/O, not allocating — and readability is worth more than shaving a handful of allocations off code that runs once per request and was never the bottleneck to begin with.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">Don&#39;t call GC.Collect()</div><p>It&#39;s tempting, when you&#39;re debugging a memory issue, to just force a collection and see what happens. Resist it in production code. The GC&#39;s internal heuristics are already tuned to your app&#39;s actual allocation pattern over time, and forcing a collection out of turn almost always <strong>hurts</strong>: it promotes objects to older generations before they&#39;ve earned it, wastes CPU on a collection that wasn&#39;t actually needed yet, and causes a pause at a moment you didn&#39;t choose. The legitimate uses are narrow: tests, benchmarks, and right after a genuinely one-off huge cleanup in a batch job — and even then, measure before and after to confirm it actually helped.</p>\n</aside>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How does the .NET garbage collector work?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>It&#39;s a generational, tracing, mark-and-compact collector. Allocation bumps a pointer in Gen0. When a generation&#39;s budget fills, a GC suspends threads, marks all objects reachable from roots (statics, stack locals, GC handles, the finalization queue), reclaims the rest, compacts survivors and promotes them to the next generation. Gen0/1 collections are frequent and cheap; Gen2 (full, including the LOH) is expensive and mostly runs concurrently in background GC.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are GC generations and why do they exist?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Gen0 holds new objects, Gen1 is a buffer, and Gen2 holds long-lived objects. Based on the generational hypothesis that most objects die young, collecting the small young generation frequently reclaims most garbage cheaply, without scanning the whole heap each time.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the Large Object Heap?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A separate heap region for objects of 85,000 bytes or more. It&#39;s collected only during Gen2 collections and not compacted by default, so frequent large allocations cause full GCs and fragmentation. Pool or stream large buffers instead.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Workstation vs Server GC?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Workstation GC uses a single heap and favours low latency and memory, and is the default for client apps. Server GC uses a heap and dedicated GC thread per core with parallel collection for higher throughput, and is the default for ASP.NET Core. .NET 9+ adds DATAS so Server GC adapts heap count to load, keeping memory proportional in containers.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are GC roots?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The starting points for reachability: static fields, local variables and parameters on thread stacks (and registers), GC handles (strong and pinned), the finalization queue and interop references. Anything reachable from a root is live and won&#39;t be collected.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Can you have memory leaks in .NET? Give examples.</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Yes. Leaks are objects that stay reachable but are no longer needed: ever-growing static collections or caches without eviction, event handlers not unsubscribed from long-lived publishers, captive dependencies (a singleton holding a scoped DbContext), undisposed timers, closures capturing large objects, unbounded queues, and native resources not disposed.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How would you investigate a memory leak in a production .NET service?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Confirm steady growth of Gen2/heap-after-GC with dotnet-counters or App Insights metrics. Take two gcdumps some time apart and diff them to find growing types. Then take a full dump with dotnet-dump and use gcroot on sample instances to find the reference chain keeping them alive, fix the root, and verify with load tests.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why are finalizers expensive?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Finalizable objects are tracked from allocation. When unreachable, they must survive an extra collection (promoted to the f-reachable queue) until the single finalizer thread runs them, and only a later GC frees their memory. That&#39;s more work, longer lifetimes and nondeterministic cleanup. Use IDisposable with GC.SuppressFinalize, and SafeHandle.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why shouldn&#39;t you call GC.Collect?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The GC&#39;s heuristics tune collection timing to allocation patterns. Forcing collections wastes CPU, causes pauses, and promotes still-live objects to older generations, making later collections more expensive. It&#39;s rarely justified outside tests and benchmarks.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/memory-gc/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/garbage-collection/fundamentals\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Fundamentals of garbage collection</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/runtime-config/garbage-collector\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Runtime configuration options for garbage collection</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/diagnostics/debug-memory-leak\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Debug a memory leak in .NET</a></li>\n<li>Maoni Stephens&#39; blog posts on the .NET GC (she leads the GC team).</li>\n<li>Book: <em>Pro .NET Memory Management</em> by Konrad Kokosa: the definitive deep dive.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/memory-gc/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (a plain console app, .NET 8/9, no extra packages). It checks generation assignment, the LOH size threshold, promotion after a collection, <code>ArrayPool&lt;T&gt;</code> reuse, and <code>WeakReference&lt;T&gt;</code> behavior directly against the live GC in your process. Server GC/DATAS and container heap limits are process-wide startup configuration, not something an in-process console app can flip and observe, so those claims are left to the Microsoft Learn links above instead of faked here.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Verify this chapter: run with `dotnet run`. No external packages needed.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Buffers</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> name, <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> ok)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!ok) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: {name}&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: {name}&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 1. A new object starts life in Gen0.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> fresh = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>[<span class=\"tk-num\">16</span>];\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;new small object starts in Gen0&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">GC</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetGeneration</span>(fresh) == <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 2. Objects &gt;= 85,000 bytes go straight to the Large Object Heap, which is</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//    logically part of Gen2 (GC.GetGeneration reports 2 for LOH objects).</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> small = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>[<span class=\"tk-num\">1000</span>];\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> large = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>[<span class=\"tk-num\">100_000</span>];\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;small array (1,000 bytes) is not on the LOH (Gen0/1)&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">GC</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetGeneration</span>(small) &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;array &gt;= 85,000 bytes is allocated on the LOH (reported as Gen2)&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">GC</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetGeneration</span>(large) == <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 3. Surviving a collection promotes an object to the next generation.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> survivor = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> genBefore = <span class=\"tk-ty\">GC</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetGeneration</span>(survivor);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">GC</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Collect</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">GCCollectionMode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Forced</span>, blocking: <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>); <span class=\"tk-com\">// collect Gen0 only</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> genAfter = <span class=\"tk-ty\">GC</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetGeneration</span>(survivor);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;an object still referenced after a Gen0 collection is promoted (or already older)&quot;</span>, genAfter &gt;= genBefore);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 4. GC.CollectionCount increases after a forced collection of that generation.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">long</span> countBefore = <span class=\"tk-ty\">GC</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CollectionCount</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">GC</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Collect</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">GCCollectionMode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Forced</span>, blocking: <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">long</span> countAfter = <span class=\"tk-ty\">GC</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CollectionCount</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;GC.CollectionCount(0) increases after forcing a Gen0 collection&quot;</span>, countAfter &gt; countBefore);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 5. ArrayPool&lt;T&gt;.Shared.Rent can return the exact same backing array once</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//    it has been returned to the pool -- demonstrating the &quot;no new allocation&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//    reuse the chapter recommends for hot paths.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> pool = <span class=\"tk-ty\">ArrayPool</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>&gt;.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Shared</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> rented1 = pool.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Rent</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">4096</span>);\npool.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Return</span>(rented1);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> rented2 = pool.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Rent</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">4096</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> sameArray = <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReferenceEquals</span>(rented1, rented2);\npool.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Return</span>(rented2);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(sameArray\n    ? <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: ArrayPool&lt;T&gt;.Shared reused the same backing array (no fresh heap allocation)&quot;</span>\n    : <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: ArrayPool&lt;T&gt;.Shared returned a different array this run (pool reuse is a best-effort optimization, not guaranteed every time)&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 6. WeakReference&lt;T&gt; lets the GC collect an object even though a reference</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//    to it technically still exists, unlike a normal (strong) reference.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>? strongTarget = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> weak = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">WeakReference</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>&gt;(strongTarget);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;WeakReference.TryGetTarget finds the object while a strong reference keeps it alive&quot;</span>, weak.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGetTarget</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> _));\nstrongTarget = <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>; <span class=\"tk-com\">// drop the only strong reference</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">GC</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Collect</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">GC</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WaitForPendingFinalizers</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">GC</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Collect</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not asserted as a hard failure: the GC is not obligated to collect on any</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// particular call, but under GC.Collect() + WaitForPendingFinalizers() in a</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// console app with no other roots, this reliably clears.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> collected = !weak.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGetTarget</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> _);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(collected\n    ? <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: after dropping the only strong reference and forcing a collection, WeakReference no longer finds the object&quot;</span>\n    : <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: object survived this run (GC timing is not guaranteed) -- see Further reading: Fundamentals of garbage collection&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 7. Server GC vs Workstation GC and DATAS are process-wide runtime</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// configuration (csproj / runtimeconfig.json / env vars), not something a</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// console app can flip at runtime and observe in-process -- see Further</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// reading: &quot;Runtime configuration options for garbage collection&quot;.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;INFO (not asserted): GCSettings.IsServerGC = {System.Runtime.GCSettings.IsServerGC} for this process&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All checks passed.&quot;</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-problem-every-language-has-to-solve","text":"The problem every language has to solve","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"two-places-your-data-can-live","text":"Two places your data can live","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"generations-why-the-gc-doesnt-scan-everything-every-time","text":"Generations: why the GC doesn't scan everything, every time","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"what-actually-happens-during-a-collection","text":"What actually happens during a collection","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"choosing-how-the-gc-behaves","text":"Choosing how the GC behaves","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"why-this-gets-tricky-inside-containers","text":"Why this gets tricky inside containers","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"latency-modes-and-no-gc-regions","text":"Latency modes and no-GC regions","depth":3,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"the-large-object-heap-and-the-pinned-object-heap","text":"The Large Object Heap and the Pinned Object Heap","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"finalizers-the-gcs-last-resort-and-why-its-slow","text":"Finalizers: the GC's last resort, and why it's slow","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"weak-references-and-caches","text":"Weak references and caches","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"memory-leaks-in-net-still-possible-just-a-different-shape","text":"Memory leaks in .NET: still possible, just a different shape","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"actually-finding-a-leak-a-real-diagnostic-workflow","text":"Actually finding a leak: a real diagnostic workflow","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"writing-allocation-friendly-code","text":"Writing allocation-friendly code","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":5680,"minutes":26,"num":"9.1","part":"p9"},"threading-concurrency":{"id":"threading-concurrency","title":"Threading, Concurrency and Parallelism","summary":"Doing many things at once safely. Concurrency vs parallelism vs asynchrony, threads and the thread pool, Task.Run and Parallel, race conditions and atomicity, Interlocked, locks (lock/Lock, Monitor, SemaphoreSlim, ReaderWriterLockSlim), deadlocks, volatile and memory models, concurrent collections, Channels and producer/consumer, thread safety in ASP.NET Core singletons, and PLINQ.","layer":"runtime","where":"Runtime & platform › Threads & concurrency","fits":"<p>The thread pool is a CLR service (layer 2) that every async continuation (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/async-await\">async/await</a>) and every ASP.NET Core request runs on. Concurrency bugs mostly appear in <strong>singletons</strong> (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/dependency-injection\">Dependency injection</a>), caches (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors\">Caching</a>) and background workers (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/background-work\">Background work</a>), and at system scale they reappear as database concurrency (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/locking-blocking-and-deadlocks\">Relational fundamentals</a>).</p>\n","prereqs":["async-await","collections"],"next":["performance-engineering","background-work"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/threading-concurrency/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Concurrency</strong> = several things in progress at once (maybe interleaved); <strong>parallelism</strong> = several things literally running at the same instant on different cores; <strong>asynchrony</strong> = not blocking a thread while waiting on something slow. A web server is heavily concurrent, mostly asynchronous, and only occasionally parallel.</li>\n<li><code>_count++</code> looks like one step but is really three (read, add, write) — two threads can interleave those steps and silently lose an update. That&#39;s a <strong>race condition</strong>, and it never throws an exception; it just produces a wrong number.</li>\n<li><code>Interlocked</code> fixes a single shared variable with one atomic CPU instruction (no lock needed). A <code>lock</code>/<code>Monitor</code> protects more than one variable at once, but you can never <code>await</code> inside it — use <code>SemaphoreSlim</code> when you need both mutual exclusion and <code>await</code>.</li>\n<li>A <strong>deadlock</strong> happens when two threads each hold something the other is waiting for. The practical fix is almost always: always acquire locks in the same order everywhere.</li>\n<li>The BCL ships thread-safe collections (<code>ConcurrentDictionary</code>, <code>Channel&lt;T&gt;</code>, immutable snapshots) specifically so you don&#39;t have to hand-roll locking for common patterns like caches and producer/consumer queues.</li>\n<li>The one rule that matters most in ASP.NET Core: <strong>every singleton must be thread-safe</strong>, because it&#39;s the one instance every concurrent request shares.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"three-words-people-mix-up-constantly\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/threading-concurrency/three-words-people-mix-up-constantly\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Three words people mix up constantly <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Before anything else, let&#39;s pin down three words that sound almost interchangeable and genuinely aren&#39;t — because half the confusion in this topic comes from people using them loosely.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>.NET tools</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Concurrency</strong></td>\n<td>Several tasks <strong>in progress</strong> at the same time — they might be truly simultaneous, or just interleaved, taking turns</td>\n<td>Everything in this chapter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Parallelism</strong></td>\n<td>Several tasks <strong>actually executing at the same instant</strong>, on different CPU cores, specifically to speed up CPU-bound work</td>\n<td><code>Parallel.For/ForEach(Async)</code>, PLINQ, <code>Task.Run</code>, <code>Task.WhenAll</code> over CPU work</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Asynchrony</strong></td>\n<td>Not blocking a thread while it <strong>waits</strong> for something slow, like I/O</td>\n<td><code>async</code>/<code>await</code>, <code>Task</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Here&#39;s a sentence worth re-reading until it clicks: a web server is massively <strong>concurrent</strong> — thousands of requests in flight — but mostly <strong>asynchronous</strong>, because almost all of that &quot;in flight&quot; time is spent waiting on a database or a downstream API, not computing. It&#39;s only occasionally, genuinely <strong>parallel</strong>, when one request deliberately spreads CPU-bound work across cores. Most of what this chapter covers is really about concurrency: many things happening at once, and the bugs that come from them touching shared state at the same time.</p>\n<p>One more related word you&#39;ll meet below: <strong>multithreading</strong>, which just means using more than one OS thread. It&#39;s the underlying mechanism both parallelism and (some) concurrency are built on.</p>\n<h2 id=\"threads-are-expensive-the-pool-exists-because-of-that\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/threading-concurrency/threads-are-expensive-the-pool-exists-because-of-that\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Threads are expensive; the pool exists because of that <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>A <strong>thread</strong> is a path of execution the operating system schedules onto a CPU core, and every single one gets its own stack — about 1 MB reserved by default. Creating a thread from scratch costs real time (milliseconds, not microseconds) and real memory, and once you have too many of them, the OS spends more time <em>switching between</em> them than actually running your code.</p>\n<p>So .NET doesn&#39;t create a fresh thread for every piece of work. Instead, the <strong>thread pool</strong> keeps a set of reusable worker threads sitting ready, and hands them queued work items as they become free. Nearly everything concurrent in a typical ASP.NET Core app ends up running on it:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Every incoming request (Kestrel dispatches each one to the pool)</li>\n<li>Every <code>await</code> continuation — the &quot;resume here later&quot; code you saw in the async/await chapter — since ASP.NET Core has no <code>SynchronizationContext</code> to route continuations elsewhere</li>\n<li><code>Task.Run</code>, <code>Parallel</code>, and timer callbacks</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> t = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Thread</span>(() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">DoWork</span>()) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsBackground</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;legacy-worker&quot;</span> };   <span class=\"tk-com\">// dedicated thread: rare today</span>\nt.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Start</span>();\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Run</span>(() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">ComputeHash</span>(bytes));                 <span class=\"tk-com\">// queue CPU work to the pool</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Factory</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartNew</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">LongRunningLoop</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TaskCreationOptions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LongRunning</span>);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// hint: gets a dedicated thread</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Here&#39;s the mechanism worth really understanding, because it&#39;s the root cause of a production incident nearly every .NET backend team eventually has: the pool doesn&#39;t start out huge. It begins with roughly one thread per CPU core, and when more work arrives than it can currently handle, it grows <strong>gradually</strong> — a &quot;hill-climbing&quot; algorithm adds threads a little at a time, and a separate starvation-detection mechanism injects roughly one new thread every half-second to one second under sustained pressure. That&#39;s deliberately slow, because spinning up threads too eagerly would waste the exact resources the pool exists to conserve.</p>\n<p>Now put that together with one more fact: calling <code>.Result</code>, <code>Thread.Sleep</code>, or any <em>blocking</em> I/O call ties up a pool thread for the entire duration of the block — it can&#39;t go do other work in the meantime. Under load, with the pool only growing a thread or two a second, a sudden burst of blocked threads can genuinely starve the queue faster than the pool can backfill it. That specific failure mode has a name — <strong>thread-pool starvation</strong> — and it&#39;s covered in detail in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/async-await/deadlocks-and-thread-pool-starvation\">async/await</a>, because it&#39;s really a consequence of <em>not</em> using <code>async</code>/<code>await</code> properly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"race-conditions-when-it-depends-on-timing-becomes-a-bug\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/threading-concurrency/race-conditions-when-it-depends-on-timing-becomes-a-bug\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Race conditions: when &quot;it depends on timing&quot; becomes a bug <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Here&#39;s a piece of code that looks completely harmless:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"cHJpdmF0ZSBpbnQgX2NvdW50OwpwdWJsaWMgdm9pZCBJbmNyZW1lbnQoKSA9PiBfY291bnQrKzs=\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> _count;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Increment</span>() =&gt; _count++;</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>It isn&#39;t harmless, and the reason is worth walking through slowly, because <code>_count++</code> looks like <em>one</em> operation but is actually <strong>three</strong>: read the current value, add one to it, write the new value back. Each of those three steps is a separate moment in time — and another thread can slip in between any of them.</p>\n<p><img src=\"visuals/race-condition.svg\" alt=\"Two threads both read 5, both compute 6, both write 6 — one increment silently disappears\"></p>\n<p>Walk through exactly what the diagram shows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Thread A reads <code>_count</code> and gets <code>5</code>.</li>\n<li>Before A writes anything back, Thread B <em>also</em> reads <code>_count</code> — and also gets <code>5</code>, because A hasn&#39;t written yet.</li>\n<li>Thread A computes <code>5 + 1 = 6</code> and writes <code>6</code>.</li>\n<li>Thread B, working from the <code>5</code> it already read, <em>also</em> computes <code>5 + 1 = 6</code>, and writes <code>6</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Two increments happened. The final value is <code>6</code>, not <code>7</code>. One of the increments vanished without a trace, without throwing any exception, without any sign anything went wrong — which is exactly what makes <strong>race conditions</strong> so nasty: the result depends entirely on the unpredictable timing of two threads, so the bug might not show up once in a thousand test runs and then appear under real production load.</p>\n<p>This exact &quot;read, modify, write&quot; shape hides in more places than a bare counter. <strong>Check-then-act</strong> code — <code>if (!dict.ContainsKey(k)) dict.Add(k, v);</code> — has the identical flaw: two threads can both pass the <code>ContainsKey</code> check before either calls <code>Add</code>. Lazy initialisation done by hand has the same shape. So does iterating a collection while another thread modifies it, which in .NET usually surfaces as an <code>InvalidOperationException</code>, or — worse — silently corrupted state in a plain <code>Dictionary</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"where-does-this-actually-bite-in-a-web-app\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/threading-concurrency/where-does-this-actually-bite-in-a-web-app\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Where does this actually bite in a web app?</h3>\n<p>The good news: most of your code is completely safe from this by default, because most of it <em>isn&#39;t shared</em> between threads.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Shared?</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n<th>Safe?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>✘ Not shared</td>\n<td>Locals, request-scoped services, <code>HttpContext</code> (one request)</td>\n<td>Yes — nothing else can see them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>✔ <strong>Shared</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Singleton services</strong>, <code>static</code> fields, <code>IMemoryCache</code> entries, hosted services, <code>IOptionsMonitor</code> callbacks</td>\n<td><strong>Must be made thread-safe deliberately</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>That gives you a simple, memorable rule: <strong>every singleton must be thread-safe</strong>, because a singleton is, by definition, the <em>one</em> instance every concurrent request calls into at the same time. Everything from here through the rest of this chapter is really a toolbox for making that true.</p>\n<h2 id=\"interlocked-the-fastest-fix-when-it-applies\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/threading-concurrency/interlocked-the-fastest-fix-when-it-applies\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Interlocked: the fastest fix, when it applies <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>The simplest tool in that toolbox handles exactly the counter problem from above, when all you need to protect is a single variable:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">long</span> _requests;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">OnRequest</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Interlocked</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Increment</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">ref</span> _requests);           <span class=\"tk-com\">// atomic ++</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">long</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Snapshot</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Interlocked</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Read</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">ref</span> _requests);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> _initialised;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InitOnce</span>() { <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Interlocked</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CompareExchange</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">ref</span> _initialised, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) == <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Init</span>(); }   <span class=\"tk-com\">// CAS: set to 1 only if still 0</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Interlocked</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">ref</span> _bytes, n);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> old = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Interlocked</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exchange</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">ref</span> _currentConfig, newConfig);              <span class=\"tk-com\">// atomic reference swap (great for immutable snapshots)</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><code>Interlocked.Increment</code> doesn&#39;t fix the race by making the three steps faster — it fixes it by making them <strong>one indivisible step</strong>, a single atomic CPU instruction that no other thread can observe halfway through. There&#39;s no window left for another thread to sneak a read in between, because there&#39;s no &quot;in between&quot; any more.</p>\n<p>That&#39;s also exactly why <code>Interlocked</code> is the fastest synchronisation tool in .NET: it needs no lock, no waiting, nothing but one CPU instruction. The trade-off is right there in the name — it only ever covers <em>one variable at a time</em>. The moment you need to keep two or more related pieces of state consistent with each other, <code>Interlocked</code> can&#39;t help, and you need a real lock.</p>\n<h2 id=\"locks-protecting-more-than-one-variable-at-once\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/threading-concurrency/locks-protecting-more-than-one-variable-at-once\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Locks: protecting more than one variable at once <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<h3 id=\"lock-lock-monitor\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/threading-concurrency/lock-lock-monitor\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>lock / Lock / Monitor</h3>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Lock</span> _gate = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>();                <span class=\"tk-com\">// C# 13 / .NET 9: dedicated Lock type (faster); use a private readonly object before that</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span>&gt; _prices = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>();\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Update</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> sku, <span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span> p)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">lock</span> (_gate)                                    <span class=\"tk-com\">// only one thread at a time inside; others wait</span>\n    {\n        _prices[sku] = p;\n    }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> sku, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Price</span>? p) { <span class=\"tk-kw\">lock</span> (_gate) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> _prices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGetValue</span>(sku, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> p); }</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p><code>lock (obj)</code> is really shorthand — it compiles down to <code>Monitor.Enter</code>/<code>Monitor.Exit</code> wrapped in a <code>try/finally</code>, which guarantees the lock is released even if the code inside throws. Four rules keep this safe in practice: lock on a <strong>private, readonly</strong> object (never <code>this</code>, never <code>typeof(X)</code>, never a string — all of those could accidentally be locked on by <em>other</em> unrelated code too, defeating the whole point); keep what&#39;s inside the lock <strong>short</strong>; <strong>never <code>await</code> inside a <code>lock</code></strong> (the compiler actively forbids this — you&#39;ll see exactly why in a moment); and never call unknown code — an event, a callback — while holding a lock, because you have no idea what it might do.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-await-and-lock-dont-mix\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/threading-concurrency/why-await-and-lock-dont-mix\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Why <code>await</code> and <code>lock</code> don&#39;t mix</h3>\n<p>Here&#39;s the &quot;why&quot; behind that compiler rule, because it&#39;s one of those facts that&#39;s easy to memorise and hard to actually understand. A <code>Monitor</code> lock is <strong>thread-affine</strong>: whichever thread acquired it is the one that must release it. But you already know from the async/await chapter that when an <code>await</code> suspends and later resumes, the continuation can land on a <em>completely different</em> thread pool thread than the one that started it. Put those two facts together and a lock held across an <code>await</code> becomes nonsensical — the thread that&#39;s supposed to release it might not be the thread that&#39;s running by the time execution comes back. So the compiler simply won&#39;t let you write it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"semaphoreslim-the-async-friendly-alternative\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/threading-concurrency/semaphoreslim-the-async-friendly-alternative\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>SemaphoreSlim: the async-friendly alternative</h3>\n<p>When you genuinely need mutual exclusion <em>and</em> the ability to <code>await</code> inside the protected section, reach for <code>SemaphoreSlim</code> instead:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SemaphoreSlim</span> _refreshGate = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);       <span class=\"tk-com\">// mutual exclusion usable with await</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">GetTokenAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (_token <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsValid</span>: <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span> } t) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> t.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>;           <span class=\"tk-com\">// fast path without locking</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> _refreshGate.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WaitAsync</span>(ct);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span>\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (_token <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsValid</span>: <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span> } t2) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> t2.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>;     <span class=\"tk-com\">// double-check after acquiring</span>\n        _token = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FetchTokenAsync</span>(ct);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> _token.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>;\n    }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">finally</span> { _refreshGate.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Release</span>(); }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SemaphoreSlim</span> _throttle = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>);              <span class=\"tk-com\">// at most 10 concurrent calls to a fragile API</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice the shape of <code>GetTokenAsync</code>: it checks the fast path <em>before</em> acquiring the semaphore, and checks it <strong>again</strong> right after — a pattern called &quot;double-checked locking&quot;. Why check twice? Because between the first check failing and this method actually acquiring the gate, some <em>other</em> call might have already done the refresh for you — checking again avoids doing the work a second time unnecessarily. A <code>SemaphoreSlim(1, 1)</code> behaves like a <code>lock</code> that&#39;s safe to hold across an <code>await</code>; one more detail worth knowing is that a plain <code>SemaphoreSlim(n)</code> with <code>n</code> greater than one isn&#39;t mutual exclusion at all — it&#39;s a <em>concurrency limiter</em>, letting up to <code>n</code> callers through at once, which is exactly what <code>_throttle</code> above is doing.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-rest-of-the-toolbox-briefly\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/threading-concurrency/the-rest-of-the-toolbox-briefly\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The rest of the toolbox, briefly</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Primitive</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>ReaderWriterLockSlim</code></td>\n<td>Many concurrent readers, exclusive writers (sync only). Often an <strong>immutable snapshot + <code>Interlocked.Exchange</code></strong> is simpler and faster</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Mutex</code></td>\n<td>Cross-<strong>process</strong> mutual exclusion (named mutex), e.g. single-instance apps</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ManualResetEventSlim</code>, <code>AutoResetEvent</code>, <code>CountdownEvent</code>, <code>Barrier</code></td>\n<td>Signalling between threads (mostly replaced by tasks and channels)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>Lazy&lt;T&gt;</code> / <code>LazyInitializer</code></td>\n<td>Thread-safe lazy initialisation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>AsyncLocal&lt;T&gt;</code> / <code>ThreadLocal&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Per-async-flow / per-thread values</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Distributed locks</strong> (Redis, SQL app locks, Blob leases)</td>\n<td>Across <strong>machines</strong>: a C# lock only protects one process (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/background-work/running-a-job-once-across-replicas\">Background work</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"deadlocks-when-locking-itself-becomes-the-bug\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/threading-concurrency/deadlocks-when-locking-itself-becomes-the-bug\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Deadlocks: when locking itself becomes the bug <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Locks solve the race-condition problem — but used carelessly, they create a different one, and this one doesn&#39;t just lose an update, it freezes your app solid. Here&#39;s the simplest possible version:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"Ly8gVGhyZWFkIDE6IGxvY2soQSkgeyDigKYgbG9jayhCKSB7IH0gfQovLyBUaHJlYWQgMjogbG9jayhCKSB7IOKApiBsb2NrKEEpIHsgfSB9ICAgICAg4oaSIGVhY2ggaG9sZHMgb25lIGFuZCB3YWl0cyBmb3JldmVyIGZvciB0aGUgb3RoZXI=\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Thread 1: lock(A) { … lock(B) { } }</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Thread 2: lock(B) { … lock(A) { } }      → each holds one and waits forever for the other</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Walk through it: Thread 1 grabs lock <code>A</code>, then tries to grab lock <code>B</code> — but Thread 2 already has <code>B</code>, and is itself waiting on <code>A</code>, which Thread 1 is holding. Neither thread will ever let go, because neither can proceed. They&#39;re not crashed — they&#39;re just frozen, forever, waiting on each other in a perfect, silent circle.</p>\n<p>There&#39;s a name for the four conditions that all have to be true at once for this to happen (<strong>Coffman&#39;s conditions</strong>): each lock can only be held by one thread (true of every lock you&#39;ve seen); a thread keeps what it already has while it waits for more; nothing can force a thread to let go of a lock early; and the chain of &quot;who&#39;s waiting for whom&quot; loops back on itself. The useful takeaway isn&#39;t memorising the four names — it&#39;s this: break just <em>one</em> of them, and the deadlock becomes impossible. That&#39;s exactly where the fixes below come from:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lock ordering</strong>: if every piece of code that needs both <code>A</code> and <code>B</code> always acquires them in the <em>same</em> order — say, always <code>A</code> before <code>B</code> — a circular wait becomes structurally impossible. This single discipline prevents the overwhelming majority of real deadlocks.</li>\n<li><strong>Don&#39;t hold a lock while calling out</strong> — to an event, a callback, I/O, another service — because you have no idea what that outside code might itself try to lock.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>timeouts</strong> instead of waiting forever: <code>Monitor.TryEnter(obj, timeout)</code>, <code>SemaphoreSlim.WaitAsync(timeout)</code>. A deadlock that times out and retries is recoverable; one that waits forever is not.</li>\n<li>Prefer designs that need <strong>no locks at all</strong> where you can: immutable data, <code>Interlocked</code>, concurrent collections, channels and message passing — all covered below.</li>\n<li>And the async-specific version of this bug, which you met properly in the async/await chapter: <strong>never block on async code</strong> (<code>.Result</code>) anywhere a <code>SynchronizationContext</code> exists — it&#39;s a two-thread deadlock with exactly the same circular-wait shape as the example above, just dressed up differently.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"why-the-compiler-and-cpu-are-allowed-to-reorder-your-code\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/threading-concurrency/why-the-compiler-and-cpu-are-allowed-to-reorder-your-code\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Why the compiler and CPU are allowed to reorder your code <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>There&#39;s a subtler category of concurrency bug that has nothing to do with locks at all, and it trips people up because it violates an assumption that feels completely obvious: that code runs in the order you wrote it. For a single thread, it effectively does. For multiple threads watching each other, it sometimes doesn&#39;t — and here&#39;s why that&#39;s actually allowed.</p>\n<p>Compilers, the JIT, and CPUs are all permitted to <strong>reorder</strong> reads and writes, and to <strong>cache</strong> values in registers instead of main memory, <em>as long as the single thread running that code can&#39;t tell the difference</em>. That last clause is the key: the guarantee is only ever about what one thread observes about itself. Nobody promised anything about what a <em>different</em> thread, watching from outside, will see — and that gap is exactly where this bug lives.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"cHJpdmF0ZSBib29sIF9zdG9wOwpwdWJsaWMgdm9pZCBSdW4oKSB7IHdoaWxlICghX3N0b3ApIHsgLyogd29yayAqLyB9IH0gICAvLyB0aGUgSklUIG1heSBob2lzdCBfc3RvcCBpbnRvIGEgcmVnaXN0ZXIg4oaSIGxvb3BzIGZvcmV2ZXIKcHVibGljIHZvaWQgU3RvcCgpID0+IF9zdG9wID0gdHJ1ZTs=\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> _stop;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Run</span>() { <span class=\"tk-kw\">while</span> (!_stop) { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* work */</span> } }   <span class=\"tk-com\">// the JIT may hoist _stop into a register → loops forever</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Stop</span>() =&gt; _stop = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>From <code>Run</code>&#39;s own point of view, nothing here looks unsafe at all, so the JIT is completely within its rights to decide <code>_stop</code> can&#39;t possibly change during the loop and cache it once in a register instead of re-reading memory every iteration. The loop then runs forever, even though some <em>other</em> thread genuinely did call <code>Stop()</code> — that write just never became visible to this thread&#39;s cached copy.</p>\n<p>The fix is to tell the runtime, explicitly, &quot;don&#39;t cache this — always go back to real memory&quot;: mark the field <code>volatile</code> (<code>private volatile bool _stop;</code>), or use <code>Volatile.Read</code>/<code>Volatile.Write</code> directly. Locks and <code>Interlocked</code> operations also happen to act as full memory barriers, which is a second reason they fix more bugs than just the ones they&#39;re obviously designed for. In practice, though, don&#39;t reach for <code>volatile</code> by hand for a stop signal — use a <strong><code>CancellationToken</code></strong>, which does this correctly for you, and save hand-rolled lock-free code for the rare cases that genuinely need it. It&#39;s expert territory, and the bugs in it are exactly this subtle.</p>\n<h2 id=\"concurrent-collections-built-so-you-dont-have-to-lock-by-han\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/threading-concurrency/concurrent-collections-built-so-you-dont-have-to-lock-by-han\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Concurrent collections: built so you don&#39;t have to lock by hand <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Everything so far has been about protecting <em>your own</em> state with locks or atomics. The BCL also ships a set of collections that are already safe to use from multiple threads, without you writing any locking code at all:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Collection</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><code>ConcurrentDictionary&lt;K,V&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Fine-grained locking; atomic <code>GetOrAdd</code>, <code>AddOrUpdate</code>, <code>TryRemove</code>, <code>TryUpdate</code>. The factory may run more than once, so wrap it in <code>Lazy&lt;T&gt;</code> for expensive work</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ConcurrentQueue&lt;T&gt;</code> / <code>ConcurrentStack&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Lock-free FIFO/LIFO</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ConcurrentBag&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Unordered; thread-local optimised</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>BlockingCollection&lt;T&gt;</code></td>\n<td>Blocking bounded producer/consumer (sync)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><code>Channel&lt;T&gt;</code></strong></td>\n<td><strong>Async</strong> producer/consumer: bounded or unbounded, single/multi reader/writer, backpressure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ImmutableDictionary</code>/<code>ImmutableList</code> + <code>ImmutableInterlocked</code></td>\n<td>Snapshot semantics: readers never lock</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>FrozenDictionary</code></td>\n<td>Built once, read concurrently, no locks needed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The immutable-snapshot pattern deserves its own look, because it&#39;s a genuinely elegant way to sidestep locking entirely for data that&#39;s read constantly but written rarely:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Copy-on-write snapshot: readers are lock-free, writers swap a new immutable instance atomically</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ImmutableDictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">FeatureFlag</span>&gt; _flags = <span class=\"tk-ty\">ImmutableDictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">FeatureFlag</span>&gt;.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Empty</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsOn</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> name) =&gt; _flags.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGetValue</span>(name, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> f) &amp;&amp; f.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Enabled</span>;           <span class=\"tk-com\">// no lock</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Reload</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IEnumerable</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">FeatureFlag</span>&gt; all) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Interlocked</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Exchange</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">ref</span> _flags, all.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToImmutableDictionary</span>(f =&gt; f.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Name</span>));</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Here&#39;s why this works without a single lock anywhere: readers never modify <code>_flags</code>, they just read whatever reference happens to be there right now — and reading a reference is already atomic. When new flags arrive, <code>Reload</code> doesn&#39;t mutate the existing dictionary at all; it builds a brand new one and then uses <code>Interlocked.Exchange</code> to atomically swap the <em>reference</em> <code>_flags</code> points at. Any reader that grabbed the old reference a moment earlier keeps working with a perfectly consistent, if slightly stale, snapshot. Nobody ever sees a half-updated dictionary, because nobody ever updates one — they only ever replace the whole thing at once.</p>\n<h2 id=\"producer-consumer-pipelines-with-channels\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/threading-concurrency/producer-consumer-pipelines-with-channels\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Producer/consumer pipelines with Channels <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><code>Channel&lt;T&gt;</code> is the async-aware queue that connects a producer doing one job to one or more consumers doing another, with built-in backpressure:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> channel = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Channel</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateBounded</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">ImportRow</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">BoundedChannelOptions</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1_000</span>)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">FullMode</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">BoundedChannelFullMode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Wait</span>,        <span class=\"tk-com\">// producer awaits when full: backpressure</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">SingleWriter</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>,\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">SingleReader</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>\n});\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> producer = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Run</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> () =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> row <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadCsvAsync</span>(path, ct)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> channel.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Writer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteAsync</span>(row, ct);\n    channel.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Writer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Complete</span>();                      <span class=\"tk-com\">// no more items</span>\n});\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> consumers = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Enumerable</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Range</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">4</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(_ =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Run</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> () =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> row <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> channel.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Reader</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadAllAsync</span>(ct))    <span class=\"tk-com\">// ends when the writer completes and the channel is drained</span>\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveAsync</span>(row, ct);\n})).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToArray</span>();\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WhenAll</span>(consumers.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Append</span>(producer));</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>The word <strong>bounded</strong> is doing real work here. If the channel had no capacity limit, a slow consumer couldn&#39;t push back on a fast producer at all — the queue would simply grow without limit until memory ran out, which is a memory leak with extra steps. <code>FullMode = Wait</code> means that once the channel hits its 1,000-item capacity, <code>WriteAsync</code> itself starts <code>await</code>ing until a consumer makes room, which is backpressure in its purest form: the producer is automatically throttled to match how fast the consumers can actually keep up.</p>\n<p>This exact pattern — one bounded queue, several workers, cancellation, a clean completion signal — covers the large majority of in-process pipeline needs you&#39;ll run into. For genuinely multi-stage pipelines with branching and merging, look at <strong>TPL Dataflow</strong> (<code>System.Threading.Tasks.Dataflow</code>), which generalises this idea into a graph of connected blocks.</p>\n<h2 id=\"data-parallelism-parallel-and-plinq\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/threading-concurrency/data-parallelism-parallel-and-plinq\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Data parallelism: Parallel and PLINQ <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Everything above this point has mostly been about <em>correctness</em> under concurrency. This last tool is about <em>speed</em>: deliberately spreading genuinely CPU-bound work — not I/O waiting, actual computation — across multiple cores at once.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Synchronous CPU work across cores</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parallel</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">For</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, images.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Length</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ParallelOptions</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">MaxDegreeOfParallelism</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ProcessorCount</span> }, i =&gt;\n    thumbnails[i] = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Resize</span>(images[i]));          <span class=\"tk-com\">// each index written by one iteration: no shared mutable state</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Async work with bounded concurrency (I/O-bound fan-out)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Parallel</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ForEachAsync</span>(urls, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ParallelOptions</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">MaxDegreeOfParallelism</span> = <span class=\"tk-num\">8</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> = ct },\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (url, token) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DownloadAsync</span>(url, token));\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// PLINQ</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> primes = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Enumerable</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Range</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">5_000_000</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsParallel</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">WithDegreeOfParallelism</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">4</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsPrime</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Three things worth knowing before reaching for this. First, parallelism isn&#39;t free: partitioning the work, scheduling it across threads, and merging the results back together all cost something, so it only pays off once the per-item work is substantial enough to be worth that overhead — parallelising a trivial loop can easily make it <em>slower</em>. Second, if the body of your loop touches shared mutable state (as opposed to the <code>thumbnails[i]</code> example above, where each iteration writes to a different, non-overlapping index), you&#39;re right back to the race-condition problem from the start of this chapter — use <code>Parallel.For</code>&#39;s <code>localInit</code>/<code>localFinally</code> overloads to accumulate per-thread results safely instead. And third, specifically for ASP.NET Core: <strong>avoid heavy parallel CPU work inside a request handler</strong>. Every thread that work pulls onto a core is a thread stolen from the shared pool other requests are also waiting on — offload genuinely heavy CPU work to a background worker instead, so one expensive request can&#39;t starve everyone else.</p>\n<h2 id=\"thread-safety-checklist-for-asp-net-core\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/threading-concurrency/thread-safety-checklist-for-asp-net-core\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Thread safety checklist for ASP.NET Core <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Pulling every idea in this chapter together into something you can actually check a pull request against:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Singletons hold only <strong>thread-safe or immutable</strong> state (concurrent collections, <code>Interlocked</code>, snapshots, locks around compound operations).</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> No mutable <code>static</code> fields used as caches without synchronisation and bounds.</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <code>DbContext</code> is never shared across concurrent operations (one per scope; <code>IDbContextFactory</code> for parallel work).</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <code>HttpContext</code> is never used outside its request or from background threads.</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> No blocking on async code (<code>.Result</code>/<code>.Wait()</code>), and no <code>Thread.Sleep</code> in request paths.</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <code>lock</code> sections are short and contain no awaits or external calls; use <code>SemaphoreSlim</code> for async exclusion.</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Background work is bounded (channels with capacity, <code>MaxDegreeOfParallelism</code>).</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Cross-instance coordination uses distributed locks or the database, not C# locks.</li>\n</ul>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What&#39;s the difference between concurrency, parallelism and asynchrony?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Concurrency is multiple tasks in progress at once (possibly interleaved). Parallelism is executing multiple tasks simultaneously on multiple cores for CPU-bound speed-up. Asynchrony is not blocking a thread while waiting for I/O, so threads can do other work. A web server is highly concurrent and mostly asynchronous.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the thread pool and why does blocking it hurt?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A pool of reusable worker threads that runs queued work items: ASP.NET Core requests, await continuations, Task.Run. It grows slowly when saturated, so blocking threads (sync-over-async, Thread.Sleep, sync I/O) causes thread-pool starvation, where queued work waits and latency spikes while CPU stays low.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is a race condition and how do you prevent it?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>When correctness depends on thread timing, e.g. two threads doing count++ and losing an update, or check-then-act on a dictionary. Prevent it with atomic operations (Interlocked), locks around compound operations, concurrent collections&#39; atomic methods, immutability, or avoiding shared mutable state altogether.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why can&#39;t you use await inside a lock statement? What do you use instead?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Monitor locks are thread-affine (they must be released by the thread that acquired them), but after an await the continuation may run on a different thread, and holding a lock across awaits risks long blocking and deadlocks. The compiler forbids it. Use SemaphoreSlim(1,1) with WaitAsync/Release in try/finally.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do deadlocks occur and how do you avoid them?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Two or more threads each hold a resource another needs and wait in a cycle. Avoid them with consistent lock ordering, short critical sections without external calls, timeouts on acquisition, lock-free or message-passing designs, and not blocking on async code where a SynchronizationContext exists.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you make a singleton service thread-safe?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Prefer immutable state, or swap immutable snapshots atomically with Interlocked.Exchange. Use concurrent collections with their atomic methods, Interlocked for counters, and locks or SemaphoreSlim around compound operations. Don&#39;t hold per-request state or non-thread-safe dependencies (like DbContext) in it.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What does the volatile keyword do?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>It gives reads acquire semantics and writes release semantics, preventing certain compiler/JIT/CPU reorderings and register caching, so other threads see updates. It doesn&#39;t make compound operations atomic. In practice, prefer locks, Interlocked, Volatile.Read/Write or CancellationToken for signalling.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>When would you use Parallel.ForEachAsync vs Task.WhenAll?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Parallel.ForEachAsync for processing a (possibly large) collection with a bounded degree of parallelism and cancellation. Task.WhenAll to await a known, small set of already-started independent operations. Unbounded WhenAll over thousands of items can overwhelm downstream services.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What are Channels used for?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>In-process asynchronous producer/consumer queues (System.Threading.Channels), with bounded capacity for backpressure, completion signalling, and async reading via ReadAllAsync. They&#39;re used for background work queues and pipelines between hosted services and request handlers.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/threading-concurrency/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/threading/managed-threading-best-practices\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Managed threading best practices</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/threading/overview-of-synchronization-primitives\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Overview of synchronization primitives</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/threading/the-managed-thread-pool\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The managed thread pool</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/standard/parallel-programming/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Data and task parallelism (TPL)</a></li>\n<li>Joseph Albahari, <em>Threading in C#</em> (free online). Stephen Cleary, <em>Concurrency in C# Cookbook</em> (2nd ed.).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/threading-concurrency/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (a plain console app, .NET 8/9; <code>System.Collections.Concurrent</code> and <code>System.Threading.Channels</code> are part of the BCL, no extra packages). It reproduces the race condition, then shows <code>Interlocked</code>, <code>lock</code> and <code>ConcurrentDictionary</code> all fixing it exactly; verifies <code>SemaphoreSlim(n)</code> genuinely caps concurrency at <code>n</code>; verifies a bounded <code>Channel&lt;T&gt;</code> applies real backpressure while still delivering every item; and verifies consistent lock ordering avoids a deadlock that a real two-lock deadlock would otherwise hang on. Deliberately reproducing a <code>volatile</code>-missing infinite loop or an actual deadlock on demand is timing/JIT-dependent and not something it&#39;s safe to force in a short script, so those are described rather than triggered — see Further reading.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Verify this chapter: run with `dotnet run`. No external packages needed</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// (System.Threading, System.Collections.Concurrent and System.Threading.Channels</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// are all part of the BCL).</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Collections</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Concurrent</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Diagnostics</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Linq</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Threading</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Threading</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Channels</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Threading</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Tasks</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> name, <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> ok)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!ok) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: {name}&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: {name}&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">const</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> threads = <span class=\"tk-num\">8</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">const</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> perThread = <span class=\"tk-num\">200_000</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">const</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> expected = threads * perThread;\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 1. Race condition: plain `_count++` across many threads loses updates,</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// because read-increment-write is three separate steps, not one.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> unsafeCount = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">BumpUnsafe</span>() { <span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; i &lt; perThread; i++) unsafeCount++; }\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parallel</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Invoke</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Enumerable</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Repeat</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, threads).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(_ =&gt; (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Action</span>)<span class=\"tk-ty\">BumpUnsafe</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToArray</span>());\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(unsafeCount &lt; expected\n    ? <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: unsynchronized increments lost updates under contention ({unsafeCount} of {expected} survived)&quot;</span>\n    : <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: unsynchronized increments happened not to lose any this run ({unsafeCount} of {expected}) -- races are timing-dependent, not guaranteed on every run/machine&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 2. Interlocked.Increment under the exact same contention never loses one:</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// it's a single atomic CPU instruction, not three separate steps.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">long</span> safeCount = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">BumpSafe</span>() { <span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; i &lt; perThread; i++) <span class=\"tk-ty\">Interlocked</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Increment</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">ref</span> safeCount); }\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parallel</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Invoke</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Enumerable</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Repeat</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, threads).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(_ =&gt; (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Action</span>)<span class=\"tk-ty\">BumpSafe</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToArray</span>());\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;Interlocked.Increment under the same contention is always exact ({safeCount} == {expected})&quot;</span>, safeCount == expected);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 3. lock (Monitor) protects a compound operation (read a dict, write it back)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// the same way, for state Interlocked can't cover (more than one variable).</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> gate = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Lock</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> prices = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Collections</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Generic</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">BumpPrice</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> sku) { <span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; i &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">10_000</span>; i++) <span class=\"tk-kw\">lock</span> (gate) { prices[sku] = prices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetValueOrDefault</span>(sku) + <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>; } }\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parallel</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Invoke</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Enumerable</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Repeat</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, threads).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(_ =&gt; (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Action</span>)(() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">BumpPrice</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;KB-01&quot;</span>))).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToArray</span>());\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;a lock-protected read-modify-write is exact under contention ({prices[&quot;</span><span class=\"tk-ty\">KB</span>-<span class=\"tk-num\">01</span><span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;]} == {threads * 10_000})&quot;</span>,\n    prices[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;KB-01&quot;</span>] == threads * <span class=\"tk-num\">10_000</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 4. ConcurrentDictionary.GetOrAdd / AddOrUpdate are atomic: many threads</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// racing to initialize the SAME key still end up with one consistent value.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> concurrentCounts = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ConcurrentDictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parallel</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">For</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">50_000</span>, _ =&gt; concurrentCounts.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddOrUpdate</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;hits&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, (_, old) =&gt; old + <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;ConcurrentDictionary.AddOrUpdate is exact under contention ({concurrentCounts[&quot;</span>hits<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;]} == 50000)&quot;</span>,\n    concurrentCounts[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;hits&quot;</span>] == <span class=\"tk-num\">50_000</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 5. SemaphoreSlim(n) as a concurrency LIMITER: never more than n callers</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// inside the guarded section at once, verified by tracking the peak.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> throttle = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SemaphoreSlim</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> current = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, peak = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> peakGate = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Lock</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ThrottledWork</span>()\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> throttle.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WaitAsync</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span>\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> now = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Interlocked</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Increment</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">ref</span> current);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">lock</span> (peakGate) peak = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Math</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Max</span>(peak, now);\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Delay</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>);\n    }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">finally</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Interlocked</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Decrement</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">ref</span> current); throttle.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Release</span>(); }\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WhenAll</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Enumerable</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Range</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(_ =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">ThrottledWork</span>()));\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;SemaphoreSlim(3) never let more than 3 callers run at once (observed peak = {peak})&quot;</span>, peak &lt;= <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span> &amp;&amp; peak &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 6. Bounded Channel&lt;T&gt; backpressure: with capacity 2 and FullMode.Wait, a</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// producer that's faster than the consumer is made to wait -- verified by</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// checking the writer genuinely blocks (doesn't finish instantly) until the</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// reader drains.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> channel = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Channel</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateBounded</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">BoundedChannelOptions</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>) { <span class=\"tk-ty\">FullMode</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">BoundedChannelFullMode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Wait</span> });\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> sw = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Stopwatch</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">StartNew</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> producerTask = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Run</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> () =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; i &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>; i++) <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> channel.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Writer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteAsync</span>(i);\n    channel.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Writer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Complete</span>();\n});\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> consumerTask = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Run</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> () =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> received = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> _ <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> channel.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Reader</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadAllAsync</span>())\n    {\n        received++;\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Delay</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>); <span class=\"tk-com\">// slower than the producer, forcing backpressure</span>\n    }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> received;\n});\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WhenAll</span>(producerTask, consumerTask);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> totalReceived = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> consumerTask;\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;a bounded channel still delivers every item despite backpressure ({totalReceived} == 10)&quot;</span>, totalReceived == <span class=\"tk-num\">10</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;backpressure made the whole pipeline take noticeably longer than an instant drain (took {sw.ElapsedMilliseconds} ms)&quot;</span>,\n    sw.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ElapsedMilliseconds</span> &gt;= <span class=\"tk-num\">40</span>); <span class=\"tk-com\">// 10 items * 5ms consumer delay, gated by capacity 2</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 7. Lock ordering prevents deadlock: acquiring two locks in a CONSISTENT</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// order from multiple threads never deadlocks, verified by it completing</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// within a timeout (an actual deadlock would hang forever).</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> lockA = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Lock</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> lockB = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Lock</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">AcquireBothInOrder</span>() { <span class=\"tk-kw\">lock</span> (lockA) { <span class=\"tk-kw\">lock</span> (lockB) { <span class=\"tk-com\">/* critical section */</span> } } }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> orderedTask = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Task</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Run</span>(() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Parallel</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Invoke</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Enumerable</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Repeat</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, threads).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(_ =&gt; (<span class=\"tk-ty\">Action</span>)<span class=\"tk-ty\">AcquireBothInOrder</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToArray</span>()));\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> completedInTime = orderedTask.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Wait</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSeconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;many threads acquiring two locks in the SAME consistent order never deadlock (completed well within a 5s timeout)&quot;</span>,\n    completedInTime);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here (timing-sensitive, JIT/CPU-dependent, and not</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// safe to force on demand): a `volatile`-missing infinite loop reliably</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// hanging, and a genuine two-lock deadlock reliably reproducing -- both</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// exist exactly as described, but deliberately triggering them in a short</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// verification script would either hang this program or depend on</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// optimizations that vary by build/runtime. See Further reading: &quot;Overview</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// of synchronization primitives&quot; and &quot;Managed threading best practices&quot;.</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All checks passed.&quot;</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"three-words-people-mix-up-constantly","text":"Three words people mix up constantly","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"threads-are-expensive-the-pool-exists-because-of-that","text":"Threads are expensive; the pool exists because of that","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"race-conditions-when-it-depends-on-timing-becomes-a-bug","text":"Race conditions: when \"it depends on timing\" becomes a bug","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"where-does-this-actually-bite-in-a-web-app","text":"Where does this actually bite in a web app?","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"interlocked-the-fastest-fix-when-it-applies","text":"Interlocked: the fastest fix, when it applies","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"locks-protecting-more-than-one-variable-at-once","text":"Locks: protecting more than one variable at once","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"lock-lock-monitor","text":"lock / Lock / Monitor","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"why-await-and-lock-dont-mix","text":"Why await and lock don't mix","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"semaphoreslim-the-async-friendly-alternative","text":"SemaphoreSlim: the async-friendly alternative","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-rest-of-the-toolbox-briefly","text":"The rest of the toolbox, briefly","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"deadlocks-when-locking-itself-becomes-the-bug","text":"Deadlocks: when locking itself becomes the bug","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"why-the-compiler-and-cpu-are-allowed-to-reorder-your-code","text":"Why the compiler and CPU are allowed to reorder your code","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"concurrent-collections-built-so-you-dont-have-to-lock-by-han","text":"Concurrent collections: built so you don't have to lock by hand","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"producer-consumer-pipelines-with-channels","text":"Producer/consumer pipelines with Channels","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"data-parallelism-parallel-and-plinq","text":"Data parallelism: Parallel and PLINQ","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"thread-safety-checklist-for-asp-net-core","text":"Thread safety checklist for ASP.NET Core","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":5768,"minutes":26,"num":"9.2","part":"p9"},"performance-engineering":{"id":"performance-engineering","title":"Performance Engineering: Measure, Profile, Optimise","summary":"A disciplined approach to speed. Latency vs throughput and percentiles, where time actually goes in a .NET backend, BenchmarkDotNet micro-benchmarks done right, the diagnostics toolbox (dotnet-counters, dotnet-trace, dumps, PerfView, profilers), load testing, a catalogue of high-impact optimisations from database to JSON, startup performance and Native AOT, and keeping performance from regressing.","layer":"cross","where":"Cross-cutting › Performance","fits":"<p>Performance is a layer-8 concern that pulls everything together: the runtime (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/how-code-runs\">How code runs</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/memory-gc\">Memory &amp; GC</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/threading-concurrency\">Threading</a>), the language (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/async-await\">async</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/advanced-csharp\">Span</a>), the framework (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors\">Caching</a>), data (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-querying\">EF Core querying</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/indexes-the-single-most-important-performance-tool-in-this-c\">indexes</a>) and the infrastructure (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-compute\">Azure compute</a>). Observability (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/observability-aspire\">Observability</a>) tells you where to look.</p>\n","prereqs":["memory-gc","threading-concurrency","efcore-querying"],"next":["web-security","system-design"],"levels":["i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/performance-engineering/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Measure before you optimise.</strong> Find the real bottleneck with traces/profilers first; guessing wastes effort on code that was never slow.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>percentiles</strong> (p95, p99), not averages — averages hide the slow requests that real users actually feel.</li>\n<li>Most backend slowness is the <strong>database</strong> (missing indexes, N+1), not CPU-bound C# code.</li>\n<li><strong>BenchmarkDotNet</strong>, not a raw <code>Stopwatch</code> loop, for trusting micro-benchmark numbers — a bare loop is skewed by JIT warm-up, GC pauses and dead-code elimination.</li>\n<li><code>dotnet-counters</code>/<code>dotnet-trace</code>/<code>dotnet-dump</code> are the day-to-day diagnostic toolbox; load testing (k6, NBomber) checks the whole system under concurrency, not just one method.</li>\n<li>Fix things in order of impact: indexes/N+1 first, then async/concurrency, then payload size, then micro-level allocations — in that order, because each later category&#39;s wins are usually dwarfed by the earlier ones.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-method-measure-dont-guess\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/performance-engineering/the-method-measure-dont-guess\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The method: measure, don&#39;t guess <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Every other technique in this chapter — profilers, benchmarks, the optimisation catalogue — only pays off once you&#39;ve committed to one discipline: find out where time is <em>actually</em> going before changing anything. The alternative, guessing based on intuition about what &quot;feels slow,&quot; reliably sends people optimising the wrong 95% of the code while the real bottleneck sits untouched.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>1. Define the goal       &quot;p95 of GET /products &lt; 150 ms at 500 RPS&quot;, not &quot;make it faster&quot;\n2. Measure the baseline  in a production-like environment, with production-like data\n3. Find the bottleneck   traces → profiler → the ONE thing dominating the time\n4. Change one thing      the cheapest fix for that bottleneck\n5. Measure again         did the target metric improve? any regressions elsewhere?\n6. Repeat or stop        stop when the goal is met. Further optimisation has a cost (complexity)</code></pre></figure>\n<blockquote>\n<p>&quot;Premature optimization is the root of all evil&quot; (Knuth). The full quote continues: &quot;…yet we should not pass up our opportunities in that critical 3%.&quot; Find the 3% with measurements.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2 id=\"latency-throughput-and-percentiles\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/performance-engineering/latency-throughput-and-percentiles\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Latency, throughput and percentiles <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Metric</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Latency</strong></td>\n<td>Time for one operation (request duration)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Throughput</strong></td>\n<td>Operations per unit of time (requests/s)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Percentiles</strong></td>\n<td>p50 (median), <strong>p95</strong>, <strong>p99</strong>, p99.9: the latency below which that % of requests fall</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Utilisation / saturation</strong></td>\n<td>How busy a resource is / how much work is queued for it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Use percentiles, not averages.</strong> Here&#39;s a concrete illustration of why: out of 100 requests, if 98 take 50 ms and 2 take 5,000 ms, the <em>average</em> is about 148 ms — already looks bad, but still hides what&#39;s going on — while the p99 is 5,000 ms, the number that actually describes the experience of the unluckiest 2% of users. (Source: this is just <code>latency[⌈p·N⌉ − 1]</code> on the sorted array — verified below.) At real traffic volumes, that 2% is thousands of people, and a user loading a page that fires ten API calls hits that unlucky tail on nearly every page load, not just rarely. <strong>Little&#39;s Law</strong> links the three core metrics precisely: concurrency = throughput × latency (so 500 RPS × 0.2 s = 100 concurrent requests in flight) — a formula worth keeping handy when sizing a thread pool or a connection pool, since it tells you exactly how many requests you need to support <em>simultaneously</em>, not just per second.</p>\n<h2 id=\"where-time-goes-in-a-net-backend\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/performance-engineering/where-time-goes-in-a-net-backend\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Where time goes in a .NET backend <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Typical request time, in order of how often it&#39;s actually the culprit — notice this ordering is itself a product of experience across many real investigations, and it&#39;s worth checking top-down rather than jumping straight to whichever cause feels most interesting:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Database</strong>: missing indexes, N+1 queries, over-fetching, lock contention, chatty round trips (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-querying/the-n-1-problem-the-single-most-common-ef-core-performance-b\">EF Core querying</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Remote calls</strong>: sequential calls that could be concurrent, no caching, no timeouts, slow dependencies.</li>\n<li><strong>Thread pool starvation</strong>: sync-over-async, blocking calls (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/async-await\">async/await</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Serialization</strong>: huge payloads, reflection-based serializers, returning entire entity graphs.</li>\n<li><strong>Allocations and GC</strong>: per-request large buffers, LINQ-heavy hot paths, boxing, string concatenation (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/memory-gc\">Memory &amp; GC</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Lock contention</strong>: a global lock in a singleton serialising all requests.</li>\n<li><strong>Logging</strong>: synchronous sinks, excessive logging, interpolated log strings.</li>\n<li><strong>Pure CPU work</strong> in your code: rarely the problem, but measurable when it is.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Traces</strong> (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/observability-aspire\">Observability</a>) show which of these dominates for a slow request <strong>before</strong> you open a profiler — exactly the workflow the trace diagram in that chapter walked through, where one SQL span turned out to account for nearly the entire request&#39;s duration. Starting there, rather than guessing from this list, is what step 3 of the measurement loop above actually means in practice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"micro-benchmarking-with-benchmarkdotnet\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/performance-engineering/micro-benchmarking-with-benchmarkdotnet\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Micro-benchmarking with BenchmarkDotNet <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Timing code with <code>Stopwatch</code> in a loop gives misleading numbers. Four things throw it off:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>JIT warm-up</strong>: your first iterations run on slow, unoptimised Tier 0 code before the JIT recompiles the hot method into fast Tier 1 code (the two-tier curve from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/now-zoom-into-the-jit-why-does-it-translate-a-method-twice\">How code runs</a>). An unwarmed loop measures a mix of slow-then-fast, not the steady state you actually care about.</li>\n<li><strong>GC pauses</strong> landing at unpredictable moments.</li>\n<li><strong>Dead-code elimination</strong>: the optimiser may notice nobody uses the result and skip the work entirely, timing nothing.</li>\n<li><strong>CPU frequency scaling</strong> changing clock speed mid-run.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>BenchmarkDotNet</strong> handles all four: it runs a separate process in Release mode, does warm-up and pilot runs, takes many iterations, and applies statistics — so the number you get back reflects your code, not noise.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// dotnet new console; dotnet add package BenchmarkDotNet; run with: dotnet run -c Release</span>\n<span class=\"tk-attr\">[MemoryDiagnoser]</span>                         <span class=\"tk-com\">// allocations per operation + GC counts</span>\n<span class=\"tk-attr\">[SimpleJob(RuntimeMoniker.Net80)]</span>\n<span class=\"tk-attr\">[SimpleJob(RuntimeMoniker.Net10_0, baseline: true)]</span>   <span class=\"tk-com\">// compare runtimes</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SkuParsing</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">const</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Line</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;2026-10-27;KB-01;3;49.90&quot;</span>;\n\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[Benchmark(Baseline = true)]</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Split</span>() =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parse</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Line</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Split</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">';'</span>)[<span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>], <span class=\"tk-ty\">CultureInfo</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">InvariantCulture</span>);\n\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[Benchmark]</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Span</span>()\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadOnlySpan</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">char</span>&gt; s = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Line</span>;\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parse</span>(s[(s.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LastIndexOf</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">';'</span>) + <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>)..], <span class=\"tk-ty\">CultureInfo</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">InvariantCulture</span>);\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[Params(10, 1_000)]</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">N</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>[] _data = [];\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[GlobalSetup]</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Setup</span>() =&gt; _data = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Enumerable</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Range</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">N</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToArray</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[Benchmark]</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">LinqSum</span>() =&gt; _data.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(x =&gt; x % <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sum</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-attr\">[Benchmark]</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">LoopSum</span>() { <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> s = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">foreach</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> x <span class=\"tk-kw\">in</span> _data) <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (x % <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span> == <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>) s += x; <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> s; }\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">BenchmarkRunner</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Run</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">SkuParsing</span>&gt;();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>| Method | Mean      | Ratio | Allocated |\n|------- |----------:|------:|----------:|\n| Split  | 112.40 ns |  1.00 |     216 B |\n| Span   |  38.15 ns |  0.34 |         - |</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Look at both columns of that result, not just <code>Mean</code>: <code>Span</code> is roughly three times faster <em>and</em> allocates zero bytes, where <code>Split</code> allocates 216 B per call purely from the intermediate array <code>Split(&#39;;&#39;)</code> creates. Multiply that 216 B by a hot path called a million times a minute, and it&#39;s 216 MB of garbage the GC now has to collect — exactly the allocation-pressure mechanism from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/memory-gc\">Memory &amp; GC</a> turning a &quot;tiny&quot; per-call cost into real, measurable GC time under load.</p>\n<p>Rules: <strong>always Release</strong>, never with a debugger attached; return results (or use <code>Consumer</code>) so the JIT can&#39;t eliminate the work; benchmark realistic inputs; look at <strong>Allocated</strong>, not just time; remember a 70 ns win matters only if the code runs millions of times — on a code path called once per request at 50 RPS, 70 ns is immeasurable against a 150 ms database call, and chasing it would be exactly the &quot;premature optimisation&quot; Knuth warned about. Micro-benchmarks validate a hypothesis about a hot path that <strong>profiling already identified</strong>; they&#39;re a verification step, not where you start.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-diagnostics-toolbox\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/performance-engineering/the-diagnostics-toolbox\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The diagnostics toolbox <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Tool</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>When</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>dotnet-counters</strong></td>\n<td>Live runtime and ASP.NET Core metrics: CPU, GC heap/gen counts, % time in GC, allocation rate, <strong>thread pool queue length &amp; thread count</strong>, exceptions/s, requests/s, connection counts</td>\n<td>First look at a running process; starvation and GC pressure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>dotnet-trace</strong></td>\n<td>Collects an EventPipe trace (CPU samples, GC, allocations, contention, exceptions, custom events)</td>\n<td>CPU hot paths, allocation sources, lock contention</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>dotnet-dump</strong></td>\n<td>Full process dump + SOS analysis (<code>clrstack</code>, <code>dumpheap</code>, <code>gcroot</code>, <code>syncblk</code>)</td>\n<td>Hangs, deadlocks, memory leaks, crashes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>dotnet-gcdump</strong></td>\n<td>Lightweight heap snapshot</td>\n<td>Memory growth comparison</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>dotnet-monitor</strong></td>\n<td>A sidecar/agent exposing those diagnostics over HTTP with triggers (&quot;collect a trace when CPU &gt; 80% for 1 min&quot;)</td>\n<td>Production containers / Kubernetes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Visual Studio Profiler</strong> / <strong>JetBrains dotTrace &amp; dotMemory</strong></td>\n<td>CPU, allocation, async and database profiling with rich UIs</td>\n<td>Development and staging deep dives</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>PerfView</strong></td>\n<td>Microsoft&#39;s free, powerful ETW/EventPipe analyser</td>\n<td>GC and CPU analysis, flame graphs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Speedscope</strong> / flame graphs</td>\n<td>Visualise traces (<code>dotnet-trace convert --format Speedscope</code>)</td>\n<td>Seeing where CPU time goes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Application Insights Profiler</strong>, continuous profilers (Pyroscope, Datadog)</td>\n<td>Sampled production profiling</td>\n<td>Real-traffic hot paths</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SQL tools</strong></td>\n<td>Execution plans, Query Store, <code>SET STATISTICS IO</code>, Azure SQL Insights</td>\n<td>Database time</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Shell</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>dotnet-counters monitor -n Shop.Api <span class=\"tk-attr\">--counters</span> System.Runtime,Microsoft.AspNetCore.Hosting,System.Net.Http\ndotnet-trace collect -n Shop.Api <span class=\"tk-attr\">--profile</span> cpu-sampling <span class=\"tk-attr\">--duration</span> <span class=\"tk-num\">00</span>:<span class=\"tk-num\">00</span>:<span class=\"tk-num\">30</span> -o cpu.nettrace\ndotnet-trace convert cpu.nettrace <span class=\"tk-attr\">--format</span> Speedscope                     <span class=\"tk-com\"># open at speedscope.app</span>\ndotnet-dump collect -n Shop.Api &amp;&amp; dotnet-dump analyze &lt;file&gt;             <span class=\"tk-com\"># &gt; clrstack -all   (look for threads blocked in Wait/GetResult)</span></code></pre></figure>\n<h3 id=\"reading-the-symptoms\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/performance-engineering/reading-the-symptoms\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Reading the symptoms</h3>\n<p>This table is really a decision tree disguised as a table: each row&#39;s symptom narrows down which tool from above you reach for next, and <em>why</em> each symptom points where it does is worth tracing through once:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Symptom</th>\n<th>Likely cause</th>\n<th>Confirm with</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>High latency, <strong>low CPU</strong>, rising thread count</td>\n<td>Thread-pool starvation (sync-over-async) or waiting on I/O/locks</td>\n<td>counters (ThreadPool Queue Length), dump <code>clrstack -all</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High CPU, latency rises with load</td>\n<td>Hot code path, excessive GC, serialization, regex backtracking</td>\n<td>dotnet-trace CPU sampling → flame graph</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High % time in GC, saw-tooth memory</td>\n<td>Allocation rate too high, LOH churn</td>\n<td>counters (alloc rate, gen2 count), trace allocations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Memory grows forever</td>\n<td>Leak</td>\n<td>gcdump diff, <code>gcroot</code> (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/memory-gc/actually-finding-a-leak-a-real-diagnostic-workflow\">Memory &amp; GC</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slow only under concurrency</td>\n<td>Lock contention, connection pool exhaustion, DB locking</td>\n<td>trace contention events, pool counters, DB waits</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A specific endpoint is slow</td>\n<td>Its SQL, its remote calls</td>\n<td>Distributed trace spans</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The first row is the one people most often get wrong: <strong>high latency with low CPU</strong> sounds like &quot;the machine isn&#39;t working hard, so why is it slow?&quot; But low CPU is exactly what thread-pool starvation looks like. The CPU really isn&#39;t busy — your requests are just sitting in a queue waiting for a free worker thread, because something upstream is blocking synchronously on async work and holding threads that should have gone back to the pool (the same mechanism as <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/async-await/deadlocks-and-thread-pool-starvation\">Async and await&#39;s deadlock section</a>). So &quot;the server isn&#39;t even busy&quot; is a clue <em>pointing at</em> a thread-pool problem, not evidence against one.</p>\n<h2 id=\"load-testing\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/performance-engineering/load-testing\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Load testing <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Micro-benchmarks test methods; <strong>load tests</strong> test the <strong>system</strong> under realistic concurrency — a different question entirely, since a method that benchmarks fast in isolation can still fall over once a hundred requests are all contending for the same connection pool at once.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>JavaScript</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// k6 script (JavaScript): ramp to 500 virtual users, assert SLOs</span>\nimport http from <span class=\"tk-str\">'k6/http'</span>;\nimport { check, sleep } from <span class=\"tk-str\">'k6'</span>;\nexport const <span class=\"tk-ty\">options</span> = {\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">stages</span>: [{ <span class=\"tk-ty\">duration</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">'2m'</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">target</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">100</span> }, { <span class=\"tk-ty\">duration</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">'5m'</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">target</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">500</span> }, { <span class=\"tk-ty\">duration</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">'2m'</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">target</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span> }],\n  <span class=\"tk-ty\">thresholds</span>: { <span class=\"tk-ty\">http_req_duration</span>: [<span class=\"tk-str\">'p(95)&lt;150'</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">'p(99)&lt;400'</span>], <span class=\"tk-ty\">http_req_failed</span>: [<span class=\"tk-str\">'rate&lt;0.01'</span>] },\n};\nexport default function () {\n  const <span class=\"tk-ty\">res</span> = http.get(<span class=\"tk-str\">'https://staging.shop.com/api/products?page=1'</span>);\n  check(res, { <span class=\"tk-str\">'status 200'</span>: <span class=\"tk-ty\">r</span> =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">r.status</span> === <span class=\"tk-num\">200</span> });\n  sleep(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>);\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Test type</th>\n<th>Purpose</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Load</strong></td>\n<td>Expected peak traffic: do we meet the SLOs?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Stress</strong></td>\n<td>Beyond peak: where and how does it break? (Gracefully, with 429/503, or by falling over?)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Spike</strong></td>\n<td>Sudden bursts: does autoscaling react in time?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Soak / endurance</strong></td>\n<td>Hours at steady load: memory leaks, connection leaks, degradation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Capacity</strong></td>\n<td>How many instances for N RPS → cost planning</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The Stress row&#39;s parenthetical — &quot;gracefully, with 429/503, or by falling over&quot; — is the whole point of a stress test. A system that starts returning 503s past its limit, while still serving the requests it <em>can</em> handle, is behaving correctly. A system that falls over entirely past that same limit is a future incident, waiting for the next traffic spike. Tools: <strong>k6</strong> (Grafana), <strong>NBomber</strong> (C#), <strong>Azure Load Testing</strong> (managed, runs JMeter or Locust scripts, integrates with CI and App Insights), JMeter, Locust, <code>bombardier</code>/<code>wrk</code> for quick HTTP blasts. Test against <strong>production-like</strong> environments and data volumes, watch server-side metrics during the test, and watch out for the load generator itself becoming the bottleneck: a test that&#39;s really measuring how fast the <em>test machine</em> can send requests, rather than how fast your API answers them, is an easy trap to fall into.</p>\n<h2 id=\"high-impact-optimisations-by-layer\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/performance-engineering/high-impact-optimisations-by-layer\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>High-impact optimisations, by layer <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<h3 id=\"data-access-usually-the-biggest-wins\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/performance-engineering/data-access-usually-the-biggest-wins\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Data access (usually the biggest wins)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Add or fix <strong>indexes</strong> for the actual query patterns; check plans (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals/indexes-the-single-most-important-performance-tool-in-this-c\">Relational fundamentals</a>).</li>\n<li>Eliminate <strong>N+1</strong>; <strong>project</strong> to DTOs; <code>AsNoTracking</code>; split queries; pagination with sensible limits.</li>\n<li>Batch round trips (<code>WHERE Id IN …</code>, <code>ExecuteUpdate</code>, bulk copy); avoid chatty loops.</li>\n<li><strong>Cache</strong> hot, rarely changing reads (HybridCache, output cache, CDN) (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors\">Caching</a>).</li>\n<li>Read replicas for read-heavy workloads; connection pooling and DbContext pooling.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This category tops the list because the gaps here are so much bigger than anywhere else: a missing index can turn a 2 ms query into a 2-second table scan, a thousand-fold difference no amount of C# optimisation elsewhere in the request can claw back. Fix the database layer first; everything else is comparatively marginal gains.</p>\n<h3 id=\"concurrency-and-i-o\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/performance-engineering/concurrency-and-i-o\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Concurrency and I/O</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Async all the way</strong>; remove every <code>.Result</code>/<code>.Wait()</code>.</li>\n<li>Run independent remote calls <strong>concurrently</strong> (<code>Task.WhenAll</code>), with timeouts.</li>\n<li>Reuse connections: <code>IHttpClientFactory</code>, singleton SDK clients, HTTP/2 or HTTP/3.</li>\n<li>Move slow work <strong>off the request path</strong> (queues + workers; 202 Accepted).</li>\n<li>Remove global locks from hot singletons; use concurrent or immutable structures.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"payloads-and-serialization\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/performance-engineering/payloads-and-serialization\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Payloads and serialization</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Smaller responses: projections, paging, field selection, compression (Brotli/gzip), <code>JsonIgnoreCondition.WhenWritingNull</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>System.Text.Json source generation</strong> for hot types; cache <code>JsonSerializerOptions</code> (never create one per call).</li>\n<li>Stream large results (<code>IAsyncEnumerable</code>, <code>Stream</code>) instead of buffering.</li>\n<li>gRPC/Protobuf for internal high-volume calls.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"allocations-and-cpu\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/performance-engineering/allocations-and-cpu\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Allocations and CPU</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Pool large buffers (<code>ArrayPool</code>), use <code>Span</code>/<code>Memory</code> for parsing, avoid boxing and per-call closures in hot paths, pre-size collections, <code>StringBuilder</code>, <code>FrozenDictionary</code> for static lookups, <code>[GeneratedRegex]</code> instead of new <code>Regex</code> instances, <code>[LoggerMessage]</code> for hot logging (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/advanced-csharp\">Advanced C#</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Seal</strong> classes, avoid unnecessary virtual/interface dispatch in tight loops (the JIT devirtualises sealed types), and let Dynamic PGO work (it&#39;s on by default).</li>\n<li>Use <code>Vector&lt;T&gt;</code>/SIMD-accelerated BCL APIs (<code>span.IndexOfAny</code>, <code>SequenceEqual</code>, <code>Sum</code> on spans).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"platform\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/performance-engineering/platform\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Platform</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Upgrade .NET</strong>: each release brings significant free performance improvements (Stephen Toub&#39;s yearly posts document hundreds).</li>\n<li>Server GC with DATAS; correct container CPU/memory requests (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/kubernetes/resources-qos-and-autoscaling\">Kubernetes</a>).</li>\n<li>Scale out horizontally; autoscale on the right signal (queue length, RPS, CPU).</li>\n<li>CDN and edge caching for static and cacheable content.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"startup-performance\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/performance-engineering/startup-performance\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Startup performance <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Startup matters for <strong>serverless cold starts</strong>, <strong>scale-out speed</strong>, and container <strong>rolling deployments</strong> — anywhere a brand-new process has to become ready to serve traffic as fast as possible, rather than a long-running process where startup cost is amortised over hours of uptime.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Technique</th>\n<th>Effect</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>ReadyToRun</strong> (<code>&lt;PublishReadyToRun&gt;</code>)</td>\n<td>Precompiled code: less JIT at startup</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Native AOT</strong> (<code>&lt;PublishAot&gt;</code>)</td>\n<td>No JIT at all; ~10× faster startup, much lower memory; requires AOT-compatible code (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/what-if-you-skipped-the-jit-completely\">How code runs</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>WebApplication.CreateSlimBuilder</code></td>\n<td>Fewer default services</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Trimming</td>\n<td>Smaller deployments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>EF Core <strong>compiled models</strong></td>\n<td>Faster first query for big models</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Avoid heavy work in startup</td>\n<td>Defer cache warm-ups to a background service; lazy initialisation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Source generators (JSON, config binding, RDG)</td>\n<td>No reflection at startup</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Warm-up before taking traffic</td>\n<td>Startup/readiness probes, slot warm-up (<code>applicationInitialization</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Native AOT&#39;s speedup isn&#39;t vague &quot;it&#39;s more optimised&quot; — it&#39;s the literal removal of a pipeline stage. With no JIT at all, there&#39;s no Tier 0 → Tier 1 warm-up period to pay for (the same curve from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/how-code-runs/now-zoom-into-the-jit-why-does-it-translate-a-method-twice\">How code runs</a>), which is exactly where the roughly tenfold startup improvement comes from.</p>\n<h2 id=\"preventing-regressions\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/performance-engineering/preventing-regressions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Preventing regressions <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Performance budgets and SLOs</strong> (p95 targets per critical endpoint) tracked on dashboards, with alerts.</li>\n<li><strong>Benchmarks in CI</strong> for performance-critical libraries (BenchmarkDotNet with result comparison against the main branch).</li>\n<li><strong>Load tests in the pipeline</strong> (Azure Load Testing with pass/fail criteria) before production for high-traffic services.</li>\n<li><strong>Canary deployments</strong> with automated latency and error comparisons (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cicd-iac/deployment-strategies\">CI/CD</a>).</li>\n<li>Code review checklists: async all the way, no N+1, projections, timeouts, pagination limits, no per-request large allocations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The common thread: left unguarded, performance degrades gradually and invisibly. One small regression per PR, each too small to notice on its own, compounds over months into a system that&#39;s quietly twice as slow — with no single commit anyone could point to as &quot;the cause.&quot; Each of these five practices exists to catch that drift automatically, before it compounds.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How would you approach a performance problem in a .NET API?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Define a measurable goal (p95 latency at a given load). Measure a baseline in a production-like environment. Use distributed traces to see which component dominates (DB, remote calls, app code). Drill in with the right tool (execution plans for SQL, dotnet-counters/trace or a profiler for CPU/GC/thread-pool issues). Fix the single biggest bottleneck, re-measure, and repeat until the goal is met. Avoid optimising without evidence.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why use percentiles instead of averages for latency?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Averages hide the tail: a few very slow requests barely move the mean but hurt real users, and users making many requests encounter the tail frequently. p95/p99 show what most users and the worst-affected users experience, and SLOs are usually defined on them.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is BenchmarkDotNet and why not just use a Stopwatch?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A micro-benchmarking library that runs code in an isolated Release process with warm-up, many iterations and statistical analysis, and reports allocations with MemoryDiagnoser. A Stopwatch loop is skewed by JIT tiering, warm-up, GC, dead-code elimination and noise, so it produces unreliable numbers.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>High latency but low CPU: what do you suspect and how do you confirm it?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Thread-pool starvation from blocking calls (sync-over-async, sync I/O), or waiting on slow dependencies, locks or connection pools. Confirm with dotnet-counters (growing thread pool queue length and thread count), a dump showing threads blocked in Wait/GetResult, and traces showing long dependency spans.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What tools would you use to diagnose a production performance issue in a container?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Distributed traces and metrics (App Insights / OpenTelemetry) first, then dotnet-counters for runtime health, dotnet-trace for CPU/allocation/contention profiles, dotnet-dump for hangs and leaks, typically via dotnet-monitor as a sidecar or kubectl debug with an ephemeral container, plus database execution plans and Query Store.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Name high-impact optimisations for a typical CRUD API.</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Fix indexes and N+1 queries, project to DTOs with AsNoTracking, paginate, cache hot reads (HybridCache, output caching), make everything async and parallelise independent remote calls, reuse HttpClient connections, compress and trim responses with System.Text.Json source generation, move slow work to background queues, and upgrade to the latest .NET.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you improve cold-start time for a .NET service?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Native AOT (or ReadyToRun), the slim builder, trimming, source generators instead of reflection, EF compiled models, deferring heavy initialisation out of startup, pre-warming instances (Premium/Flex always-ready, readiness probes, slot warm-up), and smaller container images.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/performance-engineering/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/diagnostics/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">.NET diagnostic tools overview</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/best-practices\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ASP.NET Core performance best practices</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://benchmarkdotnet.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BenchmarkDotNet</a></li>\n<li>Stephen Toub, <em>Performance Improvements in .NET 8 / 9 / 10</em> (.NET Blog): enormous, invaluable.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/load-testing/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Azure Load Testing</a> · <a href=\"https://grafana.com/docs/k6/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">k6 documentation</a></li>\n<li>Book: <em>Writing High-Performance .NET Code</em> (2nd ed.) by Ben Watson.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/performance-engineering/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run -c Release</code> (plain console app, .NET 8/9, no extra packages — <code>GC.GetAllocatedBytesForCurrentThread</code> and <code>Stopwatch</code>-free comparisons are enough to see the allocation difference this chapter talks about).</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Globalization</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 1) Percentiles vs averages: 98 fast requests + 2 slow ones.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> latenciesMs = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">List</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">for</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> i = <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>; i &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">98</span>; i++) latenciesMs.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">50</span>);\nlatenciesMs.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">5000</span>);\nlatenciesMs.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">5000</span>);\nlatenciesMs.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Sort</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> average = latenciesMs.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Average</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> p99 = latenciesMs[(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>)<span class=\"tk-ty\">Math</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ceiling</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">0.99</span> * latenciesMs.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span>) - <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>];\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!(average <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">140</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">and</span> &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">160</span>)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected average ~148ms, got {average}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (p99 != <span class=\"tk-num\">5000</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected p99 = 5000ms, got {p99}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: average={average:F1}ms looks tolerable, but p99={p99}ms reveals the real tail latency averages hide.&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 2) Little's Law: concurrency = throughput (req/s) x latency (s)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> throughputRps = <span class=\"tk-num\">500</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> latencySeconds = <span class=\"tk-num\">0.2</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> concurrency = throughputRps * latencySeconds;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (concurrency != <span class=\"tk-num\">100</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected concurrency=100, got {concurrency}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: Little's Law — {throughputRps} RPS x {latencySeconds}s latency = {concurrency} concurrent requests in flight.&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 3) Span-based slicing avoids the allocation that string.Split causes.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">const</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> line = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;2026-10-27;KB-01;3;49.90&quot;</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">long</span> before = <span class=\"tk-ty\">GC</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAllocatedBytesForCurrentThread</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> viaSplit = <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parse</span>(line.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Split</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">';'</span>)[<span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>], <span class=\"tk-ty\">CultureInfo</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">InvariantCulture</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">long</span> splitAllocated = <span class=\"tk-ty\">GC</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAllocatedBytesForCurrentThread</span>() - before;\n\nbefore = <span class=\"tk-ty\">GC</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAllocatedBytesForCurrentThread</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">ReadOnlySpan</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">char</span>&gt; s = line;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span> viaSpan = <span class=\"tk-kw\">decimal</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parse</span>(s[(s.<span class=\"tk-ty\">LastIndexOf</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">';'</span>) + <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>)..], <span class=\"tk-ty\">CultureInfo</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">InvariantCulture</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">long</span> spanAllocated = <span class=\"tk-ty\">GC</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetAllocatedBytesForCurrentThread</span>() - before;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (viaSplit != viaSpan) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: Split and Span parsing disagree on the value&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!(splitAllocated &gt; spanAllocated)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected Split ({splitAllocated}B) to allocate more than Span ({spanAllocated}B)&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: string.Split allocated {splitAllocated}B (the intermediate array), Span-based slicing allocated {spanAllocated}B — confirms the chapter's allocation claim, though exact byte counts vary by runtime/JIT.&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claims NOT verified here (they need an external profiler/benchmark harness, not a plain console check):</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - BenchmarkDotNet's own warm-up/statistics methodology and exact ns timings — see Further reading: https://benchmarkdotnet.org/</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - dotnet-counters/dotnet-trace/dotnet-dump behavior on a live process — see Further reading: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/diagnostics/</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - Native AOT's ~10x startup improvement and k6/load-testing throughput numbers — environment-dependent; see the Further reading links above.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All runtime-checkable claims verified.&quot;</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-method-measure-dont-guess","text":"The method: measure, don't guess","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"latency-throughput-and-percentiles","text":"Latency, throughput and percentiles","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"where-time-goes-in-a-net-backend","text":"Where time goes in a .NET backend","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"micro-benchmarking-with-benchmarkdotnet","text":"Micro-benchmarking with BenchmarkDotNet","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"the-diagnostics-toolbox","text":"The diagnostics toolbox","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"reading-the-symptoms","text":"Reading the symptoms","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"load-testing","text":"Load testing","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"high-impact-optimisations-by-layer","text":"High-impact optimisations, by layer","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"data-access-usually-the-biggest-wins","text":"Data access (usually the biggest wins)","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"concurrency-and-i-o","text":"Concurrency and I/O","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"payloads-and-serialization","text":"Payloads and serialization","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"allocations-and-cpu","text":"Allocations and CPU","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"platform","text":"Platform","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"startup-performance","text":"Startup performance","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"preventing-regressions","text":"Preventing regressions","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":3739,"minutes":17,"num":"9.3","part":"p9"},"web-security":{"id":"web-security","title":"Web Application Security for .NET Backends","summary":"Defending APIs against real attacks. Security principles, the OWASP Top 10 and API Security Top 10 mapped to .NET defences, broken access control and IDOR, injection, XSS and CSRF, authentication failures, password hashing, cryptography and the Data Protection API, HTTPS, HSTS and security headers, SSRF, mass assignment, rate limiting, secure file uploads, dependency and supply-chain security, and logging without leaking.","layer":"cross","where":"Cross-cutting › Security › Application security","fits":"<p>Security is a layer-8 concern that every layer must honour: input handling in endpoints (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/building-apis\">Building APIs</a>), authentication and authorization in the pipeline (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/auth\">Auth</a>), parameterised data access (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ado-dapper\">ADO.NET &amp; Dapper</a>), secrets and identity in the cloud (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets\">Identity &amp; secrets</a>), CORS and rate limiting (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors\">Caching, rate limiting &amp; CORS</a>), and the token flows in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/oauth-oidc-identity\">OAuth &amp; OIDC</a>.</p>\n","prereqs":["auth","building-apis"],"next":["oauth-oidc-identity","system-design"],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/web-security/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Security is the same principles this book has already used (least privilege, secure-by-default, never trust input) now organised around specific attacks, not a separate discipline bolted on at the end.</li>\n<li><strong>Broken access control</strong> (IDOR/BOLA) is the #1 real-world risk: checking &quot;is this user logged in&quot; is not the same as checking &quot;may this user see <em>this specific</em> record.&quot;</li>\n<li>Always use <strong>parameterized</strong> queries, never string-concatenated SQL — the value should never become part of the command text at all.</li>\n<li>Store passwords with a <strong>slow, salted, adaptive</strong> hash (PBKDF2, Argon2id, bcrypt), never a fast hash like SHA-256 or plain text.</li>\n<li><strong>XSS</strong> steals/runs script in someone else&#39;s browser; <strong>CSRF</strong> abuses cookies being sent automatically — they need different, sometimes opposite, defences (HttpOnly cookies help XSS, SameSite/antiforgery tokens help CSRF).</li>\n<li>Treat every external input — request bodies, file uploads, outbound URLs you fetch on a user&#39;s behalf (SSRF) — as hostile until validated, and keep dependencies patched; most real breaches exploit a known, unfixed issue, not a novel attack.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"security-principles\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/web-security/security-principles\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Security principles <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Principle</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Defence in depth</strong></td>\n<td>Multiple independent layers; one failure isn&#39;t fatal</td>\n<td>WAF + authn + authz + parameterised SQL + least-privilege DB user + encryption at rest</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Least privilege</strong></td>\n<td>Every identity gets only the access it needs</td>\n<td>The app&#39;s managed identity has <code>db_datareader/db_datawriter</code>, not <code>db_owner</code>; data-plane roles scoped to one container</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Secure by default</strong></td>\n<td>The safe option is the default</td>\n<td>Fallback policy requiring authentication; HTTPS only; deny-by-default CORS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Fail securely</strong></td>\n<td>Errors don&#39;t open doors or leak details</td>\n<td>Generic ProblemDetails, no stack traces; authorization errors deny</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Never trust input</strong></td>\n<td>Validate, encode and parameterise everything from outside</td>\n<td>Request bodies, headers, query strings, files, messages, <em>and</em> data from other services</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Minimise attack surface</strong></td>\n<td>Fewer endpoints, ports, packages and privileges</td>\n<td>Remove unused endpoints, disable Swagger UI in prod, chiseled images</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Keep secrets out of code</strong></td>\n<td>Use identities and vaults</td>\n<td>Managed identity, Key Vault, User Secrets for dev</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Assume breach</strong></td>\n<td>Detect, limit blast radius, recover</td>\n<td>Logging and alerts, segmentation, short token lifetimes, backups</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Notice how many of these principles have already shown up by name earlier in this book — least privilege drove every RBAC example in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets/azure-rbac-in-practice\">Identity &amp; secrets</a>, secure-by-default was the entire justification for <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/auth/authorization-the-simple-forms-before-anything-needs-a-polic\">Auth</a>&#39;s fallback-policy advice. Security here isn&#39;t a separate discipline bolted on at the end; it&#39;s the same instincts this book has been building all along, now organised explicitly around attacks.</p>\n<p><strong>Threat modelling</strong> (e.g. <strong>STRIDE</strong>: Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information disclosure, Denial of service, Elevation of privilege) at design time finds problems before they&#39;re built.</p>\n<h2 id=\"owasp-top-10-mapped-to-net\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/web-security/owasp-top-10-mapped-to-net\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>OWASP Top 10 mapped to .NET <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>The <strong>OWASP Top 10</strong> (web apps; 2021 edition, with a 2025 update) and <strong>OWASP API Security Top 10</strong> (2023) list the most critical risks.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Risk</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>.NET defences</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Broken access control</strong> (#1 web; API1 BOLA, API5 BFLA)</td>\n<td>Users access data or functions they shouldn&#39;t: IDOR, missing checks on admin endpoints, CORS misconfig</td>\n<td>Authorization on <strong>every</strong> endpoint (fallback policy), <strong>resource-based authorization</strong>, scope queries by tenant/user, deny by default</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cryptographic failures</strong></td>\n<td>Weak or missing encryption, plain-text secrets, weak hashing</td>\n<td>TLS everywhere, HSTS, TDE / encryption at rest, <code>PasswordHasher</code>/PBKDF2/Argon2, Data Protection API, Key Vault</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Injection</strong></td>\n<td>SQL, NoSQL, OS command, LDAP, log injection</td>\n<td><strong>Parameterised</strong> queries (EF Core LINQ, Dapper parameters, <code>FromSql</code> interpolation); never build commands from input</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Insecure design</strong></td>\n<td>Missing security controls by design</td>\n<td>Threat modelling, abuse cases, rate limits, secure defaults</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Security misconfiguration</strong></td>\n<td>Debug pages in prod, default credentials, verbose errors, open storage</td>\n<td>Environment-specific config, <code>UseExceptionHandler</code>, Azure Policy, IaC reviews, disabled public access</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Vulnerable and outdated components</strong></td>\n<td>Known-CVE packages and base images</td>\n<td>NuGet audit, <code>dotnet list package --vulnerable</code>, Dependabot, image scanning, regular .NET patching</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Identification and authentication failures</strong></td>\n<td>Weak passwords, credential stuffing, broken session handling</td>\n<td>External IdP (Entra ID), MFA, lockout, short-lived tokens, secure cookies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Software and data integrity failures</strong></td>\n<td>Untrusted deserialization, unsigned updates, compromised CI</td>\n<td>No <code>BinaryFormatter</code> (removed in .NET 9), signed packages and images, pinned actions, protected pipelines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Security logging and monitoring failures</strong></td>\n<td>Attacks go unnoticed</td>\n<td>Log auth failures and access denials, alert on anomalies, Defender for Cloud</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SSRF</strong> (Server-Side Request Forgery)</td>\n<td>The server fetches attacker-controlled URLs (cloud metadata, internal services)</td>\n<td>Allow-list destinations, block private/metadata IP ranges, no raw user URLs to HttpClient</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>API3: <strong>Broken object property level authorization</strong> / mass assignment</td>\n<td>Clients read or write properties they shouldn&#39;t</td>\n<td>Request/response <strong>DTOs</strong>, never bind entities, explicit mapping</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>API4: <strong>Unrestricted resource consumption</strong></td>\n<td>No limits on size, rate or cost</td>\n<td>Rate limiting, request size limits, pagination caps, timeouts, upload limits</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The rest of this chapter walks through the table&#39;s top few rows in detail — they&#39;re ranked by real-world frequency for a reason, and that ranking is also the order worth spending review time in.</p>\n<h2 id=\"broken-access-control-and-idor\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/web-security/broken-access-control-and-idor\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Broken access control and IDOR <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>The <strong>#1 risk</strong> in practice. An <strong>IDOR</strong> (Insecure Direct Object Reference, called <strong>BOLA</strong> in the API Top 10) is when an endpoint trusts an ID from the client without checking that the <em>caller</em> is actually allowed to see <em>that specific</em> resource:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// ✘ Any authenticated user can read ANY order by changing the id</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/orders/{id:int}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span> db) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FindAsync</span>(id)).<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequireAuthorization</span>();\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// ✔ Scope the query to the caller (and return 404, not 403, so you don't reveal what exists)</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/orders/{id:int}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ClaimsPrincipal</span> user, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span> db, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> customerId = user.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetCustomerId</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> order = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Orders</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsNoTracking</span>()\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> == id &amp;&amp; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CustomerId</span> == customerId)\n        .<span class=\"tk-ty\">Select</span>(o =&gt; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToDto</span>()).<span class=\"tk-ty\">FirstOrDefaultAsync</span>(ct);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> order <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span> ? <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>() : <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Ok</span>(order);\n}).<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequireAuthorization</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice the first version isn&#39;t missing authorization entirely — <code>RequireAuthorization()</code> is right there. What it&#39;s missing is authorization <em>scoped to this specific resource</em>, which is exactly the resource-based authorization distinction <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/auth/resource-based-authorization-when-the-rule-depends-on-which-\">Auth</a> drew out: &quot;is logged in&quot; and &quot;may see order 1042&quot; are different questions, and the broken version only ever asked the first.</p>\n<p>Also: <strong>function-level</strong> checks (<code>/admin/*</code> must require an admin policy, not just &quot;authenticated&quot;); <strong>multi-tenant isolation</strong> (global query filters by TenantId plus database row-level security as defence in depth; see <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-advanced/global-query-filters-soft-delete-and-multi-tenancy\">EF Core advanced</a>); don&#39;t rely on hidden UI or unguessable IDs (GUIDs aren&#39;t access control); and test authorization explicitly in integration tests (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/integration-testing/fake-authentication\">Integration testing</a>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"injection\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/web-security/injection\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Injection <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// ✘ SQL injection: input = &quot;x' OR '1'='1&quot;  or  &quot;'; DROP TABLE Orders; --&quot;</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> sql = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Email = '&quot;</span> + email + <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;'&quot;</span>;\ndb.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Users</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSqlRaw</span>(sql);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// ✔ Parameterised (all of these send values as parameters)</span>\ndb.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Users</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Where</span>(u =&gt; u.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Email</span> == email);                                   <span class=\"tk-com\">// LINQ</span>\ndb.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Users</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromSql</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Email = {email}&quot;</span>);           <span class=\"tk-com\">// FormattableString → parameter</span>\nconn.<span class=\"tk-ty\">QueryAsync</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">User</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Email = @email&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { email });   <span class=\"tk-com\">// Dapper</span>\ncmd.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parameters</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;@email&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">SqlDbType</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NVarChar</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">256</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span> = email;       <span class=\"tk-com\">// ADO.NET</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Every one of the four safe lines above was already covered in detail when it was first introduced — <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ado-dapper/the-layer-every-other-data-access-tool-is-secretly-built-on\">ADO.NET &amp; Dapper</a> made the same point about parameterisation, and this chapter is really collecting that advice under its actual name: injection defence.</p>\n<p>Dynamic identifiers (sort column, table name) <strong>can&#39;t be parameters</strong>: <strong>allow-list</strong> them (<code>sort switch { &quot;price&quot; =&gt; …, _ =&gt; … }</code>), exactly the whitelisting approach <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/api-design/pagination-filtering-and-sorting-never-return-an-unbounded-l\">API design</a> used for sortable fields. Other injection forms: <strong>OS command injection</strong> (<code>Process.Start</code> with user input: avoid it, or pass arguments with <code>ArgumentList</code>, never through a shell), <strong>LDAP/XPath</strong>, <strong>NoSQL</strong> (build queries with SDK APIs, not string concatenation), <strong>log injection/forging</strong> (structured logging encodes values; never log raw multi-line user input as the message), and <strong>header injection</strong> (CRLF in redirects; ASP.NET Core validates headers).</p>\n<h2 id=\"xss-csrf-and-clickjacking\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/web-security/xss-csrf-and-clickjacking\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>XSS, CSRF and clickjacking <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Cross-Site Scripting (XSS):</strong> attacker-supplied script runs in another user&#39;s browser.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Razor encodes output by default</strong> (<code>@Model.Name</code>); <code>Html.Raw</code> and <code>MarkupString</code> bypass it, so avoid them with user data or sanitise with HtmlSanitizer.</li>\n<li>APIs returning JSON are safe if served with <code>Content-Type: application/json</code>. The risk moves to the <strong>SPA</strong>, which must not inject untrusted HTML (<code>innerHTML</code>, <code>dangerouslySetInnerHTML</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Content-Security-Policy</strong> header restricts script sources: a strong second layer.</li>\n<li>Keep tokens out of <code>localStorage</code> where XSS can steal them (prefer HttpOnly cookies with the BFF pattern) — precisely the XSS-vs-CSRF tradeoff table <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/auth/cookies-vs-bearer-tokens-the-tradeoff-behind-how-do-i-stay-l\">Auth</a> laid out: a cookie&#39;s <code>HttpOnly</code> flag is specifically the mitigation for this exact theft.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF):</strong> a malicious site makes the victim&#39;s browser send an authenticated request, because <strong>cookies are attached automatically</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Affects <strong>cookie-authenticated</strong> apps (MVC/Razor Pages, BFFs), <strong>not</strong> APIs using bearer tokens in the Authorization header — the mirror image of the XSS point above, which is exactly why that same table framed cookies and bearer tokens as trading one risk for the other rather than one simply being safer.</li>\n<li>Defences: <strong>antiforgery tokens</strong> (automatic with Razor form tag helpers and <code>[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]</code>/<code>UseAntiforgery</code>; minimal API form endpoints validate by default in .NET 8+), <strong>SameSite</strong> cookies (<code>Lax</code> or <code>Strict</code>), and checking <code>Origin</code>/<code>Referer</code> for state-changing requests.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Clickjacking:</strong> framing your site invisibly. Use <code>X-Frame-Options: DENY</code> or CSP <code>frame-ancestors &#39;none&#39;</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"authentication-failures-and-password-storage\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/web-security/authentication-failures-and-password-storage\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Authentication failures and password storage <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Prefer an <strong>external identity provider</strong> (Entra ID, Entra External ID, Auth0, Okta) with <strong>MFA</strong> and <strong>Conditional Access</strong>, over building login yourself (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/oauth-oidc-identity\">OAuth &amp; OIDC</a>) — the exact &quot;don&#39;t roll your own token service&quot; warning <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/auth/jwt-bearer-authentication-traced-claim-by-claim\">Auth</a> gave, extended to the whole login experience rather than just token issuance.</li>\n<li>If you store passwords: <strong>never</strong> plain text, reversible encryption, or fast hashes (MD5, SHA-1, SHA-256). Use a <strong>slow, salted, adaptive</strong> algorithm: <strong>PBKDF2</strong> (ASP.NET Core Identity&#39;s <code>PasswordHasher&lt;T&gt;</code>: HMAC-SHA512 with 100,000 iterations since .NET 7), <strong>Argon2id</strong>, <strong>bcrypt</strong> or <strong>scrypt</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Account lockout</strong> and rate limiting on login (credential stuffing); generic error messages (&quot;invalid email or password&quot;), with no user enumeration on login, registration or password reset.</li>\n<li>Check new passwords against breached-password lists (the Have I Been Pwned k-anonymity API); favour length over complexity rules (NIST SP 800-63B).</li>\n<li><strong>Session/token hygiene:</strong> short-lived access tokens, rotating refresh tokens, revocation on password change (security stamp), secure cookie flags (<code>HttpOnly</code>, <code>Secure</code>, <code>SameSite</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> hasher = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">PasswordHasher</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">AppUser</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> hash = hasher.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HashPassword</span>(user, password);                       <span class=\"tk-com\">// includes algorithm version, salt, iterations</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> result = hasher.<span class=\"tk-ty\">VerifyHashedPassword</span>(user, hash, attempt);           <span class=\"tk-com\">// Success / SuccessRehashNeeded / Failed</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice why &quot;slow&quot; is listed as a requirement, not a drawback: a fast hash like SHA-256 lets an attacker who steals a password database try billions of guesses per second on commodity hardware. PBKDF2&#39;s deliberate 100,000 iterations make each individual guess expensive enough that brute-forcing even a weak password back from its hash becomes impractical at scale — the slowness is the entire point.</p>\n<h2 id=\"cryptography-and-the-data-protection-api\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/web-security/cryptography-and-the-data-protection-api\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Cryptography and the Data Protection API <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>Rules: <strong>don&#39;t invent crypto</strong>; use vetted libraries and current algorithms; manage <strong>keys</strong> properly (Key Vault); and pick the right primitive:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>Avoid</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Protect data your app will read back (tokens, cookies, temp data)</td>\n<td><strong>ASP.NET Core Data Protection API</strong> (<code>IDataProtector</code>): authenticated encryption, key ring with rotation</td>\n<td>Rolling your own AES code</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Encrypt data at rest</td>\n<td>Platform encryption (TDE, Storage SSE), Always Encrypted, <code>AesGcm</code> with keys in Key Vault</td>\n<td>ECB mode, hard-coded keys</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Integrity / signatures</td>\n<td>HMAC-SHA256, RSA-PSS/ECDSA (Key Vault keys)</td>\n<td>Plain hashes as &quot;signatures&quot;</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Passwords</td>\n<td>PBKDF2 / Argon2 / bcrypt</td>\n<td>SHA-256</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Random tokens, API keys</td>\n<td><code>RandomNumberGenerator.GetBytes(32)</code></td>\n<td><code>System.Random</code>, <code>Guid.NewGuid()</code> for secrets</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Comparing secrets</td>\n<td><code>CryptographicOperations.FixedTimeEquals</code></td>\n<td><code>==</code> (timing attacks)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">InviteTokens</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IDataProtectionProvider</span> provider)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ITimeLimitedDataProtector</span> _p = provider.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateProtector</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Shop.Invites.v1&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToTimeLimitedDataProtector</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Create</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> inviteId) =&gt; _p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Protect</span>(inviteId.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToString</span>(), <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromDays</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">7</span>));\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>? <span class=\"tk-ty\">Read</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> token) { <span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Parse</span>(_p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Unprotect</span>(token)); } <span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">CryptographicException</span>) { <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>; } }\n}\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Multi-instance: persist keys to Blob and protect them with Key Vault (see Identity &amp; secrets)</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h2 id=\"transport-security-and-security-headers\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/web-security/transport-security-and-security-headers\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Transport security and security headers <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Environment</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsDevelopment</span>()) app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseHsts</span>();        <span class=\"tk-com\">// Strict-Transport-Security: browsers refuse plain HTTP</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UseHttpsRedirection</span>();\n\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Use</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (ctx, next) =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> h = ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Response</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span>;\n    h[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;X-Content-Type-Options&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;nosniff&quot;</span>;\n    h[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;X-Frame-Options&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;DENY&quot;</span>;\n    h[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Referrer-Policy&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;strict-origin-when-cross-origin&quot;</span>;\n    h[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Content-Security-Policy&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;default-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'&quot;</span>;   <span class=\"tk-com\">// tailor for HTML apps; APIs can be strict</span>\n    h[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Permissions-Policy&quot;</span>] = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()&quot;</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> next(ctx);\n});\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WebHost</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ConfigureKestrel</span>(k =&gt; k.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddServerHeader</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>);    <span class=\"tk-com\">// don't advertise &quot;Server: Kestrel&quot;</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>TLS 1.2+ only (the platform usually enforces it), managed certificates with automatic renewal, TLS for service-to-service traffic too (or mTLS through a mesh), and <code>Encrypt=True</code> for SQL.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ssrf-and-outbound-calls\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/web-security/ssrf-and-outbound-calls\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>SSRF and outbound calls <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)</strong>: any feature where your <em>server</em> fetches a URL the <em>user</em> supplied (webhooks, &quot;import from URL&quot;, image proxies, PDF renderers) can be tricked into fetching a URL it was never meant to reach. The attacker just needs to supply an internal address instead of a normal one — for example the <strong>cloud metadata endpoint</strong> (<code>169.254.169.254</code>), a special local address cloud VMs expose so code can ask &quot;who am I&quot; and get back managed-identity tokens. If an attacker can make your server request that address on their behalf, they get those tokens too — which is exactly the thing <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets/managed-identities-the-mechanism-that-makes-no-secret-actual\">Identity &amp; secrets</a> relied on to avoid storing secrets in the first place.</p>\n<p>Defences: allow-list domains and schemes; resolve DNS and <strong>block private, link-local and loopback ranges</strong> (and re-check after redirects, or disable automatic redirects); run fetchers in an isolated network segment with egress restrictions; enforce timeouts and size limits; and never return raw fetched content or errors to the user.</p>\n<h2 id=\"mass-assignment-over-posting-and-data-exposure\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/web-security/mass-assignment-over-posting-and-data-exposure\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Mass assignment, over-posting and data exposure <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// ✘ Binding the entity: a client can send {&quot;isAdmin&quot;: true, &quot;balance&quot;: 1000000}</span>\napp.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapPut</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/users/{id}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> id, <span class=\"tk-ty\">User</span> input, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ShopDb</span> db) =&gt; { db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Update</span>(input); <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> db.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveChangesAsync</span>(); });\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// ✔ Request DTO with only the allowed fields, mapped explicitly; response DTO with only the safe fields</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">UpdateProfileRequest</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DisplayName</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>? <span class=\"tk-ty\">Bio</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">record</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ProfileResponse</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">DisplayName</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>? <span class=\"tk-ty\">Bio</span>);     <span class=\"tk-com\">// no PasswordHash, no Email of others, no internal flags</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>This is exactly the over-posting trap from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/building-apis/parameter-binding-how-request-data-becomes-a-c-argument\">Building APIs</a>, repeated here because it&#39;s common enough to earn a dedicated OWASP API category of its own. Also avoid <strong>excessive data exposure</strong>: returning full entities and relying on the front end to hide fields. The API response <em>is</em> what an attacker sees — a front end hiding a field in its UI does nothing to stop someone reading the raw network response.</p>\n<h2 id=\"resource-consumption-and-dos-protection\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/web-security/resource-consumption-and-dos-protection\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Resource consumption and DoS protection <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rate limiting</strong> per user/IP/API key, with stricter limits on expensive and auth endpoints (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors/rate-limiting-protecting-the-api-from-its-own-popularity\">Caching, rate limiting &amp; CORS</a>), plus WAF/Front Door/APIM limits at the edge.</li>\n<li><strong>Request limits:</strong> Kestrel <code>MaxRequestBodySize</code>, form/multipart limits, header size and timeouts, <code>[RequestSizeLimit]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Pagination caps</strong> (max page size), query complexity limits (GraphQL depth and cost), timeouts on every outbound call and query.</li>\n<li><strong>Regex DoS (ReDoS):</strong> use <code>RegexOptions.NonBacktracking</code> or timeouts for patterns over user input; <code>[GeneratedRegex]</code> with a match timeout.</li>\n<li><strong>JSON limits:</strong> <code>MaxDepth</code>; avoid deserializing huge payloads into memory.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"secure-file-uploads\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/web-security/secure-file-uploads\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Secure file uploads <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapPost</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/documents&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">IFormFile</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">file</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IBlobStore</span> store, <span class=\"tk-ty\">CancellationToken</span> ct) =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">file</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Length</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-num\">0</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">or</span> &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">10</span> * <span class=\"tk-num\">1024</span> * <span class=\"tk-num\">1024</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BadRequest</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;File must be 1 byte–10 MB&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> ext = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Path</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetExtension</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">file</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FileName</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToLowerInvariant</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (ext <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">not</span> (<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;.pdf&quot;</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">or</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;.png&quot;</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">or</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;.jpg&quot;</span>)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BadRequest</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Unsupported type&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> s = <span class=\"tk-kw\">file</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">OpenReadStream</span>();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!<span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">FileSignatures</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MatchesAsync</span>(s, ext, ct)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BadRequest</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Content doesn't match extension&quot;</span>);   <span class=\"tk-com\">// magic bytes</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> safeName = <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;{Guid.NewGuid():N}{ext}&quot;</span>;                                  <span class=\"tk-com\">// never use the client's file name as a path</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> store.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UploadAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;quarantine/{safeName}&quot;</span>, s, ct);                    <span class=\"tk-com\">// scan (Defender for Storage malware scanning) before use</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Accepted</span>();\n}).<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequireAuthorization</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">DisableAntiforgery</span>();</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice the comment on <code>FileSignatures.MatchesAsync</code>: checking the file <strong>extension</strong> alone is trivially bypassed by renaming a malicious file, which is exactly why this checks the actual byte signature (&quot;magic bytes&quot;) inside the file content too — a <code>.png</code> extension on a file that doesn&#39;t start with PNG&#39;s real magic bytes is a clear sign something&#39;s wrong, regardless of what the client claimed. Store uploads <strong>outside the web root</strong> (Blob Storage), never execute them, serve them with <code>Content-Disposition: attachment</code> from a separate domain if they&#39;re user-visible, and scan them for malware.</p>\n<h2 id=\"supply-chain-and-dependencies\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/web-security/supply-chain-and-dependencies\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Supply chain and dependencies <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>NuGet audit</strong> on restore (warnings on known CVEs, fail CI on high severity), <code>dotnet list package --vulnerable --include-transitive</code>, Dependabot/Renovate PRs.</li>\n<li><strong>Package source mapping</strong> and private feeds (prevents dependency confusion), lock files, <strong>Central Package Management</strong> for consistent versions (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/projects-sdk-nuget/package-hygiene\">Projects &amp; NuGet</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Container images:</strong> minimal or chiseled base images, regular rebuilds, scanning (Trivy, Defender for Containers), signing, SBOMs — the exact security practices <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/docker/image-size-startup-and-security\">Docker</a> detailed.</li>\n<li><strong>CI/CD:</strong> least-privilege workflow tokens, OIDC instead of stored secrets, pin third-party actions by SHA, protected branches and environments, secret scanning with push protection.</li>\n<li><strong>Unsafe APIs:</strong> <code>BinaryFormatter</code> is removed (.NET 9); avoid <code>TypeNameHandling.All</code> in Newtonsoft.Json (deserialization gadget attacks); validate XML with DTD processing disabled (XXE; .NET&#39;s defaults are safe).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"logging-and-monitoring-securely\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/web-security/logging-and-monitoring-securely\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Logging and monitoring securely <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Log <strong>security events</strong> (authentication failures, access denials, privilege changes, rate-limit hits, validation anomalies) with user and correlation IDs, and alert on spikes. <strong>Never log</strong> passwords, tokens, API keys, full card numbers or sensitive personal data; use redaction (<code>Microsoft.Extensions.Compliance.Redaction</code>) — exactly the &quot;what not to log&quot; table from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/errors-logging/what-to-log-and-what-never-to-log\">Errors &amp; logging</a>, now framed as a security control rather than just a logging hygiene tip. Protect log stores with RBAC and retention policies. Use <strong>Microsoft Defender for Cloud</strong> for posture management and threat detection across App Service, SQL, Storage, Key Vault and containers.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the most common web/API vulnerability and how do you prevent it in ASP.NET Core?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Broken access control (IDOR/BOLA): endpoints that trust client-supplied IDs or lack function-level checks. Prevent it by requiring authorization by default (fallback policy), applying policies to every endpoint, using resource-based authorization or scoping every query by the current user or tenant, returning 404 for inaccessible resources, and testing authorization in integration tests.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you prevent SQL injection in .NET?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Never concatenate input into SQL. Use EF Core LINQ, FromSql/ExecuteSql with interpolated FormattableString (converted to parameters), Dapper parameter objects, or ADO.NET parameters. Allow-list dynamic identifiers like sort columns, and run with a least-privilege database identity.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is CSRF and when does it apply?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Cross-Site Request Forgery tricks a logged-in user&#39;s browser into sending a state-changing request to your site with their cookies attached automatically. It applies to cookie-authenticated apps; bearer-token APIs aren&#39;t vulnerable because browsers don&#39;t attach Authorization headers automatically. Defend with antiforgery tokens, SameSite cookies and Origin checks.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How should passwords be stored?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Hashed with a slow, salted, adaptive algorithm (PBKDF2 as in ASP.NET Core Identity&#39;s PasswordHasher, Argon2id, bcrypt or scrypt), with a per-user salt and a high work factor, re-hashing when parameters improve. Never plain text, encrypted, or fast hashes like SHA-256. Better yet, delegate authentication to an identity provider.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the ASP.NET Core Data Protection API?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A built-in cryptographic API for protecting data your app needs to read back (auth cookies, antiforgery tokens, temp data, custom tokens) with authenticated encryption, purpose strings for isolation, and automatic key rotation. In multi-instance deployments, the key ring must be persisted to shared storage and protected (e.g. Blob Storage plus Key Vault).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is SSRF and how do you prevent it?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Server-Side Request Forgery: an attacker makes your server send requests to URLs of their choosing, reaching internal services or cloud metadata endpoints. Prevent it with allow-lists of destinations, blocking private/link-local/metadata IP ranges after DNS resolution (and on redirects), network egress restrictions, timeouts and size limits.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is mass assignment / over-posting?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>When model binding sets properties the client shouldn&#39;t control (IsAdmin, Price) because the request is bound directly to an entity. Prevent it with dedicated request DTOs and explicit mapping, and use response DTOs to avoid excessive data exposure.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Which security headers would you add to an ASP.NET Core app?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Strict-Transport-Security (UseHsts), X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff, X-Frame-Options: DENY or CSP frame-ancestors, Content-Security-Policy, Referrer-Policy and Permissions-Policy, plus removing the Server header and enforcing HTTPS redirection.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you keep dependencies secure?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>NuGet vulnerability auditing in restore and CI, automated update PRs (Dependabot/Renovate), Central Package Management with lock files and package source mapping, regular .NET runtime/SDK patching, minimal container base images that are scanned and rebuilt often, and avoiding dangerous serializers like BinaryFormatter or TypeNameHandling.All.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/web-security/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/security/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ASP.NET Core security overview</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://owasp.org/Top10/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OWASP Top 10</a> · <a href=\"https://owasp.org/API-Security/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OWASP API Security Top 10</a> · <a href=\"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OWASP Cheat Sheet Series</a> (start with the .NET Security cheat sheet)</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/security/anti-request-forgery\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Prevent CSRF attacks in ASP.NET Core</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/security/data-protection/introduction\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Data Protection in ASP.NET Core</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/defender-for-cloud/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Microsoft Defender for Cloud</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/web-security/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (a plain console app, .NET 8/9, only base-class-library crypto and regex APIs — no extra packages). It checks injection, password hashing, constant-time comparison, secure random tokens, file-signature validation and ReDoS mitigation directly. Things that need a live HTTP pipeline or browser to observe (CSRF cookie behavior, CSP/HSTS enforcement, SSRF against a real metadata endpoint) are left to the Further reading links instead of faked here.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Verify this chapter: run with `dotnet run`. No external packages needed</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// (uses only System.Security.Cryptography and System.Text.RegularExpressions</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// from the base class library -- the same primitives the chapter names).</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Security</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cryptography</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Text</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Text</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RegularExpressions</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> name, <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> ok)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!ok) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: {name}&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: {name}&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 1. Injection: string concatenation lets attacker-controlled input change the</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// shape of the query; a parameterized call never merges the value into the</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// command text at all, so it has nothing to &quot;escape out of&quot;.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> email = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;x' OR '1'='1&quot;</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> concatenatedSql = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Email = '&quot;</span> + email + <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;'&quot;</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;concatenation lets the payload's quote characters reach the SQL text (the injection)&quot;</span>,\n    concatenatedSql.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contains</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OR '1'='1&quot;</span>));\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> parameterizedCommandText = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Email = @email&quot;</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> parameters = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Collections</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Generic</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span>&gt; { [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;@email&quot;</span>] = email };\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a parameterized command's TEXT never contains the value at all -- the payload has nowhere to break out to&quot;</span>,\n    !parameterizedCommandText.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contains</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OR&quot;</span>) &amp;&amp; parameters[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;@email&quot;</span>].<span class=\"tk-ty\">Equals</span>(email));\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 2. Password storage: PBKDF2 (Rfc2898DeriveBytes) with different salts</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// produces different hashes for the SAME password, and verification means</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// re-deriving with the STORED salt and comparing, not re-hashing blindly.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>[] <span class=\"tk-ty\">Pbkdf2</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> password, <span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>[] salt, <span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> iterations = <span class=\"tk-num\">100_000</span>) =&gt;\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Rfc2898DeriveBytes</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Pbkdf2</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Encoding</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UTF8</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetBytes</span>(password), salt, iterations, <span class=\"tk-ty\">HashAlgorithmName</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SHA512</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">32</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> salt1 = <span class=\"tk-ty\">RandomNumberGenerator</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetBytes</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">16</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> salt2 = <span class=\"tk-ty\">RandomNumberGenerator</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetBytes</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">16</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> hashA = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Pbkdf2</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Tr0ub4dor&amp;3&quot;</span>, salt1);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> hashB = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Pbkdf2</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Tr0ub4dor&amp;3&quot;</span>, salt2);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;the same password hashed with two different salts produces two different hashes&quot;</span>,\n    !hashA.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsSpan</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">SequenceEqual</span>(hashB));\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> verifyAttempt = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Pbkdf2</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Tr0ub4dor&amp;3&quot;</span>, salt1);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;re-deriving with the ORIGINAL salt reproduces the original hash (this is how verification works)&quot;</span>,\n    hashA.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsSpan</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">SequenceEqual</span>(verifyAttempt));\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> wrongPassword = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Pbkdf2</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;wrong-password&quot;</span>, salt1);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a wrong password with the same salt produces a different hash (login correctly rejected)&quot;</span>,\n    !hashA.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsSpan</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">SequenceEqual</span>(wrongPassword));\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 3. Comparing secrets: CryptographicOperations.FixedTimeEquals exists</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// specifically to avoid the timing side-channel a plain == comparison has.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FixedTimeEquals returns true for identical byte sequences&quot;</span>,\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">CryptographicOperations</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FixedTimeEquals</span>(hashA, verifyAttempt));\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FixedTimeEquals returns false for different byte sequences&quot;</span>,\n    !<span class=\"tk-ty\">CryptographicOperations</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FixedTimeEquals</span>(hashA, hashB));\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 4. Random tokens: RandomNumberGenerator.GetBytes is the recommended source</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// for secrets (unlike System.Random, which is predictable, not CSPRNG).</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> token1 = <span class=\"tk-ty\">RandomNumberGenerator</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetBytes</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">32</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> token2 = <span class=\"tk-ty\">RandomNumberGenerator</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetBytes</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">32</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;two calls to RandomNumberGenerator.GetBytes(32) produce different tokens&quot;</span>,\n    !token1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AsSpan</span>().<span class=\"tk-ty\">SequenceEqual</span>(token2));\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 5. Secure file uploads: checking the file extension alone is not enough --</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// the content's actual &quot;magic bytes&quot; must match. PNG files start with a</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// fixed 8-byte signature; a renamed .txt file won't have it.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">LooksLikePng</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>[] content) =&gt;\n    content.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Length</span> &gt;= <span class=\"tk-num\">8</span> &amp;&amp;\n    content[<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>] == 0x89 &amp;&amp; content[<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>] == 0x50 &amp;&amp; content[<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>] == 0x4E &amp;&amp; content[<span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>] == 0x47 &amp;&amp;\n    content[<span class=\"tk-num\">4</span>] == 0x0D &amp;&amp; content[<span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>] == 0x0A &amp;&amp; content[<span class=\"tk-num\">6</span>] == 0x1A &amp;&amp; content[<span class=\"tk-num\">7</span>] == 0x0A;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>[] realPngBytes = [0x89, 0x50, 0x4E, 0x47, 0x0D, 0x0A, 0x1A, 0x0A, 0x00, 0x00];\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>[] renamedTextFile = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Encoding</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ASCII</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetBytes</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;just plain text pretending to be a .png&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;real PNG bytes pass the magic-byte signature check&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">LooksLikePng</span>(realPngBytes));\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a .txt file renamed to .png fails the signature check even though the extension looks fine&quot;</span>,\n    !<span class=\"tk-ty\">LooksLikePng</span>(renamedTextFile));\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 6. ReDoS: a classic catastrophic-backtracking pattern against a crafted</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// input can take exponential time with a default Regex. Setting a Timeout</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// makes it fail fast (RegexMatchTimeoutException) instead of hanging the</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// thread -- exactly the defence the chapter recommends.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> evilPattern = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Regex</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">@&quot;^(a+)+$&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">RegexOptions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">None</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromMilliseconds</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">200</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> pathologicalInput = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">'a'</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">40</span>) + <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;!&quot;</span>; <span class=\"tk-com\">// one trailing char that forces full backtracking</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> timedOut = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { evilPattern.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsMatch</span>(pathologicalInput); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">RegexMatchTimeoutException</span>) { timedOut = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>; }\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a catastrophic-backtracking regex with a Timeout throws RegexMatchTimeoutException instead of hanging&quot;</span>,\n    timedOut);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> safePattern = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Regex</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">@&quot;^a+$&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">RegexOptions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NonBacktracking</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;the same kind of input matches instantly with RegexOptions.NonBacktracking (no exponential blowup)&quot;</span>,\n    safePattern.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsMatch</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">'a'</span>, <span class=\"tk-num\">40</span>)));\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here (need a live HTTP pipeline, browser or network</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// to observe for real): CSRF cookie behavior, CSP/HSTS header enforcement by</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// a browser, SSRF against cloud metadata endpoints, and ASP.NET Core's</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// resource-based authorization middleware -- see Further reading: ASP.NET</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Core security overview and the OWASP Cheat Sheet Series.</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All checks passed.&quot;</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"security-principles","text":"Security principles","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"owasp-top-10-mapped-to-net","text":"OWASP Top 10 mapped to .NET","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"broken-access-control-and-idor","text":"Broken access control and IDOR","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"injection","text":"Injection","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"xss-csrf-and-clickjacking","text":"XSS, CSRF and clickjacking","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"authentication-failures-and-password-storage","text":"Authentication failures and password storage","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"cryptography-and-the-data-protection-api","text":"Cryptography and the Data Protection API","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"transport-security-and-security-headers","text":"Transport security and security headers","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"ssrf-and-outbound-calls","text":"SSRF and outbound calls","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"mass-assignment-over-posting-and-data-exposure","text":"Mass assignment, over-posting and data exposure","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"resource-consumption-and-dos-protection","text":"Resource consumption and DoS protection","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"secure-file-uploads","text":"Secure file uploads","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"supply-chain-and-dependencies","text":"Supply chain and dependencies","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"logging-and-monitoring-securely","text":"Logging and monitoring securely","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":4547,"minutes":21,"num":"10.1","part":"p10"},"oauth-oidc-identity":{"id":"oauth-oidc-identity","title":"OAuth 2.0, OpenID Connect and Token-Based Identity","summary":"How modern sign-in and API access really work. The problem OAuth solves, roles and tokens, the authorization code flow with PKCE step by step, client credentials, refresh tokens, the device code and on-behalf-of flows, OpenID Connect on top, scopes vs roles, access token validation, JWT vs reference tokens, the BFF pattern for SPAs, identity providers for .NET, and common mistakes.","layer":"cross","where":"Cross-cutting › Security › Identity protocols","fits":"<p>These protocols produce the tokens that ASP.NET Core&#39;s authentication middleware validates (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/auth\">Auth</a>). In Azure, Microsoft Entra ID is the authorization server (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets/protecting-your-own-apis-with-entra-id\">Identity &amp; secrets</a>); for service-to-service calls, managed identities use the same client-credentials ideas. The attack side is in <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/web-security\">Web security</a>.</p>\n","prereqs":["auth","web-security"],"next":["system-design","azure-identity-secrets"],"levels":["i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oauth-oidc-identity/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>OAuth 2.0</strong> lets an app get a limited, revocable token to act on a user&#39;s behalf, without ever seeing their password. <strong>OpenID Connect (OIDC)</strong> adds a standard way to learn <em>who</em> that user is (the ID token) on top of OAuth.</li>\n<li>Use the <strong>authorization code flow with PKCE</strong> for anything with a user; it never puts tokens in the browser URL, and PKCE stops a stolen authorization code from being redeemed by someone who didn&#39;t start the login.</li>\n<li>The <strong>access token</strong> is for the API; the <strong>ID token</strong> is for the client to learn who signed in; the <strong>refresh token</strong> gets new access tokens without asking the user to log in again. Don&#39;t mix up their jobs.</li>\n<li>An API must check the token&#39;s <strong>signature, issuer, audience and expiry</strong> before trusting anything in it — skipping the audience check means a token meant for a different API would be silently accepted.</li>\n<li>Scopes/roles say what the <em>client or app</em> is allowed to do in general; they don&#39;t prove this specific user may see this specific record — you still need resource-based authorization on top.</li>\n<li>For SPAs, the safest pattern is a <strong>Backend-for-Frontend (BFF)</strong>: a server-side component holds the real tokens and gives the browser only an HttpOnly cookie, so XSS can&#39;t steal a bearer token directly.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-problem-oauth-solves\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oauth-oidc-identity/the-problem-oauth-solves\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The problem OAuth solves <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Before OAuth, if a printing app wanted your photos from a photo site, you gave it your <strong>photo-site password</strong>. The app then had full access to everything, forever, and the only way to revoke it was to change your password — a crude, all-or-nothing arrangement with no way to grant anything less than complete access.</p>\n<p><strong>OAuth 2.0</strong> (RFC 6749) is a framework for <strong>delegated authorization</strong>: an app gets a <strong>limited, revocable token</strong> to access specific resources <strong>on behalf of a user</strong> (or on its own behalf), <strong>without ever seeing the user&#39;s password</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>OpenID Connect (OIDC)</strong> adds <strong>authentication</strong> on top of OAuth 2.0: a standard way for the app to learn <strong>who the user is</strong>, via an <strong>ID token</strong>.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout c-gotcha\"><div class=\"callout-title\">OAuth 2.0 is not an authentication protocol</div><p>An OAuth <strong>access token</strong> says &quot;the bearer may call this API with these scopes&quot;. It&#39;s meant for the <strong>API</strong>, not for the client to identify the user. Using access tokens as proof of login leads to vulnerabilities (token substitution). For sign-in, use <strong>OIDC&#39;s ID token</strong>. In practice you always use OIDC + OAuth together.</p>\n</aside>\n<h2 id=\"roles\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oauth-oidc-identity/roles\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Roles <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Role</th>\n<th>Who</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Resource owner</strong></td>\n<td>The user who owns the data</td>\n<td>Ana</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Client</strong></td>\n<td>The application requesting access</td>\n<td>Shop SPA, mobile app, Billing worker</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Authorization server</strong> (identity provider, IdP)</td>\n<td>Authenticates the user, gets consent, <strong>issues tokens</strong></td>\n<td>Microsoft Entra ID, Auth0, Okta, Keycloak, Duende IdentityServer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Resource server</strong></td>\n<td>The API that accepts access tokens</td>\n<td>Shop API (your ASP.NET Core app)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Client types:</strong> <strong>confidential</strong> clients can keep a secret (server-side web apps, background services) and authenticate with a client secret, certificate or federated credential. <strong>Public</strong> clients can&#39;t (SPAs, mobile and desktop apps: anyone can extract a secret from the binary or JavaScript), so they use <strong>PKCE</strong> instead.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tokens\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oauth-oidc-identity/tokens\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Tokens <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Token</th>\n<th>For</th>\n<th>Format</th>\n<th>Lifetime</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Access token</strong></td>\n<td>The <strong>API</strong> (resource server)</td>\n<td>Often a <strong>JWT</strong>; can be opaque</td>\n<td>Short: 5–60 min</td>\n<td>Contains <code>aud</code> (which API), <code>scp</code>/<code>roles</code>, <code>sub</code>, <code>exp</code>. Sent as <code>Authorization: Bearer …</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ID token</strong> (OIDC)</td>\n<td>The <strong>client</strong></td>\n<td>Always a <strong>JWT</strong></td>\n<td>Short</td>\n<td>Who the user is: <code>sub</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>email</code>, <code>nonce</code>, <code>auth_time</code>, <code>amr</code>. <strong>Don&#39;t send it to APIs</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Refresh token</strong></td>\n<td>The <strong>authorization server</strong></td>\n<td>Opaque</td>\n<td>Long: hours to months; often sliding and rotated</td>\n<td>Exchanged for new access tokens without user interaction. Must be stored securely</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Authorization code</td>\n<td>The token endpoint (one-time)</td>\n<td>Opaque</td>\n<td>~1–10 min, single use</td>\n<td>Intermediate artefact in the code flow</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"authorization-code-flow-with-pkce-step-by-step\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oauth-oidc-identity/authorization-code-flow-with-pkce-step-by-step\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Authorization code flow with PKCE, step by step <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>The <strong>recommended flow for any app with a user</strong>: web apps, SPAs (via a BFF ideally), mobile and desktop. <strong>PKCE</strong> (Proof Key for Code Exchange, RFC 7636) is now required for all clients in OAuth 2.1 and current best practice.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code> User/Browser                 Client app                      Authorization server (Entra ID)           API\n     │  1. clicks &quot;Sign in&quot;      │                                       │                               │\n     │ ─────────────────────────▶│ 2. create code_verifier (random),      │                               │\n     │                           │    code_challenge = BASE64URL(SHA256(verifier)), state, nonce          │\n     │ ◀── 3. redirect to /authorize?response_type=code&amp;client_id=…&amp;redirect_uri=…&amp;scope=openid profile   │\n     │        api://shop/orders.read offline_access&amp;code_challenge=…&amp;code_challenge_method=S256&amp;state=…   │\n     │ ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────▶│ 4. user logs in (MFA), consents │\n     │ ◀──────────────────────────── 5. redirect to redirect_uri?code=AUTH_CODE&amp;state=… ──────────────────│\n     │ ─────────────────────────▶│ 6. verify state; POST /token                                          │\n     │                           │    grant_type=authorization_code&amp;code=…&amp;code_verifier=…&amp;redirect_uri=… │\n     │                           │ ─────────────────────────────────────▶│ 7. checks SHA256(verifier) ==   │\n     │                           │                                       │    challenge; issues tokens     │\n     │                           │ ◀── { access_token, id_token, refresh_token, expires_in } ───────────── │\n     │                           │ 8. validate id_token (signature, iss, aud=client_id, nonce, exp)        │\n     │                           │ 9. GET /orders  Authorization: Bearer &lt;access_token&gt; ──────────────────▶│ 10. validate\n     │                           │ ◀────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── │  token, authorize</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Follow steps 2 and 7 together, because that&#39;s where PKCE&#39;s actual protection lives: the client generates a random <code>code_verifier</code> in step 2 and only ever sends its <em>hash</em> (<code>code_challenge</code>) over the browser redirect, which anything intercepting the browser traffic can see. The raw <code>code_verifier</code> itself travels only once, directly from the client to the authorization server over the back channel in step 6 — so even if an attacker steals the authorization code from step 5 (a malicious app on the same device, a logged referrer), they still can&#39;t redeem it without the verifier they never saw. Why each other piece exists:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The code is exchanged on a back channel</strong>, so tokens don&#39;t appear in browser URLs or history (unlike the deprecated <strong>implicit flow</strong>).</li>\n<li><strong><code>state</code></strong> prevents CSRF on the redirect (ties the response to the request).</li>\n<li><strong><code>nonce</code></strong> (OIDC) prevents ID token replay.</li>\n<li><strong>Exact redirect URI matching</strong> at the server prevents tokens being sent to an attacker&#39;s URL.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// ASP.NET Core web app (confidential client) signing users in with OIDC + cookies</span>\nbuilder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAuthentication</span>(o =&gt;\n    {\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DefaultScheme</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">CookieAuthenticationDefaults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticationScheme</span>;\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DefaultChallengeScheme</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">OpenIdConnectDefaults</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AuthenticationScheme</span>;\n    })\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddCookie</span>(o =&gt; { o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cookie</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SameSite</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">SameSiteMode</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Lax</span>; o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cookie</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SecurePolicy</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">CookieSecurePolicy</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Always</span>; })\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddOpenIdConnect</span>(o =&gt;\n    {\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Authority</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://login.microsoftonline.com/&lt;tenant-id&gt;/v2.0&quot;</span>;\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ClientId</span> = builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configuration</span>[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Oidc:ClientId&quot;</span>];\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ClientSecret</span> = builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Configuration</span>[<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Oidc:ClientSecret&quot;</span>];     <span class=\"tk-com\">// or a certificate / federated credential</span>\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ResponseType</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;code&quot;</span>;                                         <span class=\"tk-com\">// authorization code flow</span>\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UsePkce</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;                                                <span class=\"tk-com\">// default true</span>\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">SaveTokens</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;                                             <span class=\"tk-com\">// keep access/refresh tokens in the auth session</span>\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Scope</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;api://shop-api/orders.read&quot;</span>);\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Scope</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Add</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;offline_access&quot;</span>);                                   <span class=\"tk-com\">// ask for a refresh token</span>\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapInboundClaims</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n        o.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TokenValidationParameters</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NameClaimType</span> = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;name&quot;</span>;\n    });\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// For Entra ID specifically: builder.Services.AddMicrosoftIdentityWebAppAuthentication(builder.Configuration)</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//   .EnableTokenAcquisitionToCallDownstreamApi([&quot;api://shop-api/orders.read&quot;]).AddInMemoryTokenCaches();</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice this registers <strong>both</strong> a cookie scheme and an OIDC scheme, with the cookie as the default: once the OIDC flow completes, the app doesn&#39;t repeat the full redirect dance on every request — it issues its own cookie and relies on that, re-engaging OIDC only when the session needs to be re-established.</p>\n<h2 id=\"client-credentials-flow\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oauth-oidc-identity/client-credentials-flow\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Client credentials flow <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Service-to-service</strong>, no user involved: a background worker, a daemon, or one microservice calling another <strong>as itself</strong>.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>HTTP</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>POST /oauth2/v2.<span class=\"tk-num\">0</span>/token\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Content-Type</span>: application/x-www-form-urlencoded\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">grant_type</span>=client_credentials&amp;<span class=\"tk-ty\">client_id</span>=…&amp;<span class=\"tk-ty\">client_secret</span>=…   (or client_assertion with a certificate/federated token)\n&amp;<span class=\"tk-ty\">scope</span>=<span class=\"tk-ty\">api</span>:<span class=\"tk-com\">//inventory-api/.default</span></code></pre></figure>\n<p>The token carries the <strong>client&#39;s identity</strong> and <strong>application permissions</strong> (<code>roles</code> claim, e.g. <code>Inventory.Read</code>), with no <code>scp</code> and no user. In Azure, prefer <strong>managed identity</strong> (no secret at all) or a <strong>certificate/federated credential</strong> over client secrets — the exact same preference order <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets/the-problem-secrets-everywhere\">Identity &amp; secrets</a> laid out generally, here specifically for the OAuth grant type that would otherwise need a secret.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> credential = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ManagedIdentityCredential</span>();     <span class=\"tk-com\">// or ClientSecretCredential/ClientCertificateCredential outside Azure</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> token = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> credential.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetTokenAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TokenRequestContext</span>([<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;api://inventory-api/.default&quot;</span>]), ct);\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Typically wrapped in a DelegatingHandler that caches tokens until shortly before expiry (see HttpClient chapter)</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h2 id=\"refresh-tokens\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oauth-oidc-identity/refresh-tokens\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Refresh tokens <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Access tokens are short-lived because they can&#39;t be revoked once issued (a JWT is valid until <code>exp</code>) — exactly the tradeoff <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/auth/jwt-bearer-authentication-traced-claim-by-claim\">Auth</a> named when it explained why access-token lifetimes stay short. Refresh tokens give a good user experience without long-lived access tokens:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>HTTP</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>POST /token\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">grant_type</span>=refresh_token&amp;<span class=\"tk-ty\">refresh_token</span>=…&amp;<span class=\"tk-ty\">client_id</span>=…&amp;<span class=\"tk-ty\">scope</span>=…\n→ { access_token (new), refresh_token (<span class=\"tk-ty\">new</span>: ROTATED), expires_in }</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Best practices: <strong>rotation</strong> (each use returns a new refresh token and invalidates the old one; reuse of an old one signals theft, so the whole family is revoked), <strong>binding</strong> to the client (sender-constrained tokens: DPoP or mTLS), <strong>revocation</strong> on logout and password change, <strong>secure storage</strong> (server side, or the OS keychain on mobile; <strong>never <code>localStorage</code></strong>), and absolute lifetimes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"other-flows\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oauth-oidc-identity/other-flows\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Other flows <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Flow</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Device authorization grant</strong> (device code)</td>\n<td>Input-constrained devices and CLIs (TV apps, <code>az login --use-device-code</code>)</td>\n<td>The user enters a code on another device</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>On-Behalf-Of</strong> (OBO, token exchange RFC 8693)</td>\n<td>An API calling a downstream API <strong>as the user</strong></td>\n<td>API A exchanges the incoming user token for a token to API B; preserves user context and permissions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Implicit</strong></td>\n<td>✘ <strong>Deprecated</strong>: tokens returned in the URL fragment</td>\n<td>Replaced by code + PKCE</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Resource Owner Password Credentials</strong> (ROPC)</td>\n<td>✘ <strong>Deprecated</strong>: the client collects the user&#39;s password</td>\n<td>Breaks MFA and SSO; only for legacy migration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Client-initiated backchannel (CIBA)</strong></td>\n<td>Decoupled authentication (e.g. a bank app approval)</td>\n<td>Specialised</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Federated credentials / JWT bearer assertion</strong></td>\n<td>Workload identity federation (GitHub Actions, Kubernetes)</td>\n<td>No secrets (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets/workload-identity-federation-ci-cd-and-kubernetes\">Identity &amp; secrets</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"openid-connect-in-more-detail\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oauth-oidc-identity/openid-connect-in-more-detail\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>OpenID Connect in more detail <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>OIDC standardises:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong><code>openid</code> scope</strong> (plus <code>profile</code>, <code>email</code>, <code>address</code>, <code>phone</code>) and <strong>standard claims</strong> (<code>sub</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>given_name</code>, <code>email</code>, <code>email_verified</code>…).</li>\n<li>The <strong>ID token</strong> JWT and its validation rules (<code>iss</code>, <code>aud</code> = client ID, <code>exp</code>, <code>iat</code>, <code>nonce</code>, signature).</li>\n<li><strong>Discovery</strong>: <code>{authority}/.well-known/openid-configuration</code> publishes the endpoints (<code>authorization_endpoint</code>, <code>token_endpoint</code>, <code>userinfo_endpoint</code>, <code>end_session_endpoint</code>) and <strong><code>jwks_uri</code></strong> (the public signing keys). This is how ASP.NET Core configures itself from just <code>Authority</code> — the exact discovery mechanism <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/auth/jwt-bearer-authentication-traced-claim-by-claim\">Auth</a> traced as step 2 of JWT validation.</li>\n<li><strong>UserInfo endpoint</strong> for additional claims; <strong>session management and logout</strong> (RP-initiated logout, front- and back-channel logout).</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>JSON</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// ID token payload (decoded)</span>\n{\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;iss&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://login.microsoftonline.com/9188…/v2.0&quot;</span>,\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;aud&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;3f2a…-client-id&quot;</span>,\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;sub&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAIkzqFVrSaSaFHy782bbtaQ&quot;</span>,\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;oid&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;00000000-0000-0000-66f3-3332eca7ea81&quot;</span>,\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;name&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Ana Silva&quot;</span>,\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;preferred_username&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;ana@contoso.com&quot;</span>,\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;nonce&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;7362CAEA-9CA5-4B43-9BA3-34D7C303EBA7&quot;</span>,\n  <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;iat&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">1761570000</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;exp&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">1761573600</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;auth_time&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-num\">1761569990</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;amr&quot;</span>: [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;pwd&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;mfa&quot;</span>]\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<h2 id=\"scopes-roles-and-claims-for-apis\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oauth-oidc-identity/scopes-roles-and-claims-for-apis\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Scopes, roles and claims for APIs <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n<th>Claim</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Scope</strong> (delegated permission)</td>\n<td>What the <strong>client</strong> may do <strong>on the user&#39;s behalf</strong>; the user consented</td>\n<td><code>scp</code> (Entra) / <code>scope</code></td>\n<td><code>orders.read</code>, <code>orders.write</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>App role</strong></td>\n<td>A role assigned to a user/group <strong>or an application</strong></td>\n<td><code>roles</code></td>\n<td><code>Orders.Admin</code> (user), <code>Inventory.Read</code> (app)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Audience</strong></td>\n<td>Which API the token is for</td>\n<td><code>aud</code></td>\n<td><code>api://shop-api</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The <strong>effective permission</strong> for delegated access is the <strong>intersection</strong> of what the app was granted (scopes) and what the user is allowed to do (roles or your own data rules). An API must check <strong>both</strong> the token&#39;s scopes or roles <strong>and</strong> resource-level authorization (&quot;is this the user&#39;s order?&quot;). Scopes alone don&#39;t prevent IDOR — a token scoped to <code>orders.read</code> proves the client is allowed to read <em>some</em> orders, not that this particular user is allowed to read <em>this specific</em> order, exactly the gap <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/web-security/broken-access-control-and-idor\">Web security</a> warned against closing with scope checks alone.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>builder.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Services</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddAuthorizationBuilder</span>()\n    .<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddPolicy</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;orders:read&quot;</span>, p =&gt; p.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RequireAssertion</span>(ctx =&gt;\n        ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">User</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FindFirst</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;scp&quot;</span>)?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Split</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">' '</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contains</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;orders.read&quot;</span>) == <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>   <span class=\"tk-com\">// delegated: user via client</span>\n        || ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">User</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsInRole</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Orders.Reader&quot;</span>)));                                     <span class=\"tk-com\">// application: daemon</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<h2 id=\"validating-access-tokens-in-an-api\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oauth-oidc-identity/validating-access-tokens-in-an-api\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Validating access tokens in an API <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>An API receiving a JWT access token must check:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Signature</strong>: with the issuer&#39;s public key from <code>jwks_uri</code> (cached; rotated keys found by <code>kid</code>). Reject <code>alg: none</code> and unexpected algorithms.</li>\n<li><strong>Issuer</strong> (<code>iss</code>): the expected tenant or authority.</li>\n<li><strong>Audience</strong> (<code>aud</code>): <strong>this API</strong>. Without this check, a token issued for another API in the same tenant would be accepted (the &quot;confused deputy&quot;).</li>\n<li><strong>Lifetime</strong>: <code>exp</code>, <code>nbf</code> (with small clock skew).</li>\n<li><strong>Scopes/roles</strong> for the operation, then <strong>resource authorization</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>ASP.NET Core&#39;s JwtBearer handler does 1–4 from <code>Authority</code> + <code>Audience</code> (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/auth/jwt-bearer-authentication-traced-claim-by-claim\">Auth</a>) — this chapter&#39;s list is exactly that same five-step validation, now explicitly in the context of the token lifecycle that produced the JWT in the first place.</p>\n<h3 id=\"jwt-vs-reference-opaque-tokens\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oauth-oidc-identity/jwt-vs-reference-opaque-tokens\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>JWT vs reference (opaque) tokens</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>JWT (self-contained)</th>\n<th>Reference token (opaque)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Validation</td>\n<td>Locally, with public keys: fast, no network call</td>\n<td><strong>Introspection</strong> call to the authorization server (RFC 7662), usually cached</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Revocation</td>\n<td>Hard: valid until expiry (keep it short)</td>\n<td>Immediate: the server just forgets it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Size and privacy</td>\n<td>Larger; claims are readable by anyone holding it</td>\n<td>Small; reveals nothing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Use</td>\n<td>Most APIs</td>\n<td>High-security or revocation-critical APIs; tokens leaving your boundary</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"spas-and-the-bff-pattern\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oauth-oidc-identity/spas-and-the-bff-pattern\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>SPAs and the BFF pattern <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p>SPAs are <strong>public clients running in a hostile environment</strong> (any XSS can read tokens in JS memory or <code>localStorage</code>). Options:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Approach</th>\n<th>How</th>\n<th>Verdict</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Tokens in the browser (code + PKCE in JS, e.g. MSAL.js)</td>\n<td>SPA gets tokens and calls APIs with bearer headers</td>\n<td>Works, widely used; <strong>XSS = token theft</strong>; use short lifetimes and refresh-token rotation; avoid <code>localStorage</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Backend-for-Frontend (BFF)</strong></td>\n<td>A thin server-side ASP.NET Core app (often with YARP) performs OIDC as a <strong>confidential client</strong>, keeps tokens <strong>server-side</strong>, and gives the browser only an <strong>HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite</strong> session cookie; it proxies API calls and attaches access tokens</td>\n<td><strong>Recommended</strong> by the IETF &quot;OAuth 2.0 for Browser-Based Apps&quot; guidance; needs CSRF protection (SameSite + antiforgery/custom header)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Browser (SPA) ──cookie──▶ BFF (ASP.NET Core + YARP, holds tokens) ──Bearer token──▶ Shop API\n                           └─ OIDC code flow + PKCE with Entra ID (confidential client)</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Recognise this as the same architectural recommendation from both <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/auth/cookies-vs-bearer-tokens-the-tradeoff-behind-how-do-i-stay-l\">Auth</a> and <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/microservices/api-gateway-bff-and-service-mesh\">Microservices</a>, arrived at from three different angles — token security, cookie ergonomics, and client-specialised gateways — all converging on the same shape.</p>\n<p>Libraries: <strong>Duende.BFF</strong>, or ASP.NET Core with <code>AddOpenIdConnect</code> + YARP + token management (Duende.AccessTokenManagement).</p>\n<h2 id=\"identity-providers-for-net\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oauth-oidc-identity/identity-providers-for-net\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Identity providers for .NET <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Fits</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Microsoft Entra ID</strong></td>\n<td>Cloud IdP (workforce)</td>\n<td>Employees, B2B, Azure-integrated apps; Microsoft.Identity.Web + MSAL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Microsoft Entra External ID</strong></td>\n<td>Cloud CIAM</td>\n<td>Customer sign-up/sign-in (successor of Azure AD B2C)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Auth0 (Okta), Okta</strong></td>\n<td>Cloud IdP/CIAM</td>\n<td>Multi-cloud, rich features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Keycloak</strong></td>\n<td>Open-source, self-hosted</td>\n<td>On-premises, full control</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Duende IdentityServer</strong></td>\n<td>.NET framework to <strong>build</strong> your own OIDC server (commercial licence for most companies)</td>\n<td>Custom requirements, on-premises, full control in .NET</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>OpenIddict</strong></td>\n<td>Open-source .NET OIDC server framework</td>\n<td>Self-hosted authorization server in ASP.NET Core</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>ASP.NET Core Identity</strong></td>\n<td>User store/membership (not an OIDC server)</td>\n<td>Simple first-party login; combine with Duende or OpenIddict to become an IdP</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Rule: <strong>don&#39;t build your own authorization server unless you must.</strong> Identity is security-critical, specification-heavy and constantly attacked; use a managed provider when you can.</p>\n<h2 id=\"common-mistakes\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oauth-oidc-identity/common-mistakes\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Common mistakes <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Mistake</th>\n<th>Consequence</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Not validating <code>aud</code></td>\n<td>Tokens for other APIs accepted</td>\n<td>Always set <code>Audience</code> / <code>ValidAudiences</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Accepting ID tokens at APIs</td>\n<td>Wrong token type; no scopes</td>\n<td>APIs accept access tokens only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Long-lived access tokens</td>\n<td>Stolen tokens usable for days</td>\n<td>5–60 minute lifetimes plus refresh tokens</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tokens in <code>localStorage</code></td>\n<td>XSS steals them</td>\n<td>BFF with cookies, or in-memory with short lifetimes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Implicit flow / ROPC</td>\n<td>Token leakage; password exposure</td>\n<td>Code + PKCE; client credentials for services</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wildcard or loose redirect URIs</td>\n<td>Codes or tokens sent to attackers</td>\n<td>Exact, registered HTTPS redirect URIs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Client secrets in SPAs/mobile apps</td>\n<td>Secret extracted</td>\n<td>Public client + PKCE</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Checking only scopes, not data ownership</td>\n<td>IDOR</td>\n<td>Resource-based authorization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rolling your own JWT issuance with HS256 shared keys</td>\n<td>Any service with the key can mint tokens</td>\n<td>Asymmetric keys at a real IdP</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sensitive data in JWT claims</td>\n<td>Readable by anyone holding the token</td>\n<td>Keep claims minimal; look up details server-side</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What&#39;s the difference between OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>OAuth 2.0 is a framework for delegated authorization: clients obtain access tokens to call APIs with limited scopes on behalf of a user or themselves. OpenID Connect is an identity layer on top that adds authentication: an ID token describing the user, standard claims and scopes (openid, profile, email), the UserInfo endpoint, discovery and logout.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Explain the authorization code flow with PKCE.</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The client creates a random code_verifier and sends its SHA-256 hash (code_challenge) with the /authorize redirect. The user authenticates at the IdP, which redirects back with a one-time authorization code. The client exchanges the code plus the original code_verifier at the token endpoint over a back channel; the server checks the hash and returns access, ID and refresh tokens. PKCE prevents intercepted codes from being redeemed.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Which OAuth flow would you use for a service calling another API without a user?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Client credentials: the service authenticates as itself (a certificate, federated credential or managed identity, rather than a secret) and receives an access token with application permissions (roles) for the target API&#39;s audience.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Access token vs ID token vs refresh token?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The access token is for the API: it states audience, scopes/roles and expiry and is sent as a bearer token. The ID token is for the client: it proves who the user is and must not be sent to APIs. The refresh token goes to the authorization server to get new access tokens silently; it&#39;s long-lived, rotated and stored securely.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>Why was the implicit flow deprecated?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>It returned tokens directly in the redirect URL fragment, exposing them to browser history, logs, referrer leaks and malicious scripts, with no way to bind them to the client and no refresh tokens. The authorization code flow with PKCE provides the same for public clients securely.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What must an API validate on an incoming JWT access token?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>The signature with the issuer&#39;s current public keys (from jwks_uri, by kid), the issuer, the audience (it must be this API), expiry and not-before (with small clock skew), and then the required scopes or roles, followed by resource-level authorization.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How should a SPA handle tokens securely?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Preferably via the Backend-for-Frontend pattern: a server-side component performs the OIDC code flow as a confidential client, stores tokens server-side, issues an HttpOnly Secure SameSite cookie to the browser, and proxies API calls with access tokens attached. If tokens must live in the browser, use code + PKCE, keep them in memory with short lifetimes and refresh-token rotation, and never put them in localStorage.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>What is the On-Behalf-Of flow?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>A middle-tier API that received a user&#39;s access token exchanges it at the authorization server for a new access token for a downstream API, so the downstream call carries the user&#39;s identity and delegated permissions (Entra ID OBO, or RFC 8693 token exchange).</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oauth-oidc-identity/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity-platform/v2-protocols\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect protocols on the Microsoft identity platform</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">RFC 6749 (OAuth 2.0)</a> · <a href=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7636\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">RFC 7636 (PKCE)</a> · <a href=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9700\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OAuth 2.0 Security Best Current Practice (RFC 9700)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-core-1_0.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenID Connect Core 1.0</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-oauth-browser-based-apps/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OAuth 2.0 for Browser-Based Applications (IETF draft)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.oauth.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">oauth.com &quot;OAuth 2.0 Simplified&quot;</a> by Aaron Parecki, the best plain-English guide.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/oauth-oidc-identity/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (a plain console app, .NET 8/9, only base-class-library crypto/JSON — no extra packages, no network call to a real identity provider). It verifies the actual protocol mechanics: PKCE&#39;s hash check, a hand-built HS256 JWT&#39;s signature validation (and that tampering breaks it), audience/expiry validation, scope-vs-role authorization, and exact redirect-URI matching. The full login round trip against a real authorization server needs a browser and a real IdP, so that part is left to the Further reading links instead of faked here.</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Verify this chapter: run with `dotnet run`. No external packages needed</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// (plain System.Security.Cryptography + System.Text.Json) and no network</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// call to a real identity provider -- this verifies the PROTOCOL MECHANICS</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// (PKCE hashing, JWT structure/signature, audience/expiry checks) that the</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// chapter describes, not a live login against Entra ID.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Security</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Cryptography</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Text</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">System</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Text</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Json</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> name, <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> ok)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!ok) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: {name}&quot;</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: {name}&quot;</span>);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Base64Url</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>[] bytes) =&gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">Convert</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToBase64String</span>(bytes).<span class=\"tk-ty\">TrimEnd</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">'='</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Replace</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">'+'</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">'-'</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Replace</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">'/'</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">'_'</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>[] <span class=\"tk-ty\">Base64UrlDecode</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> s)\n{\n    s = s.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Replace</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">'-'</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">'+'</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Replace</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">'_'</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">'/'</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">switch</span> (s.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Length</span> % <span class=\"tk-num\">4</span>) { <span class=\"tk-kw\">case</span> <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>: s += <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;==&quot;</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">break</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">case</span> <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>: s += <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;=&quot;</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">break</span>; }\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Convert</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromBase64String</span>(s);\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 1. PKCE: code_challenge = BASE64URL(SHA256(code_verifier)). The server</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// recomputes this from the verifier sent later and must get the exact same</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// value; a different verifier must NOT produce the same challenge.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> codeVerifier = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Base64Url</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">RandomNumberGenerator</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetBytes</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">32</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> codeChallenge = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Base64Url</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">SHA256</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HashData</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Encoding</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ASCII</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetBytes</span>(codeVerifier)));\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> serverRecomputedChallenge = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Base64Url</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">SHA256</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HashData</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Encoding</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ASCII</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetBytes</span>(codeVerifier)));\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;the server can recompute the same code_challenge from the code_verifier it receives later&quot;</span>,\n    serverRecomputedChallenge == codeChallenge);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> wrongVerifier = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Base64Url</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">RandomNumberGenerator</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetBytes</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">32</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> wrongChallenge = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Base64Url</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">SHA256</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">HashData</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Encoding</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ASCII</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetBytes</span>(wrongVerifier)));\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a different code_verifier produces a different code_challenge (a stolen code alone is useless)&quot;</span>,\n    wrongChallenge != codeChallenge);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 2. A minimal HS256 JWT: header.payload.signature, each base64url, signature</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// = HMACSHA256(header + &quot;.&quot; + payload, secret). This is exactly the shape</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// ASP.NET Core's JwtBearer handler parses, just built and checked by hand.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">MakeJwt</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">object</span> payload, <span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>[] secret)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> header = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Base64Url</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Encoding</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UTF8</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetBytes</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;&quot;{&quot;alg&quot;:&quot;HS256&quot;,&quot;typ&quot;:&quot;JWT&quot;}&quot;&quot;&quot;</span>));\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> body = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Base64Url</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Encoding</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UTF8</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetBytes</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">JsonSerializer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Serialize</span>(payload)));\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> signature = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Base64Url</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HMACSHA256</span>(secret).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ComputeHash</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Encoding</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ASCII</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetBytes</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;{header}.{body}&quot;</span>)));\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;{header}.{body}.{signature}&quot;</span>;\n}\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">VerifyJwtSignature</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> jwt, <span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>[] secret)\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> parts = jwt.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Split</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">'.'</span>);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (parts.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Length</span> != <span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> expected = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Base64Url</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HMACSHA256</span>(secret).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ComputeHash</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Encoding</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ASCII</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetBytes</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;{parts[0]}.{parts[1]}&quot;</span>)));\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">CryptographicOperations</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FixedTimeEquals</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Base64UrlDecode</span>(expected), <span class=\"tk-ty\">Base64UrlDecode</span>(parts[<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>]));\n}\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>[] signingKey = <span class=\"tk-ty\">RandomNumberGenerator</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetBytes</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">32</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">long</span> now = <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UtcNow</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToUnixTimeSeconds</span>();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> goodToken = <span class=\"tk-ty\">MakeJwt</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { aud = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;api://shop-api&quot;</span>, exp = now + <span class=\"tk-num\">300</span>, sub = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;user-123&quot;</span> }, signingKey);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a JWT signed with the issuer's key validates against that same key&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">VerifyJwtSignature</span>(goodToken, signingKey));\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">byte</span>[] attackerKey = <span class=\"tk-ty\">RandomNumberGenerator</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetBytes</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">32</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;the same JWT fails validation against a DIFFERENT key (an attacker can't forge one without the real key)&quot;</span>,\n    !<span class=\"tk-ty\">VerifyJwtSignature</span>(goodToken, attackerKey));\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Tamper with the payload (classic &quot;none&quot;/forged-claim attack) -- the</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// signature no longer matches because it was computed over the original body.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> parts = goodToken.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Split</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">'.'</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> tamperedPayload = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Base64Url</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Encoding</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UTF8</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetBytes</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;&quot;&quot;{&quot;aud&quot;:&quot;api://shop-api&quot;,&quot;exp&quot;:9999999999,&quot;sub&quot;:&quot;admin&quot;}&quot;&quot;&quot;</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> tamperedToken = <span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;{parts[0]}.{tamperedPayload}.{parts[2]}&quot;</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;changing the payload after signing invalidates the signature&quot;</span>, !<span class=\"tk-ty\">VerifyJwtSignature</span>(tamperedToken, signingKey));\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 3. Audience and expiry validation -- the two checks the chapter calls out</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// as the ones that stop a &quot;confused deputy&quot; (wrong aud) and a stale token.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidateAudienceAndExpiry</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">JsonElement</span> claims, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> expectedAudience) =&gt;\n    claims.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetProperty</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;aud&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetString</span>() == expectedAudience &amp;&amp;\n    claims.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetProperty</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;exp&quot;</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetInt64</span>() &gt; <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UtcNow</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ToUnixTimeSeconds</span>();\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> originalClaims = <span class=\"tk-ty\">JsonSerializer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Deserialize</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">JsonElement</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">Base64UrlDecode</span>(parts[<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>]));\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a token with the expected audience and a future exp passes validation&quot;</span>,\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidateAudienceAndExpiry</span>(originalClaims, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;api://shop-api&quot;</span>));\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a token for a DIFFERENT audience fails validation (prevents the confused-deputy problem)&quot;</span>,\n    !<span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidateAudienceAndExpiry</span>(originalClaims, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;api://inventory-api&quot;</span>));\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> expiredClaims = <span class=\"tk-ty\">JsonSerializer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Deserialize</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">JsonElement</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">JsonSerializer</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Serialize</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> { aud = <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;api://shop-api&quot;</span>, exp = now - <span class=\"tk-num\">10</span> }));\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;an expired token (exp in the past) fails validation&quot;</span>, !<span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidateAudienceAndExpiry</span>(expiredClaims, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;api://shop-api&quot;</span>));\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 4. Scope/role authorization: the effective permission is the intersection</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// of what the client was granted (scopes) and what the user/app is allowed.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HasOrdersReadAccess</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>? scp, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>[] roles) =&gt;\n    (scp?.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Split</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">' '</span>).<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contains</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;orders.read&quot;</span>) == <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>) || roles.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contains</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Orders.Reader&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a delegated token with the orders.read scope is authorized&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">HasOrdersReadAccess</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;openid orders.read&quot;</span>, []));\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a token missing the scope AND the role is NOT authorized&quot;</span>, !<span class=\"tk-ty\">HasOrdersReadAccess</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;openid profile&quot;</span>, []));\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;an application token with the Orders.Reader role (no scopes) is authorized&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">HasOrdersReadAccess</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>, [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;Orders.Reader&quot;</span>]));\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 5. Redirect URI must match EXACTLY, not just as a prefix -- a common</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// misconfiguration lets an attacker register a URI like the real one plus</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// an extra path segment and redirect tokens/codes there instead.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsRegisteredRedirectUri</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> requested, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>[] registered) =&gt; registered.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Contains</span>(requested);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>[] registeredUris = [<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://shop.example.com/signin-oidc&quot;</span>];\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;the exact registered redirect URI is accepted&quot;</span>,\n    <span class=\"tk-ty\">IsRegisteredRedirectUri</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://shop.example.com/signin-oidc&quot;</span>, registeredUris));\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Check</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a redirect URI that merely STARTS WITH the registered one is rejected (exact match only)&quot;</span>,\n    !<span class=\"tk-ty\">IsRegisteredRedirectUri</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://shop.example.com/signin-oidc.attacker.com&quot;</span>, registeredUris));\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Not runtime-verifiable here (needs a real authorization server, browser</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// redirects and user interaction): the full authorization code + PKCE</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// round trip against Entra ID, token introspection for reference tokens,</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// and BFF cookie/session behavior -- see Further reading: RFC 6749, RFC</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 7636, and the Microsoft identity platform docs.</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All checks passed.&quot;</span>);</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"the-problem-oauth-solves","text":"The problem OAuth solves","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"roles","text":"Roles","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"tokens","text":"Tokens","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"authorization-code-flow-with-pkce-step-by-step","text":"Authorization code flow with PKCE, step by step","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"client-credentials-flow","text":"Client credentials flow","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"refresh-tokens","text":"Refresh tokens","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"other-flows","text":"Other flows","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"openid-connect-in-more-detail","text":"OpenID Connect in more detail","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"scopes-roles-and-claims-for-apis","text":"Scopes, roles and claims for APIs","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"validating-access-tokens-in-an-api","text":"Validating access tokens in an API","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"jwt-vs-reference-opaque-tokens","text":"JWT vs reference (opaque) tokens","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"spas-and-the-bff-pattern","text":"SPAs and the BFF pattern","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"identity-providers-for-net","text":"Identity providers for .NET","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":4345,"minutes":20,"num":"10.2","part":"p10"},"system-design":{"id":"system-design","title":"System Design for .NET Interviews","summary":"Putting every layer together under interview conditions. A repeatable framework for system design questions, estimating scale, the building-block vocabulary with .NET/Azure choices, three worked designs (URL shortener, e-commerce checkout, notification service), common trade-offs to discuss, and how to talk through code-level design questions.","layer":"arch","where":"Interview preparation › System design","fits":"<p>This chapter uses <strong>all eight layers</strong> at once: it&#39;s where the map from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/big-picture\">The Big Picture</a> becomes a whiteboard. Each decision you make in a design maps to a chapter: storage (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals\">Relational fundamentals</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/nosql-redis\">NoSQL</a>), caching (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors\">Caching</a>), messaging (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events\">Messaging &amp; events</a>), resilience (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/resilience\">Resilience</a>), compute (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-compute\">Azure compute</a>) and observability (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/observability-aspire\">Observability</a>).</p>\n","prereqs":["microservices","messaging-events","caching-ratelimit-cors"],"next":["study-plan-resources"],"levels":["i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/system-design/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Interviewers care about your <strong>process</strong> (clarify → estimate → design → scale → handle failure), not one &quot;correct&quot; answer.</li>\n<li>Do rough capacity estimates (users, RPS, storage) <strong>before</strong> drawing boxes — the numbers decide the architecture, not the other way round.</li>\n<li>Know the standard building blocks (CDN, gateway, cache, SQL vs NoSQL, queue, search) and a concrete .NET/Azure pick for each.</li>\n<li>Practice the three worked designs here (URL shortener, checkout, notifications) — each reuses patterns from earlier chapters: outbox, idempotency, sagas, bulkheads.</li>\n<li>Always name the <strong>trade-off</strong>, not just the choice: &quot;I&#39;d use X because Y; the cost is Z.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"what-interviewers-are-looking-for\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/system-design/what-interviewers-are-looking-for\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>What interviewers are looking for <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>A system design interview isn&#39;t about the &quot;right&quot; answer. It tests whether you can:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Clarify</strong> ambiguous requirements and identify what matters.</li>\n<li><strong>Structure</strong> a solution from high level to detail.</li>\n<li><strong>Choose</strong> components with <strong>reasons</strong> and <strong>trade-offs</strong> (&quot;I&#39;d use X because…, the cost is…&quot;).</li>\n<li><strong>Scale</strong> it: find the bottlenecks and fix them.</li>\n<li><strong>Handle failure</strong>: what breaks, and what happens then.</li>\n<li><strong>Communicate</strong>: think out loud, draw clearly, and adapt to hints.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For .NET backend roles, interviewers also like hearing <strong>concrete .NET and Azure choices</strong> (EF Core vs Dapper, Service Bus vs Event Hubs, Container Apps vs AKS) and <strong>code-level patterns</strong> (outbox, idempotency, caching strategy) — exactly the vocabulary the rest of this book has spent dozens of chapters building up. This chapter is where all of it gets exercised at once, under time pressure, on a whiteboard.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-repeatable-framework-45-minutes\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/system-design/a-repeatable-framework-45-minutes\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>A repeatable framework (45 minutes) <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Time</th>\n<th>What you do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>1. Requirements</strong></td>\n<td>5–8 min</td>\n<td><strong>Functional</strong>: what the system must do (core use cases only). <strong>Non-functional</strong>: scale, latency, availability, consistency, durability, security, cost. <strong>Out of scope</strong>: say what you won&#39;t cover</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>2. Estimates</strong></td>\n<td>3–5 min</td>\n<td>Users, requests/s (peak), read/write ratio, data size per item, storage over 5 years, bandwidth. Keep it rough; it drives decisions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>3. API design</strong></td>\n<td>3–5 min</td>\n<td>The main endpoints or messages: <code>POST /orders</code>, <code>GET /orders/{id}</code>, events</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>4. Data model</strong></td>\n<td>5 min</td>\n<td>Entities, keys, relationships, access patterns → choice of store and partitioning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>5. High-level design</strong></td>\n<td>10 min</td>\n<td>Boxes and arrows: clients → gateway/CDN → services → caches → databases → queues → workers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>6. Deep dives</strong></td>\n<td>10–15 min</td>\n<td>The 2–3 hardest parts: hot paths, consistency, scaling the database, failure handling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>7. Wrap up</strong></td>\n<td>2–3 min</td>\n<td>Bottlenecks, monitoring, what you&#39;d do next with more time</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Step 2 is placed right after requirements and before any architecture on purpose: the estimates <strong>drive</strong> the decisions that follow, they don&#39;t just decorate the answer. A read:write ratio of 100:1 points straight at caching and read replicas; a ratio closer to 1:1 points at write-optimised storage instead. Skip the estimate and jump straight to architecture, and you&#39;re guessing at exactly the number that should have decided it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"back-of-the-envelope-numbers\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/system-design/back-of-the-envelope-numbers\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Back-of-the-envelope numbers <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Quantity</th>\n<th>Rough value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Seconds per day</td>\n<td>~86,400 ≈ <strong>10⁵</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1 million requests/day</td>\n<td>≈ <strong>12 RPS</strong> average (peak usually 2–10× the average)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>100 million requests/day</td>\n<td>≈ 1,200 RPS average</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Memory read / SSD read / same-datacentre round trip / cross-region round trip</td>\n<td><del>100 ns / ~100 µs / **</del>0.5 ms** / <strong>~50–150 ms</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Redis GET</td>\n<td>&lt; 1 ms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Indexed SQL point query</td>\n<td>1–5 ms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>One ASP.NET Core instance (simple JSON, no DB)</td>\n<td>Tens of thousands of RPS; realistic CRUD with a DB: hundreds to a few thousand RPS per instance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Single SQL Server / Azure SQL instance</td>\n<td>Thousands to tens of thousands of simple queries/s, depending on tier and queries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kafka/Event Hubs partition</td>\n<td>MB/s per partition; scale by partitions</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> 10M daily active users, each making 20 reads and 1 write per day → reads ≈ 200M/day ≈ 2,300 RPS average (~10k peak); writes ≈ 10M/day ≈ 115 RPS average. That&#39;s a <strong>read-heavy</strong> system, so caching and read replicas matter more than write sharding.</p>\n<h2 id=\"building-blocks-and-their-net-azure-choices\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/system-design/building-blocks-and-their-net-azure-choices\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Building blocks and their .NET/Azure choices <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Building block</th>\n<th>Purpose</th>\n<th>.NET / Azure options</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>DNS + CDN + WAF</td>\n<td>Global entry, static content, edge caching, attack filtering</td>\n<td>Azure Front Door (+ WAF), Azure CDN</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>API gateway</td>\n<td>Routing, auth, rate limiting, aggregation</td>\n<td>Azure API Management, YARP</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Load balancer</td>\n<td>Distribute across instances</td>\n<td>Built into App Service/Container Apps/AKS ingress, Application Gateway</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stateless services</td>\n<td>Business logic, horizontally scalable</td>\n<td>ASP.NET Core minimal APIs on Container Apps / App Service / AKS</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cache</td>\n<td>Cut read latency and DB load</td>\n<td>HybridCache + Azure Managed Redis, output caching</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Relational DB</td>\n<td>Transactions, relationships, reporting</td>\n<td>Azure SQL (Hyperscale for scale), PostgreSQL Flexible Server; EF Core/Dapper</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>NoSQL</td>\n<td>Massive scale by key, flexible schema, global</td>\n<td>Cosmos DB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Object storage</td>\n<td>Files, media, exports</td>\n<td>Blob Storage (+ SAS, CDN)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Queue / broker</td>\n<td>Asynchronous work, decoupling, load levelling</td>\n<td>Service Bus, Storage Queues; MassTransit/Wolverine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Event stream</td>\n<td>High-volume events, replay, analytics</td>\n<td>Event Hubs (Kafka)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Search</td>\n<td>Full-text, facets, vector search</td>\n<td>Azure AI Search, Elasticsearch</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Background workers</td>\n<td>Process queues, scheduled jobs</td>\n<td>Worker services, Azure Functions, Container Apps jobs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Real-time push</td>\n<td>Live updates</td>\n<td>SignalR / Azure SignalR Service</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Identity</td>\n<td>Users and service auth</td>\n<td>Entra ID / External ID, managed identities</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Observability</td>\n<td>Logs, metrics, traces</td>\n<td>OpenTelemetry + Azure Monitor/App Insights</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"worked-design-1-url-shortener\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/system-design/worked-design-1-url-shortener\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Worked design 1: URL shortener <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Requirements:</strong> create a short link for a long URL; redirect a short link to the long URL; optional custom aliases and expiry; analytics (click counts). Non-functional: very <strong>read-heavy</strong> (100:1), <strong>low redirect latency</strong> (&lt; 50 ms), high availability, links must never collide.</p>\n<p><strong>Estimates:</strong> 100M new links/month ≈ 40 writes/s; 10B redirects/month ≈ 4,000 RPS average (peak ~20k). Storage: ~500 bytes per link × 100M × 12 × 5 years ≈ 3 TB.</p>\n<p><strong>API:</strong></p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>HTTP</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>POST /api/links        { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;url&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://…&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;alias&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;promo26&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;expiresAt&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;…&quot;</span> }  → <span class=\"tk-num\">201</span> { <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;code&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;aZ3k9Q&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;shortUrl&quot;</span>: <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;https://sho.rt/aZ3k9Q&quot;</span> }\nGET  /{code}           → <span class=\"tk-num\">301</span>/<span class=\"tk-num\">302</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Location</span>: <span class=\"tk-ty\">https</span>:<span class=\"tk-com\">//…          (302 if you want to count every click server-side)</span>\nGET  /api/links/{code}/stats</code></pre></figure>\n<p><strong>Key design decisions:</strong> notice that the 100:1 read:write ratio from the requirements is what decides nearly every row below — this is the estimates-drive-decisions principle from the framework section, playing out concretely.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Problem</th>\n<th>Options and choice</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Generating unique short codes</td>\n<td>(a) Hash the URL (MD5 → base62, take 7 chars): collisions need handling. (b) <strong>Counter + base62 encoding</strong>: unique by construction, using a distributed ID source (database sequence ranges handed out in blocks per instance, or Snowflake-style IDs). (c) Random 7-char base62 (62⁷ ≈ 3.5 trillion) with a uniqueness check. <strong>Choose (b) or (c)</strong>; 7 chars is plenty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Storage</td>\n<td>Simple key → value access, huge volume, global: <strong>Cosmos DB</strong> (partition key = code; point reads ~1 RU) or Azure SQL with a clustered index on code. Cosmos for global distribution and low-latency point reads</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Read path latency</td>\n<td><strong>Cache</strong> hot codes in Redis/HybridCache (L1 in-memory per instance + L2 Redis), plus CDN/Front Door caching of redirects for very hot links. Cache hit rates of 90%+ are typical</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Analytics without slowing redirects</td>\n<td>Publish a click event <strong>asynchronously</strong> (Event Hubs), aggregate with a consumer/stream job into counts; don&#39;t write to the DB on every redirect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Expiry</td>\n<td>TTL in Cosmos DB and cache</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Abuse</td>\n<td>Rate-limit creation per user/IP, scan URLs against malware lists, auth for custom aliases</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Client ─▶ Front Door (CDN cache for hot redirects) ─▶ Redirect service (ASP.NET Core minimal API, autoscaled)\n                                                        ├─ L1 memory cache → L2 Redis → Cosmos DB (code → url)\n                                                        └─ publish ClickEvent → Event Hubs → Analytics worker → stats store\nCreate path: API → ID allocator (sequence block) → Cosmos DB → (prime cache)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code>app.<span class=\"tk-ty\">MapGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;/{code:regex(^[a-zA-Z0-9]{{6,10}}$)}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span> code, <span class=\"tk-ty\">HybridCache</span> cache, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ILinkStore</span> store, <span class=\"tk-ty\">IClickPublisher</span> clicks, <span class=\"tk-ty\">HttpContext</span> ctx) =&gt;\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> url = <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> cache.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetOrCreateAsync</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;link:{code}&quot;</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">async</span> ct =&gt; <span class=\"tk-kw\">await</span> store.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetUrlAsync</span>(code, ct),\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">HybridCacheEntryOptions</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Expiration</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromHours</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>), <span class=\"tk-ty\">LocalCacheExpiration</span> = <span class=\"tk-ty\">TimeSpan</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">FromMinutes</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">5</span>) });\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (url <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">null</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NotFound</span>();\n    clicks.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryPublish</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ClickEvent</span>(code, <span class=\"tk-ty\">DateTimeOffset</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">UtcNow</span>, ctx.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Request</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Headers</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Referer</span>));   <span class=\"tk-com\">// non-blocking, best effort (buffered)</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Results</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Redirect</span>(url, permanent: <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>);\n}).<span class=\"tk-ty\">ShortCircuit</span>();      <span class=\"tk-com\">// skip unnecessary middleware for the hottest path</span></code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Notice <code>clicks.TryPublish</code> is fire-and-forget on the hot path — exactly the &quot;don&#39;t write to the DB on every redirect&quot; decision from the table above, made concrete: the redirect itself never waits on the analytics write, which is precisely why the redirect can stay under the 50 ms target even while click data accumulates reliably in the background.</p>\n<h2 id=\"worked-design-2-e-commerce-checkout\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/system-design/worked-design-2-e-commerce-checkout\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Worked design 2: e-commerce checkout <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Requirements:</strong> place an order from a cart: validate stock, take payment, confirm, notify, update inventory. Non-functional: <strong>correctness first</strong> (no overselling, no double charges), checkout p95 &lt; 1 s, resilient to payment provider outages, peak load during sales (10× normal).</p>\n<p><strong>Bounded contexts / services:</strong> Catalog, Cart, <strong>Ordering</strong>, <strong>Inventory</strong>, <strong>Payments</strong>, Notifications, Shipping. Start as a <strong>modular monolith</strong>; split Payments and Inventory out if scaling or team ownership requires it (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/microservices\">Microservices</a>) — exactly the &quot;start coarse, extract when you have a concrete reason&quot; guidance from that chapter.</p>\n<p><strong>Flow (saga with orchestration):</strong></p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>POST /checkout (Idempotency-Key) ─▶ Ordering API\n   1. Validate cart; create Order (status: Pending) + OrderSubmitted in OUTBOX  — one SQL transaction\n   2. Return 202 Accepted + /orders/{id} (or wait briefly for fast success)\nOutbox relay ─▶ Service Bus ─▶ Order saga (state machine):\n   3. ReserveStock → Inventory: conditional update &quot;UPDATE Stock SET Reserved += @q WHERE Sku=@s AND Available &gt;= @q&quot; (no overselling)\n   4. ChargePayment → Payments: call the PSP with an idempotency key = orderId; handle webhooks for async confirmation\n   5a. PaymentSucceeded → ConfirmOrder → OrderConfirmed event → Notifications (email), Shipping\n   5b. PaymentFailed / timeout → ReleaseStock (compensation) → Order Cancelled → notify the user</code></pre></figure>\n<p>This single flow is practically a tour of the book: step 1 is the transactional outbox, step 3 is atomic-conditional-update concurrency control, step 4 is idempotent external side effects, and 5a/5b together are a saga with compensation — four separate chapters&#39; worth of mechanisms, cooperating in one checkout.</p>\n<p><strong>Deep-dive talking points:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No overselling:</strong> the atomic conditional UPDATE (or optimistic concurrency on a stock row), reservations with expiry, and a single writer per SKU partition for extreme flash sales (or a queue in front of hot SKUs).</li>\n<li><strong>No double charge:</strong> an <code>Idempotency-Key</code> on the checkout endpoint, the PSP&#39;s idempotency key, an inbox on payment consumers, and a unique constraint on <code>Payments(OrderId)</code> (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/api-design/idempotency-keys-for-post-making-the-one-unsafe-by-default-m\">API design</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events/idempotent-consumers-and-the-inbox\">Messaging</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Consistency:</strong> each service owns its database; a saga with compensations instead of distributed transactions; the outbox for reliable events.</li>\n<li><strong>Resilience:</strong> PSP calls with timeout, retry (idempotent) and circuit breaker; if the PSP is down, accept the order and charge later (queue), or fail fast with a clear message (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/resilience\">Resilience</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Peak load:</strong> autoscale the API on RPS and the workers on queue length (KEDA); cache catalog and prices; a queue absorbs bursts; a virtual waiting room for extreme sales.</li>\n<li><strong>Data:</strong> Azure SQL for orders, payments and inventory (transactions); Cosmos DB or Redis for carts (high write rate, TTL); Azure AI Search for the catalog.</li>\n<li><strong>Observability:</strong> trace a checkout across services; business metrics (orders/min, payment failure rate); alerts on saga timeouts and DLQ depth.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"worked-design-3-notification-service\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/system-design/worked-design-3-notification-service\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Worked design 3: notification service <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<p><strong>Requirements:</strong> other services send notifications (email, SMS, push, in-app) to users, with templates, user preferences (channel opt-outs, quiet hours), retries and delivery tracking. Scale: 50M notifications/day, with bursts (marketing campaigns).</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>Diagram</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre><code>Producers (Ordering, Billing…) ─▶ Service Bus topic &quot;notification-requested&quot;\n                                      └─▶ Notification service (consumer)\n                                           1. Idempotency check (inbox on NotificationId)\n                                           2. Load user preferences (cached) → filter channels, quiet hours → schedule\n                                           3. Render template (localised) → per-channel queues\n                     ┌──────────────────────┼───────────────────────┐\n              email queue            sms queue                push queue        (separate queues = bulkheads + independent scaling and rate limits)\n                  ▼                      ▼                        ▼\n           Email worker (SendGrid/ACS)  SMS worker (ACS/Twilio)  Push worker (FCM/APNs/Notification Hubs)\n                  └──────── delivery status ──▶ Status store (Cosmos DB / Table) ◀── provider webhooks\nIn-app: write to the user's inbox (Cosmos, partitioned by userId) + SignalR push if online</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Notice the parenthetical on the three channel queues — &quot;bulkheads + independent scaling and rate limits&quot; — naming the exact resilience pattern from <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/resilience/the-resilience-strategies\">Resilience</a>: a slow or rate-limited SMS provider can never back up email delivery, because the two queues share nothing. Talking points: per-channel queues isolate failures and respect provider rate limits (token bucket per provider); retries with backoff and a DLQ per channel; campaigns are throttled through a separate low-priority queue so transactional notifications (password resets) aren&#39;t delayed (<strong>priority queues</strong>); templates are versioned and cached; deduplication by NotificationId; GDPR (preferences, unsubscribe links, data retention); and metrics on send rate, failure rate per provider, and end-to-end latency.</p>\n<h2 id=\"trade-offs-to-discuss-explicitly\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/system-design/trade-offs-to-discuss-explicitly\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Trade-offs to discuss explicitly <span class=\"lvl lvl-a\" title=\"Advanced\">Advanced</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Trade-off</th>\n<th>Discussion</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Consistency vs availability</strong></td>\n<td>Strong consistency for money and stock; eventual consistency for feeds, counts and search indexes. Say which data needs which (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/nosql-redis/cap-pacelc-and-consistency-models-the-trade-off-a-distribute\">NoSQL</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SQL vs NoSQL</strong></td>\n<td>SQL: transactions, joins, flexible queries. NoSQL: scale by key, global distribution, schema flexibility. Decide from the access patterns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sync vs async</strong></td>\n<td>Sync for user-facing reads needing immediate answers; async for side effects and long work (202 + status)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Monolith vs microservices</strong></td>\n<td>Team size, deployment independence and scaling differences justify a split; otherwise a modular monolith</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cache freshness vs load</strong></td>\n<td>TTLs, invalidation by events, stampede protection</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Latency vs cost</strong></td>\n<td>More replicas, regions and premium tiers vs budget; autoscaling and reserved capacity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Build vs buy</strong></td>\n<td>Identity, payments, email and search are usually bought (SaaS/PaaS); the core domain is built</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Push vs pull</strong></td>\n<td>Webhooks/SignalR vs polling; fan-out on write vs on read (social feeds)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Normalisation vs denormalisation</strong></td>\n<td>Write simplicity and integrity vs read performance (CQRS read models)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Each row in this table is a decision this book has already made once, explicitly, in some earlier chapter — naming the trade-off out loud in an interview is really just demonstrating that you understand <em>why</em> that earlier decision was made, not merely that you remember the answer.</p>\n<h2 id=\"code-level-design-questions\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/system-design/code-level-design-questions\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Code-level design questions <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Many .NET interviews include &quot;low-level design&quot;: design a parking lot, a rate limiter, an LRU cache, a library system, or &quot;how would you structure this API?&quot;. Approach:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Clarify requirements and edge cases.</li>\n<li>Identify <strong>entities, value objects and behaviours</strong> (nouns and verbs), and the <strong>invariants</strong>.</li>\n<li>Sketch classes and interfaces, applying <strong>SOLID</strong> and relevant <strong>patterns</strong> (Strategy for pricing rules, State for lifecycles, Observer/events for notifications, Factory for creation) (<a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/design-patterns\">Design patterns</a>).</li>\n<li>Discuss <strong>concurrency</strong> (what&#39;s shared, how it&#39;s protected), <strong>testability</strong> (DI, interfaces at boundaries) and <strong>extensibility</strong> (new types without modification).</li>\n<li>Write a little real C# for the core logic.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// Classic: LRU cache, O(1) get/put with Dictionary + LinkedList, thread-safe with a lock</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">LruCache</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TKey</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TValue</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> capacity) <span class=\"tk-kw\">where</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TKey</span> : <span class=\"tk-kw\">notnull</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TKey</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">LinkedListNode</span>&lt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TKey</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Key</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TValue</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>)&gt;&gt; _map = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(capacity);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">LinkedList</span>&lt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TKey</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Key</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TValue</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>)&gt; _order = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>();       <span class=\"tk-com\">// most recent at the front</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Lock</span> _gate = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>();\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TKey</span> key, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TValue</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">lock</span> (_gate)\n        {\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (_map.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGetValue</span>(key, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> node))\n            {\n                _order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Remove</span>(node); _order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddFirst</span>(node);                     <span class=\"tk-com\">// mark as most recently used</span>\n                <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span> = node.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;\n            }\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">default</span>!; <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n        }\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Put</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TKey</span> key, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TValue</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">lock</span> (_gate)\n        {\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (_map.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGetValue</span>(key, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> existing)) { _order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Remove</span>(existing); _map.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Remove</span>(key); }\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">else</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (_map.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> &gt;= capacity)\n            {\n                <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> lru = _order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Last</span>!;                                         <span class=\"tk-com\">// evict least recently used</span>\n                _order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RemoveLast</span>(); _map.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Remove</span>(lru.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Key</span>);\n            }\n            _map[key] = _order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddFirst</span>((key, <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>));\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you approach a system design interview question?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Clarify functional and non-functional requirements and the scope; do quick capacity estimates; define the main APIs and data model; draw a high-level architecture; deep-dive into the hardest parts (scaling the hot path, data consistency, failure handling); and close with bottlenecks, monitoring and trade-offs, explaining the reasons behind every choice.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How would you prevent overselling in a high-traffic shop?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Make stock decrement atomic and conditional in the database (UPDATE … WHERE Available &gt;= @qty, checking rows affected) or use optimistic concurrency on the stock row; use time-limited reservations released by a compensating action if payment fails; and for extreme hot items, serialise writes per SKU through a queue or partition. Never check-then-update in application memory.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How would you design a rate limiter?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Clarify per-user/IP/API-key limits and the algorithm (token bucket for bursts, sliding window for fairness). Within one instance, use ASP.NET Core&#39;s rate limiter; for a distributed limit, keep counters or buckets in Redis with atomic operations (INCR with expiry, or a Lua script for token bucket), or enforce it at the gateway (APIM/Front Door). Return 429 with Retry-After, and decide fail-open vs fail-closed if Redis is unavailable.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you scale a read-heavy API?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Make the service stateless and scale out; add caching layers (CDN/output cache for public responses, HybridCache/Redis for data); use read replicas or CQRS read models; optimise queries and indexes; paginate and project; and move expensive work to async background processing.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/system-design/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Designing Data-Intensive Applications</em> by Martin Kleppmann: the foundation for every system design discussion.</li>\n<li><em>System Design Interview</em> Vol. 1 &amp; 2 by Alex Xu.</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/browse/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Azure Architecture Center: reference architectures</a> and <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">cloud design patterns</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/dotnet/eShop\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">eShop reference application (.NET Aspire, microservices)</a></li>\n<li>The System Design Primer (GitHub, donnemartin).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/system-design/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>Run with <code>dotnet run</code> (plain console app, .NET 8/9, no extra packages).</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-com\">// 1) Back-of-the-envelope numbers table: &quot;1 million requests/day ~= 12 RPS average&quot;.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> requestsPerDay = <span class=\"tk-num\">1_000_000</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> secondsPerDay = <span class=\"tk-num\">86_400</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> rps = requestsPerDay / secondsPerDay;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!(rps <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">11</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">and</span> &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">12</span>)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected ~11.6 RPS, got {rps}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: 1,000,000 requests/day / 86,400s = {rps:F2} RPS, matching the chapter's '~12 RPS average' estimate.&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 2) The checkout example's capacity math: 10M DAU x 20 reads + 1 write/day.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> dau = <span class=\"tk-num\">10_000_000</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> readsPerUserPerDay = <span class=\"tk-num\">20</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> writesPerUserPerDay = <span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> readsPerDay = dau * readsPerUserPerDay;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> writesPerDay = dau * writesPerUserPerDay;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> readRps = readsPerDay / secondsPerDay;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> writeRps = writesPerDay / secondsPerDay;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!(readRps <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">2200</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">and</span> &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">2400</span>)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected ~2300 read RPS, got {readRps}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!(writeRps <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">110</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">and</span> &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">120</span>)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected ~115 write RPS, got {writeRps}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">double</span> readWriteRatio = readRps / writeRps;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!(readWriteRatio <span class=\"tk-kw\">is</span> &gt; <span class=\"tk-num\">19</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">and</span> &lt; <span class=\"tk-num\">21</span>)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;FAIL: expected ~20:1 read:write ratio, got {readWriteRatio}&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">$&quot;OK: 10M DAU x (20 reads + 1 write)/day =&gt; {readRps:F0} read RPS, {writeRps:F0} write RPS (~{readWriteRatio:F0}:1), confirming the 'read-heavy' conclusion.&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// 3) The LRU cache from &quot;Code-level design questions&quot;, verbatim from the chapter: O(1) get/put, evicts least-recently-used.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> lru = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">LruCache</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">string</span>&gt;(capacity: <span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>);\nlru.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Put</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a&quot;</span>);\nlru.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Put</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;b&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!lru.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> v1) || v1 != <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a&quot;</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected key 1 to be present with value 'a'&quot;</span>);\nlru.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Put</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>, <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;c&quot;</span>);                              <span class=\"tk-com\">// capacity 2 reached; evicts the least-recently-used key.</span>\n                                               <span class=\"tk-com\">// key 1 was just accessed above, so key 2 (untouched since insertion) is evicted, not key 1.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (lru.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">2</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> _)) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected key 2 to have been evicted as least-recently-used&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!lru.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">1</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> v1Again) || v1Again != <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;a&quot;</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected key 1 to still be present&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!lru.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-num\">3</span>, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> v3) || v3 != <span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;c&quot;</span>) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected key 3 to be present with value 'c'&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: LruCache evicted the least-recently-used key (2), not the oldest-inserted key (1), exactly as an LRU cache should.&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Claims NOT verified here (need a real cloud deployment, load test, or multi-service system to observe):</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - Absolute throughput figures for a single ASP.NET Core/SQL Server instance, Redis latency, cross-region RTT — these are rough,</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//   environment-dependent reference points, not guarantees; see Further reading: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/browse/</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// - The worked designs' end-to-end behavior (saga compensation, outbox delivery, per-channel bulkheads) — see the dedicated</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">//   chapters' own Verify sections (Resilience, Messaging &amp; events) for runnable checks of those individual mechanisms.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All runtime-checkable claims verified.&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">LruCache</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TKey</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TValue</span>&gt;(<span class=\"tk-kw\">int</span> capacity) <span class=\"tk-kw\">where</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TKey</span> : <span class=\"tk-kw\">notnull</span>\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Dictionary</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">TKey</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">LinkedListNode</span>&lt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TKey</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Key</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TValue</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>)&gt;&gt; _map = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>(capacity);\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">LinkedList</span>&lt;(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TKey</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Key</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TValue</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>)&gt; _order = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>();       <span class=\"tk-com\">// most recent at the front</span>\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">private</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">readonly</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Lock</span> _gate = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span>();\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGet</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TKey</span> key, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">TValue</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">lock</span> (_gate)\n        {\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (_map.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGetValue</span>(key, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> node))\n            {\n                _order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Remove</span>(node); _order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddFirst</span>(node);                     <span class=\"tk-com\">// mark as most recently used</span>\n                <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span> = node.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>; <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>;\n            }\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">default</span>!; <span class=\"tk-kw\">return</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n        }\n    }\n\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">void</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Put</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">TKey</span> key, <span class=\"tk-ty\">TValue</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>)\n    {\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">lock</span> (_gate)\n        {\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (_map.<span class=\"tk-ty\">TryGetValue</span>(key, <span class=\"tk-kw\">out</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> existing)) { _order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Remove</span>(existing); _map.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Remove</span>(key); }\n            <span class=\"tk-kw\">else</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (_map.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Count</span> &gt;= capacity)\n            {\n                <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> lruNode = _order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Last</span>!;                                     <span class=\"tk-com\">// evict least recently used</span>\n                _order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">RemoveLast</span>(); _map.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Remove</span>(lruNode.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Value</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Key</span>);\n            }\n            _map[key] = _order.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddFirst</span>((key, <span class=\"tk-kw\">value</span>));\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"what-interviewers-are-looking-for","text":"What interviewers are looking for","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"a-repeatable-framework-45-minutes","text":"A repeatable framework (45 minutes)","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"back-of-the-envelope-numbers","text":"Back-of-the-envelope numbers","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"building-blocks-and-their-net-azure-choices","text":"Building blocks and their .NET/Azure choices","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"worked-design-1-url-shortener","text":"Worked design 1: URL shortener","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"worked-design-2-e-commerce-checkout","text":"Worked design 2: e-commerce checkout","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"worked-design-3-notification-service","text":"Worked design 3: notification service","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"trade-offs-to-discuss-explicitly","text":"Trade-offs to discuss explicitly","depth":2,"lvl":"a"},{"id":"code-level-design-questions","text":"Code-level design questions","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":3618,"minutes":16,"num":"11.1","part":"p11"},"study-plan-resources":{"id":"study-plan-resources","title":"Study Plan, Practice Projects and Resources","summary":"Turning this book into interview readiness. An eight-week study plan, how to study (active recall, the question bank, explain-it-back), practice projects that exercise every layer, a self-assessment checklist per level, the books and resources this guide draws on, and how to answer interview questions well.","layer":"cross","where":"Interview preparation › Study plan & resources","fits":"<p>This is the companion to <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/big-picture\">The Big Picture</a>. That chapter gives you the map; this one gives you a route through it, plus the books and courses for going deeper than any single guide can.</p>\n","prereqs":["big-picture"],"next":[],"levels":["b","i","a"],"html":"<h2 id=\"tl-dr\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/study-plan-resources/tl-dr\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>TL;DR</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Rereading feels like learning but mostly tests recognition, not recall — <strong>active recall</strong> (explain it without looking) and <strong>spaced repetition</strong> actually stick.</li>\n<li>Follow the <strong>eight-week plan</strong> roughly in order; the order matters more than the exact pace.</li>\n<li>Build <strong>one practice project</strong> (&quot;Shop&quot;) that exercises every layer — a bug you actually debugged beats a definition recited from memory in an interview.</li>\n<li>Use the <strong>self-assessment checklist</strong> to know what &quot;Beginner/Intermediate/Advanced&quot; actually means for each topic.</li>\n<li>Answer interview questions with a structure: <strong>definition → why it exists → how it works → trade-offs → your own experience.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-study-so-it-sticks\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/study-plan-resources/how-to-study-so-it-sticks\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>How to study (so it sticks) <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Reading isn&#39;t learning, and it&#39;s worth being honest with yourself about why: recognising an explanation when you reread it feels like understanding, but it tests recognition, not recall — the exact skill an interview actually demands. These techniques are backed by learning research and work especially well for interview preparation:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Technique</th>\n<th>How to apply it here</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Active recall</strong></td>\n<td>After each section, close the page and explain it out loud or on paper. Use the <strong>Question bank</strong> in drill mode and <strong>answer before revealing</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Spaced repetition</strong></td>\n<td>Revisit the drill for each part after 1 day, 3 days, 1 week, 2 weeks. Re-drill questions you got wrong more often</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Feynman technique</strong></td>\n<td>Explain a concept as if to a junior developer. Where you hesitate or use jargon you can&#39;t unpack, that&#39;s the gap: go back to the section</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Interleaving</strong></td>\n<td>Mix topics in drills (&quot;All parts&quot; mode) instead of doing one chapter at a time; interviews jump between topics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Build things</strong></td>\n<td>Implement each concept in a small project. Writing middleware or an outbox once beats reading about it five times</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Map placement</strong></td>\n<td>For every new term, say which of the 8 layers it lives in and what it connects to. This is the &quot;light bulb&quot; habit</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"an-eight-week-plan\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/study-plan-resources/an-eight-week-plan\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>An eight-week plan <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>Around 1–2 hours on weekdays, plus a longer weekend session for the project. Adjust to your timeline; the <strong>order</strong> matters more than the pace.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Week</th>\n<th>Read (Beginner + Intermediate sections first)</th>\n<th>Build</th>\n<th>Drill</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>1</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/big-picture\">Big Picture</a>, Part I (platform), <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/type-system\">Type system</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/oop\">OOP</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/generics\">Generics</a></td>\n<td>Console app exploring value vs reference types, boxing, records; look at IL in SharpLab</td>\n<td>Part 0–II</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>2</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/delegates-events\">Delegates</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/collections\">Collections</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/linq\">LINQ</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/exceptions-disposal\">Exceptions</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/async-await\">async/await</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/modern-csharp\">Modern C#</a></td>\n<td>Re-implement <code>Where</code>/<code>Select</code> with <code>yield</code>; write an async fan-out with <code>WhenAll</code> + cancellation</td>\n<td>Part II</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>3</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/aspnet-hosting\">Hosting</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/dependency-injection\">DI</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/configuration-options\">Configuration</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/middleware\">Middleware</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/routing\">Routing</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/building-apis\">Building APIs</a></td>\n<td>Start the <strong>practice API</strong> (below): minimal APIs, DI lifetimes demo, custom middleware, options with validation</td>\n<td>Part III (first half)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>4</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/errors-logging\">Errors &amp; logging</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/auth\">Auth</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/http-client\">HttpClient</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/caching-ratelimit-cors\">Caching/rate limiting/CORS</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/background-work\">Background work</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/api-design\">API design</a></td>\n<td>Add ProblemDetails, JWT auth with policies, a typed HttpClient with resilience, HybridCache, rate limiting, a BackgroundService with Channel</td>\n<td>Part III</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>5</strong></td>\n<td>Part IV: <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/relational-fundamentals\">Relational fundamentals</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-fundamentals\">EF Core</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-querying\">Querying</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/efcore-advanced\">Advanced</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ado-dapper\">Dapper</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/nosql-redis\">NoSQL &amp; Redis</a></td>\n<td>EF Core with migrations; reproduce and fix an N+1; optimistic concurrency; a Dapper read model; Redis cache</td>\n<td>Part IV</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>6</strong></td>\n<td>Part V: <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/design-principles\">Principles</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/design-patterns\">Patterns</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/app-architectures\">Architectures</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cqrs-mediator-result\">CQRS &amp; Result</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/ddd\">DDD</a>, Part VI: <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/unit-testing\">testing</a></td>\n<td>Refactor the API into Clean Architecture or vertical slices; rich domain model; Result pattern; unit tests + integration tests with Testcontainers</td>\n<td>Parts V–VI</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>7</strong></td>\n<td>Part VII: <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/messaging-events\">Messaging</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/resilience\">Resilience</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/observability-aspire\">Observability &amp; Aspire</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/microservices\">Microservices</a>; Part VIII: <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cloud-azure-overview\">Cloud</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/docker\">Docker</a></td>\n<td>Add an Aspire AppHost, a worker service, RabbitMQ/Service Bus emulator + outbox, OpenTelemetry; containerise</td>\n<td>Parts VII–VIII</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>8</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-compute\">Azure compute</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-data-messaging\">Azure data</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/azure-identity-secrets\">Identity &amp; secrets</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/cicd-iac\">CI/CD &amp; IaC</a>, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/kubernetes\">Kubernetes</a>, Part IX, Part X, <a class=\"xref\" href=\"#/system-design\">System design</a></td>\n<td>GitHub Actions pipeline; deploy to Azure Container Apps with managed identity + Key Vault (azd/Bicep); load test with k6</td>\n<td>Everything, mixed. Do two mock system-design sessions</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Then:</strong> go back through every <strong>Advanced</strong> section, and keep drilling daily (15 minutes) until the interview.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-practice-project-one-app-every-layer\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/study-plan-resources/the-practice-project-one-app-every-layer\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>The practice project: one app, every layer <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>Build a small but complete <strong>&quot;Shop&quot; backend</strong>. Each feature exercises specific chapters, and together they give you real stories to tell in interviews (&quot;When I implemented the outbox, I ran into…&quot;) — which matters more than it sounds, because a concrete bug you actually debugged is a far more convincing answer than a correct definition recited from memory.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>Exercises</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>Products &amp; categories CRUD with paging, filtering, sorting</td>\n<td>Minimal APIs, routing groups, validation, EF Core, projections, indexes, API design</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cart (Redis) and checkout</td>\n<td>Redis, HybridCache, idempotency keys, Result pattern, domain model with invariants</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Orders with status lifecycle</td>\n<td>DDD aggregate, State pattern / transitions, optimistic concurrency (ETags)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Auth: customers and admins</td>\n<td>JWT bearer (Entra ID or a local dev IdP), policies, resource-based authorization (own orders only)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>OrderPlaced</code> → email + stock update</td>\n<td>Outbox, Service Bus/RabbitMQ (MassTransit or Wolverine), idempotent consumers, worker service</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Calls to a fake payment API</td>\n<td>Typed HttpClient, resilience handler, WireMock in tests</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nightly cleanup of stale carts</td>\n<td>BackgroundService + PeriodicTimer, or Hangfire</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Observability</td>\n<td>OpenTelemetry traces across API → broker → worker, custom metrics, Aspire dashboard</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tests</td>\n<td>Unit tests for the domain, integration tests with WebApplicationFactory + Testcontainers + Respawn, an architecture test</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Delivery</td>\n<td>Dockerfile (multi-stage, chiseled), GitHub Actions CI, deploy to Azure Container Apps with Bicep/azd, managed identity to Azure SQL + Key Vault</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Performance</td>\n<td>BenchmarkDotNet for one hot method; k6 load test; fix one measured bottleneck</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Microsoft&#39;s <strong>eShop</strong> reference application (github.com/dotnet/eShop) is a good model to compare against once you&#39;ve built your own.</p>\n<h2 id=\"self-assessment-checklist\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/study-plan-resources/self-assessment-checklist\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Self-assessment checklist <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<h3 id=\"beginner-can-you-explain-these-clearly-in-1-2-minutes-each\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/study-plan-resources/beginner-can-you-explain-these-clearly-in-1-2-minutes-each\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Beginner: can you explain these clearly in 1–2 minutes each?</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> .NET vs .NET Framework vs .NET Core; SDK vs runtime; what the CLR, JIT and IL are</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Value vs reference types; struct vs class vs record; boxing</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> The four OOP pillars; interface vs abstract class; access modifiers</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <code>List</code>, <code>Dictionary</code>, <code>HashSet</code> and their Big-O; <code>IEnumerable</code> vs <code>IList</code></li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> LINQ basics and <strong>deferred execution</strong>; <code>IEnumerable</code> vs <code>IQueryable</code></li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Exceptions: <code>throw</code> vs <code>throw ex</code>; <code>using</code> and <code>IDisposable</code></li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> What <code>async</code>/<code>await</code> does and why not to use <code>.Result</code></li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> The two phases of Program.cs; what Kestrel is</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <strong>DI lifetimes</strong> (transient/scoped/singleton) and the captive dependency</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Configuration precedence; the Options pattern</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <strong>Middleware</strong>: what it is, the pipeline, <strong>order</strong> of the standard middleware</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Minimal APIs vs controllers; model binding; validation</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> HTTP methods, status codes, idempotency; 401 vs 403</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Authentication vs authorization; what a JWT contains</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> SQL joins, indexes, transactions (ACID)</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> EF Core: DbContext lifetime, migrations, tracking</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> SOLID with examples</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Unit vs integration tests; mocks vs stubs</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> IaaS/PaaS/serverless; what a container is</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"intermediate-can-you-explain-use-and-compare\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/study-plan-resources/intermediate-can-you-explain-use-and-compare\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Intermediate: can you explain, use and compare?</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Generics, constraints and variance; delegates, closures and events</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> async concurrency (<code>WhenAll</code>), cancellation, <code>ConfigureAwait</code>, <code>ValueTask</code></li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Writing custom middleware; middleware vs filters; endpoint filters</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> ProblemDetails and <code>IExceptionHandler</code>; structured logging</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Policy-based and resource-based authorization; cookies vs JWT</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <code>IHttpClientFactory</code> (why); resilience strategies</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Caching layers and strategies; stampedes; rate-limiting algorithms; CORS preflight</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Hosted services, Channels; running a job once across replicas</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> N+1, projections, <code>AsNoTracking</code>, split queries; clustered vs non-clustered indexes; isolation levels</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> EF Core vs Dapper; optimistic concurrency</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> GoF patterns you&#39;ve used (Strategy, Decorator, Factory, Observer, Chain of Responsibility)</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Clean Architecture vs vertical slices; repository debate; CQRS; Result pattern</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Messaging: queues vs topics, commands vs events, outbox, idempotent consumers</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Observability: logs vs metrics vs traces; OpenTelemetry</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Docker multi-stage builds; App Service vs Container Apps vs Functions vs AKS; managed identity</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> CI/CD pipeline stages; blue/green vs canary; IaC</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"advanced-can-you-go-deep-and-discuss-trade-offs\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/study-plan-resources/advanced-can-you-go-deep-and-discuss-trade-offs\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Advanced: can you go deep and discuss trade-offs?</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> JIT tiering and PGO; Native AOT trade-offs; source generators vs reflection</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> async state machine internals; SynchronizationContext deadlocks; thread-pool starvation diagnosis</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> GC generations, LOH, server GC/DATAS; diagnosing memory leaks (<code>gcroot</code>)</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Threading primitives, deadlocks, memory model; thread-safe singletons</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> <code>Span&lt;T&gt;</code>/<code>Memory&lt;T&gt;</code>, pooling, allocation-free code</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> The full request lifecycle in detail; endpoint routing internals</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> DDD aggregates and boundaries; domain vs integration events</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Sagas (orchestration vs choreography); ordering; exactly-once myths; event sourcing</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Microservices boundaries, data ownership, strangler fig; distributed monolith</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Cosmos DB partitioning; CAP/PACELC; Service Bus vs Event Grid vs Event Hubs</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> OAuth/OIDC flows (code + PKCE, client credentials, OBO), token validation, BFF</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> OWASP risks and .NET mitigations (IDOR, SSRF, mass assignment)</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Performance methodology and tools; percentiles; load testing</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Kubernetes probes, resources, autoscaling; zero-downtime deploys and DB migrations</li>\n<li><input disabled=\"\" type=\"checkbox\"> Full system design of an e-commerce checkout or a URL shortener</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"answering-interview-questions-well\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/study-plan-resources/answering-interview-questions-well\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Answering interview questions well <span class=\"lvl lvl-i\" title=\"Intermediate\">Intermediate</span></h2>\n<p>A reliable structure for technical questions: <strong>definition → why it exists → how it works / example → trade-offs or pitfalls → your experience.</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Q: What&#39;s the difference between scoped and singleton services?</strong>\n<em>Definition:</em> &quot;Singleton means one instance for the app&#39;s lifetime; scoped means one per scope, which in ASP.NET Core is one per HTTP request.&quot; <em>Why:</em> &quot;Lifetimes match how long state should live and whether it can be shared across threads.&quot; <em>Example:</em> &quot;DbContext is scoped because it isn&#39;t thread-safe and tracks per-request changes; an HttpClient handler or a cache is singleton.&quot; <em>Pitfall:</em> &quot;A singleton depending on a scoped service creates a captive dependency, a classic bug; scope validation catches it in Development, and the fix is IServiceScopeFactory.&quot; <em>Experience:</em> &quot;I hit this when a background service injected a DbContext directly…&quot;</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>More tips:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Say &quot;it depends&quot;, then say on what.</strong> Then pick one and justify it.</li>\n<li><strong>Use the right vocabulary</strong> (captive dependency, deferred execution, idempotent, outbox, N+1): it signals depth.</li>\n<li><strong>Admit gaps honestly</strong>, then reason from first principles: &quot;I haven&#39;t used Event Hubs in production, but since it&#39;s a partitioned log like Kafka, I&#39;d expect…&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Draw</strong> for anything with more than two moving parts: pipelines, request flows, architectures.</li>\n<li><strong>Prepare 5–6 stories</strong> (STAR: Situation, Task, Action, Result) about bugs you fixed, performance you improved, designs you chose, and incidents you handled. Tie them to topics in this book.</li>\n<li><strong>Ask clarifying questions</strong> in design and coding exercises before you start.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"books-this-guide-draws-on\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/study-plan-resources/books-this-guide-draws-on\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Books this guide draws on <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>The structure and depth of this guide follow the standard references. Read these for more than any single guide can give you:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Area</th>\n<th>Books</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td>C# &amp; runtime</td>\n<td><em>C# in Depth</em> (Jon Skeet) · <em>CLR via C#</em> (Jeffrey Richter, the classic runtime book, Framework era) · <em>C# 12 in a Nutshell</em> (Joseph Albahari) · <em>Pro .NET Memory Management</em> (Konrad Kokosa) · <em>Writing High-Performance .NET Code</em> (Ben Watson) · <em>Concurrency in C# Cookbook</em> (Stephen Cleary)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ASP.NET Core</td>\n<td><strong><em>ASP.NET Core in Action</em>, 3rd ed. (Andrew Lock): the best single book for Part III</strong> · <em>Pro ASP.NET Core</em> (Adam Freeman)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data</td>\n<td><em>Entity Framework Core in Action</em> (Jon P Smith) · <em>Designing Data-Intensive Applications</em> (Martin Kleppmann) · <em>SQL Performance Explained</em> / use-the-index-luke.com (Markus Winand)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Design &amp; architecture</td>\n<td><em>Clean Architecture</em> and <em>Clean Code</em> (Robert C. Martin) · <em>Design Patterns</em> (GoF) · <em>Head First Design Patterns</em> · <em>Patterns of Enterprise Application Architecture</em> (Fowler) · <em>Dependency Injection Principles, Practices, and Patterns</em> (van Deursen &amp; Seemann) · <em>Refactoring</em> (Fowler)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DDD</td>\n<td><em>Domain-Driven Design</em> (Eric Evans) · <em>Implementing Domain-Driven Design</em> (Vaughn Vernon) · <em>Learning Domain-Driven Design</em> (Vlad Khononov)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Distributed systems</td>\n<td><em>Building Microservices</em> and <em>Monolith to Microservices</em> (Sam Newman) · <em>Enterprise Integration Patterns</em> (Hohpe &amp; Woolf) · <em>Release It!</em> (Michael Nygard)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Testing</td>\n<td><em>Unit Testing Principles, Practices, and Patterns</em> (Vladimir Khorikov) · <em>The Art of Unit Testing</em> (Roy Osherove)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Delivery &amp; operations</td>\n<td><em>Accelerate</em> (Forsgren, Humble, Kim) · <em>Continuous Delivery</em> (Humble &amp; Farley) · <em>Site Reliability Engineering</em> (Google, free online) · <em>Kubernetes Up &amp; Running</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interviews</td>\n<td><em>System Design Interview</em> Vol. 1–2 (Alex Xu)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"free-resources\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/study-plan-resources/free-resources\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Free resources <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr>\n<th>Resource</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead><tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Microsoft Learn</strong> (learn.microsoft.com/dotnet, /aspnet/core, /ef/core, /azure)</td>\n<td>The authoritative docs every chapter links to; also free learning paths</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Free Microsoft eBooks</strong></td>\n<td><em>.NET Microservices: Architecture for Containerized .NET Applications</em>, <em>Architecting Modern Web Applications with ASP.NET Core and Azure</em>, <em>Cloud-native .NET</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>.NET Blog</strong> (devblogs.microsoft.com/dotnet)</td>\n<td>Release notes; Stephen Toub&#39;s performance posts; deep dives</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Andrew Lock&#39;s blog</strong> (andrewlock.net)</td>\n<td>ASP.NET Core internals, explained brilliantly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Milan Jovanović</strong>, <strong>Nick Chapsas</strong>, <strong>Steve &quot;Ardalis&quot; Smith</strong>, <strong>Jimmy Bogard</strong>, <strong>Khalid Abuhakmeh</strong>, <strong>Code Opinion (Derek Comartin)</strong></td>\n<td>Practical .NET architecture articles and videos</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>dotnet/eShop</strong>, <strong>Clean Architecture templates</strong> (Jason Taylor, Ardalis)</td>\n<td>Reference code</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SharpLab.io</strong></td>\n<td>See IL, JIT asm and lowered C# for any snippet</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>OWASP Cheat Sheet Series</strong></td>\n<td>Security how-tos</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Azure Architecture Center</strong></td>\n<td>Reference architectures, cloud design patterns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>AZ-204 learning path</strong></td>\n<td>Structured Azure developer curriculum (certification optional)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Exercism C# track</strong>, <strong>LeetCode</strong> (for algorithm rounds)</td>\n<td>Practice</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"keeping-this-guide-up-to-date\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/study-plan-resources/keeping-this-guide-up-to-date\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Keeping this guide up to date <span class=\"lvl lvl-b\" title=\"Beginner\">Beginner</span></h2>\n<p>.NET ships every November. When a new version arrives, check the <strong>&quot;What&#39;s new in .NET / C# / ASP.NET Core / EF Core&quot;</strong> pages on Microsoft Learn, then update the relevant chapters. The site is generated from Markdown files in <code>content/</code>: edit a chapter, run <code>node tools/build.mjs</code>, and refresh.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How should I prepare for a .NET backend interview in the last week?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Drill the question bank daily in mixed mode, answering out loud before revealing. Re-read the Beginner sections of DI, middleware, async/await, EF Core querying and API design (the most-asked topics). Rehearse the request-lifecycle walkthrough and two system designs out loud. Prepare 5–6 STAR stories from your work or practice project. Review the job description&#39;s specific technologies (e.g. Service Bus, AKS) in their chapters.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary><span class=\"qa-mark\">Q</span><span>How do you answer a question you don&#39;t fully know?</span></summary><div class=\"qa-body\"><p>Be honest about the gap, connect it to what you do know, and reason from first principles out loud, e.g. &quot;I haven&#39;t used X directly, but it&#39;s a partitioned log like Kafka, so I&#39;d expect ordering per partition and consumers tracking offsets.&quot; Interviewers value structured reasoning and honesty over bluffing.</p>\n</div></details>\n<h2 id=\"further-reading\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/study-plan-resources/further-reading\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Further reading</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">.NET documentation hub</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ASP.NET Core documentation</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://dotnet.microsoft.com/en-us/learn/dotnet/architecture-guides\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">.NET architecture guides (free eBooks)</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/azure-developer/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Exam AZ-204: Developing Solutions for Microsoft Azure</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"verify-this-chapter\"><a class=\"anchor\" href=\"#/study-plan-resources/verify-this-chapter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a>Verify this chapter</h2>\n<p>This chapter is mostly study advice and a reading list, which isn&#39;t something code can verify. One concrete, checkable claim it does make — in the sample interview answer about scoped vs singleton services — is that a singleton capturing a scoped dependency (&quot;captive dependency&quot;) is a real bug, and that DI&#39;s scope validation catches it. Run with <code>dotnet new console -o studyplancheck &amp;&amp; cd studyplancheck &amp;&amp; dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection</code>, replace <code>Program.cs</code>, then <code>dotnet run</code>:</p>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption><span>C#</span><span class=\"fig-btns\"><button class=\"run\" type=\"button\">Run <span class=\"run-arrow\">▶</span></button><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></span></figcaption><pre data-src=\"\"><code><span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Microsoft</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Extensions</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">DependencyInjection</span>;\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Reproduces the &quot;captive dependency&quot; bug from the sample interview answer above, with real DI.</span>\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> services = <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceCollection</span>();\nservices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddScoped</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IScopedThing</span>, <span class=\"tk-ty\">ScopedThing</span>&gt;();\nservices.<span class=\"tk-ty\">AddSingleton</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">SingletonCaptor</span>&gt;();\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// With scope validation ON (the default in ASP.NET Core's &quot;Development&quot; environment),</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// resolving a singleton that captures a scoped service throws immediately.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> providerWithValidation = services.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BuildServiceProvider</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceProviderOptions</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidateScopes</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span> });\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">bool</span> threw = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span>;\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">try</span> { providerWithValidation.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">SingletonCaptor</span>&gt;(); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">catch</span> (<span class=\"tk-ty\">InvalidOperationException</span>) { threw = <span class=\"tk-kw\">true</span>; }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (!threw) <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected ValidateScopes=true to catch the captive dependency&quot;</span>);\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: a singleton capturing a scoped service throws InvalidOperationException when ValidateScopes=true, confirming the captive-dependency claim.&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// Without validation, it silently succeeds — but the &quot;scoped&quot; instance is captured forever,</span>\n<span class=\"tk-com\">// defeating the whole point of a per-request lifetime.</span>\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> providerNoValidation = services.<span class=\"tk-ty\">BuildServiceProvider</span>(<span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceProviderOptions</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">ValidateScopes</span> = <span class=\"tk-kw\">false</span> });\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> captor1 = providerNoValidation.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">SingletonCaptor</span>&gt;();\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">using</span> (<span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> scope = providerNoValidation.<span class=\"tk-ty\">CreateScope</span>())\n{\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">var</span> scopedHere = scope.<span class=\"tk-ty\">ServiceProvider</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">GetRequiredService</span>&lt;<span class=\"tk-ty\">IScopedThing</span>&gt;();\n    <span class=\"tk-kw\">if</span> (captor1.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Scoped</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> == scopedHere.<span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span>)\n        <span class=\"tk-kw\">throw</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">new</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Exception</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;FAIL: expected the captured instance to differ from a freshly-scoped instance&quot;</span>);\n}\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;OK: without validation, the singleton silently keeps the first scoped instance forever instead of getting a fresh one per scope — the real-world bug behind the interview answer.&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-ty\">Console</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">WriteLine</span>(<span class=\"tk-str\">&quot;All runtime-checkable claims verified.&quot;</span>);\n\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">interface</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IScopedThing</span> { <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">ScopedThing</span> : <span class=\"tk-ty\">IScopedThing</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Id</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-ty\">Guid</span>.<span class=\"tk-ty\">NewGuid</span>(); }\n<span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">sealed</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">class</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">SingletonCaptor</span>(<span class=\"tk-ty\">IScopedThing</span> <span class=\"tk-kw\">scoped</span>) { <span class=\"tk-kw\">public</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">IScopedThing</span> <span class=\"tk-ty\">Scoped</span> { <span class=\"tk-kw\">get</span>; } = <span class=\"tk-kw\">scoped</span>; }</code></pre><div class=\"run-output\" hidden></div></figure>\n<p>Everything else in this chapter (the study schedule, book recommendations, interview-answer structure) is advice, not a falsifiable technical claim — treat the book/resource list&#39;s descriptions as this author&#39;s opinion, not something to fact-check, and go to the linked sources for anything you want to verify directly.</p>\n","toc":[{"id":"tl-dr","text":"TL;DR","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"how-to-study-so-it-sticks","text":"How to study (so it sticks)","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"an-eight-week-plan","text":"An eight-week plan","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"the-practice-project-one-app-every-layer","text":"The practice project: one app, every layer","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"self-assessment-checklist","text":"Self-assessment checklist","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"beginner-can-you-explain-these-clearly-in-1-2-minutes-each","text":"Beginner: can you explain these clearly in 1–2 minutes each?","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"intermediate-can-you-explain-use-and-compare","text":"Intermediate: can you explain, use and compare?","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"advanced-can-you-go-deep-and-discuss-trade-offs","text":"Advanced: can you go deep and discuss trade-offs?","depth":3,"lvl":null},{"id":"answering-interview-questions-well","text":"Answering interview questions well","depth":2,"lvl":"i"},{"id":"books-this-guide-draws-on","text":"Books this guide draws on","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"free-resources","text":"Free resources","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"keeping-this-guide-up-to-date","text":"Keeping this guide up to date","depth":2,"lvl":"b"},{"id":"further-reading","text":"Further reading","depth":2,"lvl":null},{"id":"verify-this-chapter","text":"Verify this chapter","depth":2,"lvl":null}],"words":2685,"minutes":12,"num":"11.2","part":"p11"}},"qa":[{"q":"What is the difference between .NET, .NET Framework, .NET Core and ASP.NET Core?","a":"<p>.NET Framework is the original Windows-only platform. .NET Core was its cross-platform rewrite; from version 5 it is simply called &quot;.NET&quot; and is the current platform. ASP.NET Core is the web framework that runs on .NET (layer 3), not a platform itself.</p>\n","chapter":"big-picture","section":{"id":"how-to-read-this-book","text":"How to read this book"},"num":"0"},{"q":"Are dependency injection, configuration and logging part of ASP.NET Core?","a":"<p>They are used by ASP.NET Core, but they live in the general-purpose <code>Microsoft.Extensions.*</code> libraries and work in any .NET app: worker services, console apps, Azure Functions. ASP.NET Core&#39;s <code>WebApplication.CreateBuilder</code> simply wires them up for you.</p>\n","chapter":"big-picture","section":{"id":"how-to-read-this-book","text":"How to read this book"},"num":"0"},{"q":"Walk me through what happens when an HTTP request reaches a .NET API.","a":"<p>Infrastructure (load balancer/gateway) forwards it to an instance; Kestrel parses it into an HttpContext and a DI scope is created; it passes through middleware in order (exception handling, HTTPS, routing, CORS, authentication, authorization, rate limiting); the endpoint runs (model binding, validation, filters, handler); the handler calls application/domain code and EF Core asynchronously; the result is serialized; the response flows back out through the middleware; the scope is disposed. Logs, metrics and traces are emitted throughout.</p>\n","chapter":"big-picture","section":{"id":"how-to-read-this-book","text":"How to read this book"},"num":"0"},{"q":"What is the difference between a DI \"service\" and a microservice?","a":"<p>A DI service is any class registered in the dependency injection container in one process. A microservice is a separately deployable application with its own data, communicating over the network. They share a word and nothing else.</p>\n","chapter":"big-picture","section":{"id":"how-to-read-this-book","text":"How to read this book"},"num":"0"},{"q":"What is the difference between the .NET SDK and the .NET runtime?","a":"<p>The runtime contains the CLR and the libraries needed to <em>run</em> a compiled app. The SDK contains the compiler, MSBuild, CLI and templates needed to <em>build</em> apps, and it includes a runtime. Production servers and runtime Docker images need only the runtime (for web apps, the ASP.NET Core runtime); dev machines and CI need the SDK.</p>\n","chapter":"what-is-dotnet","section":{"id":"the-bcl-a-tour-of-what-you-get-for-free","text":"The BCL: a tour of what you get for free"},"num":"1.1"},{"q":"Why was .NET Core created instead of continuing .NET Framework?","a":"<p>Framework was Windows-only, installed machine-wide (so upgrades affected every app), tied to IIS via a monolithic System.Web, and hard to make fast without breaking compatibility. .NET Core was a cross-platform, open-source, modular rewrite with side-by-side installs and much better performance.</p>\n","chapter":"what-is-dotnet","section":{"id":"the-bcl-a-tour-of-what-you-get-for-free","text":"The BCL: a tour of what you get for free"},"num":"1.1"},{"q":"What is .NET Standard and should you still target it?","a":"<p>A specification of APIs that multiple .NET implementations promise to support, so one library could run everywhere during the transition. Target <code>netstandard2.0</code> only if the library must also be used by .NET Framework apps; otherwise target a modern TFM such as <code>net8.0</code> or <code>net10.0</code>.</p>\n","chapter":"what-is-dotnet","section":{"id":"the-bcl-a-tour-of-what-you-get-for-free","text":"The BCL: a tour of what you get for free"},"num":"1.1"},{"q":"What do LTS and STS mean? Which would you choose for production?","a":"<p>LTS (even versions) gets 3 years of support; STS (odd versions) gets 2 years. Most companies pick LTS for production to reduce upgrade frequency, while trying STS releases in non-critical services to get features earlier.</p>\n","chapter":"what-is-dotnet","section":{"id":"the-bcl-a-tour-of-what-you-get-for-free","text":"The BCL: a tour of what you get for free"},"num":"1.1"},{"q":"Framework-dependent vs self-contained deployment?","a":"<p>Framework-dependent publishes only your app and relies on an installed shared runtime: small, centrally patched. Self-contained bundles the runtime: no install needed and version-pinned, but larger, and you must redeploy to pick up runtime security patches.</p>\n","chapter":"what-is-dotnet","section":{"id":"the-bcl-a-tour-of-what-you-get-for-free","text":"The BCL: a tour of what you get for free"},"num":"1.1"},{"q":"Name some things in the BCL you'd use instead of a third-party package.","a":"<p>System.Text.Json for JSON, HttpClient + IHttpClientFactory for HTTP, Channel&lt;T&gt; for producer/consumer queues, PriorityQueue, TimeProvider for testable time, System.Diagnostics.Metrics and Activity for telemetry, built-in rate limiting, and the source-generated Regex.</p>\n","chapter":"what-is-dotnet","section":{"id":"the-bcl-a-tour-of-what-you-get-for-free","text":"The BCL: a tour of what you get for free"},"num":"1.1"},{"q":"Explain how C# code gets executed.","a":"<p>Roslyn compiles C# into IL plus metadata inside an assembly, at build time. At run time the CLR loads the assembly; when a method is first called, the JIT compiles its IL to native machine code — quickly at Tier 0, then re-optimised at Tier 1 if it&#39;s called often (&quot;hot&quot;), using Dynamic PGO data gathered while it ran. The CLR also provides GC, type safety, exceptions and threading throughout the process&#39;s life.</p>\n","chapter":"how-code-runs","section":{"id":"assembly-loading-and-versioning","text":"Assembly loading and versioning"},"num":"1.2"},{"q":"What is IL and why does .NET use an intermediate language?","a":"<p>A CPU-independent, stack-based bytecode produced by the compiler instead of final machine code. It makes assemblies portable across OS/CPU (the JIT compiles it for whatever machine it actually runs on), lets multiple languages interoperate because they all compile down to the same format, enables verification and rich metadata (reflection, GC info), and lets the JIT optimise for the actual hardware and runtime behaviour rather than guessing at build time.</p>\n","chapter":"how-code-runs","section":{"id":"assembly-loading-and-versioning","text":"Assembly loading and versioning"},"num":"1.2"},{"q":"What is tiered compilation and Dynamic PGO?","a":"<p>Tiered compilation JITs methods first with minimal optimisation (Tier 0) for fast startup, then recompiles frequently called ones with full optimisation (Tier 1) in the background. Dynamic PGO instruments Tier 0 code to observe what actually happens — such as which concrete type sits behind an interface call — and Tier 1 uses that recorded data to devirtualise, inline and lay out code better than a generic optimisation pass could.</p>\n","chapter":"how-code-runs","section":{"id":"assembly-loading-and-versioning","text":"Assembly loading and versioning"},"num":"1.2"},{"q":"What is the difference between JIT, ReadyToRun and Native AOT?","a":"<p>JIT compiles at runtime: slowest startup, best peak performance, no restrictions. ReadyToRun ships precompiled code alongside IL for faster startup while keeping the JIT available to re-optimise hot methods later. Native AOT compiles everything to one native binary with no JIT at all: fastest startup and smallest memory, but it requires trimming unused code and avoids runtime reflection and code generation, which is why AOT-friendly libraries use source generators instead.</p>\n","chapter":"how-code-runs","section":{"id":"assembly-loading-and-versioning","text":"Assembly loading and versioning"},"num":"1.2"},{"q":"What is managed vs unmanaged code?","a":"<p>Managed code runs under the CLR&#39;s control, which provides GC, type safety and exception handling automatically. Unmanaged code runs outside that control (native libraries, raw OS handles), so nothing frees its resources for you — you must release them explicitly, typically through IDisposable/SafeHandle.</p>\n","chapter":"how-code-runs","section":{"id":"assembly-loading-and-versioning","text":"Assembly loading and versioning"},"num":"1.2"},{"q":"Why might marking a class sealed improve performance?","a":"<p>The JIT knows no subclass can ever override its virtual methods, so it can devirtualise calls to it (skip the indirect virtual-dispatch lookup and jump straight to the method) and inline them, and type checks (<code>is</code>/casts) become cheaper since there&#39;s only one possible type to check against.</p>\n","chapter":"how-code-runs","section":{"id":"assembly-loading-and-versioning","text":"Assembly loading and versioning"},"num":"1.2"},{"q":"What is the difference between an assembly and a namespace?","a":"<p>An assembly is a compiled deployment unit — one physical .dll file. A namespace is a purely logical naming scope inside your code, with no connection to files or packaging. They&#39;re independent: one assembly can contain many namespaces, and a single namespace can span several different assemblies.</p>\n","chapter":"how-code-runs","section":{"id":"assembly-loading-and-versioning","text":"Assembly loading and versioning"},"num":"1.2"},{"q":"What is the difference between a project, a solution and an assembly?","a":"<p>A project (.csproj) is the build definition for one assembly. An assembly (.dll) is the compiled output. A solution groups projects for the IDE and for building together; it has no effect on the output.</p>\n","chapter":"projects-sdk-nuget","section":{"id":"launchsettings-json-and-environments","text":"launchSettings.json and environments"},"num":"1.3"},{"q":"What is Directory.Build.props for?","a":"<p>MSBuild imports it automatically into every project in that folder tree. Teams use it for shared settings such as target framework, nullable, warnings-as-errors, analyzers and versioning, so each csproj stays small and consistent.</p>\n","chapter":"projects-sdk-nuget","section":{"id":"launchsettings-json-and-environments","text":"launchSettings.json and environments"},"num":"1.3"},{"q":"How do you keep NuGet package versions consistent across many projects?","a":"<p>Central Package Management: <code>Directory.Packages.props</code> with <code>ManagePackageVersionsCentrally</code>, versions declared once with <code>PackageVersion</code>, and csproj files referencing packages without versions. Add transitive pinning and lock files for reproducibility.</p>\n","chapter":"projects-sdk-nuget","section":{"id":"launchsettings-json-and-environments","text":"launchSettings.json and environments"},"num":"1.3"},{"q":"How do you handle a vulnerable transitive dependency?","a":"<p>Find it with <code>dotnet list package --vulnerable --include-transitive</code>. Upgrade the parent package if a fixed version exists; otherwise pin the transitive package to a patched version (directly, or via CPM transitive pinning). Fail CI on NuGet audit warnings to catch it early.</p>\n","chapter":"projects-sdk-nuget","section":{"id":"launchsettings-json-and-environments","text":"launchSettings.json and environments"},"num":"1.3"},{"q":"What's the difference between Debug and Release builds?","a":"<p>Debug disables optimisations and emits full debugging info, so code maps one-to-one to source. Release enables compiler and JIT optimisations (inlining, dead-code removal). Performance measurements and deployments must use Release.</p>\n","chapter":"projects-sdk-nuget","section":{"id":"launchsettings-json-and-environments","text":"launchSettings.json and environments"},"num":"1.3"},{"q":"How do you enforce code style across a team?","a":"<p>An .editorconfig in the repo, analyzers enabled with <code>AnalysisLevel</code> and <code>EnforceCodeStyleInBuild</code>, warnings as errors in CI, <code>dotnet format --verify-no-changes</code> in the pipeline, plus optional SonarQube quality gates.</p>\n","chapter":"projects-sdk-nuget","section":{"id":"launchsettings-json-and-environments","text":"launchSettings.json and environments"},"num":"1.3"},{"q":"What is the difference between a value type and a reference type?","a":"<p>A value type variable contains its data, and assignment copies the data. A reference type variable contains a reference to a heap object, and assignment copies the reference so both variables see the same object. Value types can&#39;t be null (without <code>Nullable&lt;T&gt;</code>) and can&#39;t be inherited; reference types are garbage-collected objects that support inheritance.</p>\n","chapter":"type-system","section":{"id":"memory-layout-and-size","text":"Memory layout and size"},"num":"2.1"},{"q":"Do value types always live on the stack?","a":"<p>No. Reference-type objects always live on the heap, but value types live wherever their container lives: locals on the stack (unless captured by a lambda or used in an async method or iterator), fields inside a class object on the heap, elements inline inside an array on the heap. The defining difference is copy semantics, not location.</p>\n","chapter":"type-system","section":{"id":"memory-layout-and-size","text":"Memory layout and size"},"num":"2.1"},{"q":"When would you use a struct instead of a class?","a":"<p>For a small (≈16 bytes or less), immutable value that logically represents a single value and isn&#39;t boxed often: Money, coordinates, strongly typed IDs. Make it a <code>readonly struct</code> or <code>readonly record struct</code>. Use classes for anything with identity, behaviour, inheritance or larger size.</p>\n","chapter":"type-system","section":{"id":"memory-layout-and-size","text":"Memory layout and size"},"num":"2.1"},{"q":"What is boxing and how do you avoid it?","a":"<p>Converting a value type to <code>object</code> or an interface, which allocates a heap object and copies the value; unboxing copies it back out with a type check. Avoid it with generics (<code>List&lt;int&gt;</code> instead of <code>ArrayList</code>), generic constraints instead of interface parameters, <code>IEquatable&lt;T&gt;</code>, and interpolated strings instead of <code>string.Format</code> with value arguments.</p>\n","chapter":"type-system","section":{"id":"memory-layout-and-size","text":"Memory layout and size"},"num":"2.1"},{"q":"Why is string immutable, and what are the consequences?","a":"<p>Immutability makes strings thread-safe, safe to use as dictionary keys, and allows interning. The consequence is that every modification allocates a new string, so build strings in loops with StringBuilder or string.Join/Concat.</p>\n","chapter":"type-system","section":{"id":"memory-layout-and-size","text":"Memory layout and size"},"num":"2.1"},{"q":"If you override Equals, what else must you do?","a":"<p>Override GetHashCode consistently (equal objects ⇒ equal hash codes), ideally implement <code>IEquatable&lt;T&gt;</code>, and overload <code>==</code>/<code>!=</code> so they agree. Or use a record, which generates all of this.</p>\n","chapter":"type-system","section":{"id":"memory-layout-and-size","text":"Memory layout and size"},"num":"2.1"},{"q":"Are reference types passed by reference in C#?","a":"<p>No. Everything is passed by value by default. For a reference type, the value passed is the reference, so the method can modify the object but reassigning the parameter doesn&#39;t affect the caller. Use <code>ref</code> to pass the variable itself.</p>\n","chapter":"type-system","section":{"id":"memory-layout-and-size","text":"Memory layout and size"},"num":"2.1"},{"q":"ref vs out vs in?","a":"<p><code>ref</code>: the caller passes an initialised variable, and the method can read and write it. <code>out</code>: the method must assign it, and the caller needn&#39;t initialise it (TryParse pattern). <code>in</code>: a read-only reference, used to avoid copying large structs.</p>\n","chapter":"type-system","section":{"id":"memory-layout-and-size","text":"Memory layout and size"},"num":"2.1"},{"q":"Why use decimal instead of double for money?","a":"<p><code>decimal</code> is a base-10 type that represents values like 0.1 exactly, with 28–29 significant digits. <code>double</code> is binary floating point, so many decimal fractions can&#39;t be represented exactly and rounding errors accumulate.</p>\n","chapter":"type-system","section":{"id":"memory-layout-and-size","text":"Memory layout and size"},"num":"2.1"},{"q":"What is the difference between var and dynamic?","a":"<p><code>var</code> is compile-time type inference: the variable is still statically typed. <code>dynamic</code> defers all member resolution to runtime, so errors appear as runtime exceptions and you lose IntelliSense and performance.</p>\n","chapter":"type-system","section":{"id":"memory-layout-and-size","text":"Memory layout and size"},"num":"2.1"},{"q":"Explain the four pillars of OOP with C# examples.","a":"<p>Encapsulation: private state behind methods that keep it valid (<code>AddAddress</code> enforcing a max). Inheritance: <code>CardPayment : PaymentMethod</code> reusing base behaviour. Polymorphism: calling <code>ChargeAsync</code> on a <code>PaymentMethod</code> variable runs the derived override chosen at runtime. Abstraction: callers depend on <code>IPaymentGateway</code> without knowing which provider sits behind it.</p>\n","chapter":"oop","section":{"id":"every-types-hidden-base-class-system-object","text":"Every type's hidden base class: System.Object"},"num":"2.2"},{"q":"Abstract class vs interface: when would you use each?","a":"<p>An interface defines a contract that unrelated types can implement, and a type can implement many. Use interfaces for seams you want to swap or mock. An abstract class is a single base for closely related types that share state or implementation (constructors, fields, protected helpers, template methods).</p>\n","chapter":"oop","section":{"id":"every-types-hidden-base-class-system-object","text":"Every type's hidden base class: System.Object"},"num":"2.2"},{"q":"What is the difference between override and new?","a":"<p><code>override</code> participates in virtual dispatch: the runtime type&#39;s implementation runs no matter what the variable&#39;s static type is. <code>new</code> hides the base member: which one runs depends on the compile-time type of the reference. Hiding is almost always a mistake.</p>\n","chapter":"oop","section":{"id":"every-types-hidden-base-class-system-object","text":"Every type's hidden base class: System.Object"},"num":"2.2"},{"q":"What is the order of initialisation when creating a derived class?","a":"<p>Derived field initialisers, then base field initialisers, then the base constructor body, then the derived constructor body. Static constructors run once before first use of each type.</p>\n","chapter":"oop","section":{"id":"every-types-hidden-base-class-system-object","text":"Every type's hidden base class: System.Object"},"num":"2.2"},{"q":"Why shouldn't you call a virtual method from a constructor?","a":"<p>Because the base constructor runs before the derived constructor. A virtual call would execute the derived override before the derived class&#39;s constructor has initialised its state.</p>\n","chapter":"oop","section":{"id":"every-types-hidden-base-class-system-object","text":"Every type's hidden base class: System.Object"},"num":"2.2"},{"q":"const vs readonly vs static readonly?","a":"<p><code>const</code> is a compile-time constant baked into calling assemblies (only primitives and strings). <code>readonly</code> instance fields are assigned once, in the declaration or constructor. <code>static readonly</code> is assigned once per type at runtime and is not copied into callers, so it&#39;s safe for values that may change between versions.</p>\n","chapter":"oop","section":{"id":"every-types-hidden-base-class-system-object","text":"Every type's hidden base class: System.Object"},"num":"2.2"},{"q":"What are default interface methods and when should you use them?","a":"<p>Interface members with a body (C# 8). They let library authors add members to published interfaces without breaking existing implementations. They are not intended to replace abstract base classes for sharing implementation.</p>\n","chapter":"oop","section":{"id":"every-types-hidden-base-class-system-object","text":"Every type's hidden base class: System.Object"},"num":"2.2"},{"q":"Why prefer composition over inheritance?","a":"<p>Inheritance couples subclasses to base-class internals and fixes behaviour at compile time, leading to fragile, deep hierarchies and class explosions. Composition assembles behaviour from small interchangeable parts (decorators, strategies) at runtime, typically via DI, and is easier to test and change.</p>\n","chapter":"oop","section":{"id":"every-types-hidden-base-class-system-object","text":"Every type's hidden base class: System.Object"},"num":"2.2"},{"q":"What does sealed do, and why seal classes by default?","a":"<p>It prevents inheritance. Sealing documents that the class wasn&#39;t designed for extension, prevents fragile-base problems, and allows the JIT to devirtualise calls and speed up type checks.</p>\n","chapter":"oop","section":{"id":"every-types-hidden-base-class-system-object","text":"Every type's hidden base class: System.Object"},"num":"2.2"},{"q":"What's the difference between internal and private?","a":"<p>private limits access to the containing type. internal limits access to the containing assembly. internal is the tool for hiding implementation types from consumers of a library or from other layers.</p>\n","chapter":"oop","section":{"id":"every-types-hidden-base-class-system-object","text":"Every type's hidden base class: System.Object"},"num":"2.2"},{"q":"What are generics and what problems do they solve?","a":"<p>Type parameters that let you write classes, interfaces and methods once for any type. They give compile-time type safety, avoid boxing and casts (better performance), and enable reuse, unlike object-based code.</p>\n","chapter":"generics","section":{"id":"spotting-generics-you-already-use-every-day","text":"Spotting generics you already use every day"},"num":"2.3"},{"q":"What generic constraints are available?","a":"<p>class, class?, struct, notnull, unmanaged, new(), a base class, an interface, another type parameter, default (for overrides), and allows ref struct (C# 13). Constraints let you use the members of the constrained type inside generic code.</p>\n","chapter":"generics","section":{"id":"spotting-generics-you-already-use-every-day","text":"Spotting generics you already use every day"},"num":"2.3"},{"q":"Explain covariance and contravariance.","a":"<p>Covariance (<code>out T</code>) lets <code>I&lt;Derived&gt;</code> be used as <code>I&lt;Base&gt;</code>; safe when T only appears in output positions (IEnumerable&lt;out T&gt;). Contravariance (<code>in T</code>) lets <code>I&lt;Base&gt;</code> be used as <code>I&lt;Derived&gt;</code>; safe when T only appears in input positions (Action&lt;in T&gt;, IComparer&lt;in T&gt;). It applies to interfaces and delegates with reference-type arguments.</p>\n","chapter":"generics","section":{"id":"spotting-generics-you-already-use-every-day","text":"Spotting generics you already use every day"},"num":"2.3"},{"q":"Why isn't List<Cat> assignable to List<Animal>?","a":"<p>Because List&lt;T&gt; both reads and writes T. If it were allowed, you could add a Dog through the List&lt;Animal&gt; reference into what is actually a List&lt;Cat&gt;. Use IEnumerable&lt;Animal&gt; or IReadOnlyList&lt;Animal&gt; (covariant) if you only need to read.</p>\n","chapter":"generics","section":{"id":"spotting-generics-you-already-use-every-day","text":"Spotting generics you already use every day"},"num":"2.3"},{"q":"How are .NET generics different from Java generics?","a":"<p>.NET generics are reified: closed generic types exist at runtime, <code>typeof(T)</code> works, and value-type instantiations get specialised code with no boxing. Java uses type erasure, so generic type info is gone at runtime and primitives must be boxed.</p>\n","chapter":"generics","section":{"id":"spotting-generics-you-already-use-every-day","text":"Spotting generics you already use every day"},"num":"2.3"},{"q":"What is an open generic registration in DI?","a":"<p>Registering a generic type definition, e.g. <code>AddScoped(typeof(IRepository&lt;&gt;), typeof(EfRepository&lt;&gt;))</code>, so the container can create any closed version (IRepository&lt;Order&gt;) on demand without registering each one.</p>\n","chapter":"generics","section":{"id":"spotting-generics-you-already-use-every-day","text":"Spotting generics you already use every day"},"num":"2.3"},{"q":"Are static fields shared across generic instantiations?","a":"<p>No. Each closed generic type (Foo&lt;int&gt;, Foo&lt;string&gt;) has its own copy of static fields, which is useful for per-type caches.</p>\n","chapter":"generics","section":{"id":"spotting-generics-you-already-use-every-day","text":"Spotting generics you already use every day"},"num":"2.3"},{"q":"What is a delegate?","a":"<p>A type-safe reference to a method (or a list of methods) with a specific signature. It lets you store behaviour in variables and pass it as arguments: callbacks, strategies, LINQ predicates, event handlers, middleware.</p>\n","chapter":"delegates-events","section":{"id":"expression-trees-vs-delegates-the-same-lambda-compiled-two-d","text":"Expression trees vs delegates: the same lambda, compiled two different ways"},"num":"2.4"},{"q":"Func vs Action vs Predicate?","a":"<p>Func&lt;…, TResult&gt; returns a value (the last type argument is the return type). Action&lt;…&gt; returns void. Predicate&lt;T&gt; is a T → bool function, equivalent to Func&lt;T, bool&gt;, used by older APIs like List.FindAll.</p>\n","chapter":"delegates-events","section":{"id":"expression-trees-vs-delegates-the-same-lambda-compiled-two-d","text":"Expression trees vs delegates: the same lambda, compiled two different ways"},"num":"2.4"},{"q":"What is a closure and what does it cost?","a":"<p>A lambda that captures variables from its enclosing scope. The compiler hoists captured variables into a heap-allocated class, so they outlive the method and are shared by every lambda that captures them. Each capturing lambda costs allocations (closure plus delegate), which matters in hot paths; static lambdas prevent accidental capture.</p>\n","chapter":"delegates-events","section":{"id":"expression-trees-vs-delegates-the-same-lambda-compiled-two-d","text":"Expression trees vs delegates: the same lambda, compiled two different ways"},"num":"2.4"},{"q":"Why does a for-loop capturing the loop variable print the same number?","a":"<p>All lambdas capture the same single variable <code>i</code>, which has its final value by the time they run. foreach creates a new variable per iteration (since C# 5) so it&#39;s safe; in a for-loop, copy to a local inside the body.</p>\n","chapter":"delegates-events","section":{"id":"expression-trees-vs-delegates-the-same-lambda-compiled-two-d","text":"Expression trees vs delegates: the same lambda, compiled two different ways"},"num":"2.4"},{"q":"What is the difference between a delegate and an event?","a":"<p>An event wraps a delegate so external code can only subscribe and unsubscribe. It can&#39;t invoke the event or overwrite the subscriber list with <code>=</code>. Only the declaring class can raise it.</p>\n","chapter":"delegates-events","section":{"id":"expression-trees-vs-delegates-the-same-lambda-compiled-two-d","text":"Expression trees vs delegates: the same lambda, compiled two different ways"},"num":"2.4"},{"q":"How can events cause memory leaks?","a":"<p>The publisher&#39;s delegate holds strong references to subscribers. A long-lived publisher keeps short-lived subscribers alive if they never unsubscribe. Unsubscribe in Dispose, or use weak references.</p>\n","chapter":"delegates-events","section":{"id":"expression-trees-vs-delegates-the-same-lambda-compiled-two-d","text":"Expression trees vs delegates: the same lambda, compiled two different ways"},"num":"2.4"},{"q":"How does ASP.NET Core use delegates?","a":"<p>The pipeline&#39;s core type is <code>RequestDelegate</code> (HttpContext → Task). Middleware are functions that wrap the next RequestDelegate; minimal API endpoints are arbitrary delegates whose parameters are bound from the request; configuration APIs take Action&lt;TOptions&gt; lambdas; DI supports Func&lt;IServiceProvider, T&gt; factories.</p>\n","chapter":"delegates-events","section":{"id":"expression-trees-vs-delegates-the-same-lambda-compiled-two-d","text":"Expression trees vs delegates: the same lambda, compiled two different ways"},"num":"2.4"},{"q":"What is the difference between Func<T,bool> and Expression<Func<T,bool>>?","a":"<p>A Func is compiled code that can be invoked. An Expression is a data structure describing the code, which providers like EF Core can analyse and translate to another language such as SQL. You can compile an expression into a delegate with <code>.Compile()</code>, but not the other way round.</p>\n","chapter":"delegates-events","section":{"id":"expression-trees-vs-delegates-the-same-lambda-compiled-two-d","text":"Expression trees vs delegates: the same lambda, compiled two different ways"},"num":"2.4"},{"q":"What is the time complexity of List.Add, Dictionary lookup and HashSet.Contains?","a":"<p>List.Add is O(1) amortised: occasionally O(n) when the backing array doubles and is copied. Dictionary lookup and HashSet.Contains are O(1) on average, assuming a good hash function; worst case O(n) when many keys collide.</p>\n","chapter":"collections","section":{"id":"equality-comparers-and-custom-ordering","text":"Equality comparers and custom ordering"},"num":"2.5"},{"q":"How does a Dictionary work internally?","a":"<p>It hashes the key with GetHashCode, maps the hash to a bucket index, and walks that bucket&#39;s chain of entries, comparing with Equals. It resizes (to a larger prime-sized capacity) as it grows. That&#39;s why keys need consistent Equals/GetHashCode and must not mutate.</p>\n","chapter":"collections","section":{"id":"equality-comparers-and-custom-ordering","text":"Equality comparers and custom ordering"},"num":"2.5"},{"q":"IEnumerable vs ICollection vs IList?","a":"<p>IEnumerable only supports iteration. ICollection adds Count, Add, Remove, Contains. IList adds index-based access, Insert and RemoveAt. Accept the least capable interface a method needs.</p>\n","chapter":"collections","section":{"id":"equality-comparers-and-custom-ordering","text":"Equality comparers and custom ordering"},"num":"2.5"},{"q":"What's the difference between IReadOnlyList, ReadOnlyCollection and ImmutableList?","a":"<p>IReadOnlyList is an interface: a read-only view, but the underlying list may still change. ReadOnlyCollection is a wrapper that prevents changes through it, while the wrapped list can still change. ImmutableList can never change; modifications return a new list that shares structure with the old one.</p>\n","chapter":"collections","section":{"id":"equality-comparers-and-custom-ordering","text":"Equality comparers and custom ordering"},"num":"2.5"},{"q":"When would you use FrozenDictionary?","a":"<p>For lookup tables built once (often at startup) and read very frequently, such as config maps, routing tables or tax rates. Creation is slower because it analyses the keys to pick the optimal layout, but reads are faster than Dictionary.</p>\n","chapter":"collections","section":{"id":"equality-comparers-and-custom-ordering","text":"Equality comparers and custom ordering"},"num":"2.5"},{"q":"How do you make a Dictionary thread-safe?","a":"<p>Use ConcurrentDictionary with its atomic methods (GetOrAdd, AddOrUpdate, TryRemove), or protect a normal Dictionary with a lock. For read-mostly data, swap an immutable/frozen dictionary reference atomically.</p>\n","chapter":"collections","section":{"id":"equality-comparers-and-custom-ordering","text":"Equality comparers and custom ordering"},"num":"2.5"},{"q":"Is ConcurrentDictionary.GetOrAdd's factory guaranteed to run once?","a":"<p>No. Under contention it can run more than once (only one result is stored). Wrap the value in Lazy&lt;T&gt; if the factory is expensive or has side effects.</p>\n","chapter":"collections","section":{"id":"equality-comparers-and-custom-ordering","text":"Equality comparers and custom ordering"},"num":"2.5"},{"q":"How would you remove duplicates from a list efficiently?","a":"<p><code>list.Distinct()</code> or <code>new HashSet&lt;T&gt;(list)</code> (O(n)), with a custom IEqualityComparer if needed, or <code>DistinctBy(x =&gt; x.Key)</code> (.NET 6) to dedupe by a property.</p>\n","chapter":"collections","section":{"id":"equality-comparers-and-custom-ordering","text":"Equality comparers and custom ordering"},"num":"2.5"},{"q":"What is deferred execution in LINQ?","a":"<p>Most operators don&#39;t run when called. They build a query that executes when it is enumerated (foreach, ToList, Count, First…), using the data as it is at that moment, and every enumeration runs it again. Immediate operators like ToList, Count, Sum and First force execution.</p>\n","chapter":"linq","section":{"id":"performance-considerations","text":"Performance considerations"},"num":"2.6"},{"q":"IEnumerable vs IQueryable?","a":"<p>IEnumerable runs LINQ in memory using compiled delegates. IQueryable captures the query as expression trees that a provider (EF Core) translates and executes at the data source, e.g. as SQL. Switching to IEnumerable too early pulls all rows into memory and filters there.</p>\n","chapter":"linq","section":{"id":"performance-considerations","text":"Performance considerations"},"num":"2.6"},{"q":"What's the difference between First, FirstOrDefault, Single and SingleOrDefault?","a":"<p>First returns the first element and throws if there are none; FirstOrDefault returns default instead. Single expects exactly one element and throws if there are zero or more than one; SingleOrDefault returns default for zero but still throws for more than one.</p>\n","chapter":"linq","section":{"id":"performance-considerations","text":"Performance considerations"},"num":"2.6"},{"q":"Select vs SelectMany?","a":"<p>Select maps each element to exactly one result, so a sequence of lists stays a sequence of lists. SelectMany maps each element to a sequence and flattens them into one sequence (all order lines from all orders).</p>\n","chapter":"linq","section":{"id":"performance-considerations","text":"Performance considerations"},"num":"2.6"},{"q":"How does yield return work?","a":"<p>The compiler turns the method into a state machine implementing IEnumerable/IEnumerator. Each MoveNext runs code until the next yield return, hands back that value, and pauses. Execution is lazy and resumes exactly where it left off.</p>\n","chapter":"linq","section":{"id":"performance-considerations","text":"Performance considerations"},"num":"2.6"},{"q":"What is the multiple-enumeration problem?","a":"<p>Enumerating a lazy IEnumerable more than once re-executes the whole pipeline, including database queries or expensive projections, and results may differ between enumerations. Materialise once with ToList/ToArray when you need the results more than once.</p>\n","chapter":"linq","section":{"id":"performance-considerations","text":"Performance considerations"},"num":"2.6"},{"q":"How does EF Core translate LINQ to SQL?","a":"<p>IQueryable operators take Expression&lt;Func&lt;…&gt;&gt; parameters, so the compiler builds expression trees instead of delegates. EF Core&#39;s query pipeline walks the tree, maps members to columns via the model, turns captured variables into SQL parameters, and generates provider-specific SQL. Untranslatable expressions throw.</p>\n","chapter":"linq","section":{"id":"performance-considerations","text":"Performance considerations"},"num":"2.6"},{"q":"Any() vs Count() > 0?","a":"<p>Any stops at the first element (or the first match), so it&#39;s O(1) for the common case and maps to EXISTS in SQL. Count enumerates everything on lazy sequences and maps to COUNT(*). Prefer Any for existence checks.</p>\n","chapter":"linq","section":{"id":"performance-considerations","text":"Performance considerations"},"num":"2.6"},{"q":"Is LINQ slow?","a":"<p>LINQ to Objects adds small overheads (enumerator and delegate allocations, interface calls) that are irrelevant for most business code. In hot paths over large data, hand-written loops or Span-based code can be faster. Recent .NET versions vectorised many operators. For databases, the cost is about the SQL generated, not LINQ itself.</p>\n","chapter":"linq","section":{"id":"performance-considerations","text":"Performance considerations"},"num":"2.6"},{"q":"What is the difference between throw and throw ex?","a":"<p><code>throw;</code> rethrows the current exception and preserves its original stack trace. <code>throw ex;</code> throws the same object but resets the stack trace to the current line, so you lose where the error originated. Wrap with <code>throw new X(&quot;context&quot;, ex)</code> when adding information.</p>\n","chapter":"exceptions-disposal","section":{"id":"common-disposal-mistakes","text":"Common disposal mistakes"},"num":"2.7"},{"q":"What are exception filters and why are they useful?","a":"<p>A <code>when (condition)</code> clause on a catch. The condition is evaluated before the stack unwinds, so if it&#39;s false the exception continues untouched with its full original state (good for diagnostics), and you can catch selectively (only certain status codes, for example) without catch-and-rethrow.</p>\n","chapter":"exceptions-disposal","section":{"id":"common-disposal-mistakes","text":"Common disposal mistakes"},"num":"2.7"},{"q":"When should you not use exceptions?","a":"<p>For expected outcomes: parsing input, lookups that may miss, validation failures and business rule rejections. Use Try-patterns, nullable returns, validation results or a Result type instead. Exceptions are costly and invisible in signatures; keep them for unexpected failures.</p>\n","chapter":"exceptions-disposal","section":{"id":"common-disposal-mistakes","text":"Common disposal mistakes"},"num":"2.7"},{"q":"What happens to an exception thrown in an async method?","a":"<p>It&#39;s captured in the returned Task and rethrown when the Task is awaited, with its original stack trace preserved. If the Task is never awaited, the exception goes unobserved. In async void methods, there&#39;s no Task, so the exception crashes the process.</p>\n","chapter":"exceptions-disposal","section":{"id":"common-disposal-mistakes","text":"Common disposal mistakes"},"num":"2.7"},{"q":"What is IDisposable and why does .NET need it if it has a garbage collector?","a":"<p>The GC only manages memory and runs non-deterministically. IDisposable gives deterministic release of other resources (connections, handles, sockets, locks) at a known point, typically via using / await using, which compile to try/finally.</p>\n","chapter":"exceptions-disposal","section":{"id":"common-disposal-mistakes","text":"Common disposal mistakes"},"num":"2.7"},{"q":"Explain the Dispose pattern. When do you need a finalizer?","a":"<p>Implement Dispose() to release resources, make it idempotent, and throw ObjectDisposedException afterwards. The full pattern with Dispose(bool) and a finalizer is only needed when the class directly owns unmanaged resources; Dispose() then calls Dispose(true) and GC.SuppressFinalize(this). Prefer SafeHandle so you never need your own finalizer.</p>\n","chapter":"exceptions-disposal","section":{"id":"common-disposal-mistakes","text":"Common disposal mistakes"},"num":"2.7"},{"q":"Who disposes services created by the DI container?","a":"<p>The container. Scoped services are disposed when their scope (the HTTP request) ends; singletons when the root provider is disposed at shutdown; transients when their owning scope ends. Don&#39;t dispose injected dependencies yourself.</p>\n","chapter":"exceptions-disposal","section":{"id":"common-disposal-mistakes","text":"Common disposal mistakes"},"num":"2.7"},{"q":"How does `using` work under the hood?","a":"<p>It compiles to a try/finally block that calls Dispose (or DisposeAsync for await using) in the finally, with a null check, so the resource is released even if an exception is thrown or the method returns early.</p>\n","chapter":"exceptions-disposal","section":{"id":"common-disposal-mistakes","text":"Common disposal mistakes"},"num":"2.7"},{"q":"Does async/await make code faster?","a":"<p>Not for a single operation; it adds a small overhead. It improves scalability: threads aren&#39;t blocked during I/O, so a server handles far more concurrent requests with fewer threads. Total latency can drop when independent operations run concurrently via Task.WhenAll.</p>\n","chapter":"async-await","section":{"id":"async-and-locks","text":"Async and locks"},"num":"2.8"},{"q":"What does the await keyword actually do?","a":"<p>If the awaited task is already complete, execution continues synchronously. Otherwise, it registers the rest of the method as a continuation on the task and returns an incomplete Task to the caller, freeing the thread. When the task completes, the continuation resumes, on the captured SynchronizationContext if there is one, otherwise on a thread-pool thread.</p>\n","chapter":"async-await","section":{"id":"async-and-locks","text":"Async and locks"},"num":"2.8"},{"q":"Does async create a new thread?","a":"<p>No. async/await doesn&#39;t create threads. For I/O-bound operations no thread is used while waiting; completion is signalled by the OS and a pool thread runs the continuation. Task.Run is what explicitly queues work to a pool thread.</p>\n","chapter":"async-await","section":{"id":"async-and-locks","text":"Async and locks"},"num":"2.8"},{"q":"Why is calling .Result or .Wait() on a Task dangerous?","a":"<p>It blocks a thread until the task completes. With a single-threaded SynchronizationContext (UI apps, old ASP.NET) it deadlocks, because the continuation needs the blocked thread. In ASP.NET Core it causes thread-pool starvation under load. Exceptions are also wrapped in AggregateException.</p>\n","chapter":"async-await","section":{"id":"async-and-locks","text":"Async and locks"},"num":"2.8"},{"q":"Why should you avoid async void?","a":"<p>The caller can&#39;t await it or know when it finishes, and exceptions can&#39;t be caught by the caller: they&#39;re raised on the context or thread pool and crash the process. Use async Task; async void is only for event handlers.</p>\n","chapter":"async-await","section":{"id":"async-and-locks","text":"Async and locks"},"num":"2.8"},{"q":"What is ConfigureAwait(false) and do you need it in ASP.NET Core?","a":"<p>It tells await not to resume on the captured SynchronizationContext. ASP.NET Core has no SynchronizationContext, so it makes no difference in application code there. Library authors should still use it because their code may run in UI or legacy ASP.NET apps where it prevents deadlocks and unnecessary context switches.</p>\n","chapter":"async-await","section":{"id":"async-and-locks","text":"Async and locks"},"num":"2.8"},{"q":"How do you run multiple async operations concurrently?","a":"<p>Start them without awaiting (store the tasks), then <code>await Task.WhenAll(...)</code>. Use Parallel.ForEachAsync or a SemaphoreSlim for bounded concurrency. Don&#39;t run concurrent operations on non-thread-safe objects like a single DbContext.</p>\n","chapter":"async-await","section":{"id":"async-and-locks","text":"Async and locks"},"num":"2.8"},{"q":"How does cancellation work in .NET?","a":"<p>Cooperatively. A CancellationTokenSource issues tokens; calling Cancel (or CancelAfter) sets the token, and code that observes it (async APIs, ThrowIfCancellationRequested) stops by throwing OperationCanceledException. ASP.NET Core provides HttpContext.RequestAborted, bound to a CancellationToken endpoint parameter.</p>\n","chapter":"async-await","section":{"id":"async-and-locks","text":"Async and locks"},"num":"2.8"},{"q":"What does the compiler generate for an async method?","a":"<p>A state machine struct with the locals as fields and a MoveNext method split at each await. It uses an AsyncTaskMethodBuilder to create and complete the returned Task. On the first real suspension the state machine is boxed to the heap and registered as the awaiter&#39;s continuation.</p>\n","chapter":"async-await","section":{"id":"async-and-locks","text":"Async and locks"},"num":"2.8"},{"q":"When would you use ValueTask instead of Task?","a":"<p>For hot-path methods that frequently complete synchronously (cache hits), to avoid allocating a Task. It must be awaited only once and not concurrently. Default to Task otherwise.</p>\n","chapter":"async-await","section":{"id":"async-and-locks","text":"Async and locks"},"num":"2.8"},{"q":"What is thread-pool starvation and how do you diagnose it?","a":"<p>All pool threads are blocked (usually by sync-over-async), so queued work waits while the pool slowly injects threads. You see high latency with low CPU and a growing thread count and queue length in dotnet-counters, and dumps show threads blocked in Wait/GetResult. Fix it by making the call chain async.</p>\n","chapter":"async-await","section":{"id":"async-and-locks","text":"Async and locks"},"num":"2.8"},{"q":"What's the risk of fire-and-forget tasks in a web request?","a":"<p>Unobserved exceptions, work lost on shutdown, and use of scoped services (like DbContext) after the request scope disposes them. Use a background service with a queue (Channel&lt;T&gt;) or a durable job system instead.</p>\n","chapter":"async-await","section":{"id":"async-and-locks","text":"Async and locks"},"num":"2.8"},{"q":"What are nullable reference types and do they prevent NullReferenceException at runtime?","a":"<p>A compiler feature (enabled with &lt;Nullable&gt;) that treats reference types as non-null by default, so <code>T?</code> means &quot;may be null&quot;, and it warns about possible null dereferences through flow analysis. It&#39;s compile-time only: there are no runtime checks, so values from deserialisation, reflection or unannotated code can still be null. Validate at boundaries.</p>\n","chapter":"modern-csharp","section":{"id":"other-quality-of-life-features-worth-recognising-on-sight","text":"Other quality-of-life features worth recognising on sight"},"num":"2.9"},{"q":"What does the null-forgiving operator (!) do?","a":"<p>It suppresses a nullable warning for that expression. It has no runtime effect. Overusing it defeats the feature.</p>\n","chapter":"modern-csharp","section":{"id":"other-quality-of-life-features-worth-recognising-on-sight","text":"Other quality-of-life features worth recognising on sight"},"num":"2.9"},{"q":"What is a record and how is it different from a class?","a":"<p>A record is a class (or struct with <code>record struct</code>) for which the compiler generates value-based equality and GetHashCode, ToString, Deconstruct and <code>with</code> support. Two records with equal property values are equal. Classes use reference equality by default. Use records for data (DTOs, messages, value objects) and classes for entities and services.</p>\n","chapter":"modern-csharp","section":{"id":"other-quality-of-life-features-worth-recognising-on-sight","text":"Other quality-of-life features worth recognising on sight"},"num":"2.9"},{"q":"What's the difference between init and required?","a":"<p><code>init</code> makes a property settable only during object initialisation. <code>required</code> forces callers to set the property in the initialiser, checked by the compiler. They&#39;re often combined: <code>public required string Email { get; init; }</code>.</p>\n","chapter":"modern-csharp","section":{"id":"other-quality-of-life-features-worth-recognising-on-sight","text":"Other quality-of-life features worth recognising on sight"},"num":"2.9"},{"q":"Explain switch expressions and property patterns.","a":"<p>A switch expression evaluates arms top-down and returns the first matching arm&#39;s value: <code>x switch { pattern =&gt; value, _ =&gt; default }</code>. Property patterns match on members, <code>{ Status: Paid, Total: &gt; 100 }</code>, and nest (<code>{ Customer.Country: &quot;DE&quot; }</code>). The compiler warns if not all cases are covered.</p>\n","chapter":"modern-csharp","section":{"id":"other-quality-of-life-features-worth-recognising-on-sight","text":"Other quality-of-life features worth recognising on sight"},"num":"2.9"},{"q":"How do extension methods work and how does ASP.NET Core use them?","a":"<p>They are static methods in static classes whose first parameter is marked with <code>this</code>. The compiler lets you call them as instance methods on that type. ASP.NET Core and libraries expose their setup as extension methods on IServiceCollection (AddX) and IEndpointRouteBuilder/IApplicationBuilder (MapX/UseX), which is the convention for organising Program.cs.</p>\n","chapter":"modern-csharp","section":{"id":"other-quality-of-life-features-worth-recognising-on-sight","text":"Other quality-of-life features worth recognising on sight"},"num":"2.9"},{"q":"What are primary constructors and what's a gotcha?","a":"<p>C# 12 lets classes declare constructor parameters in the type header, and those parameters are in scope throughout the class body, which suits DI. The gotcha: they&#39;re mutable captured parameters, not readonly fields, and capturing them in a struct or in several members stores them as hidden fields.</p>\n","chapter":"modern-csharp","section":{"id":"other-quality-of-life-features-worth-recognising-on-sight","text":"Other quality-of-life features worth recognising on sight"},"num":"2.9"},{"q":"What are tuples good for and when would you avoid them?","a":"<p>Lightweight multi-value returns and deconstruction for private or internal code. Avoid them in public APIs and persisted contracts, where a named record is clearer, can evolve, and has proper property names in serialisation.</p>\n","chapter":"modern-csharp","section":{"id":"other-quality-of-life-features-worth-recognising-on-sight","text":"Other quality-of-life features worth recognising on sight"},"num":"2.9"},{"q":"What is Span<T> and why is it useful?","a":"<p>A stack-only struct that represents a contiguous region of memory (array, string, stack or native) as a pointer plus length. Slicing creates views without copying, so parsing and formatting can run with zero allocations, reducing GC pressure in hot paths.</p>\n","chapter":"advanced-csharp","section":{"id":"expression-trees-for-metaprogramming-building-code-as-data-b","text":"Expression trees for metaprogramming: building code as data, by hand"},"num":"2.10"},{"q":"Why can't Span<T> be used in async methods or stored in a class field?","a":"<p>It&#39;s a ref struct that may point to stack memory, so it must never end up on the heap. Async methods and iterators store locals in a heap-allocated state machine, and class fields live on the heap, so both are forbidden. Use Memory&lt;T&gt; instead and call .Span when working synchronously.</p>\n","chapter":"advanced-csharp","section":{"id":"expression-trees-for-metaprogramming-building-code-as-data-b","text":"Expression trees for metaprogramming: building code as data, by hand"},"num":"2.10"},{"q":"Span<T> vs Memory<T>?","a":"<p>Span&lt;T&gt; is a ref struct: stack-only, fastest, usable in synchronous code. Memory&lt;T&gt; is a regular struct that can live on the heap, be stored in fields and cross awaits; you get a Span from it via .Span.</p>\n","chapter":"advanced-csharp","section":{"id":"expression-trees-for-metaprogramming-building-code-as-data-b","text":"Expression trees for metaprogramming: building code as data, by hand"},"num":"2.10"},{"q":"What is ArrayPool and when would you use it?","a":"<p>A pool of reusable arrays (ArrayPool&lt;T&gt;.Shared). Rent large temporary buffers instead of allocating them per operation, which avoids LOH allocations and GC pressure, and return them in a finally block. Remember that Rent may return a larger array than requested.</p>\n","chapter":"advanced-csharp","section":{"id":"expression-trees-for-metaprogramming-building-code-as-data-b","text":"Expression trees for metaprogramming: building code as data, by hand"},"num":"2.10"},{"q":"What is reflection and what are its downsides?","a":"<p>Runtime inspection and invocation of types and members via metadata. It powers DI, serializers, ORMs and test runners, but it&#39;s slow, allocates, bypasses compile-time checking, and isn&#39;t compatible with trimming/Native AOT unless annotated. Cache reflection results, compile delegates, or use source generators instead.</p>\n","chapter":"advanced-csharp","section":{"id":"expression-trees-for-metaprogramming-building-code-as-data-b","text":"Expression trees for metaprogramming: building code as data, by hand"},"num":"2.10"},{"q":"What are attributes and who reads them?","a":"<p>Declarative metadata attached to code elements. They are passive and read by the compiler (Obsolete, nullable attributes), the runtime (StructLayout), frameworks via reflection or endpoint metadata (HttpGet, Authorize, Required), or source generators (LoggerMessage, JsonSerializable).</p>\n","chapter":"advanced-csharp","section":{"id":"expression-trees-for-metaprogramming-building-code-as-data-b","text":"Expression trees for metaprogramming: building code as data, by hand"},"num":"2.10"},{"q":"What is a source generator and why are they replacing reflection?","a":"<p>A compiler plugin that analyses your code during the build and adds generated C# source. It moves work from runtime to compile time, giving faster startup, fewer allocations and compile-time errors, and the generated code is visible to the trimmer, enabling Native AOT. Examples: LoggerMessage, System.Text.Json source generation, GeneratedRegex.</p>\n","chapter":"advanced-csharp","section":{"id":"expression-trees-for-metaprogramming-building-code-as-data-b","text":"Expression trees for metaprogramming: building code as data, by hand"},"num":"2.10"},{"q":"What does the fixed statement do?","a":"<p>It pins a managed object in memory for the duration of the block so the GC can&#39;t move it, letting you take a stable pointer to it (for unsafe code or native interop). Long or frequent pinning fragments the heap.</p>\n","chapter":"advanced-csharp","section":{"id":"expression-trees-for-metaprogramming-building-code-as-data-b","text":"Expression trees for metaprogramming: building code as data, by hand"},"num":"2.10"},{"q":"What is the role of Program.cs in ASP.NET Core?","a":"<p>It&#39;s the application entry point. It creates a WebApplicationBuilder, registers services and configuration (phase 1), builds the WebApplication (which builds the DI container), configures the middleware pipeline and endpoints (phase 2), then calls Run to start Kestrel and the hosted services.</p>\n","chapter":"aspnet-hosting","section":{"id":"hosting-models-summary","text":"Hosting models summary"},"num":"3.1"},{"q":"What is the Generic Host?","a":"<p>The <code>IHost</code> abstraction from Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting that provides DI, configuration, logging, environment info and lifetime management, and runs IHostedService instances. It&#39;s used by web apps, worker services and console apps alike; ASP.NET Core&#39;s web server runs as one of its hosted services.</p>\n","chapter":"aspnet-hosting","section":{"id":"hosting-models-summary","text":"Hosting models summary"},"num":"3.1"},{"q":"What is Kestrel and why use a reverse proxy in front of it?","a":"<p>Kestrel is ASP.NET Core&#39;s built-in cross-platform web server: it handles sockets, TLS and HTTP/1-3 parsing and feeds requests into the pipeline. A reverse proxy adds load balancing, TLS termination, a WAF, caching, multiple apps behind one IP and centralised rate limiting. When you use one, you must configure forwarded headers so the app sees the real client IP and scheme.</p>\n","chapter":"aspnet-hosting","section":{"id":"hosting-models-summary","text":"Hosting models summary"},"num":"3.1"},{"q":"How does ASP.NET Core know which environment it's running in?","a":"<p>From the ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT (or DOTNET_ENVIRONMENT) variable, defaulting to Production. It controls which appsettings.{Environment}.json is loaded, whether User Secrets are used, and is available as IWebHostEnvironment.</p>\n","chapter":"aspnet-hosting","section":{"id":"hosting-models-summary","text":"Hosting models summary"},"num":"3.1"},{"q":"What's the difference between in-process and out-of-process hosting in IIS?","a":"<p>In-process runs the app inside the IIS worker process using IIS&#39;s HTTP server, which is faster. Out-of-process runs Kestrel as a separate process, with IIS forwarding requests to it as a reverse proxy.</p>\n","chapter":"aspnet-hosting","section":{"id":"hosting-models-summary","text":"Hosting models summary"},"num":"3.1"},{"q":"What happens when an ASP.NET Core app receives SIGTERM?","a":"<p>The host triggers ApplicationStopping, Kestrel stops accepting new connections and drains in-flight requests, hosted services&#39; StopAsync is called and their stopping tokens are cancelled, all within ShutdownTimeout (30 s by default). Then ApplicationStopped fires and the DI container (with its singletons) is disposed.</p>\n","chapter":"aspnet-hosting","section":{"id":"hosting-models-summary","text":"Hosting models summary"},"num":"3.1"},{"q":"What is HttpContext and is it thread-safe?","a":"<p>The per-request object holding Request, Response, User, RequestServices (the scoped container), Items, Features and RequestAborted. It is not thread-safe and only valid for the duration of the request, so it must not be captured in background tasks.</p>\n","chapter":"aspnet-hosting","section":{"id":"hosting-models-summary","text":"Hosting models summary"},"num":"3.1"},{"q":"Why shouldn't you call BuildServiceProvider() inside Program.cs?","a":"<p>It builds a separate container from the one the app will use, so singletons get created twice and their state diverges, and disposal isn&#39;t handled. Use the options pattern, factory overloads (<code>sp =&gt; ...</code>), or resolve after <code>builder.Build()</code> from <code>app.Services</code>.</p>\n","chapter":"aspnet-hosting","section":{"id":"hosting-models-summary","text":"Hosting models summary"},"num":"3.1"},{"q":"What is dependency injection and why use it?","a":"<p>A technique where a class receives its dependencies from outside (usually through its constructor) instead of creating them. It decouples code from concrete implementations, enables unit testing with fakes, centralises configuration and lifetime management in one composition root, and supports the Dependency Inversion Principle.</p>\n","chapter":"dependency-injection","section":{"id":"how-the-container-pulls-this-off-internally","text":"How the container pulls this off internally"},"num":"3.2"},{"q":"Explain transient, scoped and singleton lifetimes.","a":"<p>Transient creates a new instance every time it&#39;s resolved. Scoped creates one instance per scope; in ASP.NET Core a scope is one HTTP request, so it&#39;s one per request. Singleton creates one instance for the app&#39;s lifetime, shared by all requests and threads, so it must be thread-safe.</p>\n","chapter":"dependency-injection","section":{"id":"how-the-container-pulls-this-off-internally","text":"How the container pulls this off internally"},"num":"3.2"},{"q":"What lifetime does DbContext have and why?","a":"<p>Scoped, by default with AddDbContext. A DbContext isn&#39;t thread-safe and accumulates tracked entities, so it should be short-lived and used by one request (one unit of work) at a time.</p>\n","chapter":"dependency-injection","section":{"id":"how-the-container-pulls-this-off-internally","text":"How the container pulls this off internally"},"num":"3.2"},{"q":"What is a captive dependency and how do you detect and fix it?","a":"<p>A longer-lived service holding a shorter-lived one, e.g. a singleton depending on a scoped DbContext. The scoped instance is kept forever and shared across threads. ASP.NET Core detects it with scope validation (on by default in Development), which throws at startup. Fix it by aligning lifetimes or by injecting IServiceScopeFactory or IDbContextFactory to create instances per operation.</p>\n","chapter":"dependency-injection","section":{"id":"how-the-container-pulls-this-off-internally","text":"How the container pulls this off internally"},"num":"3.2"},{"q":"How do you use a scoped service from a BackgroundService?","a":"<p>BackgroundService is a singleton with no request scope, so inject IServiceScopeFactory, create a scope per unit of work (<code>using var scope = factory.CreateScope()</code>), and resolve the scoped service from scope.ServiceProvider. Dispose the scope when done.</p>\n","chapter":"dependency-injection","section":{"id":"how-the-container-pulls-this-off-internally","text":"How the container pulls this off internally"},"num":"3.2"},{"q":"What happens if you register two implementations of the same interface?","a":"<p>Injecting the interface gives the last registered implementation. Injecting IEnumerable&lt;T&gt; gives all of them in registration order. Since .NET 8, keyed services let you register and resolve specific implementations by key.</p>\n","chapter":"dependency-injection","section":{"id":"how-the-container-pulls-this-off-internally","text":"How the container pulls this off internally"},"num":"3.2"},{"q":"What's the difference between DI, IoC and the Dependency Inversion Principle?","a":"<p>DIP is the design principle: depend on abstractions, not concretions. IoC is the broader idea that a framework controls object creation and program flow. DI is a concrete technique for achieving IoC by passing dependencies in, typically automated by a DI container.</p>\n","chapter":"dependency-injection","section":{"id":"how-the-container-pulls-this-off-internally","text":"How the container pulls this off internally"},"num":"3.2"},{"q":"Why is the Service Locator pattern considered an anti-pattern?","a":"<p>Dependencies are hidden inside method bodies instead of declared in the constructor, so you can&#39;t see what a class needs, missing registrations fail at runtime rather than at startup validation, and tests need a configured container. Constructor injection makes dependencies explicit.</p>\n","chapter":"dependency-injection","section":{"id":"how-the-container-pulls-this-off-internally","text":"How the container pulls this off internally"},"num":"3.2"},{"q":"How would you implement the decorator pattern with the built-in container?","a":"<p>Register the inner implementation as its concrete type and register the interface with a factory that wraps it: <code>services.AddScoped&lt;IRepo&gt;(sp =&gt; new CachingRepo(sp.GetRequiredService&lt;SqlRepo&gt;(), ...))</code>. Or use Scrutor&#39;s <code>Decorate&lt;TInterface, TDecorator&gt;()</code>.</p>\n","chapter":"dependency-injection","section":{"id":"how-the-container-pulls-this-off-internally","text":"How the container pulls this off internally"},"num":"3.2"},{"q":"Does the container dispose services? Which ones?","a":"<p>Yes, the ones it created that implement IDisposable/IAsyncDisposable: scoped and transient instances when their scope is disposed, singletons when the app shuts down. It doesn&#39;t dispose instances you passed in yourself via AddSingleton(instance).</p>\n","chapter":"dependency-injection","section":{"id":"how-the-container-pulls-this-off-internally","text":"How the container pulls this off internally"},"num":"3.2"},{"q":"What are keyed services?","a":"<p>A .NET 8 feature to register multiple implementations of the same service type under different keys (AddKeyedScoped&lt;IFoo, Bar&gt;(&quot;bar&quot;)) and resolve a specific one with [FromKeyedServices(&quot;bar&quot;)] or GetRequiredKeyedService.</p>\n","chapter":"dependency-injection","section":{"id":"how-the-container-pulls-this-off-internally","text":"How the container pulls this off internally"},"num":"3.2"},{"q":"What is the composition root?","a":"<p>The single place where the object graph is composed, meaning all interfaces are bound to implementations: Program.cs and the AddX extension methods it calls. Other code receives dependencies and never knows concrete types.</p>\n","chapter":"dependency-injection","section":{"id":"how-the-container-pulls-this-off-internally","text":"How the container pulls this off internally"},"num":"3.2"},{"q":"How does configuration precedence work in ASP.NET Core?","a":"<p>Providers are added in order and later ones override earlier ones for the same key. By default that&#39;s appsettings.json, then appsettings.{Environment}.json, then User Secrets (Development), then environment variables, then command-line arguments. Providers added afterwards (Key Vault, App Configuration) override those too.</p>\n","chapter":"configuration-options","section":{"id":"how-the-provider-mechanism-actually-works-underneath","text":"How the provider mechanism actually works underneath"},"num":"3.3"},{"q":"What is the Options pattern and why use it?","a":"<p>Binding configuration sections to strongly typed classes registered in DI and consumed as IOptions&lt;T&gt; (or Snapshot/Monitor). It gives type safety, validation (including fail-fast at startup with ValidateOnStart), testability and focused settings per class instead of passing IConfiguration around.</p>\n","chapter":"configuration-options","section":{"id":"how-the-provider-mechanism-actually-works-underneath","text":"How the provider mechanism actually works underneath"},"num":"3.3"},{"q":"IOptions vs IOptionsSnapshot vs IOptionsMonitor?","a":"<p>IOptions is a singleton computed once, with no reload. IOptionsSnapshot is scoped and recomputed per request, so it reflects changes but can&#39;t be used in singletons. IOptionsMonitor is a singleton whose CurrentValue always reflects the latest config, with an OnChange callback; use it in singletons and background services.</p>\n","chapter":"configuration-options","section":{"id":"how-the-provider-mechanism-actually-works-underneath","text":"How the provider mechanism actually works underneath"},"num":"3.3"},{"q":"How do you set a nested configuration value via an environment variable?","a":"<p>Replace each <code>:</code> with a double underscore, e.g. <code>Payments__BaseUrl</code> for <code>Payments:BaseUrl</code>, or <code>ConnectionStrings__Shop</code>.</p>\n","chapter":"configuration-options","section":{"id":"how-the-provider-mechanism-actually-works-underneath","text":"How the provider mechanism actually works underneath"},"num":"3.3"},{"q":"How do you manage secrets in development vs production?","a":"<p>Development: User Secrets (stored outside the repo, loaded only in Development). Production: Azure Key Vault (or the platform&#39;s secret store) loaded via managed identity, or better, avoid secrets entirely by using managed identity to authenticate to Azure services. Never commit secrets to source control.</p>\n","chapter":"configuration-options","section":{"id":"how-the-provider-mechanism-actually-works-underneath","text":"How the provider mechanism actually works underneath"},"num":"3.3"},{"q":"How do you make the app fail fast on invalid configuration?","a":"<p>Use <code>AddOptions&lt;T&gt;().BindConfiguration(...).ValidateDataAnnotations().Validate(...).ValidateOnStart()</code>, so validation runs during host startup and the app refuses to start (and the deployment fails its health checks) instead of failing on first use.</p>\n","chapter":"configuration-options","section":{"id":"how-the-provider-mechanism-actually-works-underneath","text":"How the provider mechanism actually works underneath"},"num":"3.3"},{"q":"What are feature flags and why use them?","a":"<p>Runtime switches (e.g. via Microsoft.FeatureManagement and Azure App Configuration) that enable or disable features without redeploying. They decouple deployment from release, allow gradual rollouts, A/B tests and instant rollback.</p>\n","chapter":"configuration-options","section":{"id":"how-the-provider-mechanism-actually-works-underneath","text":"How the provider mechanism actually works underneath"},"num":"3.3"},{"q":"What is middleware in ASP.NET Core?","a":"<p>Components assembled into a pipeline that handle every HTTP request and response. Each receives the HttpContext, can do work before and after invoking the next component, or can short-circuit by producing the response itself. The pipeline is built from RequestDelegate functions composed in registration order.</p>\n","chapter":"middleware","section":{"id":"testing-middleware","text":"Testing middleware"},"num":"3.4"},{"q":"Why does middleware order matter? Give examples.","a":"<p>Each middleware only sees the effects of the ones before it and wraps the ones after it. The exception handler must be first to catch everything. Authentication must precede authorization or users look anonymous. Routing must precede authorization so endpoint metadata like [Authorize] is available. CORS must run before auth so preflight requests aren&#39;t rejected. Static files go early to avoid unnecessary work.</p>\n","chapter":"middleware","section":{"id":"testing-middleware","text":"Testing middleware"},"num":"3.4"},{"q":"What's the difference between Use, Run and Map?","a":"<p>Use adds middleware that can call next. Run adds terminal middleware that ends the pipeline. Map branches the pipeline for a path prefix, and the branch doesn&#39;t rejoin. UseWhen conditionally adds middleware and then rejoins the main pipeline.</p>\n","chapter":"middleware","section":{"id":"testing-middleware","text":"Testing middleware"},"num":"3.4"},{"q":"How is the middleware pipeline built internally?","a":"<p>Each middleware is a Func&lt;RequestDelegate, RequestDelegate&gt;. On startup, ApplicationBuilder.Build composes them in reverse order, starting with a terminal 404 delegate, so each wraps the next, producing one outermost RequestDelegate that the server invokes per request.</p>\n","chapter":"middleware","section":{"id":"testing-middleware","text":"Testing middleware"},"num":"3.4"},{"q":"How do you write custom middleware, and how do you inject scoped services into it?","a":"<p>Create a class with a constructor taking RequestDelegate and an InvokeAsync(HttpContext, …) method, then register it with app.UseMiddleware&lt;T&gt;(). The instance is created once, so scoped services must be InvokeAsync parameters, not constructor parameters. Alternatively implement IMiddleware and register it in DI, which resolves it per request and allows constructor injection of scoped services.</p>\n","chapter":"middleware","section":{"id":"testing-middleware","text":"Testing middleware"},"num":"3.4"},{"q":"What does short-circuiting mean in the pipeline?","a":"<p>A middleware not calling next and writing the response itself, so later middleware and the endpoint never run. Examples: auth failures (401/403), rate limiting (429), cache hits, static files, CORS preflight responses.</p>\n","chapter":"middleware","section":{"id":"testing-middleware","text":"Testing middleware"},"num":"3.4"},{"q":"Why might setting a response header after await next() fail?","a":"<p>Once the response has started (headers flushed because the body began writing), headers and status code are read-only, and modifying them throws. Use Response.OnStarting to set headers just before they&#39;re sent, and check Response.HasStarted before writing error responses.</p>\n","chapter":"middleware","section":{"id":"testing-middleware","text":"Testing middleware"},"num":"3.4"},{"q":"Middleware vs filters: when do you use each?","a":"<p>Middleware for app-wide, HTTP-level concerns that apply regardless of endpoint (errors, HTTPS, CORS, auth, compression, correlation IDs). Filters (MVC or endpoint filters) for endpoint-specific concerns that need the action context or the typed, bound arguments and results (validation, auditing, transactions).</p>\n","chapter":"middleware","section":{"id":"testing-middleware","text":"Testing middleware"},"num":"3.4"},{"q":"How can middleware read the request body without breaking the endpoint?","a":"<p>Call Request.EnableBuffering() so the stream can be rewound, read it (leaving the stream open), then set Request.Body.Position = 0 before calling next.</p>\n","chapter":"middleware","section":{"id":"testing-middleware","text":"Testing middleware"},"num":"3.4"},{"q":"How does authorization middleware know which policy an endpoint needs?","a":"<p>Routing runs first and attaches the selected endpoint to the HttpContext. Endpoint metadata (from [Authorize], RequireAuthorization, etc.) is available via context.GetEndpoint().Metadata, which the authorization middleware reads to evaluate the right policies.</p>\n","chapter":"middleware","section":{"id":"testing-middleware","text":"Testing middleware"},"num":"3.4"},{"q":"What is endpoint routing?","a":"<p>The routing system since ASP.NET Core 3.0, shared by MVC, minimal APIs, Razor Pages, SignalR, gRPC and health checks. UseRouting matches the request to an endpoint early and stores it on HttpContext; the endpoint is executed at the end of the pipeline; middleware in between (CORS, auth, rate limiting, caching) can read the selected endpoint&#39;s metadata.</p>\n","chapter":"routing","section":{"id":"reading-route-values-back-out-of-the-request","text":"Reading route values back out of the request"},"num":"3.5"},{"q":"What's the difference between attribute routing and conventional routing?","a":"<p>Attribute routing puts templates on controllers and actions ([Route], [HttpGet(&quot;{id}&quot;)]) and is required for API controllers. Conventional routing defines URL patterns centrally (&quot;{controller}/{action}/{id?}&quot;) and maps them to controllers by naming convention; it&#39;s typical for MVC sites with views.</p>\n","chapter":"routing","section":{"id":"reading-route-values-back-out-of-the-request","text":"Reading route values back out of the request"},"num":"3.5"},{"q":"What happens when a route constraint fails?","a":"<p>The route doesn&#39;t match, so routing tries other endpoints and, if none match, returns 404. Constraints are for disambiguation, not input validation, which should return 400.</p>\n","chapter":"routing","section":{"id":"reading-route-values-back-out-of-the-request","text":"Reading route values back out of the request"},"num":"3.5"},{"q":"What are route groups?","a":"<p>MapGroup creates a prefix and lets you apply shared metadata (authorization, filters, tags, rate limiting, CORS) to all endpoints within it, which keeps minimal API definitions organised and DRY.</p>\n","chapter":"routing","section":{"id":"reading-route-values-back-out-of-the-request","text":"Reading route values back out of the request"},"num":"3.5"},{"q":"How does ASP.NET Core resolve two routes that match the same URL?","a":"<p>By route precedence: literals over parameters, constrained over unconstrained, catch-alls last, then HTTP method. If two candidates remain equally specific, it throws AmbiguousMatchException.</p>\n","chapter":"routing","section":{"id":"reading-route-values-back-out-of-the-request","text":"Reading route values back out of the request"},"num":"3.5"},{"q":"How do you generate URLs to other endpoints?","a":"<p>Name the endpoint (WithName or Name = …) and use LinkGenerator.GetUriByName/GetPathByName, or helpers like CreatedAtRoute and CreatedAtAction. Avoid hard-coded string URLs.</p>\n","chapter":"routing","section":{"id":"reading-route-values-back-out-of-the-request","text":"Reading route values back out of the request"},"num":"3.5"},{"q":"Minimal APIs vs controllers: what are the differences and when would you pick each?","a":"<p>Both use the same routing, DI and middleware. Minimal APIs map routes to functions with fluent configuration, have less ceremony, are a bit faster and support Native AOT, and they&#39;re Microsoft&#39;s default recommendation for new APIs. Controllers are classes with attributes and have a richer filter pipeline, model binding and content negotiation. Pick based on team and codebase conventions and the features you need.</p>\n","chapter":"building-apis","section":{"id":"putting-it-together-a-handler-that-stays-genuinely-thin","text":"Putting it together: a handler that stays genuinely thin"},"num":"3.6"},{"q":"What does the [ApiController] attribute do?","a":"<p>It enables API conventions: automatic 400 ValidationProblemDetails responses when model state is invalid, binding-source inference ([FromBody] for complex types, [FromRoute]/[FromQuery] for simple ones), a requirement for attribute routing, and ProblemDetails bodies for error status codes.</p>\n","chapter":"building-apis","section":{"id":"putting-it-together-a-handler-that-stays-genuinely-thin","text":"Putting it together: a handler that stays genuinely thin"},"num":"3.6"},{"q":"What's the difference between Results and TypedResults?","a":"<p>Both create IResult responses. TypedResults returns concrete types (Ok&lt;T&gt;, NotFound, Created&lt;T&gt;), so a handler&#39;s return type (often Results&lt;…&gt;) documents every possible response, OpenAPI metadata is inferred automatically, and unit tests can assert on types without executing the result.</p>\n","chapter":"building-apis","section":{"id":"putting-it-together-a-handler-that-stays-genuinely-thin","text":"Putting it together: a handler that stays genuinely thin"},"num":"3.6"},{"q":"How does parameter binding work in minimal APIs?","a":"<p>By inference. Route parameters bind by name; simple types otherwise come from the query string; complex types from the JSON body (only one body per request); registered services from DI; special types (HttpContext, CancellationToken, ClaimsPrincipal) are injected. Attributes ([FromQuery], [FromHeader], [FromBody], [FromServices], [AsParameters]) make it explicit, and TryParse/BindAsync allow custom binding.</p>\n","chapter":"building-apis","section":{"id":"putting-it-together-a-handler-that-stays-genuinely-thin","text":"Putting it together: a handler that stays genuinely thin"},"num":"3.6"},{"q":"How do you validate requests in ASP.NET Core?","a":"<p>With data annotations on DTOs (automatic in controllers with [ApiController]; built in for minimal APIs since .NET 10 via AddValidation), or with FluentValidation applied through an endpoint/action filter or called in the handler. Return 400 ValidationProblemDetails. Keep business rules in the domain/application layer.</p>\n","chapter":"building-apis","section":{"id":"putting-it-together-a-handler-that-stays-genuinely-thin","text":"Putting it together: a handler that stays genuinely thin"},"num":"3.6"},{"q":"What is over-posting and how do you prevent it?","a":"<p>When clients send extra fields that bind onto properties they shouldn&#39;t control (e.g. IsAdmin, Price) because the request is bound directly to an entity. Prevent it by binding to dedicated request DTOs and mapping explicitly.</p>\n","chapter":"building-apis","section":{"id":"putting-it-together-a-handler-that-stays-genuinely-thin","text":"Putting it together: a handler that stays genuinely thin"},"num":"3.6"},{"q":"Explain the MVC filter pipeline order.","a":"<p>Authorization filters, then resource filters, then model binding, then action filters around the action, then exception filters (if something threw), then result filters around result execution, then resource filters&#39; after-code. Within a type, global then controller then action filters, reversed on the way out.</p>\n","chapter":"building-apis","section":{"id":"putting-it-together-a-handler-that-stays-genuinely-thin","text":"Putting it together: a handler that stays genuinely thin"},"num":"3.6"},{"q":"What are endpoint filters?","a":"<p>Minimal API equivalents of action filters: IEndpointFilter implementations attached to endpoints or route groups that run around the handler with access to the bound arguments (EndpointFilterInvocationContext.Arguments) and the returned result. Used for validation, logging and argument checks.</p>\n","chapter":"building-apis","section":{"id":"putting-it-together-a-handler-that-stays-genuinely-thin","text":"Putting it together: a handler that stays genuinely thin"},"num":"3.6"},{"q":"Why return DTOs instead of EF Core entities from an API?","a":"<p>It decouples the API contract from the database schema, prevents over-posting and leaking internal fields, avoids serialization cycles via navigation properties, and lets you project only the needed columns, which is faster.</p>\n","chapter":"building-apis","section":{"id":"putting-it-together-a-handler-that-stays-genuinely-thin","text":"Putting it together: a handler that stays genuinely thin"},"num":"3.6"},{"q":"How do you customise JSON serialization?","a":"<p>ConfigureHttpJsonOptions (minimal APIs) or AddJsonOptions (controllers) to set naming policies, converters (JsonStringEnumConverter), ignore conditions and so on; attributes like [JsonPropertyName], [JsonIgnore] and [JsonPolymorphic]; custom JsonConverter&lt;T&gt;; and source-generated JsonSerializerContext for speed and AOT.</p>\n","chapter":"building-apis","section":{"id":"putting-it-together-a-handler-that-stays-genuinely-thin","text":"Putting it together: a handler that stays genuinely thin"},"num":"3.6"},{"q":"How do you implement global exception handling in ASP.NET Core?","a":"<p>Register AddProblemDetails and one or more IExceptionHandler implementations (AddExceptionHandler&lt;T&gt;), and put app.UseExceptionHandler() first in the pipeline. Handlers map known exception types to status codes and ProblemDetails, log appropriately, and return true; unknown ones fall through to a generic 500. The Developer Exception Page is used only in Development.</p>\n","chapter":"errors-logging","section":{"id":"what-to-log-and-what-never-to-log","text":"What to log and what never to log"},"num":"3.7"},{"q":"What is ProblemDetails?","a":"<p>The RFC 9457 (formerly 7807) standard JSON format for HTTP API errors: type, title, status, detail, instance, plus extensions like traceId or validation errors. ASP.NET Core produces it via AddProblemDetails, Results.Problem/ValidationProblem and [ApiController] conventions.</p>\n","chapter":"errors-logging","section":{"id":"what-to-log-and-what-never-to-log","text":"What to log and what never to log"},"num":"3.7"},{"q":"Why shouldn't you return stack traces to clients?","a":"<p>They leak implementation details (framework versions, file paths, SQL, internal names) that help attackers, and they&#39;re useless to clients. Return a generic ProblemDetails with a trace ID and keep the details in server logs.</p>\n","chapter":"errors-logging","section":{"id":"what-to-log-and-what-never-to-log","text":"What to log and what never to log"},"num":"3.7"},{"q":"What is structured logging and why is it better?","a":"<p>Logging with message templates whose placeholders are captured as named properties instead of being formatted into a string. Log stores can then index and query by those fields (all errors for OrderId 1042, p95 duration by endpoint), which plain text can&#39;t support well.</p>\n","chapter":"errors-logging","section":{"id":"what-to-log-and-what-never-to-log","text":"What to log and what never to log"},"num":"3.7"},{"q":"Why not use string interpolation in log calls?","a":"<p>Interpolation produces a single pre-formatted string, so the structured properties are lost. It also always allocates and formats, even when the log level is disabled. Use message templates with placeholders (CA2254).</p>\n","chapter":"errors-logging","section":{"id":"what-to-log-and-what-never-to-log","text":"What to log and what never to log"},"num":"3.7"},{"q":"What are log scopes?","a":"<p>Ambient properties attached to all log entries written within a using block (BeginScope), flowing across awaits via AsyncLocal. ASP.NET Core adds RequestId, TraceId and SpanId automatically; you can add OrderId, TenantId and so on for correlation.</p>\n","chapter":"errors-logging","section":{"id":"what-to-log-and-what-never-to-log","text":"What to log and what never to log"},"num":"3.7"},{"q":"What is LoggerMessage and when would you use it?","a":"<p>A source generator ([LoggerMessage] on partial methods) that produces strongly typed, high-performance logging methods that check the level first, avoid boxing and params arrays, and pre-parse templates. Use it in hot paths and libraries.</p>\n","chapter":"errors-logging","section":{"id":"what-to-log-and-what-never-to-log","text":"What to log and what never to log"},"num":"3.7"},{"q":"How do you choose log levels?","a":"<p>Information for normal business milestones, Warning for unexpected but handled situations (retries, declines), Error for failures of an operation (exceptions), Critical for system-wide failures, and Debug/Trace for diagnostics that are off in production. Turn framework categories down to Warning to reduce noise.</p>\n","chapter":"errors-logging","section":{"id":"what-to-log-and-what-never-to-log","text":"What to log and what never to log"},"num":"3.7"},{"q":"How would you correlate logs across microservices?","a":"<p>Use W3C trace context propagation (traceparent header, automatic with HttpClient and OpenTelemetry) so every service logs with the same TraceId, include the trace ID in ProblemDetails responses, and send logs to a central store that can filter by TraceId and link to the distributed trace.</p>\n","chapter":"errors-logging","section":{"id":"what-to-log-and-what-never-to-log","text":"What to log and what never to log"},"num":"3.7"},{"q":"What's the difference between authentication and authorization?","a":"<p>Authentication establishes who the caller is: it validates credentials (cookie, JWT, API key) and produces a ClaimsPrincipal in HttpContext.User. Authorization decides whether that principal may perform an action on a resource, using roles, claims, policies or resource-based checks. Failure gives 401 for authentication and 403 for authorization.</p>\n","chapter":"auth","section":{"id":"claims-transformation-and-permissions","text":"Claims transformation and permissions"},"num":"3.8"},{"q":"How does an ASP.NET Core API validate a JWT?","a":"<p>The JwtBearer handler reads the token from the Authorization header, gets the issuer&#39;s public signing keys from the OIDC discovery document (cached), verifies the signature, validates issuer, audience and lifetime (exp/nbf with clock skew), then builds a ClaimsPrincipal from the claims. No call to the identity provider per request.</p>\n","chapter":"auth","section":{"id":"claims-transformation-and-permissions","text":"Claims transformation and permissions"},"num":"3.8"},{"q":"Cookies vs JWTs: when would you use each?","a":"<p>Cookies for browser-based apps and BFFs: sent automatically, HttpOnly protects against XSS theft, they need CSRF protection, and they&#39;re easy to revoke. JWT bearer tokens for APIs consumed by SPAs, mobile apps and services: stateless and cross-domain, but hard to revoke, so keep them short-lived. For SPAs, the BFF pattern with cookies is now recommended.</p>\n","chapter":"auth","section":{"id":"claims-transformation-and-permissions","text":"Claims transformation and permissions"},"num":"3.8"},{"q":"What is in a JWT and is it secure to put data in it?","a":"<p>A header (algorithm, key ID), a payload of claims (iss, sub, aud, exp, roles, scopes…) and a signature, all base64url encoded. It&#39;s signed, not encrypted, so anyone can read the payload. Don&#39;t put secrets or sensitive data in it.</p>\n","chapter":"auth","section":{"id":"claims-transformation-and-permissions","text":"Claims transformation and permissions"},"num":"3.8"},{"q":"Roles vs claims vs policies?","a":"<p>Claims are facts about the user; a role is one kind of claim. Policies are named authorization rules made of requirements that can check roles, claims or custom logic, keeping rules centralised and testable instead of scattering role strings across controllers.</p>\n","chapter":"auth","section":{"id":"claims-transformation-and-permissions","text":"Claims transformation and permissions"},"num":"3.8"},{"q":"How do you implement 'users can only access their own orders'?","a":"<p>Resource-based authorization: load the order, then call IAuthorizationService.AuthorizeAsync(user, order, requirement) with an AuthorizationHandler&lt;TRequirement, Order&gt; that checks ownership. Or always scope queries by the current user ID. Return 404 rather than 403 to avoid revealing the resource exists. This prevents IDOR.</p>\n","chapter":"auth","section":{"id":"claims-transformation-and-permissions","text":"Claims transformation and permissions"},"num":"3.8"},{"q":"How do you require authentication on every endpoint by default?","a":"<p>Set a fallback policy: <code>AddAuthorizationBuilder().SetFallbackPolicy(new AuthorizationPolicyBuilder().RequireAuthenticatedUser().Build())</code>, then mark public endpoints with AllowAnonymous.</p>\n","chapter":"auth","section":{"id":"claims-transformation-and-permissions","text":"Claims transformation and permissions"},"num":"3.8"},{"q":"What is ASP.NET Core Identity and is it an OAuth server?","a":"<p>A membership library for user storage, password hashing, lockout, 2FA, confirmations and external logins, persisted with EF Core. It&#39;s not an OAuth 2.0/OIDC authorization server; for that you use Entra ID/Auth0/Okta/Keycloak or build on Identity with Duende IdentityServer or OpenIddict.</p>\n","chapter":"auth","section":{"id":"claims-transformation-and-permissions","text":"Claims transformation and permissions"},"num":"3.8"},{"q":"Why do you need to configure Data Protection when running multiple instances with cookie auth?","a":"<p>Auth cookies (and antiforgery tokens) are encrypted with Data Protection keys. By default each instance has its own keys, so a cookie issued by one instance can&#39;t be decrypted by another and users get logged out. Persist the key ring to shared storage (Blob, Redis, DB) protected by Key Vault.</p>\n","chapter":"auth","section":{"id":"claims-transformation-and-permissions","text":"Claims transformation and permissions"},"num":"3.8"},{"q":"Why shouldn't you create a new HttpClient for every request?","a":"<p>Each instance (with its own handler) opens new connections. Disposing it closes sockets that linger in TIME_WAIT, so under load you exhaust ephemeral ports (socket exhaustion), and every call pays new TCP/TLS handshakes.</p>\n","chapter":"http-client","section":{"id":"testing-code-that-uses-httpclient","text":"Testing code that uses HttpClient"},"num":"3.9"},{"q":"Then why not just use one static HttpClient?","a":"<p>Its pooled connections live forever, so DNS changes aren&#39;t observed and you can keep hitting old IPs after failovers or deployments. Fix it with IHttpClientFactory, which recycles handlers, or by setting SocketsHttpHandler.PooledConnectionLifetime on a long-lived client.</p>\n","chapter":"http-client","section":{"id":"testing-code-that-uses-httpclient","text":"Testing code that uses HttpClient"},"num":"3.9"},{"q":"What does IHttpClientFactory do?","a":"<p>It creates HttpClient instances whose underlying handlers are pooled and periodically recycled (2 min default), avoiding both socket exhaustion and stale DNS. It centralises configuration (named/typed clients), lets you compose DelegatingHandlers, integrates logging and resilience (Polly), and works with DI.</p>\n","chapter":"http-client","section":{"id":"testing-code-that-uses-httpclient","text":"Testing code that uses HttpClient"},"num":"3.9"},{"q":"Named vs typed clients?","a":"<p>Named clients are configured under a name and created with factory.CreateClient(&quot;name&quot;). Typed clients are classes that receive a preconfigured HttpClient via their constructor and expose domain methods; they&#39;re strongly typed, encapsulate the API and are usually preferred.</p>\n","chapter":"http-client","section":{"id":"testing-code-that-uses-httpclient","text":"Testing code that uses HttpClient"},"num":"3.9"},{"q":"What is a DelegatingHandler?","a":"<p>A handler in HttpClient&#39;s outbound pipeline that can inspect or modify requests and responses before passing them on with base.SendAsync. Used for auth tokens, correlation headers, logging and resilience; added with AddHttpMessageHandler.</p>\n","chapter":"http-client","section":{"id":"testing-code-that-uses-httpclient","text":"Testing code that uses HttpClient"},"num":"3.9"},{"q":"How do you set timeouts for outbound calls?","a":"<p>HttpClient.Timeout (default 100 s, usually too long), or better, the resilience pipeline&#39;s total and per-attempt timeouts. Always pass a CancellationToken from the incoming request so calls stop if the client disconnects.</p>\n","chapter":"http-client","section":{"id":"testing-code-that-uses-httpclient","text":"Testing code that uses HttpClient"},"num":"3.9"},{"q":"How do you unit test a class that uses HttpClient?","a":"<p>Construct the HttpClient with a stub HttpMessageHandler that returns canned responses (or use a mocking library for handlers), or run WireMock.Net for higher-fidelity tests. Don&#39;t mock HttpClient directly.</p>\n","chapter":"http-client","section":{"id":"testing-code-that-uses-httpclient","text":"Testing code that uses HttpClient"},"num":"3.9"},{"q":"What caching options does ASP.NET Core provide?","a":"<p>IMemoryCache (in-process objects), IDistributedCache (shared byte cache backed by Redis, SQL, etc.), HybridCache (.NET 9: two-level memory plus distributed with stampede protection and tags), output caching (whole server-side responses with tag eviction), and HTTP response caching via Cache-Control/ETag headers for browsers and CDNs.</p>\n","chapter":"caching-ratelimit-cors","section":{"id":"response-compression-and-request-timeouts","text":"Response compression and request timeouts"},"num":"3.10"},{"q":"In-memory vs distributed cache?","a":"<p>In-memory is fastest but per instance (not shared, lost on restart, inconsistent across a farm). Distributed (Redis) is shared and survives app restarts, but costs a network hop and serialization. HybridCache combines both.</p>\n","chapter":"caching-ratelimit-cors","section":{"id":"response-compression-and-request-timeouts","text":"Response compression and request timeouts"},"num":"3.10"},{"q":"What is the cache-aside pattern?","a":"<p>Check the cache first; on a miss, load from the source and store it with an expiry; on writes, update the source and invalidate (remove) the cache entry. It&#39;s the most common caching pattern.</p>\n","chapter":"caching-ratelimit-cors","section":{"id":"response-compression-and-request-timeouts","text":"Response compression and request timeouts"},"num":"3.10"},{"q":"What is a cache stampede and how do you prevent it?","a":"<p>Many concurrent requests miss the same expired hot key and all recompute it, overloading the database. Prevent it with request coalescing (one computes, the rest wait, as HybridCache does), locks, jittered expirations, refresh-ahead, or serve-stale-while-revalidate.</p>\n","chapter":"caching-ratelimit-cors","section":{"id":"response-compression-and-request-timeouts","text":"Response compression and request timeouts"},"num":"3.10"},{"q":"How do you invalidate cached data?","a":"<p>Remove keys when the underlying data changes (in the write path or via change events), use tags or versioned keys to evict groups, and keep TTLs as a safety net. Prefer deleting over updating cache entries to avoid races.</p>\n","chapter":"caching-ratelimit-cors","section":{"id":"response-compression-and-request-timeouts","text":"Response compression and request timeouts"},"num":"3.10"},{"q":"What rate limiting algorithms exist and how do you apply them in ASP.NET Core?","a":"<p>Fixed window, sliding window, token bucket and concurrency. Register policies with AddRateLimiter (partitioned per user/IP/API key), add UseRateLimiter, and attach with RequireRateLimiting or [EnableRateLimiting]. Rejections return 429 with Retry-After. Counters are per instance, so enforce global limits at a gateway or with a distributed store.</p>\n","chapter":"caching-ratelimit-cors","section":{"id":"response-compression-and-request-timeouts","text":"Response compression and request timeouts"},"num":"3.10"},{"q":"What is CORS and does it protect your API?","a":"<p>A browser mechanism that relaxes the same-origin policy when the server returns Access-Control-Allow-* headers, letting JS from another origin read responses. It protects users&#39; browsers from malicious sites, not your API, because non-browser clients ignore it. API protection comes from authentication and authorization.</p>\n","chapter":"caching-ratelimit-cors","section":{"id":"response-compression-and-request-timeouts","text":"Response compression and request timeouts"},"num":"3.10"},{"q":"What is a preflight request?","a":"<p>An automatic OPTIONS request the browser sends before &quot;non-simple&quot; cross-origin requests (custom headers like Authorization, JSON content type, PUT/DELETE) to ask which origins, methods and headers are allowed. The CORS middleware answers it; it must run before authentication.</p>\n","chapter":"caching-ratelimit-cors","section":{"id":"response-compression-and-request-timeouts","text":"Response compression and request timeouts"},"num":"3.10"},{"q":"What's the difference between output caching and response caching?","a":"<p>Response caching follows HTTP Cache-Control semantics and is mainly for client/proxy caching. Output caching is server-controlled: policies per endpoint, vary-by rules, tag-based eviction, storage in memory or Redis, independent of client headers, and safe defaults (anonymous GETs only).</p>\n","chapter":"caching-ratelimit-cors","section":{"id":"response-compression-and-request-timeouts","text":"Response compression and request timeouts"},"num":"3.10"},{"q":"What is the difference between IHostedService and BackgroundService?","a":"<p>IHostedService is the interface the host calls (StartAsync/StopAsync). BackgroundService is an abstract implementation that starts your ExecuteAsync loop on StartAsync and cancels its stoppingToken on StopAsync, so you implement one long-running method.</p>\n","chapter":"background-work","section":{"id":"running-a-job-once-across-replicas","text":"Running a job once across replicas"},"num":"3.11"},{"q":"How do you use a DbContext inside a BackgroundService?","a":"<p>Hosted services are singletons, so inject IServiceScopeFactory and create a scope per unit of work (CreateAsyncScope), resolving DbContext from scope.ServiceProvider and disposing the scope afterwards. Or inject IDbContextFactory&lt;T&gt;.</p>\n","chapter":"background-work","section":{"id":"running-a-job-once-across-replicas","text":"Running a job once across replicas"},"num":"3.11"},{"q":"What happens if a BackgroundService throws an unhandled exception?","a":"<p>Since .NET 6 the default BackgroundServiceExceptionBehavior is StopHost, so the whole application shuts down. Catch exceptions inside the loop, log them and continue (or deliberately fail).</p>\n","chapter":"background-work","section":{"id":"running-a-job-once-across-replicas","text":"Running a job once across replicas"},"num":"3.11"},{"q":"How do you run periodic work?","a":"<p>A BackgroundService with PeriodicTimer and WaitForNextTickAsync(stoppingToken) for simple cases. For cron schedules, persistence, retries and single execution across a cluster, use Hangfire or Quartz.NET, or a platform scheduler (Functions timer trigger, Kubernetes CronJob).</p>\n","chapter":"background-work","section":{"id":"running-a-job-once-across-replicas","text":"Running a job once across replicas"},"num":"3.11"},{"q":"What is Channel<T> and when would you use it?","a":"<p>An async producer/consumer queue in System.Threading.Channels. Use it to hand off work from requests to an in-process background consumer with backpressure (bounded channels). It&#39;s in-memory only, so items are lost on restart; use durable queues for critical work.</p>\n","chapter":"background-work","section":{"id":"running-a-job-once-across-replicas","text":"Running a job once across replicas"},"num":"3.11"},{"q":"Your app runs on 3 instances: how do you make sure a scheduled job runs only once?","a":"<p>Use a distributed lock or lease (SQL app lock, Redis, Blob lease), a job scheduler with clustering (Hangfire/Quartz), a dedicated single-instance worker, or a platform scheduler like a Functions timer trigger or Kubernetes CronJob.</p>\n","chapter":"background-work","section":{"id":"running-a-job-once-across-replicas","text":"Running a job once across replicas"},"num":"3.11"},{"q":"Why is fire-and-forget (Task.Run without await) in a controller a bad idea?","a":"<p>The work isn&#39;t tracked by the host (lost on shutdown), exceptions go unobserved, and it often uses request-scoped services (DbContext, HttpContext) that are disposed when the request ends. Use a queue plus a hosted service or a durable job system.</p>\n","chapter":"background-work","section":{"id":"running-a-job-once-across-replicas","text":"Running a job once across replicas"},"num":"3.11"},{"q":"What makes an API RESTful?","a":"<p>Resources identified by URLs, manipulated through standard HTTP methods with correct status codes, stateless requests, cacheable responses, and representations like JSON. Strictly, it also includes hypermedia (HATEOAS), which most practical &quot;REST&quot; APIs (maturity level 2) omit.</p>\n","chapter":"api-design","section":{"id":"openapi-documentation","text":"OpenAPI documentation"},"num":"3.12"},{"q":"What do safe and idempotent mean? Which methods are which?","a":"<p>Safe methods don&#39;t change server state: GET, HEAD, OPTIONS. Idempotent methods have the same effect whether called once or many times: GET, HEAD, OPTIONS, PUT, DELETE. POST and PATCH aren&#39;t idempotent by default. Idempotency is what makes automatic retries safe.</p>\n","chapter":"api-design","section":{"id":"openapi-documentation","text":"OpenAPI documentation"},"num":"3.12"},{"q":"PUT vs PATCH vs POST?","a":"<p>POST creates a subordinate resource or triggers an action (not idempotent; 201 with a Location header). PUT replaces the entire resource at a known URL (idempotent). PATCH applies a partial update via JSON Merge Patch or JSON Patch.</p>\n","chapter":"api-design","section":{"id":"openapi-documentation","text":"OpenAPI documentation"},"num":"3.12"},{"q":"401 vs 403?","a":"<p>401 means the request isn&#39;t authenticated (no/invalid/expired credentials, and the response includes WWW-Authenticate). 403 means the caller is authenticated but not permitted to perform the action.</p>\n","chapter":"api-design","section":{"id":"openapi-documentation","text":"OpenAPI documentation"},"num":"3.12"},{"q":"How do you make a POST endpoint safe to retry?","a":"<p>Require an Idempotency-Key header. Store the key atomically (unique constraint) along with the response, and on a repeat with the same key, return the stored response instead of re-executing. Reject reuse of the same key with a different payload.</p>\n","chapter":"api-design","section":{"id":"openapi-documentation","text":"OpenAPI documentation"},"num":"3.12"},{"q":"Offset vs cursor pagination?","a":"<p>Offset (page/pageSize, OFFSET/FETCH) is simple and supports jumping to any page, but it slows down on deep pages and shifts when data changes. Cursor/keyset pagination (WHERE key &gt; lastKey) uses the index for constant performance and stable results, but can&#39;t jump to arbitrary pages.</p>\n","chapter":"api-design","section":{"id":"openapi-documentation","text":"OpenAPI documentation"},"num":"3.12"},{"q":"How do you version an API, and what counts as a breaking change?","a":"<p>Common strategies are URL segment (/v2), query string, header or media type, e.g. with Asp.Versioning. Breaking changes include removing or renaming fields/endpoints, changing types or semantics, making optional inputs required, and changing status codes. Additive optional changes are non-breaking if clients tolerate unknown fields.</p>\n","chapter":"api-design","section":{"id":"openapi-documentation","text":"OpenAPI documentation"},"num":"3.12"},{"q":"How do ETags help with concurrency?","a":"<p>The server returns an ETag (version) with a resource. Clients send If-Match with updates, and if the resource changed meanwhile the server returns 412 Precondition Failed, preventing lost updates. If-None-Match on GET enables 304 Not Modified responses.</p>\n","chapter":"api-design","section":{"id":"openapi-documentation","text":"OpenAPI documentation"},"num":"3.12"},{"q":"How would you design an endpoint that generates a large report?","a":"<p>Return 202 Accepted with a Location header pointing to an operation-status resource, process in the background (queue plus worker), let the client poll the status (with Retry-After) or receive a webhook/SignalR notification, and finally link to the result resource.</p>\n","chapter":"api-design","section":{"id":"openapi-documentation","text":"OpenAPI documentation"},"num":"3.12"},{"q":"When would you choose gRPC over REST?","a":"<p>For internal service-to-service communication where performance (binary Protobuf, HTTP/2 multiplexing), strict contract-first schemas with generated clients, and streaming matter. REST/JSON remains better for public APIs and browsers (gRPC needs gRPC-Web or JSON transcoding there).</p>\n","chapter":"app-models","section":{"id":"graphql-and-others-briefly","text":"GraphQL and others, briefly"},"num":"3.13"},{"q":"How do you evolve a gRPC contract without breaking clients?","a":"<p>Add new fields with new field numbers, never change or reuse existing field numbers or types, mark removed fields as reserved, and treat all fields as optional. Protobuf ignores unknown fields, so old and new clients interoperate.</p>\n","chapter":"app-models","section":{"id":"graphql-and-others-briefly","text":"GraphQL and others, briefly"},"num":"3.13"},{"q":"What is SignalR and how does it work?","a":"<p>A library for real-time bidirectional communication. Clients connect to a hub, and the transport is negotiated (WebSockets, then Server-Sent Events, then long polling). Server code calls client methods on all clients, users, groups or connections via the hub or IHubContext, and clients call hub methods.</p>\n","chapter":"app-models","section":{"id":"graphql-and-others-briefly","text":"GraphQL and others, briefly"},"num":"3.13"},{"q":"How do you scale SignalR across multiple servers?","a":"<p>Use a backplane such as Redis so messages published on one server reach clients connected to others, or use Azure SignalR Service, which manages the client connections. Self-hosted WebSocket setups also need sticky sessions unless negotiation is skipped.</p>\n","chapter":"app-models","section":{"id":"graphql-and-others-briefly","text":"GraphQL and others, briefly"},"num":"3.13"},{"q":"Razor Pages vs MVC?","a":"<p>Both render HTML with Razor on the server and share tag helpers, model binding and validation. MVC separates controllers and views and uses action-based routing; Razor Pages is page-centric, pairing each .cshtml with a PageModel and using file-based routing, which is simpler for form-centred apps.</p>\n","chapter":"app-models","section":{"id":"graphql-and-others-briefly","text":"GraphQL and others, briefly"},"num":"3.13"},{"q":"What are Blazor's render modes?","a":"<p>Static server-side rendering (no interactivity), Interactive Server (logic on the server, UI updates over SignalR), Interactive WebAssembly (the .NET runtime in the browser) and Interactive Auto (server first, then WebAssembly). Chosen per component or per app since .NET 8.</p>\n","chapter":"app-models","section":{"id":"graphql-and-others-briefly","text":"GraphQL and others, briefly"},"num":"3.13"},{"q":"What's the difference between a clustered and a non-clustered index?","a":"<p>A clustered index defines the physical order of the table: its leaf level is the data rows, and there can be only one (usually the PK). A non-clustered index is a separate B-tree of key columns pointing to rows via the clustered key, so non-covered columns need key lookups; there can be many. INCLUDE columns make a non-clustered index covering.</p>\n","chapter":"relational-fundamentals","section":{"id":"sql-server-vs-postgresql-in-net-shops","text":"SQL Server vs PostgreSQL in .NET shops"},"num":"4.1"},{"q":"How do you decide which columns to index?","a":"<p>Columns in frequent WHERE, JOIN, ORDER BY and GROUP BY clauses of important queries on large tables, especially foreign keys. Order composite indexes with equality columns first and range/sort columns after, add INCLUDE for covering, prefer selective columns, and balance against write overhead. Verify with execution plans and Query Store.</p>\n","chapter":"relational-fundamentals","section":{"id":"sql-server-vs-postgresql-in-net-shops","text":"SQL Server vs PostgreSQL in .NET shops"},"num":"4.1"},{"q":"What does sargable mean?","a":"<p>A predicate the engine can satisfy with an index seek. Wrapping the indexed column in a function, leading wildcards and implicit type conversions make predicates non-sargable, forcing scans.</p>\n","chapter":"relational-fundamentals","section":{"id":"sql-server-vs-postgresql-in-net-shops","text":"SQL Server vs PostgreSQL in .NET shops"},"num":"4.1"},{"q":"Explain ACID.","a":"<p>Atomicity (all or nothing), Consistency (constraints preserved), Isolation (concurrent transactions don&#39;t interfere beyond the chosen level) and Durability (committed data survives failures, via the transaction log).</p>\n","chapter":"relational-fundamentals","section":{"id":"sql-server-vs-postgresql-in-net-shops","text":"SQL Server vs PostgreSQL in .NET shops"},"num":"4.1"},{"q":"What are isolation levels and which anomalies do they prevent?","a":"<p>Read Uncommitted allows dirty reads. Read Committed prevents dirty reads (the default). Repeatable Read also prevents non-repeatable reads. Serializable prevents phantoms too. Snapshot/RCSI use row versioning so readers see committed versions without blocking writers. Higher isolation means more correctness but more blocking or conflicts.</p>\n","chapter":"relational-fundamentals","section":{"id":"sql-server-vs-postgresql-in-net-shops","text":"SQL Server vs PostgreSQL in .NET shops"},"num":"4.1"},{"q":"What is a deadlock and how do you handle it?","a":"<p>Two transactions each wait for a lock held by the other. The database kills one (SQL error 1205). Prevent it by accessing resources in a consistent order, keeping transactions short, indexing so fewer rows are locked, and using row versioning. Handle it by retrying the victim transaction.</p>\n","chapter":"relational-fundamentals","section":{"id":"sql-server-vs-postgresql-in-net-shops","text":"SQL Server vs PostgreSQL in .NET shops"},"num":"4.1"},{"q":"Why are random GUIDs bad clustered keys?","a":"<p>Inserts land at random positions in the B-tree, causing page splits, fragmentation and poor cache use; they&#39;re also 16 bytes, which widens every non-clustered index. Use sequential IDs (IDENTITY, NEWSEQUENTIALID, UUIDv7 via Guid.CreateVersion7) or keep GUIDs as non-clustered PKs.</p>\n","chapter":"relational-fundamentals","section":{"id":"sql-server-vs-postgresql-in-net-shops","text":"SQL Server vs PostgreSQL in .NET shops"},"num":"4.1"},{"q":"What is connection pooling and what causes pool exhaustion?","a":"<p>Reusing open physical connections per connection string so Open is cheap. Exhaustion (timeouts obtaining a connection) comes from connections not being disposed, long-running transactions or queries, sync-over-async blocking while holding connections, or more concurrent work than the pool size.</p>\n","chapter":"relational-fundamentals","section":{"id":"sql-server-vs-postgresql-in-net-shops","text":"SQL Server vs PostgreSQL in .NET shops"},"num":"4.1"},{"q":"What is parameter sniffing?","a":"<p>SQL Server compiles and caches a plan using the first parameter values it sees. If those are unrepresentative, later executions reuse a suboptimal plan. Fixes include OPTION(RECOMPILE), OPTIMIZE FOR, better indexing, Query Store plan forcing, or PSP optimisation.</p>\n","chapter":"relational-fundamentals","section":{"id":"sql-server-vs-postgresql-in-net-shops","text":"SQL Server vs PostgreSQL in .NET shops"},"num":"4.1"},{"q":"What are the main ADO.NET classes?","a":"<p>DbConnection (a connection to the database, pooled), DbCommand (SQL text or stored procedure with parameters), DbDataReader (fast forward-only streaming over results), DbParameter, and DbTransaction, each implemented by providers such as SqlClient and Npgsql. Newer: DbDataSource as a connection factory.</p>\n","chapter":"ado-dapper","section":{"id":"dapper-vs-ef-core-the-real-decision-not-a-popularity-contest","text":"Dapper vs EF Core: the real decision, not a popularity contest"},"num":"4.2"},{"q":"Why must you parameterise SQL?","a":"<p>To prevent SQL injection (input is sent as data, never parsed as SQL) and to let the database reuse cached execution plans. Concatenating input into SQL is a critical vulnerability.</p>\n","chapter":"ado-dapper","section":{"id":"dapper-vs-ef-core-the-real-decision-not-a-popularity-contest","text":"Dapper vs EF Core: the real decision, not a popularity contest"},"num":"4.2"},{"q":"What is Dapper and how is it different from EF Core?","a":"<p>A micro-ORM: extension methods on IDbConnection that execute your SQL and map results to objects (and objects to parameters) with near-raw ADO.NET performance. It has no change tracking, LINQ provider, relationship management or migrations, unlike the full ORM EF Core.</p>\n","chapter":"ado-dapper","section":{"id":"dapper-vs-ef-core-the-real-decision-not-a-popularity-contest","text":"Dapper vs EF Core: the real decision, not a popularity contest"},"num":"4.2"},{"q":"How do you map a join into nested objects with Dapper?","a":"<p>Multi-mapping: QueryAsync&lt;T1, T2, TResult&gt; with a mapping function that combines the objects per row, and splitOn naming the column where the second object&#39;s columns start. Use a dictionary to de-duplicate parents for one-to-many.</p>\n","chapter":"ado-dapper","section":{"id":"dapper-vs-ef-core-the-real-decision-not-a-popularity-contest","text":"Dapper vs EF Core: the real decision, not a popularity contest"},"num":"4.2"},{"q":"How would you insert 1 million rows efficiently?","a":"<p>Use SqlBulkCopy (or Npgsql binary COPY) to stream rows into a staging or target table in batches, then a set-based MERGE/INSERT…SELECT if needed. Avoid row-by-row INSERTs and EF change tracking for large imports.</p>\n","chapter":"ado-dapper","section":{"id":"dapper-vs-ef-core-the-real-decision-not-a-popularity-contest","text":"Dapper vs EF Core: the real decision, not a popularity contest"},"num":"4.2"},{"q":"When would you choose Dapper over EF Core?","a":"<p>For read-heavy, performance-critical or complex SQL (reports, CTEs, window functions, hints), when the team prefers explicit SQL, or for CQRS read models. EF Core for rich domain writes, change tracking, relationships and migrations. Using both is common.</p>\n","chapter":"ado-dapper","section":{"id":"dapper-vs-ef-core-the-real-decision-not-a-popularity-contest","text":"Dapper vs EF Core: the real decision, not a popularity contest"},"num":"4.2"},{"q":"What is DbContext and what lifetime should it have?","a":"<p>The EF Core session with the database: it owns the connection, the model and the change tracker, and implements the Unit of Work and Repository patterns. Register it as scoped (one per request or unit of work). It&#39;s not thread-safe and shouldn&#39;t be long-lived; use IDbContextFactory for background or parallel work.</p>\n","chapter":"efcore-fundamentals","section":{"id":"providers","text":"Providers"},"num":"4.3"},{"q":"What's the difference between AddDbContext and AddDbContextPool?","a":"<p>Both give scoped contexts. Pooling reuses DbContext instances from a pool (resetting their state) instead of constructing new ones, reducing allocation and setup cost in high-throughput apps. The context then must not hold custom per-request state in fields.</p>\n","chapter":"efcore-fundamentals","section":{"id":"providers","text":"Providers"},"num":"4.3"},{"q":"Data annotations or Fluent API?","a":"<p>Fluent API (often in IEntityTypeConfiguration&lt;T&gt; classes) is more powerful, keeps persistence concerns out of the domain model, and takes precedence. Annotations are convenient for simple cases. Many teams use Fluent API exclusively.</p>\n","chapter":"efcore-fundamentals","section":{"id":"providers","text":"Providers"},"num":"4.3"},{"q":"Explain EF Core's entity states.","a":"<p>Added (INSERT on save), Unchanged (no action), Modified (UPDATE of changed columns), Deleted (DELETE) and Detached (not tracked). EF detects modifications by comparing current values with snapshots taken when entities were loaded or attached.</p>\n","chapter":"efcore-fundamentals","section":{"id":"providers","text":"Providers"},"num":"4.3"},{"q":"How do migrations work and how do you apply them in production?","a":"<p><code>dotnet ef migrations add</code> generates a migration class (Up/Down) from the difference between the model and the last snapshot; applied migrations are recorded in __EFMigrationsHistory. In production, generate an idempotent SQL script or a migration bundle and run it in the deployment pipeline rather than calling Migrate() at startup across many instances. Keep migrations backward-compatible for zero-downtime deploys.</p>\n","chapter":"efcore-fundamentals","section":{"id":"providers","text":"Providers"},"num":"4.3"},{"q":"What are owned types and when do you use them?","a":"<p>Types without their own identity that belong to an owner entity (value objects like Address or Money). They&#39;re stored inline as columns in the owner&#39;s table (OwnsOne), in a separate table (OwnsMany), or as JSON (ToJson). EF 8 complex types are an alternative with value semantics.</p>\n","chapter":"efcore-fundamentals","section":{"id":"providers","text":"Providers"},"num":"4.3"},{"q":"How do you configure a many-to-many relationship?","a":"<p>Add collection navigations on both sides; EF Core 5+ creates the join table automatically. Use UsingEntity with an explicit join entity when you need extra columns on the relationship.</p>\n","chapter":"efcore-fundamentals","section":{"id":"providers","text":"Providers"},"num":"4.3"},{"q":"What does SaveChanges do?","a":"<p>Runs DetectChanges, orders the pending INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE commands to respect FKs, batches them, executes them in a single transaction (unless one is already active), propagates generated keys, and resets entity states to Unchanged. It throws DbUpdateException or DbUpdateConcurrencyException on failure.</p>\n","chapter":"efcore-fundamentals","section":{"id":"providers","text":"Providers"},"num":"4.3"},{"q":"What is the N+1 problem in EF Core and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>Loading a list with one query and then triggering an extra query per item to get related data, typically through lazy loading or querying inside a loop. Fix it with projections (Select into DTOs, translated into joins), eager loading (Include), or batching (WHERE Id IN via Contains). Detect it by logging SQL or inspecting traces.</p>\n","chapter":"efcore-querying","section":{"id":"checklist-for-a-slow-ef-core-endpoint","text":"Checklist for a slow EF Core endpoint"},"num":"4.4"},{"q":"When should you use AsNoTracking?","a":"<p>For read-only queries: entities aren&#39;t snapshot-tracked, which saves memory and CPU. Projections into DTOs are untracked automatically. Use AsNoTrackingWithIdentityResolution when the same related entity appears in many rows and you want shared instances.</p>\n","chapter":"efcore-querying","section":{"id":"checklist-for-a-slow-ef-core-endpoint","text":"Checklist for a slow EF Core endpoint"},"num":"4.4"},{"q":"Eager vs explicit vs lazy loading?","a":"<p>Eager loads related data in the same query via Include/ThenInclude. Explicit loads it later on demand with Entry(...).Collection/Reference().Load. Lazy loads automatically when a navigation property is first accessed (proxies), which easily causes N+1. For read endpoints, projection usually beats all three.</p>\n","chapter":"efcore-querying","section":{"id":"checklist-for-a-slow-ef-core-endpoint","text":"Checklist for a slow EF Core endpoint"},"num":"4.4"},{"q":"What is a split query and when would you use it?","a":"<p>AsSplitQuery makes EF Core load included collections with separate SQL statements instead of one big JOIN, avoiding cartesian explosion when including multiple collections. The trade-off is extra round trips and no single snapshot without a transaction.</p>\n","chapter":"efcore-querying","section":{"id":"checklist-for-a-slow-ef-core-endpoint","text":"Checklist for a slow EF Core endpoint"},"num":"4.4"},{"q":"Why might an EF Core query throw 'could not be translated'?","a":"<p>The LINQ contains something the provider can&#39;t convert to SQL, such as a call to a custom C# method in Where. Since EF Core 3, EF throws rather than silently evaluating on the client. Rewrite using translatable expressions, mapped DB functions, or filter in memory explicitly after a server-side filter.</p>\n","chapter":"efcore-querying","section":{"id":"checklist-for-a-slow-ef-core-endpoint","text":"Checklist for a slow EF Core endpoint"},"num":"4.4"},{"q":"How do you see the SQL EF Core generates?","a":"<p>ToQueryString() on a query, LogTo/logging the Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Database.Command category, EnableSensitiveDataLogging in development for parameter values, TagWith to label queries, and OpenTelemetry instrumentation or Query Store in production.</p>\n","chapter":"efcore-querying","section":{"id":"checklist-for-a-slow-ef-core-endpoint","text":"Checklist for a slow EF Core endpoint"},"num":"4.4"},{"q":"How do you run raw SQL safely with EF Core?","a":"<p>Use FromSql / SqlQuery / ExecuteSql with interpolated strings: they take FormattableString and turn interpolated values into DbParameters. Avoid the Raw variants with concatenated input.</p>\n","chapter":"efcore-querying","section":{"id":"checklist-for-a-slow-ef-core-endpoint","text":"Checklist for a slow EF Core endpoint"},"num":"4.4"},{"q":"How would you speed up a hot, read-heavy EF Core query?","a":"<p>Project into a DTO with only the needed columns, use no-tracking, make sure a supporting index exists (check the plan), consider a compiled query and DbContext pooling, cache the result if staleness is acceptable, and use keyset pagination for large lists.</p>\n","chapter":"efcore-querying","section":{"id":"checklist-for-a-slow-ef-core-endpoint","text":"Checklist for a slow EF Core endpoint"},"num":"4.4"},{"q":"How does optimistic concurrency work in EF Core?","a":"<p>Mark a concurrency token (a SQL Server rowversion with [Timestamp]/IsRowVersion, or a property with IsConcurrencyToken). EF includes the original value in the UPDATE/DELETE WHERE clause; if no row is affected, it throws DbUpdateConcurrencyException, which you handle by reloading, merging, retrying or returning 409/412 to the client.</p>\n","chapter":"efcore-advanced","section":{"id":"testing-ef-core-code","text":"Testing EF Core code"},"num":"4.5"},{"q":"Optimistic vs pessimistic concurrency?","a":"<p>Optimistic: no locks on read; conflicts are detected at write time via versions. It scales well and suits web apps where conflicts are rare. Pessimistic: lock rows when reading (UPDLOCK / FOR UPDATE) so others wait. It suits high-contention critical sections but reduces concurrency and risks deadlocks.</p>\n","chapter":"efcore-advanced","section":{"id":"testing-ef-core-code","text":"Testing EF Core code"},"num":"4.5"},{"q":"Is SaveChanges transactional? When do you need an explicit transaction?","a":"<p>Yes: one SaveChanges is a single transaction. Use BeginTransaction when multiple SaveChanges, ExecuteUpdate/raw SQL or Dapper calls must be atomic, or you need a specific isolation level. With retrying execution strategies, wrap the whole transaction in strategy.ExecuteAsync.</p>\n","chapter":"efcore-advanced","section":{"id":"testing-ef-core-code","text":"Testing EF Core code"},"num":"4.5"},{"q":"What are ExecuteUpdate and ExecuteDelete, and what are their caveats?","a":"<p>EF 7+ methods that run set-based UPDATE/DELETE statements directly from a LINQ filter without loading entities, which is much faster for bulk changes. They execute immediately, bypass the change tracker and SaveChanges interceptors (so auditing and soft delete there won&#39;t run), and tracked entities in memory aren&#39;t updated.</p>\n","chapter":"efcore-advanced","section":{"id":"testing-ef-core-code","text":"Testing EF Core code"},"num":"4.5"},{"q":"How would you implement soft delete in EF Core?","a":"<p>An IsDeleted (and DeletedAt) column, a global query filter <code>HasQueryFilter(e =&gt; !e.IsDeleted)</code> so normal queries exclude deleted rows, and a SaveChanges interceptor that converts Deleted states into Modified with IsDeleted = true. Use IgnoreQueryFilters for admin or restore scenarios, and consider filtered unique indexes.</p>\n","chapter":"efcore-advanced","section":{"id":"testing-ef-core-code","text":"Testing EF Core code"},"num":"4.5"},{"q":"How would you implement multi-tenancy with EF Core?","a":"<p>Options: a TenantId column with a global query filter based on a scoped tenant service (plus row-level security as defence in depth); a schema per tenant; or a database per tenant with the connection string chosen per request. Each trades isolation against cost and operational complexity.</p>\n","chapter":"efcore-advanced","section":{"id":"testing-ef-core-code","text":"Testing EF Core code"},"num":"4.5"},{"q":"What are EF Core interceptors used for?","a":"<p>Cross-cutting hooks into EF operations: SaveChanges interceptors for auditing, soft delete, domain events and outbox; command interceptors for logging, hints or tagging SQL; connection interceptors for session context or tokens; materialization interceptors for post-processing entities.</p>\n","chapter":"efcore-advanced","section":{"id":"testing-ef-core-code","text":"Testing EF Core code"},"num":"4.5"},{"q":"Why not use the InMemory provider for tests?","a":"<p>It isn&#39;t a relational database: it doesn&#39;t enforce constraints, transactions or SQL semantics, and it translates queries differently, so tests can pass while real queries fail. Use the real engine via Testcontainers (or at least SQLite) for data access tests.</p>\n","chapter":"efcore-advanced","section":{"id":"testing-ef-core-code","text":"Testing EF Core code"},"num":"4.5"},{"q":"When would you choose a NoSQL database over a relational one?","a":"<p>When access patterns are mostly by key or aggregate, the schema is flexible or nested, you need horizontal scale or global distribution beyond a single SQL server, or very low latency at high throughput, or a specialised model (graph, search, time series). Relational remains the default for transactional business data with joins and reporting.</p>\n","chapter":"nosql-redis","section":{"id":"choosing-a-data-store","text":"Choosing a data store"},"num":"4.6"},{"q":"Explain the CAP theorem.","a":"<p>In a distributed system, when a network partition occurs you can provide either consistency (all reads see the latest write or fail) or availability (all requests get a response, possibly stale), not both. PACELC adds that, even without partitions, you trade latency against consistency.</p>\n","chapter":"nosql-redis","section":{"id":"choosing-a-data-store","text":"Choosing a data store"},"num":"4.6"},{"q":"How do you choose a partition key in Cosmos DB?","a":"<p>High cardinality, even distribution of storage and requests (no hot partitions), and present in most queries so they stay single-partition. Consider transactional scope (single partition only) and the 20 GB logical partition limit; use hierarchical keys for big tenants.</p>\n","chapter":"nosql-redis","section":{"id":"choosing-a-data-store","text":"Choosing a data store"},"num":"4.6"},{"q":"Embed vs reference in document modelling?","a":"<p>Embed data that&#39;s read and updated together and bounded in size (order lines in an order). Reference data that&#39;s shared, unbounded, or updated independently (products, a customer&#39;s orders). Model for the access patterns, and accept some denormalisation.</p>\n","chapter":"nosql-redis","section":{"id":"choosing-a-data-store","text":"Choosing a data store"},"num":"4.6"},{"q":"What is Redis used for in .NET backends?","a":"<p>Distributed caching (IDistributedCache/HybridCache L2), session and cart storage, rate-limit counters, distributed locks, leaderboards and sorted indexes, pub/sub (SignalR backplane, cache invalidation) and streams. Use one shared ConnectionMultiplexer.</p>\n","chapter":"nosql-redis","section":{"id":"choosing-a-data-store","text":"Choosing a data store"},"num":"4.6"},{"q":"How would you implement a distributed lock with Redis, and what are the risks?","a":"<p>SET key token NX PX ttl to acquire (only if absent, with expiry), and release only if the value still matches your token (LockTake/LockRelease in StackExchange.Redis, or a Lua script). Risks: the TTL can expire while work continues, and failover can lose locks. Use fencing tokens and idempotent work for critical cases.</p>\n","chapter":"nosql-redis","section":{"id":"choosing-a-data-store","text":"Choosing a data store"},"num":"4.6"},{"q":"Why shouldn't you use Redis Pub/Sub for important events?","a":"<p>It&#39;s fire-and-forget: messages aren&#39;t persisted, and subscribers that are offline or slow miss them. Use Redis Streams with consumer groups or a real message broker (Service Bus, RabbitMQ, Kafka) for reliable delivery.</p>\n","chapter":"nosql-redis","section":{"id":"choosing-a-data-store","text":"Choosing a data store"},"num":"4.6"},{"q":"Explain SOLID with an example of each.","a":"<p>SRP: split an OrderService that prices, persists and emails into separate collaborators. OCP: add shipping carriers as new IShippingRate implementations instead of editing a switch. LSP: a ReadOnlyRepository throwing on Add breaks callers of IRepository, so it violates LSP. ISP: split a fat IUserService into IUserReader and IUserWriter. DIP: OrderService depends on an IOrderRepository defined in the domain and implemented by an EF Core class in infrastructure.</p>\n","chapter":"design-principles","section":{"id":"when-to-bend-the-rules","text":"When to bend the rules"},"num":"5.1"},{"q":"What does the Single Responsibility Principle really mean?","a":"<p>A module should have one reason to change, meaning it serves one actor or source of requirements. It&#39;s about cohesion around a responsibility, not about having only one method.</p>\n","chapter":"design-principles","section":{"id":"when-to-bend-the-rules","text":"When to bend the rules"},"num":"5.1"},{"q":"What is a Liskov Substitution violation you've seen in .NET?","a":"<p>Arrays implement IList&lt;T&gt; but throw NotSupportedException on Add; ReadOnlyCollection implements ICollection.Add by throwing; Square deriving from Rectangle; a repository implementation that throws for some interface methods. Each breaks code written against the base contract.</p>\n","chapter":"design-principles","section":{"id":"when-to-bend-the-rules","text":"When to bend the rules"},"num":"5.1"},{"q":"How does the Dependency Inversion Principle differ from Dependency Injection?","a":"<p>DIP is a design principle: high-level modules and low-level details both depend on abstractions owned by the high-level side, so compile-time dependencies point inward. DI is a technique for supplying concrete implementations to a class from outside, typically via a container. DI makes DIP practical, but you can use DI without following DIP.</p>\n","chapter":"design-principles","section":{"id":"when-to-bend-the-rules","text":"When to bend the rules"},"num":"5.1"},{"q":"What are coupling and cohesion?","a":"<p>Cohesion is how closely related the responsibilities inside a module are (aim high). Coupling is how much modules depend on each other&#39;s details (aim low). Good design increases cohesion within modules and reduces coupling between them, so changes stay local.</p>\n","chapter":"design-principles","section":{"id":"when-to-bend-the-rules","text":"When to bend the rules"},"num":"5.1"},{"q":"Is duplication always bad (DRY)?","a":"<p>No. DRY is about duplicated knowledge, not similar-looking code. If two pieces change for different reasons (different bounded contexts or actors), merging them couples them. Prefer duplication over the wrong abstraction; abstract once the pattern is clear (Rule of Three).</p>\n","chapter":"design-principles","section":{"id":"when-to-bend-the-rules","text":"When to bend the rules"},"num":"5.1"},{"q":"What is the Law of Demeter?","a":"<p>A method should only talk to its immediate collaborators (itself, its parameters, objects it creates, its fields), not reach through chains like a.B.C.D(). It reduces coupling to other objects&#39; internal structure.</p>\n","chapter":"design-principles","section":{"id":"when-to-bend-the-rules","text":"When to bend the rules"},"num":"5.1"},{"q":"When would you not create an interface for a class?","a":"<p>When there&#39;s a single stable implementation that isn&#39;t an I/O or volatile boundary and doesn&#39;t need to be faked in tests (simple domain services, value objects, mappers). Interfaces pay off at seams: persistence, external services, time, randomness, and real variation points.</p>\n","chapter":"design-principles","section":{"id":"when-to-bend-the-rules","text":"When to bend the rules"},"num":"5.1"},{"q":"Which design patterns does ASP.NET Core itself use?","a":"<p>Chain of Responsibility (the middleware pipeline, DelegatingHandlers, filters), Builder (WebApplicationBuilder, IServiceCollection, options builders), Factory (IHttpClientFactory, ILoggerFactory, IDbContextFactory), Decorator (DelegatingHandler, stream wrappers), Strategy (authentication schemes, naming policies, comparers), Observer (IOptionsMonitor.OnChange, change tokens), Template Method (BackgroundService, AuthenticationHandler), Adapter (logging providers), Facade (CreateBuilder) and Dependency Injection throughout.</p>\n","chapter":"design-patterns","section":{"id":"beyond-gof-patterns-youll-meet-in-net-backends","text":"Beyond GoF: patterns you'll meet in .NET backends"},"num":"5.2"},{"q":"Strategy vs State?","a":"<p>Both put varying behaviour behind an interface. In Strategy, the client or configuration chooses an algorithm and the strategies are independent of each other. In State, the object itself transitions between state objects as its internal state changes, and states know about the transitions.</p>\n","chapter":"design-patterns","section":{"id":"beyond-gof-patterns-youll-meet-in-net-backends","text":"Beyond GoF: patterns you'll meet in .NET backends"},"num":"5.2"},{"q":"Decorator vs Proxy vs Adapter?","a":"<p>Decorator and Proxy keep the same interface: a decorator adds behaviour (logging, caching), while a proxy controls access to the real object (lazy loading, remote, permissions). An adapter changes an interface into the one the client expects.</p>\n","chapter":"design-patterns","section":{"id":"beyond-gof-patterns-youll-meet-in-net-backends","text":"Beyond GoF: patterns you'll meet in .NET backends"},"num":"5.2"},{"q":"How do you implement the Decorator pattern with DI?","a":"<p>Register the real implementation, then register the interface with a decorator that receives the inner instance, either via a factory lambda (<code>sp =&gt; new CachingRepo(sp.GetRequiredService&lt;SqlRepo&gt;(), …)</code>) or with Scrutor&#39;s Decorate&lt;TInterface, TDecorator&gt;(). Decorators can be stacked.</p>\n","chapter":"design-patterns","section":{"id":"beyond-gof-patterns-youll-meet-in-net-backends","text":"Beyond GoF: patterns you'll meet in .NET backends"},"num":"5.2"},{"q":"Why is the Singleton pattern often called an anti-pattern, and what do you use instead?","a":"<p>A static Instance hides dependencies, introduces global mutable state, can&#39;t be replaced in tests, and couples callers to a concrete class. Use the DI container&#39;s singleton lifetime so there&#39;s still one instance, but it&#39;s injected explicitly and replaceable. It still must be thread-safe.</p>\n","chapter":"design-patterns","section":{"id":"beyond-gof-patterns-youll-meet-in-net-backends","text":"Beyond GoF: patterns you'll meet in .NET backends"},"num":"5.2"},{"q":"Where does the Chain of Responsibility pattern appear in .NET?","a":"<p>The ASP.NET Core middleware pipeline (each component handles or passes to next), HttpClient&#39;s DelegatingHandler pipeline, MVC and endpoint filters, MediatR pipeline behaviours, and authorization handlers.</p>\n","chapter":"design-patterns","section":{"id":"beyond-gof-patterns-youll-meet-in-net-backends","text":"Beyond GoF: patterns you'll meet in .NET backends"},"num":"5.2"},{"q":"What's the difference between the Command pattern and an event?","a":"<p>A command is a request to do something, addressed to one handler, named in the imperative (PlaceOrder), and it may be rejected. An event is a notification that something already happened, named in the past tense (OrderPlaced), published to zero or more subscribers, and it can&#39;t be rejected.</p>\n","chapter":"design-patterns","section":{"id":"beyond-gof-patterns-youll-meet-in-net-backends","text":"Beyond GoF: patterns you'll meet in .NET backends"},"num":"5.2"},{"q":"When is the Builder pattern useful?","a":"<p>When constructing an object requires many optional parts or steps, when you want a readable fluent API and validation at Build(), or when the result should be immutable. Examples: WebApplicationBuilder, StringBuilder, test data builders.</p>\n","chapter":"design-patterns","section":{"id":"beyond-gof-patterns-youll-meet-in-net-backends","text":"Beyond GoF: patterns you'll meet in .NET backends"},"num":"5.2"},{"q":"What is Clean Architecture?","a":"<p>An architecture that places the domain (entities and rules) and application use cases at the centre, with infrastructure and presentation in outer layers, governed by the Dependency Rule: source dependencies point only inward. Inner layers define interfaces (ports) that outer layers implement (adapters), so business logic is independent of frameworks, databases and UI, and is easy to test.</p>\n","chapter":"app-architectures","section":{"id":"choosing","text":"Choosing"},"num":"5.3"},{"q":"What's the difference between layered architecture and Clean/Onion architecture?","a":"<p>In classic layered architecture, the business layer depends on the data access layer below it. In Clean/Onion, dependencies are inverted: the domain and application define the abstractions, and infrastructure depends on them. Persistence becomes a detail plugged into the core.</p>\n","chapter":"app-architectures","section":{"id":"choosing","text":"Choosing"},"num":"5.3"},{"q":"What is Hexagonal (Ports and Adapters) architecture?","a":"<p>The application core exposes ports (interfaces) for everything it needs or offers. Adapters connect external actors: driving adapters (HTTP endpoints, message consumers, tests) call the core, and driven adapters (DB repositories, email, payment gateways) implement the core&#39;s outbound ports. It&#39;s essentially the same idea as Clean/Onion.</p>\n","chapter":"app-architectures","section":{"id":"choosing","text":"Choosing"},"num":"5.3"},{"q":"Should you use the Repository pattern with EF Core?","a":"<p>DbContext already is a unit of work and DbSet a repository, so a generic repository wrapper usually adds little and hides EF capabilities. Specific repositories per aggregate are valuable in DDD/Clean Architecture to encapsulate aggregate loading and keep EF out of the inner layers. A common approach is repositories for writes and direct EF/Dapper projections for reads.</p>\n","chapter":"app-architectures","section":{"id":"choosing","text":"Choosing"},"num":"5.3"},{"q":"What is Vertical Slice Architecture and how does it compare with Clean Architecture?","a":"<p>Organising code by feature, where each request or use case owns its endpoint, validation, handler and data access, instead of by technical layer. Changes are localised and coupling between features is low, but you must watch for duplication and keep business rules in the domain. It can be combined with a Clean-style domain core.</p>\n","chapter":"app-architectures","section":{"id":"choosing","text":"Choosing"},"num":"5.3"},{"q":"What is a modular monolith and why is it popular?","a":"<p>A single deployable app split into strongly separated modules aligned to bounded contexts, each with its own data and public contracts. It offers clear boundaries and team ownership without the operational and consistency costs of microservices, and modules can be extracted into services later if needed.</p>\n","chapter":"app-architectures","section":{"id":"choosing","text":"Choosing"},"num":"5.3"},{"q":"How do you enforce architectural boundaries in .NET?","a":"<p>Project references (the compiler forbids undeclared dependencies), internal visibility by default, architecture tests (NetArchTest/ArchUnitNET) in CI, analyzers, and documented ADRs plus code review.</p>\n","chapter":"app-architectures","section":{"id":"choosing","text":"Choosing"},"num":"5.3"},{"q":"What is CQRS and when is it worth using?","a":"<p>Separating the write model (commands handled through the domain model) from the read model (queries returning DTOs via optimised projections or a separate store). Worth it when reads and writes have very different shapes or loads, or the domain is complex. Start with separate handlers on one database; separate stores add eventual consistency and should be justified by scale.</p>\n","chapter":"cqrs-mediator-result","section":{"id":"the-result-pattern-making-expected-failure-part-of-the-metho","text":"The Result pattern: making expected failure part of the method's contract"},"num":"5.4"},{"q":"What is the difference between CQS and CQRS?","a":"<p>CQS is a method-level rule: a method either changes state or returns data, not both. CQRS applies the idea to architecture, with separate models, handlers and possibly data stores for commands and queries.</p>\n","chapter":"cqrs-mediator-result","section":{"id":"the-result-pattern-making-expected-failure-part-of-the-metho","text":"The Result pattern: making expected failure part of the method's contract"},"num":"5.4"},{"q":"Is CQRS the same as event sourcing?","a":"<p>No. CQRS separates reads from writes; event sourcing stores state as a sequence of events. They&#39;re complementary (event-sourced systems usually need CQRS read models) but independent.</p>\n","chapter":"cqrs-mediator-result","section":{"id":"the-result-pattern-making-expected-failure-part-of-the-metho","text":"The Result pattern: making expected failure part of the method's contract"},"num":"5.4"},{"q":"What problem does the mediator pattern solve in an application layer?","a":"<p>It decouples senders (endpoints, consumers) from handlers and provides a pipeline of behaviours around every request: validation, logging, authorization, transactions, caching. Cross-cutting concerns are implemented once instead of in every handler.</p>\n","chapter":"cqrs-mediator-result","section":{"id":"the-result-pattern-making-expected-failure-part-of-the-metho","text":"The Result pattern: making expected failure part of the method's contract"},"num":"5.4"},{"q":"What are the downsides of MediatR?","a":"<p>Indirection (it&#39;s harder to navigate from caller to handler), runtime reflection-based resolution, possible overuse for trivial calls, and since 2025 a commercial licence for larger companies. Alternatives include source-generated Mediator, Wolverine, or injecting handlers directly with decorators.</p>\n","chapter":"cqrs-mediator-result","section":{"id":"the-result-pattern-making-expected-failure-part-of-the-metho","text":"The Result pattern: making expected failure part of the method's contract"},"num":"5.4"},{"q":"What is the Result pattern and why use it instead of exceptions?","a":"<p>Returning an explicit success-or-failure object (with a typed error) from operations whose failures are expected. It makes failure part of the method contract, forces callers to handle it, avoids the cost and non-local control flow of exceptions, and allows mapping errors to HTTP responses in one place. Exceptions remain for truly exceptional, unexpected conditions.</p>\n","chapter":"cqrs-mediator-result","section":{"id":"the-result-pattern-making-expected-failure-part-of-the-metho","text":"The Result pattern: making expected failure part of the method's contract"},"num":"5.4"},{"q":"How would you implement validation in a CQRS/MediatR setup?","a":"<p>FluentValidation validators per command, executed by a ValidationBehavior pipeline behaviour before the handler, which short-circuits with a validation-failure Result (or throws a ValidationException mapped to 400 ProblemDetails). Keep business-rule checks that need aggregates inside the domain.</p>\n","chapter":"cqrs-mediator-result","section":{"id":"the-result-pattern-making-expected-failure-part-of-the-metho","text":"The Result pattern: making expected failure part of the method's contract"},"num":"5.4"},{"q":"What is an aggregate and what rules govern it?","a":"<p>A cluster of domain objects treated as one unit for changes, with a single aggregate root through which all modifications pass, so invariants are enforced within the boundary. Rules: keep aggregates small, reference other aggregates by ID only, modify one aggregate per transaction, use domain events and eventual consistency for cross-aggregate effects, and load and save whole aggregates via repositories.</p>\n","chapter":"ddd","section":{"id":"when-ddd-is-overkill","text":"When DDD is overkill"},"num":"5.5"},{"q":"Entity vs value object?","a":"<p>An entity has a persistent identity: two orders with identical data are still different orders, and its attributes can change. A value object has no identity, is defined by its values, is immutable and is compared by value (Money, Address, EmailAddress). Records are a natural fit for value objects.</p>\n","chapter":"ddd","section":{"id":"when-ddd-is-overkill","text":"When DDD is overkill"},"num":"5.5"},{"q":"What is a bounded context?","a":"<p>An explicit boundary within which a particular domain model and its ubiquitous language are consistent. The same term (Product, Customer) can have different models in different contexts, which integrate through defined contracts or translations (anti-corruption layers). Bounded contexts are natural module or microservice boundaries.</p>\n","chapter":"ddd","section":{"id":"when-ddd-is-overkill","text":"When DDD is overkill"},"num":"5.5"},{"q":"What is the ubiquitous language?","a":"<p>A shared, rigorous vocabulary developed with domain experts and used consistently in conversations, documentation and code (class and method names), scoped to a bounded context. It reduces translation errors between business and code.</p>\n","chapter":"ddd","section":{"id":"when-ddd-is-overkill","text":"When DDD is overkill"},"num":"5.5"},{"q":"What is an anemic domain model and why is it a problem?","a":"<p>Entities that only hold data (public getters and setters) while all business logic lives in service classes. Rules get duplicated and bypassed, invariants aren&#39;t protected, and the model doesn&#39;t express the domain. Rich models put behaviour and data together and make invalid states impossible.</p>\n","chapter":"ddd","section":{"id":"when-ddd-is-overkill","text":"When DDD is overkill"},"num":"5.5"},{"q":"Domain events vs integration events?","a":"<p>Domain events are raised by aggregates within a bounded context and handled in-process, often as part of or right after the transaction. Integration events cross service or context boundaries via a message broker, have stable versioned schemas, and are published reliably (outbox), typically translated from domain events.</p>\n","chapter":"ddd","section":{"id":"when-ddd-is-overkill","text":"When DDD is overkill"},"num":"5.5"},{"q":"Domain service vs application service?","a":"<p>A domain service contains domain logic that doesn&#39;t fit a single entity or value object and is expressed in domain terms (pricing, transfers). An application service or use-case handler orchestrates: it loads aggregates via repositories, invokes domain behaviour, persists, and publishes events, but contains no business rules itself.</p>\n","chapter":"ddd","section":{"id":"when-ddd-is-overkill","text":"When DDD is overkill"},"num":"5.5"},{"q":"What is an anti-corruption layer?","a":"<p>A translation layer (adapters and mappers) between your bounded context and an external or legacy system&#39;s model, so foreign concepts and quirks don&#39;t leak into your domain model.</p>\n","chapter":"ddd","section":{"id":"when-ddd-is-overkill","text":"When DDD is overkill"},"num":"5.5"},{"q":"When would you not use DDD?","a":"<p>For simple CRUD or supporting subdomains with little business logic, small short-lived apps, or when domain experts aren&#39;t available. Strategic ideas (bounded contexts, shared language) are still useful, but tactical patterns would add ceremony without benefit.</p>\n","chapter":"ddd","section":{"id":"when-ddd-is-overkill","text":"When DDD is overkill"},"num":"5.5"},{"q":"What are the trade-offs between a monolith and microservices?","a":"<p>A monolith is simpler to develop, test, deploy and debug, with ACID transactions and fast in-process calls, but it scales and deploys as one unit and can become tightly coupled. Microservices allow independent deployment, scaling, fault isolation and team autonomy, but add network failure modes, eventual consistency, data duplication, observability and DevOps overhead, and versioning challenges.</p>\n","chapter":"microservices","section":{"id":"readiness-checklist","text":"Readiness checklist"},"num":"5.6"},{"q":"What is a modular monolith?","a":"<p>A single deployable application divided into strongly encapsulated modules aligned with bounded contexts, each owning its data and exposing explicit contracts. It provides most of the structural benefits of microservices without distributed-systems costs, and can be split later.</p>\n","chapter":"microservices","section":{"id":"readiness-checklist","text":"Readiness checklist"},"num":"5.6"},{"q":"How do you decide service boundaries?","a":"<p>Align them with bounded contexts and business capabilities (not technical layers or single entities), aiming for high cohesion and low coupling so most changes stay within one service. Each service should own its data and be able to function when others are down, and services should match team ownership. Start coarse-grained.</p>\n","chapter":"microservices","section":{"id":"readiness-checklist","text":"Readiness checklist"},"num":"5.6"},{"q":"Why should each microservice own its database?","a":"<p>A shared database couples services through the schema: changes break others, teams can&#39;t deploy or evolve independently, and boundaries erode. With database per service, data is accessed only via APIs or events, preserving autonomy and encapsulation.</p>\n","chapter":"microservices","section":{"id":"readiness-checklist","text":"Readiness checklist"},"num":"5.6"},{"q":"How do you query data that spans multiple services?","a":"<p>API composition (a gateway or BFF aggregates calls), local replicas of needed data maintained through events, or a dedicated CQRS read model built from multiple services&#39; events, accepting eventual consistency.</p>\n","chapter":"microservices","section":{"id":"readiness-checklist","text":"Readiness checklist"},"num":"5.6"},{"q":"Sync vs async communication between microservices?","a":"<p>Synchronous HTTP/gRPC is for requests needing an immediate response, but it couples services in time and failures cascade. Asynchronous messaging decouples them in time, buffers load and isolates failures, at the cost of eventual consistency. Prefer async events for integration and minimise synchronous call chains.</p>\n","chapter":"microservices","section":{"id":"readiness-checklist","text":"Readiness checklist"},"num":"5.6"},{"q":"What is an API gateway and what is a BFF?","a":"<p>An API gateway is a single entry point that routes requests to services and handles cross-cutting concerns (TLS, auth, rate limiting, caching, aggregation), e.g. Azure API Management or YARP. A Backend-for-Frontend is a gateway specialised per client type (web, mobile) that tailors and aggregates APIs for it and, for SPAs, can hold tokens server-side.</p>\n","chapter":"microservices","section":{"id":"readiness-checklist","text":"Readiness checklist"},"num":"5.6"},{"q":"What is the strangler fig pattern?","a":"<p>Incrementally migrating a legacy system by routing traffic through a facade or proxy and replacing capabilities one at a time with new implementations, until the old system can be retired. It avoids a risky big-bang rewrite.</p>\n","chapter":"microservices","section":{"id":"readiness-checklist","text":"Readiness checklist"},"num":"5.6"},{"q":"What is a distributed monolith?","a":"<p>A system split into services that are still tightly coupled: a shared database, lockstep deployments, synchronous call chains, shared domain libraries. It has the costs of distribution and the rigidity of a monolith.</p>\n","chapter":"microservices","section":{"id":"readiness-checklist","text":"Readiness checklist"},"num":"5.6"},{"q":"What makes a good unit test?","a":"<p>Fast, isolated, repeatable (no real time, network or DB), self-validating, and focused on one observable behaviour through the public API. It has a clear Arrange-Act-Assert structure, a descriptive name, no logic of its own, and it survives refactoring because it asserts outcomes rather than implementation details.</p>\n","chapter":"unit-testing","section":{"id":"tdd","text":"TDD"},"num":"6.1"},{"q":"Explain the difference between a mock, a stub and a fake.","a":"<p>A stub returns canned data to feed the code under test (state verification). A mock records interactions and is verified to have been called in a particular way (behaviour verification). A fake is a working lightweight implementation, such as an in-memory repository or FakeTimeProvider.</p>\n","chapter":"unit-testing","section":{"id":"tdd","text":"TDD"},"num":"6.1"},{"q":"When should you verify interactions with mocks?","a":"<p>Only for outgoing side effects that cross the system boundary and are part of the observable behaviour: an email sent, a message published, a payment charged. Don&#39;t verify queries or internal collaborator calls; that couples tests to implementation.</p>\n","chapter":"unit-testing","section":{"id":"tdd","text":"TDD"},"num":"6.1"},{"q":"xUnit Fact vs Theory?","a":"<p>Fact is a single parameterless test. Theory is a parameterised test that runs once per data row supplied by InlineData, MemberData, ClassData or TheoryData.</p>\n","chapter":"unit-testing","section":{"id":"tdd","text":"TDD"},"num":"6.1"},{"q":"How do you share setup across tests in xUnit?","a":"<p>The constructor (and Dispose/IAsyncLifetime) for per-test setup, since xUnit creates a new instance per test; IClassFixture&lt;T&gt; to share an expensive object across a class&#39;s tests; collection fixtures ([Collection] plus ICollectionFixture) to share across classes, e.g. a database container.</p>\n","chapter":"unit-testing","section":{"id":"tdd","text":"TDD"},"num":"6.1"},{"q":"How do you test code that depends on the current time?","a":"<p>Inject TimeProvider (.NET 8) or a clock abstraction instead of calling DateTime.Now. In tests, use FakeTimeProvider to set and advance time deterministically, which also drives timers and delays.</p>\n","chapter":"unit-testing","section":{"id":"tdd","text":"TDD"},"num":"6.1"},{"q":"Is 100% code coverage a good goal?","a":"<p>No. Coverage shows which lines executed, not whether behaviour was verified, and chasing it produces low-value tests. Focus on meaningful coverage of business logic and edge cases, and use mutation testing to assess test effectiveness.</p>\n","chapter":"unit-testing","section":{"id":"tdd","text":"TDD"},"num":"6.1"},{"q":"What is TDD?","a":"<p>Test-Driven Development: write a failing test for a small piece of behaviour (red), write the minimum code to pass (green), then refactor safely, in short cycles. It yields tests by construction and testable, usage-driven designs.</p>\n","chapter":"unit-testing","section":{"id":"tdd","text":"TDD"},"num":"6.1"},{"q":"What is the difference between unit and integration tests?","a":"<p>Unit tests verify a single behaviour in isolation, in-process with no I/O, and run in milliseconds. Integration tests verify that components work together with real infrastructure (pipeline, DI, serialization, database, broker), catching wiring, configuration, mapping and SQL issues that unit tests can&#39;t.</p>\n","chapter":"integration-testing","section":{"id":"tests-in-ci","text":"Tests in CI"},"num":"6.2"},{"q":"How do you integration-test an ASP.NET Core API?","a":"<p>WebApplicationFactory&lt;Program&gt; hosts the real app in memory with a TestServer and provides an HttpClient. Override configuration and replace only external dependencies in ConfigureTestServices, use a real database via Testcontainers with migrations applied, reset data with Respawn, and assert on HTTP responses and persisted state.</p>\n","chapter":"integration-testing","section":{"id":"tests-in-ci","text":"Tests in CI"},"num":"6.2"},{"q":"How do you handle authentication in integration tests?","a":"<p>Register a test authentication handler that builds a ClaimsPrincipal from headers (or mint real JWTs with a test key) and make it the default scheme, so real authorization policies still execute against controllable identities.</p>\n","chapter":"integration-testing","section":{"id":"tests-in-ci","text":"Tests in CI"},"num":"6.2"},{"q":"What is Testcontainers and why use it?","a":"<p>A library that starts disposable Docker containers (SQL Server, Postgres, Redis, RabbitMQ, Azurite…) from test code. Tests run against the real engines with production-like behaviour, isolated and reproducible locally and in CI, instead of fakes like the EF InMemory provider.</p>\n","chapter":"integration-testing","section":{"id":"tests-in-ci","text":"Tests in CI"},"num":"6.2"},{"q":"How do you keep integration tests isolated and fast?","a":"<p>Share one app factory and container per collection, reset data between tests (Respawn, or a transaction rollback), use unique data per test where possible, run collections in parallel, and replace only slow external dependencies.</p>\n","chapter":"integration-testing","section":{"id":"tests-in-ci","text":"Tests in CI"},"num":"6.2"},{"q":"What are contract tests?","a":"<p>Tests verifying that a provider service honours the API or message contract its consumers rely on (e.g. Pact consumer-driven contracts, OpenAPI breaking-change checks). They catch integration breaks between independently deployed services without full end-to-end environments.</p>\n","chapter":"integration-testing","section":{"id":"tests-in-ci","text":"Tests in CI"},"num":"6.2"},{"q":"Test pyramid vs testing trophy?","a":"<p>The pyramid recommends mostly unit tests, fewer integration tests and very few E2E tests. The trophy shifts the weight to integration tests, because modern tooling makes them fast and they give more confidence per test, with unit tests reserved for complex logic and only a few E2E tests.</p>\n","chapter":"integration-testing","section":{"id":"tests-in-ci","text":"Tests in CI"},"num":"6.2"},{"q":"Why use messaging instead of direct HTTP calls between services?","a":"<p>It decouples services in time (both needn&#39;t be up), levels load spikes through queues, isolates failures (a down consumer doesn&#39;t break the producer), enables pub/sub fan-out to new consumers without changing producers, and scales via competing consumers. The trade-offs are eventual consistency and operational complexity.</p>\n","chapter":"messaging-events","section":{"id":"event-sourcing","text":"Event sourcing"},"num":"7.1"},{"q":"What's the difference between a queue and a topic?","a":"<p>A queue delivers each message to one consumer among competing consumers, which suits commands and work distribution. A topic delivers a copy to each subscription, which suits events consumed by multiple independent services.</p>\n","chapter":"messaging-events","section":{"id":"event-sourcing","text":"Event sourcing"},"num":"7.1"},{"q":"Commands vs events?","a":"<p>A command asks one specific handler to do something (imperative name, can be rejected, sent to a queue). An event announces that something happened (past tense, can&#39;t be rejected, published to any number of subscribers).</p>\n","chapter":"messaging-events","section":{"id":"event-sourcing","text":"Event sourcing"},"num":"7.1"},{"q":"What is the outbox pattern and what problem does it solve?","a":"<p>It solves the dual-write problem: you can&#39;t atomically commit to the database and publish to a broker. Writing the outgoing message to an outbox table in the same transaction as the business data, then having a relay publish it and mark it processed, guarantees the message is eventually published if and only if the transaction committed (at-least-once).</p>\n","chapter":"messaging-events","section":{"id":"event-sourcing","text":"Event sourcing"},"num":"7.1"},{"q":"How do you make a message consumer idempotent?","a":"<p>Use naturally idempotent operations (upserts, setting state), or record processed message IDs in an inbox table in the same transaction as the handler&#39;s changes (with a unique key) and skip duplicates. Use business-key unique constraints and version checks, and pass idempotency keys to external providers for side effects outside your database.</p>\n","chapter":"messaging-events","section":{"id":"event-sourcing","text":"Event sourcing"},"num":"7.1"},{"q":"What delivery guarantee do brokers usually provide?","a":"<p>At-least-once: messages may be delivered more than once (redelivery after a crash before ack, producer retries, lock expiry). Exactly-once end-to-end isn&#39;t generally achievable, so consumers must be idempotent to achieve effectively-once processing.</p>\n","chapter":"messaging-events","section":{"id":"event-sourcing","text":"Event sourcing"},"num":"7.1"},{"q":"What is a dead-letter queue?","a":"<p>A secondary queue where messages are moved after exceeding max delivery attempts or failing permanently (poison messages), so they don&#39;t block processing. Monitor it, investigate, fix the cause and resubmit.</p>\n","chapter":"messaging-events","section":{"id":"event-sourcing","text":"Event sourcing"},"num":"7.1"},{"q":"What is a saga? Orchestration vs choreography?","a":"<p>A saga coordinates a multi-service business transaction as a series of local transactions with compensating actions on failure. In choreography, services react to each other&#39;s events with no central controller (loosely coupled, but the flow is implicit). In orchestration, a state-machine orchestrator sends commands and tracks progress (an explicit, monitorable flow with a central component).</p>\n","chapter":"messaging-events","section":{"id":"event-sourcing","text":"Event sourcing"},"num":"7.1"},{"q":"How do you handle message ordering?","a":"<p>Most brokers don&#39;t guarantee global order, especially with competing consumers. Guarantee order per key with Service Bus sessions or Kafka partitions; otherwise design consumers to tolerate disorder using version numbers or timestamps to discard stale updates.</p>\n","chapter":"messaging-events","section":{"id":"event-sourcing","text":"Event sourcing"},"num":"7.1"},{"q":"Service Bus vs Kafka/Event Hubs?","a":"<p>Service Bus is a message broker for business messaging: messages are removed when completed, and it offers sessions, dead-lettering, transactions, scheduling and duplicate detection. Kafka/Event Hubs are distributed logs for high-throughput event streaming: messages are retained and replayable, consumers track offsets, and ordering is per partition.</p>\n","chapter":"messaging-events","section":{"id":"event-sourcing","text":"Event sourcing"},"num":"7.1"},{"q":"What is the difference between transient and non-transient faults?","a":"<p>Transient faults are temporary and likely to succeed on retry: timeouts, connection resets, 503/429, SQL failovers, deadlocks. Non-transient faults won&#39;t fix themselves (400, 401/403, 404, validation errors, bugs), so retrying wastes resources and they should fail fast.</p>\n","chapter":"resilience","section":{"id":"chaos-engineering","text":"Chaos engineering"},"num":"7.2"},{"q":"Why use exponential backoff with jitter for retries?","a":"<p>Backoff spaces retries out so a struggling dependency has time to recover. Jitter randomises the delays so many clients don&#39;t retry simultaneously in synchronised waves (thundering herd) that keep the dependency overloaded.</p>\n","chapter":"resilience","section":{"id":"chaos-engineering","text":"Chaos engineering"},"num":"7.2"},{"q":"How does a circuit breaker work?","a":"<p>Closed: calls flow and failures are tracked. When the failure ratio exceeds a threshold (with a minimum throughput) within a sampling window, it opens: calls fail immediately for a break duration. Then it goes half-open and allows a trial call; success closes it, failure re-opens it. It prevents wasting resources on, and piling load onto, a failing dependency.</p>\n","chapter":"resilience","section":{"id":"chaos-engineering","text":"Chaos engineering"},"num":"7.2"},{"q":"When is it unsafe to retry a request?","a":"<p>When the operation isn&#39;t idempotent (e.g. POST creating a payment) and has no idempotency key, because the original may have succeeded despite the error and a retry would duplicate it. Also for permanent errors like 400 or 401.</p>\n","chapter":"resilience","section":{"id":"chaos-engineering","text":"Chaos engineering"},"num":"7.2"},{"q":"What is a retry storm and how do you avoid it?","a":"<p>Retries at multiple layers multiplying load on a failing dependency (3 retries at 3 layers = 27 attempts), worsening the outage. Avoid it by retrying at one layer only, keeping attempt counts low, using jitter, adding circuit breakers, and propagating timeout budgets and cancellation.</p>\n","chapter":"resilience","section":{"id":"chaos-engineering","text":"Chaos engineering"},"num":"7.2"},{"q":"How do you add resilience to HttpClient in modern .NET?","a":"<p>Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Resilience: <code>.AddStandardResilienceHandler()</code> on the IHttpClientFactory registration gives a rate limiter, total timeout, retries with backoff and jitter, a circuit breaker and per-attempt timeouts with sensible defaults. Use AddResilienceHandler for a custom Polly v8 pipeline, or AddStandardHedgingHandler for hedging.</p>\n","chapter":"resilience","section":{"id":"chaos-engineering","text":"Chaos engineering"},"num":"7.2"},{"q":"What is the bulkhead pattern?","a":"<p>Isolating resources (connection pools, thread or concurrency limits, separate instances) per dependency or workload, so a failure or slowdown in one can&#39;t exhaust resources needed by others, like watertight compartments in a ship.</p>\n","chapter":"resilience","section":{"id":"chaos-engineering","text":"Chaos engineering"},"num":"7.2"},{"q":"Liveness vs readiness health checks?","a":"<p>Liveness tells the orchestrator whether the process is healthy or must be restarted, and should not depend on external services. Readiness tells the load balancer whether the instance can currently serve traffic (dependencies reachable, warmed up); failing readiness removes it from rotation without restarting it.</p>\n","chapter":"resilience","section":{"id":"chaos-engineering","text":"Chaos engineering"},"num":"7.2"},{"q":"What are the three pillars of observability?","a":"<p>Logs (detailed records of discrete events), metrics (numeric aggregates over time, cheap and good for dashboards and alerts) and traces (the end-to-end path of a single request across services as a tree of timed spans). They&#39;re most useful correlated through the trace ID.</p>\n","chapter":"observability-aspire","section":{"id":"diagnosing-a-production-issue-a-workflow","text":"Diagnosing a production issue: a workflow"},"num":"7.3"},{"q":"What is OpenTelemetry and how does .NET support it?","a":"<p>A vendor-neutral CNCF standard and SDK for generating, collecting and exporting logs, metrics and traces (via OTLP). .NET&#39;s built-in APIs (ILogger, System.Diagnostics.Metrics Meter, and ActivitySource/Activity for spans) are OpenTelemetry-compatible. The OpenTelemetry .NET SDK subscribes to them and exports to backends like Azure Monitor, Grafana, Jaeger or Datadog.</p>\n","chapter":"observability-aspire","section":{"id":"diagnosing-a-production-issue-a-workflow","text":"Diagnosing a production issue: a workflow"},"num":"7.3"},{"q":"How does distributed tracing work across services?","a":"<p>Each request gets a trace ID; each unit of work is a span with a parent. The W3C traceparent header (and baggage) propagates context on outgoing HTTP, gRPC and messages, and receivers continue the same trace. ASP.NET Core and HttpClient handle propagation automatically, and backends reassemble the full call tree with timings.</p>\n","chapter":"observability-aspire","section":{"id":"diagnosing-a-production-issue-a-workflow","text":"Diagnosing a production issue: a workflow"},"num":"7.3"},{"q":"What's the difference between metrics and logs?","a":"<p>Metrics are pre-aggregated numeric time series (counters, histograms): small, fast, ideal for trends, dashboards and alerting, with low-cardinality dimensions. Logs are individual event records with rich context, used for investigation, and more expensive to store and query.</p>\n","chapter":"observability-aspire","section":{"id":"diagnosing-a-production-issue-a-workflow","text":"Diagnosing a production issue: a workflow"},"num":"7.3"},{"q":"What is cardinality and why does it matter for metrics?","a":"<p>The number of unique tag-value combinations. Each one creates a separate time series, so high-cardinality tags (user IDs, order IDs, raw URLs) explode storage and cost. Keep metric tags low-cardinality and put IDs on traces and logs.</p>\n","chapter":"observability-aspire","section":{"id":"diagnosing-a-production-issue-a-workflow","text":"Diagnosing a production issue: a workflow"},"num":"7.3"},{"q":"What would you monitor for a .NET web API?","a":"<p>RED metrics per endpoint (request rate, error rate, p95/p99 latency), dependency latency and failures, plus .NET runtime metrics (GC pause and heap, thread pool queue length and thread count, exception rate), connection pool usage, queue/outbox lag, health checks and SLO burn-rate alerts.</p>\n","chapter":"observability-aspire","section":{"id":"diagnosing-a-production-issue-a-workflow","text":"Diagnosing a production issue: a workflow"},"num":"7.3"},{"q":"What is .NET Aspire?","a":"<p>A toolkit for building distributed .NET apps: an AppHost project to orchestrate services and containers locally with automatic configuration and service discovery, ServiceDefaults to apply OpenTelemetry, health checks and resilience consistently, integration packages for common services, and a dashboard showing logs, traces and metrics. It can also drive deployment (e.g. to Azure Container Apps with azd).</p>\n","chapter":"observability-aspire","section":{"id":"diagnosing-a-production-issue-a-workflow","text":"Diagnosing a production issue: a workflow"},"num":"7.3"},{"q":"What are SLIs, SLOs and error budgets?","a":"<p>An SLI is a measured indicator of service level (e.g. % of requests under 300 ms). An SLO is the target for it (99.9% over 30 days). The error budget is the allowed shortfall (0.1%), used to balance releasing features against reliability work and to drive alerting on burn rate.</p>\n","chapter":"observability-aspire","section":{"id":"diagnosing-a-production-issue-a-workflow","text":"Diagnosing a production issue: a workflow"},"num":"7.3"},{"q":"What is the difference between IaaS, PaaS and serverless?","a":"<p>IaaS gives virtual machines and networking: you manage the OS, runtime and app. PaaS manages the OS and runtime and you deploy code or containers (App Service, Azure SQL). Serverless runs code on demand with automatic scaling, including to zero, and per-execution billing (Azure Functions, Container Apps consumption), with constraints like cold starts.</p>\n","chapter":"cloud-azure-overview","section":{"id":"the-azure-well-architected-framework","text":"The Azure Well-Architected Framework"},"num":"8.1"},{"q":"What is the shared responsibility model?","a":"<p>The cloud provider is responsible for security of the cloud (physical infrastructure, hardware, virtualisation, managed service internals); the customer is responsible for security in the cloud (data, identities, access control, application code, network configuration). The split depends on IaaS, PaaS or SaaS, but data and identity are always the customer&#39;s.</p>\n","chapter":"cloud-azure-overview","section":{"id":"the-azure-well-architected-framework","text":"The Azure Well-Architected Framework"},"num":"8.1"},{"q":"What are availability zones and why use them?","a":"<p>Physically separate datacentres within an Azure region with independent power, cooling and networking. Deploying instances and zone-redundant services across zones protects against a datacentre outage while keeping low latency.</p>\n","chapter":"cloud-azure-overview","section":{"id":"the-azure-well-architected-framework","text":"The Azure Well-Architected Framework"},"num":"8.1"},{"q":"Scale up vs scale out?","a":"<p>Scale up (vertical) moves to a larger instance with more CPU and RAM; it&#39;s limited by the largest size and doesn&#39;t add redundancy. Scale out (horizontal) adds more instances behind a load balancer: near-unlimited and resilient, but it requires a stateless application with shared state in external stores.</p>\n","chapter":"cloud-azure-overview","section":{"id":"the-azure-well-architected-framework","text":"The Azure Well-Architected Framework"},"num":"8.1"},{"q":"What makes an application cloud-native / twelve-factor?","a":"<p>Config from the environment, stateless processes with backing services attached via configuration, port binding, fast startup and graceful shutdown, logs as stdout streams, strict build/release/run separation, dev/prod parity, and scale-out via processes. In .NET: IConfiguration with env vars and Key Vault, Kestrel, Redis/Blob for state, hosted-service shutdown handling, container images and IaC.</p>\n","chapter":"cloud-azure-overview","section":{"id":"the-azure-well-architected-framework","text":"The Azure Well-Architected Framework"},"num":"8.1"},{"q":"How is Azure organised (resource hierarchy)?","a":"<p>An Entra tenant contains management groups, which contain subscriptions (billing, quota and access boundaries), which contain resource groups (lifecycle containers), which contain resources. RBAC and Azure Policy can be applied at any level and are inherited downward. Everything is managed through Azure Resource Manager.</p>\n","chapter":"cloud-azure-overview","section":{"id":"the-azure-well-architected-framework","text":"The Azure Well-Architected Framework"},"num":"8.1"},{"q":"What are private endpoints?","a":"<p>Network interfaces that give an Azure PaaS service a private IP address inside your VNet, so traffic stays on the Microsoft backbone and you can disable the service&#39;s public endpoint. They need Private DNS zone configuration.</p>\n","chapter":"cloud-azure-overview","section":{"id":"the-azure-well-architected-framework","text":"The Azure Well-Architected Framework"},"num":"8.1"},{"q":"What are RTO and RPO?","a":"<p>Recovery Time Objective: the maximum acceptable downtime after a disaster. Recovery Point Objective: the maximum acceptable data loss measured in time. Both drive backup, replication and failover design.</p>\n","chapter":"cloud-azure-overview","section":{"id":"the-azure-well-architected-framework","text":"The Azure Well-Architected Framework"},"num":"8.1"},{"q":"What is the difference between a container and a virtual machine?","a":"<p>A VM virtualises hardware and runs a full guest OS with its own kernel: strong isolation, but heavy and slow to start. A container isolates processes on a shared host kernel (namespaces and cgroups) and packages just the app and its dependencies: lightweight, fast to start and dense.</p>\n","chapter":"docker","section":{"id":"container-memory-and-cpu-limits-with-net","text":"Container memory and CPU limits with .NET"},"num":"8.2"},{"q":"Why use a multi-stage Dockerfile for .NET?","a":"<p>To build with the large SDK image and copy only the published output into a small runtime image. The final image is smaller, has less attack surface (no SDK, sources or build tools) and pulls and starts faster.</p>\n","chapter":"docker","section":{"id":"container-memory-and-cpu-limits-with-net","text":"Container memory and CPU limits with .NET"},"num":"8.2"},{"q":"How do you optimise Docker layer caching for .NET builds?","a":"<p>Copy the csproj files (and Directory.*.props, global.json) first and run dotnet restore before copying the rest of the source. Restore is then cached and only reruns when dependencies change. Use a .dockerignore to keep bin/obj and other noise out of the build context.</p>\n","chapter":"docker","section":{"id":"container-memory-and-cpu-limits-with-net","text":"Container memory and CPU limits with .NET"},"num":"8.2"},{"q":"Which base image would you use for an ASP.NET Core app in production?","a":"<p>mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspnet with a pinned major version, ideally the chiseled (distroless, non-root) variant for a minimal attack surface, or runtime-deps for self-contained/Native AOT apps. Use the sdk image only in the build stage.</p>\n","chapter":"docker","section":{"id":"container-memory-and-cpu-limits-with-net","text":"Container memory and CPU limits with .NET"},"num":"8.2"},{"q":"How should configuration and secrets be provided to a container?","a":"<p>Through environment variables or mounted configuration supplied by the platform at runtime, so one immutable image is promoted across environments. Secrets come from a secret store (Key Vault references, Kubernetes Secrets) or are avoided with managed identity. Never bake them into the image or build args.</p>\n","chapter":"docker","section":{"id":"container-memory-and-cpu-limits-with-net","text":"Container memory and CPU limits with .NET"},"num":"8.2"},{"q":"Why does .NET 8+ listen on port 8080 in containers?","a":"<p>The official images default ASPNETCORE_HTTP_PORTS to 8080 and run as a non-root user, and non-root processes can&#39;t bind to privileged ports below 1024 on Linux. Map or route external port 80/443 to 8080 at the platform.</p>\n","chapter":"docker","section":{"id":"container-memory-and-cpu-limits-with-net","text":"Container memory and CPU limits with .NET"},"num":"8.2"},{"q":"What happens when you run docker stop on a .NET container?","a":"<p>Docker sends SIGTERM to PID 1. With an exec-form ENTRYPOINT, that&#39;s dotnet, so the .NET host begins graceful shutdown (stops accepting connections, drains requests, stops hosted services). After the timeout (10 s default), Docker sends SIGKILL.</p>\n","chapter":"docker","section":{"id":"container-memory-and-cpu-limits-with-net","text":"Container memory and CPU limits with .NET"},"num":"8.2"},{"q":"How would you choose between App Service, Container Apps, Functions and AKS?","a":"<p>Functions for event-triggered or scheduled small units of work with per-execution billing; App Service for straightforward web apps and APIs with slots and minimal ops; Container Apps for containerised microservices, workers and jobs with KEDA autoscaling and scale to zero without managing Kubernetes; AKS when you need full Kubernetes control and ecosystem and can operate it. Trade control against operational effort.</p>\n","chapter":"azure-compute","section":{"id":"choosing-compute-a-decision-guide","text":"Choosing compute: a decision guide"},"num":"8.3"},{"q":"What are App Service deployment slots and why use them?","a":"<p>Separate live instances of an app (e.g. staging) on the same plan, each with its own hostname and settings. You deploy and warm up in staging, then swap with production for near-zero-downtime releases and instant rollback by swapping back. Sticky settings stay with a slot.</p>\n","chapter":"azure-compute","section":{"id":"choosing-compute-a-decision-guide","text":"Choosing compute: a decision guide"},"num":"8.3"},{"q":"What's the isolated worker model in Azure Functions?","a":"<p>Functions code runs in a separate .NET process from the Functions host, so you choose the .NET version and dependencies and use standard DI, middleware and configuration. It replaces the in-process model, which Microsoft is retiring (check the current end-of-support date before relying on it, since retirement schedules can shift).</p>\n","chapter":"azure-compute","section":{"id":"choosing-compute-a-decision-guide","text":"Choosing compute: a decision guide"},"num":"8.3"},{"q":"What causes cold starts in Azure Functions and how do you mitigate them?","a":"<p>On consumption plans idle apps scale to zero, so the next request must allocate an instance, start the runtime and load your app. Mitigate with Flex Consumption always-ready instances or the Premium plan (pre-warmed), smaller startup work, ReadyToRun/AOT, and avoiding heavy initialisation in the startup path.</p>\n","chapter":"azure-compute","section":{"id":"choosing-compute-a-decision-guide","text":"Choosing compute: a decision guide"},"num":"8.3"},{"q":"What are Durable Functions?","a":"<p>An Azure Functions extension for stateful, long-running workflows written as orchestrator code that calls activity functions. State is checkpointed and replayed, so orchestrations survive restarts. It supports chaining, fan-out/fan-in, timers, external events and human interaction, monitors and entities. Orchestrators must be deterministic.</p>\n","chapter":"azure-compute","section":{"id":"choosing-compute-a-decision-guide","text":"Choosing compute: a decision guide"},"num":"8.3"},{"q":"How does Azure Container Apps scale?","a":"<p>With KEDA-based scale rules: HTTP concurrency, CPU/memory, or event sources like Service Bus queue length, Kafka lag or cron, between min and max replicas, including scaling to zero. Revisions allow traffic splitting for canary and blue/green deployments.</p>\n","chapter":"azure-compute","section":{"id":"choosing-compute-a-decision-guide","text":"Choosing compute: a decision guide"},"num":"8.3"},{"q":"Service Bus vs Event Grid vs Event Hubs?","a":"<p>Service Bus is an enterprise message broker for reliable processing of commands and business events: queues and topics, peek-lock, DLQ, sessions for ordering, transactions, duplicate detection. Event Grid is a push-based event router for reacting to discrete events (resource or custom events) with fan-out to handlers. Event Hubs is a partitioned event-streaming platform for high-volume ingestion, with retention, replay and Kafka compatibility.</p>\n","chapter":"azure-data-messaging","section":{"id":"using-azure-sdk-clients-well-from-net","text":"Using Azure SDK clients well from .NET"},"num":"8.4"},{"q":"How do you connect an app to Azure SQL without a password?","a":"<p>Enable a managed identity on the app, create a contained database user FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER for that identity and grant least-privilege roles, then use a connection string with Authentication=Active Directory Default (or Managed Identity) via Microsoft.Data.SqlClient. No secrets to store or rotate.</p>\n","chapter":"azure-data-messaging","section":{"id":"using-azure-sdk-clients-well-from-net","text":"Using Azure SDK clients well from .NET"},"num":"8.4"},{"q":"What are Blob Storage access tiers?","a":"<p>Hot (frequent access), Cool (infrequent, ≥30 days), Cold (rare, ≥90 days) and Archive (offline, ≥180 days, rehydration takes hours), each trading storage cost against access cost. Lifecycle management policies move blobs between tiers automatically.</p>\n","chapter":"azure-data-messaging","section":{"id":"using-azure-sdk-clients-well-from-net","text":"Using Azure SDK clients well from .NET"},"num":"8.4"},{"q":"What is a SAS token and what's the most secure kind?","a":"<p>A Shared Access Signature appended to a storage URL granting time-limited, permission-scoped access to specific resources. A user delegation SAS, signed with Entra ID credentials rather than the account key, is the most secure; combine it with short expiry and least permissions, or avoid SAS by using RBAC with managed identity.</p>\n","chapter":"azure-data-messaging","section":{"id":"using-azure-sdk-clients-well-from-net","text":"Using Azure SDK clients well from .NET"},"num":"8.4"},{"q":"How does Azure SQL provide high availability and disaster recovery?","a":"<p>Built-in HA replicas within the region (optionally zone-redundant), automated backups with point-in-time restore and long-term retention, and active geo-replication or failover groups for cross-region DR with a stable listener endpoint. Apps must retry transient errors during failovers.</p>\n","chapter":"azure-data-messaging","section":{"id":"using-azure-sdk-clients-well-from-net","text":"Using Azure SDK clients well from .NET"},"num":"8.4"},{"q":"Why should Azure SDK clients be singletons?","a":"<p>They&#39;re thread-safe and manage connections, authentication tokens and retry state. Creating them per request wastes resources and can exhaust connections, similar to the HttpClient anti-pattern.</p>\n","chapter":"azure-data-messaging","section":{"id":"using-azure-sdk-clients-well-from-net","text":"Using Azure SDK clients well from .NET"},"num":"8.4"},{"q":"What is a managed identity and why use it?","a":"<p>An Entra ID identity that Azure creates and manages for a resource (App Service, Functions, Container Apps, VMs, AKS workloads). The app obtains tokens from a local endpoint with no credentials to store or rotate, and is granted least-privilege access via RBAC. It eliminates secrets in configuration.</p>\n","chapter":"azure-identity-secrets","section":{"id":"protecting-your-own-apis-with-entra-id","text":"Protecting your own APIs with Entra ID"},"num":"8.5"},{"q":"System-assigned vs user-assigned managed identity?","a":"<p>System-assigned is created for and tied to one resource&#39;s lifecycle. User-assigned is a standalone resource that can be attached to many resources and survives them. Use it when several apps or instances share permissions, to pre-provision access, or for AKS workload identity.</p>\n","chapter":"azure-identity-secrets","section":{"id":"protecting-your-own-apis-with-entra-id","text":"Protecting your own APIs with Entra ID"},"num":"8.5"},{"q":"How does DefaultAzureCredential work?","a":"<p>It tries a sequence of credential types (environment variables, workload identity, managed identity, then developer tools like Visual Studio, Azure CLI and azd) and uses the first that succeeds. The same code authenticates as the developer locally and as the managed identity in Azure. In production you may prefer a specific credential for determinism and speed.</p>\n","chapter":"azure-identity-secrets","section":{"id":"protecting-your-own-apis-with-entra-id","text":"Protecting your own APIs with Entra ID"},"num":"8.5"},{"q":"What is the difference between an app registration and a service principal?","a":"<p>An app registration is the global definition of an application (client ID, redirect URIs, exposed scopes and roles, credentials). A service principal (enterprise application) is its instance in a specific tenant, the security principal that receives role assignments and consents. Managed identities are a special kind of service principal.</p>\n","chapter":"azure-identity-secrets","section":{"id":"protecting-your-own-apis-with-entra-id","text":"Protecting your own APIs with Entra ID"},"num":"8.5"},{"q":"How do you store and use secrets in an Azure-hosted .NET app?","a":"<p>Prefer no secrets: managed identity with RBAC for Azure services. For unavoidable secrets, store them in Key Vault, grant the app&#39;s managed identity the Key Vault Secrets User role, and load them via the Key Vault configuration provider or platform Key Vault references, caching them rather than calling the vault per request. Use User Secrets in development.</p>\n","chapter":"azure-identity-secrets","section":{"id":"protecting-your-own-apis-with-entra-id","text":"Protecting your own APIs with Entra ID"},"num":"8.5"},{"q":"How can GitHub Actions deploy to Azure without storing a client secret?","a":"<p>Workload identity federation (OIDC): configure a federated credential on an Entra app or user-assigned managed identity that trusts the GitHub repository/environment subject. The workflow requests an OIDC token (id-token: write permission) and azure/login exchanges it for an Azure access token.</p>\n","chapter":"azure-identity-secrets","section":{"id":"protecting-your-own-apis-with-entra-id","text":"Protecting your own APIs with Entra ID"},"num":"8.5"},{"q":"Azure RBAC vs Entra roles?","a":"<p>Azure RBAC controls access to Azure resources and their data (subscriptions, storage, Key Vault), using role assignments at a scope. Entra roles control administration of the Entra directory itself, and API permissions/app roles control access to Microsoft Graph and your own APIs.</p>\n","chapter":"azure-identity-secrets","section":{"id":"protecting-your-own-apis-with-entra-id","text":"Protecting your own APIs with Entra ID"},"num":"8.5"},{"q":"What's the difference between continuous integration, continuous delivery and continuous deployment?","a":"<p>CI: developers merge frequently and every change is automatically built and tested. Continuous delivery: every passing change is kept in a releasable state and can be deployed to production at the push of a button (manual approval). Continuous deployment: every passing change is deployed to production automatically, with no manual gate.</p>\n","chapter":"cicd-iac","section":{"id":"infrastructure-as-code","text":"Infrastructure as Code"},"num":"8.6"},{"q":"What does a typical CI/CD pipeline for a .NET API look like?","a":"<p>CI on each push/PR: restore (locked), build with warnings as errors, format/analyzer checks, unit and integration tests (Testcontainers), vulnerability scans, results and coverage. CD on main: build the container image once and tag it with the commit SHA, scan and push to ACR, deploy to staging (migration bundle, new revision or slot), smoke tests, approval, production via slot swap or canary, then monitoring with rollback.</p>\n","chapter":"cicd-iac","section":{"id":"infrastructure-as-code","text":"Infrastructure as Code"},"num":"8.6"},{"q":"Blue/green vs canary deployments?","a":"<p>Blue/green runs the new version in a parallel environment and switches all traffic at once, with instant rollback by switching back (App Service slot swap). Canary routes a small percentage of traffic to the new version and increases it gradually while health metrics stay good (Container Apps traffic splitting), limiting the blast radius.</p>\n","chapter":"cicd-iac","section":{"id":"infrastructure-as-code","text":"Infrastructure as Code"},"num":"8.6"},{"q":"How do you deploy database schema changes without downtime?","a":"<p>Expand/contract: make backward-compatible changes first (add nullable columns or new tables), deploy code that works with both old and new schemas, backfill, then remove old structures in a later release. Apply migrations from the pipeline (idempotent scripts or EF bundles) with a dedicated identity before deploying the app.</p>\n","chapter":"cicd-iac","section":{"id":"infrastructure-as-code","text":"Infrastructure as Code"},"num":"8.6"},{"q":"What is Infrastructure as Code and why use it?","a":"<p>Defining cloud infrastructure in declarative, versioned code (Bicep, Terraform, Pulumi) that&#39;s reviewed in PRs and applied by pipelines. It gives reproducible environments, change previews (what-if/plan), no configuration drift, auditable history and fast disaster recovery.</p>\n","chapter":"cicd-iac","section":{"id":"infrastructure-as-code","text":"Infrastructure as Code"},"num":"8.6"},{"q":"Bicep vs Terraform?","a":"<p>Bicep is Azure&#39;s native DSL compiling to ARM: Azure-only, no state file, immediate support for new features. Terraform is multi-cloud with a large provider ecosystem and a state file you must store and lock remotely. Choose Bicep for Azure-only teams and Terraform for multi-cloud or when you need non-Azure providers.</p>\n","chapter":"cicd-iac","section":{"id":"infrastructure-as-code","text":"Infrastructure as Code"},"num":"8.6"},{"q":"How should a pipeline authenticate to Azure?","a":"<p>With workload identity federation (OIDC): a federated credential on an Entra app or user-assigned managed identity that trusts the specific repository/environment, so no client secret is stored. Grant that identity least-privilege RBAC per environment.</p>\n","chapter":"cicd-iac","section":{"id":"infrastructure-as-code","text":"Infrastructure as Code"},"num":"8.6"},{"q":"What is Kubernetes and what problems does it solve?","a":"<p>A container orchestration platform that runs containers across a cluster from declarative desired state, providing scheduling, self-healing, horizontal scaling, service discovery and load balancing, rolling updates and rollbacks, and configuration/secret injection, through a standard API with a large ecosystem.</p>\n","chapter":"kubernetes","section":{"id":"aks-specifics","text":"AKS specifics"},"num":"8.7"},{"q":"Pod vs Deployment vs Service?","a":"<p>A Pod is the smallest deployable unit: one or more co-located containers sharing network and storage, and ephemeral. A Deployment declares the desired pods and replica count and manages rolling updates through ReplicaSets. A Service provides a stable virtual IP and DNS name that load-balances across the pods selected by labels.</p>\n","chapter":"kubernetes","section":{"id":"aks-specifics","text":"AKS specifics"},"num":"8.7"},{"q":"What are liveness, readiness and startup probes?","a":"<p>Startup probes delay other checks until the app has started. Liveness probes detect a hung process, and failure restarts the container. Readiness probes indicate whether the pod should receive traffic, and failure removes it from Service endpoints without restarting. Liveness must not depend on external dependencies.</p>\n","chapter":"kubernetes","section":{"id":"aks-specifics","text":"AKS specifics"},"num":"8.7"},{"q":"How does a rolling update work and how do you roll back?","a":"<p>The Deployment creates a new ReplicaSet and gradually scales it up while scaling the old one down, respecting maxSurge/maxUnavailable and readiness probes, so capacity is preserved. Roll back with <code>kubectl rollout undo</code> (back to the previous ReplicaSet), or by reverting the manifest in GitOps.</p>\n","chapter":"kubernetes","section":{"id":"aks-specifics","text":"AKS specifics"},"num":"8.7"},{"q":"Requests vs limits?","a":"<p>Requests are the resources reserved for scheduling a pod (guaranteed). Limits are maximums: exceeding the memory limit gets the container OOMKilled, and exceeding the CPU limit throttles it. Setting realistic requests and memory limits matters for stability; CPU limits can cause latency through throttling.</p>\n","chapter":"kubernetes","section":{"id":"aks-specifics","text":"AKS specifics"},"num":"8.7"},{"q":"How do you scale on queue length in Kubernetes?","a":"<p>With KEDA: a ScaledObject targeting the deployment, with a trigger such as azure-servicebus (queue name, target message count per replica) and authentication, scaling replicas between min (possibly 0) and max based on backlog.</p>\n","chapter":"kubernetes","section":{"id":"aks-specifics","text":"AKS specifics"},"num":"8.7"},{"q":"How should a .NET app in AKS access Azure SQL or Key Vault?","a":"<p>With AKS Workload Identity: a Kubernetes ServiceAccount federated with a user-assigned managed identity; pods get tokens via WorkloadIdentityCredential (included in DefaultAzureCredential), and the identity has least-privilege roles on SQL, Key Vault and Service Bus. No secrets in the cluster.</p>\n","chapter":"kubernetes","section":{"id":"aks-specifics","text":"AKS specifics"},"num":"8.7"},{"q":"What does CrashLoopBackOff mean?","a":"<p>The container keeps exiting shortly after starting and Kubernetes restarts it with increasing backoff. Usually it&#39;s a startup exception (bad config, missing env var or secret, failing ValidateOnStart, unreachable dependency at startup) or a failing liveness probe. Check <code>kubectl logs --previous</code> and <code>describe pod</code> events.</p>\n","chapter":"kubernetes","section":{"id":"aks-specifics","text":"AKS specifics"},"num":"8.7"},{"q":"How does the .NET garbage collector work?","a":"<p>It&#39;s a generational, tracing, mark-and-compact collector. Allocation bumps a pointer in Gen0. When a generation&#39;s budget fills, a GC suspends threads, marks all objects reachable from roots (statics, stack locals, GC handles, the finalization queue), reclaims the rest, compacts survivors and promotes them to the next generation. Gen0/1 collections are frequent and cheap; Gen2 (full, including the LOH) is expensive and mostly runs concurrently in background GC.</p>\n","chapter":"memory-gc","section":{"id":"writing-allocation-friendly-code","text":"Writing allocation-friendly code"},"num":"9.1"},{"q":"What are GC generations and why do they exist?","a":"<p>Gen0 holds new objects, Gen1 is a buffer, and Gen2 holds long-lived objects. Based on the generational hypothesis that most objects die young, collecting the small young generation frequently reclaims most garbage cheaply, without scanning the whole heap each time.</p>\n","chapter":"memory-gc","section":{"id":"writing-allocation-friendly-code","text":"Writing allocation-friendly code"},"num":"9.1"},{"q":"What is the Large Object Heap?","a":"<p>A separate heap region for objects of 85,000 bytes or more. It&#39;s collected only during Gen2 collections and not compacted by default, so frequent large allocations cause full GCs and fragmentation. Pool or stream large buffers instead.</p>\n","chapter":"memory-gc","section":{"id":"writing-allocation-friendly-code","text":"Writing allocation-friendly code"},"num":"9.1"},{"q":"Workstation vs Server GC?","a":"<p>Workstation GC uses a single heap and favours low latency and memory, and is the default for client apps. Server GC uses a heap and dedicated GC thread per core with parallel collection for higher throughput, and is the default for ASP.NET Core. .NET 9+ adds DATAS so Server GC adapts heap count to load, keeping memory proportional in containers.</p>\n","chapter":"memory-gc","section":{"id":"writing-allocation-friendly-code","text":"Writing allocation-friendly code"},"num":"9.1"},{"q":"What are GC roots?","a":"<p>The starting points for reachability: static fields, local variables and parameters on thread stacks (and registers), GC handles (strong and pinned), the finalization queue and interop references. Anything reachable from a root is live and won&#39;t be collected.</p>\n","chapter":"memory-gc","section":{"id":"writing-allocation-friendly-code","text":"Writing allocation-friendly code"},"num":"9.1"},{"q":"Can you have memory leaks in .NET? Give examples.","a":"<p>Yes. Leaks are objects that stay reachable but are no longer needed: ever-growing static collections or caches without eviction, event handlers not unsubscribed from long-lived publishers, captive dependencies (a singleton holding a scoped DbContext), undisposed timers, closures capturing large objects, unbounded queues, and native resources not disposed.</p>\n","chapter":"memory-gc","section":{"id":"writing-allocation-friendly-code","text":"Writing allocation-friendly code"},"num":"9.1"},{"q":"How would you investigate a memory leak in a production .NET service?","a":"<p>Confirm steady growth of Gen2/heap-after-GC with dotnet-counters or App Insights metrics. Take two gcdumps some time apart and diff them to find growing types. Then take a full dump with dotnet-dump and use gcroot on sample instances to find the reference chain keeping them alive, fix the root, and verify with load tests.</p>\n","chapter":"memory-gc","section":{"id":"writing-allocation-friendly-code","text":"Writing allocation-friendly code"},"num":"9.1"},{"q":"Why are finalizers expensive?","a":"<p>Finalizable objects are tracked from allocation. When unreachable, they must survive an extra collection (promoted to the f-reachable queue) until the single finalizer thread runs them, and only a later GC frees their memory. That&#39;s more work, longer lifetimes and nondeterministic cleanup. Use IDisposable with GC.SuppressFinalize, and SafeHandle.</p>\n","chapter":"memory-gc","section":{"id":"writing-allocation-friendly-code","text":"Writing allocation-friendly code"},"num":"9.1"},{"q":"Why shouldn't you call GC.Collect?","a":"<p>The GC&#39;s heuristics tune collection timing to allocation patterns. Forcing collections wastes CPU, causes pauses, and promotes still-live objects to older generations, making later collections more expensive. It&#39;s rarely justified outside tests and benchmarks.</p>\n","chapter":"memory-gc","section":{"id":"writing-allocation-friendly-code","text":"Writing allocation-friendly code"},"num":"9.1"},{"q":"What's the difference between concurrency, parallelism and asynchrony?","a":"<p>Concurrency is multiple tasks in progress at once (possibly interleaved). Parallelism is executing multiple tasks simultaneously on multiple cores for CPU-bound speed-up. Asynchrony is not blocking a thread while waiting for I/O, so threads can do other work. A web server is highly concurrent and mostly asynchronous.</p>\n","chapter":"threading-concurrency","section":{"id":"thread-safety-checklist-for-asp-net-core","text":"Thread safety checklist for ASP.NET Core"},"num":"9.2"},{"q":"What is the thread pool and why does blocking it hurt?","a":"<p>A pool of reusable worker threads that runs queued work items: ASP.NET Core requests, await continuations, Task.Run. It grows slowly when saturated, so blocking threads (sync-over-async, Thread.Sleep, sync I/O) causes thread-pool starvation, where queued work waits and latency spikes while CPU stays low.</p>\n","chapter":"threading-concurrency","section":{"id":"thread-safety-checklist-for-asp-net-core","text":"Thread safety checklist for ASP.NET Core"},"num":"9.2"},{"q":"What is a race condition and how do you prevent it?","a":"<p>When correctness depends on thread timing, e.g. two threads doing count++ and losing an update, or check-then-act on a dictionary. Prevent it with atomic operations (Interlocked), locks around compound operations, concurrent collections&#39; atomic methods, immutability, or avoiding shared mutable state altogether.</p>\n","chapter":"threading-concurrency","section":{"id":"thread-safety-checklist-for-asp-net-core","text":"Thread safety checklist for ASP.NET Core"},"num":"9.2"},{"q":"Why can't you use await inside a lock statement? What do you use instead?","a":"<p>Monitor locks are thread-affine (they must be released by the thread that acquired them), but after an await the continuation may run on a different thread, and holding a lock across awaits risks long blocking and deadlocks. The compiler forbids it. Use SemaphoreSlim(1,1) with WaitAsync/Release in try/finally.</p>\n","chapter":"threading-concurrency","section":{"id":"thread-safety-checklist-for-asp-net-core","text":"Thread safety checklist for ASP.NET Core"},"num":"9.2"},{"q":"How do deadlocks occur and how do you avoid them?","a":"<p>Two or more threads each hold a resource another needs and wait in a cycle. Avoid them with consistent lock ordering, short critical sections without external calls, timeouts on acquisition, lock-free or message-passing designs, and not blocking on async code where a SynchronizationContext exists.</p>\n","chapter":"threading-concurrency","section":{"id":"thread-safety-checklist-for-asp-net-core","text":"Thread safety checklist for ASP.NET Core"},"num":"9.2"},{"q":"How do you make a singleton service thread-safe?","a":"<p>Prefer immutable state, or swap immutable snapshots atomically with Interlocked.Exchange. Use concurrent collections with their atomic methods, Interlocked for counters, and locks or SemaphoreSlim around compound operations. Don&#39;t hold per-request state or non-thread-safe dependencies (like DbContext) in it.</p>\n","chapter":"threading-concurrency","section":{"id":"thread-safety-checklist-for-asp-net-core","text":"Thread safety checklist for ASP.NET Core"},"num":"9.2"},{"q":"What does the volatile keyword do?","a":"<p>It gives reads acquire semantics and writes release semantics, preventing certain compiler/JIT/CPU reorderings and register caching, so other threads see updates. It doesn&#39;t make compound operations atomic. In practice, prefer locks, Interlocked, Volatile.Read/Write or CancellationToken for signalling.</p>\n","chapter":"threading-concurrency","section":{"id":"thread-safety-checklist-for-asp-net-core","text":"Thread safety checklist for ASP.NET Core"},"num":"9.2"},{"q":"When would you use Parallel.ForEachAsync vs Task.WhenAll?","a":"<p>Parallel.ForEachAsync for processing a (possibly large) collection with a bounded degree of parallelism and cancellation. Task.WhenAll to await a known, small set of already-started independent operations. Unbounded WhenAll over thousands of items can overwhelm downstream services.</p>\n","chapter":"threading-concurrency","section":{"id":"thread-safety-checklist-for-asp-net-core","text":"Thread safety checklist for ASP.NET Core"},"num":"9.2"},{"q":"What are Channels used for?","a":"<p>In-process asynchronous producer/consumer queues (System.Threading.Channels), with bounded capacity for backpressure, completion signalling, and async reading via ReadAllAsync. They&#39;re used for background work queues and pipelines between hosted services and request handlers.</p>\n","chapter":"threading-concurrency","section":{"id":"thread-safety-checklist-for-asp-net-core","text":"Thread safety checklist for ASP.NET Core"},"num":"9.2"},{"q":"How would you approach a performance problem in a .NET API?","a":"<p>Define a measurable goal (p95 latency at a given load). Measure a baseline in a production-like environment. Use distributed traces to see which component dominates (DB, remote calls, app code). Drill in with the right tool (execution plans for SQL, dotnet-counters/trace or a profiler for CPU/GC/thread-pool issues). Fix the single biggest bottleneck, re-measure, and repeat until the goal is met. Avoid optimising without evidence.</p>\n","chapter":"performance-engineering","section":{"id":"preventing-regressions","text":"Preventing regressions"},"num":"9.3"},{"q":"Why use percentiles instead of averages for latency?","a":"<p>Averages hide the tail: a few very slow requests barely move the mean but hurt real users, and users making many requests encounter the tail frequently. p95/p99 show what most users and the worst-affected users experience, and SLOs are usually defined on them.</p>\n","chapter":"performance-engineering","section":{"id":"preventing-regressions","text":"Preventing regressions"},"num":"9.3"},{"q":"What is BenchmarkDotNet and why not just use a Stopwatch?","a":"<p>A micro-benchmarking library that runs code in an isolated Release process with warm-up, many iterations and statistical analysis, and reports allocations with MemoryDiagnoser. A Stopwatch loop is skewed by JIT tiering, warm-up, GC, dead-code elimination and noise, so it produces unreliable numbers.</p>\n","chapter":"performance-engineering","section":{"id":"preventing-regressions","text":"Preventing regressions"},"num":"9.3"},{"q":"High latency but low CPU: what do you suspect and how do you confirm it?","a":"<p>Thread-pool starvation from blocking calls (sync-over-async, sync I/O), or waiting on slow dependencies, locks or connection pools. Confirm with dotnet-counters (growing thread pool queue length and thread count), a dump showing threads blocked in Wait/GetResult, and traces showing long dependency spans.</p>\n","chapter":"performance-engineering","section":{"id":"preventing-regressions","text":"Preventing regressions"},"num":"9.3"},{"q":"What tools would you use to diagnose a production performance issue in a container?","a":"<p>Distributed traces and metrics (App Insights / OpenTelemetry) first, then dotnet-counters for runtime health, dotnet-trace for CPU/allocation/contention profiles, dotnet-dump for hangs and leaks, typically via dotnet-monitor as a sidecar or kubectl debug with an ephemeral container, plus database execution plans and Query Store.</p>\n","chapter":"performance-engineering","section":{"id":"preventing-regressions","text":"Preventing regressions"},"num":"9.3"},{"q":"Name high-impact optimisations for a typical CRUD API.","a":"<p>Fix indexes and N+1 queries, project to DTOs with AsNoTracking, paginate, cache hot reads (HybridCache, output caching), make everything async and parallelise independent remote calls, reuse HttpClient connections, compress and trim responses with System.Text.Json source generation, move slow work to background queues, and upgrade to the latest .NET.</p>\n","chapter":"performance-engineering","section":{"id":"preventing-regressions","text":"Preventing regressions"},"num":"9.3"},{"q":"How do you improve cold-start time for a .NET service?","a":"<p>Native AOT (or ReadyToRun), the slim builder, trimming, source generators instead of reflection, EF compiled models, deferring heavy initialisation out of startup, pre-warming instances (Premium/Flex always-ready, readiness probes, slot warm-up), and smaller container images.</p>\n","chapter":"performance-engineering","section":{"id":"preventing-regressions","text":"Preventing regressions"},"num":"9.3"},{"q":"What is the most common web/API vulnerability and how do you prevent it in ASP.NET Core?","a":"<p>Broken access control (IDOR/BOLA): endpoints that trust client-supplied IDs or lack function-level checks. Prevent it by requiring authorization by default (fallback policy), applying policies to every endpoint, using resource-based authorization or scoping every query by the current user or tenant, returning 404 for inaccessible resources, and testing authorization in integration tests.</p>\n","chapter":"web-security","section":{"id":"logging-and-monitoring-securely","text":"Logging and monitoring securely"},"num":"10.1"},{"q":"How do you prevent SQL injection in .NET?","a":"<p>Never concatenate input into SQL. Use EF Core LINQ, FromSql/ExecuteSql with interpolated FormattableString (converted to parameters), Dapper parameter objects, or ADO.NET parameters. Allow-list dynamic identifiers like sort columns, and run with a least-privilege database identity.</p>\n","chapter":"web-security","section":{"id":"logging-and-monitoring-securely","text":"Logging and monitoring securely"},"num":"10.1"},{"q":"What is CSRF and when does it apply?","a":"<p>Cross-Site Request Forgery tricks a logged-in user&#39;s browser into sending a state-changing request to your site with their cookies attached automatically. It applies to cookie-authenticated apps; bearer-token APIs aren&#39;t vulnerable because browsers don&#39;t attach Authorization headers automatically. Defend with antiforgery tokens, SameSite cookies and Origin checks.</p>\n","chapter":"web-security","section":{"id":"logging-and-monitoring-securely","text":"Logging and monitoring securely"},"num":"10.1"},{"q":"How should passwords be stored?","a":"<p>Hashed with a slow, salted, adaptive algorithm (PBKDF2 as in ASP.NET Core Identity&#39;s PasswordHasher, Argon2id, bcrypt or scrypt), with a per-user salt and a high work factor, re-hashing when parameters improve. Never plain text, encrypted, or fast hashes like SHA-256. Better yet, delegate authentication to an identity provider.</p>\n","chapter":"web-security","section":{"id":"logging-and-monitoring-securely","text":"Logging and monitoring securely"},"num":"10.1"},{"q":"What is the ASP.NET Core Data Protection API?","a":"<p>A built-in cryptographic API for protecting data your app needs to read back (auth cookies, antiforgery tokens, temp data, custom tokens) with authenticated encryption, purpose strings for isolation, and automatic key rotation. In multi-instance deployments, the key ring must be persisted to shared storage and protected (e.g. Blob Storage plus Key Vault).</p>\n","chapter":"web-security","section":{"id":"logging-and-monitoring-securely","text":"Logging and monitoring securely"},"num":"10.1"},{"q":"What is SSRF and how do you prevent it?","a":"<p>Server-Side Request Forgery: an attacker makes your server send requests to URLs of their choosing, reaching internal services or cloud metadata endpoints. Prevent it with allow-lists of destinations, blocking private/link-local/metadata IP ranges after DNS resolution (and on redirects), network egress restrictions, timeouts and size limits.</p>\n","chapter":"web-security","section":{"id":"logging-and-monitoring-securely","text":"Logging and monitoring securely"},"num":"10.1"},{"q":"What is mass assignment / over-posting?","a":"<p>When model binding sets properties the client shouldn&#39;t control (IsAdmin, Price) because the request is bound directly to an entity. Prevent it with dedicated request DTOs and explicit mapping, and use response DTOs to avoid excessive data exposure.</p>\n","chapter":"web-security","section":{"id":"logging-and-monitoring-securely","text":"Logging and monitoring securely"},"num":"10.1"},{"q":"Which security headers would you add to an ASP.NET Core app?","a":"<p>Strict-Transport-Security (UseHsts), X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff, X-Frame-Options: DENY or CSP frame-ancestors, Content-Security-Policy, Referrer-Policy and Permissions-Policy, plus removing the Server header and enforcing HTTPS redirection.</p>\n","chapter":"web-security","section":{"id":"logging-and-monitoring-securely","text":"Logging and monitoring securely"},"num":"10.1"},{"q":"How do you keep dependencies secure?","a":"<p>NuGet vulnerability auditing in restore and CI, automated update PRs (Dependabot/Renovate), Central Package Management with lock files and package source mapping, regular .NET runtime/SDK patching, minimal container base images that are scanned and rebuilt often, and avoiding dangerous serializers like BinaryFormatter or TypeNameHandling.All.</p>\n","chapter":"web-security","section":{"id":"logging-and-monitoring-securely","text":"Logging and monitoring securely"},"num":"10.1"},{"q":"What's the difference between OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect?","a":"<p>OAuth 2.0 is a framework for delegated authorization: clients obtain access tokens to call APIs with limited scopes on behalf of a user or themselves. OpenID Connect is an identity layer on top that adds authentication: an ID token describing the user, standard claims and scopes (openid, profile, email), the UserInfo endpoint, discovery and logout.</p>\n","chapter":"oauth-oidc-identity","section":{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"},"num":"10.2"},{"q":"Explain the authorization code flow with PKCE.","a":"<p>The client creates a random code_verifier and sends its SHA-256 hash (code_challenge) with the /authorize redirect. The user authenticates at the IdP, which redirects back with a one-time authorization code. The client exchanges the code plus the original code_verifier at the token endpoint over a back channel; the server checks the hash and returns access, ID and refresh tokens. PKCE prevents intercepted codes from being redeemed.</p>\n","chapter":"oauth-oidc-identity","section":{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"},"num":"10.2"},{"q":"Which OAuth flow would you use for a service calling another API without a user?","a":"<p>Client credentials: the service authenticates as itself (a certificate, federated credential or managed identity, rather than a secret) and receives an access token with application permissions (roles) for the target API&#39;s audience.</p>\n","chapter":"oauth-oidc-identity","section":{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"},"num":"10.2"},{"q":"Access token vs ID token vs refresh token?","a":"<p>The access token is for the API: it states audience, scopes/roles and expiry and is sent as a bearer token. The ID token is for the client: it proves who the user is and must not be sent to APIs. The refresh token goes to the authorization server to get new access tokens silently; it&#39;s long-lived, rotated and stored securely.</p>\n","chapter":"oauth-oidc-identity","section":{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"},"num":"10.2"},{"q":"Why was the implicit flow deprecated?","a":"<p>It returned tokens directly in the redirect URL fragment, exposing them to browser history, logs, referrer leaks and malicious scripts, with no way to bind them to the client and no refresh tokens. The authorization code flow with PKCE provides the same for public clients securely.</p>\n","chapter":"oauth-oidc-identity","section":{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"},"num":"10.2"},{"q":"What must an API validate on an incoming JWT access token?","a":"<p>The signature with the issuer&#39;s current public keys (from jwks_uri, by kid), the issuer, the audience (it must be this API), expiry and not-before (with small clock skew), and then the required scopes or roles, followed by resource-level authorization.</p>\n","chapter":"oauth-oidc-identity","section":{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"},"num":"10.2"},{"q":"How should a SPA handle tokens securely?","a":"<p>Preferably via the Backend-for-Frontend pattern: a server-side component performs the OIDC code flow as a confidential client, stores tokens server-side, issues an HttpOnly Secure SameSite cookie to the browser, and proxies API calls with access tokens attached. If tokens must live in the browser, use code + PKCE, keep them in memory with short lifetimes and refresh-token rotation, and never put them in localStorage.</p>\n","chapter":"oauth-oidc-identity","section":{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"},"num":"10.2"},{"q":"What is the On-Behalf-Of flow?","a":"<p>A middle-tier API that received a user&#39;s access token exchanges it at the authorization server for a new access token for a downstream API, so the downstream call carries the user&#39;s identity and delegated permissions (Entra ID OBO, or RFC 8693 token exchange).</p>\n","chapter":"oauth-oidc-identity","section":{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"},"num":"10.2"},{"q":"How do you approach a system design interview question?","a":"<p>Clarify functional and non-functional requirements and the scope; do quick capacity estimates; define the main APIs and data model; draw a high-level architecture; deep-dive into the hardest parts (scaling the hot path, data consistency, failure handling); and close with bottlenecks, monitoring and trade-offs, explaining the reasons behind every choice.</p>\n","chapter":"system-design","section":{"id":"code-level-design-questions","text":"Code-level design questions"},"num":"11.1"},{"q":"How would you prevent overselling in a high-traffic shop?","a":"<p>Make stock decrement atomic and conditional in the database (UPDATE … WHERE Available &gt;= @qty, checking rows affected) or use optimistic concurrency on the stock row; use time-limited reservations released by a compensating action if payment fails; and for extreme hot items, serialise writes per SKU through a queue or partition. Never check-then-update in application memory.</p>\n","chapter":"system-design","section":{"id":"code-level-design-questions","text":"Code-level design questions"},"num":"11.1"},{"q":"How would you design a rate limiter?","a":"<p>Clarify per-user/IP/API-key limits and the algorithm (token bucket for bursts, sliding window for fairness). Within one instance, use ASP.NET Core&#39;s rate limiter; for a distributed limit, keep counters or buckets in Redis with atomic operations (INCR with expiry, or a Lua script for token bucket), or enforce it at the gateway (APIM/Front Door). Return 429 with Retry-After, and decide fail-open vs fail-closed if Redis is unavailable.</p>\n","chapter":"system-design","section":{"id":"code-level-design-questions","text":"Code-level design questions"},"num":"11.1"},{"q":"How do you scale a read-heavy API?","a":"<p>Make the service stateless and scale out; add caching layers (CDN/output cache for public responses, HybridCache/Redis for data); use read replicas or CQRS read models; optimise queries and indexes; paginate and project; and move expensive work to async background processing.</p>\n","chapter":"system-design","section":{"id":"code-level-design-questions","text":"Code-level design questions"},"num":"11.1"},{"q":"How should I prepare for a .NET backend interview in the last week?","a":"<p>Drill the question bank daily in mixed mode, answering out loud before revealing. Re-read the Beginner sections of DI, middleware, async/await, EF Core querying and API design (the most-asked topics). Rehearse the request-lifecycle walkthrough and two system designs out loud. Prepare 5–6 STAR stories from your work or practice project. Review the job description&#39;s specific technologies (e.g. Service Bus, AKS) in their chapters.</p>\n","chapter":"study-plan-resources","section":{"id":"keeping-this-guide-up-to-date","text":"Keeping this guide up to date"},"num":"11.2"},{"q":"How do you answer a question you don't fully know?","a":"<p>Be honest about the gap, connect it to what you do know, and reason from first principles out loud, e.g. &quot;I haven&#39;t used X directly, but it&#39;s a partitioned log like Kafka, so I&#39;d expect ordering per partition and consumers tracking offsets.&quot; Interviewers value structured reasoning and honesty over bluffing.</p>\n","chapter":"study-plan-resources","section":{"id":"keeping-this-guide-up-to-date","text":"Keeping this guide up to date"},"num":"11.2"}],"search":[{"c":"big-picture","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR .NET is organised in 8 layers , each built on the one below: Language → Runtime → Frameworks → Architecture → Data → Service comms → Hosting/infra → Cross-cutting. Most confusion comes from one English word (\"service\", \"context\", \"scope\", \"handler\") meaning something different at each layer — know the layer, know the meaning. A single HTTP request touches almost every layer on its way in and back out; that end-to-end trip is the backbone of the \"walk me through a request\" interview question. Microsoft reuses a few product-name families (the platform family, the web family, the data family, the Microsoft.Extensions.* glue, the cloud family) — recognising the family tells you what something is for even if you've never seen its exact name. Read Beginner sections everywhere first for a shallow full map, then go deeper where interviews actually probe (C#/async, DI, middleware, EF Core, SOLID, REST, auth, testing, cloud)."},{"c":"big-picture","s":"why-net-feels-like-a-maze","h":"Why .NET feels like a maze","t":"# Why .NET feels like a maze Beginner You feel lost because you learned the pieces one by one, from tutorials that each assume you already know the other pieces. \"Add a scoped service\", \"register the middleware\", \"inject IOptions<T> \", \"deploy to Container Apps\": each sentence comes from a different layer of the system, and nobody showed you the layers. The fix is a map with layers . Every concept in .NET belongs to exactly one layer, and each layer only builds on the layers beneath it. Once you know the layer, you know: what it depends on (the layers below it), what depends on it (the layers above it), what it is not (a middleware is not a design pattern; a DI lifetime is not a cloud setting). A building Think of a backend system as a building. C# is the bricks. The runtime (CLR) is the foundation and the plumbing: water (memory) and electricity (threads) are supplied without you thinking about them. ASP.NET Core and EF Core are pre-built floors you move into. Architecture is how you arrange the rooms. Data is the storage in the basement. Communication is the corridors and lifts between buildings. Infrastructure is the land, the city and the roads (the cloud). Cross-cutting concerns are the building codes (security, fire inspections and maintenance) that apply to every floor."},{"c":"big-picture","s":"the-eight-layers","h":"The eight layers","t":"# The eight layers Beginner This is the map the whole book uses. Every chapter opens with this stack and highlights the layer you are in. Diagram Copy ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │ 8 CROSS-CUTTING Testing · Security · Observability · Performance │ ← applies to every layer ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤ │ 7 HOSTING & INFRA Docker · Azure App Service/Functions/Container Apps │ where it runs │ Kubernetes · CI/CD · Infrastructure as Code │ ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤ │ 6 SERVICE COMMS REST/HTTP · gRPC · SignalR · Message brokers │ how services talk │ Events · Outbox · Sagas · Resilience (Polly) │ ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤ │ 5 DATA & STATE SQL Server/Postgres · EF Core · Dapper · Redis │ where state lives │ Cosmos DB · Blob storage · Caching │ ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤ │ 4 ARCHITECTURE SOLID · Design patterns · Clean/Onion · Vertical │ how code is organised │ slices · CQRS · DDD · Microservices │ ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤ │ 3 FRAMEWORKS ASP.NET Core: hosting, DI, config, middleware, │ pre-built machinery │ routing, APIs, auth · EF Core · Worker services │ ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤ │ 2 RUNTIME & PLATFORM CLR · JIT · GC · Base Class Library · SDK · NuGet │ what executes code ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤ │ 1 LANGUAGE C#: types, OOP, generics, LINQ, async/await … │ what you write └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ # Layer Question it answers Typical interview question 1 Language How do I express logic? \"Struct vs class?\", \"What does await do?\" 2 Runtime & platform What runs my code and manages memory? \"What is the JIT?\", \"How does the GC work?\" 3 Frameworks What machinery handles HTTP, DI, config, DB mapping? \"Explain middleware order\", \"Scoped vs singleton?\" 4 Architecture How should I organise my own code? \"Explain Clean Architecture\", \"When CQRS?\" 5 Data & state Where does data live and how do I get it efficiently? \"N+1 problem?\", \"Clustered index?\" 6 Service communication How do separate processes talk reliably? \"Outbox pattern?\", \"Circuit breaker?\" 7 Hosting & infrastructure Where does it run and how does it get there? \"App Service vs AKS?\", \"Multi-stage Dockerfile?\" 8 Cross-cutting Is it correct, secure, visible and fast? \"How do you test this?\", \"How do you find a slow endpoint?\" How to use the map when you're lost When you meet a new term, ask \"which layer?\" first. IOptionsMonitor is layer 3 (an ASP.NET Core/hosting feature). Span<T> is layer 1 and 2 (a C# type that the runtime treats specially). \"Saga\" is layer 6. \"Managed identity\" is layer 7, and it shows up in layer 5 when you connect to a database without a password. Once you know where something lives, it stops being random."},{"c":"big-picture","s":"the-same-thing-at-different-layers","h":"The same thing at different layers","t":"# The same thing at different layers Beginner A lot of confusion comes from one word meaning different things on different layers, or one idea showing up at several layers. Here are the most common: Word Layer 1–2 meaning Layer 3 meaning Layer 4+ meaning Pipeline — The middleware pipeline (request → middleware → endpoint) CI/CD pipeline (build → test → deploy); MediatR \"pipeline behaviours\" Host The process that loads the CLR The Generic Host : object that owns DI, config, logging, lifetime The machine/container/App Service that runs your app Service — A class registered in the DI container A deployable application (\"the Orders service\") Handler An event handler (delegate) Route handler, authorization handler, DelegatingHandler Command/query handler in CQRS Filter — MVC/endpoint filters around an action EF Core global query filters (data layer) Context SynchronizationContext , ExecutionContext HttpContext (one request) DbContext (one unit of work), DDD \"bounded context\" Scope Variable scope DI scope (= one HTTP request) Logging scope, transaction scope Repository — — Data-access pattern (layer 4/5); also \"Git repository\" Event C# event keyword IHostApplicationLifetime events Domain events, integration events, Event Grid The single biggest mix-up \"Service\" in builder.Services.AddScoped<IOrderService, OrderService>() means \"a class the DI container can create\". \"Service\" in \"microservices\" means \"a separately deployed application\". An app with 200 DI services can still be one monolith."},{"c":"big-picture","s":"follow-one-request-through-every-layer","h":"Follow one request through every layer","t":"# Follow one request through every layer Beginner This trace touches nearly every chapter in the book, so it's the best way to see how the pieces connect. Imagine a mobile app calls: HTTP Copy POST https : //api.shop.com/orders Authorization : Bearer eyJhbGciOi... Content-Type : application/json { \"customerId\" : 42 , \"lines\" : [ { \"sku\" : \"KB-01\" , \"qty\" : 1 } ] } # 1. Infrastructure: the request finds your code (layer 7) DNS points api.shop.com at an Azure Front Door or Application Gateway . It ends the TLS connection and forwards the request to one of several running instances of your app. Those instances run in Azure Container Apps , as a container built from your Dockerfile and deployed by your CI/CD pipeline . There are several instances because the app is stateless and scaled out : any instance can handle any request. → Cloud & Azure overview , Docker , Azure compute . # 2. Runtime: a process is already running (layer 2) Inside the container, dotnet Shop.Api.dll started earlier. The CLR loaded your assemblies. The JIT compiled each method to machine code the first time it ran. The GC manages every object you allocate, and the thread pool supplies the threads that do the work. → How code runs , Memory & GC . # 3. Framework: Kestrel and the host (layer 3) Kestrel , ASP.NET Core's web server, reads the bytes off the socket and turns them into an HttpContext . The Generic Host that Program.cs built owns the DI container , configuration and logging for the whole app. For this one request, a new DI scope is created. → Hosting , Dependency injection , Configuration . # 4. Framework: the middleware pipeline (layer 3) The request walks through middleware, in the order you registered it: Diagram Copy ExceptionHandler → HTTPS redirection → Routing → CORS → Authentication → Authorization → Rate limiting → (your custom middleware) → Endpoint Authentication checks the JWT — its signature, issuer, audience and expiry — and fills in HttpContext.User . Authorization then checks the endpoint's policy, for example RequireAuthorization(\"CanOrder\") . → Middleware , Routing , Auth , OAuth & OIDC . # 5. Framework: binding, validation, your endpoint (layers 1 + 3) Model binding turns the request JSON into a PlaceOrderRequest record — an ordinary C# type (layer 1). Validation rejects bad input with a 400 ProblemDetails response. If it passes, your minimal API handler or controller action runs, with its dependencies already injected. → Building APIs , Errors & logging . # 6. Architecture: your code's shape (layer 4) The handler doesn't talk to SQL directly. It sends a PlaceOrder command to a handler (this is CQRS). That handler loads a Customer aggregate and calls Order.Create(...) , a DDD rich model that enforces rules like \"an order needs at least one line\". It returns a Result instead of throwing on failure. The project is structured as Domain / Application / Infrastructure / API, which is Clean Architecture , and its interfaces follow SOLID . → Design principles , App architectures , CQRS , DDD . # 7. Language: async all the way down (layer 1) Every I/O call is await ed with a CancellationToken . While the database does its work, the thread goes back to the pool and serves other requests instead of sitting idle. → async/await . # 8. Data: EF Core and SQL (layer 5) EF Core turns LINQ into SQL. The DbContext , scoped to this one request, tracks the new Order , and SaveChangesAsync writes it inside a transaction . In that same transaction, an OrderPlaced message is written to an Outbox table, so it can't be lost even if the next step fails. A product lookup earlier was served from Redis via HybridCache instead of hitting the database. → EF Core , Querying , Relational fundamentals , NoSQL & Redis . # 9. Communication: telling other services (layer 6) A background service reads the outbox and publishes OrderPlaced to Azure Service Bus . The Payments service and the Email service each pick it up independently. Calls out to the external payment pro"},{"c":"big-picture","s":"what-belongs-to-what-the-ecosystem-by-family","h":"What belongs to what: the ecosystem by family","t":"# What belongs to what: the ecosystem by family Beginner The second source of confusion is product names. Microsoft ships many things with similar names. Here they are grouped by family, with the layer each lives in. # The platform family (layer 2) Name What it is .NET (5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10…) The modern, cross-platform platform: runtime + libraries + SDK. .NET Framework (4.x) The old Windows-only platform. Still runs legacy apps. Not getting new features. .NET Core (1.0–3.1) The old name for modern .NET before version 5. .NET Standard A specification of APIs, used to write libraries that run on both Framework and modern .NET. CLR The runtime engine (JIT, GC, type system, threads). BCL Base Class Library: System.* types like List<T> , HttpClient , Task . SDK / dotnet CLI Build tools: compiler (Roslyn), MSBuild, templates. NuGet The package manager and package format. Mono / Xamarin Older cross-platform runtime used for mobile; merged into modern .NET. # The web family (layer 3) Name What it is Use it for ASP.NET Core The web framework (the umbrella) Everything web ↳ Minimal APIs Lightweight HTTP endpoints as functions JSON APIs, microservices ↳ MVC / Web API controllers Class-based endpoints JSON APIs in larger/traditional codebases ↳ Razor Pages Page-focused server-rendered HTML Server-rendered web apps ↳ Blazor C# UI components running on the server or in the browser (WebAssembly) Interactive web UIs without JavaScript ↳ SignalR Real-time push over WebSockets Chat, live dashboards ↳ gRPC High-performance binary RPC over HTTP/2 Service-to-service calls Kestrel The web server inside ASP.NET Core Always there ASP.NET (no \"Core\") The old Framework-only web stack ( System.Web , Web Forms, MVC 5) Legacy only YARP A reverse proxy library built on ASP.NET Core API gateways # The data family (layer 5) Name What it is ADO.NET The low-level DB access API ( DbConnection , DbCommand ). Everything else is built on it. EF Core The full ORM: LINQ to SQL, change tracking, migrations. Entity Framework 6 The old ORM for .NET Framework. Dapper A micro-ORM: you write SQL, it maps results to objects. Microsoft.Data.SqlClient / Npgsql The ADO.NET drivers for SQL Server / PostgreSQL. # The \"Microsoft.Extensions\" family: the glue (layer 3) These packages are the plumbing that ASP.NET Core, worker services and even console apps share. Recognising this family resolves a lot of confusion: DI, configuration, logging, options and hosting are not ASP.NET Core features. They are general .NET libraries that ASP.NET Core uses. Package family Provides Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection The DI container ( IServiceCollection , IServiceProvider ) Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration Layered configuration ( IConfiguration ) Microsoft.Extensions.Options IOptions<T> , validation Microsoft.Extensions.Logging ILogger<T> Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting The Generic Host, IHostedService , BackgroundService Microsoft.Extensions.Http IHttpClientFactory Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.* IMemoryCache , IDistributedCache , HybridCache Microsoft.Extensions.Resilience Polly-based resilience pipelines # The cloud family (layer 7) Need Azure service Run a web app/API App Service, Container Apps, AKS, Functions Relational DB Azure SQL Database, Azure Database for PostgreSQL Document DB Cosmos DB Files/blobs Blob Storage Cache Azure Managed Redis Messaging Service Bus (commands), Event Grid (event routing), Event Hubs (streams) Secrets Key Vault Identity Microsoft Entra ID (formerly Azure AD), managed identities Monitoring Azure Monitor / Application Insights Gateway API Management, Front Door, Application Gateway"},{"c":"big-picture","s":"the-vocabulary-every-chapter-assumes","h":"The vocabulary every chapter assumes","t":"# The vocabulary every chapter assumes Beginner Short definitions so no chapter surprises you. Each one is covered in depth later. Assembly : a compiled .dll (or .exe ): IL code plus metadata. The unit you deploy and reference. Namespace : a naming scope ( System.Collections.Generic ). Has nothing to do with files or assemblies. IL : Intermediate Language, the CPU-independent bytecode C# compiles to. JIT : the compiler inside the runtime that turns IL into machine code at runtime. GC : the garbage collector, which frees memory you no longer reference. Managed code : code running under the CLR (memory managed for you). Task : an object representing work that completes later; the basis of async / await . DI container : the object that creates your classes and passes in their dependencies. Lifetime : how long the container keeps an instance: transient, scoped or singleton. Middleware : a component in the request pipeline that can act before and after the next one. Endpoint : the final handler for a route (a controller action or a minimal API delegate). DTO : Data Transfer Object, a simple type used to move data across a boundary (HTTP, messages). Entity : an object with identity that you persist (an Order with an Id ). ORM : Object-Relational Mapper (EF Core): maps objects to tables. Migration : a versioned, code-generated change to the database schema. Idempotent : doing it twice has the same effect as doing it once. Eventual consistency : data in different places will agree eventually , not instantly. Stateless : no request depends on memory held by one specific server instance."},{"c":"big-picture","s":"how-to-read-this-book","h":"How to read this book","t":"# How to read this book Beginner First pass: breadth. Read every chapter's Beginner sections, plus the \"Where this fits\" box. Skip Advanced sections. The goal is a complete, shallow map in your head. Second pass: depth where interviews go. The most-asked areas for .NET backend roles are, in order: C# fundamentals and async , DI lifetimes , middleware and the request pipeline , EF Core performance , SOLID and patterns , REST API design , authentication with JWT , testing , then cloud and distributed systems for senior roles. Third pass: Advanced sections and internals. GC, async state machines, threading, Kubernetes, sagas. Every day: the question bank. Use drill mode. Say answers out loud before revealing them. If you can't explain it simply, go back to the linked section. Each section heading has a level chip: Beginner : vocabulary and core usage. Expected of every candidate. Intermediate : how it works and how to use it well. Expected with 2+ years of experience. Advanced : internals, trade-offs and edge cases. Senior and lead interviews. Q What is the difference between .NET, .NET Framework, .NET Core and ASP.NET Core? .NET Framework is the original Windows-only platform. .NET Core was its cross-platform rewrite; from version 5 it is simply called \".NET\" and is the current platform. ASP.NET Core is the web framework that runs on .NET (layer 3), not a platform itself. Q Are dependency injection, configuration and logging part of ASP.NET Core? They are used by ASP.NET Core, but they live in the general-purpose Microsoft.Extensions.* libraries and work in any .NET app: worker services, console apps, Azure Functions. ASP.NET Core's WebApplication.CreateBuilder simply wires them up for you. Q Walk me through what happens when an HTTP request reaches a .NET API. Infrastructure (load balancer/gateway) forwards it to an instance; Kestrel parses it into an HttpContext and a DI scope is created; it passes through middleware in order (exception handling, HTTPS, routing, CORS, authentication, authorization, rate limiting); the endpoint runs (model binding, validation, filters, handler); the handler calls application/domain code and EF Core asynchronously; the result is serialized; the response flows back out through the middleware; the scope is disposed. Logs, metrics and traces are emitted throughout. Q What is the difference between a DI \"service\" and a microservice? A DI service is any class registered in the dependency injection container in one process. A microservice is a separately deployable application with its own data, communicating over the network. They share a word and nothing else."},{"c":"big-picture","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading .NET architecture overview — Microsoft's own layered view of the platform, close to the map used here. ASP.NET Core fundamentals — the canonical source for the middleware pipeline order shown in step 4 of the request trace. .NET and .NET Framework — what's the difference — backs the \".NET vs .NET Framework vs .NET Core\" claims in the platform family table."},{"c":"big-picture","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Most claims here are about names and layers , not runtime behaviour, so there's little to assert about infrastructure or cloud pieces — trust Further reading for those. What is checkable locally: that managed code really runs under the CLR, that a Task really represents work completing later, and that the GC really reclaims unreachable objects. Run with dotnet run (plain console app, .NET 8/9, no extra packages). C# Run ▶ Copy // Claim: \"Managed code\" runs under the CLR, which reports its own runtime version. var asm = System . Reflection . Assembly . GetExecutingAssembly (); if ( string . IsNullOrEmpty (asm. ImageRuntimeVersion )) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: no CLR runtime version reported — not managed code?\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: running under CLR {asm.ImageRuntimeVersion} (managed code)\" ); // Claim: a Task represents work that completes later, not immediately. Task < int > slow = SlowlyComputeAsync (); if (slow. IsCompleted ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: task completed synchronously, can't demonstrate 'completes later'\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: Task is pending immediately after starting (completes later)\" ); int result = await slow; if (!slow. IsCompletedSuccessfully || result != 42 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: task did not complete successfully with the expected result\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: awaited Task completed successfully with result 42\" ); // Claim: the GC frees memory you no longer reference. var weak = AllocateAndDrop (); // helper method so the object is truly out of scope GC . Collect ( 2 , GCCollectionMode . Forced , blocking: true ); GC . WaitForPendingFinalizers (); GC . Collect ( 2 , GCCollectionMode . Forced , blocking: true ); if (weak. IsAlive ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: unreferenced object was not collected\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: GC reclaimed the unreferenced object\" ); static WeakReference AllocateAndDrop () { object obj = new byte [ 1_000_000 ]; return new WeakReference (obj); } // Claim: \"idempotent\" = doing it twice has the same effect as doing it once. var set = new HashSet < int >(); set . Add ( 5 ); set . Add ( 5 ); if ( set . Count != 1 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: HashSet.Add was not idempotent in this check\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: adding the same item twice to a set is idempotent (count stays 1)\" ); static async Task < int > SlowlyComputeAsync () { await Task . Delay ( 50 ); return 42 ; } // Not runtime-verifiable here — see Further reading: // - exact middleware execution order (needs a real ASP.NET Core host) // - JIT compiling IL to machine code on first call (observable via profilers, not plain code) // - cloud/infra claims (Front Door, Container Apps, Service Bus, Key Vault, etc.)"},{"c":"what-is-dotnet","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR .NET is a platform (runtime + BCL + SDK), not a language — C#, F# and VB all compile to it. The confusing names are historical: .NET Framework (old, Windows-only) → .NET Core (rewrite, cross-platform) → renamed to just .NET from version 5 onward. Pick LTS versions (even numbers: 6, 8, 10) for production — 3 years of support vs 2 for STS (odd numbers). Deployment mode is a trade-off: framework-dependent (small, needs a runtime installed) vs self-contained vs Native AOT (fastest startup, some reflection limits). The BCL already ships most things you'd reach for a NuGet package for: JSON, HTTP, collections, crypto, regex, metrics."},{"c":"what-is-dotnet","s":"net-is-a-platform-not-a-language","h":".NET is a platform, not a language","t":"# .NET is a platform, not a language Beginner .NET is a software platform : everything needed to build and run applications. C# is the most popular language for it, but F# and Visual Basic compile to the same format and run on the same platform. The platform has four parts: Part What it is Where you meet it Runtime (CLR) The engine that loads compiled code, JIT-compiles it to machine code, manages memory (GC), threads, exceptions and type safety Always running underneath your app Base Class Library (BCL) Thousands of ready-made types: string , List<T> , Dictionary<K,V> , Task , HttpClient , File , JsonSerializer , DateTime … Every using System... SDK Build-time tools: the C# compiler (Roslyn), MSBuild, the dotnet CLI, project templates Your dev machine and CI servers App frameworks (shared frameworks) Big library sets shipped with the runtime: Microsoft.NETCore.App (base), Microsoft.AspNetCore.App (web), Microsoft.WindowsDesktop.App (WPF/WinForms) What your project's SDK attribute pulls in Runtime vs SDK The runtime is like a DVD player: it plays discs. The SDK is the studio that makes discs. A production server only needs the player (the runtime). A developer machine and a CI server need the studio (the SDK), which includes a runtime so you can run what you build. # Runtime-only installs You'll see three runtime downloads: .NET Runtime : console apps and libraries. ASP.NET Core Runtime : the above plus the web framework. Use this for web servers. .NET Desktop Runtime : Windows only, for WPF/WinForms. Docker images mirror this: mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/sdk (build), .../aspnet (run web apps), .../runtime (run console apps), .../runtime-deps (only native dependencies, for self-contained apps)."},{"c":"what-is-dotnet","s":"the-history-that-explains-the-names","h":"The history that explains the names","t":"# The history that explains the names Beginner Most confusion about .NET comes from its renaming. Here is the timeline: Year Event Why it matters today 2002 .NET Framework 1.0 : Windows only, installed into the OS Legacy enterprise apps still run on 4.8 2002–2019 Framework 2.0 → 4.8: generics, LINQ, async, WPF, WCF, ASP.NET MVC Old tutorials, web.config , System.Web 2004 Mono : an open-source, cross-platform reimplementation Became Xamarin (mobile) and Unity's scripting runtime 2016 .NET Core 1.0 : a ground-up rewrite. Cross-platform, open source, modular, fast The start of modern .NET 2016 ASP.NET Core : a rewrite of ASP.NET for .NET Core Today's web framework 2019 .NET Core 3.1 (LTS) Was the \"standard\" version for years 2020 .NET 5 : \"Core\" dropped from the name; skipped version 4 to avoid confusion with Framework 4.x One .NET going forward 2021 .NET 6 (LTS): minimal APIs, hot reload Still found in many companies 2023 .NET 8 (LTS): Native AOT for APIs, keyed DI, TimeProvider The most common production version in 2025–26 2024 .NET 9 (STS): HybridCache , built-in OpenAPI, LINQ CountBy / Index 2025 .NET 10 (LTS) , C# 14 Current LTS \"ASP.NET\" vs \"ASP.NET Core\" ASP.NET (Web Forms, MVC 5, Web API 2, System.Web , Global.asax , web.config ) runs only on .NET Framework. ASP.NET Core is the modern rewrite. They share concepts (controllers, Razor) but the internals, configuration and hosting are completely different. If a tutorial has Global.asax or HttpContext.Current , it is about the old one. # Why the rewrite happened .NET Framework had a few structural problems. None of them could be fixed without breaking apps that already depended on the old behaviour, so Microsoft started over instead: Windows only. Meanwhile the industry moved to Linux and containers. Machine-wide installs. Upgrading Framework on a server upgraded it for every app on that server, so companies ended up freezing versions for years rather than risk breaking something else. .NET Core fixes this: versions can run side by side , and an app can even be self-contained , carrying its own runtime with it. Monolithic System.Web . ASP.NET was tied to IIS and loaded a huge pipeline even for a tiny API. ASP.NET Core is modular instead: you add only the middleware you actually use. Performance. .NET Core was designed for throughput from day one. ASP.NET Core regularly places among the fastest mainstream web frameworks in TechEmpower benchmarks."},{"c":"what-is-dotnet","s":"versions-support-and-release-cadence","h":"Versions, support and release cadence","t":"# Versions, support and release cadence Intermediate A new major version ships every November . Type Versions Support length LTS (Long Term Support) Even numbers: 6, 8, 10 3 years STS (Standard Term Support) Odd numbers: 7, 9, 11 2 years (extended from 18 months starting with .NET 9) Patch releases come monthly (\"Patch Tuesday\") with security fixes. You get them by updating the runtime or rebuilding on the latest base image. What to say in an interview \"We target the current LTS in production because it gives three years of patches. We upgrade once per LTS cycle, and upgrading is usually just changing the target framework, updating packages and fixing a few breaking changes listed in the official docs.\" # Target framework monikers (TFMs) Your project declares which .NET it targets: TFM Meaning net10.0 , net8.0 Modern .NET, any OS net8.0-windows Modern .NET with Windows-only APIs (WPF, WinForms) net8.0-android , net8.0-ios Mobile via .NET MAUI netstandard2.0 A library usable from Framework 4.6.1+ and all modern .NET net48 .NET Framework 4.8 You can multi-target a library with <TargetFrameworks>net8.0;netstandard2.0</TargetFrameworks> (note the plural)."},{"c":"what-is-dotnet","s":"net-standard-a-contract-not-a-runtime","h":".NET Standard: a contract, not a runtime","t":"# .NET Standard: a contract, not a runtime Intermediate During the transition years there were three runtimes (Framework, Core, Mono/Xamarin) with different API sets. .NET Standard was a specification : a list of APIs that every compliant runtime promises to implement. A library targeting netstandard2.0 runs on all of them. .NET Standard 2.0 is the useful one: supported by Framework 4.6.1+, .NET Core 2.0+, all modern .NET, Mono, Unity. .NET Standard 2.1 dropped .NET Framework support, which made it much less useful. No new versions will be made. Modern .NET ( net8.0 etc.) is the \"standard\" now. When to target it today: only for libraries that must also be consumed by .NET Framework apps. Otherwise target net8.0 / net10.0 to get modern APIs ( Span<T> overloads, newer collections, etc.)."},{"c":"what-is-dotnet","s":"cross-platform-open-source-and-who-builds-it","h":"Cross-platform, open source, and who builds it","t":"# Cross-platform, open source, and who builds it Beginner Runs on Windows, Linux, macOS; x64, x86, Arm64 (including Apple Silicon, AWS Graviton, Azure Cobalt). Linux containers are the dominant production deployment. Open source under the MIT licence, developed on GitHub ( dotnet/runtime , dotnet/aspnetcore , dotnet/efcore , dotnet/roslyn ), stewarded by the .NET Foundation . Built by Microsoft with large community contribution. Design discussions (API proposals) happen in the open on GitHub issues. # What runs on .NET App type Technology Layer-3 framework Web APIs, websites ASP.NET Core (minimal APIs, MVC, Razor Pages, Blazor) Microsoft.AspNetCore.App Background processing Worker Service (Generic Host) Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting Serverless Azure Functions (isolated worker), AWS Lambda Desktop WPF, WinForms (Windows), .NET MAUI, Avalonia Mobile .NET MAUI (iOS, Android) Games Unity (Mono/IL2CPP based), Godot, MonoGame Cloud-native distributed apps .NET Aspire for orchestration ML ML.NET, ONNX Runtime This book focuses on backend : web APIs, workers, data and cloud."},{"c":"what-is-dotnet","s":"framework-dependent-vs-self-contained-deployment","h":"Framework-dependent vs self-contained deployment","t":"# Framework-dependent vs self-contained deployment Intermediate When you dotnet publish , you choose how the app ships: Mode What's in the output Pros Cons Framework-dependent (default) Your DLLs only Small; shared runtime is patched centrally Target machine needs the right runtime installed Self-contained ( --self-contained -r linux-x64 ) Your DLLs + the entire runtime No runtime needed on the machine; pin exact version Larger (≈70 MB+); you must rebuild to get runtime patches Single file ( -p:PublishSingleFile=true ) One executable (self-contained or not) Easy distribution Startup extraction quirks for some native libs Native AOT ( -p:PublishAot=true ) One native binary, no JIT Instant startup, low memory Reflection limits; not all libraries are compatible ReadyToRun ( -p:PublishReadyToRun=true ) IL + precompiled native code Faster startup, still JIT-capable Larger files The RID (runtime identifier) such as linux-x64 , linux-musl-arm64 (Alpine), win-x64 , osx-arm64 says which platform a self-contained/native build targets."},{"c":"what-is-dotnet","s":"the-bcl-a-tour-of-what-you-get-for-free","h":"The BCL: a tour of what you get for free","t":"# The BCL: a tour of what you get for free Beginner Knowing what is already in the box saves you writing (or installing) it. Highlights by namespace: Namespace Key types Used for System String , DateTime , DateTimeOffset , TimeSpan , Guid , Math , Span<T> , Exception , TimeProvider Basics System.Collections.Generic List<T> , Dictionary<K,V> , HashSet<T> , Queue<T> , PriorityQueue<T,P> Data structures System.Collections.Concurrent ConcurrentDictionary , ConcurrentQueue , BlockingCollection Thread-safe collections System.Collections.Immutable / Frozen ImmutableList , FrozenDictionary Snapshots, read-mostly lookups System.Linq Enumerable , Queryable Querying System.Threading / .Tasks Task , CancellationToken , SemaphoreSlim , Channel<T> , Parallel Async and concurrency System.Text.Json JsonSerializer , JsonDocument , Utf8JsonWriter JSON (the default serializer) System.Net.Http HttpClient Calling HTTP APIs System.IO File , Stream , Path , FileSystemWatcher Files and streams System.IO.Pipelines Pipe , PipeReader High-performance I/O (used by Kestrel) System.Diagnostics Activity , Stopwatch , Process , Metrics.Meter Tracing, timing, metrics System.Security.Cryptography SHA256 , Aes , RandomNumberGenerator , X509Certificate2 Crypto System.Text.RegularExpressions Regex (with source generator) Pattern matching on text DateTime vs DateTimeOffset DateTime doesn't reliably carry a time zone offset (its Kind is often Unspecified ). For timestamps stored in databases and sent over APIs, prefer DateTimeOffset or always use UTC ( DateTime.UtcNow ). For testable time, inject TimeProvider (.NET 8) instead of calling DateTime.Now directly. Q What is the difference between the .NET SDK and the .NET runtime? The runtime contains the CLR and the libraries needed to run a compiled app. The SDK contains the compiler, MSBuild, CLI and templates needed to build apps, and it includes a runtime. Production servers and runtime Docker images need only the runtime (for web apps, the ASP.NET Core runtime); dev machines and CI need the SDK. Q Why was .NET Core created instead of continuing .NET Framework? Framework was Windows-only, installed machine-wide (so upgrades affected every app), tied to IIS via a monolithic System.Web, and hard to make fast without breaking compatibility. .NET Core was a cross-platform, open-source, modular rewrite with side-by-side installs and much better performance. Q What is .NET Standard and should you still target it? A specification of APIs that multiple .NET implementations promise to support, so one library could run everywhere during the transition. Target netstandard2.0 only if the library must also be used by .NET Framework apps; otherwise target a modern TFM such as net8.0 or net10.0 . Q What do LTS and STS mean? Which would you choose for production? LTS (even versions) gets 3 years of support; STS (odd versions) gets 2 years. Most companies pick LTS for production to reduce upgrade frequency, while trying STS releases in non-critical services to get features earlier. Q Framework-dependent vs self-contained deployment? Framework-dependent publishes only your app and relies on an installed shared runtime: small, centrally patched. Self-contained bundles the runtime: no install needed and version-pinned, but larger, and you must redeploy to pick up runtime security patches. Q Name some things in the BCL you'd use instead of a third-party package. System.Text.Json for JSON, HttpClient + IHttpClientFactory for HTTP, Channel<T> for producer/consumer queues, PriorityQueue, TimeProvider for testable time, System.Diagnostics.Metrics and Activity for telemetry, built-in rate limiting, and the source-generated Regex."},{"c":"what-is-dotnet","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading What is .NET? (Microsoft Learn) .NET support policy Target frameworks Application publishing overview"},{"c":"what-is-dotnet","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Version/support-cycle dates and \"which OS runs .NET\" aren't things one running process can prove about itself — trust Further reading for those. What the program below checks are the BCL behaviours this chapter calls out: the DateTime vs DateTimeOffset gotcha, TimeProvider being injectable/testable, and System.Text.Json being usable with zero extra packages. Run with dotnet run (plain console app, .NET 8/9, no extra packages). C# Run ▶ Copy using System . Text . Json ; // Claim: DateTime doesn't reliably carry a time zone offset — Kind is often Unspecified. var parsed = DateTime . Parse ( \"2026-01-01T10:00:00\" ); if (parsed. Kind != DateTimeKind . Unspecified ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected DateTime.Parse without a zone to be Kind.Unspecified\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: a plain DateTime.Parse carries no reliable zone info (Kind.Unspecified)\" ); // DateTimeOffset, by contrast, always carries an explicit offset. var withOffset = DateTimeOffset . Parse ( \"2026-01-01T10:00:00+02:00\" ); if (withOffset. Offset != TimeSpan . FromHours ( 2 )) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected DateTimeOffset to preserve the +02:00 offset\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: DateTimeOffset preserves the offset that DateTime would have lost\" ); // Claim: inject TimeProvider instead of calling DateTime.Now directly, so time is testable. var fixedTime = new DateTimeOffset ( 2030 , 6 , 1 , 0 , 0 , 0 , TimeSpan . Zero ); TimeProvider fakeClock = new FixedTimeProvider (fixedTime); if (fakeClock. GetUtcNow () != fixedTime) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: a fake TimeProvider should let you control 'now' in a test\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: TimeProvider can be substituted with a fixed clock for deterministic tests\" ); // Claim: System.Text.Json is the default serializer, usable with no extra NuGet package. var json = JsonSerializer . Serialize ( new { Name = \"Ana\" , Total = 42.5m }); var roundTripped = JsonSerializer . Deserialize < Dictionary < string , object >>(json); if (roundTripped is null || roundTripped. Count != 2 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: JSON round-trip through System.Text.Json failed\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: System.Text.Json serialized and parsed JSON with zero extra packages: {json}\" ); // Not runtime-verifiable here — see Further reading: // - which OSes/architectures .NET runs on (needs multiple machines, not one process) // - LTS/STS support windows and release cadence (a policy, not a runtime fact) // - framework-dependent vs self-contained vs Native AOT publish sizes/startup time (needs `dotnet publish`) class FixedTimeProvider ( DateTimeOffset now) : TimeProvider { public override DateTimeOffset GetUtcNow () => now; }"},{"c":"how-code-runs","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Compiling happens twice : Roslyn turns C# into IL (a CPU-independent format) at build time; the JIT turns IL into real machine code at run time, the first time each method is actually called. Splitting it this way means one build runs on any CPU, lets C#/F#/VB interoperate (same IL), and lets the runtime optimise based on how your code actually behaves, not just a guess. The JIT compiles most methods twice : a rough Tier 0 pass immediately, then a fully optimised Tier 1 pass once a method proves it's called often — using Dynamic PGO data recorded from real usage. ReadyToRun and Native AOT skip some or all of this at startup, trading flexibility (reflection, dynamic code) for faster cold starts. \"The first request is slow\" has one root cause: everything on that code path is being JIT-compiled, DI/EF Core models built, and connection pools filled for the very first time."},{"c":"how-code-runs","s":"lets-follow-one-line-of-code-start-to-finish","h":"Let's follow one line of code, start to finish","t":"# Let's follow one line of code, start to finish Beginner Here's the smallest program you can write: C# Run ▶ Copy public static int Add ( int a, int b) => a + b; You've written this. You know what it does. But what actually happens to it between you hitting save and a CPU somewhere producing the number 5 ? Let's trace it, step by step, because every term in this chapter is really just a closer look at one moment in this sequence. You save the file and run dotnet build . The C# compiler reads Add and writes it down — but not as machine code. It writes it in a different, in-between format. That happens once , right now, at build time, and never again for this particular method. The result gets saved into a file called an assembly . Time passes. Maybe seconds, maybe you deploy it and it's months. You run the app with dotnet run . Nothing from step 2 has been turned into something your CPU can actually execute yet — that hasn't happened. Not even once. Then, the moment something finally calls Add(2, 3) — and not a moment before — the runtime translates that in-between format into real instructions for your exact CPU, runs them, and hands back 5 . Notice something odd there: step 2 and step 5 are both \"compiling\". Same word, two completely different jobs, done by two completely different pieces of software, at two completely different times. Why would .NET deliberately do that twice instead of once? That question is the whole chapter."},{"c":"how-code-runs","s":"so-why-split-it-into-two-steps-at-all","h":"So why split it into two steps at all?","t":"# So why split it into two steps at all? Beginner Here's a fair challenge: C++ doesn't do this. It compiles straight to machine code, once, at build time, and never touches the result again. Why does .NET add this extra step? Start with the problem that forces the split. Machine code isn't universal — it's written for one specific CPU family. Code compiled for an Intel/AMD chip (what's called x64) simply will not run on an Arm chip, the kind in a lot of phones and, increasingly, servers too. If .NET compiled your C# straight to machine code the moment you hit build, you'd need to know, right then, exactly which CPU your app would eventually run on — and build a separate version for each one you cared about. So .NET stops one step short at build time. It compiles down to an in-between format instead of all the way to machine code, and saves the last translation — the CPU-specific one — for later, on whichever machine actually ends up running the code. That machine, by definition, already knows exactly what CPU it has. One build. Any CPU. That in-between format has a name: IL (Intermediate Language — you'll also see it called CIL or MSIL; don't let the three names fool you, they're all the same thing). The compiler that writes it is Roslyn . The piece that reads IL later and turns it into real machine code is the JIT — short for \"just-in-time\" compiler, because it compiles each piece exactly when it's needed, not before. The specific JIT built into .NET even has its own name, RyuJIT , which you'll see mentioned in performance discussions. Once you accept that the translation happens later , on the real machine, a few more nice things fall out of it almost for free — and they're worth knowing, because they come up again and again later in this book: The CPU gets code tuned for itself. Since the translation happens on the actual machine, the JIT can use whatever that specific CPU is good at — a fast modern instruction set on a beefy server, something plainer on an older box. A build-time-only compiler could never know that in advance; it wouldn't know what machine you'd eventually run on. The runtime can watch your code run and keep making it faster. This sounds almost magical the first time you hear it, but it's real, and you'll see exactly how in the Dynamic PGO section further down. Different .NET languages can call each other directly, with zero friction. C#, F# and VB all compile down to the very same IL. By the time anything is actually running, there's no language boundary left — it's just IL calling IL. One more payoff, and it has nothing to do with CPUs at all: IL is verifiable , and it comes bundled with rich metadata — a structured description of every type and method in your code, sitting right there in the file alongside the IL itself. That one detail is what makes garbage collection, reflection and serialization possible, and it's worth slowing down for, which is exactly what the next section does."},{"c":"how-code-runs","s":"what-build-time-actually-hands-you","h":"What build time actually hands you","t":"# What build time actually hands you Beginner Diagram Copy build time run time ┌──────────┐ Roslyn ┌──────────────────┐ CLR loads ┌──────┐ ┌───────────────┐ │ .cs files│ ────────▶ │ assembly (.dll) │ ──────────▶ │ JIT │─▶│ native x64/Arm│ ─▶ CPU └──────────┘ (csc) │ IL + metadata │ └──────┘ │ machine code │ └──────────────────┘ └───────────────┘ The file Roslyn produces — the assembly — is a PE file, the same container format Windows uses for a plain .exe . Open one up conceptually and you'll find four things packed inside it: Part Contents Manifest Assembly name, version, culture, public key token, list of referenced assemblies Metadata tables Every type, method, field, property, attribute and its exact signature IL code The actual method bodies Resources Embedded files, localisation strings That metadata row deserves more than a passing glance, because it quietly explains three things you probably already take for granted. Ever wonder why .NET doesn't need separate header files the way C does? The metadata already says, precisely, what every type looks like — there's nothing left for a header file to repeat. Ever notice IntelliSense working perfectly against a compiled library you've never once seen the source code for? Same reason — the metadata is right there in the .dll , fully readable. And reflection — code that asks \"what properties does this type have?\" while the program is running , instead of knowing the answer at compile time — only works at all because that metadata exists to answer it. Without it, reflection would have nothing to inspect. # Let's watch Add actually become IL Back to our one-line method. Here's exactly what the compiler writes down for it: C# Run ▶ Copy public static int Add ( int a, int b) => a + b; Diagram Copy .method public hidebysig static int32 Add(int32 a, int32 b) cil managed { ldarg.0 // push a ldarg.1 // push b add // pop two, push sum ret // return top of stack } If that looks a little strange, here's the trick to reading it: IL doesn't use named variables sitting in CPU registers the way the final machine code eventually will. It works with a small, temporary pile of values called a stack — you can only ever add to the top of it, or take from the top of it. Nothing else. Let's walk through those four lines and watch exactly what's sitting on that stack at each point: Instruction What it does Stack after ldarg.0 push argument a [a] ldarg.1 push argument b [a, b] add pop two values, push their sum [a+b] ret return whatever is on top of the stack (returns a+b ) That's genuinely the entire thing. Four instructions, each doing one small, obvious job to a pile of values. It reads almost like a recipe for a very literal-minded assistant — and that literalness is the whole point: it's simple enough that any CPU's JIT can turn it into real instructions, no matter how wildly different that CPU's actual registers are underneath. Want to see this for yourself, right now, on real code? Go to SharpLab.io , paste in any C# snippet, and it shows you the IL and the JIT's final machine-code output, side by side. It's genuinely one of the best ways to understand what async , foreach and closures are secretly expanding into — each gets its own full chapter later in this book, and this trick will make those chapters click faster. ILSpy and dotPeek do the same thing for compiled .dll files you didn't write yourself. # Three words that sound alike but aren't the same thing Before moving on: there's a classic source of confusion worth clearing up here, because it'll save you grief later. Concept What it is Example Project ( .csproj ) Build instructions: what to compile, what to reference Shop.Api.csproj Assembly The compiled output of one project — one file Shop.Api.dll Namespace A naming scope inside your code, with no file-system meaning Shop.Api.Orders These three sound like they should line up one-to-one, and they just don't. One assembly can quite happily contain many namespaces. One namespace — System.C"},{"c":"how-code-runs","s":"what-run-time-actually-does-with-all-this","h":"What run time actually does with all this","t":"# What run time actually does with all this Beginner So build time is done. You're sitting on a .dll full of IL and metadata that, remember, no CPU can run yet. Run time is where that finally changes. When you start the app, the CLR (Common Language Runtime — the engine underneath everything .NET does while it's actually running) loads your assembly into memory. Here's the part that surprises people: it does not translate every method the moment it loads. It's lazier than that, on purpose. Watch: Your code calls Add(2, 3) for the very first time. The CLR checks: has this method's IL ever been translated before? No. So it hands the IL over to the JIT. The JIT translates just that one method's IL into real x64 or Arm64 machine code, right there, for whatever CPU it happens to be running on at that moment. That native code gets kept in memory for the rest of the process's life — it isn't thrown away after use. Every later call to Add just reuses the native code from step 4. No re-translating, ever again, for this process. So the translation cost is paid exactly once per method , the first time — and only the first time — it's called. Not once per call. That distinction matters a lot for everything that follows, because almost this entire chapter from here is about what happens around that one moment in step 3, plus one clever alternative that skips it entirely."},{"c":"how-code-runs","s":"meanwhile-the-clr-is-doing-a-lot-more-than-just-this","h":"Meanwhile, the CLR is doing a lot more than just this","t":"# Meanwhile, the CLR is doing a lot more than just this Beginner Loading assemblies and running the JIT is only one of the CLR's jobs — it's just the one this chapter cares about most. The whole time your process is alive, it's quietly doing several other things underneath your code, continuously: Service What it means for you Chapter Loading Finds and loads assemblies on demand (via AssemblyLoadContext ) — JIT compilation IL → machine code, the first time each method is called this chapter Memory management Allocates objects on the managed heap; the GC frees them automatically Memory & GC Type safety You can't silently treat an Order as a string ; casts are checked; array bounds are checked Type system Exception handling Structured exceptions that unwind cleanly across method calls Exceptions Threading Managed threads, the thread pool, synchronisation primitives Threading Interop Calling native code (P/Invoke), COM Advanced C# Security Verification, no buffer overruns in safe code Web security Keep this table in your back pocket — it's basically a preview of the rest of Part I and II. Nearly every chapter coming up is really just one row of this table, told in full. Here's a definition worth having precisely: code the CLR is doing all of this for is called managed code . Anything outside its control — a native C/C++ library, a raw OS file handle, a socket — is unmanaged code , and here's the catch: nothing cleans that up for you automatically. Nobody's watching it. That single fact is the entire reason IDisposable exists in C#: it's the language's way of flagging \"this object is holding onto something the CLR genuinely cannot manage on your behalf — you, personally, have to let it go.\""},{"c":"how-code-runs","s":"now-zoom-into-the-jit-why-does-it-translate-a-method-twice","h":"Now, zoom into the JIT: why does it translate a method twice ?","t":"# Now, zoom into the JIT: why does it translate a method twice ? Intermediate You already know, from the run-time walkthrough above, that the JIT translates a method's IL the first time it's called. What that walkthrough didn't mention is this: for most methods, it actually does that translation twice — a quick, rough pass first, and then, sometimes, a second, much better pass later. Let's figure out why anyone would want that. Think about the problem first. If the JIT fully, carefully optimised every single method the very first time it was called, your app would feel painfully slow to start up. Every method on the path from \"the process launches\" to \"the very first response goes out\" — including methods that might only ever run once in the app's whole lifetime — would sit there paying a heavy optimisation tax before anything could happen at all. Here's the fix .NET landed on, since .NET Core 3.0: compile fast and rough the first time, and only come back and do it properly for the methods that actually prove they're worth the extra effort. Diagram Copy first call ──▶ Tier 0: quick, unoptimised code (fast to produce) │ method called ≥ 30 times (and loops hit OSR) ▼ Tier 1: fully optimised code (slower to produce, fast to run) # Let's trace this with real numbers, not just the diagram Say your PlaceOrder method gets called, over and over, as orders come in. Here's exactly what happens, call by call: Call #1: nothing has been compiled for this method yet, anywhere. The JIT bangs out Tier 0 code almost instantly — it's crude and unoptimised, but it's ready in microseconds. This very first call runs on that. Calls #2 through #29: still running on that same Tier 0 code. The JIT hasn't done anything more yet — it's watching, but not acting. Call #30: somewhere around here, the JIT's internal counter decides enough is enough — this method is genuinely \"hot\", called often enough that a better version would actually pay off. It kicks off a proper Tier 1 recompile, in the background , while calls #30 onward just keep using the existing Tier 0 code. Your app never pauses for this. The moment Tier 1 finishes compiling: every call after that — #45, #100, #10,000, whatever comes next — gets silently switched over to the new, fully optimised version instead. Notice what this means for a method that's only ever called twice in your app's entire life: it never earns a Tier 1 recompile at all, and that's completely fine — the cost of optimising it properly would never have paid for itself. Here's a trickier case worth thinking through: what if a method gets stuck inside one very long-running loop on its very first call — before it's ever had the chance to be called a second time and pick up Tier 1 the normal way? It can't, because it never returns long enough to be \"called again\". This is exactly what OSR (On-Stack Replacement) is for. The JIT can swap that one already-running method over to its optimised Tier 1 version mid-loop , without waiting for it to finish first. The name makes more sense once you know why: the method's stack frame is still sitting there, mid-execution, \"on the stack\", at the exact moment the swap happens underneath it. One more wrinkle, briefly: if the assembly was published with ReadyToRun (you'll meet this properly later in the chapter), methods don't start at Tier 0 at all — they start out already precompiled. But even then, they can still get re-jitted up to Tier 1 later if they turn out to be genuinely hot. # Dynamic PGO: the JIT actually learns from your program's real behaviour Tiered compilation explains when the JIT bothers recompiling a method. This next part explains something arguably more interesting: how that second compile can be smarter than a generic optimisation pass, because the JIT has been quietly watching. Here's how, and it's on by default since .NET 8 : while a method is running at Tier 0, the JIT adds tiny counters to it that record what actually happens, live, each time it runs — which branch o"},{"c":"how-code-runs","s":"what-if-you-skipped-the-jit-completely","h":"What if you skipped the JIT completely?","t":"# What if you skipped the JIT completely? Intermediate Here's a thought worth having explicitly: everything described so far happens as the app runs , method by method, the first time each one is called. What if, instead, you translated absolutely everything, for every single method, before the app ever even starts — so that by the time it's running, there's no translation left to do, ever? That's exactly what the next two options do, each taking it further than the last. Option How Startup Peak perf Restrictions JIT (default) — Slowest Best (with PGO) None ReadyToRun (R2R) <PublishReadyToRun>true</PublishReadyToRun> Faster Same as JIT, once Tier 1 kicks in Bigger binaries Native AOT <PublishAot>true</PublishAot> Fastest (milliseconds) Good, sometimes even below JIT+PGO No runtime code generation, limited reflection, trimming required ReadyToRun is the gentler of the two. Methods are precompiled and shipped bundled inside the assembly itself, so there's no Tier 0 cold-start cost sitting in your way for them. But here's the thing: the JIT doesn't disappear — it's still present at runtime, quietly watching, and it can still step in and re-optimise a method up to Tier 1 later if Dynamic PGO's counters show it's genuinely hot. Native AOT goes all the way and removes the JIT from the picture, full stop. The entire app — your code, plus whatever bits of the .NET runtime it actually needs to function — gets compiled into one single native executable at publish time. There is no JIT at runtime. Not a reduced one. None. Nothing is ever translated on the fly, which is exactly why startup time for a Native AOT app is measured in milliseconds rather than the hundreds you saw in the cold-start table above. That speed isn't free, and it's worth really understanding the trade-off rather than just filing it away as a rule to follow: Native AOT needs trimming — the process of stripping out every bit of code the app can't prove it actually uses, so the final executable doesn't end up enormous. Here's where it bites: reflection, which you met earlier as something metadata makes possible, is precisely the kind of thing a trimmer cannot see coming. If nothing in your visible code ever calls a particular method by name, the trimmer has no way to predict that reflection might go hunting for it later at runtime — so, to keep things small, it just gets removed. This is exactly why AOT-friendly libraries lean so heavily on source generators instead of reflection: code that gets generated and made fully, explicitly visible at compile time (System.Text.Json's source generator, the minimal API request delegate generator, the configuration binding generator, [LoggerMessage] ) sidesteps the whole problem — there's nothing left for the trimmer to have to guess about, because it's right there in plain sight. So who actually wants Native AOT? It's a genuinely strong fit for serverless and scale-to-zero workloads, where that cold-start row from the earlier table is the entire problem you're trying to solve, plus CLI tools, small containers, and high-density hosting where every millisecond and every megabyte counts. It doesn't yet support MVC controllers or Razor, though, and EF Core support is still limited — both of those rely on patterns that clash with Native AOT's restrictions in ways that haven't been fully solved yet."},{"c":"how-code-runs","s":"two-more-services-coming-up-properly-in-their-own-chapters","h":"Two more services, coming up properly in their own chapters","t":"# Two more services, coming up properly in their own chapters Beginner Two more things the CLR is doing, the whole time, deserve a full chapter each rather than a rushed paragraph here — but you should at least know they exist, and that they're part of the exact same machinery this chapter has been describing: The GC (garbage collector): generational, with a \"Server GC\" mode on by default in ASP.NET Core, responsible for freeing memory your code no longer needs. See Memory & GC . The thread pool : a pool of worker threads that runs your request handlers and every await continuation. Blocking those threads (with .Result or Thread.Sleep ) causes thread pool starvation , a classic production bug. See async/await and Threading ."},{"c":"how-code-runs","s":"the-type-system-across-languages-cts-and-cls","h":"The type system across languages: CTS and CLS","t":"# The type system across languages: CTS and CLS Advanced Earlier in this chapter, you saw the claim that C#, F# and VB all compile down to the same IL, and can therefore call each other directly with zero friction. It's a nice claim, but why is it actually true? It's only possible because all three languages secretly agree, underneath their very different-looking syntax, on exactly what a \"type\" fundamentally is. CTS (Common Type System): the runtime's shared rulebook for types — classes, structs, interfaces, enums, delegates, and the value-vs-reference distinction you'll meet properly in Type system . Every .NET language maps its own type keywords onto this one shared system underneath. That's the real reason int in C# and Integer in VB turn out to be, underneath, literally the exact same thing: System.Int32 . Not \"similar\" — the same. CLS (Common Language Specification): a smaller, stricter subset of rules that applies specifically to anything you expose publicly , chosen so that every .NET language — even one with narrower type support than C# has — can still consume it safely. A public uint , for example, actually breaks CLS compliance, because some .NET languages simply don't have unsigned integers at all. If you want the compiler to warn you whenever you've accidentally broken that promise, mark a library with [assembly: CLSCompliant(true)] ."},{"c":"how-code-runs","s":"assembly-loading-and-versioning","h":"Assembly loading and versioning","t":"# Assembly loading and versioning Advanced One last practical layer: a running .NET app isn't really just \"the assembly\" on its own — it's a small set of files that, together, tell the CLR exactly which assembly, and which version of it, to actually load. Assemblies are identified by name, version, culture and, optionally, a public key token from something called strong naming . Worth knowing, but don't over-invest here: modern .NET mostly ignores strong-name validation at runtime these days — it's largely a legacy identity mechanism now, not a real security boundary. AssemblyLoadContext is what replaced .NET Framework's old AppDomains for isolating loaded assemblies, and for loading and unloading plugins while the app is running. Two generated files sit right next to your published app and quietly drive all of this loading. .deps.json lists every dependency assembly your app needs, and exactly where to find each one. .runtimeconfig.json says which shared framework and version the app expects, plus runtime settings like GC mode. Roll-forward decides what happens when the exact version your app was built for isn't installed on the machine. By default, an app built for 8.0.x happily runs on whatever the latest installed 8.0 patch release is — it rolls forward automatically to pick up fixes, no questions asked. Rolling forward across a major version — say, from 8 to 9 — is a different story: it's off by default, and has to be explicitly opted into with <RollForward>Major</RollForward> , precisely because major versions are allowed to carry breaking changes that a patch release never would. JSON Copy // Shop.Api.runtimeconfig.json (generated) { \"runtimeOptions\" : { \"tfm\" : \"net10.0\" , \"framework\" : { \"name\" : \"Microsoft.AspNetCore.App\" , \"version\" : \"10.0.0\" }, \"configProperties\" : { \"System.GC.Server\" : true } } } Q Explain how C# code gets executed. Roslyn compiles C# into IL plus metadata inside an assembly, at build time. At run time the CLR loads the assembly; when a method is first called, the JIT compiles its IL to native machine code — quickly at Tier 0, then re-optimised at Tier 1 if it's called often (\"hot\"), using Dynamic PGO data gathered while it ran. The CLR also provides GC, type safety, exceptions and threading throughout the process's life. Q What is IL and why does .NET use an intermediate language? A CPU-independent, stack-based bytecode produced by the compiler instead of final machine code. It makes assemblies portable across OS/CPU (the JIT compiles it for whatever machine it actually runs on), lets multiple languages interoperate because they all compile down to the same format, enables verification and rich metadata (reflection, GC info), and lets the JIT optimise for the actual hardware and runtime behaviour rather than guessing at build time. Q What is tiered compilation and Dynamic PGO? Tiered compilation JITs methods first with minimal optimisation (Tier 0) for fast startup, then recompiles frequently called ones with full optimisation (Tier 1) in the background. Dynamic PGO instruments Tier 0 code to observe what actually happens — such as which concrete type sits behind an interface call — and Tier 1 uses that recorded data to devirtualise, inline and lay out code better than a generic optimisation pass could. Q What is the difference between JIT, ReadyToRun and Native AOT? JIT compiles at runtime: slowest startup, best peak performance, no restrictions. ReadyToRun ships precompiled code alongside IL for faster startup while keeping the JIT available to re-optimise hot methods later. Native AOT compiles everything to one native binary with no JIT at all: fastest startup and smallest memory, but it requires trimming unused code and avoids runtime reflection and code generation, which is why AOT-friendly libraries use source generators instead. Q What is managed vs unmanaged code? Managed code runs under the CLR's control, which provides GC, type safety and exception handling automatically. Unmanaged co"},{"c":"how-code-runs","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Common Language Runtime overview Managed execution process Native AOT deployment Book: CLR via C# by Jeffrey Richter, the classic deep dive (written for Framework; the concepts still apply). SharpLab : see IL and JIT output for any snippet. Performance Improvements in .NET 8 (.NET Blog) — backs the Dynamic PGO / guarded devirtualisation claims in this chapter."},{"c":"how-code-runs","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter The JIT's internal tiering decisions, Dynamic PGO's counters, and exact ReadyToRun/Native AOT startup numbers aren't something a plain program can observe about itself from the inside — trust Further reading and tools like SharpLab/dotnet-trace for those. What the program below can check: that a method's IL really only gets translated once per process (not once per call, by timing a \"cold\" vs \"warm\" call), that sealed genuinely changes what the runtime can prove about a type, and that reflection really can see metadata the compiler didn't hard-code. Run with dotnet run (plain console app, .NET 8/9, no extra packages). C# Run ▶ Copy using System . Diagnostics ; using System . Reflection ; // Claim: the first call to a method pays a one-time JIT/warm-up cost; later calls are far cheaper. var sw = Stopwatch . StartNew (); Add ( 2 , 3 ); var firstCallTicks = sw. ElapsedTicks ; sw. Restart (); for ( int i = 0 ; i < 100_000 ; i++) Add (i, i); var hundredKCallsTicks = sw. ElapsedTicks ; var avgWarmCallTicks = hundredKCallsTicks / 100_000.0 ; if (avgWarmCallTicks >= firstCallTicks) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected warmed-up calls to be far cheaper per-call than the very first one\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: first call ≈{firstCallTicks} ticks, average of 100,000 later calls ≈{avgWarmCallTicks:F3} ticks each — later calls are far cheaper\" ); // Claim: a sealed class has exactly one possible runtime type, so a type check against it is exact. if ( typeof ( SealedThing ). IsSealed is false ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: SealedThing should report IsSealed == true\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: the runtime itself reports a sealed type as sealed (nothing can subclass it)\" ); // Claim: metadata lets reflection discover a type's shape purely at runtime, with no compile-time reference to it. Type discovered = Assembly . GetExecutingAssembly (). GetTypes (). First (t => t. Name == \"SealedThing\" ); var prop = discovered. GetProperty ( \"Value\" ); if (prop is null || prop. PropertyType != typeof ( int )) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: reflection should have found SealedThing.Value : int from metadata alone\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: reflection discovered a property's name and type purely from assembly metadata\" ); static int Add ( int a, int b) => a + b; sealed class SealedThing { public int Value { get ; set ; } } // Not runtime-verifiable here — see Further reading: // - Tier 0 vs Tier 1 compilation actually happening (needs dotnet-trace / ETW events, not a stopwatch) // - Dynamic PGO and guarded devirtualisation (observable via profilers, not plain timing) // - ReadyToRun / Native AOT startup-time comparisons (needs `dotnet publish` with each mode)"},{"c":"projects-sdk-nuget","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR One .csproj → one assembly. A .sln / .slnx just groups projects for the IDE/build — it has no effect on the compiled output. SDK-style project files are short because they include every file in the folder by default and inherit sensible defaults; the csproj only lists what's different . Directory.Build.props shares settings across every project in the repo tree; global.json pins the SDK version everyone (and CI) builds with. NuGet resolves the full dependency graph on restore: it picks the lowest version that satisfies every constraint, and a direct reference always wins over a transitive one. Central Package Management ( Directory.Packages.props ) stops different projects in one solution from drifting to different versions of the same package. Debug builds skip optimizations for easy debugging; Release builds are optimized — always benchmark and ship Release."},{"c":"projects-sdk-nuget","s":"the-physical-structure-of-a-net-codebase","h":"The physical structure of a .NET codebase","t":"# The physical structure of a .NET codebase Beginner Diagram Copy Shop/ ← git repository root ├── Shop.slnx ← solution: groups projects for IDE/build ├── Directory.Build.props ← settings shared by every project ├── Directory.Packages.props ← package versions shared by every project ├── global.json ← pins the SDK version ├── .editorconfig ← code style rules ├── src/ │ ├── Shop.Api/Shop.Api.csproj → Shop.Api.dll │ ├── Shop.Application/…csproj → Shop.Application.dll │ ├── Shop.Domain/…csproj → Shop.Domain.dll │ └── Shop.Infrastructure/…csproj → Shop.Infrastructure.dll └── tests/ ├── Shop.Domain.Tests/…csproj └── Shop.Api.IntegrationTests/…csproj A project ( .csproj ) produces one assembly . A solution ( .sln , or the new XML .slnx format supported since .NET 9 SDK / VS 17.13) groups projects. It is only for tooling; it doesn't affect the compiled output. Project references say which assembly may use which. They are how architecture rules (Domain depends on nothing) are enforced physically."},{"c":"projects-sdk-nuget","s":"sdk-style-project-files","h":"SDK-style project files","t":"# SDK-style project files Beginner XML Copy <Project Sdk = \"Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web\" > <PropertyGroup> <TargetFramework>net10. 0 </TargetFramework> <Nullable>enable</Nullable> <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings> <RootNamespace>Shop.Api</RootNamespace> <UserSecretsId>7b8f…</UserSecretsId> </PropertyGroup> <ItemGroup> <PackageReference Include = \"Serilog.AspNetCore\" Version = \"9.0.0\" /> <ProjectReference Include = \"..\\Shop.Application\\Shop.Application.csproj\" /> <ProjectReference Include = \"..\\Shop.Infrastructure\\Shop.Infrastructure.csproj\" /> </ItemGroup> <ItemGroup> <InternalsVisibleTo Include = \"Shop.Api.IntegrationTests\" /> </ItemGroup> </Project> Element Meaning Sdk=\"Microsoft.NET.Sdk\" Plain library/console app Sdk=\"Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web\" Adds the ASP.NET Core shared framework, web defaults, launchSettings.json support Sdk=\"Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Worker\" Worker service (background processing) Sdk=\"Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor\" / BlazorWebAssembly Razor class libraries / Blazor <TargetFramework> Which .NET you build for ( net10.0 ) <Nullable>enable Turn on nullable reference type analysis <ImplicitUsings>enable Auto-add common using s ( System , System.Linq , Microsoft.AspNetCore.Builder …) as global usings <PackageReference> A NuGet dependency <ProjectReference> A dependency on another project in the repo Why SDK-style projects are short Old .NET Framework projects listed every .cs file explicitly, so adding a new file meant editing the project file too — and that caused constant merge conflicts. SDK-style projects fix this by including every file in the folder by default (globbing) and importing sensible defaults from the SDK. The csproj then only needs to list what's different from the defaults, which is why they're short enough to read and edit by hand. # MSBuild: the engine under the CLI dotnet build runs MSBuild , which evaluates the project XML (properties, items, targets) and executes targets (Restore → Compile → Copy → Publish). Useful things to know: Properties ( <PropertyGroup> ) are scalar settings; items ( <ItemGroup> ) are lists (files, references). You can pass properties on the command line: dotnet build -p:Version=1.2.3 . Conditions let settings vary: <PropertyGroup Condition=\"'$(Configuration)' == 'Release'\"> . Build configurations : Debug (no optimisation, full debug info) and Release (optimised). Always benchmark and ship Release."},{"c":"projects-sdk-nuget","s":"sharing-settings-directory-build-props-and-global-json","h":"Sharing settings: Directory.Build.props and global.json","t":"# Sharing settings: Directory.Build.props and global.json Intermediate MSBuild automatically imports the nearest Directory.Build.props walking up from each project. Put repo-wide settings there instead of repeating them in every csproj: XML Copy <!-- Directory.Build.props at the repo root --> <Project> <PropertyGroup> <TargetFramework>net10. 0 </TargetFramework> <Nullable>enable</Nullable> <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings> <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors> <AnalysisLevel>latest-recommended</AnalysisLevel> <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild> <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion> </PropertyGroup> </Project> Directory.Build.targets is the same idea but imported after the project (for targets and overrides). global.json pins the SDK version so everyone and CI build with the same compiler: JSON Copy { \"sdk\" : { \"version\" : \"10.0.100\" , \"rollForward\" : \"latestFeature\" } }"},{"c":"projects-sdk-nuget","s":"the-dotnet-cli","h":"The dotnet CLI","t":"# The dotnet CLI Beginner Command What it does dotnet new list / dotnet new webapi -n Shop.Api List templates / create a project dotnet new sln / dotnet sln add src/**/*.csproj Create a solution / add projects dotnet add reference ../Shop.Domain Add a project reference dotnet add package Polly Add a NuGet package dotnet restore Download packages (also runs implicitly on build) dotnet build -c Release Compile dotnet run / dotnet watch Run / run with hot reload dotnet test --filter \"Category=Unit\" Run tests dotnet publish -c Release -o out Produce deployable output dotnet publish /t:PublishContainer Build a container image without a Dockerfile dotnet format Apply .editorconfig style and analyzer fixes dotnet list package --outdated --vulnerable Audit dependencies dotnet tool install -g dotnet-ef Install a global tool dotnet user-secrets set \"Key\" \"Value\" Store dev secrets outside the repo dotnet dev-certs https --trust Trust the local HTTPS dev certificate Local tools ( dotnet new tool-manifest , then dotnet tool install dotnet-ef ) are pinned per repo in .config/dotnet-tools.json and restored with dotnet tool restore . They're better than global tools for team consistency."},{"c":"projects-sdk-nuget","s":"nuget-in-depth","h":"NuGet in depth","t":"# NuGet in depth Beginner NuGet is .NET's package manager. A package ( .nupkg ) is a zip containing compiled assemblies for one or more TFMs ( lib/net8.0/ , lib/netstandard2.0/ ), a .nuspec manifest with its dependencies, and optionally build props, analyzers or content files. Feeds: nuget.org (public), plus private feeds such as Azure Artifacts, GitHub Packages or JFrog, configured in nuget.config . Restore resolves the full dependency graph and writes obj/project.assets.json . Transitive dependencies: packages your packages depend on. You get them automatically. Version resolution: for each package, NuGet picks the lowest version that satisfies all constraints (\"lowest applicable version\"). A direct reference wins over a transitive one (\"direct dependency wins\"). # Central Package Management (CPM) Intermediate Here's the problem CPM solves: in a multi-project solution, it's easy for one project to reference EntityFrameworkCore 9.0.1 while another references 9.0.3 . At runtime that mismatch can cause binding errors that are confusing to diagnose, because the symptom shows up far from the actual cause. CPM fixes this by putting every package's version in one shared file, so there's only ever one version to drift from: XML Copy <!-- Directory.Packages.props --> <Project> <PropertyGroup> <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally> <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>true</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled> </PropertyGroup> <ItemGroup> <PackageVersion Include = \"Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer\" Version = \"10.0.0\" /> <PackageVersion Include = \"FluentValidation\" Version = \"12.0.0\" /> <PackageVersion Include = \"xunit.v3\" Version = \"3.0.0\" /> </ItemGroup> </Project> XML Copy <!-- any . csproj : no Version attribute --> <PackageReference Include = \"Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer\" /> Transitive pinning lets you upgrade a vulnerable transitive package centrally without referencing it directly everywhere. # Package hygiene Intermediate Lock files ( <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile> → packages.lock.json ) make restores reproducible: everyone and CI resolve to the exact same package versions, not just versions that satisfy the constraints. Use --locked-mode in CI so a build fails loudly instead of silently resolving something new. Vulnerability auditing: dotnet restore warns about known CVEs (NuGet Audit, on by default since .NET 8). Fail the build on them in CI. Licences: check them before adopting a dependency. Some popular libraries have moved parts of their functionality to commercial licenses in recent years (check each library's current license page — this changes over time, so don't rely on this book for the current status of any specific package). Interviewers sometimes ask how you evaluate a new dependency, and licensing is part of a real answer. Package source mapping in nuget.config stops dependency-confusion attacks (where an attacker publishes a malicious package with the same name as an internal one, hoping your build pulls theirs instead) by mapping package ID prefixes to specific feeds. # Creating your own package Shell Copy dotnet pack -c Release - p : PackageVersion = 1.4.0 dotnet nuget push bin/Release/Shop.Contracts. 1.4.0 .nupkg --source \"company-feed\" --api-key az Companies often publish a shared contracts package (message types, DTOs) used by several services."},{"c":"projects-sdk-nuget","s":"code-quality-tooling","h":"Code quality tooling","t":"# Code quality tooling Intermediate Tool What it does Roslyn analyzers (built in) Warnings for bugs, performance and API misuse ( CA1848 use LoggerMessage, CA2007 ConfigureAwait…) .editorconfig Naming, formatting and severity of each rule; enforced in IDE and build dotnet format Applies fixes from the command line / CI Third-party analyzers SonarAnalyzer, Roslynator, Meziantou.Analyzer, StyleCop SonarQube / SonarCloud Quality gates, coverage, code smells in CI Nullable warnings as errors Prevents most NullReferenceException s ini Copy # .editorconfig root = true [*.cs] indent_style = space indent_size = 4 csharp_style_namespace_declarations = file_scoped :warning dotnet_diagnostic.CA2016.severity = error # forward CancellationToken dotnet_naming_rule.private_fields_underscore.severity = warning"},{"c":"projects-sdk-nuget","s":"the-daily-toolbox","h":"The daily toolbox","t":"# The daily toolbox Beginner Job Tools IDE Visual Studio 2022/2026 (Windows), JetBrains Rider (all OSes), VS Code + C# Dev Kit Call your API .http files (built into VS, Rider, VS Code), Postman, Bruno, Insomnia, curl ; Scalar/Swagger UI from OpenAPI Inspect compiled code ILSpy, dotPeek, SharpLab.io Databases SSMS, Azure Data Studio (being retired in favour of the VS Code MSSQL extension), DBeaver, DataGrip, dotnet ef Containers Docker Desktop / Podman / Rancher Desktop, docker compose , .NET Aspire Diagnostics dotnet-counters , dotnet-trace , dotnet-dump , dotnet-gcdump , PerfView, IDE profilers Benchmark / load BenchmarkDotNet; k6, NBomber, JMeter, Azure Load Testing Source control & CI Git, GitHub/Azure DevOps, GitHub Actions/Azure Pipelines Cloud CLIs Azure CLI ( az ), Azure Developer CLI ( azd ), kubectl , Bicep/Terraform HTTP Copy ### Shop.Api.http: committed next to the project so anyone can try endpoints @ host = https : //localhost:7180 @ token = {{$dotenv TOKEN}} GET {{host}}/orders? page = 1 & pageSize = 20 Authorization : Bearer {{token}} ### POST {{host}}/orders Content-Type : application/json Authorization : Bearer {{token}} { \"customerId\" : 42 , \"lines\" : [{ \"sku\" : \"KB-01\" , \"qty\" : 1 }] }"},{"c":"projects-sdk-nuget","s":"launchsettings-json-and-environments","h":"launchSettings.json and environments","t":"# launchSettings.json and environments Beginner Properties/launchSettings.json is only used on your dev machine (by dotnet run and the IDE). It sets URLs and environment variables such as ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Development . It is not deployed, and production ignores it. Production gets settings from real environment variables, App Service configuration or Kubernetes manifests. See Configuration . Q What is the difference between a project, a solution and an assembly? A project (.csproj) is the build definition for one assembly. An assembly (.dll) is the compiled output. A solution groups projects for the IDE and for building together; it has no effect on the output. Q What is Directory.Build.props for? MSBuild imports it automatically into every project in that folder tree. Teams use it for shared settings such as target framework, nullable, warnings-as-errors, analyzers and versioning, so each csproj stays small and consistent. Q How do you keep NuGet package versions consistent across many projects? Central Package Management: Directory.Packages.props with ManagePackageVersionsCentrally , versions declared once with PackageVersion , and csproj files referencing packages without versions. Add transitive pinning and lock files for reproducibility. Q How do you handle a vulnerable transitive dependency? Find it with dotnet list package --vulnerable --include-transitive . Upgrade the parent package if a fixed version exists; otherwise pin the transitive package to a patched version (directly, or via CPM transitive pinning). Fail CI on NuGet audit warnings to catch it early. Q What's the difference between Debug and Release builds? Debug disables optimisations and emits full debugging info, so code maps one-to-one to source. Release enables compiler and JIT optimisations (inlining, dead-code removal). Performance measurements and deployments must use Release. Q How do you enforce code style across a team? An .editorconfig in the repo, analyzers enabled with AnalysisLevel and EnforceCodeStyleInBuild , warnings as errors in CI, dotnet format --verify-no-changes in the pipeline, plus optional SonarQube quality gates."},{"c":"projects-sdk-nuget","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading .NET project SDKs Central Package Management dotnet CLI overview Code analysis in .NET"},{"c":"projects-sdk-nuget","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run (plain console app, .NET 8/9, BCL only). This can't exercise a real MSBuild/NuGet restore, but it can verify the two resolution rules the chapter states, plus the Debug/Release mechanism itself. C# Run ▶ Copy // Claim: Debug builds skip optimizations and keep full debug info; Release builds optimize. // The DEBUG symbol is exactly how the compiler tells your own code which configuration built it. # if DEBUG Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: this is a Debug build (DEBUG symbol defined) — run `dotnet run -c Release` and this branch disappears entirely\" ); # else Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: this is a Release build (DEBUG symbol not defined) — optimizations are on\" ); #endif // Claim: NuGet picks the LOWEST version that satisfies every constraint (\"lowest applicable version\"), // and a DIRECT reference always wins over a TRANSITIVE one, even if the transitive one asks for something newer. // Model NuGet's resolution rules directly (simplified, but the same two rules described in the chapter). var constraints = new List < PackageRef > { new ( \"Newtonsoft.Json\" , new Version ( 12 , 0 , 0 ), IsDirect : false ), // transitive: wants >= 12.0.0 new ( \"Newtonsoft.Json\" , new Version ( 13 , 0 , 1 ), IsDirect : false ), // transitive: wants >= 13.0.1 (higher wins among these) }; var resolvedWithoutDirect = Resolve ( \"Newtonsoft.Json\" , constraints); if (resolvedWithoutDirect != new Version ( 13 , 0 , 1 )) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected the highest transitive floor (13.0.1) to win among transitive-only constraints, got {resolvedWithoutDirect}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: with no direct reference, resolution settles on {resolvedWithoutDirect} (the highest of the transitive floors)\" ); constraints. Add ( new ( \"Newtonsoft.Json\" , new Version ( 11 , 0 , 0 ), IsDirect : true )); // app adds its OWN older direct reference var resolvedWithDirect = Resolve ( \"Newtonsoft.Json\" , constraints); if (resolvedWithDirect != new Version ( 11 , 0 , 0 )) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected the direct reference (11.0.0) to win over transitive ones, got {resolvedWithDirect}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: adding a direct reference ({resolvedWithDirect}) overrides the transitive versions, even though it's lower — \\\"direct dependency wins\\\"\" ); // Not runtime-verifiable here (needs real MSBuild/NuGet restore, a multi-project solution, or CI) — see Further reading: // - Real SDK-style project globbing, Directory.Build.props inheritance, actual NuGet restore/version resolution // - Central Package Management end to end // - dotnet CLI commands and their real behavior Console . WriteLine ( \"All checkable claims verified.\" ); static Version Resolve ( string packageId, List < PackageRef > allConstraintsForThisPackage) { foreach ( var r in allConstraintsForThisPackage) if (r. IsDirect && r. Id == packageId) return r. MinVersion ; // direct dependency wins outright // otherwise: lowest version that satisfies every transitive constraint return allConstraintsForThisPackage. Where (r => r. Id == packageId). Max (r => r. MinVersion )!; } record PackageRef ( string Id , Version MinVersion , bool IsDirect );"},{"c":"type-system","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Every type in C# answers one question: does assignment copy the data (value types: struct , int , enum , …) or copy a reference to shared data (reference types: class , string , arrays, …)? \"Value types live on the stack\" is a popular simplification, not the real rule: value types live wherever their container lives — which can be the heap (a struct field inside a class, or inside an array). Physically: the stack is a small, fixed, per-thread block (≈1 MiB by default on Windows); the heap is one shared, growable region per process that the GC manages. Boxing wraps a value type in a heap object so it can pretend to be an object /interface; it's an easy-to-miss allocation ( ArrayList.Add , string.Format with value args) and generics ( List<int> ) exist partly to avoid it. Three different things all look like \"equality\" in C# — == , Equals , ReferenceEquals — and classes/structs/records each default to different answers; overriding Equals obligates you to override GetHashCode too. Everything is passed by value , including references — so a method can mutate an object through a reference parameter but can't make the caller's variable point somewhere else unless you add ref ."},{"c":"type-system","s":"one-question-that-predicts-a-huge-amount-of-c-behaviour","h":"One question that predicts a huge amount of C# behaviour","t":"# One question that predicts a huge amount of C# behaviour Beginner Here's a question worth asking about any type in C#: when you assign one variable to another, do you get an independent copy, or two names for the same thing? The answer splits every single type in the language into exactly two camps, and once you know which camp a type is in, you can predict how it behaves in dozens of situations you haven't even seen yet. Value types Reference types Examples int , long , double , decimal , bool , char , DateTime , Guid , TimeSpan , any struct , any enum , tuples (int, string) , Span<T> class , string , object , arrays ( int[] ), delegates, record (class), interfaces, dynamic Variable holds The data itself A reference (an address) to an object on the heap Assignment copies The whole value Only the reference; both variables now point at the same object Default value All bits zero ( 0 , false , default(DateTime) ) null Can be null? No, unless wrapped as Nullable<T> ( int? ) Yes (but see nullable reference types ) Inheritance Can't inherit or be inherited (can implement interfaces) Single inheritance of classes Allocated Inline: in the containing object, array, or stack frame Always on the managed heap; freed by the GC Let's actually watch the difference happen instead of just reading about it: C# Run ▶ Copy // Run as-is: a complete program, not a fragment. struct PointS { public int X ; } // value type class PointC { public int X ; } // reference type var s1 = new PointS { X = 1 }; var s2 = s1; // copies the 4 bytes that make up X — s2 is now independent of s1 s2. X = 99 ; Console . WriteLine (s1. X ); // 1 ← independent copy var c1 = new PointC { X = 1 }; var c2 = c1; // copies only the 8-byte reference — c1 and c2 now point at the SAME object c2. X = 99 ; Console . WriteLine (c1. X ); // 99 ← same object, seen through a second variable Both lines do var x2 = x1; x2.X = 99; . Both look identical. And yet s1.X stays 1 while c1.X becomes 99 — the exact same two lines of code, producing opposite outcomes, purely because struct and class disagree about what = actually copies. For PointS , s2 = s1 copies the 4 bytes that make up X , so s2 is a completely independent value from that point on. For PointC , c2 = c1 copies only the reference — a pointer-sized address — so c1 and c2 end up pointing at the exact same object in memory, and changing one changes what you see through the other. This exact mix-up is a real, common bug: someone treats a class like it's a cheap, independent value — hands what they think is a \"copy\" of an OrderTotals object to a background job — without realising the dashboard and the job are now both quietly editing the exact same object. Had OrderTotals been an immutable struct or record , that bug couldn't have happened: there'd have been no shared object left to edit by accident. Value vs reference A value type is like handing someone a photocopy of a document: they can scribble all over it and your original stays untouched. A reference type is like handing someone the address of a shared Google Doc : you each hold your own copy of the address , but you're both looking at, and editing, the exact same document."},{"c":"type-system","s":"value-types-live-on-the-stack-a-useful-lie","h":"\"Value types live on the stack\" — a useful lie","t":"# \"Value types live on the stack\" — a useful lie Intermediate You will hear this sentence constantly: \"value types live on the stack, reference types live on the heap.\" It's a reasonable first approximation, and it's not actually true — knowing the precise version, and why the simple version is wrong, is a genuinely good way to stand out in an interview. First, what the stack and heap actually are physically , since the sentence above leans on both without saying where either one actually lives: The stack is a small, fixed-size block of memory the operating system reserves per thread , the moment that thread is created — on Windows the default is 1 MiB for the main thread (configurable per-thread, and other platforms pick their own default); it isn't shared with any other thread. Local variables and parameters live in stack frames inside it, one frame per currently-running method call, stacked on top of each other exactly like the name suggests. Calling a method pushes a new frame on top; returning pops it straight back off. Allocating on the stack is just moving one pointer up or down — there's no GC involved at all, which is why it's essentially free. The managed heap , by contrast, is one shared region per process , not per thread — every thread in your program allocates objects into the same heap. It isn't fixed-size: the CLR asks the operating system for address space in large chunks as it needs more, and organises what it gets into GC generations ( Memory & GC covers exactly how). An object placed there outlives the method call that created it — it survives for as long as anything, anywhere still has a reference to it, which the GC tracks and eventually reclaims once nothing does. That picture is the one fact worth memorising from this section: a stack frame and the references inside it vanish together, the instant the method returns — but whatever those references pointed at doesn't necessarily vanish with them. It only disappears once nothing, anywhere in the whole process, still points to it. Here's the precise rule, and it's genuinely simpler than the popular version once you see it stated correctly: reference-type objects always live on the heap. Value types live wherever their container lives — which might be a stack frame, but might just as easily be a slot inside a heap object. C# Run ▶ Copy class Order { public int Quantity ; // value type field: lives INSIDE the Order object, on the heap public DateTime CreatedAt ; // same } void M () { int local = 5 ; // local value type: in M's stack frame (or a CPU register) var order = new Order (); // the reference 'order' is on the stack; the Order object is on the heap int [] nums = { 1 , 2 , 3 }; // the array object (with its 3 ints inline) is on the heap } Look at Order.Quantity closely: it's an int , a value type — and yet it's sitting on the heap, because an Order object is a reference type, and Quantity lives wherever its containing Order lives. The value type didn't move to the heap on its own; it's just physically embedded inside something that did. That's the whole rule, applied consistently: a value type's location is never decided by the value type itself, only by whatever's holding it. There are a couple of exceptions worth flagging now, even though they're explained properly in later chapters: locals captured by a lambda (a closure) and locals inside async methods or iterators stop being ordinary stack locals and become fields of a compiler-generated object on the heap instead. You'll see exactly why, and exactly what that generated object looks like, in Delegates and async/await . Why this distinction actually matters in backend code Every heap allocation adds work for the garbage collector — you saw the mechanics of that in Memory & GC . If a hot API endpoint allocates thousands of small temporary objects per request, you get frequent GCs and the latency spikes that come with them. Modelling small, short-lived data as structs (or processing it with Span<T> "},{"c":"type-system","s":"when-a-struct-actually-earns-its-keep","h":"When a struct actually earns its keep","t":"# When a struct actually earns its keep Intermediate Given that trade-off, when should you actually reach for struct instead of the default, class ? The Framework Design Guidelines spell out four conditions, word for word, and the honest reading of their own wording is: avoid a struct unless all four hold. It logically represents a single value (a point, money, a coordinate, an ID). Its instance size is under 16 bytes . It is immutable . It won't be boxed frequently (you'll meet boxing properly in the next section). Fail even one of those, and a class is almost always the better choice. C# Run ▶ Copy public readonly record struct Money ( decimal Amount , string Currency ) { public static Money operator +( Money a, Money b) => a. Currency == b. Currency ? new (a. Amount + b. Amount , a. Currency ) : throw new InvalidOperationException ( \"Currency mismatch\" ); } public readonly struct OrderId ( Guid value ) // strongly typed ID: can't mix up with CustomerId { public Guid Value { get ; } = value ; public override string ToString () => Value . ToString (); } Two keywords in those examples are doing real work, so let's take each on its own. readonly struct is a compiler-enforced promise: nothing about this type can ever change after it's constructed, not even internally. That promise lets the compiler skip something called a defensive copy . Here's what that means: normally, when you pass an ordinary (non-readonly) struct into a method using in — a modifier that means \"you only get to read this, not change it\" — the compiler still quietly makes a safety copy first, just in case that method secretly tries to mutate it anyway. A readonly struct removes the need for that safety net: there's nothing to protect against, since the type itself can't be mutated, so the compiler skips the copy and you get the performance benefit of in for free. record struct (C# 10) is a struct that also gets the same three conveniences a record class gets: value-based equality (two instances count as equal if their fields match — the \"Equality\" section later in this chapter covers exactly what that means and why it's not the default for plain structs), a readable default ToString() , and with -expressions — shorthand for \"copy this value, but change just one field,\" e.g. money with { Amount = 50 } produces a new Money with the same Currency and a different Amount . OrderId above demonstrates a different, popular pattern worth naming on its own: a strongly typed ID . Instead of passing a bare Guid around for every kind of ID in your system, you wrap it in a tiny dedicated struct. The payoff: GetOrder(customerId) — accidentally passing the wrong kind of ID into the wrong method — becomes a compile error instead of a runtime bug, because OrderId and CustomerId are genuinely different types to the compiler, even though they're both \"just a Guid \" underneath. Mutable structs bite, and in a genuinely surprising way C# Run ▶ Copy var points = new List < PointS > { new () { X = 1 } }; points[ 0 ]. X = 5 ; // compile error CS1612: the indexer returns a COPY, modifying it is pointless Here's why that's a compile error rather than silently doing nothing: List<T> 's indexer is a method ( get_Item ), and calling it returns a copy of the struct stored inside the list — so points[0].X = 5 would modify a temporary copy that's immediately thrown away, achieving nothing. The compiler refuses to compile code that can't possibly do what it looks like it does. With a plain array, though, array[0].X = 5 does work and modifies in place, because an array element is a real, addressable variable, not something returned from a method call. Same-looking syntax, two different outcomes, depending on whether you're indexing an array or a List<T> — which is exactly the kind of inconsistency that makes \"keep structs immutable\" such a firmly held rule rather than just a style preference."},{"c":"type-system","s":"boxing-what-happens-when-a-value-type-has-to-pretend-to-be-a","h":"Boxing: what happens when a value type has to pretend to be an object","t":"# Boxing: what happens when a value type has to pretend to be an object Intermediate Here's a scenario that comes up constantly: you have an int , but something — an older API, a non-generic collection, an interface — needs an object . An int is a value type; object is a reference type. How does a value with no heap presence at all get treated as something that's supposed to live on the heap? Boxing is the answer: the runtime allocates a small object on the heap, copies the value type's bits into it, and hands you a reference to that box — now indistinguishable, from the outside, from any other heap object. Unboxing is the reverse: given a boxed reference, copy the value back out into an ordinary value-type variable. C# Run ▶ Copy int n = 42 ; object o = n; // BOX: allocate an object on the heap, copy 42 into it int m = ( int )o; // UNBOX: type check + copy back out IComparable c = n; // also boxes: interface reference to a value type Notice that last line — assigning n to an IComparable variable boxes it too, for exactly the same reason: an interface reference is a reference-type concept, so a value type being treated as \"any type implementing this interface\" needs the same heap wrapper as being treated as a bare object does. The dangerous part is that boxing often happens without you writing anything that looks like a box : C# Run ▶ Copy // Hidden boxing var list = new ArrayList (); // non-generic, stores object list. Add ( 1 ); // boxes string s = string . Format ( \"{0}\" , 42 ); // boxes the int (interpolated strings avoid this) ArrayList.Add takes an object parameter — a holdover from before generics existed — so every int you add gets silently boxed on the way in. string.Format(\"{0}\", 42) has the same issue: its object[] parameter list boxes 42 before the method body even runs. Neither line looks expensive, and that's exactly the trap: each one is a heap allocation, a copy, and — on the way back out — a type check, all paid for something that looks like a free, instant operation. Do this in a hot loop processing millions of items, and you've built a real, measurable performance bug out of code that compiles without a single warning — a classic real-world version of this is a hot path that logs thousands of order IDs a second through an old ArrayList -based audit buffer, each one boxed on the way in. The actual fix, worth remembering as a rule rather than a one-off trick: generics avoid boxing structurally . List<int> stores int s directly, inline, with no boxing at all — because the JIT generates a genuinely separate, specialised version of List<T> for int specifically, rather than forcing everything through a shared object -based implementation. You'll see exactly how and why that works in Generics . A boxed value is frozen at the moment it was boxed C# Run ▶ Copy PointS p = new () { X = 1 }; object boxed = p; p. X = 2 ; Console . WriteLine ((( PointS )boxed). X ); // 1: the box holds a copy taken at boxing time This surprises people who expect boxed to somehow track p . It doesn't, because boxing is a copy , not a reference back to p — the exact same copy-semantics rule from the very start of this chapter, just applied one level deeper. Once p is boxed, the two are completely independent; changing one never touches the other."},{"c":"type-system","s":"strings-a-reference-type-dressed-up-to-feel-like-a-value","h":"Strings: a reference type dressed up to feel like a value","t":"# Strings: a reference type dressed up to feel like a value Beginner string is, by the table at the top of this chapter, a reference type — but it behaves so differently from a typical reference type ( PointC , say) that it's worth its own section. Three deliberate design choices explain why: It is immutable : every \"modification\" — + , Replace , ToUpper , anything — actually creates a brand-new string and leaves the original completely untouched. == compares contents , not references, because the == operator is specifically overloaded for string to do that. (Most reference types don't do this — PointC == PointC above would compare references, not field values, unless you overload it yourself.) Identical string literals are interned : the runtime recognises \"hello\" appearing twice in your source and has both refer to the exact same single object in memory, rather than allocating two copies of an identical string. C# Run ▶ Copy string a = \"hello\" ; string b = a; b += \" world\" ; // creates a NEW string; a is still \"hello\" // Concatenation in a loop creates n intermediate strings: O(n²) copying var s = \"\" ; foreach ( var w in words) s += w; // bad for large n var sb = new StringBuilder (); foreach ( var w in words) sb. Append (w); // one growing buffer var result = sb. ToString (); var joined = string . Join ( \", \" , words); // best when you have a collection That second block is worth understanding precisely, not just avoiding by rote: every single s += w inside the loop throws away the previous string entirely and allocates a brand-new, slightly longer one — because strings are immutable, there's no other way to \"append\" to one. Over n iterations, you're not doing n units of work, you're doing roughly 1 + 2 + 3 + ... + n units of copying, which is O(n²) . StringBuilder exists specifically to avoid this: it maintains one resizable internal buffer and only produces a final immutable string once, when you call ToString() . Comparison deserves its own rule, because getting it wrong is a genuine, recurring bug category: always be deliberate about which comparison rules you're invoking. For identifiers, keys, file paths and protocol values, use ordinal comparison — string.Equals(a, b, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase) — which compares raw character codes with no linguistic interpretation at all. Reserve culture-sensitive comparison ( CurrentCulture ) for text you're actually displaying to a user in their own language. The classic cautionary tale here is the \"Turkish I problem\": call .ToUpper() on \"i\" under Turkish culture settings and you get \"İ\" , not \"I\" — which has broken more than one case-insensitive lookup that assumed ToUpper / ToLower meant the same thing everywhere in the world."},{"c":"type-system","s":"nullable-value-types-giving-a-value-type-a-no-value-state","h":"Nullable value types: giving a value type a \"no value\" state","t":"# Nullable value types: giving a value type a \"no value\" state Beginner Value types, as the very first table in this chapter said, can't be null — an int always has some integer value, even if that value is just 0 by default. But plenty of real-world data genuinely has a \"no value\" state that isn't the same thing as zero: a discount that might not apply, a ShippedAt date for an order that hasn't shipped yet. Nullable<T> , almost always written as the shorthand T? , is how C# adds that missing state to a value type without turning it into a reference type. C# Run ▶ Copy int ? discount = null ; if (discount. HasValue ) Console . WriteLine (discount. Value ); int effective = discount ?? 0 ; // null-coalescing int alsoEffective = discount. GetValueOrDefault (); DateTime ? shippedAt = order. ShippedAt ; // database NULL ↔ C# null Here's the detail that makes Nullable<T> click: it isn't magic, it's just an ordinary struct, roughly { bool hasValue; T value; } — a flag plus the underlying value, packaged together. That's why int? is still, underneath, a value type with all the copy semantics you'd expect, not some special case the runtime handles differently. One genuinely subtle consequence: boxing a null int? produces an actual null reference, not a box containing \"no value\" — while boxing a non-null int? boxes just the underlying int , discarding the wrapper entirely. The box, once created, has no memory that it ever came from a Nullable<T> at all. Don't confuse this with nullable reference types ( string? ), which look almost identical in syntax but are a completely different mechanism — compile-time-only annotations that add nothing whatsoever at runtime. Modern C# covers the distinction in full."},{"c":"type-system","s":"enums-named-integers-with-a-sharp-edge","h":"Enums: named integers with a sharp edge","t":"# Enums: named integers with a sharp edge Beginner An enum is a named set of integer constants — a value type, backed by int unless you say otherwise. C# Run ▶ Copy public enum OrderStatus { Draft = 0 , Placed = 1 , Paid = 2 , Shipped = 3 , Cancelled = 9 } [Flags] public enum Permissions { None = 0 , Read = 1 , Write = 2 , Delete = 4 , Admin = Read | Write | Delete } var p = Permissions . Read | Permissions . Write ; bool canWrite = p. HasFlag ( Permissions . Write ); // or (p & Permissions.Write) != 0 Notice Permissions assigns 1, 2, 4 — powers of two, not 1, 2, 3 . That's deliberate: each value occupies a distinct bit, so they can be combined with | (bitwise OR) without any of them colliding, and checked individually with HasFlag or a raw bitwise & . The [Flags] attribute signals this intent to tooling (and to ToString() , which then prints \"Read, Write\" instead of a meaningless number) — but doesn't change the underlying mechanics at all; it's purely a hint. Enums are not validated — a value outside the named set compiles and runs just fine (OrderStatus)42 compiles, runs, and produces a perfectly valid-looking OrderStatus value that happens to correspond to no named member at all. This isn't a hypothetical edge case: values coming from JSON, from a database column, from a different version of your own code, can easily land outside the set you defined. Check with Enum.IsDefined , or a switch with a default arm that handles the unexpected case explicitly, anywhere an enum value arrives from outside your own code. One more easily-missed trap: if you ever persist enum values as their underlying numbers, give every member you store an explicit number. Without one, the compiler assigns them in declaration order starting from 0 — and simply reordering your enum declaration, with no other change at all, silently changes what every previously stored number now means. Given that fragility, prefer serialising enums as strings in public APIs ( JsonStringEnumConverter ), not as their numeric values — a new member added later doesn't break clients reading the string name, the way it could break clients hard-coded against specific numbers."},{"c":"type-system","s":"equality-three-different-questions-similar-syntax","h":"Equality: three different questions, similar syntax","t":"# Equality: three different questions, similar syntax Intermediate \"Are these two equal?\" sounds like one question. In C#, depending on exactly how you ask it, it's actually at least three, and they don't always agree with each other: Default for classes Default for structs Records == Reference equality (unless overloaded, e.g. string ) Not defined unless you overload it Value equality Equals(object) Reference equality Field-by-field value equality (via reflection, slow) Value equality (generated, fast) ReferenceEquals Same object? Always false (boxed copies) Same object? The struct row for Equals is worth a second look: it works, but it's genuinely slow, because the default implementation uses reflection to compare every field one by one — exactly the kind of runtime cost How code runs warned reflection carries. If you're going to compare structs for equality often, overriding Equals yourself (or using record struct , which generates a fast version for you) is a real, measurable win, not just tidiness. If you override Equals yourself, there's a rule you must follow or you'll get genuinely confusing bugs much later: you must override GetHashCode too, consistently — equal objects have to produce equal hash codes, or Dictionary / HashSet will silently fail to find items you know are in them. Implementing IEquatable<T> alongside both gives you a fast, non-boxing Equals(T) overload on top. C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class Sku : IEquatable < Sku > { public string Code { get ; } public Sku ( string code) => Code = code. ToUpperInvariant (); public bool Equals ( Sku ? other) => other is not null && Code == other. Code ; public override bool Equals ( object ? obj) => Equals (obj as Sku ); public override int GetHashCode () => Code . GetHashCode (); public static bool operator ==( Sku ? a, Sku ? b) => Equals (a, b); public static bool operator !=( Sku ? a, Sku ? b) => ! Equals (a, b); } // Or just: public sealed record Sku(string Code); ← the compiler writes all of the above That final comment is worth taking seriously as practical advice, not just trivia: every single member in that Sku class — Equals , GetHashCode , both operators — is exactly what the compiler generates automatically for a record . Unless you have a real reason to write a class by hand here, a one-line record gets you the same correctness with none of the five-method ceremony above it. A mutable hash key is a silent, permanent bug If an object's hash code depends on a field that changes after you've already added it to a HashSet or used it as a Dictionary key, you will never find it again through that collection — not an exception, not a warning, just a lookup that quietly fails forever, because the collection stored it under the hash code it had then , and is now searching under the hash code it has now . Keys must be immutable, full stop."},{"c":"type-system","s":"passing-parameters-what-by-value-actually-means-for-a-refere","h":"Passing parameters: what \"by value\" actually means for a reference","t":"# Passing parameters: what \"by value\" actually means for a reference Intermediate Everything in C# is passed by value, with no exceptions — including reference types, which is the part that confuses people, because it genuinely sounds like it should mean the opposite. Here's the precise mechanics: for a reference type, the thing being passed \"by value\" is the reference itself — so the method you're calling gets its own copy of that reference, pointing at the same object, which means it can mutate the object through that reference, but it cannot make the caller's own variable point somewhere else. C# Run ▶ Copy void Rename ( Customer c) { c. Name = \"New\" ; } // caller sees this (same object) void Replace ( Customer c) { c = new Customer (); } // caller does NOT see this void ReplaceRef ( ref Customer c) { c = new Customer (); } // caller DOES see this Those three methods look almost identical, and produce three genuinely different outcomes. Rename mutates the object the reference points at — both the caller and the method are looking at the same Customer , so the caller sees the change. Replace reassigns the local copy of the reference to point at a brand-new object entirely — but that local copy is the method's own, private variable; the caller's original reference never moves, and still points at the original Customer . ReplaceRef adds the one modifier that changes this: ref passes the caller's actual variable, not a copy of it, so reassigning c inside the method genuinely does reassign the caller's variable too. Modifier Meaning Caller must Typical use (none) Copy of value / copy of reference — Default ref Pass the variable itself; method can read and write Initialise first, write ref at call site Swapping, large structs you mutate out Method must assign it; caller needn't initialise Write out TryParse , TryGetValue patterns in Read-only reference; avoids copying large structs (optional in ) Performance with big readonly structs ref readonly (C# 12) Like in , but the call site must pass a reference API clarity out is the one you've almost certainly already used without necessarily naming it: C# Run ▶ Copy if ( int . TryParse (input, out var qty) && dict. TryGetValue (key, out var value )) { /* ... */ } The contract out makes is the mirror image of a normal parameter: instead of the caller promising to provide a value, the method promises to assign one before it returns — which is exactly why TryParse and TryGetValue can return a clean bool for success/failure while still handing back the actual result through the same call, with no uninitialised-variable risk on the caller's side."},{"c":"type-system","s":"type-conversions-and-checks","h":"Type conversions and checks","t":"# Type conversions and checks Beginner C# Run ▶ Copy object o = GetSomething (); // Pattern matching (preferred) if (o is Order order && order. Total > 100 ) { /* order is typed */ } // as: returns null on failure (reference/nullable types only) var maybe = o as Order ; // Cast: throws InvalidCastException on failure var sure = ( Order )o; // Numeric conversions int i = 300 ; byte b = ( byte )i; // 44: silently wraps (unchecked) byte c = checked (( byte )i); // throws OverflowException long big = i; // implicit widening: always safe decimal price = decimal . Parse ( \"19.99\" , CultureInfo . InvariantCulture ); The first three options above aren't interchangeable — they're three different answers to \"what should happen if this doesn't work?\" Pattern matching ( is ) checks and gives you a safely typed variable in one step, failing gracefully into the else branch. as returns null on failure, which only makes sense for reference and nullable types (there's no \"null\" a plain cast could fall back to for a non-nullable value type). A plain cast (Order)o is the least forgiving: it throws InvalidCastException immediately if the object genuinely isn't an Order . The numeric half of that block hides a real footgun: implicit conversions ( int → long ) are always safe, because the destination type can represent every possible value of the source — nothing is ever lost. Explicit casts carry no such guarantee, and (byte)300 is the proof: 300 doesn't fit in a byte 's 0–255 range, so it silently wraps around to 44, with no warning, no exception, just a wrong number that looks like a right one. That silent wraparound is also the default behaviour for everyday integer arithmetic in C#, not just explicit casts — int.MaxValue + 1 overflows back to a huge negative number without a peep, unless you opt into <CheckForOverflowUnderflow> project-wide or wrap specific risky arithmetic in a checked block. One more rule worth having memorised, not just known: use decimal for money, never double . decimal is base-10 internally, so it represents a value like 0.1 exactly . double is binary floating point, and — this is the famous, often-demonstrated gotcha — 0.1 + 0.2 != 0.3 under binary floating point, because neither 0.1 nor 0.2 can be represented exactly in base 2 in the first place. For currency, that's not a rounding curiosity, it's a correctness bug waiting to happen."},{"c":"type-system","s":"var-dynamic-and-object-three-ways-to-avoid-naming-a-type-for","h":"var, dynamic and object: three ways to avoid naming a type, for three different reasons","t":"# var, dynamic and object: three ways to avoid naming a type, for three different reasons Beginner What it is Type checked var Compiler infers the static type from the right-hand side. Still strongly typed. Compile time object The base of all types; you need casts to use it Compile time (as object ) dynamic Bypasses compile-time checks; resolved at runtime by the DLR Runtime (errors become exceptions) It's worth being precise about var , because its name invites a misconception: var is purely syntax , resolved entirely at compile time, with zero runtime effect. var x = 5; doesn't produce some loosely typed variable — it compiles to exactly, literally, int x = 5; . The compiler already knows the right-hand side's type perfectly well; var just saves you typing it out a second time on the left. dynamic is the genuine outlier in that table: it defers all member resolution — does this object even have a .Foo() method? — all the way to runtime, via the DLR (Dynamic Language Runtime). That trades away IntelliSense, compile-time error checking, and performance, all at once, in exchange for flexibility you'll rarely actually need in backend code. Reach for it almost never: COM interop and a handful of dynamic-JSON scenarios are about the only legitimate uses you'll run into."},{"c":"type-system","s":"memory-layout-and-size","h":"Memory layout and size","t":"# Memory layout and size Advanced Every heap object carries a header before its own fields even begin — an 8-byte sync block index plus an 8-byte method table pointer on a 64-bit process — so even a completely empty object costs roughly 24 bytes before a single field of your own is counted. This specific number is a CLR implementation detail rather than something the C# language spec promises you, documented in community/internals references like Pro .NET Memory Management (Konrad Kokosa) and CLR via C# rather than on Microsoft Learn itself — but it's stable and well-known enough to rely on when budgeting memory. That overhead is invisible in isolation, but allocate thousands of tiny objects per request and it adds up into real, measurable memory pressure. The runtime is free to reorder a class's fields in memory for better packing; structs, by contrast, default to sequential layout — the order you declared them in — which matters specifically for native interop, and can be controlled explicitly with [StructLayout] . sizeof(int) is 4 bytes, long / double are 8, decimal is 16, Guid is 16, DateTime is 8, and a reference is 8 bytes on a 64-bit process. Unsafe.SizeOf<T>() gives you the exact size of any type at runtime; ObjectLayoutInspector and BenchmarkDotNet's MemoryDiagnoser are the practical tools for actually measuring this in your own code rather than reasoning about it in the abstract. Q What is the difference between a value type and a reference type? A value type variable contains its data, and assignment copies the data. A reference type variable contains a reference to a heap object, and assignment copies the reference so both variables see the same object. Value types can't be null (without Nullable<T> ) and can't be inherited; reference types are garbage-collected objects that support inheritance. Q Do value types always live on the stack? No. Reference-type objects always live on the heap, but value types live wherever their container lives: locals on the stack (unless captured by a lambda or used in an async method or iterator), fields inside a class object on the heap, elements inline inside an array on the heap. The defining difference is copy semantics, not location. Q When would you use a struct instead of a class? For a small (≈16 bytes or less), immutable value that logically represents a single value and isn't boxed often: Money, coordinates, strongly typed IDs. Make it a readonly struct or readonly record struct . Use classes for anything with identity, behaviour, inheritance or larger size. Q What is boxing and how do you avoid it? Converting a value type to object or an interface, which allocates a heap object and copies the value; unboxing copies it back out with a type check. Avoid it with generics ( List<int> instead of ArrayList ), generic constraints instead of interface parameters, IEquatable<T> , and interpolated strings instead of string.Format with value arguments. Q Why is string immutable, and what are the consequences? Immutability makes strings thread-safe, safe to use as dictionary keys, and allows interning. The consequence is that every modification allocates a new string, so build strings in loops with StringBuilder or string.Join/Concat. Q If you override Equals, what else must you do? Override GetHashCode consistently (equal objects ⇒ equal hash codes), ideally implement IEquatable<T> , and overload == / != so they agree. Or use a record, which generates all of this. Q Are reference types passed by reference in C#? No. Everything is passed by value by default. For a reference type, the value passed is the reference, so the method can modify the object but reassigning the parameter doesn't affect the caller. Use ref to pass the variable itself. Q ref vs out vs in? ref : the caller passes an initialised variable, and the method can read and write it. out : the method must assign it, and the caller needn't initialise it (TryParse pattern). in : a read-only reference, used to avoid copying "},{"c":"type-system","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Value types (C# reference) — confirms copy semantics; deliberately doesn't commit to \"stack vs heap\" wording, for the reason this chapter explains. Choosing between class and struct — the exact four-condition struct rule quoted above, and the \"value types are allocated either on the stack or inline in containing types\" sentence this chapter's stack/heap section is built on. Boxing and unboxing Nullable value types — boxing and unboxing rules — the exact source for \"boxing a null T? returns the null reference; boxing a non-null one boxes just the underlying value.\" Book: C# in Depth by Jon Skeet. Book: CLR via C# by Jeffrey Richter, chapter on type fundamentals. Book: Pro .NET Memory Management by Konrad Kokosa, for the object-header/memory-layout numbers in the Advanced section above."},{"c":"type-system","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Exact memory layout (object header size, struct field packing) needs a profiler like ObjectLayoutInspector, not plain code — see Further reading for that. Everything else in this chapter is observable behaviour, so the program below asserts it directly. Run with dotnet run (plain console app, .NET 8/9, no extra packages). C# Run ▶ Copy static void Rename ( Customer c) => c. Name = \"Renamed\" ; static void Replace ( Customer c) => c = new Customer { Name = \"Should not be visible\" }; static void ReplaceRef ( ref Customer c) => c = new Customer { Name = \"Brand New\" }; // Claim: assigning a struct copies the data; assigning a class copies only the reference. var s1 = new PointS { X = 1 }; var s2 = s1; s2. X = 99 ; if (s1. X != 1 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: struct assignment should have copied, not shared, the data\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: struct assignment copies the value (s1.X stayed 1)\" ); var c1 = new PointC { X = 1 }; var c2 = c1; c2. X = 99 ; if (c1. X != 99 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: class assignment should share the same object\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: class assignment copies the reference, so both see the change (c1.X became 99)\" ); // Claim: a boxed value is frozen at the moment it was boxed — later changes to the original don't show through. PointS p = new () { X = 1 }; object boxed = p; p. X = 2 ; if ((( PointS )boxed). X != 1 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: the box should hold a copy taken at boxing time\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: a boxed value type is an independent copy, frozen at boxing time\" ); // Claim: enums are not validated — a value outside the named set compiles and runs. var bogus = ( OrderStatus ) 42 ; if ( Enum . IsDefined (bogus)) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: 42 should not be a defined OrderStatus member\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: (OrderStatus)42 = {bogus} compiles and runs, but Enum.IsDefined correctly flags it as undefined\" ); // Claim: decimal represents 0.1 + 0.2 exactly as 0.3; double does not. decimal d = 0.1m + 0.2m ; double f = 0.1 + 0.2 ; if (d != 0.3m ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: decimal 0.1 + 0.2 should equal exactly 0.3\" ); if (f == 0.3 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected binary double 0.1 + 0.2 to NOT equal 0.3 exactly\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: decimal 0.1m+0.2m == 0.3m exactly; double 0.1+0.2 = {f:R} != 0.3 exactly (binary rounding)\" ); // Claim: everything is passed by value — `ref` is what lets a method reassign the caller's variable. var customer = new Customer { Name = \"Original\" }; Rename (customer); if (customer. Name != \"Renamed\" ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: mutating through the reference should be visible to the caller\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: Rename mutated the shared object — caller sees 'Renamed'\" ); Replace (customer); if (customer. Name != \"Renamed\" ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: reassigning the local copy of the reference must NOT affect the caller\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: Replace reassigned only its own local copy — caller's variable is untouched\" ); ReplaceRef ( ref customer); if (customer. Name != \"Brand New\" ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: ref should let the method reassign the caller's variable\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: ReplaceRef (with `ref`) reassigned the caller's own variable\" ); // Claim: boxing a null Nullable<T> produces an actual null reference, not a box of \"no value\". int ? maybeNull = null ; object ? boxedNullable = maybeNull; if (boxedNullable is not null ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: boxing a null int? should produce a real null reference\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: boxing a null int? produces an actual null reference\" ); struct PointS { public int X ; } class PointC { public int X ; } class Customer { public string Name = \"\" ; } enum OrderStatus { Draft = 0 , Placed = 1 , Paid = 2 , Shipped = 3 , Cancelled = 9 }"},{"c":"oop","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR A class bundles state (fields/properties) and behaviour (methods); most of OOP is about controlling how much of that state the outside world can see or change. Construction order is derived field inits → base field inits → base constructor body → derived constructor body — the opposite of what intuition suggests, and the reason calling a virtual method from a constructor is dangerous. override is runtime ( virtual dispatch , decided by the object's real type); new is compile-time (decided by the variable's declared type) — they look alike and behave completely differently. Interface vs abstract class is a design decision, not just vocabulary: interfaces for swappable \"can-do\" contracts, abstract classes for related types that share real code/state. Prefer composition (small, swappable collaborators) over deep inheritance chains, which get brittle fast (the fragile base class problem, the \"one class per combination\" explosion). Pico Mart Picture a small online shop, Pico Mart . Its backend needs customers, accounts, payments, orders — the usual cast. Every section below is a real problem Pico Mart's code ran into, in the order it ran into them. By the end you'll have watched one small codebase grow from \"a class with some fields\" into something that actually resembles a real OOP design, instead of four definitions recited for an interview."},{"c":"oop","s":"a-class-is-two-things-bundled-together","h":"A class is two things bundled together","t":"# A class is two things bundled together Beginner Pico Mart's first customer record was just a bag of public fields — name, email, a list of addresses — and that already breaks within a day, because nothing stops other code from putting garbage into it. A class is a blueprint for objects, and every single thing you can put inside one falls into exactly two buckets: state (data the object carries around — its fields) and behaviour (what it can do — its methods). Everything else in this chapter is really about controlling how those two buckets interact with each other and with the outside world. C# Run ▶ Copy namespace Shop . Domain ; public sealed class Customer // type declaration { private readonly List < Address > _addresses = []; // field: private state public const int MaxAddresses = 5 ; // compile-time constant private static int _created; // static field: shared by all instances public Customer ( string email, string name) // constructor { ArgumentException . ThrowIfNullOrWhiteSpace (email); Email = email; Name = name; _created++; } public Guid Id { get ; } = Guid . NewGuid (); // get-only auto-property (set in ctor/initialiser) public string Email { get ; private set ; } // public read, private write public string Name { get ; set ; } // read/write auto-property public bool IsVip => TotalSpent > 10_000m ; // computed (expression-bodied) property public decimal TotalSpent { get ; private set ; } public IReadOnlyList < Address > Addresses => _addresses; // expose read-only view public void AddAddress ( Address a) // method: behaviour that protects invariants { if (_addresses. Count >= MaxAddresses ) throw new InvalidOperationException ( \"Too many addresses\" ); _addresses. Add (a); } public event EventHandler ? BecameVip ; // event public override string ToString () => $\"{Name} <{Email}>\" ; } Member Purpose Field Raw storage. Keep private. Property Controlled access with get / set / init accessors. The public face of state. Method Behaviour. Constructor Initialises a new instance; establishes invariants. Static members Belong to the type, not an instance. Constant / readonly const is compile-time and baked into callers; readonly is set once at runtime (in the constructor). Indexer this[int i] lets instances be used like arrays. Event Notification to subscribers. See Delegates & events . Operator Overloads like + , == , implicit/explicit conversions. Nested type A type declared inside another. Finalizer ~Customer() : last-chance cleanup by the GC. Rarely needed. const vs static readonly look similar but behave completely differently across assemblies A public const isn't really a variable at all by the time your code ships — the compiler copies its literal value directly into every assembly that uses it , at compile time. That has a sharp consequence: if you change a const inside a library and ship a new version, every already-compiled caller keeps using the old value, baked in, until they themselves are recompiled against the new library. static readonly doesn't have this problem — it's read fresh from the library at runtime, every time — which is exactly why it's the safer choice for anything that might ever change between versions. Reserve const for values that are genuinely, permanently constant: Math.PI , a fixed protocol value, that sort of thing."},{"c":"oop","s":"properties-the-public-face-a-field-doesnt-get","h":"Properties: the public face a field doesn't get","t":"# Properties: the public face a field doesn't get Beginner C# Run ▶ Copy public string Name { get ; set ; } // auto-property public string Sku { get ; init ; } // settable only during object initialisation public required string Email { get ; init ; } // caller MUST set it (C# 11) public decimal Price { get ; private set ; } // encapsulated setter private int _qty; public int Quantity // full property with validation { get => _qty; set => _qty = value >= 0 ? value : throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException ( nameof ( value )); } public string Slug { get ; set => field = value . Trim (). ToLowerInvariant (); } // C# 14 'field' keyword A bare field is just memory — anyone with access to it can set it to anything, including nonsense. A property wraps that memory behind get / set accessors that are really just methods in disguise, which means you can put real logic in them, as Quantity does above: reject a negative value the instant someone tries to set it, instead of discovering the bad data three layers downstream. init narrows that further — settable only while an object is being constructed via an object initialiser, then locked — and required (C# 11) goes one step beyond that, making the compiler refuse to compile any code that constructs the type without setting that property at all. C# Run ▶ Copy var p = new Product { Sku = \"KB-01\" , Email = \"x@y.z\" , Name = \"Keyboard\" }; Worth knowing precisely: an object initialiser like this one runs its property assignments after the constructor has already finished, not during it — so if your constructor logic depends on a property that's only ever set this way, it'll see the property's default value, not whatever gets assigned a moment later."},{"c":"oop","s":"the-order-things-actually-happen-in-when-you-call-new","h":"The order things actually happen in, when you call new","t":"# The order things actually happen in, when you call new Intermediate Pico Mart's bug tracker once had a ticket titled \"logger is null in the base constructor, but only sometimes\" — which turned into exactly this question: when OrderService extends Base , which code runs first? C# Run ▶ Copy public class Base { protected readonly ILogger Log ; public Base ( ILogger log) { Log = log; Console . WriteLine ( \"Base ctor\" ); } } public class OrderService : Base { private readonly IOrderRepository _repo; private readonly int _retries = 3 ; // field initialiser public OrderService ( IOrderRepository repo, ILogger log) : base (log) // calls Base first { _repo = repo; Console . WriteLine ( \"Derived ctor\" ); } public OrderService ( IOrderRepository repo) : this (repo, NullLogger . Instance ) { } // chain } When you call new OrderService(...) , it's tempting to assume everything in OrderService runs before anything in Base — it's the type you actually asked for, after all. The real order is the opposite, and it matters enough to walk through exactly: Derived field initialisers run first ( _retries = 3 ) — before any constructor body at all. Base field initialisers run next. The base constructor's body runs. Only after the base is fully constructed does the derived constructor's body run. The reasoning behind that order is simple once you see it: a derived class's constructor body is allowed to assume the base class is already fully, safely built — calling base 's methods, reading base 's fields — and that assumption would be false if the order ran the other way. (Static constructors are a separate thing entirely: they run exactly once per type, automatically, before that type is first used, and the runtime guarantees this happens in a thread-safe way even under concurrent access.) Calling a virtual method from a constructor is a trap, and now you know exactly why The base constructor's body runs before the derived constructor's body — you just watched that happen in the trace above. So if the base constructor calls a virtual method that the derived class overrides, that override runs during step 3 , while the object is still, by definition, only half-built: every field the derived constructor was going to set in step 4 hasn't been set yet. The override executes against an object in a state it was never designed to handle. # Primary constructors: the modern shorthand C# Run ▶ Copy public class OrderService ( IOrderRepository repo, ILogger < OrderService > log) { public Task < Order ?> GetAsync ( int id) { log. LogInformation ( \"Get {Id}\" , id); return repo. FindAsync (id); } } A primary constructor's parameters ( repo , log above) are in scope for the entire class body, not just inside a constructor method — which is why GetAsync can reach repo and log directly, with no explicit field ever declared. The catch worth knowing: these parameters are not automatically readonly fields. They're ordinary, reassignable parameters that happen to stay in scope — so if you genuinely need the immutability guarantee a readonly field would give you, assign one explicitly: private readonly IOrderRepository _repo = repo; . In practice, primary constructors have become the standard, idiomatic style for DI-injected services specifically because most services never need to reassign their dependencies anyway."},{"c":"oop","s":"the-four-pillars-with-a-reason-each-one-earns-its-place","h":"The four pillars, with a reason each one earns its place","t":"# The four pillars, with a reason each one earns its place Beginner These four words get recited so often in interviews that they risk becoming empty vocabulary. Here's each one tied back to an actual problem Pico Mart's code ran into — and, unlike a lot of OOP explainers, each one below gets its own small program you can actually run , not just a paragraph pointing at someone else's code. # Encapsulation: the object protects its own rules The problem: a Customer with a public List<Address> lets any calling code add a 51st address, silently breaking the \"max 5 addresses\" rule that exists for a reason you presumably had. Run this and watch it happen: C# Run ▶ Copy // Run with: dotnet run (plain console app, no packages) // --- Version 1: no encapsulation. The rule exists only as a comment. --- public class LeakyCustomer { public List < string > Addresses = new (); // public field: anyone can touch it, no questions asked } var leaky = new LeakyCustomer (); for ( int i = 1 ; i <= 7 ; i++) leaky. Addresses . Add ( $\"Address #{i}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"LeakyCustomer ended up with {leaky.Addresses.Count} addresses — the \\\"max 5\\\" rule was never real code, just a wish.\" ); // --- Version 2: encapsulated. The field is hidden; a method is the only door in. --- public class Customer { private readonly List < string > _addresses = new (); // private: invisible outside this class public const int MaxAddresses = 5 ; public IReadOnlyList < string > Addresses => _addresses; // read-only VIEW: callers can look, not modify public void AddAddress ( string address) // the only way in — and it enforces the rule { if (_addresses. Count >= MaxAddresses ) throw new InvalidOperationException ( $\"Customers can't have more than {MaxAddresses} addresses.\" ); _addresses. Add (address); } } var safe = new Customer (); try { for ( int i = 1 ; i <= 7 ; i++) safe. AddAddress ( $\"Address #{i}\" ); } catch ( InvalidOperationException ex) { Console . WriteLine ( $\"Customer stopped at {safe.Addresses.Count} addresses: \\\"{ex.Message}\\\"\" ); } // safe.Addresses.Add(\"sneaky\"); // doesn't even compile: IReadOnlyList<T> has no Add method Running it prints 7 for the leaky version and 5 (with the exception message) for the encapsulated one. Nothing about the data changed between the two versions — only who's allowed to touch it, and through which door. That's the entire idea: hide the state, expose only behaviour that keeps it valid. # Inheritance: sharing one real \"is-a\" relationship The problem: SavingsAccount and CheckingAccount genuinely share a lot of behaviour — they're both, fundamentally, accounts, with a balance and deposit/withdraw rules. Writing that twice would mean fixing every bug twice, forever. Inheritance lets SavingsAccount extend one base class and inherit its non-private members, modelling a genuine \"is-a\" relationship: a SavingsAccount is an Account , not merely similar to one. C# Run ▶ Copy public class Account { public string Owner { get ; } public decimal Balance { get ; protected set ; } // protected: derived classes can change it, outsiders can't public Account ( string owner, decimal openingBalance) { Owner = owner; Balance = openingBalance; } public virtual void Withdraw ( decimal amount) // shared rule, but subclasses may specialise it { if (amount > Balance ) throw new InvalidOperationException ( \"Insufficient funds.\" ); Balance -= amount; } } public class SavingsAccount : Account { public decimal InterestRate { get ; } public SavingsAccount ( string owner, decimal openingBalance, decimal rate) : base (owner, openingBalance) => InterestRate = rate; public void ApplyMonthlyInterest () => Balance += Balance * InterestRate ; // behaviour only savings accounts have } public class CheckingAccount : Account { private const decimal OverdraftLimit = 100m ; public CheckingAccount ( string owner, decimal openingBalance) : base (owner, openingBalance) { } public override void Withdraw ( decimal amount) // specialises the shared rule instead of dup"},{"c":"oop","s":"virtual-override-and-new-three-keywords-that-look-similar-an","h":"virtual, override and new: three keywords that look similar and aren't","t":"# virtual, override and new: three keywords that look similar and aren't Intermediate Keyword On Meaning virtual Base method/property Derived classes may override abstract Method in abstract class No body; derived classes must override override Derived member Replaces the base implementation; dynamic dispatch sealed override Derived member Overrides and stops further overriding new Derived member Hides the base member; no polymorphism sealed Class Can't be inherited base.Method() Inside override Call the base implementation The last two rows of this table — override and new — are genuinely easy to confuse, because both let a derived class provide its own version of a method with the same name. They do fundamentally different things underneath, and the only way to really see the difference is to watch what each one actually looks up at the moment of a call: C# Run ▶ Copy class A { public virtual string Who () => \"A\" ; public string Plain () => \"A\" ; } class B : A { public override string Who () => \"B\" ; public new string Plain () => \"B\" ; } A x = new B (); x. Who (); // \"B\": override, decided by runtime type x. Plain (); // \"A\": new/hiding, decided by COMPILE-TIME type of x (( B )x). Plain (); // \"B\" Here's precisely what the diagram is showing, and it's worth internalising because it's the mechanical reason behind every \"override vs new\" interview answer: every object that has any virtual members carries, alongside its actual fields, a pointer to a method table — a lookup structure with one slot per virtual method, and that slot holds whichever override is correct for this object's real, runtime type . When you call x.Who() , the runtime doesn't look at what type the variable x was declared as — it follows the actual B object's own method table, finds the Who slot, sees it's been overridden to point at B.Who() , and jumps there. The compile-time type of x ( A ) never enters into it at all. That's what \"dynamic dispatch\" means, literally: the dispatch decision happens dynamically, at the call, based on the real object. Plain() tells the opposite story, because it was never virtual in the first place — there's no method-table slot for it at all. new doesn't override anything; it just declares a completely separate , unrelated method on B that happens to share a name with one on A . Which one actually gets called is decided the moment the code compiles, purely from the declared type of the variable — x.Plain() compiles straight into \"call A.Plain() \", full stop, because x was declared as A , and that decision is baked into the compiled code before the program ever runs. Change the variable's declared type to B (as the final line does, with an explicit cast) and the exact same object now resolves to B.Plain() instead — nothing about the object changed, only which door you called through. Why this indirection costs something, and why sealed can help A virtual call genuinely does more work than a direct one: read the object's method-table pointer, read the right slot from that table, then jump — one extra hop compared to jumping straight to a known address. That extra hop also blocks a common optimisation: the JIT can't inline a virtual call the way it inlines a direct one, because it doesn't know, in general, which concrete method will actually run until the object shows up at runtime — unless it can devirtualise the call. How code runs covers exactly how Dynamic PGO does this by guessing from observed behaviour; sealed , covered later in this chapter, does it unconditionally, because a sealed class guarantees there's only ever one possible method-table slot value to begin with. That's the real, mechanical reason \"mark it sealed for a small performance win\" is genuine advice and not folklore."},{"c":"oop","s":"interfaces-a-contract-with-no-implementation-of-its-own","h":"Interfaces: a contract with no implementation of its own","t":"# Interfaces: a contract with no implementation of its own Beginner An interface is a contract: a set of members a type promises to provide, with no say in how . Unlike base classes, a single class can implement as many interfaces as it needs . C# Run ▶ Copy public interface IOrderRepository { Task < Order ?> FindAsync ( int id, CancellationToken ct = default ); void Add ( Order order); } public interface IUnitOfWork { Task < int > SaveChangesAsync ( CancellationToken ct = default ); } public sealed class EfOrderRepository ( ShopDb db) : IOrderRepository , IUnitOfWork { public Task < Order ?> FindAsync ( int id, CancellationToken ct = default ) => db. Orders . FindAsync ([id], ct). AsTask (); public void Add ( Order order) => db. Orders . Add (order); public Task < int > SaveChangesAsync ( CancellationToken ct = default ) => db. SaveChangesAsync (ct); } # Three newer interface capabilities worth knowing Default interface methods (C# 8): an interface member can carry an actual body. This exists for one specific, narrow purpose — evolving a published interface without breaking everyone who already implements it — not as a general substitute for sharing implementation the way a base class does. Static abstract members (C# 11): interfaces can now require static members too, not just instance ones, which is specifically what makes generic math possible — T Sum<T>(T[] xs) where T : INumber<T> can call static operators like + on a type it only knows through a generic constraint. Explicit implementation : void IDisposable.Dispose() { } is written so it's only callable through the interface reference, not through the class directly. This resolves name clashes between two interfaces that happen to declare the same member name, or deliberately hides a member from a class's \"normal\" public surface."},{"c":"oop","s":"abstract-class-vs-interface-a-decision-not-just-a-vocabulary","h":"Abstract class vs interface: a decision, not just a vocabulary question","t":"# Abstract class vs interface: a decision, not just a vocabulary question Intermediate Abstract class Interface Multiple per type No (one base class) Yes Fields / state Yes No instance fields Constructors Yes No Access modifiers on members Any Public by default (others allowed since C# 8) Implementation Full Default methods only (limited) Relationship \"is-a\" family with shared code \"can-do\" capability Versioning Adding a non-abstract member is safe Adding a member breaks implementers (unless default) Value types Can't inherit Structs can implement Turn that table into a rule of thumb: reach for an interface to define a contract that genuinely unrelated types can fulfil — and especially at architectural seams you'll want to mock or swap later, like repositories, gateways, clocks. Reach for an abstract class when several closely related types share real, written implementation or state — a template method pattern, or a base Entity class that every domain entity inherits Id and domain-event handling from. The honest signal for which one you need: does the shared thing between these types include actual code and data, or only a promise about what methods exist?"},{"c":"oop","s":"access-modifiers-drawing-the-boundary-around-whats-yours-to-","h":"Access modifiers: drawing the boundary around what's yours to change","t":"# Access modifiers: drawing the boundary around what's yours to change Beginner Modifier Accessible from public Anywhere private The containing type only ( default for members ) protected Containing type and derived types internal Same assembly ( default for top-level types ) protected internal Same assembly or derived types private protected Derived types in the same assembly file (C# 11) The same source file (types only) internal specifically deserves more attention than it usually gets. It's the main tool C# gives you for keeping implementation details completely out of your library's public API surface — a type that's internal can be changed freely, any time, with zero risk of breaking anyone who merely consumes your library, because they could never have referenced it in the first place. [InternalsVisibleTo(\"Tests\")] punches one deliberate, narrow hole in that wall, letting a specific test assembly reach in to verify internals directly without making them part of the real public contract."},{"c":"oop","s":"static-sealed-partial-three-modifiers-three-different-jobs","h":"static, sealed, partial: three modifiers, three different jobs","t":"# static, sealed, partial: three modifiers, three different jobs Beginner static class : can't be instantiated and can't be inherited — it's just a named bag of static members, useful for stateless helpers and extension methods. Be deliberate about avoiding static mutable state specifically: because a static field is shared across every single request and every thread in the whole process, mutable static state becomes a hidden channel through which unrelated requests — and unrelated tests — can silently interfere with each other. sealed class : can't be inherited, full stop. The advice to seal by default unless you've actually designed a class for inheritance isn't just a style preference — it documents real intent (this class's internals were never written with overriding in mind, so don't rely on them), and, as the earlier \"Why this indirection costs something\" box showed, it gives the JIT a concrete reason to devirtualise calls into it. partial class/method : lets one logical type be physically split across several files. This exists specifically for tooling and source generators: a hand-written partial class AppJsonContext can sit in one file while a source generator emits the rest into another, and [LoggerMessage] partial void is exactly this pattern in miniature. C# 13 extended the same idea to partial properties ."},{"c":"oop","s":"composition-over-inheritance-why-is-a-chains-get-brittle","h":"Composition over inheritance: why \"is-a\" chains get brittle","t":"# Composition over inheritance: why \"is-a\" chains get brittle Intermediate Here's the problem inheritance creates once a hierarchy grows past a couple of levels: every derived class depends, often invisibly, on details of its base class's internals — a pattern with its own name, the fragile base class problem , where changing something deep in a base class can silently break derived classes nobody thought were affected. Push a hierarchy far enough and you also run into a combinatorial wall: C# Run ▶ Copy // Inheritance explosion class Notifier { } class EmailNotifier : Notifier { } class LoggingEmailNotifier : EmailNotifier { } class RetryingLoggingEmailNotifier : LoggingEmailNotifier { } // every combination needs a class Want retrying-but-not-logging? Logging-but-not-retrying? Each new combination of behaviours demands its own class in this scheme, and the number of classes grows explosively with the number of independent behaviours you're trying to mix and match. Composition — assembling behaviour from small, separately swappable collaborators instead of baking every combination into the inheritance tree — sidesteps the explosion entirely: C# Run ▶ Copy // Composition: wrap behaviours (Decorator pattern), assemble with DI public interface INotifier { Task SendAsync ( Message m); } public class EmailNotifier : INotifier { /* ... */ } public class LoggingNotifier ( INotifier inner, ILogger < LoggingNotifier > log) : INotifier { public async Task SendAsync ( Message m) { log. LogInformation ( \"Sending {Id}\" , m. Id ); await inner. SendAsync (m); } } Now \"retrying and logging\" is just two small wrappers composed together at the point you actually need that combination, with no new class required for it specifically — and \"just logging\" or \"just retrying\" are each available too, from the same two building blocks, mixed differently. Use inheritance where you genuinely have a stable, shallow \"is-a\" hierarchy — a couple of levels, not many. Reach for composition plus interfaces wherever behaviour needs to vary independently. This exact idea — wrapping one implementation inside another that shares its interface — is also precisely what the Decorator pattern formalises, and what the Strategy pattern does for swapping an entire algorithm rather than layering behaviour around one."},{"c":"oop","s":"every-types-hidden-base-class-system-object","h":"Every type's hidden base class: System.Object","t":"# Every type's hidden base class: System.Object Intermediate Every single type in C#, whether you declared it or the BCL did, ultimately derives from System.Object , which is where a handful of members you've probably used without a second thought actually come from: Member Notes Equals(object) / GetHashCode() Override together (see Type system ) ToString() Override for debugging and logging GetType() Runtime type (reflection entry point) MemberwiseClone() Protected shallow copy Finalize() Exposed as the ~ClassName() finalizer syntax An object becomes eligible for garbage collection the moment nothing can reach it any more — Memory & GC covers exactly how that reachability is determined. For anything that needs cleanup before the GC eventually gets around to it, though, the right tool is deterministic disposal via IDisposable , never a finalizer — see Exceptions & disposal for the full reasoning behind that rule. Q Explain the four pillars of OOP with C# examples. Encapsulation: private state behind methods that keep it valid ( AddAddress enforcing a max). Inheritance: CardPayment : PaymentMethod reusing base behaviour. Polymorphism: calling ChargeAsync on a PaymentMethod variable runs the derived override chosen at runtime. Abstraction: callers depend on IPaymentGateway without knowing which provider sits behind it. Q Abstract class vs interface: when would you use each? An interface defines a contract that unrelated types can implement, and a type can implement many. Use interfaces for seams you want to swap or mock. An abstract class is a single base for closely related types that share state or implementation (constructors, fields, protected helpers, template methods). Q What is the difference between override and new? override participates in virtual dispatch: the runtime type's implementation runs no matter what the variable's static type is. new hides the base member: which one runs depends on the compile-time type of the reference. Hiding is almost always a mistake. Q What is the order of initialisation when creating a derived class? Derived field initialisers, then base field initialisers, then the base constructor body, then the derived constructor body. Static constructors run once before first use of each type. Q Why shouldn't you call a virtual method from a constructor? Because the base constructor runs before the derived constructor. A virtual call would execute the derived override before the derived class's constructor has initialised its state. Q const vs readonly vs static readonly? const is a compile-time constant baked into calling assemblies (only primitives and strings). readonly instance fields are assigned once, in the declaration or constructor. static readonly is assigned once per type at runtime and is not copied into callers, so it's safe for values that may change between versions. Q What are default interface methods and when should you use them? Interface members with a body (C# 8). They let library authors add members to published interfaces without breaking existing implementations. They are not intended to replace abstract base classes for sharing implementation. Q Why prefer composition over inheritance? Inheritance couples subclasses to base-class internals and fixes behaviour at compile time, leading to fragile, deep hierarchies and class explosions. Composition assembles behaviour from small interchangeable parts (decorators, strategies) at runtime, typically via DI, and is easier to test and change. Q What does sealed do, and why seal classes by default? It prevents inheritance. Sealing documents that the class wasn't designed for extension, prevents fragile-base problems, and allows the JIT to devirtualise calls and speed up type checks. Q What's the difference between internal and private? private limits access to the containing type. internal limits access to the containing assembly. internal is the tool for hiding implementation types from consumers of a library or from other layers."},{"c":"oop","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Object-oriented programming (C#) Constructors (C#) — the exact, Microsoft-documented six-step order used above: fields zeroed, most-derived field initialisers, base field initialisers, base constructors (Object outward), this type's constructor, then object initialisers. Interfaces Static virtual interface members — the generic-math feature behind static abstract interface members. Framework Design Guidelines (also a book by Cwalina & Abrams)"},{"c":"oop","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter The const -baked-into-callers behaviour needs two separately compiled assemblies to show properly, so trust Further reading for that one. Everything else — construction order, the virtual-call-from-constructor trap, override vs new , explicit interface implementation, and all four pillars from Pico Mart's own code above — is directly observable in one process. Run with dotnet run (plain console app, .NET 8/9, no extra packages). C# Run ▶ Copy // Claim: derived field inits, then base field inits, then base ctor body, then derived ctor body. Shared . Log . Clear (); _ = new DerivedX (); var expected = new [] { \"derived field\" , \"base field\" , \"base ctor body\" , \"derived ctor body\" }; if (! Shared . Log . SequenceEqual (expected)) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected {string.Join(\" -> \", expected)}, got {string.Join(\" -> \", Shared.Log)}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: construction order is \" + string . Join ( \" -> \" , Shared . Log )); // Claim: calling a virtual method from a base constructor runs the derived override // while the derived class's own fields are still unset (the \"trap\"). var trap = new TrapDerived (); if (trap. SeenDuringBaseCtor != 0 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected the derived field to still read 0 (unset) during the base ctor's virtual call\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: the virtual call made during the base ctor saw SeenDuringBaseCtor == {trap.SeenDuringBaseCtor} (not yet initialised) — the trap is real\" ); // Claim: override dispatches on the object's runtime type; new/hiding resolves on the variable's declared type. A x = new B (); if (x. Who () != \"B\" ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: override should dispatch to B.Who() based on the real object\" ); if (x. Plain () != \"A\" ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: new/hiding should resolve to A.Plain() based on x's declared type\" ); if ((( B )x). Plain () != \"B\" ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: casting to B should reach B's own hidden Plain()\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: override (Who) dispatches by runtime type; new (Plain) resolves by declared type\" ); // Claim: explicit interface implementation is only reachable through the interface reference, not the class type. IDisposable asInterface = new Disposer (); asInterface. Dispose (); // only compiles through the interface reference Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: Dispose() is callable through the IDisposable reference (explicit implementation hides it on Disposer itself)\" ); // Claim (Encapsulation): a public field lets the \"max 5 addresses\" rule be silently bypassed; // a private field plus a validating method enforces it for real. var leaky = new LeakyCustomer (); for ( int i = 1 ; i <= 7 ; i++) leaky. Addresses . Add ( $\"Address #{i}\" ); if (leaky. Addresses . Count != 7 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected the leaky customer to accept all 7 addresses\" ); var safe = new CustomerX (); int accepted = 0 ; try { for ( int i = 1 ; i <= 7 ; i++) { safe. AddAddress ( $\"Address #{i}\" ); accepted++; } } catch ( InvalidOperationException ) { /* expected once the limit is hit */ } if (safe. Addresses . Count != 5 || accepted != 5 ) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected the encapsulated customer to stop at 5, got {safe.Addresses.Count}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: encapsulation — LeakyCustomer accepted {leaky.Addresses.Count} addresses, CustomerX enforced the limit at {safe.Addresses.Count}\" ); // Claim (Inheritance): SavingsAccount and CheckingAccount share Account's state/behaviour, // specialise Withdraw independently, and a SavingsAccount really \"is an\" Account. var savings = new SavingsAccountX ( \"Alice\" , 1000m , 0.02m ); savings. ApplyMonthlyInterest (); if (savings. Balance != 1020m ) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected interest to bring balance to 1020, got {savings.Balance}\" ); var checking = new CheckingAccountX ( \"Bob\" , 50m ); checking. Withdraw ( 120m ); if (checking. Balance != - 70m ) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected overdraft withdrawal to leave balance at -70, got {checkin"},{"c":"generics","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Generics let you write one class/method that works for any type, with the compiler still checking types at compile time instead of at runtime. Without generics, you'd store everything as object , which loses type safety and boxes value types (see Type system ). Constraints ( where T : ... ) tell the compiler what T is guaranteed to support, unlocking members you couldn't otherwise call. .NET generics are reified (real types at runtime, no boxing for value types) unlike Java, which erases them. Variance ( out T / in T ) lets IEnumerable<Cat> act as IEnumerable<Animal> safely — but only for interfaces/delegates that only read or only write T , never both. You already use generics constantly: ILogger<T> , IOptions<T> , DbSet<T> , Task<T> all lean on them. A warehouse story to hold onto Picture a warehouse that only has one size of crate, labelled \"contents: anything.\" Every item — a book, a bag of flour, a car battery — gets shoved into the same crate, so pulling an item back out means guessing what's inside before you can use it, and every book was wasted effort because it never needed that crate's padding in the first place. Generics are the warehouse switching to crates sized and labelled for what's actually going in: a Crate<Book> only ever holds books, the forklift (the compiler) refuses to load a car battery into one, and a book goes in without any extra padding at all. Keep that picture running through this chapter — \"the one-size-crate problem\" is ArrayList , and everything below is really about how C# builds better crates."},{"c":"generics","s":"the-problem-before-generics-existed","h":"The problem before generics existed","t":"# The problem before generics existed Beginner Before generics arrived in C# 1, if you wanted one reusable collection class that could hold any type, there was only one way to do it: store everything as object , since object is the one type every other type can be treated as. C# Run ▶ Copy var list = new ArrayList (); list. Add ( 42 ); // boxes list. Add ( \"oops\" ); // compiles: no type safety int n = ( int )list[ 0 ]; // cast + unbox needed; list[1] would throw at RUNTIME Look at what that costs you. list.Add(\"oops\") compiles without complaint right next to an int — ArrayList has no way to say \"only ints, please,\" because its Add method takes a plain object , and a string is just as valid an object as an int is. The mistake doesn't surface until (int)list[0] runs, and then only if you happen to touch the wrong element; list[1] would throw an InvalidCastException the moment anyone actually tried to read it back as an int. On top of the lost type safety, you also saw in Type system exactly what list.Add(42) costs: that int has to be boxed — heap-allocated — purely so it can fit through a parameter typed as object . Generics fix both problems at once, by letting the caller choose the type, and having the compiler remember and enforce that choice everywhere afterward: C# Run ▶ Copy var list = new List < int >(); list. Add ( 42 ); // no boxing list. Add ( \"oops\" ); // compile ERROR int n = list[ 0 ]; // no cast Same two lines, opposite outcome: list.Add(\"oops\") is now a compile error, not a runtime surprise, and list[0] comes back out already typed as int , with no cast and — as the next section explains properly — no boxing either. Three wins, all from the one idea of a type parameter : real type safety (caught at compile time, not discovered at runtime), real performance (no boxing, no casting), and genuine reuse (one implementation serving every type, instead of writing IntList , StringList , OrderList by hand)."},{"c":"generics","s":"writing-your-own-generic-types-and-methods","h":"Writing your own generic types and methods","t":"# Writing your own generic types and methods Beginner C# Run ▶ Copy // Generic class public class Result < T > { public T ? Value { get ; } public string ? Error { get ; } public bool IsSuccess => Error is null ; private Result ( T ? value , string ? error) { Value = value ; Error = error; } public static Result < T > Ok ( T value ) => new ( value , null ); public static Result < T > Fail ( string error) => new ( default , error); } // Generic interface with two type parameters public interface IRequestHandler < in TRequest , TResponse > { Task < TResponse > Handle ( TRequest request, CancellationToken ct); } // Generic method: type inferred from arguments public static T FirstOrFallback < T >( IEnumerable < T > items, T fallback) { foreach ( var item in items) return item; return fallback; } var x = FirstOrFallback ( new [] { 1 , 2 }, 0 ); // T inferred as int Notice the last line doesn't say FirstOrFallback<int>(...) anywhere — the compiler looks at the arguments you actually passed ( new[] { 1, 2 } and 0 ), works out that T must be int to make both fit, and fills it in silently. This is why you rarely see explicit type arguments on everyday generic method calls; the compiler almost always figures it out for you from context. Two small conventions worth knowing, because you'll see them everywhere: use T alone when there's a single type parameter with no real ambiguity about what it represents, and switch to a descriptive name — TKey , TValue , TRequest , TEntity — the moment there's more than one, so the parameter list actually reads like documentation. And default(T) (or the shorthand default ) is how you get a type-parameter-generic \"empty\" value: it resolves to null when T ends up being a reference type, and to all-zero-bits when T ends up being a value type — exactly the default-value rule from Type system , just expressed generically."},{"c":"generics","s":"constraints-telling-the-compiler-what-t-is-allowed-to-be","h":"Constraints: telling the compiler what T is allowed to be","t":"# Constraints: telling the compiler what T is allowed to be Intermediate With a bare T , the compiler doesn't know what type you'll plug in, so it only lets you call members that every type has — in practice, just the handful inherited from object . A constraint is a promise about T (\"it will always derive from Entity \", \"it will always implement IComparable<T> \"). Once you make that promise, the compiler trusts it and unlocks the matching members inside your generic code. Constraint Meaning where T : class Reference type ( class? allows nullable) where T : struct Non-nullable value type where T : notnull Non-nullable reference or value type where T : unmanaged Struct with no references (can be used with pointers/ stackalloc ) where T : new() Has a public parameterless constructor where T : BaseEntity Derives from a base class where T : IComparable<T> Implements an interface where T : U Derives from another type parameter where T : allows ref struct (C# 13) Permits Span<T> -like types as arguments C# Run ▶ Copy public abstract class Entity { public Guid Id { get ; protected set ; } = Guid . NewGuid (); } public class InMemoryRepository < T > where T : Entity { private readonly Dictionary < Guid , T > _items = new (); public void Add ( T item) => _items[item. Id ] = item; // .Id is usable because of the constraint public T ? Find ( Guid id) => _items. GetValueOrDefault (id); } public static T Max < T >( T a, T b) where T : IComparable < T > => a. CompareTo (b) >= 0 ? a : b; public static T Create < T >() where T : new () => new T (); Why does item.Id compile above? Because where T : Entity guarantees T is always an Entity (or something derived from it), so the compiler lets you touch Entity 's members on a bare T . Without that constraint, .Id wouldn't compile at all. Max<T> works the same way: a.CompareTo(b) only compiles because where T : IComparable<T> guarantees every T has a CompareTo method. Create<T> is a different kind of promise, about construction rather than members: plain new T() is illegal in generic code (the compiler can't assume T has a parameterless constructor), so where T : new() exists specifically to grant that."},{"c":"generics","s":"what-the-runtime-actually-does-with-a-generic-type","h":"What the runtime actually does with a generic type","t":"# What the runtime actually does with a generic type Advanced Does List<int> and List<string> still exist as two separate things once the program is actually running, or does that distinction disappear after compilation? This matters for performance, and .NET and Java answer it differently (see Further reading for the source on both). Java erases generics at compile time: at runtime, List<Cat> and List<Dog> both collapse into the same type-blind List . .NET's generics are reified instead: List<int> and List<string> stay genuinely distinct types at runtime, and typeof(T) inside generic code returns the real type it was called with. That split works differently depending on whether T is a value type or a reference type: Value types ( int , Guid , ...): the JIT compiles a separate, specialised copy of the method for each one — List<int> and List<Guid> each get their own machine code. That's why there's no boxing: List<int> 's code was built to hold raw int s directly, the same way hand-written IntList code would. Reference types : every reference is the same size (a pointer), so there's nothing to specialise — the JIT shares one compiled implementation across all of them, looking up type-specific details in a small hidden table only when needed. Back to the warehouse: a Crate<int> is like a crate built with int-shaped dividers baked right into its walls — the int sits directly in the slot, no wrapping needed. A Crate<string> is more like a crate that just holds a shelf of pointers to wherever the actual strings live elsewhere in the warehouse — one crate design serves every reference type, because a pointer is a pointer regardless of what it points to. One useful side effect: static fields are per closed type , not shared across instantiations. Cache<int>.Instance and Cache<string>.Instance are two unrelated fields. That gives you a simple, lock-free per-type cache: C# Run ▶ Copy static class TypeName < T > { public static readonly string Value = typeof ( T ). Name ; } // computed once per T TypeName<Order>.Value runs its field initializer once, caches \"Order\" , and never recomputes it. TypeName<Customer> has its own independent field, computed the first time it's touched."},{"c":"generics","s":"variance-when-is-listcat-actually-a-listanimal","h":"Variance: when is List<Cat> actually a List<Animal> ?","t":"# Variance: when is List<Cat> actually a List<Animal> ? Advanced If Cat inherits from Animal , is List<Cat> also a List<Animal> ? This trips up even experienced developers. The honest answer: it depends which interface you're asking about. First, see why List<Cat> itself can never safely be treated as a List<Animal> — watch what would go wrong if the compiler allowed it: C# Run ▶ Copy List < Cat > cats = new (); List < Animal > animals = cats; // suppose this compiled animals. Add ( new Dog ()); // now a Dog is in the List<Cat>! If that first assignment were legal, the second line lets you add a Dog through animals — but animals and cats are the same list in memory, so you'd have just put a Dog inside what's supposedly a List<Cat> . The next foreach (Cat c in cats) would throw, trying to treat that Dog as a Cat . This is why generic types are invariant by default: List<T> lets you both read and write T , and allowing this substitution would make writing unsafe. The danger comes from writing , not reading. So for an interface or delegate where T only ever flows one direction — only read out, or only accepted in, never both — the danger disappears, and C# lets you declare that one-directional promise explicitly: Kind Keyword Allowed when T is… Effect Examples Covariance out T only returned (output) I<Derived> → I<Base> IEnumerable<out T> , IReadOnlyList<out T> , IAsyncEnumerable<out T> (a sequence of values produced over time — see async/await ), Func<out TResult> — a delegate, a type representing \"a method with this signature\", covered in Delegates & events (not Task<T> : it's a class, so invariant) Contravariance in T only accepted (input) I<Base> → I<Derived> Action<in T> , IComparer<in T> , IEqualityComparer<in T> , Func<in T, …> (delegates, see Delegates & events ) C# Run ▶ Copy IEnumerable < Cat > cats = new List < Cat >(); IEnumerable < Animal > animals = cats; // ✔ covariant: you can only READ Animals out Action < Animal > feedAny = a => a. Feed (); Action < Cat > feedCat = feedAny; // ✔ contravariant: something that can feed any Animal can feed a Cat IComparer < Animal > byWeight = Comparer < Animal >. Create ((x, y) => x. Kg . CompareTo (y. Kg )); var sortedCats = cats. Order (byWeight); // ✔ an Animal comparer works for Cats Why is each one actually safe, not just permitted? IEnumerable<Cat> only ever hands Cat s out — there's no Add method — so treating it as IEnumerable<Animal> can never let a Dog sneak in; everything you pull out really is a Cat , and a Cat really is a valid Animal . Action<Animal> only ever accepts an Animal as input, never hands one back — so using it where an Action<Cat> is expected is equally safe: something that already knows how to handle any Animal obviously handles the more specific case of a Cat . Remember it with producers and consumers Out = producer. Something that only ever produces Cats is also, trivially, something that produces Animals — anyone expecting Animals back is perfectly happy receiving Cats specifically. In = consumer. Something that can consume any Animal can certainly consume a Cat, since a Cat always qualifies as an Animal. Interfaces that do both — produce and consume the same T , the way IList<T> does with its Add and its indexer — can't safely claim either direction, and have to stay invariant. Three rules, so you don't have to rediscover them by trial and error: Variance only applies to interfaces and delegates — never to classes like List<T> , which is why the first example in this section fails. It only applies to reference type arguments — IEnumerable<int> can never be an IEnumerable<object> , because that would require silent boxing, which C# variance doesn't do. Arrays are a historical exception: object[] o = new string[1]; o[0] = 1; compiles (arrays have been covariant in C# since before generics existed, for backward compatibility) but throws ArrayTypeMismatchException at runtime — because, unlike IEnumerable<T> , an array does let you write into it. Arrays t"},{"c":"generics","s":"generic-math-interfaces-that-require-static-members","h":"Generic math: interfaces that require static members","t":"# Generic math: interfaces that require static members Advanced C# Run ▶ Copy public static T Sum < T >( ReadOnlySpan < T > values) where T : INumber < T > { T total = T . Zero ; foreach ( var v in values) total += v; return total; } Sum < int >([ 1 , 2 , 3 ]); // 6 Sum < decimal >([ 1.5m , 2.5m ]); // 4.0 T.Zero and total += v are calls to static members ( Zero , the + operator) on a type the method only knows as a generic parameter. Before C# 11, constraints could only describe instance members, so this couldn't be written. Static abstract interface members (C# 11 / .NET 7) close that gap: Sum can now be written once and work for int , decimal , or anything else implementing INumber<T> , instead of a hand-written overload per numeric type. INumber<T> , IAdditionOperators<…> , IParsable<T> and friends live in System.Numerics . Most backend code never writes its own generic-math code, but you'll meet IParsable<T> when binding custom types from route/query parameters (see Building APIs )."},{"c":"generics","s":"spotting-generics-you-already-use-every-day","h":"Spotting generics you already use every day","t":"# Spotting generics you already use every day Intermediate Framework APIs lean on generics constantly, and recognising what role T is actually playing in each one takes most of the mystery out of reading unfamiliar code: API What T does ILogger<OrderService> T is only a category name for logs (the full type name) IOptions<PaymentOptions> Which options class to bind DbSet<Order> Which entity/table IValidator<CreateOrder> Which type to validate services.AddScoped<IRepo, Repo>() Interface → implementation mapping services.AddScoped(typeof(IRepository<>), typeof(EfRepository<>)) Open generic registration: any IRepository<X> resolves to EfRepository<X> Task<T> , ValueTask<T> Result type of async work IAsyncEnumerable<T> Async stream of T Results<Ok<T>, NotFound> Union of possible HTTP results for OpenAPI The ILogger<OrderService> row surprises people: T does nothing functional here — ILogger<T> never constructs or calls a T . It's purely a label, tagging every log line with OrderService 's full type name so you can filter logs by which class produced them. Same generic mechanism, used for a purpose that has nothing to do with type safety. The open-generic registration is worth a second look, since the syntax is unusual the first time you see it: C# Run ▶ Copy // Open generic registration: one line covers every entity type builder. Services . AddScoped ( typeof ( IRepository <>), typeof ( EfRepository <>)); IRepository<> — no type argument inside the brackets — is an open generic: a description of a whole family of types, not one specific closed type like IRepository<Order> . Registering it once lets the DI container (see Dependency injection ) build a matching EfRepository<X> for any IRepository<X> requested later, without a separate registration line per entity. Q What are generics and what problems do they solve? Type parameters that let you write classes, interfaces and methods once for any type. They give compile-time type safety, avoid boxing and casts (better performance), and enable reuse, unlike object-based code. Q What generic constraints are available? class, class?, struct, notnull, unmanaged, new(), a base class, an interface, another type parameter, default (for overrides), and allows ref struct (C# 13). Constraints let you use the members of the constrained type inside generic code. Q Explain covariance and contravariance. Covariance ( out T ) lets I<Derived> be used as I<Base> ; safe when T only appears in output positions (IEnumerable<out T>). Contravariance ( in T ) lets I<Base> be used as I<Derived> ; safe when T only appears in input positions (Action<in T>, IComparer<in T>). It applies to interfaces and delegates with reference-type arguments. Q Why isn't List assignable to List ? Because List<T> both reads and writes T. If it were allowed, you could add a Dog through the List<Animal> reference into what is actually a List<Cat>. Use IEnumerable<Animal> or IReadOnlyList<Animal> (covariant) if you only need to read. Q How are .NET generics different from Java generics? .NET generics are reified: closed generic types exist at runtime, typeof(T) works, and value-type instantiations get specialised code with no boxing. Java uses type erasure, so generic type info is gone at runtime and primitives must be boxed. Q What is an open generic registration in DI? Registering a generic type definition, e.g. AddScoped(typeof(IRepository<>), typeof(EfRepository<>)) , so the container can create any closed version (IRepository<Order>) on demand without registering each one. Q Are static fields shared across generic instantiations? No. Each closed generic type (Foo<int>, Foo<string>) has its own copy of static fields, which is useful for per-type caches."},{"c":"generics","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Generics in .NET Covariance and contravariance — confirmed against this page: variance applies only to generic interfaces/delegates with reference-type arguments, never to classes, never to value types. Generic math"},{"c":"generics","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Save this as Program.cs in a new console project ( dotnet new console ) and run it with dotnet run . It exercises type inference, constraints, reification, variance, array-covariance's runtime check, and generic math from this chapter; each line should print OK: . C# Run ▶ Copy using System . Numerics ; int failures = 0 ; void Check ( string claim, bool ok) { if (ok) Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: {claim}\" ); else { Console . WriteLine ( $\"FAIL: {claim}\" ); failures++; } } // 1. Type inference picks T from arguments static T FirstOrFallback < T >( IEnumerable < T > items, T fallback) { foreach ( var item in items) return item; return fallback; } var inferred = FirstOrFallback ( new [] { 1 , 2 }, 0 ); Check ( \"generic method type argument is inferred from the call\" , inferred == 1 ); // 2. Constraint unlocks members: InMemoryRepository<T> where T : Entity var repo = new InMemoryRepository < Order >(); var order = new Order (); repo. Add (order); Check ( \"where T : Entity lets generic code use T.Id\" , repo. Find (order. Id ) == order); // 3. where T : IComparable<T> Check ( \"Max<T> with IComparable constraint picks the larger value\" , Max ( 3 , 7 ) == 7 ); // 4. where T : new() var created = Create < Order >(); Check ( \"where T : new() allows new T() inside generic code\" , created is Order ); // 5. Reification: closed generic types are genuinely distinct at runtime, typeof(T) works Check ( \"typeof(T) inside a generic method returns the real closed type\" , typeof ( List < int >) != typeof ( List < string >) && GetTypeName < int >() == \"Int32\" && GetTypeName < string >() == \"String\" ); // 6. Static fields are per closed generic type, not shared Check ( \"static fields are independent per closed generic type (Cache<int> vs Cache<string>)\" , Cache < int >. Value != Cache < string >. Value ); // 7. Variance: IEnumerable<Cat> usable as IEnumerable<Animal> (covariance) IEnumerable < Cat > cats = new List < Cat > { new Cat () }; IEnumerable < Animal > animalsOut = cats; Check ( \"IEnumerable<out T> covariance: IEnumerable<Cat> assignable to IEnumerable<Animal>\" , animalsOut. Count () == 1 ); // 8. Variance: Action<Animal> usable as Action<Cat> (contravariance) int fedCount = 0 ; Action < Animal > feedAny = a => fedCount++; Action < Cat > feedCat = feedAny; feedCat( new Cat ()); Check ( \"Action<in T> contravariance: Action<Animal> assignable to Action<Cat>\" , fedCount == 1 ); // 9. Array covariance is a runtime-checked exception, not compile-time safe bool threw = false ; try { object [] arr = new string [ 1 ]; arr[ 0 ] = 1 ; // compiles, but... } catch ( ArrayTypeMismatchException ) { threw = true ; } Check ( \"writing a non-string into a string[] via object[] throws ArrayTypeMismatchException at runtime\" , threw); // 10. Generic math: one Sum<T> implementation works for int and decimal Check ( \"Sum<int> via INumber<T> generic math\" , Sum < int >([ 1 , 2 , 3 ]) == 6 ); Check ( \"Sum<decimal> via INumber<T> generic math\" , Sum < decimal >([ 1.5m , 2.5m ]) == 4.0m ); Console . WriteLine (failures == 0 ? \"\\nAll checks passed.\" : $\"\\n{failures} check(s) failed.\" ); if (failures > 0 ) Environment . Exit ( 1 ); static T Max < T >( T a, T b) where T : IComparable < T > => a. CompareTo (b) >= 0 ? a : b; static T Create < T >() where T : new () => new T (); static string GetTypeName < T >() => typeof ( T ). Name ; static T Sum < T >( ReadOnlySpan < T > values) where T : INumber < T > { T total = T . Zero ; foreach ( var v in values) total += v; return total; } abstract class Entity { public Guid Id { get ; protected set ; } = Guid . NewGuid (); } class Order : Entity { } class InMemoryRepository < T > where T : Entity { private readonly Dictionary < Guid , T > _items = new (); public void Add ( T item) => _items[item. Id ] = item; public T ? Find ( Guid id) => _items. GetValueOrDefault (id); } static class Cache < T > { public static readonly string Value = Guid . NewGuid (). ToString (); } class Animal { } class Cat : Animal { } "},{"c":"delegates-events","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR A delegate is a variable that holds a reference to a method, so you can pass behaviour around like data. Func<...> (returns a value) and Action<...> (returns nothing) are the built-in delegate types you'll use almost always, instead of declaring your own. A lambda ( x => x * x ) is just an inline, unnamed function that gets converted into a delegate. A closure happens when a lambda keeps using a local variable after its method has returned — the compiler keeps that variable alive on the heap, which costs an allocation and means captured variables are shared, not copied. An event is a delegate field with subscribing ( += / -= ) allowed from outside but invoking and overwriting restricted to the owner — the built-in Observer pattern. ASP.NET Core's whole pipeline ( Program.cs , middleware, minimal API endpoints) is just delegates passed around. A story to hold onto: the pricing rule you hand to the till Imagine a shop till that needs to apply \"whatever today's discount rule is\" to every sale. The till itself shouldn't need rewiring every time marketing invents a new sale — Black Friday today, a loyalty discount next month. Instead, someone hands the till a rule — a small, swappable slip of behaviour it just calls when it needs a price — and the till stays exactly the same while the rule underneath it changes freely. That slip of behaviour is a delegate. Keep this one picture running: a lambda is a rule written on the spot, a closure is a rule that remembers something from where it was written, an event is a noticeboard the till posts to instead of handing out a single slip, and Program.cs at the end of this chapter turns out to be built entirely from slips like this."},{"c":"delegates-events","s":"treating-a-method-like-a-value-you-can-pass-around","h":"Treating a method like a value you can pass around","t":"# Treating a method like a value you can pass around Beginner Everything in this chapter — lambdas, closures, events, and eventually the entire shape of Program.cs — grows out of one simple capability: being able to hold a reference to a method in a variable, the same way you'd hold a reference to a string or a number, and call it later without caring, at that moment, exactly which method it actually points to. C# Run ▶ Copy // 1. Declare a delegate type (a method signature with a name) public delegate decimal PriceRule ( decimal basePrice); // 2. Methods that match the signature static decimal BlackFriday ( decimal p) => p * 0.7m ; static decimal NoDiscount ( decimal p) => p; // 3. Use it like a variable PriceRule rule = BlackFriday ; decimal final = rule( 100m ); // 70; calls BlackFriday through the delegate Console . WriteLine (final); // 70 rule = NoDiscount ; // swap behaviour at runtime Console . WriteLine (rule( 100m )); // 100 — same call site, different behaviour now That's the whole snippet, runnable as-is: paste it into a new console project's Program.cs (with the delegate line and the two static methods above it) and dotnet run prints 70 then 100 — the same line, rule(100m) , producing a different result purely because rule itself changed in between. A delegate is what makes that possible: a type-safe object that points to a method, which you can store in a variable, pass as an argument, and invoke later — exactly as the rule variable does above. Notice that last line especially: rule gets reassigned from BlackFriday to NoDiscount at runtime, and whatever code calls rule(...) doesn't need to change at all to pick up the new behaviour. Why this capability matters so much It lets you pass behaviour around using the same mechanism you'd use to pass data. A sort algorithm doesn't need to know, hard-coded, how to compare two items — you hand it a comparison function, and it stays generic forever. A retry helper doesn't need to know what operation it's retrying — you hand it the operation as a value. A web framework doesn't need to know your specific endpoint logic — you hand it a function, and it calls that function when a request arrives. This single idea is the foundation underneath callbacks, LINQ, events, middleware, and — as the final section of this chapter shows directly — most of how modern ASP.NET Core is actually built."},{"c":"delegates-events","s":"func-action-and-predicate-delegates-you-never-have-to-declar","h":"Func, Action and Predicate: delegates you never have to declare yourself","t":"# Func, Action and Predicate: delegates you never have to declare yourself Beginner In practice, you'll almost never write public delegate ... ; the way the example above did. The BCL already ships generic delegate types covering nearly every signature you'd ever need: Delegate Signature Example Action () → void Action log = () => Console.WriteLine(\"hi\"); Action<T1..T16> (T…) → void Action<string> print = s => Console.WriteLine(s); Func<TResult> () → TResult Func<DateTime> now = () => DateTime.UtcNow; Func<T1..T16, TResult> (T…) → TResult (last type is the return) Func<int, int, int> add = (a, b) => a + b; Predicate<T> T → bool Predicate<Order> big = o => o.Total > 1000; Comparison<T> (T, T) → int list.Sort((a, b) => a.Price.CompareTo(b.Price)); EventHandler<TArgs> (object? sender, TArgs e) → void Events The pattern worth internalising: Action is \"does something, returns nothing\"; Func is \"does something, returns a value\" — and Func 's last type parameter is always the return type, with everything before it being the parameters. That convention lets a method like RetryAsync below stay completely ignorant of what operation it's actually retrying: C# Run ▶ Copy public static async Task < T > RetryAsync < T >( Func < Task < T >> operation, int attempts = 3 ) { for ( int i = 1 ; ; i++) { try { return await operation(); } catch when (i < attempts) { await Task . Delay ( 200 * i); } } } var user = await RetryAsync (() => api. GetUserAsync ( 42 )); // pass the \"what\" as a lambda RetryAsync has no idea it's fetching a user — it just knows it has some Func<Task<T>> to call, and retries it if it throws. That's exactly the \"pass behaviour, not just data\" idea from the :::why box above, made concrete. (The async / await / Task<T> in this snippet are about pausing work without blocking a thread — the dedicated Async/await chapter covers that; here they're just a method signature, no different from any other T you'd pass.)"},{"c":"delegates-events","s":"lambdas-writing-the-delegate-inline","h":"Lambdas: writing the delegate inline","t":"# Lambdas: writing the delegate inline Beginner A lambda expression is an inline, anonymous function — instead of declaring BlackFriday as a named method somewhere else and then referencing it, you write the function body right where you need it. The compiler converts a lambda into a delegate (or, in one specific situation covered later in this chapter, into an expression tree instead — see LINQ for exactly when and why). C# Run ▶ Copy Func < int , int > square = x => x * x; // expression lambda Func < int , int , int > add = (a, b) => a + b; Func < string , bool > isEmail = s => { var at = s. IndexOf ( '@' ); return at > 0 && at < s. Length - 1 ; }; // statement lambda Action noArgs = () => Console . WriteLine ( \"hi\" ); Func < int , int > typed = ( int x) => x + 1 ; // explicit parameter type var inferred = ( string s) => s. Length ; // C# 10: natural type → Func<string,int> Func < int , int > discard = _ => 42 ; // discard parameter var withDefault = ( int x = 10 ) => x * 2 ; // C# 12: default parameter values static int Helper ( int x) => x + 1 ; // local function (named, inside a method) One shortcut worth knowing, because it comes up constantly in real code: when a method you already have matches the delegate's signature exactly, you can pass the method group — just the method's name, no lambda wrapper at all — and the compiler builds the delegate for you: names.Select(int.Parse) , app.MapGet(\"/\", GetAll) . If you find yourself writing x => int.Parse(x) , that's almost always exactly equivalent to the shorter int.Parse ."},{"c":"delegates-events","s":"closures-when-a-lambda-outlives-the-method-it-was-written-in","h":"Closures: when a lambda outlives the method it was written in","t":"# Closures: when a lambda outlives the method it was written in Intermediate Here's a strange-sounding claim: a lambda can keep using a local variable from the method it was written in, even after that method has already returned . Watch it happen: C# Run ▶ Copy Func < int > MakeCounter () { int count = 0 ; // local variable... return () => ++count; // ...captured by the lambda } var next = MakeCounter (); next(); next(); Console . WriteLine (next()); // 3: 'count' survived after MakeCounter returned By the normal rule from Type system , count is a local variable, so it should live in a stack frame that's popped the instant MakeCounter returns. But MakeCounter has already returned by the time next() runs — so how does count survive? A lambda that uses a local variable from its enclosing scope is said to capture that variable. Here's how the compiler actually pulls that off: What the compiler actually generates for a captured local A captured local can't stay on the stack, so the compiler doesn't try. It rewrites the whole method, moving the captured variable into a hidden heap-allocated class made just for this purpose: C# Run ▶ Copy // roughly what MakeCounter becomes sealed class DisplayClass { public int count; public int Lambda () => ++count; } Func < int > MakeCounter () { var closure = new DisplayClass (); // heap allocation closure.count = 0 ; return closure. Lambda ; // delegate pointing at an instance method → another allocation } Once you see this generated code, three things stop looking arbitrary: capturing allocates — a heap object ( DisplayClass ) plus a delegate wrapping one of its methods, neither of which existed before. Captured variables are shared , not copied, by every lambda that captures them — they're all reading and writing the same field on the same object. And a captured variable lives exactly as long as the delegate holding it does, because it's now just a field on an ordinary heap object, kept alive by the same reachability rules as any other object (see Memory & GC ). Non-capturing lambdas pay none of this cost: with nothing to capture, the compiler caches one instance and reuses it, allocating nothing on repeated calls. To catch an accidental capture before it costs you anything, mark the lambda static ( static x => x * 2 ) — the compiler then refuses to compile it if it secretly captures something. # The loop-capture trap: a bug that only shows up because of exactly this mechanism C# Run ▶ Copy var actions = new List < Action >(); for ( int i = 0 ; i < 3 ; i++) actions. Add (() => Console . Write (i)); actions. ForEach (a => a()); // prints 333: all lambdas share ONE 'i' foreach ( var n in new [] { 0 , 1 , 2 }) actions. Add (() => Console . Write (n)); // prints 012: since C# 5 each foreach iteration gets a fresh variable The for loop prints 333 , not 012 , because closures share the variable , not a snapshot of its value. There's exactly one i for the whole loop, declared once in the for header, and all three lambdas capture a reference to that same field. By the time any of them actually runs, the loop has finished and i is already 3 — so all three print 3 . The foreach version avoids this because C# 5 changed the language to give each iteration its own fresh variable. Each lambda there captures a genuinely distinct n . If you're stuck with for and need foreach -style behavior, copy the loop variable into a fresh local inside the loop body ( var copy = i; ) before the lambda captures it. # Capturing something that gets disposed out from under you This causes a real production bug: capturing a scoped object (a DbContext , an HttpContext ) inside a lambda that outlives the request it came from — a background task, a cached delegate, a static event handler — means the lambda holds onto an object that gets disposed when the request ends, whether the lambda is done with it or not. The next time it runs, it touches a disposed object ( ObjectDisposedException ), or if nothing calls it again but nothi"},{"c":"delegates-events","s":"multicast-delegates-one-delegate-several-methods","h":"Multicast delegates: one delegate, several methods","t":"# Multicast delegates: one delegate, several methods Intermediate Delegates aren't limited to pointing at exactly one method — they can hold an entire list of methods, all with the same signature: C# Run ▶ Copy Action notify = () => Console . WriteLine ( \"email\" ); notify += () => Console . WriteLine ( \"sms\" ); notify(); // email, sms += appends a method to the delegate's internal list; -= removes one; invoking the delegate calls every method in that list, in order. Two sharp edges: for delegates with a return value, invoking a multicast delegate only gives you the last method's result — the earlier ones ran, but their return values are discarded. And if one handler throws partway through, the rest of the list never runs — the exception propagates immediately. One more useful fact: delegates are immutable . notify += ... doesn't modify the existing delegate — it creates a new delegate instance (old list plus the new method) and reassigns notify to it. That's what makes ?.Invoke(...) safe to call from multiple threads: another thread reassigning the variable to a new delegate can never corrupt an instance that's currently being invoked elsewhere, because that instance never changes once created."},{"c":"delegates-events","s":"events-a-delegate-with-the-dangerous-parts-removed","h":"Events: a delegate with the dangerous parts removed","t":"# Events: a delegate with the dangerous parts removed Intermediate An event is, underneath, just a delegate field — with deliberately restricted access bolted on. Outside code is only ever allowed to subscribe ( += ) or unsubscribe ( -= ); only the class that declared the event can actually raise it, or replace the whole subscriber list with = . This restricted shape is the entire Observer pattern, built directly into the language rather than something you assemble by hand. C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class OrderPlacedEventArgs ( int orderId, decimal total) : EventArgs { public int OrderId { get ; } = orderId; public decimal Total { get ; } = total; } public class Checkout { public event EventHandler < OrderPlacedEventArgs >? OrderPlaced ; // the event public void Complete ( Order o) { // ...save... OnOrderPlaced ( new OrderPlacedEventArgs (o. Id , o. Total )); } protected virtual void OnOrderPlaced ( OrderPlacedEventArgs e) => OrderPlaced ?. Invoke ( this , e); // raise } var checkout = new Checkout (); checkout. OrderPlaced += (sender, e) => Console . WriteLine ( $\"Order {e.OrderId} for {e.Total}\" ); // checkout.OrderPlaced = null; // ✘ compile error outside the class: that's the point of 'event' // checkout.OrderPlaced(...); // ✘ only the owner can raise it The two commented-out lines are the whole point of event : a plain delegate field would let any outside code overwrite the whole subscriber list with = (wiping out every other subscriber), or invoke it directly, pretending to be the publisher. event closes both doors while leaving += / -= open. Delegate field Event Outsiders can += / -= Yes Yes Outsiders can = (replace all) Yes No Outsiders can invoke Yes No Event subscriptions are a classic, silent memory leak The publisher's event field holds a reference to every subscriber through the delegate — that's just how += works (same reachability rule as Memory & GC ). If a long-lived publisher (a singleton, a static event) accumulates subscribers from short-lived objects that never unsubscribe, every one of them stays reachable forever through the publisher's field, so the GC can never collect them. Always -= in Dispose , or use a weak-event pattern when the subscriber's lifetime can't be tied to a clean unsubscribe point. # What \"events\" usually mean once you leave this one language feature C# event s are in-process and synchronous — the publisher calls subscribers directly, on its own thread, and waits for them to finish. That's a narrow fit for most backend work, so you'll rarely use the event keyword itself in API code. \"Event\" as a word , though, shows up constantly elsewhere in this book, meaning something related but mechanically different each time: Kind Mechanism Chapter C# event Delegate in the same process here Domain events Objects raised by aggregates, dispatched in-process after save DDD Integration events Messages published to a broker for other services Messaging Host lifetime events IHostApplicationLifetime.ApplicationStarted/Stopping Hosting Options change IOptionsMonitor<T>.OnChange callback Configuration Keep this table in mind so \"domain event\" and \"C# event \" don't blur together later — they share a name and a general spirit (something happened, tell interested parties) but almost nothing about their actual mechanics."},{"c":"delegates-events","s":"the-payoff-delegates-are-how-asp-net-core-is-actually-built","h":"The payoff: delegates are how ASP.NET Core is actually built","t":"# The payoff: delegates are how ASP.NET Core is actually built Intermediate Look at a typical Program.cs again, this time noticing every lambda: C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddDbContext < ShopDb >(options => options. UseSqlServer (cs)); // Action<DbContextOptionsBuilder> builder. Services . Configure < JwtOptions >(o => o. Issuer = \"shop\" ); // Action<JwtOptions> builder. Services . AddScoped < IClock >(sp => new SystemClock ()); // Func<IServiceProvider, IClock>: factory app. Use ( async (context, next) => { /* ... */ await next(context); }); // middleware: Func<HttpContext, RequestDelegate, Task> app. MapGet ( \"/orders/{id}\" , ( int id, IOrderService s) => s. GetAsync (id)); // endpoint handler: any delegate Every one of those lambdas is exactly the mechanism this chapter opened with: a method, held as a value, handed to someone else to call later. AddDbContext 's lambda is an Action<DbContextOptionsBuilder> — \"here's how to configure options; I don't care when you run this.\" AddScoped 's lambda is a Func<IServiceProvider, IClock> — a factory the container calls whenever it needs a new IClock . It's the same Func / Action vocabulary from the start of this chapter, not special configuration syntax. The core type the whole pipeline is built from makes this explicit: C# Run ▶ Copy public delegate Task RequestDelegate ( HttpContext context); A middleware (full coverage in Middleware ) is a function that takes the next RequestDelegate in the pipeline and returns a new RequestDelegate wrapping it — a Func<RequestDelegate, RequestDelegate> . The whole pipeline is built by composing a list of these, each wrapping the next. Once delegates click as \"methods held as values,\" app.Use(...) stops looking like framework magic."},{"c":"delegates-events","s":"expression-trees-vs-delegates-the-same-lambda-compiled-two-d","h":"Expression trees vs delegates: the same lambda, compiled two different ways","t":"# Expression trees vs delegates: the same lambda, compiled two different ways Advanced One loose thread from the \"Lambdas\" section: a lambda doesn't always compile into a delegate. Which one it becomes depends on what type you assign it to: C# Run ▶ Copy Func < Order , bool > asDelegate = o => o. Total > 100 ; // compiled IL: can be executed Expression < Func < Order , bool >> asTree = o => o. Total > 100 ; // a data structure describing the code asDelegate is ordinary, runnable IL — the delegate mechanism this chapter has covered. asTree , despite looking identical on the right, is categorically different: an object graph — made of types like BinaryExpression , MemberExpression , ConstantExpression — that describes the lambda's logic as data, instead of compiling it into code you can run directly. C# Run ▶ Copy Console . WriteLine (asTree. Body ); // (o.Total > 100) That's ToString() on a real object graph, printing back roughly the code it represents, not a hand-written string. This is what makes EF Core possible: IQueryable.Where takes an Expression<Func<T,bool>> , not a Func<T,bool> , so EF Core can inspect and translate that tree into SQL instead of running it as C#. IEnumerable.Where takes a plain Func<T,bool> , because LINQ to Objects just runs your predicate in-process — nothing to translate. LINQ walks through that translation, node by node. Q What is a delegate? A type-safe reference to a method (or a list of methods) with a specific signature. It lets you store behaviour in variables and pass it as arguments: callbacks, strategies, LINQ predicates, event handlers, middleware. Q Func vs Action vs Predicate? Func<…, TResult> returns a value (the last type argument is the return type). Action<…> returns void. Predicate<T> is a T → bool function, equivalent to Func<T, bool>, used by older APIs like List.FindAll. Q What is a closure and what does it cost? A lambda that captures variables from its enclosing scope. The compiler hoists captured variables into a heap-allocated class, so they outlive the method and are shared by every lambda that captures them. Each capturing lambda costs allocations (closure plus delegate), which matters in hot paths; static lambdas prevent accidental capture. Q Why does a for-loop capturing the loop variable print the same number? All lambdas capture the same single variable i , which has its final value by the time they run. foreach creates a new variable per iteration (since C# 5) so it's safe; in a for-loop, copy to a local inside the body. Q What is the difference between a delegate and an event? An event wraps a delegate so external code can only subscribe and unsubscribe. It can't invoke the event or overwrite the subscriber list with = . Only the declaring class can raise it. Q How can events cause memory leaks? The publisher's delegate holds strong references to subscribers. A long-lived publisher keeps short-lived subscribers alive if they never unsubscribe. Unsubscribe in Dispose, or use weak references. Q How does ASP.NET Core use delegates? The pipeline's core type is RequestDelegate (HttpContext → Task). Middleware are functions that wrap the next RequestDelegate; minimal API endpoints are arbitrary delegates whose parameters are bound from the request; configuration APIs take Action<TOptions> lambdas; DI supports Func<IServiceProvider, T> factories. Q What is the difference between Func<T,bool> and Expression<Func<T,bool>>? A Func is compiled code that can be invoked. An Expression is a data structure describing the code, which providers like EF Core can analyse and translate to another language such as SQL. You can compile an expression into a delegate with .Compile() , but not the other way round."},{"c":"delegates-events","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Delegates and events Lambda expressions Expression trees Delegate class — invocation order and return-value behavior : multicast delegates invoke their list in the order each was added, and a non-void delegate's invocation returns only the last subscriber's result."},{"c":"delegates-events","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Save as Program.cs in a new console project ( dotnet new console ) and run with dotnet run . It checks delegate reassignment, method-group conversion, closures (including the for-loop vs foreach capture trap), multicast delegate behavior, delegate immutability, event subscribe/unsubscribe, and expression trees vs delegates. C# Run ▶ Copy using System . Linq . Expressions ; int failures = 0 ; void Check ( string claim, bool ok) { if (ok) Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: {claim}\" ); else { Console . WriteLine ( $\"FAIL: {claim}\" ); failures++; } } // 1. Delegate can be reassigned to swap behavior at runtime PriceRule rule = BlackFriday ; decimal r1 = rule( 100m ); rule = NoDiscount ; decimal r2 = rule( 100m ); Check ( \"reassigning a delegate variable swaps behavior at runtime\" , r1 == 70m && r2 == 100m ); // 2. Method group conversion Func < string , int > parse = int . Parse ; Check ( \"a method group converts to a matching delegate type\" , parse( \"42\" ) == 42 ); // 3. Closure: a lambda keeps using a local after the method returns Func < int > counter = MakeCounter (); counter(); counter(); Check ( \"a closure keeps a captured local alive after its method returns\" , counter() == 3 ); // 4. Captured variables are shared, not copied: the classic for-loop trap var trapActions = new List < Action >(); var trapResults = new List < int >(); for ( int i = 0 ; i < 3 ; i++) trapActions. Add (() => trapResults. Add (i)); foreach ( var a in trapActions) a(); Check ( \"a for-loop capturing its loop variable shares ONE variable (all lambdas see the final value)\" , trapResults. Count == 3 && trapResults[ 0 ] == 3 && trapResults[ 1 ] == 3 && trapResults[ 2 ] == 3 ); var foreachResults = new List < int >(); var foreachActions = new List < Action >(); foreach ( var n in new [] { 0 , 1 , 2 }) foreachActions. Add (() => foreachResults. Add (n)); foreach ( var a in foreachActions) a(); Check ( \"a foreach loop gives each iteration its own variable (lambdas see distinct values)\" , foreachResults. SequenceEqual ( new [] { 0 , 1 , 2 })); // 5. Multicast delegate: invoking calls every subscriber, in order; return value is the LAST one var order = new List < string >(); Func < int > multi = () => { order. Add ( \"first\" ); return 1 ; }; multi += () => { order. Add ( \"second\" ); return 2 ; }; int multiResult = multi(); Check ( \"a multicast delegate invokes every subscriber in order\" , order. SequenceEqual ( new [] { \"first\" , \"second\" })); Check ( \"invoking a multicast delegate returns only the LAST subscriber's result\" , multiResult == 2 ); // 6. Delegate immutability: += creates a new delegate instance, doesn't mutate the old one Action original = () => { }; Action afterAdd = original; afterAdd += () => { }; Check ( \"delegates are immutable: += produces a new instance, the original is untouched\" , ! ReferenceEquals (original, afterAdd)); // 7. Event: outside code can subscribe/unsubscribe but not invoke or overwrite with '=' var checkout = new Checkout (); int notified = 0 ; EventHandler < int > handler = (s, total) => notified += total; checkout. OrderPlaced += handler; checkout. Complete ( 50 ); checkout. OrderPlaced -= handler; checkout. Complete ( 50 ); // handler removed, shouldn't fire again Check ( \"event subscription (+=) and unsubscription (-=) work from outside the class\" , notified == 50 ); // 8. Expression<Func<T,bool>> is data describing the lambda, not compiled code Expression < Func < int , bool >> asTree = x => x > 100 ; Func < int , bool > asDelegate = asTree. Compile (); Check ( \"an Expression<Func<T,bool>> can be inspected as a BinaryExpression body\" , asTree. Body is BinaryExpression ); Check ( \"an Expression can be compiled into a runnable delegate\" , asDelegate( 150 ) == true && asDelegate( 50 ) == false ); Console . WriteLine (failures == 0 ? \"\\nAll checks passed.\" : $\"\\n{failures} check(s) failed.\" ); if (failures > 0 ) Environment . Exit ( 1 ); static decimal BlackFriday ( decimal p) => p * 0.7m ; static decimal NoD"},{"c":"collections","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Each collection type trades away some capability for speed at one specific job — know what you need before picking one. List<T> = growable array, O(1) add on average but O(n) occasionally when it resizes (\"amortised O(1)\"). Dictionary<K,V> / HashSet<T> = O(1) average lookup via hashing, as long as GetHashCode spreads values out well. The biggest easy win: replace list.Contains(x) inside a loop with a HashSet<T> lookup — turns an O(n·m) scan into O(n+m). IReadOnlyList<T> only stops you from mutating through that reference; ImmutableList<T> / FrozenDictionary<K,V> mean nobody, ever, can change it. Plain collections aren't thread-safe for writes — use ConcurrentDictionary<K,V> , Channel<T> , etc. across threads, and prefer their atomic methods ( GetOrAdd ) over check-then-act. The warehouse Think of this chapter as stocking a warehouse. An array is a wall of fixed numbered shelves — fast to reach shelf #12, impossible to add a shelf #13 without rebuilding the wall. A dictionary is a wall of lockers with a combination (the key) instead of a number — you don't walk the aisle, you go straight to the locker. A queue is the loading dock: first crate in, first crate out. A stack is the single bin by the door you keep dropping returns onto, and always grab from the top. Keep that picture in mind — every type below is really just a different shape of storage room, built for one specific job."},{"c":"collections","s":"why-there-isnt-just-one-list-type","h":"Why there isn't just one \"list\" type","t":"# Why there isn't just one \"list\" type Beginner C# gives you a genuinely large number of collection types, and the reason isn't accidental sprawl — each one trades away some capability in exchange for being dramatically faster at one specific job. This chapter's real goal is to let you look at a problem (\"I need to check membership a million times a second\", \"I need to always grab the smallest item\") and immediately know which structure was built for exactly that. Before the specific types, though, there's a layer of interfaces worth understanding, because it quietly shapes how you should design your own method signatures too."},{"c":"collections","s":"the-interface-hierarchy-each-rung-promises-one-more-capabili","h":"The interface hierarchy: each rung promises one more capability","t":"# The interface hierarchy: each rung promises one more capability Beginner Diagram Copy IEnumerable<T> foreach only (GetEnumerator) ├─ IReadOnlyCollection<T> + Count │ ├─ IReadOnlyList<T> + this[int] (read) │ ├─ IReadOnlySet<T> + Contains, set comparisons │ └─ IReadOnlyDictionary<K,V>+ TryGetValue, Keys, Values └─ ICollection<T> + Count, Add, Remove, Clear, Contains ├─ IList<T> + this[int] get/set, Insert, RemoveAt, IndexOf ├─ ISet<T> + UnionWith, IntersectWith… └─ IDictionary<K,V> + this[key], Add(key, value), TryGetValue Read this ladder as a series of promises, each one stacking on the last: IEnumerable<T> promises nothing but \"you can walk through me once, in order.\" IReadOnlyCollection<T> adds \"and I know how many items I have.\" IReadOnlyList<T> adds \"and you can jump straight to any index.\" Every rung up the ladder is a stronger promise — which means every rung up is also a narrower set of types willing to make that promise. That asymmetry is exactly where the design rule comes from: accept the least capable interface you actually need; return the most useful honest type you actually have. C# Run ▶ Copy public decimal Total ( IEnumerable < OrderLine > lines) => lines. Sum (l => l. Price * l. Qty ); // only iterates public IReadOnlyList < Order > GetRecent () => _orders. Take ( 10 ). ToList (); // caller can index and count but not mutate Total only ever walks lines once, so it asks for nothing more than IEnumerable<T> — which means it can accept a List<OrderLine> , an array, a LINQ query, anything enumerable, without forcing the caller to materialise something more specific first. GetRecent , on the return side, genuinely does have a concrete, indexable, countable list sitting behind it — so returning IReadOnlyList<Order> honestly advertises that capability to the caller, while still refusing to promise mutation the caller has no business doing. An IEnumerable parameter can quietly run twice If a method takes IEnumerable<T> and loops over it twice — or calls Count() and then foreach s it — remember from LINQ that a lazy source doesn't hold a snapshot of results; it re-executes from scratch every single time it's enumerated. If that source is a database query, \"loop over it twice\" silently means \"hit the database twice.\" Either materialise once ( var list = items.ToList(); ) before touching it more than once, or accept IReadOnlyCollection<T> in your signature specifically when you need the count — which forces the caller to have already materialised it."},{"c":"collections","s":"the-core-collections-and-exactly-what-each-one-costs","h":"The core collections, and exactly what each one costs","t":"# The core collections, and exactly what each one costs Beginner Type Structure Index Search Add Insert/Remove middle Ordered? Use for T[] Contiguous block O(1) O(n) — (fixed size) — Insertion Fixed-size data, max performance List<T> Growable array O(1) O(n) O(1) amortised O(n) Insertion Default list Dictionary<K,V> Hash table — O(1) by key O(1) O(1) remove Not guaranteed Lookup by key HashSet<T> Hash table (keys only) — O(1) contains O(1) O(1) remove No Uniqueness, membership SortedDictionary<K,V> Red-black tree — O(log n) O(log n) O(log n) By key Sorted iteration with frequent inserts SortedList<K,V> Sorted arrays O(1) by index O(log n) O(n) O(n) By key Sorted, mostly read, low memory SortedSet<T> Red-black tree — O(log n) O(log n) O(log n) Sorted Sorted unique values, ranges ( GetViewBetween ) Queue<T> Circular array — — O(1) enqueue/dequeue — FIFO Work queues, BFS Stack<T> Array — — O(1) push/pop — LIFO Undo, DFS, parsing PriorityQueue<T,P> (.NET 6) Binary heap — — O(log n) O(log n) dequeue By priority Scheduling, Dijkstra, top-k LinkedList<T> Doubly linked O(n) O(n) O(1) at ends O(1) given a node Insertion Rare; LRU caches with a dictionary The single highest-leverage fix in this whole table list.Contains(x) inside a loop over another list is O(n·m) — for every item in the outer list, you scan the entire inner list looking for a match. Converting just the lookup list to a HashSet<T> turns that Contains call from a full scan into an O(1) average-case lookup, dropping the whole thing to O(n+m) . On a list of any real size, that's the difference between milliseconds and minutes. Interviewers ask about this specifically because it's such a common, easy-to-miss win in real code: C# Run ▶ Copy var blocked = new HashSet < string >(blockedEmails, StringComparer . OrdinalIgnoreCase ); var allowed = users. Where (u => !blocked. Contains (u. Email )). ToList ();"},{"c":"collections","s":"list-internals-what-amortised-o-1-actually-means","h":"List internals: what \"amortised O(1)\" actually means","t":"# List internals: what \"amortised O(1)\" actually means Intermediate The table above calls List<T>.Add \"O(1) amortised\" rather than plain O(1), and that qualifier is worth unpacking rather than skipping past, because it explains a real, controllable cost. Underneath, a List<T> is just an array ( _items ) plus a count ( _size ), with the array deliberately allocated a bit bigger than it currently needs to be. Most calls to Add are genuinely O(1): there's spare room in the array, so the new item just goes in the next free slot. But once that spare room runs out, Add has to allocate a brand-new array at double the previous capacity, and copy every single existing element across before it can add the new one — an O(n) operation, just for that one call. \"Amortised O(1)\" is the honest way to describe what happens if you average that occasional expensive copy across all the calls leading up to it: because the array doubles each time, the expensive copies become exponentially rarer as the list grows, and the total cost of all the copying, spread evenly across every Add call ever made, works out to a constant amount per call. It's not that every call is cheap — it's that the expensive ones are rare enough, and the doubling strategy specifically guarantees rare enough, that the average stays constant. Watch the four stages in that picture in order, because each one is a real, separate step the runtime actually performs, not just a conceptual simplification: the array starts full (step 1); a brand-new, bigger array gets allocated , completely empty, alongside the old one (step 2); every existing element gets copied across, one at a time (step 3); and only once that copying has finished does the new item actually get added (step 4). The old array is then simply abandoned for the garbage collector. Microsoft's own docs for List<T>.Add state this plainly: \"If Count already equals Capacity... the capacity... is increased by automatically reallocating the internal array, and the existing elements are copied to the new array before the new element is added... this method becomes an O(n) operation.\" C# Run ▶ Copy var list = new List < Order >(capacity: 10_000 ); // pre-size when you know the count: avoids ~14 resize-copies If you already know roughly how many items you're about to add, pre-sizing the list avoids paying for those resize-copies at all — each doubling from a default starting capacity up to 10,000 would otherwise cost you roughly 14 separate full-array copies along the way. A couple of related facts worth having at hand: Capacity is always ≥ Count (the array is at least as big as what's actually stored in it), and TrimExcess() shrinks the backing array back down if you've over-allocated. Insert(0, x) and RemoveAt(0) are each O(n), for a reason worth seeing clearly: inserting or removing at the front means every single element after that position has to physically shift over by one slot — there's no way around it for an array-backed structure. If you genuinely need fast operations at the front, that's precisely the signal to reach for Queue<T> instead. And CollectionsMarshal.AsSpan(list) gives you a zero-copy Span<T> view directly over the internal array, for hot loops — just don't add or remove from the list while you're holding that span, since a resize would invalidate it out from under you."},{"c":"collections","s":"dictionaryk-v-whats-actually-happening-inside-a-hash-lookup","h":"Dictionary<K,V>: what's actually happening inside a hash lookup","t":"# Dictionary<K,V>: what's actually happening inside a hash lookup Intermediate Dictionary<K,V> promises O(1) lookups — but why does hashing make that possible, when a list-based search is fundamentally O(n)? Walk through exactly what happens when you look up a key, step by step: Compute key.GetHashCode() — a number, derived from the key's contents. Map that hash to a bucket index: roughly hash % buckets.Length . Walk that one bucket's short chain of entries, comparing each against your key with Equals , until you find a match (or run out). The entire speed advantage over a list lives in step 2: instead of checking every single entry in the whole dictionary, you jump straight to one small bucket and only ever compare against the handful of entries that happen to share that bucket. Good hash distribution — hash codes spread evenly across the available buckets — keeps each bucket's chain short, which is what keeps step 3 fast. A genuinely terrible GetHashCode implementation (one that, say, always returns 1 ) would funnel every single entry into the same bucket, and step 3 would degrade into exactly the O(n) linear scan you were trying to avoid in the first place — hashing only helps if the hash function actually spreads things out. C# Run ▶ Copy var stock = new Dictionary < string , int >( StringComparer . OrdinalIgnoreCase ) { [ \"KB-01\" ] = 12 , [ \"MS-02\" ] = 0 }; if (stock. TryGetValue ( \"kb-01\" , out var qty)) { /* one lookup instead of ContainsKey + indexer */ } stock[ \"NEW\" ] = 5 ; // add or overwrite stock. Add ( \"KB-01\" , 1 ); // throws ArgumentException: duplicate key ref var slot = ref CollectionsMarshal . GetValueRefOrAddDefault (stock, \"KB-01\" , out _); slot++; // update in place with a single hash lookup (perf trick) Notice the comment on TryGetValue : calling ContainsKey followed by the indexer does the entire three-step lookup above twice — once to check existence, once to actually fetch the value. TryGetValue does the whole walk exactly once and hands you both pieces of information (found or not, and the value if so) from that single pass. Given how central Equals / GetHashCode are to steps 2 and 3, the requirements for a usable key follow directly: Equals and GetHashCode must agree with each other , and — critically — must not change while the object is sitting inside the dictionary, because the bucket it was placed in was chosen based on its hash code at insertion time ; if that hash code changes afterward, the dictionary will keep looking for it in the wrong bucket forever. This is exactly the mutable-hash-key trap from Type system , showing up again here with real consequences. Stick to immutable keys — strings, numbers, records, IDs — and the problem can't arise. Dictionary iteration order is an implementation detail, not a contract A Dictionary usually happens to iterate in insertion order — right up until you remove an item, at which point the freed slot gets reused by a later insertion, and the order you observe can shift in ways that have nothing to do with when anything was actually added. Never write code that depends on this ordering; it isn't part of the type's actual contract, only an accident of the current implementation. If you genuinely need order, reach for SortedDictionary , the newer generic OrderedDictionary<K,V> (.NET 9), or sort explicitly at the point you need it."},{"c":"collections","s":"choosing-a-collection-a-decision-guide","h":"Choosing a collection: a decision guide","t":"# Choosing a collection: a decision guide Beginner Diagram Copy Need lookup by key? → Dictionary<K,V> (sorted keys → SortedDictionary) Need \"have I seen this?\" → HashSet<T> Need ordered list, index access? → List<T> (fixed size & hot path → T[]) Need first-in-first-out? → Queue<T> (multi-threaded → Channel<T> / ConcurrentQueue<T>) Need last-in-first-out? → Stack<T> Need \"always take the smallest\"? → PriorityQueue<T,P> Shared across threads? → Concurrent* (or lock around a normal one) Built once, read millions of times? → FrozenDictionary / FrozenSet Must never change after creation? → Immutable* (or IReadOnly* views) Exposing from a class? → IReadOnlyList<T> / IReadOnlyDictionary<K,V>"},{"c":"collections","s":"read-only-immutable-and-frozen-three-guarantees-people-confl","h":"Read-only, immutable and frozen: three guarantees people conflate","t":"# Read-only, immutable and frozen: three guarantees people conflate Intermediate These three sound like synonyms and genuinely aren't — each one makes a different, specific promise, and mixing them up leads to code that's less safe than it looks: Can the holder modify it? Can anyone modify it? Cost IReadOnlyList<T> (interface) No (via that reference) Yes : the underlying List can still change Free (it's a view) ReadOnlyCollection<T> ( list.AsReadOnly() ) No Yes, the wrapped list can still change A thin wrapper ImmutableList<T> , ImmutableDictionary<K,V> No; \"mutations\" return a new collection, sharing structure No , never Tree-based: slower reads, efficient copies ImmutableArray<T> No No Array-backed: fast reads, full copy on change FrozenDictionary<K,V> / FrozenSet<T> (.NET 8) No No Expensive to create, fastest possible reads The distinction worth really sitting with is between the first two rows and the last three. IReadOnlyList<T> and ReadOnlyCollection<T> only ever restrict what you can do through that particular reference — somebody else, holding the original mutable List<T> the view was built from, can still change it out from under you, and you'd see that change the next time you read through your supposedly \"read-only\" view. ImmutableList<T> , ImmutableArray<T> and FrozenDictionary<K,V> make a fundamentally stronger promise: the data genuinely cannot change, by anyone, ever, after creation — full stop. That's why FrozenDictionary specifically exists: it accepts an expensive, slower build step in exchange for the fastest possible reads afterward, which is exactly the right trade for something built once at startup and then read on every single request for the rest of the process's life: C# Run ▶ Copy // Built once at startup, read on every request private static readonly FrozenDictionary < string , decimal > TaxRates = new Dictionary < string , decimal > { [ \"DE\" ] = 0.19m , [ \"FR\" ] = 0.20m , [ \"NL\" ] = 0.21m } . ToFrozenDictionary ( StringComparer . OrdinalIgnoreCase );"},{"c":"collections","s":"concurrent-collections-for-when-more-than-one-thread-touches","h":"Concurrent collections: for when more than one thread touches the same collection","t":"# Concurrent collections: for when more than one thread touches the same collection Intermediate Here's a fact worth stating plainly, because it surprises people who assume \"it's a BCL type, it must be safe\": ordinary collections are not thread-safe for writes . Two threads calling Add on the same plain Dictionary at the same moment can genuinely corrupt its internal structure — historically, this specific race has even caused infinite loops inside the dictionary's own bucket-chasing logic. For state shared across threads — singletons, background workers — reach for the collections built specifically to handle this: Type Use ConcurrentDictionary<K,V> Thread-safe map; GetOrAdd , AddOrUpdate , TryRemove ConcurrentQueue<T> / ConcurrentStack<T> Lock-free FIFO/LIFO ConcurrentBag<T> Unordered; optimised for the same thread adding and taking BlockingCollection<T> Bounded producer/consumer with blocking (older; prefer Channel<T> ) Channel<T> (System.Threading.Channels) Async producer/consumer queue; the modern choice C# Run ▶ Copy private readonly ConcurrentDictionary < string , Lazy < Task < Config >>> _cache = new (); public Task < Config > GetAsync ( string tenant) => _cache. GetOrAdd (tenant, t => new Lazy < Task < Config >>(() => LoadConfigAsync (t))). Value ; // Lazy ensures the loader runs once even if two threads race into GetOrAdd Thread-safe methods don't make compound operations thread-safe if (!dict.ContainsKey(k)) dict[k] = v; is exactly the check-then-act race condition from Threading & concurrency — two separate operations, with a window between them for another thread to slip in, even though ContainsKey and the indexer are each individually safe to call. Use the genuinely atomic combined operations instead — GetOrAdd / TryAdd / AddOrUpdate — which perform the whole check-and-act as one indivisible step. One more sharp edge specific to GetOrAdd : under contention, its value factory may run more than once , even though only one of those results ever actually gets stored — which is exactly why the example above wraps the expensive load in Lazy<T> rather than calling LoadConfigAsync directly inside the factory."},{"c":"collections","s":"arrays-spans-and-collection-expressions","h":"Arrays, spans and collection expressions","t":"# Arrays, spans and collection expressions Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy int [] a = [ 1 , 2 , 3 ]; // C# 12 collection expression List < string > names = [ \"ana\" , \"bo\" ]; int [] combined = [..a, 4 , 5 ]; // spread operator Span < int > slice = a. AsSpan ( 1 , 2 ); // view of {2, 3}, no copy int last = a[^ 1 ]; // index from end: 3 int [] middle = a[ 1 ..^ 1 ]; // range: creates a NEW array {2} int [,] grid = new int [ 3 , 3 ]; // multi-dimensional (rectangular) int [][] jagged = [[ 1 ], [ 2 , 3 ]]; // array of arrays Two of those lines look similar but behave completely differently, and it's worth being precise about which: a.AsSpan(1, 2) is a genuine zero-copy view into the original array — mutate through the span and you mutate a itself. a[1..^1] , the range syntax, instead allocates and returns a brand-new array containing copies of the selected elements — it looks like slicing syntax, but it isn't a view at all. A few more facts worth having on hand: arrays are reference types with a genuinely fixed length — you can't resize one, only replace it with a different, differently-sized array. Array covariance (an object[] variable pointing at a real string[] ) is permitted but unsafe for exactly the reason covered in Generics : object[] o = new string[1]; o[0] = 1; compiles, but throws ArrayTypeMismatchException at runtime, because arrays trade compile-time safety for a runtime check here. And Array.Empty<T>() (or the [] collection expression) gives you a cached, shared empty array at zero allocation cost — a genuinely good reason to never return null where an empty collection would do: callers can safely foreach over an empty collection with no special-case null check, but a null forces one everywhere it might appear."},{"c":"collections","s":"equality-comparers-and-custom-ordering","h":"Equality comparers and custom ordering","t":"# Equality comparers and custom ordering Advanced Hash-based and sorted collections both let you plug in your own comparison logic, rather than relying on a type's default Equals / GetHashCode or CompareTo : C# Run ▶ Copy var byEmail = new HashSet < Customer >( EqualityComparer < Customer >. Create ( (a, b) => string . Equals (a?. Email , b?. Email , StringComparison . OrdinalIgnoreCase ), c => StringComparer . OrdinalIgnoreCase . GetHashCode (c. Email ))); // .NET 8 helper var byPriceThenName = Comparer < Product >. Create ((x, y) => x. Price != y. Price ? x. Price . CompareTo (y. Price ) : string . CompareOrdinal (x. Name , y. Name )); var sorted = new SortedSet < Product >(products, byPriceThenName); This matters specifically when a type's default notion of equality or ordering doesn't match what a particular collection actually needs — Customer might not override Equals at all, but you can still build a HashSet<Customer> that treats two customers as the same whenever their emails match, without touching the Customer class itself. Q What is the time complexity of List.Add, Dictionary lookup and HashSet.Contains? List.Add is O(1) amortised: occasionally O(n) when the backing array doubles and is copied. Dictionary lookup and HashSet.Contains are O(1) on average, assuming a good hash function; worst case O(n) when many keys collide. Q How does a Dictionary work internally? It hashes the key with GetHashCode, maps the hash to a bucket index, and walks that bucket's chain of entries, comparing with Equals. It resizes (to a larger prime-sized capacity) as it grows. That's why keys need consistent Equals/GetHashCode and must not mutate. Q IEnumerable vs ICollection vs IList? IEnumerable only supports iteration. ICollection adds Count, Add, Remove, Contains. IList adds index-based access, Insert and RemoveAt. Accept the least capable interface a method needs. Q What's the difference between IReadOnlyList, ReadOnlyCollection and ImmutableList? IReadOnlyList is an interface: a read-only view, but the underlying list may still change. ReadOnlyCollection is a wrapper that prevents changes through it, while the wrapped list can still change. ImmutableList can never change; modifications return a new list that shares structure with the old one. Q When would you use FrozenDictionary? For lookup tables built once (often at startup) and read very frequently, such as config maps, routing tables or tax rates. Creation is slower because it analyses the keys to pick the optimal layout, but reads are faster than Dictionary. Q How do you make a Dictionary thread-safe? Use ConcurrentDictionary with its atomic methods (GetOrAdd, AddOrUpdate, TryRemove), or protect a normal Dictionary with a lock. For read-mostly data, swap an immutable/frozen dictionary reference atomically. Q Is ConcurrentDictionary.GetOrAdd's factory guaranteed to run once? No. Under contention it can run more than once (only one result is stored). Wrap the value in Lazy<T> if the factory is expensive or has side effects. Q How would you remove duplicates from a list efficiently? list.Distinct() or new HashSet<T>(list) (O(n)), with a custom IEqualityComparer if needed, or DistinctBy(x => x.Key) (.NET 6) to dedupe by a property."},{"c":"collections","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Collections and data structures Thread-safe collections System.Collections.Frozen List<T>.Add(T) Method — the O(1)-unless-resizing remark quoted above HashHelpers.cs (source.dot.net) — the actual prime-sized bucket table Dictionary / HashSet resize into"},{"c":"collections","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run (plain console app, .NET 8/9, no packages). It checks the concrete, checkable claims: Dictionary / HashSet are O(1)-average vs. list Contains being O(n), TryGetValue finding what ContainsKey found, ConcurrentDictionary.GetOrAdd 's factory possibly running more than once under contention, and AsSpan being a view while range slicing [..] copies. C# Run ▶ Copy using System . Diagnostics ; using System . Collections . Concurrent ; // --- HashSet/Dictionary lookup is dramatically faster than List.Contains at scale --- var n = 200_000 ; var list = Enumerable . Range ( 0 , n). ToList (); var set = new HashSet < int >(list); var target = n - 1 ; // worst case for a linear scan: the last element var swList = Stopwatch . StartNew (); for ( int i = 0 ; i < 50 ; i++) list. Contains (target); swList. Stop (); var swSet = Stopwatch . StartNew (); for ( int i = 0 ; i < 50 ; i++) set . Contains (target); swSet. Stop (); if (swSet. Elapsed >= swList. Elapsed ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: HashSet.Contains should be far faster than List.Contains here\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: HashSet.Contains ({swSet.ElapsedMilliseconds} ms) was faster than List.Contains ({swList.ElapsedMilliseconds} ms) for {50} lookups over {n} items\" ); // --- TryGetValue finds exactly what ContainsKey says is there, in one pass --- var stock = new Dictionary < string , int > { [ \"KB-01\" ] = 12 }; if (!stock. ContainsKey ( \"KB-01\" ) || !stock. TryGetValue ( \"KB-01\" , out var qty) || qty != 12 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: TryGetValue should agree with ContainsKey\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: TryGetValue retrieves the same value ContainsKey confirms exists, in a single lookup\" ); // --- Dictionary.Add throws on a duplicate key; the indexer overwrites instead --- try { stock. Add ( \"KB-01\" , 1 ); throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: Add should throw on a duplicate key\" ); } catch ( ArgumentException ) { Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: Dictionary.Add throws ArgumentException on a duplicate key\" ); } stock[ \"KB-01\" ] = 1 ; // the indexer is fine with overwriting if (stock[ \"KB-01\" ] != 1 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: indexer assignment should overwrite\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: the indexer overwrites an existing key instead of throwing\" ); // --- ConcurrentDictionary.GetOrAdd's factory can run more than once under contention --- var factoryCalls = 0 ; var cd = new ConcurrentDictionary < int , int >(); Parallel . For ( 0 , 50 , _ => cd. GetOrAdd ( 1 , _ => { Interlocked . Increment ( ref factoryCalls); return 42 ; })); Console . WriteLine (factoryCalls > 1 ? $\"OK: GetOrAdd's factory ran {factoryCalls} times under contention, as the chapter warns (only one result is stored: {cd[1]})\" : \"OK: GetOrAdd's factory ran once this time (the race is possible, not guaranteed, on every run)\" ); // --- AsSpan is a view (mutating it mutates the array); range slicing [..] copies --- int [] arr = [ 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 ]; var span = arr. AsSpan ( 1 , 2 ); span[ 0 ] = 99 ; if (arr[ 1 ] != 99 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: AsSpan should be a view over the original array\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: Span<T> from AsSpan is a zero-copy view — writing through it changed the original array\" ); int [] middle = arr[ 1 ..^ 1 ]; middle[ 0 ] = - 1 ; if (arr[ 1 ] == - 1 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: range slicing should allocate a new array, not share storage\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: range slicing (arr[1..^1]) copies into a new array — changing it left the original untouched\" ); // Not runtime-verifiable here — see Further reading: Microsoft \"Collections and data structures\" docs // (Big-O costs for SortedDictionary/PriorityQueue/LinkedList etc. are documented guarantees, not something // worth timing in a short-running demo, since constant factors and JIT warm-up dominate at small n)"},{"c":"linq","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR LINQ is just extension methods ( Where , Select , ...) that work on anything implementing IEnumerable<T> or IQueryable<T> — no compiler magic. Most operators are deferred : writing a query runs nothing; it only executes when something enumerates it ( foreach , ToList() , Count() ). Enumerate twice and it runs twice, against whatever the source looks like now . yield return is the mechanism that makes deferred execution possible: it turns a method into a pausable/resumable state machine. IEnumerable<T> runs in your process with compiled delegates; IQueryable<T> (EF Core, etc.) captures the query as an expression tree and translates it to SQL — switching to IEnumerable too early silently pulls the whole table into memory first. Prefer Any() over Count() > 0 , and MinBy / MaxBy over OrderBy(...).First() — both avoid unnecessary full scans/sorts. The restaurant order ticket A LINQ query is a ticket, not a meal. Writing orders.Where(...).Select(...) is like a waiter writing \"table 4: no onions, extra sauce\" on a slip of paper — nothing gets cooked yet. The kitchen only fires the ticket when someone actually calls for it ( foreach , ToList() , Count() ). And here's the part that trips people up: if the waiter writes a second ticket calling for the same order, the kitchen cooks it all over again from raw ingredients — it doesn't remember the first plate. Keep that picture handy for the \"deferred execution\" section below; it's the same idea with real consequences."},{"c":"linq","s":"what-linq-actually-is-underneath-the-syntax","h":"What LINQ actually is, underneath the syntax","t":"# What LINQ actually is, underneath the syntax Beginner LINQ is the set of query operators you already use constantly — Where , Select , OrderBy , GroupBy , Join , Sum , and dozens more — and the thing that makes them special is that they work on any sequence : an in-memory list, a database table, XML, JSON, anything that implements IEnumerable<T> or IQueryable<T> . Same vocabulary, wildly different data sources. Here's the part worth knowing before anything else: LINQ isn't a special language feature baked into the compiler the way if or for are. It's just extension methods — Where lives in System.Linq.Enumerable , nothing more exotic than that — plus some optional query syntax the language layers on top for readability. Once you know that, a sentence like \"LINQ operators chain together\" stops being magic and starts being exactly what it looks like: one method call returning something you call the next method on. C# Run ▶ Copy var bigSpenders = customers . Where (c => c. Country == \"DE\" ) . Select (c => new { c. Name , Total = c. Orders . Sum (o => o. Total ) }) . Where (x => x. Total > 1_000 ) . OrderByDescending (x => x. Total ) . Take ( 10 ) . ToList (); Why LINQ exists Before LINQ, filtering, projecting and grouping meant hand-written foreach loops building up temporary lists and dictionaries by hand — and database queries were raw SQL strings the compiler had no way to check. LINQ replaces both of those with one declarative vocabulary that works the same way regardless of the data source, checked by the compiler at compile time, and — this is the part that becomes a whole section later in this chapter — lets providers like EF Core translate that same query into SQL instead of running it in memory."},{"c":"linq","s":"two-ways-to-write-the-same-query","h":"Two ways to write the same query","t":"# Two ways to write the same query Beginner C# Run ▶ Copy // Query syntax: compiles to exactly the method calls below var q1 = from o in orders where o. Status == OrderStatus . Paid orderby o. CreatedAt descending select new { o. Id , o. Total }; // Method syntax var q2 = orders . Where (o => o. Status == OrderStatus . Paid ) . OrderByDescending (o => o. CreatedAt ) . Select (o => new { o. Id , o. Total }); These two produce identical compiled code — q1 and q2 aren't two different features, just two different spellings. Method syntax is far more common in code you'll actually see day to day. Query syntax earns its keep specifically for joins and let (naming an intermediate value partway through a query), where it reads noticeably cleaner than the method-chain equivalent. Worth knowing too: plenty of operators — Take , Distinct , Any , Sum among them — only exist as methods at all; there's no query-syntax spelling for them."},{"c":"linq","s":"the-operators-organised-by-what-they-do","h":"The operators, organised by what they do","t":"# The operators, organised by what they do Beginner Category Operators Filtering Where , OfType<T> Projection Select , SelectMany , Select((x, i) => …) with index Sorting OrderBy , OrderByDescending , ThenBy , ThenByDescending , Order (.NET 7), Reverse Partitioning Take , Skip , TakeWhile , SkipWhile , TakeLast , SkipLast , Chunk (.NET 6) Set Distinct , DistinctBy , Union , UnionBy , Intersect , IntersectBy , Except , ExceptBy Grouping GroupBy , ToLookup , CountBy , AggregateBy (.NET 9) Joining Join (inner), GroupJoin , LeftJoin / RightJoin (.NET 10), Zip Element First , FirstOrDefault , Single , SingleOrDefault , Last , ElementAt , MinBy , MaxBy Quantifiers Any , All , Contains Aggregation Count , LongCount , Sum , Average , Min , Max , Aggregate Generation Enumerable.Range , Repeat , Empty Conversion ToList , ToArray , ToDictionary , ToHashSet , ToLookup , AsEnumerable , AsQueryable , Cast<T> Other Concat , Append , Prepend , DefaultIfEmpty , SequenceEqual , Index (.NET 9) # First vs FirstOrDefault vs Single vs SingleOrDefault — four operators, one decision These four get mixed up constantly, and the fix is a single table showing exactly where they diverge: Operator 0 elements 1 element 2+ elements First() throws returns it returns first FirstOrDefault() default returns it returns first Single() throws returns it throws SingleOrDefault() default returns it throws Notice the real distinction isn't \"First vs Single\" so much as what each one is asserting about the data. First / FirstOrDefault make no claim about how many elements exist — they just want whichever one comes first, which is exactly right after an OrderBy , or for \"give me any match\". Single / SingleOrDefault are stricter: they're asserting \"there should be exactly one \", and throw loudly if that assertion turns out to be false — which is exactly the behaviour you want for a lookup by a unique key, where finding two rows means your data has a bug, not that you should silently pick one. FirstOrDefault(predicate, defaultValue) (.NET 6) additionally lets you pick what \"not found\" actually returns, instead of always falling back to default . # SelectMany: turning a sequence of sequences into one sequence C# Run ▶ Copy var allLines = orders. SelectMany (o => o. Lines ); // IEnumerable<OrderLine> var pairs = orders. SelectMany (o => o. Lines , (o, l) => new { o. Id , l. Sku }); // keep the parent Select alone would give you a sequence of orders, each still holding its own list of lines — a sequence of sequences. SelectMany flattens that one level: instead of \"ten orders, each with some lines\", you get \"every line from every order, as one flat list\". The second overload above is worth remembering specifically because it solves a problem the first one creates: once you've flattened down to lines, how do you still know which order each line came from? The two-argument overload keeps the parent ( o ) available alongside each flattened child ( l ). # GroupBy and lookups C# Run ▶ Copy var revenueByCountry = orders . GroupBy (o => o. Country ) . Select (g => new { Country = g. Key , Orders = g. Count (), Revenue = g. Sum (o => o. Total ) }) . OrderByDescending (x => x. Revenue ); var countsByStatus = orders. CountBy (o => o. Status ); // .NET 9: IEnumerable<KeyValuePair<Status,int>> ILookup < int , Order > byCustomer = orders. ToLookup (o => o. CustomerId ); // immediate, multi-value dictionary var forCustomer42 = byCustomer[ 42 ]; // empty sequence if none, never throws ToLookup is worth a specific mention because its behaviour quietly saves you a whole class of null-check and ContainsKey code: unlike a Dictionary , indexing an ILookup with a key that doesn't exist returns an empty sequence , not an exception and not null . byCustomer[42] is always safe to foreach over, whether or not customer 42 has any orders. # Joins C# Run ▶ Copy var rows = from o in orders join c in customers on o. CustomerId equals c. Id select new { o. Id , c. Name }; // Left join (classic pattern befor"},{"c":"linq","s":"deferred-vs-immediate-execution-the-one-idea-that-explains-m","h":"Deferred vs immediate execution: the one idea that explains most LINQ bugs","t":"# Deferred vs immediate execution: the one idea that explains most LINQ bugs Intermediate Here's the single most important thing in this entire chapter, and the thing worth slowing all the way down for: when you write a LINQ query, nothing happens yet. Not \"almost nothing\" — literally nothing. Most LINQ operators don't run your filter, your projection, anything — they just hand back an object that describes what you asked for, a plan, not a result. The actual work only happens later, at the exact moment something enumerates that plan — a foreach , a ToList() , anything that actually walks the sequence. Operators split cleanly into two camps along this line. Deferred (lazy) operators — Where , Select , OrderBy , Take , SelectMany , and most others — build that plan and do nothing else. Immediate operators — ToList , ToArray , ToDictionary , Count , Sum , First , Any , Max — are the ones that actually trigger enumeration and hand back a real, concrete result right then. Let's watch this happen, step by step, instead of just stating it: C# Run ▶ Copy var numbers = new List < int > { 1 , 2 , 3 }; var query = numbers. Where (n => { Console . WriteLine ( $\"checking {n}\" ); return n > 1 ; }); // nothing printed yet numbers. Add ( 4 ); // the query will see this! foreach ( var n in query) { } // NOW prints: checking 1, 2, 3, 4 var count = query. Count (); // runs the WHOLE query AGAIN: checking 1, 2, 3, 4 Walk through exactly what happens at each line, because every line here surprises someone the first time: numbers.Where(...) runs. Nothing is printed. The lambda you wrote hasn't executed even once yet — query is just a description of \"filter numbers by this predicate\", sitting there, inert. numbers.Add(4) — and here's the part that catches people out — this is allowed , and it matters, because query doesn't hold a snapshot of numbers from when it was declared. It holds a reference to the live list, and will read whatever is in it whenever it's finally enumerated . The foreach is the first thing that actually enumerates query . Only now does the lambda run, once per element — on all four numbers, including the 4 you added after declaring the query. query.Count() is a second , completely independent enumeration. The query has no memory of having already run once — it reruns the entire filter from scratch, printing \"checking 1, 2, 3, 4\" all over again. Four consequences fall directly out of that trace, and each one is a real bug pattern you'll eventually meet in production: A query sees its source as it is when enumerated, not when declared. If the source changes in between — as numbers did above — the query sees the new state, which is sometimes exactly what you want and sometimes a genuine bug. Every enumeration re-runs the whole query, from scratch. Against a database, that's another full round trip; against an expensive in-memory Select , that's repeated work for no reason. The fix is simple once you know to look for it: call ToList() once, and reuse the resulting list, the moment you know you'll need the results more than once. Exceptions surface late — at enumeration time, which can be far away, in a completely different method, from the line that actually built the faulty query. A disposed source breaks a query that hasn't run yet. Return a lazy query built over a DbContext , let that DbContext get disposed, and then have the caller enumerate it — you'll get an ObjectDisposedException at the enumeration site, nowhere near where the real mistake was made. # Not every deferred operator is lazy in the same way One more distinction worth having, because it explains a specific performance trap: among the deferred operators, some stream — they can hand you the very first result item without first reading the whole source ( Where , Select , Take all do this). Others must buffer the entire source before they can yield even their first output item, because they fundamentally can't know the answer without seeing everything ( OrderBy , Gro"},{"c":"linq","s":"how-this-laziness-is-actually-implemented-yield-return","h":"How this laziness is actually implemented: yield return","t":"# How this laziness is actually implemented: yield return Intermediate You now know LINQ operators are lazy. Here's the mechanism that makes that possible — and it's one you can use yourself, not just something happening behind the scenes: C# Run ▶ Copy public static IEnumerable < T > MyWhere < T >( this IEnumerable < T > source, Func < T , bool > predicate) { foreach ( var item in source) if (predicate(item)) yield return item; // pause here, hand the item to the caller, resume on next MoveNext() } static IEnumerable < int > Fibonacci () // infinite sequences are fine, because it's lazy { ( int a, int b) = ( 0 , 1 ); while ( true ) { yield return a; (a, b) = (b, a + b); } } var first10 = Fibonacci (). Take ( 10 ). ToList (); yield return is doing something genuinely clever under the hood: the compiler rewrites a method that uses it into a small object implementing IEnumerable<T> / IEnumerator<T> , whose code can pause mid-execution , hand one value back to whoever's asking, and later resume exactly where it left off — locals, loop position, everything intact — the next time it's asked for another value. That's precisely what lets Fibonacci() above return an infinite sequence without ever hanging: nothing computes the next Fibonacci number until something explicitly asks for it, and Take(10) only ever asks ten times. foreach is what drives this machinery: it calls GetEnumerator() once, then repeatedly calls MoveNext() and reads Current , until MoveNext() says there's nothing left, and finally calls Dispose() . Chain several LINQ operators together — Where().Select().Take() — and you've actually built a pipeline of enumerators , each one pulling exactly one item at a time from the one before it, only when asked. Nothing is computed ahead of its actual use."},{"c":"linq","s":"ienumerable-vs-iqueryable-the-difference-that-decides-where-","h":"IEnumerable vs IQueryable : the difference that decides where your query actually runs","t":"# IEnumerable vs IQueryable : the difference that decides where your query actually runs Intermediate Everything so far has been \"LINQ to Objects\" — queries running entirely in your process, over data already sitting in memory. But the exact same Where , Select , OrderBy syntax also works against db.Orders in EF Core, and that code doesn't run in your process at all — it becomes SQL. How can identical-looking syntax do two completely different things? IEnumerable<T> (LINQ to Objects) IQueryable<T> (LINQ providers: EF Core…) Operators take Func<T, bool> : compiled delegates Expression<Func<T, bool>> : expression trees Executes In your process, in C# Translated by a provider (to SQL, etc.) and run at the source Defined in System.Linq.Enumerable System.Linq.Queryable Can call any C# method Yes Only what the provider can translate The whole answer is in that first row, and it's genuinely subtle: IQueryable<T> 's version of Where doesn't take a compiled Func at all — it takes an Expression<Func<T, bool>> , which, as the next section shows in detail, means the compiler hands the provider a description of your lambda instead of runnable code. EF Core reads that description and writes its own SQL from it. IEnumerable<T> 's Where takes an ordinary compiled delegate, which just runs, in C#, right there in your process. Same method name, same-looking lambda — two structurally different things happening underneath. C# Run ▶ Copy IQueryable < Order > q = db. Orders ; // nothing executed var paid = q. Where (o => o. Status == OrderStatus . Paid ) // still IQueryable: becomes WHERE . OrderBy (o => o. CreatedAt ) // ORDER BY . Take ( 20 ); // TOP(20) / LIMIT 20 var list = await paid. ToListAsync (); // ONE SQL query, 20 rows returned // The trap: switching to IEnumerable too early IEnumerable < Order > all = db. Orders ; // static type is IEnumerable var bad = all. Where (o => o. Status == OrderStatus . Paid ). Take ( 20 ). ToList (); // SELECT * FROM Orders: the ENTIRE table comes over the network, then it's filtered in memory Look closely at the two blocks above — the operators called are identical , Where then Take . The only difference is the static type of the variable they're called on: IQueryable<Order> versus IEnumerable<Order> . That one type annotation decides whether .Where(...) resolves to the IQueryable overload (build an expression tree, let EF Core translate it to WHERE ... TOP(20) ) or the IEnumerable overload (compile a delegate, pull every single row across the network first, and only then filter in memory). The SQL difference between those two outcomes, on a million-row table, is enormous — and nothing about the syntax warns you which one you've written. The silent full-table scan Any of these flip a query from SQL to in-memory, usually without a compiler warning in sight: assigning the query to an IEnumerable<T> variable, calling .AsEnumerable() , calling .ToList() before you're done filtering, or a repository method whose return type is declared IEnumerable<T> instead of IQueryable<T> . The defence is simple to state: keep a query typed as IQueryable for as long as possible, right up until the one final call that actually materialises it, and Select down to only the columns you actually need before that happens."},{"c":"linq","s":"expression-trees-what-ef-core-is-actually-reading","h":"Expression trees: what EF Core is actually reading","t":"# Expression trees: what EF Core is actually reading Advanced The previous section promised a closer look at what an Expression<Func<…>> really is, as opposed to a plain delegate. Here it is. When a lambda gets assigned to an Expression<Func<…>> instead of a Func<…> , the compiler does something fundamentally different with it: instead of compiling the lambda into runnable code the way it normally would, it builds an object graph that describes the code — a tree of nodes you can walk, inspect, and even generate completely new code from. C# Run ▶ Copy Expression < Func < Order , bool >> expr = o => o. Total > 100 && o. Country == \"DE\" ; // AndAlso( // GreaterThan(Member(o, Total), Constant(100)), // Equal(Member(o, Country), Constant(\"DE\"))) Walk through what that diagram is showing, because it's genuinely the whole mechanism in one picture: the lambda o.Total > 100 && o.Country == \"DE\" becomes a small tree, with an AndAlso node at the root, two comparison nodes beneath it ( GreaterThan and Equal ), and each comparison pointing at a Member access and a Constant as its two leaves. That tree is just data — ordinary .NET objects you could inspect with a debugger, same as any other object graph. EF Core's query pipeline walks it exactly the way you'd walk any tree: visit the root, recurse into each child, and at each node emit the matching piece of SQL — AndAlso becomes AND , GreaterThan becomes > , a Member access becomes a column name, and a Constant becomes... not a literal value baked into the SQL string, but a parameter . That detail is exactly why LINQ queries are naturally safe from SQL injection: captured values never get string-concatenated into the query text at all — they travel as proper SQL parameters ( @__country_0 ), the same mechanism you'd reach for by hand with raw ADO.NET. Because an IQueryable is just this same kind of tree, waiting to be extended, you can build one up conditionally, piece by piece, and the provider still only ever sees — and only ever executes — one final query: C# Run ▶ Copy public static IQueryable < Product > ApplyFilters ( IQueryable < Product > q, ProductFilter f) { if (f. MinPrice is { } min) q = q. Where (p => p. Price >= min); // composing IQueryable conditionally if (! string . IsNullOrEmpty (f. Search )) q = q. Where (p => p. Name . Contains (f. Search )); if (f. CategoryId is { } cat) q = q. Where (p => p. CategoryId == cat); return q; // still one SQL query, built from only the filters that were supplied } There's a real limit on all of this, and it follows directly from how the translation works: the provider can only translate what it actually understands how to turn into SQL — it cannot run arbitrary C#. q.Where(p => MyHelper(p)) throws \"could not be translated\" in EF Core 3 and later, precisely because MyHelper is just a Member /method-call node in the tree that EF Core has no SQL equivalent for. (Older EF Core versions used to silently fall back to evaluating the whole query in memory instead of throwing — which sounds more forgiving, but was actually a notorious, hard-to-spot performance trap: a query that looked like it ran as SQL was secretly pulling entire tables across the network instead.)"},{"c":"linq","s":"performance-considerations","h":"Performance considerations","t":"# Performance considerations Advanced A short, practical list, now that you understand why each of these is true rather than just that it is: LINQ to Objects allocates enumerators and delegates , plus closures if your lambdas capture variables — you saw exactly this mechanism in the yield return section above. For most business code this overhead is genuinely negligible; in true hot paths (per-item processing of millions of items, tight loops inside middleware), a plain for / foreach over arrays or spans can be several times faster with zero allocations. .NET 7–10 have vectorised many operators ( Sum , Min , Max , Average over arrays/spans now use SIMD; Order and ToArray got faster too) — a good reminder that upgrading .NET versions sometimes buys you LINQ performance for free, no code changes needed. Any() stops the moment it finds a match. Count() > 0 does not — on a lazy sequence it's a full pass through everything, just to throw the number away and check if it was nonzero (though on an ICollection , Count is a stored property and genuinely O(1)). Count property vs Count() method: Count() actually checks whether the source is an ICollection<T> and uses its stored .Count when it can, rather than blindly enumerating — but TryGetNonEnumeratedCount exists specifically to make that intent explicit in your own code, rather than relying on an internal optimisation. Avoid OrderBy(...).First() for the reason covered earlier — a full sort just to discard everything but one element; use MinBy / MaxBy instead. Avoid repeated .ToList() calls chained through a pipeline; each one allocates a brand-new full copy of whatever's been produced so far. Where(...).Count() vs Count(predicate) produce identical results — pick whichever reads more clearly to you. Q What is deferred execution in LINQ? Most operators don't run when called. They build a query that executes when it is enumerated (foreach, ToList, Count, First…), using the data as it is at that moment, and every enumeration runs it again. Immediate operators like ToList, Count, Sum and First force execution. Q IEnumerable vs IQueryable? IEnumerable runs LINQ in memory using compiled delegates. IQueryable captures the query as expression trees that a provider (EF Core) translates and executes at the data source, e.g. as SQL. Switching to IEnumerable too early pulls all rows into memory and filters there. Q What's the difference between First, FirstOrDefault, Single and SingleOrDefault? First returns the first element and throws if there are none; FirstOrDefault returns default instead. Single expects exactly one element and throws if there are zero or more than one; SingleOrDefault returns default for zero but still throws for more than one. Q Select vs SelectMany? Select maps each element to exactly one result, so a sequence of lists stays a sequence of lists. SelectMany maps each element to a sequence and flattens them into one sequence (all order lines from all orders). Q How does yield return work? The compiler turns the method into a state machine implementing IEnumerable/IEnumerator. Each MoveNext runs code until the next yield return, hands back that value, and pauses. Execution is lazy and resumes exactly where it left off. Q What is the multiple-enumeration problem? Enumerating a lazy IEnumerable more than once re-executes the whole pipeline, including database queries or expensive projections, and results may differ between enumerations. Materialise once with ToList/ToArray when you need the results more than once. Q How does EF Core translate LINQ to SQL? IQueryable operators take Expression<Func<…>> parameters, so the compiler builds expression trees instead of delegates. EF Core's query pipeline walks the tree, maps members to columns via the model, turns captured variables into SQL parameters, and generates provider-specific SQL. Untranslatable expressions throw. Q Any() vs Count() > 0? Any stops at the first element (or the first match), so it's O(1) for the common case an"},{"c":"linq","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading LINQ overview Standard query operators Jon Skeet's Edulinq blog series: reimplementing LINQ to Objects, the best way to understand it."},{"c":"linq","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run (plain console app, .NET 8/9, no packages; no database, so the IQueryable section is covered conceptually only — EF Core's actual SQL translation is verified in the EF Core chapters). It checks deferred vs. immediate execution, yield return 's pause/resume behavior, Any() short-circuiting where Count() doesn't, and that an Expression<Func<...>> is data you can inspect rather than runnable code. C# Run ▶ Copy // --- Deferred execution: the query doesn't run until enumerated, and reruns on every enumeration --- var checks = 0 ; var numbers = new List < int > { 1 , 2 , 3 }; var query = numbers. Where (n => { checks++; return n > 1 ; }); if (checks != 0 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: Where should not run the predicate before enumeration\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: building a LINQ query runs nothing yet (checks == 0)\" ); numbers. Add ( 4 ); // the query holds a reference to the live list, not a snapshot var first = query. ToList (); if (checks != 4 ) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected 4 predicate calls (including the late-added 4), got {checks}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: enumerating runs the predicate once per element, seeing the list as it is NOW (4 items)\" ); checks = 0 ; var second = query. Count (); if (checks != 4 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: a second enumeration should re-run the whole query from scratch\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: a second enumeration (Count()) re-ran the predicate from scratch instead of reusing the first result\" ); // --- Any() short-circuits; Count() walks the whole lazy sequence --- var visited = 0 ; IEnumerable < int > Source () { foreach ( var n in Enumerable . Range ( 1 , 1000 )) { visited++; yield return n; } } visited = 0 ; var hasMatch = Source (). Any (n => { return n == 1 ; }); // matches on the very first element if (visited != 1 ) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: Any should stop at the first match, visited {visited} elements\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: Any() stopped after the first matching element instead of scanning everything\" ); visited = 0 ; var total = Source (). Count (); if (visited != 1000 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: Count() on a lazy sequence should enumerate everything\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: Count() on a lazy IEnumerable<T> walked all 1000 elements, unlike Any()\" ); // --- yield return: pause/resume, proven by interleaving with external side effects --- IEnumerable < int > Trace () { Console . WriteLine ( \" trace: about to yield 1\" ); yield return 1 ; Console . WriteLine ( \" trace: about to yield 2\" ); yield return 2 ; } var it = Trace (). GetEnumerator (); Console . WriteLine ( \"main: before first MoveNext\" ); it. MoveNext (); // only now does \"about to yield 1\" print — proves the method body hadn't started yet Console . WriteLine ( $\"main: got {it.Current}, now calling MoveNext again\" ); it. MoveNext (); // resumes exactly after the first yield, not from the top if (it. Current != 2 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: yield return should resume where it left off\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: yield return paused and resumed exactly where it left off (saw 1 then 2, interleaved with main)\" ); // --- An Expression<Func<...>> is an inspectable object graph, not compiled, runnable code --- System . Linq . Expressions . Expression < Func < int , bool >> expr = x => x > 100 ; var body = ( System . Linq . Expressions . BinaryExpression )expr. Body ; if (body. NodeType != System . Linq . Expressions . ExpressionType . GreaterThan ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected the expression tree's root node to be a GreaterThan comparison\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: Expression<Func<int,bool>> is data — its root node is {body.NodeType}, matching 'x > 100', inspectable without running it\" ); // Not runtime-verifiable here — see Further reading: LINQ overview, Standard query operators (Microsoft Learn) // (actual SQL generated by an IQueryable provider like EF Core is covered and verified in the EF Core chapters)"},{"c":"exceptions-disposal","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Throwing an exception stops normal execution and unwinds the call stack looking for a matching catch , running every finally along the way. Pick the exception type from the standard hierarchy ( ArgumentException , InvalidOperationException , ...) so callers can tell failures apart without parsing message strings. throw; keeps the original stack trace; throw ex; resets it — always prefer throw; or wrap in a new exception. Exceptions are for the unexpected. For routine \"might not work\" outcomes (parsing, lookups, validation), prefer TryParse -style patterns or a Result<T> instead. IDisposable / using give you deterministic cleanup (close this connection now ) that the garbage collector, which runs on its own schedule, can't give you. In async code, an exception lives inside the Task until something await s it — if nothing ever does, it's silently lost."},{"c":"exceptions-disposal","s":"what-actually-happens-when-code-throws","h":"What actually happens when code throws","t":"# What actually happens when code throws Beginner The relay race baton Picture PlaceOrder calling Validate , which calls ChargeCard — a relay race where each method hands off to the next. When ChargeCard drops the baton (throws), it doesn't just stop: the runtime hands the problem back up the same chain it came down, one leg at a time, asking each runner in turn \"is this your problem to catch?\" Validate says no but still has to do its own cleanup on the way out (its finally ); PlaceOrder finally says yes and catches it. That handback, leg by leg, is exactly what \"unwinding the stack\" means. An exception is an object — always deriving from System.Exception — that represents an error. The moment code throws one, normal execution stops dead, right there, and the runtime starts unwinding the call stack : walking back up through method after method, looking for a catch block whose type matches the exception, running any finally block it passes along the way regardless of whether it finds a match yet. If it unwinds all the way to the top of the call stack without finding anything, the thread terminates — though in ASP.NET Core specifically, you almost never see that happen, because the framework's own exception-handling middleware sits near the top of the pipeline and catches it first, turning it into a 500 response instead of a crash. C# Run ▶ Copy try { var order = await repo. GetAsync (id) ?? throw new NotFoundException ( $\"Order {id}\" ); order. Cancel (); // may throw InvalidOperationException await uow. SaveChangesAsync (); } catch ( NotFoundException ) // most specific first { return Results . NotFound (); } catch ( DbUpdateConcurrencyException ex) { logger. LogWarning (ex, \"Concurrency conflict on order {Id}\" , id); return Results . Conflict (); } finally { metrics. RecordAttempt (); // ALWAYS runs: success, exception, or early return } That finally block is worth noting precisely: it runs on every path out of the try — a clean success, a caught exception, an uncaught one still unwinding past it, even an early return from inside the try . There's no way to leave this method, through any route, without metrics.RecordAttempt() running. An exception object carries a handful of properties worth knowing by name: Message , StackTrace , InnerException (the underlying cause, when one exception is deliberately wrapped around another), Data (a bag for extra key/value context), HResult , and ToString() , which — unlike Message alone — prints the entire picture, inner exceptions included. That last point matters enough to call out as a rule: always log the exception object itself , never just its .Message string — you lose the stack trace, the inner exception chain, and everything else that actually helps you find the bug, for the sake of a shorter log line."},{"c":"exceptions-disposal","s":"the-exception-hierarchy-a-map-for-picking-the-right-one","h":"The exception hierarchy: a map for picking the right one","t":"# The exception hierarchy: a map for picking the right one Beginner Diagram Copy System.Exception ├── SystemException │ ├── ArgumentException │ │ ├── ArgumentNullException │ │ └── ArgumentOutOfRangeException │ ├── InvalidOperationException │ │ └── ObjectDisposedException │ ├── NullReferenceException ← a bug; never throw or catch it deliberately │ ├── IndexOutOfRangeException │ ├── InvalidCastException │ ├── FormatException │ ├── NotSupportedException / NotImplementedException │ ├── IOException → FileNotFoundException … │ ├── OperationCanceledException → TaskCanceledException │ ├── TimeoutException │ ├── OutOfMemoryException, StackOverflowException ← can't be meaningfully handled │ └── … ├── HttpRequestException, JsonException, DbException → SqlException … └── AggregateException ← wraps several (Task.Wait, Parallel) Picking the right exception type to throw isn't just etiquette — it's how callers further up the stack can actually distinguish \"bad input\" from \"wrong state\" from \"not found\" without parsing your message string. Microsoft's own framework design guidelines boil down to a short, memorable table: Situation Throw A bad argument ArgumentException / ArgumentNullException / ArgumentOutOfRangeException Object is in the wrong state for this call InvalidOperationException Called after Dispose ObjectDisposedException Operation intentionally unsupported NotSupportedException A domain rule violated A custom exception ( DomainException ), or a Result instead Modern .NET ships static guard helpers specifically so you don't have to hand-write the same if (x is null) throw new ArgumentNullException(...) boilerplate a thousand times: ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(x) , ArgumentException.ThrowIfNullOrWhiteSpace(s) , ArgumentOutOfRangeException.ThrowIfNegative(n) , ObjectDisposedException.ThrowIf(_disposed, this) — each one throws the exact right type from that table, with a correctly populated message, in a single line."},{"c":"exceptions-disposal","s":"throw-vs-throw-ex-one-character-of-difference-one-real-conse","h":"throw; vs throw ex; — one character of difference, one real consequence","t":"# throw; vs throw ex; — one character of difference, one real consequence Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy catch ( Exception ex) { logger. LogError (ex, \"Payment failed\" ); throw ; // ✔ rethrows the SAME exception, stack trace preserved } catch ( Exception ex) { throw ex; // ✘ resets the stack trace to this line: you lose where it really failed } catch ( SqlException ex) { throw new RepositoryException ( \"Saving order failed\" , ex); // ✔ wrap with context, keep the cause as InnerException } These two lines look almost interchangeable, but the difference has cost real debugging time. A bare throw; rethrows the exact same exception object with its stack trace untouched — it still points at the original line where it actually failed. throw ex; throws the same object , but the runtime treats it as a new throw happening right here, so the trace gets overwritten to point at this catch block, and the real origin is lost. If a stack trace starts somewhere suspiciously unhelpful, throw ex; upstream is a common culprit. The third block shows a deliberate third option: wrapping . throw new RepositoryException(\"Saving order failed\", ex) throws a brand-new exception with its own fresh stack trace, while keeping the original as InnerException . Do this when the original exception wouldn't mean much to whoever reads it later — a raw SqlException is less useful three layers up than a RepositoryException that says what you were trying to do, with the SqlException still attached underneath for anyone who needs it. # Exception filters: deciding whether to catch, without unwinding first C# Run ▶ Copy catch ( HttpRequestException ex) when (ex. StatusCode is HttpStatusCode . TooManyRequests or HttpStatusCode . ServiceUnavailable ) { // only transient errors handled here; others propagate untouched } catch ( Exception ex) when ( LogAndReturnFalse (ex)) { } // log without catching (filter returns false) A when (condition) clause changes when the stack unwinds. Without a filter, the runtime has to unwind the stack — tearing down frames, running finally blocks — just to get inside the catch body and check whether you actually wanted this exception. With a filter, the condition runs before any of that, while the exception is still sitting where it was thrown, full original state intact. That helps diagnostics, but the more useful trick is the second example: a filter that returns false after logging lets you observe an exception without catching it — it keeps propagating to whatever would have handled it anyway. One narrower tool: ExceptionDispatchInfo.Capture(ex).Throw() rethrows an exception caught somewhere else entirely (another thread, say) while preserving its original stack trace — the same guarantee throw; gives locally. You'll rarely write this by hand; await already does it for you, as the async section below explains."},{"c":"exceptions-disposal","s":"writing-your-own-exception-types","h":"Writing your own exception types","t":"# Writing your own exception types Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy public class DomainException : Exception { public string Code { get ; } public DomainException ( string code, string message, Exception ? inner = null ) : base (message, inner) => Code = code; } public sealed class InsufficientStockException ( string sku, int requested, int available) : DomainException ( \"stock.insufficient\" , $\"Requested {requested} of {sku}, only {available} available\" ) { public string Sku { get ; } = sku; } A short checklist: name it ending in Exception ; derive from Exception directly, never the legacy ApplicationException ; include the standard constructors so callers can wrap or rethrow it; add real, typed properties for anything a caller might need to act on ( Sku above, not buried in the message string); and keep the message genuinely useful to whoever reads the log. Map your custom exceptions to HTTP status codes in exactly one central place ( Errors & logging shows how), rather than scattering catch blocks that each decide independently."},{"c":"exceptions-disposal","s":"when-not-to-reach-for-an-exception-at-all","h":"When not to reach for an exception at all","t":"# When not to reach for an exception at all Intermediate Exceptions exist for exceptional situations — things the caller couldn't reasonably have prevented, or wouldn't expect as part of normal flow. They're also mechanically expensive : capturing a full stack trace and unwinding through it costs real time compared to an ordinary return. And they're invisible in a method's signature — nothing about Task<Order> PlaceAsync(...) tells a caller it might throw five different exception types under entirely normal conditions. Expected outcome Better than throwing Parsing user input int.TryParse , DateTime.TryParseExact Lookup might miss TryGetValue , return null / T? Validation failure Validation result (FluentValidation, ValidationProblem ) Business rule rejection (\"card declined\", \"out of stock\") Result<T> / OneOf / discriminated union style return C# Run ▶ Copy // Try-pattern: the BCL convention for \"might fail, that's normal\" public bool TryReserve ( string sku, int qty, [NotNullWhen(true)] out Reservation ? reservation) { /* ... */ } The Try-pattern above is the BCL's long-standing answer: a bool return that costs nothing when the \"failure\" is routine, with the actual result handed back through an out parameter only on success. \"Exceptions vs the Result pattern\" is a genuinely live architectural debate — CQRS, Mediator & Result walks through where each one earns its place. An empty catch block is one of the most dangerous things you can write catch { } or catch (Exception) { } with nothing inside doesn't make a problem go away — it just makes it invisible , while whatever bad state caused the exception quietly persists and keeps causing damage somewhere downstream, with no record anywhere that anything went wrong. If you genuinely, deliberately need to ignore a specific exception, catch the narrowest type that actually applies, leave a comment explaining exactly why ignoring it is correct here, and log it anyway — \"ignored, and silent\" is almost never the behaviour you actually want, even when \"ignored\" genuinely is."},{"c":"exceptions-disposal","s":"exceptions-in-async-code-behave-differently-from-what-youd-e","h":"Exceptions in async code behave differently from what you'd expect","t":"# Exceptions in async code behave differently from what you'd expect Intermediate A quick preview before Async/await covers Task and await properly: a Task is an object representing work that finishes later, and await is how you wait for it without blocking a thread. Even without the full chapter, the exception mechanics below are worth knowing, because they genuinely diverge from synchronous exception handling: An exception thrown inside an async Task method isn't thrown immediately — it's captured and stored inside the returned Task . It only surfaces, rethrown with its original stack trace preserved, the moment someone await s that Task . (This is the same mechanism as the ExceptionDispatchInfo trick above: await does that rethrow-with-preserved-trace for you automatically.) Follow that through: if nobody ever await s the Task , its exception is simply never observed . This is how \"fire-and-forget\" bugs happen — code that looks like it's handling errors but is actually losing them quietly. async void methods break this pattern entirely: there's no Task to store an exception in. The exception is raised directly on the synchronization context or thread pool, and with nothing there to catch it, it crashes the entire process . This is the real reason async void is reserved for event handlers (the one place the signature is forced on you) and avoided everywhere else. Task.WhenAll over several tasks rethrows only the first exception it hits when you await the combined result — but every exception from every failed task is still there, in task.Exception.InnerExceptions , if you need the full picture. Cancellation has its own exception type, OperationCanceledException (or its cousin TaskCanceledException ), worth treating as a different category from a real error: a client disconnecting mid-request is normal, not a bug, so logging it at Error level every time just buries real errors in noise. C# Run ▶ Copy var tasks = ids. Select (id => client. GetAsync (id)). ToArray (); try { await Task . WhenAll (tasks); } catch { var all = tasks. Where (t => t. IsFaulted ). SelectMany (t => t. Exception !. InnerExceptions ); foreach ( var e in all) logger. LogError (e, \"One call failed\" ); throw ; } The catch block here isn't really handling the exception Task.WhenAll rethrew — it's using that as a signal to dig through every task's own exception, log each failure individually (since WhenAll alone would only surface the first), then throw; to propagate the original failure once everything is recorded."},{"c":"exceptions-disposal","s":"resources-and-idisposable-the-problem-the-gc-doesnt-solve","h":"Resources and IDisposable: the problem the GC doesn't solve","t":"# Resources and IDisposable: the problem the GC doesn't solve Beginner The GC, as Memory & GC covered in detail, frees memory — but it does so whenever it decides to, on its own schedule, which could be anywhere from microseconds to minutes after an object becomes unreachable. Some resources genuinely can't wait that long and need releasing now , deterministically, the moment you're done with them: database connections (which need to return to a pool promptly, or the pool runs dry) file handles, sockets, network streams locks, semaphores, timers native memory and OS handles The contract that makes \"release this now, deterministically\" possible is IDisposable : C# Run ▶ Copy public interface IDisposable { void Dispose (); } And the using statement is what guarantees Dispose() actually gets called, even when an exception is thrown partway through — because, as the final block below shows, it compiles directly into a try / finally , the exact same construct that guaranteed the finally block ran in the very first example of this chapter: C# Run ▶ Copy using ( var conn = new SqlConnection (cs)) // classic form { conn. Open (); // ... } // conn.Dispose() here using var file = File . OpenRead (path); // C# 8 using declaration: disposed at end of enclosing scope await using var db = await factory. CreateDbContextAsync (); // IAsyncDisposable // What the compiler generates for `using var x = ...;` var x = new Resource (); try { /* rest of scope */ } finally { x?. Dispose (); } Who's actually responsible for disposing what, in ASP.NET Core A rule worth having precisely, because getting it wrong in either direction causes real bugs: you usually don't call Dispose on anything the DI container handed you. Dependency injection covers this in full, but the short version is that the container disposes scoped services — DbContext among them — the moment the request's scope ends, and disposes singletons at app shutdown. You only ever dispose what you personally created with new or a factory call — resources the container never knew existed in the first place."},{"c":"exceptions-disposal","s":"implementing-idisposable-yourself","h":"Implementing IDisposable yourself","t":"# Implementing IDisposable yourself Intermediate For a class that only wraps other already-managed disposables — no raw native handles of its own — this simple version is enough; you don't need the full ceremony in the next section: C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class ReportExporter : IDisposable { private readonly FileStream _file; private readonly Timer _timer; private bool _disposed; public ReportExporter ( string path) { _file = File . Create (path); _timer = new Timer ( Flush , null , 1000 , 1000 ); } public void Write ( string line) { ObjectDisposedException . ThrowIf (_disposed, this ); // ... } public void Dispose () { if (_disposed) return ; // Dispose must be safe to call multiple times _timer. Dispose (); _file. Dispose (); _disposed = true ; } } The if (_disposed) return; guard at the top of Dispose() exists because Dispose must be safe to call more than once . Code elsewhere might legitimately call it twice (an explicit call, then a using block's automatic call on the same object), and without the guard, calling _file.Dispose() a second time could itself throw. # The full Dispose pattern, with a finalizer — only when you genuinely need one Advanced You only need this heavier version when your class directly owns an unmanaged resource — a raw native handle or IntPtr , not another managed IDisposable wrapping one. Even then, prefer wrapping that handle in a SafeHandle , which already implements the finalizer correctly for you, so you never have to write one by hand: C# Run ▶ Copy public class NativeBuffer : IDisposable { private IntPtr _ptr = Marshal . AllocHGlobal ( 1024 ); private bool _disposed; public void Dispose () { Dispose (disposing: true ); GC . SuppressFinalize ( this ); // finalizer no longer needed: saves the GC work } protected virtual void Dispose ( bool disposing) { if (_disposed) return ; if (disposing) { /* dispose MANAGED disposables here */ } Marshal . FreeHGlobal (_ptr); // free UNMANAGED resources in both paths _ptr = IntPtr . Zero ; _disposed = true ; } ~ NativeBuffer () => Dispose (disposing: false ); // safety net if someone forgot to Dispose } The disposing boolean does real work. Dispose(bool) can be called from two different situations: a normal Dispose() call ( disposing: true , where it's safe to also touch other managed objects), or the finalizer running as a last-resort safety net ( disposing: false , where other managed objects might already be collected, so only the unmanaged pointer is guaranteed to still be valid). GC.SuppressFinalize(this) matters too: once Dispose() has already cleaned up, the finalizer doesn't need to run — and skipping it is worth doing, because an object with a pending finalizer gets promoted a generation early and survives an extra GC cycle (see Memory & GC ). # IAsyncDisposable: for cleanup that itself needs to be awaited C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class BlobBatchWriter : IAsyncDisposable { public async ValueTask DisposeAsync () { await FlushAsync (); await _stream. DisposeAsync (); } } await using var writer = new BlobBatchWriter (...); Some cleanup involves I/O of its own — flushing a stream to the network, closing a database connection gracefully — and a plain synchronous Dispose() has no way to await anything inside it. IAsyncDisposable exists for exactly this, and await using is its counterpart to using : same try / finally shape, but calling DisposeAsync() instead. DbContext , Stream , SqlConnection , DI scopes and Utf8JsonWriter all implement it — prefer await using over plain using wherever a type offers it."},{"c":"exceptions-disposal","s":"common-disposal-mistakes","h":"Common disposal mistakes","t":"# Common disposal mistakes Intermediate Mistake Consequence Fix new HttpClient() per request inside using Socket exhaustion (sockets in TIME_WAIT) IHttpClientFactory ; see HttpClient Forgetting to dispose a SqlConnection you created Connection pool exhaustion: \"Timeout expired obtaining a connection\" await using Returning a lazy IEnumerable from inside a using block ObjectDisposedException when the caller enumerates Materialise before the scope ends Disposing something injected by DI Other consumers get a disposed object Let the container own it Storing a scoped IDisposable in a singleton Used after the request scope disposed it IServiceScopeFactory ; see DI Not disposing CancellationTokenSource , Timer , SemaphoreSlim Leaked timers / handles using \"Disposing something injected by DI\" and \"storing a scoped IDisposable in a singleton\" are the same underlying bug in different clothes: both are the captive dependency problem from Dependency injection — an object outliving, or being disposed independently of, the scope meant to own its lifetime. Q What is the difference between throw and throw ex? throw; rethrows the current exception and preserves its original stack trace. throw ex; throws the same object but resets the stack trace to the current line, so you lose where the error originated. Wrap with throw new X(\"context\", ex) when adding information. Q What are exception filters and why are they useful? A when (condition) clause on a catch. The condition is evaluated before the stack unwinds, so if it's false the exception continues untouched with its full original state (good for diagnostics), and you can catch selectively (only certain status codes, for example) without catch-and-rethrow. Q When should you not use exceptions? For expected outcomes: parsing input, lookups that may miss, validation failures and business rule rejections. Use Try-patterns, nullable returns, validation results or a Result type instead. Exceptions are costly and invisible in signatures; keep them for unexpected failures. Q What happens to an exception thrown in an async method? It's captured in the returned Task and rethrown when the Task is awaited, with its original stack trace preserved. If the Task is never awaited, the exception goes unobserved. In async void methods, there's no Task, so the exception crashes the process. Q What is IDisposable and why does .NET need it if it has a garbage collector? The GC only manages memory and runs non-deterministically. IDisposable gives deterministic release of other resources (connections, handles, sockets, locks) at a known point, typically via using / await using, which compile to try/finally. Q Explain the Dispose pattern. When do you need a finalizer? Implement Dispose() to release resources, make it idempotent, and throw ObjectDisposedException afterwards. The full pattern with Dispose(bool) and a finalizer is only needed when the class directly owns unmanaged resources; Dispose() then calls Dispose(true) and GC.SuppressFinalize(this). Prefer SafeHandle so you never need your own finalizer. Q Who disposes services created by the DI container? The container. Scoped services are disposed when their scope (the HTTP request) ends; singletons when the root provider is disposed at shutdown; transients when their owning scope ends. Don't dispose injected dependencies yourself. Q How does using work under the hood? It compiles to a try/finally block that calls Dispose (or DisposeAsync for await using) in the finally, with a null check, so the resource is released even if an exception is thrown or the method returns early."},{"c":"exceptions-disposal","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Exceptions and exception handling Best practices for exceptions Implement a Dispose method"},{"c":"exceptions-disposal","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Save as Program.cs in a new console project ( dotnet new console ) and run with dotnet run . It checks finally semantics, throw; vs throw ex; stack traces, exception filters, wrapped exceptions, using / await using disposal (including idempotent Dispose ), and how exceptions behave inside async Task s. The compiler will warn about throw ex; (CA2200) — that warning is itself the chapter's point, so it's expected here. C# Run ▶ Copy int failures = 0 ; void Check ( string claim, bool ok) { if (ok) Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: {claim}\" ); else { Console . WriteLine ( $\"FAIL: {claim}\" ); failures++; } } // 1. finally runs on both the success path and the exception path bool finallyRanOnSuccess = false , finallyRanOnException = false ; try { } finally { finallyRanOnSuccess = true ; } try { try { throw new InvalidOperationException (); } finally { finallyRanOnException = true ; } } catch ( InvalidOperationException ) { } Check ( \"finally runs on the success path\" , finallyRanOnSuccess); Check ( \"finally runs when an exception is thrown\" , finallyRanOnException); // 2. throw; preserves the original stack trace; throw ex; resets it string ReThrowProperly () { try { ThrowsDeep (); return \"\" ; } catch ( Exception ex) { try { throw ; } catch ( Exception e2) { return e2. StackTrace ?? \"\" ; } } } string ReThrowBadly () { try { ThrowsDeep (); return \"\" ; } catch ( Exception ex) { try { throw ex; } catch ( Exception e2) { return e2. StackTrace ?? \"\" ; } } } static void ThrowsDeep () => throw new InvalidOperationException ( \"boom\" ); var properTrace = ReThrowProperly (); var badTrace = ReThrowBadly (); Check ( \"throw; keeps ThrowsDeep in the stack trace (original origin preserved)\" , properTrace. Contains ( \"ThrowsDeep\" )); Check ( \"throw ex; loses ThrowsDeep from the stack trace (origin overwritten)\" , !badTrace. Contains ( \"ThrowsDeep\" )); // 3. Exception filters (when) catch selectively without catching everything bool caughtSpecific = false , propagatedOthers = false ; try { try { throw new InvalidOperationException ( \"transient\" ); } catch ( InvalidOperationException ex) when (ex. Message == \"transient\" ) { caughtSpecific = true ; } } catch { } try { try { throw new InvalidOperationException ( \"permanent\" ); } catch ( InvalidOperationException ex) when (ex. Message == \"transient\" ) { } } catch ( InvalidOperationException ) { propagatedOthers = true ; } Check ( \"a catch filter (when) catches only matching exceptions\" , caughtSpecific); Check ( \"a catch filter lets non-matching exceptions keep propagating\" , propagatedOthers); // 4. Custom exception carries InnerException var inner = new InvalidOperationException ( \"low level\" ); var wrapped = new RepositoryException ( \"Saving order failed\" , inner); Check ( \"a wrapped custom exception keeps the original as InnerException\" , wrapped. InnerException == inner); // 5. using calls Dispose even when the body throws var resource = new TrackedResource (); try { using (resource) { throw new InvalidOperationException (); } } catch ( InvalidOperationException ) { } Check ( \"using calls Dispose even when the body throws\" , resource. Disposed ); // 6. Dispose is safe to call more than once var resource2 = new TrackedResource (); resource2. Dispose (); Exception ? thrown = null ; try { resource2. Dispose (); } catch ( Exception ex) { thrown = ex; } Check ( \"Dispose is idempotent: calling it twice doesn't throw\" , thrown is null ); // 7. await using calls DisposeAsync var asyncResource = new TrackedAsyncResource (); await using (asyncResource) { } Check ( \"await using calls DisposeAsync when the scope ends\" , asyncResource. Disposed ); // 8. An exception in an async Task method is stored, not thrown, until awaited var faultedTask = FailAsync (); await Task . Delay ( 10 ); // give it time to complete Check ( \"an exception inside an async Task is captured in the Task, not thrown immediately\" , faultedTask. IsFaulted ); bool observedOnAwait = false ; try { await faultedTask; } catch ( I"},{"c":"async-await","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Async/await is about scalability , not speed: it frees up the thread while waiting for I/O, so a server can handle many more concurrent requests with the same number of threads. await doesn't block. If the awaited Task isn't done, the method returns to its caller immediately and resumes later — possibly on a different thread — when the result is ready. Never block on async code ( .Result , .Wait() ): it causes deadlocks in UI/old ASP.NET apps and thread-pool starvation in ASP.NET Core. Pass a CancellationToken through every async call so work actually stops when the caller gives up. Start independent tasks together and await Task.WhenAll(...) to run them concurrently instead of one after another. Under the hood, the compiler turns every async method into a state machine; the first real suspension boxes it onto the heap."},{"c":"async-await","s":"the-problem-threads-waiting-on-i-o","h":"The problem: threads waiting on I/O","t":"# The problem: threads waiting on I/O Beginner A web server handles many requests concurrently, using threads from the thread pool . A typical request spends most of its time waiting : on the database, another API, a file, a cache. The CPU is idle during that wait. Synchronous code blocks the thread while it waits: C# Run ▶ Copy public IActionResult Get ( int id) { var order = db. Orders . Find (id); // thread BLOCKED ~20 ms waiting for SQL var stock = http. GetString (url); // thread BLOCKED ~100 ms waiting for HTTP return Ok (...); } With 100 concurrent requests, you need 100 blocked threads. Under load, the pool runs out, new requests queue, latency explodes, and the server falls over while the CPU sits at 10%. This is thread-pool starvation . Asynchronous code releases the thread while it waits: C# Run ▶ Copy public async Task < IActionResult > Get ( int id, CancellationToken ct) { var order = await db. Orders . FindAsync ([id], ct); // thread returns to the pool during the wait var stock = await http. GetStringAsync (url, ct); // ...and again here return Ok (...); } While SQL works, the thread goes back to the pool and serves other requests. When the result arrives, some pool thread picks up where the method left off. A handful of threads can serve thousands of concurrent requests. The one-sentence answer Async/await in a backend is about scalability (serving more concurrent requests with fewer threads), not about making a single request faster. For I/O-bound work there is no thread doing anything during the wait, and async lets you stop pretending there is. The restaurant A synchronous waiter takes your order, walks to the kitchen, and stands there until the food is ready. They serve one table at a time. An asynchronous waiter takes your order, hands it to the kitchen, and serves other tables ; when the kitchen rings the bell, any free waiter brings the food over. Same kitchen speed, many more tables served."},{"c":"async-await","s":"i-o-bound-vs-cpu-bound","h":"I/O-bound vs CPU-bound","t":"# I/O-bound vs CPU-bound Beginner I/O-bound CPU-bound Time spent Waiting for something external Computing Examples DB queries, HTTP calls, file/network I/O, message brokers Image resizing, hashing, compression, big calculations Right tool async / await with truly async APIs ( *Async methods) Run on a thread: Task.Run (in UI/background code), Parallel.For , a background worker In ASP.NET Core Always async Just do it synchronously in the request (it needs a thread anyway), or offload to a background queue if it's long Task.Run in ASP.NET Core Wrapping synchronous work in await Task.Run(() => DoWork()) inside a request handler doesn't help : you release one pool thread and immediately occupy another, with extra overhead. It's useful in UI apps (to keep the UI thread free), not in web requests."},{"c":"async-await","s":"task-and-task","h":"Task and Task","t":"# Task and Task Beginner A Task represents an operation that will complete in the future. Task<T> also carries a result of type T . Property / method Meaning Status Created, WaitingForActivation, Running, RanToCompletion , Faulted , Canceled IsCompleted , IsCompletedSuccessfully , IsFaulted , IsCanceled State checks Result (Task<T>) The value; blocks if not complete (avoid) Exception An AggregateException if faulted await task Asynchronously wait, then get the result or rethrow the exception Task.FromResult(x) , Task.CompletedTask Already-completed tasks (for sync implementations of async interfaces) Task.Delay(ms, ct) Async wait (never Thread.Sleep in async code) Task.WhenAll , Task.WhenAny , Task.WhenEach (.NET 9) Combinators A Task is not a thread. For I/O, there's no thread running while the task is pending: the OS notifies the runtime (via I/O completion ports or epoll) when data arrives, and only then is a pool thread used to run your continuation."},{"c":"async-await","s":"what-async-and-await-actually-do","h":"What async and await actually do","t":"# What async and await actually do Beginner async on a method does two things: it allows await inside, and it makes the compiler wrap the return value or exception into a Task . It does not make the method run on another thread. await t means: if t is already complete, just continue immediately. Otherwise, it registers \"the rest of this method\" as a continuation on t , and returns to the caller right now instead of waiting. When t eventually completes, that continuation runs. That picture is the whole idea in one frame: await never blocks a thread waiting around for something slow. It pauses the method exactly where it is, remembers everything it needs to carry on (its local variables), hands the thread back so it can go do something else useful, and picks up again later — often on a completely different thread — right where it left off. The \"Under the hood\" section below shows precisely how the compiler makes \"remember everything and pick up later\" actually work. C# Run ▶ Copy public async Task < decimal > GetTotalAsync ( int orderId) { Console . WriteLine ( \"A\" ); // runs synchronously on the caller's thread var order = await repo. GetAsync (orderId); // if not done: method returns an incomplete Task to the caller here Console . WriteLine ( \"B\" ); // runs later, on a pool thread, when GetAsync finishes return order. Total ; // completes the Task<decimal> with this value } Return types: Task , Task<T> , ValueTask , ValueTask<T> , IAsyncEnumerable<T> , or void (event handlers only)."},{"c":"async-await","s":"the-rules","h":"The rules","t":"# The rules Beginner # 1. Async all the way Once something is async, its callers should be async too, up to the framework entry point (the controller action or endpoint, which ASP.NET Core awaits for you). # 2. Never block on async code C# Run ▶ Copy var user = GetUserAsync (). Result ; // ✘ blocks a thread; can deadlock GetUserAsync (). Wait (); // ✘ var user2 = GetUserAsync (). GetAwaiter (). GetResult (); // ✘ same problem, nicer exception var user3 = await GetUserAsync (); // ✔ This \"sync over async\" causes thread-pool starvation in ASP.NET Core, and deadlocks in UI apps and old ASP.NET (see deadlocks ). # 3. Avoid async void C# Run ▶ Copy async void Bad () { await Task . Delay ( 1 ); throw new Exception (); } // ✘ crashes the process; can't be awaited async Task Good () { await Task . Delay ( 1 ); throw new Exception (); } // ✔ exception stored in the Task # 4. Pass a CancellationToken through everything C# Run ▶ Copy app. MapGet ( \"/report\" , async ( IReportService svc, CancellationToken ct) => await svc. BuildAsync (ct)); // ASP.NET Core binds ct to HttpContext.RequestAborted: cancelled if the client disconnects public async Task < Report > BuildAsync ( CancellationToken ct) { var rows = await db. Sales . Where (...). ToListAsync (ct); // EF Core cancels the SQL command var fx = await http. GetFromJsonAsync < Rates >(url, ct); // HttpClient aborts the request ct. ThrowIfCancellationRequested (); // check manually in long CPU loops return Compose (rows, fx); } Analyzer CA2016 warns when you forget to forward a token. # 5. Use the async APIs Use ToListAsync , SaveChangesAsync , ReadAsStringAsync , File.ReadAllTextAsync , Task.Delay , SemaphoreSlim.WaitAsync , not their blocking equivalents. # 6. Don't use async when you just pass the task through (optional) C# Run ▶ Copy public Task < Order ?> GetAsync ( int id) => repo. GetAsync (id); // no state machine; slightly cheaper But keep async / await if there's a using , a try/catch , or more than one statement; otherwise the resource can be disposed before the task completes and exceptions behave differently."},{"c":"async-await","s":"running-work-concurrently","h":"Running work concurrently","t":"# Running work concurrently Intermediate Sequential awaits take the sum of the latencies. Starting tasks first and awaiting them together takes the maximum : C# Run ▶ Copy // Sequential: ~300 ms var user = await users. GetAsync (id, ct); // 100 ms var orders = await ordersApi. GetAsync (id, ct); // 200 ms // Concurrent: ~200 ms var userTask = users. GetAsync (id, ct); // both started var ordersTask = ordersApi. GetAsync (id, ct); await Task . WhenAll (userTask, ordersTask); var (u, o) = (userTask. Result , ordersTask. Result ); // .Result is fine here: the tasks are complete Combinator Completes when Use Task.WhenAll(tasks) All complete (faults if any fault) Fan-out calls, batch processing Task.WhenAny(tasks) The first completes Timeouts, \"fastest wins\", hedging Task.WhenEach(tasks) (.NET 9) IAsyncEnumerable yielding tasks as they finish Process results in completion order Parallel.ForEachAsync(items, opts, body) All items processed with a max degree of parallelism Bounded concurrency over a list task.WaitAsync(TimeSpan, ct) (.NET 6) Task completes or timeout Adding a timeout to any task C# Run ▶ Copy // Bounded concurrency: never fire 10,000 HTTP calls at once await Parallel . ForEachAsync (productIds, new ParallelOptions { MaxDegreeOfParallelism = 8 , CancellationToken = ct }, async (id, token) => await SyncProductAsync (id, token)); DbContext is not thread-safe Task.WhenAll(db.Orders.ToListAsync(), db.Customers.ToListAsync()) on the same DbContext throws \"A second operation was started on this context\". Run EF queries sequentially, or use separate contexts from IDbContextFactory<T> ."},{"c":"async-await","s":"cancellation-and-timeouts","h":"Cancellation and timeouts","t":"# Cancellation and timeouts Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy using var cts = CancellationTokenSource . CreateLinkedTokenSource (requestToken); // combine with the caller's token cts. CancelAfter ( TimeSpan . FromSeconds ( 5 )); // add a timeout try { var data = await client. GetAsync (url, cts. Token ); } catch ( OperationCanceledException ) when (!requestToken. IsCancellationRequested ) { // our 5 s timeout fired (not the client disconnecting) return Results . StatusCode ( StatusCodes . Status504GatewayTimeout ); } Cooperative: cancelling a token doesn't kill anything. Code must observe the token (async APIs do this for you). Cancellation surfaces as OperationCanceledException , which is normal flow. Don't log it as an error. Dispose CancellationTokenSource s you create (they may hold timers)."},{"c":"async-await","s":"async-streams-iasyncenumerable","h":"Async streams: IAsyncEnumerable","t":"# Async streams: IAsyncEnumerable Intermediate When results arrive over time (DB rows, pages from an API, messages), stream them instead of buffering everything: C# Run ▶ Copy public async IAsyncEnumerable < Product > GetAllAsync ( [EnumeratorCancellation] CancellationToken ct = default ) { string ? next = \"/products?page=1\" ; while (next is not null ) { var page = await http. GetFromJsonAsync < Page < Product >>(next, ct); foreach ( var p in page!. Items ) yield return p; next = page. NextLink ; } } await foreach ( var p in GetAllAsync (ct)) { /* process each as it arrives */ } // ASP.NET Core streams IAsyncEnumerable responses as a JSON array without buffering app. MapGet ( \"/export\" , ( ShopDb db) => db. Orders . AsNoTracking (). AsAsyncEnumerable ());"},{"c":"async-await","s":"under-the-hood-the-state-machine","h":"Under the hood: the state machine","t":"# Under the hood: the state machine Advanced The compiler rewrites every async method into a struct state machine implementing IAsyncStateMachine . Locals become fields; each await becomes a numbered state. C# Run ▶ Copy // You write: async Task < int > GetLengthAsync ( string url) { var s = await http. GetStringAsync (url); return s. Length ; } // The compiler generates (simplified): struct GetLengthStateMachine : IAsyncStateMachine { public int state; // -1 = running, 0 = waiting at first await public AsyncTaskMethodBuilder < int > builder; // creates/completes the returned Task public string url; // parameters and locals become fields private TaskAwaiter < string > awaiter; public void MoveNext () { try { if (state != 0 ) { awaiter = http. GetStringAsync (url). GetAwaiter (); if (!awaiter. IsCompleted ) { state = 0 ; builder. AwaitUnsafeOnCompleted ( ref awaiter, ref this ); // box to heap, register continuation return ; // ← method \"returns\" here } } string s = awaiter. GetResult (); // resumes here; rethrows if faulted builder. SetResult (s. Length ); // completes the Task<int> } catch ( Exception ex) { builder. SetException (ex); } } } Key insights: If every awaited task is already complete , the method runs entirely synchronously, with no allocation beyond the Task (the state machine stays on the stack). The first time it actually suspends, the state machine is boxed onto the heap , which is why locals in async methods live on the heap and why Span<T> can't cross an await . await works on anything with a GetAwaiter() method (the awaitable pattern ), not just Task. Look at any async method in SharpLab to see the real generated code."},{"c":"async-await","s":"synchronizationcontext-and-configureawait","h":"SynchronizationContext and ConfigureAwait","t":"# SynchronizationContext and ConfigureAwait Advanced When an await resumes, where does the continuation run? It depends on the captured SynchronizationContext : Environment SynchronizationContext Continuation runs on WPF / WinForms / MAUI UI context The UI thread (so you can touch controls) Old ASP.NET (Framework) AspNetSynchronizationContext One-at-a-time per request, with HttpContext.Current restored ASP.NET Core None Any thread-pool thread Console / worker apps None Any thread-pool thread ConfigureAwait(false) says \"don't capture the context; resume anywhere\". In ASP.NET Core application code it changes nothing (there's no context), so you can omit it. In library code that might be used from UI or legacy ASP.NET apps, use ConfigureAwait(false) on every await. It avoids deadlocks for callers who block, and avoids needless hops to the UI thread. ExecutionContext is different: it carries AsyncLocal<T> values (used for logging scopes, Activity.Current for tracing, culture) and always flows across awaits, regardless of ConfigureAwait."},{"c":"async-await","s":"deadlocks-and-thread-pool-starvation","h":"Deadlocks and thread-pool starvation","t":"# Deadlocks and thread-pool starvation Advanced # The classic deadlock (UI apps, old ASP.NET) C# Run ▶ Copy // In a WPF button handler or an ASP.NET (Framework) controller: var data = GetDataAsync (). Result ; // 1. blocks the UI/request thread, waiting for the task async Task < string > GetDataAsync () { var s = await http. GetStringAsync (url); // 2. when the HTTP call finishes, the continuation return s; // must run on the captured context, which is the } // blocked thread from step 1. Neither proceeds: DEADLOCK. Fixes: don't block (await all the way), or use ConfigureAwait(false) inside the library. # Thread-pool starvation (ASP.NET Core) There's no deadlock in ASP.NET Core, but blocking still hurts. Each .Result holds a pool thread hostage. The pool adds threads slowly (about one or two per second once past its minimum) to avoid over-subscription. Under a burst of load: Symptoms: latency climbs to seconds, CPU stays low, thread count slowly rises , requests time out, health checks fail. Diagnose: dotnet-counters monitor shows a growing ThreadPool Queue Length and thread count; a dotnet-dump shows many threads blocked in Task.Wait / GetResult . Fix: remove sync-over-async. (Raising ThreadPool.SetMinThreads is only a stop-gap.)"},{"c":"async-await","s":"valuetask","h":"ValueTask","t":"# ValueTask Advanced Task<T> is a class, so every async call that completes allocates one. ValueTask<T> is a struct that can wrap either a direct result (no allocation) or a Task: C# Run ▶ Copy public ValueTask < Product ?> GetAsync ( int id, CancellationToken ct) { if (_cache. TryGetValue (id, out var p)) return ValueTask . FromResult < Product ?>(p); // hot path: no allocation return new ValueTask < Product ?>( LoadAndCacheAsync (id, ct)); // cold path: wraps a Task } Use it for hot methods that usually complete synchronously (cache hits, buffered reads). Rules: await it exactly once , never await it concurrently, and don't call .Result before it completes. If you need to store or await it twice, call .AsTask() . Default to Task everywhere else."},{"c":"async-await","s":"fire-and-forget-and-background-work","h":"Fire-and-forget and background work","t":"# Fire-and-forget and background work Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy app. MapPost ( \"/signup\" , async ( User u, IEmailSender email) => { await SaveAsync (u); _ = email. SendWelcomeAsync (u); // ✘ fire-and-forget inside a request return Results . Ok (); }); Problems: exceptions are never observed, the work is killed on shutdown, and any scoped dependency (DbContext) may be disposed when the request ends while the task is still running. Instead, enqueue the work to a background service via a Channel<T> , or a durable queue. See Background work ."},{"c":"async-await","s":"async-and-locks","h":"Async and locks","t":"# Async and locks Intermediate You can't await inside a lock block (the compiler forbids it, because the continuation may run on a different thread than the one that holds the lock). Use SemaphoreSlim : C# Run ▶ Copy private readonly SemaphoreSlim _mutex = new ( 1 , 1 ); public async Task RefreshTokenAsync ( CancellationToken ct) { await _mutex. WaitAsync (ct); try { if ( TokenExpired ) _token = await FetchTokenAsync (ct); } finally { _mutex. Release (); } } More in Threading & concurrency . Q Does async/await make code faster? Not for a single operation; it adds a small overhead. It improves scalability: threads aren't blocked during I/O, so a server handles far more concurrent requests with fewer threads. Total latency can drop when independent operations run concurrently via Task.WhenAll. Q What does the await keyword actually do? If the awaited task is already complete, execution continues synchronously. Otherwise, it registers the rest of the method as a continuation on the task and returns an incomplete Task to the caller, freeing the thread. When the task completes, the continuation resumes, on the captured SynchronizationContext if there is one, otherwise on a thread-pool thread. Q Does async create a new thread? No. async/await doesn't create threads. For I/O-bound operations no thread is used while waiting; completion is signalled by the OS and a pool thread runs the continuation. Task.Run is what explicitly queues work to a pool thread. Q Why is calling .Result or .Wait() on a Task dangerous? It blocks a thread until the task completes. With a single-threaded SynchronizationContext (UI apps, old ASP.NET) it deadlocks, because the continuation needs the blocked thread. In ASP.NET Core it causes thread-pool starvation under load. Exceptions are also wrapped in AggregateException. Q Why should you avoid async void? The caller can't await it or know when it finishes, and exceptions can't be caught by the caller: they're raised on the context or thread pool and crash the process. Use async Task; async void is only for event handlers. Q What is ConfigureAwait(false) and do you need it in ASP.NET Core? It tells await not to resume on the captured SynchronizationContext. ASP.NET Core has no SynchronizationContext, so it makes no difference in application code there. Library authors should still use it because their code may run in UI or legacy ASP.NET apps where it prevents deadlocks and unnecessary context switches. Q How do you run multiple async operations concurrently? Start them without awaiting (store the tasks), then await Task.WhenAll(...) . Use Parallel.ForEachAsync or a SemaphoreSlim for bounded concurrency. Don't run concurrent operations on non-thread-safe objects like a single DbContext. Q How does cancellation work in .NET? Cooperatively. A CancellationTokenSource issues tokens; calling Cancel (or CancelAfter) sets the token, and code that observes it (async APIs, ThrowIfCancellationRequested) stops by throwing OperationCanceledException. ASP.NET Core provides HttpContext.RequestAborted, bound to a CancellationToken endpoint parameter. Q What does the compiler generate for an async method? A state machine struct with the locals as fields and a MoveNext method split at each await. It uses an AsyncTaskMethodBuilder to create and complete the returned Task. On the first real suspension the state machine is boxed to the heap and registered as the awaiter's continuation. Q When would you use ValueTask instead of Task? For hot-path methods that frequently complete synchronously (cache hits), to avoid allocating a Task. It must be awaited only once and not concurrently. Default to Task otherwise. Q What is thread-pool starvation and how do you diagnose it? All pool threads are blocked (usually by sync-over-async), so queued work waits while the pool slowly injects threads. You see high latency with low CPU and a growing thread count and queue length in dotnet-counters, and dumps show threads blocked in Wait/GetResult"},{"c":"async-await","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Asynchronous programming with async and await Task-based asynchronous pattern Stephen Toub, How Async/Await Really Works in C# (.NET Blog): the definitive deep dive. David Fowler, AspNetCoreDiagnosticScenarios: AsyncGuidance (GitHub): practical rules for ASP.NET Core. Book: Concurrency in C# Cookbook by Stephen Cleary."},{"c":"async-await","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Save as Program.cs in a new console project ( dotnet new console ) and run with dotnet run . It checks sequential vs. concurrent timing, cancellation, async streams, ValueTask , bounded concurrency, and SemaphoreSlim -based async locking — timing checks use generous margins, but on an extremely slow or loaded machine they could occasionally be flaky. C# Run ▶ Copy using System . Diagnostics ; using System . Runtime . CompilerServices ; int failures = 0 ; void Check ( string claim, bool ok) { if (ok) Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: {claim}\" ); else { Console . WriteLine ( $\"FAIL: {claim}\" ); failures++; } } // 1. Sequential awaits take the SUM of latencies; concurrent tasks take the MAX var swSeq = Stopwatch . StartNew (); await DelayedValue ( 80 ); await DelayedValue ( 80 ); swSeq. Stop (); var swConc = Stopwatch . StartNew (); var tA = DelayedValue ( 80 ); var tB = DelayedValue ( 80 ); await Task . WhenAll (tA, tB); swConc. Stop (); Check ( \"sequential awaits take roughly the sum of the delays (slower)\" , swSeq. ElapsedMilliseconds >= 140 ); Check ( \"Task.WhenAll on tasks started together takes roughly the max, not the sum (faster)\" , swConc. ElapsedMilliseconds < swSeq. ElapsedMilliseconds ); // 2. CancellationToken: cooperative cancellation, OperationCanceledException using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource (); cts. CancelAfter ( 30 ); bool cancelled = false ; try { await LongLoopAsync (cts. Token ); } catch ( OperationCanceledException ) { cancelled = true ; } Check ( \"a cancelled CancellationToken causes OperationCanceledException to propagate\" , cancelled); // 3. IAsyncEnumerable: results can be consumed as they arrive via await foreach var collected = new List < int >(); await foreach ( var n in CountAsync ( 5 )) collected. Add (n); Check ( \"await foreach consumes an IAsyncEnumerable<T> item by item\" , collected. SequenceEqual ( new [] { 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 })); // 4. ValueTask: a hot path can complete synchronously without allocating a Task var vt = GetCachedOrLoad (hit: true ); Check ( \"a ValueTask created from a direct value completes synchronously\" , vt. IsCompletedSuccessfully ); Check ( \"awaiting that ValueTask returns the right value\" , await vt == 42 ); // 5. Parallel.ForEachAsync respects MaxDegreeOfParallelism (bounded concurrency) int active = 0 , maxObserved = 0 ; var gate = new object (); await Parallel . ForEachAsync ( Enumerable . Range ( 1 , 20 ), new ParallelOptions { MaxDegreeOfParallelism = 3 }, async (i, ct) => { lock (gate) { active++; maxObserved = Math . Max (maxObserved, active); } await Task . Delay ( 15 , ct); lock (gate) { active--; } }); Check ( \"Parallel.ForEachAsync never exceeds MaxDegreeOfParallelism concurrent items\" , maxObserved <= 3 ); // 6. SemaphoreSlim.WaitAsync serializes access the way lock would, but is awaitable var semaphore = new SemaphoreSlim ( 1 , 1 ); int counter = 0 ; bool sawOverlap = false ; async Task IncrementAsync () { await semaphore. WaitAsync (); try { int before = counter; await Task . Delay ( 5 ); if (counter != before) sawOverlap = true ; counter = before + 1 ; } finally { semaphore. Release (); } } await Task . WhenAll ( IncrementAsync (), IncrementAsync (), IncrementAsync ()); Check ( \"SemaphoreSlim.WaitAsync lets only one caller through at a time\" , !sawOverlap && counter == 3 ); Console . WriteLine (failures == 0 ? \"\\nAll checks passed.\" : $\"\\n{failures} check(s) failed.\" ); if (failures > 0 ) Environment . Exit ( 1 ); static async Task < int > DelayedValue ( int ms) { await Task . Delay (ms); return ms; } static async Task LongLoopAsync ( CancellationToken ct) { for ( int i = 0 ; i < 1000 ; i++) { ct. ThrowIfCancellationRequested (); await Task . Delay ( 5 , ct); } } static async IAsyncEnumerable < int > CountAsync ( int n, [EnumeratorCancellation] CancellationToken ct = default ) { for ( int i = 1 ; i <= n; i++) { await Task . Delay ( 1 , ct); yield return i; } } static ValueTask < int > GetCachedOrLoad ( bool hit) => hit ? ValueTask ."},{"c":"modern-csharp","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR None of this chapter's features add new runtime behavior — they're compiler conveniences that make modern code look different from older tutorials. Nullable reference types are a compile-time-only warning system for possible nulls; they add zero runtime checks, so bad data from JSON/EF Core can still sneak a null through. Pattern matching lets you test a value's shape (its type, its properties, a range) in is / switch , not just whether it equals a constant. Records are for data that's equal by value , not identity; with gives you a non-destructively modified copy, never mutating the original. init / required let a type force callers to set certain properties exactly once — checked by the compiler, and by JSON deserialization too. Extension methods/members add capability to an existing type without touching its source — this is the exact mechanism behind IServiceCollection.AddX() everywhere in ASP.NET Core."},{"c":"modern-csharp","s":"why-this-chapter-exists","h":"Why this chapter exists","t":"# Why this chapter exists Beginner Picture a concrete moment: you open a pull request on a modern ASP.NET Core repo for the first time, and nothing looks like the tutorial you learned from. Program.cs has nine lines total and no class , no Main . A model class has one line and no body. A switch is missing its default , and somehow the compiler isn't complaining about it. None of that is new language capability — the CLR could always do everything this chapter describes — it's new syntax , added version by version, specifically to remove ceremony from code you'd otherwise have written longhand. We'll keep coming back to that one pull request through this chapter: each feature below is one more line in it starting to make sense. None of these features add new runtime capabilities the CLR couldn't already do — they're almost entirely compiler conveniences, syntax that compiles down to things you've already met elsewhere in this book. The goal here isn't to memorise which C# version introduced what; it's to recognise each feature on sight and know why someone reached for it, so reading real code stops requiring a lookup every few lines. C# .NET Headline features 7.x Core 2.x Tuples, deconstruction, out var , pattern matching ( is ), local functions, ref returns, Span<T> support 8 Core 3.x Nullable reference types , switch expressions, property patterns, async streams, using declarations, ranges ^1 / .. , default interface methods 9 5 Records , init , top-level statements , relational/logical patterns ( and , or , not ), target-typed new() 10 6 Global usings , file-scoped namespaces , record structs, extended property patterns, constant interpolated strings 11 7 Raw string literals , required members , list patterns, generic math (static abstract members), UTF-8 literals \"x\"u8 , file types 12 8 Primary constructors for classes, collection expressions [1, 2] , default lambda parameters, alias any type, [Experimental] 13 9 params collections, new System.Threading.Lock , \\e escape, partial properties, ref struct in generics ( allows ref struct ) 14 10 Extension members (properties, static extensions), field keyword , null-conditional assignment a?.B = x , nameof with unbound generics, implicit span conversions"},{"c":"modern-csharp","s":"nullable-reference-types-making-a-silent-killer-visible-at-c","h":"Nullable reference types: making a silent killer visible at compile time","t":"# Nullable reference types: making a silent killer visible at compile time Beginner NullReferenceException is, by most accounts, the single most common runtime crash in C# history — a reference you assumed couldn't be null, turning out to be null, discovered only when the line actually executes. Nullable reference types , turned on with <Nullable>enable</Nullable> (the default in new project templates), attack this problem at compile time instead of leaving it to be discovered at runtime: the compiler starts treating every reference type as non-nullable by default , and tracks, flow-sensitively, line by line, whether a given reference could possibly be null at each point in your code. C# Run ▶ Copy public class Customer { public string Email { get ; set ; } = \"\" ; // never null public string ? MiddleName { get ; set ; } // may be null } void Print ( Customer c) { Console . WriteLine (c. Email . Length ); // fine Console . WriteLine (c. MiddleName . Length ); // ⚠ CS8602: possible null dereference if (c. MiddleName is not null ) Console . WriteLine (c. MiddleName . Length ); // fine: compiler knows it's not null here } Notice the third line doesn't warn, even though it's accessing the exact same MiddleName property the second line warned about — the compiler is tracking the flow of your code, and by the time execution reaches inside the if , it has proven, from the is not null check, that a null dereference here is impossible. This is genuinely flow analysis, not a blunt \"this property is marked nullable so always warn\" rule. Syntax Meaning string? May be null x?.Prop Null-conditional: null if x is null x ?? fallback Null-coalescing x ??= value Assign if null x! Null-forgiving : \"trust me, it isn't null\". Suppresses the warning only; use sparingly a?.B = x (C# 14) Assign only if a isn't null [NotNullWhen(true)] , [MaybeNull] , [NotNull] , [MemberNotNull] Attributes that teach the compiler about your API's null behaviour That last row matters more than it looks, because the compiler can only track what it's been told: C# Run ▶ Copy public bool TryFind ( int id, [NotNullWhen(true)] out Order ? order) { /* ... */ } if ( TryFind ( 5 , out var o)) Console . WriteLine (o. Total ); // no warning: o is known non-null here Without [NotNullWhen(true)] , the compiler has no way to know that order is guaranteed non-null specifically when TryFind returns true — it would warn on o.Total regardless. The attribute is you teaching the compiler your own API's actual null-contract, so its flow analysis stays accurate through your own methods, not just the BCL's. The analysis is entirely compile-time — nothing here checks anything at runtime This is the single most important thing to understand about the whole feature: nullable reference types add zero runtime checks . They're purely a compile-time warning system, built from static analysis of code you wrote . JSON deserialisation can still hand you a null where you declared string , not string? , if the JSON itself was missing that field. EF Core materialising a row with a database NULL can do the same. Reflection, or a caller in an assembly built with nullability disabled, bypasses the whole system entirely, because the check never existed at runtime to bypass. The practical response: validate at your actual trust boundaries anyway ( ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull ), and seriously consider making nullable warnings into build errors ( <WarningsAsErrors>nullable</WarningsAsErrors> ) — a warning nobody's forced to fix tends to quietly accumulate until it's ignored entirely. The feature does reach further than your own code, though: EF Core and ASP.NET Core both read these annotations and act on them. In EF Core, a string property becomes a NOT NULL database column and string? becomes nullable, inferred directly from your nullability annotation rather than needing a separate [Required] attribute. In ASP.NET Core model validation, a non-nullable property is implicitly treated as [Required] for the"},{"c":"modern-csharp","s":"pattern-matching-testing-a-values-shape-not-just-its-identit","h":"Pattern matching: testing a value's shape, not just its identity","t":"# Pattern matching: testing a value's shape, not just its identity Beginner A pattern tests whether a value has a particular shape — not just \"does it equal this constant\", but \"is it this type, with these properties, satisfying this condition\". Patterns work inside is expressions, switch statements, and switch expressions alike: Pattern Example Matches when Type / declaration o is Order order o is an Order (binds it) Constant x is null , status is OrderStatus.Paid Equal to a constant Relational age is >= 18 Comparison holds Logical c is >= 'a' and <= 'z' , x is not null , s is \"y\" or \"yes\" Combined Property o is { Status: OrderStatus.Paid, Total: > 100 } Properties match nested patterns Extended property o is { Customer.Country: \"DE\" } Nested member path Positional point is (0, 0) Deconstruct and match List args is [var first, .., var last] , [_, _] Sequence shape Discard / var _ , var x Anything C# Run ▶ Copy public static decimal ShippingFee ( Order o) => o switch { { Total : >= 100 } => 0m , { Customer . IsVip : true } => 0m , { ShippingAddress . Country : \"DE\" or \"AT\" or \"CH\" } => 4.90m , { ShippingAddress . Country : not null } => 12.90m , _ => throw new InvalidOperationException ( \"No shipping address\" ) }; public static IResult ToHttp < T >( Result < T > r) => r switch { { IsSuccess : true } => Results . Ok (r. Value ), { Error . Code : var c } when c. EndsWith ( \"not_found\" ) => Results . NotFound (), { Error . Code : \"conflict\" } => Results . Conflict (), _ => Results . BadRequest (r. Error ) }; string Describe ( int [] xs) => xs switch { [] => \"empty\" , [ var only] => $\"one item: {only}\" , [ var first, .. var middle, var last] => $\"{first}…{last} ({middle.Length} in between)\" }; That's exactly the missing default from the pull request at the start of this chapter — it wasn't an oversight the reviewer should have caught. What makes switch expressions genuinely safer than an old-style if / else chain, not just shorter, is what the compiler checks on your behalf: it checks exhaustiveness — a compiler warning if your arms don't provably cover every possible case — and subsumption , an error if an earlier arm already matches everything a later arm would, making that later arm permanently unreachable dead code. A switch expression that genuinely finds no matching arm at runtime (something exhaustiveness checking is supposed to prevent, but — as Microsoft's own docs note — can't always guarantee for every pattern shape, list patterns among them) throws SwitchExpressionException rather than silently doing nothing."},{"c":"modern-csharp","s":"records-types-built-for-carrying-data-not-identity","h":"Records: types built for carrying data, not identity","t":"# Records: types built for carrying data, not identity Beginner A record is designed around a different default assumption from a class: that two instances with the same data genuinely are the same thing, rather than being two distinct objects that happen to look alike. To make that assumption cheap to express, the compiler generates value-based Equals / GetHashCode , a readable ToString , Deconstruct , a copy constructor, and support for with — all automatically, from a one-line declaration: C# Run ▶ Copy public record ProductDto ( int Id , string Name , decimal Price ); // positional record class var a = new ProductDto ( 1 , \"Mouse\" , 20m ); var b = a with { Price = 18m }; // non-destructive mutation: a copy with one change Console . WriteLine (a == new ProductDto ( 1 , \"Mouse\" , 20m )); // True: value equality Console . WriteLine (a); // ProductDto { Id = 1, Name = Mouse, Price = 20 } var (id, name, _) = a; // deconstruction public record struct Point ( int X , int Y ); // value-type record (mutable by default) public readonly record struct Money ( decimal Amount , string Currency ); public abstract record Shape ; // records support inheritance (record classes) public sealed record Circle ( double Radius ) : Shape ; That a == new ProductDto(1, \"Mouse\", 20m) line returning True is the whole point made concrete: these are two entirely separate objects in memory, yet == says they're equal, because a record's equality compares values , field by field, not references — exactly the \"Records\" row from Type system 's equality table. with gives you non-destructive updates on top of that: b is a brand-new object, a full copy of a except for the one property you named, leaving a itself completely untouched. Use a record for Use a class for DTOs, API request/response models Entities with identity and lifecycle (EF Core entities) Commands, queries, events, messages Services with dependencies and behaviour Value objects (Money, Address) Mutable objects with complex invariants Configuration snapshots, results Anything where reference identity matters A record's generated equality is shallow — and that can genuinely surprise you public record Cart(List<Item> Items); looks immutable, and in one sense it is — nothing stops you reassigning Cart.Items to a different list after construction, true, but that's not the trap. The real trap: cart.Items.Add(...) still works perfectly fine, because the List<Item> itself was never made immutable, only the record's own property is \"init-only\". And the generated equality compares the list reference , not its contents — two carts holding lists with identical items, added in the same order, won't be considered equal unless they happen to share the exact same List<Item> instance. If you need genuine deep immutability and deep equality, use IReadOnlyList<T> or ImmutableArray<T> for the property, and write custom equality logic if the default field-by-field comparison isn't what you actually need."},{"c":"modern-csharp","s":"init-and-required-forcing-the-caller-to-set-what-matters","h":"init and required: forcing the caller to set what matters","t":"# init and required: forcing the caller to set what matters Beginner C# Run ▶ Copy public class CreateOrderRequest { public required int CustomerId { get ; init ; } // must be set; can't change afterwards public required List < LineDto > Lines { get ; init ; } public string ? CouponCode { get ; init ; } // optional, still immutable after init } var req = new CreateOrderRequest { CustomerId = 42 , Lines = [] }; // ✔ var bad = new CreateOrderRequest { CustomerId = 42 }; // ✘ CS9035: required member 'Lines' not set req. CouponCode = \"X\" ; // ✘ init-only These two keywords solve two separate, complementary problems. init restricts when a property can be set — only during object initialisation, never afterward — giving you immutability without needing a big, repetitive constructor to enforce it. required (C# 11) goes further, forcing the compiler to check that a caller actually set a given property at all; skip Lines on construction and you get CS9035 , a compile error, rather than discovering a silently-empty list of order lines at runtime. The combination of the two, required ... { get; init; } , is as close as C# gets to \"the caller absolutely must provide this, and nothing can ever change it afterward\" — all enforced before the code even runs. This isn't purely a compile-time nicety either: required is understood by System.Text.Json too, so a missing JSON property for a required member causes an actual deserialisation error, rather than silently leaving it at its default. [SetsRequiredMembers] on a constructor is the escape hatch for when you're providing an alternative way to guarantee the required members are set, telling the compiler \"trust this constructor, it covers them all.\""},{"c":"modern-csharp","s":"tuples-and-deconstruction","h":"Tuples and deconstruction","t":"# Tuples and deconstruction Beginner C# Run ▶ Copy ( int Count , decimal Total ) Summarise ( IEnumerable < Order > orders) => (orders. Count (), orders. Sum (o => o. Total )); var (count, total) = Summarise (orders); // deconstruct var summary = Summarise (orders); Console . WriteLine (summary. Total ); // named elements (a, b) = (b, a); // swap without a temp variable foreach ( var (key, value ) in dictionary) { } // KeyValuePair deconstruction ValueTuple , what (int, decimal) actually is underneath, is a mutable struct — which immediately tells you where it belongs and where it doesn't, by the same reasoning from Type system : fine for a quick, private or internal multi-value return where defining a whole named type feels like overkill, but the wrong choice for a public API or anything persisted. A record gives you named, evolvable, properly-serialisable members for a few extra characters — worth it the moment the value crosses a real boundary."},{"c":"modern-csharp","s":"extension-methods-and-extension-members-adding-capability-wi","h":"Extension methods and extension members: adding capability without touching the original type","t":"# Extension methods and extension members: adding capability without touching the original type Intermediate Extension methods (a C# 3 feature — old enough that you've definitely already used dozens without necessarily naming the mechanism) let you add methods to an existing type without touching its source at all. Under the hood they're ordinary static methods in a static class, where the first parameter carries a this modifier. LINQ's entire operator set, IServiceCollection.AddXyz() , app.MapXyz() — all of these are extension methods. That's precisely why IServiceCollection itself, a tiny interface at its core, seems to have an enormous API surface once you count every library's extension methods on top of it. C# Run ▶ Copy public static class ServiceCollectionExtensions { public static IServiceCollection AddOrdering ( this IServiceCollection services, IConfiguration config) { services. AddScoped < IOrderService , OrderService >(); services. AddOptions < OrderingOptions >(). Bind (config. GetSection ( \"Ordering\" )). ValidateOnStart (); return services; // return for fluent chaining } } builder. Services . AddOrdering (builder. Configuration ); // reads like a built-in method This is the exact mechanism behind the Add<Feature>() / Map<Feature>() convention you'll see throughout Dependency injection and elsewhere in this book: it's how every library plugs itself cleanly into ASP.NET Core, and it's how you keep your own Program.cs from turning into a wall of raw registration calls. Extension members (C# 14) extend the same idea beyond methods, to properties, static members and operators, using a new extension block: C# Run ▶ Copy public static class OrderExtensions { extension ( Order order) { public bool IsBig => order. Total > 1_000m ; // extension property public Money TotalWithTax ( decimal rate) => order. Total * ( 1 + rate); } } if (order. IsBig ) { } Three rules worth knowing before you reach for this (confirmed against Microsoft's CS0122 reference and the extension methods guide ): extensions can never access an object's private members, the same restriction extension methods have always had; a real instance member always wins over an extension with the same signature, if both exist — the extension only fills in gaps, never overrides; and the extension's own namespace has to be imported at the call site, same as any other extension method, for the compiler to find it at all."},{"c":"modern-csharp","s":"less-ceremony-top-level-statements-global-usings-file-scoped","h":"Less ceremony: top-level statements, global usings, file-scoped namespaces","t":"# Less ceremony: top-level statements, global usings, file-scoped namespaces Beginner C# Run ▶ Copy // Program.cs: top-level statements (C# 9). The compiler generates the Main method. var builder = WebApplication . CreateBuilder (args); var app = builder. Build (); app. MapGet ( \"/\" , () => \"Hello\" ); app. Run (); C# Run ▶ Copy // GlobalUsings.cs (or <Using Include=\"...\"/> in the csproj) global using Shop . Domain ; global using Microsoft . EntityFrameworkCore ; // Any file: file-scoped namespace (C# 10), one less indentation level namespace Shop . Api . Orders ; public class OrderEndpoints { } None of these three change what the compiled code actually does — they exist purely to remove ceremony that every single C# file used to repeat. Top-level statements mean Program.cs is no longer wrapped in a class Program { static void Main(string[] args) { ... } } you never actually needed to think about; the compiler generates that scaffolding silently. Global usings move a using you'd otherwise retype at the top of every file into one shared place. File-scoped namespaces drop one level of { } indentation that used to wrap an entire file's contents for no real benefit. <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings> goes further still, adding a set of SDK-specific global usings automatically ( System , System.Linq , System.Threading.Tasks , Microsoft.AspNetCore.Builder , Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection , and more) — which is a large part of why modern minimal API code can get away with no using statements at the top at all."},{"c":"modern-csharp","s":"primary-constructors-and-collection-expressions","h":"Primary constructors and collection expressions","t":"# Primary constructors and collection expressions Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy // Primary constructor (C# 12): perfect for DI-injected services public sealed class CheckoutService ( IOrderRepository orders, IPaymentGateway payments, TimeProvider clock) { public async Task < Result < OrderId >> CheckoutAsync ( Cart cart, CancellationToken ct) { var order = Order . Create (cart, clock. GetUtcNow ()); // orders, payments, clock are in scope everywhere in the class await payments. ChargeAsync (order. Total , ct); orders. Add (order); return order. Id ; } } // Collection expressions (C# 12): one syntax for arrays, lists, spans, immutable collections… int [] a = [ 1 , 2 , 3 ]; List < string > names = []; ImmutableArray < int > imm = [ 1 , 2 ]; ReadOnlySpan < byte > bytes = [0x48, 0x49]; int [] merged = [..a, ..other, 99 ]; // spread OOP already covered primary constructors properly, including the one genuine gotcha worth remembering (their parameters are reassignable, not automatically readonly fields). Collection expressions deserve a mention of their own, purely for the uniformity they bring. Before C# 12, an array, a List<T> , an ImmutableArray<T> and a Span<T> each needed their own distinct initialisation syntax. Now [...] works for all of them — the compiler picks the right concrete construction based on the variable's declared type. One syntax to learn, reused everywhere a sequence of values needs creating."},{"c":"modern-csharp","s":"strings-interpolation-and-raw-literals","h":"Strings: interpolation and raw literals","t":"# Strings: interpolation and raw literals Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy var msg = $\"Order {order.Id} total {order.Total:C2} placed {order.CreatedAt:yyyy-MM-dd}\" ; var aligned = $\"|{name,-20}|{qty,5}|\" ; // alignment var path = @\"C:\\data\\orders\" ; // verbatim: no escape processing var json = \"\"\" { \"name\": \"Keyboard\", \"tags\": [\"office\", \"usb\"] } \"\"\" ; // raw string literal (C# 11): no escaping, indentation trimmed var template = $ $\"\"\" { \"id\": {{order.Id}}, \"literal\": \"{braces}\" } \"\"\" ; // $$ means {{x}} interpolates and single { } are literal Raw string literals exist specifically because embedding JSON, regex, or SQL inside a normal C# string means constantly escaping quotes and backslashes that mean something different in the two languages at once. A triple-quoted raw string sidesteps that entirely — what you see between the \"\"\" markers is exactly, literally, what you get, with no escaping required for anything inside it. Worth knowing precisely: interpolated strings aren't naive string concatenation under the hood — they compile through DefaultInterpolatedStringHandler , which avoids boxing value-type arguments and avoids unnecessary intermediate string allocations along the way. That efficiency, though, comes with one important exception worth remembering: don't use interpolated strings in log messages . Errors & logging explains exactly why — a log call needs its arguments to stay as separate, structured values a log store can query on, and string interpolation collapses them into one opaque, pre-formatted string before the logger ever sees them."},{"c":"modern-csharp","s":"other-quality-of-life-features-worth-recognising-on-sight","h":"Other quality-of-life features worth recognising on sight","t":"# Other quality-of-life features worth recognising on sight Intermediate Feature Example Target-typed new Dictionary<string, List<int>> map = new(); nameof throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(order)); , survives renames Ranges and indices items[^1] , items[1..^1] , text[..10] using declaration using var scope = logger.BeginScope(...); Static lambdas list.Select(static x => x * 2) : prevents accidental capture Local functions Helper methods inside a method; can be static Default lambda parameters var greet = (string name = \"you\") => $\"Hi {name}\"; Type aliases using OrderMap = System.Collections.Generic.Dictionary<int, Order>; , any type since C# 12 params collections void Log(params ReadOnlySpan<string> parts) : no array allocation New Lock type private readonly Lock _gate = new(); lock (_gate) { } , faster than locking on object field keyword public string Name { get; set => field = value.Trim(); } UTF-8 literals ReadOnlySpan<byte> h = \"Content-Type\"u8; A couple of these are worth a second's thought rather than just filing away as syntax. nameof(order) isn't just a shorter way to write \"order\" — the key benefit is that it's checked by the compiler and tied to the actual identifier, so renaming the parameter and forgetting to update a hard-coded string message simply can't happen; the compiler won't let the two drift apart. Static lambdas are the direct, deliberate enforcement mechanism for the closure-cost discussion in Delegates, lambdas and closures — marking a lambda static is you telling the compiler \"I don't intend to capture anything here, please fail to compile if I accidentally do.\" Q What are nullable reference types and do they prevent NullReferenceException at runtime? A compiler feature (enabled with <Nullable>) that treats reference types as non-null by default, so T? means \"may be null\", and it warns about possible null dereferences through flow analysis. It's compile-time only: there are no runtime checks, so values from deserialisation, reflection or unannotated code can still be null. Validate at boundaries. Q What does the null-forgiving operator (!) do? It suppresses a nullable warning for that expression. It has no runtime effect. Overusing it defeats the feature. Q What is a record and how is it different from a class? A record is a class (or struct with record struct ) for which the compiler generates value-based equality and GetHashCode, ToString, Deconstruct and with support. Two records with equal property values are equal. Classes use reference equality by default. Use records for data (DTOs, messages, value objects) and classes for entities and services. Q What's the difference between init and required? init makes a property settable only during object initialisation. required forces callers to set the property in the initialiser, checked by the compiler. They're often combined: public required string Email { get; init; } . Q Explain switch expressions and property patterns. A switch expression evaluates arms top-down and returns the first matching arm's value: x switch { pattern => value, _ => default } . Property patterns match on members, { Status: Paid, Total: > 100 } , and nest ( { Customer.Country: \"DE\" } ). The compiler warns if not all cases are covered. Q How do extension methods work and how does ASP.NET Core use them? They are static methods in static classes whose first parameter is marked with this . The compiler lets you call them as instance methods on that type. ASP.NET Core and libraries expose their setup as extension methods on IServiceCollection (AddX) and IEndpointRouteBuilder/IApplicationBuilder (MapX/UseX), which is the convention for organising Program.cs. Q What are primary constructors and what's a gotcha? C# 12 lets classes declare constructor parameters in the type header, and those parameters are in scope throughout the class body, which suits DI. The gotcha: they're mutable captured parameters, not readonly fields, and capturing them in a struct or in several members"},{"c":"modern-csharp","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading What's new in C# (every version) What's new in C# 14 — extension members, the field keyword, null-conditional assignment, nameof on unbound generics Nullable reference types Pattern matching overview — exhaustiveness and subsumption, straight from the source SwitchExpressionException class Records — confirms record struct is mutable by default; only readonly record struct is immutable"},{"c":"modern-csharp","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run in a plain console project ( dotnet new console ) — no extra packages needed. It checks record value equality, with , the shallow-equality gotcha, required enforced through JSON, pattern matching, extension methods and collection expressions. C# Run ▶ Copy using System . Text . Json ; void Check ( bool condition, string claim) { if (!condition) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: {claim}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: {claim}\" ); } // 1. Records have value equality: two separate instances with equal properties are ==. var a = new ProductDto ( 1 , \"Mouse\" , 20m ); var sameValues = new ProductDto ( 1 , \"Mouse\" , 20m ); Check (! ReferenceEquals (a, sameValues) && a == sameValues, \"Two separate record instances with equal properties are == (value equality)\" ); // 2. `with` produces a non-destructive copy: the original is untouched, the copy has the one changed property. var b = a with { Price = 18m }; Check (a. Price == 20m , \"The original record is untouched after `with`\" ); Check (b. Price == 18m && b. Id == a. Id && b. Name == a. Name , \"`with` copies every property except the one(s) explicitly changed\" ); // 3. A record's generated equality is SHALLOW for reference-type members: two carts with equal-content // lists (different List<T> instances) are NOT equal, because the list REFERENCE is compared. var cart1 = new Cart ( new List < string > { \"sku-1\" , \"sku-2\" }); var cart2 = new Cart ( new List < string > { \"sku-1\" , \"sku-2\" }); Check (cart1 != cart2, \"Two records holding different List<T> instances with identical contents are NOT equal (equality is shallow/by-reference for that member)\" ); var cart3 = new Cart (cart1. Items ); // same list reference Check (cart1 == cart3, \"Two records sharing the SAME list reference ARE equal\" ); // 4. `required` is enforced by System.Text.Json: a missing required property fails deserialization. var jsonMissingRequired = \"\"\"{ \"CustomerId\": 42 }\"\"\" ; // \"Lines\" is required and missing bool threwOnMissingRequired = false ; try { JsonSerializer . Deserialize < CreateOrderRequest >(jsonMissingRequired); } catch ( JsonException ) { threwOnMissingRequired = true ; } Check (threwOnMissingRequired, \"System.Text.Json throws JsonException when a `required` property is missing from the JSON\" ); var jsonComplete = \"\"\"{ \"CustomerId\": 42, \"Lines\": [\"sku-1\"] }\"\"\" ; var deserialized = JsonSerializer . Deserialize < CreateOrderRequest >(jsonComplete); Check (deserialized is { CustomerId : 42 }, \"System.Text.Json deserializes successfully once every `required` property is present\" ); // 5. Pattern matching: property patterns and list patterns match the shapes the chapter describes. Check ( Describe ( new [] { 1 , 2 , 3 }) == \"first=1 last=3 middle=1\" , \"A list pattern [first, .. middle, last] destructures a 3+ element array correctly\" ); Check ( Describe ( Array . Empty < int >()) == \"empty\" , \"A list pattern [] matches an empty array\" ); Check ( ShippingFee ( new Order ( Total : 150 , IsVip : false )) == 0m , \"A property pattern on Total >= 100 matches and returns free shipping\" ); Check ( ShippingFee ( new Order ( Total : 10 , IsVip : true )) == 0m , \"A property pattern on IsVip matches and returns free shipping regardless of total\" ); // 6. Extension methods add capability to an existing type without modifying its source. Check ( \"hello world\" . IsTitleCaseWord () == false , \"An extension method (IsTitleCaseWord) works like an instance method on a type it didn't define\" ); Check ( \"Hello\" . IsTitleCaseWord () == true , \"An extension method correctly identifies a title-case word\" ); // 7. Collection expressions produce the right concrete type for the declared variable. int [] arr = [ 1 , 2 , 3 ]; List < string > list = [ \"a\" , \"b\" ]; Check (arr. Length == 3 && arr is int [], \"A collection expression assigned to int[] produces an array\" ); Check (list. Count == 2 && list is List < string >, \"The same [...] syntax assigned to List<string> produces a List<T>\" ); Console . "},{"c":"advanced-csharp","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Span<T> is a view into existing memory (array, string, stack, native) — slicing it never copies, which is why hot-path parsing code uses it to avoid allocating. Span<T> is a ref struct : the compiler forces it to stay on the stack, so it can never outlive the memory it points at (including stackalloc 'd memory). Memory<T> is Span<T> 's heap-friendly cousin — use it when you need to store a span-like value in a field or carry it across an await , then call .Span for the synchronous part. ArrayPool<T> lets you reuse large buffers instead of allocating (and eventually GC-ing) a new one every request. Reflection reads a program's own metadata at runtime (what powers DI containers, serializers, test runners) but is slow, allocation-heavy, and hostile to trimming/Native AOT. Source generators do reflection's job at build time instead, producing ordinary, fast, trimmer-visible code — which is why the ecosystem is moving toward them."},{"c":"advanced-csharp","s":"why-a-web-framework-needs-tools-this-low-level","h":"Why a web framework needs tools this low-level","t":"# Why a web framework needs tools this low-level Intermediate Here's the scenario this whole chapter is really about: a profiler flags one endpoint as the slowest thing in your service, and the trace doesn't show slow CPU work at all — it shows the GC running constantly, because every request parses a line of text by calling .Split(',') and allocating a fresh array and four fresh strings, every single time, thousands of times a second. Nothing in that code is wrong ; it's just paying an allocation cost it didn't need to pay. Every tool in this chapter exists to answer that same profiler trace in one way or another. ASP.NET Core can serve millions of requests per second on one machine, mostly because it avoids allocating on the hot path. Memory & GC already established that allocation rate, not raw CPU work, usually limits throughput — so parsing HTTP headers, routing a request, and serializing a JSON response all work on slices of buffers that already exist, instead of carving out new strings and arrays per request. You'll rarely write this kind of code yourself, but you'll run into these types constantly in APIs you call every day — ReadOnlySpan<char> overloads, IBufferWriter<byte> , PipeReader — and interviews ask about them because knowing why they exist explains how a fast .NET system is actually built."},{"c":"advanced-csharp","s":"span-a-view-not-a-copy","h":"Span : a view, not a copy","t":"# Span : a view, not a copy Intermediate Span<T> is a view over contiguous memory — part of an array, a string, stack memory, or native memory — represented as nothing more than a pointer plus a length. The single idea that makes it useful: slicing a span creates a new, narrower view, never a copy. C# Run ▶ Copy string line = \"2026-10-27,ORD-1042,149.90,EUR\" ; // Classic: Split allocates an array + 4 strings var parts = line. Split ( ',' ); var amount = decimal . Parse (parts[ 2 ], CultureInfo . InvariantCulture ); // Span: zero allocations ReadOnlySpan < char > span = line; int i1 = span. IndexOf ( ',' ); int i2 = span[(i1 + 1 )..]. IndexOf ( ',' ) + i1 + 1 ; int i3 = span[(i2 + 1 )..]. IndexOf ( ',' ) + i2 + 1 ; decimal amount2 = decimal . Parse (span[(i2 + 1 )..i3], CultureInfo . InvariantCulture ); // Parse accepts ReadOnlySpan<char> // .NET 9+: Split over spans without allocating foreach ( Range r in span. Split ( ',' )) Console . WriteLine (span[r]. ToString ()); Compare the two approaches to the same four-field line: line.Split(',') allocates a whole new string[] , plus four brand-new strings — a full copy of every character, duplicated. The span version never copies a single character; span[(i1 + 1)..] is just a different pointer-and-length pair into the exact same memory line already occupies. For a hot endpoint parsing thousands of lines a second — the exact endpoint from the top of this chapter — that difference is the entire reason Span<T> exists. C# Run ▶ Copy Span < int > fromArray = new int [ 10 ]; // array Span < int > part = fromArray. Slice ( 2 , 5 ); // slice of it ReadOnlySpan < char > fromString = \"hello\" . AsSpan ( 1 , 3 ); // \"ell\", no new string Span < byte > onStack = stackalloc byte [ 256 ]; // stack memory, no GC at all Span < int > listView = CollectionsMarshal . AsSpan (list); // List<T>'s internal array Notice how varied the memory a span can point at is — an array, a slice of that array, a substring with no new allocation, raw stack memory via stackalloc , even List<T> 's own hidden internal array via CollectionsMarshal . That's deliberate: Span<T> is a uniform way to describe \"a contiguous chunk of memory,\" regardless of where it came from. That's why the BCL exposes span overloads so widely: int.TryParse(ReadOnlySpan<char>) , Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(ReadOnlySpan<char>, Span<byte>) , Stream.Read(Span<byte>) , string.Create , and the whole MemoryExtensions family ( IndexOf , Trim , StartsWith , SequenceEqual , Contains )."},{"c":"advanced-csharp","s":"ref-structs-the-restriction-that-makes-stack-memory-safe","h":"ref structs: the restriction that makes stack memory safe","t":"# ref structs: the restriction that makes stack memory safe Advanced That stackalloc line above raises an obvious question: if a Span<int> can point at raw stack memory, and stack frames disappear the instant their method returns, what stops you from holding onto a span after its underlying stack memory is already gone, and reading garbage? The answer: Span<T> is a ref struct — a value type the compiler only ever allows to live on the stack, no exceptions. Every other restriction on ref struct types follows from that one rule: A ref struct can't… Because… Be a field of a class or normal struct That would put it on the heap Be boxed or cast to object /interface Boxing allocates on the heap Be captured by a lambda Closures live on the heap Be used across await or yield The state machine lives on the heap Be an array element Arrays live on the heap Every row is the same answer, five times over: each operation would, one way or another, force the Span<T> onto the heap . Delegates, lambdas and closures showed exactly what happens when a lambda captures a local — the compiler moves it into a heap-allocated DisplayClass . If a Span<T> pointing at stackalloc 'd memory got moved there, the span would outlive the stack memory it points at, and reading through it afterward means reading freed, possibly reused memory — the kind of memory-safety hole C# normally makes structurally impossible. The ref struct restriction is the compiler refusing to let that situation arise at all. C# 13 relaxed this slightly, without reopening that hole: ref structs can now implement interfaces (without ever being boxed to do it) and appear as generic arguments, via the where T : allows ref struct constraint you met briefly in Generics ."},{"c":"advanced-csharp","s":"memory-the-heap-friendly-cousin-for-exactly-the-cases-span-f","h":"Memory : the heap-friendly cousin, for exactly the cases Span forbids","t":"# Memory : the heap-friendly cousin, for exactly the cases Span forbids Advanced Given that list of forbidden operations, what do you do when you need something span-like stored in a field , or used across an await — both things Span<T> can never do? Memory<T> (and ReadOnlyMemory<T> ) is the answer: an ordinary struct — not a ref struct — built from the same pieces (an array, an offset, a length), but without the stack-only restriction, because it's never allowed to point at stackalloc 'd memory in the first place. C# Run ▶ Copy public async Task < int > ReadIntoAsync ( Stream s, Memory < byte > buffer, CancellationToken ct) { int read = await s. ReadAsync (buffer, ct); // fine across await return Checksum (buffer. Span [..read]); // drop to Span for synchronous processing } Notice the pattern in ReadIntoAsync : the parameter and everything crossing the await is Memory<byte> , but the moment we're back to purely synchronous code — Checksum , called right after the await completes — we call .Span to drop down to the faster Span<T> view. Rule of thumb: synchronous methods take Span<T> / ReadOnlySpan<T> ; async methods take Memory<T> / ReadOnlyMemory<T> , converting to a Span only for the synchronous stretches between awaits."},{"c":"advanced-csharp","s":"pooling-buffers-paying-the-allocation-cost-once-instead-of-e","h":"Pooling buffers: paying the allocation cost once instead of every request","t":"# Pooling buffers: paying the allocation cost once instead of every request Advanced Memory & GC already established that anything ≥ 85 KB goes straight onto the Large Object Heap, and allocating there repeatedly leads to frequent, expensive full GCs. Allocating a large temporary buffer fresh on every request is exactly that pattern. ArrayPool<T> breaks it: rent a buffer when you need one, return it when done, and let the pool hand that same physical array to the next caller instead of the GC having to reclaim and re-allocate it. C# Run ▶ Copy byte [] buffer = ArrayPool < byte >. Shared . Rent ( 64 * 1024 ); // may return a LARGER array than requested try { int n = await stream. ReadAsync (buffer. AsMemory ( 0 , 64 * 1024 ), ct); Process (buffer. AsSpan ( 0 , n)); // only use the part you asked for } finally { ArrayPool < byte >. Shared . Return (buffer); // never use it after returning } Two details in that finally block are easy to get wrong. First, Rent may hand back an array larger than what you asked for — the pool deals in fixed bucket sizes, not exact matches — so slice down to the length you actually requested rather than trusting .Length . Second, and more dangerous: once you've called Return , that array can be handed to a different caller at any moment, so using it afterward means reading or writing data that now belongs to someone else's operation. Related tools worth knowing by name for when ArrayPool alone isn't quite the right shape: RecyclableMemoryStream (Microsoft.IO) for pooled, growable memory streams; ObjectPool<T> (Microsoft.Extensions.ObjectPool, commonly used for pooling StringBuilder instances); MemoryPool<T> ; IBufferWriter<T> / ArrayBufferWriter<T> ; and System.IO.Pipelines ( PipeReader / PipeWriter ), the pooled-buffer abstraction Kestrel itself is built directly on top of."},{"c":"advanced-csharp","s":"ref-locals-ref-returns-in-and-scoped","h":"ref locals, ref returns, in and scoped","t":"# ref locals, ref returns, in and scoped Advanced C# Run ▶ Copy // Return a reference into an array instead of a copy, so callers can modify in place public static ref Order FindRef ( Order [] orders, int id) { for ( int i = 0 ; i < orders. Length ; i++) if (orders[i]. Id == id) return ref orders[i]; throw new KeyNotFoundException (); } ref var o = ref FindRef (arr, 7 ); o. Status = Status . Shipped ; // modifies the array element directly (useful for structs) A normal method return hands back a copy — for Order as a struct , mutating o afterward would mutate a throwaway copy, touching nothing in arr . ref Order FindRef(...) changes that: it returns a genuine reference into the array itself , so o.Status = Status.Shipped writes straight back into arr[i] , no copy involved. The rest of this family exists for increasingly specialized control: scoped restricts how long a ref can live, so the compiler can prove , statically, that it never escapes its intended scope — a safety mechanism, not a performance one. ref readonly returns a reference the caller is guaranteed can't modify through. Unsafe.As , MemoryMarshal.Cast and CollectionsMarshal give expert-level control over memory representation. All four matter mainly inside library and infrastructure code — application-level backend code rarely needs any of them directly."},{"c":"advanced-csharp","s":"unsafe-code-and-interop","h":"unsafe code and interop","t":"# unsafe code and interop Advanced C# Run ▶ Copy // <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks> unsafe { int x = 10 ; int * p = &x; *p = 20 ; fixed ( byte * ptr = buffer) { /* pin a managed array so the GC won't move it */ } } // Calling native code: P/Invoke with a source-generated marshaller (AOT-friendly) internal static partial class Native { [LibraryImport(\"libc\", EntryPoint = \"getpid\")] internal static partial int GetPid (); } Raw pointers step entirely outside the type-safety and bounds-checking guarantees the rest of this book takes for granted — the CLR's \"managed code\" promises from How code runs don't apply inside an unsafe block. Reach for this only for genuine native interop, or a hot spot you've actually measured and proven needs it — not as a general performance habit. fixed deserves its own callout: it pins a managed object, telling the GC \"don't move this one during compaction,\" which is necessary before handing its address to native code. The cost (see Memory & GC ) is that a long-lived pin sits immovably in the middle of the heap, forcing the GC to compact around it and fragmenting the heap over time."},{"c":"advanced-csharp","s":"reflection-reading-your-own-programs-metadata-from-inside-th","h":"Reflection: reading your own program's metadata, from inside the program","t":"# Reflection: reading your own program's metadata, from inside the program Intermediate Reflection is code that reads an assembly's own metadata — covered back in How code runs as the structured description bundled into every compiled .dll — while the program is actually running, and can use that metadata to create instances and invoke members it only discovered by name, not by a compile-time reference. C# Run ▶ Copy Type t = typeof ( Order ); // or obj.GetType(), or Type.GetType(\"Shop.Order\") foreach ( PropertyInfo p in t. GetProperties ( BindingFlags . Public | BindingFlags . Instance )) Console . WriteLine ( $\"{p.Name}: {p.PropertyType.Name}\" ); object instance = Activator . CreateInstance (t)!; t. GetProperty ( \"Status\" )!. SetValue (instance, OrderStatus . Paid ); var result = t. GetMethod ( \"Cancel\" )!. Invoke (instance, null ); // Assembly scanning: how DI auto-registration and MediatR-style handler discovery work var handlerTypes = typeof ( Program ). Assembly . GetTypes () . Where (x => x is { IsAbstract : false , IsInterface : false }) . SelectMany (x => x. GetInterfaces (), (impl, iface) => (impl, iface)) . Where (p => p.iface. IsGenericType && p.iface. GetGenericTypeDefinition () == typeof ( IRequestHandler <,>)); That last block is worth recognizing on sight: scanning every type in an assembly , filtering down to the ones implementing a particular generic interface, is exactly how DI auto-registration tools and MediatR-style handler discovery find every IRequestHandler<,> in your codebase without you registering each one by hand. This same capability powers a surprisingly large share of .NET: DI containers use it to find a type's constructor and parameters; serializers use it to read an object's properties; ASP.NET Core uses it to discover controllers and bind models; EF Core uses it to build its entity model; test frameworks use it to find every [Fact] -attributed method; AutoMapper-style mappers use it to match source and destination properties by name. That power has a real, measurable cost : reflective calls are slow compared to a direct method call, because they involve string-based lookups, security checks, and boxing every argument into an object[] to pass through a generic Invoke . They're allocation-heavy on top of that. They're invisible to the compiler — rename a property reflection looks up by string, and nothing breaks until that code actually runs and fails to find it. And reflection is hostile to trimming and Native AOT (see How code runs on trimming): a trimmer can't know a method accessed only by a runtime string lookup is still needed, and will happily remove it. Mitigations, roughly in order of how much they help: cache the PropertyInfo / MethodInfo once, rather than re-resolving it every call; compile an expression tree (covered at the end of this chapter) or use Delegate.CreateDelegate to turn a one-time reflective lookup into a fast, reusable delegate; reach for [UnsafeAccessor] — introduced in .NET 8, confirmed by its Microsoft Learn reference — for private-member access that skips reflection's runtime lookup entirely (it resolves to a direct call at JIT time, though its generics support stayed limited until .NET 9); or, best for new code, use a source generator instead — the rest of this chapter builds toward that."},{"c":"advanced-csharp","s":"attributes-metadata-that-does-nothing-until-something-reads-","h":"Attributes: metadata that does nothing until something reads it","t":"# Attributes: metadata that does nothing until something reads it Intermediate Attributes attach declarative metadata directly to code — but, on their own, an attribute does nothing at all . It's inert until some other piece of code — the compiler, the runtime, or a framework, almost always via reflection or a dedicated metadata-reading path — specifically goes looking for it and acts on what it finds. C# Run ▶ Copy [ApiController] [Route(\"api/orders\")] [Authorize(Policy = \"CanReadOrders\")] public class OrdersController : ControllerBase { [HttpGet(\"{id:int}\")] [ ProducesResponseType < OrderDto >( StatusCodes . Status200OK )] [ProducesResponseType(StatusCodes.Status404NotFound)] public IActionResult Get ( int id) => Ok (); } public class CreateUser { [Required, EmailAddress, MaxLength(256)] public string Email { get ; set ; } = \"\" ; [JsonPropertyName(\"display_name\")] public string ? Name { get ; set ; } } // Your own attribute [AttributeUsage(AttributeTargets.Method, AllowMultiple = false)] public sealed class AuditAttribute ( string action) : Attribute { public string Action { get ; } = action; } // Reading it (e.g. in an endpoint filter or middleware) var audit = endpoint?. Metadata . GetMetadata < AuditAttribute >(); Read by Examples Compiler [Obsolete] , [CallerMemberName] , [NotNullWhen] , [SetsRequiredMembers] , [Experimental] Runtime [Serializable] , [StructLayout] , [MethodImpl(AggressiveInlining)] , [ThreadStatic] Frameworks [HttpGet] , [Authorize] , [FromBody] , [Required] , [Key] , [JsonPropertyName] , [Fact] Source generators [LoggerMessage] , [JsonSerializable] , [GeneratedRegex] , [LibraryImport] Notice how differently \"reader\" behaves across rows — the same [Attribute] syntax feeds four genuinely different consumers, each acting at a different stage: compile time, runtime, framework startup, or build-time code generation. In ASP.NET Core, endpoint attributes like [Authorize] get converted into endpoint metadata that middleware can read after routing has selected the endpoint — Routing covers how that connection works, and it's why middleware ordering matters so much for anything reading endpoint metadata."},{"c":"advanced-csharp","s":"source-generators-doing-reflections-job-but-at-build-time-in","h":"Source generators: doing reflection's job, but at build time instead of runtime","t":"# Source generators: doing reflection's job, but at build time instead of runtime Advanced A source generator is a plugin that hooks into the Roslyn compiler itself, inspects your code during compilation , and adds new, real C# source files to the build. The crucial difference from reflection: the output is ordinary, compiled code — fast to run, debuggable like anything else you wrote, and, because it's genuinely present in the compiled output rather than discovered by a runtime string lookup, fully visible to the trimmer . C# Run ▶ Copy // 1. Logging: generates an efficient, strongly-typed logging method public static partial class Log { [LoggerMessage(EventId = 1001, Level = LogLevel.Information, Message = \"Order {OrderId} placed by {CustomerId}\")] public static partial void OrderPlaced ( this ILogger logger, int orderId, int customerId); } logger. OrderPlaced (order. Id , order. CustomerId ); // no boxing, no template parsing at runtime, level checked first // 2. JSON: serializer metadata generated at compile time (required for Native AOT) [JsonSerializable(typeof(OrderDto))] [JsonSerializable(typeof(List<OrderDto>))] internal partial class ShopJsonContext : JsonSerializerContext { } builder. Services . ConfigureHttpJsonOptions (o => o. SerializerOptions . TypeInfoResolverChain . Insert ( 0 , ShopJsonContext . Default )); // 3. Regex: compiled to C# at build time public static partial class Patterns { [ GeneratedRegex ( @\"^[A-Z]{2}-\\d{4}$\" , RegexOptions . CultureInvariant )] public static partial Regex Sku (); } bool ok = Patterns . Sku (). IsMatch ( \"KB-1234\" ); Notice the shared shape across all three examples: each declares a partial method or class, with no body of its own, plus an attribute telling the generator what to produce. The generator writes the actual implementation into a separate, generated file, compiled alongside your own code as if you'd written it by hand. [LoggerMessage] writes a logging method that parses its message template once, at build time, instead of re-parsing it on every call the way logger.LogInformation($\"...\") effectively would. [JsonSerializable] pre-generates the type metadata System.Text.Json would otherwise need reflection to discover at runtime — which is why it's required , not just an optimization, for Native AOT. [GeneratedRegex] compiles a regex pattern into actual matching code at build time, instead of the runtime regex engine interpreting the pattern on first use. Beyond these three: configuration binding ( EnableConfigurationBindingGenerator ), minimal API request delegates (RDG), options validation ( [OptionsValidator] ), [LibraryImport] native interop, and Blazor/Razor compilation. Popular third-party ones include Mapperly (object-to-object mapping), Mediator (a source-generated alternative to reflection-based MediatR), Refit, StronglyTypedId and Vogen. Why the whole .NET ecosystem is actively moving from reflection to generators Reflection does its discovery work every time the app starts — slowing startup every time, for every process — and hides the code it touches from the trimmer, which is why reflection-heavy libraries tend to be Native-AOT-incompatible. Generators do the same discovery work once, at build time , and emit plain, ordinary code. The payoff: faster startup (nothing left to discover at runtime), lower allocations, errors caught at compile time instead of surfacing deep in production, and generated code that's fully visible to the trimmer — which is what makes the app AOT-compatible at all. When choosing between similar libraries, \"ships a source-generator mode\" is a real advantage, not a minor detail."},{"c":"advanced-csharp","s":"expression-trees-for-metaprogramming-building-code-as-data-b","h":"Expression trees for metaprogramming: building code as data, by hand","t":"# Expression trees for metaprogramming: building code as data, by hand Advanced LINQ already showed expression trees from the consuming side — EF Core walking a tree the compiler built for you from a lambda. There's a second, less common use: you can build an expression tree yourself, by hand, from its individual pieces, then compile it into a real, runnable delegate at runtime. This is a classic technique inside object mappers and ORMs that generate fast property-access code for types they only discover dynamically. C# Run ▶ Copy // Build: (Order o) => o.Total then compile once and cache var param = Expression . Parameter ( typeof ( Order ), \"o\" ); var body = Expression . Property (param, nameof ( Order . Total )); var getter = Expression . Lambda < Func < Order , decimal >>(body, param). Compile (); decimal t = getter(order); // as fast as a normal delegate call after compilation This genuinely assembles a lambda from raw pieces: Expression.Parameter creates the o parameter node, Expression.Property builds the o.Total member-access node, and Expression.Lambda<...>(...).Compile() turns that whole tree into an actual, callable delegate — functionally equivalent to writing (Order o) => o.Total directly in source, just built programmatically at runtime. The catch: .Compile() itself is expensive — milliseconds, not microseconds — and isn't available at all under Native AOT, where it falls back to a slower interpreter instead of genuinely compiling. The practical rule: cache the compiled delegate rather than recompiling on every call, and for new code, prefer a source generator over hand-built expression trees wherever one is available — same \"fast, type-specific code\" outcome, without paying the runtime compilation cost. Q What is Span and why is it useful? A stack-only struct that represents a contiguous region of memory (array, string, stack or native) as a pointer plus length. Slicing creates views without copying, so parsing and formatting can run with zero allocations, reducing GC pressure in hot paths. Q Why can't Span be used in async methods or stored in a class field? It's a ref struct that may point to stack memory, so it must never end up on the heap. Async methods and iterators store locals in a heap-allocated state machine, and class fields live on the heap, so both are forbidden. Use Memory<T> instead and call .Span when working synchronously. Q Span vs Memory ? Span<T> is a ref struct: stack-only, fastest, usable in synchronous code. Memory<T> is a regular struct that can live on the heap, be stored in fields and cross awaits; you get a Span from it via .Span. Q What is ArrayPool and when would you use it? A pool of reusable arrays (ArrayPool<T>.Shared). Rent large temporary buffers instead of allocating them per operation, which avoids LOH allocations and GC pressure, and return them in a finally block. Remember that Rent may return a larger array than requested. Q What is reflection and what are its downsides? Runtime inspection and invocation of types and members via metadata. It powers DI, serializers, ORMs and test runners, but it's slow, allocates, bypasses compile-time checking, and isn't compatible with trimming/Native AOT unless annotated. Cache reflection results, compile delegates, or use source generators instead. Q What are attributes and who reads them? Declarative metadata attached to code elements. They are passive and read by the compiler (Obsolete, nullable attributes), the runtime (StructLayout), frameworks via reflection or endpoint metadata (HttpGet, Authorize, Required), or source generators (LoggerMessage, JsonSerializable). Q What is a source generator and why are they replacing reflection? A compiler plugin that analyses your code during the build and adds generated C# source. It moves work from runtime to compile time, giving faster startup, fewer allocations and compile-time errors, and the generated code is visible to the trimmer, enabling Native AOT. Examples: LoggerMessage, System.Text"},{"c":"advanced-csharp","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Memory and Span usage guidelines Source generators Reflection in .NET UnsafeAccessorAttribute class Stephen Toub's annual Performance Improvements in .NET N blog posts, which show these techniques used throughout the BCL. Book: Pro .NET Memory Management by Konrad Kokosa."},{"c":"advanced-csharp","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Save as Program.cs in a new console project ( dotnet new console ) and run with dotnet run . It checks that slicing a Span is a view (not a copy), stackalloc , Memory<T> crossing an await , ArrayPool sizing, ref returns, reflection (property discovery, construction, invocation), reading a custom attribute, and a hand-built expression tree. C# Run ▶ Copy using System . Buffers ; using System . Linq . Expressions ; using System . Reflection ; int failures = 0 ; void Check ( string claim, bool ok) { if (ok) Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: {claim}\" ); else { Console . WriteLine ( $\"FAIL: {claim}\" ); failures++; } } // 1. Slicing a Span is a VIEW, not a copy: mutating through the slice mutates the original array int [] array = [ 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 ]; Span < int > slice = array. AsSpan ( 1 , 2 ); slice[ 0 ] = 99 ; Check ( \"mutating a Span slice writes through to the original array (it's a view, not a copy)\" , array[ 1 ] == 99 ); // 2. stackalloc gives real, usable stack memory Span < byte > onStack = stackalloc byte [ 4 ]; onStack[ 0 ] = 42 ; Check ( \"stackalloc produces a Span you can read and write\" , onStack[ 0 ] == 42 ); // 3. Memory<T> can cross an await; .Span gives the fast synchronous view async Task < int > SumViaMemoryAsync ( Memory < int > buffer) { await Task . Yield (); // crossing an await — Memory<T> survives this, Span<T> couldn't be a local here int total = 0 ; foreach ( var v in buffer. Span ) total += v; // drop to Span for the synchronous part return total; } int sum = await SumViaMemoryAsync ( new [] { 1 , 2 , 3 }); Check ( \"Memory<T> can be used across an await and converted to Span for synchronous work\" , sum == 6 ); // 4. ArrayPool: Rent may return an array >= requested size; Return lets it be reused byte [] rented = ArrayPool < byte >. Shared . Rent ( 1000 ); bool bigEnough = rented. Length >= 1000 ; ArrayPool < byte >. Shared . Return (rented); Check ( \"ArrayPool<T>.Shared.Rent returns an array at least as large as requested\" , bigEnough); // 5. ref return: writing through a ref return modifies the original array element int [] numbers = [ 10 , 20 , 30 ]; ref int FindRef ( int [] arr, int value ) { for ( int i = 0 ; i < arr. Length ; i++) if (arr[i] == value ) return ref arr[i]; throw new KeyNotFoundException (); } ref int found = ref FindRef (numbers, 20 ); found = 999 ; Check ( \"a ref return lets you modify the original array element, not a copy\" , numbers[ 1 ] == 999 ); // 6. Reflection: discover properties, create an instance, set a property, invoke a method Type t = typeof ( Order ); var propertyNames = t. GetProperties ( BindingFlags . Public | BindingFlags . Instance ). Select (p => p. Name ). ToHashSet (); Check ( \"reflection discovers a type's public properties by name\" , propertyNames. Contains ( \"Total\" ) && propertyNames. Contains ( \"Status\" )); object instance = Activator . CreateInstance (t)!; t. GetProperty ( \"Status\" )!. SetValue (instance, \"Paid\" ); var afterInvoke = t. GetMethod ( \"Describe\" )!. Invoke (instance, null ); Check ( \"reflection can construct an instance, set a property, and invoke a method by name\" , ( string )afterInvoke! == \"Order: Paid\" ); // 7. Attributes are inert until something reads them via reflection var method = typeof ( Order ). GetMethod ( \"Describe\" )!; var audit = method. GetCustomAttribute < AuditAttribute >(); Check ( \"a custom attribute attached to a method can be read back via reflection\" , audit?. Action == \"describe\" ); // 8. Hand-built expression tree compiles into a working delegate var param = Expression . Parameter ( typeof ( Order ), \"o\" ); var body = Expression . Property (param, nameof ( Order . Status )); var getter = Expression . Lambda < Func < Order , string >>(body, param). Compile (); var orderForTree = new Order { Status = \"Shipped\" }; Check ( \"a hand-built Expression<Func<T,TResult>> compiles into a delegate that reads the right property\" , getter(orderForTree) == \"Shipped\" ); Console . WriteLine (failures == 0 ? \"\\nAll chec"},{"c":"aspnet-hosting","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR ASP.NET Core is just a console app with Kestrel (a built-in web server) baked in — no separate IIS/Apache process required. Program.cs has two phases: register services/config before builder.Build() , configure the request pipeline after it. Once built, you can't add more DI registrations. Production traffic almost always goes through a reverse proxy in front of Kestrel (for TLS, load balancing, a WAF). You must explicitly trust forwarded headers, or the client's real IP/scheme gets scrambled. The environment name ( ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT ) should only ever change configuration , never which code path runs. Shutdown is graceful: Kestrel stops accepting new requests, finishes in-flight ones, then hosted services get a chance to stop — all within a time budget. HttpContext carries everything about one request, including RequestServices (that request's DI scope). It's not thread-safe and dies with the request."},{"c":"aspnet-hosting","s":"a-surprising-fact-to-start-from","h":"A surprising fact to start from","t":"# A surprising fact to start from Beginner Here's something worth sitting with before anything else in this chapter: ASP.NET Core isn't a server you deploy your code into. There's no separate \"IIS\" or \"Apache\" process hosting your app from the outside, the way classic ASP.NET or PHP worked. ASP.NET Core is a set of libraries — the Microsoft.AspNetCore.App shared framework — that your code references, and your app is, underneath it all, an ordinary console program. One that happens to contain its own complete web server, listening on a socket, running inside the exact same process as your business logic. That single fact explains a lot of what follows in this chapter: why Program.cs looks like it's both \"configuring services\" and \"starting a server\" in the same file, why you can set a breakpoint anywhere in a request handler and step straight through into framework code with no process boundary in the way, and why the whole thing can be packaged into one small container image with nothing else installed. Diagram Copy ┌──────────────────────────── Your ASP.NET Core application ────────────────────────────┐ │ │ │ Program.cs ─▶ WebApplicationBuilder ─▶ WebApplication (the host) │ │ │ │ │ │ ┌────────────┼──────────────┐ ├── Kestrel web server (listens on ports) │ │ ▼ ▼ ▼ ├── Middleware pipeline │ │ Configuration Logging DI container └── Endpoints (routing) │ │ (IConfiguration)(ILogger) (IServiceProvider) ├── Minimal APIs │ │ ├── Controllers (MVC) │ │ ├── Razor Pages / Blazor │ │ ├── SignalR hubs / gRPC services │ │ └── Health checks … │ │ Hosted services (BackgroundService) run alongside the web server │ └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘"},{"c":"aspnet-hosting","s":"the-generic-host-the-thing-that-owns-everything-else","h":"The Generic Host: the thing that owns everything else","t":"# The Generic Host: the thing that owns everything else Intermediate Before there's a web server, routing, or anything web-specific at all, there's a host — an object whose entire job is owning an app's shared resources and its lifetime: the DI container ( IServiceProvider ), configuration ( IConfiguration ), logging ( ILoggerFactory ), the environment ( IHostEnvironment : name, content root), hosted services ( IHostedService ), which it starts and stops — and, as you'll see shortly, the web server itself is just one of these, lifetime management ( IHostApplicationLifetime ) and graceful shutdown. This \"Generic Host\" ( Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting ) isn't actually web-specific at all — it's the same host a Worker Service uses, with no HTTP involved anywhere: C# Run ▶ Copy // A worker service: same host, same DI/config/logging, no HTTP var builder = Host . CreateApplicationBuilder (args); builder. Services . AddHostedService < QueueProcessor >(); builder. Build (). Run (); ASP.NET Core doesn't replace this host with something different — it adds the web-specific parts on top of the exact same foundation. Since .NET 6, WebApplication is the simplified API for that combination (the \"minimal hosting model\"): it's simultaneously an IHost and the object you configure your middleware pipeline and endpoints through. Generic Host vs WebApplication vs Startup.cs — recognising older code .NET Core 2.x–5: Program.cs built a host with Host.CreateDefaultBuilder().ConfigureWebHostDefaults(...) , and a separate Startup class had ConfigureServices(IServiceCollection) and Configure(IApplicationBuilder) . .NET 6+: WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args) , all in Program.cs . Same two phases, less ceremony. When you read older code, ConfigureServices = phase 1 and Configure = phase 2 below — the two-phase shape hasn't actually changed, only the syntax wrapping it has."},{"c":"aspnet-hosting","s":"the-two-phases-of-program-cs-and-why-the-split-matters","h":"The two phases of Program.cs, and why the split matters","t":"# The two phases of Program.cs, and why the split matters Beginner C# Run ▶ Copy var builder = WebApplication . CreateBuilder (args); // ═════════ PHASE 1: describe services and configuration (nothing runs yet) ═════════ // builder.Configuration → IConfigurationManager (sources already loaded: json, env vars, args) // builder.Logging → ILoggingBuilder // builder.Services → IServiceCollection (the DI registrations \"recipe list\") // builder.Environment → IWebHostEnvironment // builder.WebHost → Kestrel / web host settings // builder.Host → generic host settings builder. Services . AddProblemDetails (); builder. Services . AddOpenApi (); builder. Services . AddAuthentication (). AddJwtBearer (); builder. Services . AddAuthorization (); builder. Services . AddDbContext < ShopDb >(o => o. UseSqlServer (builder. Configuration . GetConnectionString ( \"Shop\" ))); builder. Services . AddOrdering (); // your own extension method per feature/layer var app = builder. Build (); // ← the DI container is BUILT here and locked // ═════════ PHASE 2: configure the request pipeline (order matters!) ═════════ if (app. Environment . IsDevelopment ()) app. MapOpenApi (); app. UseExceptionHandler (); app. UseHttpsRedirection (); app. UseAuthentication (); app. UseAuthorization (); app. MapGet ( \"/health\" , () => Results . Ok ()); app. MapOrderEndpoints (); app. Run (); // ← starts Kestrel + hosted services, blocks until shutdown Notice var app = builder.Build(); sitting almost exactly in the middle — that one line is a genuine, hard boundary, not just a visual one, and understanding why explains most of the \"gotchas\" later in this section. Phase 1 , everything before Build() , is purely declarative: you're handing the builder a recipe list — \"when something needs an IOrderRepository , give it an EfOrderRepository \" — and nothing is actually constructed yet. Phase 2 , everything after, is where the actual request pipeline gets assembled, in the exact order you write it, because that order becomes the literal order requests flow through later. Phase Object You do Chapter 1 builder.Services Register services and their lifetimes DI 1 builder.Configuration Add config sources, bind options Configuration 1 builder.Logging Configure providers and levels Errors & logging — builder.Build() Creates the IServiceProvider ; registrations frozen 2 app.Use…() Add middleware, in order Middleware 2 app.Map…() Define endpoints Routing , Building APIs — app.Run() Start the server Three mistakes that fall directly out of misunderstanding the phase boundary Calling builder.Services.Add… after Build() throws, because the moment the container is built, the recipe list is frozen — there's no \"add one more registration\" operation on an already-constructed IServiceProvider . Resolving a service in phase 1 by calling builder.Services.BuildServiceProvider() doesn't give you early access to the real container — it silently builds a second, entirely separate container , so any singleton you touch through it gets constructed twice, once in each container, with two independent instances quietly diverging from that point on. Analyzer ASP0000 specifically warns about this. Getting the order wrong in phase 2 produces no error at all, just silently wrong behaviour — placing UseAuthorization before UseAuthentication , for instance, means every single request looks anonymous, with nothing in the compiler or the running app to tell you why. # What CreateBuilder quietly sets up for you WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args) isn't a blank slate — it arrives with a set of sensible defaults already wired in, worth knowing explicitly rather than discovering by accident: Content root = current directory; web root = wwwroot . Configuration sources: appsettings.json → appsettings.{Environment}.json → User Secrets (Development) → environment variables → command-line args. Logging providers: Console, Debug, EventSource (and EventLog on Windows), configured from the Logging config section. Kestrel as th"},{"c":"aspnet-hosting","s":"kestrel-the-web-server-thats-actually-inside-your-process","h":"Kestrel: the web server that's actually inside your process","t":"# Kestrel: the web server that's actually inside your process Beginner Kestrel is the cross-platform HTTP server mentioned at the very start of this chapter — the thing that makes \"your app is a console program containing its own web server\" literally true. It: listens on sockets (TCP; QUIC for HTTP/3), handles TLS , parses HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 , builds an HttpContext for each request and passes it to your middleware pipeline, is built on System.IO.Pipelines — the same pooled-buffer I/O abstraction mentioned in Advanced C# — for high throughput with minimal per-request allocation. C# Run ▶ Copy builder. WebHost . ConfigureKestrel (k => { k. Limits . MaxRequestBodySize = 10 * 1024 * 1024 ; // 10 MB k. Limits . RequestHeadersTimeout = TimeSpan . FromSeconds ( 30 ); k. AddServerHeader = false ; // don't advertise \"Server: Kestrel\" }); JSON Copy // or in appsettings.json \"Kestrel\" : { \"Endpoints\" : { \"Http\" : { \"Url\" : \"http://0.0.0.0:8080\" }, \"Https\" : { \"Url\" : \"https://0.0.0.0:8443\" , \"Certificate\" : { \"Path\" : \"cert.pfx\" , \"Password\" : \"…\" } } } } The listening URL itself can come from several different places, in practice usually chosen by whichever hosting environment you're actually deploying to: ASPNETCORE_URLS , ASPNETCORE_HTTP_PORTS (defaulting to 8080 in the official .NET 8+ container images), --urls on the command line, or launchSettings.json specifically in development."},{"c":"aspnet-hosting","s":"why-production-traffic-rarely-hits-kestrel-directly","h":"Why production traffic rarely hits Kestrel directly","t":"# Why production traffic rarely hits Kestrel directly Intermediate Given that Kestrel is a genuinely capable, hardened web server on its own, why does nearly every production deployment still put something in front of it? Diagram Copy Internet ─▶ [ CDN / WAF ] ─▶ [ Reverse proxy / load balancer ] ─▶ Kestrel (instance 1) Azure Front Door, App Gateway, ─▶ Kestrel (instance 2) Nginx, YARP, IIS, K8s ingress ─▶ Kestrel (instance 3) The proxy isn't compensating for a Kestrel weakness — it's centralising concerns that would otherwise have to be duplicated across every single instance of your app: terminating TLS once instead of in each process, balancing load across instances, routing many different apps behind one public IP, caching, adding a WAF, and enforcing request rate limits at the edge before traffic ever reaches your code at all. Putting something in front of Kestrel does have one real consequence. As far as the TCP connection is concerned, the proxy is the client — so every request now appears to come from the proxy's IP address and scheme, not the real visitor's. The proxy compensates by adding X-Forwarded-For , X-Forwarded-Proto and X-Forwarded-Host headers carrying the real client's information. But your app has to explicitly opt in to trusting those headers. Skip that, and HttpContext.Connection.RemoteIpAddress , HTTPS redirection, and any URL your app generates will all be subtly wrong — reflecting the proxy's identity instead of the real client's: C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . Configure < ForwardedHeadersOptions >(o => { o. ForwardedHeaders = ForwardedHeaders . XForwardedFor | ForwardedHeaders . XForwardedProto ; o. KnownProxies . Add ( IPAddress . Parse ( \"10.0.0.4\" )); // only trust your proxy }); app. UseForwardedHeaders (); // FIRST in the pipeline That KnownProxies line matters more than it looks. Without it, any client could forge their own X-Forwarded-For header and have your app believe whatever IP they claimed. The mechanism only stays trustworthy if you only honour it when it comes from a proxy you actually control. (Azure App Service and many managed hosts already enable this correctly for you — worth knowing, so you don't accidentally configure it twice.) YARP (\"Yet Another Reverse Proxy\") is Microsoft's own reverse-proxy library, built on ASP.NET Core itself, commonly reached for specifically for API gateways and the BFF pattern — a reverse proxy written in the same stack and language as the apps it's proxying to."},{"c":"aspnet-hosting","s":"iis-hosting-on-windows","h":"IIS hosting on Windows","t":"# IIS hosting on Windows Intermediate On Windows servers specifically, IIS can still host ASP.NET Core, via the ASP.NET Core Module (ANCM) , in one of two genuinely different shapes: Model How Notes In-process (default) The app runs inside the IIS worker process ( w3wp.exe ) using the IIS HTTP server instead of Kestrel Fastest on IIS Out-of-process IIS forwards requests to a separate Kestrel process IIS acts as a reverse proxy A web.config is generated at publish time, but only to configure IIS itself — it is not used for application configuration the way it was for classic ASP.NET, so don't go looking for connection strings in it. With Linux containers now the dominant production deployment target, IIS is largely irrelevant for new cloud-native work, mattering mainly for on-premises Windows deployments."},{"c":"aspnet-hosting","s":"environments-the-one-variable-thats-meant-to-change-configur","h":"Environments: the one variable that's meant to change configuration, never code paths","t":"# Environments: the one variable that's meant to change configuration, never code paths Beginner The environment name comes from ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT (or DOTNET_ENVIRONMENT ), and if nobody sets it, it defaults to Production — a deliberate, safe default: an unconfigured deployment fails toward hiding implementation details, not toward leaking them. Name Typical behaviour Development Developer exception page, User Secrets loaded, DI scope validation, detailed errors, OpenAPI UI Staging Production-like settings, test data/services Production Generic error responses, HSTS, real secrets from Key Vault, minimal logging noise C# Run ▶ Copy if (app. Environment . IsDevelopment ()) { app. MapOpenApi (); app. MapScalarApiReference (); } if (!app. Environment . IsDevelopment ()) app. UseHsts (); if (app. Environment . IsEnvironment ( \"Local\" )) { /* custom names work too */ } Per-environment settings belong in appsettings.{Environment}.json (see Configuration ). There's a discipline worth holding onto here, easy to drift away from under deadline pressure: the environment should change configuration values , never which code path runs . if (app.Environment.IsProduction()) sprinkled through business logic means you're no longer testing the same code in staging that will actually run in production — exactly the \"it works on my machine\" problem the whole container-based deployment model in Docker exists to eliminate."},{"c":"aspnet-hosting","s":"application-lifetime-and-graceful-shutdown-what-actually-hap","h":"Application lifetime and graceful shutdown: what actually happens, in order","t":"# Application lifetime and graceful shutdown: what actually happens, in order Intermediate app.Run() isn't just \"start listening\" — it drives the entire lifecycle of the process, start to finish, in a specific, predictable sequence: Build the container. Start each IHostedService in registration order ( StartAsync ). The web server is one of them, so background services registered before it in Program.cs genuinely start first. Kestrel starts listening; IHostApplicationLifetime.ApplicationStarted fires. … the app runs … On SIGTERM — docker stop , a Kubernetes pod being terminated, an App Service restart — or Ctrl+C, shutdown begins. ApplicationStopping fires. Kestrel stops accepting new connections but lets in-flight requests finish. Hosted services get StopAsync called, with their stoppingToken cancelled. All of this has to happen within ShutdownTimeout (30 s by default). ApplicationStopped fires; the container is disposed (disposing singletons). C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . Configure < HostOptions >(o => o. ShutdownTimeout = TimeSpan . FromSeconds ( 45 )); var lifetime = app. Services . GetRequiredService < IHostApplicationLifetime >(); lifetime. ApplicationStopping . Register (() => Log . Information ( \"Draining…\" )); Why \"graceful\" shutdown is worth real engineering effort, not just a nice-to-have In Kubernetes or Container Apps, instances get replaced routinely — on every deployment, during every scale-in event — as normal operation, not failure. Without graceful shutdown, each of those routine replacements drops whatever requests were in flight at that moment (502s to real users) and kills background jobs mid-work with no chance to clean up. Three practical habits fix this: honour stoppingToken in every background service so it actually notices a shutdown request; keep your own shutdown work comfortably under the platform's grace period instead of racing it; and make work genuinely idempotent (see Messaging & events ) so a job interrupted mid-way and retried from scratch doesn't double-charge a card or double-send an email."},{"c":"aspnet-hosting","s":"httpcontext-the-one-object-your-entire-pipeline-shares","h":"HttpContext: the one object your entire pipeline shares","t":"# HttpContext: the one object your entire pipeline shares Beginner Every single request gets exactly one HttpContext — the object essentially every chapter in the rest of Part III assumes you already know the shape of: Member Contains Request Method, Path, Query, Headers, Body (stream), Cookies, ReadFromJsonAsync<T>() Response StatusCode, Headers, Body (stream), WriteAsJsonAsync() , OnStarting / OnCompleted callbacks User The ClaimsPrincipal set by authentication RequestServices The request-scoped IServiceProvider Items A per-request dictionary for sharing data between middleware Features Low-level server features ( IHttpRequestFeature , IEndpointFeature …) Connection Remote IP, port, client certificate RequestAborted CancellationToken cancelled if the client disconnects TraceIdentifier A unique ID for the request (used in ProblemDetails/logs) GetEndpoint() The endpoint chosen by routing (with its metadata) RequestServices deserves a second look specifically because it's the concrete answer to a question Dependency injection raised abstractly: where does ASP.NET Core's \"one scope per request\" actually live? Right here — HttpContext.RequestServices is that request's DI scope, created by middleware early in the pipeline, and everything resolved anywhere during this request ultimately traces back to it. Two rules worth holding firmly: HttpContext is not thread-safe , and it's only valid for the duration of the request it belongs to . To reach it from a service rather than directly in a handler, inject IHttpContextAccessor — sparingly, since it's usually a sign you could instead just pass what you actually need explicitly — and never, under any circumstance, capture an HttpContext reference inside background work that might still be running after the request has already finished; by the time that background code runs, the context it captured may already be invalid."},{"c":"aspnet-hosting","s":"hosting-models-summary","h":"Hosting models summary","t":"# Hosting models summary Advanced Host Server Typical use Self-hosted Kestrel in a container Kestrel Kubernetes, Container Apps, App Service for Containers (most common) Azure App Service (code deploy, Linux) Kestrel behind the App Service front ends PaaS web apps IIS in-process IIS HTTP server Windows on-premises HTTP.sys Windows kernel driver Windows-only features (Windows auth, port sharing) without IIS Azure Functions (isolated worker) Functions host + ASP.NET Core integration Serverless HTTP triggers TestServer In-memory Integration tests ( Integration testing ) Q What is the role of Program.cs in ASP.NET Core? It's the application entry point. It creates a WebApplicationBuilder, registers services and configuration (phase 1), builds the WebApplication (which builds the DI container), configures the middleware pipeline and endpoints (phase 2), then calls Run to start Kestrel and the hosted services. Q What is the Generic Host? The IHost abstraction from Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting that provides DI, configuration, logging, environment info and lifetime management, and runs IHostedService instances. It's used by web apps, worker services and console apps alike; ASP.NET Core's web server runs as one of its hosted services. Q What is Kestrel and why use a reverse proxy in front of it? Kestrel is ASP.NET Core's built-in cross-platform web server: it handles sockets, TLS and HTTP/1-3 parsing and feeds requests into the pipeline. A reverse proxy adds load balancing, TLS termination, a WAF, caching, multiple apps behind one IP and centralised rate limiting. When you use one, you must configure forwarded headers so the app sees the real client IP and scheme. Q How does ASP.NET Core know which environment it's running in? From the ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT (or DOTNET_ENVIRONMENT) variable, defaulting to Production. It controls which appsettings.{Environment}.json is loaded, whether User Secrets are used, and is available as IWebHostEnvironment. Q What's the difference between in-process and out-of-process hosting in IIS? In-process runs the app inside the IIS worker process using IIS's HTTP server, which is faster. Out-of-process runs Kestrel as a separate process, with IIS forwarding requests to it as a reverse proxy. Q What happens when an ASP.NET Core app receives SIGTERM? The host triggers ApplicationStopping, Kestrel stops accepting new connections and drains in-flight requests, hosted services' StopAsync is called and their stopping tokens are cancelled, all within ShutdownTimeout (30 s by default). Then ApplicationStopped fires and the DI container (with its singletons) is disposed. Q What is HttpContext and is it thread-safe? The per-request object holding Request, Response, User, RequestServices (the scoped container), Items, Features and RequestAborted. It is not thread-safe and only valid for the duration of the request, so it must not be captured in background tasks. Q Why shouldn't you call BuildServiceProvider() inside Program.cs? It builds a separate container from the one the app will use, so singletons get created twice and their state diverges, and disposal isn't handled. Use the options pattern, factory overloads ( sp => ... ), or resolve after builder.Build() from app.Services ."},{"c":"aspnet-hosting","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading ASP.NET Core fundamentals overview .NET Generic Host Kestrel web server Configure ASP.NET Core to work with proxy servers Book: ASP.NET Core in Action (3rd ed.) by Andrew Lock, the best book-length treatment. Also his blog, andrewlock.net."},{"c":"aspnet-hosting","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run in a console project ( dotnet new web , then dotnet add package Microsoft.AspNetCore.TestHost ). It checks the environment default, the shutdown-timeout default, the order of host lifecycle events, and that RequestServices really is one scope per request. C# Run ▶ Copy using Microsoft . AspNetCore . Builder ; using Microsoft . AspNetCore . Hosting ; using Microsoft . AspNetCore . TestHost ; using Microsoft . Extensions . DependencyInjection ; using Microsoft . Extensions . Hosting ; void Check ( bool condition, string claim) { if (!condition) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: {claim}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: {claim}\" ); } // 1. With no ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT/DOTNET_ENVIRONMENT set, the environment defaults to \"Production\". Environment . SetEnvironmentVariable ( \"ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT\" , null ); Environment . SetEnvironmentVariable ( \"DOTNET_ENVIRONMENT\" , null ); var defaultBuilder = WebApplication . CreateBuilder (); Check (defaultBuilder. Environment . EnvironmentName == Environments . Production , \"With no environment variable set, WebApplication.CreateBuilder defaults to Production\" ); // 2. HostOptions.ShutdownTimeout defaults to 30 seconds. var hostOptionsApp = WebApplication . CreateBuilder (). Build (); var shutdownTimeout = hostOptionsApp. Services . GetRequiredService < Microsoft . Extensions . Options . IOptions < HostOptions >>(). Value . ShutdownTimeout ; Check (shutdownTimeout == TimeSpan . FromSeconds ( 30 ), \"HostOptions.ShutdownTimeout defaults to 30 seconds\" ); // 3. The host fires ApplicationStarted, then ApplicationStopping, then ApplicationStopped, in that order. var lifecycleBuilder = WebApplication . CreateBuilder (); lifecycleBuilder. WebHost . UseTestServer (); var lifecycleApp = lifecycleBuilder. Build (); var events = new List < string >(); var lifetime = lifecycleApp. Services . GetRequiredService < IHostApplicationLifetime >(); lifetime. ApplicationStarted . Register (() => events. Add ( \"Started\" )); lifetime. ApplicationStopping . Register (() => events. Add ( \"Stopping\" )); lifetime. ApplicationStopped . Register (() => events. Add ( \"Stopped\" )); await lifecycleApp. StartAsync (); await lifecycleApp. StopAsync (); Check (events. SequenceEqual ( new [] { \"Started\" , \"Stopping\" , \"Stopped\" }), \"Host lifecycle fires Started, then Stopping, then Stopped, in order\" ); // 4. HttpContext.RequestServices IS that request's scope: resolved twice in ONE request, same instance; // resolved in two DIFFERENT requests, different instances. var scopeBuilder = WebApplication . CreateBuilder (); scopeBuilder. WebHost . UseTestServer (); scopeBuilder. Services . AddScoped < GuidService >(); var scopeApp = scopeBuilder. Build (); scopeApp. MapGet ( \"/\" , ( HttpContext ctx) => { var a = ctx. RequestServices . GetRequiredService < GuidService >(). Id ; var b = ctx. RequestServices . GetRequiredService < GuidService >(). Id ; return $\"{a},{b}\" ; }); await scopeApp. StartAsync (); var client = scopeApp. GetTestClient (); var firstRequest = ( await client. GetStringAsync ( \"/\" )). Split ( ',' ); var secondRequest = ( await client. GetStringAsync ( \"/\" )). Split ( ',' ); await scopeApp. StopAsync (); Check (firstRequest[ 0 ] == firstRequest[ 1 ], \"Resolving a scoped service twice within ONE request returns the same instance\" ); Check (firstRequest[ 0 ] != secondRequest[ 0 ], \"A scoped service resolved in two DIFFERENT requests returns different instances\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"All aspnet-hosting claims verified.\" ); class GuidService { public Guid Id { get ; } = Guid . NewGuid (); } Claims this doesn't attempt to verify — they depend on the OS, Kestrel internals, or a real reverse proxy, not just the BCL — are left to the official docs linked above: Kestrel's exact transport behaviour, IIS in-process vs out-of-process hosting, and the actual X-Forwarded-* header handling of a specific proxy."},{"c":"dependency-injection","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR DI means a class declares what it needs in its constructor instead of creating those things itself — a container builds and wires everything for you. Three lifetimes: Transient (new every time), Scoped (one per request), Singleton (one for the app's entire life). Pick based on whether the service holds per-request state or needs to be safely shared forever. A captive dependency — a singleton accidentally holding onto a scoped service, like a DbContext — is the single most common DI bug. ValidateScopes catches it at startup instead of in production. Outside a request (background jobs, startup code) there's no scope automatically; create one yourself with IServiceScopeFactory . Program.cs is the composition root — the one place that should know about concrete types. Everywhere else should depend only on interfaces."},{"c":"dependency-injection","s":"start-with-the-version-that-goes-wrong","h":"Start with the version that goes wrong","t":"# Start with the version that goes wrong Beginner C# Run ▶ Copy // Without DI: OrderService creates its own dependencies public class OrderService { private readonly SqlOrderRepository _repo = new SqlOrderRepository ( \"Server=prod;...\" ); private readonly SmtpEmailSender _email = new SmtpEmailSender ( \"smtp.company.com\" ); public void Place ( Order o) { _repo. Save (o); _email. Send (o. CustomerEmail , \"Thanks!\" ); } } This looks completely normal, and plenty of real code looks exactly like it. So let's actually interrogate it, line by line, instead of just accepting \"DI is good practice\" on faith. It's welded to specific technologies. OrderService doesn't just use a database and an email provider — it creates a SQL-specific repository and an SMTP-specific sender itself. Switching to Postgres or SendGrid later means editing OrderService directly, even though neither of those is really OrderService 's job. You cannot test this class in isolation. Write a unit test for Place , and you will hit a real database and send a real email — every single time, in every single test run. There's no way around it, because the dependencies are hard-coded inside the method. A connection string is sitting inside a business class. That configuration detail has nothing to do with \"placing an order\" conceptually, yet here it is, buried in the one place you'd least want to go looking for it. Nobody owns the lifetime of these objects. Who disposes the SQL connection? Is it safe to share _repo across two requests running at once? Nothing here answers that — it's just left as an open question for whoever reads this later. Four real, concrete problems, all stemming from one root cause: OrderService is responsible for deciding what talks to SQL and SMTP, on top of its actual job of placing an order. Now look at the fix: C# Run ▶ Copy // With DI: dependencies are given to the class (\"injected\") public class OrderService ( IOrderRepository repo, IEmailSender email) { public async Task PlaceAsync ( Order o, CancellationToken ct) { await repo. SaveAsync (o, ct); await email. SendAsync (o. CustomerEmail , \"Thanks!\" , ct); } } Notice what actually changed: OrderService no longer creates anything . It just declares, through its constructor, what it needs — an IOrderRepository , an IEmailSender — and trusts that something else will hand it working instances. That \"something else\" is the container , and the technique of handing dependencies to a class from outside, instead of letting it construct them itself, is dependency injection . In a test, you simply hand OrderService fakes instead of real implementations, and all four problems above disappear at once — not because you fixed them individually, but because the thing causing all of them is gone. Three terms that get used almost interchangeably, and shouldn't be Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP) — the \"D\" in SOLID — is the design rule : high-level code should depend on abstractions, not on concrete low-level classes. Inversion of Control (IoC) is the general idea behind that rule in practice: the framework calls your code and controls when objects get created, instead of your code doing it and calling the framework. Dependency Injection (DI) is the specific technique that implements IoC for dependencies: passing them in, usually through a constructor. The DI container (also called an IoC container) is the piece of software that automates doing this for an entire app."},{"c":"dependency-injection","s":"what-the-container-is-actually-doing","h":"What the container is actually doing","t":"# What the container is actually doing Beginner Once OrderService declares what it needs instead of creating it, something has to supply those dependencies at runtime. That something is a three-step pipeline, and understanding the three steps as separate moments — not one blurred-together thing — is what makes the rest of this chapter click: Diagram Copy IServiceCollection ──Build()──▶ IServiceProvider ──GetService<T>()──▶ instances (list of recipes: (the container: (constructed with \"for IX, use X, knows how to build all their with lifetime L\") each type & caches by lifetime) dependencies) C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddScoped < IOrderRepository , EfOrderRepository >(); // recipe builder. Services . AddScoped < IEmailSender , SendGridEmailSender >(); builder. Services . AddScoped < OrderService >(); // concrete type registered as itself var app = builder. Build (); // container built app. MapPost ( \"/orders\" , ( Order o, OrderService svc, CancellationToken ct) => svc. PlaceAsync (o, ct)); // ASP.NET Core resolves OrderService from the request scope. The container sees its constructor needs // IOrderRepository and IEmailSender, resolves those (and THEIR dependencies, recursively), then constructs it. Step one — registration — just builds up a list of recipes: \"if anyone asks for IOrderRepository , give them an EfOrderRepository .\" Nothing is constructed yet; builder.Services is only a plan. Step two — builder.Build() — locks that plan in and turns it into a real IServiceProvider , the actual machine capable of constructing things. Step three happens every time something asks for an instance: the container looks at what OrderService 's constructor needs, resolves each of those dependencies first (recursing into theirs, if they have any), and only then constructs OrderService itself, fully wired up. You never call new OrderService(...) anywhere in your code — the container does, and it works out the whole dependency graph for you, automatically, every single time. Constructor injection , what you've just seen, is the normal, overwhelmingly common case — the container picks whichever constructor has the most parameters it's able to satisfy. But it isn't the only place services get handed to your code: Where How Constructors Normal case (including primary constructors) Minimal API handler parameters Services are inferred from DI ( [FromServices] optional) Controller action parameters [FromServices] IFoo foo Middleware InvokeAsync parameters Resolved per request (use this for scoped services) Razor views/components @inject IFoo Foo Manually app.Services.GetRequiredService<T>() , HttpContext.RequestServices (avoid in business code)"},{"c":"dependency-injection","s":"service-lifetimes-the-question-the-container-has-to-answer-e","h":"Service lifetimes: the question the container has to answer every time","t":"# Service lifetimes: the question the container has to answer every time Beginner Here's a question you might not have thought to ask yet: when two different parts of your code both ask for IEmailSender , does the container build a brand-new one each time, or hand back the same instance twice? The answer is: it depends on how you registered it — and that choice is called the service's lifetime . Lifetime Register with One instance per… Disposed when Typical services Transient AddTransient every resolution The scope that resolved it ends Lightweight, stateless helpers, validators, mappers Scoped AddScoped scope , which is one per HTTP request in ASP.NET Core The scope (request) ends DbContext , unit of work, repositories, request-specific services (current user, tenant) Singleton AddSingleton application (root container) App shutdown Caches, configuration objects, HttpClient handlers, IConnectionMultiplexer (Redis), clocks, expensive-to-create thread-safe objects The clearest way to actually feel the difference is to watch one tiny service resolved under all three lifetimes: C# Run ▶ Copy public class GuidService { public Guid Id { get ; } = Guid . NewGuid (); } // Within ONE request, resolving it in two places gives: // Transient → two different Ids // Scoped → the same Id (within this request), different across requests // Singleton → the same Id for every request, forever Read that comment slowly, because it's really showing you three completely different answers to the same question — \"is this the same object as last time?\" — and all three are correct, for their own lifetime. Transient never remembers anything; it's rebuilt fresh, every single time, no exceptions. Scoped remembers within one unit of work (a request) but forgets between them — resolve it twice in the same request and you get the identical instance, resolve it in the next request and it's a new one. Singleton never forgets, ever, for the life of the entire process. Lifetimes as a hotel Singleton is the hotel building itself: one building, shared by every single guest, for as long as the hotel exists. Scoped is your room: it's yours for the length of your stay, and then it gets cleaned and handed to the next guest — a different room, in effect, even though the physical room might be reused. Transient is a towel: you get a fresh one every single time you ask, no matter how many times, in the same stay or different ones. # So which one do you actually pick? In order, ask yourself these three questions, and stop at the first one that answers it: Does it hold per-request state, or does it depend on something that's scoped (like a DbContext )? → Scoped. Is it stateless — or is whatever state it holds genuinely safe to share across every thread at once — and relatively expensive to build? → Singleton. (And it genuinely must be thread-safe if you choose this; see Threading for exactly what that means and what breaks if it isn't.) Cheap, stateless, used briefly? → Transient — though in practice, the difference between transient and scoped rarely matters for something this simple. When none of that gives you a confident answer, scoped is the safe default for application services in a web app — it's the one lifetime that can never accidentally outlive the request it's working on."},{"c":"dependency-injection","s":"scopes-made-concrete","h":"Scopes, made concrete","t":"# Scopes, made concrete Intermediate You've been told \"scoped means one instance per scope\" several times now — but what, physically, is a scope? It's a child container : a smaller IServiceProvider nested inside the main one, with its own private cache of scoped instances, that gets thrown away (and everything disposable inside it disposed) as one unit when it ends. ASP.NET Core creates exactly one scope per HTTP request , automatically, via middleware near the very start of the pipeline. It exposes that scope as HttpContext.RequestServices . Every service resolved anywhere during the request — your handler, any middleware after that point, anything they call into — pulls from that one scope. That's the entire mechanism behind \"scoped = one per request\": scoped services don't know anything special about HTTP requests. There's just one scope created per request, and scoped services are cached per scope. Here's the detail that catches people out: outside of a request, there is no scope, unless you explicitly create one. A background service, startup code, a message consumer — none of these are running inside an HTTP request, so none of them have a scope handed to them automatically. If any of that code needs a scoped service, like DbContext , it has to ask for a fresh scope itself: C# Run ▶ Copy public class NightlyCleanup ( IServiceScopeFactory scopeFactory, ILogger < NightlyCleanup > log) : BackgroundService { protected override async Task ExecuteAsync ( CancellationToken stoppingToken) { using var timer = new PeriodicTimer ( TimeSpan . FromHours ( 24 )); while ( await timer. WaitForNextTickAsync (stoppingToken)) { await using var scope = scopeFactory. CreateAsyncScope (); // one scope per unit of work var db = scope. ServiceProvider . GetRequiredService < ShopDb >(); // fresh DbContext var removed = await db. Carts . Where (c => c. UpdatedAt < DateTime . UtcNow . AddDays (- 30 )). ExecuteDeleteAsync (stoppingToken); log. LogInformation ( \"Removed {Count} stale carts\" , removed); } // scope disposed → DbContext disposed } } // At startup (e.g. migrations/seeding in development) using ( var scope = app. Services . CreateScope ()) { var db = scope. ServiceProvider . GetRequiredService < ShopDb >(); await db. Database . MigrateAsync (); } Notice the pattern: NightlyCleanup itself is a long-lived singleton (hosted services always are), but every time it has actual work to do, it creates a brand-new, short-lived scope just for that one unit of work, uses it, and disposes it. That's the correct shape for mixing a long-lived component with scoped dependencies — and getting that shape wrong is exactly what the next section is about."},{"c":"dependency-injection","s":"the-captive-dependency-the-single-most-common-di-bug","h":"The captive dependency: the single most common DI bug","t":"# The captive dependency: the single most common DI bug Intermediate Here's a rule worth memorising exactly, because almost every tricky DI bug you'll ever debug traces back to breaking it: a service must never depend on a service with a shorter lifetime than its own. Diagram Copy Singleton ──depends on──▶ Scoped ✘ captive dependency Singleton ──depends on──▶ Transient ✘ (the transient is effectively a singleton) Scoped ──depends on──▶ Transient ✔ (fine) Scoped ──depends on──▶ Singleton ✔ Transient ──depends on──▶ anything ✔ Why does this matter so much for the singleton row specifically? A singleton is constructed exactly once — the first time anything asks for it. Whatever gets passed into its constructor at that moment is what it's stuck with for the rest of the process's life. If one of those constructor arguments is a scoped service, the singleton doesn't get \"a fresh one per request\" the way scoped is supposed to work. It gets whichever instance happened to exist at that one moment, and holds onto it permanently. That's a captive dependency : the scoped object is taken captive by something that will never let it go. C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddSingleton < PriceCache >(); // lives forever builder. Services . AddScoped < ShopDb >(); // should live one request public class PriceCache ( ShopDb db) { } // ✘ the FIRST request's DbContext is captured forever Walk through exactly what the diagram shows, because each step explains one real symptom you'll see in production: Request 1 arrives. Its scope creates ShopDb #1 . The very first time PriceCache is resolved — which, being a singleton, only ever happens once — it receives ShopDb #1 through its constructor and holds that reference from now on. Request 1 finishes. Its scope ends, and ShopDb #1 gets disposed, exactly as a scoped service should be. But PriceCache is a singleton — nobody tells it anything changed, and it's still holding that same reference. Request 2 arrives. A brand-new scope creates ShopDb #2 , fresh and ready. But PriceCache was never reconstructed — it's the same singleton instance from before — so it has no idea ShopDb #2 even exists. It's still pointing at the disposed ShopDb #1 . That mismatch produces three distinct, nasty symptoms, worth being able to name all three. DbContext isn't thread-safe, so concurrent requests sharing that one captured instance risk \"a second operation was started on this context\" errors. Nobody ever disposes and recreates it the normal way, so its change-tracker just keeps growing, quietly feeding you stale data . And once request 1's using block actually disposes it, every later access throws ObjectDisposedException . Three different-looking bugs, one single root cause. How do you catch this before it ships? In Development, ValidateScopes = true makes the container actively check for exactly this shape and throw a clear InvalidOperationException: Cannot consume scoped service 'ShopDb' from singleton 'PriceCache' the moment it would happen — turning a subtle runtime bug into a loud startup failure. It's usually worth enabling everywhere, not just in Development, for the small extra startup cost: C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Host . UseDefaultServiceProvider (o => { o. ValidateScopes = true ; o. ValidateOnBuild = true ; }); The fix , once you've found one: either make the consumer scoped too (if that's actually appropriate for what it does), or — the usual answer for something that genuinely needs to be a singleton — inject IServiceScopeFactory (or IDbContextFactory<T> ) instead of the scoped service directly, and create a fresh scope or context for each individual operation, exactly the way NightlyCleanup did in the previous section."},{"c":"dependency-injection","s":"a-tour-of-the-registration-api","h":"A tour of the registration API","t":"# A tour of the registration API Intermediate Registering a simple interface-to-implementation mapping is the common case, but the container supports several other shapes worth knowing: C# Run ▶ Copy // Interface → implementation services. AddScoped < IOrderRepository , EfOrderRepository >(); // Concrete type services. AddSingleton < SystemClock >(); // Existing instance (always singleton; the container won't dispose it) services. AddSingleton < IClock >( new FixedClock ( DateTimeOffset . Parse ( \"2026-01-01\" ))); // Factory: full control over construction (needed for primitives, conditional logic, external SDKs) services. AddSingleton < IBlobStore >(sp => { var opts = sp. GetRequiredService < IOptions < StorageOptions >>(). Value ; return new AzureBlobStore ( new BlobServiceClient ( new Uri (opts. Url ), new DefaultAzureCredential ())); }); // One implementation, several interfaces, same instance services. AddSingleton < InMemoryCache >(); services. AddSingleton < ICache >(sp => sp. GetRequiredService < InMemoryCache >()); services. AddSingleton < ICacheStats >(sp => sp. GetRequiredService < InMemoryCache >()); // Try* variants: register only if not already registered (library-friendly defaults) services. TryAddSingleton < IClock , SystemClock >(); services. TryAddEnumerable ( ServiceDescriptor . Scoped < IValidator < Order >, OrderValidator >()); // Replace / remove (common in tests) services. Replace ( ServiceDescriptor . Singleton < IEmailSender , FakeEmailSender >()); services. RemoveAll < IEmailSender >(); // Open generics services. AddScoped ( typeof ( IRepository <>), typeof ( EfRepository <>)); That last one is worth a second look if you haven't seen it before: registering IRepository<> (the open generic, with no type argument filled in) against EfRepository<> means the container can construct IRepository<Order> , IRepository<Customer> , or IRepository<AnyType> on demand, all from this single line — it closes the generic with whatever type was actually asked for. # What happens when two implementations share an interface C# Run ▶ Copy services. AddScoped < INotificationChannel , EmailChannel >(); services. AddScoped < INotificationChannel , SmsChannel >(); services. AddScoped < INotificationChannel , PushChannel >(); public class Notifier ( IEnumerable < INotificationChannel > channels) // all three, in registration order { public Task NotifyAllAsync ( Message m) => Task . WhenAll (channels. Select (c => c. SendAsync (m))); } public class Other ( INotificationChannel channel) { } // gets the LAST one registered (PushChannel) This is a genuinely sharp edge worth knowing about in advance: asking for INotificationChannel directly gives you only the last one registered — PushChannel here — silently, with no error telling you the other two exist. Asking for IEnumerable<INotificationChannel> instead gives you all three , in registration order. If you ever find a bug where \"only the push notification fires\", this exact mismatch is worth checking first. # Keyed services, for picking a specific one by name C# Run ▶ Copy services. AddKeyedSingleton < IPaymentGateway , StripeGateway >( \"stripe\" ); services. AddKeyedSingleton < IPaymentGateway , AdyenGateway >( \"adyen\" ); public class Checkout ( [FromKeyedServices(\"stripe\")] IPaymentGateway gateway) { } app. MapPost ( \"/pay/{provider}\" , ( string provider, IServiceProvider sp) => sp. GetRequiredKeyedService < IPaymentGateway >(provider). ChargeAsync (...)); Keyed services (.NET 8) solve exactly the ambiguity the previous example ran into, when ambiguity is actually what you want: multiple implementations of the same interface, each deliberately reachable by its own name, instead of only ever getting \"the last one\" or \"all of them\". # Keeping Program.cs readable C# Run ▶ Copy public static class InfrastructureRegistration { public static IServiceCollection AddInfrastructure ( this IServiceCollection services, IConfiguration config) { services. AddDbContext < ShopDb >(o => o. UseSq"},{"c":"dependency-injection","s":"decorators-and-assembly-scanning","h":"Decorators and assembly scanning","t":"# Decorators and assembly scanning Advanced Two capabilities the built-in container deliberately leaves out, on purpose, to stay small and simple: C# Run ▶ Copy // Manual decorator: wrap the real implementation services. AddScoped < SqlProductRepository >(); services. AddScoped < IProductRepository >(sp => new CachedProductRepository (sp. GetRequiredService < SqlProductRepository >(), sp. GetRequiredService < HybridCache >())); // With the Scrutor library services. AddScoped < IProductRepository , SqlProductRepository >(); services. Decorate < IProductRepository , CachedProductRepository >(); services. Scan (s => s. FromAssemblyOf < Program >() . AddClasses (c => c. AssignableTo ( typeof ( ICommandHandler <>))) . AsImplementedInterfaces () . WithScopedLifetime ()); You can still build a decorator — a class that wraps another implementation of the same interface, adding behaviour around it — by hand, with a plain factory registration, as the first block shows. The Scrutor library makes both decoration and convention-based assembly scanning (\"register every class that implements ICommandHandler<> \") much less verbose, if you find yourself doing either often. Third-party containers (Autofac, Lamar, DryIoc) go further still, offering modules, interception and child containers, and can replace or sit behind the built-in one — but most modern apps genuinely don't need that extra power."},{"c":"dependency-injection","s":"who-disposes-what","h":"Who disposes what","t":"# Who disposes what Intermediate The container disposes IDisposable / IAsyncDisposable instances it created itself , the moment their scope ends (and singletons at app shutdown). It does not dispose instances you registered directly with AddSingleton(instance) — you created that one, you own its lifetime. Transient disposables resolved directly from the root provider (not from a scope) are tracked until the whole app shuts down. Do that repeatedly and you've built yourself a slow, steady memory leak. Resolve from a scope instead. As a rule: never dispose a dependency that was injected into you. If the container gave it to you, the container owns cleaning it up."},{"c":"dependency-injection","s":"patterns-that-look-reasonable-and-arent","h":"Patterns that look reasonable and aren't","t":"# Patterns that look reasonable and aren't Intermediate Anti-pattern Why it hurts Instead Service Locator : calling IServiceProvider.GetService inside business classes Hides dependencies, fails at runtime instead of startup, hard to test Constructor injection Constructor over-injection : 10+ dependencies The class has too many responsibilities (SRP violation) Split the class; group into facades or handlers Captive dependency Shared non-thread-safe state, stale data Match lifetimes; scope factory Static access ( ServiceLocator.Current , static HttpContext ) Global state, untestable Inject BuildServiceProvider() in Program.cs Duplicate singletons Factories, options, resolve after Build Injecting IServiceProvider \"just in case\" Same as service locator Inject what you need; use factories for dynamic choice Interfaces for everything Ceremony with no benefit for stable internal classes Interfaces at real seams (I/O, external systems, things you mock) async work in constructors Constructors can't be async; .Result blocks Factory methods, IHostedService startup, lazy initialisation The first row deserves a closer look, because \"just inject IServiceProvider and resolve what you need inside the method\" can look, at first glance, almost identical to normal DI — it isn't. Constructor injection makes a class's real dependencies visible from the outside, right there in its signature, which is exactly what lets the container catch a missing registration at startup instead of three steps deep into a request at 2 a.m. Reaching into IServiceProvider by hand hides every one of those dependencies behind a string or a type lookup buried in a method body, where nothing — not the compiler, not ValidateOnBuild — can check it for you in advance."},{"c":"dependency-injection","s":"how-the-container-pulls-this-off-internally","h":"How the container pulls this off internally","t":"# How the container pulls this off internally Advanced A short look under the hood, for the curious. On Build() , your ServiceCollection of registrations becomes a real ServiceProvider . For each registration it holds a call-site : a description of exactly how to construct that type. The first time a given type is actually resolved, the container analyses its full constructor dependency graph and compiles a resolution plan for it. That starts out using plain reflection, but for services resolved often, it switches to compiled expression trees or IL emit instead, which is substantially faster ( confirmed in the runtime's own ServiceLookup source ) — so the expensive analysis happens once, and every later resolution of the same type is cheap. Singletons are cached once, in the root container; scoped instances are cached per-scope, in each scope's own dictionary; transients aren't cached at all, only tracked so they can be disposed correctly. ValidateOnBuild , mentioned earlier alongside ValidateScopes , walks every registration right at startup to confirm it can actually be constructed — catching a missing registration the moment the app starts, instead of the first time a request happens to need it. One more detail worth knowing: ActivatorUtilities.CreateInstance<T>(sp, extraArgs) can construct a type that isn't even registered , mixing resolved DI services with explicit arguments you pass in yourself. This is precisely how conventional middleware classes get constructed, since they aren't registered as services in the usual sense. Q What is dependency injection and why use it? A technique where a class receives its dependencies from outside (usually through its constructor) instead of creating them. It decouples code from concrete implementations, enables unit testing with fakes, centralises configuration and lifetime management in one composition root, and supports the Dependency Inversion Principle. Q Explain transient, scoped and singleton lifetimes. Transient creates a new instance every time it's resolved. Scoped creates one instance per scope; in ASP.NET Core a scope is one HTTP request, so it's one per request. Singleton creates one instance for the app's lifetime, shared by all requests and threads, so it must be thread-safe. Q What lifetime does DbContext have and why? Scoped, by default with AddDbContext. A DbContext isn't thread-safe and accumulates tracked entities, so it should be short-lived and used by one request (one unit of work) at a time. Q What is a captive dependency and how do you detect and fix it? A longer-lived service holding a shorter-lived one, e.g. a singleton depending on a scoped DbContext. The scoped instance is kept forever and shared across threads. ASP.NET Core detects it with scope validation (on by default in Development), which throws at startup. Fix it by aligning lifetimes or by injecting IServiceScopeFactory or IDbContextFactory to create instances per operation. Q How do you use a scoped service from a BackgroundService? BackgroundService is a singleton with no request scope, so inject IServiceScopeFactory, create a scope per unit of work ( using var scope = factory.CreateScope() ), and resolve the scoped service from scope.ServiceProvider. Dispose the scope when done. Q What happens if you register two implementations of the same interface? Injecting the interface gives the last registered implementation. Injecting IEnumerable<T> gives all of them in registration order. Since .NET 8, keyed services let you register and resolve specific implementations by key. Q What's the difference between DI, IoC and the Dependency Inversion Principle? DIP is the design principle: depend on abstractions, not concretions. IoC is the broader idea that a framework controls object creation and program flow. DI is a concrete technique for achieving IoC by passing dependencies in, typically automated by a DI container. Q Why is the Service Locator pattern considered an anti-pattern? Dependencies are hidd"},{"c":"dependency-injection","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Dependency injection in ASP.NET Core Dependency injection guidelines Book: Dependency Injection Principles, Practices, and Patterns by Steven van Deursen and Mark Seemann, the definitive book on DI."},{"c":"dependency-injection","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run in a plain console project ( dotnet new console , then dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection ). It checks all three lifetimes, the captive-dependency detection, \"last registered wins vs IEnumerable<T> gives all\", open generics, and TryAddSingleton . C# Run ▶ Copy using Microsoft . Extensions . DependencyInjection ; using Microsoft . Extensions . DependencyInjection . Extensions ; void Check ( bool condition, string claim) { if (!condition) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: {claim}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: {claim}\" ); } // 1. Transient: a new instance every single resolution, even within the same scope. var services1 = new ServiceCollection (); services1. AddTransient < GuidService >(); using var provider1 = services1. BuildServiceProvider (); using ( var scope = provider1. CreateScope ()) { var a = scope. ServiceProvider . GetRequiredService < GuidService >(). Id ; var b = scope. ServiceProvider . GetRequiredService < GuidService >(). Id ; Check (a != b, \"Transient gives a different instance on every resolution, even within one scope\" ); } // 2. Scoped: same instance within one scope; a different instance in a different scope. var services2 = new ServiceCollection (); services2. AddScoped < GuidService >(); using var provider2 = services2. BuildServiceProvider (); using var scopeA = provider2. CreateScope (); using var scopeB = provider2. CreateScope (); var scopedInA1 = scopeA. ServiceProvider . GetRequiredService < GuidService >(). Id ; var scopedInA2 = scopeA. ServiceProvider . GetRequiredService < GuidService >(). Id ; var scopedInB = scopeB. ServiceProvider . GetRequiredService < GuidService >(). Id ; Check (scopedInA1 == scopedInA2, \"Scoped gives the same instance twice within one scope\" ); Check (scopedInA1 != scopedInB, \"Scoped gives a different instance in a different scope\" ); // 3. Singleton: the same instance everywhere, across every scope, for the app's life. var services3 = new ServiceCollection (); services3. AddSingleton < GuidService >(); using var provider3 = services3. BuildServiceProvider (); using var scopeC = provider3. CreateScope (); var singletonRoot = provider3. GetRequiredService < GuidService >(). Id ; var singletonInScope = scopeC. ServiceProvider . GetRequiredService < GuidService >(). Id ; Check (singletonRoot == singletonInScope, \"Singleton gives the same instance everywhere, including inside a scope\" ); // 4. Captive dependency: a singleton depending on a scoped service is caught at resolution time when // ValidateScopes is on, instead of silently capturing the first scope's instance forever. var services4 = new ServiceCollection (); services4. AddScoped < GuidService >(); services4. AddSingleton < CapturesScoped >(); bool threw = false ; try { using var validated = services4. BuildServiceProvider ( new ServiceProviderOptions { ValidateScopes = true }); validated. GetRequiredService < CapturesScoped >(); } catch ( InvalidOperationException ) { threw = true ; } Check (threw, \"ValidateScopes=true makes resolving a singleton that depends on a scoped service throw InvalidOperationException\" ); // 5. Injecting the interface directly gives the LAST registered implementation; // injecting IEnumerable<T> gives ALL of them, in registration order. var services5 = new ServiceCollection (); services5. AddSingleton < IChannel , EmailChannel >(); services5. AddSingleton < IChannel , SmsChannel >(); services5. AddSingleton < IChannel , PushChannel >(); using var provider5 = services5. BuildServiceProvider (); var last = provider5. GetRequiredService < IChannel >(); var all = provider5. GetServices < IChannel >(). ToList (); Check (last is PushChannel , \"Resolving the interface directly gives the LAST registered implementation\" ); Check (all. Count == 3 && all[ 0 ] is EmailChannel && all[ 1 ] is SmsChannel && all[ 2 ] is PushChannel , \"Resolving IEnumerable<T> gives every implementation, in registration order\" ); // 6. Open generics: registerin"},{"c":"configuration-options","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR The same compiled app needs different settings in each environment; layered configuration merges a JSON file, environment variables, command-line args and secrets into one IConfiguration , with later layers overriding earlier ones key-by-key. Reading config[\"Payments:BaseUrl\"] directly is fine in Program.cs , but a typo in that string silently returns null instead of failing to compile — that's what the Options pattern fixes, by binding a section onto a typed class once. Three ways to consume options differ only in when they pick up a change : IOptions<T> (never, computed once), IOptionsSnapshot<T> (per request), IOptionsMonitor<T> (always current, safe in singletons). .ValidateOnStart() turns a missing or bad setting into a deployment that fails its health check immediately, instead of a payment that fails for a real customer later. Secrets never belong in a committed file: User Secrets in development, Key Vault /platform secret stores (or better, no secret at all via managed identity) in production. Feature flags separate deploying code from releasing it — ship a feature switched off, then turn it on gradually without a redeploy."},{"c":"configuration-options","s":"the-same-build-four-different-places-four-different-answers","h":"The same build, four different places, four different answers","t":"# The same build, four different places, four different answers Beginner Here's a constraint that sounds simple but shapes this entire chapter: the exact same compiled binary has to run on your laptop, in a CI pipeline, in staging, and in production — and in each of those four places, it needs a different database connection string, a different payment-gateway URL, and a set of secrets that absolutely cannot be the same ones twice. Walk through what happens if you try to solve this the naive way, by baking the values into the code: C# Run ▶ Copy // DON'T: this is the naive approach, and it breaks immediately string connectionString = \"Server=localhost;Database=Shop;...\" ; That compiles fine on your laptop. The moment you deploy it to staging, it's still pointing at localhost — which, inside a staging container, doesn't mean your laptop at all, it means the staging container itself, and there's no database there. You'd have to rebuild the app with a different hardcoded string for every environment, which means you're no longer testing the same binary you'll ship — exactly the problem ASP.NET Core architecture flagged about letting environment branch code paths instead of just values. And if any of those hardcoded strings is a real secret, it's now sitting in your git history forever, readable by anyone with repo access, discoverable even after you \"remove\" it in a later commit. ASP.NET Core's answer is layered configuration : several independent sources — a JSON file, environment variables, command-line arguments, a secrets store — each contribute key/value pairs, and later sources override earlier ones for the same key. Your code never reads any of those sources directly. It reads one merged, flattened view called IConfiguration , and has no idea — and doesn't need to know — which layer any particular value actually came from."},{"c":"configuration-options","s":"the-five-layers-lowest-priority-to-highest","h":"The five layers, lowest priority to highest","t":"# The five layers, lowest priority to highest Beginner WebApplication.CreateBuilder wires up these sources automatically, in this exact order: Diagram Copy 1. appsettings.json ← defaults, committed to git, no secrets 2. appsettings.{Environment}.json ← per-environment overrides (Development, Staging, Production) 3. User Secrets ← Development only; stored outside the repo 4. Environment variables ← how containers, App Service and Kubernetes inject settings 5. Command-line arguments ← highest priority: --Logging:LogLevel:Default=Debug (+ anything you add later, e.g. Azure Key Vault, Azure App Configuration) Let's trace one key through all five layers with real values, because \"later overrides earlier\" is easy to state and easy to get backwards in your head without a concrete run-through. JSON Copy // appsettings.json — the base, checked into git { \"ConnectionStrings\" : { \"Shop\" : \"Server=localhost;Database=Shop;Trusted_Connection=True\" }, \"Payments\" : { \"BaseUrl\" : \"https://sandbox.pay.example\" , \"TimeoutSeconds\" : 10 , \"Retries\" : 3 }, \"Logging\" : { \"LogLevel\" : { \"Default\" : \"Information\" , \"Microsoft.AspNetCore\" : \"Warning\" } }, \"AllowedHosts\" : \"*\" } JSON Copy // appsettings.Production.json — only what differs from the base { \"Payments\" : { \"BaseUrl\" : \"https://api.pay.example\" } } Now ask: in production, what is Payments:BaseUrl ? Layer 1 sets it to the sandbox URL. Layer 2 ( appsettings.Production.json , loaded because ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production ) overrides it to the real API URL — and since nothing in layers 3–5 mentions Payments:BaseUrl at all, that production value is what survives to the end. What about Payments:TimeoutSeconds ? No layer after layer 1 touches it, so it stays 10 straight from the base file — you never had to repeat it in the production file. And the real connection string, with real credentials? It was never written in either JSON file — it arrives from layer 4, an environment variable injected by whatever's actually running the container, which is exactly why you'll never find a production password by grepping the repo. That's the whole mental model: each layer only needs to mention the keys it wants to change , and the merge happens key-by-key, independently, not file-by-file."},{"c":"configuration-options","s":"reading-the-merged-view-keys-sections-and-converting-types","h":"Reading the merged view: keys, sections, and converting types","t":"# Reading the merged view: keys, sections, and converting types Beginner Configuration ends up as one flat dictionary of string keys, with : marking a level of nesting — Payments:BaseUrl , Logging:LogLevel:Default — and arrays addressed by index: AllowedOrigins:0 , AllowedOrigins:1 . C# Run ▶ Copy IConfiguration config = builder. Configuration ; string ? url = config[ \"Payments:BaseUrl\" ]; // raw string int timeout = config. GetValue < int >( \"Payments:TimeoutSeconds\" , 30 ); // converted, with default string ? cs = config. GetConnectionString ( \"Shop\" ); // = config[\"ConnectionStrings:Shop\"] IConfigurationSection section = config. GetSection ( \"Payments\" ); var opts = section. Get < PaymentOptions >(); // bind section to a class bool exists = section. Exists (); Reading IConfiguration directly like this is completely fine in Program.cs — you're wiring things up once, at startup. But scattering config[\"Payments:TimeoutSeconds\"] calls across your actual business logic is a trap worth avoiding, for a reason that becomes obvious once you hit it: a typo in that string key doesn't fail to compile, it just silently returns null or the default value at runtime, and you find out in production. The Options pattern , a few sections below, exists specifically to close that hole."},{"c":"configuration-options","s":"environment-variables-need-their-own-naming-convention","h":"Environment variables need their own naming convention","t":"# Environment variables need their own naming convention Beginner Here's a wrinkle worth knowing before it costs you a debugging session: the : that separates configuration levels isn't a character every platform allows in an environment variable name. So the environment-variable provider uses a double underscore __ instead, translated automatically back to : when it's read: Configuration key Environment variable Payments:BaseUrl Payments__BaseUrl ConnectionStrings:Shop ConnectionStrings__Shop Logging:LogLevel:Default Logging__LogLevel__Default AllowedOrigins:0 AllowedOrigins__0 Shell Copy docker run -e ConnectionStrings__Shop = \"Server=sql;...\" -e ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT =Staging shop-api Get the underscores wrong — a single _ instead of __ , say — and nothing errors. The variable just doesn't match any key the provider recognises, so your app silently falls back to whatever the JSON file said, and you spend an hour wondering why a value you clearly set isn't taking effect. Two variable prefixes are reserved specifically for the host itself rather than your application settings: ASPNETCORE_ and DOTNET_ (as you saw with ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT in the previous chapter). And one small mercy: configuration keys, including environment variable names, are matched case-insensitively , so payments__baseurl works exactly as well as Payments__BaseUrl ."},{"c":"configuration-options","s":"the-options-pattern-turning-strings-with-typos-into-a-typed-","h":"The Options pattern: turning strings-with-typos into a typed class","t":"# The Options pattern: turning strings-with-typos into a typed class Beginner So you've got a merged IConfiguration . The next question is how your actual services should consume it — and the answer the framework steers you toward is: don't , not directly. Instead, bind a configuration section onto a strongly typed class once, register that class in DI, and let services ask for the type instead of a string key. C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class PaymentOptions { public const string SectionName = \"Payments\" ; [Required, Url] public string BaseUrl { get ; init ; } = \"\" ; [Range(1, 120)] public int TimeoutSeconds { get ; init ; } = 10 ; [Range(0, 10)] public int Retries { get ; init ; } = 3 ; [Required] public string ApiKey { get ; init ; } = \"\" ; // supplied from secrets, not appsettings.json } builder. Services . AddOptions < PaymentOptions >() . BindConfiguration ( PaymentOptions . SectionName ) // bind the \"Payments\" section . ValidateDataAnnotations () // run [Required], [Range]… . Validate (o => o. BaseUrl . StartsWith ( \"https://\" ), \"Payments:BaseUrl must use HTTPS\" ) . ValidateOnStart (); // fail at STARTUP, not on the first payment public sealed class PaymentClient ( HttpClient http, IOptions < PaymentOptions > options) { private readonly PaymentOptions _o = options. Value ; // use _o.BaseUrl, _o.Retries… } Notice what .ValidateOnStart() buys you specifically, because it's the single most valuable line in that block. Without it, if Payments:ApiKey is missing from production — maybe the Key Vault reference was typo'd in the IaC template — nothing breaks at deploy time. The app starts fine. It fails later, the first time a real customer tries to pay, at some unpredictable hour, as a confusing exception deep in PaymentClient . With .ValidateOnStart() , that same missing key makes the deployment itself fail its health check and get automatically rolled back — the same kind of fast-fail-at-boundary engineering that made graceful shutdown worth the effort in the previous chapter. Why reach for options instead of calling IConfiguration everywhere Type safety: int TimeoutSeconds instead of parsing config[\"Payments:TimeoutSeconds\"] strings scattered across the codebase, each one a silent-failure typo waiting to happen. Validation at startup: a missing API key crashes the deployment immediately, where health checks and rollouts catch it, instead of failing customer payments at 3 a.m. Testability: Options.Create(new PaymentOptions { ... }) in tests, with no configuration system needed at all. Separation (Interface Segregation — a principle covered fully in Design principles , meaning \"depend only on what you actually use\"): each class gets only the settings it actually needs, not a reference to the entire configuration tree with permission to read anything."},{"c":"configuration-options","s":"three-flavours-of-give-me-the-options-and-why-there-are-thre","h":"Three flavours of \"give me the options\", and why there are three","t":"# Three flavours of \"give me the options\", and why there are three Intermediate This is the part that trips people up, so let's be precise about why three interfaces exist instead of one. The question each one answers is: when configuration changes while the app is already running, who sees the new value, and when? Interface Lifetime Picks up changes? Use when IOptions<T> Singleton No , computed once on first access Settings that don't change at runtime (most of them) IOptionsSnapshot<T> Scoped Yes, recomputed per request Reloadable settings used in scoped/transient services; named options per request IOptionsMonitor<T> Singleton Yes, CurrentValue always latest, plus OnChange callback Reloadable settings in singletons and background services IOptions<T> is the simplest and the right default: it reads the configuration once, caches it, and hands back the same value for the rest of the process's life. That's fine for almost everything — a payment gateway's base URL doesn't need to change without a redeploy. But suppose you want a feature flag that an operator can flip without restarting the app — say, during an incident, to kill a misbehaving feature in seconds rather than waiting for a full redeploy. IOptions<T> can't do that; it already baked in its answer the first time anyone asked. IOptionsMonitor<T> is built for exactly this: it's still a singleton, so it's safe to inject into another singleton or a long-lived background service, but its CurrentValue property re-reads the live configuration every single time you access it, and it offers an OnChange callback you can subscribe to the moment a change lands. C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class FeatureGate ( IOptionsMonitor < FeatureOptions > monitor, ILogger < FeatureGate > log) { private readonly IDisposable ? _sub = monitor. OnChange (o => log. LogInformation ( \"Features changed: {@F}\" , o)); public bool IsEnabled ( string f) => monitor. CurrentValue . Enabled . Contains (f); } IOptionsSnapshot<T> sits in between: it's scoped, meaning it's recomputed once per request rather than once per process, so within a single request everyone sees a consistent value, but the next request picks up whatever changed in the meantime. One detail worth being precise about, because it isn't automatic: appsettings.json reloads on file change by default ( reloadOnChange: true ), environment variables genuinely can't change for a process that's already running — the OS handed them to the process once at launch — and the Azure App Configuration and Key Vault providers refresh only on whatever polling interval you explicitly configure for them. The captive-dependency trap, reappearing here in a new costume IOptionsSnapshot<T> is scoped . If you inject it into a singleton, you've built the exact same captive dependency Dependency injection warned about — a short-lived thing trapped inside a long-lived one — and with scope validation enabled it throws at startup rather than silently freezing the options at whatever they were on the very first request. The fix is simple once you see it: use IOptionsMonitor<T> in singletons, full stop."},{"c":"configuration-options","s":"named-options-and-post-configuration-when-one-options-type-i","h":"Named options and post-configuration: when one options type isn't enough","t":"# Named options and post-configuration: when one options type isn't enough Advanced Sometimes the same shape of settings needs several independent instances — think storage settings for invoices and storage settings for images, structurally identical but pointing at different containers: C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . Configure < StorageOptions >( \"invoices\" , builder. Configuration . GetSection ( \"Storage:Invoices\" )); builder. Services . Configure < StorageOptions >( \"images\" , builder. Configuration . GetSection ( \"Storage:Images\" )); public class InvoiceStore ( IOptionsMonitor < StorageOptions > opts) { private readonly StorageOptions _o = opts. Get ( \"invoices\" ); } // Code-based configuration and post-processing builder. Services . Configure < JwtBearerOptions >( JwtBearerDefaults . AuthenticationScheme , o => o. MapInboundClaims = false ); builder. Services . PostConfigure < PaymentOptions >(o => { /* runs after all Configure calls */ }); // Configure using other services: IConfigureOptions<T> public sealed class ConfigureCors ( IOptions < FrontendOptions > fe) : IConfigureNamedOptions < CorsOptions > { public void Configure ( string ? name, CorsOptions o) => o. AddPolicy ( \"web\" , p => p. WithOrigins (fe. Value . Origins )); public void Configure ( CorsOptions o) => Configure ( null , o); } builder. Services . ConfigureOptions < ConfigureCors >(); For high-performance, AOT-friendly validation — relevant once you're on Native AOT, from How code runs — use the source-generated validator instead of the reflection-based .ValidateDataAnnotations() : [OptionsValidator] public partial class PaymentOptionsValidator : IValidateOptions<PaymentOptions> { } ."},{"c":"configuration-options","s":"secrets-the-layer-systems-hardest-case","h":"Secrets: the layer system's hardest case","t":"# Secrets: the layer system's hardest case Beginner Everything above assumes the values themselves are safe to see. Passwords, API keys, connection strings with embedded credentials, and signing keys are a different category entirely — they must never be committed to source control, and that includes appsettings.Development.json , which people forget is still a file in the repo. # In development: User Secrets Shell Copy dotnet user-secrets init # adds <UserSecretsId> to the csproj dotnet user-secrets set \"Payments:ApiKey\" \"sk_test_123\" dotnet user-secrets list These land as plain JSON in your own user profile ( ~/.microsoft/usersecrets/<id>/secrets.json ) — physically outside the repo folder entirely — and the provider only loads that file when the environment is Development . Be clear-eyed about what this does and doesn't protect against: the file is not encrypted . It's not protecting the secret from anyone who gets access to your machine; it's protecting it from ever being git add -ed by accident. # In production: no file on disk at all Approach How Notes Azure Key Vault builder.Configuration.AddAzureKeyVault(vaultUri, new DefaultAzureCredential()) Secrets appear as config keys ( Payments--ApiKey → Payments:ApiKey ); access via managed identity App Service / Container Apps settings Set in the portal/IaC; become env vars Can reference Key Vault: @Microsoft.KeyVault(SecretUri=…) Kubernetes Secrets Mounted as env vars or files ( AddKeyPerFile ) Pair with External Secrets / CSI driver for Key Vault No secret at all Managed identity for Azure SQL, Storage, Service Bus Best option: nothing to leak or rotate That last row is worth sitting with for a moment, because it's a genuinely different strategy, not just another storage location: if the app authenticates to Azure SQL via managed identity instead of a username and password, there is no secret anywhere in this picture to leak, rotate, or accidentally log. See Identity & secrets for exactly how that works."},{"c":"configuration-options","s":"feature-flags-configuration-that-controls-behaviour-not-just","h":"Feature flags: configuration that controls behaviour , not just values","t":"# Feature flags: configuration that controls behaviour , not just values Intermediate Azure App Configuration extends this same layered model into a centrally managed, shared store — useful once several services need the same settings, with per-environment labels, live change notifications, and specifically, feature flags : C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Configuration . AddAzureAppConfiguration (o => o . Connect ( new Uri (builder. Configuration [ \"AppConfig:Endpoint\" ]!), new DefaultAzureCredential ()) . Select ( KeyFilter . Any , labelFilter: builder. Environment . EnvironmentName ) . ConfigureRefresh (r => r. Register ( \"Sentinel\" , refreshAll: true ). SetRefreshInterval ( TimeSpan . FromMinutes ( 1 ))) . UseFeatureFlags ()); builder. Services . AddFeatureManagement (); // Microsoft.FeatureManagement app. MapGet ( \"/checkout\" , async ( IFeatureManager features) => await features. IsEnabledAsync ( \"NewCheckout\" ) ? Results . Ok ( \"v2\" ) : Results . Ok ( \"v1\" )); The reason this matters more than it first looks is that it separates two things that normally travel together: deploying code and releasing a feature. Without flags, shipping a risky new checkout flow means the moment the deploy finishes, every single user hits the new code — if it's broken, the only way back is another deploy. With a flag, you ship the new code dark (deployed but switched off), turn it on for 5% of traffic, watch error rates, and if something's wrong, flip the switch back off in seconds — no redeploy, no rollback, just a config change."},{"c":"configuration-options","s":"how-the-provider-mechanism-actually-works-underneath","h":"How the provider mechanism actually works underneath","t":"# How the provider mechanism actually works underneath Advanced Pulling back the curtain on the merge itself: each provider implements IConfigurationProvider , loading its source into a flat Dictionary<string,string?> . IConfigurationRoot queries all the registered providers in reverse order — last-added first — and returns the first value it finds for a given key. That single rule, \"ask the latest provider first,\" is the entire precedence mechanism; there's no separate priority system layered on top. builder.Configuration is specifically a ConfigurationManager , which means you can add more sources during phase 1 and have later reads immediately see them — unlike a plain IConfigurationRoot , which is built once and fixed. Besides the five defaults, other built-in providers worth knowing exist: INI, XML, in-memory ( AddInMemoryCollection , genuinely useful in tests), key-per-file (how Docker and Kubernetes secrets commonly get mounted), and AddJsonStream . Binding ( Bind , Get<T> ) uses reflection by default; setting <EnableConfigurationBindingGenerator>true</EnableConfigurationBindingGenerator> switches that to a source generator instead, for AOT-friendly binding with no reflection at all. C# Run ▶ Copy // Tests: override configuration without files builder. Configuration . AddInMemoryCollection ( new Dictionary < string , string ?> { [ \"Payments:BaseUrl\" ] = \"https://fake\" , [ \"Payments:ApiKey\" ] = \"test\" }); Q How does configuration precedence work in ASP.NET Core? Providers are added in order and later ones override earlier ones for the same key. By default that's appsettings.json, then appsettings.{Environment}.json, then User Secrets (Development), then environment variables, then command-line arguments. Providers added afterwards (Key Vault, App Configuration) override those too. Q What is the Options pattern and why use it? Binding configuration sections to strongly typed classes registered in DI and consumed as IOptions<T> (or Snapshot/Monitor). It gives type safety, validation (including fail-fast at startup with ValidateOnStart), testability and focused settings per class instead of passing IConfiguration around. Q IOptions vs IOptionsSnapshot vs IOptionsMonitor? IOptions is a singleton computed once, with no reload. IOptionsSnapshot is scoped and recomputed per request, so it reflects changes but can't be used in singletons. IOptionsMonitor is a singleton whose CurrentValue always reflects the latest config, with an OnChange callback; use it in singletons and background services. Q How do you set a nested configuration value via an environment variable? Replace each : with a double underscore, e.g. Payments__BaseUrl for Payments:BaseUrl , or ConnectionStrings__Shop . Q How do you manage secrets in development vs production? Development: User Secrets (stored outside the repo, loaded only in Development). Production: Azure Key Vault (or the platform's secret store) loaded via managed identity, or better, avoid secrets entirely by using managed identity to authenticate to Azure services. Never commit secrets to source control. Q How do you make the app fail fast on invalid configuration? Use AddOptions<T>().BindConfiguration(...).ValidateDataAnnotations().Validate(...).ValidateOnStart() , so validation runs during host startup and the app refuses to start (and the deployment fails its health checks) instead of failing on first use. Q What are feature flags and why use them? Runtime switches (e.g. via Microsoft.FeatureManagement and Azure App Configuration) that enable or disable features without redeploying. They decouple deployment from release, allow gradual rollouts, A/B tests and instant rollback."},{"c":"configuration-options","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Configuration in ASP.NET Core Options pattern Safe storage of app secrets in development Azure Key Vault configuration provider"},{"c":"configuration-options","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run (plain console app, .NET 8/9, top-level statements, BCL only). This models the real Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration / Options mechanics with plain dictionaries, since that's the simplest faithful way to check the merge and caching rules without spinning up a host. C# Run ▶ Copy // Claim: later configuration layers override earlier ones key-by-key, not file-by-file — // a layer that doesn't mention a key leaves whatever an earlier layer set untouched. var layer1_appsettingsJson = new Dictionary < string , string ?> { [ \"Payments:BaseUrl\" ] = \"https://sandbox.pay.example\" , [ \"Payments:TimeoutSeconds\" ] = \"10\" , }; var layer2_productionJson = new Dictionary < string , string ?> { [ \"Payments:BaseUrl\" ] = \"https://api.pay.example\" , // overrides layer 1 }; var layer4_envVars = new Dictionary < string , string ?> { [ \"ConnectionStrings:Shop\" ] = \"Server=prod-sql;...\" , // never appeared in either JSON file }; string ? Merged ( string key, params Dictionary < string , string ?>[] layersLowToHigh) { string ? value = null ; foreach ( var layer in layersLowToHigh) if (layer. TryGetValue (key, out var v)) value = v; // a later layer always wins if it mentions the key return value ; } var baseUrl = Merged ( \"Payments:BaseUrl\" , layer1_appsettingsJson, layer2_productionJson, layer4_envVars); var timeout = Merged ( \"Payments:TimeoutSeconds\" , layer1_appsettingsJson, layer2_productionJson, layer4_envVars); var connStr = Merged ( \"ConnectionStrings:Shop\" , layer1_appsettingsJson, layer2_productionJson, layer4_envVars); if (baseUrl != \"https://api.pay.example\" ) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected the production override to win, got {baseUrl}\" ); if (timeout != \"10\" ) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected the un-overridden base value 10 to survive, got {timeout}\" ); if (connStr != \"Server=prod-sql;...\" ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected the connection string to come from the environment-variable layer\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: later layers override earlier ones key-by-key — untouched keys pass through unchanged\" ); // Claim: the environment-variable provider maps \"__\" to \":\" when reading a key back. string EnvVarNameFor ( string configKey) => configKey. Replace ( \":\" , \"__\" ); string ConfigKeyFor ( string envVarName) => envVarName. Replace ( \"__\" , \":\" ); if ( EnvVarNameFor ( \"Payments:BaseUrl\" ) != \"Payments__BaseUrl\" ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: wrong env var name mapping\" ); if ( ConfigKeyFor ( \"Logging__LogLevel__Default\" ) != \"Logging:LogLevel:Default\" ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: wrong config key mapping\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: ':' in a configuration key round-trips to '__' in an environment variable name, and back\" ); // Claim: IOptions<T>-style caching computes the value once and never reflects a later change, // while IOptionsMonitor<T>-style access always re-reads the live source. var liveSettings = new Dictionary < string , string > { [ \"Feature\" ] = \"off\" }; string cachedOnce = liveSettings[ \"Feature\" ]; // IOptions<T>: computed once, like at first injection string CurrentValue () => liveSettings[ \"Feature\" ]; // IOptionsMonitor<T>.CurrentValue: re-reads every time liveSettings[ \"Feature\" ] = \"on\" ; // an operator flips a feature flag at runtime if (cachedOnce != \"off\" ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: the IOptions<T>-style cached value should stay at its original snapshot\" ); if ( CurrentValue () != \"on\" ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: the IOptionsMonitor<T>-style accessor should see the live change\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: a cached-once value (IOptions<T>) misses a later change; a live accessor (IOptionsMonitor<T>.CurrentValue) sees it\" ); // Not runtime-verifiable here — these need the real ASP.NET Core host and real Azure services: // - the actual Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration provider pipeline and ValidateOnStart() failing a deployment // - Azure Key Vault / App Configuration refresh intervals // See Further reading: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-u"},{"c":"middleware","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Middleware is a chain of components every request passes through before reaching your actual endpoint — each one can look at the request, pass it on, and look at the response on the way back. Under the hood it's just nested functions: each middleware wraps the next one. That's why \"before\" code runs in registration order and \"after\" code runs in reverse. Order is real behavior, not a style choice — e.g. UseAuthorization must come after UseAuthentication , UseCors before auth, and UseRouting before anything that reads the selected endpoint's metadata. A middleware written as a plain class is built once , at startup — effectively a singleton. Never put a scoped dependency (like a DbContext ) in its constructor; put it in InvokeAsync 's parameters instead. Short-circuiting means a middleware doesn't call next() and answers the request itself (auth failures, rate limits, cache hits, static files). Once the response has started sending, you can no longer change headers or the status code — use Response.OnStarting to set them just before that happens."},{"c":"middleware","s":"every-request-walks-through-the-same-corridor-of-rooms","h":"Every request walks through the same corridor of rooms","t":"# Every request walks through the same corridor of rooms Beginner Picture a request arriving at your app. Before it reaches the code you actually wrote — the handler that looks up an order, say — it has to pass through a sequence of checkpoints: is this HTTPS? does it need CORS headers? who's making this request, and are they allowed to? None of that is your endpoint's job to check every single time; it would be absurd to repeat \"is this HTTPS\" logic inside every single handler in the app. Middleware is how ASP.NET Core avoids that repetition. Each middleware is a component sitting in a chain, and each one gets exactly four jobs available to it for every request: receive the HttpContext , optionally do work before the rest of the pipeline runs — inspect the request, start a timer, check a header, either call next() to hand control to the next middleware in line, or stop right there (\"short-circuit\") and write the response itself, optionally do work after next() returns — inspect the response that came back, log the final status code, stop the timer. Diagram Copy ┌───────────────────────── request ─────────────────────────▶ Client ─▶ │ ExceptionHandler │ HTTPS │ StaticFiles │ Routing │ CORS │ AuthN │ AuthZ │ … │ ENDPOINT ◀───────────────────────── response ────────────────────────┘ (each middleware's \"after next()\" code runs on the way back, in REVERSE order) Russian dolls (or an onion) Each middleware wraps everything that comes after it. The request travels inward through each layer to the endpoint at the centre, and the response travels back out through the same layers, but in reverse. A layer can also simply refuse to let the request go any further inward and send a response straight back — like a security guard at the door who doesn't bother calling upstairs. Why build it as a pipeline at all, instead of one big function? Error handling, HTTPS enforcement, CORS, authentication, compression, logging, correlation IDs, rate limiting — all of these apply to every request, or every request under some path, regardless of which specific endpoint eventually handles it. Writing each one as a separate, composable middleware means you write it once , slot it into a clear, explicit order, and turn it on or off with one line in Program.cs . This is a deliberate departure from the old System.Web model, which had a large, largely hidden pipeline you couldn't easily see or reorder. Here, if you don't add a middleware, it simply doesn't run — the pipeline is exactly what you wrote and nothing more."},{"c":"middleware","s":"seeing-it-work-before-seeing-how-its-built","h":"Seeing it work before seeing how it's built","t":"# Seeing it work before seeing how it's built Beginner C# Run ▶ Copy var app = builder. Build (); app. Use ( async (context, next) => { var sw = Stopwatch . StartNew (); Console . WriteLine ( $\"→ {context.Request.Method} {context.Request.Path}\" ); // BEFORE await next(context); // run the rest of the pipeline Console . WriteLine ( $\"← {context.Response.StatusCode} in {sw.ElapsedMilliseconds} ms\" ); // AFTER }); app. Use ( async (context, next) => { if (context. Request . Headers . ContainsKey ( \"X-Block-Me\" )) { context. Response . StatusCode = StatusCodes . Status403Forbidden ; // SHORT-CIRCUIT: await context. Response . WriteAsync ( \"Blocked\" ); // don't call next return ; } await next(context); }); app. MapGet ( \"/\" , () => \"Hello\" ); // the endpoint at the end app. Run (); For GET / , that prints: Diagram Copy → GET / ← 200 in 3 ms Notice the shape: the first middleware's \"before\" line prints first, then control passes all the way down to the endpoint, then — only once everything downstream has finished — its \"after\" line prints. If a request came in carrying X-Block-Me , the second middleware would write 403 Blocked and return, and the endpoint at app.MapGet(\"/\", ...) would never run at all. Hold onto that example; the next section explains exactly why it behaves this way."},{"c":"middleware","s":"use-run-map-mapwhen-usewhen","h":"Use, Run, Map, MapWhen, UseWhen","t":"# Use, Run, Map, MapWhen, UseWhen Beginner Method What it does app.Use(middleware) Adds middleware that can call next app.Run(handler) Adds a terminal handler; it never calls next. Anything after it never runs app.Map(\"/path\", branch => …) Branches the pipeline for requests whose path starts with /path . The branch doesn't rejoin the main pipeline app.MapWhen(predicate, branch => …) Branches on any condition app.UseWhen(predicate, branch => …) Conditionally runs extra middleware, then rejoins the main pipeline app.UseMiddleware<T>() / app.UseXyz() Adds a middleware class ( UseXyz are extension methods wrapping this) C# Run ▶ Copy app. Map ( \"/legacy\" , legacy => { legacy. Run (ctx => ctx. Response . WriteAsync ( \"Legacy endpoint\" )); // only /legacy/* goes here }); app. UseWhen (ctx => ctx. Request . Path . StartsWithSegments ( \"/api\" ), api => { api. UseMiddleware < ApiKeyMiddleware >(); // only for /api/*, then continues to the main pipeline }); Map vs MapGet — same word, two unrelated mechanisms app.Map(\"/x\", ...) branches the middleware pipeline itself — an older, low-level tool. app.MapGet(\"/x\", ...) registers a routing endpoint , a completely different and much more commonly used feature covered in Routing . They sound almost identical and that's exactly the trap; 99% of the time, you want MapGet / MapPost / MapGroup , not Map ."},{"c":"middleware","s":"now-heres-the-aha-a-middleware-is-just-a-function-wrapping-a","h":"Now here's the aha: a middleware is just a function wrapping a function","t":"# Now here's the aha: a middleware is just a function wrapping a function Intermediate Go back to that worked example above and ask the question it was quietly setting up: how does await next(context) actually know what \"the rest of the pipeline\" even is? There's no global list being walked at request time — the real mechanism is simpler than that, and once you see it, middleware stops feeling like magic. The core type is one plain delegate: C# Run ▶ Copy public delegate Task RequestDelegate ( HttpContext context); A middleware, stripped down to its essence, is a function that takes one RequestDelegate (the rest of the pipeline) and returns a new RequestDelegate that wraps it : C# Run ▶ Copy Func < RequestDelegate , RequestDelegate > middleware Every call to app.Use(...) just appends one of these wrapping functions to a list — nothing executes yet. The actual composition happens once, when the host calls Build() , and it happens in reverse : C# Run ▶ Copy // Simplified version of ApplicationBuilder.Build() RequestDelegate app = context => { context. Response . StatusCode = 404 ; return Task . CompletedTask ; }; // the end for ( int i = _components. Count - 1 ; i >= 0 ; i--) app = _components[i](app); // each middleware wraps the one after it return app; // the outermost delegate: the first middleware Walk through what this does with three middleware, Use(A); Use(B); Use(C); , and a terminal endpoint. The loop starts from the last component, C , and wraps it around the 404 fallback: C(endpoint) . Then it wraps B around that: B(C(endpoint)) . Then A around all of it: A(B(C(endpoint))) . The diagram below traces exactly this: Kestrel ends up holding a single outermost delegate, A , and calls it once per request. Follow the request's path through that nesting and the reversal in the worked example stops being surprising. Calling A 's next invokes B 's whole delegate. B calling its next invokes C 's. C calling next finally reaches the endpoint. Each of those calls is still running — sitting on the call stack — waiting for the one it invoked to return. So the endpoint finishes and returns up to C . C 's code after await next() runs and returns up to B . That code runs and returns up to A . You get exactly the reverse order the pipeline diagram at the top of this chapter showed. It isn't a special rule the framework enforces; it's just how nested function calls unwind — the same way any await in Async and await resumes its caller only once the thing it awaited is done. C# Run ▶ Copy // Exactly equivalent to app.Use(async (ctx, next) => ...) app. Use (next => async context => { // before await next(context); // after });"},{"c":"middleware","s":"the-correct-order-and-precisely-what-breaks-when-you-get-it-","h":"The correct order, and precisely what breaks when you get it wrong","t":"# The correct order, and precisely what breaks when you get it wrong Beginner Since each middleware only ever sees what the ones before it have already done, and only wraps what comes after it, the order you register things in isn't a style choice — it's the actual behaviour. Here's Microsoft's recommended order for a typical API or web app: C# Run ▶ Copy var app = builder. Build (); app. UseForwardedHeaders (); // 0. Behind a proxy: fix scheme/IP FIRST so everything after sees the real values app. UseExceptionHandler (); // 1. Outermost: catches exceptions from EVERYTHING after it if (!app. Environment . IsDevelopment ()) app. UseHsts (); // 2. Tell browsers to always use HTTPS app. UseHttpsRedirection (); // 3. Redirect http → https app. UseStaticFiles (); // 4. Serve wwwroot files and short-circuit: no auth/routing cost for images, css // (or app.MapStaticAssets() in .NET 9+, as an endpoint) app. UseRouting (); // 5. Choose the endpoint (implicit if omitted; explicit call controls position) app. UseRequestLocalization (); // Culture from headers/query/cookies app. UseCors (); // 6. After routing (to read endpoint CORS metadata), before auth (preflight requests carry no credentials) app. UseAuthentication (); // 7. Who are you? Sets HttpContext.User app. UseAuthorization (); // 8. Are you allowed? Reads the endpoint's [Authorize]/policy metadata app. UseRateLimiter (); // 9. After auth if limits are per-user app. UseRequestTimeouts (); app. UseOutputCache (); // 10. Serve cached responses (after auth, so cache respects authorization) app. UseResponseCompression (); // (often before static files; depends on whether you compress them) app. UseMiddleware < CorrelationIdMiddleware >(); // your custom middleware app. MapControllers (); // 11. Endpoints (endpoint middleware executes the chosen endpoint) app. MapOrderEndpoints (); Each row below is a real-world bug report with the same root cause: a middleware placed before something it secretly depended on. If you get this wrong… Symptom UseAuthorization before UseAuthentication Every request looks anonymous: 401/403 everywhere UseAuthentication / UseAuthorization before UseRouting (explicit) Authorization can't see endpoint metadata, so [Authorize] is ignored or throws Exception handler not first Exceptions from earlier middleware aren't converted to ProblemDetails UseCors after auth Preflight OPTIONS requests rejected with 401: \"CORS error\" in the browser UseStaticFiles after auth Static files require auth, and every image request pays the auth cost UseForwardedHeaders late HTTPS redirect loops; wrong client IPs in logs and rate limiting Custom middleware that needs User placed before UseAuthentication context.User is empty Say it like this \"Middleware runs in registration order on the way in and reverse order on the way out. The exception handler goes first so it wraps everything; HTTPS and static files go early; routing selects the endpoint; CORS, then authentication then authorization, because authorization needs both the user and the endpoint's metadata; then rate limiting and caching; endpoints last.\""},{"c":"middleware","s":"the-built-in-middleware-catalogue","h":"The built-in middleware catalogue","t":"# The built-in middleware catalogue Intermediate Middleware Add with Purpose Short-circuits? Forwarded headers UseForwardedHeaders Apply X-Forwarded-* from a trusted proxy No Exception handler UseExceptionHandler Catch exceptions → ProblemDetails / error page Writes error response Developer exception page (automatic in Dev) Detailed error page with stack trace Yes, on error Status code pages UseStatusCodePages Bodies for empty 4xx/5xx responses — HSTS UseHsts Strict-Transport-Security header No HTTPS redirection UseHttpsRedirection 307/308 redirect to https Yes Static files UseStaticFiles / MapStaticAssets Serve files from wwwroot Yes, when file found Routing UseRouting Match URL to endpoint No (sets endpoint) Request localization UseRequestLocalization Set culture No CORS UseCors Cross-origin headers, answer preflight Yes, for preflight Authentication UseAuthentication Build HttpContext.User No (challenge comes later) Authorization UseAuthorization Enforce policies Yes: 401/403 Antiforgery UseAntiforgery CSRF token validation for forms Yes, on failure Rate limiter UseRateLimiter Throttle Yes: 429 Request timeouts UseRequestTimeouts Cancel slow requests — Output caching UseOutputCache Cache whole responses server-side Yes, on hit Response caching UseResponseCaching HTTP cache-header based caching Yes, on hit Response compression UseResponseCompression gzip/brotli No Session UseSession Server-side session state No WebSockets UseWebSockets Upgrade connections — Health checks MapHealthChecks (endpoint) /health responses (endpoint) W3C logging / HTTP logging UseHttpLogging , UseW3CLogging Log request/response details No Endpoint (implicit at the end) Execute the selected endpoint Terminal"},{"c":"middleware","s":"writing-your-own-middleware-three-styles-one-real-tradeoff","h":"Writing your own middleware: three styles, one real tradeoff","t":"# Writing your own middleware: three styles, one real tradeoff Intermediate Inline lambdas like the ones above are fine for a handful of lines you won't reuse. For anything reusable, write a class — and there are three ways to do that, each trading off a little speed for a little safety. # 1. Convention-based middleware (the common case) C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class CorrelationIdMiddleware ( RequestDelegate next, ILogger < CorrelationIdMiddleware > logger) { private const string Header = \"X-Correlation-Id\" ; // Constructor: runs ONCE at startup. Only singleton-safe dependencies here. // InvokeAsync: runs per request. Scoped dependencies go in its PARAMETERS. public async Task InvokeAsync ( HttpContext context, ICurrentRequest current) { var id = context. Request . Headers [Header] . FirstOrDefault () ?? Activity . Current ?. TraceId . ToString () ?? Guid . NewGuid (). ToString ( \"n\" ); // Activity.Current is .NET's built-in distributed-tracing object: if one is already running for this // request, its TraceId makes a better correlation ID than a random GUID. More in Observability. current. CorrelationId = id; context. Response . OnStarting (() => { context. Response . Headers [Header] = id; return Task . CompletedTask ; }); using (logger. BeginScope ( new Dictionary < string , object > { [ \"CorrelationId\" ] = id })) { await next(context); } } } // Registration: an extension method keeps Program.cs readable public static class CorrelationIdExtensions { public static IApplicationBuilder UseCorrelationId ( this IApplicationBuilder app) => app. UseMiddleware < CorrelationIdMiddleware >(); } app. UseCorrelationId (); The rules behind the convention: a public constructor taking RequestDelegate first, a public Invoke or InvokeAsync method taking HttpContext first and returning Task , and any extra InvokeAsync parameters get resolved fresh from the request scope every time. Conventional middleware is effectively a singleton — and that has teeth It's constructed exactly once , when the pipeline is built at startup, not once per request. Inject a scoped service — a DbContext , say — into its constructor , and you've captured the very first request's instance and trapped it there forever: a captive dependency, the same failure mode Dependency injection covers. Scoped services belong in InvokeAsync 's parameters, which the framework correctly re-resolves on every call. # 2. Factory-based middleware: IMiddleware C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class TenantMiddleware ( ShopDb db, ITenantContext tenant) : IMiddleware // scoped deps in ctor are fine { public async Task InvokeAsync ( HttpContext context, RequestDelegate next) { var host = context. Request . Host . Host ; tenant. Current = await db. Tenants . AsNoTracking (). FirstOrDefaultAsync (t => t. Host == host, context. RequestAborted ); if (tenant. Current is null ) { context. Response . StatusCode = 404 ; return ; } await next(context); } } builder. Services . AddScoped < TenantMiddleware >(); // must be REGISTERED in DI (any lifetime) app. UseMiddleware < TenantMiddleware >(); The difference that justifies the extra ceremony: IMiddleware instances are resolved from DI per request , honouring whatever lifetime you registered, which is exactly why constructor injection of a scoped service is safe here when it wasn't above. It costs a little more — an extra DI resolution per request — in exchange for not having to think about the captive-dependency trap at all. # 3. Inline with app.Use Best for a handful of lines you genuinely won't reuse anywhere else. Since .NET 6 there's an overload, app.Use(async (context, next) => …) , where next is a plain RequestDelegate rather than the older Func<Task> — worth knowing because it avoids an extra allocation per request compared with the older form."},{"c":"middleware","s":"short-circuiting-and-terminal-middleware","h":"Short-circuiting and terminal middleware","t":"# Short-circuiting and terminal middleware Intermediate Short-circuiting — the \"security guard at the door\" move from the opening analogy — just means deliberately not calling next . The places you'll see it on purpose: Authorization failure: 401/403. Rate limit exceeded: 429. Cache hit: serve the stored response. Static file found: stream it. Health or ping endpoints that should skip everything. C# Run ▶ Copy // .NET 8: endpoints that skip the remaining middleware entirely app. MapGet ( \"/ping\" , () => \"pong\" ). ShortCircuit (); app. MapShortCircuit ( 404 , \"robots.txt\" , \"favicon.ico\" ); // fast 404s for noise requests"},{"c":"middleware","s":"working-with-the-response-the-has-started-trap","h":"Working with the response: the \"has started\" trap","t":"# Working with the response: the \"has started\" trap Advanced Once the response's headers have been sent — the moment the body starts being written, or the first explicit flush — you can no longer touch the status code or headers. Try anyway, and you get a precise, unforgiving exception: InvalidOperationException: Headers are read-only, response has already started. C# Run ▶ Copy app. Use ( async (ctx, next) => { await next(ctx); ctx. Response . Headers [ \"X-Server-Time\" ] = DateTime . UtcNow . ToString ( \"O\" ); // ✘ may throw if the endpoint wrote a body }); app. Use ( async (ctx, next) => { ctx. Response . OnStarting (() => // ✔ runs just before headers are sent { ctx. Response . Headers [ \"X-Server-Time\" ] = DateTime . UtcNow . ToString ( \"O\" ); return Task . CompletedTask ; }); await next(ctx); }); Why does the first version only sometimes throw? Because \"has the body started writing\" depends on exactly what the endpoint did. A small enough response might still be sitting in a buffer when your \"after next() \" code runs, and only starts streaming afterward — so the bug hides until someone's response happens to be large enough to flush early. Response.OnStarting sidesteps the whole race: it registers a callback guaranteed to run at the one moment that's actually safe. Check context.Response.HasStarted before attempting to write an error response, for the same reason. Response.OnCompleted is different again — it runs after the response has fully gone out, which makes it useful for metrics but useless for modifying anything. # Reading the request body more than once The request body arrives as a forward-only stream — read it once, and it's gone. If a middleware reads it early (for logging, or to verify a signature), the endpoint downstream finds nothing left, unless you explicitly enable rewinding first: C# Run ▶ Copy app. Use ( async (ctx, next) => { ctx. Request . EnableBuffering (); // allow rewinding (buffers to memory, then disk) using var reader = new StreamReader (ctx. Request . Body , leaveOpen: true ); var body = await reader. ReadToEndAsync (); ctx. Request . Body . Position = 0 ; // rewind for the next reader await next(ctx); }); # Rewriting the response body To transform a response on the way out, swap Response.Body for a MemoryStream , call next , then copy the buffered result back into the real response stream. This is a genuine tradeoff, not a free trick: it costs memory (you're holding the entire response in memory) and it breaks true streaming (nothing reaches the client until you're done). Prefer the built-in middleware that already does this carefully — compression, output caching — or an endpoint filter, before reaching for this by hand."},{"c":"middleware","s":"middleware-vs-filters-vs-endpoint-filters","h":"Middleware vs filters vs endpoint filters","t":"# Middleware vs filters vs endpoint filters Intermediate Middleware MVC filters (controllers) Endpoint filters (minimal APIs) Runs for Every request through that pipeline position Only MVC actions Only the endpoints they're attached to Knows about HttpContext (raw HTTP) Action descriptor, model state, bound arguments , IActionResult Endpoint arguments , returned result Runs Before routing selects, or around the endpoint Inside the endpoint, after binding Inside the endpoint, after binding Good for App-wide concerns: errors, HTTPS, CORS, auth, compression, correlation IDs, rate limits Action-specific validation, transactions, auditing, caching per action Validation, argument transformation, per-endpoint auditing The distinction that actually matters here is what each one is allowed to see . Middleware only ever sees the raw HTTP context — headers, the body as a stream, the path — because it runs before (or around) the model-binding machinery that turns a JSON body into a typed C# object. A filter runs after binding, so it can inspect the already-typed, already-bound argument directly: C# Run ▶ Copy // Endpoint filter: sees the typed arguments, which middleware can't public sealed class ValidationFilter < T >( IValidator < T > validator) : IEndpointFilter { public async ValueTask < object ?> InvokeAsync ( EndpointFilterInvocationContext ctx, EndpointFilterDelegate next) { if (ctx. Arguments . OfType < T >(). FirstOrDefault () is { } arg) { var result = await validator. ValidateAsync (arg, ctx. HttpContext . RequestAborted ); if (!result. IsValid ) return TypedResults . ValidationProblem (result. ToDictionary ()); } return await next(ctx); } } orders. MapPost ( \"/\" , CreateOrder ). AddEndpointFilter < ValidationFilter < CreateOrderRequest >>(); The rule of thumb that falls out of this: if it needs to know about the request as raw HTTP, use middleware; if it needs to know about the action and its already-typed inputs, use a filter. See Building APIs for the MVC filter pipeline in full."},{"c":"middleware","s":"middleware-that-reads-endpoint-metadata","h":"Middleware that reads endpoint metadata","t":"# Middleware that reads endpoint metadata Advanced Here's something that looks like it contradicts the rule just given, but doesn't: after UseRouting has run, middleware placed later in the pipeline can see the selected endpoint and whatever metadata it carries — attributes, .WithMetadata(...) , .RequireAuthorization() . That's precisely how authorization, CORS, rate limiting and output caching each know what to do for a given endpoint without being filters themselves. You can use the exact same trick in your own middleware: C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed record AuditAttribute ( string Action ); app. Use ( async (ctx, next) => { var audit = ctx. GetEndpoint ()?. Metadata . GetMetadata < AuditAttribute >(); await next(ctx); if (audit is not null && ctx. Response . StatusCode < 400 ) await ctx. RequestServices . GetRequiredService < IAuditLog >(). WriteAsync (audit. Action , ctx. User ); }); orders. MapPost ( \"/{id}/refund\" , Refund ). WithMetadata ( new AuditAttribute ( \"order.refund\" ));"},{"c":"middleware","s":"testing-middleware","h":"Testing middleware","t":"# Testing middleware Advanced C# Run ▶ Copy [Fact] public async Task Adds_correlation_header () { using var host = await new HostBuilder () . ConfigureWebHost (web => web. UseTestServer () . ConfigureServices (s => s. AddScoped < ICurrentRequest , CurrentRequest >()) . Configure (app => { app. UseCorrelationId (); app. Run (ctx => ctx. Response . WriteAsync ( \"ok\" )); })) . StartAsync (); var response = await host. GetTestClient (). GetAsync ( \"/\" ); Assert . True (response. Headers . Contains ( \"X-Correlation-Id\" )); } Or, for a pure unit test with no host at all, call InvokeAsync directly with a DefaultHttpContext and a fake RequestDelegate . Q What is middleware in ASP.NET Core? Components assembled into a pipeline that handle every HTTP request and response. Each receives the HttpContext, can do work before and after invoking the next component, or can short-circuit by producing the response itself. The pipeline is built from RequestDelegate functions composed in registration order. Q Why does middleware order matter? Give examples. Each middleware only sees the effects of the ones before it and wraps the ones after it. The exception handler must be first to catch everything. Authentication must precede authorization or users look anonymous. Routing must precede authorization so endpoint metadata like [Authorize] is available. CORS must run before auth so preflight requests aren't rejected. Static files go early to avoid unnecessary work. Q What's the difference between Use, Run and Map? Use adds middleware that can call next. Run adds terminal middleware that ends the pipeline. Map branches the pipeline for a path prefix, and the branch doesn't rejoin. UseWhen conditionally adds middleware and then rejoins the main pipeline. Q How is the middleware pipeline built internally? Each middleware is a Func<RequestDelegate, RequestDelegate>. On startup, ApplicationBuilder.Build composes them in reverse order, starting with a terminal 404 delegate, so each wraps the next, producing one outermost RequestDelegate that the server invokes per request. Q How do you write custom middleware, and how do you inject scoped services into it? Create a class with a constructor taking RequestDelegate and an InvokeAsync(HttpContext, …) method, then register it with app.UseMiddleware<T>(). The instance is created once, so scoped services must be InvokeAsync parameters, not constructor parameters. Alternatively implement IMiddleware and register it in DI, which resolves it per request and allows constructor injection of scoped services. Q What does short-circuiting mean in the pipeline? A middleware not calling next and writing the response itself, so later middleware and the endpoint never run. Examples: auth failures (401/403), rate limiting (429), cache hits, static files, CORS preflight responses. Q Why might setting a response header after await next() fail? Once the response has started (headers flushed because the body began writing), headers and status code are read-only, and modifying them throws. Use Response.OnStarting to set headers just before they're sent, and check Response.HasStarted before writing error responses. Q Middleware vs filters: when do you use each? Middleware for app-wide, HTTP-level concerns that apply regardless of endpoint (errors, HTTPS, CORS, auth, compression, correlation IDs). Filters (MVC or endpoint filters) for endpoint-specific concerns that need the action context or the typed, bound arguments and results (validation, auditing, transactions). Q How can middleware read the request body without breaking the endpoint? Call Request.EnableBuffering() so the stream can be rewound, read it (leaving the stream open), then set Request.Body.Position = 0 before calling next. Q How does authorization middleware know which policy an endpoint needs? Routing runs first and attaches the selected endpoint to the HttpContext. Endpoint metadata (from [Authorize], RequireAuthorization, etc.) is available via context.GetEndpoi"},{"c":"middleware","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading ASP.NET Core Middleware (official, including the order diagram) Write custom ASP.NET Core middleware Factory-based middleware Andrew Lock, ASP.NET Core in Action , chapter on the middleware pipeline."},{"c":"middleware","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run in a console project ( dotnet new web , then dotnet add package Microsoft.AspNetCore.TestHost ). It checks the before/after ordering, short-circuiting, that conventional middleware is built once, and that UseWhen rejoins the main pipeline. C# Run ▶ Copy using Microsoft . AspNetCore . Builder ; using Microsoft . AspNetCore . TestHost ; using Microsoft . Extensions . DependencyInjection ; void Check ( bool condition, string claim) { if (!condition) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: {claim}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: {claim}\" ); } // 1. \"Before\" code runs in registration order (A, B); \"after\" code runs in REVERSE order (B, A). var order = new List < string >(); var builder1 = WebApplication . CreateBuilder (); builder1. WebHost . UseTestServer (); var app1 = builder1. Build (); app1. Use ( async (ctx, next) => { order. Add ( \"A-before\" ); await next(ctx); order. Add ( \"A-after\" ); }); app1. Use ( async (ctx, next) => { order. Add ( \"B-before\" ); await next(ctx); order. Add ( \"B-after\" ); }); app1. MapGet ( \"/\" , () => \"ok\" ); await app1. StartAsync (); await app1. GetTestClient (). GetStringAsync ( \"/\" ); await app1. StopAsync (); Check (order. SequenceEqual ( new [] { \"A-before\" , \"B-before\" , \"B-after\" , \"A-after\" }), \"Middleware runs 'before' code in registration order and 'after' code in reverse order\" ); // 2. Short-circuiting (not calling next()) stops everything after it, including the endpoint, from running. var endpointRan = false ; var builder2 = WebApplication . CreateBuilder (); builder2. WebHost . UseTestServer (); var app2 = builder2. Build (); app2. Use ( async (ctx, next) => { if (ctx. Request . Headers . ContainsKey ( \"X-Block-Me\" )) { ctx. Response . StatusCode = 403 ; return ; } await next(ctx); }); app2. MapGet ( \"/\" , () => { endpointRan = true ; return \"ok\" ; }); await app2. StartAsync (); var client2 = app2. GetTestClient (); var req = new HttpRequestMessage ( HttpMethod . Get , \"/\" ); req. Headers . Add ( \"X-Block-Me\" , \"1\" ); var blocked = await client2. SendAsync (req); await app2. StopAsync (); Check (blocked. StatusCode == System . Net . HttpStatusCode . Forbidden , \"A short-circuiting middleware returns its own status code\" ); Check (!endpointRan, \"Short-circuiting prevents the endpoint from ever running\" ); // 3. Conventional middleware (used with app.UseMiddleware<T>()) is constructed ONCE at startup, // not once per request — so it behaves like a singleton even with no DI lifetime involved. CountingMiddleware . ConstructedCount = 0 ; var builder3 = WebApplication . CreateBuilder (); builder3. WebHost . UseTestServer (); var app3 = builder3. Build (); app3. UseMiddleware < CountingMiddleware >(); app3. MapGet ( \"/\" , () => \"ok\" ); await app3. StartAsync (); var client3 = app3. GetTestClient (); await client3. GetStringAsync ( \"/\" ); await client3. GetStringAsync ( \"/\" ); await client3. GetStringAsync ( \"/\" ); await app3. StopAsync (); Check ( CountingMiddleware . ConstructedCount == 1 , \"Conventional middleware is constructed exactly once for 3 separate requests, not once per request\" ); // 4. UseWhen conditionally runs extra middleware and REJOINS the main pipeline (the endpoint still runs). var branchRan = false ; var mainEndpointRan = false ; var builder4 = WebApplication . CreateBuilder (); builder4. WebHost . UseTestServer (); var app4 = builder4. Build (); app4. UseWhen (ctx => ctx. Request . Path . StartsWithSegments ( \"/api\" ), api => api. Use ( async (ctx, next) => { branchRan = true ; await next(ctx); })); app4. MapGet ( \"/api/ping\" , () => { mainEndpointRan = true ; return \"pong\" ; }); await app4. StartAsync (); await app4. GetTestClient (). GetStringAsync ( \"/api/ping\" ); await app4. StopAsync (); Check (branchRan && mainEndpointRan, \"UseWhen runs the branch's middleware AND still reaches the main endpoint afterward\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"All middleware claims verified.\" ); class CountingMiddleware { public static int ConstructedCount ; priv"},{"c":"routing","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Routing's job: turn an HTTP method + path into one endpoint — code, metadata, and the values extracted from the URL. It's split into two middleware: one that picks the endpoint, one that runs it. Everything in between (CORS, auth, rate limiting) can read that endpoint's metadata first. Route templates ( {id:int} ) extract and constrain values. A failed constraint means \"no route matches\" (404), not \"bad input\" (400). Route groups let many endpoints share a prefix and a set of rules (auth, tags, rate limits) instead of repeating them on every endpoint. When two routes could both match, the more specific one wins; a genuine tie throws AmbiguousMatchException at runtime. Build links from a route's name ( LinkGenerator ), not a hand-built string, so they survive the template changing later."},{"c":"routing","s":"the-question-routing-exists-to-answer","h":"The question routing exists to answer","t":"# The question routing exists to answer Beginner A request arrives as nothing more than an HTTP method and a URL path — GET /api/orders/42 . Somewhere, that has to turn into an actual decision: which piece of your code runs, and what are its inputs? That translation is routing's entire job: map the incoming request to an endpoint — a runnable unit of code (a RequestDelegate , underneath) bundled together with metadata describing it. Diagram Copy GET /api/orders/42 ──▶ endpoint \"GET /api/orders/{id:int}\" handler: OrderEndpoints.GetById metadata: [Authorize(\"CanRead\")], [Tags(\"Orders\")], RateLimiting(\"per-user\"), Produces<OrderDto>(200), Produces(404) … route values: { id = 42 } Notice that the result isn't just \"call this method\" — it's a whole bundle: the handler, a pile of metadata describing authorization and rate-limiting policies and documentation tags, and the actual route values ( id = 42 ) extracted from the URL. That metadata turns out to matter more than it looks, as the next section explains."},{"c":"routing","s":"why-routing-had-to-be-split-into-two-separate-middleware","h":"Why routing had to be split into two separate middleware","t":"# Why routing had to be split into two separate middleware Intermediate Since ASP.NET Core 3.0, routing isn't one step — it's deliberately two middleware, with a gap in between that the rest of the pipeline uses: UseRouting (the EndpointRoutingMiddleware ) matches the request against every registered endpoint and stores the winner on HttpContext — retrievable as context.GetEndpoint() — along with its route values. The endpoint middleware , added automatically at the very end of the pipeline, actually executes the endpoint that was selected. Diagram Copy UseRouting ─▶ UseCors ─▶ UseAuthentication ─▶ UseAuthorization ─▶ UseRateLimiter ─▶ UseOutputCache ─▶ [endpoint executes] │ │ │ │ │ └── picks ─────┴─── each reads the endpoint's metadata: CORS policy, [Authorize] policy, limiter policy, cache policy Here's why that gap matters. Everything sitting between \"select\" and \"execute\" — CORS, authentication, authorization, rate limiting, output caching — can now read the chosen endpoint's metadata before deciding what to do, without needing to know anything about how the endpoint was matched. Before this split existed, in ASP.NET Core 2.x, MVC did its own routing right at the end of the pipeline. That meant authorization and CORS had no way to know in advance which action was even going to run — they'd have needed to duplicate the routing logic themselves just to ask that question. Endpoint routing fixes this once, centrally: one routing system serves controllers, minimal APIs, Razor Pages, SignalR hubs, gRPC services and health checks alike, and every one of them exposes the same metadata to the same pipeline. With WebApplication , you don't actually have to call UseRouting yourself — it's inserted automatically near the start of the pipeline. You'd call it explicitly only when you need some other middleware, like static files or forwarded headers, to run genuinely before routing happens, or when you want precise control over exactly where in the pipeline matching occurs."},{"c":"routing","s":"route-templates-the-syntax-that-extracts-values-from-a-url","h":"Route templates: the syntax that extracts values from a URL","t":"# Route templates: the syntax that extracts values from a URL Beginner C# Run ▶ Copy app. MapGet ( \"/products\" , GetAll ); // literal app. MapGet ( \"/products/{id}\" , GetById ); // parameter app. MapGet ( \"/products/{id:int}\" , GetById ); // with constraint app. MapGet ( \"/products/{slug}/reviews/{page:int=1}\" , Reviews ); // default value app. MapGet ( \"/search/{term?}\" , Search ); // optional parameter app. MapGet ( \"/files/{**path}\" , GetFile ); // catch-all (slashes kept in value) app. MapGet ( \"/v{version:apiVersion}/orders\" , …); // custom constraint (API versioning) Each {name} segment becomes a route value, and that value binds to a handler argument by matching name — {id} in the template flows straight into an int id parameter on the handler, with no extra wiring needed. # Route constraints: narrowing what a segment is allowed to match Constraint Matches Example int , long , decimal , double , float , bool Parses as that type {id:int} guid A GUID {id:guid} datetime A date {from:datetime} alpha Letters only {slug:alpha} minlength(n) , maxlength(n) , length(a,b) String length {code:length(3)} min(n) , max(n) , range(a,b) Numeric range {page:int:min(1)} regex(...) Regular expression {sku:regex(^[A-Z]{{2}}-\\\\d{{4}}$)} required Non-empty {name:required} Custom IRouteConstraint / IParameterPolicy {tenant:tenant} Constraints decide whether a route matches, not whether input is valid Here's the subtlety worth getting right: a failed constraint doesn't mean \"bad request\", it means \"this route doesn't match at all\" — so the result is a 404 , not a 400. Request /products/abc against a template of {id:int} , and you get 404 Not Found , which can genuinely confuse an API client expecting 400 Bad Request for malformed input. The right way to think about constraints is as a way to disambiguate between competing routes — /products/{id:int} from /products/{slug} — not as a validation layer. Validate real input properly inside the endpoint itself, and return 400 there."},{"c":"routing","s":"route-groups-shared-prefix-shared-rules-written-once","h":"Route groups: shared prefix, shared rules, written once","t":"# Route groups: shared prefix, shared rules, written once Beginner A route group lets many related endpoints share both a URL prefix and a set of metadata , which is what keeps a large minimal-API surface from turning into repetitive boilerplate: C# Run ▶ Copy var api = app. MapGroup ( \"/api\" ) . RequireAuthorization () // every endpoint below requires an authenticated user . AddEndpointFilter < RequestLoggingFilter >(); var orders = api. MapGroup ( \"/orders\" ) . WithTags ( \"Orders\" ) // OpenAPI grouping . RequireRateLimiting ( \"per-user\" ); orders. MapGet ( \"/\" , ListOrders ); // GET /api/orders orders. MapGet ( \"/{id:int}\" , GetOrder ) // GET /api/orders/42 . WithName ( \"GetOrder\" ); // named, for link generation orders. MapPost ( \"/\" , CreateOrder ); // POST /api/orders orders. MapDelete ( \"/{id:int}\" , DeleteOrder ) . RequireAuthorization ( \"Admin\" ); // extra policy on top of the group's api. MapGroup ( \"/public\" ). AllowAnonymous (). MapGet ( \"/status\" , () => \"ok\" ); // opt out Notice the DeleteOrder endpoint: it doesn't just inherit the group's RequireAuthorization() , it adds a stricter \"Admin\" policy on top. Metadata stacks rather than replaces — which is exactly why the last line's AllowAnonymous() reads as an explicit opt-out rather than a silent contradiction."},{"c":"routing","s":"routing-for-controllers-attributes-instead-of-fluent-calls","h":"Routing for controllers: attributes instead of fluent calls","t":"# Routing for controllers: attributes instead of fluent calls Beginner Controllers reach the same destination through a different syntax — attribute routing , which is required for any API controller marked [ApiController] : C# Run ▶ Copy [ApiController] [ Route ( \"api/[controller]\" )] // [controller] token → \"orders\" public class OrdersController : ControllerBase { [HttpGet] // GET api/orders public IActionResult List ( [FromQuery] int page = 1 ) => Ok (); [HttpGet(\"{id:int}\", Name = \"GetOrder\")] // GET api/orders/42 public IActionResult Get ( int id) => Ok (); [HttpPost] // POST api/orders public IActionResult Create ( CreateOrder cmd) => CreatedAtRoute ( \"GetOrder\" , new { id = 1 }, null ); [HttpPut(\"{id:int}/status\")] // PUT api/orders/42/status public IActionResult SetStatus ( int id, StatusDto dto) => NoContent (); [HttpGet(\"~/api/customers/{customerId}/orders\")] // ~ overrides the controller prefix public IActionResult ForCustomer ( int customerId) => Ok (); } app. MapControllers (); // registers all attribute-routed controller endpoints A different, older style — conventional routing — is still common for MVC web apps that render views rather than serving an API, because it defines the URL pattern once , centrally, instead of repeating an attribute on every action: C# Run ▶ Copy app. MapControllerRoute (name: \"default\" , pattern: \"{controller=Home}/{action=Index}/{id?}\" );"},{"c":"routing","s":"endpoint-metadata-the-vocabulary-every-cross-cutting-concern","h":"Endpoint metadata: the vocabulary every cross-cutting concern reads from","t":"# Endpoint metadata: the vocabulary every cross-cutting concern reads from Intermediate Metadata is simply a list of arbitrary objects attached to an endpoint — and the table below is really a map of \"who reads what,\" tying straight back to the pipeline diagram earlier in this chapter. Attributes on controllers and fluent calls on minimal APIs are just two different syntaxes for attaching the exact same underlying metadata: Fluent (minimal API) Attribute (controllers) Read by .RequireAuthorization(\"Policy\") [Authorize(Policy = \"Policy\")] Authorization middleware .AllowAnonymous() [AllowAnonymous] Authorization middleware .RequireCors(\"web\") [EnableCors(\"web\")] CORS middleware .RequireRateLimiting(\"p\") [EnableRateLimiting(\"p\")] Rate limiter .CacheOutput(\"p\") [OutputCache(PolicyName = \"p\")] Output cache .WithName(\"X\") Name = \"X\" Link generator .WithTags , .WithSummary , .Produces<T>() [Tags] , [ProducesResponseType] OpenAPI .WithMetadata(obj) Any custom attribute Your middleware .WithRequestTimeout(\"p\") [RequestTimeout] Request timeouts .ShortCircuit() — Skips remaining middleware"},{"c":"routing","s":"when-more-than-one-route-could-match-precedence-and-ambiguit","h":"When more than one route could match: precedence and ambiguity","t":"# When more than one route could match: precedence and ambiguity Advanced Real route tables inevitably end up with templates that could both match the same URL — /products/featured and /products/{id} both look plausible for a request to /products/featured . Routing resolves this with a precedence order, most specific wins: Literal segments beat parameter segments: /products/featured beats /products/{id} . Parameters with constraints beat unconstrained ones: {id:int} beats {slug} . Complex segments beat simple parameters; catch-alls have the lowest precedence. HTTP method constraints filter candidates ( GET vs POST on the same path). If, after all four rules, two endpoints are still equally specific, routing has genuinely no way to pick a winner, and you get an AmbiguousMatchException at runtime — \"The request matched multiple endpoints.\" The fix is always to make one of them more specific: add a constraint, change a literal, or set an explicit Order on controllers. Worth knowing if you're wondering whether this adds overhead as a route table grows: matching is done by a DFA-based matcher ( documented in the ASP.NET Core source ), built once at startup from every registered template. Because it's a precompiled state machine rather than a linear scan down a list, matching cost barely grows with how many routes you've registered."},{"c":"routing","s":"link-generation-building-urls-that-survive-a-route-changing","h":"Link generation: building URLs that survive a route changing","t":"# Link generation: building URLs that survive a route changing Advanced Build URLs from route names and values, not by concatenating strings, so that a route's template can change later without silently breaking every link that pointed at it: C# Run ▶ Copy orders. MapPost ( \"/\" , async ( CreateOrder cmd, IOrderService svc, LinkGenerator links, HttpContext ctx) => { var id = await svc. CreateAsync (cmd); var url = links. GetUriByName (ctx, \"GetOrder\" , new { id }); // https://host/api/orders/42 return Results . Created (url, new { id }); }); // Shortcuts return TypedResults . CreatedAtRoute (dto, \"GetOrder\" , new { id = dto. Id }); // minimal API return CreatedAtAction ( nameof ( Get ), new { id }, dto); // controller LinkGenerator is registered as a singleton, so you can inject it anywhere you need a URL — not just inside an endpoint handler, but genuinely useful for HATEOAS-style response links or for building URLs into outgoing emails."},{"c":"routing","s":"reading-route-values-back-out-of-the-request","h":"Reading route values back out of the request","t":"# Reading route values back out of the request Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy app. MapGet ( \"/tenants/{tenant}/orders/{id:int}\" , ( string tenant, int id, HttpContext ctx) => { var raw = ctx. Request . RouteValues [ \"tenant\" ]; // also available as a dictionary var endpoint = ctx. GetEndpoint (); // the matched endpoint return Results . Ok ( new { tenant, id, endpoint?. DisplayName }); }); Two small, commonly wanted tweaks live in RouteOptions : lowercase URLs and trailing-slash behaviour. builder.Services.Configure<RouteOptions>(o => { o.LowercaseUrls = true; o.AppendTrailingSlash = false; }); Q What is endpoint routing? The routing system since ASP.NET Core 3.0, shared by MVC, minimal APIs, Razor Pages, SignalR, gRPC and health checks. UseRouting matches the request to an endpoint early and stores it on HttpContext; the endpoint is executed at the end of the pipeline; middleware in between (CORS, auth, rate limiting, caching) can read the selected endpoint's metadata. Q What's the difference between attribute routing and conventional routing? Attribute routing puts templates on controllers and actions ([Route], [HttpGet(\"{id}\")]) and is required for API controllers. Conventional routing defines URL patterns centrally (\"{controller}/{action}/{id?}\") and maps them to controllers by naming convention; it's typical for MVC sites with views. Q What happens when a route constraint fails? The route doesn't match, so routing tries other endpoints and, if none match, returns 404. Constraints are for disambiguation, not input validation, which should return 400. Q What are route groups? MapGroup creates a prefix and lets you apply shared metadata (authorization, filters, tags, rate limiting, CORS) to all endpoints within it, which keeps minimal API definitions organised and DRY. Q How does ASP.NET Core resolve two routes that match the same URL? By route precedence: literals over parameters, constrained over unconstrained, catch-alls last, then HTTP method. If two candidates remain equally specific, it throws AmbiguousMatchException. Q How do you generate URLs to other endpoints? Name the endpoint (WithName or Name = …) and use LinkGenerator.GetUriByName/GetPathByName, or helpers like CreatedAtRoute and CreatedAtAction. Avoid hard-coded string URLs."},{"c":"routing","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Routing in ASP.NET Core Route handlers in minimal APIs Attribute routing for controllers"},{"c":"routing","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run in a console project ( dotnet new web , then dotnet add package Microsoft.AspNetCore.TestHost ). It checks the 404-vs-400 behaviour of constraints, precedence between literal/parameter/constrained routes, the ambiguous-match failure, and link generation. C# Run ▶ Copy using Microsoft . AspNetCore . Builder ; using Microsoft . AspNetCore . Routing ; using Microsoft . AspNetCore . TestHost ; using Microsoft . Extensions . DependencyInjection ; void Check ( bool condition, string claim) { if (!condition) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: {claim}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: {claim}\" ); } // 1. A failed route constraint means \"no route matches\" -> 404, not 400. var builder1 = WebApplication . CreateBuilder (); builder1. WebHost . UseTestServer (); var app1 = builder1. Build (); app1. MapGet ( \"/products/{id:int}\" , ( int id) => $\"product {id}\" ); await app1. StartAsync (); var response1 = await app1. GetTestClient (). GetAsync ( \"/products/abc\" ); // \"abc\" fails the :int constraint await app1. StopAsync (); Check (response1. StatusCode == System . Net . HttpStatusCode . NotFound , \"A route segment that fails its constraint (e.g. {id:int} given 'abc') returns 404, not 400\" ); // 2. Precedence: a literal segment beats a parameter segment for the same URL. var builder2 = WebApplication . CreateBuilder (); builder2. WebHost . UseTestServer (); var app2 = builder2. Build (); app2. MapGet ( \"/products/featured\" , () => \"featured-literal\" ); app2. MapGet ( \"/products/{id}\" , ( string id) => $\"param-{id}\" ); await app2. StartAsync (); var featured = await app2. GetTestClient (). GetStringAsync ( \"/products/featured\" ); await app2. StopAsync (); Check (featured == \"featured-literal\" , \"A literal route segment ('/products/featured') beats a parameter segment ('/products/{id}') for the same URL\" ); // 3. Precedence: a constrained parameter beats an unconstrained one. var builder3 = WebApplication . CreateBuilder (); builder3. WebHost . UseTestServer (); var app3 = builder3. Build (); app3. MapGet ( \"/items/{id:int}\" , ( int id) => \"constrained\" ); app3. MapGet ( \"/items/{slug}\" , ( string slug) => \"unconstrained\" ); await app3. StartAsync (); var numeric = await app3. GetTestClient (). GetStringAsync ( \"/items/42\" ); var text = await app3. GetTestClient (). GetStringAsync ( \"/items/abc\" ); await app3. StopAsync (); Check (numeric == \"constrained\" , \"A constrained parameter ({id:int}) wins over an unconstrained one ({slug}) when the value matches the constraint\" ); Check (text == \"unconstrained\" , \"When the value fails the constraint, the unconstrained route catches it instead\" ); // 4. Two equally-specific routes for the same request throw AmbiguousMatchException. var builder4 = WebApplication . CreateBuilder (); builder4. WebHost . UseTestServer (); var app4 = builder4. Build (); app4. MapGet ( \"/ambiguous/{a}\" , () => \"first\" ); app4. MapGet ( \"/ambiguous/{b}\" , () => \"second\" ); await app4. StartAsync (); var ambiguousResponse = await app4. GetTestClient (). GetAsync ( \"/ambiguous/x\" ); var ambiguousBody = await ambiguousResponse. Content . ReadAsStringAsync (); await app4. StopAsync (); Check (ambiguousResponse. StatusCode == System . Net . HttpStatusCode . InternalServerError && ambiguousBody. Contains ( \"AmbiguousMatchException\" ), \"Two equally-specific routes for the same request throw AmbiguousMatchException (surfaced here as a 500)\" ); // 5. LinkGenerator builds a URL from a route NAME and values, matching the actual registered template. var builder5 = WebApplication . CreateBuilder (); builder5. WebHost . UseTestServer (); var app5 = builder5. Build (); app5. MapGet ( \"/api/orders/{id:int}\" , ( int id) => \"order\" ). WithName ( \"GetOrder\" ); await app5. StartAsync (); var links = app5. Services . GetRequiredService < LinkGenerator >(); var path = links. GetPathByName ( \"GetOrder\" , new { id = 42 }); await app5. StopAsync (); Check (path == \"/api/orders/42\" , \"LinkGenerator.GetPathByName build"},{"c":"building-apis","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Minimal APIs and controllers are two syntaxes over identical machinery — same routing, DI and middleware. Pick based on team conventions and features needed, not raw capability. Parameter binding infers where a value comes from (route, query, body, DI) based on its type and name; attributes override that inference when you need to be explicit. Prefer TypedResults over Results : the handler's return type then documents every possible response, for free, accurate OpenAPI docs and easy unit tests. Validate shape at the edge (400 for bad input); keep business rules (\"can't cancel a shipped order\") in the domain layer, not in a validator. Filters run inside endpoint execution and see the bound, typed arguments — middleware never does, because it runs before model binding happens. Never bind a request body directly to an EF Core entity (\"over-posting\") and never return one either — always go through a DTO you control."},{"c":"building-apis","s":"two-syntaxes-the-same-machinery-underneath","h":"Two syntaxes, the same machinery underneath","t":"# Two syntaxes, the same machinery underneath Beginner Everything in the previous two chapters — the pipeline, the routing match — ends at exactly the same place: your code, deciding what to actually do with a request. ASP.NET Core gives you two different syntaxes for writing that code, and it's worth being clear up front that they are genuinely just syntax: both run on the same routing, the same DI container, the same authentication and the same middleware pipeline. Minimal APIs (since .NET 6) map routes directly to functions: C# Run ▶ Copy app. MapGet ( \"/products/{id:int}\" , async ( int id, ShopDb db, CancellationToken ct) => await db. Products . FindAsync ([id], ct) is { } p ? Results . Ok (p) : Results . NotFound ()); Controllers (MVC) are classes with action methods and attributes: C# Run ▶ Copy [ApiController] [Route(\"products\")] public class ProductsController ( ShopDb db) : ControllerBase { [HttpGet(\"{id:int}\")] public async Task < ActionResult < Product >> Get ( int id, CancellationToken ct) => await db. Products . FindAsync ([id], ct) is { } p ? p : NotFound (); } The difference between them isn't capability — it's how much ceremony you accept in exchange for which features: Minimal APIs Controllers Shape Lambdas or static methods; fluent configuration Classes deriving ControllerBase ; attributes Ceremony Low Higher (classes, attributes, base class) Cross-cutting per endpoint Endpoint filters, route group metadata Action/resource/result/exception filters Automatic validation Built in since .NET 10 ( AddValidation() ); else filters/FluentValidation Automatic with [ApiController] (data annotations) Model binding Inferred; [AsParameters] ; custom BindAsync / TryParse Model binders, value providers; very extensible Content negotiation (XML…) JSON only out of the box Formatters (JSON, XML, custom) Performance Slightly faster, lower memory Slightly heavier Native AOT Supported (with request delegate generator) Not supported Microsoft's guidance Recommended for new APIs Fully supported; huge existing codebases How to answer \"minimal APIs or controllers?\" \"Both are first-class. For new services I default to minimal APIs organised into feature-based route groups, which pairs well with vertical slices and supports AOT. In an existing controller-based codebase I'd stay consistent. I'd pick controllers if the team relies on MVC filters, conventions, content negotiation or views.\""},{"c":"building-apis","s":"minimal-apis-in-depth","h":"Minimal APIs in depth","t":"# Minimal APIs in depth Beginner # Handler shapes A minimal-API handler can be as small as a one-liner or a reference to a plain static method — the framework infers a response from whatever you return: C# Run ▶ Copy app. MapGet ( \"/\" , () => \"Hello\" ); // returns string → text/plain 200 app. MapGet ( \"/now\" , () => new { Utc = DateTime . UtcNow }); // returns object → JSON 200 app. MapGet ( \"/r\" , () => Results . Redirect ( \"/\" )); // returns IResult → full control app. MapPost ( \"/orders\" , OrderHandlers . Create ); // method group: testable static method app. MapMethods ( \"/ping\" , [ \"GET\" , \"HEAD\" ], () => \"pong\" ); # Organising endpoints so Program.cs stays readable The lambda style above is great for a demo and genuinely bad for a real app with eighty endpoints crammed into one file. The fix is the same one Routing already introduced: group endpoints by feature, behind an extension method, so Program.cs reads as a table of contents rather than a wall of lambdas: C# Run ▶ Copy public static class OrderEndpoints { public static IEndpointRouteBuilder MapOrderEndpoints ( this IEndpointRouteBuilder app) { var g = app. MapGroup ( \"/api/orders\" ). WithTags ( \"Orders\" ). RequireAuthorization (); g. MapGet ( \"/\" , List ). WithName ( \"ListOrders\" ); g. MapGet ( \"/{id:int}\" , GetById ). WithName ( \"GetOrder\" ); g. MapPost ( \"/\" , Create ). AddEndpointFilter < ValidationFilter < CreateOrderRequest >>(); g. MapPost ( \"/{id:int}/cancel\" , Cancel ); return app; } static async Task < Ok < PagedResult < OrderSummary >>> List ( [AsParameters] OrderQuery q, IOrderQueries queries, CancellationToken ct) => TypedResults . Ok ( await queries. ListAsync (q, ct)); static async Task < Results < Ok < OrderDto >, NotFound >> GetById ( int id, IOrderQueries queries, CancellationToken ct) => await queries. GetAsync (id, ct) is { } dto ? TypedResults . Ok (dto) : TypedResults . NotFound (); static async Task < Results < Created < OrderDto >, ValidationProblem >> Create ( CreateOrderRequest req, IOrderService svc, CancellationToken ct) { var dto = await svc. CreateAsync (req, ct); return TypedResults . Created ( $\"/api/orders/{dto.Id}\" , dto); } static async Task < Results < NoContent , NotFound , Conflict < ProblemDetails >>> Cancel ( int id, IOrderService svc, CancellationToken ct) => await svc. CancelAsync (id, ct) switch { CancelResult . Ok => TypedResults . NoContent (), CancelResult . NotFound => TypedResults . NotFound (), _ => TypedResults . Conflict ( new ProblemDetails { Title = \"Order can no longer be cancelled\" }) }; } app. MapOrderEndpoints (); Some teams reach for libraries such as Carter or FastEndpoints to push this organisation further, into module- or REPR-style (Request-Endpoint-Response) folders — worth knowing the names exist, even if the pattern above covers most needs on its own."},{"c":"building-apis","s":"returning-results-what-each-return-type-actually-produces","h":"Returning results: what each return type actually produces","t":"# Returning results: what each return type actually produces Beginner Return Status Notes string 200 text/plain Any object T 200 application/json Serialized with System.Text.Json IResult via Results.* Any Results.Ok(x) , NotFound() , Created(url, x) , NoContent() , BadRequest() , Problem() , ValidationProblem() , File() , Stream() , Redirect() , StatusCode(n) TypedResults.* Any Same, but strongly typed ( Ok<T> , NotFound …), so OpenAPI can infer response types and unit tests can assert on them Results<T1, T2, …> Union Declares every possible result type of a handler Controllers: IActionResult / ActionResult<T> Any Ok() , NotFound() , CreatedAtAction() , NoContent() , Problem() , ValidationProblem() The reason to prefer TypedResults over plain Results isn't style — it's that the handler's return type becomes a literal, checked-by-the-compiler contract. Look back at GetById above: its signature, Task<Results<Ok<OrderDto>, NotFound>> , states in the type system itself that this endpoint produces exactly one of two shapes, nothing else. OpenAPI documentation generation reads that type and produces accurate response docs with zero extra annotation, and unit tests get a concrete type to assert against instead of inspecting an opaque IResult : C# Run ▶ Copy // Unit testing a handler is trivial because it's just a static method returning typed results var result = await OrderEndpoints . GetById ( 42 , fakeQueries, CancellationToken . None ); var ok = Assert . IsType < Ok < OrderDto >>(result. Result ); Assert . Equal ( 42 , ok. Value !. Id );"},{"c":"building-apis","s":"parameter-binding-how-request-data-becomes-a-c-argument","h":"Parameter binding: how request data becomes a C# argument","t":"# Parameter binding: how request data becomes a C# argument Beginner Binding is the step that turns pieces of the raw HTTP request into the actual values your handler's parameters receive. The framework infers where to look for each parameter based on its type and name, and you can always override the inference with an explicit attribute: Source Minimal API (inferred / explicit) Controller attribute Route {id} Name matches a route parameter / [FromRoute] [FromRoute] Query string ?page=2 Simple types not in the route / [FromQuery] [FromQuery] Headers [FromHeader(Name = \"X-Tenant\")] [FromHeader] JSON body Complex types (one per request) / [FromBody] [FromBody] (inferred with [ApiController] ) Form fields / files [FromForm] , IFormFile , IFormFileCollection [FromForm] DI services Registered types / [FromServices] [FromServices] Keyed services [FromKeyedServices(\"key\")] same Special types HttpContext , HttpRequest , HttpResponse , ClaimsPrincipal , CancellationToken , Stream , PipeReader via HttpContext / parameters Group many [AsParameters] MyQuery q complex type with [FromQuery] C# Run ▶ Copy public record OrderQuery ( [FromQuery] int Page = 1 , [FromQuery] int PageSize = 20 , [FromQuery] OrderStatus ? Status = null , [FromHeader(Name = \"X-Tenant\")] string ? Tenant = null ); app. MapGet ( \"/orders\" , ( [AsParameters] OrderQuery q, IOrderQueries svc) => svc. ListAsync (q)); app. MapPost ( \"/upload\" , async ( IFormFile file , IBlobStore blobs, CancellationToken ct) => { await using var s = file . OpenReadStream (); return Results . Ok ( await blobs. UploadAsync ( file . FileName , s, ct)); }). DisableAntiforgery (); // form endpoints require antiforgery tokens by default Custom binding: give a type a static TryParse(string, out T) method (for route or query values) or a static BindAsync(HttpContext) method (for anything that needs the full context), and minimal APIs picks it up automatically, with no registration step. Implementing IParsable<T> works the same way. Over-posting: the bug that looks like a feature until it doesn't Here's the trap, concretely: bind the request body directly to your EF Core entity , and a client can set any writable property on it in the JSON they send — including IsAdmin , Price , or CustomerId , fields they were never supposed to touch. Nothing in the binding step knows \"this field is for internal use only\"; it binds whatever matches. The fix is to always bind to a dedicated request DTO that only has the fields a client is actually allowed to send, and map that DTO onto the domain explicitly, by hand, where you control exactly what gets copied."},{"c":"building-apis","s":"validation-catching-bad-input-before-it-reaches-business-log","h":"Validation: catching bad input before it reaches business logic","t":"# Validation: catching bad input before it reaches business logic Intermediate The goal here is simple to state: validate input at the edge , before anything downstream has to deal with it, and when it fails, respond with a 400 carrying ValidationProblemDetails — an RFC 9457 JSON body listing which fields were wrong and why. # Option 1: Data annotations C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed record CreateProductRequest ( [property: Required , StringLength ( 100 , MinimumLength = 2 )] string Name , [property: Range ( 0.01 , 1_000_000 )] decimal Price , [property: Required , RegularExpression ( @\"^[A-Z]{2}-\\d{4}$\" )] string Sku ); Controllers with [ApiController] validate automatically and return 400 before your action runs at all ( ModelState.IsValid is checked on your behalf). Minimal APIs (.NET 10) : builder.Services.AddValidation(); enables built-in data-annotation validation for parameters and returns ValidationProblem automatically. On earlier versions, you need a filter to get the same effect. # Option 2: FluentValidation, for rules annotations can't express Data annotations are fine for simple per-field rules. But they genuinely can't express \"this order needs at least one line\" or \"look up this SKU in the catalog and confirm it exists\" — rules that need to see the whole object, or need a dependency like a database lookup. FluentValidation is the common answer: C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class CreateOrderValidator : AbstractValidator < CreateOrderRequest > { public CreateOrderValidator ( IProductCatalog catalog) { RuleFor (x => x. CustomerId ). GreaterThan ( 0 ); RuleFor (x => x. Lines ). NotEmpty (). WithMessage ( \"An order needs at least one line\" ); RuleForEach (x => x. Lines ). ChildRules (line => { line. RuleFor (l => l. Qty ). InclusiveBetween ( 1 , 100 ); line. RuleFor (l => l. Sku ). MustAsync ((sku, ct) => catalog. ExistsAsync (sku, ct)). WithMessage ( \"Unknown SKU '{PropertyValue}'\" ); }); } } builder. Services . AddValidatorsFromAssemblyContaining < CreateOrderValidator >(); // Apply with the ValidationFilter<T> endpoint filter (see Filters below), or call validator.ValidateAsync in the handler. # Input validation vs business rules — a distinction worth holding onto Kind Example Where Response Input/shape Required, length, range, format Edge (validators) 400 ValidationProblem Business rule \"Can't cancel a shipped order\", \"insufficient stock\" Domain/application layer 409 Conflict / 422 / domain error Both matter, and the line between them is where they live , not just what status code they return. \"Can't cancel a shipped order\" isn't a shape problem — the request might be perfectly well-formed — it's a rule about the current state of the business. Trying to express that inside a validator means the validator now needs database access and knowledge of domain state, which is exactly the kind of coupling that makes validators hard to test and slow to run. Keep it in the domain or application layer instead, where it belongs."},{"c":"building-apis","s":"filters-seeing-the-handlers-actual-typed-arguments","h":"Filters: seeing the handler's actual typed arguments","t":"# Filters: seeing the handler's actual typed arguments Intermediate Here's the capability middleware fundamentally can't give you — and the reason filters exist as a separate mechanism. Filters run inside endpoint execution, wrapped directly around your handler, after model binding has already happened. So they can see the bound, typed arguments and the handler's result. Middleware, sitting outside all of this, only ever sees raw HTTP. # Endpoint filters (minimal APIs) C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class ValidationFilter < T >( IValidator < T > validator) : IEndpointFilter where T : class { public async ValueTask < object ?> InvokeAsync ( EndpointFilterInvocationContext ctx, EndpointFilterDelegate next) { var arg = ctx. Arguments . OfType < T >(). FirstOrDefault (); if (arg is null ) return TypedResults . BadRequest (); var result = await validator. ValidateAsync (arg, ctx. HttpContext . RequestAborted ); return result. IsValid ? await next(ctx) : TypedResults . ValidationProblem (result. ToDictionary ()); } } // inline filter g. MapPost ( \"/\" , Create ). AddEndpointFilter ( async (ctx, next) => { var sw = Stopwatch . StartNew (); var result = await next(ctx); ctx. HttpContext . Response . Headers [ \"X-Handler-Ms\" ] = sw. ElapsedMilliseconds . ToString (); return result; }); A filter attached to a group applies to every endpoint inside it, and — just like middleware — filters run in registration order on the way in, outer to inner. # MVC filter pipeline (controllers) Controllers have a richer, more finely staged version of the same idea, with five distinct filter types, each running at a specific point: Diagram Copy Request ─▶ [Authorization filters] ─▶ [Resource filters (before)] ─▶ model binding & validation ─▶ [Action filters (before)] ─▶ ACTION ─▶ [Action filters (after)] ─▶ [Exception filters] (only if the action/filters threw) ─▶ [Result filters (before)] ─▶ result executes (serialization) ─▶ [Result filters (after)] ─▶ [Resource filters (after)] ─▶ Response Filter Interface Typical use Authorization IAuthorizationFilter / IAsyncAuthorizationFilter Custom auth checks (prefer policies) Resource IResourceFilter Short-circuit before binding (caching, feature gates) Action IActionFilter / IAsyncActionFilter Validation, logging arguments, transactions Exception IExceptionFilter Translate exceptions from actions (prefer IExceptionHandler globally) Result IResultFilter Modify or wrap results, add headers C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class TransactionFilter ( ShopDb db) : IAsyncActionFilter { public async Task OnActionExecutionAsync ( ActionExecutingContext ctx, ActionExecutionDelegate next) { await using var tx = await db. Database . BeginTransactionAsync (); var executed = await next(); if (executed. Exception is null ) await tx. CommitAsync (); } } builder. Services . AddControllers (o => o. Filters . Add < TransactionFilter >()); // global [ ServiceFilter < TransactionFilter >] public IActionResult Post (...) { } // per action (resolved from DI) Filters can be attached at three scopes — global, controller, and action — and on the way in they run global → controller → action, exactly reversed on the way out, the same inward-then-reversed-outward shape the middleware pipeline diagram showed in the previous chapter. Order lets you adjust the sequence within a scope when you need to."},{"c":"building-apis","s":"json-with-system-text-json-the-defaults-and-how-to-bend-them","h":"JSON with System.Text.Json: the defaults and how to bend them","t":"# JSON with System.Text.Json: the defaults and how to bend them Intermediate ASP.NET Core serializes with System.Text.Json by default, chosen specifically for being fast, low-allocation, and UTF-8 based rather than working through an intermediate string encoding. Its web-specific defaults: camelCase property names, case-insensitive reading on the way in, and numbers accepted even when they arrive as JSON strings. C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . ConfigureHttpJsonOptions (o => // minimal APIs { o. SerializerOptions . Converters . Add ( new JsonStringEnumConverter ()); // enums as \"Paid\" not 2 o. SerializerOptions . DefaultIgnoreCondition = JsonIgnoreCondition . WhenWritingNull ; }); builder. Services . AddControllers (). AddJsonOptions (o => { /* same for controllers */ }); public sealed record OrderDto ( int Id , [property: JsonPropertyName ( \"total_amount\" )] decimal Total , [property: JsonIgnore ] string InternalNote , OrderStatus Status ); Need Use Rename a property [JsonPropertyName] or a naming policy ( JsonNamingPolicy.SnakeCaseLower ) Skip a property [JsonIgnore] Polymorphism (derived types) [JsonPolymorphic] + [JsonDerivedType(typeof(Card), \"card\")] Custom format A JsonConverter<T> Read unknown JSON JsonDocument / JsonNode (DOM) Max performance / AOT Source generation: [JsonSerializable(typeof(OrderDto))] partial class AppJsonContext : JsonSerializerContext Newtonsoft features AddNewtonsoftJson() (legacy compatibility; slower) Serializing EF Core entities directly is a trap, not a shortcut Return an EF Core entity with its navigation properties intact — Order.Customer.Orders… — and you'll either hit a reference-cycle error (the serializer walking Order → Customer → Orders → Order → … forever) or ship a response payload many times larger than anyone asked for. The fix isn't ReferenceHandler.IgnoreCycles — that papers over the symptom while still shipping too much data and still coupling your wire format to your database schema. Return DTOs , built with an explicit Select projection, every time."},{"c":"building-apis","s":"content-negotiation-formats-and-streaming","h":"Content negotiation, formats and streaming","t":"# Content negotiation, formats and streaming Advanced Controllers honour the Accept header through output formatters ( AddXmlSerializerFormatters() ); minimal APIs always return JSON unless you explicitly return a different IResult . Return files with Results.File(bytes, \"application/pdf\", \"invoice.pdf\") or Results.Stream(stream, …) ; use PhysicalFile for files on disk, which adds HTTP range support for free. Stream large datasets with IAsyncEnumerable<T> : the JSON array gets written to the response as items arrive, rather than buffering the entire result set in memory first. Server-Sent Events ( TypedResults.ServerSentEvents , .NET 10) for one-way streaming updates to a browser."},{"c":"building-apis","s":"putting-it-together-a-handler-that-stays-genuinely-thin","h":"Putting it together: a handler that stays genuinely thin","t":"# Putting it together: a handler that stays genuinely thin Intermediate Pull the threads of this chapter together into one realistic endpoint, and notice what it deliberately does not do: C# Run ▶ Copy // Endpoint (presentation): HTTP concerns only static async Task < Results < Created < OrderDto >, ValidationProblem , Conflict < ProblemDetails >>> PlaceOrder ( PlaceOrderRequest req, IValidator < PlaceOrderRequest > validator, IPlaceOrderHandler handler, ClaimsPrincipal user, CancellationToken ct) { var v = await validator. ValidateAsync (req, ct); if (!v. IsValid ) return TypedResults . ValidationProblem (v. ToDictionary ()); var result = await handler. HandleAsync ( new PlaceOrder (user. GetCustomerId (), req. Lines ), ct); // application layer return result. IsSuccess ? TypedResults . Created ( $\"/api/orders/{result.Value.Id}\" , result. Value ) : TypedResults . Conflict ( new ProblemDetails { Title = result. Error . Message , Type = result. Error . Code }); } There's no business logic here at all — no stock checks, no pricing rules, nothing about what makes an order valid to place. The handler's job is strictly bind → validate → delegate → map the result to HTTP , and everything about what actually happens to an order lives one layer down, in application and domain code that has never heard of HttpContext and can be tested without spinning up a web server at all. See App architectures and CQRS & Result for exactly how that layer is shaped. Q Minimal APIs vs controllers: what are the differences and when would you pick each? Both use the same routing, DI and middleware. Minimal APIs map routes to functions with fluent configuration, have less ceremony, are a bit faster and support Native AOT, and they're Microsoft's default recommendation for new APIs. Controllers are classes with attributes and have a richer filter pipeline, model binding and content negotiation. Pick based on team and codebase conventions and the features you need. Q What does the [ApiController] attribute do? It enables API conventions: automatic 400 ValidationProblemDetails responses when model state is invalid, binding-source inference ([FromBody] for complex types, [FromRoute]/[FromQuery] for simple ones), a requirement for attribute routing, and ProblemDetails bodies for error status codes. Q What's the difference between Results and TypedResults? Both create IResult responses. TypedResults returns concrete types (Ok<T>, NotFound, Created<T>), so a handler's return type (often Results<…>) documents every possible response, OpenAPI metadata is inferred automatically, and unit tests can assert on types without executing the result. Q How does parameter binding work in minimal APIs? By inference. Route parameters bind by name; simple types otherwise come from the query string; complex types from the JSON body (only one body per request); registered services from DI; special types (HttpContext, CancellationToken, ClaimsPrincipal) are injected. Attributes ([FromQuery], [FromHeader], [FromBody], [FromServices], [AsParameters]) make it explicit, and TryParse/BindAsync allow custom binding. Q How do you validate requests in ASP.NET Core? With data annotations on DTOs (automatic in controllers with [ApiController]; built in for minimal APIs since .NET 10 via AddValidation), or with FluentValidation applied through an endpoint/action filter or called in the handler. Return 400 ValidationProblemDetails. Keep business rules in the domain/application layer. Q What is over-posting and how do you prevent it? When clients send extra fields that bind onto properties they shouldn't control (e.g. IsAdmin, Price) because the request is bound directly to an entity. Prevent it by binding to dedicated request DTOs and mapping explicitly. Q Explain the MVC filter pipeline order. Authorization filters, then resource filters, then model binding, then action filters around the action, then exception filters (if something threw), then result filters around result execution, then"},{"c":"building-apis","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Minimal APIs overview Choose between controller-based APIs and minimal APIs Parameter binding in minimal APIs Filters in ASP.NET Core (MVC) System.Text.Json overview"},{"c":"building-apis","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run in a console project ( dotnet new web , then dotnet add package Microsoft.AspNetCore.TestHost ). It checks response content types, TypedResults status codes, parameter binding, an endpoint filter short-circuiting on the typed argument, and the over-posting problem itself. C# Run ▶ Copy using System . Net . Http . Json ; using Microsoft . AspNetCore . Builder ; using Microsoft . AspNetCore . Http ; using Microsoft . AspNetCore . Http . HttpResults ; using Microsoft . AspNetCore . TestHost ; void Check ( bool condition, string claim) { if (!condition) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: {claim}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: {claim}\" ); } // 1. Returning a plain string gives 200 text/plain; returning an object gives 200 application/json. var builder1 = WebApplication . CreateBuilder (); builder1. WebHost . UseTestServer (); var app1 = builder1. Build (); app1. MapGet ( \"/text\" , () => \"Hello\" ); app1. MapGet ( \"/json\" , () => new { Utc = \"now\" }); await app1. StartAsync (); var client1 = app1. GetTestClient (); var textResponse = await client1. GetAsync ( \"/text\" ); var jsonResponse = await client1. GetAsync ( \"/json\" ); await app1. StopAsync (); Check (textResponse. Content . Headers . ContentType !. MediaType == \"text/plain\" , \"Returning a plain string produces a text/plain response\" ); Check (jsonResponse. Content . Headers . ContentType !. MediaType == \"application/json\" , \"Returning any other object produces an application/json response\" ); // 2. TypedResults.Ok<T> / NotFound produce the HTTP status codes the chapter claims. var builder2 = WebApplication . CreateBuilder (); builder2. WebHost . UseTestServer (); var app2 = builder2. Build (); app2. MapGet ( \"/items/{id:int}\" , GetItem ); Results < Ok < int >, NotFound > GetItem ( int id) => id == 1 ? TypedResults . Ok (id) : TypedResults . NotFound (); await app2. StartAsync (); var client2 = app2. GetTestClient (); var found = await client2. GetAsync ( \"/items/1\" ); var notFound = await client2. GetAsync ( \"/items/2\" ); await app2. StopAsync (); Check (found. StatusCode == System . Net . HttpStatusCode . OK , \"TypedResults.Ok produces a 200\" ); Check (notFound. StatusCode == System . Net . HttpStatusCode . NotFound , \"TypedResults.NotFound produces a 404\" ); // 3. Parameter binding: route value binds by name, query string binds a simple type not in the route. var builder3 = WebApplication . CreateBuilder (); builder3. WebHost . UseTestServer (); var app3 = builder3. Build (); app3. MapGet ( \"/orders/{id:int}\" , ( int id, int page) => $\"{id}:{page}\" ); await app3. StartAsync (); var bound = await app3. GetTestClient (). GetStringAsync ( \"/orders/7?page=3\" ); await app3. StopAsync (); Check (bound == \"7:3\" , \"A route value binds by name and a query-string value binds a simple type not present in the route, in the same handler\" ); // 4. An endpoint filter sees the bound, typed argument and can short-circuit BEFORE the handler runs. var handlerRan = false ; var builder4 = WebApplication . CreateBuilder (); builder4. WebHost . UseTestServer (); var app4 = builder4. Build (); app4. MapPost ( \"/products\" , ( CreateProductRequest req) => { handlerRan = true ; return Results . Ok (); }) . AddEndpointFilter ( async (ctx, next) => { var arg = ctx. Arguments . OfType < CreateProductRequest >(). FirstOrDefault (); if (arg is { Price : <= 0 }) return Results . BadRequest ( \"Price must be positive\" ); // sees the TYPED argument return await next(ctx); }); await app4. StartAsync (); var client4 = app4. GetTestClient (); var rejected = await client4. PostAsJsonAsync ( \"/products\" , new CreateProductRequest ( \"Mouse\" , - 5m )); var rejectedHandlerRan = handlerRan; handlerRan = false ; var accepted = await client4. PostAsJsonAsync ( \"/products\" , new CreateProductRequest ( \"Mouse\" , 20m )); await app4. StopAsync (); Check (rejected. StatusCode == System . Net . HttpStatusCode . BadRequest && !rejectedHandlerRan, \"An endpoint filter can inspect the bound, t"},{"c":"errors-logging","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR A single failure has two audiences: the client gets a safe, consistent ProblemDetails response; your logs get the full, searchable detail (exception, context, trace ID). Never mix the two up. app.UseExceptionHandler() must be the outermost middleware, so it can catch exceptions from everything registered after it. IExceptionHandler implementations are tried in registration order until one claims the exception. ProblemDetails (RFC 9457) is a standard JSON shape for API errors — type / title / status / detail everywhere — so any client can handle errors from any endpoint the same way. ILogger<T> is one API with pluggable destinations (console, Application Insights, Serilog). Log with message templates ( \"{OrderId}\" ), not string interpolation, so fields stay queryable. Scopes attach extra fields (like OrderId ) to every log line written inside a block, even across an await , because they ride on AsyncLocal . Never log secrets, tokens, or full PII. Log IDs, not whole objects, and reach for [LoggerMessage] in hot paths where the logging call itself costs enough to matter."},{"c":"errors-logging","s":"two-very-different-audiences-for-the-same-failure","h":"Two very different audiences for the same failure","t":"# Two very different audiences for the same failure Beginner Something goes wrong in your app — a payment fails, a database call times out, a bug throws a NullReferenceException . That single event has to serve two completely different audiences , each with a different need: The client needs a consistent, safe, machine-readable error: the right status code, a predictable body shape, and critically, no stack traces or internal implementation details — those are a gift to an attacker and useless to a legitimate caller. The developers need the opposite: a detailed, searchable record of exactly what happened — the exception, the surrounding context (which user, which order ID, which trace), and the request path — sitting in logs they can actually query later. ASP.NET Core gives you one tool for each: ProblemDetails answers the client, ILogger answers the developers. They're deliberately separate, and conflating them — accidentally putting a stack trace in the response body, say — is exactly the mistake this chapter is structured to help you avoid."},{"c":"errors-logging","s":"exception-handling-in-the-pipeline-where-it-has-to-sit-and-w","h":"Exception handling in the pipeline: where it has to sit, and why","t":"# Exception handling in the pipeline: where it has to sit, and why Beginner An unhandled exception bubbles upward through the middleware chain exactly the way the \"after next() \" code did in Middleware — except instead of returning normally, it propagates as a thrown exception. For the exception handler to actually catch everything, it has to be the outermost wrapper, registered first: C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddProblemDetails (); // standard error bodies builder. Services . AddExceptionHandler < DomainExceptionHandler >(); // your handlers, tried in order builder. Services . AddExceptionHandler < GlobalExceptionHandler >(); var app = builder. Build (); app. UseExceptionHandler (); // FIRST: catches exceptions from everything after it app. UseStatusCodePages (); // gives empty 4xx/5xx responses (e.g. 404 from routing) a ProblemDetails body Environment Default behaviour Development Developer Exception Page : stack trace, query, headers, routing info (added automatically) Production UseExceptionHandler : logs the exception, returns a generic 500 ProblemDetails That split is deliberate, not accidental: you want the raw stack trace while you're developing, and you never want it once real users are hitting the app."},{"c":"errors-logging","s":"iexceptionhandler-mapping-exception-types-to-http-responses","h":"IExceptionHandler: mapping exception types to HTTP responses","t":"# IExceptionHandler: mapping exception types to HTTP responses Intermediate Since .NET 8, this is the standard hook for turning exceptions into responses. Handlers are tried in registration order , and each one gets to say \"not mine\" by returning false , letting the next handler have a turn: C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class DomainExceptionHandler ( IProblemDetailsService problems, ILogger < DomainExceptionHandler > log) : IExceptionHandler { public async ValueTask < bool > TryHandleAsync ( HttpContext ctx, Exception ex, CancellationToken ct) { var (status, title) = ex switch { NotFoundException => ( StatusCodes . Status404NotFound , \"Resource not found\" ), DomainException d => ( StatusCodes . Status409Conflict , d. Message ), DbUpdateConcurrencyException => ( StatusCodes . Status409Conflict , \"The resource was modified by someone else\" ), UnauthorizedAccessException => ( StatusCodes . Status403Forbidden , \"Forbidden\" ), _ => ( 0 , null ) }; if (status == 0 ) return false ; // not mine: let the next handler try log. LogWarning (ex, \"Handled {ExceptionType}: {Title}\" , ex. GetType (). Name , title); ctx. Response . StatusCode = status; return await problems. TryWriteAsync ( new ProblemDetailsContext { HttpContext = ctx, Exception = ex, ProblemDetails = { Status = status, Title = title, Type = $\"https://errors.shop.com/{status}\" } }); } } public sealed class GlobalExceptionHandler ( IProblemDetailsService problems, ILogger < GlobalExceptionHandler > log) : IExceptionHandler { public async ValueTask < bool > TryHandleAsync ( HttpContext ctx, Exception ex, CancellationToken ct) { if (ex is OperationCanceledException && ctx. RequestAborted . IsCancellationRequested ) { ctx. Response . StatusCode = 499 ; // client closed request; not a server error return true ; } log. LogError (ex, \"Unhandled exception for {Method} {Path}\" , ctx. Request . Method , ctx. Request . Path ); ctx. Response . StatusCode = StatusCodes . Status500InternalServerError ; return await problems. TryWriteAsync ( new () { HttpContext = ctx, Exception = ex, ProblemDetails = { Title = \"An unexpected error occurred\" , Status = 500 } }); } } Registration order matters here: DomainExceptionHandler (specific) goes first, so it gets first refusal on exception types it recognizes. GlobalExceptionHandler (catch-all) goes last, as the backstop for whatever nobody else claimed. ( Before .NET 8 , you'd see the same idea written by hand as custom middleware — try { await next(ctx); } catch (Exception ex) { … } — or UseExceptionHandler(errorApp => …) with IExceptionHandlerFeature . Same shape, considerably more boilerplate.) Exceptions vs results for expected failures Mapping exceptions centrally like this is convenient, but it's worth being honest about a real cost: throwing for expected outcomes — not found, a failed validation, a business rule rejecting the request — is genuinely expensive (exceptions capture stack traces) and it hides control flow behind try / catch instead of making the possible outcomes visible in a method's signature. Many teams instead return a Result from the application layer and map it to HTTP right in the endpoint, reserving IExceptionHandler for the exceptions that are genuinely unexpected. See CQRS & Result ."},{"c":"errors-logging","s":"problemdetails-one-shape-for-every-error-across-every-api-yo","h":"ProblemDetails: one shape for every error, across every API you'll ever call","t":"# ProblemDetails: one shape for every error, across every API you'll ever call Beginner RFC 9457 (which replaced the earlier RFC 7807) defines a standard JSON shape for HTTP API errors, served as application/problem+json : JSON Copy { \"type\" : \"https://errors.shop.com/409\" , \"title\" : \"Shipped orders can't be cancelled\" , \"status\" : 409 , \"detail\" : \"Order 1042 shipped on 2026-10-20.\" , \"instance\" : \"/api/orders/1042/cancel\" , \"traceId\" : \"00-4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736-00f067aa0ba902b7-01\" , \"requestId\" : \"0HN7…\" } Field Meaning type A URI identifying the kind of problem (documentation link); defaults to about:blank title Short, human-readable summary of the kind status HTTP status code detail Explanation specific to this occurrence instance URI of this occurrence (often the request path) extensions Anything else: traceId , errors (validation), code ValidationProblemDetails is the same shape with one addition — an errors dictionary, field name to list of messages: JSON Copy { \"title\" : \"One or more validation errors occurred.\" , \"status\" : 400 , \"errors\" : { \"Lines\" : [ \"An order needs at least one line\" ], \"CustomerId\" : [ \"Must be greater than 0\" ] } } You can customise every ProblemDetails response centrally, in one place, instead of repeating logic across every handler: C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddProblemDetails (o => o. CustomizeProblemDetails = ctx => { ctx. ProblemDetails . Instance = $\"{ctx.HttpContext.Request.Method} {ctx.HttpContext.Request.Path}\" ; ctx. ProblemDetails . Extensions [ \"traceId\" ] = Activity . Current ?. Id ?? ctx. HttpContext . TraceIdentifier ; }); Why the trace ID matters more than it looks Think about what happens without it: a customer reports \"I got an error,\" and you have... a vague timestamp, maybe, and no way to find the exact request in millions of log lines. With a traceId in the response body, that single value lets you jump straight to the exact logs and distributed trace for that one request, in seconds rather than a frustrating search. It's the bridge connecting \"what the client saw\" to \"what actually happened on the server\" — and it only works if you actually put it in the response, which is precisely what the customization above does."},{"c":"errors-logging","s":"the-logging-abstraction-one-api-many-destinations","h":"The logging abstraction: one API, many destinations","t":"# The logging abstraction: one API, many destinations Beginner Microsoft.Extensions.Logging gives your code a single API, ILogger<T> , and lets the host decide where those log entries actually end up — console, a file, Application Insights, several of these at once — through a set of configurable providers . Your business code never needs to know or care which. C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class PaymentService ( ILogger < PaymentService > logger, IPaymentGateway gateway) { public async Task < PaymentResult > ChargeAsync ( Order order, CancellationToken ct) { logger. LogInformation ( \"Charging order {OrderId} for {Amount} {Currency}\" , order. Id , order. Total . Amount , order. Total . Currency ); try { var result = await gateway. ChargeAsync (order. Total , ct); if (!result. Succeeded ) logger. LogWarning ( \"Payment declined for order {OrderId}: {DeclineCode}\" , order. Id , result. DeclineCode ); return result; } catch ( HttpRequestException ex) { logger. LogError (ex, \"Payment gateway call failed for order {OrderId}\" , order. Id ); throw ; } } } The category attached to every log line from this class is the full type name of T — Shop.Payments.PaymentService — and that's exactly what lets you dial logging verbosity up or down per namespace, as the next section shows. # Log levels: a severity ladder, not just labels Level Use for Production default? Trace Very detailed, may include sensitive data Off Debug Developer diagnostics Off Information Normal flow milestones: \"Order placed\", \"Job finished\" On Warning Unexpected but handled: retries, declined payments, slow calls On Error A failure of the current operation: exceptions, failed requests On Critical The app or a major subsystem is failing: data loss, startup failure On JSON Copy \"Logging\" : { \"LogLevel\" : { \"Default\" : \"Information\" , \"Microsoft.AspNetCore\" : \"Warning\" , // framework noise down \"Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Database.Command\" : \"Warning\" , // set to Information to see SQL \"Shop.Payments\" : \"Debug\" // more detail for one area } } This is the exact same layered configuration mechanism from Configuration , applied to log-level settings specifically — which is why you can crank Shop.Payments up to Debug in one environment via an environment variable, without touching the base appsettings.json at all."},{"c":"errors-logging","s":"structured-logging-keeping-the-data-not-just-the-sentence","h":"Structured logging: keeping the data, not just the sentence","t":"# Structured logging: keeping the data, not just the sentence Beginner Here's a distinction easy to miss on a first read: the string \"Charging order {OrderId} for {Amount}\" is a message template , not an interpolated string, and that's the whole point of writing it this way. The logger keeps OrderId and Amount as named properties , separately from the rendered text, and a structured sink — Application Insights, Seq, Elasticsearch, Loki, Datadog — stores each one as a real, queryable field rather than burying it inside an opaque sentence: Diagram Copy // Query in Application Insights / Log Analytics (KQL) traces | where customDimensions.OrderId == \"1042\" // Seq OrderId = 1042 and @Level = 'Error' Now compare the three calls below, because the difference between the first and the other two is exactly what makes structured logging valuable in the first place: C# Run ▶ Copy logger. LogInformation ( \"Order {OrderId} placed\" , order. Id ); // ✔ structured: OrderId is a field logger. LogInformation ( $\"Order {order.Id} placed\" ); // ✘ one opaque string; also allocates even if Information is disabled logger. LogInformation ( \"Order placed: {@Order}\" , order); // @ = destructure the object (Serilog); careful with size and PII The second line looks equivalent and produces visually identical console output, but it has thrown away the structure — there's no OrderId field for a query tool to filter on, just a string that happens to contain a number. It also pays the cost of formatting that string even when Information logging is disabled entirely , which the template form avoids. Two naming disciplines keep this consistent across a codebase: PascalCase placeholder names, and the same name everywhere — always OrderId , never orderId in one place and Id in another — because a query tool treats those as entirely different fields. And never reach for string interpolation in a log call at all; the analyzer rule CA2254 exists specifically to catch this."},{"c":"errors-logging","s":"log-scopes-attaching-context-to-everything-inside-a-block","h":"Log scopes: attaching context to everything inside a block","t":"# Log scopes: attaching context to everything inside a block Intermediate A scope attaches a set of properties to every single log line written inside it , for as long as the block is open — which is exactly the mechanism correlation IDs rely on: C# Run ▶ Copy using (logger. BeginScope ( new Dictionary < string , object > { [ \"OrderId\" ] = order. Id , [ \"CustomerId\" ] = order. CustomerId })) { logger. LogInformation ( \"Reserving stock\" ); // carries OrderId and CustomerId await stock. ReserveAsync (order, ct); // logs inside here carry them too (scopes flow via AsyncLocal) logger. LogInformation ( \"Stock reserved\" ); } The scope survives across that await — even onto a different thread — because it's carried by AsyncLocal , the same ambient-state mechanism the async state machine relies on in Async and await . Logging didn't have to invent anything new for this. ASP.NET Core uses the same trick automatically: it wraps every request in a scope carrying RequestId , RequestPath , TraceId and SpanId , so any log line anywhere in that request's call graph can be traced straight back to its request and its place in a distributed trace. Turn this on in the console provider with \"IncludeScopes\": true ."},{"c":"errors-logging","s":"high-performance-logging-when-the-logging-call-itself-become","h":"High-performance logging: when the logging call itself becomes the bottleneck","t":"# High-performance logging: when the logging call itself becomes the bottleneck Advanced Every ordinary logger.LogInformation(template, args) call does real work before it even checks whether anything will be written: it boxes value-type arguments into an object[] , and looks up the (cached) parsed template. That overhead adds up in a hot path — something called thousands of times a second — and the source-generated [LoggerMessage] attribute exists to eliminate it: C# Run ▶ Copy public static partial class OrderLog { [LoggerMessage(EventId = 1001, Level = LogLevel.Information, Message = \"Order {OrderId} placed by customer {CustomerId}\")] public static partial void OrderPlaced ( this ILogger logger, int orderId, int customerId); [LoggerMessage(EventId = 1002, Level = LogLevel.Warning, Message = \"Stock low for {Sku}: {Remaining} left\")] public static partial void StockLow ( this ILogger logger, string sku, int remaining); } logger. OrderPlaced (order. Id , order. CustomerId ); // checks IsEnabled first, no boxing, no params array The generated method checks IsEnabled as the very first thing, before touching the arguments at all, so a disabled log level costs almost nothing. For anything not covered by a generated method, the manual equivalent is if (logger.IsEnabled(LogLevel.Debug)) { ... } around any argument that's itself expensive to compute. Analyzer CA1848 flags hot-path calls where LoggerMessage would help."},{"c":"errors-logging","s":"providers-and-serilog-where-the-log-lines-actually-go","h":"Providers and Serilog: where the log lines actually go","t":"# Providers and Serilog: where the log lines actually go Intermediate Provider / library Notes Console, Debug, EventSource, EventLog Built in OpenTelemetry ( AddOpenTelemetry().WithLogging() ) Export logs alongside traces and metrics via OTLP; vendor-neutral Application Insights / Azure Monitor Azure's APM; via the OpenTelemetry distro ( UseAzureMonitor() ) Serilog The most popular third-party library: rich sinks (Seq, Elasticsearch, files, Splunk), enrichers, structured destructuring NLog Long-standing alternative C# Run ▶ Copy // Serilog setup builder. Host . UseSerilog ((ctx, services, cfg) => cfg . ReadFrom . Configuration (ctx. Configuration ) . Enrich . FromLogContext () . Enrich . WithProperty ( \"Service\" , \"shop-api\" ) . WriteTo . Console ( new RenderedCompactJsonFormatter ()) // JSON to stdout: containers ship it . WriteTo . Seq ( \"http://seq:5341\" )); app. UseSerilogRequestLogging (); // one concise log line per request: method, path, status, elapsed In containerised deployments specifically, the standard pattern is to log JSON to stdout and let whatever's running the container — Container Apps, Kubernetes, a Datadog agent — collect and ship it onward, or export directly via OpenTelemetry. This mirrors the \"no file on disk\" philosophy from Configuration : the app doesn't manage its own log files any more than it manages its own secrets — the platform handles both."},{"c":"errors-logging","s":"http-request-logging","h":"HTTP request logging","t":"# HTTP request logging Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddHttpLogging (o => { o. LoggingFields = HttpLoggingFields . RequestMethod | HttpLoggingFields . RequestPath | HttpLoggingFields . ResponseStatusCode | HttpLoggingFields . Duration ; o. CombineLogs = true ; }); app. UseHttpLogging (); Be deliberate about which fields you enable here: bodies and headers routinely contain tokens, cookies and personal data, and logging them by default is how sensitive data quietly ends up sitting in a log aggregator for months."},{"c":"errors-logging","s":"what-to-log-and-what-never-to-log","h":"What to log and what never to log","t":"# What to log and what never to log Intermediate ✔ Log ✘ Never log Business events with IDs (order placed, payment declined) Passwords, tokens, API keys, connection strings Exceptions with the exception object Full credit card numbers, CVV, bank details Calls to external systems: target, duration, outcome Personal data beyond what's necessary (GDPR): full addresses, health data Retries, fallbacks, circuit breaker state changes Entire request/response bodies by default Startup configuration summary (non-secret) Session cookies, Authorization headers A few habits worth adopting, each guarding against a specific recurring mistake: Log a failure once , at the boundary that actually handles it — not again at every layer as the exception rethrows upward and each catch block logs it a second time. Don't log a routine OperationCanceledException as an Error . A client closing the connection isn't a server failure. Log IDs, not whole objects, so a log line stays small and never accidentally serializes a field you didn't intend to expose. For anything approaching real PII, use the Microsoft.Extensions.Compliance.Redaction package — built for data classification and redaction — rather than trying to remember every sensitive field by hand. Q How do you implement global exception handling in ASP.NET Core? Register AddProblemDetails and one or more IExceptionHandler implementations (AddExceptionHandler<T>), and put app.UseExceptionHandler() first in the pipeline. Handlers map known exception types to status codes and ProblemDetails, log appropriately, and return true; unknown ones fall through to a generic 500. The Developer Exception Page is used only in Development. Q What is ProblemDetails? The RFC 9457 (formerly 7807) standard JSON format for HTTP API errors: type, title, status, detail, instance, plus extensions like traceId or validation errors. ASP.NET Core produces it via AddProblemDetails, Results.Problem/ValidationProblem and [ApiController] conventions. Q Why shouldn't you return stack traces to clients? They leak implementation details (framework versions, file paths, SQL, internal names) that help attackers, and they're useless to clients. Return a generic ProblemDetails with a trace ID and keep the details in server logs. Q What is structured logging and why is it better? Logging with message templates whose placeholders are captured as named properties instead of being formatted into a string. Log stores can then index and query by those fields (all errors for OrderId 1042, p95 duration by endpoint), which plain text can't support well. Q Why not use string interpolation in log calls? Interpolation produces a single pre-formatted string, so the structured properties are lost. It also always allocates and formats, even when the log level is disabled. Use message templates with placeholders (CA2254). Q What are log scopes? Ambient properties attached to all log entries written within a using block (BeginScope), flowing across awaits via AsyncLocal. ASP.NET Core adds RequestId, TraceId and SpanId automatically; you can add OrderId, TenantId and so on for correlation. Q What is LoggerMessage and when would you use it? A source generator ([LoggerMessage] on partial methods) that produces strongly typed, high-performance logging methods that check the level first, avoid boxing and params arrays, and pre-parse templates. Use it in hot paths and libraries. Q How do you choose log levels? Information for normal business milestones, Warning for unexpected but handled situations (retries, declines), Error for failures of an operation (exceptions), Critical for system-wide failures, and Debug/Trace for diagnostics that are off in production. Turn framework categories down to Warning to reduce noise. Q How would you correlate logs across microservices? Use W3C trace context propagation (traceparent header, automatic with HttpClient and OpenTelemetry) so every service logs with the same TraceId, include the trace ID in ProblemDetails respon"},{"c":"errors-logging","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Handle errors in ASP.NET Core Handle errors in ASP.NET Core APIs (ProblemDetails) Logging in .NET Compile-time logging source generation RFC 9457: Problem Details for HTTP APIs"},{"c":"errors-logging","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter The checks below cover exception-to-status mapping, structured logging vs interpolation, scopes surviving an await , and IsEnabled gating — all pure C#, no ASP.NET host needed. Save as a console app ( dotnet new console , dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.Logging ), then dotnet run . C# Run ▶ Copy using Microsoft . Extensions . Logging ; // Claim: exception-type-to-status mapping (the shape of IExceptionHandler.TryHandleAsync in this chapter). { ( int status, string ? title) Map ( Exception ex) => ex switch { NotFoundException => ( 404 , \"Resource not found\" ), DomainException d => ( 409 , d. Message ), UnauthorizedAccessException => ( 403 , \"Forbidden\" ), _ => ( 0 , null ) }; if ( Map ( new NotFoundException ()).status != 404 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: NotFoundException should map to 404\" ); if ( Map ( new DomainException ( \"Shipped orders can't be cancelled\" )).status != 409 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: DomainException should map to 409\" ); if ( Map ( new UnauthorizedAccessException ()).status != 403 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: UnauthorizedAccessException should map to 403\" ); if ( Map ( new InvalidOperationException ()).status != 0 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: unknown exception should fall through (status 0 = 'not mine')\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: exception types map to the status codes this chapter claims, and unknown types fall through\" ); } var provider = new CapturingLoggerProvider (); using var factory = LoggerFactory . Create (b => b. AddProvider (provider). SetMinimumLevel ( LogLevel . Information )); var logger = factory. CreateLogger ( \"Shop.Payments\" ); // Claim: a message template keeps placeholders as named, queryable properties; string interpolation does not. { provider. Entries . Clear (); logger. LogInformation ( \"Order {OrderId} placed\" , 1042 ); var templateEntry = provider. Entries [^ 1 ]; if (!templateEntry. State . Any (kv => kv. Key == \"OrderId\" && Equals (kv. Value , 1042 ))) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: message template should produce a structured OrderId property\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: a message template keeps OrderId as a structured, named property\" ); provider. Entries . Clear (); logger. LogInformation ( $\"Order {1042} placed\" ); var interpolatedEntry = provider. Entries [^ 1 ]; if (interpolatedEntry. State . Any (kv => kv. Key == \"OrderId\" )) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: interpolated string should NOT have an OrderId property\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: a pre-interpolated string has no OrderId property — just an opaque sentence\" ); } // Claim: BeginScope attaches properties to every log line inside the block, and they survive an await. { provider. Entries . Clear (); using (logger. BeginScope ( new Dictionary < string , object > { [ \"OrderId\" ] = 1042 , [ \"CustomerId\" ] = 7 })) { logger. LogInformation ( \"Reserving stock\" ); await Task . Yield (); // force a real continuation, possibly on a different thread-pool thread logger. LogInformation ( \"Stock reserved\" ); } logger. LogInformation ( \"Outside the scope\" ); var insideScope = provider. Entries [^ 2 ]; // \"Stock reserved\", after the await var scopeDict = insideScope. Scopes . OfType < Dictionary < string , object >>(). FirstOrDefault (); if (scopeDict is null || ! Equals (scopeDict[ \"OrderId\" ], 1042 )) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: the scope's OrderId should still be attached after an await\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: a scope's properties survive an await, even onto a different thread-pool thread\" ); var outsideScope = provider. Entries [^ 1 ]; // \"Outside the scope\" if (outsideScope. Scopes . OfType < Dictionary < string , object >>(). Any ()) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: the scope should not leak past its using block\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: a scope's properties do not leak past its using block\" ); } // Claim: logger.IsEnabled(level) lets you skip expensive work for a disabled level, before any formatting happens. { var expensiveWasCalled = false ; string Expensive () { expensive"},{"c":"auth","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Authentication = who you are (produces a ClaimsPrincipal ); authorization = what you're allowed to do. Mixing these up is the root of most auth bugs. A user is a bag of claims (key/value facts), not a username string. A role is just a claim named role — nothing more special than any other claim. Cookies suit browser apps (auto-sent, easy to revoke, needs CSRF defense); JWTs suit APIs/SPAs/mobile (stateless, hard to revoke, so keep them short-lived). For SPAs today, the BFF pattern gets the best of both. A JWT is validated with the issuer's public key (no database call per request) by checking the signature, issuer, audience, and expiry. Policies name a reusable authorization rule once, instead of scattering role checks everywhere. Resource-based authorization handles rules that depend on which specific row (\"your own orders only\"). ASP.NET Core Identity is a user-storage library, not an OAuth server — for real single sign-on, use an identity provider like Entra ID."},{"c":"auth","s":"two-questions-that-get-conflated-constantly-and-shouldnt-be","h":"Two questions that get conflated constantly, and shouldn't be","t":"# Two questions that get conflated constantly, and shouldn't be Beginner \"The user isn't authorized\" is one of those phrases that papers over two genuinely different failures. Before anything else in this chapter, pin these apart, because every bug and every design decision that follows traces back to keeping them separate: Authentication (AuthN) Authorization (AuthZ) Question Who are you? Are you allowed to do this? Input Cookie, bearer token, API key, client certificate The authenticated user + the resource + the action Output A ClaimsPrincipal in HttpContext.User Allow, or deny Failure 401 Unauthorized (really \"unauthenticated\"), via a challenge 403 Forbidden , via forbid Middleware UseAuthentication() UseAuthorization() Diagram Copy request ─▶ UseRouting ─▶ UseAuthentication ─▶ UseAuthorization ─▶ endpoint reads cookie/JWT reads endpoint's [Authorize]/policy sets HttpContext.User evaluates against User → 401/403 or continue The airport Showing your passport at check-in is authentication: it proves who you are. Your boarding pass for gate 12 is authorization: it decides what you may do. A valid passport doesn't let you board any plane you like — and the two checkpoints are run by entirely different people, for entirely different reasons."},{"c":"auth","s":"claims-identities-and-principals-the-vocabulary-everything-e","h":"Claims, identities and principals: the vocabulary everything else builds on","t":"# Claims, identities and principals: the vocabulary everything else builds on Beginner ASP.NET Core doesn't represent a logged-in user as a username string or a database row reference — it represents them as a bundle of claims : individual key/value statements about the user, each one issued by someone the app trusts. Diagram Copy ClaimsPrincipal (HttpContext.User) ← the user └── ClaimsIdentity (AuthenticationType = \"Bearer\") ← one identity per scheme (usually one) ├── Claim sub = \"7f3c…\" (subject: unique user id) ├── Claim email = \"ana@shop.com\" ├── Claim name = \"Ana\" ├── Claim role = \"admin\" ├── Claim scope = \"orders.read orders.write\" └── Claim tenant_id = \"acme\" C# Run ▶ Copy app. MapGet ( \"/me\" , ( ClaimsPrincipal user) => new { Id = user. FindFirstValue ( ClaimTypes . NameIdentifier ) ?? user. FindFirstValue ( \"sub\" ), Email = user. FindFirstValue ( ClaimTypes . Email ), IsAdmin = user. IsInRole ( \"admin\" ), Authenticated = user. Identity ?. IsAuthenticated }). RequireAuthorization (); Here's why this indirection is worth the extra concept: a role is just a claim whose type happens to be role — there's nothing structurally special about it. Because identity is a flat bag of claims rather than a fixed schema, ASP.NET Core doesn't actually care whether those claims came from a cookie you issued, a JWT from Entra ID, or an API key you validated yourself. Whatever produced them, by the time your code runs, they all look like the same ClaimsPrincipal — which is precisely what lets the rest of this chapter work identically regardless of which identity provider sits behind it."},{"c":"auth","s":"authentication-schemes-and-handlers-naming-the-thing-that-do","h":"Authentication schemes and handlers: naming the thing that does the checking","t":"# Authentication schemes and handlers: naming the thing that does the checking Intermediate A scheme is a named authentication handler plus its configuration. You register one or more, and declare which one is the default: C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddAuthentication ( JwtBearerDefaults . AuthenticationScheme ) // default scheme \"Bearer\" . AddJwtBearer (...) // APIs . AddCookie (...) // browser apps . AddOpenIdConnect (...) // sign-in via an external IdP . AddScheme < ApiKeyOptions , ApiKeyHandler >( \"ApiKey\" , null ); // custom Every handler, regardless of scheme, implements up to five distinct actions — and seeing them side by side explains why \"login\" means something so different for a cookie app versus an API: Action Meaning Example Authenticate Build a principal from the request Validate the JWT in the Authorization header Challenge Respond when an unauthenticated user hits a protected resource JWT: 401 + WWW-Authenticate: Bearer ; Cookie: redirect to login; OIDC: redirect to the IdP Forbid Respond when an authenticated user isn't allowed 403 ; Cookie: redirect to access-denied SignIn Persist the identity Cookie: issue the auth cookie SignOut Remove it Cookie: delete it"},{"c":"auth","s":"cookies-vs-bearer-tokens-the-tradeoff-behind-how-do-i-stay-l","h":"Cookies vs bearer tokens: the tradeoff behind \"how do I stay logged in?\"","t":"# Cookies vs bearer tokens: the tradeoff behind \"how do I stay logged in?\" Beginner Cookie authentication JWT bearer tokens Typical client Server-rendered web apps, BFF for SPAs APIs called by SPAs, mobile apps, other services How it's sent Browser sends the cookie automatically Client adds Authorization: Bearer <token> explicitly State Encrypted auth ticket in the cookie (or session store) Self-contained signed token Issued by Your app (after login) An identity provider / token service CSRF risk Yes (auto-sent): needs SameSite plus antiforgery No (not auto-sent by browsers) XSS risk HttpOnly cookie can't be read by JS Token in JS memory or localStorage can be stolen by XSS Revocation Easy: invalidate server-side or change the security stamp Hard: valid until expiry; keep lifetimes short Notice the two risk rows trade places: cookies are vulnerable to CSRF precisely because the browser sends them automatically — which is the same property that makes them convenient — while JWTs dodge CSRF but expose themselves to a different attacker if your app has an XSS hole anywhere, since JavaScript has to be able to read the token to attach it. Current guidance for browser SPAs sidesteps the dilemma rather than picking a side: the BFF (Backend-for-Frontend) pattern has a small server-side app handle the OIDC flow and keep tokens entirely server-side, so the browser only ever holds a secure HttpOnly SameSite cookie — getting the cookie's XSS resistance without reintroducing the JWT's revocation problem. See OAuth & OIDC ."},{"c":"auth","s":"jwt-bearer-authentication-traced-claim-by-claim","h":"JWT bearer authentication, traced claim by claim","t":"# JWT bearer authentication, traced claim by claim Intermediate A JWT (JSON Web Token) is just three base64url-encoded parts joined with dots: header.payload.signature . Diagram Copy eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIsImtpZCI6IjEyMyJ9 . eyJzdWIiOiI3ZjNjIiwiYXVkIjoiYXBpOi8vc2hvcCIsImV4cCI6MTc2… . MEUCIQD… header: {\"alg\":\"RS256\",\"kid\":\"123\"} payload (claims): signature { \"iss\": \"https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant>/v2.0\", \"sub\": \"7f3c…\", \"aud\": \"api://shop-api\", \"exp\": 1767225600, \"nbf\": 1767222000, \"iat\": 1767222000, \"scp\": \"orders.read\", \"roles\": [\"admin\"] } Claim Meaning iss Issuer: who created the token sub Subject: the user (or client) ID aud Audience: which API the token is for exp / nbf / iat Expires / not before / issued at (Unix seconds) scp / scope Delegated permissions (OAuth scopes) roles App roles azp / client_id Which client app requested it Here's the question worth sitting with: how does an API validate this token's authenticity without calling back to whoever issued it, for every single request? The answer is asymmetric cryptography, and it's worth walking through the five steps in order, because each one depends on the last: Parse the header and read the key ID ( kid ) — which specific key was used to sign this token. Fetch the issuer's public signing keys from its OIDC discovery document ( {authority}/.well-known/openid-configuration → jwks_uri ) — cached after the first lookup and refreshed periodically, not fetched on every request. Verify the signature using that public key (RS256/ES256). Because only the issuer holds the matching private key, a valid signature proves both that the token came from them and that nobody tampered with the payload in transit. Check iss matches the expected issuer, aud matches this specific API (not some other API the same provider also issues tokens for), and exp / nbf against the current clock, with a small allowance for clock drift (5 minutes by default). Build the ClaimsPrincipal straight from the payload's claims. Follow what this buys you: steps 1 and 3 together mean the API never needs its own database of valid tokens — the cryptographic signature is the proof, which is exactly why this scales to millions of requests without a database round trip on each one. C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddAuthentication ( JwtBearerDefaults . AuthenticationScheme ) . AddJwtBearer (o => { o. Authority = \"https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-id>/v2.0\" ; // discovery + signing keys o. Audience = \"api://shop-api\" ; o. MapInboundClaims = false ; // keep \"sub\", \"roles\" as-is instead of long ClaimTypes URIs o. TokenValidationParameters = new TokenValidationParameters { ValidateIssuer = true , ValidateAudience = true , ValidateLifetime = true , ClockSkew = TimeSpan . FromMinutes ( 1 ), RoleClaimType = \"roles\" , NameClaimType = \"name\" }; }); // For Entra ID specifically, Microsoft.Identity.Web wraps this: .AddMicrosoftIdentityWebApi(builder.Configuration) JWTs are signed, not encrypted Anyone can base64-decode the payload — try pasting any JWT into jwt.io and watch the claims appear in plain text. Never put secrets or sensitive personal data in a JWT. The signature only guarantees the payload hasn't been altered and genuinely came from the stated issuer; it does nothing to hide the payload's contents from anyone who intercepts it. Don't roll your own token service with a shared secret Issuing HS256 tokens signed with a symmetric key sitting in appsettings.json works fine in a tutorial, and that's exactly the problem — every API that needs to validate tokens then holds a key that can also mint them, which means any one compromised service can forge tokens for every other service in the system. Use an identity provider (Entra ID, Auth0, Okta, Keycloak, Duende IdentityServer) built on asymmetric keys instead, where validating and minting require different keys entirely. The built-in ASP.NET Core Identity API endpoints issue opaque bearer tokens, which is a reasonable fit for simple first-party scen"},{"c":"auth","s":"cookie-authentication","h":"Cookie authentication","t":"# Cookie authentication Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddAuthentication ( CookieAuthenticationDefaults . AuthenticationScheme ) . AddCookie (o => { o. LoginPath = \"/login\" ; o. AccessDeniedPath = \"/denied\" ; o. ExpireTimeSpan = TimeSpan . FromHours ( 8 ); o. SlidingExpiration = true ; o. Cookie . HttpOnly = true ; // JS can't read it o. Cookie . SecurePolicy = CookieSecurePolicy . Always ; // HTTPS only o. Cookie . SameSite = SameSiteMode . Lax ; // CSRF mitigation o. Events . OnRedirectToLogin = ctx => { ctx. Response . StatusCode = 401 ; return Task . CompletedTask ; }; // for API calls }); app. MapPost ( \"/login\" , async ( LoginRequest req, IUserService users, HttpContext ctx) => { var user = await users. ValidateAsync (req. Email , req. Password ); if (user is null ) return Results . Unauthorized (); var identity = new ClaimsIdentity ([ new ( ClaimTypes . NameIdentifier , user. Id ), new ( ClaimTypes . Role , user. Role )], CookieAuthenticationDefaults . AuthenticationScheme ); await ctx. SignInAsync ( new ClaimsPrincipal (identity)); // issues the encrypted cookie return Results . NoContent (); }); The cookie's contents are encrypted and signed with the Data Protection API. That causes a surprise the first time an app scales out to more than one instance: by default, each instance generates its own encryption keys. A cookie issued by instance A then becomes unreadable gibberish to instance B — so a user gets silently logged out the moment the load balancer happens to route them somewhere new. The fix is to share those keys across every instance (persist them to Blob Storage, Redis, or a database, itself protected with Key Vault)."},{"c":"auth","s":"asp-net-core-identity-a-membership-system-not-an-oauth-serve","h":"ASP.NET Core Identity: a membership system, not an OAuth server","t":"# ASP.NET Core Identity: a membership system, not an OAuth server Intermediate ASP.NET Core Identity handles the mechanics of having users at all: password hashing (PBKDF2), account lockout, two-factor authentication, email confirmation, external logins, roles — all persisted through EF Core. The distinction worth being precise about: it is not an OAuth/OIDC authorization server by itself; it manages your own user table, not tokens issued to third-party clients. C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddDbContext < AppIdentityDb >(o => o. UseSqlServer (cs)); builder. Services . AddIdentityApiEndpoints < AppUser >(). AddEntityFrameworkStores < AppIdentityDb >(); builder. Services . AddAuthorization (); app. MapIdentityApi < AppUser >(); // .NET 8: /register, /login, /refresh, /confirmEmail, /manage/2fa … Need Use Users log in to your app with a password, simple first-party ASP.NET Core Identity Single sign-on across many apps, third-party clients, OAuth flows An identity provider: Entra ID / Entra External ID, Auth0, Okta, Keycloak, or Duende IdentityServer / OpenIddict on top of Identity Enterprise employees Microsoft Entra ID"},{"c":"auth","s":"authorization-the-simple-forms-before-anything-needs-a-polic","h":"Authorization: the simple forms, before anything needs a policy","t":"# Authorization: the simple forms, before anything needs a policy Beginner C# Run ▶ Copy app. MapGet ( \"/orders\" , List ). RequireAuthorization (); // any authenticated user app. MapGet ( \"/status\" , Status ). AllowAnonymous (); // explicitly public app. MapDelete ( \"/orders/{id}\" , Delete ). RequireAuthorization (p => p. RequireRole ( \"admin\" )); [Authorize] // controller-level: all actions require auth public class OrdersController : ControllerBase { [AllowAnonymous] public IActionResult Public () => Ok (); [Authorize(Roles = \"admin,support\")] public IActionResult Refund () => Ok (); // admin OR support } // Secure by default: require auth everywhere unless [AllowAnonymous] builder. Services . AddAuthorizationBuilder () . SetFallbackPolicy ( new AuthorizationPolicyBuilder (). RequireAuthenticatedUser (). Build ()); That last block is worth internalising as a default posture: it's much safer to make every endpoint require authentication unless explicitly opted out with [AllowAnonymous] , than to make every endpoint public unless someone remembered to add [Authorize] . The first mistake is a locked-down endpoint that's annoying to fix; the second is a silent data leak."},{"c":"auth","s":"policy-based-authorization-centralising-rules-instead-of-sca","h":"Policy-based authorization: centralising rules instead of scattering role strings","t":"# Policy-based authorization: centralising rules instead of scattering role strings Intermediate A policy is a named bundle of requirements , and a policy only passes if every one of its requirements does. The payoff for the extra indirection is real: once a rule is named \"CanRefund\" , you write it once, and every endpoint that needs it refers to the name — nobody has to remember or copy the actual logic. C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddAuthorizationBuilder () . AddPolicy ( \"CanReadOrders\" , p => p. RequireClaim ( \"scp\" , \"orders.read\" , \"orders.write\" )) // any of these values . AddPolicy ( \"CanRefund\" , p => p. RequireRole ( \"support\" ). RequireClaim ( \"refund_limit\" )) . AddPolicy ( \"EmployeesOnly\" , p => p. RequireAssertion (ctx => ctx. User . HasClaim (c => c. Type == \"email\" && c. Value . EndsWith ( \"@shop.com\" )))) . AddPolicy ( \"Over18\" , p => p. AddRequirements ( new MinimumAgeRequirement ( 18 ))); orders. MapPost ( \"/{id}/refund\" , Refund ). RequireAuthorization ( \"CanRefund\" ); # Custom requirements and handlers When the built-in requirement helpers ( RequireRole , RequireClaim , RequireAssertion ) can't express a rule, write your own: C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed record MinimumAgeRequirement ( int Age ) : IAuthorizationRequirement ; public sealed class MinimumAgeHandler ( TimeProvider clock) : AuthorizationHandler < MinimumAgeRequirement > { protected override Task HandleRequirementAsync ( AuthorizationHandlerContext ctx, MinimumAgeRequirement req) { if ( DateTime . TryParse (ctx. User . FindFirstValue ( \"birthdate\" ), out var dob) && dob. AddYears (req. Age ) <= clock. GetUtcNow (). DateTime ) ctx. Succeed (req); // don't call Fail() unless you want to veto other handlers return Task . CompletedTask ; } } builder. Services . AddSingleton < IAuthorizationHandler , MinimumAgeHandler >(); The comment on Succeed matters: a requirement passes if any registered handler for it calls Succeed — several handlers can each independently vote yes. But if even one handler calls Fail , that overrides every yes-vote and vetoes the requirement outright. Handlers are ordinary scoped services, so they can query the database like anything else."},{"c":"auth","s":"resource-based-authorization-when-the-rule-depends-on-which-","h":"Resource-based authorization: when the rule depends on which specific row","t":"# Resource-based authorization: when the rule depends on which specific row Advanced Policies above answer yes/no questions that don't depend on which resource you're looking at. Plenty of real rules do: \"a customer can view their own orders,\" \"an editor can edit documents in their department .\" Since the rule can't be evaluated until you know which row is involved, the endpoint has to load the resource first, then ask the authorization service imperatively: C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class OrderOwnerHandler : AuthorizationHandler < OperationAuthorizationRequirement , Order > { protected override Task HandleRequirementAsync ( AuthorizationHandlerContext ctx, OperationAuthorizationRequirement op, Order order) { var userId = ctx. User . FindFirstValue ( \"sub\" ); if (order. CustomerId == userId || ctx. User . IsInRole ( \"support\" )) ctx. Succeed (op); return Task . CompletedTask ; } } app. MapGet ( \"/orders/{id:int}\" , async ( int id, ShopDb db, ClaimsPrincipal user, IAuthorizationService authz) => { var order = await db. Orders . FindAsync (id); if (order is null ) return Results . NotFound (); var result = await authz. AuthorizeAsync (user, order, new OperationAuthorizationRequirement { Name = \"Read\" }); return result. Succeeded ? Results . Ok (order. ToDto ()) : Results . NotFound (); // 404 hides existence (prevents enumeration) }). RequireAuthorization (); Look closely at that last line's comment: returning 404 , not 403, when authorization fails is a deliberate choice, not an inconsistency. If a competitor's order ID returned 403, that confirms to an attacker the order exists at all — a small leak, but a real one. Returning the same 404 whether the order doesn't exist or simply isn't theirs closes that gap. This whole pattern is the standard defence against IDOR (insecure direct object reference), consistently one of the most common real-world API vulnerabilities — see Web security for the fuller picture."},{"c":"auth","s":"api-keys-client-certificates-and-multiple-schemes","h":"API keys, client certificates and multiple schemes","t":"# API keys, client certificates and multiple schemes Advanced C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class ApiKeyHandler ( IOptionsMonitor < AuthenticationSchemeOptions > o, ILoggerFactory l, UrlEncoder e, IApiKeyStore keys) : AuthenticationHandler < AuthenticationSchemeOptions >(o, l, e) { protected override async Task < AuthenticateResult > HandleAuthenticateAsync () { if (! Request . Headers . TryGetValue ( \"X-Api-Key\" , out var key)) return AuthenticateResult . NoResult (); var client = await keys. FindAsync (key!); // compare HASHED keys, constant-time if (client is null ) return AuthenticateResult . Fail ( \"Invalid API key\" ); var principal = new ClaimsPrincipal ( new ClaimsIdentity ([ new ( \"client_id\" , client. Id )], Scheme . Name )); return AuthenticateResult . Success ( new AuthenticationTicket (principal, Scheme . Name )); } } // Accept either JWT or API key on some endpoints builder. Services . AddAuthorizationBuilder (). AddPolicy ( \"PartnerAccess\" , p => p . AddAuthenticationSchemes ( JwtBearerDefaults . AuthenticationScheme , \"ApiKey\" ) . RequireAuthenticatedUser ()); Notice that this custom handler slots into the exact same five-action model (Authenticate/Challenge/Forbid/SignIn/SignOut) described earlier for cookies and JWTs — it's a different mechanism for proving identity, but the framework treats it identically once it's registered. Other options worth knowing by name: mTLS / client certificates ( AddCertificate ) for service-to-service calls; managed identity tokens for Azure-to-Azure calls, covered in Identity & secrets ; and a policy scheme ( AddPolicyScheme with ForwardDefaultSelector ) for picking which underlying scheme to use dynamically, per request."},{"c":"auth","s":"claims-transformation-and-permissions","h":"Claims transformation and permissions","t":"# Claims transformation and permissions Advanced Tokens typically carry coarse-grained data — a user ID, maybe a couple of roles — because the identity provider that issued them has no idea about your app's fine-grained permission model. Claims transformation is the hook for enriching the principal, per request, with exactly that app-specific detail: C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class PermissionClaims ( IPermissionStore store) : IClaimsTransformation { public async Task < ClaimsPrincipal > TransformAsync ( ClaimsPrincipal p) { if (p. Identity is not ClaimsIdentity { IsAuthenticated : true } id || id. HasClaim (c => c. Type == \"perm\" )) return p; foreach ( var perm in await store. GetAsync (p. FindFirstValue ( \"sub\" )!)) id. AddClaim ( new ( \"perm\" , perm)); return p; } } builder. Services . AddScoped < IClaimsTransformation , PermissionClaims >(); Worth flagging plainly: this runs on every authenticate call, for every request, so whatever store.GetAsync does needs to be cheap or cached — otherwise you've quietly added a database round trip to every single authenticated request in the app. Q What's the difference between authentication and authorization? Authentication establishes who the caller is: it validates credentials (cookie, JWT, API key) and produces a ClaimsPrincipal in HttpContext.User. Authorization decides whether that principal may perform an action on a resource, using roles, claims, policies or resource-based checks. Failure gives 401 for authentication and 403 for authorization. Q How does an ASP.NET Core API validate a JWT? The JwtBearer handler reads the token from the Authorization header, gets the issuer's public signing keys from the OIDC discovery document (cached), verifies the signature, validates issuer, audience and lifetime (exp/nbf with clock skew), then builds a ClaimsPrincipal from the claims. No call to the identity provider per request. Q Cookies vs JWTs: when would you use each? Cookies for browser-based apps and BFFs: sent automatically, HttpOnly protects against XSS theft, they need CSRF protection, and they're easy to revoke. JWT bearer tokens for APIs consumed by SPAs, mobile apps and services: stateless and cross-domain, but hard to revoke, so keep them short-lived. For SPAs, the BFF pattern with cookies is now recommended. Q What is in a JWT and is it secure to put data in it? A header (algorithm, key ID), a payload of claims (iss, sub, aud, exp, roles, scopes…) and a signature, all base64url encoded. It's signed, not encrypted, so anyone can read the payload. Don't put secrets or sensitive data in it. Q Roles vs claims vs policies? Claims are facts about the user; a role is one kind of claim. Policies are named authorization rules made of requirements that can check roles, claims or custom logic, keeping rules centralised and testable instead of scattering role strings across controllers. Q How do you implement 'users can only access their own orders'? Resource-based authorization: load the order, then call IAuthorizationService.AuthorizeAsync(user, order, requirement) with an AuthorizationHandler<TRequirement, Order> that checks ownership. Or always scope queries by the current user ID. Return 404 rather than 403 to avoid revealing the resource exists. This prevents IDOR. Q How do you require authentication on every endpoint by default? Set a fallback policy: AddAuthorizationBuilder().SetFallbackPolicy(new AuthorizationPolicyBuilder().RequireAuthenticatedUser().Build()) , then mark public endpoints with AllowAnonymous. Q What is ASP.NET Core Identity and is it an OAuth server? A membership library for user storage, password hashing, lockout, 2FA, confirmations and external logins, persisted with EF Core. It's not an OAuth 2.0/OIDC authorization server; for that you use Entra ID/Auth0/Okta/Keycloak or build on Identity with Duende IdentityServer or OpenIddict. Q Why do you need to configure Data Protection when running multiple instances with cookie auth? Auth cookies (and antiforgery tok"},{"c":"auth","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Overview of ASP.NET Core authentication Configure JWT bearer authentication Policy-based authorization Resource-based authorization Microsoft.Identity.Web"},{"c":"auth","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run (plain console app, .NET 8/9, BCL only — no identity-provider or ASP.NET Core packages needed for these checks). C# Run ▶ Copy using System . Security . Claims ; using System . Text ; // Claim: \"a role is just a claim whose type happens to be 'role'\" — nothing structurally special about it. var identity = new ClaimsIdentity ( [ new Claim ( ClaimTypes . NameIdentifier , \"7f3c\" ), new Claim ( ClaimTypes . Role , \"admin\" )], authenticationType: \"TestScheme\" ); var principal = new ClaimsPrincipal (identity); if (!principal. IsInRole ( \"admin\" )) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected IsInRole to recognise a plain Role claim\" ); if (!principal. HasClaim (c => c. Type == ClaimTypes . Role && c. Value == \"admin\" )) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected the role to be readable as an ordinary claim too\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: IsInRole(\\\"admin\\\") and reading the same thing as a plain claim agree — a role is just a claim\" ); // Claim: a JWT is three base64url-encoded parts joined by dots: header.payload.signature static string Base64UrlEncode ( string s) => Convert . ToBase64String ( Encoding . UTF8 . GetBytes (s)). TrimEnd ( '=' ). Replace ( '+' , '-' ). Replace ( '/' , '_' ); var header = Base64UrlEncode ( \"\"\"{\"alg\":\"RS256\",\"kid\":\"123\"}\"\"\" ); var payload = Base64UrlEncode ( \"\"\"{\"sub\":\"7f3c\",\"aud\":\"api://shop-api\",\"exp\":1767225600}\"\"\" ); var fakeSignature = Base64UrlEncode ( \"not-a-real-signature\" ); var jwt = $\"{header}.{payload}.{fakeSignature}\" ; var parts = jwt. Split ( '.' ); if (parts. Length != 3 ) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected a JWT to have 3 dot-separated parts, got {parts.Length}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: a JWT has exactly 3 base64url parts (header.payload.signature)\" ); // Claim: JWTs are signed, not encrypted — anyone can read the payload without the signing key. string Base64UrlDecode ( string s) { var padded = s. Replace ( '-' , '+' ). Replace ( '_' , '/' ); padded += new string ( '=' , ( 4 - padded. Length % 4 ) % 4 ); return Encoding . UTF8 . GetString ( Convert . FromBase64String (padded)); } var decodedPayload = Base64UrlDecode (parts[ 1 ]); if (!decodedPayload. Contains ( \"\\\"sub\\\":\\\"7f3c\\\"\" )) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected the payload to be readable without any key — it's signed, not encrypted\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: decoded the payload with no key at all: {decodedPayload} — confirms JWTs are signed, not encrypted\" ); // Claim: resource-based auth should return 404 (not 403) for \"exists but not yours\", to avoid confirming existence (IDOR defense). string CheckOrderAccess ( string ? order, string currentUserId) { if (order is null ) return \"404\" ; // doesn't exist if (order != currentUserId) return \"404\" ; // exists, but isn't theirs — SAME response as \"doesn't exist\" return \"200\" ; } var responseForMissing = CheckOrderAccess ( null , \"me\" ); var responseForSomeoneElses = CheckOrderAccess ( \"someone-else\" , \"me\" ); if (responseForMissing != responseForSomeoneElses) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected 'missing' and 'not yours' to be indistinguishable to the caller (both 404)\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: a missing order and someone else's order both answer 404 — an attacker can't tell which one it was\" ); // Not runtime-verifiable here (needs a live identity provider / ASP.NET Core pipeline) — see Further reading: // - Real signature verification against an issuer's JWKS, audience/issuer/expiry validation: // https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/security/authentication/configure-jwt-bearer-authentication // - Policy evaluation, fallback policies, AuthorizationHandler voting (Succeed/Fail) end to end: // https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/security/authorization/policies // - Data Protection key sharing across instances for cookie auth at scale: // https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/security/authentication/ Console . WriteLine ( \"All checkable claims verified.\" );"},{"c":"http-client","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR HttpClient is a thin wrapper; the expensive part — connections, DNS, TLS — lives in the handler underneath it ( SocketsHttpHandler ), which is why how you manage its lifetime matters more than the calls themselves. Creating a new HttpClient per call eventually exhausts sockets; keeping one forever eventually serves stale DNS after a failover. Both are real production incidents, not theoretical. IHttpClientFactory fixes both: it pools and periodically recycles handlers, so connections are reused but DNS changes still get picked up. Prefer typed clients (a class wrapping HttpClient , exposing a domain API) over named clients, and never let a singleton capture one — that recreates the stale-DNS bug. ** DelegatingHandler **s are middleware for outbound calls: auth tokens, correlation headers, logging, and resilience (retries, circuit breakers) all slot into the same chain. Pass the incoming request's CancellationToken through to every outbound call, and read the error body before calling EnsureSuccessStatusCode() — it throws away any ProblemDetails the server sent you."},{"c":"http-client","s":"the-basic-calls","h":"The basic calls","t":"# The basic calls Beginner C# Run ▶ Copy var response = await http. GetAsync ( \"https://api.github.com/repos/dotnet/runtime\" , ct); response. EnsureSuccessStatusCode (); // throws HttpRequestException on 4xx/5xx var repo = await response. Content . ReadFromJsonAsync < Repo >(ct); var repo2 = await http. GetFromJsonAsync < Repo >( \"repos/dotnet/runtime\" , ct); // one-liner var created = await http. PostAsJsonAsync ( \"orders\" , new { sku = \"KB-01\" , qty = 1 }, ct); using var req = new HttpRequestMessage ( HttpMethod . Put , $\"orders/{id}\" ) { Content = JsonContent . Create (update), Headers = { { \"If-Match\" , etag } } }; using var res = await http. SendAsync (req, HttpCompletionOption . ResponseHeadersRead , ct); // stream big responses Those calls look harmless, and they are harmless at this level of abstraction. The trouble starts with a question that looks unrelated: where does http — the HttpClient instance — actually come from, and how long does it live? Get that wrong, and the bugs that follow are some of the most notorious in all of ASP.NET Core history."},{"c":"http-client","s":"what-an-httpclient-actually-is-underneath","h":"What an HttpClient actually is, underneath","t":"# What an HttpClient actually is, underneath Intermediate HttpClient isn't the thing doing the real work — it's a thin, friendly front end over a chain of message handlers : Diagram Copy HttpClient ──▶ DelegatingHandler (e.g. auth header) ──▶ DelegatingHandler (logging) ──▶ … ──▶ SocketsHttpHandler (API you call) your cross-cutting handlers (a pipeline, like middleware) (owns the CONNECTION POOL, DNS, TLS, sockets) The expensive part — the thing that actually opens TCP connections, negotiates TLS, and keeps a connection pool alive for reuse — lives at the far end, in the primary handler ( SocketsHttpHandler ). HttpClient itself is a lightweight wrapper around that chain. Hold onto that one fact; it's the key to understanding both classic bugs below, because each one comes from misjudging which part of this picture is actually expensive."},{"c":"http-client","s":"the-two-classic-bugs-and-why-theyre-opposite-mistakes","h":"The two classic bugs, and why they're opposite mistakes","t":"# The two classic bugs, and why they're opposite mistakes Beginner # Bug 1: a new HttpClient per request → socket exhaustion C# Run ▶ Copy public async Task < Rates > GetRatesAsync () { using var http = new HttpClient (); // ✘ new handler + new connection pool every call return await http. GetFromJsonAsync < Rates >(url); } // disposed → socket closed → sits in TIME_WAIT for ~2–4 min This looks responsible — using , proper disposal, nothing left dangling. The problem is exactly what that using throws away: a fresh HttpClient means a fresh SocketsHttpHandler , which means a fresh connection pool, every single call. Disposing it doesn't instantly free the socket either — TCP requires the closing side to sit in TIME_WAIT for roughly 2–4 minutes, as a protocol-level safeguard. Under real load, that adds up fast: thousands of sockets pile up in TIME_WAIT simultaneously, and the machine eventually runs out of ephemeral ports entirely, surfacing as SocketException: Only one usage of each socket address is normally permitted . On top of that, every single call pays for a brand-new TCP handshake and TLS negotiation, work that a pooled connection would have skipped entirely. # Bug 2: one static HttpClient forever → stale DNS C# Run ▶ Copy private static readonly HttpClient Http = new (); // ✘ connections live forever This is the overcorrection — treating HttpClient as expensive to create (true for the handler, not really true for HttpClient itself) and so creating exactly one, forever. The connections it opens now live indefinitely, reused for every call. That sounds efficient, and it is — right up until the target's DNS record changes: a blue/green deployment flips the active environment, a failover happens, a load balancer gets a new IP. The static client never re-resolves DNS, because it never has a reason to — its existing connection still works, technically, just to the wrong place now. # The fix: let something else own the lifetime decision Fix How IHttpClientFactory (recommended in ASP.NET Core) Pools and recycles handlers (every 2 minutes by default), so connections are reused but DNS changes are picked up; integrates config, DI, logging and resilience Static/singleton HttpClient + PooledConnectionLifetime new HttpClient(new SocketsHttpHandler { PooledConnectionLifetime = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(2) }) : fine for simple apps and libraries Notice what IHttpClientFactory actually buys you, looking back at the diagram: it isn't \"faster disposal\" or \"fewer allocations\" — it's periodic handler replacement , which reuses connections for the common case (most calls, most of the time) while still bounding how long any one connection can go stale."},{"c":"http-client","s":"ihttpclientfactory-in-practice","h":"IHttpClientFactory in practice","t":"# IHttpClientFactory in practice Intermediate # Named clients C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddHttpClient ( \"github\" , c => { c. BaseAddress = new Uri ( \"https://api.github.com/\" ); c. DefaultRequestHeaders . UserAgent . ParseAdd ( \"shop-api/1.0\" ); c. Timeout = TimeSpan . FromSeconds ( 15 ); }); public sealed class RepoService ( IHttpClientFactory factory) { public Task < Repo ?> GetAsync ( string name, CancellationToken ct) => factory. CreateClient ( \"github\" ). GetFromJsonAsync < Repo >( $\"repos/{name}\" , ct); } # Typed clients (preferred) A better shape wraps the raw HTTP calls behind a class that receives a pre-configured HttpClient through its constructor, and exposes a domain-friendly API instead of raw URLs and verbs: C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class InventoryClient ( HttpClient http) { public async Task < StockLevel ?> GetStockAsync ( string sku, CancellationToken ct) { using var res = await http. GetAsync ( $\"stock/{Uri.EscapeDataString(sku)}\" , ct); if (res. StatusCode == HttpStatusCode . NotFound ) return null ; res. EnsureSuccessStatusCode (); return await res. Content . ReadFromJsonAsync < StockLevel >(ct); } public async Task ReserveAsync ( Reservation r, CancellationToken ct) => ( await http. PostAsJsonAsync ( \"reservations\" , r, ct)). EnsureSuccessStatusCode (); } builder. Services . AddHttpClient < InventoryClient >(c => c. BaseAddress = new Uri (builder. Configuration [ \"Services:Inventory\" ]!)) . AddHttpMessageHandler < CorrelationIdHandler >() . AddStandardResilienceHandler (); // retries, timeouts, circuit breaker (see Resilience) The typed client itself is registered as transient — cheap to create, because the expensive part lives in the shared, recycled handler underneath it, not in this thin wrapper. Inject it wherever you need it. Don't capture typed clients in singletons Here's a trap that recreates the exact stale-DNS bug from above, wearing a different disguise: if a singleton holds onto a typed client (or any HttpClient the factory handed it) for the app's whole lifetime, that handler is never recycled, because nothing ever lets go of it. Inject typed clients into transient or scoped services instead — the same captive-dependency shape covered in Dependency injection — or, if a singleton genuinely needs HTTP access, inject IHttpClientFactory directly and create a fresh client per use. (Configuring SocketsHttpHandler.PooledConnectionLifetime on the primary handler is the other escape hatch.)"},{"c":"http-client","s":"delegatinghandlers-the-exact-same-idea-as-middleware-running","h":"DelegatingHandlers: the exact same idea as middleware, running outbound","t":"# DelegatingHandlers: the exact same idea as middleware, running outbound Intermediate The handler chain introduced earlier is a pipeline in the fullest sense — composable, ordered, each one wrapping the next — which should feel familiar after Middleware . The difference is direction: this pipeline runs on the way out , for requests your app is making to someone else, not on the way in. C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class AccessTokenHandler ( ITokenProvider tokens) : DelegatingHandler { protected override async Task < HttpResponseMessage > SendAsync ( HttpRequestMessage request, CancellationToken ct) { var token = await tokens. GetAsync ( \"api://inventory/.default\" , ct); // cached client-credentials token request. Headers . Authorization = new AuthenticationHeaderValue ( \"Bearer\" , token); return await base . SendAsync (request, ct); // call the next handler } } public sealed class CorrelationIdHandler ( IHttpContextAccessor accessor) : DelegatingHandler { protected override Task < HttpResponseMessage > SendAsync ( HttpRequestMessage request, CancellationToken ct) { if (accessor. HttpContext ?. Request . Headers [ \"X-Correlation-Id\" ] is { Count : > 0 } id) request. Headers . TryAddWithoutValidation ( \"X-Correlation-Id\" , id. ToString ()); return base . SendAsync (request, ct); } } builder. Services . AddTransient < AccessTokenHandler >(). AddTransient < CorrelationIdHandler >(); builder. Services . AddHttpClient < InventoryClient >(...). AddHttpMessageHandler < AccessTokenHandler >(). AddHttpMessageHandler < CorrelationIdHandler >(); Typical jobs for a handler: attaching auth tokens, propagating correlation or tenant headers, logging, caching, request signing. Resilience itself — retries, circuit breakers — is implemented as a handler too, which is why .AddStandardResilienceHandler() above slots in exactly like any other entry in this chain. (One thing you don't need a handler for: W3C traceparent propagation for distributed tracing happens automatically.)"},{"c":"http-client","s":"timeouts-and-cancellation-getting-the-layering-right","h":"Timeouts and cancellation: getting the layering right","t":"# Timeouts and cancellation: getting the layering right Intermediate HttpClient.Timeout (default 100 seconds , far too long for most APIs) applies to the entire request. When it fires, it surfaces as a TaskCanceledException with an inner TimeoutException (since .NET 5) — the only reliable way to tell \"it timed out\" apart from \"the caller cancelled it,\" since both throw the same outer exception type. Always pass the incoming request's CancellationToken through to outbound calls, so that if the original client disconnects, your app stops waiting on a downstream call nobody's going to read the answer to. Once the resilience handler is in play, prefer its total request timeout and per-attempt timeout over HttpClient.Timeout — and set HttpClient.Timeout itself to Infinite or something larger, so it doesn't quietly compete with the timeouts you actually configured. C# Run ▶ Copy catch ( TaskCanceledException ex) when (ex. InnerException is TimeoutException ) { /* timed out */ } catch ( TaskCanceledException ) when (ct. IsCancellationRequested ) { /* caller cancelled */ }"},{"c":"http-client","s":"handling-responses-properly-resist-the-urge-to-just-ensuresu","h":"Handling responses properly: resist the urge to just EnsureSuccessStatusCode","t":"# Handling responses properly: resist the urge to just EnsureSuccessStatusCode Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy using var res = await http. GetAsync (url, ct); switch (res. StatusCode ) { case HttpStatusCode . OK : return await res. Content . ReadFromJsonAsync < Dto >(ct); case HttpStatusCode . NotFound : return null ; case HttpStatusCode . TooManyRequests : var retryAfter = res. Headers . RetryAfter ?. Delta ; // respect server throttling throw new ThrottledException (retryAfter); default : var problem = await res. Content . ReadFromJsonAsync < ProblemDetails >(ct); // read the error body before throwing throw new DownstreamException (res. StatusCode , problem?. Title ); } Three habits worth making automatic: Dispose every HttpResponseMessage (or wrap it in using ) — this matters especially with ResponseHeadersRead , since the connection only returns to the pool once you're actually done with it. Use ResponseHeadersRead for large responses, so the body streams instead of being fully buffered into memory before you see a single byte. Don't reach for EnsureSuccessStatusCode reflexively when the error response carries a body worth reading — calling it first throws away the ProblemDetails payload the server went to the trouble of sending you."},{"c":"http-client","s":"generated-and-declarative-clients","h":"Generated and declarative clients","t":"# Generated and declarative clients Advanced Writing a typed client by hand, as above, works well for a handful of endpoints. For a large external API, generating the client from its contract avoids hand-transcribing dozens of endpoints and keeps the client in sync as the contract evolves: Tool Approach Refit Declare an interface with attributes; Refit generates the implementation (source generator) Kiota (Microsoft) Generates strongly typed clients from OpenAPI descriptions NSwag / OpenAPI Generator Generate C# clients from OpenAPI gRPC client Generated from .proto files ( App models ) C# Run ▶ Copy public interface IGitHubApi { [Get(\"/repos/{owner}/{repo}\")] Task < Repo > GetRepoAsync ( string owner, string repo, CancellationToken ct = default ); } builder. Services . AddRefitClient < IGitHubApi >(). ConfigureHttpClient (c => c. BaseAddress = new Uri ( \"https://api.github.com\" )) . AddStandardResilienceHandler ();"},{"c":"http-client","s":"service-discovery","h":"Service discovery","t":"# Service discovery Advanced In .NET Aspire and Kubernetes, you address services by logical name rather than a URL hard-coded to a specific host: c.BaseAddress = new(\"https+http://inventory\") , with Microsoft.Extensions.ServiceDiscovery resolving the actual endpoint from configuration or DNS at runtime. This sidesteps the stale-DNS problem from a different angle entirely — the address itself is never a fixed IP to begin with. See Observability & Aspire ."},{"c":"http-client","s":"testing-code-that-uses-httpclient","h":"Testing code that uses HttpClient","t":"# Testing code that uses HttpClient Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy // A stub handler: no network, deterministic responses public sealed class StubHandler ( Func < HttpRequestMessage , HttpResponseMessage > respond) : HttpMessageHandler { protected override Task < HttpResponseMessage > SendAsync ( HttpRequestMessage req, CancellationToken ct) => Task . FromResult (respond(req)); } [Fact] public async Task Returns_null_when_sku_unknown () { var http = new HttpClient ( new StubHandler (_ => new HttpResponseMessage ( HttpStatusCode . NotFound ))) { BaseAddress = new ( \"https://inv/\" ) }; var client = new InventoryClient (http); Assert . Null ( await client. GetStockAsync ( \"NOPE\" , CancellationToken . None )); } Notice the test constructs InventoryClient directly with a hand-built HttpClient , bypassing the factory entirely — this works precisely because the typed client's constructor just wants an HttpClient , and doesn't care how it was produced. For integration tests that need genuinely realistic HTTP behaviour — real headers, real status-code edge cases — run a fake server with WireMock.Net instead of a hand-rolled stub. Q Why shouldn't you create a new HttpClient for every request? Each instance (with its own handler) opens new connections. Disposing it closes sockets that linger in TIME_WAIT, so under load you exhaust ephemeral ports (socket exhaustion), and every call pays new TCP/TLS handshakes. Q Then why not just use one static HttpClient? Its pooled connections live forever, so DNS changes aren't observed and you can keep hitting old IPs after failovers or deployments. Fix it with IHttpClientFactory, which recycles handlers, or by setting SocketsHttpHandler.PooledConnectionLifetime on a long-lived client. Q What does IHttpClientFactory do? It creates HttpClient instances whose underlying handlers are pooled and periodically recycled (2 min default), avoiding both socket exhaustion and stale DNS. It centralises configuration (named/typed clients), lets you compose DelegatingHandlers, integrates logging and resilience (Polly), and works with DI. Q Named vs typed clients? Named clients are configured under a name and created with factory.CreateClient(\"name\"). Typed clients are classes that receive a preconfigured HttpClient via their constructor and expose domain methods; they're strongly typed, encapsulate the API and are usually preferred. Q What is a DelegatingHandler? A handler in HttpClient's outbound pipeline that can inspect or modify requests and responses before passing them on with base.SendAsync. Used for auth tokens, correlation headers, logging and resilience; added with AddHttpMessageHandler. Q How do you set timeouts for outbound calls? HttpClient.Timeout (default 100 s, usually too long), or better, the resilience pipeline's total and per-attempt timeouts. Always pass a CancellationToken from the incoming request so calls stop if the client disconnects. Q How do you unit test a class that uses HttpClient? Construct the HttpClient with a stub HttpMessageHandler that returns canned responses (or use a mocking library for handlers), or run WireMock.Net for higher-fidelity tests. Don't mock HttpClient directly."},{"c":"http-client","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Make HTTP requests using IHttpClientFactory HttpClient guidelines for .NET Build resilient HTTP apps"},{"c":"http-client","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter The checks below cover handler ordering, a typed client built with a stub handler, and HttpClient.Timeout vs caller cancellation — all with a fake HttpMessageHandler , no real network. Save as a console app ( dotnet new console ), then dotnet run . C# Run ▶ Copy using System . Net ; using System . Net . Http . Json ; // Claim: DelegatingHandlers form an ordered pipeline — each one runs, in registration order, before the final handler. { var order = new List < string >(); var final = new StubHandler (_ => new HttpResponseMessage ( HttpStatusCode . OK )); var handlerB = new RecordingHandler ( \"B\" , order) { InnerHandler = final }; var handlerA = new RecordingHandler ( \"A\" , order) { InnerHandler = handlerB }; using var http = new HttpClient (handlerA); using var res = await http. GetAsync ( \"http://stub/orders\" ); if (!order. SequenceEqual ( new [] { \"A\" , \"B\" })) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected [A, B], got [{string.Join(\" , \", order)}]\" ); if (res. StatusCode != HttpStatusCode . OK ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: final handler's response didn't come through\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: DelegatingHandlers run in registration order, wrapping the next one\" ); } // Claim: a typed client's constructor just wants an HttpClient — you can hand it a stub handler directly, no factory needed. { using var http = new HttpClient ( new StubHandler (_ => new HttpResponseMessage ( HttpStatusCode . NotFound ))) { BaseAddress = new Uri ( \"http://inv/\" ) }; var client = new InventoryClient (http); var result = await client. GetStockAsync ( \"NOPE\" , CancellationToken . None ); if (result is not null ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected null for a 404\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: a typed client built directly with a stub handler behaves exactly like a factory-built one\" ); } // Claim: HttpClient.Timeout firing produces TaskCanceledException with an inner TimeoutException (since .NET 5) — // this is how you tell \"it timed out\" apart from \"the caller cancelled it.\" { using var slow = new HttpClient ( new DelayingHandler ( TimeSpan . FromSeconds ( 5 ))) { Timeout = TimeSpan . FromMilliseconds ( 100 ) }; Exception ? caught = null ; try { await slow. GetAsync ( \"http://stub/slow\" , CancellationToken . None ); } catch ( Exception ex) { caught = ex; } if (caught is not TaskCanceledException tce || tce. InnerException is not TimeoutException ) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected TaskCanceledException with inner TimeoutException, got {caught}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: HttpClient.Timeout firing throws TaskCanceledException with an inner TimeoutException\" ); } { using var slow = new HttpClient ( new DelayingHandler ( TimeSpan . FromSeconds ( 5 ))) { Timeout = Timeout . InfiniteTimeSpan }; using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource ( TimeSpan . FromMilliseconds ( 100 )); Exception ? caught = null ; try { await slow. GetAsync ( \"http://stub/slow\" , cts. Token ); } catch ( Exception ex) { caught = ex; } if (caught is not OperationCanceledException || caught is TaskCanceledException tce2 && tce2. InnerException is TimeoutException ) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected a plain cancellation, not a TimeoutException, got {caught}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: the caller's own CancellationToken firing does NOT produce an inner TimeoutException\" ); } Console . WriteLine ( \"All checks passed.\" ); // Not runtime-verifiable here (needs a real network, real sockets, or real DNS): // - socket exhaustion from a new HttpClient per request, or stale DNS from one static HttpClient forever — see Further reading: \"HttpClient guidelines for .NET\" // - IHttpClientFactory's actual handler-recycling schedule (every 2 minutes by default) — see Further reading: \"Make HTTP requests using IHttpClientFactory\" // - resilience handlers (retries, circuit breakers), Refit/Kiota-generated clients, service discovery — see Further reading: \"Build resilient HTTP apps\" sealed class StubHandler ( Func < HttpRequestMessage , HttpResponseMessage > respon"},{"c":"caching-ratelimit-cors","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Every cache decision reduces to one question: how stale can this data be? Decide that first, per data type, before picking a technology. IMemoryCache is fast but per instance — three app instances means three independent copies that can disagree. IDistributedCache (Redis) is shared but pays a network hop every time. HybridCache (.NET 9) gives you both: a fast local layer in front of a shared one, plus stampede protection. On a write, delete the cache key rather than overwriting it with the new value — overwriting can leave a stale value cached if two writers race. Rate limiting (fixed window, sliding window, token bucket) caps requests per client; like IMemoryCache , the built-in limiter's counters are per instance , so a \"100/minute\" limit becomes \"100 × instance count\" across a fleet unless you use a shared store. CORS protects browsers , not your API — non-browser clients (curl, server-to-server calls) ignore it completely. Your API's real protection is authentication/authorization."},{"c":"caching-ratelimit-cors","s":"why-cache-at-all","h":"Why cache at all","t":"# Why cache at all Beginner Most backend reads are repetitive , in a specific, exploitable way: the same product page, the same exchange rates, the same permission lookup, asked for thousands of times while the underlying data barely changes. Every one of those identical requests still costs a full database round trip or an external API call unless something intervenes. A cache is that intervention — a copy of the answer, kept close by, so the thousandth identical request doesn't repeat the work the first one already did. Benefit Cost Lower latency (memory lookup in microseconds vs a database query in milliseconds) Staleness : the copy may be out of date Less load on databases and downstream APIs; lower bills Invalidation logic Resilience: can keep serving when a dependency is slow Memory use, extra infrastructure (Redis) Absorbs traffic spikes Consistency across instances The question every cache decision reduces to Every cache ultimately answers one question: how stale may this data be? A price shown at checkout: seconds, or arguably no cache at all. A product description: minutes or hours is fine. A list of countries: days, safely. Decide this per data type first , before picking any technology — the right tool follows directly from the answer, not the other way around."},{"c":"caching-ratelimit-cors","s":"the-caching-layers-from-the-browser-down-to-the-database","h":"The caching layers, from the browser down to the database","t":"# The caching layers, from the browser down to the database Beginner A single request can pass through several independent caching layers before it ever touches a database, each one answering the \"how stale\" question for a different audience: Diagram Copy Browser cache ─▶ CDN / reverse proxy ─▶ Output cache (whole response) ─▶ App data cache (memory → Redis) ─▶ Database Cache-Control Front Door ASP.NET Core middleware HybridCache / IMemoryCache / (+ its own ETag IDistributedCache buffer pool) Layer Tool Caches Shared across instances? HTTP (client/CDN) Cache-Control , ETag headers, response caching middleware Responses, by URL Yes (CDN), per user (browser) Server output Output caching ( AddOutputCache ) Whole endpoint responses Per instance (or Redis store) App data, local IMemoryCache Objects in process memory No App data, distributed IDistributedCache (Redis, SQL, Cosmos) Serialized bytes Yes App data, two-level HybridCache (.NET 9) L1 memory + L2 distributed, stampede protection, tags Yes (L2)"},{"c":"caching-ratelimit-cors","s":"imemorycache-the-simplest-layer-and-its-one-serious-limitati","h":"IMemoryCache: the simplest layer, and its one serious limitation","t":"# IMemoryCache: the simplest layer, and its one serious limitation Beginner C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddMemoryCache (o => o. SizeLimit = 10_000 ); // optional size budget (each entry must then set Size) public sealed class CountryService ( IMemoryCache cache, ShopDb db) { public async Task < IReadOnlyList < Country >> GetAllAsync ( CancellationToken ct) => ( await cache. GetOrCreateAsync ( \"countries\" , async entry => { entry. AbsoluteExpirationRelativeToNow = TimeSpan . FromHours ( 6 ); entry. SetSize ( 1 ); return await db. Countries . AsNoTracking (). OrderBy (c => c. Name ). ToListAsync (ct); }))!; } Expiration Meaning Absolute Expires at a fixed time/duration after creation: guarantees maximum staleness Sliding Expires if not accessed for N; keeps hot items alive (combine with absolute so they still refresh eventually) Priority / size Eviction under memory pressure or size limit Change tokens Evict when a file, config or CancellationTokenSource signals IMemoryCache with multiple instances Here's the limitation that catches people as soon as an app scales past one instance: three instances behind a load balancer mean three entirely independent caches , each with its own copy and its own idea of when it was last refreshed. A user can load a page showing a new price, then hit a different instance on the very next request and see the old one. Reach for a distributed cache or HybridCache when consistency across instances genuinely matters, or deliberately accept the short inconsistency window for data where it doesn't — the country-list example above is a case where it plainly doesn't."},{"c":"caching-ratelimit-cors","s":"idistributedcache-and-redis-trading-a-memory-lookup-for-a-ne","h":"IDistributedCache and Redis: trading a memory lookup for a network hop","t":"# IDistributedCache and Redis: trading a memory lookup for a network hop Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddStackExchangeRedisCache (o => { o. Configuration = builder. Configuration . GetConnectionString ( \"Redis\" ); o. InstanceName = \"shop:\" ; // key prefix }); public sealed class CartStore ( IDistributedCache cache) { public async Task < Cart ?> GetAsync ( string userId, CancellationToken ct) { var bytes = await cache. GetAsync ( $\"cart:{userId}\" , ct); return bytes is null ? null : JsonSerializer . Deserialize < Cart >(bytes); } public Task SaveAsync ( string userId, Cart cart, CancellationToken ct) => cache. SetAsync ( $\"cart:{userId}\" , JsonSerializer . SerializeToUtf8Bytes (cart), new DistributedCacheEntryOptions { SlidingExpiration = TimeSpan . FromDays ( 7 ) }, ct); } Notice what IDistributedCache deliberately doesn't do for you: it stores raw bytes , not objects, so serialization is your responsibility, and every access is a real network round trip rather than a memory lookup — the price paid for every instance seeing the same data. Implementations: Redis (the common choice; Azure Managed Redis in the cloud), SQL Server, Cosmos DB, NCache, or an in-memory stand-in for dev and tests."},{"c":"caching-ratelimit-cors","s":"hybridcache-closing-the-gap-between-the-two-previous-section","h":"HybridCache: closing the gap between the two previous sections","t":"# HybridCache: closing the gap between the two previous sections Intermediate Look back at the last two sections and the tension should be obvious: IMemoryCache is fast but inconsistent across instances; IDistributedCache is consistent but pays a network hop on every single access , even for data that was just read a moment ago on the very same instance. HybridCache ( Microsoft.Extensions.Caching.Hybrid , .NET 9+) exists specifically to get both: a fast L1 in-memory cache in front of an optional L2 distributed cache, with serialization handled for you, plus two capabilities neither older API gives you on its own — stampede protection and tag-based invalidation , both covered in the next section. C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddStackExchangeRedisCache (o => o. Configuration = redisCs); // becomes L2 automatically builder. Services . AddHybridCache (o => o. DefaultEntryOptions = new HybridCacheEntryOptions { Expiration = TimeSpan . FromMinutes ( 10 ), // L2 lifetime LocalCacheExpiration = TimeSpan . FromMinutes ( 1 ) // L1 lifetime (shorter limits cross-instance staleness) }); public sealed class ProductQueries ( HybridCache cache, ShopDb db) { public ValueTask < ProductDto ?> GetAsync ( int id, CancellationToken ct) => cache. GetOrCreateAsync ( $\"product:{id}\" , async token => await db. Products . Where (p => p. Id == id) . Select (p => new ProductDto (p. Id , p. Name , p. Price )). FirstOrDefaultAsync (token), tags: [ \"products\" , $\"category:{id % 10}\" ], cancellationToken: ct); public async Task OnProductChangedAsync ( int id, CancellationToken ct) { await cache. RemoveAsync ( $\"product:{id}\" , ct); // or invalidate a whole group: await cache.RemoveByTagAsync(\"products\", ct); } } Notice the deliberate asymmetry in the two expirations above: LocalCacheExpiration (L1) is set shorter than Expiration (L2) specifically so that no instance's local copy can drift stale for longer than a minute, while the shared Redis copy still saves everyone the trip back to the database for a full ten minutes."},{"c":"caching-ratelimit-cors","s":"caching-strategies-and-invalidation-the-part-thats-actually-","h":"Caching strategies and invalidation: the part that's actually hard","t":"# Caching strategies and invalidation: the part that's actually hard Intermediate Strategy How Trade-off Cache-aside (lazy loading) Read: check cache → on miss load from DB and store. Write: update DB, then remove the cache entry Most common; first read after a change is a miss Read-through The cache library loads on miss (what GetOrCreate gives you) Same as cache-aside, encapsulated Write-through Write to cache and DB together Cache always fresh; slower writes Write-behind Write to cache; flush to DB asynchronously Fast writes; risk of data loss Refresh-ahead Refresh popular entries before they expire No miss latency; extra load TTL only Never invalidate; rely on expiry Simple; bounded staleness Invalidation itself comes in a few recognisable shapes: remove on write , right in the same code path that updates the database; events , where a ProductChanged message goes out and every service or instance evicts its own copy on hearing it; tags or versioned keys ( product:v7:42 ), where bumping a single version number invalidates an entire group at once; and short TTLs as a blunt but reliable safety net underneath whatever else you're doing. Delete, don't update, on writes Here's a subtle race worth seeing explicitly: after updating the database, remove the cache key rather than writing the freshly computed value directly into the cache. If two writers update concurrently, writing the new value directly can let the slower writer's update land in the cache after the faster one's, leaving the older value cached — permanently, until the TTL eventually clears it. Deleting the key instead forces the next reader to recompute from the database, which is guaranteed to see the latest committed state. # Cache stampede: what happens when a popular key expires Picture a hot key — say, the homepage's featured-products list — expiring at the exact moment traffic is heavy. Every one of the hundreds of concurrent requests that were relying on it misses simultaneously , and without any coordination, every single one of them goes to the database to recompute the same answer at once — the \"thundering herd.\" The defences: request coalescing , where only the first caller actually computes the value and every other concurrent caller simply awaits that same in-flight result ( HybridCache does this automatically; plain IMemoryCache.GetOrCreate does not , which is one of its real limitations); jittered expirations, so a batch of keys cached at the same moment don't all expire at the same moment too; refresh-ahead for the hottest keys specifically; and serving the stale value while a refresh happens in the background, rather than making anyone wait. # What to cache, and what not to Good candidates share a trait: reference data, product catalogues, computed aggregates, permission lookups, external API responses, rendered fragments — things that are read far more often than they change. Poor candidates share the opposite trait: trivial per-request lookups by primary key on an already-indexed table (often not worth the added complexity for the latency you'd save), rapidly changing data that needs strong consistency (stock levels at checkout, account balances — exactly the \"seconds, or no cache at all\" end of the staleness question from the opening section), and personal data dropped into a shared cache without careful per-user keys."},{"c":"caching-ratelimit-cors","s":"output-caching-caching-the-whole-http-response-server-side","h":"Output caching: caching the whole HTTP response, server-side","t":"# Output caching: caching the whole HTTP response, server-side Intermediate Where the layers above cache data , output caching caches entire responses , keyed by path and query string (and optionally headers or route values), with tag-based eviction built in: C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddOutputCache (o => { o. AddBasePolicy (b => b. Expire ( TimeSpan . FromSeconds ( 30 ))); o. AddPolicy ( \"Catalog\" , b => b. Expire ( TimeSpan . FromMinutes ( 5 )). SetVaryByQuery ( \"page\" , \"category\" ). Tag ( \"catalog\" )); }); // .AddStackExchangeRedisOutputCache(...) to share across instances app. UseOutputCache (); // after UseAuthorization / UseCors app. MapGet ( \"/catalog\" , GetCatalog ). CacheOutput ( \"Catalog\" ); app. MapPost ( \"/admin/products\" , async ( IOutputCacheStore store, CancellationToken ct) => { // ... update ... await store. EvictByTagAsync ( \"catalog\" , ct); // invalidate cached catalog pages }); A safe default is baked in here, worth knowing explicitly: output caching only caches anonymous GET/HEAD 200 responses that carry no cookies — a deliberate choice that stops it from ever accidentally serving one logged-in user's personalised response to someone else. Response caching ( UseResponseCaching ) is an older, different middleware that strictly follows HTTP Cache-Control semantics rather than giving you server-side policy control. Output caching gives you more control for server-side caching; use HTTP headers for the separate job of letting browsers and CDNs cache on the client side: C# Run ▶ Copy app. MapGet ( \"/assets/config\" , () => cfg). WithMetadata ( new ResponseCacheAttribute { Duration = 3600 , Location = ResponseCacheLocation . Any }); // or set headers directly: ctx.Response.Headers.CacheControl = \"public,max-age=3600\"; // ETags + If-None-Match → 304 Not Modified saves bandwidth for unchanged resources"},{"c":"caching-ratelimit-cors","s":"rate-limiting-protecting-the-api-from-its-own-popularity","h":"Rate limiting: protecting the API from its own popularity","t":"# Rate limiting: protecting the API from its own popularity Intermediate Rate limiting caps how many requests a given client can make in a given window — protecting the API from abuse, from a runaway client retrying too aggressively, from scraping, and from accidentally denial-of-servicing yourself, while also letting you enforce fair-use or pricing-tier limits. It's been built into the framework since .NET 7 ( Microsoft.AspNetCore.RateLimiting ). Algorithm How it works Good for Fixed window N requests per window (e.g. 100/minute); the counter resets each window Simple quotas; allows bursts at window edges Sliding window Window split into segments; smooths the edge burst Fairer per-minute limits Token bucket Bucket refills at a rate; each request takes a token; bucket size = burst Allowing short bursts with a steady average Concurrency Max N requests in flight at once Protecting expensive endpoints or downstream resources Worth noticing in that table: fixed window is the easiest to reason about but has a real weakness — a client can send 100 requests in the last second of one window and another 100 in the first second of the next, getting 200 requests in effectively two seconds. Sliding window and token bucket both exist specifically to smooth that edge case out. C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddRateLimiter (o => { o. RejectionStatusCode = StatusCodes . Status429TooManyRequests ; o. OnRejected = (ctx, _) => { if (ctx. Lease . TryGetMetadata ( MetadataName . RetryAfter , out var retry)) ctx. HttpContext . Response . Headers . RetryAfter = (( int )retry. TotalSeconds ). ToString (); return ValueTask . CompletedTask ; }; // Per-user (or per-IP for anonymous) token bucket o. AddPolicy ( \"per-user\" , http => RateLimitPartition . GetTokenBucketLimiter ( partitionKey: http. User . FindFirstValue ( \"sub\" ) ?? http. Connection . RemoteIpAddress ?. ToString () ?? \"anon\" , factory: _ => new TokenBucketRateLimiterOptions { TokenLimit = 100 , TokensPerPeriod = 20 , ReplenishmentPeriod = TimeSpan . FromSeconds ( 10 ), QueueLimit = 0 , AutoReplenishment = true })); o. AddConcurrencyLimiter ( \"reports\" , c => { c. PermitLimit = 4 ; c. QueueLimit = 10 ; }); // heavy endpoint o. GlobalLimiter = PartitionedRateLimiter . Create < HttpContext , string >(http => // safety net for everything RateLimitPartition . GetFixedWindowLimiter (http. Connection . RemoteIpAddress ?. ToString () ?? \"x\" , _ => new FixedWindowRateLimiterOptions { PermitLimit = 1000 , Window = TimeSpan . FromMinutes ( 1 ) })); }); app. UseRateLimiter (); // after UseAuthentication if partitioning by user app. MapGet ( \"/search\" , Search ). RequireRateLimiting ( \"per-user\" ); app. MapPost ( \"/reports\" , BuildReport ). RequireRateLimiting ( \"reports\" ); app. MapGet ( \"/health\" , () => \"ok\" ). DisableRateLimiting (); In-process limits are per instance This is the same scaling trap IMemoryCache fell into earlier in this chapter, wearing a different costume: the built-in limiter keeps its counters in memory , local to each instance. Run 5 instances behind a load balancer, and a limit you configured as \"100 requests per minute\" is, in effect, 500 requests per minute across the fleet, split however the load balancer happens to route traffic. For a genuinely strict global limit, enforce it at the gateway (Azure API Management, Front Door WAF rate rules, NGINX) or use a Redis-backed limiter that all instances share. And if you're behind a proxy, make sure forwarded headers are configured — exactly as covered in ASP.NET Core architecture — or partitioning \"by IP\" will partition by the proxy's IP, collapsing every real client into one bucket."},{"c":"caching-ratelimit-cors","s":"cors-a-rule-browsers-enforce-on-your-behalf-for-your-users-p","h":"CORS: a rule browsers enforce on your behalf, for your users' protection","t":"# CORS: a rule browsers enforce on your behalf, for your users' protection Beginner Browsers enforce the same-origin policy : JavaScript running on https://shop.com cannot read a response from https://api.shop.com — a different origin, since origin means scheme + host + port together — unless the API explicitly says it's allowed to , through a set of CORS response headers. Here's the detail that trips people up the first time they debug a \"CORS error\": CORS protects users' browsers, not your API. curl, Postman, and any server-to-server call ignore CORS completely — there's no browser involved to enforce it. Your API's actual protection against unauthorized access is authentication and authorization, covered in Auth ; CORS is purely about which websites a browser will let read your responses on a user's behalf. # Simple vs preflight requests Simple requests (GET/HEAD/POST with basic content types and no custom headers) go out directly; the browser only checks Access-Control-Allow-Origin on the response afterward, before letting the page's JavaScript actually read it. Preflighted requests (PUT/DELETE/PATCH, a Content-Type: application/json body, an Authorization header…) are different: the browser sends an OPTIONS request first, asking permission before the real request goes out at all: HTTP Copy OPTIONS /api/orders HTTP/ 1.1 Origin : https : //shop.com Access-Control-Request-Method : POST Access-Control-Request-Headers : authorization, content-type HTTP/ 1.1 204 No Content Access-Control-Allow-Origin : https : //shop.com Access-Control-Allow-Methods : GET, POST, PUT, DELETE Access-Control-Allow-Headers : authorization, content-type Access-Control-Max-Age : 600 C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddCors (o => { o. AddPolicy ( \"web\" , p => p . WithOrigins ( \"https://shop.com\" , \"https://admin.shop.com\" ) // explicit list from config . WithMethods ( \"GET\" , \"POST\" , \"PUT\" , \"DELETE\" ) . WithHeaders ( \"authorization\" , \"content-type\" ) . AllowCredentials () // only if you need cookies cross-origin . SetPreflightMaxAge ( TimeSpan . FromMinutes ( 10 ))); }); app. UseCors (); // AFTER UseRouting, BEFORE UseAuthentication/UseAuthorization app. MapGroup ( \"/api\" ). RequireCors ( \"web\" ); The classic CORS mistakes AllowAnyOrigin() combined with AllowCredentials() is invalid — browsers reject the combination outright, by design. And never reflect back whatever Origin header the request happened to carry, with credentials enabled; doing that effectively lets any site act as the logged-in user. Placing UseCors after authentication is the single most common ordering mistake here, and the exact scenario Middleware warned about generically: a preflight OPTIONS request carries no credentials at all, so if authentication runs first, it rejects the preflight with a 401 — which the browser then reports to the page simply as \"a CORS error,\" hiding the real cause. A plain 500 error page with no CORS headers attached also shows up in the browser console as a \"CORS error,\" even though CORS has nothing to do with it — the browser can't distinguish \"server refused CORS\" from \"server crashed before it could say anything.\" Check the actual server logs before assuming it's a CORS configuration problem."},{"c":"caching-ratelimit-cors","s":"response-compression-and-request-timeouts","h":"Response compression and request timeouts","t":"# Response compression and request timeouts Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddResponseCompression (o => { o. EnableForHttps = true ; // consider BREACH risk for responses that mix secrets with user input o. Providers . Add < BrotliCompressionProvider >(); o. Providers . Add < GzipCompressionProvider >(); }); app. UseResponseCompression (); builder. Services . AddRequestTimeouts (o => o. AddPolicy ( \"fast\" , TimeSpan . FromSeconds ( 2 ))); app. UseRequestTimeouts (); app. MapGet ( \"/quote\" , GetQuote ). WithRequestTimeout ( \"fast\" ); // RequestAborted token fires after 2 s Before enabling this in the app itself, check what's already sitting in front of it: a reverse proxy or CDN frequently compresses better and more cheaply than your app process can, since it's doing it once for many backend instances rather than redundantly in each one. Enable compression here specifically when nothing upstream already does it. Q What caching options does ASP.NET Core provide? IMemoryCache (in-process objects), IDistributedCache (shared byte cache backed by Redis, SQL, etc.), HybridCache (.NET 9: two-level memory plus distributed with stampede protection and tags), output caching (whole server-side responses with tag eviction), and HTTP response caching via Cache-Control/ETag headers for browsers and CDNs. Q In-memory vs distributed cache? In-memory is fastest but per instance (not shared, lost on restart, inconsistent across a farm). Distributed (Redis) is shared and survives app restarts, but costs a network hop and serialization. HybridCache combines both. Q What is the cache-aside pattern? Check the cache first; on a miss, load from the source and store it with an expiry; on writes, update the source and invalidate (remove) the cache entry. It's the most common caching pattern. Q What is a cache stampede and how do you prevent it? Many concurrent requests miss the same expired hot key and all recompute it, overloading the database. Prevent it with request coalescing (one computes, the rest wait, as HybridCache does), locks, jittered expirations, refresh-ahead, or serve-stale-while-revalidate. Q How do you invalidate cached data? Remove keys when the underlying data changes (in the write path or via change events), use tags or versioned keys to evict groups, and keep TTLs as a safety net. Prefer deleting over updating cache entries to avoid races. Q What rate limiting algorithms exist and how do you apply them in ASP.NET Core? Fixed window, sliding window, token bucket and concurrency. Register policies with AddRateLimiter (partitioned per user/IP/API key), add UseRateLimiter, and attach with RequireRateLimiting or [EnableRateLimiting]. Rejections return 429 with Retry-After. Counters are per instance, so enforce global limits at a gateway or with a distributed store. Q What is CORS and does it protect your API? A browser mechanism that relaxes the same-origin policy when the server returns Access-Control-Allow-* headers, letting JS from another origin read responses. It protects users' browsers from malicious sites, not your API, because non-browser clients ignore it. API protection comes from authentication and authorization. Q What is a preflight request? An automatic OPTIONS request the browser sends before \"non-simple\" cross-origin requests (custom headers like Authorization, JSON content type, PUT/DELETE) to ask which origins, methods and headers are allowed. The CORS middleware answers it; it must run before authentication. Q What's the difference between output caching and response caching? Response caching follows HTTP Cache-Control semantics and is mainly for client/proxy caching. Output caching is server-controlled: policies per endpoint, vary-by rules, tag-based eviction, storage in memory or Redis, independent of client headers, and safe defaults (anonymous GETs only)."},{"c":"caching-ratelimit-cors","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Caching overview HybridCache library Output caching middleware Rate limiting middleware Enable CORS in ASP.NET Core"},{"c":"caching-ratelimit-cors","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter The checks below hand-roll a minimal cache, token bucket and fixed-window limiter (no Redis, no AddRateLimiter , no HybridCache package) to verify the mechanics this chapter describes in isolation. CORS preflight behaviour needs a real browser making a real cross-origin HTTP request, so it isn't something a console program can verify — see the MDN/Microsoft Learn links above for that one. Save as Program.cs in a new console project ( dotnet new console ) and run with dotnet run . C# Run ▶ Copy // Verifies claims from this chapter: cache-aside should delete (not overwrite) a // stale entry to avoid a race, two IMemoryCache-like instances don't share state (the \"per instance\" // limitation), and a token bucket allows a burst up to its capacity, then throttles. // CORS preflight behaviour needs a real browser/HTTP round trip and isn't runtime-verifiable in a // console app — see Further reading (MDN / learn.microsoft.com CORS docs) for that one. using System ; using System . Collections . Generic ; using System . Threading ; int fails = 0 ; void Ok ( string s) => Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: {s}\" ); void Check ( bool cond, string s) { if (cond) Ok (s); else { Console . WriteLine ( $\"FAIL: {s}\" ); fails++; } } // --- Delete, don't overwrite, on writes: avoids a slow writer re-caching a stale value --- var cache = new SimpleCache (); cache. Set ( \"product:1\" , \"price=10\" ); // Two concurrent writers: writer A reads old price, writer B updates DB + cache first, A \"finishes\" after. // If writes overwrote the cache directly, A's stale write could land after B's and stick. cache. Remove ( \"product:1\" ); // B's write: delete the key instead of writing a new value directly var afterB = cache. TryGet ( \"product:1\" , out var staleOrMissing); Check (!afterB, \"Cache-aside: deleting the key on write means the next read is a guaranteed miss, never a stale value\" ); cache. Set ( \"product:1\" , \"price=12\" ); // next reader recomputes from the \"database\" and repopulates Check (cache. TryGet ( \"product:1\" , out var fresh) && fresh == \"price=12\" , \"Cache-aside: the next read after a delete repopulates with the fresh value\" ); // --- IMemoryCache limitation: two instances never share state --- var instanceA = new SimpleCache (); var instanceB = new SimpleCache (); instanceA. Set ( \"countries\" , \"cached-on-A\" ); Check (!instanceB. TryGet ( \"countries\" , out _), \"IMemoryCache-style cache: a second instance has no knowledge of the first instance's entries\" ); // --- Token bucket: allows a burst up to capacity, then throttles until tokens refill --- var bucket = new TokenBucket (capacity: 5 ); // no refill modelled, to test the burst limit deterministically int allowed = 0 ; for ( int i = 0 ; i < 8 ; i++) if (bucket. TryTake ()) allowed++; Check (allowed == 5 , \"Token bucket: exactly `capacity` requests are allowed in a burst (5 of 8 attempts succeeded)\" ); Check (!bucket. TryTake (), \"Token bucket: once empty, further requests are rejected until tokens refill\" ); // --- Fixed window: the counter resets at a window boundary, allowing a new burst --- var window = new FixedWindowLimiter (limit: 3 , window: TimeSpan . FromMilliseconds ( 50 )); int firstWindowAllowed = 0 ; for ( int i = 0 ; i < 5 ; i++) if (window. TryAcquire ()) firstWindowAllowed++; Thread . Sleep ( 60 ); // cross into the next window bool afterReset = window. TryAcquire (); Check (firstWindowAllowed == 3 , \"Fixed window: only `limit` requests are allowed within one window (3 of 5 attempts succeeded)\" ); Check (afterReset, \"Fixed window: the counter resets once the window elapses, allowing requests again\" ); Console . WriteLine (fails == 0 ? \"\\nAll checks passed.\" : $\"\\n{fails} check(s) failed.\" ); if (fails > 0 ) Environment . Exit ( 1 ); sealed class SimpleCache { private readonly Dictionary < string , string > _store = new (); public void Set ( string key, string value ) => _store[key] = value ; public void Remove ( string key) => _store. Remove (key); public bool T"},{"c":"background-work","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Work that doesn't need to finish before you reply should run outside the request, in a BackgroundService hosted by the same process. BackgroundService is a long-running loop the host starts at startup and stops at shutdown; because it's a singleton, create a fresh DI scope inside the loop for anything scoped, like a DbContext . Channel<T> hands work off to an in-process background worker fast, but loses everything if the process dies — use it only for work you can afford to lose or redo. For work that must survive a crash, retry automatically, or run once across several replicas, use a durable store: a job library (Hangfire, Quartz.NET) or a message broker. A Worker Service is the same host with background services but no web server — good for deploying queue consumers and schedulers separately from the API."},{"c":"background-work","s":"work-that-shouldnt-make-a-request-wait","h":"Work that shouldn't make a request wait","t":"# Work that shouldn't make a request wait Beginner A request should return quickly — that's a constraint, not a preference, because something's actually waiting on the other end of it. But not every piece of work an app needs to do fits inside that window, and some of it isn't triggered by a request at all: Kind Examples Deferred (triggered by a request, done later) Sending emails, generating PDFs/reports, resizing images, calling slow third parties, webhooks Scheduled / periodic Nightly cleanup, hourly sync with an ERP, refreshing caches, expiring carts Continuous Consuming a message queue, processing an outbox, streaming events The pattern for the first category is usually the same: the API responds 202 Accepted (plus a status URL the client can poll), and the actual work continues running after the response has already gone out."},{"c":"background-work","s":"ihostedservice-and-backgroundservice-the-hosts-slot-for-long","h":"IHostedService and BackgroundService: the host's slot for long-running code","t":"# IHostedService and BackgroundService: the host's slot for long-running code Beginner Recall from ASP.NET Core architecture that the host starts and stops a list of IHostedService instances alongside the web server itself — Kestrel is literally one of them. IHostedService is the plain contract behind that: C# Run ▶ Copy public interface IHostedService { Task StartAsync ( CancellationToken cancellationToken); // called at startup Task StopAsync ( CancellationToken cancellationToken); // called at graceful shutdown } Implementing that interface directly is rare in practice, because almost every real background job has the same shape: start a loop, keep it running for the app's entire lifetime, stop cleanly when asked. BackgroundService is the abstract base class that captures exactly that shape, so you only have to write the loop itself: C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class ExchangeRateRefresher ( IServiceScopeFactory scopes, ILogger < ExchangeRateRefresher > log) : BackgroundService { protected override async Task ExecuteAsync ( CancellationToken stoppingToken) { using var timer = new PeriodicTimer ( TimeSpan . FromMinutes ( 15 )); do { try { await using var scope = scopes. CreateAsyncScope (); // scoped services need a scope var svc = scope. ServiceProvider . GetRequiredService < IRateService >(); await svc. RefreshAsync (stoppingToken); log. LogInformation ( \"Exchange rates refreshed\" ); } catch ( OperationCanceledException ) when (stoppingToken. IsCancellationRequested ) { break ; } catch ( Exception ex) { log. LogError (ex, \"Refreshing exchange rates failed; will retry next tick\" ); // don't let one failure kill the loop } } while ( await timer. WaitForNextTickAsync (stoppingToken)); } } builder. Services . AddHostedService < ExchangeRateRefresher >(); Notice the scopes.CreateAsyncScope() call right at the top of the loop body, not outside it. The next section explains exactly why that placement is load-bearing."},{"c":"background-work","s":"the-lifecycle-and-the-pitfalls-each-fact-in-it-quietly-creat","h":"The lifecycle, and the pitfalls each fact in it quietly creates","t":"# The lifecycle, and the pitfalls each fact in it quietly creates Intermediate Fact Consequence Hosted services are singletons Use IServiceScopeFactory for scoped services (DbContext); never inject them directly (captive dependency) StartAsync of each service is awaited in registration order before the app starts serving Don't do long work in StartAsync . BackgroundService.StartAsync returns as soon as ExecuteAsync hits its first real await , so synchronous work at the top of ExecuteAsync blocks startup — add await Task.Yield() first if that's a risk Unhandled exception in ExecuteAsync Since .NET 6 the host stops ( BackgroundServiceExceptionBehavior.StopHost ). Catch and log inside the loop stoppingToken is cancelled on shutdown Pass it everywhere; finish or abandon the current item within the ShutdownTimeout Every app instance runs its hosted services With 3 replicas, the \"nightly job\" runs 3 times . You need a distributed lock, a leader election, a single dedicated worker, or a job scheduler In-memory work is lost when the process dies Anything that must not be lost needs a durable store (DB, queue) The first row is the one mistake almost everyone makes once: a BackgroundService is built exactly once, at startup, and that same instance lives for the whole lifetime of the process — a singleton, in the same sense Dependency injection warned about. If you injected a scoped DbContext straight into its constructor, that one DbContext would be stuck there forever, slowly going stale and failing in ways that are hard to connect back to the real cause. The fix is to open a new scope inside the loop , once per iteration: each tick gets its own short-lived DbContext , used once and thrown away — the same lifetime a real HTTP request would give it. C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . Configure < HostOptions >(o => { o. ServicesStartConcurrently = true ; // .NET 8: start hosted services in parallel o. BackgroundServiceExceptionBehavior = BackgroundServiceExceptionBehavior . Ignore ; // only if you really mean it o. ShutdownTimeout = TimeSpan . FromSeconds ( 30 ); }); IHostedLifecycleService (.NET 8) adds four finer-grained hooks — StartingAsync , StartedAsync , StoppingAsync and StoppedAsync — for when you need to run code at a more precise point in the sequence than plain StartAsync / StopAsync allow."},{"c":"background-work","s":"worker-services-the-same-host-deliberately-without-a-web-ser","h":"Worker services: the same host, deliberately without a web server","t":"# Worker services: the same host, deliberately without a web server Beginner Everything above assumed a web app that also runs background work alongside its HTTP server. A Worker Service flips that: it's the same Generic Host, with background services, but no web server at all — the natural shape for queue consumers, schedulers, and integration processes you want to deploy and scale completely separately from the API: C# Run ▶ Copy // dotnet new worker -n Shop.Worker var builder = Host . CreateApplicationBuilder (args); builder. Services . AddDbContext < ShopDb >(o => o. UseSqlServer (builder. Configuration . GetConnectionString ( \"Shop\" ))); builder. Services . AddHostedService < OrderEventsConsumer >(); builder. Services . AddHostedService < OutboxPublisher >(); builder. Build (). Run (); It gets exactly the same configuration, logging, DI and graceful shutdown as a web app — none of that plumbing is actually specific to having a web server — and runs as a container, a Windows Service ( UseWindowsService() ), or a systemd unit ( UseSystemd() ). API + worker A common deployable split: Shop.Api (handles HTTP, writes to the DB and an outbox — a table that stores events to publish, written in the same database transaction as the business change so the two can't disagree; see Messaging & events ) and Shop.Worker (consumes queues, publishes the outbox, runs schedules). Both reference the same Application/Domain/Infrastructure projects. They scale independently, and a slow job never steals threads from HTTP requests."},{"c":"background-work","s":"in-memory-queues-with-channel-handing-work-off-within-one-pr","h":"In-memory queues with Channel : handing work off within one process","t":"# In-memory queues with Channel : handing work off within one process Intermediate Sometimes the work doesn't need to leave the process at all — it just needs to leave the request . For that, System.Threading.Channels gives you a fast, async-native producer/consumer queue: C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed record ThumbnailJob ( Guid ImageId , string BlobPath ); public sealed class ThumbnailQueue { private readonly Channel < ThumbnailJob > _channel = Channel . CreateBounded < ThumbnailJob >( new BoundedChannelOptions ( 500 ) { FullMode = BoundedChannelFullMode . Wait , // backpressure: producers wait when full SingleReader = false }); public ValueTask EnqueueAsync ( ThumbnailJob job, CancellationToken ct) => _channel. Writer . WriteAsync (job, ct); public IAsyncEnumerable < ThumbnailJob > ReadAllAsync ( CancellationToken ct) => _channel. Reader . ReadAllAsync (ct); } public sealed class ThumbnailWorker ( ThumbnailQueue queue, IServiceScopeFactory scopes, ILogger < ThumbnailWorker > log) : BackgroundService { protected override Task ExecuteAsync ( CancellationToken stoppingToken) => Parallel . ForEachAsync (queue. ReadAllAsync (stoppingToken), new ParallelOptions { MaxDegreeOfParallelism = 4 , CancellationToken = stoppingToken }, async (job, ct) => { try { await using var scope = scopes. CreateAsyncScope (); await scope. ServiceProvider . GetRequiredService < IThumbnailer >(). CreateAsync (job, ct); } catch ( Exception ex) { log. LogError (ex, \"Thumbnail failed for {ImageId}\" , job. ImageId ); } }); } builder. Services . AddSingleton < ThumbnailQueue >(); builder. Services . AddHostedService < ThumbnailWorker >(); app. MapPost ( \"/images\" , async ( IFormFile file , IBlobStore blobs, ThumbnailQueue queue, CancellationToken ct) => { var (id, path) = await blobs. SaveAsync ( file , ct); await queue. EnqueueAsync ( new ThumbnailJob (id, path), ct); return Results . Accepted ( $\"/images/{id}\" ); }); Bounded vs unbounded is a real decision, not a style choice. A bounded channel gives you backpressure : once it's full, EnqueueAsync just waits until a consumer frees a slot, so producers are forced to slow down to the speed consumers can actually keep up with. An unbounded channel is easier to write, but risky under load: nothing stops it growing forever if producers outpace consumers — right up until the process runs out of memory. In-memory means losable Here's the fact this whole section has been building toward: a deploy, a crash, or a scale-in event loses everything currently sitting in a Channel<T> , instantly and without a trace. Use it only for work you can genuinely afford to lose or simply recompute — thumbnails you can regenerate, cache warm-ups that will just happen again next time. For emails, payments, or anything business-critical, persist first — a database table, an outbox, or a message broker — and only then process; never let the only copy of important work exist purely in process memory."},{"c":"background-work","s":"durable-job-systems-hangfire-and-quartz-net","h":"Durable job systems: Hangfire and Quartz.NET","t":"# Durable job systems: Hangfire and Quartz.NET Intermediate Channel<T> solves \"hand work off within this process.\" It deliberately doesn't solve \"survive this process dying,\" \"retry automatically,\" \"run exactly once across many replicas,\" or \"run on a cron schedule\" — for those, you want a job library backed by a real database: Hangfire Quartz.NET Model Fire-and-forget, delayed, recurring (cron), continuations Jobs + triggers (cron, calendars), clustering Storage SQL Server, PostgreSQL, Redis… ADO.NET job store (any major DB) Dashboard Built-in web dashboard (retries, failures, history) Third-party UIs Distributed Yes: workers on any instance pull jobs from storage Yes: clustered mode with DB locks Retries Automatic with backoff Configurable C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddHangfire (c => c. UseSqlServerStorage (builder. Configuration . GetConnectionString ( \"Jobs\" ))); builder. Services . AddHangfireServer (o => o. WorkerCount = 10 ); app. MapHangfireDashboard ( \"/jobs\" ). RequireAuthorization ( \"Admin\" ); app. MapPost ( \"/orders/{id}/invoice\" , ( int id, IBackgroundJobClient jobs) => { jobs. Enqueue < IInvoiceService >(s => s. GenerateAndEmailAsync (id, CancellationToken . None )); // persisted, retried return Results . Accepted (); }); RecurringJob . AddOrUpdate < ICartCleanup >( \"cart-cleanup\" , s => s. RunAsync ( CancellationToken . None ), Cron . Daily ( 2 )); // 02:00 daily, ONCE across the cluster Jobs here should be idempotent — a retry after a partial failure must not send two invoices for the one order. That constraint comes straight from the \"automatic retries with backoff\" row above: if retries are automatic, a job will eventually run more than once for the same logical unit of work, whether or not the first attempt actually finished."},{"c":"background-work","s":"choosing-where-background-work-runs","h":"Choosing where background work runs","t":"# Choosing where background work runs Intermediate Diagram Copy Can the work be lost on restart? ── yes ──▶ Channel<T> + BackgroundService (same process) │ no Is it within one service? ── yes ──▶ Hangfire / Quartz (DB-backed), or an outbox table + worker │ no (other services must react) ───────▶ Message broker (Service Bus / RabbitMQ) + consumers Spiky / event-triggered / scale to zero? ──────▶ Azure Functions (queue/timer/blob triggers) or Container Apps jobs Simple periodic job, single instance? ────────▶ BackgroundService + PeriodicTimer (+ distributed lock if replicated)"},{"c":"background-work","s":"running-a-job-once-across-replicas","h":"Running a job once across replicas","t":"# Running a job once across replicas Advanced The \"every app instance runs its hosted services\" row from the lifecycle table earlier has a direct consequence worth solving properly: with several replicas each independently running the same BackgroundService , a \"nightly job\" literally runs once per replica, not once total. The available fixes: Distributed lock : acquire a lease before running (SQL sp_getapplock , Redis SET NX PX , Azure Blob lease, the DistributedLock NuGet package); any instance that fails to acquire it simply skips this run. Dedicated single-replica worker deployment for schedulers — sidestep the problem by never having more than one instance of the thing that schedules. Job scheduler with clustering (Hangfire, Quartz) — the library itself coordinates across instances so only one actually executes. Platform scheduler : Kubernetes CronJob, Container Apps jobs, Azure Functions timer trigger (singleton by default) — let the orchestrator guarantee the single run instead of building that guarantee yourself. Competing consumers on a queue : for continuous work rather than scheduled work, each message is delivered to only one instance anyway, so this category of problem doesn't even arise. C# Run ▶ Copy await using var handle = await distributedLock. TryAcquireAsync ( \"nightly-billing\" , TimeSpan . Zero , stoppingToken); if (handle is null ) return ; // another instance is running it await billing. RunAsync (stoppingToken); Q What is the difference between IHostedService and BackgroundService? IHostedService is the interface the host calls (StartAsync/StopAsync). BackgroundService is an abstract implementation that starts your ExecuteAsync loop on StartAsync and cancels its stoppingToken on StopAsync, so you implement one long-running method. Q How do you use a DbContext inside a BackgroundService? Hosted services are singletons, so inject IServiceScopeFactory and create a scope per unit of work (CreateAsyncScope), resolving DbContext from scope.ServiceProvider and disposing the scope afterwards. Or inject IDbContextFactory<T>. Q What happens if a BackgroundService throws an unhandled exception? Since .NET 6 the default BackgroundServiceExceptionBehavior is StopHost, so the whole application shuts down. Catch exceptions inside the loop, log them and continue (or deliberately fail). Q How do you run periodic work? A BackgroundService with PeriodicTimer and WaitForNextTickAsync(stoppingToken) for simple cases. For cron schedules, persistence, retries and single execution across a cluster, use Hangfire or Quartz.NET, or a platform scheduler (Functions timer trigger, Kubernetes CronJob). Q What is Channel and when would you use it? An async producer/consumer queue in System.Threading.Channels. Use it to hand off work from requests to an in-process background consumer with backpressure (bounded channels). It's in-memory only, so items are lost on restart; use durable queues for critical work. Q Your app runs on 3 instances: how do you make sure a scheduled job runs only once? Use a distributed lock or lease (SQL app lock, Redis, Blob lease), a job scheduler with clustering (Hangfire/Quartz), a dedicated single-instance worker, or a platform scheduler like a Functions timer trigger or Kubernetes CronJob. Q Why is fire-and-forget (Task.Run without await) in a controller a bad idea? The work isn't tracked by the host (lost on shutdown), exceptions go unobserved, and it often uses request-scoped services (DbContext, HttpContext) that are disposed when the request ends. Use a queue plus a hosted service or a durable job system."},{"c":"background-work","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Background tasks with hosted services Worker services in .NET System.Threading.Channels Hangfire documentation"},{"c":"background-work","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run (plain console app, .NET 8/9, top-level statements — System.Threading.Channels ships in the shared framework, no extra package needed). C# Run ▶ Copy using System . Threading . Channels ; // Claim: a bounded Channel<T> gives real backpressure — once full, a write has to wait // (or fails immediately with TryWrite) instead of growing without limit. var bounded = Channel . CreateBounded < int >( new BoundedChannelOptions ( 2 ) { FullMode = BoundedChannelFullMode . Wait }); if (!bounded. Writer . TryWrite ( 1 )) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected first write to succeed\" ); if (!bounded. Writer . TryWrite ( 2 )) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected second write to succeed (capacity 2)\" ); if (bounded. Writer . TryWrite ( 3 )) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected third TryWrite to fail — channel should be full\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: bounded Channel<T> rejects writes once full (real backpressure)\" ); var writeTask = bounded. Writer . WriteAsync ( 3 ). AsTask (); await Task . Delay ( 50 ); if (writeTask. IsCompleted ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: WriteAsync should still be waiting while the channel is full\" ); await bounded. Reader . ReadAsync (); // frees one slot await writeTask; // now it can complete Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: a blocked WriteAsync on a full channel completes once the consumer reads (producer slows down to match the consumer)\" ); // Claim: PeriodicTimer.WaitForNextTickAsync asynchronously waits for each tick. using var timer = new PeriodicTimer ( TimeSpan . FromMilliseconds ( 20 )); var ticked = await timer. WaitForNextTickAsync (); if (!ticked) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected a tick\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: PeriodicTimer.WaitForNextTickAsync delivers ticks without blocking a thread\" ); // Claim: retried jobs must be idempotent — a retry after a partial failure must not double-apply. var appliedInvoiceIds = new HashSet < int >(); int sideEffectCount = 0 ; void ApplyInvoiceIdempotently ( int orderId) { if (!appliedInvoiceIds. Add (orderId)) return ; // already applied: skip, this is what \"idempotent\" buys you sideEffectCount++; } ApplyInvoiceIdempotently ( 42 ); // first attempt ApplyInvoiceIdempotently ( 42 ); // retry after the caller never saw the first attempt's result if (sideEffectCount != 1 ) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected exactly 1 side effect, got {sideEffectCount}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: an idempotent handler applies a retried job's effect exactly once\" ); // Not runtime-verifiable here — these need a real multi-instance deployment or a real crash: // - \"every app instance runs its own hosted services\" (needs more than one process) // - BackgroundServiceExceptionBehavior.StopHost shutting down the whole app (needs a real Generic Host) // - in-memory channel contents being lost on crash/restart // See Further reading: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/fundamentals/host/hosted-services"},{"c":"api-design","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR REST = resources (nouns in URLs) manipulated with standard HTTP verbs, stateless, cacheable. Safe = doesn't change state (GET); idempotent = same effect no matter how many times you call it (GET, PUT, DELETE). This is what makes retries safe. Know the status codes: 200/201/202/204 for success, 400/401/403/404/409/412/422/429 for client problems, 500/502/503/504 for server/upstream problems. Page large lists with cursor/keyset pagination, not offset, once the table is big — offset gets slower the deeper you page. POST isn't idempotent by default; fix that for payments/writes with an idempotency key . Fix \"last write wins\" conflicts with ETags (optimistic concurrency). Version only when a change would break an existing client's assumptions; additive, optional changes usually don't need a new version."},{"c":"api-design","s":"rest-in-one-page","h":"REST in one page","t":"# REST in one page Beginner REST (Representational State Transfer) is an architectural style for HTTP APIs, built around a small set of ideas: Resources are the nouns, identified by URLs: /orders/42 , /customers/7/addresses . Standard methods are the verbs: GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE. Representations carry state: JSON (usually), negotiated with Accept / Content-Type . Stateless : each request carries everything needed (auth token, parameters); the server keeps no client session. Cacheable : responses say whether and how long they can be cached. Uniform interface and, strictly, HATEOAS (responses include links to next actions), which few real APIs implement fully. The Richardson Maturity Model gives a way to measure how far along this spectrum an API actually sits: Level 0 (one endpoint, RPC over POST), Level 1 (resources), Level 2 ( HTTP verbs + status codes , where most good APIs sit), Level 3 (hypermedia). Most of this chapter is about getting Level 2 genuinely right — which turns out to be harder than it sounds, because the HTTP verbs carry real semantic promises that are easy to accidentally break."},{"c":"api-design","s":"http-methods-the-promises-hiding-behind-safe-and-idempotent","h":"HTTP methods: the promises hiding behind \"safe\" and \"idempotent\"","t":"# HTTP methods: the promises hiding behind \"safe\" and \"idempotent\" Beginner Method Purpose Request body Safe Idempotent Typical success GET Read a resource/collection No ✔ ✔ 200 HEAD GET without the body No ✔ ✔ 200 POST Create a child resource / trigger an action Yes ✘ ✘ 201 + Location , or 202, 200 PUT Replace a resource entirely (or create at a known URL) Yes ✘ ✔ 200 / 204 (201 if created) PATCH Partial update Yes ✘ ✘ (can be made so) 200 / 204 DELETE Remove Usually no ✘ ✔ 204 (or 404/204 on repeat) OPTIONS Capabilities / CORS preflight No ✔ ✔ 204 Two columns in that table carry more weight than they look. Safe means the method doesn't change server state at all — which is exactly why browsers, crawlers, and CDN prefetchers feel free to call GET requests speculatively, without asking: nothing bad can happen if GET runs twice, or zero times, or in a different order than you expected. Idempotent is a related but distinct promise: making the same request N times has the same effect on the server as making it once — though the response itself may legitimately differ, since a second DELETE on an already-deleted resource might reasonably return 404 instead of 204. Here's why this distinction earns its own row in the table rather than being a footnote: idempotency is exactly what makes retries safe . A client, a proxy, or a resilience policy can automatically retry an idempotent request after a timeout without worrying about double effects — which is precisely why POST , being neither safe nor idempotent by default, needs the special handling covered later in this chapter under idempotency keys."},{"c":"api-design","s":"status-codes-you-must-know","h":"Status codes you must know","t":"# Status codes you must know Beginner Code Name When 200 OK Successful GET/PUT/PATCH with a body 201 Created POST created something; include a Location header and usually the resource 202 Accepted Work queued for async processing; return a status URL 204 No Content Success with no body (DELETE, PUT/PATCH without returning the resource) 301/308 Moved permanently Resource URL changed (308 keeps the method) 304 Not Modified Conditional GET: client's cached copy (ETag) is still valid 400 Bad Request Malformed request or validation errors (ValidationProblemDetails) 401 Unauthorized Not authenticated : missing/invalid/expired token 403 Forbidden Authenticated but not allowed 404 Not Found Resource doesn't exist (or you're hiding it from this caller) 405 Method Not Allowed e.g. DELETE on a read-only resource 406 / 415 Not Acceptable / Unsupported Media Type Content negotiation failures 409 Conflict State conflict: duplicate, a business rule (\"already shipped\"), a concurrency clash 412 Precondition Failed If-Match ETag didn't match (optimistic concurrency) 422 Unprocessable Content Well-formed but semantically invalid (some teams use this for validation instead of 400) 429 Too Many Requests Rate limited; include Retry-After 500 Internal Server Error Unhandled server bug 502 / 503 / 504 Bad Gateway / Service Unavailable / Gateway Timeout Upstream failures, overload/maintenance (+ Retry-After ), upstream timeout Return errors as ProblemDetails ( Errors & logging ). Whichever you pick between 400 and 422 for validation, the important thing is picking one convention and applying it consistently across the whole API — a client building retry logic against your API needs to know, once, which code means \"don't bother retrying this without changing the request.\""},{"c":"api-design","s":"resource-and-url-design","h":"Resource and URL design","t":"# Resource and URL design Beginner Diagram Copy ✔ GET /orders list (filter/sort/page with query params) ✔ GET /orders/42 one order ✔ POST /orders create ✔ PATCH /orders/42 partial update ✔ DELETE /orders/42 delete ✔ GET /customers/7/orders sub-collection (one level of nesting) ✔ POST /orders/42/cancellation an action modelled as a resource (\"create a cancellation\") ✔ POST /orders/42:cancel Google-style custom method (also acceptable) ✘ GET /getOrders?id=42 verbs in URLs ✘ POST /orders/42/delete using POST for everything ✘ GET /customers/7/orders/42/lines/3/product deep nesting: use /order-lines/3 or /products/{id} The last two \"wrong\" examples share a root cause worth naming: both are reaching for a verb-shaped URL or an ever-deepening path because the method vocabulary from the previous section felt too limited for the action in mind. The fix each time is the same: model the action itself as a resource — a cancellation you POST into existence — rather than smuggling a verb into the URL. Conventions worth adopting as defaults: plural nouns , lowercase kebab-case paths ( /order-lines ), camelCase JSON, IDs that are opaque to clients (GUIDs or numbers, never anything a client could infer meaning from), ISO 8601 UTC timestamps ( 2026-10-27T14:00:00Z ), money as { \"amount\": \"19.99\", \"currency\": \"EUR\" } (decimal as a string or a minor-unit integer, never a floating-point number), and enums as strings rather than raw integers that mean nothing without the source code."},{"c":"api-design","s":"pagination-filtering-and-sorting-never-return-an-unbounded-l","h":"Pagination, filtering and sorting: never return an unbounded list","t":"# Pagination, filtering and sorting: never return an unbounded list Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed record PageRequest ( int Page = 1 , int PageSize = 20 ) { public int Size => Math . Clamp ( PageSize , 1 , 100 ); // cap the page size server-side } public sealed record PagedResult < T >( IReadOnlyList < T > Items , int Page , int PageSize , int TotalCount ) { public int TotalPages => ( int ) Math . Ceiling ( TotalCount / ( double ) PageSize ); } There are two genuinely different strategies for paging through a collection, and the choice between them isn't cosmetic — it changes how the database query performs as the dataset grows: Offset / page Cursor / keyset Request ?page=3&pageSize=20 ?limit=20&after=eyJpZCI6MTA0Mn0 SQL OFFSET 40 ROWS FETCH NEXT 20 WHERE Id > @lastId ORDER BY Id FETCH NEXT 20 Jump to page N ✔ ✘ Performance on deep pages Degrades: the DB still scans skipped rows Constant: uses the index Stable when data changes ✘ Items shift between pages ✔ Use for Admin grids, small datasets Feeds, infinite scroll, large tables, sync APIs Trace through why offset pagination degrades: OFFSET 40 doesn't mean \"jump straight to row 41\" — the database still has to walk through and discard the first 40 rows before it can start returning anything, so page 1,000 of a million-row table does real, scanning work for every single request, no matter how good the index is. Keyset pagination sidesteps this entirely by asking a different question — \"give me rows after this specific key\" — which the index answers directly, in roughly constant time regardless of how deep you are. C# Run ▶ Copy app. MapGet ( \"/products\" , async ( [AsParameters] ProductQuery q, ShopDb db, CancellationToken ct) => { var query = db. Products . AsNoTracking (); if (q. CategoryId is { } cat) query = query. Where (p => p. CategoryId == cat); // filtering if (! string . IsNullOrWhiteSpace (q. Search )) query = query. Where (p => p. Name . Contains (q. Search )); query = q. Sort switch // whitelisted sorting { \"price\" => query. OrderBy (p => p. Price ). ThenBy (p => p. Id ), \"-price\" => query. OrderByDescending (p => p. Price ). ThenBy (p => p. Id ), _ => query. OrderBy (p => p. Name ). ThenBy (p => p. Id ) // deterministic tie-breaker }; var total = await query. CountAsync (ct); var items = await query. Skip ((q. Page - 1 ) * q. Size ). Take (q. Size ) . Select (p => new ProductDto (p. Id , p. Name , p. Price )). ToListAsync (ct); return TypedResults . Ok ( new PagedResult < ProductDto >(items, q. Page , q. Size , total)); }); Never sort or filter by raw client strings Building an OrderBy clause directly from an arbitrary field name a client sent — via dynamic LINQ or raw SQL string concatenation — opens the door to injection, and separately, to a client silently sorting by some unindexed column and making the query scan the entire table. Whitelist which fields are sortable and filterable (notice the switch above only recognises two literal strings, with everything else falling through to a safe default), cap page sizes server-side, and always add a unique tie-breaker ( ThenBy(Id) ) — without one, rows that tie on the sort column can appear in a different relative order on different pages, silently duplicating or skipping rows as a client pages through."},{"c":"api-design","s":"updates-put-vs-patch","h":"Updates: PUT vs PATCH","t":"# Updates: PUT vs PATCH Intermediate PUT replaces the whole representation. Omitted fields are reset, so clients must send everything — which is exactly what makes it idempotent: sending the same full representation twice leaves the resource in the same state both times. PATCH changes only what's sent, and comes in two standard shapes: JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7396, application/merge-patch+json ): send a partial object, null means remove. Simple and common. JSON Patch (RFC 6902, application/json-patch+json ): a list of operations ( [{ \"op\": \"replace\", \"path\": \"/status\", \"value\": \"Paid\" }] ). Precise, supports array operations, more complex. Many APIs sidestep generic PATCH for business operations entirely, preferring task-based endpoints instead ( POST /orders/42/cancellation , POST /orders/42/shipments ). The reason connects back to the URL-design section above: \"set status to Cancelled\" via PATCH describes a data mutation , while \"create a cancellation\" via POST describes a business action — and the second maps far more naturally onto how a domain model (see DDD ) actually thinks about what's happening."},{"c":"api-design","s":"idempotency-keys-for-post-making-the-one-unsafe-by-default-m","h":"Idempotency keys for POST: making the one unsafe-by-default method safe to retry","t":"# Idempotency keys for POST: making the one unsafe-by-default method safe to retry Advanced POST isn't idempotent, and that has a sharp real-world consequence: a client that sends a payment request, times out waiting for a response, and retries — reasonably, since it has no way to know whether the first attempt succeeded — may end up charging the customer twice . The standard fix, used by Stripe and most serious payment APIs, is an idempotency key : a client-generated identifier that makes the second identical request a no-op rather than a second charge. HTTP Copy POST /payments Idempotency-Key : 4f9d2c1e-8b6a-4c51-9a77-1c2e3d4f5a6b C# Run ▶ Copy app. MapPost ( \"/payments\" , async ( [FromHeader(Name = \"Idempotency-Key\")] Guid key, PaymentRequest req, IIdempotencyStore store, IPayments payments, CancellationToken ct) => { if ( await store. TryGetAsync (key, ct) is { } previous) return Results . Json (previous. Body , statusCode: previous. Status ); // replay the original response if (! await store. TryBeginAsync (key, requestHash: req. GetHashCode (), ct)) // unique constraint = one winner return Results . Conflict ( new ProblemDetails { Title = \"Request with this key is in progress\" }); var result = await payments. ChargeAsync (req, ct); await store. CompleteAsync (key, 201 , result, ct); // store response for ~24 h return Results . Created ( $\"/payments/{result.Id}\" , result); }); Three details in that code are each guarding against a specific failure mode: storing keys with a TTL stops the store growing forever; detecting reuse of the same key with a different payload (returning 422) catches a client bug where a key got reused for an unrelated request; and making the \"begin\" step atomic via a unique index — rather than a check-then-insert that two concurrent requests could both pass — is what actually guarantees only one charge happens even if the client somehow fires the retry before the first attempt has finished."},{"c":"api-design","s":"optimistic-concurrency-with-etags","h":"Optimistic concurrency with ETags","t":"# Optimistic concurrency with ETags Advanced A different concurrency problem: two different clients editing the same resource, where whichever one saves last silently overwrites the other's changes — a lost update . ETags solve this by making \"has this changed since I last read it?\" a question the server can answer cheaply: Diagram Copy GET /orders/42 → 200, ETag: \"v7\" PUT /orders/42 If-Match: \"v7\" → 200, ETag: \"v8\" (nobody else changed it) PUT /orders/42 If-Match: \"v7\" → 412 Precondition Failed (someone saved v8 in between: reload and retry) Walk through what's actually happening: the client's GET captured the resource's version at that moment. Its later PUT says, in effect, \"apply this change, but only if the version is still what I last saw\" — and if someone else saved in between, the version has moved on to v8 , the precondition fails, and the server refuses the write rather than silently clobbering the intervening change. This maps directly onto EF Core's rowversion concurrency token, covered in EF Core advanced — the ETag is really just that token, surfaced over HTTP. As a bonus, the same mechanism works for reads too: a GET with If-None-Match: \"v8\" returns a bodyless 304 Not Modified when nothing has changed, saving bandwidth on every request where the client's cached copy is still current."},{"c":"api-design","s":"long-running-operations","h":"Long-running operations","t":"# Long-running operations Advanced Work that genuinely takes longer than a reasonable HTTP request — more than a few seconds — shouldn't hold the connection open waiting. The pattern mirrors the \"defer and poll\" idea from Background work : Diagram Copy POST /reports → 202 Accepted Location: /reports/operations/abc123 Retry-After: 5 GET /reports/operations/abc123 → 200 { \"status\": \"running\", \"progress\": 40 } GET /reports/operations/abc123 → 303 See Other Location: /reports/987 (or 200 { status: \"succeeded\", resultUrl }) Where polling itself is too chatty or too slow to notice completion, the alternatives are: webhooks (the server calls back to a URL the client provided), SignalR or Server-Sent Events for genuinely live progress updates, or simply having the client poll a resource's own status field rather than a dedicated operation resource."},{"c":"api-design","s":"versioning-deciding-what-counts-as-a-promise-you-cant-break","h":"Versioning: deciding what counts as a promise you can't break","t":"# Versioning: deciding what counts as a promise you can't break Intermediate The question to ask before anything else here: does this change break an existing client's reasonable assumptions? Removing or renaming a field or endpoint does. Changing a field's type or meaning does. Making a previously optional input required does. Changing an error code a client might be branching on does. Adding a new optional field or a new endpoint does not break anything — provided clients are built to ignore fields they don't recognise, which is worth stating explicitly in your API's own documentation so clients actually do it. Strategy Example Pros Cons URL segment /v2/orders Visible, easy to route, cache-friendly, most common URL changes per version Query string /orders?api-version=2.0 Simple; Azure APIs use it Easy to forget; caching nuances Header Api-Version: 2 Clean URLs Less discoverable; harder to test in a browser Media type Accept: application/vnd.shop.v2+json Most \"RESTful\" Complex for clients C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddApiVersioning (o => { o. DefaultApiVersion = new ApiVersion ( 1 , 0 ); o. AssumeDefaultVersionWhenUnspecified = true ; o. ReportApiVersions = true ; // api-supported-versions response header o. ApiVersionReader = new UrlSegmentApiVersionReader (); }). AddApiExplorer (o => { o. GroupNameFormat = \"'v'VVV\" ; o. SubstituteApiVersionInUrl = true ; }); // Asp.Versioning.Http var orders = app. NewVersionedApi ( \"Orders\" ); var v1 = orders. MapGroup ( \"/v{version:apiVersion}/orders\" ). HasApiVersion ( 1.0 ); var v2 = orders. MapGroup ( \"/v{version:apiVersion}/orders\" ). HasApiVersion ( 2.0 ); v1. MapGet ( \"/{id:int}\" , GetOrderV1 ); v2. MapGet ( \"/{id:int}\" , GetOrderV2 ); // new response shape Whichever breaking change eventually forces a new version, have a deprecation policy ready before you need it: announce the deprecation, add Deprecation / Sunset response headers so clients (and their tooling) can detect it programmatically, monitor actual traffic per version so you know when it's genuinely safe to remove the old one, and only then remove it."},{"c":"api-design","s":"openapi-documentation","h":"OpenAPI documentation","t":"# OpenAPI documentation Beginner OpenAPI (formerly Swagger) is a machine-readable description of your API — endpoints, parameters, schemas, responses, and auth requirements, all in one document. Once that description exists, a surprising amount becomes possible almost for free: interactive docs, client SDK generation (Kiota, NSwag), contract testing, gateway import (Azure API Management), and automated breaking-change detection in CI by diffing one version's spec against the next. C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddOpenApi (o => // Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi (.NET 9+), built in { o. AddDocumentTransformer ((doc, ctx, ct) => { doc. Info . Title = \"Shop API\" ; return Task . CompletedTask ; }); }); app. MapOpenApi (); // /openapi/v1.json if (app. Environment . IsDevelopment ()) app. MapScalarApiReference (); // Scalar UI (Scalar.AspNetCore); or Swagger UI via Swashbuckle orders. MapGet ( \"/{id:int}\" , GetById ) . WithName ( \"GetOrder\" ) . WithSummary ( \"Get an order by id\" ) . WithDescription ( \"Returns 404 if the order doesn't exist or isn't visible to the caller.\" ) . Produces < OrderDto >( 200 ) . ProducesProblem ( 404 ); // TypedResults usually make Produces* unnecessary A few names worth recognising in this space: Swashbuckle (Swagger UI and generator) was the default before .NET 9 and remains widely used in existing codebases; NSwag is an alternative generator with its own client-generation story; XML doc comments and TypedResults both feed richer documents into the generated spec automatically, with no extra attributes needed. And one design choice worth naming explicitly: design-first (write the OpenAPI spec by hand, generate server stubs from it) versus code-first (generate the spec from the running code, as the example above does) — code-first is the more common approach in .NET, mostly because TypedResults already makes the code itself an accurate, type-checked source of truth for the contract. Q What makes an API RESTful? Resources identified by URLs, manipulated through standard HTTP methods with correct status codes, stateless requests, cacheable responses, and representations like JSON. Strictly, it also includes hypermedia (HATEOAS), which most practical \"REST\" APIs (maturity level 2) omit. Q What do safe and idempotent mean? Which methods are which? Safe methods don't change server state: GET, HEAD, OPTIONS. Idempotent methods have the same effect whether called once or many times: GET, HEAD, OPTIONS, PUT, DELETE. POST and PATCH aren't idempotent by default. Idempotency is what makes automatic retries safe. Q PUT vs PATCH vs POST? POST creates a subordinate resource or triggers an action (not idempotent; 201 with a Location header). PUT replaces the entire resource at a known URL (idempotent). PATCH applies a partial update via JSON Merge Patch or JSON Patch. Q 401 vs 403? 401 means the request isn't authenticated (no/invalid/expired credentials, and the response includes WWW-Authenticate). 403 means the caller is authenticated but not permitted to perform the action. Q How do you make a POST endpoint safe to retry? Require an Idempotency-Key header. Store the key atomically (unique constraint) along with the response, and on a repeat with the same key, return the stored response instead of re-executing. Reject reuse of the same key with a different payload. Q Offset vs cursor pagination? Offset (page/pageSize, OFFSET/FETCH) is simple and supports jumping to any page, but it slows down on deep pages and shifts when data changes. Cursor/keyset pagination (WHERE key > lastKey) uses the index for constant performance and stable results, but can't jump to arbitrary pages. Q How do you version an API, and what counts as a breaking change? Common strategies are URL segment (/v2), query string, header or media type, e.g. with Asp.Versioning. Breaking changes include removing or renaming fields/endpoints, changing types or semantics, making optional inputs required, and changing status codes. Additive optional change"},{"c":"api-design","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Microsoft REST API Guidelines Azure Architecture Center: API design best practices ASP.NET Core OpenAPI Asp.Versioning on GitHub Book: RESTful Web APIs (Richardson, Amundsen). Book: API Design Patterns (JJ Geewax)."},{"c":"api-design","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run (plain console app, .NET 8/9, no packages). It checks the claims that are actually checkable in isolated code: idempotency-key replay, offset-vs-keyset pagination cost shape, and ETag-style optimistic concurrency. Claims about HTTP method semantics, status codes and versioning are protocol conventions, not something a console program can \"run\" — see Further reading above for the specs they come from. C# Run ▶ Copy using System . Collections . Concurrent ; // --- Idempotency key: second request with the same key replays the first response, doesn't recharge --- var store = new ConcurrentDictionary < Guid , string >(); string Charge ( Guid key, string payload) { if (store. TryGetValue (key, out var previous)) return previous; // replay, no new charge var result = $\"charged:{payload}\" ; store[key] = result; return result; } var k = Guid . NewGuid (); var first = Charge (k, \"$10\" ); var second = Charge (k, \"$10\" ); // simulates a client retry after a timeout if (first != second) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: idempotency key did not replay the original response\" ); if (store. Count != 1 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: idempotency key allowed a second charge to be stored\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: idempotency key makes a repeated POST a no-op instead of a second charge\" ); // --- Offset pagination does more work the deeper you page; keyset pagination doesn't --- var rows = Enumerable . Range ( 1 , 100_000 ). ToList (); // stand-in for an indexed, sorted table int OffsetScanCost ( int page, int pageSize) => page * pageSize; // must walk & discard every prior row int KeysetScanCost ( int pageSize) => pageSize; // index seek straight to the key, every time if ( OffsetScanCost (page: 4000 , pageSize: 20 ) <= KeysetScanCost (pageSize: 20 )) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: offset pagination should cost more than keyset pagination on a deep page\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: offset pagination's cost grows with page depth; keyset pagination's doesn't\" ); // --- ETag / If-Match: a write with a stale version is rejected instead of silently overwriting --- var resource = new { Body = \"original\" , Version = \"v7\" }; string ? ApplyIfMatch ( string ifMatchVersion, string currentVersion, string newBody) => ifMatchVersion == currentVersion ? newBody : null ; // null = 412 Precondition Failed var ok = ApplyIfMatch ( \"v7\" , resource. Version , \"edited by A\" ); if (ok is null ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: a matching If-Match version should be accepted\" ); // someone else saves in between, moving the version to v8 — our old \"v7\" token is now stale var stale = ApplyIfMatch ( \"v7\" , \"v8\" , \"edited by B, using a stale version\" ); if (stale is not null ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: a stale If-Match version should be rejected (412), not applied\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: a write with a stale ETag is rejected instead of clobbering a newer save\" ); // Not runtime-verifiable here — see Further reading: Microsoft REST API Guidelines, Azure Architecture Center // (which status/verb to use, versioning strategy, OpenAPI tooling behavior are conventions, not runtime facts)"},{"c":"app-models","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR ASP.NET Core isn't just JSON APIs: the same host/DI/middleware/routing plumbing also serves gRPC, SignalR, server-rendered HTML (MVC/Razor Pages), and Blazor — you can mix several in one app. gRPC : contract-first ( .proto ), binary, HTTP/2, built-in streaming. Best for internal service-to-service calls, not browsers. SignalR : lets the server push to clients first, instead of waiting for a request. Scaling past one server needs a backplane (Redis) or Azure SignalR Service. MVC/Razor Pages : server-rendered HTML with real <form> posts; relies on cookie auth + antiforgery tokens instead of bearer tokens. Blazor : UI written in C#, with a choice of where it runs — static HTML, on the server (via SignalR), in the browser (WebAssembly), or both. GraphQL, OData, raw WebSockets and Server-Sent Events cover narrower niches on top of the same host."},{"c":"app-models","s":"one-framework-many-endpoint-types","h":"One framework, many endpoint types","t":"# One framework, many endpoint types Beginner Everything in the last several chapters — the host, DI, the middleware pipeline, routing — was described as the foundation under your API endpoints. It's worth being explicit about something that follows directly from that: minimal APIs and controllers are not the only thing that foundation supports. The exact same WebApplication can simultaneously serve JSON APIs, a high-performance internal RPC protocol, a live push channel to browsers, and fully server-rendered HTML pages — because all of them are just different endpoint types sitting on the same shared plumbing. Diagram Copy ┌──────── same WebApplication: host, DI, config, logging, middleware ────────┐ Endpoint type ───▶ │ Minimal APIs │ Controllers │ gRPC services │ SignalR hubs │ Razor Pages │ Blazor │ Health checks │ Speaks ───▶ │ JSON/HTTP │ JSON/HTTP │ Protobuf/HTTP2│ WebSockets… │ HTML │ HTML+WS│ JSON │ Typical client ───▶ │ SPA, mobile │ SPA, mobile │ other services│ browsers,apps│ browsers │browsers│ load balancer │ You can genuinely mix these in one app: a REST API for the mobile client, a gRPC endpoint for internal service calls, a SignalR hub for live updates, and health checks, all registered in the same Program.cs . The question this chapter answers, model by model, is which one actually fits a given job: Need Choose Public/partner API, SPAs, mobile apps REST/JSON (minimal APIs or controllers) Fast, strongly typed internal service-to-service calls, streaming gRPC Server pushes to clients in real time (chat, notifications, live dashboards) SignalR Server-rendered website with forms Razor Pages or MVC with views Rich interactive web UI written in C# instead of JavaScript Blazor Flexible client-driven queries over a graph of data GraphQL (Hot Chocolate library)"},{"c":"app-models","s":"grpc-when-the-client-is-another-service-not-a-browser","h":"gRPC: when the client is another service, not a browser","t":"# gRPC: when the client is another service, not a browser Intermediate gRPC is a high-performance RPC framework from Google. Its design rests on one assumption: both ends of the call are code you (or a cooperating team) control. Nobody needs to read the wire format by eye, and no browser needs to understand it directly. Because of that assumption, gRPC can afford to be contract-first — you define the service in Protocol Buffers ( .proto ), and both client and server code are generated from that contract. It also uses a binary wire format instead of text JSON (smaller, faster to parse), runs over HTTP/2 , and treats streaming as a built-in feature instead of a workaround. Protobuf Copy // Protos/inventory.proto syntax = \"proto3\" ; option csharp_namespace = \"Shop.Inventory.Grpc\" ; service Inventory { rpc GetStock (StockRequest) returns (StockReply); // unary rpc WatchStock (StockRequest) returns (stream StockReply); // server streaming } message StockRequest { string sku = 1 ; } message StockReply { string sku = 1 ; int32 quantity = 2 ; } XML Copy <ItemGroup> <Protobuf Include = \"Protos\\inventory.proto\" GrpcServices = \"Server\" /> <!-- \"Client\" in the consumer project --> </ItemGroup> C# Run ▶ Copy // Server public sealed class InventoryService ( IStockRepository stock) : Inventory . InventoryBase // base class generated from .proto { public override async Task < StockReply > GetStock ( StockRequest req, ServerCallContext ctx) => new () { Sku = req. Sku , Quantity = await stock. GetAsync (req. Sku , ctx. CancellationToken ) }; } builder. Services . AddGrpc (); app. MapGrpcService < InventoryService >(); // Client (another service) builder. Services . AddGrpcClient < Inventory . InventoryClient >(o => o. Address = new Uri ( \"https://inventory\" )) . AddStandardResilienceHandler (); var reply = await inventoryClient. GetStockAsync ( new StockRequest { Sku = \"KB-01\" }, cancellationToken: ct); REST/JSON gRPC Contract OpenAPI (often generated after the fact) .proto , contract-first, strict Payload Text JSON (bigger, human-readable) Binary Protobuf (smaller, faster to parse) Transport HTTP/1.1 or 2 HTTP/2 (required), HTTP/3 Streaming Limited (SSE, chunked) Client, server and bidirectional streaming Browser support Native Needs gRPC-Web or JSON transcoding Tooling/debugging curl, browser, Postman Postman/grpcurl, less ubiquitous Best for Public APIs, browsers Internal microservice calls, low latency, polyglot systems Look at the \"Browser support\" row — it explains most of the real-world adoption pattern. Browsers can't speak the raw HTTP/2 trailers gRPC needs, so gRPC stays mostly internal (service-to-service), while REST stays the public-facing contract. One rule matters once a gRPC service is live: in .proto , the field numbers are the real contract, not the names. Never reuse or renumber a field number — always add new fields with new numbers. That's what lets an old client and a new client talk to the same service as it evolves. (If you ever need both REST and gRPC on the same service, gRPC JSON transcoding exposes one as the other.)"},{"c":"app-models","s":"signalr-when-the-server-needs-to-speak-first","h":"SignalR: when the server needs to speak first","t":"# SignalR: when the server needs to speak first Intermediate Every pattern so far in this book has the client going first — it sends a request, and a response comes back. SignalR is for the opposite case: the server learns something changed (an order shipped, a chat message arrived) before any client asked about it, and needs to push that update immediately, instead of making clients poll for it. Transports, negotiated automatically: WebSockets → Server-Sent Events → long polling — SignalR picks the best one the client and network actually support, falling back gracefully. Hubs : server classes whose methods clients can call, and which can in turn call methods defined on the clients. Addressing: all clients, a specific connection, a user (meaning all of that user's connections, since they might have several tabs open), or groups . Clients: JavaScript/TypeScript, .NET, Java, Swift. C# Run ▶ Copy public interface IOrderClient { Task StatusChanged ( int orderId, string status); } // strongly typed client methods [Authorize] public sealed class OrderHub : Hub < IOrderClient > { public Task Follow ( int orderId) => Groups . AddToGroupAsync ( Context . ConnectionId , $\"order:{orderId}\" ); public override Task OnConnectedAsync () { /* Context.UserIdentifier is the user's id */ return base . OnConnectedAsync (); } } builder. Services . AddSignalR (); app. MapHub < OrderHub >( \"/hubs/orders\" ); // Push from anywhere (an endpoint, a message consumer, a background service) public sealed class ShippingNotifier ( IHubContext < OrderHub , IOrderClient > hub) { public Task OrderShippedAsync ( int orderId) => hub. Clients . Group ( $\"order:{orderId}\" ). StatusChanged (orderId, \"Shipped\" ); } JavaScript Copy const conn = new signalR.HubConnectionBuilder().withUrl( \"/hubs/orders\" , { accessTokenFactory : () => token }).withAutomaticReconnect().build(); conn.on( \"StatusChanged\" , (id, status) => render(id, status)); await conn.start(); await conn.invoke( \"Follow\" , 42 ); Notice that ShippingNotifier is not a hub — it's just an ordinary service with IHubContext<OrderHub, IOrderClient> injected into it. It can push a message from anywhere the real event happens: an endpoint, a message consumer, a background service. \"Something shipped\" doesn't have to originate inside a hub method at all. Scaling to more than one server creates a real problem: say your code sends a message on server A, but the client's persistent WebSocket connection happens to be held open by server B. Each server only knows about its own connections — server A has no way to reach that client directly. Two fixes exist: a backplane (Redis, via AddStackExchangeRedis() ) that relays messages between all server instances, or Azure SignalR Service , which holds every client connection centrally for you — usually the simpler choice once you're on Azure. If you self-host behind a load balancer instead, you also need sticky sessions for WebSockets (or you skip the negotiation step entirely), otherwise a reconnect can land on a different server than the one that originally set up the connection — and that server won't recognize it."},{"c":"app-models","s":"server-rendered-html-mvc-and-razor-pages","h":"Server-rendered HTML: MVC and Razor Pages","t":"# Server-rendered HTML: MVC and Razor Pages Beginner Step away from APIs and real-time push entirely: sometimes the actual deliverable is a web page rendered as HTML, with forms a browser posts back to directly, no separate frontend framework involved. Razor is the templating syntax underneath both options here — C# mixed directly into HTML markup ( @Model.Name , @foreach ). MVC (Model-View-Controller) Razor Pages Organisation Controllers with actions return views; views in Views/{Controller}/ One page = .cshtml file + a PageModel class Routing Conventional {controller}/{action}/{id?} or attributes File-system based: Pages/Orders/Edit.cshtml → /Orders/Edit Good for Large sites with shared controllers; teams used to MVC Page-focused apps: forms, admin tools, CRUD Shared Tag helpers, layouts, partials, view components, model binding, validation, antiforgery same C# Run ▶ Copy // Razor Page: Pages/Products/Edit.cshtml.cs public sealed class EditModel ( ShopDb db) : PageModel { [BindProperty] public ProductForm Input { get ; set ; } = new (); public async Task < IActionResult > OnGetAsync ( int id) { var p = await db. Products . FindAsync (id); if (p is null ) return NotFound (); Input = new ProductForm { Id = p. Id , Name = p. Name , Price = p. Price }; return Page (); } public async Task < IActionResult > OnPostAsync () { if (! ModelState . IsValid ) return Page (); // redisplay with validation messages // ... save ... return RedirectToPage ( \"Index\" ); // Post-Redirect-Get pattern } } Notice OnPostAsync redirects on success rather than returning a view directly — the Post-Redirect-Get pattern, which exists specifically so that refreshing the page after a successful save doesn't resubmit the form a second time. Because this model relies on the browser submitting real HTML forms rather than an API client attaching a bearer token, server-rendered apps use cookie auth by default and need antiforgery tokens on every form — added automatically by the form tag helpers. This defends against CSRF (cross-site request forgery: a malicious site tricking your browser into submitting a form to a real site where you're already logged in, using your cookies without your consent) — exactly the risk Auth flags as cookies' particular weak point."},{"c":"app-models","s":"blazor-c-instead-of-javascript-with-a-choice-of-where-it-act","h":"Blazor: C# instead of JavaScript, with a choice of where it actually runs","t":"# Blazor: C# instead of JavaScript, with a choice of where it actually runs Intermediate Blazor builds interactive web UIs from Razor components written entirely in C# — no JavaScript framework needed. The one question that matters for understanding Blazor is: where does that C# actually run — on the server, or in the browser? Since .NET 8, you don't have to pick one answer for the whole app. A single unified model (\"Blazor Web App\") lets you choose a render mode per component: Render mode Where the code runs Trade-offs Static SSR Server renders HTML once; no interactivity (forms still work) Fastest, SEO-friendly Interactive Server Server; UI events and diffs sent over a SignalR connection Small download, full .NET on the server; needs a persistent connection; latency on every interaction; server memory per user Interactive WebAssembly In the browser, on a .NET runtime compiled to WebAssembly Works offline and scales like a SPA; larger initial download; calls APIs over HTTP Interactive Auto Server first, switches to WebAssembly once downloaded Best of both, more complexity Look at the \"Interactive Server\" row again: this is just SignalR (described above), used to ship every button click and keystroke to the server and stream the resulting UI change back. It isn't a separate real-time technology. That's also why Blazor Server inherits SignalR's scale-out problem the moment you run more than one server instance — it needs the same fix: a backplane or Azure SignalR Service. razor Copy @page \"/orders\" @rendermode InteractiveServer @inject IOrderQueries Orders <h1>Orders</h1> @if (_orders is null) { <p>Loading…</p> } else { <ul>@foreach (var o in _orders) { <li>@ o.Id : @o.Total.ToString( \"C\" )</li> }</ul> } <button @ onclick = \"Refresh\" >Refresh</button> @code { private IReadOnlyList<OrderSummary>? _orders; protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync() => await Refresh(); private async Task Refresh() => _orders = await Orders.RecentAsync(); } For a backend-focused interview, the useful depth here is knowing what Blazor is, how the render modes trade off against each other, and specifically that Blazor Server relies on SignalR rather than being its own independent real-time mechanism. .NET MAUI Blazor Hybrid reuses the same components inside native desktop and mobile apps, for teams wanting one UI codebase across web and native."},{"c":"app-models","s":"graphql-and-others-briefly","h":"GraphQL and others, briefly","t":"# GraphQL and others, briefly Advanced GraphQL (via Hot Chocolate ): clients send queries describing exactly the fields they need across related data, served from a single endpoint. Great for complex front ends aggregating many sources; harder to cache over HTTP, needs query cost limits and has an N+1 risk (solved with DataLoaders). OData : queryable REST conventions ( $filter , $select , $expand ) over IQueryable ; common in enterprise and Microsoft ecosystems. WebSockets directly ( app.UseWebSockets() ) for custom protocols. Server-Sent Events ( TypedResults.ServerSentEvents in .NET 10): simple one-way server-to-browser streams over plain HTTP. Health checks ( MapHealthChecks ) are endpoints too. See Observability . Q When would you choose gRPC over REST? For internal service-to-service communication where performance (binary Protobuf, HTTP/2 multiplexing), strict contract-first schemas with generated clients, and streaming matter. REST/JSON remains better for public APIs and browsers (gRPC needs gRPC-Web or JSON transcoding there). Q How do you evolve a gRPC contract without breaking clients? Add new fields with new field numbers, never change or reuse existing field numbers or types, mark removed fields as reserved, and treat all fields as optional. Protobuf ignores unknown fields, so old and new clients interoperate. Q What is SignalR and how does it work? A library for real-time bidirectional communication. Clients connect to a hub, and the transport is negotiated (WebSockets, then Server-Sent Events, then long polling). Server code calls client methods on all clients, users, groups or connections via the hub or IHubContext, and clients call hub methods. Q How do you scale SignalR across multiple servers? Use a backplane such as Redis so messages published on one server reach clients connected to others, or use Azure SignalR Service, which manages the client connections. Self-hosted WebSocket setups also need sticky sessions unless negotiation is skipped. Q Razor Pages vs MVC? Both render HTML with Razor on the server and share tag helpers, model binding and validation. MVC separates controllers and views and uses action-based routing; Razor Pages is page-centric, pairing each .cshtml with a PageModel and using file-based routing, which is simpler for form-centred apps. Q What are Blazor's render modes? Static server-side rendering (no interactivity), Interactive Server (logic on the server, UI updates over SignalR), Interactive WebAssembly (the .NET runtime in the browser) and Interactive Auto (server first, then WebAssembly). Chosen per component or per app since .NET 8."},{"c":"app-models","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Overview for gRPC on .NET Overview of ASP.NET Core SignalR Introduction to Razor Pages ASP.NET Core Blazor Hot Chocolate (GraphQL)"},{"c":"app-models","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Most claims here (gRPC's transport, SignalR's scale-out, Blazor's render modes) need a real network/hosting environment and aren't something a console program can check. What can be verified locally, with no extra packages, is the actual mechanism behind two of this chapter's claims: that binary framing beats JSON on size, and that Protobuf-style field numbers , not names, are the real contract. Run with dotnet run (plain console app, .NET 8/9). C# Run ▶ Copy using System . Text ; using System . Text . Json ; // Claim: \"binary wire format instead of text JSON\" is smaller for the same data. // This isn't a real Protobuf encoder, but a minimal tagged-binary encoding (type tag + // length-prefixed value) is a fair stand-in: binary framing skips field names, quotes and punctuation. var sku = \"KB-01\" ; var quantity = 42 ; var json = JsonSerializer . Serialize ( new { sku, quantity }); var jsonBytes = Encoding . UTF8 . GetByteCount (json); using var ms = new MemoryStream (); using var bw = new BinaryWriter (ms); bw. Write (( byte ) 1 ); bw. Write (sku); // field 1: sku bw. Write (( byte ) 2 ); bw. Write (quantity); // field 2: quantity var binaryBytes = ( int )ms. Length ; if (binaryBytes >= jsonBytes) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected binary ({binaryBytes}B) to be smaller than JSON ({jsonBytes}B)\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: binary framing ({binaryBytes}B) is smaller than JSON ({jsonBytes}B) for the same fields\" ); // Claim: in a Protobuf-style contract, field NUMBERS are the contract, not names or order. // Model this with a tiny tag->value dictionary (mirrors how Protobuf decodes by field number). static Dictionary < int , object > Encode ( params ( int tag, object value )[] fields) => fields. ToDictionary (f => f.tag, f => f. value ); var oldWire = Encode (( 1 , \"KB-01\" ), ( 2 , 42 )); // \"old\" server var newWire = Encode (( 1 , \"KB-01\" ), ( 2 , 42 ), ( 3 , \"warehouse-7\" )); // \"new\" server adds field 3 string OldClientReads ( Dictionary < int , object > wire) => $\"{wire[1]}:{wire[2]}\" ; // old client only knows fields 1 and 2 if ( OldClientReads (oldWire) != OldClientReads (newWire)) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: old client reading by field number should be unaffected by a new field\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: adding a new field number doesn't break a client that only reads the old field numbers\" ); // Now the failure mode the chapter warns against: REUSING a field number changes its meaning. var corrupted = Encode (( 1 , \"KB-01\" ), ( 2 , \"NOT-A-QUANTITY-ANYMORE\" )); // field 2 reused for something else if (corrupted[ 2 ] is not string ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected the reused field number to silently corrupt the old client's read\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: reusing a field number (as the chapter warns against) silently corrupts old readers\" ); // Not runtime-verifiable here (needs a real network/hosting environment) — see Further reading: // - gRPC transport, HTTP/2, streaming: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/grpc/ // - SignalR transport negotiation and scale-out backplane behavior: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/signalr/introduction // - Razor Pages/MVC routing, Post-Redirect-Get: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/razor-pages/ // - Blazor render modes: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/blazor/ Console . WriteLine ( \"All checkable claims verified.\" );"},{"c":"relational-fundamentals","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Tables are linked by keys (primary/foreign); normalise to avoid duplicate facts, denormalise only on purpose for read speed. Indexes are what make queries fast: a B+tree that lets the engine seek instead of scanning every row. Composite index column order matters (leftmost-prefix rule). Transactions give you ACID; isolation levels are a menu of which race-condition anomalies (dirty read, phantom, lost update, …) you're willing to risk in exchange for less blocking. Deadlocks happen when two transactions each wait on a lock the other holds; the database kills one and your code should retry it. Connection pooling reuses expensive-to-open connections; most \"pool exhausted\" errors trace back to code not disposing connections/DbContexts promptly."},{"c":"relational-fundamentals","s":"the-relational-model","h":"The relational model","t":"# The relational model Beginner A relational database stores data in tables (relations) of rows with typed columns . Tables are linked by keys : Concept Meaning Example Primary key (PK) Uniquely identifies a row; not null Orders.Id Foreign key (FK) Column referencing another table's PK; enforces referential integrity Orders.CustomerId → Customers.Id Unique constraint No duplicates in a column set Customers.Email Check constraint Row-level rule Quantity > 0 Natural vs surrogate key Business value (email, ISBN) vs generated ID ( int IDENTITY , uniqueidentifier ) Prefer surrogate PKs plus unique constraints on natural keys # Relationships Type Modelled as Example One-to-many FK on the \"many\" side Customer 1 → * Orders ( Orders.CustomerId ) One-to-one FK with a unique constraint (or shared PK) User 1 → 1 UserProfile Many-to-many Join table with two FKs Products * ↔ * Categories via ProductCategories(ProductId, CategoryId) SQL Copy CREATE TABLE Customers ( Id INT IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY , Email NVARCHAR( 256 ) NOT NULL UNIQUE , Name NVARCHAR( 200 ) NOT NULL , CreatedAt DATETIME2 NOT NULL DEFAULT SYSUTCDATETIME() ); CREATE TABLE Orders ( Id INT IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY , CustomerId INT NOT NULL REFERENCES Customers(Id), Status TINYINT NOT NULL , Total DECIMAL( 18 , 2 ) NOT NULL CHECK (Total >= 0 ), CreatedAt DATETIME2 NOT NULL , RowVersion ROWVERSION -- optimistic concurrency token );"},{"c":"relational-fundamentals","s":"normalisation-and-when-to-break-it","h":"Normalisation (and when to break it)","t":"# Normalisation (and when to break it) Intermediate Normalisation removes redundancy so each fact is stored exactly once, which is the whole point: with one copy of a customer's name, changing it means one UPDATE . Without normalisation, the same fact duplicated across 500 order rows means an update anomaly waiting to happen — update 499 of them and miss one, and now the database contains a contradiction with no way to tell which copy is right. Form Rule (simplified) Violation example 1NF Atomic values; no repeating groups Phones = \"123, 456\" in one column 2NF 1NF + every non-key column depends on the whole key In OrderLines(OrderId, ProductId, ProductName) , ProductName depends only on ProductId 3NF 2NF + no column depends on another non-key column Orders(CustomerId, CustomerCity) : city depends on the customer, not the order Denormalisation is the deliberate, informed decision to reintroduce exactly that duplication, in exchange for read speed: storing Order.Total instead of recomputing a sum over its lines every time, or maintaining a dedicated read-model table for a dashboard. It's a reasonable trade specifically when reads vastly outnumber writes and you've consciously decided how to keep the duplicate in sync — often via CQRS read models, see CQRS . Snapshot data is not duplication An order line should store the price at the time of purchase , even though the product has a different, current price today. That's worth being precise about: it isn't redundancy at all, because it's recording a genuinely different fact — \"what the customer actually paid\" — not a stale copy of \"what the product costs.\""},{"c":"relational-fundamentals","s":"sql-you-must-be-able-to-write","h":"SQL you must be able to write","t":"# SQL you must be able to write Beginner SQL Copy -- Filter, sort, page SELECT Id, Name, Price FROM Products WHERE CategoryId = @categoryId AND Price BETWEEN @min AND @max AND Name LIKE @search + '%' ORDER BY Price DESC , Id OFFSET @skip ROWS FETCH NEXT @take ROWS ONLY ; -- SQL Server; Postgres: LIMIT @take OFFSET @skip -- Aggregate with grouping and a filter on the aggregate SELECT c.Country, COUNT (*) AS Orders, SUM (o.Total) AS Revenue FROM Orders o JOIN Customers c ON c.Id = o.CustomerId WHERE o.CreatedAt >= '2026-01-01' GROUP BY c.Country HAVING SUM (o.Total) > 10000 ORDER BY Revenue DESC ; -- Customers with NO orders (anti-join) SELECT c.* FROM Customers c WHERE NOT EXISTS ( SELECT 1 FROM Orders o WHERE o.CustomerId = c.Id); -- Top order per customer (window function) SELECT * FROM ( SELECT o.*, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY CustomerId ORDER BY Total DESC ) AS rn FROM Orders o ) x WHERE rn = 1 ; -- Upsert MERGE Stock AS t USING ( VALUES ( @sku , @qty )) AS s(Sku, Qty) ON t.Sku = s.Sku WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET Quantity = t.Quantity + s.Qty WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT (Sku, Quantity) VALUES (s.Sku, s.Qty); -- Postgres: INSERT … ON CONFLICT (Sku) DO UPDATE SET … Also know: UNION vs UNION ALL (the latter skips the duplicate-removal sort), CTEs ( WITH x AS (…) ), CASE WHEN , COALESCE , IN vs EXISTS , and DISTINCT ."},{"c":"relational-fundamentals","s":"joins","h":"Joins","t":"# Joins Beginner Join Returns INNER JOIN Only rows with a match on both sides LEFT [OUTER] JOIN All left rows; right columns NULL when no match RIGHT JOIN All right rows (rare; flip to LEFT) FULL OUTER JOIN All rows from both, matched where possible CROSS JOIN Every combination (Cartesian product) Self join A table joined to itself (employee → manager) Diagram Copy Customers Orders INNER JOIN on CustomerId LEFT JOIN Customers → Orders Id Name Id CustomerId Ana #1 Ana #1 1 Ana 1 1 Ana #2 Ana #2 2 Bo 2 1 Bo NULL ← Bo has no orders"},{"c":"relational-fundamentals","s":"indexes-the-single-most-important-performance-tool-in-this-c","h":"Indexes: the single most important performance tool in this chapter","t":"# Indexes: the single most important performance tool in this chapter Intermediate Here's the problem an index solves, stated plainly: without one, finding a matching row means a table scan — the database reading every single row, one by one, to check if it matches. On a table with a few hundred rows that's instant and nobody notices. On a table with fifty million rows, it's the difference between a 2-millisecond query and a 4-second one, and it's the single most common cause of \"why is this endpoint slow\" tickets in real production systems. An index is a sorted B+tree structure built specifically so the database can seek directly to matching rows in O(log n) time instead of scanning everything. # Clustered vs non-clustered (SQL Server) Clustered index Non-clustered index What it is The table itself , stored in key order (leaf pages contain the full rows) A separate B-tree of key columns + a pointer (the clustered key) to the row How many One per table Many (up to 999) Default The primary key Created explicitly (and for unique constraints) Lookup cost Seek straight to the row Seek in the index, then a key lookup into the clustered index per row (unless covering) SQL Copy -- Composite index for: WHERE CustomerId = @c AND CreatedAt >= @from ORDER BY CreatedAt DESC CREATE INDEX IX_Orders_Customer_Created ON Orders (CustomerId, CreatedAt DESC ) INCLUDE (Status, Total); -- INCLUDE makes it COVERING: no key lookups for these columns # Index design rules Index columns used in WHERE, JOIN, ORDER BY and GROUP BY on large tables. Foreign keys usually need an index (EF Core creates them). Column order matters in composite indexes : an index on (CustomerId, CreatedAt) serves WHERE CustomerId = … and WHERE CustomerId = … AND CreatedAt > … , but not WHERE CreatedAt > … alone (leftmost-prefix rule). Put equality columns first, then range/sort columns. Covering indexes ( INCLUDE ) avoid lookups for hot queries. Selectivity : indexes help when they filter to a small fraction of rows. An index on a boolean IsDeleted alone rarely helps (consider a filtered index : WHERE IsDeleted = 0 ). Indexes cost writes and storage : every INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE maintains every index. Don't index everything. Sargability : the predicate must let the engine use the index. Rule 2's leftmost-prefix rule isn't an arbitrary restriction — it falls directly out of how a B-tree is physically sorted. An index on (CustomerId, CreatedAt) stores rows sorted first by CustomerId , and within each CustomerId , by CreatedAt . Think of a phone book sorted by last name, then first name. You can jump straight to \"Smith\" (equality on the leading column), and from there to \"Smith, John\" (a range on the trailing column). But there's no way to jump straight to \"everyone named John\" without checking every last name, because the book was never sorted that way. Same with the index: query by CreatedAt alone, with no CustomerId filter, and the index can't help — the engine falls back to a scan. SQL Copy WHERE YEAR(CreatedAt) = 2026 -- ✘ function on the column → scan WHERE CreatedAt >= '2026-01-01' AND CreatedAt < '2027-01-01' -- ✔ range → seek WHERE Name LIKE '%board' -- ✘ leading wildcard → scan WHERE Name LIKE 'Key%' -- ✔ seek WHERE CAST(Sku AS INT) = 5 / WHERE NVarcharCol = @varcharParam -- ✘ implicit conversions can kill seeks Notice the shared pattern across all three \"✘\" lines: each one wraps the indexed column in a function, a leading wildcard, or an implicit conversion before comparing it. The engine can't use a sorted structure to answer \"what does YEAR(CreatedAt) equal\" without first computing YEAR(CreatedAt) for every row — which is a scan by another name. Rewrite the predicate so the raw column is what's being compared, as the range-based version does, and the seek comes back. # Clustered key choice and GUIDs A clustered key should be narrow, unique, static and ever-increasing — and that last property deserves a concrete reason, not just a rule to memorise. Since the clustered "},{"c":"relational-fundamentals","s":"execution-plans-watching-the-database-explain-its-own-decisi","h":"Execution plans: watching the database explain its own decisions","t":"# Execution plans: watching the database explain its own decisions Intermediate The query optimiser decides how to execute a piece of SQL — which indexes to use, which join algorithm, in which order — based on statistics it keeps about how the data is actually distributed. When a query is slow, the execution plan is where you go to see exactly what it decided and why: Operator Meaning Usually Index Seek Navigate the B-tree to matching rows ✔ Good Index/Table Scan Read everything ✘ on big tables (fine on small ones) Key Lookup Fetch extra columns from the clustered index per row ⚠ Many lookups → add INCLUDE columns Nested Loops / Hash Match / Merge Join Join algorithms Depends on row counts Sort Explicit sorting ⚠ Can spill to tempdb; an index may provide order Implicit conversion warning Type mismatch ✘ Fix parameter types Tools: SSMS / Azure Data Studio \"Include Actual Execution Plan\", SET STATISTICS IO, TIME ON , Query Store (tracks regressions over time), Azure SQL Query Performance Insight and automatic index recommendations, Postgres EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) . Parameter sniffing is a subtler trap. SQL Server compiles and caches a query plan the first time it sees that query, built around whatever parameter values happened to be passed in on that first call. Say a query for \"a customer's orders\" first runs for a customer with exactly one order — the cached plan gets optimised for tiny result sets. That same plan is then reused, unchanged, the next time the query runs for a customer with a million orders, where it performs terribly. Mitigations: OPTION (RECOMPILE) for that specific query, OPTIMIZE FOR , better indexes, Query Store plan forcing, or Parameter Sensitive Plan optimisation (SQL Server 2022 / Azure SQL)."},{"c":"relational-fundamentals","s":"transactions-and-acid-the-unit-the-database-actually-guarant","h":"Transactions and ACID: the unit the database actually guarantees","t":"# Transactions and ACID: the unit the database actually guarantees Beginner A transaction groups several statements into one all-or-nothing unit — and the four letters of ACID are really four separate promises the database is making about that unit: Property Guarantee Atomicity All statements succeed, or none take effect (rollback) Consistency Constraints hold before and after Isolation Concurrent transactions don't see each other's partial work (to a configurable degree) Durability Once committed, it survives crashes (write-ahead log) SQL Copy BEGIN TRANSACTION ; UPDATE Accounts SET Balance = Balance - @amount WHERE Id = @from AND Balance >= @amount ; IF @ @ROWCOUNT = 0 BEGIN ROLLBACK ; THROW 50001 , 'Insufficient funds' , 1 ; END UPDATE Accounts SET Balance = Balance + @amount WHERE Id = @to ; INSERT INTO Transfers (FromId, ToId, Amount) VALUES ( @from , @to , @amount ); COMMIT ; That worked example earns its place in the chapter because it shows why atomicity matters, not just what it means: without the transaction wrapping all three statements, a crash between the debit and the credit would leave money subtracted from one account and never added to the other — a real, silent loss. In EF Core, a single SaveChanges() call is already wrapped in one transaction automatically; explicit transactions are for when you need to span multiple SaveChanges() calls, or mix EF Core with raw SQL. See EF Core advanced . Keep transactions short , as a hard rule: never wait on an HTTP call or user input while a transaction is open, because every lock it holds stays held until commit — and the next section covers exactly how that turns into a very different kind of problem."},{"c":"relational-fundamentals","s":"isolation-levels-and-the-anomalies-each-one-actually-prevent","h":"Isolation levels and the anomalies each one actually prevents","t":"# Isolation levels and the anomalies each one actually prevents Advanced Run two transactions at the same time against overlapping data, and several specific, nameable things can go wrong depending on how strictly the database isolates them from each other: Anomaly What happens Dirty read Reading another transaction's uncommitted changes (which may roll back) Non-repeatable read Reading the same row twice in a transaction gives different values (someone updated and committed in between) Phantom read Re-running a range query returns new rows (someone inserted) Lost update Two transactions read, modify and write the same row; one overwrites the other Write skew Two transactions read overlapping data and make disjoint writes that together break a rule (two doctors both go off call) Each isolation level is really a statement about which of these anomalies the database is willing to prevent, at the cost of more blocking: Isolation level Dirty Non-repeatable Phantom Notes Read Uncommitted ( NOLOCK ) ✘ possible possible possible Almost never correct in business code Read Committed prevented possible possible SQL Server default (locking); Postgres default (MVCC) Read Committed Snapshot (RCSI) prevented possible possible Readers see the last committed version: readers don't block writers . Default in Azure SQL Repeatable Read prevented prevented possible (SQL Server) Holds shared locks until commit Snapshot prevented prevented prevented Transaction-consistent view via row versioning; update conflicts raise errors Serializable prevented prevented prevented As if transactions ran one at a time; range locks; most blocking WITH (NOLOCK) is not a performance fix It's tempting to reach for NOLOCK the moment a query feels slow, but look at what it actually gives up: Read Uncommitted can return data that was never actually committed, skip rows entirely, or read the same row twice if a page happens to split while the scan is running — none of which is a safe trade for speed in business code. RCSI or Snapshot isolation give you non-blocking reads without any of those correctness risks, which is almost always the fix people were actually reaching for."},{"c":"relational-fundamentals","s":"locking-blocking-and-deadlocks","h":"Locking, blocking and deadlocks","t":"# Locking, blocking and deadlocks Advanced Under locking-based isolation, a writer takes an exclusive lock on the rows it's touching, and a reader takes a shared lock (at row, page, or table granularity, escalating as needed). Blocking is simply one transaction waiting for another's lock to release. A deadlock is the specific, unresolvable case where two transactions each hold a lock the other one needs — meaning neither can ever proceed, no matter how long either one waits: Diagram Copy Tx A: UPDATE Accounts WHERE Id = 1 (locks row 1) → UPDATE Accounts WHERE Id = 2 (waits for B) Tx B: UPDATE Accounts WHERE Id = 2 (locks row 2) → UPDATE Accounts WHERE Id = 1 (waits for A) ⇒ DEADLOCK The database can't wait this out — there's no amount of patience that resolves a genuine cycle — so it actively detects the cycle and kills one of the two transactions outright (the deadlock victim , surfacing to your code as SQL Server error 1205 ). That victim transaction should always be retried , because the deadlock was a timing accident, not a real problem with the data. Prevention strategies, each one attacking a different part of how the cycle forms: access tables and rows in a consistent order across your codebase (lowest ID first, say), so two transactions can never form the crossed pattern shown above; keep transactions short, so the window where a lock is held is small; add indexes so updates lock fewer rows (a scan, by contrast, locks far more); use RCSI so readers never contribute to the blocking in the first place; and retry on deadlock and transient errors automatically (EF Core's EnableRetryOnFailure ). Pessimistic locking , when you genuinely need it (\"reserve this seat\" — where you cannot allow even a brief race): SELECT … WITH (UPDLOCK, ROWLOCK) in SQL Server or SELECT … FOR UPDATE in Postgres, inside a short transaction. Most web apps, though, prefer optimistic concurrency (version columns, the ETag mechanism from API design ) instead, because it avoids holding locks across the round trip to the client entirely."},{"c":"relational-fundamentals","s":"connection-pooling-the-cost-hiding-behind-every-open-call","h":"Connection pooling: the cost hiding behind every Open() call","t":"# Connection pooling: the cost hiding behind every Open() call Intermediate Opening a physical database connection — a TCP handshake, TLS negotiation, authentication — is expensive, in exactly the same way opening a fresh HTTP connection per request was expensive in Calling other services . ADO.NET providers solve it the same way: a pool of already-open connections, kept per unique connection string, where Open() just borrows one and Dispose() / Close() returns it rather than actually tearing it down. Default max pool size is 100 (SqlClient). Under load, if connections aren't returned quickly enough, you get \"Timeout expired. The timeout period elapsed prior to obtaining a connection from the pool\" . Causes: not disposing connections or DbContexts, long transactions, slow queries, sync-over-async holding a connection open while a thread blocks on something that should have been awaited, or a sudden spike of parallel work exceeding the pool's capacity. The operating discipline that avoids all of this: don't open connections early or hold them open across unrelated work. Open late, close early. Diagram Copy Server=tcp:shop.database.windows.net;Database=shop;Authentication=Active Directory Default; Encrypt=True;Max Pool Size=200;Connect Timeout=15;Application Name=shop-api"},{"c":"relational-fundamentals","s":"sql-server-vs-postgresql-in-net-shops","h":"SQL Server vs PostgreSQL in .NET shops","t":"# SQL Server vs PostgreSQL in .NET shops Intermediate SQL Server / Azure SQL PostgreSQL / Azure Database for PostgreSQL Driver Microsoft.Data.SqlClient Npgsql EF Core provider Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL Concurrency model Locking (plus optional row versioning, RCSI) MVCC by default Identity IDENTITY , sequences GENERATED … AS IDENTITY , sequences Notable features Columnstore, temporal tables, Query Store, Always On JSONB, arrays, rich extensions (PostGIS, pgvector), ON CONFLICT Licence Commercial (free Express/Developer editions) Open source Both are excellent, genuinely mature databases. EF Core abstracts away most of the differences for everyday CRUD, but raw SQL and the fine details of performance tuning — the exact default isolation level, how each one physically organises a clustered table — do differ, which is why this chapter calls out SQL Server's locking model specifically rather than treating \"the database\" as one interchangeable thing. Q What's the difference between a clustered and a non-clustered index? A clustered index defines the physical order of the table: its leaf level is the data rows, and there can be only one (usually the PK). A non-clustered index is a separate B-tree of key columns pointing to rows via the clustered key, so non-covered columns need key lookups; there can be many. INCLUDE columns make a non-clustered index covering. Q How do you decide which columns to index? Columns in frequent WHERE, JOIN, ORDER BY and GROUP BY clauses of important queries on large tables, especially foreign keys. Order composite indexes with equality columns first and range/sort columns after, add INCLUDE for covering, prefer selective columns, and balance against write overhead. Verify with execution plans and Query Store. Q What does sargable mean? A predicate the engine can satisfy with an index seek. Wrapping the indexed column in a function, leading wildcards and implicit type conversions make predicates non-sargable, forcing scans. Q Explain ACID. Atomicity (all or nothing), Consistency (constraints preserved), Isolation (concurrent transactions don't interfere beyond the chosen level) and Durability (committed data survives failures, via the transaction log). Q What are isolation levels and which anomalies do they prevent? Read Uncommitted allows dirty reads. Read Committed prevents dirty reads (the default). Repeatable Read also prevents non-repeatable reads. Serializable prevents phantoms too. Snapshot/RCSI use row versioning so readers see committed versions without blocking writers. Higher isolation means more correctness but more blocking or conflicts. Q What is a deadlock and how do you handle it? Two transactions each wait for a lock held by the other. The database kills one (SQL error 1205). Prevent it by accessing resources in a consistent order, keeping transactions short, indexing so fewer rows are locked, and using row versioning. Handle it by retrying the victim transaction. Q Why are random GUIDs bad clustered keys? Inserts land at random positions in the B-tree, causing page splits, fragmentation and poor cache use; they're also 16 bytes, which widens every non-clustered index. Use sequential IDs (IDENTITY, NEWSEQUENTIALID, UUIDv7 via Guid.CreateVersion7) or keep GUIDs as non-clustered PKs. Q What is connection pooling and what causes pool exhaustion? Reusing open physical connections per connection string so Open is cheap. Exhaustion (timeouts obtaining a connection) comes from connections not being disposed, long-running transactions or queries, sync-over-async blocking while holding connections, or more concurrent work than the pool size. Q What is parameter sniffing? SQL Server compiles and caches a plan using the first parameter values it sees. If those are unrepresentative, later executions reuse a suboptimal plan. Fixes include OPTION(RECOMPILE), OPTIMIZE FOR, better indexing, Query Store plan forcing, or PSP optimisation."},{"c":"relational-fundamentals","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading SQL Server index architecture and design guide Transaction isolation levels Use the Index, Luke : the best free guide to SQL indexing, for developers. Book: Designing Data-Intensive Applications by Martin Kleppmann (chapters 2, 3 and 7)."},{"c":"relational-fundamentals","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter This chapter is mostly about a real SQL engine's internals (B-trees, locking, the query optimiser), which a plain console app can't observe directly — trust Further reading for those, especially the isolation-level table and parameter sniffing. What the code below can genuinely check is the logic behind two claims: the INNER/LEFT JOIN results shown earlier, and the leftmost-prefix rule (why an index on (CustomerId, CreatedAt) can't serve a CreatedAt -only query). Run with dotnet run (plain console app, .NET 8/9, no extra packages). C# Run ▶ Copy var customers = new [] { ( Id : 1 , Name : \"Ana\" ), ( Id : 2 , Name : \"Bo\" ) }; var orders = new [] { ( Id : 1 , CustomerId : 1 ), ( Id : 2 , CustomerId : 1 ) }; // Claim: INNER JOIN only returns customers that have a matching order. var inner = customers. Join (orders, c => c. Id , o => o. CustomerId , (c, o) => (c. Name , o. Id )); if (inner. Count () != 2 || inner. Any (x => x. Name == \"Bo\" )) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: INNER JOIN should return only Ana's 2 orders, never Bo\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: INNER JOIN returns only rows with a match on both sides\" ); // Claim: LEFT JOIN keeps every customer, with NULL order columns when there's no match. var left = customers. GroupJoin (orders, c => c. Id , o => o. CustomerId , (c, os) => (c. Name , OrderIds : os. Select (o => ( int ?)o. Id ). DefaultIfEmpty ( null ). ToList ())); var bo = left. First (x => x. Name == \"Bo\" ); if (bo. OrderIds . Count != 1 || bo. OrderIds [ 0 ] != null ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: LEFT JOIN should keep Bo with a NULL order\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: LEFT JOIN keeps unmatched rows (Bo) with NULL on the right side\" ); // Claim: a composite index on (CustomerId, CreatedAt) sorts by CustomerId first, then CreatedAt — // so it can serve \"WHERE CustomerId = x\" but is useless for \"WHERE CreatedAt > y\" alone. var rows = new [] { ( CustomerId : 2 , CreatedAt : new DateTime ( 2026 , 1 , 1 )), ( CustomerId : 1 , CreatedAt : new DateTime ( 2026 , 3 , 1 )), ( CustomerId : 1 , CreatedAt : new DateTime ( 2026 , 1 , 1 )), ( CustomerId : 2 , CreatedAt : new DateTime ( 2026 , 2 , 1 )), }; var indexOrder = rows. OrderBy (r => r. CustomerId ). ThenBy (r => r. CreatedAt ). ToArray (); bool sortedByCreatedAtAlone = indexOrder. Zip (indexOrder. Skip ( 1 ), (a, b) => a. CreatedAt <= b. CreatedAt ). All (x => x); if (sortedByCreatedAtAlone) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected the composite order to NOT be a plain CreatedAt order\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: sorting by (CustomerId, CreatedAt) does not give a usable CreatedAt-only order (leftmost-prefix rule holds)\" ); // Claim: ACID atomicity — a transfer either fully happens or leaves no partial trace. var accounts = new Dictionary < string , int > { [ \"A\" ] = 100 , [ \"B\" ] = 50 }; bool TryTransfer ( string from, string to, int amount) { if (accounts[from] < amount) return false ; // insufficient funds: no partial write accounts[from] -= amount; accounts[to] += amount; return true ; } var before = accounts[ \"A\" ] + accounts[ \"B\" ]; if ( TryTransfer ( \"A\" , \"B\" , 1000 )) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: an overdrawing transfer should have been rejected atomically\" ); if (accounts[ \"A\" ] + accounts[ \"B\" ] != before) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: a failed transfer must leave total balance unchanged (no partial effect)\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: a failed transfer leaves no partial effect — total balance unchanged (atomicity)\" ); // Not runtime-verifiable here — see Further reading: // - real index seeks vs. scans, execution plans, parameter sniffing (needs an actual query optimiser) // - locking, blocking and deadlock detection (needs a real multi-connection database engine) // - connection pool exhaustion and its default size of 100 (SqlClient-specific behaviour)"},{"c":"ado-dapper","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR ADO.NET ( DbConnection , DbCommand , DbDataReader , DbParameter ) is the low-level API every .NET data-access tool — EF Core, Dapper, hand-rolled code — is eventually built on. Always parameterize SQL: it stops SQL injection and lets the database reuse cached query plans. Dapper is a thin layer over ADO.NET: you write the SQL, Dapper maps the rows to objects. No change tracking, no migrations, near-raw performance. For bulk inserts, row-by-row INSERT s are too slow — use SqlBulkCopy / COPY , table-valued parameters, or set-based updates instead. A common pattern: EF Core for writes (business rules, change tracking, migrations), Dapper or raw SQL for fast, flat reads."},{"c":"ado-dapper","s":"the-layer-every-other-data-access-tool-is-secretly-built-on","h":"The layer every other data-access tool is secretly built on","t":"# The layer every other data-access tool is secretly built on Beginner Whichever way you query a .NET database — EF Core's LINQ, Dapper's SQL strings, or a hand-rolled reader loop — every single one of them eventually bottoms out in the same four objects. ADO.NET ( System.Data ) is that provider-agnostic, low-level database API, and each database ships a provider implementing it: Abstract class SQL Server ( Microsoft.Data.SqlClient ) PostgreSQL ( Npgsql ) SQLite DbConnection SqlConnection NpgsqlConnection SqliteConnection DbCommand SqlCommand NpgsqlCommand SqliteCommand DbDataReader SqlDataReader NpgsqlDataReader SqliteDataReader DbParameter SqlParameter NpgsqlParameter SqliteParameter DbTransaction SqlTransaction NpgsqlTransaction … DbDataSource (.NET 7) SqlDataSource * NpgsqlDataSource … *Use Microsoft.Data.SqlClient , not the legacy System.Data.SqlClient . C# Run ▶ Copy await using var conn = new SqlConnection (connectionString); // borrows from the pool on Open await conn. OpenAsync (ct); await using var cmd = new SqlCommand ( \"SELECT Id, Name, Price FROM Products WHERE CategoryId = @cat AND Price <= @max ORDER BY Name\" , conn); cmd. Parameters . Add ( new SqlParameter ( \"@cat\" , SqlDbType . Int ) { Value = categoryId }); cmd. Parameters . Add ( new SqlParameter ( \"@max\" , SqlDbType . Decimal ) { Precision = 18 , Scale = 2 , Value = maxPrice }); await using var reader = await cmd. ExecuteReaderAsync (ct); // forward-only, streaming cursor var products = new List < ProductDto >(); while ( await reader. ReadAsync (ct)) { products. Add ( new ProductDto ( reader. GetInt32 ( 0 ), reader. GetString ( 1 ), reader. GetDecimal ( 2 ))); } Method Returns Use ExecuteReaderAsync DbDataReader Row sets ExecuteScalarAsync First column of first row COUNT(*) , SCOPE_IDENTITY() ExecuteNonQueryAsync Rows affected INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE Always parameterise C# Run ▶ Copy var sql = $\"SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Email = '{email}'\" ; // ✘ SQL injection: email = \"' OR 1=1 --\" cmd. Parameters . AddWithValue ( \"@email\" , email); // ✔ (prefer typed Add over AddWithValue to avoid type-inference issues) Parameters also let the database reuse cached plans , exactly the mechanism Relational fundamentals covered — concatenated literals compile a brand-new plan for every distinct value, throwing that caching away entirely on top of the injection risk. # DataSet and DataTable The old disconnected in-memory model ( DataSet , DataTable , DataAdapter ) is still around in legacy code and for bulk-copy staging. Modern apps map to POCOs with readers, Dapper or EF Core."},{"c":"ado-dapper","s":"transactions-in-ado-net","h":"Transactions in ADO.NET","t":"# Transactions in ADO.NET Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy await using var conn = new SqlConnection (cs); await conn. OpenAsync (ct); await using var tx = await conn. BeginTransactionAsync ( IsolationLevel . ReadCommitted , ct); try { await using ( var debit = new SqlCommand ( \"UPDATE Accounts SET Balance -= @a WHERE Id = @id AND Balance >= @a\" , conn, ( SqlTransaction )tx)) { debit. Parameters . AddWithValue ( \"@a\" , amount); debit. Parameters . AddWithValue ( \"@id\" , fromId); if ( await debit. ExecuteNonQueryAsync (ct) == 0 ) throw new InsufficientFundsException (); } // ... credit, insert transfer row (each command enlisted with tx) ... await tx. CommitAsync (ct); } catch { await tx. RollbackAsync (ct); throw ; } TransactionScope (System.Transactions) gives ambient transactions spanning multiple connections — convenient, but it silently escalates to a distributed transaction (MSDTC) the moment more than one connection or resource manager is involved, and MSDTC is unavailable or unwanted in most cloud setups. If you use it with async code, pass new TransactionScope(TransactionScopeAsyncFlowOption.Enabled) , or the ambient transaction won't flow across the await ."},{"c":"ado-dapper","s":"bulk-operations-when-row-by-row-stops-being-viable","h":"Bulk operations: when row-by-row stops being viable","t":"# Bulk operations: when row-by-row stops being viable Intermediate Inserting 100,000 rows one INSERT at a time takes minutes, because each one pays its own network round trip and transaction-log write. The options that avoid that cost each trade something for speed: Approach Speed Notes SqlBulkCopy (SQL Server) / COPY binary import (Npgsql) Fastest Streams rows into a table; great for imports and staging tables Table-valued parameters (SQL Server) Fast Pass a set of rows to a stored proc / MERGE Batched multi-row INSERTs Good EF Core batches automatically on SaveChanges EF Core ExecuteUpdate / ExecuteDelete Fast for set-based updates No entity loading Libraries (EFCore.BulkExtensions, linq2db) Fast Commercial licences for some C# Run ▶ Copy using var bulk = new SqlBulkCopy (conn) { DestinationTableName = \"StagingPrices\" , BatchSize = 10_000 }; bulk. ColumnMappings . Add ( \"Sku\" , \"Sku\" ); bulk. ColumnMappings . Add ( \"Price\" , \"Price\" ); await bulk. WriteToServerAsync (dataReaderOrDataTable, ct); // then a set-based MERGE from staging into the real table"},{"c":"ado-dapper","s":"dapper-the-micro-orm-that-stays-out-of-your-way","h":"Dapper: the micro-ORM that stays out of your way","t":"# Dapper: the micro-ORM that stays out of your way Beginner Dapper (by Stack Overflow) is, at its core, just a set of extension methods on IDbConnection . The division of labour it offers is deliberately minimal: you write the SQL; Dapper maps the resulting rows to objects (and your objects to parameters), with performance close enough to hand-written ADO.NET that the difference rarely matters. There's no change tracking, no LINQ translation layer, and no migrations — Dapper simply isn't trying to be a full ORM. C# Run ▶ Copy await using var conn = new SqlConnection (cs); // Dapper opens/closes it if it's closed // Query → IEnumerable<T>; anonymous object → parameters var products = await conn. QueryAsync < ProductDto >( \"SELECT Id, Name, Price FROM Products WHERE CategoryId = @CategoryId ORDER BY Name\" , new { CategoryId = categoryId }); var one = await conn. QuerySingleOrDefaultAsync < ProductDto >( \"SELECT Id, Name, Price FROM Products WHERE Id = @id\" , new { id }); var count = await conn. ExecuteScalarAsync < int >( \"SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Orders WHERE CustomerId = @customerId\" , new { customerId }); var affected = await conn. ExecuteAsync ( \"UPDATE Products SET Price = @Price WHERE Id = @Id\" , new { Id = 5 , Price = 19.99m }); // IN lists are expanded automatically var some = await conn. QueryAsync < ProductDto >( \"SELECT * FROM Products WHERE Id IN @ids\" , new { ids = new [] { 1 , 2 , 3 } }); // Pass a CancellationToken via CommandDefinition var rows = await conn. QueryAsync < OrderRow >( new CommandDefinition (sql, args, cancellationToken: ct)); The mapping itself works by matching column name to property or constructor parameter name , case-insensitively — no attributes, no configuration. Records with positional constructors work out of the box; where a column name doesn't match a property name, a SQL alias ( AS CustomerName ) closes the gap."},{"c":"ado-dapper","s":"dapper-advanced-usage","h":"Dapper: advanced usage","t":"# Dapper: advanced usage Intermediate # Multi-mapping: turning a join back into an object graph The one thing Dapper's simplicity doesn't give you for free is what EF Core's navigation properties handle automatically — reconstructing a nested object from a flat, joined row. Multi-mapping is Dapper's answer: C# Run ▶ Copy const string sql = \"\"\" SELECT o.Id, o.Total, o.CreatedAt, c.Id, c.Name, c.Email FROM Orders o JOIN Customers c ON c.Id = o.CustomerId WHERE o.CreatedAt >= @since \"\"\" ; var orders = await conn. QueryAsync < OrderView , CustomerView , OrderView >( sql, (order, customer) => order with { Customer = customer }, new { since }, splitOn: \"Id\" ); // where the second object's columns start (the second \"Id\") splitOn is doing the real work: the query returns one flat row with both an order's Id and a customer's Id , so Dapper has to be told explicitly where the first object's columns end and the second one's begin — a row is just a sequence of columns, with no structural hint of its own. # Multiple result sets in one round trip C# Run ▶ Copy using var multi = await conn. QueryMultipleAsync ( \"\"\" SELECT * FROM Orders WHERE Id = @id; SELECT * FROM OrderLines WHERE OrderId = @id; \"\"\" , new { id }); var order = await multi. ReadSingleAsync < OrderDetail >(); var lines = ( await multi. ReadAsync < OrderLineDto >()). ToList (); This is worth reaching for specifically when an \"order with its lines\" screen would otherwise need two separate round trips: here, both statements travel over the wire together, and the single open connection reads each result set in turn. # Stored procedures, output parameters, transactions C# Run ▶ Copy var p = new DynamicParameters (); p. Add ( \"@CustomerId\" , customerId); p. Add ( \"@NewOrderId\" , dbType: DbType . Int32 , direction: ParameterDirection . Output ); await conn. ExecuteAsync ( \"dbo.CreateOrder\" , p, commandType: CommandType . StoredProcedure ); int newId = p. Get < int >( \"@NewOrderId\" ); await using var tx = await conn. BeginTransactionAsync (); await conn. ExecuteAsync (sql1, args1, tx); await conn. ExecuteAsync (sql2, args2, tx); await tx. CommitAsync (); # Type handlers Map custom types (strongly typed IDs, JSON columns, DateOnly on older providers) with SqlMapper.AddTypeHandler(new OrderIdHandler()) ."},{"c":"ado-dapper","s":"registering-connections-in-di","h":"Registering connections in DI","t":"# Registering connections in DI Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy // Modern: DbDataSource (Npgsql) — a singleton factory for pooled connections builder. Services . AddNpgsqlDataSource (builder. Configuration . GetConnectionString ( \"Shop\" )!); public sealed class ProductQueries ( NpgsqlDataSource db) { public async Task < IEnumerable < ProductDto >> ListAsync () { await using var c = await db. OpenConnectionAsync (); return await c. QueryAsync < ProductDto >( \"…\" ); } } // SQL Server: a small factory abstraction keeps code testable public interface IDbConnectionFactory { SqlConnection Create (); } public sealed class SqlConnectionFactory ( IConfiguration cfg) : IDbConnectionFactory { private readonly string _cs = cfg. GetConnectionString ( \"Shop\" )!; public SqlConnection Create () => new (_cs); } builder. Services . AddSingleton < IDbConnectionFactory , SqlConnectionFactory >();"},{"c":"ado-dapper","s":"dapper-vs-ef-core-the-real-decision-not-a-popularity-contest","h":"Dapper vs EF Core: the real decision, not a popularity contest","t":"# Dapper vs EF Core: the real decision, not a popularity contest Intermediate EF Core Dapper You write LINQ (SQL optional) SQL Change tracking / unit of work ✔ ✘ Migrations / schema management ✔ ✘ (use DbUp, Flyway, sqlproj/DACPAC) Relationships & loading ✔ Include, navigations Manual (multi-mapping) Compile-time query checking Partially (LINQ is typed) ✘ (SQL strings) Raw performance Very good (gap much smaller since EF Core 6–8) Excellent, minimal overhead Complex reporting SQL (CTEs, window functions, hints) Possible via FromSql / SqlQuery Natural Learning curve Higher (tracking, loading, translation rules) Low (if you know SQL) This table describes two tools built for different jobs, which is why a pragmatic combination uses both at once, naturally falling out of CQRS: EF Core for the write side (aggregates, business rules, change tracking, migrations — where EF Core's extra machinery earns its keep) and Dapper, or EF Core's own raw SQL, for the read side (fast, flat, tailored queries returning DTOs directly, with no tracking overhead for data nobody's going to mutate). EF Core can run raw SQL this way too, without Dapper at all: db.Database.SqlQuery<ProductDto>($\"SELECT …\") . Q What are the main ADO.NET classes? DbConnection (a connection to the database, pooled), DbCommand (SQL text or stored procedure with parameters), DbDataReader (fast forward-only streaming over results), DbParameter, and DbTransaction, each implemented by providers such as SqlClient and Npgsql. Newer: DbDataSource as a connection factory. Q Why must you parameterise SQL? To prevent SQL injection (input is sent as data, never parsed as SQL) and to let the database reuse cached execution plans. Concatenating input into SQL is a critical vulnerability. Q What is Dapper and how is it different from EF Core? A micro-ORM: extension methods on IDbConnection that execute your SQL and map results to objects (and objects to parameters) with near-raw ADO.NET performance. It has no change tracking, LINQ provider, relationship management or migrations, unlike the full ORM EF Core. Q How do you map a join into nested objects with Dapper? Multi-mapping: QueryAsync<T1, T2, TResult> with a mapping function that combines the objects per row, and splitOn naming the column where the second object's columns start. Use a dictionary to de-duplicate parents for one-to-many. Q How would you insert 1 million rows efficiently? Use SqlBulkCopy (or Npgsql binary COPY) to stream rows into a staging or target table in batches, then a set-based MERGE/INSERT…SELECT if needed. Avoid row-by-row INSERTs and EF change tracking for large imports. Q When would you choose Dapper over EF Core? For read-heavy, performance-critical or complex SQL (reports, CTEs, window functions, hints), when the team prefers explicit SQL, or for CQRS read models. EF Core for rich domain writes, change tracking, relationships and migrations. Using both is common."},{"c":"ado-dapper","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading ADO.NET overview Microsoft.Data.SqlClient Dapper on GitHub Npgsql documentation"},{"c":"ado-dapper","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Needs two packages: dotnet add package Dapper and dotnet add package Microsoft.Data.Sqlite in a new console project, then dotnet run . It uses an in-memory SQLite database to check raw ADO.NET parameterized commands, that parameterization defuses SQL-injection-shaped input, Dapper's column-to-object mapping, IN list expansion, multi-mapping with splitOn , QueryMultipleAsync , and ExecuteAsync 's affected-row count. C# Run ▶ Copy using Dapper ; using Microsoft . Data . Sqlite ; int failures = 0 ; void Check ( string claim, bool ok) { if (ok) Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: {claim}\" ); else { Console . WriteLine ( $\"FAIL: {claim}\" ); failures++; } } await using var conn = new SqliteConnection ( \"Data Source=:memory:\" ); await conn. OpenAsync (); await conn. ExecuteAsync ( \"\"\" CREATE TABLE Products (Id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, Name TEXT NOT NULL, Price REAL NOT NULL, CategoryId INTEGER NOT NULL); CREATE TABLE Customers (Id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, Name TEXT NOT NULL); CREATE TABLE Orders (Id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, Total REAL NOT NULL, CustomerId INTEGER NOT NULL); \"\"\" ); // 1. Raw ADO.NET: parameterized command + DbDataReader await using ( var insert = conn. CreateCommand ()) { insert. CommandText = \"INSERT INTO Products (Name, Price, CategoryId) VALUES (@name, @price, @cat)\" ; insert. Parameters . AddWithValue ( \"@name\" , \"Widget\" ); insert. Parameters . AddWithValue ( \"@price\" , 9.99 ); insert. Parameters . AddWithValue ( \"@cat\" , 1 ); await insert. ExecuteNonQueryAsync (); } await using ( var cmd = conn. CreateCommand ()) { cmd. CommandText = \"SELECT Id, Name, Price FROM Products WHERE CategoryId = @cat\" ; cmd. Parameters . AddWithValue ( \"@cat\" , 1 ); await using var reader = await cmd. ExecuteReaderAsync (); bool gotRow = false ; while ( await reader. ReadAsync ()) { gotRow = reader. GetString ( 1 ) == \"Widget\" && reader. GetDouble ( 2 ) == 9.99 ; } Check ( \"a parameterized DbCommand + DbDataReader round-trips a row correctly\" , gotRow); } // 2. Parameterization defuses SQL injection: a malicious-looking value is stored as plain data var maliciousName = \"Robert'); DROP TABLE Products; --\" ; await conn. ExecuteAsync ( \"INSERT INTO Products (Name, Price, CategoryId) VALUES (@Name, @Price, @CategoryId)\" , new { Name = maliciousName, Price = 1.0 , CategoryId = 2 }); var stillThere = await conn. QuerySingleAsync < int >( \"SELECT COUNT(*) FROM Products\" ); var storedLiteral = await conn. QuerySingleAsync < string >( \"SELECT Name FROM Products WHERE CategoryId = @cat\" , new { cat = 2 }); Check ( \"a parameterized value containing SQL syntax is stored literally, not executed\" , stillThere == 2 && storedLiteral == maliciousName); // 3. Dapper QueryAsync<T> maps columns to a record by name var products = ( await conn. QueryAsync < ProductDto >( \"SELECT Id, Name, Price FROM Products WHERE CategoryId = @CategoryId\" , new { CategoryId = 1 })). ToList (); Check ( \"Dapper QueryAsync<T> maps result columns onto a record's constructor parameters by name\" , products. Count == 1 && products[ 0 ]. Name == \"Widget\" && products[ 0 ]. Price == 9.99 ); // 4. IN list expansion var byIds = await conn. QueryAsync < ProductDto >( \"SELECT Id, Name, Price FROM Products WHERE Id IN @ids\" , new { ids = new [] { 1 , 2 } }); Check ( \"Dapper expands an array parameter into a SQL IN list\" , byIds. Count () == 2 ); // 5. Multi-mapping: a joined flat row reconstructed into a nested object await conn. ExecuteAsync ( \"INSERT INTO Customers (Id, Name) VALUES (1, 'Ana')\" ); await conn. ExecuteAsync ( \"INSERT INTO Orders (Id, Total, CustomerId) VALUES (1, 42.5, 1)\" ); var orders = await conn. QueryAsync < OrderJoinRow , CustomerRow , OrderWithCustomer >( \"SELECT o.Id, o.Total, c.Id, c.Name FROM Orders o JOIN Customers c ON c.Id = o.CustomerId\" , (order, customer) => new OrderWithCustomer (order. Id , order. Total , customer), splitOn: \"Id\" ); var joined = orders. Single (); Check ( \"Dapper multi-mapping reconstructs a nested object from a joined row via splitO"},{"c":"efcore-fundamentals","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR EF Core does four jobs: map classes↔tables, translate LINQ↔SQL, track what changed, and turn SaveChanges() into INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE statements. DbContext = one unit of work, scoped to one request/job — never a singleton, never shared across threads. EF infers a lot from naming conventions ( Id , nullability, navigation properties); Fluent API ( IEntityTypeConfiguration<T> ) overrides conventions and keeps persistence detail out of the domain classes. Change tracking works by snapshotting each entity's values when loaded, then diffing against that snapshot — which is why read-only queries should use AsNoTracking() to skip the diffing entirely. Migrations are a versioned diff of your model over time; in production, generate a SQL script/bundle rather than calling MigrateAsync() at startup across multiple replicas (they'd race)."},{"c":"efcore-fundamentals","s":"what-an-orm-is-actually-for","h":"What an ORM is actually for","t":"# What an ORM is actually for Beginner Think back to ADO.NET & Dapper : raw ADO.NET gives you a connection, a command, and a reader that hands back columns one at a time, by index or name. Every entity you want to work with needs its own hand-written SQL, its own mapping code from columns to properties, and its own tracking of what changed since you loaded it. An Object-Relational Mapper exists to take that repetitive, error-prone work off your hands. Entity Framework Core does four specific jobs: Mapping: classes ↔ tables, properties ↔ columns, references ↔ foreign keys. Querying: LINQ ↔ SQL, then results → objects (\"materialisation\"). Change tracking: remembers loaded entities and detects what changed. Persistence: SaveChanges() generates INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE in one transaction. Plus migrations , covered later in this chapter, to evolve the database schema in step with your model as it changes over time. Why use an ORM Without one, every entity needs hand-written SQL for each operation plus manual mapping code, relationships and transactions and change detection are all your problem, and schema changes are manual scripts. EF Core removes most of that boilerplate, gives type-safe queries checked by the compiler, and keeps the schema versioned with the code. The cost is a learning curve — tracking, loading, translation rules — and the risk of generating bad SQL if you don't understand what it's doing, which the next chapter covers."},{"c":"efcore-fundamentals","s":"dbcontext-and-dbset-your-session-with-the-database","h":"DbContext and DbSet: your session with the database","t":"# DbContext and DbSet: your session with the database Beginner The DbContext is the object that holds everything an ORM needs to do its four jobs: a database connection, the model (metadata describing how classes map to tables), and the change tracker . Each DbSet<T> is your entry point to one table — something you can query and add new entities to. C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class ShopDb ( DbContextOptions < ShopDb > options) : DbContext (options) { public DbSet < Customer > Customers => Set < Customer >(); public DbSet < Order > Orders => Set < Order >(); public DbSet < Product > Products => Set < Product >(); protected override void OnModelCreating ( ModelBuilder modelBuilder) { modelBuilder. ApplyConfigurationsFromAssembly ( typeof ( ShopDb ). Assembly ); // picks up IEntityTypeConfiguration<T> classes } protected override void ConfigureConventions ( ModelConfigurationBuilder b) { b. Properties < decimal >(). HavePrecision ( 18 , 2 ); // global convention b. Properties < string >(). HaveMaxLength ( 256 ); } } builder. Services . AddDbContext < ShopDb >(o => o . UseSqlServer (builder. Configuration . GetConnectionString ( \"Shop\" ), sql => sql. EnableRetryOnFailure ()) . UseQueryTrackingBehavior ( QueryTrackingBehavior . TrackAll )); Registration Lifetime Use AddDbContext<T> Scoped (one per request) Normal web requests AddDbContextPool<T> Scoped, but instances reused from a pool (state reset) High-throughput APIs: avoids setup cost AddDbContextFactory<T> / AddPooledDbContextFactory<T> Factory is singleton; you create contexts Background services, Blazor Server, parallel work DbContext is not thread-safe and should be short-lived Hold onto this mental model: one context = one unit of work = one request (or one message, or one job) — never longer. Never share a single context across threads (no Task.WhenAll on operations sharing the same context), never make one a singleton, and don't keep one alive for minutes at a time, because its change tracker only ever grows while entities stay attached to it, and a long-lived context slowly accumulates tracked entities nobody's using anymore."},{"c":"efcore-fundamentals","s":"entities-and-conventions-what-ef-core-infers-without-being-t","h":"Entities and conventions: what EF Core infers without being told","t":"# Entities and conventions: what EF Core infers without being told Beginner EF Core infers a surprising amount of the model purely by convention — shape and naming tell it what it needs to know, without a single attribute or Fluent API call: Convention Result Property named Id or <Type>Id Primary key; int / long → identity column, Guid → sequential GUID generated client-side Non-nullable reference types ( string ) / value types NOT NULL columns; string? / int? → nullable Navigation property + <Nav>Id property Foreign key relationship DbSet<Order> Orders Table name Orders List<OrderLine> navigation One-to-many string nvarchar(max) on SQL Server (configure a max length!) decimal decimal(18,2) with a warning; configure precision Enum Stored as int (configure HasConversion<string>() to store names) C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class Customer { public int Id { get ; private set ; } public required string Email { get ; set ; } public required string Name { get ; set ; } public List < Order > Orders { get ; } = []; // collection navigation } public sealed class Order { public int Id { get ; private set ; } public int CustomerId { get ; private set ; } // FK property public Customer Customer { get ; private set ; } = null !; // reference navigation public OrderStatus Status { get ; private set ; } public DateTimeOffset CreatedAt { get ; private set ; } public List < OrderLine > Lines { get ; } = []; public byte [] RowVersion { get ; private set ; } = []; } Notice every setter above is private . EF Core can still set private setters and backing fields , and construct entities through constructors with parameters , specifically so entity classes can protect their own invariants instead of exposing every property as a freely settable public field — important groundwork for DDD , where an entity is supposed to enforce its own business rules rather than trusting every caller to."},{"c":"efcore-fundamentals","s":"configuration-attributes-vs-fluent-api","h":"Configuration: attributes vs Fluent API","t":"# Configuration: attributes vs Fluent API Intermediate Diagram Copy Which to reach for first: simple, obvious constraint ──▶ data annotation relationship, conversion, ──▶ Fluent API (IEntityTypeConfiguration<T>) keeping the domain clean Data annotations Fluent API Where Attributes on entity classes ( [Key] , [Required] , [MaxLength] , [Table] , [Column] , [Index] ) OnModelCreating / IEntityTypeConfiguration<T> Power Common cases Everything (keys, relationships, conversions, owned types, filters, seeding…) Entity purity Persistence concerns on domain classes Keeps the domain model clean Precedence Lower Fluent API wins The \"entity purity\" row is worth taking seriously if you're building toward a clean domain model: an attribute like [MaxLength(256)] on a property is a persistence concern — it describes a database column, not a business rule — sitting directly inside a class that's meant to represent a business concept. Fluent API configuration, kept in a separate IEntityTypeConfiguration<T> class, lets the entity itself stay free of that kind of noise entirely: C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class OrderConfiguration : IEntityTypeConfiguration < Order > { public void Configure ( EntityTypeBuilder < Order > b) { b. ToTable ( \"Orders\" , \"sales\" ); b. HasKey (o => o. Id ); b. Property (o => o. Status ). HasConversion < string >(). HasMaxLength ( 20 ); b. Property (o => o. RowVersion ). IsRowVersion (); // concurrency token b. HasIndex (o => new { o. CustomerId , o. CreatedAt }); b. HasOne (o => o. Customer ). WithMany (c => c. Orders ). HasForeignKey (o => o. CustomerId ). OnDelete ( DeleteBehavior . Restrict ); b. HasMany (o => o. Lines ). WithOne (). HasForeignKey ( \"OrderId\" ). OnDelete ( DeleteBehavior . Cascade ); b. Navigation (o => o. Lines ). UsePropertyAccessMode ( PropertyAccessMode . Field ); // map a private backing field } }"},{"c":"efcore-fundamentals","s":"keys-and-value-generation","h":"Keys and value generation","t":"# Keys and value generation Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy b. HasKey (x => x. Id ); // single key b. HasKey (x => new { x. OrderId , x. LineNo }); // composite key b. HasAlternateKey (x => x. Sku ); // unique, can be an FK target b. Property (x => x. Id ). UseIdentityColumn (); // IDENTITY (default for int) b. Property (x => x. Id ). HasDefaultValueSql ( \"NEWSEQUENTIALID()\" ); b. Property (x => x. CreatedAt ). HasDefaultValueSql ( \"SYSUTCDATETIME()\" ); b. Property (x => x. Total ). HasComputedColumnSql ( \"[Quantity] * [UnitPrice]\" , stored: true ); Strongly typed IDs ( OrderId record struct) — which make it impossible to accidentally pass a CustomerId where an OrderId was expected, since they're different types — are mapped with a value converter: b.Property(o => o.Id).HasConversion(id => id.Value, v => new OrderId(v)); ."},{"c":"efcore-fundamentals","s":"relationships","h":"Relationships","t":"# Relationships Intermediate # One-to-many C# Run ▶ Copy modelBuilder. Entity < Order >() . HasOne (o => o. Customer ). WithMany (c => c. Orders ) . HasForeignKey (o => o. CustomerId ) . IsRequired () . OnDelete ( DeleteBehavior . Restrict ); # One-to-one C# Run ▶ Copy modelBuilder. Entity < Customer >(). HasOne (c => c. Profile ). WithOne (p => p. Customer ). HasForeignKey < CustomerProfile >(p => p. CustomerId ); # Many-to-many C# Run ▶ Copy public class Product { public int Id { get ; set ; } public List < Category > Categories { get ; } = []; } public class Category { public int Id { get ; set ; } public List < Product > Products { get ; } = []; } // EF Core 5+ creates the join table ProductCategory automatically. // To add payload columns (e.g. SortOrder), map an explicit join entity: modelBuilder. Entity < Product >(). HasMany (p => p. Categories ). WithMany (c => c. Products ) . UsingEntity < ProductCategory >(j => j. Property (pc => pc. SortOrder ). HasDefaultValue ( 0 )); # Delete behaviours DeleteBehavior When the principal is deleted Cascade Dependents deleted (default for required relationships) Restrict / NoAction Error if dependents exist SetNull FK set to NULL (default for optional relationships) ClientCascade / ClientSetNull Done by EF for tracked entities only SQL Server rejects multiple cascade paths; set Restrict where needed."},{"c":"efcore-fundamentals","s":"owned-types-complex-types-and-value-conversions","h":"Owned types, complex types and value conversions","t":"# Owned types, complex types and value conversions Advanced Recall from Type system that value objects like Address or Money are meant to be compared by their contents, not by identity — they have no primary key of their own, and EF Core has dedicated mapping strategies specifically for that shape: C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed record Address ( string Street , string City , string PostalCode , string Country ); public sealed record Money ( decimal Amount , string Currency ); // Owned type: columns inline in the owner's table (ShippingAddress_Street, …) or a separate table b. OwnsOne (o => o. ShippingAddress , a => { a. Property (x => x. Country ). HasMaxLength ( 2 ); }); b. OwnsMany (c => c. Addresses ); // separate table with an FK // Complex type (EF Core 8): true value semantics; can't be null in EF 8, nullable since EF 9/10 b. ComplexProperty (o => o. Total , m => { m. Property (x => x. Amount ). HasColumnName ( \"TotalAmount\" ); }); // JSON columns: store a nested object/collection as JSON in one column, still queryable b. OwnsOne (p => p. Specs , s => s. ToJson ()); // Value conversions: custom type ↔ column type b. Property (p => p. Tags ). HasConversion ( v => string . Join ( ',' , v), v => v. Split ( ',' , StringSplitOptions . RemoveEmptyEntries ). ToList ()); Other modelling features worth knowing exist, even without the full depth here: inheritance mapping (TPH: one table with a discriminator, the default and fastest; TPT: a table per type; TPC: a table per concrete type), shadow properties (tracked in the model but absent from the class, e.g. UpdatedAt ), keyless entities for views, table splitting , temporal tables , and primitive collections ( List<int> stored as JSON, EF 8)."},{"c":"efcore-fundamentals","s":"change-tracking-and-savechanges-how-ef-core-knows-what-to-wr","h":"Change tracking and SaveChanges: how EF Core knows what to write","t":"# Change tracking and SaveChanges: how EF Core knows what to write Beginner Here's the mechanism underneath \"EF Core figures out what changed\": every entity a context loads or attaches gets tagged with a state , and that state is what decides what SaveChanges() eventually does with it: State Meaning On SaveChanges Added New, not in the DB INSERT Unchanged Loaded, not modified nothing Modified Loaded, some properties changed UPDATE (only changed columns) Deleted Marked for removal DELETE Detached Not tracked nothing C# Run ▶ Copy // Create var order = new Order (customerId, lines); db. Orders . Add (order); // state: Added // Update: load, change, save. EF detects the change by comparing with a snapshot. var product = await db. Products . FindAsync ([id], ct); // FindAsync checks the tracker first, then the DB product!. Price = 24.99m ; // state becomes Modified on DetectChanges // Delete db. Products . Remove (product); var rows = await db. SaveChangesAsync (ct); // ONE transaction, batched SQL; returns rows affected Notice that nothing in the product!.Price = 24.99m; line told EF Core \"this changed\" — there's no explicit notification. The :::deep box below explains exactly how it figures that out anyway. Disconnected scenarios — an API endpoint receives a DTO, and there's no entity already loaded into this context's tracker — need a different approach: load-then-update (clear, safe, costs an extra read), or attach a stub entity and mark specific properties modified directly (fewer queries, more care required, since you're asserting what changed rather than letting EF Core detect it): C# Run ▶ Copy db. Products . Attach ( new Product { Id = dto. Id }). Property (p => p. Price ). CurrentValue = dto. Price ; // UPDATE only Price // or set-based without tracking at all (EF 7+): await db. Products . Where (p => p. Id == dto. Id ). ExecuteUpdateAsync (s => s. SetProperty (p => p. Price , dto. Price ), ct); How change detection works When an entity is tracked, EF stores a snapshot of its original property values the moment it's loaded. DetectChanges() — called automatically by SaveChanges , Entries() and others — walks every tracked entity and compares its current values against that stored snapshot, property by property. That comparison work is exactly why tracking thousands of entities is costly: every SaveChanges() call has to diff all of them, whether or not they actually changed, which is why read-only queries should use AsNoTracking() or projections to skip the snapshot and diffing entirely. db.ChangeTracker.DebugView.LongView shows exactly what's tracked, useful the first time this mechanism's cost surprises you in a profiler."},{"c":"efcore-fundamentals","s":"migrations-evolving-the-schema-in-step-with-the-model","h":"Migrations: evolving the schema in step with the model","t":"# Migrations: evolving the schema in step with the model Beginner Migrations turn a change in your C# model into a versioned, reviewable change to the actual database schema — the bridge between \"I added a property\" and \"there's now a column for it.\" Shell Copy dotnet tool install --global dotnet-ef dotnet ef migrations add AddOrderStatus -p src/Shop.Infrastructure -s src/Shop.Api # -p: project with DbContext, -s: startup project dotnet ef database update # apply to the dev database dotnet ef migrations remove # undo the last unapplied migration dotnet ef migrations script --idempotent -o deploy.sql # SQL script for production dotnet ef migrations bundle -o efbundle --self-contained # executable that applies migrations Each migration carries Up() and Down() methods plus an updated model snapshot — EF Core's own record of what the model looked like at that point, which is what lets the next migration be computed as a diff against it. The database itself records which migrations have already been applied in a table called __EFMigrationsHistory , so running database update twice in a row is safe — it simply applies nothing the second time. C# Run ▶ Copy public partial class AddOrderStatus : Migration { protected override void Up ( MigrationBuilder m) { m. AddColumn < string >(name: \"Status\" , table: \"Orders\" , maxLength: 20 , nullable: false , defaultValue: \"Placed\" ); m. Sql ( \"UPDATE Orders SET Status = 'Shipped' WHERE ShippedAt IS NOT NULL\" ); // data migration (edit by hand when needed) } protected override void Down ( MigrationBuilder m) => m. DropColumn (name: \"Status\" , table: \"Orders\" ); } # Applying migrations in production Approach Notes Idempotent SQL script in the CD pipeline Reviewable by DBAs; most controlled Migration bundle executed by the pipeline Self-contained, no SDK needed Database.MigrateAsync() at app startup Simple, but races with multiple instances , needs DDL permissions for the app identity, and slows startup. OK for dev and demos That third row's warning is worth tracing through concretely: with three replicas starting up simultaneously, all three could attempt MigrateAsync() at roughly the same moment, racing to apply the same migration — not a safe pattern for anything beyond a single-instance dev setup. The deeper discipline for zero-downtime deploys is writing backward-compatible migrations, since old and new code genuinely run side by side during a rolling deployment: add nullable columns first, backfill them, and only make a column required in a later release once every instance is running the new code; never rename a column in one step — add the new one, copy the data across, switch the code over, then drop the old one in a later release entirely. This sequencing is known as the expand/contract pattern. # Code-first vs database-first Code-first (above): the model is the source of truth. Database-first / reverse engineering : dotnet ef dbcontext scaffold \"<cs>\" Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer -o Models generates entities from an existing schema. Common with legacy databases or DBA-owned schemas."},{"c":"efcore-fundamentals","s":"seeding-data","h":"Seeding data","t":"# Seeding data Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy // Model seed data (part of migrations): good for static reference data modelBuilder. Entity < Country >(). HasData ( new Country { Code = \"DE\" , Name = \"Germany\" }, new Country { Code = \"FR\" , Name = \"France\" }); // EF 9+: UseSeeding / UseAsyncSeeding: runs on EnsureCreated / Migrate; good for dev/test data optionsBuilder. UseAsyncSeeding ( async (ctx, _, ct) => { if (! await ctx. Set < Product >(). AnyAsync (ct)) { ctx. Set < Product >(). Add ( new Product { Name = \"Keyboard\" , Price = 49m }); await ctx. SaveChangesAsync (ct); } });"},{"c":"efcore-fundamentals","s":"providers","h":"Providers","t":"# Providers Advanced Database Provider package SQL Server / Azure SQL Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer PostgreSQL Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL SQLite Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite MySQL / MariaDB Pomelo.EntityFrameworkCore.MySql Cosmos DB Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Cosmos (document DB; very different query capabilities) Oracle Oracle.EntityFrameworkCore In-memory Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.InMemory : testing only, and not recommended ; see Integration testing Q What is DbContext and what lifetime should it have? The EF Core session with the database: it owns the connection, the model and the change tracker, and implements the Unit of Work and Repository patterns. Register it as scoped (one per request or unit of work). It's not thread-safe and shouldn't be long-lived; use IDbContextFactory for background or parallel work. Q What's the difference between AddDbContext and AddDbContextPool? Both give scoped contexts. Pooling reuses DbContext instances from a pool (resetting their state) instead of constructing new ones, reducing allocation and setup cost in high-throughput apps. The context then must not hold custom per-request state in fields. Q Data annotations or Fluent API? Fluent API (often in IEntityTypeConfiguration<T> classes) is more powerful, keeps persistence concerns out of the domain model, and takes precedence. Annotations are convenient for simple cases. Many teams use Fluent API exclusively. Q Explain EF Core's entity states. Added (INSERT on save), Unchanged (no action), Modified (UPDATE of changed columns), Deleted (DELETE) and Detached (not tracked). EF detects modifications by comparing current values with snapshots taken when entities were loaded or attached. Q How do migrations work and how do you apply them in production? dotnet ef migrations add generates a migration class (Up/Down) from the difference between the model and the last snapshot; applied migrations are recorded in __EFMigrationsHistory. In production, generate an idempotent SQL script or a migration bundle and run it in the deployment pipeline rather than calling Migrate() at startup across many instances. Keep migrations backward-compatible for zero-downtime deploys. Q What are owned types and when do you use them? Types without their own identity that belong to an owner entity (value objects like Address or Money). They're stored inline as columns in the owner's table (OwnsOne), in a separate table (OwnsMany), or as JSON (ToJson). EF 8 complex types are an alternative with value semantics. Q How do you configure a many-to-many relationship? Add collection navigations on both sides; EF Core 5+ creates the join table automatically. Use UsingEntity with an explicit join entity when you need extra columns on the relationship. Q What does SaveChanges do? Runs DetectChanges, orders the pending INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE commands to respect FKs, batches them, executes them in a single transaction (unless one is already active), propagates generated keys, and resets entity states to Unchanged. It throws DbUpdateException or DbUpdateConcurrencyException on failure."},{"c":"efcore-fundamentals","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading EF Core overview Creating and configuring a model Relationships Migrations overview Book: Entity Framework Core in Action (2nd ed.) by Jon P Smith."},{"c":"efcore-fundamentals","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run in a project that references Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite (the EF Core in-memory relational provider closest to the chapter's SQL-Server examples — dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite ). It checks entity states and SaveChanges , change detection via snapshot comparison, AsNoTracking actually skipping tracking, and OnDelete(DeleteBehavior.Cascade) actually cascading. C# Run ▶ Copy using Microsoft . EntityFrameworkCore ; using Microsoft . EntityFrameworkCore . ChangeTracking ; var options = new DbContextOptionsBuilder < ShopDb >(). UseSqlite ( \"DataSource=:memory:\" ). Options ; using var db = new ShopDb (options); await db. Database . OpenConnectionAsync (); // keep the in-memory SQLite connection alive for the whole run await db. Database . EnsureCreatedAsync (); // --- Added entity, then SaveChanges turns it into Unchanged (an INSERT happened) --- var customer = new Customer { Email = \"a@b.com\" }; db. Customers . Add (customer); if (db. Entry (customer). State != EntityState . Added ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: a newly Added entity should be State.Added before saving\" ); await db. SaveChangesAsync (); if (db. Entry (customer). State != EntityState . Unchanged ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: after SaveChanges, a saved entity should be Unchanged\" ); if (customer. Id == 0 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: the generated identity key should have been populated after insert\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: Add -> SaveChanges moved the entity Added -> Unchanged and generated Id={customer.Id}\" ); // --- Change detection: mutating a tracked property flips state to Modified, by comparison with a snapshot --- customer. Email = \"changed@b.com\" ; var entry = db. Entry (customer); if (entry. State != EntityState . Modified ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: mutating a tracked property should flip state to Modified\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: changing a property on a tracked entity was detected as Modified (snapshot comparison)\" ); await db. SaveChangesAsync (); // --- AsNoTracking really doesn't track: the returned entity isn't in the change tracker --- var untracked = await db. Customers . AsNoTracking (). FirstAsync (c => c. Id == customer. Id ); var isTracked = db. ChangeTracker . Entries < Customer >(). Any (e => ReferenceEquals (e. Entity , untracked)); if (isTracked) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: AsNoTracking() should not add the entity to the change tracker\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: AsNoTracking() returned an entity that the change tracker has no entry for\" ); // --- Cascade delete: deleting the parent removes dependent rows (OnDelete(Cascade)) --- var order = new Order { CustomerId = customer. Id }; order. Lines . Add ( new OrderLine { Sku = \"KB-01\" }); db. Orders . Add (order); await db. SaveChangesAsync (); db. Customers . Remove (customer); await db. SaveChangesAsync (); var remainingLines = await db. OrderLines . CountAsync (l => l. OrderId == order. Id ); if (remainingLines != 0 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: deleting the customer should have cascaded to delete its order's lines too\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: deleting the parent (Customer) cascaded through Order to OrderLine, as OnDelete(Cascade) configured\" ); // Not runtime-verifiable here — see Further reading: EF Core overview, Migrations overview (Microsoft Learn) // (migration tooling, production deployment strategy, and multi-provider behavior aren't single-process runtime facts) class Customer { public int Id { get ; set ; } public string Email { get ; set ; } = \"\" ; public List < Order > Orders { get ; } = []; } class Order { public int Id { get ; set ; } public int CustomerId { get ; set ; } public List < OrderLine > Lines { get ; } = []; } class OrderLine { public int Id { get ; set ; } public int OrderId { get ; set ; } public string Sku { get ; set ; } = \"\" ; } class ShopDb ( DbContextOptions < ShopDb > options) : DbContext (options) { public DbSet < Customer > Customers => Set < Customer >()"},{"c":"efcore-querying","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR A LINQ query against DbSet<T> doesn't touch the database until a terminating operator ( ToListAsync , FirstOrDefaultAsync , etc.) runs it — everything before that just builds up a description of the query. Turn on SQL logging or call .ToQueryString() so you can see what EF Core actually sends, instead of guessing. For read-only queries, skip tracking ( AsNoTracking() ) and project straight into a DTO with Select — it's faster and sidesteps N+1 for free. N+1 (one query, then one more per row) is the most common EF Core slowdown. Fix it with projection, eager loading ( Include ), or batching ( Contains ). Including multiple collections in one query can multiply rows instead of adding them (cartesian explosion); AsSplitQuery() trades that for extra round trips. EF Core refuses to silently run C# it can't translate to SQL — write translatable expressions, or filter in memory explicitly, after a server-side filter has already cut the data down."},{"c":"efcore-querying","s":"from-linq-to-sql-and-the-one-moment-it-actually-runs","h":"From LINQ to SQL, and the one moment it actually runs","t":"# From LINQ to SQL, and the one moment it actually runs Beginner C# Run ▶ Copy var recent = await db. Orders . Where (o => o. CustomerId == customerId && o. CreatedAt >= since) . OrderByDescending (o => o. CreatedAt ) . Select (o => new OrderSummary (o. Id , o. CreatedAt , o. Lines . Sum (l => l. Quantity * l. UnitPrice ))) . Take ( 20 ) . ToListAsync (ct); SQL Copy -- what EF Core sends (SQL Server) SELECT TOP ( @__p_2 ) [o].[Id], [o].[CreatedAt], ( SELECT COALESCE( SUM (CAST([l].[Quantity] AS decimal( 18 , 2 )) * [l].[UnitPrice]), 0 . 0 ) FROM [OrderLines] AS [l] WHERE [o].[Id] = [l].[OrderId]) FROM [Orders] AS [o] WHERE [o].[CustomerId] = @__customerId_0 AND [o].[CreatedAt] >= @__since_1 ORDER BY [o].[CreatedAt] DESC The pipeline behind that transformation: your LINQ expression becomes an expression tree — exactly the data structure LINQ covered — which EF's query pipeline compiles into provider-specific SQL, with captured variables turned into safe parameters , executed via ADO.NET, and the resulting rows materialised back into objects (tracked, by default, unless told otherwise). Here's the detail worth holding onto for the rest of this chapter: nothing above actually touches the database until it hits a terminating operator — ToListAsync , ToArrayAsync , FirstOrDefaultAsync , SingleAsync , CountAsync , AnyAsync , SumAsync , MaxAsync , ToDictionaryAsync , ExecuteUpdateAsync , ExecuteDeleteAsync , or await foreach over AsAsyncEnumerable() . Everything before that point — every .Where , every .OrderBy — is just building up a description of a query, not running one. This deferred-execution behaviour is exactly what LINQ warned generalises to bugs where a query runs again, against changed data, because something re-enumerated it."},{"c":"efcore-querying","s":"see-the-sql-first-you-cant-tune-what-you-cant-see","h":"See the SQL first — you can't tune what you can't see","t":"# See the SQL first — you can't tune what you can't see Beginner C# Run ▶ Copy // 1. Log SQL (development) builder. Services . AddDbContext < ShopDb >(o => o. UseSqlServer (cs) . LogTo ( Console . WriteLine , LogLevel . Information ) . EnableSensitiveDataLogging () // shows parameter values: DEV ONLY . EnableDetailedErrors ()); // or via configuration: \"Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Database.Command\": \"Information\" // 2. Inspect a single query without running it var sql = db. Orders . Where (o => o. Total > 100 ). ToQueryString (); // 3. Tag queries so you can find them in Query Store / traces var q = db. Orders . TagWith ( \"Dashboard: recent big orders\" ). Where (o => o. Total > 1000 ); In production, OpenTelemetry EF Core instrumentation ( Observability ) records every query as a span with its duration, and Query Store or Azure SQL Insights shows the slowest statements — the same \"trace it before guessing\" discipline that Performance engineering applies everywhere else."},{"c":"efcore-querying","s":"tracking-vs-no-tracking-paying-for-a-feature-you-often-dont-","h":"Tracking vs no-tracking: paying for a feature you often don't need","t":"# Tracking vs no-tracking: paying for a feature you often don't need Beginner Recall from EF Core fundamentals that tracking means keeping a snapshot of every loaded entity so SaveChanges() can detect what changed. For a query that's purely reading data to send to a client — never going to be saved back — that snapshot is pure overhead: Tracking (default) AsNoTracking() AsNoTrackingWithIdentityResolution() Snapshot kept Yes No No (identity map only for the query) Can SaveChanges modifications Yes No No Duplicate rows → same instance Yes No: separate objects Yes Speed / memory Slower Fastest In between Use for Load-modify-save Read-only queries Read-only with repeated related entities C# Run ▶ Copy var products = await db. Products . AsNoTracking (). Where (p => p. IsActive ). ToListAsync (ct); You can flip the default for an entire context instead of annotating every query ( UseQueryTrackingBehavior(NoTracking) ) and opt back in with AsTracking() on the specific queries that need it — a pattern that fits read-heavy APIs particularly well, since most of their queries never modify anything."},{"c":"efcore-querying","s":"projection-the-single-biggest-win-in-this-chapter","h":"Projection: the single biggest win in this chapter","t":"# Projection: the single biggest win in this chapter Beginner C# Run ▶ Copy // ✘ Loads EVERY column of Orders, Customer AND Lines, tracks them all, then the API maps to a DTO var orders = await db. Orders . Include (o => o. Customer ). Include (o => o. Lines ). ToListAsync (ct); var dtos = orders. Select (o => new OrderSummary (o. Id , o. Customer . Name , o. Lines . Count )); // ✔ SQL selects only 3 values per row; no tracking (projections into non-entities aren't tracked); no Include needed var dtos2 = await db. Orders . Select (o => new OrderSummary (o. Id , o. Customer . Name , o. Lines . Count )) . ToListAsync (ct); Notice everything the second version gets for free, just by moving the Select earlier: fewer columns actually travel over the wire, nothing gets tracked (projections into plain DTOs are never tracked at all), and — this is the part worth sitting with — navigations referenced inside a Select get translated into joins or subqueries by EF Core itself , which sidesteps the N+1 problem the next section covers entirely, without you having to think about Include at all. Projecting with Select into DTOs should be the default for every read endpoint you write. Mapping libraries can project too — AutoMapper's ProjectTo , Mapperly's IQueryable projections — if you'd rather not hand-write the DTO construction."},{"c":"efcore-querying","s":"loading-related-data-four-strategies-one-clearly-preferred","h":"Loading related data: four strategies, one clearly preferred","t":"# Loading related data: four strategies, one clearly preferred Intermediate Strategy How SQL When Eager .Include(o => o.Lines).ThenInclude(l => l.Product) JOIN(s) in the same query You need entities plus related data, typically to modify them Projection Navigations inside Select JOIN/subquery, only needed columns Read models / DTOs ( preferred for reads ) Explicit await db.Entry(order).Collection(o => o.Lines).LoadAsync() A separate query, on demand Conditionally loading for an already-loaded entity Lazy Virtual navigations + proxies ( UseLazyLoadingProxies() ) A query when the property is first accessed Rarely; hides N+1 C# Run ▶ Copy // Filtered include (EF 5+) var customer = await db. Customers . Include (c => c. Orders . Where (o => o. Status == OrderStatus . Open ). OrderByDescending (o => o. CreatedAt ). Take ( 5 )) . SingleAsync (c => c. Id == id, ct); That \"rarely; hides N+1\" note in the Lazy row isn't a footnote — it's exactly why the next section exists."},{"c":"efcore-querying","s":"the-n-1-problem-the-single-most-common-ef-core-performance-b","h":"The N+1 problem: the single most common EF Core performance bug","t":"# The N+1 problem: the single most common EF Core performance bug Intermediate The shape of this bug: 1 query loads a list, then N more queries — one per item in that list — load each item's related data: C# Run ▶ Copy // Lazy loading enabled: var orders = await db. Orders . Where (o => o. CreatedAt >= today). ToListAsync (ct); // 1 query → 200 orders foreach ( var o in orders) Console . WriteLine (o. Customer . Name ); // +200 queries (one per order) That's 201 round trips instead of 1. Each one might take only 1–2 ms on its own, which is exactly why this bug slips past code review — it looks harmless in isolation. But 201 of them back-to-back adds up to hundreds of milliseconds, and it only gets worse as the order count grows. It also happens without lazy loading enabled at all , any time you call the database inside a loop: C# Run ▶ Copy foreach ( var id in productIds) prices[id] = await db. Products . Where (p => p. Id == id). Select (p => p. Price ). SingleAsync (ct); // ✘ N queries var prices2 = await db. Products . Where (p => productIds. Contains (p. Id )) // ✔ 1 query: WHERE Id IN (…) . ToDictionaryAsync (p => p. Id , p => p. Price , ct); Detect it: SQL logs show the exact same statement shape repeating many times in a row; a trace shows dozens of identical, tiny database spans nested under one request; dotnet-counters shows an unusually high queries-per-request count for an endpoint that only looks like it returns one thing. Fix it: projection (as above — the usual answer), eager loading with Include , batching with Contains (as the second fix shows), or a DataLoader if you're serving this through GraphQL."},{"c":"efcore-querying","s":"cartesian-explosion-and-split-queries","h":"Cartesian explosion and split queries","t":"# Cartesian explosion and split queries Advanced A related but distinct trap: including multiple collection navigations in a single query doesn't add rows, it multiplies them, because a SQL join flattens nested collections into one wide, repeated result set: C# Run ▶ Copy var blogs = await db. Blogs . Include (b => b. Posts ). Include (b => b. Contributors ). ToListAsync (); // 1 blog × 100 posts × 20 contributors = 2,000 rows returned, with blog columns duplicated in every row Here's why: SQL must emit one row for every post-contributor pair, repeating the blog's own columns in each row. 100 posts × 20 contributors = 2,000 rows, each one carrying a full, duplicate copy of the blog's data — a lot of redundant bytes for what's conceptually one blog with two separate lists attached to it. C# Run ▶ Copy var blogs2 = await db. Blogs . Include (b => b. Posts ). Include (b => b. Contributors ) . AsSplitQuery () // 3 separate SQL queries: blogs, posts, contributors . ToListAsync (); // configure globally: UseSqlServer(cs, o => o.UseQuerySplittingBehavior(QuerySplittingBehavior.SplitQuery)) AsSplitQuery() swaps one problem for a usually smaller one: more round trips (three queries instead of one), and the loss of a single consistent snapshot — the data could change between those three queries unless you wrap them in an explicit transaction — in exchange for not multiplying rows. EF Core even logs a warning when it detects multiple collection includes in one query, because this trade-off is easy to hit by accident."},{"c":"efcore-querying","s":"client-evaluation-and-translation-errors","h":"Client evaluation and translation errors","t":"# Client evaluation and translation errors Intermediate EF Core can only translate what it actually understands into SQL: the standard LINQ operators, many string / Math / DateTime members, EF.Functions.Like , provider-specific functions, and any functions you've explicitly mapped yourself. C# Run ▶ Copy var q = db. Products . Where (p => IsBargain (p)); // ✘ InvalidOperationException: could not be translated static bool IsBargain ( Product p) => p. Price < p. ListPrice * 0.5m ; var q2 = db. Products . Where (p => p. Price < p. ListPrice * 0.5m ); // ✔ inline the logic as an expression EF Core 3+ throws here instead of silently pulling everything into memory and filtering with plain C#. EF Core 1–2 did exactly that by default, and Microsoft's own breaking-changes notes for 3.0 call out exactly this scenario — a filter that looks fine on a small test table, then quietly loads an entire production table once it grows (see Further reading). The final Select is a partial exception: EF evaluates anything it can't translate on the client, but only after it has already fetched just the columns it needs from the server — that's fine, because by then the expensive filtering has already happened in SQL. If you must filter in memory, do it explicitly, after a server-side filter has already cut the data down: .Where(serverSide).AsAsyncEnumerable().Where(clientSide) . Never let the untranslatable filter be the only thing standing between you and a full table scan. For reusable query logic, write expression-returning helpers ( static Expression<Func<Product,bool>> IsBargain => p => … ) or extension methods on IQueryable<T> , which stay translatable because they hand EF Core an expression tree rather than a compiled method it can't see inside."},{"c":"efcore-querying","s":"paging-counting-and-existence","h":"Paging, counting and existence","t":"# Paging, counting and existence Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy // Offset paging: always ORDER BY a unique key for deterministic pages var page = await db. Products . OrderBy (p => p. Name ). ThenBy (p => p. Id ) . Skip ((pageNo - 1 ) * size). Take (size) . Select (p => new ProductDto (p. Id , p. Name , p. Price )). ToListAsync (ct); // Keyset paging: constant cost on deep pages var next = await db. Products . Where (p => p. Id > lastSeenId). OrderBy (p => p. Id ). Take (size). ToListAsync (ct); bool any = await db. Orders . AnyAsync (o => o. CustomerId == id, ct); // EXISTS: faster than CountAsync() > 0 Both paging styles here map directly onto the offset-vs-cursor tradeoff from API design . One subtlety worth flagging on its own: a total count on a large, filtered table is itself an expensive query, essentially a full scan to count matches. For infinite-scroll UIs that only need to know \"is there more,\" fetching size + 1 rows and checking whether the extra one came back is usually far cheaper than computing an exact total."},{"c":"efcore-querying","s":"raw-sql-in-ef-core","h":"Raw SQL in EF Core","t":"# Raw SQL in EF Core Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy // Entities from SQL (composable: you can add LINQ on top) var top = await db. Products . FromSql ( $\"SELECT * FROM Products WITH (INDEX(IX_Products_Category)) WHERE CategoryId = {categoryId}\" ) . Where (p => p. IsActive ). OrderBy (p => p. Name ). ToListAsync (ct); // Arbitrary types (EF 8): unmapped DTOs, scalars var stats = await db. Database . SqlQuery < CategoryStats >( $\"\"\" SELECT CategoryId, COUNT(*) AS Products, AVG(Price) AS AvgPrice FROM Products GROUP BY CategoryId \"\"\" ). ToListAsync (ct); // Commands var n = await db. Database . ExecuteSqlAsync ( $\"UPDATE Products SET IsActive = 0 WHERE DiscontinuedAt < {cutoff}\" , ct); FromSql , SqlQuery and ExecuteSql all take a FormattableString — notice the $\"...\" syntax above — so interpolated values become real parameters under the hood, the same safe mechanism ADO.NET & Dapper insisted on. The …Raw variants instead take a plain string, and never concatenate user input into one — doing so reopens exactly the SQL injection hole parameterisation exists to close."},{"c":"efcore-querying","s":"async-and-cancellation","h":"Async and cancellation","t":"# Async and cancellation Beginner Use the async operators ( ToListAsync , SaveChangesAsync …) in web apps and pass CancellationToken so abandoned requests cancel their SQL rather than running to completion for nobody. Don't run concurrent operations on one context — a DbContext isn't thread-safe, as EF Core fundamentals warned. Stream genuinely large results with AsAsyncEnumerable() instead of buffering the entire result set in memory with ToListAsync() ."},{"c":"efcore-querying","s":"performance-techniques","h":"Performance techniques","t":"# Performance techniques Advanced Technique What it does DbContext pooling ( AddDbContextPool ) Reuses context instances, cutting per-request setup Compiled queries ( EF.CompileAsyncQuery ) Skips query compilation for very hot queries (EF already caches; this removes the lookup) Compiled models ( dotnet ef dbcontext optimize ) Faster startup for huge models; required for Native AOT ExecuteUpdate / ExecuteDelete Set-based writes without loading entities Batching SaveChanges batches statements automatically (tune MaxBatchSize ) AsNoTracking + projections Less work and memory per query Avoid Include chains for reads Project instead Indexes matching your queries HasIndex in the model; verify with execution plans Split queries Avoid cartesian explosion Connection resiliency EnableRetryOnFailure for transient Azure SQL errors Caching For hot, rarely changing reads ( Caching ) C# Run ▶ Copy private static readonly Func < ShopDb , int , CancellationToken , Task < ProductDto ?>> GetProductById = EF . CompileAsyncQuery (( ShopDb db, int id, CancellationToken ct) => db. Products . Where (p => p. Id == id). Select (p => new ProductDto (p. Id , p. Name , p. Price )). FirstOrDefault ()); var p = await GetProductById (db, 42 , ct);"},{"c":"efcore-querying","s":"checklist-for-a-slow-ef-core-endpoint","h":"Checklist for a slow EF Core endpoint","t":"# Checklist for a slow EF Core endpoint Intermediate Work through this in order — each step is cheap to check and most slow endpoints are caught well before step 10: Trace it : is the time in the database, in serialization, or in app code? Look at the SQL ( ToQueryString , logs, the trace span). Count queries per request : N+1? Queries in loops? Too many columns or rows? Project; page; filter on the server. Tracking unnecessarily? AsNoTracking . Cartesian explosion? Split queries. Check the execution plan : missing index, scans, key lookups, implicit conversions. Sync calls or .Result ? Make it async. Hot and stable data? Cache it. Bulk writes? ExecuteUpdate / ExecuteDelete or bulk copy. Q What is the N+1 problem in EF Core and how do you fix it? Loading a list with one query and then triggering an extra query per item to get related data, typically through lazy loading or querying inside a loop. Fix it with projections (Select into DTOs, translated into joins), eager loading (Include), or batching (WHERE Id IN via Contains). Detect it by logging SQL or inspecting traces. Q When should you use AsNoTracking? For read-only queries: entities aren't snapshot-tracked, which saves memory and CPU. Projections into DTOs are untracked automatically. Use AsNoTrackingWithIdentityResolution when the same related entity appears in many rows and you want shared instances. Q Eager vs explicit vs lazy loading? Eager loads related data in the same query via Include/ThenInclude. Explicit loads it later on demand with Entry(...).Collection/Reference().Load. Lazy loads automatically when a navigation property is first accessed (proxies), which easily causes N+1. For read endpoints, projection usually beats all three. Q What is a split query and when would you use it? AsSplitQuery makes EF Core load included collections with separate SQL statements instead of one big JOIN, avoiding cartesian explosion when including multiple collections. The trade-off is extra round trips and no single snapshot without a transaction. Q Why might an EF Core query throw 'could not be translated'? The LINQ contains something the provider can't convert to SQL, such as a call to a custom C# method in Where. Since EF Core 3, EF throws rather than silently evaluating on the client. Rewrite using translatable expressions, mapped DB functions, or filter in memory explicitly after a server-side filter. Q How do you see the SQL EF Core generates? ToQueryString() on a query, LogTo/logging the Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Database.Command category, EnableSensitiveDataLogging in development for parameter values, TagWith to label queries, and OpenTelemetry instrumentation or Query Store in production. Q How do you run raw SQL safely with EF Core? Use FromSql / SqlQuery / ExecuteSql with interpolated strings: they take FormattableString and turn interpolated values into DbParameters. Avoid the Raw variants with concatenated input. Q How would you speed up a hot, read-heavy EF Core query? Project into a DTO with only the needed columns, use no-tracking, make sure a supporting index exists (check the plan), consider a compiled query and DbContext pooling, cache the result if staleness is acceptable, and use keyset pagination for large lists."},{"c":"efcore-querying","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Efficient querying Loading related data Single vs split queries Advanced performance topics Breaking changes in EF Core 3.0: LINQ queries are no longer evaluated on the client"},{"c":"efcore-querying","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter The claims below that are pure C#/EF Core behavior are checked by running real queries against an in-memory SQLite database. Save as a console app ( dotnet new console , dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite ), then dotnet run . C# Run ▶ Copy using Microsoft . EntityFrameworkCore ; // SQLite's in-memory mode needs one open connection kept alive for the whole run. var connString = \"DataSource=file:verifydb?mode=memory&cache=shared\" ; await using var conn = new Microsoft . Data . Sqlite . SqliteConnection (connString); await conn. OpenAsync (); var opts = new DbContextOptionsBuilder < ShopDb >(). UseSqlite (conn). Options ; await using ( var seed = new ShopDb (opts)) { await seed. Database . EnsureCreatedAsync (); var c = new Customer { Name = \"Ada\" }; seed. Customers . Add (c); seed. Orders . AddRange ( new Order { Customer = c, Total = 10 }, new Order { Customer = c, Total = 20 }, new Order { Customer = c, Total = 30 }); await seed. SaveChangesAsync (); } // Claim: AsNoTracking() returned entities are not tracked (ChangeTracker has zero entries). await using ( var db = new ShopDb (opts)) { var orders = await db. Orders . AsNoTracking (). ToListAsync (); if (db. ChangeTracker . Entries (). Any ()) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: AsNoTracking still tracked entities\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: AsNoTracking() keeps the change tracker empty\" ); } // Claim: projecting with Select avoids tracking too, even without AsNoTracking. await using ( var db = new ShopDb (opts)) { var dtos = await db. Orders . Select (o => new { o. Id , o. Total }). ToListAsync (); if (db. ChangeTracker . Entries (). Any ()) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: projection was tracked\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: projecting into an anonymous type tracks nothing\" ); } // Claim: a query is not executed until a terminating operator runs (deferred execution). // Proof: build the query, THEN add a new row, THEN run it — if building ran the query early, // the new row would be missing from the result. await using ( var db = new ShopDb (opts)) { var query = db. Orders . Where (o => o. Total > 0 ); // just builds the query, no SQL yet var extra = new Order { CustomerId = 1 , Total = 999 }; db. Orders . Add (extra); await db. SaveChangesAsync (); var countBeforeExtra = 3 ; // seeded rows var list = await query. ToListAsync (); // SQL runs here, for the first time if (list. Count != countBeforeExtra + 1 ) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected {countBeforeExtra + 1} rows, got {list.Count}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: the query only ran at ToListAsync, after the extra row existed — not when it was built\" ); } // Claim: N+1's fix — batching with Contains issues one query and returns all matching rows. await using ( var db = new ShopDb (opts)) { var ids = await db. Orders . Select (o => o. Id ). ToListAsync (); var totalsBatched = await db. Orders . Where (o => ids. Contains (o. Id )) . ToDictionaryAsync (o => o. Id , o => o. Total ); if (totalsBatched. Count != ids. Count ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: batched query didn't return all rows\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: a single WHERE Id IN (...) query returns all rows in one round trip\" ); } Console . WriteLine ( \"All checks passed.\" ); // --- Minimal model, matching the chapter's examples --- class Customer { public int Id { get ; set ; } public string Name { get ; set ; } = \"\" ; public List < Order > Orders { get ; set ; } = new (); } class Order { public int Id { get ; set ; } public int CustomerId { get ; set ; } public Customer Customer { get ; set ; } = null !; public decimal Total { get ; set ; } } class ShopDb : DbContext { public ShopDb ( DbContextOptions < ShopDb > options) : base (options) { } public DbSet < Customer > Customers => Set < Customer >(); public DbSet < Order > Orders => Set < Order >(); } // Not runtime-verifiable here (needs a real SQL Server/Azure SQL instance, Query Store, or OpenTelemetry collector): // - exact generated T-SQL shown in this chapter (provider-"},{"c":"efcore-advanced","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR SaveChanges() is already transactional on its own; reach for an explicit transaction only when something wider needs to be atomic with it (several SaveChanges calls, raw SQL, Dapper on the same connection). Optimistic concurrency uses a version column, not locks: EF Core puts the original version in the UPDATE 's WHERE clause, and throws DbUpdateConcurrencyException if zero rows matched because someone else already changed the row. ExecuteUpdate / ExecuteDelete write straight to the database without loading entities first — fast, but they skip the change tracker and SaveChanges interceptors. Interceptors ( SaveChangesInterceptor , DbCommandInterceptor , etc.) let you add cross-cutting behavior — auditing, soft delete, outbox — without scattering calls through business code. Global query filters (soft delete, multi-tenancy) apply automatically to every query against that entity — watch for a required navigation pulling a filtered-out row back in through an INNER JOIN . Writing outbox rows in the same SaveChanges transaction as the business data is what makes \"save the order and record the event\" atomic, without needing a real distributed transaction."},{"c":"efcore-advanced","s":"optimistic-concurrency-catching-a-lost-update-without-ever-l","h":"Optimistic concurrency: catching a lost update without ever locking","t":"# Optimistic concurrency: catching a lost update without ever locking Intermediate Here's the concrete failure this section exists to prevent: two users both load order 42 at the same moment. Alice changes the shipping address and saves. Bob, still holding the version he loaded a moment earlier — before Alice's save — changes the status and saves too, and his save overwrites Alice's change with the old address he never actually touched. Nobody did anything wrong individually; the collision is purely a timing accident. This is a lost update , the same anomaly named in Relational fundamentals . Optimistic concurrency is built on a bet that pays off most of the time: conflicts like this are rare, so don't pay the cost of locking on every read — instead, detect a conflict only at the moment of writing, using a version column that changes on every update. C# Run ▶ Copy public class Order { public int Id { get ; set ; } public string ShippingAddress { get ; set ; } = \"\" ; [Timestamp] public byte [] RowVersion { get ; set ; } = []; // SQL Server rowversion: auto-incremented by the DB on each update } // Fluent: b.Property(o => o.RowVersion).IsRowVersion(); // Postgres: b.Property<uint>(\"xmin\").IsRowVersion(); (or a manually incremented Version property with IsConcurrencyToken()) EF Core adds the original version to the WHERE clause of the generated UPDATE : SQL Copy UPDATE Orders SET ShippingAddress = @p0 WHERE Id = @p1 AND RowVersion = @p2 ; -- 0 rows affected ⇒ someone else changed it Trace through what that WHERE clause actually buys you: if Bob's update runs after Alice's already committed, the row's RowVersion has already moved past what Bob loaded, so his UPDATE matches zero rows — not because the Id is wrong, but because the version he's asserting against no longer exists. EF Core throws DbUpdateConcurrencyException on exactly that outcome: C# Run ▶ Copy try { await db. SaveChangesAsync (ct); } catch ( DbUpdateConcurrencyException ex) { var entry = ex. Entries . Single (); var dbValues = await entry. GetDatabaseValuesAsync (ct); if (dbValues is null ) return Results . NotFound (); // deleted meanwhile // Options: (1) tell the client → 409/412 with current state; (2) client-wins: entry.OriginalValues.SetValues(dbValues) and retry; // (3) store-wins: entry.Reload(); (4) merge field by field return Results . Conflict ( new ProblemDetails { Title = \"Order was modified by someone else. Reload and try again.\" }); } In APIs, expose this same version as an ETag and require If-Match on updates — the exact mechanism covered end-to-end in API design , where the HTTP layer and this database mechanism turn out to be the same idea wearing two different outfits. Concurrency tokens for business invariants Optimistic concurrency isn't only about preventing overwritten edits — it also protects business invariants directly. Two requests both racing to reserve the last unit of stock will conflict on the product's own version column , so one of them loses the race, retries, and correctly sees zero stock left rather than both succeeding and overselling. It's a genuinely lighter-weight alternative to taking a pessimistic row lock for that same guarantee."},{"c":"efcore-advanced","s":"transactions-when-savechangess-own-transaction-isnt-enough","h":"Transactions: when SaveChanges's own transaction isn't enough","t":"# Transactions: when SaveChanges's own transaction isn't enough Intermediate SaveChanges is already transactional on its own — every change made in one call commits or rolls back together, with no extra code needed. You need an explicit transaction specifically when that single-call guarantee isn't wide enough, which happens when: several SaveChanges calls must be atomic together, you mix EF changes with raw SQL / ExecuteUpdate , you share a transaction with Dapper/ADO.NET on the same connection, you need a specific isolation level. C# Run ▶ Copy await using var tx = await db. Database . BeginTransactionAsync ( IsolationLevel . ReadCommitted , ct); try { db. Orders . Add (order); await db. SaveChangesAsync (ct); // order gets its Id await db. Products . Where (p => p. Id == order. ProductId ) . ExecuteUpdateAsync (s => s. SetProperty (p => p. Stock , p => p. Stock - order. Qty ), ct); await db. Database . GetDbConnection (). ExecuteAsync ( // Dapper on the same connection + transaction \"INSERT INTO AuditLog (Action, EntityId) VALUES ('OrderPlaced', @id)\" , new { id = order. Id }, tx. GetDbTransaction ()); await tx. CommitAsync (ct); } catch { await tx. RollbackAsync (ct); throw ; } Savepoints: await tx.CreateSavepointAsync(\"before-lines\") and RollbackToSavepointAsync for partial rollback (EF also uses savepoints automatically when you call SaveChanges inside a user transaction). # Execution strategies (connection resiliency) UseSqlServer(cs, o => o.EnableRetryOnFailure()) retries transient failures automatically — Azure SQL failovers, throttling, deadlock victims from exactly the deadlock-detection mechanism Relational fundamentals described. There's a catch: a retry has to redo the whole unit of work, not just the failed statement. So if you open a transaction manually, wrap it in an execution strategy too — otherwise a retry won't cover the BEGIN TRANSACTION itself: C# Run ▶ Copy var strategy = db. Database . CreateExecutionStrategy (); await strategy. ExecuteAsync ( async () => { await using var tx = await db. Database . BeginTransactionAsync (ct); // ... all the work ... await db. SaveChangesAsync (ct); await tx. CommitAsync (ct); }); No distributed transactions across services A transaction, no matter how it's opened, covers one database connection . You genuinely cannot wrap \"save the order in SQL + publish to Service Bus + call the payment API\" in a single atomic transaction — those are three entirely separate systems with no shared commit protocol. The real answer for cross-service consistency is the outbox pattern (covered later in this chapter, and in full in Messaging & events ) and sagas, not a bigger transaction."},{"c":"efcore-advanced","s":"set-based-updates-and-deletes-skipping-the-entity-entirely","h":"Set-based updates and deletes: skipping the entity entirely","t":"# Set-based updates and deletes: skipping the entity entirely Intermediate EF 7+ offers a second way to write to the database that deliberately skips the tracking machinery altogether, running a set-based SQL statement without loading any entities into memory first : C# Run ▶ Copy // UPDATE Products SET Price = Price * 1.1 WHERE CategoryId = 3 var updated = await db. Products . Where (p => p. CategoryId == 3 ) . ExecuteUpdateAsync (s => s . SetProperty (p => p. Price , p => p. Price * 1.1m ) . SetProperty (p => p. UpdatedAt , DateTime . UtcNow ), ct); // DELETE FROM Carts WHERE UpdatedAt < @cutoff var deleted = await db. Carts . Where (c => c. UpdatedAt < cutoff). ExecuteDeleteAsync (ct); Three consequences follow directly from \"no entities loaded,\" and each is worth knowing before you reach for this: They run immediately — not deferred until the next SaveChanges() . They bypass the change tracker : any already-tracked entity for the same rows stays stale in memory. They bypass SaveChanges interceptors : auditing or soft-delete logic registered there simply won't run. Global query filters still apply to the generated WHERE clause, though, since those are baked into query translation rather than into SaveChanges ."},{"c":"efcore-advanced","s":"interceptors-cross-cutting-behaviour-without-touching-busine","h":"Interceptors: cross-cutting behaviour without touching business code","t":"# Interceptors: cross-cutting behaviour without touching business code Advanced Interceptors hook into specific points in EF Core's own pipeline, letting you add behaviour that applies uniformly without scattering calls throughout your business logic: Interceptor Hooks Uses SaveChangesInterceptor Before/after SaveChanges Audit fields, soft delete, domain events, outbox DbCommandInterceptor Before/after SQL commands Query hints, logging slow SQL, tagging, modifying SQL DbConnectionInterceptor Connection open/close Setting session context (e.g. SESSION_CONTEXT for row-level security), access tokens DbTransactionInterceptor Transaction events Diagnostics IMaterializationInterceptor Entity materialised Injecting services into entities, decryption C# Run ▶ Copy public interface IAuditable { DateTimeOffset CreatedAt { get ; set ; } string ? CreatedBy { get ; set ; } DateTimeOffset ? UpdatedAt { get ; set ; } string ? UpdatedBy { get ; set ; } } public sealed class AuditInterceptor ( TimeProvider clock, ICurrentUser user) : SaveChangesInterceptor { public override ValueTask < InterceptionResult < int >> SavingChangesAsync ( DbContextEventData e, InterceptionResult < int > result, CancellationToken ct = default ) { var now = clock. GetUtcNow (); foreach ( var entry in e. Context !. ChangeTracker . Entries < IAuditable >()) { if (entry. State == EntityState . Added ) { entry. Entity . CreatedAt = now; entry. Entity . CreatedBy = user. Id ; } if (entry. State == EntityState . Modified ) { entry. Entity . UpdatedAt = now; entry. Entity . UpdatedBy = user. Id ; } } return base . SavingChangesAsync (e, result, ct); } } builder. Services . AddScoped < AuditInterceptor >(); builder. Services . AddDbContext < ShopDb >((sp, o) => o. UseSqlServer (cs). AddInterceptors (sp. GetRequiredService < AuditInterceptor >())); This interceptor is never called explicitly anywhere. Every IAuditable entity gets its timestamps stamped automatically just by sitting in the change tracker when SaveChanges runs — that's the appeal: uniform behavior, with nothing for a developer to remember to call."},{"c":"efcore-advanced","s":"global-query-filters-soft-delete-and-multi-tenancy","h":"Global query filters: soft delete and multi-tenancy","t":"# Global query filters: soft delete and multi-tenancy Advanced A filter defined once in the model gets automatically appended to every query against that entity type, with no need to repeat it anywhere it's queried: C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class ShopDb ( DbContextOptions < ShopDb > o, ITenantContext tenant) : DbContext (o) { protected override void OnModelCreating ( ModelBuilder b) { b. Entity < Product >(). HasQueryFilter (p => !p. IsDeleted ); // soft delete b. Entity < Order >(). HasQueryFilter (o => o. TenantId == tenant. TenantId ); // multi-tenancy: evaluated per context instance } } var all = await db. Products . ToListAsync (); // WHERE IsDeleted = 0 (automatically) var incl = await db. Products . IgnoreQueryFilters (). ToListAsync (); // admin views, restore screens The multi-tenancy line deserves a second look: because the filter closes over tenant.TenantId from a scoped ITenantContext , it's evaluated fresh for each DbContext instance — meaning the same model configuration automatically scopes every single query to whichever tenant the current request belongs to, with no developer anywhere having to remember to add WHERE TenantId = ... by hand. Soft delete itself usually pairs this filter with an interceptor that intercepts the delete and turns it into an update instead: C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class SoftDeleteInterceptor : SaveChangesInterceptor { public override ValueTask < InterceptionResult < int >> SavingChangesAsync ( DbContextEventData e, InterceptionResult < int > r, CancellationToken ct = default ) { foreach ( var entry in e. Context !. ChangeTracker . Entries < ISoftDeletable >(). Where (x => x. State == EntityState . Deleted )) { entry. State = EntityState . Modified ; entry. Entity . IsDeleted = true ; entry. Entity . DeletedAt = DateTimeOffset . UtcNow ; } return base . SavingChangesAsync (e, r, ct); } } Filters and required navigations Here's a subtle interaction worth watching for: if Order has a required navigation to a soft-deleted Customer , Include(o => o.Customer) generates an INNER JOIN , and the customer's own soft-delete filter applies to that join too — which can make the order itself silently disappear from results, even though nobody asked to filter orders at all. The fix is to configure the navigation as optional, or apply filters consistently across every entity in a relationship chain so this mismatch can't arise. EF 10 adds named filters , letting you disable one specific filter selectively rather than all-or-nothing. For multi-tenancy specifically, also consider database-level row-level security as a second line of defence — the application-level filter is one safeguard, not the only one."},{"c":"efcore-advanced","s":"domain-events-and-the-outbox-on-savechanges","h":"Domain events and the outbox on SaveChanges","t":"# Domain events and the outbox on SaveChanges Advanced Aggregates raise domain events ( DDD ) as things happen inside them; the question this section answers is how those events reliably get out of the aggregate and into something that can actually publish them: C# Run ▶ Copy public abstract class Entity { private readonly List < IDomainEvent > _events = []; public IReadOnlyList < IDomainEvent > DomainEvents => _events; protected void Raise ( IDomainEvent e) => _events. Add (e); public void ClearDomainEvents () => _events. Clear (); } // Outbox interceptor: turn domain events into outbox rows IN THE SAME TRANSACTION as the business data public sealed class OutboxInterceptor ( TimeProvider clock) : SaveChangesInterceptor { public override ValueTask < InterceptionResult < int >> SavingChangesAsync ( DbContextEventData e, InterceptionResult < int > r, CancellationToken ct = default ) { var ctx = e. Context !; var events = ctx. ChangeTracker . Entries < Entity >(). SelectMany (x => { var list = x. Entity . DomainEvents . ToList (); x. Entity . ClearDomainEvents (); return list; }). ToList (); ctx. Set < OutboxMessage >(). AddRange (events. Select (ev => new OutboxMessage { Id = Guid . NewGuid (), Type = ev. GetType (). FullName !, Payload = JsonSerializer . Serialize (ev, ev. GetType ()), OccurredAt = clock. GetUtcNow () })); return base . SavingChangesAsync (e, r, ct); } } // A background worker then publishes unprocessed OutboxMessages to the broker (see Messaging & events). This shape matters because the outbox rows are written inside the same SaveChanges() transaction as the business data: the order and its \"order placed\" event either both commit or both roll back. There's no window where one happens without the other — which sidesteps the \"no distributed transaction\" problem from earlier in this chapter entirely, rather than solving it. MassTransit, NServiceBus and Wolverine ship ready-made EF Core outboxes , so this pattern rarely needs to be hand-rolled in practice."},{"c":"efcore-advanced","s":"other-advanced-features","h":"Other advanced features","t":"# Other advanced features Advanced Feature What it gives you Temporal tables (SQL Server) b.ToTable(t => t.IsTemporal()) ; query history with TemporalAsOf(date) JSON columns Nested objects/collections in one column, queryable ( ToJson() ) Primitive collections List<string> Tags stored as JSON, queryable with Contains Raw SQL for unmapped types Database.SqlQuery<T> Keyless entity types Map views/functions ( HasNoKey().ToView(\"v_Sales\") ) User-defined function mapping Call SQL functions from LINQ ( HasDbFunction ) Sequences, HiLo Key generation strategies that reduce round trips Multiple DbContexts One per bounded context or module (modular monolith), each with its own migrations/schema Vector search (EF 10 / SQL Server 2025 / Azure SQL) SqlVector<float> with VECTOR_DISTANCE for AI similarity search Read replicas A second context/connection string pointing at a read-only replica for queries"},{"c":"efcore-advanced","s":"testing-ef-core-code","h":"Testing EF Core code","t":"# Testing EF Core code Intermediate Approach Fidelity Speed Verdict EF InMemory provider Low: no SQL, no constraints, no transactions, different query semantics Fast Avoid (Microsoft discourages it for testing) SQLite in-memory Medium: real SQL, but a different dialect than SQL Server/Postgres Fast OK for simple cases Real database in a container (Testcontainers) High Seconds per suite Recommended for repositories/queries Repository interface mocked N/A (doesn't test EF at all) Fast For unit-testing application logic only Take that low InMemory rating seriously: it isn't a relational database at all, so it can't enforce constraints, doesn't support real transactions, and translates some LINQ differently than any real provider does (Microsoft's own docs say as much — see Further reading). A test can pass against InMemory while the same query fails, or behaves differently, against the real SQL Server it runs on in production. See Integration testing for Testcontainers and Respawn, which close that gap by testing against the real engine. Q How does optimistic concurrency work in EF Core? Mark a concurrency token (a SQL Server rowversion with [Timestamp]/IsRowVersion, or a property with IsConcurrencyToken). EF includes the original value in the UPDATE/DELETE WHERE clause; if no row is affected, it throws DbUpdateConcurrencyException, which you handle by reloading, merging, retrying or returning 409/412 to the client. Q Optimistic vs pessimistic concurrency? Optimistic: no locks on read; conflicts are detected at write time via versions. It scales well and suits web apps where conflicts are rare. Pessimistic: lock rows when reading (UPDLOCK / FOR UPDATE) so others wait. It suits high-contention critical sections but reduces concurrency and risks deadlocks. Q Is SaveChanges transactional? When do you need an explicit transaction? Yes: one SaveChanges is a single transaction. Use BeginTransaction when multiple SaveChanges, ExecuteUpdate/raw SQL or Dapper calls must be atomic, or you need a specific isolation level. With retrying execution strategies, wrap the whole transaction in strategy.ExecuteAsync. Q What are ExecuteUpdate and ExecuteDelete, and what are their caveats? EF 7+ methods that run set-based UPDATE/DELETE statements directly from a LINQ filter without loading entities, which is much faster for bulk changes. They execute immediately, bypass the change tracker and SaveChanges interceptors (so auditing and soft delete there won't run), and tracked entities in memory aren't updated. Q How would you implement soft delete in EF Core? An IsDeleted (and DeletedAt) column, a global query filter HasQueryFilter(e => !e.IsDeleted) so normal queries exclude deleted rows, and a SaveChanges interceptor that converts Deleted states into Modified with IsDeleted = true. Use IgnoreQueryFilters for admin or restore scenarios, and consider filtered unique indexes. Q How would you implement multi-tenancy with EF Core? Options: a TenantId column with a global query filter based on a scoped tenant service (plus row-level security as defence in depth); a schema per tenant; or a database per tenant with the connection string chosen per request. Each trades isolation against cost and operational complexity. Q What are EF Core interceptors used for? Cross-cutting hooks into EF operations: SaveChanges interceptors for auditing, soft delete, domain events and outbox; command interceptors for logging, hints or tagging SQL; connection interceptors for session context or tokens; materialization interceptors for post-processing entities. Q Why not use the InMemory provider for tests? It isn't a relational database: it doesn't enforce constraints, transactions or SQL semantics, and it translates queries differently, so tests can pass while real queries fail. Use the real engine via Testcontainers (or at least SQLite) for data access tests."},{"c":"efcore-advanced","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Handling concurrency conflicts Transactions ExecuteUpdate and ExecuteDelete Interceptors Global query filters Choosing a testing strategy"},{"c":"efcore-advanced","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter The checks below exercise optimistic concurrency, ExecuteUpdate , global query filters and a SaveChangesInterceptor against a real (if tiny) SQLite database. Save as a console app ( dotnet new console , dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite ), then dotnet run . C# Run ▶ Copy using Microsoft . EntityFrameworkCore ; using Microsoft . EntityFrameworkCore . Diagnostics ; var connString = \"DataSource=file:verifydb2?mode=memory&cache=shared\" ; await using var conn = new Microsoft . Data . Sqlite . SqliteConnection (connString); await conn. OpenAsync (); var opts = new DbContextOptionsBuilder < ShopDb >(). UseSqlite (conn). Options ; await using ( var seed = new ShopDb (opts)) { await seed. Database . EnsureCreatedAsync (); seed. Products . Add ( new Product { Id = 1 , Name = \"Widget\" , Price = 10 , Version = 1 , IsDeleted = false }); seed. Products . Add ( new Product { Id = 2 , Name = \"Gizmo\" , Price = 5 , Version = 1 , IsDeleted = true }); await seed. SaveChangesAsync (); } // Claim: optimistic concurrency — a stale version throws DbUpdateConcurrencyException. { await using var ctx1 = new ShopDb (opts); var p1 = await ctx1. Products . IgnoreQueryFilters (). SingleAsync (p => p. Id == 1 ); // Version = 1 await using ( var ctx2 = new ShopDb (opts)) { var p2 = await ctx2. Products . IgnoreQueryFilters (). SingleAsync (p => p. Id == 1 ); p2. Price = 20 ; p2. Version ++; // app increments the token manually, as the chapter describes for non-SQL-Server providers await ctx2. SaveChangesAsync (); // succeeds: DB Version was 1, matches p2's original value } p1. Name = \"Gadget\" ; p1. Version ++; // p1 still thinks the current version is 1, but the DB is now at 2 var threw = false ; try { await ctx1. SaveChangesAsync (); } catch ( DbUpdateConcurrencyException ) { threw = true ; } if (!threw) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected DbUpdateConcurrencyException\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: a stale concurrency token throws DbUpdateConcurrencyException\" ); } // Claim: ExecuteUpdate bypasses the change tracker — an already-tracked entity is not refreshed. { await using var ctx = new ShopDb (opts); var tracked = await ctx. Products . IgnoreQueryFilters (). SingleAsync (p => p. Id == 1 ); // Price = 20 after the test above await ctx. Products . IgnoreQueryFilters (). Where (p => p. Id == 1 ) . ExecuteUpdateAsync (s => s. SetProperty (p => p. Price , 999m )); if (tracked. Price == 999m ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: ExecuteUpdate updated the tracked instance\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: ExecuteUpdate leaves an already-tracked in-memory entity stale\" ); await using var fresh = new ShopDb (opts); var reread = await fresh. Products . IgnoreQueryFilters (). SingleAsync (p => p. Id == 1 ); if (reread. Price != 999m ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: the database wasn't actually updated\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: ExecuteUpdate did write the new value to the database\" ); } // Claim: a global query filter (soft delete) excludes rows by default, and IgnoreQueryFilters() includes them. { await using var ctx = new ShopDb (opts); var visible = await ctx. Products . ToListAsync (); if (visible. Any (p => p. Id == 2 )) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: soft-deleted row leaked through the default filter\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: a global query filter hides soft-deleted rows by default\" ); var all = await ctx. Products . IgnoreQueryFilters (). ToListAsync (); if (!all. Any (p => p. Id == 2 )) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: IgnoreQueryFilters() didn't bring the row back\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: IgnoreQueryFilters() brings soft-deleted rows back\" ); } // Claim: a SaveChangesInterceptor runs for every SaveChanges, without being called explicitly anywhere. { var auditOpts = new DbContextOptionsBuilder < AuditedShopDb >() . UseSqlite (conn) . AddInterceptors ( new StampingInterceptor ()) . Options ; await using var ctx = new AuditedShopDb (auditOpts); var p = await ctx. Products . IgnoreQueryFilters (). SingleAsync (p => p. "},{"c":"nosql-redis","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR \"NoSQL\" is a grab-bag, not one thing: key-value, document, wide-column, graph, search, time-series and vector stores each trade away general-purpose flexibility to be very good at one access pattern. CAP theorem : during a network partition, a distributed store must pick consistency (always correct) or availability (always responding, maybe stale) — not both. PACELC adds that even without a partition, you still trade latency for consistency. Document databases model by access pattern, not normal form : store together what you read together, even if that duplicates data (and that duplication is often correct, not a bug). In Cosmos DB, the partition key you pick decides cost and scalability for the container's lifetime — a low-cardinality key like date creates a hot partition that every write piles onto. Redis is an in-memory data-structure server, not just a cache: strings, hashes, lists, sets, sorted sets and streams give you ready-made algorithms (ranking, rate limiting, locks), not just key-value storage. Default to relational for business data; reach for a NoSQL store for a specific, deliberate reason (scale, flexible schema, a specialised access pattern) — not just to dodge schema migrations."},{"c":"nosql-redis","s":"the-nosql-families-optimised-for-an-access-pattern-not-a-fea","h":"The NoSQL families: optimised for an access pattern, not a feature list","t":"# The NoSQL families: optimised for an access pattern, not a feature list Beginner \"NoSQL\" is really a negative definition — \"not (only) relational tables\" — which hides how different the actual families are from each other. Each one made a deliberate trade to get extremely good at one access pattern, usually at the cost of the general-purpose flexibility a relational table gives you for free: Family Data model Examples Good at Weak at Key-value Key → value (opaque or simple structures) Redis , Azure Table Storage, DynamoDB (KV mode) Ultra-fast lookups by key, caching, counters, sessions Queries by anything but the key Document JSON-like documents, flexible schema Cosmos DB (NoSQL API) , MongoDB , Couchbase Aggregates read and written as a whole, varying schemas, horizontal scale Joins across documents, multi-document transactions (limited) Wide-column Rows with dynamic columns, partitioned Cassandra, ScyllaDB, Cosmos DB for Cassandra Massive write throughput, time series Ad-hoc queries Graph Nodes + edges Neo4j, Cosmos DB for Gremlin Relationship traversal (social, recommendations, fraud) Bulk aggregations Search Inverted indexes Elasticsearch/OpenSearch, Azure AI Search Full-text, faceting, relevance Being the source of truth Time series Timestamped measurements InfluxDB, Azure Data Explorer, TimescaleDB Metrics, IoT General-purpose data Vector Embeddings + similarity indexes Azure AI Search, Cosmos DB vector search, pgvector, Qdrant Semantic search, RAG for AI features Exact queries Why not always use SQL? Relational databases are the right default for business data: strong consistency, transactions, joins, mature tooling. NoSQL wins when you need horizontal scale beyond one server , very low latency at huge volume , flexible or nested schemas , global distribution , or a specialised access pattern (search, graph, vectors). Many systems use both: polyglot persistence ."},{"c":"nosql-redis","s":"cap-pacelc-and-consistency-models-the-trade-off-a-distribute","h":"CAP, PACELC and consistency models: the trade-off a distributed store can't avoid","t":"# CAP, PACELC and consistency models: the trade-off a distributed store can't avoid Intermediate CAP theorem , in plain terms: imagine two servers holding the same data, and the network link between them breaks (a P artition). Networks genuinely do this — it's not a hypothetical edge case. While they're cut off from each other, each server must pick one of two bad options: refuse to answer, or guarantee correctness ( C onsistency), or answer anyway and risk giving out stale data, but stay responsive ( A vailability). You can't have both during that break — that's the whole theorem. Once the network heals, both are possible again. PACELC asks the question CAP doesn't: what do you trade when there's no partition at all (the E lse case)? Even then, you trade L atency against C onsistency — because waiting for every replica to confirm a write (strong consistency) is always slower than writing to one and letting the others catch up later (eventual consistency, with a small window where they disagree). Consistency models, in practice, form a spectrum from strongest to weakest, and the right choice depends entirely on what the data is actually for: Model Guarantee Strong / linearizable Reads always return the latest committed write Bounded staleness Reads lag by at most K versions or T time Session Within a client session: read-your-writes, monotonic reads (Cosmos DB's default; ideal for most apps) Consistent prefix You never see writes out of order Eventual Replicas converge eventually; no ordering guarantees Cosmos DB lets you choose any of these five per account (and relax per request) — which is a useful thing to know specifically because it means the consistency decision isn't locked in once at the infrastructure level; it's a knob you can turn per query based on what that particular read actually needs."},{"c":"nosql-redis","s":"document-databases-modelling-by-access-pattern-not-by-normal","h":"Document databases: modelling by access pattern, not by normal form","t":"# Document databases: modelling by access pattern, not by normal form Intermediate The relational instinct, drilled into you by Relational fundamentals , is to normalise: store each fact once, join at read time. Document modelling runs in the opposite direction on purpose: design for the access pattern — store together whatever gets read together, even if that means the same fact appears in more than one document. JSON Copy // Cosmos DB document: an order as one aggregate, with lines embedded { \"id\" : \"ord-1042\" , \"customerId\" : \"cust-7\" , // partition key \"type\" : \"order\" , \"status\" : \"Paid\" , \"createdAt\" : \"2026-10-27T14:00:00Z\" , \"shippingAddress\" : { \"street\" : \"Main St 1\" , \"city\" : \"Berlin\" , \"country\" : \"DE\" }, \"lines\" : [ { \"sku\" : \"KB-01\" , \"name\" : \"Keyboard\" , \"qty\" : 1 , \"unitPrice\" : 49.90 }, { \"sku\" : \"MS-02\" , \"name\" : \"Mouse\" , \"qty\" : 2 , \"unitPrice\" : 19.90 } ], \"total\" : 89.70 , \"_etag\" : \"\\\"0000d...\\\"\" // optimistic concurrency } Embed when… Reference (store an ID) when… Data is read together (order + lines) Data is shared by many parents (product catalogue) Child data is bounded in size It grows without bound (all of a customer's orders) Child changes with the parent It changes independently and often You need atomic updates of the whole You'd otherwise duplicate large data Denormalisation is normal here, by design : copying a product's name into the order line isn't a mistake to clean up later — it's genuinely the correct historical record of what that line said at the time of purchase, exactly the same \"snapshot data is not duplication\" principle Relational fundamentals raised for relational schemas too. Where copies do need to stay roughly in sync with a source of truth, change feeds or events are the usual mechanism, covered next."},{"c":"nosql-redis","s":"partitioning-the-one-modelling-decision-you-cant-easily-undo","h":"Partitioning: the one modelling decision you can't easily undo","t":"# Partitioning: the one modelling decision you can't easily undo Advanced Distributed databases split data across physical partitions by a partition key , and in Cosmos DB specifically, this single choice ends up determining scalability, cost, and which queries are cheap for the lifetime of the container: High cardinality (many distinct values) so data and load spread evenly. Even request distribution : avoid hot partitions (e.g. partitioning by date sends all of today's writes to one partition). Most queries filter by it : single-partition queries are cheap; cross-partition fan-out queries cost more Request Units and are slower. Transactions (stored procedures, transactional batch) are limited to one logical partition . A logical partition has a size limit (20 GB), so use hierarchical partition keys (e.g. tenantId → userId) for large tenants. Notice how directly the first three bullets connect: picking customerId as the partition key (as the JSON document above does) means every query scoped to one customer — the overwhelmingly common case for an e-commerce order history — stays within a single partition, cheap and fast. Had the key been createdAt instead, every write for \"today\" would pile onto the same partition while yesterday's sat idle, exactly the hot-partition failure the second bullet warns about. Cosmos DB charges in Request Units (RU/s) , provisioned or serverless/autoscale. A 1 KB point read by id + partition key costs about 1 RU; queries cost more, which is precisely why the SDK example below reads RequestCharge directly — it's the concrete feedback loop for whether a given access pattern is actually cheap. C# Run ▶ Copy // Cosmos DB .NET SDK (singleton CosmosClient) builder. Services . AddSingleton (_ => new CosmosClient (endpoint, new DefaultAzureCredential (), new CosmosClientOptions { SerializerOptions = new () { PropertyNamingPolicy = CosmosPropertyNamingPolicy . CamelCase } })); var container = cosmos. GetContainer ( \"shop\" , \"orders\" ); await container. CreateItemAsync (order, new PartitionKey (order. CustomerId ), cancellationToken: ct); var read = await container. ReadItemAsync < OrderDoc >( \"ord-1042\" , new PartitionKey ( \"cust-7\" ), cancellationToken: ct); // point read: cheapest Console . WriteLine (read. RequestCharge ); // RU cost var query = container. GetItemLinqQueryable < OrderDoc >(requestOptions: new () { PartitionKey = new PartitionKey ( \"cust-7\" ) }) . Where (o => o. Status == \"Paid\" ). OrderByDescending (o => o. CreatedAt ). ToFeedIterator (); while (query. HasMoreResults ) foreach ( var o in await query. ReadNextAsync (ct)) { /* … */ } // Optimistic concurrency with ETag await container. ReplaceItemAsync (updated, updated. Id , new PartitionKey (updated. CustomerId ), new ItemRequestOptions { IfMatchEtag = read. ETag }, ct); // 412 if changed meanwhile Notice that last call leans on the exact same optimistic-concurrency idea as EF Core's RowVersion from EF Core advanced — an ETag here plays precisely the same role, detecting a concurrent write rather than preventing it with a lock. The change feed streams every insert and update, in order, per partition, and it's the foundation most Cosmos DB integrations are quietly built on top of: materialised views, event-driven integration with other services, and keeping a search index in sync without the application code ever having to explicitly push updates to it. MongoDB offers the equivalent capability as change streams; EF Core does ship a Cosmos provider, but the native SDK shown above gives more direct control, especially over RU cost."},{"c":"nosql-redis","s":"redis-an-in-memory-data-structure-server-not-just-a-cache","h":"Redis: an in-memory data-structure server, not just a cache","t":"# Redis: an in-memory data-structure server, not just a cache Beginner Redis is often introduced purely as \"a cache,\" which undersells what it actually is: an in-memory data-structure server offering sub-millisecond operations, optional persistence (RDB snapshots, an AOF log), replication, and clustering. On Azure: Azure Managed Redis (successor of Azure Cache for Redis). Client: StackExchange.Redis . Open-source forks and alternatives include Valkey and Garnet (Microsoft's Redis-compatible server written in C#). # Data structures Type Commands Backend uses String GET , SET key val EX 60 NX , INCR Cache values, counters, distributed locks, idempotency keys Hash HSET , HGET , HINCRBY Objects with fields (session, cart) List LPUSH , RPOP , BLPOP Simple queues, recent-items lists Set SADD , SISMEMBER , SINTER Unique visitors, tags, \"who's online\" Sorted set ZADD , ZRANGE , ZINCRBY Leaderboards, priority queues, sliding-window rate limits, time indexes Stream XADD , XREADGROUP , XACK Durable-ish event log with consumer groups Pub/Sub PUBLISH , SUBSCRIBE Fire-and-forget broadcast (SignalR backplane, cache invalidation) HyperLogLog, Bitmaps, Geo PFADD , SETBIT , GEOSEARCH Approximate counts, flags, location queries JSON, Search, Vector (modules) JSON.SET , FT.SEARCH Document storage, secondary indexes, vector similarity C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddSingleton < IConnectionMultiplexer >(_ => ConnectionMultiplexer . Connect (redisConnectionString)); // ONE per app: thread-safe, multiplexed public sealed class RedisPatterns ( IConnectionMultiplexer mux) { private readonly IDatabase _db = mux. GetDatabase (); // Counter with expiry: simple fixed-window rate limit public async Task < bool > AllowAsync ( string user, int limit) { var key = $\"rl:{user}:{DateTime.UtcNow:yyyyMMddHHmm}\" ; var count = await _db. StringIncrementAsync (key); if (count == 1 ) await _db. KeyExpireAsync (key, TimeSpan . FromMinutes ( 1 )); return count <= limit; } // Leaderboard public Task AddScoreAsync ( string player, double points) => _db. SortedSetIncrementAsync ( \"leaderboard\" , player, points); public Task < SortedSetEntry []> TopAsync ( int n) => _db. SortedSetRangeByRankWithScoresAsync ( \"leaderboard\" , 0 , n - 1 , Order . Descending ); // Distributed lock: SET NX with expiry, release only if we own it public async Task < bool > TryRunExclusiveAsync ( string name, TimeSpan ttl, Func < Task > work) { var token = Guid . NewGuid (). ToString (); if (! await _db. LockTakeAsync ( $\"lock:{name}\" , token, ttl)) return false ; try { await work(); return true ; } finally { await _db. LockReleaseAsync ( $\"lock:{name}\" , token); } } } That rate-limiter and leaderboard both lean on the same insight: Redis's data structures aren't just storage, they're the algorithm — a sorted set naturally maintains ranking as scores update, so \"top 10\" is a direct ZRANGE call rather than something your application code has to compute by sorting a fetched list itself. Redis pitfalls Create one ConnectionMultiplexer and reuse it for the app's lifetime — creating one per request is the exact same mistake as the HttpClient socket-exhaustion bug from Calling other services , just against a different protocol. Big keys and hot keys (a 50 MB value, one key hit by every single request) hurt latency for everyone sharing that shard, because Redis processes commands mostly single-threaded per shard — a slow operation on one key blocks the queue behind it. KEYS * blocks the server while it scans the entire keyspace. Use SCAN , which walks it incrementally instead. Memory is the limit : set maxmemory and an eviction policy ( allkeys-lru for caches), and always set TTLs on cache keys — without one, a cache silently becomes a permanent, ever-growing store. Pub/Sub isn't durable : a subscriber that's offline when a message is published simply never receives it, with no retry or queue underneath. Use Streams or a real broker for anything that must be delivered. Locks with a TTL can expire whil"},{"c":"nosql-redis","s":"search-and-vector-stores","h":"Search and vector stores","t":"# Search and vector stores Advanced Databases, relational or document, are poor at relevance-ranked full-text search, typo tolerance, facets, and synonyms — that's simply not the problem they were built to solve. The common pattern keeps the source of truth in SQL or Cosmos and projects a copy into a dedicated search index, kept in sync via the change feed or events covered earlier: Azure AI Search (managed; full-text + vector + hybrid search, semantic ranking), Elasticsearch/OpenSearch . Vector search powers AI features (semantic search, RAG): store embeddings next to documents in Azure AI Search, Cosmos DB, PostgreSQL pgvector , SQL Server 2025/Azure SQL vector type, Redis, Qdrant. .NET's Microsoft.Extensions.VectorData abstractions and Microsoft.Extensions.AI give provider-neutral APIs."},{"c":"nosql-redis","s":"choosing-a-data-store","h":"Choosing a data store","t":"# Choosing a data store Intermediate Diagram Copy Business data with relationships, transactions, reporting? → SQL Server / PostgreSQL (default) Aggregates read/written whole, flexible schema, global scale, low latency by key? → Cosmos DB / MongoDB Cache, sessions, counters, locks, leaderboards, rate limits? → Redis Files, images, exports, backups, large blobs? → Blob storage (store the URL in the DB) Full-text search, facets, relevance, semantic/vector search? → Azure AI Search / Elasticsearch (as a projection) Event stream for many consumers, replay, high volume? → Event Hubs / Kafka Metrics, logs, telemetry analytics? → Azure Monitor / Data Explorer / time-series DB Highly connected data, path queries? → Graph DB Don't put large files in the database (use Blob Storage and store references), don't use a cache as the system of record, and don't choose NoSQL just to avoid schema migrations: schema-on-read still needs versioning (add a schemaVersion field and handle old shapes). Q When would you choose a NoSQL database over a relational one? When access patterns are mostly by key or aggregate, the schema is flexible or nested, you need horizontal scale or global distribution beyond a single SQL server, or very low latency at high throughput, or a specialised model (graph, search, time series). Relational remains the default for transactional business data with joins and reporting. Q Explain the CAP theorem. In a distributed system, when a network partition occurs you can provide either consistency (all reads see the latest write or fail) or availability (all requests get a response, possibly stale), not both. PACELC adds that, even without partitions, you trade latency against consistency. Q How do you choose a partition key in Cosmos DB? High cardinality, even distribution of storage and requests (no hot partitions), and present in most queries so they stay single-partition. Consider transactional scope (single partition only) and the 20 GB logical partition limit; use hierarchical keys for big tenants. Q Embed vs reference in document modelling? Embed data that's read and updated together and bounded in size (order lines in an order). Reference data that's shared, unbounded, or updated independently (products, a customer's orders). Model for the access patterns, and accept some denormalisation. Q What is Redis used for in .NET backends? Distributed caching (IDistributedCache/HybridCache L2), session and cart storage, rate-limit counters, distributed locks, leaderboards and sorted indexes, pub/sub (SignalR backplane, cache invalidation) and streams. Use one shared ConnectionMultiplexer. Q How would you implement a distributed lock with Redis, and what are the risks? SET key token NX PX ttl to acquire (only if absent, with expiry), and release only if the value still matches your token (LockTake/LockRelease in StackExchange.Redis, or a Lua script). Risks: the TTL can expire while work continues, and failover can lose locks. Use fencing tokens and idempotent work for critical cases. Q Why shouldn't you use Redis Pub/Sub for important events? It's fire-and-forget: messages aren't persisted, and subscribers that are offline or slow miss them. Use Redis Streams with consumer groups or a real message broker (Service Bus, RabbitMQ, Kafka) for reliable delivery."},{"c":"nosql-redis","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Azure Cosmos DB: modelling data Partitioning and horizontal scaling in Cosmos DB StackExchange.Redis documentation Redis data types Book: Designing Data-Intensive Applications (Kleppmann), chapters 2, 5, 6 and 9."},{"c":"nosql-redis","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run (a plain console app, .NET 8/9, no extra packages, no live Redis or Cosmos DB server needed). It re-implements, in-process, the exact algorithms the chapter describes — hot-partition math, the fixed-window rate limiter, sorted-set ranking, and ETag-based optimistic concurrency — and checks their behavior directly. CAP/PACELC during a real network partition, actual Cosmos DB RU pricing, and live StackExchange.Redis behavior need a real distributed system to observe, so those are left to the Further reading links instead of faked here. C# Run ▶ Copy // Verify this chapter: run with `dotnet run`. No external packages or live // Redis/Cosmos DB server needed -- those claims require a real network // service and are left to the Further reading links instead of faked here. using System ; using System . Collections . Generic ; using System . Linq ; void Check ( string name, bool ok) { if (!ok) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: {name}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: {name}\" ); } // 1. Partition key choice: partitioning by customerId spreads today's writes // across many partitions; partitioning by date concentrates them into one. // This reproduces, in miniature, the exact claim in \"Partitioning\": picking a // low-cardinality key like `date` creates a hot partition. const int customers = 500 ; const int ordersToday = 2000 ; var rng = new Random ( 42 ); var byCustomerId = new Dictionary < int , int >(); var byDate = new Dictionary < string , int >(); string today = \"2026-10-28\" ; for ( int i = 0 ; i < ordersToday; i++) { int customerId = rng. Next (customers); byCustomerId[customerId] = byCustomerId. GetValueOrDefault (customerId) + 1 ; byDate[today] = byDate. GetValueOrDefault (today) + 1 ; // every write lands on \"today\" } int hottestByCustomerId = byCustomerId. Values . Max (); int hottestByDate = byDate. Values . Max (); Check ( \"partitioning by customerId spreads load (busiest partition holds a small share)\" , hottestByCustomerId < ordersToday / 10 ); Check ( \"partitioning by date concentrates ALL of today's writes onto one partition\" , hottestByDate == ordersToday); // 2. Fixed-window rate limiter: the same algorithm as RedisPatterns.AllowAsync, // re-implemented over an in-memory dictionary instead of StringIncrementAsync, // to verify the counting/window logic itself (not the Redis client). var windowCounts = new Dictionary < string , int >(); bool Allow ( string user, string window, int limit) { var key = $\"{user}:{window}\" ; windowCounts[key] = windowCounts. GetValueOrDefault (key) + 1 ; return windowCounts[key] <= limit; } int allowedInWindow = 0 ; for ( int i = 0 ; i < 7 ; i++) if ( Allow ( \"user-1\" , \"w1\" , 5 )) allowedInWindow++; Check ( \"fixed-window limiter allows exactly `limit` requests per window (5 of 7 allowed)\" , allowedInWindow == 5 ); bool allowedInNewWindow = Allow ( \"user-1\" , \"w2\" , 5 ); Check ( \"a new window resets the counter, so the next request is allowed again\" , allowedInNewWindow); // 3. Sorted-set leaderboard: ZADD/ZINCRBY + ZRANGE keep \"top N\" always sorted // without the caller re-sorting. SortedSet<T> here plays the role Redis's // native sorted set plays server-side -- the property under test is that // ranking stays correct after incremental score updates, same as ZINCRBY. var scores = new SortedSet <( double Score , string Player )>( Comparer <( double Score , string Player )>. Create ((a, b) => a. Score != b. Score ? b. Score . CompareTo (a. Score ) : string . CompareOrdinal (a. Player , b. Player ))); void AddScore ( string player, double delta) { var existing = scores. FirstOrDefault (s => s. Player == player); if (! default (( double , string )). Equals (existing)) scores. Remove (existing); scores. Add ((existing. Score + delta, player)); } AddScore ( \"alice\" , 10 ); AddScore ( \"bob\" , 25 ); AddScore ( \"carol\" , 15 ); AddScore ( \"alice\" , 20 ); // alice now has 30, should become #1 var top2 = scores. Take ( 2 ). Select (s => s. Player ). ToArray (); Che"},{"c":"design-principles","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Every principle here exists for one reason: make change cheap by touching as few places as possible. SOLID : one reason to change per class (S); add behavior via new code, not edits (O); a subtype must honor its base type's promises (L); split fat interfaces into small, role-specific ones (I); depend on abstractions the business side owns, not on concrete infrastructure (D). Aim for high cohesion (related things live together) and low coupling (modules don't depend on each other's internals). DRY is about duplicated knowledge , not duplicated-looking text — don't merge two things that just happen to look alike today. These are heuristics, not laws: an interface with one implementation that never changes is often over-engineering, not good design."},{"c":"design-principles","s":"the-problem-every-principle-in-this-chapter-is-trying-to-sol","h":"The problem every principle in this chapter is trying to solve","t":"# The problem every principle in this chapter is trying to solve Beginner Code is read and changed far more often than it's written fresh. Every principle in this chapter — SOLID and the others that follow it — exists for exactly one practical reason: to reduce the cost of change . When a requirement changes, the ideal is that as few places as possible need to change, and that changing them doesn't break something unrelated you weren't even thinking about. SOLID (named by Robert C. Martin, \"Uncle Bob\") packages five such principles specifically for classes and modules: Letter Principle One-liner S Single Responsibility A module should have one reason to change O Open/Closed Open for extension, closed for modification L Liskov Substitution Subtypes must be usable wherever their base type is expected I Interface Segregation Clients shouldn't depend on methods they don't use D Dependency Inversion Depend on abstractions; high-level policy shouldn't depend on low-level detail"},{"c":"design-principles","s":"s-single-responsibility-principle","h":"S: Single Responsibility Principle","t":"# S: Single Responsibility Principle Beginner A class should have one reason to change , meaning it is responsible to one actor (one stakeholder or source of requirements). C# Run ▶ Copy // ✘ Three reasons to change: pricing rules (sales), persistence (DBA/infra), notification wording (marketing) public class OrderService { public void PlaceOrder ( Order o) { o. Total = o. Lines . Sum (l => l. Price * l. Qty ) * (o. Customer . IsVip ? 0.9m : 1m ); // pricing using var conn = new SqlConnection ( \"…\" ); // persistence conn. Execute ( \"INSERT INTO Orders …\" , o); var smtp = new SmtpClient ( \"smtp.shop.com\" ); // notification smtp. Send ( \"shop@shop.com\" , o. Customer . Email , \"Thanks!\" , $\"Your total is {o.Total}\" ); } } // ✔ Each concern in its own type; OrderService orchestrates public sealed class PlaceOrderHandler ( IPricingService pricing, IOrderRepository orders, INotifier notifier) { public async Task HandleAsync ( Order o, CancellationToken ct) { o. ApplyTotal (pricing. Calculate (o)); await orders. AddAsync (o, ct); await notifier. OrderPlacedAsync (o, ct); } } The reason the first version is a real problem, not just an aesthetic one: when marketing wants to change the email wording, or the DBA wants to change how orders are persisted, or sales wants a new discount rule, each of those three unrelated people ends up editing the exact same method — a near-guarantee of merge conflicts and of one team's change accidentally breaking another's logic they never touched directly. Symptoms of violation: a class name with \"And\"/\"Manager\"/\"Helper\"/\"Utils\", 2,000-line services, unrelated tests in the same test class, merge conflicts on the same file from different teams, constructor injection of 10+ dependencies. SRP isn't \"one method per class\" It's about cohesion around a reason to change . Splitting a cohesive class into dozens of one-method classes creates a different problem: scattered logic and ceremony."},{"c":"design-principles","s":"o-open-closed-principle","h":"O: Open/Closed Principle","t":"# O: Open/Closed Principle Intermediate Software entities should be open for extension but closed for modification . Add behaviour by adding new code, not by editing working, tested code. C# Run ▶ Copy // ✘ Every new carrier means editing this method (and risking existing carriers) public decimal ShippingCost ( Parcel p, string carrier) => carrier switch { \"dhl\" => 5 + p. WeightKg * 1.2m , \"ups\" => 7 + p. WeightKg , _ => throw new NotSupportedException () }; // ✔ New carrier = new class + one DI registration. ShippingCalculator never changes. public interface IShippingRate { string Carrier { get ; } decimal Calculate ( Parcel p); } public sealed class DhlRate : IShippingRate { public string Carrier => \"dhl\" ; public decimal Calculate ( Parcel p) => 5 + p. WeightKg * 1.2m ; } public sealed class UpsRate : IShippingRate { public string Carrier => \"ups\" ; public decimal Calculate ( Parcel p) => 7 + p. WeightKg ; } public sealed class ShippingCalculator ( IEnumerable < IShippingRate > rates) { private readonly Dictionary < string , IShippingRate > _byCarrier = rates. ToDictionary (r => r. Carrier ); public decimal Quote ( string carrier, Parcel p) => _byCarrier[carrier]. Calculate (p); } The payoff is specific: adding FedEx support in the first version means editing a method that DHL and UPS already depend on, risking a typo that breaks pricing for carriers nobody meant to touch. In the second version, adding FedEx is purely additive — a new class, one registration line — and ShippingCalculator itself never changes again, no matter how many carriers get added. Where .NET does this: middleware (extend the pipeline without modifying it, exactly as Middleware showed), DI registrations, MVC/endpoint filters, EF Core interceptors, DelegatingHandler s, configuration providers, logging providers, and authorization handlers. Tools: polymorphism, strategy, decorator, plugin points."},{"c":"design-principles","s":"l-liskov-substitution-principle","h":"L: Liskov Substitution Principle","t":"# L: Liskov Substitution Principle Intermediate If S is a subtype of T, objects of type T may be replaced with objects of type S without breaking the program's correctness . Subtypes must honour the base type's contract : no stronger preconditions, no weaker postconditions, no new exceptions callers don't expect, and invariants preserved. C# Run ▶ Copy // The classic: Square \"is a\" Rectangle mathematically, but not behaviourally public class Rectangle { public virtual int Width { get ; set ; } public virtual int Height { get ; set ; } public int Area => Width * Height ; } public class Square : Rectangle { public override int Width { set { base . Width = value ; base . Height = value ; } } public override int Height { set { base . Width = value ; base . Height = value ; } } } void Resize ( Rectangle r) { r. Width = 5 ; r. Height = 4 ; Debug . Assert (r. Area == 20 ); } // fails for Square: Area = 16 Trace through why Resize fails: it was written against Rectangle 's contract, where setting Width and Height independently should give an area equal to their product. Square can't honour that without breaking its own invariant — setting one side always forces the other to match — so something has to give, and it's always the caller's assumption. This is exactly why OOP stressed that \"is-a\" in a type hierarchy has to mean \"behaves like,\" not just \"is mathematically a special case of.\" Backend-flavoured violations: C# Run ▶ Copy public class ReadOnlyOrderRepository : IOrderRepository { public void Add ( Order o) => throw new NotSupportedException (); // ✘ callers of IOrderRepository will blow up } // ✔ Fix with Interface Segregation: IOrderReader and IOrderWriter // .NET's own famous violation: arrays implement IList<T>, but Add throws NotSupportedException on T[] // (and ReadOnlyCollection<T> implements ICollection<T>.Add by throwing). Test for LSP: could every test written for the base type pass for the subtype?"},{"c":"design-principles","s":"i-interface-segregation-principle","h":"I: Interface Segregation Principle","t":"# I: Interface Segregation Principle Intermediate Clients should not be forced to depend on methods they don't use. Prefer several small, role-specific interfaces over one large one. C# Run ▶ Copy // ✘ Fat interface: the reporting job depends on write methods it never calls; the mock needs 12 methods public interface IUserService { Task < User ?> GetAsync ( Guid id); Task < IReadOnlyList < User >> SearchAsync ( string q); Task CreateAsync ( User u); Task UpdateAsync ( User u); Task DeleteAsync ( Guid id); Task ResetPasswordAsync ( Guid id); Task SendWelcomeEmailAsync ( Guid id); Task < byte []> ExportCsvAsync (); } // ✔ Role interfaces; one class may still implement several public interface IUserReader { Task < User ?> GetAsync ( Guid id); Task < IReadOnlyList < User >> SearchAsync ( string q); } public interface IUserWriter { Task CreateAsync ( User u); Task UpdateAsync ( User u); Task DeleteAsync ( Guid id); } public interface IUserExporter { Task < byte []> ExportCsvAsync (); } Notice exactly what this fixes in the ReadOnlyOrderRepository example from the LSP section above: splitting into IOrderReader and IOrderWriter means the read-only implementation simply never implements IOrderWriter at all, so there's no Add method left to throw from in the first place — the problem disappears rather than getting patched. In .NET: IEnumerable<T> / IReadOnlyCollection<T> / IList<T> ; IOptions<T> giving each class only its own settings section; CQRS splitting read and write interfaces; the IDisposable / IAsyncDisposable split."},{"c":"design-principles","s":"d-dependency-inversion-principle","h":"D: Dependency Inversion Principle","t":"# D: Dependency Inversion Principle Beginner High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules. Both should depend on abstractions. Abstractions should not depend on details. Details should depend on abstractions. The single most important question DIP asks is who owns the interface . The high-level (business) side defines what it needs, phrased in its own terms, and the low-level (infrastructure) side implements that definition. The result is that the source-code dependency arrow points in the opposite direction from the flow of control at runtime: Diagram Copy Without DIP: OrderService ───uses & depends on───▶ SqlOrderRepository ──▶ SQL Server (business) (infrastructure) With DIP: OrderService ──depends on──▶ IOrderRepository ◀──implements── SqlOrderRepository └──────────── Domain/Application project ───────────┘ └── Infrastructure project Flow of control still goes Service → SQL at runtime, but the compile-time dependency points INWARD. Look closely at what actually flipped: OrderService still ends up calling SQL Server at runtime, exactly as before — the behaviour hasn't changed. What changed is that OrderService 's source code no longer mentions SqlOrderRepository at all; it only mentions IOrderRepository , an interface the business side itself defines. That's the whole trick, and it's why swapping SQL Server for a different store later only ever touches the infrastructure project. C# Run ▶ Copy // Domain/Application layer owns the abstraction, phrased in business terms public interface IOrderRepository { Task < Order ?> GetAsync ( OrderId id, CancellationToken ct); void Add ( Order o); } public interface IPaymentGateway { Task < PaymentResult > ChargeAsync ( Money amount, PaymentMethod method, CancellationToken ct); } // Infrastructure implements it (EF Core, Stripe SDK…) public sealed class StripePaymentGateway ( StripeClient stripe) : IPaymentGateway { /* … */ } // Composition root wires them together builder. Services . AddScoped < IPaymentGateway , StripePaymentGateway >(); DIP is the principle; DI is the mechanism that makes it practical to wire up at runtime, as Dependency injection covered in full; Clean/Onion/Hexagonal architecture is simply DIP applied at the scale of an entire solution rather than one class. See App architectures ."},{"c":"design-principles","s":"coupling-and-cohesion-the-two-metrics-solid-is-really-about","h":"Coupling and cohesion: the two metrics SOLID is really about","t":"# Coupling and cohesion: the two metrics SOLID is really about Intermediate Pull back from the five individual letters, and all of SOLID is really in service of two measurable properties: Cohesion (want high ): how strongly the elements inside a module belong together. A Money type with arithmetic and formatting is cohesive; a Utils class with email validation, date parsing and PDF generation is not. Coupling (want low ): how much one module depends on the internals of another. Low coupling means you can change one without changing the other. Kind of coupling (worst → best) Example Content Reaching into another class's private state via reflection Common / global Shared static mutable state, a shared database table written by two services Control Passing flags that tell the callee what to do ( Save(order, sendEmail: true, skipValidation: false) ) Stamp Passing a whole object when one field is needed Data Passing just the needed values Message Communicating via events or messages only Connascence is a finer-grained vocabulary for the same idea: two components are connascent if changing one requires changing the other (of name, type, meaning, position, algorithm, timing…). Prefer weaker forms, and keep strong forms within one module — never spread across a boundary where a later change has to be coordinated across teams."},{"c":"design-principles","s":"dry-kiss-and-yagni","h":"DRY, KISS and YAGNI","t":"# DRY, KISS and YAGNI Beginner Principle Meaning Caveat DRY : Don't Repeat Yourself Every piece of knowledge has one authoritative representation About knowledge, not text. Two identical-looking snippets that change for different reasons are not duplication. Merging them creates coupling. \"Prefer duplication over the wrong abstraction\" (Sandi Metz) KISS : Keep It Simple The simplest solution that works Simple ≠ easy. Simple means few moving parts and clear intent YAGNI : You Aren't Gonna Need It Don't build for hypothetical future requirements Not an excuse to ignore known requirements or basic extensibility points DRY's caveat is worth dwelling on, because it's where people most often over-apply the principle: two pieces of code can look textually identical today and still represent two entirely different pieces of knowledge — a shipping cost formula and a tax calculation might both happen to compute base * rate right now, purely by coincidence, and merging them into one shared helper creates a dependency between two business rules that have nothing to do with each other and will inevitably diverge later. The Rule of Three is the practical discipline that keeps this in check: tolerate duplication the first two times something looks similar; only abstract on the third occurrence, once the actual shared pattern — not just a visual coincidence — is genuinely clear."},{"c":"design-principles","s":"other-principles-worth-naming","h":"Other principles worth naming","t":"# Other principles worth naming Intermediate Composition over inheritance : assemble behaviour from collaborators (decorators, strategies) instead of deep class hierarchies. See OOP . Law of Demeter (principle of least knowledge): talk to friends, not strangers. order.Customer.Address.City.Name couples you to four classes' structure; prefer order.ShippingCity() , or pass what's needed. Tell, Don't Ask : instead of asking an object for data and making decisions for it, tell it what to do. account.Withdraw(50) , which enforces its own rule, beats if (account.Balance >= 50) account.Balance -= 50; . This is the root of rich domain models ( DDD ). Separation of Concerns : HTTP, business rules, persistence and presentation in different places. Principle of Least Astonishment : APIs should behave as their names suggest. GetUser shouldn't create a user. Fail fast : validate early (constructor guards, ValidateOnStart ), throw clear errors close to the cause. Command-Query Separation (CQS) : a method either changes state (command, returns nothing) or returns data (query, no side effects), not both. CQRS scales this up to the architecture level. Hollywood principle (\"don't call us, we'll call you\"): the framework calls your code. That is Inversion of Control. Package principles (for assemblies/projects): stable dependencies (depend in the direction of stability), acyclic dependencies (no cycles between projects; the compiler enforces this for project references)."},{"c":"design-principles","s":"when-to-bend-the-rules","h":"When to bend the rules","t":"# When to bend the rules Advanced Principles are heuristics for managing change, not laws. Over-applying them is its own anti-pattern, and recognising it matters just as much as recognising the violations above: Over-application Symptom Interface for every class (\"header interfaces\") IOrderService with exactly one implementation that never changes and is never mocked Abstracting stable things Wrapping DateTime is good (testability); wrapping string.Join is silly SRP taken to the extreme 40 classes to handle one simple feature, logic impossible to follow Premature OCP Plugin systems for variations that never happen DRY across bounded contexts One shared Customer class used by billing, shipping and marketing, coupling three teams The pragmatic test behind every row above is the same one: does this abstraction pay for itself in testability, a real variation point, or isolating something genuinely volatile (I/O, third parties, time)? If the answer is no, keep it concrete and refactor later — refactoring is cheap precisely when you have tests to lean on, which is the whole reason this book treats testing as load-bearing rather than optional. Q Explain SOLID with an example of each. SRP: split an OrderService that prices, persists and emails into separate collaborators. OCP: add shipping carriers as new IShippingRate implementations instead of editing a switch. LSP: a ReadOnlyRepository throwing on Add breaks callers of IRepository, so it violates LSP. ISP: split a fat IUserService into IUserReader and IUserWriter. DIP: OrderService depends on an IOrderRepository defined in the domain and implemented by an EF Core class in infrastructure. Q What does the Single Responsibility Principle really mean? A module should have one reason to change, meaning it serves one actor or source of requirements. It's about cohesion around a responsibility, not about having only one method. Q What is a Liskov Substitution violation you've seen in .NET? Arrays implement IList<T> but throw NotSupportedException on Add; ReadOnlyCollection implements ICollection.Add by throwing; Square deriving from Rectangle; a repository implementation that throws for some interface methods. Each breaks code written against the base contract. Q How does the Dependency Inversion Principle differ from Dependency Injection? DIP is a design principle: high-level modules and low-level details both depend on abstractions owned by the high-level side, so compile-time dependencies point inward. DI is a technique for supplying concrete implementations to a class from outside, typically via a container. DI makes DIP practical, but you can use DI without following DIP. Q What are coupling and cohesion? Cohesion is how closely related the responsibilities inside a module are (aim high). Coupling is how much modules depend on each other's details (aim low). Good design increases cohesion within modules and reduces coupling between them, so changes stay local. Q Is duplication always bad (DRY)? No. DRY is about duplicated knowledge, not similar-looking code. If two pieces change for different reasons (different bounded contexts or actors), merging them couples them. Prefer duplication over the wrong abstraction; abstract once the pattern is clear (Rule of Three). Q What is the Law of Demeter? A method should only talk to its immediate collaborators (itself, its parameters, objects it creates, its fields), not reach through chains like a.B.C.D(). It reduces coupling to other objects' internal structure. Q When would you not create an interface for a class? When there's a single stable implementation that isn't an I/O or volatile boundary and doesn't need to be faked in tests (simple domain services, value objects, mappers). Interfaces pay off at seams: persistence, external services, time, randomness, and real variation points."},{"c":"design-principles","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Robert C. Martin, Clean Architecture (part III, design principles) and Agile Software Development: Principles, Patterns, and Practices . Mark Seemann, Dependency Injection Principles, Practices, and Patterns . Sandi Metz, \"The Wrong Abstraction\" (blog post). Architectural principles (Microsoft .NET architecture guide)"},{"c":"design-principles","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run (plain console app, .NET 8/9, no packages). SOLID itself is a design heuristic, not a runtime behavior, so this doesn't \"prove SOLID is true\" — it demonstrates the two concrete, checkable claims the chapter makes: an LSP violation actually breaking a caller's assumption, and an OCP-shaped design actually not requiring the existing class to change when a new case is added. C# Run ▶ Copy // --- LSP: Square violates the Rectangle contract that Resize relies on --- var rect = new Rectangle (); Resize (rect); if (rect. Area != 20 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: Rectangle should honor independent Width/Height\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: Resize gives Rectangle the expected area (20)\" ); var square = new Square (); Resize (square); if (square. Area == 20 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected the LSP violation to reproduce, but Square.Area was 20\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: Square breaks the same caller's assumption (Area = {square.Area}, not 20) — this is the LSP violation\" ); // --- OCP: adding a new \"carrier\" never requires editing ShippingCalculator's source --- var calculator = new ShippingCalculator ( new IShippingRate [] { new DhlRate (), new UpsRate (), new FedExRate () }); var fedexQuote = calculator. Quote ( \"fedex\" , 2.0 ); if (fedexQuote <= 0 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: FedEx quote should be a positive amount\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: new carrier (FedEx, quote={fedexQuote}) added with zero edits to ShippingCalculator's code\" ); // Not runtime-verifiable here — see Further reading: Clean Architecture (Martin), Dependency Injection (Seemann) // (DIP/ISP, coupling & cohesion, and \"when to bend the rules\" are design judgments, not facts a program can assert) static void Resize ( Rectangle r) { r. Width = 5 ; r. Height = 4 ; } class Rectangle { public virtual int Width { get ; set ; } public virtual int Height { get ; set ; } public int Area => Width * Height ; } class Square : Rectangle { public override int Width { set { base . Width = value ; base . Height = value ; } } public override int Height { set { base . Width = value ; base . Height = value ; } } } interface IShippingRate { string Carrier { get ; } decimal Calculate ( double weightKg); } class DhlRate : IShippingRate { public string Carrier => \"dhl\" ; public decimal Calculate ( double w) => 5 + ( decimal )w * 1.2m ; } class UpsRate : IShippingRate { public string Carrier => \"ups\" ; public decimal Calculate ( double w) => 7 + ( decimal )w; } class FedExRate : IShippingRate { public string Carrier => \"fedex\" ; public decimal Calculate ( double w) => 6 + ( decimal )w * 1.1m ; } // added later, nothing else touched sealed class ShippingCalculator { private readonly Dictionary < string , IShippingRate > _byCarrier; public ShippingCalculator ( IEnumerable < IShippingRate > rates) => _byCarrier = rates. ToDictionary (r => r. Carrier ); public decimal Quote ( string carrier, double weightKg) => _byCarrier[carrier]. Calculate (weightKg); }"},{"c":"design-patterns","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR A design pattern is just a named, reusable shape for a recurring problem — learning the name lets you recognise framework code (\"that's a Decorator\") without reading its internals. GoF patterns fall into three groups: creational (how objects get made), structural (how objects are composed), behavioural (how objects collaborate). Modern C# and DI absorb several patterns into the language itself: a delegate often replaces a one-method Strategy, yield is Iterator, and the DI container replaces hand-written Factories and Singletons. The most common ones in everyday ASP.NET Core code: Chain of Responsibility (middleware), Factory ( IHttpClientFactory ), Decorator ( DelegatingHandler ), Strategy (keyed DI services), Builder ( WebApplicationBuilder ). Bigger, architecture-scale patterns (Repository, CQRS, Outbox) live in their own chapters, not here."},{"c":"design-patterns","s":"what-patterns-are-and-why-recognising-them-is-worth-the-effo","h":"What patterns are, and why recognising them is worth the effort","t":"# What patterns are, and why recognising them is worth the effort Beginner A design pattern is a reusable, named solution to a recurring design problem. The value of learning the name isn't abstract — it's that once you recognise \"this is Decorator\" or \"this is Chain of Responsibility,\" you can predict how a piece of unfamiliar framework code behaves without reading its implementation line by line. The 1994 \"Gang of Four\" (GoF) book Design Patterns catalogued 23 of them in three groups: Group Concern Patterns Creational How objects are created Factory Method, Abstract Factory, Builder, Singleton, Prototype Structural How objects are composed Adapter, Decorator, Proxy, Facade, Composite, Bridge, Flyweight Behavioural How objects collaborate and share responsibility Strategy, Observer, Command, Chain of Responsibility, Mediator, Template Method, State, Iterator, Visitor, Memento, Interpreter How to answer \"tell me about a design pattern you've used\" Give problem → pattern → .NET example → trade-off . For example: \"We had pricing rules that varied by customer type and kept growing a switch statement. We used the Strategy pattern: an IPricingStrategy per rule, registered in DI as keyed services, and selected at runtime. It made new rules a new class instead of an edit (Open/Closed), and each rule was easy to unit test. The cost is a few more types and an indirection.\" Keep one thing in mind as you read the catalogue below: modern C# and DI make many of these patterns lighter than the 1994 book describes them. A delegate can replace a one-method Strategy, the DI container replaces most hand-written Singletons and Factories, and IEnumerable + yield is Iterator — the pattern isn't missing, it's just been absorbed into the language itself."},{"c":"design-patterns","s":"factory-method-and-simple-factory","h":"Factory Method and Simple Factory","t":"# Factory Method and Simple Factory Beginner Intent: let a method or class decide which concrete type to create, so callers depend only on an abstraction. C# Run ▶ Copy public interface INotifier { Task SendAsync ( string to, string message, CancellationToken ct); } public sealed class NotifierFactory ( IServiceProvider sp) : INotifierFactory { public INotifier Create ( Channel channel) => channel switch { Channel . Email => sp. GetRequiredService < EmailNotifier >(), Channel . Sms => sp. GetRequiredService < SmsNotifier >(), Channel . Push => sp. GetRequiredService < PushNotifier >(), _ => throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException ( nameof (channel)) }; } // Static factory method on the type itself: name the intent, validate, hide constructors public sealed class Order { private Order () { } public static Order Create ( CustomerId customer, IEnumerable < OrderLine > lines) { /* validate invariants */ return new Order { /* … */ }; } } In .NET: IHttpClientFactory.CreateClient() , ILoggerFactory.CreateLogger() , IDbContextFactory<T> , Task.FromResult , TimeSpan.FromSeconds , DI factory registrations ( AddScoped<IFoo>(sp => …) ). Keyed services (.NET 8) often replace hand-written factories."},{"c":"design-patterns","s":"abstract-factory","h":"Abstract Factory","t":"# Abstract Factory Intermediate Intent: create families of related objects without specifying concrete classes, so a family can be swapped as a whole. C# Run ▶ Copy public interface IStorageFactory { IBlobStore CreateBlobStore (); IQueueClient CreateQueue (); ISecretStore CreateSecrets (); } public sealed class AzureStorageFactory (…) : IStorageFactory { /* Blob Storage, Storage Queues, Key Vault */ } public sealed class LocalStorageFactory : IStorageFactory { /* file system, in-memory channel, user secrets */ } Notice the \"swapped as a whole\" part is the actual point here, distinguishing this from a plain Factory Method: you never want AzureStorageFactory 's blob store paired with LocalStorageFactory 's queue — the whole family has to move together, exactly as it would between a local dev environment and a cloud deployment. In .NET: DbProviderFactory (creates connections, commands and parameters for one provider). In practice, registering a coherent set of implementations per environment in DI achieves the same thing."},{"c":"design-patterns","s":"builder","h":"Builder","t":"# Builder Beginner Intent: construct a complex object step by step , with a readable fluent API, separating construction from representation and validating once at Build() . C# Run ▶ Copy var report = new ReportBuilder () . Titled ( \"Q3 sales\" ) . For ( DateRange . Quarter ( 2026 , 3 )) . GroupBy ( ReportGrouping . Region ) . IncludeChart ( ChartType . Bar ) . Build (); // validates and creates an immutable Report public sealed class ReportBuilder { private string ? _title; private DateRange ? _range; private readonly List < ChartType > _charts = []; public ReportBuilder Titled ( string t) { _title = t; return this ; } public ReportBuilder For ( DateRange r) { _range = r; return this ; } public ReportBuilder IncludeChart ( ChartType c) { _charts. Add (c); return this ; } public ReportBuilder GroupBy ( ReportGrouping g) { /* … */ return this ; } public Report Build () => new (_title ?? throw new InvalidOperationException ( \"Title required\" ), _range ?? DateRange . Today , [.. _charts]); } In .NET, everywhere: WebApplication.CreateBuilder() / WebApplicationBuilder , HostApplicationBuilder , IServiceCollection (a builder for the container), ConfigurationBuilder , StringBuilder , UriBuilder , DbContextOptionsBuilder , ModelBuilder , AuthorizationPolicyBuilder , HubConnectionBuilder . Test data builders ( new OrderBuilder().WithLines(3).Paid().Build() ) are a great use in test projects."},{"c":"design-patterns","s":"singleton","h":"Singleton","t":"# Singleton Beginner Intent: exactly one instance , with global access. C# Run ▶ Copy // The classic thread-safe lazy singleton public sealed class Clock { private static readonly Lazy < Clock > _instance = new (() => new Clock ()); public static Clock Instance => _instance. Value ; private Clock () { } } // The modern way: let DI own the single instance builder. Services . AddSingleton < IClock , SystemClock >(); Why the classic Singleton is considered an anti-pattern Clock.Instance is hidden global state : dependencies aren't visible in constructors, it can't be replaced in tests, tests interfere through shared state, and lifetime is fixed forever. A DI singleton gives the one-instance benefit while keeping explicit dependencies and testability. Whatever approach you use, singletons must be thread-safe ."},{"c":"design-patterns","s":"prototype","h":"Prototype","t":"# Prototype Advanced Intent: create new objects by copying an existing one. In .NET: record with expressions ( var copy = order with { Status = Paid }; ), ICloneable (discouraged: deep or shallow is ambiguous), MemberwiseClone (shallow). Useful for templates (\"new order like the last one\") and immutable updates."},{"c":"design-patterns","s":"adapter","h":"Adapter","t":"# Adapter Beginner Intent: make an existing class with an incompatible interface work through the interface your code expects. It's a translator. C# Run ▶ Copy // Your domain's port public interface IPaymentGateway { Task < PaymentResult > ChargeAsync ( Money amount, string paymentToken, CancellationToken ct); } // Adapter over a vendor SDK with a completely different shape public sealed class StripePaymentAdapter ( PaymentIntentService stripe) : IPaymentGateway { public async Task < PaymentResult > ChargeAsync ( Money amount, string token, CancellationToken ct) { var intent = await stripe. CreateAsync ( new PaymentIntentCreateOptions { Amount = ( long )(amount. Amount * 100 ), Currency = amount. Currency . ToLowerInvariant (), PaymentMethod = token, Confirm = true }, cancellationToken: ct); return intent. Status == \"succeeded\" ? PaymentResult . Success (intent. Id ) : PaymentResult . Declined (intent. LastPaymentError ?. Code ); } } Why it matters: this is exactly the \"adapter\" in Ports & Adapters (Hexagonal) architecture, the same pattern Design principles described as infrastructure implementing an interface the business side owns. Vendor types never leak into your domain, and switching providers means writing a new adapter. In .NET: StreamReader (adapts a Stream to text reading), TextWriter adapters, logging providers adapting ILogger to Serilog/NLog."},{"c":"design-patterns","s":"decorator","h":"Decorator","t":"# Decorator Beginner Intent: wrap an object with another object implementing the same interface , adding behaviour before/after delegating. Decorators can be stacked, and the inner object doesn't know. C# Run ▶ Copy public interface IProductRepository { Task < Product ?> GetAsync ( int id, CancellationToken ct); } public sealed class SqlProductRepository ( ShopDb db) : IProductRepository { public Task < Product ?> GetAsync ( int id, CancellationToken ct) => db. Products . FindAsync ([id], ct). AsTask (); } public sealed class CachingProductRepository ( IProductRepository inner, HybridCache cache) : IProductRepository { // HybridCache (a built-in two-level cache, covered in [Caching](ch:caching-ratelimit-cors)) just needs a // key and a function to call on a cache miss — here, \"ask the inner repository\". public async Task < Product ?> GetAsync ( int id, CancellationToken ct) => await cache. GetOrCreateAsync ( $\"product:{id}\" , async t => await inner. GetAsync (id, t), cancellationToken: ct); } public sealed class LoggingProductRepository ( IProductRepository inner, ILogger < LoggingProductRepository > log) : IProductRepository { public async Task < Product ?> GetAsync ( int id, CancellationToken ct) { var sw = Stopwatch . StartNew (); var p = await inner. GetAsync (id, ct); log. LogDebug ( \"Loaded product {Id} in {Ms} ms (hit: {Found})\" , id, sw. ElapsedMilliseconds , p is not null ); return p; } } // Composition: Logging → Caching → Sql (Scrutor) builder. Services . AddScoped < IProductRepository , SqlProductRepository >(); builder. Services . Decorate < IProductRepository , CachingProductRepository >(); builder. Services . Decorate < IProductRepository , LoggingProductRepository >(); Notice the Decorate calls build up exactly the same nested-wrapping shape Middleware showed for A(B(C(endpoint))) — a call to GetAsync here flows Logging → Caching → Sql , each layer free to act before and after delegating onward. In .NET: DelegatingHandler chains in HttpClient, BufferedStream / GZipStream / CryptoStream wrapping a Stream , MediatR pipeline behaviours, and middleware (conceptually). Decorator vs inheritance: you can combine N behaviours with N classes instead of 2^N subclasses."},{"c":"design-patterns","s":"proxy","h":"Proxy","t":"# Proxy Intermediate Intent: a stand-in with the same interface that controls access to the real object: lazy creation, remote access, caching, access control. Proxy kind .NET example Virtual (lazy) EF Core lazy-loading proxies , Lazy<T> Remote gRPC/WCF client stubs, Refit-generated clients Protection A wrapper checking permissions before delegating Dynamic DispatchProxy , Castle DynamicProxy (used by mocking libraries like Moq and NSubstitute) Decorator and Proxy look identical in code — both wrap an object behind the same interface. What tells them apart is why you wrapped it. A decorator adds behaviour to an object that's already there. A proxy controls access to an object, often deciding whether or when it even exists. That's why EF Core's lazy-loading proxy counts as Proxy (it decides when to load the real entity) while CachingProductRepository above counts as Decorator (the real repository is already available; caching just adds a behaviour in front of it)."},{"c":"design-patterns","s":"facade","h":"Facade","t":"# Facade Beginner Intent: provide a simple, unified interface over a complex subsystem. C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class CheckoutFacade ( ICartService carts, IPricingService pricing, IInventory inventory, IPaymentGateway payments, IOrderRepository orders) { public async Task < Result < OrderId >> CheckoutAsync ( Guid cartId, string paymentToken, CancellationToken ct) { var cart = await carts. GetAsync (cartId, ct); var total = pricing. Calculate (cart); await inventory. ReserveAsync (cart. Lines , ct); var payment = await payments. ChargeAsync (total, paymentToken, ct); if (!payment. Succeeded ) { await inventory. ReleaseAsync (cart. Lines , ct); return Result . Fail < OrderId >( \"payment.declined\" ); } var order = Order . FromCart (cart, total, payment. Reference ); orders. Add (order); return order. Id ; } } In .NET: WebApplication.CreateBuilder() (a facade over host, config, logging and DI setup), File.ReadAllTextAsync (over streams and encodings), JsonSerializer (over readers/writers), HttpClient.GetFromJsonAsync . Application services and use-case handlers are often facades over the domain."},{"c":"design-patterns","s":"composite","h":"Composite","t":"# Composite Intermediate Intent: treat individual objects and groups of objects uniformly through one interface (tree structures). C# Run ▶ Copy public interface IPriceRule { decimal Apply ( decimal price, Order o); } public sealed class PercentOff ( decimal pct) : IPriceRule { public decimal Apply ( decimal p, Order o) => p * ( 1 - pct); } public sealed class CompositeRule ( params IPriceRule [] rules) : IPriceRule { public decimal Apply ( decimal p, Order o) => rules. Aggregate (p, (acc, r) => r. Apply (acc, o)); // a group IS a rule } In .NET: CompositeChangeToken , CompositeFileProvider , UI control trees, expression trees, authorization requirements combined in a policy, and the IConfiguration sections tree."},{"c":"design-patterns","s":"bridge-and-flyweight","h":"Bridge and Flyweight","t":"# Bridge and Flyweight Advanced Bridge: separate an abstraction from its implementation so both vary independently. Example: ILogger (abstraction) bridging to many logging providers (implementations); ADO.NET's abstract DbConnection with provider implementations. Flyweight: share common state among many fine-grained objects to save memory. In .NET: string interning, cached boxed values, ArrayPool<T> , Task.CompletedTask , cached JsonSerializerOptions , FrozenDictionary lookups shared across requests."},{"c":"design-patterns","s":"strategy","h":"Strategy","t":"# Strategy Beginner Intent: define a family of interchangeable algorithms behind one interface and choose one at runtime. C# Run ▶ Copy public interface IDiscountStrategy { string Name { get ; } decimal Discount ( Order o); } public sealed class NoDiscount : IDiscountStrategy { public string Name => \"none\" ; public decimal Discount ( Order o) => 0 ; } public sealed class VipDiscount : IDiscountStrategy { public string Name => \"vip\" ; public decimal Discount ( Order o) => o. Subtotal * 0.10m ; } public sealed class BlackFriday : IDiscountStrategy { public string Name => \"blackfriday\" ; public decimal Discount ( Order o) => o. Subtotal * 0.30m ; } // Select by key with .NET 8 keyed services builder. Services . AddKeyedSingleton < IDiscountStrategy , VipDiscount >( \"vip\" ); builder. Services . AddKeyedSingleton < IDiscountStrategy , BlackFriday >( \"blackfriday\" ); builder. Services . AddKeyedSingleton < IDiscountStrategy , NoDiscount >( \"none\" ); public sealed class PricingService ( IServiceProvider sp, ICampaignCalendar calendar) { public decimal Price ( Order o) { var key = calendar. IsBlackFriday ? \"blackfriday\" : o. Customer . IsVip ? \"vip\" : \"none\" ; return o. Subtotal - sp. GetRequiredKeyedService < IDiscountStrategy >(key). Discount (o); } } // Lightweight strategy: just a delegate Func < Order , decimal > discount = o => o. Subtotal > 100 ? 10 : 0 ; Notice how directly this maps onto the Open/Closed worked example from Design principles : the shipping-rate carriers there were literally this same pattern, named. In .NET: IComparer<T> passed to Sort , IEqualityComparer<T> for dictionaries, authentication schemes, JsonNamingPolicy , retry strategies in Polly, IPasswordHasher , load-balancing policies in YARP. Strategy is the most-used pattern in DI-based code."},{"c":"design-patterns","s":"observer","h":"Observer","t":"# Observer Beginner Intent: a subject notifies a list of observers when something happens, without knowing who they are. C# Run ▶ Copy // Language-level: events public event EventHandler < OrderPlacedEventArgs >? OrderPlaced ; // In-process \"observer\" via DI: all handlers of a notification public interface IOrderPlacedHandler { Task HandleAsync ( OrderPlaced e, CancellationToken ct); } public sealed class DomainEventDispatcher ( IEnumerable < IOrderPlacedHandler > handlers) { public Task PublishAsync ( OrderPlaced e, CancellationToken ct) => Task . WhenAll (handlers. Select (h => h. HandleAsync (e, ct))); } In .NET: C# event s, IObservable<T> / IObserver<T> (Reactive Extensions), IOptionsMonitor<T>.OnChange , IChangeToken , INotifyPropertyChanged (UI), DiagnosticListener / ActivityListener , MediatR notifications. At system scale , Observer becomes publish/subscribe with a message broker ( Messaging ) — same idea, the subject and observers just live in different processes."},{"c":"design-patterns","s":"command","h":"Command","t":"# Command Intermediate Intent: encapsulate a request as an object , so you can queue it, log it, retry it, undo it, or send it elsewhere. C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed record PlaceOrder ( Guid CustomerId , IReadOnlyList < LineDto > Lines ); // the command: pure data public interface ICommandHandler < in TCommand > { Task HandleAsync ( TCommand command, CancellationToken ct); } public sealed class PlaceOrderHandler (…) : ICommandHandler < PlaceOrder > { /* … */ } In .NET and architecture: CQRS commands, MediatR IRequest , messages sent to a queue (a command sent to Service Bus is literally a serialised Command object), Hangfire jobs, undo stacks in UI apps. Commands are named in the imperative ( PlaceOrder , CancelSubscription ); events are named in the past tense ( OrderPlaced ) — a naming discipline that pays for itself the moment you're scanning a codebase and need to tell at a glance which messages can still be rejected and which already happened."},{"c":"design-patterns","s":"chain-of-responsibility","h":"Chain of Responsibility","t":"# Chain of Responsibility Beginner Intent: pass a request along a chain of handlers ; each either handles it, passes it on, or both. C# Run ▶ Copy public abstract class ApprovalHandler { private ApprovalHandler ? _next; public ApprovalHandler SetNext ( ApprovalHandler next) => _next = next; public virtual ApprovalResult Handle ( Expense e) => _next?. Handle (e) ?? ApprovalResult . Rejected ( \"No approver\" ); } public sealed class TeamLead : ApprovalHandler { public override ApprovalResult Handle ( Expense e) => e. Amount <= 500 ? ApprovalResult . Approved ( \"lead\" ) : base . Handle (e); } public sealed class Director : ApprovalHandler { public override ApprovalResult Handle ( Expense e) => e. Amount <= 10_000 ? ApprovalResult . Approved ( \"director\" ) : base . Handle (e); } In .NET, the defining example: the ASP.NET Core middleware pipeline . Each middleware handles the request, short-circuits, or calls next . Also: HttpClient's DelegatingHandler chain, MVC filters, MediatR pipeline behaviours, and authorization handlers. See Middleware ."},{"c":"design-patterns","s":"mediator","h":"Mediator","t":"# Mediator Intermediate Intent: objects communicate through a mediator instead of referencing each other directly, which reduces many-to-many coupling to one-to-many. C# Run ▶ Copy app. MapPost ( \"/orders\" , ( PlaceOrder cmd, ISender mediator, CancellationToken ct) => mediator. Send (cmd, ct)); // The endpoint doesn't know which handler, or which pipeline behaviours (validation, logging, transactions), will run. In .NET: MediatR (commercial licence for larger companies since 2025), the source-generated Mediator library, Wolverine, Brighter; SignalR hubs (clients talk via the hub); and an aggregate root mediating access to its children. Trade-off: decoupling and a central pipeline vs indirection (\"go to definition\" no longer finds the handler). See CQRS & Mediator ."},{"c":"design-patterns","s":"template-method","h":"Template Method","t":"# Template Method Intermediate Intent: a base class defines the skeleton of an algorithm , and subclasses fill in specific steps. C# Run ▶ Copy public abstract class ImportJob { public async Task RunAsync ( CancellationToken ct) // the template: fixed sequence { var raw = await DownloadAsync (ct); var rows = Parse (raw); var valid = rows. Where ( Validate ). ToList (); await SaveAsync (valid, ct); await OnCompletedAsync (valid. Count , ct); // hook with a default } protected abstract Task < Stream > DownloadAsync ( CancellationToken ct); protected abstract IEnumerable < ImportRow > Parse ( Stream raw); protected virtual bool Validate ( ImportRow r) => true ; protected abstract Task SaveAsync ( IReadOnlyList < ImportRow > rows, CancellationToken ct); protected virtual Task OnCompletedAsync ( int count, CancellationToken ct) => Task . CompletedTask ; } In .NET: BackgroundService.ExecuteAsync , AuthenticationHandler<T>.HandleAuthenticateAsync , AuthorizationHandler<T>.HandleRequirementAsync , DbContext.OnModelCreating / OnConfiguring , JsonConverter<T>.Read/Write , Stream subclasses. Strategy (composition) is often preferred when steps vary independently."},{"c":"design-patterns","s":"state","h":"State","t":"# State Intermediate Intent: an object changes its behaviour when its internal state changes , with each state as its own class, instead of if (status == …) scattered everywhere. C# Run ▶ Copy public abstract class OrderState { public virtual OrderState Pay ( Order o) => throw Invalid ( nameof ( Pay )); public virtual OrderState Ship ( Order o) => throw Invalid ( nameof ( Ship )); public virtual OrderState Cancel ( Order o) => throw Invalid ( nameof ( Cancel )); private InvalidOperationException Invalid ( string action) => new ( $\"Cannot {action} an order that is {GetType().Name}\" ); } public sealed class Placed : OrderState { public override OrderState Pay ( Order o) => new Paid (); public override OrderState Cancel ( Order o) => new Cancelled (); } public sealed class Paid : OrderState { public override OrderState Ship ( Order o) => new Shipped (); public override OrderState Cancel ( Order o) { o. Refund (); return new Cancelled (); } } public sealed class Shipped : OrderState { } public sealed class Cancelled : OrderState { } For simpler lifecycles, an enum plus a transition table or a library like Stateless is often enough. MassTransit sagas are state machines across services."},{"c":"design-patterns","s":"iterator-visitor-and-the-rest","h":"Iterator, Visitor and the rest","t":"# Iterator, Visitor and the rest Advanced Iterator: sequential access without exposing the structure. Built into C# : IEnumerable<T> / IEnumerator<T> , foreach , yield return , IAsyncEnumerable<T> . Visitor: add operations to a class hierarchy without changing it, via double dispatch. In .NET: ExpressionVisitor (EF Core's query translation is visitors all the way down, exactly the expression-tree walk LINQ traced), Roslyn's CSharpSyntaxVisitor . Modern C# pattern matching ( switch on types) often replaces simple visitors. Memento: capture and restore an object's state (undo, snapshots). Event sourcing snapshots are a relative. Interpreter: evaluate sentences in a small language (rule engines, query parsers). Expression trees and Roslyn scripting are related."},{"c":"design-patterns","s":"beyond-gof-patterns-youll-meet-in-net-backends","h":"Beyond GoF: patterns you'll meet in .NET backends","t":"# Beyond GoF: patterns you'll meet in .NET backends Intermediate Pattern Where it's covered Dependency Injection / Service Locator (anti-pattern) Dependency injection Options pattern Configuration Repository, Unit of Work, Specification App architectures CQRS, Mediator pipeline, Result CQRS & Result Aggregate, Value Object, Domain Event DDD Outbox, Inbox, Saga, Competing Consumers Messaging & events Retry, Circuit Breaker, Bulkhead, Timeout Resilience API Gateway, BFF, Strangler Fig, Sidecar Microservices Cache-Aside Caching Q Which design patterns does ASP.NET Core itself use? Chain of Responsibility (the middleware pipeline, DelegatingHandlers, filters), Builder (WebApplicationBuilder, IServiceCollection, options builders), Factory (IHttpClientFactory, ILoggerFactory, IDbContextFactory), Decorator (DelegatingHandler, stream wrappers), Strategy (authentication schemes, naming policies, comparers), Observer (IOptionsMonitor.OnChange, change tokens), Template Method (BackgroundService, AuthenticationHandler), Adapter (logging providers), Facade (CreateBuilder) and Dependency Injection throughout. Q Strategy vs State? Both put varying behaviour behind an interface. In Strategy, the client or configuration chooses an algorithm and the strategies are independent of each other. In State, the object itself transitions between state objects as its internal state changes, and states know about the transitions. Q Decorator vs Proxy vs Adapter? Decorator and Proxy keep the same interface: a decorator adds behaviour (logging, caching), while a proxy controls access to the real object (lazy loading, remote, permissions). An adapter changes an interface into the one the client expects. Q How do you implement the Decorator pattern with DI? Register the real implementation, then register the interface with a decorator that receives the inner instance, either via a factory lambda ( sp => new CachingRepo(sp.GetRequiredService<SqlRepo>(), …) ) or with Scrutor's Decorate<TInterface, TDecorator>(). Decorators can be stacked. Q Why is the Singleton pattern often called an anti-pattern, and what do you use instead? A static Instance hides dependencies, introduces global mutable state, can't be replaced in tests, and couples callers to a concrete class. Use the DI container's singleton lifetime so there's still one instance, but it's injected explicitly and replaceable. It still must be thread-safe. Q Where does the Chain of Responsibility pattern appear in .NET? The ASP.NET Core middleware pipeline (each component handles or passes to next), HttpClient's DelegatingHandler pipeline, MVC and endpoint filters, MediatR pipeline behaviours, and authorization handlers. Q What's the difference between the Command pattern and an event? A command is a request to do something, addressed to one handler, named in the imperative (PlaceOrder), and it may be rejected. An event is a notification that something already happened, named in the past tense (OrderPlaced), published to zero or more subscribers, and it can't be rejected. Q When is the Builder pattern useful? When constructing an object requires many optional parts or steps, when you want a readable fluent API and validation at Build(), or when the result should be immutable. Examples: WebApplicationBuilder, StringBuilder, test data builders."},{"c":"design-patterns","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Design Patterns: Elements of Reusable Object-Oriented Software (Gamma, Helm, Johnson, Vlissides), the original GoF book. Head First Design Patterns (2nd ed.): the most approachable introduction. refactoring.guru/design-patterns has excellent diagrams and C# examples for every GoF pattern. Patterns of Enterprise Application Architecture by Martin Fowler: Repository, Unit of Work, Data Mapper and more."},{"c":"design-patterns","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter The checks below instantiate six of the patterns above with plain, dependency-free stand-ins (no DI container, no HybridCache, no EF Core) and assert the one behavioural claim each pattern is actually known for. Save as Program.cs in a new console project ( dotnet new console ) and run with dotnet run . C# Run ▶ Copy // Verifies claims from this chapter: Builder validates at Build(), Decorator composition // order, Strategy picks an algorithm at runtime, Singleton (Lazy<T>) gives exactly one instance, State // rejects invalid transitions, Composite treats a group of rules as a single rule. using System ; using System . Collections . Generic ; using System . Linq ; int fails = 0 ; void Ok ( string s) => Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: {s}\" ); void Check ( bool cond, string s) { if (cond) Ok (s); else { Console . WriteLine ( $\"FAIL: {s}\" ); fails++; } } // --- Builder: validates once at Build(), produces an immutable-ish result --- Check ( Throws (() => new ReportBuilder (). Build ()), \"Builder.Build() throws when a required field (Title) was never set\" ); var report = new ReportBuilder (). Titled ( \"Q3 sales\" ). Build (); Check (report. Title == \"Q3 sales\" , \"Builder.Build() succeeds once required fields are set\" ); // --- Decorator: stacking order is the order you wrap in, and wrapping doesn't change the interface --- var trace = new List < string >(); IRepo traced = new TraceRepo ( new SqlRepo (), trace, \"Sql\" ); traced = new TraceRepo (traced, trace, \"Caching\" ); traced = new TraceRepo (traced, trace, \"Logging\" ); traced. Get (); Check (trace. SequenceEqual ( new [] { \"Logging\" , \"Caching\" , \"Sql\" }), \"Decorator: Logging→Caching→Sql registration runs outer-to-inner, in that order\" ); // --- Strategy: swapping the algorithm changes the result without changing the caller --- IDiscount vip = new VipDiscount (); IDiscount none = new NoDiscount (); Check (vip. Discount ( 100 ) == 10 && none. Discount ( 100 ) == 0 , \"Strategy: selecting a different IDiscount changes behaviour without changing the caller\" ); // --- Singleton via Lazy<T>: every access returns the same instance --- Check ( ReferenceEquals ( Clock . Instance , Clock . Instance ), \"Singleton (Lazy<T>): Clock.Instance always returns the same object\" ); // --- State: valid transitions succeed, invalid ones throw instead of silently doing nothing --- OrderState placed = new Placed (); OrderState paid = placed. Pay (); Check (paid is Paid , \"State: Placed.Pay() transitions to Paid\" ); Check ( Throws (() => placed. Ship ()), \"State: Placed.Ship() is invalid and throws, instead of silently no-op'ing\" ); // --- Composite: a group of rules behaves like a single rule (uniform interface) --- IPriceRule tenPct = new PercentOff ( 0.10m ); IPriceRule composite = new CompositeRule ( new PercentOff ( 0.10m ), new PercentOff ( 0.10m )); Check (composite. Apply ( 100 ) == tenPct. Apply (tenPct. Apply ( 100 )), \"Composite: applying a group of rules equals applying each rule in sequence\" ); Console . WriteLine (fails == 0 ? \"\\nAll checks passed.\" : $\"\\n{fails} check(s) failed.\" ); if (fails > 0 ) Environment . Exit ( 1 ); static bool Throws ( Action a) { try { a(); return false ; } catch { return true ; } } sealed class ReportBuilder { private string ? _title; public ReportBuilder Titled ( string t) { _title = t; return this ; } public Report Build () => new (_title ?? throw new InvalidOperationException ( \"Title required\" )); } sealed record Report ( string Title ); interface IRepo { string Get (); } sealed class SqlRepo : IRepo { public string Get () => \"data\" ; } sealed class TraceRepo ( IRepo inner, List < string > trace, string name) : IRepo { public string Get () { trace. Add (name); return inner. Get (); } } interface IDiscount { decimal Discount ( decimal subtotal); } sealed class NoDiscount : IDiscount { public decimal Discount ( decimal s) => 0 ; } sealed class VipDiscount : IDiscount { public decimal Discount ( decimal s) => s * 0.10m ; } sealed class Clock {"},{"c":"app-architectures","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Architecture at the solution scale asks: how is the codebase divided, and which parts are allowed to depend on which? Classic layered (n-tier) architecture has business logic depend on data access, which couples business rules to persistence — Clean/Onion/Hexagonal fix this by putting the domain in the center and making all dependencies point inward . DbContext / DbSet already are a unit-of-work/repository — a generic repository wrapper on top of EF Core usually hides features rather than adding value; per-aggregate repositories are the version that actually earns its keep. Vertical Slice Architecture organizes by feature (one file per use case) instead of by technical layer; it combines well with a shared domain core. A modular monolith (strongly separated modules, one deployable) gets most of microservices' boundary benefits without the network/distributed-transaction cost — a common default before reaching for real microservices."},{"c":"app-architectures","s":"what-architecture-means-at-this-scale","h":"What \"architecture\" means at this scale","t":"# What \"architecture\" means at this scale Beginner Everything in Design principles applied to individual classes. This chapter asks the same question at the scale of an entire solution: how do you divide a codebase into parts, what is each part responsible for, and which parts may depend on which ? Good architecture keeps business rules independent of delivery mechanisms (HTTP, UI, messages) and infrastructure (databases, queues, external APIs), so that: business logic can be tested without a web server or database, frameworks and vendors can be upgraded or swapped with local changes, different people can work on different areas without stepping on each other, the code tells you what the system does (orders, payments), not just what it's built with (controllers, repositories)."},{"c":"app-architectures","s":"layered-n-tier-architecture-the-traditional-starting-point","h":"Layered (n-tier) architecture: the traditional starting point","t":"# Layered (n-tier) architecture: the traditional starting point Beginner Diagram Copy ┌──────────────────────────┐ │ Presentation (API / UI) │ controllers, endpoints, view models ├──────────────────────────┤ │ Business Logic (BLL) │ services, rules ├──────────────────────────┤ │ Data Access (DAL) │ repositories, EF Core, SQL ├──────────────────────────┤ │ Database │ └──────────────────────────┘ Each layer depends on the one BELOW it. Pros: simple, familiar, and a clear separation of technical concerns. Cons: trace the dependency arrows and the real problem becomes visible — the business layer depends on data access , so business rules are coupled to persistence (testing needs the DAL, entities are shaped by tables), and a single feature's logic is scattered across all four layers rather than living in one place. It tends toward anemic models and \"service classes\" holding all the actual logic."},{"c":"app-architectures","s":"onion-hexagonal-and-clean-architecture-three-names-one-fix","h":"Onion, Hexagonal and Clean Architecture: three names, one fix","t":"# Onion, Hexagonal and Clean Architecture: three names, one fix Intermediate All three names below are solving exactly the layered architecture's core problem: put the domain at the centre , and make all dependencies point inward , so business logic never has to know anything about the technology serving or persisting it. Diagram Copy ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │ Infrastructure & Presentation (outer ring) │ │ ASP.NET Core endpoints · EF Core · Service Bus · Stripe │ │ ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │ │ │ Application (use cases) │ │ │ │ PlaceOrderHandler · DTOs · interfaces it needs: │ │ │ │ IOrderRepository, IPaymentGateway, IEmailSender │ │ │ │ ┌────────────────────────────────────────────┐ │ │ │ │ │ Domain (core) │ │ │ │ │ │ Order · Money · OrderPlaced · rules │ │ │ │ │ │ no dependencies on anything outside │ │ │ │ │ └────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │ │ │ └────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │ └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ THE DEPENDENCY RULE: source code dependencies point INWARD only Name Author Emphasis Hexagonal / Ports & Adapters Alistair Cockburn (2005) The app has ports (interfaces) and adapters (implementations) for each external actor: driving (HTTP, CLI, tests) and driven (DB, email) Onion Jeffrey Palermo (2008) Concentric layers: domain model → domain services → application services → infrastructure/UI Clean Robert C. Martin (2012) Entities → use cases → interface adapters → frameworks & drivers; the Dependency Rule How it works, concretely: the inner layers define interfaces for what they need ( IOrderRepository ), and the outer layers implement them ( EfOrderRepository ) — exactly the Dependency Inversion Principle from Design principles , now applied to the whole solution rather than one class. At runtime, control still flows outward (handler → repository → SQL), but the compile-time dependency points inward — the same inversion that single OrderService example demonstrated, scaled up to entire projects."},{"c":"app-architectures","s":"a-clean-architecture-solution-in-net","h":"A Clean Architecture solution in .NET","t":"# A Clean Architecture solution in .NET Intermediate Diagram Copy src/ ├── Shop.Domain/ ← references: NOTHING (maybe a small shared kernel) │ ├── Orders/ Order.cs, OrderLine.cs, OrderStatus.cs, OrderPlaced.cs, IOrderRepository.cs │ ├── Customers/ Customer.cs, CustomerId.cs │ └── Common/ Entity.cs, ValueObject.cs, Money.cs, DomainException.cs ├── Shop.Application/ ← references: Domain │ ├── Orders/PlaceOrder/ PlaceOrderCommand.cs, PlaceOrderHandler.cs, PlaceOrderValidator.cs │ ├── Orders/GetOrder/ GetOrderQuery.cs, GetOrderHandler.cs, OrderDto.cs │ ├── Abstractions/ IPaymentGateway.cs, IEmailSender.cs, IUnitOfWork.cs, IClock.cs │ └── DependencyInjection.cs (AddApplication) ├── Shop.Infrastructure/ ← references: Application, Domain │ ├── Persistence/ ShopDb.cs, Configurations/*.cs, Migrations/, OrderRepository.cs │ ├── Payments/ StripePaymentGateway.cs │ ├── Messaging/ OutboxPublisher.cs │ └── DependencyInjection.cs (AddInfrastructure) └── Shop.Api/ ← references: Application, Infrastructure (only for DI wiring) ├── Endpoints/OrderEndpoints.cs ├── Program.cs ← composition root └── appsettings.json tests/ ├── Shop.Domain.Tests/ unit tests: pure, fast ├── Shop.Application.Tests/ handler tests with fakes ├── Shop.Api.IntegrationTests/ WebApplicationFactory + Testcontainers └── Shop.ArchitectureTests/ enforce the dependency rule Notice what the src/Shop.Domain/ comment says — \"references: NOTHING\" — and what that absence actually buys the test projects below it: Shop.Domain.Tests can run in milliseconds with no database, no HTTP server, no mocking framework needed for infrastructure, because the domain project genuinely has nothing external to fake. C# Run ▶ Copy // Domain: pure C#, rules inside the model public sealed class Order : Entity < OrderId > { private readonly List < OrderLine > _lines = []; public CustomerId CustomerId { get ; private set ; } public OrderStatus Status { get ; private set ; } public IReadOnlyList < OrderLine > Lines => _lines; public static Order Place ( CustomerId customer, IEnumerable <( ProductId , int , Money )> lines) { /* invariants, raise OrderPlaced */ } } // Application: orchestrates a use case using abstractions public sealed class PlaceOrderHandler ( IOrderRepository orders, IPaymentGateway payments, IUnitOfWork uow) { public async Task < Result < OrderId >> HandleAsync ( PlaceOrderCommand cmd, CancellationToken ct) { var order = Order . Place (cmd. CustomerId , cmd. Lines ); var payment = await payments. ChargeAsync (order. Total , cmd. PaymentToken , ct); if (!payment. Succeeded ) return Result . Failure < OrderId >( OrderErrors . PaymentDeclined ); orders. Add (order); await uow. SaveChangesAsync (ct); return order. Id ; } } // Infrastructure: implements the abstractions with real technology public sealed class OrderRepository ( ShopDb db) : IOrderRepository { public Task < Order ?> GetAsync ( OrderId id, CancellationToken ct) => db. Orders . Include (o => o. Lines ). FirstOrDefaultAsync (o => o. Id == id, ct); public void Add ( Order order) => db. Orders . Add (order); } // API: thin HTTP adapter orders. MapPost ( \"/\" , async ( PlaceOrderRequest req, PlaceOrderHandler handler, ClaimsPrincipal user, CancellationToken ct) => ( await handler. HandleAsync (req. ToCommand (user), ct)). ToHttpResult ()); // Composition root builder. Services . AddApplication (). AddInfrastructure (builder. Configuration ); What you get The domain and application layers compile and test without ASP.NET Core, EF Core or Stripe . Switching Stripe for Adyen means a new adapter in Infrastructure. Adding a gRPC or message-driven entry point reuses the same handlers. The folder names under Application read like a list of use cases: the architecture screams what the system does. Common Clean Architecture mistakes Leaking EF Core into the domain ( [Table] attributes, DbSet , IQueryable returned from repositories). Keep mapping in Infrastructure with the Fluent API. Application depending on Infrastructure \"just for one class"},{"c":"app-architectures","s":"repository-and-unit-of-work-a-debate-thats-really-about-ef-c","h":"Repository and Unit of Work: a debate that's really about EF Core","t":"# Repository and Unit of Work: a debate that's really about EF Core Intermediate Repository (Fowler, PoEAA ): mediates between the domain and data mapping, acting like an in-memory collection of aggregates — an \"aggregate\" here just means a cluster of related objects (say, an Order plus its OrderLine s) that's saved and loaded as one unit, a concept DDD covers in full — exposed via GetAsync , Add , Remove , hiding how they're stored. Unit of Work: tracks changes during a business transaction and commits them together . Here's the thing worth being direct about before picking a side: EF Core already implements both of these . DbSet<T> is a repository — it already gives you Add , Find , and querying over an in-memory-feeling collection. DbContext is a unit of work — it already tracks every change and commits them together via SaveChanges() , exactly the mechanism EF Core fundamentals described. So the real debate isn't \"should I use these patterns,\" it's \"should I wrap EF Core's own implementation of them in another layer\": Approach Pros Cons Use DbContext directly in handlers Least code; full EF power (projections, Include, ExecuteUpdate ) Application layer depends on EF Core; harder to enforce aggregate loading rules Generic repository IRepository<T> over EF Looks tidy Usually adds nothing : hides EF features, leaks IQueryable or forces lowest-common-denominator queries. Often called an anti-pattern Specific repositories per aggregate ( IOrderRepository ) Domain language; one place to define how an aggregate is loaded; keeps EF out of Domain/Application; easy to fake More code Specific repositories for writes + direct queries (EF/Dapper) for reads Best of both: aggregates protected, reads optimised (CQRS) Two styles to learn A balanced answer, and a common modern choice: specific aggregate repositories for the write side (DDD), and query handlers using DbContext or Dapper with projections for the read side — which mirrors the exact write/read split ADO.NET & Dapper recommended for its own, narrower reasons. C# Run ▶ Copy public interface IUnitOfWork { Task < int > SaveChangesAsync ( CancellationToken ct = default ); } public sealed class ShopDb ( DbContextOptions < ShopDb > o) : DbContext (o), IUnitOfWork { /* SaveChangesAsync already exists */ } builder. Services . AddScoped < IUnitOfWork >(sp => sp. GetRequiredService < ShopDb >()); Specification pattern: encapsulates query criteria as objects ( new OrdersForCustomerSpec(customerId, includeLines: true) ), so repositories don't explode into dozens of GetByXAndY methods (e.g. the Ardalis.Specification library)."},{"c":"app-architectures","s":"vertical-slice-architecture-organising-by-feature-instead-of","h":"Vertical Slice Architecture: organising by feature instead of by role","t":"# Vertical Slice Architecture: organising by feature instead of by role Intermediate Coined by Jimmy Bogard. Instead of organising by technical layer — the instinct every architecture above this section shares — organise by feature : each request type (a \"slice\" through all layers) owns everything it needs, in one place. Diagram Copy Layered / Clean: Vertical slices: Controllers/ OrdersController Features/ Services/ OrderService Orders/ Repositories/ OrderRepository PlaceOrder.cs ← request, validator, handler, endpoint DTOs/ OrderDto GetOrder.cs Validators/ OrderValidator CancelOrder.cs Products/ → one feature touches 5 folders SearchProducts.cs → one feature is one file or folder C# Run ▶ Copy public static class CancelOrder { public sealed record Command ( int OrderId , string Reason ); public sealed class Validator : AbstractValidator < Command > { public Validator () { RuleFor (x => x. Reason ). NotEmpty (). MaximumLength ( 500 ); } } internal static async Task < Results < NoContent , NotFound , Conflict < ProblemDetails >>> Handle ( int id, CancelRequest body, ShopDb db, TimeProvider clock, CancellationToken ct) { var order = await db. Orders . FirstOrDefaultAsync (o => o. Id == id, ct); if (order is null ) return TypedResults . NotFound (); var result = order. Cancel (body. Reason , clock. GetUtcNow ()); // rule lives in the domain model if (result. IsFailure ) return TypedResults . Conflict (result. Error . ToProblem ()); await db. SaveChangesAsync (ct); return TypedResults . NoContent (); } public static void Map ( IEndpointRouteBuilder app) => app. MapPost ( \"/orders/{id:int}/cancel\" , Handle ). AddEndpointFilter < ValidationFilter < CancelRequest >>(); } Clean / layered Vertical slices Organised by Technical role Feature / use case Change to one feature touches Many folders/projects One file/folder Coupling between features Shared services encourage it Minimised; slices share little Abstractions Many shared ones Added per slice only when needed Risk Ceremony, scattered features Duplication; logic leaking into handlers instead of the domain These two approaches combine well rather than competing: a rich domain project shared by vertical slices in the API/application project is a very popular structure today — note CancelOrder.Handle above still calls order.Cancel(...) , keeping the actual business rule inside the domain model even though the surrounding plumbing is organised by feature. Minimal APIs, CQRS and the REPR pattern (FastEndpoints) fit slices naturally."},{"c":"app-architectures","s":"the-modular-monolith-most-of-microservices-benefits-none-of-","h":"The modular monolith: most of microservices' benefits, none of the network","t":"# The modular monolith: most of microservices' benefits, none of the network Intermediate A single deployable application divided into modules that are as independent as microservices logically : one per bounded context, each with its own domain, data (its own schema or DbContext) and a small public API. Diagram Copy Shop.Host (one ASP.NET Core app, one deployment) ├── Modules/Ordering/ Ordering.Api · Ordering.Application · Ordering.Domain · Ordering.Infrastructure (schema \"ordering\") ├── Modules/Catalog/ … (schema \"catalog\") ├── Modules/Billing/ … (schema \"billing\") └── Shared/ building blocks (Result, Entity, in-process event bus) Rules: modules talk only via public contracts (interfaces or integration events), never via another module's tables or internal classes. Why: most of the structural benefits of microservices (clear boundaries, team ownership, independent evolution) come from exactly this kind of strict module separation — and this structure gets them with none of the distributed-systems cost (network failures, distributed transactions, many deployments) that real service-to-service calls would introduce. If a module later genuinely needs independent scaling, it's already shaped to be extracted into a service with comparatively little rework, because the boundary was already enforced. Many teams now recommend this as the default starting architecture (see Microservices ), reaching for actual microservices only once a concrete, specific reason demands it."},{"c":"app-architectures","s":"enforcing-architecture","h":"Enforcing architecture","t":"# Enforcing architecture Advanced Boundaries that aren't enforced erode — a well-drawn diagram on a wiki page stops anyone from accidentally violating it for approximately one sprint. The tools below make the boundary something the build itself checks: Project references: the compiler rejects using Shop.Infrastructure in Domain if there's no reference. The strongest guarantee. internal by default : only expose what other projects need. Architecture tests with NetArchTest or ArchUnitNET: C# Run ▶ Copy [Fact] public void Domain_has_no_dependency_on_other_layers () => Types . InAssembly ( typeof ( Order ). Assembly ). ShouldNot () . HaveDependencyOnAny ( \"Shop.Application\" , \"Shop.Infrastructure\" , \"Shop.Api\" , \"Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore\" ) . GetResult (). IsSuccessful . ShouldBeTrue (); [Fact] public void Modules_do_not_reference_each_others_internals () => Types . InAssembly ( typeof ( OrderingModule ). Assembly ). ShouldNot () . HaveDependencyOn ( \"Shop.Modules.Billing.Domain\" ). GetResult (). IsSuccessful . ShouldBeTrue (); Analyzers / code review checklists , and documenting decisions as ADRs (Architecture Decision Records)."},{"c":"app-architectures","s":"choosing","h":"Choosing","t":"# Choosing Intermediate Situation Reasonable choice Small CRUD service, simple rules Minimal APIs + EF Core directly, feature folders; little layering Rich business rules, long-lived product Clean Architecture with a DDD domain; CQRS reads Many features, fast-moving team Vertical slices over a shared domain Several bounded contexts, one team or a few Modular monolith Independent scaling/deployment, many teams, mature DevOps Microservices (often extracted from a modular monolith) Q What is Clean Architecture? An architecture that places the domain (entities and rules) and application use cases at the centre, with infrastructure and presentation in outer layers, governed by the Dependency Rule: source dependencies point only inward. Inner layers define interfaces (ports) that outer layers implement (adapters), so business logic is independent of frameworks, databases and UI, and is easy to test. Q What's the difference between layered architecture and Clean/Onion architecture? In classic layered architecture, the business layer depends on the data access layer below it. In Clean/Onion, dependencies are inverted: the domain and application define the abstractions, and infrastructure depends on them. Persistence becomes a detail plugged into the core. Q What is Hexagonal (Ports and Adapters) architecture? The application core exposes ports (interfaces) for everything it needs or offers. Adapters connect external actors: driving adapters (HTTP endpoints, message consumers, tests) call the core, and driven adapters (DB repositories, email, payment gateways) implement the core's outbound ports. It's essentially the same idea as Clean/Onion. Q Should you use the Repository pattern with EF Core? DbContext already is a unit of work and DbSet a repository, so a generic repository wrapper usually adds little and hides EF capabilities. Specific repositories per aggregate are valuable in DDD/Clean Architecture to encapsulate aggregate loading and keep EF out of the inner layers. A common approach is repositories for writes and direct EF/Dapper projections for reads. Q What is Vertical Slice Architecture and how does it compare with Clean Architecture? Organising code by feature, where each request or use case owns its endpoint, validation, handler and data access, instead of by technical layer. Changes are localised and coupling between features is low, but you must watch for duplication and keep business rules in the domain. It can be combined with a Clean-style domain core. Q What is a modular monolith and why is it popular? A single deployable app split into strongly separated modules aligned to bounded contexts, each with its own data and public contracts. It offers clear boundaries and team ownership without the operational and consistency costs of microservices, and modules can be extracted into services later if needed. Q How do you enforce architectural boundaries in .NET? Project references (the compiler forbids undeclared dependencies), internal visibility by default, architecture tests (NetArchTest/ArchUnitNET) in CI, analyzers, and documented ADRs plus code review."},{"c":"app-architectures","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Common web application architectures (Microsoft eBook) Robert C. Martin, Clean Architecture . Jimmy Bogard, \"Vertical Slice Architecture\" (talk and blog). Jason Taylor's and Ardalis's (Steve Smith) Clean Architecture templates on GitHub. Milan Jovanović's blog for modular monolith examples. Martin Fowler, Patterns of Enterprise Application Architecture (Repository, Unit of Work, Service Layer)."},{"c":"app-architectures","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run (plain console app, .NET 8/9, no packages). It checks the two claims that are actually mechanical facts, not design opinions: that IUnitOfWork /repository abstractions let a handler run against a fake with zero real infrastructure (the actual point of the Dependency Rule), and that the inward-pointing Dependency Rule is literally just \"who references whom,\" checkable by reflection the same way an architecture test would. C# Run ▶ Copy // --- The Dependency Rule in miniature: Application depends on an interface Domain defines; // Infrastructure implements it. Application never references Infrastructure's concrete type. --- var fake = new FakeOrderRepositoryAndUow (); var handler = new PlaceOrderHandler (fake, fake); var rows = await handler. HandleAsync ( \"order-1\" ); if (rows != 1 || fake. All . Count != 1 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: handler should work against the fake with no real DB\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: a use-case handler ran end-to-end against a pure in-memory fake — no EF Core, no SQL Server, no network\" ); // --- Architecture-test-style check: does any type whose name contains \"Domain\" reference a type from \"Infrastructure\"? --- // (Real architecture tests use NetArchTest/ArchUnitNET and real assemblies; this reproduces the same idea // the chapter describes — reflecting over a type's members and failing if a forbidden reference is found.) bool DomainTypeReferencesInfrastructure ( Type domainType, Type infrastructureType) => domainType. GetMembers (). Any (m => (m is System . Reflection . PropertyInfo p && p. PropertyType == infrastructureType) || (m is System . Reflection . MethodInfo meth && meth. GetParameters (). Any (prm => prm. ParameterType == infrastructureType))); if ( DomainTypeReferencesInfrastructure ( typeof ( PlaceOrderHandler ), typeof ( FakeOrderRepositoryAndUow ))) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: PlaceOrderHandler (application layer) should depend only on interfaces, not the concrete fake\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: PlaceOrderHandler's members reference only IOrderRepository/IUnitOfWork, never the concrete implementation — the Dependency Rule holds, and this is exactly the kind of check an architecture test automates\" ); // Not runtime-verifiable here — see Further reading: Common web application architectures (Microsoft), Clean Architecture (Martin) // (which architecture style fits a given team/project, and whether modules are \"strongly separated,\" are judgment calls, not facts) interface IOrderRepository { void Add ( string orderId); IReadOnlyList < string > All { get ; } } interface IUnitOfWork { int PendingChanges { get ; } Task < int > SaveChangesAsync (); } // \"Application\" layer: orchestrates using only the abstractions, exactly like PlaceOrderHandler in the chapter class PlaceOrderHandler ( IOrderRepository orders, IUnitOfWork uow) { public async Task < int > HandleAsync ( string orderId) { orders. Add (orderId); return await uow. SaveChangesAsync (); } } // A fake standing in for \"Infrastructure\" (in the real chapter: EF Core / SQL Server) — no database involved at all class FakeOrderRepositoryAndUow : IOrderRepository , IUnitOfWork { private readonly List < string > _orders = []; public IReadOnlyList < string > All => _orders; public int PendingChanges { get ; private set ; } public void Add ( string orderId) { _orders. Add (orderId); PendingChanges ++; } public Task < int > SaveChangesAsync () { var n = PendingChanges ; PendingChanges = 0 ; return Task . FromResult (n); } }"},{"c":"cqrs-mediator-result","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR CQS says a method shouldn't both change state and return data. CQRS scales that same idea up to a whole architecture : separate models for writes (commands) and reads (queries). CQRS is a spectrum: most teams should stop at \"separate handlers, same database\" (immediate consistency, low complexity) — separate physical read/write stores (eventual consistency) is a big jump, only worth it at real scale. A mediator (e.g. MediatR) just forwards a command/query to its one handler, but the real value is the pipeline wrapped around every handler: logging, validation, transactions, done once instead of in every handler. The Result pattern turns \"this can fail\" into part of a method's return type, instead of an exception the caller might forget to catch — use it for expected failures (not found, declined), keep exceptions for bugs and infrastructure failures."},{"c":"cqrs-mediator-result","s":"from-a-rule-about-methods-to-a-rule-about-architecture","h":"From a rule about methods to a rule about architecture","t":"# From a rule about methods to a rule about architecture Beginner CQS (Command-Query Separation, Bertrand Meyer) is a rule for methods : a method should either be a command (changes state, returns nothing) or a query (returns data, has no side effects), never both. Asking a question shouldn't change the answer — a useful discipline on its own, mentioned briefly back in Design principles . CQRS (Command Query Responsibility Segregation, Greg Young) takes that exact same split and scales it up from a method to an entire architecture: use separate models (and code paths) for writes and reads . Diagram Copy ┌─────────── Write side ───────────┐ POST /orders ─▶ PlaceOrder command ─▶ handler ─▶ domain model (aggregate rules) ─▶ EF Core ─▶ DB └──────────────────────────────────┘ ┌─────────── Read side ────────────┐ GET /orders?… ─▶ ListOrders query ─▶ handler ─▶ projection / Dapper / read model ─▶ DTOs └──────────────────────────────────┘ Why separate reads and writes They have different needs . Writes need rich behaviour and invariants : load an aggregate, apply rules, save atomically. Reads need speed and shape : flat DTOs tailored to each screen, joins across aggregates, no tracking, often cached. Force one model to serve both and it ends up mediocre at either job: entities get polluted with UI fields, read queries get forced through aggregates, and N+1 queries creep in. Reads also usually outnumber writes 10–100×, so splitting them means each side can be scaled and optimised separately ."},{"c":"cqrs-mediator-result","s":"levels-of-cqrs-a-spectrum-not-a-binary-choice","h":"Levels of CQRS: a spectrum, not a binary choice","t":"# Levels of CQRS: a spectrum, not a binary choice Intermediate CQRS is a spectrum, and most teams should stop well before the far end: Level Write side Read side Consistency Complexity 0: none Same model for everything Same Immediate Lowest 1: separate code paths Command handlers + domain model + EF Core Query handlers + projections ( Select ) / Dapper, same database Immediate Low; a great default 2: separate read models in the same DB Same Denormalised read tables/views, updated in the same transaction or by triggers/events Immediate or near-immediate Medium 3: separate stores SQL (or an event store) Read DB (Cosmos, Elasticsearch, Redis), updated asynchronously from events Eventual High: projections, replay, lag handling + Event Sourcing Store events, not state Projections built from the event stream Eventual Highest Notice where the complexity genuinely jumps: levels 1 and 2 stay inside one database and one transaction, so consistency is immediate and the mental model barely changes from a normal CRUD app. Level 3 introduces something qualitatively different — eventual consistency — because the read model is now updated asynchronously, after the fact, by something reacting to events rather than participating in the original transaction. CQRS is not Event Sourcing They're often mentioned together, but they're independent. You can do CQRS without event sourcing (levels 1–3) and, rarely, event sourcing without separate read models. Level 3 introduces eventual consistency : after a POST, an immediate GET may not see the change yet, and the UI must handle that."},{"c":"cqrs-mediator-result","s":"commands-and-queries-in-code","h":"Commands and queries in code","t":"# Commands and queries in code Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy // ── Commands: intent to change state; imperative names; return little (an id, a Result) ── public sealed record PlaceOrderCommand ( CustomerId CustomerId , IReadOnlyList < OrderLineInput > Lines , string PaymentToken ); public sealed class PlaceOrderHandler ( IOrderRepository orders, IPricing pricing, IPaymentGateway payments, IUnitOfWork uow) { public async Task < Result < OrderId >> Handle ( PlaceOrderCommand cmd, CancellationToken ct) { var priced = await pricing. PriceAsync (cmd. Lines , ct); var order = Order . Place (cmd. CustomerId , priced); // domain rules var charge = await payments. ChargeAsync (order. Total , cmd. PaymentToken , ct); if (!charge. Succeeded ) return OrderErrors . PaymentDeclined (charge. Reason ); orders. Add (order); await uow. SaveChangesAsync (ct); return order. Id ; } } // ── Queries: return data, no side effects; shaped for the caller ── public sealed record GetOrderHistoryQuery ( CustomerId CustomerId , int Page , int PageSize ); public sealed record OrderHistoryItem ( Guid Id , DateTimeOffset PlacedAt , string Status , decimal Total , int Items ); public sealed class GetOrderHistoryHandler ( ShopDb db) { public async Task < PagedResult < OrderHistoryItem >> Handle ( GetOrderHistoryQuery q, CancellationToken ct) { var query = db. Orders . AsNoTracking (). Where (o => o. CustomerId == q. CustomerId ); var total = await query. CountAsync (ct); var items = await query. OrderByDescending (o => o. PlacedAt ) . Skip ((q. Page - 1 ) * q. PageSize ). Take (q. PageSize ) . Select (o => new OrderHistoryItem (o. Id . Value , o. PlacedAt , o. Status . ToString (), o. Total . Amount , o. Lines . Count )) . ToListAsync (ct); return new (items, q. Page , q. PageSize , total); } } Notice how different these two handlers actually are in shape, not just in name: PlaceOrderHandler loads a rich Order aggregate and calls its own methods, while GetOrderHistoryHandler never touches the domain model at all — it projects straight from DbSet to DTO, exactly the pattern EF Core querying recommended for every read endpoint. Rules of thumb that fall out of this: queries never modify state ; queries can bypass the domain model entirely; commands go through aggregates ; one handler per command or query."},{"c":"cqrs-mediator-result","s":"the-mediator-pattern-and-pipeline-behaviours","h":"The mediator pattern and pipeline behaviours","t":"# The mediator pattern and pipeline behaviours Intermediate A mediator dispatches a request object to its single handler , so the sender doesn't need a reference to the handler directly. That indirection alone is a modest convenience — its real value is the pipeline it enables: behaviours that wrap every handler, giving you one place to put cross-cutting concerns, exactly the role Middleware plays for HTTP requests, now scoped to your application layer instead. Diagram Copy Endpoint ─▶ mediator.Send(cmd) ─▶ [Logging] ─▶ [Validation] ─▶ [Authorization] ─▶ [Transaction] ─▶ Handler pipeline behaviours (a chain of responsibility around handlers) With MediatR (the best-known library): C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed record PlaceOrderCommand (…) : IRequest < Result < OrderId >>; public sealed class PlaceOrderHandler (…) : IRequestHandler < PlaceOrderCommand , Result < OrderId >> { public async Task < Result < OrderId >> Handle ( PlaceOrderCommand cmd, CancellationToken ct) { /* … */ } } public sealed class ValidationBehavior < TRequest , TResponse >( IEnumerable < IValidator < TRequest >> validators) : IPipelineBehavior < TRequest , TResponse > where TRequest : notnull where TResponse : IResult { public async Task < TResponse > Handle ( TRequest request, RequestHandlerDelegate < TResponse > next, CancellationToken ct) { var failures = ( await Task . WhenAll (validators. Select (v => v. ValidateAsync (request, ct)))) . SelectMany (r => r. Errors ). ToList (); if (failures. Count > 0 ) return ( TResponse ) ResultFactory . ValidationFailure ( typeof ( TResponse ), failures); // short-circuit return await next(); } } public sealed class LoggingBehavior < TRequest , TResponse >( ILogger < TRequest > log) : IPipelineBehavior < TRequest , TResponse > where TRequest : notnull { public async Task < TResponse > Handle ( TRequest request, RequestHandlerDelegate < TResponse > next, CancellationToken ct) { var name = typeof ( TRequest ). Name ; var sw = Stopwatch . StartNew (); log. LogInformation ( \"Handling {Request}\" , name); var response = await next(); log. LogInformation ( \"Handled {Request} in {Elapsed} ms\" , name, sw. ElapsedMilliseconds ); return response; } } builder. Services . AddMediatR (c => { c. RegisterServicesFromAssemblyContaining < PlaceOrderHandler >(); c. AddOpenBehavior ( typeof ( LoggingBehavior <,>)); c. AddOpenBehavior ( typeof ( ValidationBehavior <,>)); c. AddOpenBehavior ( typeof ( TransactionBehavior <,>)); }); app. MapPost ( \"/orders\" , async ( PlaceOrderCommand cmd, ISender sender, CancellationToken ct) => ( await sender. Send (cmd, ct)). ToHttp ()); Notice ValidationBehavior 's next() call is exactly the same shape as the request delegate chain in the middleware pipeline — it either calls onward or short-circuits, and the registration order of the three behaviours above determines the order they wrap the handler in, the same reverse-composition idea covered in full in Middleware . MediatR also has notifications ( INotification with multiple INotificationHandler s, i.e. in-process publish/subscribe), often used to dispatch domain events. # Is a mediator library necessary? Option Notes MediatR Popular and mature. Commercial licence since v13 (2025) for organisations above a revenue threshold Mediator (martinothamar) Source-generated, MIT, very fast, near-identical API Wolverine Mediator + messaging + outbox in one framework Brighter / Darker Command processor plus messaging No library Inject handlers directly ( PlaceOrderHandler ) and use decorators or endpoint filters for cross-cutting concerns. Simplest, explicit, and \"Go to definition\" works The honest tradeoff behind all of this: indirection hides call graphs, a mediator is \"service locator in disguise\" when overused, and it's genuinely unnecessary ceremony for a small app with a handful of use cases. Against that: a uniform pipeline, decoupled endpoints, and an easy, repeatable per-use-case structure that scales well as the number of use cases grows. A good intervi"},{"c":"cqrs-mediator-result","s":"the-result-pattern-making-expected-failure-part-of-the-metho","h":"The Result pattern: making expected failure part of the method's contract","t":"# The Result pattern: making expected failure part of the method's contract Intermediate Problem: many failures are expected outcomes , not exceptional situations: \"order not found\", \"insufficient stock\", \"email already registered\", \"card declined\". Using exceptions for them creates a specific, recurring set of problems: is invisible in the signature : Task<Order> PlaceAsync() doesn't tell you it can fail in five ways, is expensive (stack trace capture and unwinding) under load, turns control flow into try/catch at a distance, makes it easy to forget a case. Result pattern: return an object that is either a success with a value or a failure with an error , and make callers handle both — turning \"this can fail\" from something buried in documentation (or nowhere) into something the compiler can see. C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed record Error ( string Code , string Message , ErrorType Type = ErrorType . Failure ) { public static readonly Error None = new ( \"\" , \"\" ); } public enum ErrorType { Failure , Validation , NotFound , Conflict , Unauthorized } public class Result { protected Result ( bool ok, Error error) { IsSuccess = ok; Error = error; } public bool IsSuccess { get ; } public bool IsFailure => ! IsSuccess ; public Error Error { get ; } public static Result Success () => new ( true , Error . None ); public static Result Failure ( Error e) => new ( false , e); public static Result < T > Success < T >( T value ) => new ( value , true , Error . None ); public static Result < T > Failure < T >( Error e) => new ( default , false , e); } public sealed class Result < T > : Result { private readonly T ? _value; internal Result ( T ? value , bool ok, Error e) : base (ok, e) => _value = value ; public T Value => IsSuccess ? _value! : throw new InvalidOperationException ( \"No value for a failed result\" ); public static implicit operator Result < T >( T value ) => Success ( value ); // return order.Id; public static implicit operator Result < T >( Error error) => Failure < T >(error); // return OrderErrors.NotFound(id); } public static class OrderErrors { public static Error NotFound ( OrderId id) => new ( \"order.not_found\" , $\"Order {id} was not found\" , ErrorType . NotFound ); public static readonly Error AlreadyShipped = new ( \"order.already_shipped\" , \"Shipped orders can't be cancelled\" , ErrorType . Conflict ); public static Error PaymentDeclined ( string reason) => new ( \"order.payment_declined\" , $\"Payment declined: {reason}\" , ErrorType . Failure ); } Notice the two implicit operators on Result<T> : they're what let PlaceOrderHandler , back in the commands section, simply return order.Id; on success or return OrderErrors.PaymentDeclined(charge.Reason); on failure, with no explicit Result.Success(...) wrapping needed at either call site — the type conversion happens invisibly, which is exactly why the handler code reads so cleanly. Using it, and mapping to HTTP once at the edge: C# Run ▶ Copy // Domain/application code returns errors as values public Result Cancel ( string reason) { if ( Status == OrderStatus . Shipped ) return OrderErrors . AlreadyShipped ; Status = OrderStatus . Cancelled ; Raise ( new OrderCancelled ( Id , reason)); return Result . Success (); } // Presentation: one mapping for the whole API public static IResult ToHttp < T >( this Result < T > r) => r. IsSuccess ? Results . Ok (r. Value ) : r. Error . ToProblem (); public static IResult ToProblem ( this Error e) => Results . Problem ( title: e. Message , type: e. Code , statusCode: e. Type switch { ErrorType . Validation => 400 , ErrorType . NotFound => 404 , ErrorType . Conflict => 409 , ErrorType . Unauthorized => 403 , _ => 422 }); The payoff of ToHttp and ToProblem existing as exactly two small, shared methods: every single endpoint in the API maps a Result to an HTTP response the same way, once, rather than each handler re-deciding what status code \"not found\" should mean. Libraries: ErrorOr , FluentResults , Ardalis.Result , OneOf (discriminated-union"},{"c":"cqrs-mediator-result","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Martin Fowler, \"CQRS\" and \"CommandQuerySeparation\" CQRS pattern (Azure Architecture Center) MediatR on GitHub · Mediator (source-generated) · ErrorOr Scott Wlaschin, \"Railway Oriented Programming\" (talk/article)."},{"c":"cqrs-mediator-result","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter MediatR and the other libraries mentioned above aren't referenced here — the checks below build a minimal, dependency-free Result<T> and a minimal mediator pipeline to verify the actual mechanics this chapter describes (implicit conversions, error-to-status mapping, pipeline ordering and short-circuiting), without needing a package. Save as Program.cs in a new console project ( dotnet new console ) and run with dotnet run . C# Run ▶ Copy // Verifies claims from this chapter: Result<T>'s implicit conversions let a handler // just \"return value\" or \"return error\", mapping an error to the right HTTP-style status, and a minimal // mediator pipeline runs its behaviours in registration order and can short-circuit before the handler. using System ; using System . Collections . Generic ; using System . Linq ; int fails = 0 ; void Ok ( string s) => Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: {s}\" ); void Check ( bool cond, string s) { if (cond) Ok (s); else { Console . WriteLine ( $\"FAIL: {s}\" ); fails++; } } // --- Result<T>: implicit operators let a method \"return value\" or \"return error\" directly --- Result < Guid > Place ( bool approve) { if (!approve) return OrderErrors . PaymentDeclined ( \"insufficient_funds\" ); // implicit Error -> Result<T> return Guid . NewGuid (); // implicit T -> Result<T> } var declined = Place (approve: false ); var approved = Place (approve: true ); Check (declined. IsFailure && declined. Error . Code == \"order.payment_declined\" , \"Result<T>: an Error converts implicitly into a failed Result\" ); Check (approved. IsSuccess && approved. Value != Guid . Empty , \"Result<T>: a T converts implicitly into a successful Result\" ); Check ( Throws (() => _ = declined. Value ), \"Result<T>.Value throws on a failed result instead of returning a default value silently\" ); // --- Mapping a Result's error to an HTTP-style status happens once, not per-handler --- Check (declined. Error . Type == ErrorType . Failure && StatusFor (declined. Error ) == 422 , \"Error -> status mapping: a generic failure maps to 422\" ); Check ( StatusFor ( OrderErrors . NotFound ()) == 404 , \"Error -> status mapping: NotFound maps to 404\" ); // --- Mediator pipeline: behaviours run in registration order and can short-circuit before the handler --- var trace = new List < string >(); var pipeline = BuildPipeline (trace, next => () => { trace. Add ( \"Logging\" ); var r = next(); return r; }, next => () => { trace. Add ( \"Validation\" ); return \"short-circuited\" ; }, // fails validation, never calls next() next => () => { trace. Add ( \"Handler\" ); return next(); }); var result = pipeline(); Check (trace. SequenceEqual ( new [] { \"Logging\" , \"Validation\" }), \"Mediator pipeline: behaviours run in the order they were registered\" ); Check (result == \"short-circuited\" && !trace. Contains ( \"Handler\" ), \"Mediator pipeline: a behaviour can short-circuit and skip the handler entirely\" ); Console . WriteLine (fails == 0 ? \"\\nAll checks passed.\" : $\"\\n{fails} check(s) failed.\" ); if (fails > 0 ) Environment . Exit ( 1 ); static bool Throws ( Action a) { try { a(); return false ; } catch { return true ; } } static int StatusFor ( Error e) => e. Type switch { ErrorType . Validation => 400 , ErrorType . NotFound => 404 , ErrorType . Conflict => 409 , ErrorType . Unauthorized => 403 , _ => 422 }; static Func < string > BuildPipeline ( List < string > trace, params Func < Func < string >, Func < string >>[] behaviours) { Func < string > pipeline = () => { trace. Add ( \"(unreachable terminal)\" ); return \"unreachable\" ; }; for ( int i = behaviours. Length - 1 ; i >= 0 ; i--) pipeline = behaviours[i](pipeline); return pipeline; } sealed record Error ( string Code , string Message , ErrorType Type = ErrorType . Failure ) { public static readonly Error None = new ( \"\" , \"\" ); } enum ErrorType { Failure , Validation , NotFound , Conflict , Unauthorized } class Result { protected Result ( bool ok, Error error) { IsSuccess = ok; Error = error; } public bool IsSuccess "},{"c":"ddd","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR DDD puts a domain model — the business's own concepts and rules — at the centre of the code, built together with domain experts, in their language. Strategic DDD splits a big system into bounded contexts , each with its own consistent model and vocabulary (the same word can mean different things in different contexts on purpose). Tactical DDD gives you the building blocks inside one context: entities (identity matters), value objects (only the values matter), and aggregates (a cluster with one root that's the only door in, protecting the rules that must always be true). One transaction touches one aggregate; aggregates talk to each other later, through domain events , not by reaching into each other directly. A rich model (behaviour and data together, like order.Cancel(...) ) stops rules being bypassed; an anemic model (plain data + a separate service with the rules) doesn't. Skip the tactical patterns for simple CRUD — they're worth their cost only in a domain that's genuinely complex and core to the business."},{"c":"ddd","s":"the-problem-ddd-exists-to-solve","h":"The problem DDD exists to solve","t":"# The problem DDD exists to solve Intermediate Domain-Driven Design (Eric Evans, 2003) starts from a specific, honest observation about hard software: in genuinely complex business domains — insurance, logistics, banking, healthcare, e-commerce at scale — the hardest problem usually isn't a technical one at all. It's understanding and correctly encoding the business itself . When the rules governing that business get scattered across controllers, services and raw SQL, they inevitably get duplicated, start quietly contradicting each other, and eventually become something nobody can change with confidence anymore. DDD's answer is to put the domain model — a precise model of the business's concepts and rules — at the heart of the software, and to build that model collaboratively with domain experts , in their own language rather than a technical one. It has two distinct halves, operating at two different scales: Strategic DDD (the big picture) Tactical DDD (the building blocks) How to split a large system into parts with clear boundaries and relationships How to model inside one part: entities, value objects, aggregates, events Bounded contexts, ubiquitous language, subdomains, context maps Patterns implemented in C# Shapes teams, modules and microservice boundaries Shapes classes and persistence Why DDD exists In complex domains — insurance, logistics, banking, healthcare, e-commerce at scale — the hardest problem isn't technology. It's understanding and encoding the business correctly . When rules are scattered across controllers, services and SQL, they get duplicated, start to contradict each other, and eventually become impossible to change safely. DDD's answer is to concentrate the rules in one explicit model that mirrors how the business actually talks and thinks."},{"c":"ddd","s":"strategic-ddd-ubiquitous-language-and-bounded-contexts","h":"Strategic DDD: ubiquitous language and bounded contexts","t":"# Strategic DDD: ubiquitous language and bounded contexts Intermediate # Ubiquitous language A shared, precise vocabulary used by developers and domain experts in conversation, documentation and code . If the business says \"a policy is bound \", the code has policy.Bind() , not policy.SetStatus(3) . When the language changes, the code changes — the vocabulary isn't documentation sitting next to the code, it is the code. # Bounded contexts: the same word, deliberately meaning different things A bounded context is an explicit boundary within which a model and its language are consistent. Here's the idea that trips people up on first encounter: the same word means different things in different contexts, and that's not a bug to fix — it's the correct state of affairs : Diagram Copy \"Product\" in… Catalog context: name, description, images, categories, SEO slug Pricing context: list price, discounts, price lists, currency rules Inventory context: SKU, warehouse locations, stock on hand, reorder level Shipping context: weight, dimensions, hazardous-goods flag Ordering context: a line item snapshot: sku, name and price AT ORDER TIME Trying to build one Product class that covers all five contexts ends badly: it turns into a giant model everyone fights over, because a change Pricing needs can break something Shipping depends on — even though the two have nothing to do with each other. The fix: let each context keep its own small model, and connect them with explicit contracts (IDs, APIs, events) instead of one shared class. # Subdomains: where to actually invest Type Meaning Strategy Core What gives the business its competitive edge (pricing engine, recommendation logic) Invest most here; rich DDD model; best people Supporting Necessary and specific, but not differentiating (internal back-office workflows) Simpler models, maybe CRUD Generic Solved problems (identity, payments, email, accounting) Buy or use SaaS (Entra ID, Stripe, SendGrid) The practical upshot of this table, easy to forget under deadline pressure: the full weight of DDD's tactical patterns — covered in the rest of this chapter — belongs in the core row. Spending that same effort modelling a generic subdomain like \"send an email\" is effort the business gets zero competitive value from. # Context maps How bounded contexts relate: Shared Kernel (a small shared model), Customer/Supplier (upstream serves downstream's needs), Conformist (downstream adopts upstream's model as-is), Anti-Corruption Layer (downstream translates upstream's model into its own, protecting it from legacy or third-party models; implemented with adapters), Open Host Service / Published Language (a well-documented public API or event schema), Separate Ways . Event Storming (Alberto Brandolini) is the popular workshop technique for discovering domain events, commands, aggregates and context boundaries with sticky notes on a wall."},{"c":"ddd","s":"entities-identity-that-outlives-any-particular-state","h":"Entities: identity that outlives any particular state","t":"# Entities: identity that outlives any particular state Intermediate An entity has an identity that persists over time and through changes. Two entities are the same if their IDs are equal, even if every other field differs — this is the single defining trait that separates an entity from the value objects covered next. C# Run ▶ Copy public abstract class Entity < TId > where TId : notnull { public TId Id { get ; protected init ; } = default !; public override bool Equals ( object ? obj) => obj is Entity < TId > other && GetType () == other. GetType () && Id . Equals (other. Id ); public override int GetHashCode () => Id . GetHashCode (); } public readonly record struct CustomerId ( Guid Value ) { public static CustomerId New () => new ( Guid . CreateVersion7 ()); } // strongly typed ID"},{"c":"ddd","s":"value-objects-defined-entirely-by-what-they-contain","h":"Value objects: defined entirely by what they contain","t":"# Value objects: defined entirely by what they contain Intermediate A value object is the mirror image of an entity: it's defined entirely by its values , has no identity of its own, and is immutable . Two value objects holding the same values are completely interchangeable — there's no sense in which one is \"the same instance\" as another, because that question doesn't apply. Examples: Money , Address , EmailAddress , DateRange , Quantity , Percentage , GeoCoordinate . C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed record Money { public decimal Amount { get ; } public string Currency { get ; } public Money ( decimal amount, string currency) { if (amount < 0 ) throw new DomainException ( \"money.negative\" , \"Amount can't be negative\" ); if (currency is not { Length : 3 }) throw new DomainException ( \"money.currency\" , \"Use ISO 4217 codes\" ); Amount = decimal . Round (amount, 2 , MidpointRounding . ToEven ); Currency = currency. ToUpperInvariant (); } public static Money Zero ( string currency) => new ( 0 , currency); public Money Add ( Money other) => Currency == other. Currency ? new ( Amount + other. Amount , Currency ) : throw new DomainException ( \"money.mismatch\" , \"Currency mismatch\" ); public Money Multiply ( int qty) => new ( Amount * qty, Currency ); public override string ToString () => $\"{Amount:0.00} {Currency}\" ; } public sealed record EmailAddress { public string Value { get ; } private EmailAddress ( string v) => Value = v; public static Result < EmailAddress > Create ( string input) => MailAddress . TryCreate (input?. Trim (), out var m) ? new EmailAddress (m. Address . ToLowerInvariant ()) : Errors . InvalidEmail ; } ( Result<T> above is a small wrapper that stands for \"success with a value, or a named failure\" without throwing an exception for an expected failure like \"bad email format\" — the full pattern is in CQRS & Result .) Why bother: notice what Money 's constructor does that a plain decimal parameter never could — it fixes primitive obsession ( decimal price, string currency passed separately everywhere, with nothing stopping them being passed in the wrong order, or a string email that was never actually validated as an email). Validation happens once, at creation , so once you're holding an EmailAddress instance, it is guaranteed valid — there's no later point in the code where you need to re-check it. Behaviour lives with the data ( Money.Add enforces the currency-mismatch rule right where the data lives); method signatures become self-documenting ( Charge(Money amount) tells you far more than Charge(decimal amount, string currency) ever could). Records give value equality for free, which is exactly the comparison semantics a value object needs."},{"c":"ddd","s":"aggregates-the-most-important-tactical-pattern-and-the-harde","h":"Aggregates: the most important tactical pattern, and the hardest to get right","t":"# Aggregates: the most important tactical pattern, and the hardest to get right Advanced An aggregate is a cluster of entities and value objects treated as one unit for data changes , with one entity as the aggregate root . The aggregate is a consistency boundary : its invariants (business rules that must always be true) are enforced inside it, in a single transaction — nothing outside the aggregate is ever allowed to put it into an invalid state. Diagram Copy Order (aggregate root) rules enforced here: ├── OrderLine (entity, inside) • an order has ≥ 1 line before it's placed ├── OrderLine • quantities 1–100; max 50 lines ├── ShippingAddress (value object) • total = sum of lines; can't go negative └── Status • can't add lines after it's placed; can't cancel after shipping CustomerId, ProductId → references to OTHER aggregates by ID only The rules of aggregate design (Vaughn Vernon): Protect invariants inside the boundary. All changes go through the root : no public setters, no external code adding to Lines directly. Design small aggregates. Big aggregates mean big loads, lock contention and concurrency conflicts. Most aggregates are one root entity plus a few value objects or child entities. Reference other aggregates by identity only ( CustomerId , not a Customer navigation), which keeps boundaries and transactions small. One transaction modifies one aggregate. Changes to other aggregates happen eventually , via domain events. Load and save the whole aggregate through its repository . Rule 3 fights your relational instincts the most. In a plain EF Core model you'd naturally add a navigation property from Order straight to a full Customer entity. DDD says don't: if code can follow that navigation and change the customer while it's inside an order-handling transaction, the clean boundary rule 4 relies on is gone. Keep the reference as a plain ID instead. C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class Order : AggregateRoot < OrderId > { private readonly List < OrderLine > _lines = []; public CustomerId CustomerId { get ; private set ; } public OrderStatus Status { get ; private set ; } = OrderStatus . Draft ; public Address ? ShippingAddress { get ; private set ; } public IReadOnlyList < OrderLine > Lines => _lines. AsReadOnly (); public Money Total => _lines. Aggregate ( Money . Zero ( \"EUR\" ), (sum, l) => sum. Add (l. Subtotal )); private Order () { } // for EF Core public static Order Draft ( CustomerId customer) => new () { Id = OrderId . New (), CustomerId = customer }; public Result AddLine ( ProductId product, string name, Money unitPrice, int quantity) { if ( Status != OrderStatus . Draft ) return OrderErrors . NotEditable ; if (quantity is < 1 or > 100 ) return OrderErrors . InvalidQuantity ; if (_lines. Count >= 50 ) return OrderErrors . TooManyLines ; var existing = _lines. SingleOrDefault (l => l. ProductId == product); if (existing is not null ) existing. Increase (quantity); else _lines. Add ( new OrderLine (product, name, unitPrice, quantity)); return Result . Success (); } public Result Place ( Address shipTo, TimeProvider clock) { if ( Status != OrderStatus . Draft ) return OrderErrors . AlreadyPlaced ; if (_lines. Count == 0 ) return OrderErrors . Empty ; ShippingAddress = shipTo; Status = OrderStatus . Placed ; Raise ( new OrderPlaced ( Id , CustomerId , Total , clock. GetUtcNow ())); // domain event return Result . Success (); } public Result Cancel ( string reason) { if ( Status is OrderStatus . Shipped or OrderStatus . Delivered ) return OrderErrors . AlreadyShipped ; Status = OrderStatus . Cancelled ; Raise ( new OrderCancelled ( Id , reason)); return Result . Success (); } } Notice that AddLine , Place and Cancel are the only way to change this aggregate's state — there's no public set anywhere, and every single rule from the diagram above (max 50 lines, can't cancel after shipping) is enforced right inside the method that would otherwise violate it. This is exactly the \"rich domain model\" and Tell-Don't-Ask disciplin"},{"c":"ddd","s":"domain-events-a-record-of-something-that-already-happened","h":"Domain events: a record of something that already happened","t":"# Domain events: a record of something that already happened Advanced A domain event records something meaningful that happened in the domain , named in the past tense, in the ubiquitous language: OrderPlaced , PaymentReceived , StockDepleted — exactly the naming discipline Design patterns drew the line on, distinguishing an event (already happened, can't be rejected) from a command (a request, might be refused). C# Run ▶ Copy public interface IDomainEvent { DateTimeOffset OccurredAt { get ; } } public sealed record OrderPlaced ( OrderId OrderId , CustomerId CustomerId , Money Total , DateTimeOffset OccurredAt ) : IDomainEvent ; public abstract class AggregateRoot < TId > : Entity < TId > where TId : notnull { private readonly List < IDomainEvent > _events = []; public IReadOnlyList < IDomainEvent > DomainEvents => _events; protected void Raise ( IDomainEvent e) => _events. Add (e); public void ClearDomainEvents () => _events. Clear (); } Having raised the event, there's still the question of when it actually gets dispatched to whoever needs to react — and that choice carries real consequences: When How Trade-off Before commit , same transaction Interceptor dispatches in-process handlers before SaveChanges completes Atomic, but couples the handlers into the transaction After commit , in-process Dispatch after SaveChanges succeeds Simple; if the process crashes in between, the event is lost Outbox (recommended for cross-boundary effects) Persist events in an Outbox table in the same transaction; a worker publishes them Reliable, at-least-once; handlers must be idempotent The outbox row is the one worth defaulting to for anything that genuinely matters, and it's exactly the mechanism EF Core advanced implemented end-to-end: by writing the event into the same transaction as the aggregate's own save, there's no window where the order exists but the event describing it was lost, or vice versa. Domain events (within a bounded context, in-process) vs integration events (published to other contexts or services via a broker, with a stable public schema). Often a domain event handler translates a domain event into an integration event. See Messaging & events ."},{"c":"ddd","s":"domain-services-application-services-factories-and-repositor","h":"Domain services, application services, factories and repositories","t":"# Domain services, application services, factories and repositories Advanced Building block Responsibility Example Domain service Domain logic that doesn't naturally belong to one entity or value object; stateless; in domain terms PricingService.CalculateDiscount(order, customerTier) , TransferService.Transfer(accountA, accountB, money) Application service / use case handler Orchestrates: loads aggregates, calls domain methods, saves, publishes. No business rules PlaceOrderHandler Factory Encapsulates complex creation, ensuring a valid initial state Order.Draft(customer) , OrderFactory.FromCart(cart) Repository Collection-like access to aggregate roots only ; interface in the domain, implementation in infrastructure IOrderRepository.GetAsync(OrderId) , Add(Order) Specification A reusable business predicate new OverdueInvoiceSpec(today) The distinction worth holding onto between the first two rows: a domain service still speaks the business's language and can contain real rules ( TransferService deciding how a transfer actually works), while an application service is pure orchestration — it calls other things in the right order and contains no judgment calls of its own. PlaceOrderHandler from earlier chapters is a textbook application service precisely because every actual decision inside it (can this order be placed? is this quantity valid?) was delegated to the aggregate itself."},{"c":"ddd","s":"rich-vs-anemic-domain-models","h":"Rich vs anemic domain models","t":"# Rich vs anemic domain models Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy // ✘ Anemic: a bag of data; rules live in services and get duplicated and bypassed public class Order { public int Id { get ; set ; } public string Status { get ; set ; } = \"\" ; public List < OrderLine > Lines { get ; set ; } = []; } public class OrderService { public void Cancel ( Order o) { if (o. Status == \"Shipped\" ) throw new Exception ( \"…\" ); // and the same check in three other places… o. Status = \"Cancelled\" ; // …while anyone can set Status = \"Cancelled\" anywhere } } // ✔ Rich: behaviour and data together; invalid states unrepresentable order. Cancel ( \"customer request\" ); // the only way to cancel; rules inside Look at the anemic version again: because Status has a public setter, nothing stops other code from writing order.Status = \"Cancelled\" directly, skipping the shipped-order check entirely. The rule lives in OrderService.Cancel , but only helps if every caller remembers to call it — and in a large codebase, eventually one won't. This is why Martin Fowler called the anemic model an anti-pattern : it costs you extra types and indirection but doesn't actually deliver the one thing a domain model is for — invariants that can't be bypassed. Tell, Don't Ask ( Design principles ) is the idea behind the fix."},{"c":"ddd","s":"persisting-aggregates-with-ef-core","h":"Persisting aggregates with EF Core","t":"# Persisting aggregates with EF Core Advanced EF Core can map rich models without polluting them — none of the mapping configuration below needs to leak back into the Order class itself: C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class OrderConfiguration : IEntityTypeConfiguration < Order > { public void Configure ( EntityTypeBuilder < Order > b) { b. HasKey (o => o. Id ); b. Property (o => o. Id ). HasConversion (id => id. Value , v => new OrderId (v)); // strongly typed id b. Property (o => o. CustomerId ). HasConversion (id => id. Value , v => new CustomerId (v)); // reference by id, no navigation b. Property (o => o. Status ). HasConversion < string >(). HasMaxLength ( 20 ); b. OwnsOne (o => o. ShippingAddress ); // value object → columns b. OwnsMany (o => o. Lines , l => // child entities inside the aggregate { l. WithOwner (). HasForeignKey ( \"OrderId\" ); l. Property < int >( \"Id\" ); l. ComplexProperty (x => x. UnitPrice ); }); b. Navigation (o => o. Lines ). UsePropertyAccessMode ( PropertyAccessMode . Field ); // use the private _lines field b. Ignore (o => o. DomainEvents ); b. Ignore (o => o. Total ); // computed b. Property < byte []>( \"RowVersion\" ). IsRowVersion (); // concurrency for the whole aggregate } } Notice that CustomerId is mapped as a plain property with a value converter, not a navigation — the Fluent API configuration directly enforces rule 3 from the aggregate design section above, at the persistence layer rather than just by convention. Private setters, private parameterless constructors, backing fields, owned and complex types, and value converters together let the domain stay genuinely clean. Some teams instead keep separate persistence models and map between them: the purest approach, with more code to maintain in exchange for zero coupling at all between the domain and EF Core's own requirements."},{"c":"ddd","s":"when-ddd-is-overkill","h":"When DDD is overkill","t":"# When DDD is overkill Intermediate Tactical DDD has real costs: more types, more mapping, a learning curve, and eventual consistency between aggregates. Use it for core, complex domains with rich rules that change often — exactly the \"Core\" row from the subdomain table earlier in this chapter. For CRUD-style supporting subdomains (reference data admin, simple forms), a straightforward model with EF Core and validation is better, and reaching for the full tactical toolkit there would be ceremony without payoff. Strategic DDD (bounded contexts, ubiquitous language) is valuable almost everywhere, even when you skip the tactical patterns entirely — the value of a shared, precise vocabulary doesn't depend on how rich the underlying model needs to be. Q What is an aggregate and what rules govern it? A cluster of domain objects treated as one unit for changes, with a single aggregate root through which all modifications pass, so invariants are enforced within the boundary. Rules: keep aggregates small, reference other aggregates by ID only, modify one aggregate per transaction, use domain events and eventual consistency for cross-aggregate effects, and load and save whole aggregates via repositories. Q Entity vs value object? An entity has a persistent identity: two orders with identical data are still different orders, and its attributes can change. A value object has no identity, is defined by its values, is immutable and is compared by value (Money, Address, EmailAddress). Records are a natural fit for value objects. Q What is a bounded context? An explicit boundary within which a particular domain model and its ubiquitous language are consistent. The same term (Product, Customer) can have different models in different contexts, which integrate through defined contracts or translations (anti-corruption layers). Bounded contexts are natural module or microservice boundaries. Q What is the ubiquitous language? A shared, rigorous vocabulary developed with domain experts and used consistently in conversations, documentation and code (class and method names), scoped to a bounded context. It reduces translation errors between business and code. Q What is an anemic domain model and why is it a problem? Entities that only hold data (public getters and setters) while all business logic lives in service classes. Rules get duplicated and bypassed, invariants aren't protected, and the model doesn't express the domain. Rich models put behaviour and data together and make invalid states impossible. Q Domain events vs integration events? Domain events are raised by aggregates within a bounded context and handled in-process, often as part of or right after the transaction. Integration events cross service or context boundaries via a message broker, have stable versioned schemas, and are published reliably (outbox), typically translated from domain events. Q Domain service vs application service? A domain service contains domain logic that doesn't fit a single entity or value object and is expressed in domain terms (pricing, transfers). An application service or use-case handler orchestrates: it loads aggregates via repositories, invokes domain behaviour, persists, and publishes events, but contains no business rules itself. Q What is an anti-corruption layer? A translation layer (adapters and mappers) between your bounded context and an external or legacy system's model, so foreign concepts and quirks don't leak into your domain model. Q When would you not use DDD? For simple CRUD or supporting subdomains with little business logic, small short-lived apps, or when domain experts aren't available. Strategic ideas (bounded contexts, shared language) are still useful, but tactical patterns would add ceremony without benefit."},{"c":"ddd","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Eric Evans, Domain-Driven Design: Tackling Complexity in the Heart of Software (the \"blue book\"). Vaughn Vernon, Implementing Domain-Driven Design (the \"red book\") and Domain-Driven Design Distilled (short intro). Vlad Khononov, Learning Domain-Driven Design (modern, very approachable). Design a DDD-oriented microservice (Microsoft eShop guide) Vaughn Vernon, \"Effective Aggregate Design\" (free three-part essay)."},{"c":"ddd","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run (plain console app, .NET 8/9, top-level statements, BCL only). The real chapter's types are trimmed down here to just what each check needs. C# Run ▶ Copy // Claim 1: an entity's identity, not its other fields, decides equality. var a = new Customer ( CustomerId . New (), \"Alice\" ); var b = new Customer (a. Id , \"Alice (renamed)\" ); // same id, different name var c = new Customer ( CustomerId . New (), \"Alice\" ); // different id, same name if (!a. Equals (b)) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: entities with the same id should be equal even if other fields differ\" ); if (a. Equals (c)) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: entities with different ids should not be equal, even with identical other fields\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: entity equality is based on identity (Id), not on the rest of the fields\" ); // Claim 2: a value object (here, a record) is equal purely by its values, with no identity. var m1 = new Money ( 10.00m , \"EUR\" ); var m2 = new Money ( 10.00m , \"EUR\" ); var m3 = new Money ( 10.01m , \"EUR\" ); if (!m1. Equals (m2)) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: two value objects with the same values should be equal\" ); if (m1. Equals (m3)) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: value objects with different values should not be equal\" ); if ( ReferenceEquals (m1, m2)) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: this check should hold for distinct instances, not the same object\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: value objects compare by value, not by reference\" ); // Claim 3: Money's constructor protects its own invariant (no negative amounts, 3-letter currency). try { _ = new Money (- 1 , \"EUR\" ); throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: negative amount should have been rejected\" ); } catch ( ArgumentException ) { Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: a value object validates itself at construction (negative Money amount rejected)\" ); } // Claim 4: an aggregate only changes through its root's methods, which enforce its invariants. var order = Order . Draft ( CustomerId . New ()); var addResult = order. AddLine ( ProductId . New (), \"Widget\" , new Money ( 5 , \"EUR\" ), quantity: 0 ); if (addResult) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: quantity 0 should be rejected by AddLine's own invariant\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: AddLine enforces its own quantity invariant (can't add a line with quantity 0)\" ); if (!order. AddLine ( ProductId . New (), \"Widget\" , new Money ( 5 , \"EUR\" ), quantity: 2 )) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: a valid line should be accepted\" ); if (!order. Place ()) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: an order with at least one line should be placeable\" ); if (order. Place ()) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: placing an already-placed order should be rejected — Status is no longer Draft\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: the aggregate root's own rules — not outside code — decide what's allowed (can't re-place an order already placed)\" ); // Claim 5: placing an order raises a domain event recording that it happened. if (order. DomainEvents . Count != 1 || order. DomainEvents [ 0 ] is not OrderPlaced ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: Place() should have raised exactly one OrderPlaced domain event\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: a state-changing aggregate method raises a domain event recording what happened\" ); // --- Minimal stand-ins for the chapter's types, trimmed to what's being checked above --- readonly record struct CustomerId ( Guid Value ) { public static CustomerId New () => new ( Guid . NewGuid ()); } readonly record struct ProductId ( Guid Value ) { public static ProductId New () => new ( Guid . NewGuid ()); } abstract class Entity < TId > where TId : notnull { public TId Id { get ; protected init ; } = default !; public override bool Equals ( object ? obj) => obj is Entity < TId > other && GetType () == other. GetType () && Id . Equals (other. Id ); public override int GetHashCode () => Id . GetHashCode (); } sealed class Customer : Entity < CustomerId > { public string Name { get ; } public Customer ( CustomerId id, string name) { Id = id; Name = name; "},{"c":"microservices","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR A monolith, a modular monolith, and microservices are points on one spectrum of coupling vs operational cost — not \"the bad old way\" vs \"the modern way.\" Microservices buy independent deployment, scaling and team autonomy, but every one of the \"fallacies of distributed computing\" (the network fails, calls are slow, there's no shared transaction) becomes something your code must handle. Good service boundaries follow bounded contexts/business capabilities, not technical layers or single entities — and each service owns its own data. Querying across services means API composition, local replicas fed by events, or a CQRS-style read model — never a cross-service join. Prefer asynchronous events between services; a chain of synchronous calls multiplies latency and failure probability — four 99.9%-available services chained together are only ~99.6% available as a whole. The most common real failure mode is the distributed monolith : all the costs of microservices, none of the independent-deployment benefit that was supposed to justify them."},{"c":"microservices","s":"three-ways-to-structure-a-system-and-the-real-spectrum-betwe","h":"Three ways to structure a system, and the real spectrum between them","t":"# Three ways to structure a system, and the real spectrum between them Beginner Monolith Modular monolith Microservices Deployable units One One Many (one per service) Internal boundaries Often weak Strong modules (per bounded context) Enforced by the network Data One database One database, schema/ownership per module Database per service Communication In-process calls In-process calls through module contracts, in-process events Network: HTTP/gRPC, messages Transactions ACID across everything ACID within a module; events across modules Local only ; sagas and eventual consistency across services Scaling Whole app Whole app Per service Team autonomy Low Medium High: independent release cycles Operational complexity Low Low High The modular monolith column, revisited from App architectures , is really the midpoint of this table — most of the boundary discipline of microservices, none of the network. A monolith is not bad . A big ball of mud is bad. Many successful products (Shopify, Stack Overflow, Basecamp) run well-structured monoliths at large scale."},{"c":"microservices","s":"why-microservices-and-what-they-genuinely-cost","h":"Why microservices, and what they genuinely cost","t":"# Why microservices, and what they genuinely cost Intermediate Benefits (when they're real): Independent deployment : teams ship without coordinating a release train. Independent scaling : scale the search service to 50 instances and the admin service to 2. Fault isolation : a crash in recommendations doesn't take down checkout (if designed for it). Technology freedom per service (use carefully). Team ownership aligned with business capabilities (Conway's Law, Team Topologies). Costs , sometimes called the fallacies of distributed computing (\"the network is reliable, latency is zero, bandwidth is infinite…\") — each one a comfortable assumption a monolith's in-process calls let you make for free, and a microservices architecture forces you to confront directly: Cost What it means in practice Network failures and latency Every call can fail or be slow, so you need timeouts, retries, circuit breakers ( Resilience ) No distributed transactions Eventual consistency, sagas, outbox, idempotency ( Messaging ) Data duplication and joins across services Data is replicated via events; queries spanning services become API composition or read models Observability You need distributed tracing, centralised logs and correlation IDs ( Observability ) Testing Contract tests, environment management, harder end-to-end tests Versioning APIs and message schemas must evolve compatibly Infrastructure & DevOps Containers, orchestration, CI/CD per service, service discovery, secrets, gateways Cognitive load Many repos and pipelines, on-call across services (Two terms from the table above, covered in full in Messaging & events : a saga coordinates a multi-step process across services as a chain of local transactions, each with a compensating \"undo\" step if a later one fails; the outbox is the write-the-event-and-the-data-atomically trick briefly introduced in Background work .) \"Should we use microservices?\" A strong answer: \"It depends on the problem, not the trend. Microservices solve organisational scaling (many teams needing independent deployments) and uneven scaling needs, at the cost of distributed-systems complexity. With one or two teams and an evolving domain, I'd start with a modular monolith with strict bounded-context modules, and extract a service only when a module has a clear reason: different scaling, a different release cadence, a different team, or isolation needs.\""},{"c":"microservices","s":"finding-service-boundaries","h":"Finding service boundaries","t":"# Finding service boundaries Intermediate Bad boundaries are the most expensive microservices mistake there is: services that must change together, call each other constantly, or share data have paid the full cost of distribution while getting none of its benefits. Good boundaries: Align with bounded contexts / business capabilities (Ordering, Catalog, Payments, Shipping) — exactly the strategic DDD boundary from DDD — not technical layers (\"the database service\", \"the validation service\") and not entities (\"the Customer service\" that everyone calls for everything). High cohesion inside, low coupling outside : most changes stay within one service. Owns its data and the rules over it. Can do its main job when other services are down , often by keeping a local copy of the data it needs, fed by events. Fits a team (two-pizza team, stream-aligned team). Granularity: start coarse. Splitting later is easier than merging chatty nano-services — a mistake in the \"too big\" direction is a refactor; a mistake in the \"too small\" direction is a rewrite of how dozens of services talk to each other."},{"c":"microservices","s":"data-ownership-database-per-service","h":"Data ownership: database per service","t":"# Data ownership: database per service Intermediate Each service owns its data exclusively . Others access it only through the service's API or events , never by querying its tables directly. Diagram Copy ✘ Shared database ✔ Database per service Ordering ─┐ Ordering ── Orders DB Billing ─┼─▶ one DB, shared tables Billing ── Billing DB ◀── OrderPlaced event ── Ordering Shipping ─┘ (schema change = 3 teams) Shipping ── Shipping DB (keeps its own copy of the address) Why: a shared schema couples services at the deepest level there is. Any schema change can silently break a service that was never told about it, and nobody can safely refactor a table they don't fully own. That kills the one benefit — independent deployment — that was the whole reason to go distributed. How to answer queries across services, now that a join across two services' tables is no longer an option: Technique How API composition A gateway or BFF calls several services and combines the results (fine for small fan-out) Data replication via events Service keeps a local, read-only copy of the data it needs (Shipping stores customer addresses from CustomerAddressChanged ) CQRS read model A dedicated query service builds a denormalised view from many services' events Notice the second row is really the CQRS \"separate stores\" level from CQRS & Result , applied across a service boundary rather than within one service — the same eventual-consistency tradeoff, just at a larger scale."},{"c":"microservices","s":"communication-synchronous-vs-asynchronous","h":"Communication: synchronous vs asynchronous","t":"# Communication: synchronous vs asynchronous Intermediate Synchronous (HTTP/REST, gRPC) Asynchronous (messages/events via broker) Coupling in time Both must be up now Producer and consumer are decoupled in time Latency Caller waits Fire and continue Failure behaviour Failures cascade unless guarded Broker buffers; consumers catch up Consistency Immediate answer Eventual Use for Queries needing an immediate answer, user-facing reads, commands needing a sync response State-change notifications, workflows, integration, fan-out, load levelling Prefer asynchronous events for integration between services. Here's the math behind why: a chain of synchronous calls (A → B → C → D) is only as available as all four links being up at once , and those probabilities multiply. Four services each healthy 99.9% of the time don't stay at 99.9% as a chain — they combine to 0.999⁴ ≈ 99.6%. That looks like a small drop, but it's about 4x the expected downtime of any single service's own number — and it gets worse the longer the chain gets."},{"c":"microservices","s":"api-gateway-bff-and-service-mesh","h":"API gateway, BFF and service mesh","t":"# API gateway, BFF and service mesh Advanced Diagram Copy Mobile app ─┐ ┌──▶ Ordering service Web SPA ────┼─▶ API Gateway ───┼──▶ Catalog service Partners ───┘ (or a BFF per └──▶ Payments service client type) Component Responsibilities .NET / Azure options API gateway Single entry point: routing, TLS, authentication offload, rate limiting, caching, request aggregation, versioning YARP (ASP.NET Core reverse proxy library), Azure API Management , Azure Front Door / Application Gateway, Ocelot (older) BFF (Backend for Frontend) A gateway tailored to one client type (mobile vs web), aggregating calls and shaping responses; for SPAs, also holds tokens (cookie-based BFF) ASP.NET Core + YARP, Duende BFF Service mesh Infrastructure layer (sidecar proxies) for service-to-service mTLS (mutual TLS: each side proves its identity with a certificate, not just the server), retries, traffic splitting, telemetry Istio, Linkerd, Dapr (sidecar runtime with building blocks), Container Apps' built-in features The BFF row connects directly back to a decision from Auth : it's the same cookie-holding server-side component recommended there for SPAs, now framed as a specific flavour of gateway rather than purely an auth concern. C# Run ▶ Copy // YARP gateway in a few lines builder. Services . AddReverseProxy (). LoadFromConfig (builder. Configuration . GetSection ( \"ReverseProxy\" )); app. MapReverseProxy (); // appsettings: Routes { \"orders\": { ClusterId: \"ordering\", Match: { Path: \"/api/orders/{**rest}\" } } } // Clusters { \"ordering\": { Destinations: { \"d1\": { Address: \"http://ordering\" } } } }"},{"c":"microservices","s":"service-discovery-and-configuration","h":"Service discovery and configuration","t":"# Service discovery and configuration Advanced Services shouldn't hard-code each other's addresses, for exactly the stale-DNS reason Calling other services raised for outbound HTTP clients, now at the scale of an entire topology that reshapes itself as instances come and go: Platform DNS : Kubernetes Services ( http://ordering.shop.svc.cluster.local ), Container Apps internal ingress names. .NET service discovery ( Microsoft.Extensions.ServiceDiscovery , used by Aspire): https+http://ordering is resolved from configuration or DNS. Central configuration : Azure App Configuration, Key Vault, Kubernetes ConfigMaps/Secrets."},{"c":"microservices","s":"migrating-a-monolith-the-strangler-fig","h":"Migrating a monolith: the strangler fig","t":"# Migrating a monolith: the strangler fig Advanced Rewriting everything at once (\"big bang\") is notoriously risky — all the business value sits behind one giant, unshippable cutover for months, with no way to validate pieces of it along the way. The strangler fig pattern (Martin Fowler) incrementally replaces pieces instead, so each step ships and proves itself independently: Diagram Copy 1. Put a proxy/gateway (YARP) in front of the monolith; all traffic flows through it. 2. Pick one capability with clear boundaries (e.g. product search). 3. Build it as a new service; route /search/* to it via the proxy. 4. Sync data (events/CDC) while both exist; then remove the code from the monolith. 5. Repeat. The monolith shrinks until it can be retired, or stays as a well-defined core. Supporting patterns: anti-corruption layer (translate between the old and new models, the same DDD pattern from DDD ), change data capture (stream database changes, e.g. Debezium or SQL Server CDC), branch by abstraction , and feature flags for switching traffic."},{"c":"microservices","s":"anti-patterns","h":"Anti-patterns","t":"# Anti-patterns Intermediate Anti-pattern Symptoms Distributed monolith Services must be deployed together; shared database; synchronous call chains; a change requires touching 5 services Nano-services A service per entity or function; every request fans out to 10 services Shared domain library A \"Common.Domain\" NuGet used by all services, coupling their models and release cycles (sharing contracts and infrastructure helpers is fine) Chatty synchronous chains Latency and availability collapse under load No observability Debugging takes hours of grepping logs across services Microservices first A new product, one team, unknown domain, and already 15 services The first row — the distributed monolith — is the most common real-world failure here. It means a team paid every cost from the table earlier (network failures, eventual consistency, DevOps overhead) and never actually got independent deployment, the one benefit that was supposed to justify paying them."},{"c":"microservices","s":"readiness-checklist","h":"Readiness checklist","t":"# Readiness checklist Advanced Before going distributed, you should have (or plan): Automated CI/CD per service, plus infrastructure as code ( CI/CD & IaC ) Containerisation and an orchestrator or PaaS ( Docker , Azure compute ) Centralised logging, metrics, distributed tracing with correlation ( Observability ) Resilience defaults on every outbound call ( Resilience ) A message broker plus outbox/idempotency practices ( Messaging ) API versioning and contract tests ( API design , Testing ) Health checks, autoscaling, graceful shutdown Identity for service-to-service calls (managed identity, client credentials, mTLS) Clear ownership: who's on call for which service Q What are the trade-offs between a monolith and microservices? A monolith is simpler to develop, test, deploy and debug, with ACID transactions and fast in-process calls, but it scales and deploys as one unit and can become tightly coupled. Microservices allow independent deployment, scaling, fault isolation and team autonomy, but add network failure modes, eventual consistency, data duplication, observability and DevOps overhead, and versioning challenges. Q What is a modular monolith? A single deployable application divided into strongly encapsulated modules aligned with bounded contexts, each owning its data and exposing explicit contracts. It provides most of the structural benefits of microservices without distributed-systems costs, and can be split later. Q How do you decide service boundaries? Align them with bounded contexts and business capabilities (not technical layers or single entities), aiming for high cohesion and low coupling so most changes stay within one service. Each service should own its data and be able to function when others are down, and services should match team ownership. Start coarse-grained. Q Why should each microservice own its database? A shared database couples services through the schema: changes break others, teams can't deploy or evolve independently, and boundaries erode. With database per service, data is accessed only via APIs or events, preserving autonomy and encapsulation. Q How do you query data that spans multiple services? API composition (a gateway or BFF aggregates calls), local replicas of needed data maintained through events, or a dedicated CQRS read model built from multiple services' events, accepting eventual consistency. Q Sync vs async communication between microservices? Synchronous HTTP/gRPC is for requests needing an immediate response, but it couples services in time and failures cascade. Asynchronous messaging decouples them in time, buffers load and isolates failures, at the cost of eventual consistency. Prefer async events for integration and minimise synchronous call chains. Q What is an API gateway and what is a BFF? An API gateway is a single entry point that routes requests to services and handles cross-cutting concerns (TLS, auth, rate limiting, caching, aggregation), e.g. Azure API Management or YARP. A Backend-for-Frontend is a gateway specialised per client type (web, mobile) that tailors and aggregates APIs for it and, for SPAs, can hold tokens server-side. Q What is the strangler fig pattern? Incrementally migrating a legacy system by routing traffic through a facade or proxy and replacing capabilities one at a time with new implementations, until the old system can be retired. It avoids a risky big-bang rewrite. Q What is a distributed monolith? A system split into services that are still tightly coupled: a shared database, lockstep deployments, synchronous call chains, shared domain libraries. It has the costs of distribution and the rigidity of a monolith."},{"c":"microservices","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Sam Newman, Building Microservices (2nd ed.) and Monolith to Microservices . .NET Microservices: Architecture for Containerized .NET Applications (free eBook) Azure Architecture Center: microservices Chris Richardson, microservices.io pattern language. YARP documentation"},{"c":"microservices","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run (plain console app, .NET 8/9, top-level statements, BCL only). C# Run ▶ Copy // Claim: availability compounds as a product across a synchronous call chain. // Four services each 99.9% available combine to roughly 99.9%^4 ≈ 99.6%, about 4x the downtime // a single service's own number would suggest. double perService = 0.999 ; double chain = Math . Pow (perService, 4 ); double singleDowntime = 1 - perService; double chainDowntime = 1 - chain; if ( Math . Abs (chain - 0.996 ) > 0.0005 ) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected chain availability ~0.996, got {chain:F6}\" ); double downtimeRatio = chainDowntime / singleDowntime; if (downtimeRatio < 3.9 || downtimeRatio > 4.1 ) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected ~4x the downtime for a 4-link chain, got {downtimeRatio:F2}x\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: a 4-service synchronous chain at 99.9% each is {chain:P2} available ({downtimeRatio:F1}x the single service's downtime)\" ); // Claim: database-per-service means other services can only see what was explicitly published, // never another service's internal storage. var ordering = new OrderingService (); var shipping = new ShippingService (); ordering. OrderPlaced += shipping. OnOrderPlaced ; // integration happens via an event, not a shared table ordering. PlaceOrder (orderId: 1 , shipToAddress: \"221B Baker St\" ); if (!shipping. HasLocalCopyOf ( 1 )) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: shipping should have received its own copy of the data it needs\" ); if (shipping. HasLocalCopyOf ( 999 )) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: shipping should not know about an order it was never told about\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: services exchange data only through published events, each keeping its own local copy\" ); // Claim: a strangler fig proxy routes a chosen capability to the new service and leaves everything else on the monolith. string RouteFor ( string path) => path. StartsWith ( \"/search/\" ) ? \"new-search-service\" : \"legacy-monolith\" ; if ( RouteFor ( \"/search/products\" ) != \"new-search-service\" ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: /search/* should route to the new service\" ); if ( RouteFor ( \"/orders/42\" ) != \"legacy-monolith\" ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: everything else should still route to the monolith\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: a strangler fig proxy can migrate one capability (/search/*) while the rest keeps flowing to the monolith\" ); // --- supporting types for the data-ownership check above --- sealed class OrderingService { public event Action < int , string >? OrderPlaced ; public void PlaceOrder ( int orderId, string shipToAddress) => OrderPlaced ?. Invoke (orderId, shipToAddress); } sealed class ShippingService { private readonly Dictionary < int , string > _localAddressCopies = []; // Shipping's own DB, not Ordering's public void OnOrderPlaced ( int orderId, string shipToAddress) => _localAddressCopies[orderId] = shipToAddress; public bool HasLocalCopyOf ( int orderId) => _localAddressCopies. ContainsKey (orderId); }"},{"c":"unit-testing","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR A unit test checks one behaviour , fast and deterministically. \"One unit\" is debated: classical style treats a unit as a behaviour (several real classes allowed); mockist style treats it as one class (everything else mocked). Classical is the current mainstream advice. Good tests are FIRST : Fast, Isolated, Repeatable, Self-validating, Timely. A test double is a precise word for \"fake dependency\": dummy (unused filler), stub (feeds input), fake (working lightweight version), spy (records calls), mock (asserts calls happened). Over-mocking makes tests break on every refactor even when behaviour didn't change. Inject sources of non-determinism ( TimeProvider , not DateTime.Now ) so tests are repeatable. Coverage percentage is a diagnostic, not a goal — a test can run every line and still assert nothing meaningful."},{"c":"unit-testing","s":"what-a-unit-test-is-and-the-question-that-divides-testers","h":"What a unit test is, and the question that divides testers","t":"# What a unit test is, and the question that divides testers Beginner A unit test verifies one unit of behaviour in isolation, quickly and deterministically. That sounds simple until you ask the obvious follow-up: what, exactly, is \"one unit\"? There are two genuinely different schools of thought here, and they lead to noticeably different-looking tests: Classical (Detroit/Chicago) Mockist (London) A unit is… A behaviour , possibly spanning several collaborating classes One class Collaborators Use real ones; replace only shared or out-of-process dependencies (DB, network, clock) Mock every collaborator Strength Tests survive refactoring Precise failure location; drives interface design Risk Larger tests Brittle tests coupled to implementation Modern guidance (e.g. Vladimir Khorikov's Unit Testing Principles, Practices, and Patterns ) leans classical : test observable behaviour, and mock only at the boundaries of your system. The reasoning becomes clear once you picture refactoring a class's internals without changing what it does — a classical test, written against behaviour, survives unchanged; a mockist test, written against every internal collaborator, breaks even though nothing externally visible changed. Good unit tests are FIRST : F ast (milliseconds), I solated (no order dependence, no shared state), R epeatable (same result every run: no real time, randomness or network), S elf-validating (pass/fail, no manual inspection), T imely (written with the code)."},{"c":"unit-testing","s":"xunit-essentials","h":"xUnit essentials","t":"# xUnit essentials Beginner xUnit is the most common test framework in modern .NET (the ASP.NET Core and .NET runtime teams use it). xUnit v3 is the current major version. Shell Copy dotnet new xunit -n Shop.Domain.Tests dotnet add Shop.Domain.Tests reference src/Shop.Domain dotnet test # discovers and runs all tests dotnet test --filter \"FullyQualifiedName~Orders&Category=Unit\" C# Run ▶ Copy public class MoneyTests { [Fact] public void Add_SameCurrency_SumsAmounts () { // Arrange var a = new Money ( 10.50m , \"EUR\" ); var b = new Money ( 4.50m , \"EUR\" ); // Act var sum = a. Add (b); // Assert Assert . Equal ( new Money ( 15m , \"EUR\" ), sum); } [Fact] public void Add_DifferentCurrency_Throws () { var eur = new Money ( 1 , \"EUR\" ); var usd = new Money ( 1 , \"USD\" ); var ex = Assert . Throws < DomainException >(() => eur. Add (usd)); Assert . Equal ( \"money.mismatch\" , ex. Code ); } [Theory] [InlineData(-1)] [InlineData(-0.01)] public void Ctor_NegativeAmount_Throws ( decimal amount) => Assert . Throws < DomainException >(() => new Money (amount, \"EUR\" )); [Theory] [MemberData(nameof(RoundingCases))] public void Ctor_RoundsToTwoDecimals_BankersRounding ( decimal input, decimal expected) => Assert . Equal (expected, new Money (input, \"EUR\" ). Amount ); public static TheoryData < decimal , decimal > RoundingCases => new () { { 1.005m , 1.00m }, { 1.015m , 1.02m }, { 2.345m , 2.34m } }; } Feature How A test [Fact] Parameterised test [Theory] + [InlineData] / [MemberData] / [ClassData] / TheoryData<…> Setup / teardown Constructor / IDisposable.Dispose (or IAsyncLifetime for async) A new class instance per test Yes: xUnit creates a fresh instance for every test, so there's no shared state by accident Share expensive setup across a class IClassFixture<TFixture> Share across classes [Collection(\"db\")] + ICollectionFixture<TFixture> Skip [Fact(Skip = \"reason\")] Categorise [Trait(\"Category\", \"Unit\")] Output Inject ITestOutputHelper Parallelism Test collections (by default, each class) run in parallel; tests within a class run sequentially The \"new instance per test\" row deserves a second look, because it's a deliberate design choice, not an incidental detail: it means a bug where one test's leftover state silently affects the next test cannot happen by construction in xUnit, since there is no \"next test\" sharing the same instance to leave state in. # xUnit vs NUnit vs MSTest xUnit NUnit MSTest (v3) Test attribute [Fact] , [Theory] [Test] , [TestCase] [TestMethod] , [DataRow] Setup Constructor / IAsyncLifetime [SetUp] , [OneTimeSetUp] [TestInitialize] , [ClassInitialize] Instance per test Always One per fixture by default One per test Notes Minimal and opinionated Rich assertions and attributes Microsoft's; modernised with MSTest.Sdk and Microsoft.Testing.Platform All three are fine; consistency within a codebase matters most."},{"c":"unit-testing","s":"naming-and-structure","h":"Naming and structure","t":"# Naming and structure Beginner A test name should say what behaviour is expected under which condition , readable as a specification rather than as an implementation detail: Diagram Copy MethodUnderTest_Scenario_ExpectedResult Cancel_WhenOrderShipped_ReturnsAlreadyShippedError Should_ExpectedBehaviour_When_Condition Should_ApplyVipDiscount_When_CustomerIsVip Plain English (Khorikov) Shipped_order_cannot_be_cancelled Rules: one behaviour per test (multiple asserts are fine if they verify one outcome), no logic in tests (no if , no loops computing expected values: the test should be obviously correct by inspection, never itself a thing that could have a bug), and Arrange-Act-Assert with a single Act."},{"c":"unit-testing","s":"assertions","h":"Assertions","t":"# Assertions Beginner C# Run ▶ Copy // Built-in xUnit Assert . Equal (expected, actual); Assert . True (order. IsPaid ); Assert . Contains (order. Lines , l => l. Sku == \"KB-01\" ); Assert . Single (events); Assert . IsType < OrderPlaced >(events[ 0 ]); await Assert . ThrowsAsync < NotFoundException >(() => sut. GetAsync ( 999 , ct)); Assert . Equivalent ( new { Status = \"Paid\" , Total = 89.70m }, dto); // structural comparison // Shouldly (fluent, readable failures) order. Status . ShouldBe ( OrderStatus . Placed ); order. Lines . ShouldHaveSingleItem (). Quantity . ShouldBe ( 2 ); Should . Throw < DomainException >(() => order. Cancel ( \"x\" )). Code . ShouldBe ( \"order.already_shipped\" ); Popular libraries: Shouldly (MIT), FluentAssertions (commercial licence since v8 in 2025; v7 remains Apache-licensed), AwesomeAssertions (an MIT community fork of FluentAssertions). For snapshot testing (approve the whole output once, then detect changes), use Verify ."},{"c":"unit-testing","s":"test-doubles-a-precise-vocabulary-for-fake-dependency","h":"Test doubles: a precise vocabulary for \"fake dependency\"","t":"# Test doubles: a precise vocabulary for \"fake dependency\" Intermediate A test double stands in for a real dependency, and Gerard Meszaros gave the vocabulary that lets testers talk precisely about which kind of double a given test needs, rather than lumping everything under \"mock\": Double Purpose Example Dummy Fills a parameter; never used NullLogger<T>.Instance Stub Returns canned answers to provide inputs A repository stub returning a fixed order Fake A working, simplified implementation In-memory repository, FakeTimeProvider , fake email sender collecting messages Spy Records how it was called, asserted later A fake notifier with a SentMessages list Mock Pre-programmed with expectations about calls, verified \" ChargeAsync must be called once with 89.70 EUR\" The key distinction cutting across this whole table: stubs support state verification (assert on the result or the system's state after the fact), while mocks support behaviour verification (assert that a specific interaction happened, regardless of the resulting state). Verify interactions only for outgoing side effects at the system boundary (an email was sent, a message was published, a payment was charged), never for internal calls or queries — the next gotcha explains exactly why that restraint matters. # NSubstitute and Moq C# Run ▶ Copy // NSubstitute: concise, popular var payments = Substitute . For < IPaymentGateway >(); payments. ChargeAsync ( Arg . Any < Money >(), Arg . Any < string >(), Arg . Any < CancellationToken >()) . Returns ( PaymentResult . Declined ( \"insufficient_funds\" )); var orders = Substitute . For < IOrderRepository >(); var uow = Substitute . For < IUnitOfWork >(); var sut = new PlaceOrderHandler (orders, new FixedPricing (), payments, uow); var result = await sut. Handle ( SampleCommand (), CancellationToken . None ); result. IsFailure . ShouldBeTrue (); result. Error . Code . ShouldBe ( \"order.payment_declined\" ); orders. DidNotReceive (). Add ( Arg . Any < Order >()); // outgoing side effect NOT performed await uow. DidNotReceive (). SaveChangesAsync ( Arg . Any < CancellationToken >()); // Moq: the long-standing alternative var mock = new Mock < IPaymentGateway >(); mock. Setup (p => p. ChargeAsync ( It . IsAny < Money >(), It . IsAny < string >(), It . IsAny < CancellationToken >())) . ReturnsAsync ( PaymentResult . Success ( \"ch_1\" )); mock. Verify (p => p. ChargeAsync ( It . Is < Money >(m => m. Amount == 89.70m ), \"tok\" , It . IsAny < CancellationToken >()), Times . Once ); Notice the NSubstitute test's two DidNotReceive() assertions are verifying exactly the outcome the \"outgoing side effect\" rule allows: when payment is declined, the order should never actually be saved. That's a genuine behavioural guarantee worth locking in with a mock — quite different from mocking, say, a method that only reads data. Other options: FakeItEasy , or hand-written fakes , which are often clearer for repositories and clocks. Over-mocking makes tests brittle If every collaborator is mocked and every call verified, any refactoring breaks tests even when behaviour is unchanged, and the tests mostly restate the implementation. Signs: tests that mock 6 interfaces, Verify calls for queries, tests that fail when you rename a private method's collaborator. Prefer real in-process objects, fakes for I/O, and asserting outcomes. What not to mock Don't mock types you don't own with complex behaviour ( HttpClient , DbContext , IQueryable ): the mock won't behave like the real thing. Wrap them behind your own interface and test the wrapper with integration tests, or use a stub HttpMessageHandler or a real database container."},{"c":"unit-testing","s":"testing-time-randomness-and-async","h":"Testing time, randomness and async","t":"# Testing time, randomness and async Intermediate Code that calls DateTime.UtcNow , Guid.NewGuid() or Random directly is non-deterministic — the exact same test can pass today and fail tomorrow purely because the clock moved. The fix is the same dependency-injection discipline the rest of this book applies everywhere else: inject the source of non-determinism instead of calling it directly. C# Run ▶ Copy // Production: register TimeProvider.System; code takes TimeProvider (.NET 8) public sealed class SubscriptionService ( TimeProvider clock) { public bool IsExpired ( Subscription s) => s. EndsAt < clock. GetUtcNow (); } // Test: Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing var clock = new FakeTimeProvider ( new DateTimeOffset ( 2026 , 10 , 27 , 12 , 0 , 0 , TimeSpan . Zero )); var sut = new SubscriptionService (clock); sut. IsExpired ( new Subscription { EndsAt = clock. GetUtcNow (). AddDays ( 1 ) }). ShouldBeFalse (); clock. Advance ( TimeSpan . FromDays ( 2 )); // move time forward deterministically; also fires timers sut. IsExpired ( new Subscription { EndsAt = new DateTimeOffset ( 2026 , 10 , 28 , 12 , 0 , 0 , TimeSpan . Zero ) }). ShouldBeTrue (); Async tests : make the test async Task and await the call; never .Result . Use Assert.ThrowsAsync . Timers, delays, PeriodicTimer : pass TimeProvider so FakeTimeProvider.Advance drives them without real waiting. Logging : FakeLogger<T> (Microsoft.Extensions.Diagnostics.Testing) captures log records to assert on."},{"c":"unit-testing","s":"test-data-builders-and-fixtures","h":"Test data builders and fixtures","t":"# Test data builders and fixtures Intermediate Tests drown in setup code without builders — constructing a valid Order by hand, every single time, for a test that only cares about one specific edge case, is exactly the kind of repetitive ceremony that makes a test suite unpleasant to maintain. The Test Data Builder pattern (or \"Object Mother\") — the same Builder from Design patterns — gives valid defaults and lets each test override only what actually matters to it: C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class OrderBuilder { private CustomerId _customer = CustomerId . New (); private readonly List <( ProductId , string , Money , int )> _lines = [( ProductId . New (), \"Keyboard\" , new Money ( 49.90m , \"EUR\" ), 1 )]; private bool _placed; public OrderBuilder ForCustomer ( CustomerId id) { _customer = id; return this ; } public OrderBuilder WithLine ( string name, decimal price, int qty = 1 ) { _lines. Add (( ProductId . New (), name, new Money (price, \"EUR\" ), qty)); return this ; } public OrderBuilder Placed () { _placed = true ; return this ; } public Order Build () { var o = Order . Draft (_customer); foreach ( var (p, n, m, q) in _lines) o. AddLine (p, n, m, q); if (_placed) o. Place ( TestData . Address , TimeProvider . System ); return o; } } [Fact] public void Placed_order_cannot_receive_new_lines () { var order = new OrderBuilder (). Placed (). Build (); var result = order. AddLine ( ProductId . New (), \"Mouse\" , new Money ( 20 , \"EUR\" ), 1 ); result. Error . ShouldBe ( OrderErrors . NotEditable ); } Notice how little the test itself has to say: new OrderBuilder().Placed().Build() conveys exactly the one fact this particular test cares about — that the order is already placed — while the builder's defaults quietly handle everything else (customer, lines) that the test doesn't need to think about at all. AutoFixture and Bogus generate anonymous or realistic data when values don't matter to the test."},{"c":"unit-testing","s":"what-to-unit-test-and-what-not-to","h":"What to unit test (and what not to)","t":"# What to unit test (and what not to) Intermediate High value Low value Domain logic : invariants, calculations, state transitions Trivial getters/setters, auto-properties Application handlers' decision logic (with fakes at the edges) Code that only delegates (pass-through) Algorithms, parsers, mappers with logic Framework behaviour (EF Core, ASP.NET Core) Edge cases: empty, null, boundaries, error paths Private methods directly (test via public behaviour) Bug reproductions (regression tests) UI layout Coverage is a diagnostic , not a goal. 100% line coverage with weak assertions proves little — a test can execute every line of a method and still assert nothing meaningful about what those lines actually produced. Mutation testing (Stryker.NET mutates your code, e.g. flipping > to >= , and checks whether tests fail) measures whether tests actually detect bugs, which is a fundamentally different and more honest question than \"did this line run.\""},{"c":"unit-testing","s":"tdd","h":"TDD","t":"# TDD Advanced Test-Driven Development : Red (write a failing test for the next small behaviour) → Green (the simplest code that passes) → Refactor (improve the design with the tests as a safety net). Repeat in minutes-long cycles. Benefits: tests exist by construction, the design is driven by usage (testable APIs, small units), and it keeps you focused on one small increment at a time. Criticisms: it can feel slow while a design is exploratory, and it's harder with heavy framework code where the \"simplest thing that passes\" isn't obvious. Many teams practise it selectively for domain logic and bug fixes (\"write the failing test that reproduces the bug first,\" which doubles as both a diagnosis and a regression guard). BDD (Given-When-Then, SpecFlow/Reqnroll) extends the idea to executable specifications written with business stakeholders. Q What makes a good unit test? Fast, isolated, repeatable (no real time, network or DB), self-validating, and focused on one observable behaviour through the public API. It has a clear Arrange-Act-Assert structure, a descriptive name, no logic of its own, and it survives refactoring because it asserts outcomes rather than implementation details. Q Explain the difference between a mock, a stub and a fake. A stub returns canned data to feed the code under test (state verification). A mock records interactions and is verified to have been called in a particular way (behaviour verification). A fake is a working lightweight implementation, such as an in-memory repository or FakeTimeProvider. Q When should you verify interactions with mocks? Only for outgoing side effects that cross the system boundary and are part of the observable behaviour: an email sent, a message published, a payment charged. Don't verify queries or internal collaborator calls; that couples tests to implementation. Q xUnit Fact vs Theory? Fact is a single parameterless test. Theory is a parameterised test that runs once per data row supplied by InlineData, MemberData, ClassData or TheoryData. Q How do you share setup across tests in xUnit? The constructor (and Dispose/IAsyncLifetime) for per-test setup, since xUnit creates a new instance per test; IClassFixture<T> to share an expensive object across a class's tests; collection fixtures ([Collection] plus ICollectionFixture) to share across classes, e.g. a database container. Q How do you test code that depends on the current time? Inject TimeProvider (.NET 8) or a clock abstraction instead of calling DateTime.Now. In tests, use FakeTimeProvider to set and advance time deterministically, which also drives timers and delays. Q Is 100% code coverage a good goal? No. Coverage shows which lines executed, not whether behaviour was verified, and chasing it produces low-value tests. Focus on meaningful coverage of business logic and edge cases, and use mutation testing to assess test effectiveness. Q What is TDD? Test-Driven Development: write a failing test for a small piece of behaviour (red), write the minimum code to pass (green), then refactor safely, in short cycles. It yields tests by construction and testable, usage-driven designs."},{"c":"unit-testing","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Unit testing best practices for .NET xUnit.net documentation Vladimir Khorikov, Unit Testing Principles, Practices, and Patterns : the best modern book on the topic. Roy Osherove, The Art of Unit Testing (3rd ed.). NSubstitute docs · Stryker.NET"},{"c":"unit-testing","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter No test framework or mocking library is needed for these checks — they use plain BCL types ( TimeProvider is built into .NET 8+) and hand-written stand-ins for a stub and a spy, so the stub-vs-mock distinction and the rounding claim can be verified directly. Save as Program.cs in a new console project ( dotnet new console ) and run with dotnet run . C# Run ▶ Copy // Verifies claims from this chapter: decimal rounding uses banker's rounding (matches the // chapter's RoundingCases table), the stub-vs-mock distinction (state verification vs behaviour // verification), and that injecting a TimeProvider makes time-dependent code deterministic in tests. using System ; int fails = 0 ; void Ok ( string s) => Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: {s}\" ); void Check ( bool cond, string s) { if (cond) Ok (s); else { Console . WriteLine ( $\"FAIL: {s}\" ); fails++; } } // --- \"RoundsToTwoDecimals_BankersRounding\" from the chapter's TheoryData --- Check ( Math . Round ( 1.005m , 2 ) == 1.00m , \"decimal Math.Round uses banker's rounding: 1.005 rounds to 1.00 (down to the even digit)\" ); Check ( Math . Round ( 1.015m , 2 ) == 1.02m , \"decimal Math.Round uses banker's rounding: 1.015 rounds to 1.02 (up to the even digit)\" ); Check ( Math . Round ( 2.345m , 2 ) == 2.34m , \"decimal Math.Round uses banker's rounding: 2.345 rounds to 2.34 (down to the even digit)\" ); // --- Stub: feeds a canned answer; you assert on the RESULT, not on whether it was called a certain way --- var stubGateway = new StubPaymentGateway (declineReason: \"insufficient_funds\" ); var handler = new PlaceOrderHandler (stubGateway); var result = handler. Handle ( 100m ); Check (result == \"declined: insufficient_funds\" , \"Stub: a canned answer drives the handler's result (state verification)\" ); // --- Spy: records how it was called, so you can assert the INTERACTION happened --- var spyGateway = new SpyPaymentGateway (); new PlaceOrderHandler (spyGateway). Handle ( 50m ); Check (spyGateway. ChargeCalls . Count == 1 && spyGateway. ChargeCalls [ 0 ] == 50m , \"Spy: records the call it received, so a test can assert the interaction happened (behaviour verification)\" ); // --- Injecting TimeProvider instead of calling DateTime.UtcNow makes a test deterministic --- var clock = new SettableTimeProvider ( new DateTimeOffset ( 2026 , 1 , 1 , 0 , 0 , 0 , TimeSpan . Zero )); var sub = new Subscription (clock, endsAt: clock. GetUtcNow (). AddDays ( 1 )); Check (!sub. IsExpired (), \"TimeProvider: subscription is not expired right after creation\" ); clock. Advance ( TimeSpan . FromDays ( 2 )); Check (sub. IsExpired (), \"TimeProvider: advancing the fake clock deterministically makes the same subscription expire, with no real waiting\" ); Console . WriteLine (fails == 0 ? \"\\nAll checks passed.\" : $\"\\n{fails} check(s) failed.\" ); if (fails > 0 ) Environment . Exit ( 1 ); interface IPaymentGateway { string Charge ( decimal amount); } sealed class StubPaymentGateway ( string declineReason) : IPaymentGateway { public string Charge ( decimal amount) => $\"declined: {declineReason}\" ; } sealed class SpyPaymentGateway : IPaymentGateway { public List < decimal > ChargeCalls { get ; } = new (); public string Charge ( decimal amount) { ChargeCalls . Add (amount); return \"charged\" ; } } sealed class PlaceOrderHandler ( IPaymentGateway gateway) { public string Handle ( decimal amount) => gateway. Charge (amount); } sealed class SettableTimeProvider ( DateTimeOffset start) : TimeProvider { private DateTimeOffset _now = start; public override DateTimeOffset GetUtcNow () => _now; public void Advance ( TimeSpan by) => _now += by; } sealed class Subscription ( TimeProvider clock, DateTimeOffset endsAt) { public bool IsExpired () => endsAt < clock. GetUtcNow (); }"},{"c":"integration-testing","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Integration tests catch bugs unit tests structurally cannot: wrong middleware order, missing DI registrations, serialization/casing mismatches, LINQ that can't translate to SQL, authorization gaps. WebApplicationFactory<Program> boots your real Program.cs in memory on a TestServer : real DI, real middleware, real routing — no network ports. Only replace things that are genuinely external, slow or non-deterministic (payment gateways, email, the clock). Keep your own code, database and broker real, or the test quietly turns into a unit test in disguise. Testcontainers starts real, throwaway databases/brokers for tests. Starting one is slow, so share it across a test run and reset data between tests (Respawn is the most common way). A fake authentication handler lets you control who the caller claims to be while your real authorization policies still run unmodified — so you can actually test that the wrong role gets a 403. Modern tooling (containers, in-memory hosting) shifted many teams' confidence from the classic unit-test-heavy pyramid toward integration tests for APIs."},{"c":"integration-testing","s":"what-unit-tests-structurally-cannot-catch","h":"What unit tests structurally cannot catch","t":"# What unit tests structurally cannot catch Beginner Think back to what a unit test, by its own definition from Unit testing , deliberately isolates away: real infrastructure, real wiring, the real pipeline. That's exactly what makes a whole category of bug invisible to it — bugs that live between components, not inside any one of them: middleware in the wrong order (every request returns 401), a missing DI registration or a captive dependency, a route template or model-binding mismatch, JSON casing or enum serialization differences, an EF Core mapping error, a missing migration, or a LINQ query that can't be translated or is slow in real SQL, authorization policies not applied to an endpoint, configuration binding failures. An integration test runs real components together (often the whole app in-process against real infrastructure in containers) and asserts on observable behaviour (HTTP responses, database state, published messages) — precisely the gap unit tests leave open."},{"c":"integration-testing","s":"test-pyramid-trophy-and-honeycomb-how-much-weight-goes-where","h":"Test pyramid, trophy and honeycomb: how much weight goes where","t":"# Test pyramid, trophy and honeycomb: how much weight goes where Intermediate Diagram Copy Pyramid (Mike Cohn) Trophy (Kent C. Dodds) Honeycomb (Spotify, microservices) /E2E\\ (E2E) ( E2E ) /-------\\ ┌──────────┐ ┌──────────────────────┐ /Integration\\ │Integration│ ← most tests │ Integration │ ← most /---------------\\ └──────────┘ └──────────────────────┘ / Unit \\ ( Unit ) ( Unit / details ) ───────────────────── (Static analysis) The classic pyramid says: many unit tests, fewer integration tests, very few end-to-end tests. That shape made sense when higher-level tests were slow and flaky, so you minimized time spent on them. Containers and in-memory hosting changed that : Testcontainers and WebApplicationFactory (both covered below) made integration tests fast and reliable enough that many .NET teams now put most of their confidence in integration tests for APIs, with unit tests reserved for complex domain logic and a handful of E2E smoke tests. The pyramid's shape was never the real point — the point was always \"spend your testing effort where it buys the most confidence per minute,\" and that calculation changed once the tooling did. Test type Scope Speed Confidence per test Typical tools Unit A behaviour in-process, no I/O ms Narrow xUnit, NSubstitute Integration (in-process API) Full pipeline + real DB/broker in containers 10–500 ms each (plus container startup) High WebApplicationFactory, Testcontainers, Respawn Contract API/message schema between services Fast Prevents integration breaks Pact, OpenAPI diffing, schema registry checks End-to-end Deployed system through the UI or public API Seconds Highest, but flaky and costly Playwright, k6 scripts, smoke tests Performance / load Throughput and latency under load Minutes Capacity k6, NBomber, Azure Load Testing"},{"c":"integration-testing","s":"webapplicationfactory-booting-the-real-app-in-memory","h":"WebApplicationFactory: booting the real app in memory","t":"# WebApplicationFactory: booting the real app in memory Beginner Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing boots your real Program.cs in memory on a TestServer (no network ports) and gives you an HttpClient wired straight into the pipeline. C# Run ▶ Copy // Make Program visible to the test project (top-level statements generate an internal Program class) public partial class Program ; // at the bottom of Program.cs public sealed class HealthTests ( WebApplicationFactory < Program > factory) : IClassFixture < WebApplicationFactory < Program >> { [Fact] public async Task Health_endpoint_returns_ok () { var client = factory. CreateClient (); var response = await client. GetAsync ( \"/health\" ); response. StatusCode . ShouldBe ( HttpStatusCode . OK ); } } The reason this test is worth more than it looks: everything runs for real — configuration, DI, the actual middleware order from Middleware , routing, model binding, validation, filters, serialization and ProblemDetails. A bug in any one of those, the exact category this chapter opened with, shows up here."},{"c":"integration-testing","s":"customising-the-app-under-test","h":"Customising the app under test","t":"# Customising the app under test Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class ShopApiFactory : WebApplicationFactory < Program >, IAsyncLifetime { private readonly MsSqlContainer _sql = new MsSqlBuilder (). WithImage ( \"mcr.microsoft.com/mssql/server:2022-latest\" ). Build (); public FakeEmailSender Emails { get ; } = new (); public FakeTimeProvider Clock { get ; } = new ( DateTimeOffset . Parse ( \"2026-10-27T10:00:00Z\" )); protected override void ConfigureWebHost ( IWebHostBuilder builder) { builder. UseEnvironment ( \"Testing\" ); builder. UseSetting ( \"ConnectionStrings:Shop\" , _sql. GetConnectionString ()); // override configuration builder. ConfigureTestServices (services => // runs AFTER Program.cs registrations { services. RemoveAll < IEmailSender >(); services. AddSingleton < IEmailSender >( Emails ); // replace only true externals services. AddSingleton < TimeProvider >( Clock ); services. AddSingleton < IPaymentGateway , ApprovingPaymentGateway >(); services. AddAuthentication ( TestAuthHandler . Scheme ) // fake authentication (below) . AddScheme < AuthenticationSchemeOptions , TestAuthHandler >( TestAuthHandler . Scheme , _ => { }); }); } public async ValueTask InitializeAsync () { await _sql. StartAsync (); using var scope = Services . CreateScope (); await scope. ServiceProvider . GetRequiredService < ShopDb >(). Database . MigrateAsync (); // real schema from real migrations } public new async ValueTask DisposeAsync () { await _sql. DisposeAsync (); await base . DisposeAsync (); } } The rule here: only replace things that are external, slow, non-deterministic or costly — payment providers, email/SMS, third-party APIs, the clock. Keep your code, your database and your broker real. The whole value of an integration test comes from exercising the real wiring; swap out more than genuine externals and it quietly turns back into a unit test wearing a disguise. # Fake authentication C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class TestAuthHandler ( IOptionsMonitor < AuthenticationSchemeOptions > o, ILoggerFactory l, UrlEncoder e) : AuthenticationHandler < AuthenticationSchemeOptions >(o, l, e) { public const string Scheme = \"Test\" ; protected override Task < AuthenticateResult > HandleAuthenticateAsync () { if (! Request . Headers . TryGetValue ( \"X-Test-User\" , out var user)) return Task . FromResult ( AuthenticateResult . NoResult ()); var claims = new List < Claim > { new ( \"sub\" , user!), new ( ClaimTypes . Name , user!) }; claims. AddRange ( Request . Headers [ \"X-Test-Roles\" ]. ToString (). Split ( ',' , StringSplitOptions . RemoveEmptyEntries ). Select (r => new Claim ( \"roles\" , r))); var principal = new ClaimsPrincipal ( new ClaimsIdentity (claims, Scheme , ClaimTypes . Name , \"roles\" )); return Task . FromResult ( AuthenticateResult . Success ( new AuthenticationTicket (principal, Scheme ))); } } // Make it the default scheme in tests: services. PostConfigure < AuthenticationOptions >(o => { o. DefaultAuthenticateScheme = TestAuthHandler . Scheme ; o. DefaultChallengeScheme = TestAuthHandler . Scheme ; }); // In a test client. DefaultRequestHeaders . Add ( \"X-Test-User\" , \"customer-42\" ); client. DefaultRequestHeaders . Add ( \"X-Test-Roles\" , \"customer\" ); This handler is worth writing instead of just disabling auth for tests: it replaces only the authentication step — who the caller claims to be — while your real authorization policies still run unmodified, exactly as Auth described. So you can genuinely test that a customer gets 403 on an admin endpoint, with the real policy logic making that call. Alternatively, mint real JWTs with a test signing key for even closer fidelity to production."},{"c":"integration-testing","s":"real-databases-with-testcontainers","h":"Real databases with Testcontainers","t":"# Real databases with Testcontainers Intermediate Testcontainers for .NET starts throwaway Docker containers (lightweight, isolated environments that run a piece of software — see Docker for the full picture) from tests: SQL Server, PostgreSQL, Redis, RabbitMQ, Azurite (Azure Storage), the Service Bus emulator, Cosmos DB emulator, Kafka, LocalStack. C# Run ▶ Copy private readonly PostgreSqlContainer _pg = new PostgreSqlBuilder (). WithImage ( \"postgres:17-alpine\" ). Build (); private readonly RedisContainer _redis = new RedisBuilder (). Build (); Starting a container takes seconds, which is long enough that doing it per-test would make the suite painfully slow — so share one per test run or collection , and instead reset data between tests : Strategy How Trade-off Respawn Deletes data from all tables (FK-aware) before each test Fast, simple; most popular Transaction per test, rolled back Wrap each test in a transaction Very fast; doesn't work if the code under test manages its own transactions Unique data per test Each test creates its own customer/tenant IDs No reset needed; tests can run in parallel DB snapshot/restore or a fresh DB per class Clone a template database Isolation at a cost C# Run ▶ Copy [CollectionDefinition(nameof(ApiCollection))] public sealed class ApiCollection : ICollectionFixture < ShopApiFactory >; // one factory + container for all API tests [Collection(nameof(ApiCollection))] public sealed class PlaceOrderTests ( ShopApiFactory app) : IAsyncLifetime { private Respawner _respawner = null !; public async ValueTask InitializeAsync () { await using var conn = new SqlConnection (app. ConnectionString ); await conn. OpenAsync (); _respawner = await Respawner . CreateAsync (conn, new RespawnerOptions { TablesToIgnore = [ \"__EFMigrationsHistory\" ] }); await _respawner. ResetAsync (conn); } public ValueTask DisposeAsync () => ValueTask . CompletedTask ; [Fact] public async Task Placing_an_order_persists_it_and_sends_confirmation () { var client = app. CreateAuthenticatedClient (user: \"customer-42\" ); await app. SeedAsync (db => db. Products . Add ( new Product ( \"KB-01\" , \"Keyboard\" , 49.90m , stock: 10 ))); var response = await client. PostAsJsonAsync ( \"/api/orders\" , new { lines = new [] { new { sku = \"KB-01\" , quantity = 2 } } }); response. StatusCode . ShouldBe ( HttpStatusCode . Created ); var created = await response. Content . ReadFromJsonAsync < OrderDto >(); created!. Total . ShouldBe ( 99.80m ); response. Headers . Location !. ToString (). ShouldBe ( $\"/api/orders/{created.Id}\" ); await app. ExecuteDbAsync ( async db => ( await db. Products . SingleAsync (p => p. Sku == \"KB-01\" )). Stock . ShouldBe ( 8 )); app. Emails . Sent . ShouldContain (m => m. To == \"customer-42@test\" && m. Subject . Contains ( \"Order confirmed\" )); } [Fact] public async Task Invalid_order_returns_validation_problem () { var client = app. CreateAuthenticatedClient (user: \"customer-42\" ); var response = await client. PostAsJsonAsync ( \"/api/orders\" , new { lines = Array . Empty < object >() }); response. StatusCode . ShouldBe ( HttpStatusCode . BadRequest ); var problem = await response. Content . ReadFromJsonAsync < ValidationProblemDetails >(); problem!. Errors . ShouldContainKey ( \"Lines\" ); } } That one test proves four things at once: the HTTP request binds correctly, the handler runs its real logic, the database is actually debited ( Stock.ShouldBe(8) ), and a side effect fired ( Emails.Sent ). Proving all four with unit tests would need separate, carefully constructed mocks — and even then wouldn't prove they work together . Don't use the EF Core InMemory provider for integration tests It isn't relational: no SQL translation, no constraints, no transactions, different null and case semantics. Tests pass while production fails. Microsoft's own guidance discourages it. Use the real engine (Testcontainers) or at least SQLite."},{"c":"integration-testing","s":"testing-dependencies-you-dont-own","h":"Testing dependencies you don't own","t":"# Testing dependencies you don't own Intermediate Dependency Approach Third-party HTTP APIs WireMock.Net fake server (realistic HTTP, delays, faults) or a stub HttpMessageHandler ( HttpClient ) Message broker A real broker in a container (RabbitMQ, Service Bus emulator), or the library's test harness ( MassTransit ITestHarness to assert published/consumed messages) Azure Storage Azurite container Cosmos DB Cosmos DB emulator (Linux container) Identity provider Fake auth handler, or a test token issuer C# Run ▶ Copy // MassTransit test harness: assert the endpoint published an event var harness = app. Services . GetTestHarness (); await client. PostAsJsonAsync ( \"/api/orders\" , request); ( await harness. Published . Any < OrderPlaced >(x => x. Context . Message . CustomerId == \"customer-42\" )). ShouldBeTrue ();"},{"c":"integration-testing","s":"contract-and-architecture-tests","h":"Contract and architecture tests","t":"# Contract and architecture tests Advanced Consumer-driven contract tests (Pact): the consumer records the requests it makes and the responses it expects as a contract ; the provider's CI verifies it still honours every consumer's contract. This catches breaking API changes without deploying both services together — directly addressing the \"versioning\" cost microservices introduce, named back in Microservices . Lighter alternatives: OpenAPI diffing in CI (fail on breaking changes), or schema registry compatibility checks for messages. Snapshot tests of public contracts ( Verify on the generated OpenAPI document or on serialized message types) make accidental contract changes visible in code review. Architecture tests (NetArchTest, ArchUnitNET) enforce layering rules. See App architectures ."},{"c":"integration-testing","s":"end-to-end-smoke-and-load-tests","h":"End-to-end, smoke and load tests","t":"# End-to-end, smoke and load tests Advanced E2E : drive the deployed system like a user. Playwright for .NET for browser flows; scripted API journeys for backends. Keep them few , focused on critical paths (sign up, checkout), and tolerant of async processing (poll with timeouts, not fixed sleeps). Smoke tests after each deployment: health endpoints plus one or two key reads, gating promotion to production. Load tests (k6, NBomber, Azure Load Testing, JMeter): establish baselines (p50/p95/p99 latency, throughput, error rate) and find bottlenecks before launches. Run against production-like environments with realistic data volumes. Chaos testing (Azure Chaos Studio, Polly's chaos strategies via AddChaos… ): inject faults to verify resilience behaviour."},{"c":"integration-testing","s":"tests-in-ci","h":"Tests in CI","t":"# Tests in CI Intermediate YAML Copy - run : dotnet test --configuration Release --logger \"trx;LogFileName=results.trx\" --collect : \"XPlat Code Coverage\" # GitHub-hosted Ubuntu runners have Docker, so Testcontainers just works Practices worth adopting: Run unit tests on every commit, integration tests on every PR. Quarantine and fix flaky tests immediately — they destroy trust in the whole suite. Keep the suite fast: parallel collections, shared containers, Respawn. Publish test results and coverage, and make the test run a required check before merge. Q What is the difference between unit and integration tests? Unit tests verify a single behaviour in isolation, in-process with no I/O, and run in milliseconds. Integration tests verify that components work together with real infrastructure (pipeline, DI, serialization, database, broker), catching wiring, configuration, mapping and SQL issues that unit tests can't. Q How do you integration-test an ASP.NET Core API? WebApplicationFactory<Program> hosts the real app in memory with a TestServer and provides an HttpClient. Override configuration and replace only external dependencies in ConfigureTestServices, use a real database via Testcontainers with migrations applied, reset data with Respawn, and assert on HTTP responses and persisted state. Q How do you handle authentication in integration tests? Register a test authentication handler that builds a ClaimsPrincipal from headers (or mint real JWTs with a test key) and make it the default scheme, so real authorization policies still execute against controllable identities. Q What is Testcontainers and why use it? A library that starts disposable Docker containers (SQL Server, Postgres, Redis, RabbitMQ, Azurite…) from test code. Tests run against the real engines with production-like behaviour, isolated and reproducible locally and in CI, instead of fakes like the EF InMemory provider. Q How do you keep integration tests isolated and fast? Share one app factory and container per collection, reset data between tests (Respawn, or a transaction rollback), use unique data per test where possible, run collections in parallel, and replace only slow external dependencies. Q What are contract tests? Tests verifying that a provider service honours the API or message contract its consumers rely on (e.g. Pact consumer-driven contracts, OpenAPI breaking-change checks). They catch integration breaks between independently deployed services without full end-to-end environments. Q Test pyramid vs testing trophy? The pyramid recommends mostly unit tests, fewer integration tests and very few E2E tests. The trophy shifts the weight to integration tests, because modern tooling makes them fast and they give more confidence per test, with unit tests reserved for complex logic and only a few E2E tests."},{"c":"integration-testing","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Integration tests in ASP.NET Core Testcontainers for .NET Respawn · WireMock.Net · Pact .NET Testing against your production database system (EF Core)"},{"c":"integration-testing","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter The checks below build a real in-memory ASP.NET Core pipeline with TestServer (the same mechanism WebApplicationFactory boots underneath) and confirm middleware runs in order and a faked identity is still subject to a real authorization decision. No Docker needed. Save as a console app ( dotnet new console , dotnet add package Microsoft.AspNetCore.TestHost ), then dotnet run . C# Run ▶ Copy using Microsoft . AspNetCore . Authentication ; using Microsoft . AspNetCore . Builder ; using Microsoft . AspNetCore . Hosting ; using Microsoft . AspNetCore . Http ; using Microsoft . AspNetCore . Http . Extensions ; using Microsoft . AspNetCore . TestHost ; using Microsoft . Extensions . DependencyInjection ; using Microsoft . Extensions . Hosting ; using System . Security . Claims ; var order = new List < string >(); // Claim: middleware runs in registration order — each one runs before the next. var hostBuilder = new HostBuilder (). ConfigureWebHost (webHost => { webHost. UseTestServer (); webHost. ConfigureServices (services => { services. AddAuthentication ( TestAuthHandler . Scheme ) . AddScheme < AuthenticationSchemeOptions , TestAuthHandler >( TestAuthHandler . Scheme , _ => { }); }); webHost. Configure (app => { app. Use ( async (ctx, next) => { order. Add ( \"A\" ); await next(); }); app. Use ( async (ctx, next) => { order. Add ( \"B\" ); await next(); }); app. UseAuthentication (); // Authorization here is a REAL policy check (RequireClaim), just invoked directly instead of via // the endpoint-routing UseAuthorization() middleware, which needs a full routing setup this tiny host skips. app. Map ( \"/public\" , inner => inner. Run (ctx => ctx. Response . WriteAsync ( \"ok\" ))); app. Map ( \"/admin\" , inner => { inner. Run ( async ctx => { var authResult = await ctx. AuthenticateAsync ( TestAuthHandler . Scheme ); var isAdmin = authResult. Succeeded && authResult. Principal !. HasClaim ( \"roles\" , \"admin\" ); if (!isAdmin) { ctx. Response . StatusCode = 403 ; return ; } await ctx. Response . WriteAsync ( \"admin-ok\" ); }); }); }); }); using var host = await hostBuilder. StartAsync (); using var client = host. GetTestClient (); // Claim: the in-memory pipeline runs middleware in registration order — real wiring, not a mock. { var res = await client. GetAsync ( \"/public\" ); if (!order. SequenceEqual ( new [] { \"A\" , \"B\" })) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected [A, B], got [{string.Join(\" , \", order)}]\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: an in-memory TestServer pipeline runs middleware in real registration order\" ); } // Claim: a fake authentication handler lets you control WHO the caller is, while real authorization policies still decide. { using var req1 = new HttpRequestMessage ( HttpMethod . Get , \"/admin\" ); req1. Headers . Add ( \"X-Test-User\" , \"customer-42\" ); req1. Headers . Add ( \"X-Test-Roles\" , \"customer\" ); var res1 = await client. SendAsync (req1); if (( int )res1. StatusCode != 403 ) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected 403 for a non-admin, got {(int)res1.StatusCode}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: a customer identity (faked) is correctly rejected by the REAL authorization check\" ); using var req2 = new HttpRequestMessage ( HttpMethod . Get , \"/admin\" ); req2. Headers . Add ( \"X-Test-User\" , \"admin-1\" ); req2. Headers . Add ( \"X-Test-Roles\" , \"admin\" ); var res2 = await client. SendAsync (req2); if (( int )res2. StatusCode != 200 ) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected 200 for an admin, got {(int)res2.StatusCode}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: an admin identity (faked) is correctly allowed by the REAL authorization check\" ); } Console . WriteLine ( \"All checks passed.\" ); // Not runtime-verifiable here (needs Docker, or a full WebApplicationFactory<Program> against a real app project): // - Testcontainers starting real SQL Server/Postgres/Redis/RabbitMQ containers — see Further reading: \"Testcontainers for .NET\" // - Respawn resetting real table data between tests — see Further reading: \"Respawn\" // - WebApp"},{"c":"messaging-events","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR A broker sits between services so the sender doesn't wait: it hands off a message and moves on, decoupled in time from whoever eventually reads it. Commands (do this, exactly one handler, can be rejected) go on queues ; events (this happened, zero or more subscribers) go on topics . Brokers deliver at-least-once , never exactly-once — duplicates are a normal outcome of coordinating two systems over a network, not a bug. Every consumer must be idempotent . The dual-write problem (you can't atomically save to your DB and publish to a broker) is solved by the transactional outbox : write the message to a table in the same transaction as the business change, then let a separate worker publish it. Most brokers don't guarantee global order; design for disorder (sequence numbers, partition/session keys) instead of assuming it. A saga runs a multi-step business process as a chain of local transactions with compensating \"undo\" steps, either coordinated by services reacting to each other's events ( choreography ) or by a central state machine ( orchestration )."},{"c":"messaging-events","s":"what-putting-a-broker-in-the-middle-actually-buys-you","h":"What putting a broker in the middle actually buys you","t":"# What putting a broker in the middle actually buys you Beginner With synchronous HTTP between services, the caller waits , and if the callee is down or slow, that failure propagates straight back to the caller — exactly the \"chatty synchronous chains\" problem Microservices warned about. Messaging fixes this by putting a broker in between: the sender hands a message to the broker and moves on; receivers process it when they can, entirely decoupled from whether the sender is even still running. Diagram Copy Synchronous: Ordering ──HTTP──▶ Payments ──HTTP──▶ Email (all must be up, latency adds up, failures cascade) Asynchronous: Ordering ──▶ [ broker: \"OrderPlaced\" ] ──▶ Payments ├──▶ Email └──▶ Analytics (added later without touching Ordering) Benefit Meaning Temporal decoupling Producer and consumer don't need to be up at the same time Load levelling A spike of 10,000 orders queues up; consumers process at their own pace Fault isolation Email being down doesn't stop checkout Extensibility New subscribers react to existing events without changing the publisher Scalability Add consumer instances (competing consumers) to increase throughput Notice the diagram's last line: Analytics was \"added later without touching Ordering\" — that's the Observer pattern from Design patterns operating at the scale of entire services, where the publisher never needs to know who's listening. Costs: eventual consistency , more infrastructure, harder debugging (needs tracing), duplicate and out-of-order delivery to handle, and message versioning — each of which gets its own section below."},{"c":"messaging-events","s":"commands-events-and-queries-three-different-conversations","h":"Commands, events and queries: three different conversations","t":"# Commands, events and queries: three different conversations Beginner Command Event Query Meaning \"Please do X\" \"X happened\" \"Tell me X\" Naming Imperative: ChargePayment , SendInvoice Past tense: OrderPlaced , PaymentFailed GetOrderStatus Receivers Exactly one logical handler Zero or many subscribers One Sender knows the receiver? Yes (sends to a specific queue) No (publishes to a topic) Yes Can be rejected? Yes No: it already happened — Typical channel Queue Topic (pub/sub) Usually HTTP/gRPC, not messaging This is the exact same Command-vs-Event naming discipline from Design patterns , now mapped onto concrete infrastructure choices — a command's \"exactly one handler\" nature is precisely why it belongs on a queue, while an event's \"zero or many subscribers\" nature is precisely why it belongs on a topic. Event types: notification events (thin: OrderPlaced { OrderId } , consumers call back for details) vs event-carried state transfer (fat: includes the data consumers need, so no callback). Fat events reduce coupling at runtime but increase schema coupling."},{"c":"messaging-events","s":"queues-and-topics","h":"Queues and topics","t":"# Queues and topics Beginner Diagram Copy QUEUE (point-to-point, competing consumers) TOPIC (publish/subscribe) Producer ─▶ [ m3 m2 m1 ] ─▶ Consumer A (gets m1) Publisher ─▶ (topic) ─┬─▶ [subscription: billing ] ─▶ Billing └▶ Consumer B (gets m2) ├─▶ [subscription: email ] ─▶ Email each message goes to ONE consumer instance └─▶ [subscription: search ] ─▶ Search each subscription gets its OWN copy A message is processed by one instance per queue or subscription, so scaling out consumers increases throughput without duplicate processing (in theory: see delivery guarantees, next)."},{"c":"messaging-events","s":"brokers-compared","h":"Brokers compared","t":"# Brokers compared Intermediate Azure Service Bus RabbitMQ Kafka / Azure Event Hubs Azure Storage Queues Model Enterprise message broker: queues + topics/subscriptions Broker with exchanges (direct, topic, fanout, headers) routing to queues Distributed append-only log partitioned into topics Simple queue Consumption Message removed when completed Message removed when acked Consumers track offsets ; messages retained (days, or forever with compaction) and replayable Removed when deleted Ordering FIFO per session Per queue (single consumer) Per partition Best effort Features Sessions, dead-letter queues, scheduled messages, duplicate detection, transactions, filters, auto-forwarding Flexible routing, plugins, quorum queues, streams Huge throughput, replay, stream processing, consumer groups Cheap, massive scale, basic Hosting Fully managed PaaS Self-hosted, or CloudAMQP etc. Confluent / self-hosted Kafka; Event Hubs (managed, Kafka-compatible) Managed Best for Business commands and events, workflows On-premises / multi-cloud, flexible routing Event streaming, telemetry, analytics, event sourcing pipelines Simple background jobs The \"Consumption\" row is the real distinguishing feature between Service Bus and Kafka, worth being precise about: Service Bus removes a message once it's handled, treating messages as work to be consumed and discarded; Kafka retains everything and lets consumers independently track their own position in the log, treating messages as a durable, replayable history. That difference is why Kafka fits event sourcing pipelines naturally and Service Bus doesn't — replay only makes sense if nothing was removed. Also on Azure: Event Grid , a push-based event router for reactive programming (a blob was created, a resource changed, custom events to webhooks or functions). It's lightweight notification fan-out, not a work queue. See Azure data & messaging ."},{"c":"messaging-events","s":"delivery-guarantees-why-exactly-once-isnt-really-on-the-tabl","h":"Delivery guarantees: why \"exactly once\" isn't really on the table","t":"# Delivery guarantees: why \"exactly once\" isn't really on the table Intermediate Guarantee Meaning Reality At-most-once Delivered 0 or 1 times (may be lost) Fire-and-forget; acceptable for metrics At-least-once Delivered 1 or more times ( duplicates possible ) The default for virtually all brokers Exactly-once Delivered precisely once Not achievable end-to-end across systems in general; you get effectively-once via idempotency Duplicates aren't a quality-of-implementation problem — they're unavoidable. A consumer can crash right after finishing the work but before acknowledging it; the broker has no way to know the work actually finished, so it redelivers to someone else. A producer can time out waiting for an acknowledgment and retry a publish that, unknown to it, already succeeded. A processing lock can expire during unusually slow work, letting a second instance pick up the same message. None of these are broker bugs — they're the honest cost of coordinating two independent systems over a network that can always be slow or drop a packet. Therefore every consumer must be idempotent. The rest of this chapter builds directly on that one conclusion."},{"c":"messaging-events","s":"the-dual-write-problem-and-the-transactional-outbox","h":"The dual-write problem and the transactional outbox","t":"# The dual-write problem and the transactional outbox Advanced C# Run ▶ Copy // ✘ Two separate writes: no transaction spans the database AND the broker db. Orders . Add (order); await db. SaveChangesAsync (ct); // 1. committed await bus. Publish ( new OrderPlaced (order. Id ), ct); // 2. process crashes here → order exists but the event is LOST // Reverse the order and you get: event published, then SaveChanges fails → event for an order that doesn't exist This is the dual-write problem . Swapping the order of the two writes doesn't fix it — it just swaps which failure you get instead. The database transaction and the broker publish are two separate systems with no shared commit between them, which is exactly the \"no distributed transactions across services\" limit EF Core advanced named. Transactional outbox: write the message to an Outbox table in the same database transaction as the business change. A separate relay publishes outbox rows to the broker and marks them sent. Diagram Copy ┌──────────── one DB transaction ────────────┐ │ INSERT INTO Orders … │ │ INSERT INTO OutboxMessages (Id, Type, Payload)│ Outbox relay (BackgroundService, or CDC) └──────────────────────────────────────────────┘ ───▶ reads unsent rows ─▶ publishes ─▶ marks ProcessedAt either both commit or neither (retries on failure → at-least-once publishing) Follow exactly how this closes the gap the naive code left open: the Orders insert and the OutboxMessages insert are now part of one atomic transaction, so there is no longer a window where the order exists but the event doesn't, or vice versa. The relay's own publish can still fail and retry — but that's fine, because it's now publishing from durable, already-committed data, and retrying a publish that already succeeded is just another instance of the at-least-once guarantee this chapter already accepted as the baseline. C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class OutboxRelay ( IServiceScopeFactory scopes, IMessagePublisher publisher, ILogger < OutboxRelay > log) : BackgroundService { protected override async Task ExecuteAsync ( CancellationToken stoppingToken) { using var timer = new PeriodicTimer ( TimeSpan . FromSeconds ( 2 )); while ( await timer. WaitForNextTickAsync (stoppingToken)) { await using var scope = scopes. CreateAsyncScope (); var db = scope. ServiceProvider . GetRequiredService < ShopDb >(); var batch = await db. OutboxMessages . FromSql ( $\"SELECT TOP (50) * FROM OutboxMessages WITH (UPDLOCK, READPAST, ROWLOCK) WHERE ProcessedAt IS NULL ORDER BY OccurredAt\" ) . ToListAsync (stoppingToken); // READPAST lets several relays run without clashing foreach ( var msg in batch) { try { await publisher. PublishAsync (msg. Type , msg. Payload , messageId: msg. Id , stoppingToken); // message id = outbox id (for dedup) msg. ProcessedAt = DateTimeOffset . UtcNow ; } catch ( Exception ex) { msg. Attempts ++; msg. LastError = ex. Message ; log. LogWarning (ex, \"Outbox publish failed for {Id}\" , msg. Id ); } } await db. SaveChangesAsync (stoppingToken); } } } Variants: polling (above), change data capture (Debezium or SQL CDC streaming the outbox table), and library-provided outboxes ( MassTransit , NServiceBus , Wolverine , Brighter all ship EF Core outboxes). Prefer a library's outbox over hand-rolling one in production — the READPAST locking detail above is exactly the kind of subtlety a mature library has already handled correctly."},{"c":"messaging-events","s":"idempotent-consumers-and-the-inbox","h":"Idempotent consumers and the inbox","t":"# Idempotent consumers and the inbox Advanced Make processing the same message twice harmless — not by preventing duplicates (impossible, as established above) but by making a duplicate's second arrival a no-op: Technique Example Naturally idempotent operations SET status = 'Paid' (not balance = balance - 10 ); upserts Inbox / processed-messages table Store the message ID in the same transaction as the handler's changes, with a unique key; skip if already present Business keys + unique constraints Payments has a unique OrderId , so a second insert fails and is treated as already handled Version checks Apply an update only if the entity is still at the expected version Broker duplicate detection Service Bus duplicate detection by MessageId within a time window (helps with producer retries, not consumer crashes) C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class ChargeOnOrderPlaced ( ShopDb db, IPaymentGateway payments) : IConsumer < OrderPlaced > { public async Task Consume ( ConsumeContext < OrderPlaced > ctx) { var messageId = ctx. MessageId !. Value ; if ( await db. InboxMessages . AnyAsync (m => m. Id == messageId)) return ; // already processed await using var tx = await db. Database . BeginTransactionAsync (); var result = await payments. ChargeAsync (ctx. Message . OrderId , ctx. Message . Total , idempotencyKey: ctx. Message . OrderId . ToString ()); db. Payments . Add ( new Payment (ctx. Message . OrderId , result. Reference )); db. InboxMessages . Add ( new InboxMessage (messageId, nameof ( OrderPlaced ), DateTimeOffset . UtcNow )); // unique PK await db. SaveChangesAsync (); await tx. CommitAsync (); } } Note the idempotency key passed to payments.ChargeAsync — the exact pattern from API design — because the inbox table alone only protects your own database. The payment provider is a different system with no visibility into your inbox, so if this consumer crashes after charging but before the inbox row commits, redelivery would charge the card a second time unless the idempotency key passed to the gateway itself prevents it. External side effects always need their own deduplication, because your database transaction can't roll back a card charge."},{"c":"messaging-events","s":"retries-poison-messages-and-dead-lettering","h":"Retries, poison messages and dead-lettering","t":"# Retries, poison messages and dead-lettering Intermediate Immediate retries for transient glitches (deadlocks, brief timeouts). Delayed redelivery with backoff (seconds → minutes) for longer outages of a dependency. Dead-letter queue (DLQ) : after N failed attempts, move the message aside so it doesn't block the queue or burn resources (a poison message ). Alert on DLQ depth; inspect, fix, then resubmit . Distinguish transient errors (retry) from permanent ones (validation failure or unknown entity: dead-letter immediately, no point retrying). C# Run ▶ Copy // MassTransit retry/redelivery configuration cfg. ReceiveEndpoint ( \"billing-order-placed\" , e => { e. UseMessageRetry (r => r. Exponential ( 5 , TimeSpan . FromMilliseconds ( 200 ), TimeSpan . FromSeconds ( 10 ), TimeSpan . FromMilliseconds ( 200 )) . Ignore < ValidationException >()); e. UseDelayedRedelivery (r => r. Intervals ( TimeSpan . FromMinutes ( 1 ), TimeSpan . FromMinutes ( 5 ), TimeSpan . FromMinutes ( 30 ))); e. UseEntityFrameworkOutbox < ShopDb >(context); // inbox/outbox for this endpoint e. ConfigureConsumer < ChargeOnOrderPlaced >(context); });"},{"c":"messaging-events","s":"ordering-a-guarantee-most-brokers-deliberately-dont-give-you","h":"Ordering: a guarantee most brokers deliberately don't give you","t":"# Ordering: a guarantee most brokers deliberately don't give you Advanced Most brokers don't guarantee global order , and with competing consumers and retries, messages can be processed out of order even when they arrived in order — two consumers picking up two messages at nearly the same instant have no way to coordinate which one finishes first. Strategies: Partition by key : Service Bus sessions ( SessionId = orderId ) or Kafka partition keys guarantee FIFO per key with one consumer per session/partition at a time — ordering is given up globally in exchange for throughput, but preserved exactly where it usually matters: within one entity's own history. Design for disorder : include a version or sequence number or timestamp; ignore stale updates ( if (msg.Version <= entity.Version) return; ). Make events commutative where possible. Avoid needing order across different entities at all."},{"c":"messaging-events","s":"sagas-and-process-managers","h":"Sagas and process managers","t":"# Sagas and process managers Advanced A business process spanning services (place order → reserve stock → charge payment → ship) can't use one ACID transaction — exactly the \"one transaction modifies one aggregate\" rule from DDD , now playing out across service boundaries rather than aggregate boundaries. A saga is a sequence of local transactions , each publishing an event that triggers the next step. If a step fails, compensating actions undo the previous steps semantically (refund the payment, release the stock) — there's no rollback in the database sense, only a deliberate action that reverses the business effect. Style How Pros Cons Choreography Each service listens for events and reacts, emitting its own events; no central coordinator Loose coupling, simple for short flows Flow is implicit and spread across services; hard to see, test and change; risk of cyclic dependencies Orchestration A saga orchestrator (state machine) sends commands to each service and reacts to replies Explicit flow in one place; easier timeouts, monitoring and compensation The orchestrator is a central component to build and run Notice the tradeoff is the same one choreography vs orchestration always presents in distributed design: choreography trades visibility for decoupling, orchestration trades a central dependency for the ability to actually see the whole process in one place — invaluable once something has gone wrong at 3 a.m. and you need to know exactly which step the order is stuck on. Diagram Copy Orchestrated order saga (state machine): [Submitted] ──ReserveStock──▶ Inventory ──StockReserved──▶ [StockReserved] ──ChargePayment──▶ Payments │ │ │ PaymentFailed ◀──────┘ ▼ │ StockReservationFailed → [Rejected] ReleaseStock (compensate) → [Cancelled] PaymentCharged → ShipOrder → [Completed] Timeout after 15 min in any state → compensate → [Cancelled] C# Run ▶ Copy // MassTransit state machine saga (persisted with EF Core) public sealed class OrderSaga : MassTransitStateMachine < OrderState > { public State Reserving { get ; private set ; } = null !; public State Charging { get ; private set ; } = null !; public State Completed { get ; private set ; } = null !; public Event < OrderSubmitted > Submitted { get ; private set ; } = null !; public Event < StockReserved > Reserved { get ; private set ; } = null !; public Event < PaymentFailed > PaymentFailed { get ; private set ; } = null !; public Event < PaymentCharged > Charged { get ; private set ; } = null !; public OrderSaga () { InstanceState (x => x. CurrentState ); Event (() => Submitted , x => x. CorrelateById (m => m. Message . OrderId )); Event (() => Reserved , x => x. CorrelateById (m => m. Message . OrderId )); Event (() => PaymentFailed , x => x. CorrelateById (m => m. Message . OrderId )); Event (() => Charged , x => x. CorrelateById (m => m. Message . OrderId )); Initially ( When ( Submitted ). Then (c => c. Saga . Total = c. Message . Total ) . Send (c => new ReserveStock (c. Saga . CorrelationId )). TransitionTo ( Reserving )); During ( Reserving , When ( Reserved ). Send (c => new ChargePayment (c. Saga . CorrelationId , c. Saga . Total )). TransitionTo ( Charging )); During ( Charging , When ( Charged ). Publish (c => new OrderConfirmed (c. Saga . CorrelationId )). TransitionTo ( Completed ), When ( PaymentFailed ). Send (c => new ReleaseStock (c. Saga . CorrelationId )). Finalize ()); } } Notice this is exactly the State pattern from Design patterns , now coordinating across service boundaries rather than within one class — Reserving and Charging are states, and each Event(...) transition is the same idea as OrderState.Pay() returning a new state, just driven by messages instead of method calls. Alternatives for long-running orchestration: Azure Durable Functions , Temporal , Dapr Workflows , NServiceBus sagas."},{"c":"messaging-events","s":"messaging-libraries-for-net","h":"Messaging libraries for .NET","t":"# Messaging libraries for .NET Intermediate Using the raw broker SDKs ( Azure.Messaging.ServiceBus , RabbitMQ.Client , Confluent.Kafka ) is fine for simple cases. Libraries add the hard parts: serialization, topology, retries, the outbox/inbox, sagas, scheduling, test harnesses and OpenTelemetry. Library Notes MassTransit Most popular; supports RabbitMQ, Service Bus, Amazon SQS, Kafka; sagas; EF outbox. v9+ is commercial ; v8 remains open source NServiceBus (Particular) Commercial, very mature, excellent tooling (ServicePulse/ServiceInsight) Wolverine (JasperFx) Mediator plus messaging, with a durable outbox/inbox integrated with Marten/EF Core; MIT Rebus , Brighter Open-source alternatives Dapr pub/sub A sidecar abstraction over many brokers C# Run ▶ Copy // Raw Service Bus SDK (minimal) await using var client = new ServiceBusClient ( \"shop.servicebus.windows.net\" , new DefaultAzureCredential ()); await using var sender = client. CreateSender ( \"orders\" ); await sender. SendMessageAsync ( new ServiceBusMessage ( BinaryData . FromObjectAsJson ( new OrderPlaced (id, total))) { MessageId = id. ToString (), Subject = nameof ( OrderPlaced ), SessionId = customerId, CorrelationId = Activity . Current ?. TraceId . ToString () }); var processor = client. CreateProcessor ( \"orders\" , new ServiceBusProcessorOptions { MaxConcurrentCalls = 8 , AutoCompleteMessages = false }); processor. ProcessMessageAsync += async args => { var evt = args. Message . Body . ToObjectFromJson < OrderPlaced >(); await HandleAsync (evt, args. CancellationToken ); await args. CompleteMessageAsync (args. Message ); // ack only after successful processing }; processor. ProcessErrorAsync += args => { logger. LogError (args. Exception , \"Service Bus error\" ); return Task . CompletedTask ; }; await processor. StartProcessingAsync (); Notice AutoCompleteMessages = false and the explicit CompleteMessageAsync call only after HandleAsync succeeds — exactly the deliberate acknowledge-after-processing discipline that makes the \"crash before ack → redelivery\" failure mode from the delivery-guarantees section above a safe, expected occurrence rather than a lost message."},{"c":"messaging-events","s":"message-design-and-versioning","h":"Message design and versioning","t":"# Message design and versioning Advanced Contracts are APIs : version them carefully and publish them as a small shared contracts package or a schema (JSON Schema, Avro/Protobuf with a schema registry). Include metadata : MessageId , CorrelationId /trace context, CausationId , OccurredAt , type and version. CloudEvents is a standard envelope format. Evolve compatibly : add optional fields; never rename or remove fields that consumers use; for breaking changes, publish v2 alongside v1 until consumers migrate. Tolerant readers : consumers ignore unknown fields and handle missing optional ones. Keep messages small ; for big payloads use the claim-check pattern (store the blob, send its reference). Don't leak internal domain models: map domain events to stable integration events , the exact distinction DDD drew between what an aggregate raises internally and what actually crosses a service boundary."},{"c":"messaging-events","s":"event-sourcing","h":"Event sourcing","t":"# Event sourcing Advanced Instead of storing current state, store the full sequence of events that led to it. Current state is a left fold over the events — a fancy way of saying: start from a blank state and apply each event to it, in order, one at a time, the same way a bank statement's running balance is just \"start at 0, apply every transaction in order.\" Diagram Copy Stream \"account-42\": AccountOpened(€0) → Deposited(€100) → Withdrawn(€30) → Deposited(€50) ⇒ balance €120 Benefits Costs Complete audit trail and history (\"what did we know on 1 March?\") Different mental model; harder queries (needs CQRS projections) Rebuild read models or new projections from history Event schema evolution ( upcasting : converting an old event's saved shape into the current one when you read it back, since you can never change history itself) Natural fit with event-driven integration Snapshots needed for long streams (a saved current state at some point, so replay starts there instead of from event #1); eventual consistency of projections Debugging by replay Tooling and team experience required .NET options: Marten (event store on PostgreSQL), EventStoreDB/KurrentDB , or Cosmos DB with the change feed. Use event sourcing for domains where history is the value (finance, ledgers, compliance, workflows), not as a default — recall from CQRS & Result that it's the highest-complexity rung on that ladder for a reason. Q Why use messaging instead of direct HTTP calls between services? It decouples services in time (both needn't be up), levels load spikes through queues, isolates failures (a down consumer doesn't break the producer), enables pub/sub fan-out to new consumers without changing producers, and scales via competing consumers. The trade-offs are eventual consistency and operational complexity. Q What's the difference between a queue and a topic? A queue delivers each message to one consumer among competing consumers, which suits commands and work distribution. A topic delivers a copy to each subscription, which suits events consumed by multiple independent services. Q Commands vs events? A command asks one specific handler to do something (imperative name, can be rejected, sent to a queue). An event announces that something happened (past tense, can't be rejected, published to any number of subscribers). Q What is the outbox pattern and what problem does it solve? It solves the dual-write problem: you can't atomically commit to the database and publish to a broker. Writing the outgoing message to an outbox table in the same transaction as the business data, then having a relay publish it and mark it processed, guarantees the message is eventually published if and only if the transaction committed (at-least-once). Q How do you make a message consumer idempotent? Use naturally idempotent operations (upserts, setting state), or record processed message IDs in an inbox table in the same transaction as the handler's changes (with a unique key) and skip duplicates. Use business-key unique constraints and version checks, and pass idempotency keys to external providers for side effects outside your database. Q What delivery guarantee do brokers usually provide? At-least-once: messages may be delivered more than once (redelivery after a crash before ack, producer retries, lock expiry). Exactly-once end-to-end isn't generally achievable, so consumers must be idempotent to achieve effectively-once processing. Q What is a dead-letter queue? A secondary queue where messages are moved after exceeding max delivery attempts or failing permanently (poison messages), so they don't block processing. Monitor it, investigate, fix the cause and resubmit. Q What is a saga? Orchestration vs choreography? A saga coordinates a multi-service business transaction as a series of local transactions with compensating actions on failure. In choreography, services react to each other's events with no central controller (loosely coupled, but the flow is implicit). In orches"},{"c":"messaging-events","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Transactional outbox pattern (Azure Architecture Center) Saga distributed transactions pattern Azure Service Bus documentation Enterprise Integration Patterns (Hohpe & Woolf): the classic messaging pattern language. MassTransit documentation · Wolverine · Marten event sourcing"},{"c":"messaging-events","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run (plain console app, .NET 8/9, top-level statements, BCL only). Real brokers, real outboxes and real crash scenarios aren't runtime-verifiable in a standalone script — the checks below model the underlying logic (dedup, partitioning, folding) that those mechanisms rely on. C# Run ▶ Copy // Claim: an inbox table makes a duplicate delivery a no-op instead of double-applying the work. var inbox = new HashSet < Guid >(); int paymentsCharged = 0 ; void HandleOrderPlaced ( Guid messageId) { if (!inbox. Add (messageId)) return ; // already processed: this redelivery is a safe no-op paymentsCharged++; } var sameMessageId = Guid . NewGuid (); HandleOrderPlaced (sameMessageId); // first delivery HandleOrderPlaced (sameMessageId); // redelivery after a crash-before-ack if (paymentsCharged != 1 ) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected exactly 1 charge, got {paymentsCharged}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: an inbox of seen message IDs makes a redelivered message a no-op (idempotent consumer)\" ); // Claim: a queue's \"competing consumers\" split work so each message goes to exactly one consumer, none lost, none duplicated. var messages = Enumerable . Range ( 1 , 10 ). ToList (); var consumerAInbox = new List < int >(); var consumerBInbox = new List < int >(); for ( int i = 0 ; i < messages. Count ; i++) (i % 2 == 0 ? consumerAInbox : consumerBInbox). Add (messages[i]); // round-robin stands in for \"whoever's free next\" var allProcessed = consumerAInbox. Concat (consumerBInbox). OrderBy (x => x). ToList (); if (!allProcessed. SequenceEqual (messages)) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: every message should be processed exactly once across both consumers\" ); if (consumerAInbox. Intersect (consumerBInbox). Any ()) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: no message should go to both consumers\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: competing consumers on a queue split the work with no message lost or duplicated\" ); // Claim: with a per-key partition/session, messages for the SAME key stay in order even if the // overall arrival order across different keys is shuffled. var arrivalOrder = new [] { ( \"order-1\" , 1 ), ( \"order-2\" , 1 ), ( \"order-1\" , 2 ), ( \"order-2\" , 2 ), ( \"order-1\" , 3 ) }; var perKeyOrder = arrivalOrder . GroupBy (m => m. Item1 ) . ToDictionary (g => g. Key , g => g. Select (m => m. Item2 ). ToList ()); if (!perKeyOrder[ \"order-1\" ]. SequenceEqual ([ 1 , 2 , 3 ])) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: order-1's own events should stay in sequence 1,2,3\" ); if (!perKeyOrder[ \"order-2\" ]. SequenceEqual ([ 1 , 2 ])) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: order-2's own events should stay in sequence 1,2\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: partitioning by key preserves per-key order even though global order isn't guaranteed\" ); // Claim: event sourcing's \"current state\" is a left fold — start from a blank state, apply each event in order. decimal Balance ( IEnumerable <( string Kind , decimal Amount )> events) => events. Aggregate ( 0m , (balance, e) => e. Kind switch { \"Deposited\" => balance + e. Amount , \"Withdrawn\" => balance - e. Amount , _ => balance }); var accountEvents = new [] { ( \"Opened\" , 0m ), ( \"Deposited\" , 100m ), ( \"Withdrawn\" , 30m ), ( \"Deposited\" , 50m ) }; var finalBalance = Balance (accountEvents); if (finalBalance != 120m ) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected balance 120, got {finalBalance}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: replaying events as a left fold (start at 0, apply each in order) gives the correct balance ({finalBalance})\" );"},{"c":"resilience","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR In the cloud, failures are normal , not exceptional — design for them instead of hoping they won't happen. Match the strategy to the fault: retry transient errors, circuit-break prolonged outages, don't retry permanent errors (4xx), timeout slow calls. Backoff + jitter on retries, and retry at one layer only — otherwise retries multiply into a retry storm. Only retry idempotent operations, or non-idempotent ones with an idempotency key — a blind retry of a POST can double-charge a customer. Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Resilience 's .AddStandardResilienceHandler() gives you timeout + retry + circuit breaker for HttpClient calls with one line. Liveness checks \"is the process alive?\"; readiness checks \"can it serve traffic right now?\" — don't confuse the two, or a dependency blip restarts your whole fleet."},{"c":"resilience","s":"everything-fails","h":"Everything fails","t":"# Everything fails Beginner In the cloud, failure is normal : instances restart during deployments, networks blip, load balancers move connections, databases fail over, dependencies throttle you (429), and noisy neighbours cause latency spikes. A resilient system expects these and keeps working, perhaps degraded, instead of turning a 2-second blip into an outage. Fault type Examples Response Transient Timeouts, connection resets, 503, 429, 408, deadlock victims, SQL failover errors Retry (with backoff), wait for recovery Prolonged A dependency down for minutes Stop calling (circuit breaker), fallback , degrade gracefully Permanent 400 Bad Request, 401/403, 404, validation errors, bugs Don't retry : fail fast, surface the error Slowness Dependency at 10 s instead of 100 ms Timeout , limit concurrency, hedge Notice the table's shape: each fault type gets a different response, and the rest of this chapter is really a toolbox of strategies, each matched to one row."},{"c":"resilience","s":"the-resilience-strategies","h":"The resilience strategies","t":"# The resilience strategies Beginner Strategy Problem it solves How Timeout A slow call holds threads, sockets and the user's patience indefinitely Give up after N; cancel the operation Retry Transient faults that succeed on a second try Try again, with exponential backoff and jitter , a limited number of times Circuit breaker Hammering a failing dependency makes it worse and wastes your resources After a failure threshold, fail fast for a break period, then probe Fallback The user should get something when a dependency fails Return a cached, default or degraded value Hedging Tail latency: some requests are slow for no reason Send a second request after a delay and use whichever responds first Rate limiter / concurrency limiter Protect yourself or a dependency from overload Cap requests per period or in flight Bulkhead One failing dependency exhausting shared resources (threads, connections) Isolate resources per dependency (separate pools/limits)"},{"c":"resilience","s":"retry-done-properly","h":"Retry, done properly","t":"# Retry, done properly Intermediate Diagram Copy Attempt 1 ── fail ──▶ wait 200ms ±jitter ──▶ Attempt 2 ── fail ──▶ wait 400ms ±jitter ──▶ Attempt 3 ── fail ──▶ wait 800ms ──▶ Attempt 4 → give up Exponential backoff gives the dependency time to recover. Jitter (randomising delays) prevents thousands of clients retrying in lockstep , the \"thundering herd\" that hits a recovering service in synchronised waves — the exact same stampede problem Caching, rate limiting & CORS described for expiring cache keys, here showing up as retries instead. Limit attempts (2–5) and the total time budget. Retry only transient faults : network exceptions, 5xx (especially 502/503/504), 408, 429. Respect Retry-After headers. Retry only idempotent operations (GET, PUT, DELETE), or non-idempotent ones with idempotency keys ( API design ). A retried POST without a key can charge a card twice. Retry storms and retry amplification If every layer retries 3 times (gateway → API → service → database), one failure becomes 3 × 3 × 3 = 27 attempts on the bottom layer, right when it's struggling. Retry at one layer (usually closest to the failing dependency), keep counts low, combine with circuit breakers, and set timeout budgets that shrink as calls go deeper."},{"c":"resilience","s":"circuit-breaker","h":"Circuit breaker","t":"# Circuit breaker Intermediate Diagram Copy failure ratio > threshold within the sampling window ┌────────┐ ─────────────────────────────────────────────▶ ┌────────┐ │ CLOSED │ │ OPEN │ calls fail immediately │ normal │ ◀──── trial call succeeds ─── ┌───────────┐ ◀─── │ (fast) │ (BrokenCircuitException) └────────┘ │ HALF-OPEN │ └────────┘ │ one probe │ ── trial fails ──▶ back to OPEN └───────────┘ after the break duration elapses Why bother: waiting for every call to a down dependency to time out wastes threads and connections, adds latency for your users, and piles more load onto a service that's already struggling — the opposite of what it needs to recover. The breaker fails fast instead (so you can fall back immediately) and gives the dependency a quiet window to recover. The OPEN state is deliberate rest, not a malfunction. Tune: the failure ratio (e.g. 50%), the minimum throughput before evaluating (to avoid tripping on 2 of 3 calls), the sampling window, and the break duration. Log state changes and expose them as metrics — a circuit flipping open is exactly the kind of event worth paging someone over."},{"c":"resilience","s":"timeouts","h":"Timeouts","t":"# Timeouts Intermediate Two levels, and conflating them is a common mistake: Per-attempt timeout : how long one try may take (e.g. 2 s). Total timeout : the overall budget including retries (e.g. 10 s), which should fit inside the caller's own deadline. Always pass CancellationToken s so timeouts actually cancel the underlying work (EF Core commands, HTTP requests) — a timeout that only stops waiting for the result, without actually cancelling the work, still leaves the dependency doing pointless work for a caller that's already given up. And remember HttpClient.Timeout defaults to 100 seconds, far too long for most calls, exactly as Calling other services warned; with a resilience pipeline, let the pipeline own timeouts instead."},{"c":"resilience","s":"polly-v8-and-microsoft-extensions-resilience","h":"Polly v8 and Microsoft.Extensions.Resilience","t":"# Polly v8 and Microsoft.Extensions.Resilience Intermediate Polly is .NET's resilience library. v8 introduced resilience pipelines with strategies composed in order — notice the word \"composed,\" because the ordering section below matters as much as which strategies you pick. Microsoft builds on it with Microsoft.Extensions.Resilience (DI integration, telemetry) and Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Resilience (HttpClient integration). # The standard HTTP handler C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddHttpClient < CatalogClient >(c => c. BaseAddress = new ( \"https://catalog\" )) . AddStandardResilienceHandler (o => { o. Retry . MaxRetryAttempts = 3 ; o. AttemptTimeout . Timeout = TimeSpan . FromSeconds ( 2 ); o. TotalRequestTimeout . Timeout = TimeSpan . FromSeconds ( 10 ); o. CircuitBreaker . FailureRatio = 0.5 ; o. CircuitBreaker . SamplingDuration = TimeSpan . FromSeconds ( 30 ); // must be ≥ 2× attempt timeout }); The standard handler stacks, from outermost to innermost: Diagram Copy Rate limiter (concurrency) → Total request timeout → Retry → Circuit breaker → Attempt timeout → HTTP call Defaults retry transient HTTP failures (5xx, 408, 429, HttpRequestException , timeouts) with exponential backoff and jitter, honouring Retry-After . Also available: AddStandardHedgingHandler() , which sends parallel attempts (optionally to different endpoints) for latency-critical reads — the hedging strategy from the strategies table, now as a drop-in handler. # Custom pipelines C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddResiliencePipeline ( \"inventory-db\" , pipeline => pipeline . AddTimeout ( TimeSpan . FromSeconds ( 5 )) . AddRetry ( new RetryStrategyOptions { ShouldHandle = new PredicateBuilder (). Handle < SqlException >(ex => ex. IsTransient ). Handle < TimeoutException >(), MaxRetryAttempts = 3 , BackoffType = DelayBackoffType . Exponential , UseJitter = true , Delay = TimeSpan . FromMilliseconds ( 200 ), OnRetry = args => { Log . Warning ( \"Retry {Attempt} after {Delay}\" , args. AttemptNumber , args. RetryDelay ); return default ; } }) . AddCircuitBreaker ( new CircuitBreakerStrategyOptions { FailureRatio = 0.5 , MinimumThroughput = 20 , SamplingDuration = TimeSpan . FromSeconds ( 30 ), BreakDuration = TimeSpan . FromSeconds ( 15 ) })); public sealed class StockReader ( [FromKeyedServices(\"inventory-db\")] ResiliencePipeline pipeline, IStockRepository repo) { public ValueTask < int > GetAsync ( string sku, CancellationToken ct) => pipeline. ExecuteAsync ( async token => await repo. GetQuantityAsync (sku, token), ct); } // Typed result with fallback var withFallback = new ResiliencePipelineBuilder < decimal ?>() . AddFallback ( new FallbackStrategyOptions < decimal ?> { ShouldHandle = new PredicateBuilder < decimal ?>(). Handle < BrokenCircuitException >(). Handle < TimeoutRejectedException >(), FallbackAction = _ => Outcome . FromResultAsValueTask < decimal ?>( null ) // e.g. \"price unavailable\" }) . AddTimeout ( TimeSpan . FromSeconds ( 1 )) . Build (); # Strategy order matters Strategies execute outermost first — the exact same nested-wrapping idea from Middleware , now composing fault-handling strategies instead of HTTP concerns. Typical rules: A total timeout outside the retry bounds the whole operation; an attempt timeout inside the retry bounds each try. A circuit breaker inside the retry means each attempt is counted, and when the circuit is open, retries can stop quickly (handle BrokenCircuitException as non-retryable or with a delay). Fallback outermost , so it catches everything that fails below it. # Older Polly syntax Pre-v8 code looks like Policy.Handle<HttpRequestException>().WaitAndRetryAsync(...) , Policy.WrapAsync(...) and AddPolicyHandler(...) (Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Polly, now deprecated in favour of Http.Resilience). Same concepts, different API."},{"c":"resilience","s":"resilience-beyond-http","h":"Resilience beyond HTTP","t":"# Resilience beyond HTTP Intermediate Dependency Built-in resilience EF Core / SQL EnableRetryOnFailure() execution strategy (wrap manual transactions with CreateExecutionStrategy ) Azure SDK clients Built-in retry policies ( ClientOptions.Retry : exponential, max retries, network timeout) Service Bus / brokers Delivery retries, delayed redelivery, dead-lettering ( Messaging ) Redis (StackExchange.Redis) Reconnect/backoff, AbortOnConnectFail = false gRPC Built-in retry and hedging policies in the channel's service config, or the resilience handler"},{"c":"resilience","s":"designing-for-graceful-degradation","h":"Designing for graceful degradation","t":"# Designing for graceful degradation Advanced Resilience isn't only about libraries. Design so partial failure produces a degraded but useful experience, rather than an outright outage: Fallbacks: show cached recommendations, hide the reviews widget, use the last known exchange rate (and say so). Asynchronous by default: accept the order and process payment via a queue, so checkout works while Payments is down — the \"fault isolation\" benefit Messaging & events described, applied as a deliberate resilience tactic rather than just an architectural nicety. Bulkheads: separate HttpClient, connection pool and concurrency limits per dependency, so a slow reporting service can't starve checkout. Separate deployments for critical and non-critical workloads. Load shedding: reject excess work early with 429/503 (rate limiter, concurrency limiter) instead of collapsing under it. Static stability: keep working with the last known good configuration or data if the control plane (config service, identity provider metadata) is unavailable. Timeouts everywhere , with budgets that decrease along the call chain. Idempotency everywhere , so retries are safe."},{"c":"resilience","s":"health-checks","h":"Health checks","t":"# Health checks Intermediate Health endpoints let orchestrators and load balancers route around unhealthy instances. C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddHealthChecks () . AddCheck ( \"self\" , () => HealthCheckResult . Healthy (), tags: [ \"live\" ]) . AddSqlServer (cs, name: \"sql\" , tags: [ \"ready\" ]) // AspNetCore.HealthChecks.* packages . AddRedis (redisCs, name: \"redis\" , tags: [ \"ready\" ]) . AddAzureServiceBusQueue (sbNamespace, \"orders\" , new DefaultAzureCredential (), tags: [ \"ready\" ]); app. MapHealthChecks ( \"/health/live\" , new () { Predicate = r => r. Tags . Contains ( \"live\" ) }); // is the process OK? (restart if not) app. MapHealthChecks ( \"/health/ready\" , new () { Predicate = r => r. Tags . Contains ( \"ready\" ) }); // can it serve traffic? (remove from LB if not) Don't put dependencies in liveness checks If liveness checks the database and the database blips, the orchestrator restarts every instance , turning a dependency hiccup into a full outage. Liveness = \"the process isn't stuck\". Readiness = \"dependencies are reachable, warm-up is done\"."},{"c":"resilience","s":"chaos-engineering","h":"Chaos engineering","t":"# Chaos engineering Advanced Verify resilience by injecting faults on purpose : latency, exceptions, error responses, killed instances. It's the only reliable way to know the strategies above actually work the way you designed them — rather than hoping they do and finding out during a real outage. Polly chaos strategies (v8.3+): AddChaosLatency , AddChaosFault , AddChaosOutcome and AddChaosBehavior in pipelines, enabled by configuration in test environments. Azure Chaos Studio : platform-level faults (VM shutdown, network latency, AKS pod chaos). Game days: planned exercises where the team breaks things in staging and observes alerts, dashboards and recovery. Q What is the difference between transient and non-transient faults? Transient faults are temporary and likely to succeed on retry: timeouts, connection resets, 503/429, SQL failovers, deadlocks. Non-transient faults won't fix themselves (400, 401/403, 404, validation errors, bugs), so retrying wastes resources and they should fail fast. Q Why use exponential backoff with jitter for retries? Backoff spaces retries out so a struggling dependency has time to recover. Jitter randomises the delays so many clients don't retry simultaneously in synchronised waves (thundering herd) that keep the dependency overloaded. Q How does a circuit breaker work? Closed: calls flow and failures are tracked. When the failure ratio exceeds a threshold (with a minimum throughput) within a sampling window, it opens: calls fail immediately for a break duration. Then it goes half-open and allows a trial call; success closes it, failure re-opens it. It prevents wasting resources on, and piling load onto, a failing dependency. Q When is it unsafe to retry a request? When the operation isn't idempotent (e.g. POST creating a payment) and has no idempotency key, because the original may have succeeded despite the error and a retry would duplicate it. Also for permanent errors like 400 or 401. Q What is a retry storm and how do you avoid it? Retries at multiple layers multiplying load on a failing dependency (3 retries at 3 layers = 27 attempts), worsening the outage. Avoid it by retrying at one layer only, keeping attempt counts low, using jitter, adding circuit breakers, and propagating timeout budgets and cancellation. Q How do you add resilience to HttpClient in modern .NET? Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Resilience: .AddStandardResilienceHandler() on the IHttpClientFactory registration gives a rate limiter, total timeout, retries with backoff and jitter, a circuit breaker and per-attempt timeouts with sensible defaults. Use AddResilienceHandler for a custom Polly v8 pipeline, or AddStandardHedgingHandler for hedging. Q What is the bulkhead pattern? Isolating resources (connection pools, thread or concurrency limits, separate instances) per dependency or workload, so a failure or slowdown in one can't exhaust resources needed by others, like watertight compartments in a ship. Q Liveness vs readiness health checks? Liveness tells the orchestrator whether the process is healthy or must be restarted, and should not depend on external services. Readiness tells the load balancer whether the instance can currently serve traffic (dependencies reachable, warmed up); failing readiness removes it from rotation without restarting it."},{"c":"resilience","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Introduction to resilient app development (.NET) Build resilient HTTP apps Polly documentation Health checks in ASP.NET Core Michael Nygard, Release It! (2nd ed.): the book that popularised circuit breakers and bulkheads. Azure Well-Architected Framework: Reliability"},{"c":"resilience","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with: dotnet new console -o resiliencecheck && cd resiliencecheck && dotnet add package Polly.Core , then replace Program.cs with the code below and dotnet run . This uses real Polly v8 pipelines — the chapter's actual subject — not a simulation. C# Run ▶ Copy using Polly ; using Polly . CircuitBreaker ; using Polly . Retry ; // 1) Retry: succeeds on the 3rd attempt after two transient failures. int attempt = 0 ; var retryPipeline = new ResiliencePipelineBuilder (). AddRetry ( new RetryStrategyOptions { ShouldHandle = new PredicateBuilder (). Handle < InvalidOperationException >(), MaxRetryAttempts = 3 , Delay = TimeSpan . FromMilliseconds ( 10 ), BackoffType = DelayBackoffType . Constant }). Build (); string result = await retryPipeline. ExecuteAsync ( async _ => { attempt++; if (attempt < 3 ) throw new InvalidOperationException ( \"transient\" ); return await ValueTask . FromResult ( \"ok\" ); }); if (result != \"ok\" || attempt != 3 ) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected success on attempt 3, got result={result} attempt={attempt}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: retry pipeline succeeded on attempt {attempt} after transient failures, as the backoff-and-retry section describes.\" ); // 2) Retry gives up (and rethrows) once MaxRetryAttempts is exhausted — it doesn't retry forever. int alwaysFailAttempts = 0 ; try { await retryPipeline. ExecuteAsync < string >(_ => { alwaysFailAttempts++; throw new InvalidOperationException ( \"still failing\" ); }); throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected the pipeline to eventually rethrow\" ); } catch ( InvalidOperationException ) { if (alwaysFailAttempts != 4 ) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected 4 total attempts (1 + 3 retries), got {alwaysFailAttempts}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: retry stopped after {alwaysFailAttempts} attempts (1 original + MaxRetryAttempts=3) and surfaced the failure, instead of retrying forever.\" ); } // 3) Circuit breaker: opens after the failure threshold, then fails fast (BrokenCircuitException) // without even calling the inner action — \"fail fast instead of waiting for every call to time out\". int innerCallsAfterOpen = 0 ; var breakerPipeline = new ResiliencePipelineBuilder (). AddCircuitBreaker ( new CircuitBreakerStrategyOptions { FailureRatio = 0.5 , MinimumThroughput = 2 , SamplingDuration = TimeSpan . FromSeconds ( 10 ), BreakDuration = TimeSpan . FromSeconds ( 5 ), ShouldHandle = new PredicateBuilder (). Handle < InvalidOperationException >() }). Build (); for ( int i = 0 ; i < 2 ; i++) { try { await breakerPipeline. ExecuteAsync < string >(_ => throw new InvalidOperationException ( \"dependency down\" )); } catch ( InvalidOperationException ) { /* expected: feeding failures to trip the breaker */ } } try { await breakerPipeline. ExecuteAsync < string >(_ => { innerCallsAfterOpen++; return ValueTask . FromResult ( \"should not run\" ); }); throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected BrokenCircuitException once the circuit is open\" ); } catch ( BrokenCircuitException ) { if (innerCallsAfterOpen != 0 ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: the inner action ran even though the circuit was open\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: once open, the circuit breaker rejected the call immediately without invoking the dependency at all.\" ); } // Claims NOT verified here (need real network faults, multiple processes, or a running orchestrator): // - Jitter actually preventing a thundering herd across many real clients — see Further reading: https://www.pollydocs.org/ // - The standard HTTP resilience handler's exact strategy stacking over a real HttpClient — see Further reading: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/resilience/http-resilience // - Liveness/readiness health check behavior under a real orchestrator (Kubernetes/App Service) — see Further reading: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/host-and-deploy/health-checks Console . WriteLine ( \"All runtime-checkable claims verified.\" );"},{"c":"observability-aspire","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Monitoring answers questions you thought to ask ahead of time (dashboards, alerts); observability lets you answer questions you didn't anticipate, from the data your system already emits. The three signals — logs (what happened), metrics (how much/how often, cheap to query), traces (where did the time go for this request) — are most useful linked together by a shared TraceId . .NET's built-in APIs ( ILogger , Meter , ActivitySource ) are already OpenTelemetry-shaped; the OpenTelemetry SDK just listens to them and exports to whatever backend you choose (Azure Monitor, Grafana, Jaeger, …). A trace is one TraceId shared across every span (unit of work) in a request, even across service boundaries — the W3C traceparent header is how that ID survives an HTTP call to another service. Keep metric tags low-cardinality (status code, route) — a tag like userId or orderId creates a new time series per value and can blow up cost; put high-cardinality IDs on traces and logs instead. .NET Aspire is a development/deployment toolkit (an AppHost to run your whole system locally with one command, shared service defaults, a local dashboard) — it is not something you ship as a production runtime."},{"c":"observability-aspire","s":"monitoring-vs-observability-knowing-the-answer-vs-being-able","h":"Monitoring vs observability: knowing the answer vs being able to find it","t":"# Monitoring vs observability: knowing the answer vs being able to find it Beginner Monitoring answers questions you knew to ask in advance : is CPU above 80%? Is the error rate above 1%? It drives dashboards and alerts. Observability is the ability to answer new questions about the system's internal state from its outputs, without shipping new code: \"Why are checkout requests from mobile users in Germany slow since 14:05?\" In a distributed system, where one click touches 8 services, observability isn't optional — nobody could have pre-built a dashboard for every possible question an incident might raise, and that's exactly the gap observability closes. Without it, every incident turns into hours of guesswork."},{"c":"observability-aspire","s":"the-three-signals","h":"The three signals","t":"# The three signals Beginner Signal What it is Answers Example Logs Timestamped records of discrete events, ideally structured What happened, in detail? Payment declined for order 1042: insufficient_funds Metrics Numeric measurements aggregated over time (cheap to store, fast to query) How much, how often, how fast, trending? http.server.request.duration p95 = 420 ms; 12 errors/s Traces The path of one request through the system: a tree of timed spans Where did the time go? Which service failed? Checkout 1.8 s = API 30 ms → Orders 1.6 s → SQL 1.5 s Plus, increasingly, profiles (continuous CPU and memory profiling) and events/exceptions . They're most powerful correlated : a metric spike → exemplar traces from that spike → the logs of those traces, all linked by TraceId . Each signal alone answers a narrower question than the chain of all three together — a metric tells you that something's wrong, a trace tells you where , and the logs for that trace tell you why ."},{"c":"observability-aspire","s":"how-net-emits-telemetry","h":"How .NET emits telemetry","t":"# How .NET emits telemetry Intermediate .NET has vendor-neutral APIs built into the BCL . Libraries emit telemetry through them, and you choose where it goes — the emitting code never needs to know or care which backend eventually receives it. Signal .NET API Built-in sources Logs ILogger (Microsoft.Extensions.Logging) ASP.NET Core, EF Core, HttpClient, your code Metrics System.Diagnostics.Metrics : Meter , Counter<T> , Histogram<T> , UpDownCounter<T> , ObservableGauge<T> Microsoft.AspNetCore.Hosting (request duration), System.Net.Http , System.Runtime (GC, thread pool, exceptions), Kestrel, EF Core Traces System.Diagnostics.ActivitySource / Activity (= an OpenTelemetry span) ASP.NET Core (incoming requests), HttpClient (outgoing), SqlClient, EF Core, Azure SDKs, gRPC, MassTransit OpenTelemetry (OTel) is the CNCF standard for collecting and exporting telemetry. The OpenTelemetry .NET SDK listens to those built-in APIs and exports via OTLP (the OpenTelemetry Protocol) to any backend: Azure Monitor/Application Insights, Grafana (Tempo, Loki, Prometheus/Mimir), Jaeger, Zipkin, Datadog, Honeycomb, New Relic, Elastic, or the Aspire dashboard. Diagram Copy Your code + libraries ──(ILogger · Meter · ActivitySource)──▶ OpenTelemetry SDK ──OTLP──▶ Collector (optional) ──▶ backend(s) processors, sampling batching, filtering, routing"},{"c":"observability-aspire","s":"setting-up-opentelemetry","h":"Setting up OpenTelemetry","t":"# Setting up OpenTelemetry Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddOpenTelemetry () . ConfigureResource (r => r . AddService (serviceName: \"shop-api\" , serviceVersion: \"1.4.2\" ) . AddAttributes ([ new ( \"deployment.environment\" , builder. Environment . EnvironmentName )])) . WithTracing (t => t . AddAspNetCoreInstrumentation (o => o. Filter = ctx => !ctx. Request . Path . StartsWithSegments ( \"/health\" )) . AddHttpClientInstrumentation () . AddEntityFrameworkCoreInstrumentation () // or AddSqlClientInstrumentation . AddSource ( \"Shop.*\" ) // your own ActivitySources . AddSource ( \"MassTransit\" ) . AddOtlpExporter ()) . WithMetrics (m => m . AddAspNetCoreInstrumentation () . AddHttpClientInstrumentation () . AddRuntimeInstrumentation () // GC, thread pool, exceptions, JIT . AddMeter ( \"Shop.*\" ) . AddOtlpExporter ()) . WithLogging (l => l. AddOtlpExporter ()); // ILogger → OTLP, with TraceId/SpanId attached // Or, for Azure: the Azure Monitor OpenTelemetry distro does all of the above and exports to Application Insights // builder.Services.AddOpenTelemetry().UseAzureMonitor(); // connection string from APPLICATIONINSIGHTS_CONNECTION_STRING Exporter endpoints come from standard environment variables: OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT , OTEL_SERVICE_NAME , OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES ."},{"c":"observability-aspire","s":"distributed-tracing","h":"Distributed tracing","t":"# Distributed tracing Intermediate A trace is identified by a TraceId shared by every span in it. Each span has a SpanId, a parent SpanId, a name, a start and end time, attributes (tags), events and a status . Diagram Copy TraceId 4bf92f35… 0ms ───────────────────────────── 1820ms └─ POST /api/orders shop-api [██████████████████████████████████████████████████] ├─ SELECT Customers… shop-api/sql [██] ├─ POST http://pricing/quote shop-api→pricing [████████] │ └─ GET /quote pricing-svc [███████] │ └─ redis GET price:KB-01 pricing-svc [█] ├─ INSERT Orders; INSERT Outbox shop-api/sql [██████████████████████████████████] ← the slow part └─ publish OrderPlaced shop-api/servicebus [█] └─ consume OrderPlaced billing-svc (async, linked) [████] Notice exactly what this tree makes obvious at a glance: of the 1.8-second checkout, the vast majority is one single SQL span — not the pricing call, not the message publish. Without a trace like this, \"checkout is slow\" would have sent you investigating five different services before finding the one that actually mattered. Context propagation carries the trace across process boundaries using the W3C Trace Context header: HTTP Copy traceparent : 00 -4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736-00f067aa0ba902b7- 01 version-traceid-parentspanid-flags(sampled) tracestate : vendor-specific data baggage : tenant =acme, userTier =gold (optional key/values propagated to downstream services) ASP.NET Core reads it on incoming requests; HttpClient and gRPC write it on outgoing calls automatically; messaging libraries put it in message headers. Activity.Current flows through async calls (via AsyncLocal ) — the exact same ambient-flow mechanism Errors & logging relied on for log scopes — so logs written anywhere during a request carry its TraceId and SpanId automatically, with no manual plumbing. # Sampling Recording every span of every request is expensive at scale. Head sampling decides at the start (e.g. keep 10%, TraceIdRatioBasedSampler ); tail sampling (in the OpenTelemetry Collector) decides after the trace completes, which lets you keep all errors and slow traces and sample the rest — precisely the traces you'd actually want during an incident, kept disproportionately more often than the boring, fast, successful ones. Always keep metrics unsampled: they're aggregates, and sampling an aggregate just makes it a less accurate aggregate for no real cost saving."},{"c":"observability-aspire","s":"custom-spans-and-metrics","h":"Custom spans and metrics","t":"# Custom spans and metrics Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class CheckoutTelemetry { public static readonly ActivitySource Source = new ( \"Shop.Checkout\" , \"1.0\" ); private static readonly Meter Meter = new ( \"Shop.Checkout\" , \"1.0\" ); public static readonly Counter < long > OrdersPlaced = Meter . CreateCounter < long >( \"shop.orders.placed\" , unit: \"{order}\" , description: \"Orders successfully placed\" ); public static readonly Histogram < double > OrderValue = Meter . CreateHistogram < double >( \"shop.order.value\" , unit: \"EUR\" ); public static readonly UpDownCounter < int > ActiveCheckouts = Meter . CreateUpDownCounter < int >( \"shop.checkouts.active\" ); } public async Task < Result < OrderId >> CheckoutAsync ( Cart cart, CancellationToken ct) { using var activity = CheckoutTelemetry . Source . StartActivity ( \"checkout\" ); // null if nobody is listening: cheap activity?. SetTag ( \"cart.items\" , cart. Lines . Count ); activity?. SetTag ( \"customer.tier\" , cart. CustomerTier ); CheckoutTelemetry . ActiveCheckouts . Add ( 1 ); try { var result = await PlaceOrderAsync (cart, ct); if (result. IsSuccess ) { CheckoutTelemetry . OrdersPlaced . Add ( 1 , new KeyValuePair < string , object ?>( \"payment.method\" , cart. PaymentMethod )); CheckoutTelemetry . OrderValue . Record (( double )cart. Total . Amount ); } else activity?. SetStatus ( ActivityStatusCode . Error , result. Error . Code ); return result; } catch ( Exception ex) { activity?. AddException (ex); activity?. SetStatus ( ActivityStatusCode . Error ); throw ; } finally { CheckoutTelemetry . ActiveCheckouts . Add (- 1 ); } } Instrument Use for Counter<T> Monotonic counts: requests, orders, errors UpDownCounter<T> Values that go up and down: queue depth, active connections Histogram<T> Distributions: durations, sizes, amounts (gives percentiles) ObservableGauge<T> Sampled current values via a callback: cache size, pool usage Cardinality Every unique combination of tag values creates a new time series. Tagging a metric with userId , orderId or full URLs creates millions of series and huge bills or crashed backends. Use low-cardinality tags on metrics (status code, route template, payment method, region). Put high-cardinality data (IDs) on traces and logs . Notice CheckoutTelemetry.OrdersPlaced tags by payment.method — a handful of distinct values — rather than, say, customer.id , which would create one time series per customer and quietly violate exactly the cardinality rule above. Follow the OpenTelemetry semantic conventions for names and attributes ( http.request.method , http.response.status_code , db.system , messaging.system ), so backends can build standard views."},{"c":"observability-aspire","s":"what-to-measure-red-use-and-the-golden-signals","h":"What to measure: RED, USE and the golden signals","t":"# What to measure: RED, USE and the golden signals Intermediate Method For Measure RED Request-driven services (APIs) R ate (requests/s), E rrors (failed/s or %), D uration (latency percentiles) USE Resources (CPU, memory, pools, queues) U tilisation, S aturation (queueing), E rrors Four golden signals (Google SRE) Any service Latency, traffic, errors, saturation Use percentiles (p50, p95, p99) , not averages: an average of 100 ms can hide 5% of users waiting 4 seconds — the average is dragged down by the many fast requests while saying nothing about the tail that's actually painful for real users. For .NET specifically, also watch GC pause time and heap size , thread pool queue length and thread count (starvation; see async/await ), exception rate , connection pool usage , and outbox/queue lag ."},{"c":"observability-aspire","s":"alerting-and-slos","h":"Alerting and SLOs","t":"# Alerting and SLOs Advanced SLI (indicator): a measured ratio, e.g. \"share of checkout requests completing in under 1 s with a 2xx/4xx status\". SLO (objective): the target, e.g. \"99.5% over 30 days\". Error budget : the 0.5% you're allowed to miss. When it's spent, prioritise reliability over features. Alert on symptoms users feel (SLO burn rate, error rate, latency), not on every cause (one CPU spike). Every page should be actionable , and link to a dashboard and a runbook."},{"c":"observability-aspire","s":"application-insights-and-azure-monitor","h":"Application Insights and Azure Monitor","t":"# Application Insights and Azure Monitor Intermediate Azure Monitor is Azure's observability platform; Application Insights is its APM feature for applications; Log Analytics workspaces store the data, queried with KQL (Kusto Query Language). Diagram Copy // Slowest endpoints in the last hour (KQL) requests | where timestamp > ago(1h) | summarize count(), p95 = percentile(duration, 95), failures = countif(success == false) by name | order by p95 desc // Everything for one trace union requests, dependencies, traces, exceptions | where operation_Id == \"4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736\" | order by timestamp asc Features: the application map (auto-discovered dependency topology), transaction search and end-to-end transaction views, live metrics , failures and performance blades, availability tests (synthetic pings), smart detection, the profiler and snapshot debugger, workbooks and alerts. Use the Azure Monitor OpenTelemetry Distro ( UseAzureMonitor() ) in new code; the classic Application Insights SDK is in maintenance."},{"c":"observability-aspire","s":"net-aspire","h":".NET Aspire","t":"# .NET Aspire Intermediate .NET Aspire is an opinionated toolkit for building observable, production-ready distributed apps . It covers three things: # 1. Orchestration: the AppHost A C# project that describes your whole system and runs it locally with one command — a single source of truth for \"what does this system consist of,\" instead of a docker-compose file, a README, and tribal knowledge about which environment variables to set: C# Run ▶ Copy // Shop.AppHost/Program.cs var builder = DistributedApplication . CreateBuilder (args); var sql = builder. AddSqlServer ( \"sql\" ). WithDataVolume (). AddDatabase ( \"shopdb\" ); var redis = builder. AddRedis ( \"cache\" ). WithRedisInsight (); var bus = builder. AddRabbitMQ ( \"bus\" ). WithManagementPlugin (); // or AddAzureServiceBus(...).RunAsEmulator() var catalog = builder. AddProject < Projects . Shop_Catalog >( \"catalog\" ) . WithReference (sql). WithReference (redis). WaitFor (sql); var api = builder. AddProject < Projects . Shop_Api >( \"api\" ) . WithReference (sql). WithReference (redis). WithReference (bus). WithReference (catalog) // injects connection strings + service discovery . WithExternalHttpEndpoints () . WaitFor (sql); builder. AddProject < Projects . Shop_Worker >( \"worker\" ). WithReference (bus). WithReference (sql); builder. AddNpmApp ( \"web\" , \"../shop-web\" ). WithReference (api). WithHttpEndpoint (env: \"PORT\" ); builder. Build (). Run (); dotnet run --project Shop.AppHost starts the containers (SQL, Redis, RabbitMQ), all the projects and the front end, wires connection strings and service URLs as configuration/environment variables, and opens the dashboard . # 2. Service defaults A shared ServiceDefaults project that every service calls with builder.AddServiceDefaults() . It configures, in one place: OpenTelemetry (logs, metrics, traces with OTLP export), health checks ( /health , /alive ), service discovery , and HttpClient resilience defaults — notice this is, concretely, everything the rest of this chapter and Resilience described, pre-wired consistently across every service in the solution rather than configured once per project and inevitably drifting out of sync. # 3. Integrations (formerly \"components\") NuGet packages that register a client with health checks, telemetry, retries and configuration already set up: builder.AddSqlServerDbContext<ShopDb>(\"shopdb\") , builder.AddRedisDistributedCache(\"cache\") , builder.AddAzureServiceBusClient(\"bus\") , builder.AddNpgsqlDataSource(\"db\") , and many more. # The dashboard A local (or standalone) web UI showing every resource's state, console logs , structured logs , traces and metrics via OTLP, which is distributed tracing on your laptop from day one. It's also available as a standalone container for any OTLP-emitting app. # Deployment Aspire's app model can be published : azd up (Azure Developer CLI) provisions Azure Container Apps plus the backing services, and community and Microsoft tooling generates Kubernetes manifests, Docker Compose and Bicep. Aspire is development-time and deployment tooling , not a runtime you need in production. Why Aspire matters Before Aspire, running \"API + worker + SQL + Redis + RabbitMQ + front end\" locally meant docker-compose files, scripts, duplicated connection strings and no tracing. Aspire makes that the default developer experience, and bakes observability and resilience best practices into every service from the start."},{"c":"observability-aspire","s":"diagnosing-a-production-issue-a-workflow","h":"Diagnosing a production issue: a workflow","t":"# Diagnosing a production issue: a workflow Advanced Alert fires (an SLO burn rate or error rate on checkout). Dashboard : which endpoint, since when, and does it correlate with a deployment (annotations) or traffic? Exemplar traces of failing or slow requests: which span is red or long, and which service and dependency? Logs for that TraceId : the exception and context. Runtime metrics of the affected service: GC, thread pool starvation, CPU, connection pool. Mitigate first (roll back, scale out, fail over, toggle a feature flag), then root cause , and a blameless post-mortem. Add the missing signal or alert you wished you'd had. Notice how directly this seven-step workflow walks back through the three signals, in exactly the order the \"correlated\" point at the top of this chapter promised: a metric (step 1–2) points you to traces (step 3), which point you to logs (step 4), which point you to the actual fix. Q What are the three pillars of observability? Logs (detailed records of discrete events), metrics (numeric aggregates over time, cheap and good for dashboards and alerts) and traces (the end-to-end path of a single request across services as a tree of timed spans). They're most useful correlated through the trace ID. Q What is OpenTelemetry and how does .NET support it? A vendor-neutral CNCF standard and SDK for generating, collecting and exporting logs, metrics and traces (via OTLP). .NET's built-in APIs (ILogger, System.Diagnostics.Metrics Meter, and ActivitySource/Activity for spans) are OpenTelemetry-compatible. The OpenTelemetry .NET SDK subscribes to them and exports to backends like Azure Monitor, Grafana, Jaeger or Datadog. Q How does distributed tracing work across services? Each request gets a trace ID; each unit of work is a span with a parent. The W3C traceparent header (and baggage) propagates context on outgoing HTTP, gRPC and messages, and receivers continue the same trace. ASP.NET Core and HttpClient handle propagation automatically, and backends reassemble the full call tree with timings. Q What's the difference between metrics and logs? Metrics are pre-aggregated numeric time series (counters, histograms): small, fast, ideal for trends, dashboards and alerting, with low-cardinality dimensions. Logs are individual event records with rich context, used for investigation, and more expensive to store and query. Q What is cardinality and why does it matter for metrics? The number of unique tag-value combinations. Each one creates a separate time series, so high-cardinality tags (user IDs, order IDs, raw URLs) explode storage and cost. Keep metric tags low-cardinality and put IDs on traces and logs. Q What would you monitor for a .NET web API? RED metrics per endpoint (request rate, error rate, p95/p99 latency), dependency latency and failures, plus .NET runtime metrics (GC pause and heap, thread pool queue length and thread count, exception rate), connection pool usage, queue/outbox lag, health checks and SLO burn-rate alerts. Q What is .NET Aspire? A toolkit for building distributed .NET apps: an AppHost project to orchestrate services and containers locally with automatic configuration and service discovery, ServiceDefaults to apply OpenTelemetry, health checks and resilience consistently, integration packages for common services, and a dashboard showing logs, traces and metrics. It can also drive deployment (e.g. to Azure Container Apps with azd). Q What are SLIs, SLOs and error budgets? An SLI is a measured indicator of service level (e.g. % of requests under 300 ms). An SLO is the target for it (99.9% over 30 days). The error budget is the allowed shortfall (0.1%), used to balance releasing features against reliability work and to drive alerting on burn rate."},{"c":"observability-aspire","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading .NET observability with OpenTelemetry Distributed tracing in .NET Creating metrics Azure Monitor OpenTelemetry for .NET .NET Aspire documentation Book: Observability Engineering (Majors, Fong-Jones, Miranda). Google's Site Reliability Engineering (free online)."},{"c":"observability-aspire","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run (a plain console app, .NET 8/9; System.Diagnostics.Activity and System.Diagnostics.Metrics are part of the BCL, no extra packages). It checks the actual .NET telemetry primitives directly: that StartActivity is a no-op without a listener, that nested spans form a parent/child trace tree, that a Counter<T> is observable, and that a traceparent header built from a real Activity matches the W3C format. Exporting to a real OpenTelemetry Collector, Azure Monitor or the Aspire dashboard needs a live backend, so that part is left to the Further reading links instead of faked here. C# Run ▶ Copy // Verify this chapter: run with `dotnet run`. No external packages needed -- // System.Diagnostics.Activity and System.Diagnostics.Metrics are in the BCL. // This checks the .NET APIs the chapter describes directly; it does not spin // up a real OpenTelemetry Collector or export to a backend (see Further // reading for that). using System ; using System . Collections . Generic ; using System . Diagnostics ; using System . Diagnostics . Metrics ; using System . Text . RegularExpressions ; void Check ( string name, bool ok) { if (!ok) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: {name}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: {name}\" ); } // 1. \"null if nobody is listening: cheap\" -- with no ActivityListener // registered, StartActivity returns null instead of doing real work. var source = new ActivitySource ( \"Shop.Checkout.Test\" , \"1.0\" ); using var noListenerActivity = source. StartActivity ( \"checkout\" ); Check ( \"StartActivity returns null when no ActivityListener is subscribed (cheap no-op)\" , noListenerActivity is null ); // 2. Once a listener subscribes, the same call produces a real Activity with // a TraceId -- this is what the OpenTelemetry SDK does under the hood. ActivitySource . AddActivityListener ( new ActivityListener { ShouldListenTo = s => s. Name == \"Shop.Checkout.Test\" , Sample = ( ref ActivityCreationOptions < ActivityContext > _) => ActivitySamplingResult . AllDataAndRecorded }); using var parent = source. StartActivity ( \"checkout\" ); Check ( \"once a listener is registered, StartActivity returns a real Activity\" , parent is not null ); Check ( \"a recorded Activity has a non-empty TraceId\" , parent!. TraceId . ToString (). Length == 32 ); // 3. Nested spans form a parent/child tree -- the mechanism behind the trace // diagrams in this chapter: a child span's ParentSpanId matches the parent's // own SpanId. using ( var child = source. StartActivity ( \"sql-query\" )) { Check ( \"a span started while a parent Activity is current records that parent's SpanId\" , child is not null && child. ParentSpanId == parent. SpanId ); Check ( \"the child shares the same TraceId as its parent (one trace, many spans)\" , child!. TraceId == parent. TraceId ); } // 4. Counter<T>: a monotonic counter that a MeterListener can observe -- // verifies CreateCounter + Add actually produces a measurement, the // mechanism OrdersPlaced.Add(1, ...) relies on in the chapter's example. var meter = new Meter ( \"Shop.Checkout.Test\" , \"1.0\" ); var counter = meter. CreateCounter < long >( \"shop.orders.placed\" ); var recordedValues = new List < long >(); using var meterListener = new MeterListener (); meterListener. InstrumentPublished = (instrument, listener) => { if (instrument. Meter . Name == \"Shop.Checkout.Test\" ) listener. EnableMeasurementEvents (instrument); }; meterListener. SetMeasurementEventCallback < long >((instrument, measurement, tags, state) => recordedValues. Add (measurement)); meterListener. Start (); counter. Add ( 1 ); counter. Add ( 1 ); counter. Add ( 3 ); Check ( \"a Counter<long>.Add is observed by a MeterListener with the exact recorded values\" , recordedValues. Count == 3 && recordedValues[ 0 ] == 1 && recordedValues[ 2 ] == 3 ); // 5. W3C Trace Context header format: version-traceid-parentid-flags, exactly // as shown in the chapter's traceparent example. string traceparent = $\"00-{parent.TraceId}-{parent.SpanId}-01\""},{"c":"cloud-azure-overview","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Cloud = renting compute/storage/services on demand, paying for use, instead of owning hardware. IaaS → PaaS → Serverless → SaaS is a sliding scale of how much the provider manages for you. For .NET backends, default to PaaS/managed services — running your own VMs is operational work you probably don't need. Shared responsibility : the provider secures the cloud's infrastructure; you always own your data, identities and access policies, regardless of service model. Scaling out (more instances) needs a genuinely stateless app; scaling up (a bigger instance) doesn't, but hits a ceiling. The twelve-factor app rules (config from environment, statelessness, graceful shutdown, logs as streams, etc.) are what makes an app safe to scale out and redeploy without drama. Azure resources nest: tenant → management groups → subscriptions → resource groups → resources, with RBAC and Policy applying at any level. The Well-Architected Framework 's five pillars (Reliability, Security, Cost, Operations, Performance) are the lens interviewers use for system-design questions."},{"c":"cloud-azure-overview","s":"what-the-cloud-means","h":"What \"the cloud\" means","t":"# What \"the cloud\" means Beginner Renting computing resources (servers, storage, databases, networking, managed services) on demand, over the internet, paying for what you use , instead of buying and running your own hardware. Essential characteristics (NIST): on-demand self-service (create a database in minutes via the portal, CLI or code), broad network access , resource pooling (multi-tenant), rapid elasticity (scale up and down quickly), and measured service (pay per use)."},{"c":"cloud-azure-overview","s":"service-models-who-manages-what","h":"Service models: who manages what","t":"# Service models: who manages what Beginner Diagram Copy On-premises IaaS PaaS Serverless / FaaS SaaS Application YOU YOU YOU YOU (functions) provider Data YOU YOU YOU YOU provider* Runtime (.NET) YOU YOU provider provider provider OS / patching YOU YOU provider provider provider Virtualisation YOU provider provider provider provider Servers/network YOU provider provider provider provider Azure examples — Virtual App Service, Azure Functions, Microsoft 365, Machines Azure SQL, Container Apps Dynamics, GitHub Container Apps (scale to zero) *You still own your data and its access control in SaaS. Reading down any one column tells you exactly where your team's operational burden starts: in IaaS, the line is drawn right after \"Virtualisation\" — you own the OS and everything above it. In PaaS, the line moves up to \"Runtime\" — Azure patches the OS and keeps the .NET runtime current, and your job is just the app itself. Model You manage Trade-off IaaS VMs, OS, runtime, app Maximum control, maximum operations work PaaS App code and configuration Fast delivery, built-in scaling, patching and backups; less control Containers-as-a-service Container images Portable packaging with managed orchestration Serverless Functions/containers; pay per execution; scale to zero No idle cost, event-driven; cold starts, execution limits SaaS Nothing technical Use it as a product Rule of thumb for .NET backends: prefer PaaS and managed services. Every VM you run is patching, monitoring, backup and security work your team owns — work that buys you very little unless you have a genuine, specific reason to need OS-level control."},{"c":"cloud-azure-overview","s":"the-shared-responsibility-model","h":"The shared responsibility model","t":"# The shared responsibility model Beginner The provider secures the cloud (datacentres, hardware, hypervisor, the services' infrastructure); you secure what you put in the cloud : your code, data, identities, access policies, network rules and configuration. The split shifts with the service model, but identities, data and access are always yours . Most cloud breaches are customer misconfigurations (public storage containers, leaked keys, over-privileged identities), not provider failures — a sobering fact worth keeping in mind every time Azure identity & secrets asks you to configure an access policy."},{"c":"cloud-azure-overview","s":"regions-availability-zones-and-resilience","h":"Regions, availability zones and resilience","t":"# Regions, availability zones and resilience Intermediate Concept Meaning Use Region A geographic area with one or more datacentres (West Europe, East US 2, Germany West Central) Latency to users, data residency (GDPR), service availability, price Availability Zone (AZ) Physically separate datacentres within a region, with independent power, cooling and network Zone-redundant deployments survive a datacentre failure Region pair Two regions in the same geography paired for disaster recovery and staggered updates Geo-redundant storage, DR Geography A set of regions within a data-residency boundary Compliance There's a ladder of resilience tiers, each one buying more availability at the cost of more complexity and money: Single instance — cheapest, but any deployment or failure means downtime. Multiple instances across zones — the standard baseline for production. Multi-region active-passive — a second region on standby for disaster recovery; you define an RTO/RPO (below) and fail over to it. Multi-region active-active — the most available and the most complex: global routing (Front Door) in front of data that's replicated across regions (Cosmos DB multi-region writes, or SQL geo-replication). Pick the tier your workload's actual availability requirement justifies — not automatically the top of the list. RTO (Recovery Time Objective): how long you can be down. RPO (Recovery Point Objective): how much data (time) you can lose."},{"c":"cloud-azure-overview","s":"scaling-and-elasticity","h":"Scaling and elasticity","t":"# Scaling and elasticity Beginner Scale up (vertical) Scale out (horizontal) How A bigger machine/tier (more CPU and RAM) More instances behind a load balancer Limit The largest SKU; usually requires a restart Effectively unlimited, if the app is stateless Resilience Still one instance Survives instance loss Requirement None Stateless application + shared state stores The \"Requirement\" row is the important one: scaling out isn't free just because the cloud makes adding instances easy — it only works if the application genuinely doesn't care which instance handles which request. That requirement is exactly what the twelve-factor discipline below is built around. Autoscaling adds or removes instances automatically, based on metrics (CPU, memory, requests per second, or queue length via KEDA , a Kubernetes add-on that scales workloads using metrics from outside the cluster, like a message queue's depth) or on a schedule. Elasticity specifically means scaling down too, not just up — so you stop paying for capacity nobody's using."},{"c":"cloud-azure-overview","s":"cloud-native-app-design-the-twelve-factors","h":"Cloud-native app design: the twelve factors","t":"# Cloud-native app design: the twelve factors Intermediate The Twelve-Factor App methodology (Heroku) summarises how to build apps that run well on cloud platforms, and ASP.NET Core supports each factor: Factor Meaning In .NET 1. Codebase One repo per app, many deploys Git, one pipeline per deployable 2. Dependencies Declare and isolate NuGet with lock files, container images 3. Config Store config in the environment IConfiguration env vars, Key Vault, App Configuration ( Configuration ) 4. Backing services Treat DBs and queues as attached resources via URLs Connection strings from config; swap without code changes 5. Build, release, run Strictly separate stages CI builds an immutable image; CD releases it with config 6. Processes Run as stateless processes No in-memory session, no local file storage; use Redis or Blob 7. Port binding Self-contained, exports HTTP via a port Kestrel on ASPNETCORE_HTTP_PORTS 8. Concurrency Scale out via processes Instances/replicas; separate web and worker processes 9. Disposability Fast startup, graceful shutdown Host shutdown handling, SIGTERM, stoppingToken ( Hosting ) 10. Dev/prod parity Keep environments similar Containers, Aspire, Testcontainers, IaC 11. Logs Treat logs as event streams (stdout) Console/OTLP logging; the platform collects them 12. Admin processes Run one-off tasks as separate processes Migration bundles, jobs, dotnet ef in the pipeline Factors 3, 6 and 9 are bolded above because they're the ones ASP.NET Core chapters earlier in this book built entire sections around — configuration-from-environment, statelessness, and graceful shutdown each got dedicated treatment precisely because getting them wrong is what breaks an app the moment it's scaled past one instance. The classic \"it works with one instance\" bugs In-memory sessions or caches assumed to be shared, files written to the local disk, scheduled jobs running on every replica, Data Protection keys not shared (logged-out users), SignalR without a backplane, and sticky-session assumptions. All of them show up the first time you scale out to 2 instances. Design for N instances from day one."},{"c":"cloud-azure-overview","s":"azures-resource-hierarchy","h":"Azure's resource hierarchy","t":"# Azure's resource hierarchy Intermediate Diagram Copy Microsoft Entra tenant (identity boundary: users, groups, app registrations) └── Management groups (policy & RBAC inheritance for many subscriptions) └── Subscriptions (billing + quota + access boundary; often one per environment or team) └── Resource groups (lifecycle container: deploy/delete together, e.g. \"rg-shop-prod-weu\") └── Resources (App Service, SQL database, Key Vault, Storage account…) Azure Resource Manager (ARM) is the control plane: every create/update goes through it, from the portal, CLI ( az ), PowerShell, SDKs, Bicep/Terraform. RBAC role assignments at any scope (management group → resource) grant who can do what (Owner, Contributor, Reader, plus data-plane roles like Storage Blob Data Reader ). Azure Policy enforces rules (\"only these regions\", \"storage must not allow public access\", \"tags required\"). Tags ( env=prod , owner=team-orders , costCenter=123 ) enable cost reporting and governance. Naming conventions (Cloud Adoption Framework): app-shop-api-prod-weu-001 , kv-shop-prod-weu ."},{"c":"cloud-azure-overview","s":"the-azure-map-for-net-backend-developers","h":"The Azure map for .NET backend developers","t":"# The Azure map for .NET backend developers Beginner Need Azure service(s) Chapter Run web apps / APIs App Service , Container Apps , AKS, Functions Azure compute Run background / event-driven code Functions , Container Apps jobs, WebJobs Azure compute Container registry Azure Container Registry (ACR) Docker Relational database Azure SQL Database , SQL Managed Instance, Azure Database for PostgreSQL Azure data NoSQL / global data Cosmos DB NoSQL Files, blobs, static content Blob Storage (Storage account), Azure Files Azure data Cache Azure Managed Redis Caching Messaging Service Bus , Event Grid , Event Hubs , Storage Queues Azure data Secrets, keys, certificates Key Vault Identity & secrets Identity Microsoft Entra ID , managed identities , Entra External ID (customers) Identity & secrets Configuration and feature flags App Configuration Configuration API gateway API Management Microservices Global entry, CDN, WAF Front Door , Application Gateway (regional L7 LB + WAF), Load Balancer (L4) — Monitoring Azure Monitor , Application Insights , Log Analytics Observability Networking Virtual Network (VNet), subnets, NSGs, Private Endpoints , Private DNS, VPN/ExpressRoute below AI Azure OpenAI / AI Foundry, AI Search — IaC and deployment ARM/ Bicep , Terraform, azd , GitHub Actions/Azure DevOps CI/CD & IaC"},{"c":"cloud-azure-overview","s":"networking-essentials","h":"Networking essentials","t":"# Networking essentials Advanced VNet : your private network in Azure, divided into subnets . NSGs filter traffic per subnet/NIC. Private Endpoints give a PaaS service (SQL, Storage, Key Vault, Service Bus) a private IP in your VNet , and you then disable public network access . A common production security requirement. Requires Private DNS zones so names resolve to the private IPs. VNet integration lets App Service/Functions/Container Apps reach private resources. Service endpoints : an older, simpler alternative to private endpoints. Hub-and-spoke topology: shared services (firewall, VPN gateway) in a hub VNet, workloads in peered spokes. Front Door (global, edge) → Application Gateway / API Management (regional) → apps."},{"c":"cloud-azure-overview","s":"cost","h":"Cost","t":"# Cost Intermediate Cloud costs grow quietly — nobody decides to overspend, it accumulates from dozens of individually-reasonable-looking choices. Know the levers: Lever Example Right-sizing P3v3 App Service plan at 10% CPU → P1v3 Autoscale and scale to zero Container Apps/Functions consumption for spiky workloads Reservations / savings plans 1–3 year commitments for steady workloads (Microsoft advertises up to ~60–70% off list price — see Reservations pricing in Further reading; actual savings depend on the SKU and term) Serverless/consumption tiers Cosmos DB serverless or autoscale, Azure SQL serverless (auto-pause) Dev/test environments Smaller SKUs, shut down at night, dev/test pricing Storage tiers Hot → Cool → Cold → Archive lifecycle policies for blobs Data egress Traffic leaving Azure or crossing regions costs money Logging volume Application Insights ingestion: sampling, log levels, retention Tools: Azure Cost Management (budgets, alerts, cost analysis by tag), Azure Advisor (cost, reliability and security recommendations), the Pricing Calculator ."},{"c":"cloud-azure-overview","s":"the-azure-well-architected-framework","h":"The Azure Well-Architected Framework","t":"# The Azure Well-Architected Framework Advanced Five pillars to evaluate any workload design. They map directly onto interview \"system design\" discussions, and worth noticing: each pillar is really a lens this entire book has already applied chapter by chapter — Reliability is Resilience , Security is Auth and Web security , Performance Efficiency is Performance engineering . Pillar Key questions Techniques Reliability Does it survive failures and recover? Zones, redundancy, retries, health probes, backups, DR, chaos testing Security Is it protected end to end? Entra ID + managed identity, least privilege, Key Vault, private endpoints, WAF, encryption, Defender for Cloud Cost Optimization Is spend aligned with value? Right-sizing, autoscale, reservations, tiering, tagging Operational Excellence Can we deploy and run it safely? IaC, CI/CD, observability, runbooks, safe deployment (slots, canaries) Performance Efficiency Does it scale to demand? Scale-out, caching, CDN, async processing, partitioning, load testing Q What is the difference between IaaS, PaaS and serverless? IaaS gives virtual machines and networking: you manage the OS, runtime and app. PaaS manages the OS and runtime and you deploy code or containers (App Service, Azure SQL). Serverless runs code on demand with automatic scaling, including to zero, and per-execution billing (Azure Functions, Container Apps consumption), with constraints like cold starts. Q What is the shared responsibility model? The cloud provider is responsible for security of the cloud (physical infrastructure, hardware, virtualisation, managed service internals); the customer is responsible for security in the cloud (data, identities, access control, application code, network configuration). The split depends on IaaS, PaaS or SaaS, but data and identity are always the customer's. Q What are availability zones and why use them? Physically separate datacentres within an Azure region with independent power, cooling and networking. Deploying instances and zone-redundant services across zones protects against a datacentre outage while keeping low latency. Q Scale up vs scale out? Scale up (vertical) moves to a larger instance with more CPU and RAM; it's limited by the largest size and doesn't add redundancy. Scale out (horizontal) adds more instances behind a load balancer: near-unlimited and resilient, but it requires a stateless application with shared state in external stores. Q What makes an application cloud-native / twelve-factor? Config from the environment, stateless processes with backing services attached via configuration, port binding, fast startup and graceful shutdown, logs as stdout streams, strict build/release/run separation, dev/prod parity, and scale-out via processes. In .NET: IConfiguration with env vars and Key Vault, Kestrel, Redis/Blob for state, hosted-service shutdown handling, container images and IaC. Q How is Azure organised (resource hierarchy)? An Entra tenant contains management groups, which contain subscriptions (billing, quota and access boundaries), which contain resource groups (lifecycle containers), which contain resources. RBAC and Azure Policy can be applied at any level and are inherited downward. Everything is managed through Azure Resource Manager. Q What are private endpoints? Network interfaces that give an Azure PaaS service a private IP address inside your VNet, so traffic stays on the Microsoft backbone and you can disable the service's public endpoint. They need Private DNS zone configuration. Q What are RTO and RPO? Recovery Time Objective: the maximum acceptable downtime after a disaster. Recovery Point Objective: the maximum acceptable data loss measured in time. Both drive backup, replication and failover design."},{"c":"cloud-azure-overview","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Azure fundamentals (Microsoft Learn path, AZ-900) Azure Well-Architected Framework Azure Architecture Center The Twelve-Factor App Azure for .NET developers Certifications that match this part: AZ-900 (fundamentals), AZ-204 (Developing Solutions for Azure) , AZ-305 (architect). Reservations pricing — source for the reservation-discount figures above."},{"c":"cloud-azure-overview","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Most of this chapter is about Azure services, pricing and network topology, which a console program can't exercise. What can be verified locally is the mechanism behind two core claims: config-from-environment (twelve-factor #3) and why scaling out needs a stateless app. Run with dotnet run (plain console app, .NET 8/9, BCL only). C# Run ▶ Copy // Claim (twelve-factor, factor 3): \"store config in the environment\" — .NET's config system reads env vars directly. Environment . SetEnvironmentVariable ( \"SHOP_API_KEY\" , \"demo-key-123\" ); // stands in for a real deployment setting this var apiKeyFromEnv = Environment . GetEnvironmentVariable ( \"SHOP_API_KEY\" ); if (apiKeyFromEnv != \"demo-key-123\" ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected config to come from the environment, not a hardcoded value\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: config read from the environment ({apiKeyFromEnv}), exactly how IConfiguration's environment provider works\" ); // Claim: scaling OUT only works if the app is genuinely stateless (shares nothing per-instance that a later request needs). // Simulate two \"instances\" with their own local, in-memory dictionary — the broken pattern the chapter warns about. var instanceA = new Dictionary < string , string >(); var instanceB = new Dictionary < string , string >(); instanceA[ \"cart-42\" ] = \"2x widget\" ; // request 1 lands on instance A, which remembers the cart locally // Request 2 for the same cart gets load-balanced to instance B instead. bool statefulInstanceBSeesIt = instanceB. ContainsKey ( \"cart-42\" ); if (statefulInstanceBSeesIt) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected instance B's local dictionary to know nothing about instance A's state\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: with local in-memory state, a second instance has no idea about the first instance's cart — this is exactly why scale-out needs a stateless app + shared state store\" ); // Now the fix: a shared store (stands in for Redis/SQL/Blob) that every instance reads and writes to instead of local memory. var sharedStore = new Dictionary < string , string >(); void HandleOnAnyInstance ( string key, string value ) => sharedStore[key] = value ; // every \"instance\" calls this the same way string ? ReadFromAnyInstance ( string key) => sharedStore. TryGetValue (key, out var v) ? v : null ; HandleOnAnyInstance ( \"cart-42\" , \"2x widget\" ); // \"instance A\" handles this var seenFromAnotherInstance = ReadFromAnyInstance ( \"cart-42\" ); // \"instance B\" handles the next request if (seenFromAnotherInstance != \"2x widget\" ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected the shared store to make the cart visible from any instance\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: with state moved to a shared store, any instance can see the same cart — this is the stateless-process pattern the twelve-factor app requires\" ); // Not runtime-verifiable here (needs real Azure resources, pricing, or network topology) — see Further reading: // - Service model boundaries (IaaS/PaaS/serverless/SaaS), shared responsibility model // - Regions, availability zones, RTO/RPO, resource hierarchy, RBAC, private endpoints // - Reservation/savings-plan discount percentages // - Well-Architected Framework pillars Console . WriteLine ( \"All checkable claims verified.\" );"},{"c":"docker","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR A container shares the host's kernel instead of running its own, so it starts in milliseconds and costs much less than a VM — at the price of weaker isolation. An image is a stack of cached, read-only layers; a container is a running instance with one writable layer on top. Many containers can share one image. A good .NET Dockerfile is multi-stage : compile with the big SDK image, then copy only the published output into a small runtime image (ideally chiseled , which has no shell and runs non-root by default). Copy .csproj files and restore before copying source, so code-only changes don't invalidate the (slow) restore layer's cache. Since .NET 8, ASP.NET Core images listen on port 8080, not 80, and run as a non-root user — both for security. Configuration comes from environment variables set by the platform, never baked into the image; secrets come from a secret store or managed identity, never COPY 'd in. .NET is container-aware: the GC and thread pool read cgroup CPU/memory limits and size themselves to the container, not the host machine. Plain-English starting point A container is your app plus everything it needs to run — the .NET runtime, OS files, config — sealed into one package that behaves the same everywhere. A Docker image is that package, built once. Running the image is what creates a container. Building an image is like stacking transparent sheets Picture a few transparent sheets, each with one paragraph of a letter printed on it. Stack three sheets and you can read a whole letter through them. That stack is your image : one sheet per layer (base OS, .NET runtime, your app files), stacked in order. Photocopy that stack and you get a letter you can hand out — that's a container : a running copy with one extra, blank sheet on top for anything it scribbles while it runs. Photocopy the same stack again and you get another, independent letter. Many containers, one image."},{"c":"docker","s":"what-a-container-is","h":"What a container is","t":"# What a container is Beginner A container is an isolated process (or group of processes) with its own filesystem, network and process space — packaged with everything it needs: your app, the .NET runtime, OS libraries. One detail explains both its biggest strength and its biggest limit: a container shares the host's kernel instead of running its own. That's why it starts in milliseconds and barely costs anything to run — but it's also why its isolation is weaker than a full virtual machine's. Virtual machine Container Virtualises Hardware: each VM has a full guest OS and kernel The OS: processes isolated with namespaces and cgroups; shared kernel Size GBs MBs (a .NET API image is ~100–250 MB, or less with chiseled/AOT) Startup Minutes Seconds or less Density Tens per host Hundreds per host Isolation Strong Good (weaker than a VM; sandboxed runtimes exist) Why containers took over \"It works on my machine\" goes away: the same immutable image runs on your laptop, in CI, in staging and in production. Dependencies are explicit, deployments are fast and reproducible, rollbacks mean redeploying the previous image tag, and every modern platform (Kubernetes, Container Apps, App Service, Functions, ECS, Cloud Run) accepts containers. The image is the contract between dev and ops."},{"c":"docker","s":"images-layers-containers-and-registries","h":"Images, layers, containers and registries","t":"# Images, layers, containers and registries Beginner Term Meaning Image A read-only template: a stack of layers (filesystem diffs) plus metadata (entrypoint, env, exposed ports, user) Layer Each Dockerfile instruction ( FROM , COPY , RUN ) creates a layer, cached and shared between images Container A running (or stopped) instance of an image, with a thin writable layer on top Registry Stores and distributes images: Docker Hub, Azure Container Registry (ACR) , GitHub Container Registry, MCR (Microsoft Container Registry, where official .NET images live) Tag A human label: shop-api:1.4.2 , :latest (mutable) Digest Immutable content hash: shop-api@sha256:3f1c… Dockerfile Instructions to build an image Shell Copy docker build -t shop-api : 1.4.2 . docker run -d -p 8080 : 8080 --name api -e ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT =Staging shop-api : 1.4.2 docker ps · docker logs -f api · docker exec -it api sh · docker stop api # stop sends SIGTERM → graceful shutdown docker tag shop-api : 1.4.2 shopacr.azurecr.io/ shop-api : 1.4.2 az acr login -n shopacr && docker push shopacr.azurecr.io/ shop-api : 1.4.2"},{"c":"docker","s":"net-container-images","h":".NET container images","t":"# .NET container images Intermediate Official images are published on mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/ : Image Contains Use sdk:10.0 Full SDK (compiler, CLI, NuGet) ~ 800 MB Build stage only aspnet:10.0 ASP.NET Core + .NET runtime Run web apps/APIs runtime:10.0 .NET runtime only Run console/worker apps runtime-deps:10.0 Only native OS dependencies (no .NET) Self-contained or Native AOT apps Variants: -alpine Alpine Linux (musl): small Smaller images; check native dependency compatibility -noble , -azurelinux3.0 Ubuntu 24.04 / Azure Linux base Default and alternative distros -noble-chiseled (and -extra ) Distroless Ubuntu: no shell, no package manager, non-root by default Smallest attack surface , ideal for production -composite ASP.NET with composite R2R images Faster startup Since .NET 8, ASP.NET Core images listen on port 8080 by default ( ASPNETCORE_HTTP_PORTS=8080 ), not 80, and include a non-root app user (UID 1654)."},{"c":"docker","s":"a-production-grade-dockerfile","h":"A production-grade Dockerfile","t":"# A production-grade Dockerfile Intermediate Dockerfile Copy # syntax=docker/dockerfile:1 # ---------- build stage ---------- FROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/ sdk : 10.0 AS build WORKDIR /src # 1. Copy only project files and restore first → this layer is cached until dependencies change COPY Directory.Build.props Directory.Packages.props global.json ./ COPY src/Shop.Api/Shop.Api.csproj src/Shop.Api/ COPY src/Shop.Application/Shop.Application.csproj src/Shop.Application/ COPY src/Shop.Domain/Shop.Domain.csproj src/Shop.Domain/ COPY src/Shop.Infrastructure/Shop.Infrastructure.csproj src/Shop.Infrastructure/ RUN dotnet restore src/Shop.Api/Shop.Api.csproj # 2. Copy the rest and publish COPY src/ src/ RUN dotnet publish src/Shop.Api/Shop.Api.csproj -c Release -o /app/publish --no-restore / p : UseAppHost =false # ---------- runtime stage ---------- FROM mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/ aspnet : 10.0 -noble-chiseled AS final WORKDIR /app COPY --from =build /app/publish . USER app # non-root (chiseled images default to it already) EXPOSE 8080 ENV ASPNETCORE_HTTP_PORTS = 8080 \\ DOTNET_gcServer = 1 ENTRYPOINT [ \"dotnet\" , \"Shop.Api.dll\" ] Key techniques: Multi-stage build: compile in the big SDK image, copy only the published output into the small runtime image. The final image contains no SDK, no source code and no build tools. Layer caching: copy the .csproj files and restore before copying source, so code changes don't invalidate the (slow) restore layer. Docker caches each layer, and only reruns a layer (plus everything after it) when that layer's own inputs change. Since the .csproj files rarely change, restore stays cached even when you've only edited a .cs file — step 1's whole point is to put the slow, rarely-changing work before the fast, frequently-changing work. .dockerignore : exclude bin/ , obj/ , .git , .vs , node_modules , *.user , secrets. It speeds up builds and avoids leaking files. Non-root user and a minimal base image (chiseled). Exec-form ENTRYPOINT (the JSON array syntax, not a plain shell string) runs dotnet directly as PID 1, instead of wrapping it in a shell. That matters because docker stop sends SIGTERM to PID 1, and only the process that actually receives that signal can start a graceful shutdown. If a shell were PID 1 instead, the shell would get the signal and dotnet wouldn't — see ASP.NET Core architecture for what graceful shutdown then does. Pin base image versions ( 10.0 gets patches; pin digests for full reproducibility) and rebuild regularly to pick up security patches. Diagram Copy # .dockerignore **/bin **/obj **/.vs **/.git **/node_modules **/*.user **/appsettings.Development.json **/secrets*.json"},{"c":"docker","s":"building-images-without-a-dockerfile","h":"Building images without a Dockerfile","t":"# Building images without a Dockerfile Intermediate The .NET SDK can build OCI images directly, with no Docker daemon needed for the build: Shell Copy dotnet publish src/Shop.Api -c Release / t :PublishContainer \\ -p ContainerRepository =shop-api \\ -p ContainerImageTags = '\"1.4.2;latest\"' \\ -p ContainerFamily =noble-chiseled \\ -p ContainerRegistry =shopacr.azurecr.io # push directly to a registry XML Copy <PropertyGroup> <ContainerRepository>shop-api</ContainerRepository> <ContainerFamily>noble-chiseled</ContainerFamily> <ContainerPort Include = \"8080\" Type = \"tcp\" /> </PropertyGroup> Great for standard apps and CI; use a Dockerfile when you need custom OS packages or more complex builds."},{"c":"docker","s":"configuration-secrets-and-storage-in-containers","h":"Configuration, secrets and storage in containers","t":"# Configuration, secrets and storage in containers Intermediate Configuration via environment variables ( ConnectionStrings__Shop , ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT ), set by the platform at runtime, never baked into the image — exactly the layered-configuration discipline Configuration covered, applied at the container level. The same image is promoted through all environments. Secrets : never COPY them into images or pass them as build args (they persist in layers). Use the platform's secret store (Key Vault references, Kubernetes Secrets, Container Apps secrets) or managed identity so there's nothing secret to inject. Filesystem is ephemeral : anything written inside a container is lost when it's replaced. Use Blob Storage or a database; mount volumes only for genuinely needed persistent paths. Data Protection keys must be persisted outside the container when running multiple instances (Blob + Key Vault), or cookies and antiforgery tokens break — exactly the shared-key-ring requirement Auth raised for cookie auth across instances. Logs to stdout/stderr (console or JSON console logging, or OTLP); the platform collects them. Health endpoints for the orchestrator's probes ( Resilience )."},{"c":"docker","s":"docker-compose-for-local-development","h":"docker compose for local development","t":"# docker compose for local development Beginner Run the API with its dependencies locally: YAML Copy # compose.yaml services : api : build : . ports : [ \"8080:8080\" ] environment : ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT : Development ConnectionStrings__Shop : \"Server=sql;Database=Shop;User Id=sa;Password=Your_strong_pw1;TrustServerCertificate=True\" ConnectionStrings__Redis : \"redis:6379\" depends_on : sql : { condition : service_healthy } redis : { condition : service_started } sql : image : mcr.microsoft.com/mssql/ server : 2022 -latest environment : { ACCEPT_EULA : \"Y\" , MSSQL_SA_PASSWORD : \"Your_strong_pw1\" } ports : [ \"1433:1433\" ] volumes : [ sqldata :/var/opt/mssql] healthcheck : test : [ \"CMD-SHELL\" , \"/opt/mssql-tools18/bin/sqlcmd -C -S localhost -U sa -P Your_strong_pw1 -Q 'SELECT 1' || exit 1\" ] interval : 10s retries : 10 redis : image : redis : 7 -alpine ports : [ \"6379:6379\" ] volumes : sqldata : Shell Copy docker compose up -d --build # start everything docker compose logs -f api docker compose down -v # stop and remove volumes .NET Aspire ( Observability & Aspire ) is the modern .NET alternative: the same orchestration in C#, with automatic wiring and a dashboard, and it's worth noticing the depends_on / WaitFor idea here is the same dependency-ordering problem Aspire's AppHost solves with actual C# instead of YAML."},{"c":"docker","s":"image-size-startup-and-security","h":"Image size, startup and security","t":"# Image size, startup and security Advanced Technique Effect Chiseled / distroless base Fewer packages → fewer CVEs; no shell for attackers Alpine base Small; musl libc (check native libraries like ICU) InvariantGlobalization=true Drops ICU libraries when you don't need culture data Trimming + self-contained on runtime-deps Smaller output Native AOT on runtime-deps (or chiseled) Tens of MB, millisecond startup ( How code runs ) ReadyToRun / composite images Faster startup Security practices: scan images in CI (Trivy, Grype, Docker Scout, Microsoft Defender for Containers on ACR); run as non-root with a read-only root filesystem ; drop Linux capabilities; don't run as privileged; sign images (Notation/cosign) and allow only signed images in production; produce an SBOM ; rebuild regularly for base-image patches (Dependabot/Renovate can bump base images)."},{"c":"docker","s":"container-memory-and-cpu-limits-with-net","h":"Container memory and CPU limits with .NET","t":"# Container memory and CPU limits with .NET Advanced .NET is container-aware : the GC and thread pool read the container's own cgroup CPU and memory limits, not the physical host's. In other words, the runtime adapts its behavior to the box it thinks it's running in — which is the container, not the machine underneath it. That's why Memory & GC and Threading & concurrency matter directly here too. With a memory limit, the GC heap hard limit defaults to 75% of the container limit (tunable via DOTNET_GCHeapHardLimitPercent ) — see Runtime configuration knobs: GC in Further reading for the exact default and how to change it. Server GC is the default for ASP.NET Core; .NET 9+ enables DATAS (Dynamic Adaptation To Application Sizes), so server GC uses far less memory in small containers. Set realistic requests and limits in Kubernetes. Too-low memory limits cause OOMKilled restarts. DOTNET_PROCESSOR_COUNT can override the detected CPU count. Q What is the difference between a container and a virtual machine? A VM virtualises hardware and runs a full guest OS with its own kernel: strong isolation, but heavy and slow to start. A container isolates processes on a shared host kernel (namespaces and cgroups) and packages just the app and its dependencies: lightweight, fast to start and dense. Q Why use a multi-stage Dockerfile for .NET? To build with the large SDK image and copy only the published output into a small runtime image. The final image is smaller, has less attack surface (no SDK, sources or build tools) and pulls and starts faster. Q How do you optimise Docker layer caching for .NET builds? Copy the csproj files (and Directory.*.props, global.json) first and run dotnet restore before copying the rest of the source. Restore is then cached and only reruns when dependencies change. Use a .dockerignore to keep bin/obj and other noise out of the build context. Q Which base image would you use for an ASP.NET Core app in production? mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspnet with a pinned major version, ideally the chiseled (distroless, non-root) variant for a minimal attack surface, or runtime-deps for self-contained/Native AOT apps. Use the sdk image only in the build stage. Q How should configuration and secrets be provided to a container? Through environment variables or mounted configuration supplied by the platform at runtime, so one immutable image is promoted across environments. Secrets come from a secret store (Key Vault references, Kubernetes Secrets) or are avoided with managed identity. Never bake them into the image or build args. Q Why does .NET 8+ listen on port 8080 in containers? The official images default ASPNETCORE_HTTP_PORTS to 8080 and run as a non-root user, and non-root processes can't bind to privileged ports below 1024 on Linux. Map or route external port 80/443 to 8080 at the platform. Q What happens when you run docker stop on a .NET container? Docker sends SIGTERM to PID 1. With an exec-form ENTRYPOINT, that's dotnet, so the .NET host begins graceful shutdown (stops accepting connections, drains requests, stops hosted services). After the timeout (10 s default), Docker sends SIGKILL."},{"c":"docker","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Containerize a .NET app .NET container images Containerize a .NET app with dotnet publish Dockerfile best practices Microsoft's eBook .NET Microservices: Architecture for Containerized .NET Applications . Runtime configuration knobs: GC — source for the 75% default GC heap limit figure above."},{"c":"docker","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Most of this chapter needs an actual Docker daemon or container runtime to exercise for real (building images, cgroup limits). What you can verify directly, with no Docker involved, is the exact SIGTERM mechanism that docker stop relies on. Run with dotnet run (plain console app, .NET 8/9, BCL only; Linux/macOS — it uses the kill command to send itself a real signal). C# Run ▶ Copy using System . Diagnostics ; using System . Runtime . InteropServices ; // Claim: \"docker stop sends SIGTERM to PID 1, and only the process that actually receives it can start // a graceful shutdown\" — demonstrate the exact mechanism .NET uses to observe SIGTERM (PosixSignalRegistration), // which is what an exec-form ENTRYPOINT (dotnet as PID 1) relies on. if (! OperatingSystem . IsWindows ()) { var received = new TaskCompletionSource (); using var registration = PosixSignalRegistration . Create ( PosixSignal . SIGTERM , ctx => { ctx. Cancel = true ; // tell the runtime we handled it ourselves (so the process doesn't just die) received. TrySetResult (); }); var pid = Environment . ProcessId ; using var killProcess = Process . Start ( new ProcessStartInfo ( \"kill\" , $\"-TERM {pid}\" ) { UseShellExecute = false }); killProcess!. WaitForExit (); var completed = await Task . WhenAny (received. Task , Task . Delay ( TimeSpan . FromSeconds ( 3 ))); if (completed != received. Task ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected this process to observe its own SIGTERM within 3 seconds\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: this process received SIGTERM and ran its own handler — this is the exact signal `docker stop` sends to PID 1, which is why exec-form ENTRYPOINT (dotnet as PID 1, not a shell) matters\" ); } else { Console . WriteLine ( \"SKIPPED: SIGTERM demo requires a POSIX OS (Linux/macOS) — containers run Linux, so this matches the real scenario\" ); } // Claim: .NET reads cgroup limits and sizes the GC/thread pool to the CONTAINER, not the host. // We can't fake cgroups here without a real container, but ProcessorCount is the exact value .NET bases that sizing on. Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: Environment.ProcessorCount reports {Environment.ProcessorCount} (this process's view — inside a real container, this reflects the cgroup CPU limit, not the host's full core count)\" ); // Not runtime-verifiable here (needs an actual container runtime / cgroup limits / Docker daemon) — see Further reading: // - Image layering, caching, multi-stage builds, registries // - .NET base images (chiseled, Alpine, runtime-deps) // - GC heap hard limit default (75%) and DOTNET_GCHeapHardLimitPercent Console . WriteLine ( \"All checkable claims verified.\" );"},{"c":"azure-compute","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Azure's compute options form a spectrum: VMs (most control, most ops work) → AKS → Container Apps → App Service → Functions (least ops work, most opinionated). App Service : classic managed web hosting. Deployment slots let you warm up a new version on its own hostname, then swap it into production with near-zero downtime. Azure Functions : small units of code triggered by events (HTTP, timer, queue, etc.), billed per execution, scaling to zero. Use the modern isolated worker model , not the legacy in-process one. Durable Functions extend Functions with stateful, checkpointed workflows that can wait for days and survive restarts. Container Apps : serverless containers on managed Kubernetes underneath — most of AKS's capability (autoscaling via KEDA, traffic-split revisions, Dapr) without having to run a cluster yourself. AKS : full Kubernetes, for when you genuinely need its API/ecosystem and have the team to operate it. Pick based on how much operational effort you want to own, not which option sounds most powerful."},{"c":"azure-compute","s":"the-options-at-a-glance","h":"The options at a glance","t":"# The options at a glance Beginner Diagram Copy More control, more ops ◀──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────▶ Less ops, more opinionated Virtual Machines AKS (Kubernetes) Container Apps App Service Azure Functions any software full K8s control serverless containers managed web hosting event-driven functions Every option on this spectrum is the exact same trade-off from Cloud fundamentals , just sliced more finely — more control always means more of the operational burden sits with your team. App Service Azure Functions Container Apps AKS Virtual Machines Unit Web app (code or container) Function (code; containers supported) Container app (one or more containers) Pods in a Kubernetes cluster VM Abstraction PaaS Serverless FaaS Serverless containers (managed K8s + KEDA + Envoy underneath) Managed Kubernetes control plane IaaS Scale Manual/autoscale rules per plan; scale out to N instances Automatic per event load; scale to zero (consumption plans) Automatic via KEDA (HTTP, queue length, CPU…); scale to zero HPA, KEDA, cluster autoscaler VM scale sets Pricing Per plan (always on) Per execution (consumption) or per plan Per vCPU-second/GiB-second (consumption) or dedicated workload profiles Nodes (VMs) + optional uptime SLA VM hours Best for Classic web apps & APIs, simple deployment Event glue, background triggers, spiky/scheduled jobs Microservices, APIs, workers, jobs, without running K8s Large platforms needing full K8s control and ecosystem Legacy, special OS needs, lift-and-shift Ops effort Low Lowest Low–medium High Highest"},{"c":"azure-compute","s":"azure-app-service","h":"Azure App Service","t":"# Azure App Service Beginner App Service hosts web apps, REST APIs and background WebJobs, on Windows or Linux, from code (.NET published output) or a container image . App Service plan = the underlying compute (VM size and instance count) that one or more apps share. The tier determines features: Tier Notes Free/Shared (F1/D1) Dev/test only; shared infrastructure Basic (B) Dedicated, manual scale, no slots Standard (S) / Premium v3 (P0v3–P5mv3) Autoscale, deployment slots , VNet integration, zone redundancy (Premium) Isolated v2 (App Service Environment) Single-tenant, fully network-isolated # Deployment slots Slots are live apps with their own hostnames (e.g. shop-api-staging.azurewebsites.net ) on the same plan. Deploy to staging , warm it up and run smoke tests, then swap with production: the swap is near-instant, and you roll back by swapping again — the same zero-downtime instinct behind blue/green deployments generally, just built directly into the platform. Shell Copy az webapp deployment slot create -g rg-shop -n shop-api --slot staging az webapp deploy -g rg-shop -n shop-api --slot staging --src-path app.zip --type zip az webapp deployment slot swap -g rg-shop -n shop-api --slot staging --target-slot production Settings can be marked slot-specific (\"sticky\"), like a connection string pointing to a staging database, so they don't swap. # Configuration and features App settings become environment variables ( Payments__ApiKey ); connection strings likewise — exactly the environment-variable binding Configuration described. Use Key Vault references : @Microsoft.KeyVault(SecretUri=https://kv-shop.vault.azure.net/secrets/StripeKey/) . Managed identity (system- or user-assigned) for passwordless access to SQL, Storage and Key Vault ( Identity & secrets ). Autoscale rules (CPU > 70% → +1 instance) or automatic scaling (Premium v3: HTTP-traffic based). Health check path: unhealthy instances are removed from the load balancer and replaced. VNet integration (outbound to private resources) and private endpoints (private inbound). Always On (keeps the app loaded; avoids idle unloading), ARR affinity (sticky sessions; turn it off for stateless apps), HTTP/2, TLS, custom domains, managed certificates, Easy Auth (built-in authentication). Diagnose and solve problems , Kudu/SCM site, log streaming, Application Insights integration. WebJobs run background tasks alongside the app (prefer hosted services or Functions in new code)."},{"c":"azure-compute","s":"azure-functions","h":"Azure Functions","t":"# Azure Functions Intermediate Azure Functions runs small pieces of code in response to triggers , with automatic scaling and pay-per-use pricing. # Triggers and bindings Triggers Bindings (inputs/outputs without SDK boilerplate) HTTP, Timer (CRON), Service Bus queue/topic, Storage Queue , Blob (or Event Grid-based blob), Event Grid , Event Hubs , Cosmos DB change feed , Kafka, RabbitMQ, SignalR, SQL change tracking Blob, Table, Cosmos DB, Service Bus, Event Hubs, SQL, SignalR, Storage Queues… # The isolated worker model Modern .NET functions run in the isolated worker model : your code runs in a separate process from the Functions host, so you control the .NET version and dependencies, and get normal DI, middleware and configuration like any .NET app. The older in-process model is being retired — check the isolated worker guide in Further reading for the current end-of-support date before relying on it, since Microsoft's retirement schedules can shift. C# Run ▶ Copy // Program.cs var builder = FunctionsApplication . CreateBuilder (args); builder. ConfigureFunctionsWebApplication (); // ASP.NET Core integration for HTTP triggers builder. Services . AddApplicationInsightsTelemetryWorkerService (). ConfigureFunctionsApplicationInsights (); builder. Services . AddDbContext < ShopDb >(o => o. UseSqlServer (builder. Configuration . GetConnectionString ( \"Shop\" ))); builder. Services . AddScoped < IInvoiceService , InvoiceService >(); builder. Build (). Run (); // Functions public sealed class OrderFunctions ( IInvoiceService invoices, ILogger < OrderFunctions > log) { [Function(nameof(GenerateInvoice))] [BlobOutput(\"invoices/{OrderId}.pdf\", Connection = \"Storage\")] // output binding: return value written to a blob public async Task < byte []> GenerateInvoice ( [ServiceBusTrigger(\"order-placed\", Connection = \"ServiceBus\")] OrderPlaced message, CancellationToken ct) { log. LogInformation ( \"Generating invoice for {OrderId}\" , message. OrderId ); return await invoices. RenderPdfAsync (message. OrderId , ct); } [Function(nameof(NightlyCleanup))] public Task NightlyCleanup ( [TimerTrigger(\"0 0 2 * * *\")] TimerInfo timer, CancellationToken ct) => invoices. PurgeDraftsAsync (ct); // 02:00 daily [Function(nameof(GetInvoice))] public async Task < IActionResult > GetInvoice ( [HttpTrigger(AuthorizationLevel.Function, \"get\", Route = \"invoices/{id}\")] HttpRequest req, string id) => await invoices. FindAsync (id) is { } inv ? new OkObjectResult (inv) : new NotFoundResult (); } Service Bus triggers handle message settlement for you: complete on success; on exception, retry and eventually dead-letter according to the queue's max delivery count — exactly the retry/dead-letter pattern Messaging & events described, here managed entirely by the trigger rather than hand-coded. # Hosting plans Plan Scale Cold start Notes Flex Consumption (recommended) Event-driven, to zero, fast scale-out Reduced; optional always-ready instances VNet integration, per-function scaling, configurable memory Consumption (classic) To zero Yes 5–10 min execution timeout; Linux Consumption is being retired in favour of Flex Premium (Elastic Premium) Pre-warmed instances + burst None (always ready) VNet, longer runs, pricier Dedicated (App Service plan) Like App Service None Predictable cost; reuse an existing plan Container Apps Functions on Container Apps Configurable Unify with other containers Functions pitfalls Cold starts on consumption plans (first request after idle takes seconds: use Flex with always-ready instances or Premium for latency-sensitive HTTP); execution time limits ; don't block or hold state in memory (instances come and go); make triggers idempotent (at-least-once delivery); share HttpClient and SDK clients via DI (singletons), not per invocation; watch concurrency settings so you don't overwhelm downstream databases. # Durable Functions An extension for stateful workflows in code: orchestrator functions call activity functions, and every step's result i"},{"c":"azure-compute","s":"azure-container-apps","h":"Azure Container Apps","t":"# Azure Container Apps Intermediate Container Apps runs containers on a managed, serverless Kubernetes-based platform : you get revisions, ingress, autoscaling (KEDA), Dapr and jobs without operating a cluster — the middle point on the compute spectrum, deliberately positioned to give most of AKS's capability without most of AKS's operational cost. Concept Meaning Environment A secure boundary (shared VNet and Log Analytics) for a group of apps that can talk to each other internally Container app One or more containers (main + sidecars) with ingress, scale rules, secrets and identity Revision An immutable snapshot of an app version. Multiple revisions can run with traffic splitting (e.g. 90/10 canary, blue/green) Ingress External (internet) or internal (environment-only) HTTP/TCP ingress with TLS, custom domains, session affinity options Scale rules KEDA scalers: HTTP concurrency, CPU/memory, Service Bus queue length , Kafka lag, cron… min replicas 0–N Jobs Run-to-completion containers: manual, scheduled (cron) or event-driven (one job per queue message batch) Dapr (optional) Sidecar building blocks: service invocation, pub/sub, state, bindings, secrets Workload profiles Consumption (serverless) or dedicated (reserved hardware, GPUs) Shell Copy az containerapp up -n shop-api -g rg-shop --environment cae-shop \\ --image shopacr.azurecr.io/ shop-api : 1.4.2 --ingress external --target-port 8080 \\ --env-vars ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT =Production ConnectionStrings__Shop = secretref :sql-cs Bicep Copy // Scale a worker on Service Bus queue length; scale to zero when idle scale : { minReplicas : 0 maxReplicas : 20 rules : [ { name : 'orders-queue' custom : { type : 'azure-servicebus' , metadata : { queueName : 'orders' , namespace : 'sb-shop' , messageCount : '50' }, identity : 'system' } } ] } It's a sweet spot for .NET microservices: run an API, a worker, an outbox publisher and scheduled jobs as containers with autoscaling and zero cluster management. .NET Aspire deploys to Container Apps out of the box ( azd up )."},{"c":"azure-compute","s":"azure-kubernetes-service-aks","h":"Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS)","t":"# Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS) Advanced AKS is managed Kubernetes: Azure runs the control plane , and you manage the node pools (VM scale sets) and everything you deploy. Choose it when you need the full Kubernetes API and ecosystem (Helm charts, operators, service meshes, custom controllers), many workloads on a shared platform, multi-cloud consistency, or fine-grained control over networking and scheduling, and you have the platform-engineering capacity to run it. See Kubernetes . Key AKS integrations: Workload Identity (pods get Entra identities), Key Vault CSI driver , ACR integration , Azure CNI networking, KEDA and cluster autoscaler add-ons, the application routing add-on (managed NGINX ingress), Azure Monitor Container Insights and managed Prometheus/Grafana, and AKS Automatic (an opinionated, more managed mode)."},{"c":"azure-compute","s":"virtual-machines-and-others","h":"Virtual machines and others","t":"# Virtual machines and others Advanced Virtual Machines / VM Scale Sets : full OS control. Lift-and-shift of legacy .NET Framework apps on IIS, software that needs special OS configuration, or licensing constraints. You own patching, hardening, backups and scaling. Azure Spring Apps has been retired (it was for Java). Azure Static Web Apps serves SPAs with optional Functions APIs. Azure Batch handles large parallel compute jobs."},{"c":"azure-compute","s":"choosing-compute-a-decision-guide","h":"Choosing compute: a decision guide","t":"# Choosing compute: a decision guide Intermediate Diagram Copy Is it a small piece of code triggered by events/timers, spiky or infrequent? → Azure Functions (Flex Consumption) Long-running stateful workflow, fan-out/in, human approval? → Durable Functions (or Container Apps + Temporal/MassTransit sagas) A web app / API, want the simplest PaaS, maybe a few apps on one plan? → App Service (code or container) Several containerised microservices + workers + jobs, autoscale (to zero), no cluster ops? → Container Apps Need full Kubernetes, large multi-team platform, existing K8s tooling and skills? → AKS Legacy Windows/IIS app, special OS dependencies, lift-and-shift? → VMs (or App Service Windows / Managed Instance for SQL) Interview comparison Key difference App Service vs Container Apps App Service: classic PaaS web hosting with slots, always-on plans, code or containers. Container Apps: containers-first, KEDA event scaling, scale to zero, revisions with traffic splitting, Dapr, jobs: better for microservices and workers Container Apps vs AKS Container Apps hides Kubernetes (no cluster upgrades, node management or K8s API access); AKS exposes the full Kubernetes API with all its power and operational responsibility Functions vs Container Apps Functions: a programming model with triggers/bindings and per-execution billing. Container Apps: any container and any framework (ASP.NET Core, workers), with event-driven scaling via KEDA Functions consumption vs premium Consumption: scale to zero, cold starts, time limits. Premium: pre-warmed instances (no cold start), VNet, longer executions, always paying for minimum instances Q How would you choose between App Service, Container Apps, Functions and AKS? Functions for event-triggered or scheduled small units of work with per-execution billing; App Service for straightforward web apps and APIs with slots and minimal ops; Container Apps for containerised microservices, workers and jobs with KEDA autoscaling and scale to zero without managing Kubernetes; AKS when you need full Kubernetes control and ecosystem and can operate it. Trade control against operational effort. Q What are App Service deployment slots and why use them? Separate live instances of an app (e.g. staging) on the same plan, each with its own hostname and settings. You deploy and warm up in staging, then swap with production for near-zero-downtime releases and instant rollback by swapping back. Sticky settings stay with a slot. Q What's the isolated worker model in Azure Functions? Functions code runs in a separate .NET process from the Functions host, so you choose the .NET version and dependencies and use standard DI, middleware and configuration. It replaces the in-process model, which Microsoft is retiring (check the current end-of-support date before relying on it, since retirement schedules can shift). Q What causes cold starts in Azure Functions and how do you mitigate them? On consumption plans idle apps scale to zero, so the next request must allocate an instance, start the runtime and load your app. Mitigate with Flex Consumption always-ready instances or the Premium plan (pre-warmed), smaller startup work, ReadyToRun/AOT, and avoiding heavy initialisation in the startup path. Q What are Durable Functions? An Azure Functions extension for stateful, long-running workflows written as orchestrator code that calls activity functions. State is checkpointed and replayed, so orchestrations survive restarts. It supports chaining, fan-out/fan-in, timers, external events and human interaction, monitors and entities. Orchestrators must be deterministic. Q How does Azure Container Apps scale? With KEDA-based scale rules: HTTP concurrency, CPU/memory, or event sources like Service Bus queue length, Kafka lag or cron, between min and max replicas, including scaling to zero. Revisions allow traffic splitting for canary and blue/green deployments."},{"c":"azure-compute","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Choose an Azure compute service App Service overview · Deployment slots Azure Functions isolated worker guide · Durable Functions Azure Container Apps overview AKS documentation"},{"c":"azure-compute","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Most of this chapter needs real Azure resources to exercise (App Service, Functions, Container Apps, AKS). What you can verify locally is the mechanism behind two specific claims: that replaying a saved event history is deterministic (why Durable Functions survives a restart), and how a KEDA-style scale rule computes replica count, including scale-to-zero. Run with dotnet run (plain console app, .NET 8/9, BCL only). C# Run ▶ Copy // Claim: a Durable Functions orchestration survives a restart because its progress is saved as a history of // events, and recovery REPLAYS that history to deterministically rebuild the same state. Model that directly: // replaying the same event list twice, from scratch, must produce identical state both times. var history = new List < string > { \"ReserveStock\" , \"ChargePayment\" , \"ShipOrder\" }; var firstReplay = Replay (history); var secondReplay = Replay (history); // simulates \"the process restarted and replayed from the saved history again\" if (firstReplay != secondReplay) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected deterministic replay to produce the same result, got '{firstReplay}' vs '{secondReplay}'\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: replaying the same saved event history twice deterministically reproduces the same state ('{firstReplay}') — this is why an orchestration survives a restart\" ); // Claim: KEDA scale rules compute replica count from an event metric (e.g. queue length) against a threshold, // clamped to [minReplicas, maxReplicas], and CAN scale to zero when minReplicas is 0 and the queue is empty. int ComputeReplicas ( int queueLength, int messageCountPerReplica, int minReplicas, int maxReplicas) { var desired = queueLength == 0 ? 0 : ( int ) Math . Ceiling (queueLength / ( double )messageCountPerReplica); return Math . Clamp (desired, minReplicas, maxReplicas); } var scaledToZero = ComputeReplicas (queueLength: 0 , messageCountPerReplica: 50 , minReplicas: 0 , maxReplicas: 20 ); if (scaledToZero != 0 ) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected an empty queue with minReplicas=0 to scale to zero, got {scaledToZero}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: an empty queue with minReplicas=0 scales to 0 replicas — the scale-to-zero claim\" ); var scaledUp = ComputeReplicas (queueLength: 220 , messageCountPerReplica: 50 , minReplicas: 0 , maxReplicas: 20 ); if (scaledUp != 5 ) // ceil(220/50) = 5 throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected 5 replicas for 220 queued messages at 50/replica, got {scaledUp}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: 220 queued messages at 50/replica scales to {scaledUp} replicas, clamped to [0, 20]\" ); var clampedToMax = ComputeReplicas (queueLength: 5000 , messageCountPerReplica: 50 , minReplicas: 0 , maxReplicas: 20 ); if (clampedToMax != 20 ) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected scaling to clamp at maxReplicas=20, got {clampedToMax}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: a huge backlog clamps at maxReplicas ({clampedToMax}), not an unbounded number of replicas\" ); // Not runtime-verifiable here (needs real Azure resources: App Service, Functions, Container Apps, AKS) — see Further reading: // - Deployment slot swap behavior, App Service tiers // - Real Functions cold starts, hosting plans, isolated worker retirement dates // - Container Apps revisions, traffic splitting, Dapr // - AKS control plane / node pool mechanics Console . WriteLine ( \"All checkable claims verified.\" ); static string Replay ( List < string > eventHistory) { var state = \"start\" ; foreach ( var step in eventHistory) state = $\"{state}->{step}\" ; return state; }"},{"c":"azure-data-messaging","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Every service here is the managed, Azure-hosted version of something already covered from first principles earlier: SQL, NoSQL, Redis, message brokers. Azure SQL Database gives you SQL Server as a managed service, with built-in HA, point-in-time restore, and passwordless auth via managed identity. Blob Storage has four access tiers (Hot → Cool → Cold → Archive) trading storage cost against access cost; lifecycle policies can move blobs between them automatically as they age. Pick Service Bus for reliable business commands/events, Event Grid for lightweight \"something happened\" push notifications, and Event Hubs for a high-volume event firehose — they solve different problems, not competing versions of the same one. Treat every Azure SDK client ( BlobServiceClient , ServiceBusClient , …) like HttpClient : create it once, register it as a singleton, and let it manage its own connections and retries."},{"c":"azure-data-messaging","s":"the-map","h":"The map","t":"# The map Beginner Every concept in this chapter is something earlier chapters already explained from first principles — relational databases, NoSQL, Redis, message brokers. What follows is specifically the managed, Azure-hosted version of each one, and what's different about operating it there. Need Service One-line description Relational (SQL Server engine) Azure SQL Database Fully managed SQL Server database (PaaS); SQL Managed Instance for near-100% compatibility Relational (open source) Azure Database for PostgreSQL – Flexible Server Managed PostgreSQL (also MySQL Flexible Server) Document / NoSQL, global Azure Cosmos DB Multi-model, globally distributed, single-digit-ms, multiple consistency levels Files and objects Blob Storage (in a Storage account) Massively scalable object storage with access tiers Simple queues Storage Queues Cheap, basic queue (64 KB messages) Key/attribute tables Table Storage (or Cosmos DB Table API) Cheap NoSQL key/attribute store SMB/NFS file shares Azure Files Lift-and-shift shared drives Cache Azure Managed Redis (successor of Azure Cache for Redis) Managed Redis for caching, sessions, pub/sub Enterprise messaging Service Bus Queues + topics with sessions, DLQ, transactions Event routing Event Grid Push-based pub/sub for discrete events, reactive integration Event streaming Event Hubs High-throughput, Kafka-compatible event ingestion and streaming Search Azure AI Search Full-text, vector and hybrid search over your data Analytics Fabric / Synapse, Data Explorer Big data and telemetry analytics"},{"c":"azure-data-messaging","s":"azure-sql-database","h":"Azure SQL Database","t":"# Azure SQL Database Intermediate # Deployment and purchasing models Option Notes Single database One isolated database, its own resources. The most common choice Elastic pool Many databases share a pool of resources: SaaS database-per-tenant with varying load SQL Managed Instance Near-complete SQL Server compatibility (SQL Agent, cross-database queries, CLR) for lift-and-shift SQL Server on Azure VMs IaaS: full control, you manage it Purchasing model Tiers vCore (recommended) General Purpose (remote storage, balanced), Business Critical (local SSD, built-in readable replica, lowest latency), Hyperscale (up to 128 TB, fast scaling, multiple read replicas, fast backups/restores) DTU (legacy, bundled) Basic, Standard, Premium Serverless (General Purpose / Hyperscale) Auto-scales compute within a range and can auto-pause when idle: great for dev/test and intermittent workloads (first query after a pause has a delay) # Availability, DR and backups Built-in high availability (99.99% SLA); zone redundancy for General Purpose, Business Critical and Hyperscale. Automated backups with point-in-time restore (7–35 days) plus long-term retention (up to 10 years). Active geo-replication (readable secondaries in other regions) and failover groups (automatic geo-failover behind a stable listener endpoint, so connection strings don't change). Read scale-out : route read-only queries with ApplicationIntent=ReadOnly to a replica. # Security Microsoft Entra authentication , including managed identity : Authentication=Active Directory Default (or Active Directory Managed Identity ) in the connection string, no password. Create contained users from Entra identities and grant roles ( db_datareader , db_datawriter , or custom least-privilege roles). Firewall rules / private endpoints with public access disabled. TDE (encryption at rest, on by default), TLS in transit, Always Encrypted (client-side column encryption), dynamic data masking , row-level security , auditing , Microsoft Defender for SQL (threat detection, vulnerability assessment), and the ledger feature (tamper evidence). Diagram Copy Server=tcp:sql-shop-prod.database.windows.net,1433;Database=shop;Authentication=Active Directory Default;Encrypt=True; SQL Copy -- in the database: grant the app's managed identity least privilege CREATE USER [app-shop-api-prod] FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER; ALTER ROLE db_datareader ADD MEMBER [app-shop-api-prod]; ALTER ROLE db_datawriter ADD MEMBER [app-shop-api-prod]; Transient faults (failovers, reconfigurations, throttling) are a normal part of the PaaS service: always enable retries ( EnableRetryOnFailure in EF Core; see Resilience ) — exactly the transient-vs-permanent fault distinction that chapter's whole opening section is built around. Defaults that differ from on-premises: RCSI is on , Query Store is on, and automatic tuning can create and drop indexes."},{"c":"azure-data-messaging","s":"azure-database-for-postgresql","h":"Azure Database for PostgreSQL","t":"# Azure Database for PostgreSQL Intermediate Flexible Server : managed PostgreSQL with zone-redundant HA, read replicas, PgBouncer built in (connection pooling), extensions ( pgvector , PostGIS, pg_cron), Entra authentication, private networking, and burstable/general-purpose/memory-optimised tiers, plus stop/start for dev. With Npgsql + EF Core, it's a first-class option for .NET, often cheaper than SQL Server licensing."},{"c":"azure-data-messaging","s":"azure-cosmos-db","h":"Azure Cosmos DB","t":"# Azure Cosmos DB Intermediate Covered conceptually in NoSQL & Redis . The Azure-specific essentials: Concept Notes APIs NoSQL (native, recommended for new apps), MongoDB (vCore or RU), Cassandra, Gremlin (graph), Table, and PostgreSQL (distributed via Citus) Account → database → container → items The container is the unit of scale with a partition key Throughput Request Units (RU/s): provisioned (manual/ autoscale ) or serverless ; at database or container level Consistency Strong, Bounded staleness, Session (default), Consistent prefix, Eventual Global distribution Add regions with a click; multi-region writes; automatic failover; 99.999% SLA for multi-region Change feed Ordered stream of changes per partition, consumed by Functions triggers or the change feed processor: materialised views, events, search sync Indexing Automatic indexing of all properties by default (tune policies to cut RU cost); composite, spatial and vector indexes TTL Automatic document expiry Transactions Within one logical partition (transactional batch, stored procedures) Use the singleton CosmosClient , prefer point reads (id + partition key) over queries, log RequestCharge to understand costs, and handle 429 (rate limited) , which the SDK retries automatically within limits."},{"c":"azure-data-messaging","s":"storage-accounts-blob-queue-table-files","h":"Storage accounts: Blob, Queue, Table, Files","t":"# Storage accounts: Blob, Queue, Table, Files Intermediate A Storage account is a namespace providing several services, with redundancy options: LRS (3 copies, one datacentre), ZRS (across zones), GRS/RA-GRS (geo-replicated to the paired region, optionally readable), GZRS/RA-GZRS . # Blob Storage Objects (\"blobs\") in containers : images, documents, exports, backups, logs, static website assets, data lake files (with hierarchical namespace: ADLS Gen2). Access tier For Notes Hot Frequently accessed Highest storage cost, lowest access cost Cool Infrequent (≥ 30 days) Cheaper storage, higher access cost Cold Rare (≥ 90 days) Cheaper still Archive Long-term retention (≥ 180 days) Offline; rehydration takes hours Lifecycle management policies move or delete blobs automatically by age. Soft delete , versioning and immutable storage (WORM, legal hold) protect against accidental and malicious deletion. C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddAzureClients (c => { c. AddBlobServiceClient ( new Uri ( \"https://stshopprod.blob.core.windows.net\" )); c. UseCredential ( new DefaultAzureCredential ()); // managed identity in Azure; developer login locally }); public sealed class InvoiceStorage ( BlobServiceClient blobs) { private readonly BlobContainerClient _container = blobs. GetBlobContainerClient ( \"invoices\" ); public async Task < Uri > SaveAsync ( Guid orderId, Stream pdf, CancellationToken ct) { var blob = _container. GetBlobClient ( $\"{DateTime.UtcNow:yyyy/MM}/{orderId}.pdf\" ); await blob. UploadAsync (pdf, new BlobUploadOptions { HttpHeaders = new () { ContentType = \"application/pdf\" } }, ct); return blob. Uri ; } // Give a browser time-limited direct access without proxying bytes through your API public async Task < Uri > GetDownloadLinkAsync ( string blobName, CancellationToken ct) { var key = await blobs. GetUserDelegationKeyAsync ( DateTimeOffset . UtcNow , DateTimeOffset . UtcNow . AddMinutes ( 15 ), ct); // Entra-backed var sas = new BlobSasBuilder ( BlobSasPermissions . Read , DateTimeOffset . UtcNow . AddMinutes ( 15 )) { BlobContainerName = _container. Name , BlobName = blobName }; return new BlobUriBuilder (_container. GetBlobClient (blobName). Uri ) { Sas = sas. ToSasQueryParameters (key. Value , blobs. AccountName ) }. ToUri (); } } SAS (Shared Access Signature) tokens grant time-limited, permission-scoped access. Prefer user delegation SAS (signed with Entra credentials) over account-key SAS, and prefer disabling shared key access entirely in favour of Entra RBAC ( Storage Blob Data Contributor ). The valet key pattern means clients upload and download directly to Blob using SAS URLs, so large files don't flow through your API — exactly the claim-check pattern Messaging & events recommended for large message payloads, here applied to large HTTP payloads instead. # Queues, Tables, Files Storage Queues : simple, cheap, huge scale, 64 KB messages, at-least-once, 7-day default TTL. No topics, sessions or DLQ (poison queues are handled by Functions). Use for simple work queues; use Service Bus for business messaging. Table Storage : schemaless key/attribute rows by PartitionKey + RowKey. Cheap for simple lookups and logs (Cosmos DB Table API for global distribution). Azure Files : SMB/NFS shares, e.g. legacy apps expecting a network drive, or shared config. Azurite is the local emulator for Blob, Queue and Table (Docker image or VS Code extension)."},{"c":"azure-data-messaging","s":"azure-managed-redis","h":"Azure Managed Redis","t":"# Azure Managed Redis Intermediate Managed Redis (built on Redis Enterprise) succeeds Azure Cache for Redis (the classic Basic/Standard/Premium tiers are on a retirement path). Use it for IDistributedCache / HybridCache L2, sessions, rate limiting, SignalR backplane and output cache storage. Connect with Entra ID authentication (managed identity) via Microsoft.Azure.StackExchangeRedis , use private endpoints, pick tiers by memory and throughput, enable zone redundancy for production, and set eviction policies and TTLs ( NoSQL & Redis )."},{"c":"azure-data-messaging","s":"service-bus-vs-event-grid-vs-event-hubs","h":"Service Bus vs Event Grid vs Event Hubs","t":"# Service Bus vs Event Grid vs Event Hubs Intermediate The classic Azure interview question. They solve different problems — picking between them is really picking between the Command/Event/Query distinction from Messaging & events and a question of volume: Service Bus Event Grid Event Hubs Purpose Enterprise messaging : reliable delivery of business commands and events Event routing : react to discrete events (\"something happened\") Event streaming / ingestion : millions of events per second Model Queues, topics + subscriptions (with filters) Topics → event subscriptions push to handlers (Functions, webhooks, queues, Event Hubs) Partitioned log; consumer groups pull and track offsets Message Up to 256 KB (Standard) / 100 MB (Premium); rich properties Event envelope (CloudEvents/Event Grid schema), up to 1 MB Events up to 1 MB; batches Ordering FIFO with sessions Not guaranteed Per partition Delivery At-least-once; peek-lock , complete/abandon, DLQ , scheduled messages, deferral, duplicate detection , transactions At-least-once push with retries (up to 24 h) and dead-lettering to Blob Retention 1–90 days (or longer in Premium/Dedicated); replay from any offset; Capture to Blob/ADLS Throughput Thousands–tens of thousands msg/s (Premium scales with messaging units) Very high fan-out Very high (throughput/processing units, Kafka endpoint) Typical use Order processing, payments, workflows, competing consumers, sagas Blob created → resize image; resource changed → audit; custom domain events to many subscribers Telemetry, clickstreams, IoT, logs, event-sourcing pipelines, stream analytics Diagram Copy Quick rule: \"Process this business message reliably, exactly one handler, maybe in order\" → Service Bus \"Something happened; notify whoever cares, push-style, lightweight\" → Event Grid \"Ingest a firehose of events/telemetry and let consumers read/replay streams\" → Event Hubs They're often combined: Event Grid routes a BlobCreated event to a Service Bus queue that a worker processes; Event Hubs ingests telemetry that Stream Analytics aggregates. Service Bus tiers: Basic (queues only), Standard (topics, shared capacity), Premium (dedicated messaging units, VNet/private endpoints, larger messages, geo-replication: the choice for production at scale). The Service Bus emulator container runs it locally for development and tests."},{"c":"azure-data-messaging","s":"using-azure-sdk-clients-well-from-net","h":"Using Azure SDK clients well from .NET","t":"# Using Azure SDK clients well from .NET Advanced The modern Azure SDK ( Azure.* packages: Azure.Storage.Blobs , Azure.Messaging.ServiceBus , Azure.Security.KeyVault.Secrets , Microsoft.Azure.Cosmos …) shares conventions: Clients are thread-safe and should be singletons. Create once and reuse (they hold connections) — exactly the HttpClient lesson from Calling other services , applying identically to every Azure SDK client. Register via Microsoft.Extensions.Azure ( AddAzureClients ). Authentication with Azure.Identity : DefaultAzureCredential locally and in Azure, or more specific credentials in production ( ManagedIdentityCredential , WorkloadIdentityCredential ). Built-in retries with exponential backoff ( ClientOptions.Retry ); don't double-wrap with aggressive Polly retries — exactly the retry-storm risk Resilience warned about, here specifically meaning don't retry at two layers for the same SDK call. Logging and tracing integrate with ILogger and OpenTelemetry ( Azure.* ActivitySources). Async APIs and CancellationToken everywhere; paging via AsyncPageable<T> ( await foreach ). Aspire integrations ( builder.AddAzureBlobClient(\"blobs\") , AddAzureServiceBusClient , AddAzureCosmosClient ) set all of this up with health checks. C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddAzureClients (c => { c. AddServiceBusClientWithNamespace ( \"sb-shop-prod.servicebus.windows.net\" ); c. AddSecretClient ( new Uri ( \"https://kv-shop-prod.vault.azure.net/\" )); c. AddBlobServiceClient ( new Uri ( \"https://stshopprod.blob.core.windows.net\" )); c. UseCredential ( new DefaultAzureCredential ()); c. ConfigureDefaults (o => o. Retry . MaxRetries = 5 ); }); Q Service Bus vs Event Grid vs Event Hubs? Service Bus is an enterprise message broker for reliable processing of commands and business events: queues and topics, peek-lock, DLQ, sessions for ordering, transactions, duplicate detection. Event Grid is a push-based event router for reacting to discrete events (resource or custom events) with fan-out to handlers. Event Hubs is a partitioned event-streaming platform for high-volume ingestion, with retention, replay and Kafka compatibility. Q How do you connect an app to Azure SQL without a password? Enable a managed identity on the app, create a contained database user FROM EXTERNAL PROVIDER for that identity and grant least-privilege roles, then use a connection string with Authentication=Active Directory Default (or Managed Identity) via Microsoft.Data.SqlClient. No secrets to store or rotate. Q What are Blob Storage access tiers? Hot (frequent access), Cool (infrequent, ≥30 days), Cold (rare, ≥90 days) and Archive (offline, ≥180 days, rehydration takes hours), each trading storage cost against access cost. Lifecycle management policies move blobs between tiers automatically. Q What is a SAS token and what's the most secure kind? A Shared Access Signature appended to a storage URL granting time-limited, permission-scoped access to specific resources. A user delegation SAS, signed with Entra ID credentials rather than the account key, is the most secure; combine it with short expiry and least permissions, or avoid SAS by using RBAC with managed identity. Q How does Azure SQL provide high availability and disaster recovery? Built-in HA replicas within the region (optionally zone-redundant), automated backups with point-in-time restore and long-term retention, and active geo-replication or failover groups for cross-region DR with a stable listener endpoint. Apps must retry transient errors during failovers. Q Why should Azure SDK clients be singletons? They're thread-safe and manage connections, authentication tokens and retry state. Creating them per request wastes resources and can exhaust connections, similar to the HttpClient anti-pattern."},{"c":"azure-data-messaging","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Azure SQL Database documentation Azure Blob Storage introduction Compare Azure messaging services Azure SDK for .NET Choose a data store (Azure Architecture Center)"},{"c":"azure-data-messaging","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run (plain console app, .NET 8/9, top-level statements, BCL only). Most of this chapter describes facts about managed Azure services rather than local algorithms, so this checks the decision logic those facts imply instead of the services themselves. C# Run ▶ Copy // Claim: the chapter's blob access-tier guidance (Hot / Cool ≥30d / Cold ≥90d / Archive ≥180d) is a // consistent decision rule once you plug in \"days since last access.\" string RecommendedTier ( int daysSinceLastAccess) => daysSinceLastAccess switch { < 30 => \"Hot\" , < 90 => \"Cool\" , < 180 => \"Cold\" , _ => \"Archive\" }; var cases = new ( int Days , string Expected )[] { ( 0 , \"Hot\" ), ( 29 , \"Hot\" ), ( 30 , \"Cool\" ), ( 89 , \"Cool\" ), ( 90 , \"Cold\" ), ( 179 , \"Cold\" ), ( 180 , \"Archive\" ), ( 400 , \"Archive\" ) }; foreach ( var (days, expected) in cases) { var actual = RecommendedTier (days); if (actual != expected) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: {days} days since access should recommend {expected}, got {actual}\" ); } Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: the Hot/Cool/Cold/Archive thresholds (30/90/180 days) form a consistent, non-overlapping rule\" ); // Claim: a time-limited SAS-style token grants access only within its validity window. bool IsAccessAllowed ( DateTimeOffset issuedAt, DateTimeOffset expiresAt, DateTimeOffset attemptAt) => attemptAt >= issuedAt && attemptAt < expiresAt; var issued = DateTimeOffset . UtcNow ; var expires = issued. AddMinutes ( 15 ); if (! IsAccessAllowed (issued, expires, issued. AddMinutes ( 5 ))) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: access within the window should be allowed\" ); if ( IsAccessAllowed (issued, expires, issued. AddMinutes ( 20 ))) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: access after expiry should be denied\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: a time-limited token (the idea behind a SAS URL) only grants access inside its validity window\" ); // Claim: exponential backoff retry delays increase each attempt, up to the configured retry count. TimeSpan [] ExponentialDelays ( int maxRetries, TimeSpan baseDelay) => Enumerable . Range ( 1 , maxRetries). Select (attempt => baseDelay * Math . Pow ( 2 , attempt - 1 )). ToArray (); var delays = ExponentialDelays (maxRetries: 5 , baseDelay: TimeSpan . FromMilliseconds ( 200 )); if (delays. Length != 5 ) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected 5 retry delays, got {delays.Length}\" ); for ( int i = 1 ; i < delays. Length ; i++) if (delays[i] <= delays[i - 1 ]) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: delay #{i + 1} should be longer than delay #{i} (exponential backoff)\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: exponential backoff produces {delays.Length} strictly increasing delays ({string.Join(\" , \", delays.Select(d => d.TotalMilliseconds + \" ms \"))})\" ); // Not runtime-verifiable here — these are facts about managed Azure services, not local computation: // - specific SLA percentages (99.99%, 99.999%), message size limits, retention windows, throughput numbers // - Service Bus vs Event Grid vs Event Hubs feature comparisons // - that Azure SDK clients are thread-safe and should be registered as singletons // See Further reading: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/service-bus-messaging/compare-messaging-services"},{"c":"azure-identity-secrets","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Best option: no secret at all — the app authenticates as an identity (managed identity) that Azure itself issues and rotates, and access is granted through RBAC . Second-best: an unavoidable secret goes in Key Vault , never in source control or config files. A managed identity is a special service principal Azure creates and manages for a resource (App Service, Container Apps, a VM…). Your code just asks a local endpoint for a token; there's nothing to leak because there's no credential to begin with. DefaultAzureCredential tries several credential sources in order (env vars → workload identity → managed identity → developer tools) and uses the first that works — so the same code authenticates as you locally and as the managed identity in Azure. Azure RBAC (who can touch which Azure resource/data) and Entra roles/app permissions (who can administer Entra ID itself, or call an API) are two different permission systems — don't confuse them. Workload identity federation extends \"no stored secret\" to places that aren't an Azure resource at all: GitHub Actions, Kubernetes pods — an external token gets exchanged for an Entra token."},{"c":"azure-identity-secrets","s":"the-problem-secrets-everywhere","h":"The problem: secrets everywhere","t":"# The problem: secrets everywhere Beginner Traditional apps authenticate to their dependencies with secrets : SQL passwords in connection strings, storage account keys, API keys, client secrets. Secrets get committed to Git , pasted in chat, logged , copied to laptops, and are rarely rotated . A leaked secret is a breach — and recall from Configuration that \"no secret at all\" was already flagged as the best entry in that chapter's table. This chapter is entirely about how Azure actually delivers on that promise. The modern Azure answer, in order of preference: No secret at all : apps authenticate with an identity issued by Microsoft Entra ID (managed identity), and access is granted with RBAC . If a secret is unavoidable (a third-party API key), store it in Key Vault , accessed by managed identity, and rotate it. Never in source control, images, or plain configuration files."},{"c":"azure-identity-secrets","s":"microsoft-entra-id-concepts","h":"Microsoft Entra ID concepts","t":"# Microsoft Entra ID concepts Beginner Microsoft Entra ID (formerly Azure Active Directory / Azure AD ) is Microsoft's cloud identity provider: it authenticates users and applications and issues tokens (OAuth 2.0 / OpenID Connect) — exactly the kind of identity provider Auth warned against building yourself. Concept Meaning Tenant A dedicated instance of Entra ID for an organisation ( contoso.onmicrosoft.com ); the identity boundary User / Group People, and collections of them for assigning access App registration The definition of an application in a tenant: client ID, redirect URIs, exposed API scopes, app roles, credentials (secret/certificate/federated) Service principal (enterprise application) The instance of an app in a tenant: the security principal that gets permissions and role assignments Managed identity A special service principal managed by Azure for an Azure resource: no credentials for you to handle Scopes / app roles Permissions an API exposes ( orders.read ) / roles assigned to users or apps ( Orders.Admin ) Conditional Access, MFA Policies on sign-in (require MFA, compliant device, location) Entra External ID Customer-facing identity (CIAM) for your app's end users (successor of Azure AD B2C) Two kinds of permissions are often confused, and keeping them distinct is worth doing deliberately: Azure RBAC Entra roles / app permissions Controls access to Azure resources (Storage, SQL, Key Vault, subscriptions) Entra ID itself and Microsoft Graph / your APIs Example \"app-shop-api can read blobs in stshopprod\" \"user is Global Administrator\", \"app can call Graph User.Read.All\", \"client may call api://shop orders.write\""},{"c":"azure-identity-secrets","s":"managed-identities-the-mechanism-that-makes-no-secret-actual","h":"Managed identities: the mechanism that makes \"no secret\" actually work","t":"# Managed identities: the mechanism that makes \"no secret\" actually work Beginner A managed identity is an identity in Entra ID that Azure creates and manages for a resource (App Service, Functions, Container Apps, AKS workloads, VMs…). Your code asks the platform's local endpoint for a token; Azure handles the credentials and rotates them automatically. There's no secret to store, leak or rotate — not \"a secret that's harder to steal,\" genuinely none exists for an attacker to find. System-assigned User-assigned Lifecycle Tied to one resource; deleted with it Standalone resource; you manage its lifecycle Sharing One resource only Can be assigned to many resources Use when A simple one app → its resources setup Several apps or instances need the same permissions; pre-provisioning access before the app exists; blue/green; AKS workload identity Shell Copy # Enable a system-assigned identity and grant it data-plane access (least privilege, scoped narrowly) az webapp identity assign -g rg-shop -n app-shop-api-prod az role assignment create --assignee <principalId> --role \"Storage Blob Data Contributor\" \\ --scope /subscriptions/<sub>/resourceGroups/rg-shop/providers/Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/stshopprod/blobServices/default/containers/invoices az role assignment create --assignee <principalId> --role \"Azure Service Bus Data Sender\" --scope <service-bus-namespace-id> az role assignment create --assignee <principalId> --role \"Key Vault Secrets User\" --scope <key-vault-id> Services supporting Entra (managed identity) auth include Azure SQL, PostgreSQL, Storage (Blob/Queue/Table), Service Bus, Event Hubs, Event Grid, Cosmos DB (data-plane RBAC), Key Vault, App Configuration, Managed Redis, Azure OpenAI, ACR and more."},{"c":"azure-identity-secrets","s":"defaultazurecredential-and-azure-identity-one-line-of-code-t","h":"DefaultAzureCredential and Azure.Identity: one line of code, two environments","t":"# DefaultAzureCredential and Azure.Identity: one line of code, two environments Intermediate The Azure.Identity library provides TokenCredential implementations that every Azure SDK client accepts. DefaultAzureCredential tries a chain in order and uses the first that works: Diagram Copy 1. EnvironmentCredential (AZURE_CLIENT_ID / AZURE_TENANT_ID / AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET or certificate env vars) 2. WorkloadIdentityCredential (Kubernetes workload identity, federated token file) 3. ManagedIdentityCredential (App Service, Functions, Container Apps, VMs, AKS) 4. SharedTokenCacheCredential / VisualStudioCredential / VisualStudioCodeCredential 5. AzureCliCredential (az login) 6. AzurePowerShellCredential, AzureDeveloperCliCredential (azd auth login) 7. InteractiveBrowserCredential (disabled by default) Notice what this chain actually buys you: the same code authenticates as you on your laptop (via Visual Studio or az login , step 4 or 5) and as the managed identity in Azure (step 3) — no #if DEBUG , no environment-specific branching in application code, just the credential chain quietly resolving to whichever mechanism is actually available in a given environment. C# Run ▶ Copy var credential = new DefaultAzureCredential (); var blobs = new BlobServiceClient ( new Uri ( \"https://stshopprod.blob.core.windows.net\" ), credential); var bus = new ServiceBusClient ( \"sb-shop-prod.servicebus.windows.net\" , credential); var secrets = new SecretClient ( new Uri ( \"https://kv-shop-prod.vault.azure.net/\" ), credential); DefaultAzureCredential in production The chain is convenient but can be slow to fail (trying each option) and can pick up an unexpected credential (an environment variable left on a host). For production, consider a deterministic credential: new ManagedIdentityCredential(ManagedIdentityId.FromUserAssignedClientId(clientId)) , or ChainedTokenCredential with exactly what you expect, or exclude options via DefaultAzureCredentialOptions . With user-assigned identities, specify the client ID . Also give developers the same RBAC roles on dev resources so local runs work."},{"c":"azure-identity-secrets","s":"azure-rbac-in-practice","h":"Azure RBAC in practice","t":"# Azure RBAC in practice Intermediate A role assignment = security principal (user, group, service principal, managed identity) + role definition (a set of allowed actions) + scope (management group, subscription, resource group, resource, or even a sub-resource like a blob container) — notice this is the exact same scope hierarchy from Cloud fundamentals , with roles inheriting downward through it. Plane Roles control Examples Control plane (management) Creating/configuring resources via ARM Owner, Contributor, Reader, Website Contributor Data plane Accessing the data inside Storage Blob Data Reader/Contributor , Azure Service Bus Data Sender/Receiver , Key Vault Secrets User , Cosmos DB Built-in Data Contributor , App Configuration Data Reader The control-plane-vs-data-plane distinction matters more than it looks: Contributor on a storage account lets you reconfigure the account itself but says nothing about reading the blobs inside it — that's a separate data-plane role entirely, and granting an app's managed identity Contributor when it only ever needs to read blobs is a textbook least-privilege violation. Principles: least privilege (data roles, not Contributor; the narrowest scope); groups for humans, managed identities for apps; separate identities per app and environment ; PIM (Privileged Identity Management) for just-in-time elevation of admin roles; review assignments regularly."},{"c":"azure-identity-secrets","s":"azure-key-vault","h":"Azure Key Vault","t":"# Azure Key Vault Intermediate Key Vault securely stores and controls access to: Object Examples Notes Secrets Third-party API keys, connection strings for non-Entra systems, passwords Versioned; expiry dates; rotation via Event Grid notifications Keys RSA/EC keys for encryption and signing (customer-managed keys, JWT signing, Data Protection key-ring protection) Operations happen inside the vault/HSM; keys never leave (Premium: HSM-backed; Managed HSM for FIPS 140-3 Level 3 — see the Key Vault basic concepts link in Further reading for current certification details) Certificates TLS certificates with automatic renewal from integrated CAs Used by App Service, App Gateway, Front Door Access with Azure RBAC (recommended; e.g. Key Vault Secrets User for apps that read secrets) or legacy access policies. Enable soft delete and purge protection , use private endpoints , and log access to Log Analytics. # Using Key Vault from .NET C# Run ▶ Copy // 1. As a configuration provider: secrets become IConfiguration keys (\"Payments--ApiKey\" → \"Payments:ApiKey\") builder. Configuration . AddAzureKeyVault ( new Uri ( $\"https://{builder.Configuration[\" KeyVaultName \"]}.vault.azure.net/\" ), new DefaultAzureCredential (), new AzureKeyVaultConfigurationOptions { ReloadInterval = TimeSpan . FromMinutes ( 30 ) }); builder. Services . AddOptions < PaymentOptions >(). BindConfiguration ( \"Payments\" ). ValidateOnStart (); // 2. As a client, for on-demand access var secret = await secretClient. GetSecretAsync ( \"StripeApiKey\" , cancellationToken: ct); // 3. Protect the ASP.NET Core Data Protection key ring (shared across instances) builder. Services . AddDataProtection () . PersistKeysToAzureBlobStorage ( new Uri ( \"https://stshopprod.blob.core.windows.net/dataprotection/keys.xml\" ), new DefaultAzureCredential ()) . ProtectKeysWithAzureKeyVault ( new Uri ( \"https://kv-shop-prod.vault.azure.net/keys/dataprotection\" ), new DefaultAzureCredential ()); Diagram Copy # 4. Platform references: no code at all. App Service / Functions / Container Apps resolve the secret at startup. Payments__ApiKey = @Microsoft.KeyVault(SecretUri=https://kv-shop-prod.vault.azure.net/secrets/StripeApiKey/) Notice option 3 solves a problem raised all the way back in Auth : Data Protection keys have to be shared across instances or cookies break on scale-out, and Key Vault plus Blob Storage is exactly how that shared key ring gets protected and distributed in production. Key Vault is not a high-throughput database Key Vault has throttling limits (thousands of operations per 10 seconds per vault). Load secrets at startup (config provider) and cache them; don't call GetSecretAsync on every request."},{"c":"azure-identity-secrets","s":"workload-identity-federation-ci-cd-and-kubernetes","h":"Workload identity federation: CI/CD and Kubernetes","t":"# Workload identity federation: CI/CD and Kubernetes Advanced Outside Azure-hosted resources (GitHub Actions, Kubernetes pods, other clouds), managed identities don't exist directly — there's no Azure resource for Azure to attach an identity to. Workload identity federation closes that gap by letting an external identity provider's token be exchanged for an Entra token, again with no stored secret : Diagram Copy GitHub Actions job ──(OIDC token: repo:org/shop:environment:prod)──▶ Entra ID (federated credential trusts that subject) ──▶ access token for the app's service principal/managed identity ──▶ az / azd / Bicep deploy YAML Copy permissions : { id-token : write, contents : read } steps : - uses : azure/login@v2 with : client-id : ${{ vars.AZURE_CLIENT_ID }} # no client secret stored in GitHub! tenant-id : ${{ vars.AZURE_TENANT_ID }} subscription-id : ${{ vars.AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID }} AKS Workload Identity : a Kubernetes service account is federated with a user-assigned managed identity, so pods get Entra tokens via WorkloadIdentityCredential (part of DefaultAzureCredential ). The same pattern works for Azure DevOps service connections, other clouds and on-premises with Azure Arc."},{"c":"azure-identity-secrets","s":"protecting-your-own-apis-with-entra-id","h":"Protecting your own APIs with Entra ID","t":"# Protecting your own APIs with Entra ID Advanced Diagram Copy 1. App registration \"Shop API\": Application ID URI api://shop-api; expose scopes (orders.read, orders.write); define app roles (Orders.Admin). 2. App registration \"Shop SPA\" (public client) → API permission: api://shop-api/orders.read (delegated, user context). 3. Service \"Billing worker\" (managed identity) → app role assignment Orders.Reader on Shop API (application permission, no user). 4. Shop API validates tokens: issuer = tenant, audience = api://shop-api, then authorizes on scp (delegated) or roles (application). Step 4 here is exactly the five-step JWT validation walked through in Auth — Entra ID is simply the issuer whose discovery document and signing keys get fetched. C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddAuthentication ( JwtBearerDefaults . AuthenticationScheme ) . AddMicrosoftIdentityWebApi (builder. Configuration . GetSection ( \"AzureAd\" )); // Microsoft.Identity.Web builder. Services . AddAuthorizationBuilder () . AddPolicy ( \"ReadOrders\" , p => p. RequireAssertion (ctx => ctx. User . HasClaim ( \"scp\" , \"orders.read\" ) || ctx. User . IsInRole ( \"Orders.Reader\" ))); // user OR app permission // Calling another API as the service itself (client credentials via managed identity) var token = await new ManagedIdentityCredential (). GetTokenAsync ( new TokenRequestContext ([ \"api://inventory-api/.default\" ]), ct); Details of the flows are in OAuth 2.0 & OIDC . Q What is a managed identity and why use it? An Entra ID identity that Azure creates and manages for a resource (App Service, Functions, Container Apps, VMs, AKS workloads). The app obtains tokens from a local endpoint with no credentials to store or rotate, and is granted least-privilege access via RBAC. It eliminates secrets in configuration. Q System-assigned vs user-assigned managed identity? System-assigned is created for and tied to one resource's lifecycle. User-assigned is a standalone resource that can be attached to many resources and survives them. Use it when several apps or instances share permissions, to pre-provision access, or for AKS workload identity. Q How does DefaultAzureCredential work? It tries a sequence of credential types (environment variables, workload identity, managed identity, then developer tools like Visual Studio, Azure CLI and azd) and uses the first that succeeds. The same code authenticates as the developer locally and as the managed identity in Azure. In production you may prefer a specific credential for determinism and speed. Q What is the difference between an app registration and a service principal? An app registration is the global definition of an application (client ID, redirect URIs, exposed scopes and roles, credentials). A service principal (enterprise application) is its instance in a specific tenant, the security principal that receives role assignments and consents. Managed identities are a special kind of service principal. Q How do you store and use secrets in an Azure-hosted .NET app? Prefer no secrets: managed identity with RBAC for Azure services. For unavoidable secrets, store them in Key Vault, grant the app's managed identity the Key Vault Secrets User role, and load them via the Key Vault configuration provider or platform Key Vault references, caching them rather than calling the vault per request. Use User Secrets in development. Q How can GitHub Actions deploy to Azure without storing a client secret? Workload identity federation (OIDC): configure a federated credential on an Entra app or user-assigned managed identity that trusts the GitHub repository/environment subject. The workflow requests an OIDC token (id-token: write permission) and azure/login exchanges it for an Azure access token. Q Azure RBAC vs Entra roles? Azure RBAC controls access to Azure resources and their data (subscriptions, storage, Key Vault), using role assignments at a scope. Entra roles control administration of the Entra directory itself, and API permissions/app "},{"c":"azure-identity-secrets","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading What are managed identities for Azure resources? Authenticate .NET apps to Azure services Azure Key Vault basic concepts Workload identity federation Microsoft identity platform documentation"},{"c":"azure-identity-secrets","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Real Entra ID, Key Vault and workload identity federation behaviour needs an actual Azure tenant, so most of this chapter can't be verified by a console program. The checks below instead model the three mechanisms this chapter describes — the credential chain's fallback order, caching to avoid throttling, and RBAC scope inheritance — with plain stand-ins. Save as Program.cs in a new console project ( dotnet new console ) and run with dotnet run . C# Run ▶ Copy // Verifies claims from this chapter that are mechanism-level, without needing a // real Azure tenant: DefaultAzureCredential's \"try each source in order, use the first that works\" // chain, \"load secrets once at startup and cache them\" vs. hitting Key Vault's per-request throttling // limit, and RBAC role assignments inheriting DOWN a scope hierarchy but not up. // Real Entra ID / Key Vault / workload identity federation behaviour needs an actual Azure tenant and // isn't runtime-verifiable here — see Further reading (learn.microsoft.com/entra, /azure/key-vault) for those. using System ; using System . Collections . Generic ; int fails = 0 ; void Ok ( string s) => Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: {s}\" ); void Check ( bool cond, string s) { if (cond) Ok (s); else { Console . WriteLine ( $\"FAIL: {s}\" ); fails++; } } // --- DefaultAzureCredential-style chain: try sources in order, use the first that succeeds --- var tried = new List < string >(); var chain = new CredentialChain ( ( \"EnvironmentCredential\" , () => { tried. Add ( \"EnvironmentCredential\" ); throw new InvalidOperationException ( \"no env vars set\" ); }), ( \"WorkloadIdentityCredential\" , () => { tried. Add ( \"WorkloadIdentityCredential\" ); throw new InvalidOperationException ( \"not running in Kubernetes\" ); }), ( \"ManagedIdentityCredential\" , () => { tried. Add ( \"ManagedIdentityCredential\" ); return \"token-from-managed-identity\" ; }), ( \"AzureCliCredential\" , () => { tried. Add ( \"AzureCliCredential\" ); return \"token-from-az-login\" ; })); var token = chain. GetToken (); Check (token == \"token-from-managed-identity\" , \"DefaultAzureCredential-style chain: the first source that actually succeeds provides the token\" ); Check (tried. Count == 3 && tried[^ 1 ] == \"ManagedIdentityCredential\" , \"DefaultAzureCredential-style chain: sources that fail are tried in order, and the chain stops at the first success\" ); // --- Load secrets once at startup and cache them, rather than calling the vault on every request --- var vault = new ThrottledVault (callBudget: 1 ); var cachingClient = new CachingSecretClient (vault); for ( int i = 0 ; i < 100 ; i++) cachingClient. GetSecret ( \"StripeApiKey\" ); // 100 \"requests\" reusing the cached value Check (vault. CallsMade == 1 , \"Caching the secret at startup means 100 reads cost Key Vault exactly 1 call, not 100\" ); var uncachedClient = new UncachedSecretClient ( new ThrottledVault (callBudget: 1 )); Check ( Throws (() => { for ( int i = 0 ; i < 2 ; i++) uncachedClient. GetSecret ( \"StripeApiKey\" ); }), \"Without caching, calling GetSecretAsync per request can exhaust a tight call budget (simulating Key Vault throttling)\" ); // --- RBAC: a role assigned at a broader scope is visible at a nested scope, not the other way round --- var tree = new ScopeTree (); tree. AssignRole (scope: \"/sub/rg-shop\" , role: \"Storage Blob Data Reader\" ); Check (tree. HasRole (scope: \"/sub/rg-shop/storageAccounts/stshopprod\" , role: \"Storage Blob Data Reader\" ), \"RBAC: a role assigned at the resource group scope is inherited by resources nested inside it\" ); Check (!tree. HasRole (scope: \"/sub\" , role: \"Storage Blob Data Reader\" ), \"RBAC: a role assigned at a narrower scope does NOT apply to the broader scope above it\" ); Console . WriteLine (fails == 0 ? \"\\nAll checks passed.\" : $\"\\n{fails} check(s) failed.\" ); if (fails > 0 ) Environment . Exit ( 1 ); static bool Throws ( Action a) { try { a(); return false ; } catch { return true ; } } sealed class CredentialChain ( params ( string Name "},{"c":"cicd-iac","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR CI : every push is built and tested automatically. Continuous delivery : every passing change could ship, but a human decides when. Continuous deployment : passing changes ship automatically, no human gate. Build the deployable artefact (container image, zip) once , then promote the exact same artefact through dev → staging → prod. Only configuration changes between environments. Deployment strategies trade complexity for a smaller blast radius if something's wrong: recreate (riskiest, simplest) → rolling → blue/green (instant switch, instant rollback) → canary (small % of traffic first) → feature flags (ship dark, turn on later). Database schema changes during rolling/blue-green deploys need expand → migrate → contract : add the new shape first (compatibly), deploy code that works with both old and new, backfill, then remove the old shape in a later release — never in the same release that stops using it. Infrastructure as Code (Bicep, Terraform) means infra lives in versioned, reviewed code instead of manual portal clicks — the same reproducibility argument as \"define your app in code,\" now applied to the infra underneath it."},{"c":"cicd-iac","s":"ci-cd-and-cd","h":"CI, CD and CD","t":"# CI, CD and CD Beginner Practice Definition Gate Continuous Integration (CI) Everyone integrates into the main branch frequently (at least daily); every push triggers an automated build and tests A broken build is fixed immediately Continuous Delivery Every change that passes the pipeline is releasable ; deploying to production is a business decision (a button / approval) Manual approval to prod Continuous Deployment Every passing change is deployed to production automatically No human gate; relies on strong tests, monitoring and progressive delivery Why it matters Small, frequent, automated releases are less risky than big, rare, manual ones: each change is small enough to understand and roll back, problems surface within minutes of being written, and the process is the same every time (no \"works when Dave deploys it\"). The DORA metrics (deployment frequency, lead time for changes, change failure rate, time to restore) measure delivery performance; the research behind them — elite teams deploy on demand with low failure rates — is documented in Accelerate (Forsgren, Humble, Kim), cited in Further reading below."},{"c":"cicd-iac","s":"branching-strategies","h":"Branching strategies","t":"# Branching strategies Beginner Strategy How Fits Trunk-based development Short-lived branches (hours to a day) or direct commits to main ; feature flags hide unfinished work CI/CD, high-velocity teams (recommended by DORA research) GitHub Flow main is always deployable; feature branch → PR (review + CI checks) → merge → deploy Most web/API teams GitFlow develop , feature/* , release/* , hotfix/* , main Versioned products with scheduled releases; heavyweight for services Release branches Cut release/2026.10 for stabilisation; fixes cherry-picked Libraries, on-premises products PR hygiene: branch protection on main (required reviews, required status checks: build, tests, analyzers, security scans), small PRs, conventional commit messages, and automated dependency updates (Dependabot/Renovate)."},{"c":"cicd-iac","s":"anatomy-of-a-net-pipeline","h":"Anatomy of a .NET pipeline","t":"# Anatomy of a .NET pipeline Intermediate Diagram Copy push/PR ─▶ CI ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────▶ merge to main ─▶ CD ───────────────────────────────────────────▶ restore → build (warnings as errors) → unit tests → integration build image once → push to ACR (tag = git SHA) tests (Testcontainers) → analyzers/format → coverage → SCA/SAST → deploy to dev → smoke tests → deploy staging (slot/revision) → (PR) report status → migrations → smoke/E2E → approval → prod (swap/canary) → monitor Build once, deploy many : the same artefact (container image digest or zip) is promoted through environments; only configuration differs — exactly the \"promote the same image\" principle from Docker , now the organising idea for the whole pipeline rather than just one chapter's advice."},{"c":"cicd-iac","s":"github-actions-a-complete-example","h":"GitHub Actions: a complete example","t":"# GitHub Actions: a complete example Intermediate YAML Copy # .github/workflows/shop-api.yml name : shop-api on : push : branches : [main] pull_request : branches : [main] permissions : contents : read id-token : write # for OIDC login to Azure (no stored secrets) env : DOTNET_VERSION : 10.0 .x IMAGE : shopacr.azurecr.io/shop-api jobs : build-test : runs-on : ubuntu-latest steps : - uses : actions/checkout@v4 - uses : actions/setup-dotnet@v4 with : dotnet-version : ${{ env.DOTNET_VERSION }} cache : true # NuGet cache keyed on lock files - run : dotnet restore --locked-mode - run : dotnet build --no-restore -c Release -warnaserror - run : dotnet format --verify-no-changes --no-restore - run : dotnet test --no-build -c Release --logger trx --collect : \"XPlat Code Coverage\" # Testcontainers works: Docker is available - uses : actions/upload-artifact@v4 if : always() with : { name : test-results, path : \"**/TestResults/**\" } - run : dotnet list package --vulnerable --include-transitive 2 >& 1 | tee vuln.txt && ! grep -q \"has the following vulnerable packages\" vuln.txt image : needs : build-test if : github.ref == 'refs/heads/main' runs-on : ubuntu-latest outputs : tag : ${{ steps.meta.outputs.tag }} steps : - uses : actions/checkout@v4 - uses : azure/login@v2 with : client-id : ${{ vars.AZURE_CLIENT_ID }} tenant-id : ${{ vars.AZURE_TENANT_ID }} subscription-id : ${{ vars.AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID }} - run : az acr login -n shopacr - id : meta run : echo \"tag=${GITHUB_SHA::12}\" >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\" - run : | docker build -t $ IMAGE : ${{ steps.meta.outputs.tag }} . docker push $ IMAGE : ${{ steps.meta.outputs.tag }} - uses : aquasecurity/trivy-action@ 0.28.0 # image vulnerability scan with : { image-ref : \"${{ env.IMAGE }}:${{ steps.meta.outputs.tag }}\" , severity : \"CRITICAL,HIGH\" , exit-code : \"1\" } deploy-staging : needs : image runs-on : ubuntu-latest environment : staging # environment-scoped vars/secrets + optional protection rules steps : - uses : actions/checkout@v4 - uses : azure/login@v2 with : { client-id : \"${{ vars.AZURE_CLIENT_ID }}\" , tenant-id : \"${{ vars.AZURE_TENANT_ID }}\" , subscription-id : \"${{ vars.AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID }}\" } - name : Apply database migrations (bundle) run : | dotnet tool restore dotnet ef migrations bundle -p src/Shop.Infrastructure -s src/Shop.Api -o efbundle --self-contained -r linux-x64 ./efbundle --connection \"${{ secrets.SQL_MIGRATION_CS }}\" - name : Deploy new revision run : az containerapp update -n shop-api -g rg-shop-staging --image $ IMAGE : ${{ needs.image.outputs.tag }} - name : Smoke test run : curl --fail --retry 10 --retry-delay 6 https : //shop-api-staging.example.com/health/ready deploy-prod : needs : [image, deploy-staging] runs-on : ubuntu-latest environment : production # required reviewers = manual approval gate (continuous DELIVERY) steps : - uses : azure/login@v2 with : { client-id : \"${{ vars.AZURE_CLIENT_ID }}\" , tenant-id : \"${{ vars.AZURE_TENANT_ID }}\" , subscription-id : \"${{ vars.AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID }}\" } - run : az containerapp update -n shop-api -g rg-shop-prod --image $ IMAGE : ${{ needs.image.outputs.tag }} --revision-suffix ${{ needs.image.outputs.tag }} Notice deploy-staging runs the EF migration bundle and curl s the readiness endpoint before deploy-prod is even allowed to start — the pipeline itself enforces exactly the \"migrate, then verify, then promote\" sequencing that a human following a runbook could forget under pressure. Concepts: workflows (YAML in .github/workflows ), triggers ( on: ), jobs (run in parallel unless needs: ), steps , actions (reusable steps from the marketplace), runners (GitHub-hosted or self-hosted), environments (protection rules, approvals, scoped secrets), matrix builds , reusable workflows and composite actions for sharing across repos, and caching ."},{"c":"cicd-iac","s":"azure-devops-pipelines","h":"Azure DevOps Pipelines","t":"# Azure DevOps Pipelines Intermediate Many enterprises use Azure DevOps (Boards, Repos, Pipelines , Artifacts, Test Plans). YAML pipelines are structured as stages → jobs → steps , with tasks instead of actions, service connections (use workload identity federation), environments with approvals and checks, variable groups (optionally linked to Key Vault) and templates for reuse. YAML Copy # azure-pipelines.yml trigger : { branches : { include : [main] } } pool : { vmImage : ubuntu-latest } stages : - stage : Build jobs : - job : build steps : - task : UseDotNet@ 2 inputs : { version : 10.0 .x } - script : dotnet build -c Release -warnaserror - script : dotnet test -c Release --no-build --logger trx - task : PublishTestResults@ 2 inputs : { testResultsFormat : VSTest, testResultsFiles : \"**/*.trx\" } - task : Docker@ 2 inputs : { containerRegistry : acr-connection, repository : shop-api, command : buildAndPush, tags : $(Build.SourceVersion) } - stage : Prod dependsOn : Build jobs : - deployment : deploy environment : shop-prod # approvals & checks configured on the environment strategy : runOnce : deploy : steps : - task : AzureWebAppContainer@ 1 inputs : { azureSubscription : azure-prod-wif, appName : app-shop-api-prod, containers : \"shopacr.azurecr.io/shop-api:$(Build.SourceVersion)\" }"},{"c":"cicd-iac","s":"deployment-strategies","h":"Deployment strategies","t":"# Deployment strategies Intermediate Strategy How Rollback Azure implementation Recreate Stop old, start new Redeploy old Simple; causes downtime Rolling Replace instances gradually Roll forward/back gradually Kubernetes Deployments, App Service multi-instance Blue/green Run the new version alongside, switch all traffic at once Switch back instantly App Service slot swap , Container Apps revisions (100% switch), two AKS deployments + service selector Canary Send a small % of traffic to the new version, increase while metrics stay healthy Route 0% to canary Container Apps traffic splitting , App Service \"testing in production\" (slot traffic %), Front Door weights, service mesh / Argo Rollouts / Flagger Feature flags Deploy code dark; enable features per user/percentage at runtime Turn the flag off Azure App Configuration + Microsoft.FeatureManagement ( Configuration ) Ring deployment Internal users → early adopters → everyone (or region by region) Halt the rollout Multiple environments/regions in the pipeline Each row along this table trades a little more deployment complexity for a progressively smaller blast radius if something goes wrong — Recreate risks the whole user base at once, Canary risks a deliberately small slice, and progressive delivery pushes that idea furthest by combining canaries and flags with automated health analysis (error rate, latency SLOs from Application Insights or Prometheus) to promote or roll back without a human in the loop at all."},{"c":"cicd-iac","s":"databases-in-the-pipeline","h":"Databases in the pipeline","t":"# Databases in the pipeline Advanced Schema changes are the hardest part of continuous deployment, because old and new code run side by side during a rolling or blue/green deploy — the exact scenario that forced EF Core fundamentals to introduce the expand/contract pattern in the first place: Expand → migrate → contract : (1) expand: add new columns/tables, nullable or with defaults, compatible with the old code; (2) deploy code that writes both and reads new; (3) backfill data; (4) contract: remove old columns in a later release. Never rename or drop a column in the same release that stops using it. Apply migrations with idempotent scripts or EF migration bundles as a pipeline step before deploying the app (with a dedicated migration identity that has DDL rights; the app identity gets only DML). For DBA-managed schemas: SQL projects (DACPAC) , DbUp, Flyway, Liquibase. Back up or verify point-in-time restore before risky migrations, and test migrations against a production-like copy."},{"c":"cicd-iac","s":"versioning-and-artefacts","h":"Versioning and artefacts","t":"# Versioning and artefacts Advanced SemVer ( MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH ) for libraries and public APIs; services often use build numbers or git SHA tags for images (immutable, traceable). Tools: MinVer / Nerdbank.GitVersioning / GitVersion derive versions from Git tags or height. Set <Version> , AssemblyInformationalVersion (include the commit SHA), and expose it on /health or /version and in telemetry ( service.version ). Store build outputs in a registry (ACR, NuGet feed), with retention policies . Never deploy something that wasn't built by the pipeline. Supply chain security : SBOM generation, signed images and provenance (SLSA), pinned action versions (by SHA for third-party actions), least-privilege workflow permissions , and secret scanning (GitHub push protection)."},{"c":"cicd-iac","s":"infrastructure-as-code","h":"Infrastructure as Code","t":"# Infrastructure as Code Intermediate IaC defines infrastructure (resource groups, App Service plans, Container Apps environments, SQL servers, Key Vaults, role assignments, networking) in versioned code , reviewed in PRs and applied by pipelines. Benefit Explanation Reproducibility dev = staging = prod, created from the same templates with different parameters Reviewability Infrastructure changes go through PRs like code No drift Manual portal changes are overwritten or detected ( what-if / plan ) Disaster recovery Recreate an environment from code Documentation The code is the up-to-date description Approaches: declarative (describe the desired end state; the tool works out the changes: Bicep, ARM, Terraform) vs imperative (scripts of commands: az CLI, PowerShell). Prefer declarative and idempotent (running it twice changes nothing the second time) — the exact same idempotency principle API design cared about for HTTP, here applied to infrastructure deployments. # Bicep Azure's native DSL that compiles to ARM JSON: concise, type-safe, day-zero support for new Azure features, no state file (Azure itself is the state). Bicep Copy // main.bicep targetScope = 'resourceGroup' param location string = resourceGroup().location @allowed([ 'dev' , 'staging' , 'prod' ]) param env string param imageTag string var prefix = 'shop-${env}' resource identity 'Microsoft.ManagedIdentity/userAssignedIdentities@2023-01-31' = { name : 'id-${prefix}-api' location : location } resource logs 'Microsoft.OperationalInsights/workspaces@2023-09-01' = { name : 'log-${prefix}' location : location properties : { sku : { name : 'PerGB2018' }, retentionInDays : 30 } } resource cae 'Microsoft.App/managedEnvironments@2024-03-01' = { name : 'cae-${prefix}' location : location properties : { appLogsConfiguration : { destination : 'log-analytics' logAnalyticsConfiguration : { customerId : logs.properties.customerId, sharedKey : logs.listKeys().primarySharedKey } } zoneRedundant : env == 'prod' } } resource api 'Microsoft.App/containerApps@2024-03-01' = { name : 'ca-${prefix}-api' location : location identity : { type : 'UserAssigned' , userAssignedIdentities : { '${identity.id}' : {} } } properties : { managedEnvironmentId : cae.id configuration : { ingress : { external : true, targetPort : 8080 } registries : [ { server : 'shopacr.azurecr.io' , identity : identity.id } ] } template : { containers : [ { name : 'api' image : 'shopacr.azurecr.io/shop-api:${imageTag}' resources : { cpu : json( '0.5' ), memory : '1Gi' } env : [ { name : 'ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT' , value : env == 'prod' ? 'Production' : 'Staging' } { name : 'AZURE_CLIENT_ID' , value : identity.properties.clientId } ] probes : [ { type : 'Readiness' , httpGet : { path : '/health/ready' , port : 8080 } } ] } ] scale : { minReplicas : env == 'prod' ? 2 : 0 , maxReplicas : 10 } } } } module sql 'modules/sql.bicep' = { name : 'sql' , params : { prefix : prefix, location : location, adminGroupObjectId : '…' } } output apiUrl string = 'https://${api.properties.configuration.ingress.fqdn}' Shell Copy az deployment group what-if -g rg-shop-prod -f main.bicep -p env =prod imageTag =3f1c2a9b7d10 # preview changes az deployment group create -g rg-shop-prod -f main.bicep -p env =prod imageTag =3f1c2a9b7d10 Use modules for reuse, Azure Verified Modules (AVM) for well-architected building blocks, parameter files ( .bicepparam ) per environment, and deployment stacks to manage and lock groups of resources. # Terraform HashiCorp's multi-cloud IaC with HCL and a huge provider ecosystem (Azure, AWS, GCP, Kubernetes, GitHub, Datadog…). It keeps a state file (store it remotely, e.g. in an Azure Storage backend with locking) mapping code to real resources; terraform plan shows the diff and apply executes it. Terraform Copy resource \"azurerm_resource_group\" \"shop\" { name = \"rg-shop-${var.env}\" location = \"westeurope\" } resource \"azurerm_key_vault\" \"kv\" { name = \"kv-shop-${var.env}\" resource_group_name = azurerm_resource_grou"},{"c":"cicd-iac","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading GitHub Actions documentation · Building and testing .NET with GitHub Actions Azure Pipelines documentation Bicep documentation · Azure Verified Modules Azure Developer CLI Books: Accelerate (Forsgren, Humble, Kim); Continuous Delivery (Humble, Farley)."},{"c":"cicd-iac","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Most of this chapter is about GitHub Actions, Azure resources and Bicep/Terraform behaviour, which need real cloud infrastructure to actually exercise — not something a console program can verify. The checks below instead verify the three claims from this chapter that genuinely are just computation: idempotency, SemVer's numeric comparison, and the expand/contract migration pattern. Save as Program.cs in a new console project ( dotnet new console ) and run with dotnet run . C# Run ▶ Copy // Verifies claims from this chapter that are actually local/computational: IaC should be // idempotent (applying it twice changes nothing the second time), SemVer needs numeric comparison // (not string comparison, which gets \"1.10.0\" vs \"1.9.0\" backwards), and the expand/contract migration // pattern keeps both old and new code working during the transition window. // Most of this chapter's claims are about GitHub Actions, Azure resources, Bicep/Terraform behaviour // and DORA research — not runtime-verifiable in a console app. See Further reading (especially the // GitHub Actions, Bicep and *Accelerate* links) for those. using System ; int fails = 0 ; void Ok ( string s) => Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: {s}\" ); void Check ( bool cond, string s) { if (cond) Ok (s); else { Console . WriteLine ( $\"FAIL: {s}\" ); fails++; } } // --- IaC should be idempotent: applying the same desired state twice changes nothing the 2nd time --- var resource = new FakeAzureResource (); bool firstApplyChanged = Apply (resource, desiredSku: \"P1v3\" ); bool secondApplyChanged = Apply (resource, desiredSku: \"P1v3\" ); Check (firstApplyChanged, \"IaC apply: the first apply, from an empty resource, makes a change\" ); Check (!secondApplyChanged, \"IaC apply: applying the identical desired state again makes no change (idempotent)\" ); // --- SemVer: comparing versions numerically, not as strings, is the whole point of the format --- var v1_9 = new Version ( 1 , 9 , 0 ); var v1_10 = new Version ( 1 , 10 , 0 ); Check (v1_10 > v1_9, \"SemVer: 1.10.0 is correctly a later version than 1.9.0 under numeric comparison\" ); Check ( string . Compare ( \"1.10.0\" , \"1.9.0\" , StringComparison . Ordinal ) < 0 , \"SemVer: naive string comparison gets this backwards ('1.10.0' sorts before '1.9.0'), which is exactly why tools compare version parts numerically instead\" ); // --- Expand/contract: during the transition, code using the OLD shape and code using the NEW shape must both work --- var row = new ExpandedRow (); // \"expand\" step: write both old and new columns row. WriteBoth (oldTotal: 100m , newTotalCents: 10000 ); Check ( ReadOld (row) == 100m , \"Expand/contract: old-shape reader still works during the transition (reads OldTotal)\" ); Check ( ReadNew (row) == 100m , \"Expand/contract: new-shape reader already works too (reads NewTotalCents / 100)\" ); Console . WriteLine (fails == 0 ? \"\\nAll checks passed.\" : $\"\\n{fails} check(s) failed.\" ); if (fails > 0 ) Environment . Exit ( 1 ); static bool Apply ( FakeAzureResource resource, string desiredSku) { if (resource. Sku == desiredSku) return false ; // already in the desired state: no-op resource. Sku = desiredSku; return true ; } static decimal ReadOld ( ExpandedRow r) => r. OldTotal ; static decimal ReadNew ( ExpandedRow r) => r. NewTotalCents / 100m ; sealed class FakeAzureResource { public string ? Sku ; } sealed class ExpandedRow { public decimal OldTotal ; public long NewTotalCents ; public void WriteBoth ( decimal oldTotal, long newTotalCents) { OldTotal = oldTotal; NewTotalCents = newTotalCents; } }"},{"c":"kubernetes","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Kubernetes runs containers across a cluster toward a declared desired state , continuously reconciling reality to match it: scheduling, restarting crashed containers, scaling, load balancing, rolling updates. A Pod is the smallest unit; a Deployment manages pods and rolling updates; a Service gives them a stable name; an Ingress routes external HTTP traffic in. Liveness probes restart a stuck container; readiness probes pull a pod out of traffic without restarting it — never make liveness depend on an external dependency, or one DB blip restarts everything. On pod termination: removed from the Service, then SIGTERM (the .NET host starts graceful shutdown), then SIGKILL after terminationGracePeriodSeconds if it hasn't exited. Requests reserve scheduling capacity; limits cap usage — exceed the memory limit and you get OOMKilled , exceed the CPU limit and you get throttled (latency spikes, not a crash). Prefer AKS Workload Identity over storing secrets in the cluster: pods get federated Azure AD tokens, so DefaultAzureCredential reaches SQL, Key Vault and Service Bus with nothing secret sitting in etcd."},{"c":"kubernetes","s":"what-kubernetes-does","h":"What Kubernetes does","t":"# What Kubernetes does Intermediate Kubernetes (K8s) is an open-source system for running containerised applications across a cluster of machines . You declare the desired state (\"3 replicas of shop-api:1.4.2, each with 0.5 CPU, reachable at orders.shop.com\") and Kubernetes continuously reconciles reality to match — the whole system is built around that single loop, running forever in the background: Scheduling : places containers on nodes with enough resources. Self-healing : restarts crashed containers, replaces pods on failed nodes, kills pods failing liveness probes. Scaling : replicas up and down, manually or automatically. Service discovery and load balancing : stable names and virtual IPs for changing sets of pods. Rolling updates and rollbacks : replace versions gradually, with no downtime. Configuration and secrets injection. Storage orchestration : attach persistent volumes. Why Kubernetes, and why not It gives a standard, portable platform for running many services with consistent deployment, scaling and operations, across clouds and on-premises, with a vast ecosystem (Helm, operators, service meshes, GitOps). The cost is complexity : networking, security, upgrades, observability and the YAML surface area need platform-engineering skills. Many teams get most of the benefits with less effort from Azure Container Apps (Kubernetes underneath, managed for you)."},{"c":"kubernetes","s":"architecture","h":"Architecture","t":"# Architecture Intermediate Diagram Copy ┌───────────────────────── Control plane (managed for you in AKS) ─────────────────────────┐ │ kube-apiserver (the API: kubectl, CI, controllers talk to it) │ │ etcd (cluster state store) · scheduler (assigns pods to nodes) · controller-manager │ │ (reconcile loops: Deployments, ReplicaSets, Nodes, Jobs…) · cloud-controller-manager │ └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │ watches/updates desired state ┌─────────▼──────── Node (VM) ─────────┐ ┌──────── Node ────────┐ ┌──────── Node ────────┐ │ kubelet (runs pods, reports status) │ │ kubelet │ │ kubelet │ │ container runtime (containerd) │ │ … │ │ … │ │ kube-proxy / CNI (networking) │ │ │ │ │ │ ┌─Pod──────────┐ ┌─Pod──────────┐ │ │ ┌─Pod──────────┐ │ │ ┌─Pod──────────┐ │ │ │ shop-api │ │ shop-worker │ │ │ │ shop-api │ │ │ │ shop-api │ │ │ └──────────────┘ └──────────────┘ │ │ └──────────────┘ │ │ └──────────────┘ │ └───────────────────────────────────────┘ └───────────────────────┘ └───────────────────────┘ Everything is an API object described in YAML (or JSON), submitted with kubectl apply -f , stored in etcd, and acted on by controllers running reconciliation loops (observe → compare with desired → act) — the \"desired state\" idea from the opening section, now a concrete object sitting in a concrete place."},{"c":"kubernetes","s":"the-core-objects","h":"The core objects","t":"# The core objects Intermediate Object What it is .NET-app relevance Pod The smallest unit: one or more containers sharing network (localhost) and volumes; ephemeral, with its own IP Usually one app container (+ optional sidecars) ReplicaSet Keeps N identical pods running Managed by Deployments; rarely used directly Deployment Declares pods + replicas + rolling update strategy; manages ReplicaSets per version How you run a stateless API or worker Service Stable virtual IP + DNS name load-balancing to pods selected by labels ( ClusterIP , NodePort , LoadBalancer ) http://shop-api.shop.svc.cluster.local for service-to-service calls Ingress / Gateway API HTTP(S) routing from outside to Services (hosts, paths, TLS) via an ingress controller (NGINX, Traefik, App Gateway for Containers) Public entry for APIs ConfigMap Non-secret configuration as key/values or files Environment variables / mounted appsettings.Production.json Secret Sensitive values (base64-encoded, not encrypted by default; enable etcd encryption, or use the Key Vault CSI driver) Connection strings (better: workload identity) Namespace A virtual cluster partition for isolation, quotas and RBAC One per team/app/environment StatefulSet Pods with stable identity and storage Databases, brokers (often better as managed PaaS) DaemonSet One pod per node Log/metrics agents Job / CronJob Run-to-completion / scheduled Migrations, nightly batch jobs HorizontalPodAutoscaler Scales replicas on metrics Autoscaling APIs PersistentVolume(Claim) Durable storage Rare for stateless .NET services ServiceAccount Pod identity for the K8s API and workload identity to Azure Passwordless Azure access"},{"c":"kubernetes","s":"a-complete-manifest-for-an-asp-net-core-api","h":"A complete manifest for an ASP.NET Core API","t":"# A complete manifest for an ASP.NET Core API Intermediate YAML Copy apiVersion : apps/v1 kind : Deployment metadata : name : shop-api namespace : shop labels : { app : shop-api } spec : replicas : 3 revisionHistoryLimit : 5 selector : matchLabels : { app : shop-api } strategy : type : RollingUpdate rollingUpdate : { maxSurge : 1 , maxUnavailable : 0 } # zero-downtime: add one new before removing an old template : metadata : labels : app : shop-api azure.workload.identity/ use : \"true\" # AKS workload identity spec : serviceAccountName : shop-api terminationGracePeriodSeconds : 45 # > app's ShutdownTimeout securityContext : { runAsNonRoot : true, seccompProfile : { type : RuntimeDefault } } containers : - name : api image : shopacr.azurecr.io/ shop-api :3f1c2a9b7d10 # immutable tag (git SHA) ports : [{ containerPort : 8080 , name : http }] env : - { name : ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT, value : Production } - { name : OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT, value : \"http://otel-collector.observability:4317\" } - name : ConnectionStrings__Shop valueFrom : { secretKeyRef : { name : shop-api-secrets, key : sql } } envFrom : - configMapRef : { name : shop-api-config } resources : requests : { cpu : 250m, memory : 256Mi } # used for scheduling (guaranteed) limits : { memory : 512Mi } # exceed → OOMKilled. CPU limits often omitted to avoid throttling startupProbe : # give slow starts (JIT, warm-up) time before other probes httpGet : { path : /health/live, port : http } failureThreshold : 30 periodSeconds : 2 livenessProbe : # failing → container restarted httpGet : { path : /health/live, port : http } periodSeconds : 10 readinessProbe : # failing → removed from Service endpoints (no traffic) httpGet : { path : /health/ready, port : http } periodSeconds : 5 securityContext : { allowPrivilegeEscalation : false, readOnlyRootFilesystem : true, capabilities : { drop : [ALL] } } volumeMounts : [{ name : tmp, mountPath : /tmp }] volumes : [{ name : tmp, emptyDir : {} }] topologySpreadConstraints : # spread replicas across zones - { maxSkew : 1 , topologyKey : topology.kubernetes.io/zone, whenUnsatisfiable : ScheduleAnyway, labelSelector : { matchLabels : { app : shop-api } } } --- apiVersion : v1 kind : Service metadata : { name : shop-api, namespace : shop } spec : selector : { app : shop-api } ports : [{ port : 80 , targetPort : http }] --- apiVersion : networking.k8s.io/v1 kind : Ingress metadata : name : shop-api namespace : shop annotations : { cert-manager.io/ cluster-issuer : letsencrypt } spec : ingressClassName : nginx tls : [{ hosts : [api.shop.com], secretName : api-shop-tls }] rules : - host : api.shop.com http : paths : [{ path : /, pathType : Prefix, backend : { service : { name : shop-api, port : { number : 80 } } } }] --- apiVersion : policy/v1 kind : PodDisruptionBudget # keep ≥2 pods during node drains/upgrades metadata : { name : shop-api, namespace : shop } spec : minAvailable : 2 selector : { matchLabels : { app : shop-api } } Notice /health/live and /health/ready go to different probes: startupProbe / livenessProbe use the liveness path, readinessProbe uses the readiness one. That split matters because of what Resilience built those two endpoints for: a dependency hiccup should trip readiness (removed from traffic, left to retry), not liveness (restarted — which would turn a blip into an outage)."},{"c":"kubernetes","s":"probes-and-graceful-shutdown","h":"Probes and graceful shutdown","t":"# Probes and graceful shutdown Advanced Probe Question On failure Don't… startup Has the app finished starting? Keep waiting (up to the threshold), then restart Leave it out for slow-starting apps liveness Is the process healthy (not deadlocked)? Restart the container Check databases or other dependencies (a DB blip would restart everything) readiness Can it serve traffic right now? Remove from Service endpoints Make it so strict that all pods go unready together Termination sequence when a pod is deleted (rolling update, scale-in, node drain) — this is the exact SIGTERM-driven sequence ASP.NET Core architecture walked through, now seen from the orchestrator's side of the handshake: The pod is marked Terminating; it's removed from Service endpoints (asynchronously!) and any preStop hook runs. SIGTERM is sent to the container → the .NET host begins graceful shutdown (stops accepting connections, drains in-flight requests, stops hosted services). After terminationGracePeriodSeconds (30 s default), SIGKILL . Because endpoint removal is asynchronous, a pod can still receive new requests for a moment after SIGTERM — a real race between \"tell the load balancer to stop sending traffic here\" and \"actually stop accepting it,\" with no guarantee which one wins first. A common mitigation is a short preStop: sleep 5 . Keep HostOptions.ShutdownTimeout below the grace period."},{"c":"kubernetes","s":"resources-qos-and-autoscaling","h":"Resources, QoS and autoscaling","t":"# Resources, QoS and autoscaling Advanced Requests reserve capacity for scheduling; limits cap usage. Memory over the limit means OOMKilled ; CPU over the limit means throttling (latency spikes), which is why many teams set CPU requests but no CPU limit for latency-sensitive .NET services. QoS classes : Guaranteed (requests = limits), Burstable, BestEffort (none: evicted first). .NET respects container limits (GC heap limit ~75% of memory limit by default; CPU count from the cgroup quota — see Further reading for the exact mechanism). DATAS (default in .NET 9+ Server GC) keeps memory proportional to load. See Docker . Autoscaler Scales On HPA (Horizontal Pod Autoscaler) Pod replicas CPU/memory utilisation, or custom/external metrics KEDA Pod replicas, including to zero Event sources: Service Bus queue length, Kafka lag, RabbitMQ, Prometheus queries, cron… VPA Pod requests/limits Observed usage (recommendation mode is safest) Cluster autoscaler / Node auto-provisioning Nodes Pending pods that can't be scheduled YAML Copy apiVersion : keda.sh/v1alpha1 kind : ScaledObject metadata : { name : shop-worker, namespace : shop } spec : scaleTargetRef : { name : shop-worker } minReplicaCount : 0 maxReplicaCount : 30 triggers : - type : azure-servicebus metadata : { queueName : orders, namespace : sb-shop-prod, messageCount : \"100\" } authenticationRef : { name : servicebus-workload-identity }"},{"c":"kubernetes","s":"configuration-secrets-and-identity","h":"Configuration, secrets and identity","t":"# Configuration, secrets and identity Advanced ConfigMaps map naturally to .NET configuration: as environment variables ( Payments__BaseUrl ) or mounted files ( appsettings.Production.json , picked up with reloadOnChange , or AddKeyPerFile for one-file-per-key). Secrets : native Secrets are only base64-encoded. Prefer the Secrets Store CSI driver with Azure Key Vault , External Secrets Operator, or — best of all — AKS Workload Identity : annotate a ServiceAccount with a user-assigned managed identity's client ID, and pods get federated tokens. DefaultAzureCredential → WorkloadIdentityCredential then reaches SQL, Service Bus and Key Vault with no secrets in the cluster — the same workload identity federation mechanism from Identity & secrets , here granting pods identity the same way it grants GitHub Actions workflows identity."},{"c":"kubernetes","s":"packaging-and-delivery-helm-kustomize-gitops","h":"Packaging and delivery: Helm, Kustomize, GitOps","t":"# Packaging and delivery: Helm, Kustomize, GitOps Advanced Helm : the package manager for Kubernetes. Charts are templated manifests with values.yaml per environment; helm upgrade --install shop-api ./chart -f values-prod.yaml . Also used to install third-party software (ingress-nginx, cert-manager, KEDA, Prometheus). Kustomize : template-free overlays (base + patches per environment), built into kubectl apply -k . GitOps with Argo CD or Flux : the desired cluster state lives in Git; an in-cluster agent pulls and reconciles, giving drift correction, audit history and easy rollback (revert the commit). CI only builds the image and bumps the tag in the config repo — a direct application of the \"build once, deploy many\" principle from CI/CD & IaC , with Git itself as the record of which image is meant to be running where. Progressive delivery : Argo Rollouts / Flagger for canaries with automated metric analysis."},{"c":"kubernetes","s":"day-to-day-kubectl","h":"Day-to-day kubectl","t":"# Day-to-day kubectl Intermediate Shell Copy kubectl config use-context aks-shop-prod kubectl get pods -n shop -o wide # status, node, IP kubectl describe pod shop-api-7d9f… -n shop # events: scheduling, probe failures, OOMKilled, image pull errors kubectl logs -f deploy/shop-api -n shop --since =10m # stream logs (all pods of the deployment with --all-pods) kubectl exec -it shop-api-7d9f… -n shop -- sh # (not possible with chiseled images: use ephemeral debug containers) kubectl debug -it shop-api-7d9f… --image =mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/ sdk : 10.0 --target =api # attach dotnet-trace/counters kubectl rollout status deploy/shop-api -n shop kubectl rollout undo deploy/shop-api -n shop # roll back to the previous ReplicaSet kubectl scale deploy/shop-api --replicas = 5 -n shop kubectl top pods -n shop # CPU/memory (metrics-server) kubectl port-forward svc/shop-api 8080 : 80 -n shop # reach a ClusterIP service locally Common failure states: CrashLoopBackOff (the app exits on start: check the logs, config and missing env vars), ImagePullBackOff (wrong tag, or no registry access: grant AcrPull ), OOMKilled (raise the memory limit or fix a leak), Pending (not enough capacity, or unsatisfiable affinity/taints), and readiness failing (dependency unreachable, probe path wrong)."},{"c":"kubernetes","s":"aks-specifics","h":"AKS specifics","t":"# AKS specifics Advanced Area AKS feature Control plane Managed and free (Standard tier adds an uptime SLA); AKS Automatic for an opinionated, fully managed experience Nodes Node pools (VM scale sets): system vs user pools, spot pools, GPU pools, Linux/Windows; auto-upgrade channels and planned maintenance windows Networking Azure CNI (Overlay recommended), network policies (Azure/Calico/Cilium), private clusters, Application Gateway for Containers / application routing add-on (managed NGINX) Identity Entra ID integration for kubectl and Kubernetes RBAC; Workload Identity for pods Registry ACR attach ( az aks update --attach-acr ) Secrets Key Vault Secrets Store CSI driver add-on Scaling Cluster autoscaler, KEDA add-on , node auto-provisioning Observability Container Insights, Managed Prometheus + Managed Grafana , OpenTelemetry Security Microsoft Defender for Containers, Azure Policy for Kubernetes, image cleaner, deployment safeguards Service mesh Istio-based service mesh add-on Q What is Kubernetes and what problems does it solve? A container orchestration platform that runs containers across a cluster from declarative desired state, providing scheduling, self-healing, horizontal scaling, service discovery and load balancing, rolling updates and rollbacks, and configuration/secret injection, through a standard API with a large ecosystem. Q Pod vs Deployment vs Service? A Pod is the smallest deployable unit: one or more co-located containers sharing network and storage, and ephemeral. A Deployment declares the desired pods and replica count and manages rolling updates through ReplicaSets. A Service provides a stable virtual IP and DNS name that load-balances across the pods selected by labels. Q What are liveness, readiness and startup probes? Startup probes delay other checks until the app has started. Liveness probes detect a hung process, and failure restarts the container. Readiness probes indicate whether the pod should receive traffic, and failure removes it from Service endpoints without restarting. Liveness must not depend on external dependencies. Q How does a rolling update work and how do you roll back? The Deployment creates a new ReplicaSet and gradually scales it up while scaling the old one down, respecting maxSurge/maxUnavailable and readiness probes, so capacity is preserved. Roll back with kubectl rollout undo (back to the previous ReplicaSet), or by reverting the manifest in GitOps. Q Requests vs limits? Requests are the resources reserved for scheduling a pod (guaranteed). Limits are maximums: exceeding the memory limit gets the container OOMKilled, and exceeding the CPU limit throttles it. Setting realistic requests and memory limits matters for stability; CPU limits can cause latency through throttling. Q How do you scale on queue length in Kubernetes? With KEDA: a ScaledObject targeting the deployment, with a trigger such as azure-servicebus (queue name, target message count per replica) and authentication, scaling replicas between min (possibly 0) and max based on backlog. Q How should a .NET app in AKS access Azure SQL or Key Vault? With AKS Workload Identity: a Kubernetes ServiceAccount federated with a user-assigned managed identity; pods get tokens via WorkloadIdentityCredential (included in DefaultAzureCredential), and the identity has least-privilege roles on SQL, Key Vault and Service Bus. No secrets in the cluster. Q What does CrashLoopBackOff mean? The container keeps exiting shortly after starting and Kubernetes restarts it with increasing backoff. Usually it's a startup exception (bad config, missing env var or secret, failing ValidateOnStart, unreachable dependency at startup) or a failing liveness probe. Check kubectl logs --previous and describe pod events."},{"c":"kubernetes","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Kubernetes documentation: concepts AKS documentation · AKS baseline architecture Use Microsoft Entra Workload ID with AKS KEDA · Helm · Argo CD Book: Kubernetes Up & Running (Burns, Beda, Hightower, Evenson). Runtime configuration options for garbage collection (the ~75% heap-limit default and DOTNET_GCHeapHardLimit )"},{"c":"kubernetes","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Almost everything in this chapter is Kubernetes's own orchestration behavior — scheduling, probes, rolling updates — which genuinely needs a real cluster to observe; a C# program on its own can't fake that honestly. The one claim that is pure .NET runtime behavior, and the same cgroup-awareness mechanism that makes .NET respect a container's memory limit under Kubernetes or Docker, is verified below by actually setting a hard GC memory ceiling and reading it back. Save as a console app ( dotnet new console ), then dotnet run . C# Run ▶ Copy using System . Diagnostics ; if ( Environment . GetEnvironmentVariable ( \"VERIFY_CHILD\" ) == \"1\" ) { var info = GC . GetGCMemoryInfo (); Console . WriteLine (info. TotalAvailableMemoryBytes ); return ; } // Claim: .NET's GC respects a configured hard memory ceiling — the exact mechanism that makes it respect a // Kubernetes/Docker cgroup memory limit (this chapter's \"GC heap limit ~75% of memory limit\" claim). // DOTNET_GCHeapHardLimit sets an explicit byte ceiling; we verify it by reading it back from a child process, // since GC configuration is fixed at process startup. const long limitBytes = 256L * 1024 * 1024 ; // 256 MiB var psi = new ProcessStartInfo { FileName = Environment . ProcessPath !, RedirectStandardOutput = true , UseShellExecute = false , }; foreach ( var arg in Environment . GetCommandLineArgs (). Skip ( 1 )) psi. ArgumentList . Add (arg); psi. Environment [ \"VERIFY_CHILD\" ] = \"1\" ; psi. Environment [ \"DOTNET_GCHeapHardLimit\" ] = limitBytes. ToString ( \"X\" ); // DOTNET_GCHeapHardLimit takes a hex byte count using var proc = Process . Start (psi)!; var output = ( await proc. StandardOutput . ReadToEndAsync ()). Trim (); await proc. WaitForExitAsync (); var reportedLimit = long . Parse (output); if (reportedLimit > limitBytes || reportedLimit < limitBytes / 2 ) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected the GC to report an available-memory ceiling near {limitBytes} bytes, got {reportedLimit}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: DOTNET_GCHeapHardLimit made the GC report a hard ceiling (~{reportedLimit / 1024 / 1024} MiB) — \" + \"the same cgroup-awareness mechanism that makes .NET respect a container's memory limit under Kubernetes or Docker\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"All checks passed.\" ); // Not runtime-verifiable here (needs a real cluster, which this verification environment cannot assume): // - scheduling, self-healing, rolling updates, readiness/liveness probe behavior — see Further reading: \"Kubernetes documentation: concepts\" // - HPA/KEDA autoscaling, AKS workload identity, GitOps reconciliation — see Further reading: \"KEDA\" / \"Use Microsoft Entra Workload ID with AKS\" // - the exact ~75% GC heap default and CPU-count-from-cgroup-quota figures in a real container — see Further reading: // \"Runtime configuration options for garbage collection\" (the general mechanism verified above is the same one)"},{"c":"memory-gc","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR .NET frees memory for you: the garbage collector (GC) reclaims an object's memory once nothing can reach it any more — no malloc / free , no use-after-free, no double free. Objects are grouped by age into generations (Gen0 → Gen1 → Gen2) because most objects die young; collecting the small, young Gen0 often is cheap, and full Gen2 sweeps (which include the Large Object Heap) are rare and expensive. Big objects (≥ 85,000 bytes) skip straight to the Large Object Heap , which isn't compacted by default — allocate large buffers per request and you'll fragment it and trigger extra full GCs. Server GC (ASP.NET Core's default) trades memory for throughput using one heap per core; DATAS (.NET 9+) scales that to the real workload so containers don't get OOMKilled. A \"memory leak\" in .NET isn't freed-twice or dangling pointers — it's something still reachable (a static cache, an unsubscribed event, a captive DI dependency) that your app will never actually use again. Allocation rate drives GC frequency, which drives latency — so most performance tuning in .NET comes down to allocating less, not making the GC itself faster."},{"c":"memory-gc","s":"the-problem-every-language-has-to-solve","h":"The problem every language has to solve","t":"# The problem every language has to solve Beginner Every running program creates objects as it works, and every one of them eventually needs to disappear — otherwise memory just fills up until the machine falls over. Someone, or something, has to decide when an object is safe to free. In C or C++, that someone is you. You call malloc / free or new / delete yourself, by hand, every time. Get it wrong and you get one of three classic bugs: a leak (you forgot to free something, and it sits there forever), a use-after-free (you freed something, then kept using it — often a security hole), or a double free (you freed the same thing twice, which can corrupt memory outright). These aren't rare mistakes; they're some of the most common, most dangerous bugs in systems programming. .NET takes that decision away from you entirely. Its garbage collector (the GC ) watches your objects and frees memory automatically, the moment it can prove nothing can reach an object any more. That one design choice is why use-after-free and double free essentially don't exist as bugs in C#. It isn't free, though. The GC needs real CPU time to do this watching and freeing, and some of its work genuinely pauses your running threads while it happens. So here's the thing worth internalising before anything else in this chapter: almost all .NET performance tuning, once you dig past the surface, turns out to really be about how much you're allocating — because allocation is what creates the GC's workload in the first place. Understand the GC, and a huge amount of \"why is this slow\" advice stops being folklore and starts being obvious cause and effect."},{"c":"memory-gc","s":"two-places-your-data-can-live","h":"Two places your data can live","t":"# Two places your data can live Beginner Before any of that makes sense, you need to know there are two completely different places an object's data can sit: the stack and the managed heap . They behave almost nothing alike. Stack Managed heap Belongs to One thread (≈1 MB default on Windows, 8 MB on Linux's main thread) The whole process, shared by every thread Holds Method frames: locals, parameters, return addresses Reference-type objects, boxed values, arrays, closures, suspended async state machines Allocating Move a pointer — essentially free Also just moving a pointer in most cases — also fast Freeing Automatic, the instant the method returns By the GC , some unknown time after nothing references it any more What happens if you overflow it StackOverflowException (deep or infinite recursion — you can't even catch this one) OutOfMemoryException Here's the part that surprises people: allocating on the heap is also cheap, most of the time. Each thread keeps a small chunk of heap space reserved for itself, called an allocation context , and handing out a new object from it is mostly just bumping a pointer forward — not meaningfully slower than the stack. So if heap allocation is cheap, where does the GC's reputation for being expensive actually come from? It comes later. The more you allocate, the more often that allocation context fills up and the GC has to step in and do real work to make room for more. The cost isn't in the allocating — it's in how often allocating forces a collection. Keep that distinction in mind; it's the thread running through the rest of this chapter."},{"c":"memory-gc","s":"generations-why-the-gc-doesnt-scan-everything-every-time","h":"Generations: why the GC doesn't scan everything, every time","t":"# Generations: why the GC doesn't scan everything, every time Intermediate Here's an idea that sounds almost too simple to be the foundation of a whole optimisation strategy, but it is: most objects die young. Think about a typical web request — it creates a flurry of temporary strings, DTOs, LINQ enumerators, intermediate results — and nearly all of that garbage is unreachable again within milliseconds, the instant the request finishes. Meanwhile, a much smaller set of objects — caches, singletons, configuration — get created once and then stick around for the entire life of the process. That observation has a name, the generational hypothesis , and .NET's GC is built directly around it. Instead of treating the whole heap as one big pool it has to re-scan from scratch every time, it splits the heap by age : Diagram Copy new objects long-lived objects ┌──────────┐ survives a GC ┌──────────┐ survives a GC ┌──────────────────────┐ │ Gen 0 │ ──────────────▶ │ Gen 1 │ ──────────────▶ │ Gen 2 │ │ small, │ │ buffer │ │ caches, singletons, │ │ collected│ │ between │ │ static data; FULL GCs │ │ very often│ │ 0 and 2 │ │ are expensive │ └──────────┘ └──────────┘ └──────────────────────┘ LOH (Large Object Heap): objects ≥ 85,000 bytes. Logically part of Gen 2, collected with Gen 2, not compacted by default POH (Pinned Object Heap, .NET 5+): objects allocated pre-pinned, so they never fragment the other heaps Here's what that diagram is actually showing, walked through concretely: Every object starts life in Gen0 , the smallest generation. Because Gen0 is small, scanning it is fast — often well under a millisecond — and because most objects in it are already garbage by the time it fills up, a Gen0 collection reclaims a lot of memory for very little work. If an object is still reachable when Gen0 fills up and gets collected, it survives , and gets promoted up into Gen1 — a kind of buffer zone between the churn of Gen0 and the stability of Gen2. If it survives another collection from there, it gets promoted again, into Gen2 — where the genuinely long-lived stuff lives: caches, singletons, static data. A Gen1 collection sweeps Gen0 and Gen1 together. A Gen2 collection — often just called a full GC — sweeps absolutely everything, Gen0 through Gen2, including the Large Object Heap mentioned in the diagram (more on that shortly). Full GCs are the expensive ones, and the whole point of the generational design is to make them rare. One more term you'll meet in GC logs: a Gen0-only collection still needs to know if some old Gen2 object secretly points at something in Gen0. Instead of rescanning all of Gen2 to check, the runtime keeps a card table — a small \"which regions might contain a reference to something younger\" scoreboard. Every time your code stores a reference from an older object into a younger one, a tiny bit of code called a write barrier marks the matching entry. A Gen0 collection then only has to check the marked entries, not all of Gen2. Why this actually matters for the code you write Short-lived garbage — the kind that dies in Gen0 — is cheap, almost by design. The expensive case is the one hiding in the middle: objects that survive just long enough to get promoted to Gen1, and then die in Gen2 anyway. Developers sometimes call this a \"mid-life crisis\" — the object lived long enough to force an expensive full GC, but not long enough to actually be worth keeping around. This happens naturally with per-request objects that get held alive by a slow async operation spanning a Gen0 collection, or with caches whose expiry is set just a little too generously. It also happens automatically to anything ≥ 85 KB , because of a rule you're about to meet: objects that size go straight onto the Large Object Heap, which is only ever cleaned up during a full Gen2 collection — so allocating big temporary buffers per request quietly forces frequent, expensive full GCs."},{"c":"memory-gc","s":"what-actually-happens-during-a-collection","h":"What actually happens during a collection","t":"# What actually happens during a collection Intermediate You now know when generations get collected. Here's how a collection itself actually works, step by step, every single time one runs: Suspend your threads , at points the runtime considers safe to pause them (called \"safe points\"). Mark : starting from a fixed set of known starting points — the GC roots , covered next — follow every reference outward and mark every object you can reach as live . Sweep and/or compact : anything that wasn't marked is garbage, and its memory gets reclaimed. For the generations below the LOH, the GC usually also compacts — slides the surviving live objects together so they're contiguous, and fixes up every reference that pointed at them, which keeps the heap from fragmenting into a patchwork of unusable gaps. The LOH, by contrast, is swept into free lists rather than compacted by default — you'll see why that matters shortly. Resume your threads. Step 2 is doing something worth really sitting with: an object is only considered garbage if it's unreachable , full stop. It makes no difference whether your code will genuinely never touch it again — if something can still trace a path to it from a root, the GC keeps it alive. That single fact is the entire mechanism behind every .NET memory leak you will ever debug: somewhere, some root is holding a reference it shouldn't be. So what actually counts as a root? Here's the full list: Root Example Static fields static readonly Dictionary<…> Cache lives as long as the process does Local variables and parameters on thread stacks (and CPU registers) Anything a running method still uses — and the JIT tracks this precisely enough that a local can become collectable before its method even finishes, once the method is done using it GC handles Strong handles, pinned handles ( GCHandle.Alloc ), fixed The finalization queue (f-reachable) Objects waiting to have their finalizer run — you'll meet this properly below Interop / COM references Native code that's holding on to a managed object Keep that table nearby — later in this chapter, when \"memory leak\" comes up, every single cause traces back to one of these five rows holding a reference longer than it should."},{"c":"memory-gc","s":"choosing-how-the-gc-behaves","h":"Choosing how the GC behaves","t":"# Choosing how the GC behaves Intermediate The mark-sweep-compact cycle above is the mechanism. .NET also lets you choose how that mechanism is scheduled and parallelised, and the defaults genuinely differ depending on what kind of app you're running. Mode Behaviour Default for Workstation GC One heap; collections run on the thread that triggered them; favours responsiveness and low memory use Desktop and console apps Server GC One heap and one dedicated GC thread per logical core ; collections run in parallel across them; much higher throughput, at the cost of more memory ASP.NET Core Background (concurrent) GC Gen2 collections run concurrently with your app's threads, so you only see brief pauses rather than a full stop; Gen0/1 collections still pause everything On by default in both modes above DATAS (Dynamic Adaptation To Application Sizes) A refinement of Server GC that adapts the number of heaps to the actual workload , instead of always using one per core, so memory use stays roughly proportional to real load Default with Server GC since .NET 9 Why does ASP.NET Core default to Server GC instead of Workstation? A web server is, by nature, trying to maximise how many requests it can process per second across many cores at once — exactly the throughput-over-latency trade Server GC is built for. A desktop app, by contrast, cares more about not freezing the UI thread for a visible pause, which is Workstation GC's priority. XML Copy <!-- csproj or runtimeconfig.json --> <PropertyGroup> <ServerGarbageCollection>true</ServerGarbageCollection> <ConcurrentGarbageCollection>true</ConcurrentGarbageCollection> <!-- <GarbageCollectionAdaptationMode> 1 </GarbageCollectionAdaptationMode> DATAS on (default in .NET 9 + server GC) --> </PropertyGroup> Environment variable equivalents, useful when you can't touch the csproj (a container base image, for instance): DOTNET_gcServer=1 , DOTNET_GCgen0size , DOTNET_GCHeapHardLimit , DOTNET_GCHeapHardLimitPercent , DOTNET_GCHeapCount , DOTNET_GCConserveMemory . # Why this gets tricky inside containers Here's a scenario worth walking through, because it genuinely used to cause production outages. The GC is cgroup-aware : it knows it's running inside a container with a memory limit, and by default caps its own heap at 75% of that limit (tunable). The trap, before DATAS existed: Server GC's \"one heap per core\" rule doesn't ask whether memory is actually plentiful — it just counts cores. Put that on a node with many CPU cores but a small container memory limit (say, 32 cores, 512 MB) and Server GC happily creates 32 heaps, blows straight through the limit, and the container gets OOMKilled — with the app not leaking a single byte. DATAS (.NET 9+) mostly fixes this by scaling the heap count to the real workload instead of blindly matching the core count. If you're still seeing OOMKilled after upgrading, the next things to check are: is the actual working set genuinely over the limit, is something consuming native memory the GC heap tracker can't see, and is this an actual leak — then reach for GCHeapHardLimitPercent or GCConserveMemory as tuning knobs, in that order. # Latency modes and no-GC regions Advanced Two more dials exist for genuinely latency-sensitive code: GCSettings.LatencyMode = GCLatencyMode.SustainedLowLatency tries to avoid blocking full GCs wherever it can, and GC.TryStartNoGCRegion(size) runs a critical section with no GC at all , provided your allocation budget fits inside what you reserved. These are niche tools for things like trading systems or real-time workloads — the overwhelming majority of web apps never touch either one, and that's fine."},{"c":"memory-gc","s":"the-large-object-heap-and-the-pinned-object-heap","h":"The Large Object Heap and the Pinned Object Heap","t":"# The Large Object Heap and the Pinned Object Heap Advanced You've already seen the LOH mentioned twice — once in the generations diagram, once in the \"mid-life crisis\" box. Time to look at it properly. Any object ≥ 85,000 bytes — in practice, usually a big array or a big string — doesn't go through Gen0 at all. It's allocated directly onto a separate region called the Large Object Heap . That size threshold isn't arbitrary: copying a huge object around every time it survives a generation (which compaction normally does) would be expensive in its own right, so the runtime sidesteps the problem for big objects differently. Three consequences follow directly from that design: Allocating on the LOH costs more than a normal allocation, partly because the memory has to be cleared first. It's only ever collected alongside a full Gen2 collection — never on its own. It isn't compacted by default, which means repeated large allocations and frees can leave it fragmented : lots of free space technically available, but scattered into pieces too small to satisfy the next big request. The practical upshot: avoid allocating large objects per request if you can help it. Rent reusable buffers from ArrayPool<T>.Shared instead of new byte[64 * 1024] -ing a fresh one every time, stream data instead of loading whole files into one byte array, reach for RecyclableMemoryStream when you need a big in-memory stream, and lean on System.IO.Pipelines for high-throughput I/O. You can force a one-off LOH compaction ( GCSettings.LargeObjectHeapCompactionMode = GCLargeObjectHeapCompactionMode.CompactOnce; GC.Collect(); ), but reaching for that is rarely the right fix — it's treating a symptom. One more related idea: pinning . Normally the GC is free to move objects around during compaction — that's exactly what keeps the heap tidy. But some scenarios, mainly native interop and async I/O buffers, need an object to stay at a fixed memory address for a while, so the GC can be told not to move a specific object via fixed or GCHandle.Alloc(obj, Pinned) . The catch: a long-lived pin sits in the middle of the heap like a boulder the GC has to compact around , which fragments things. The Pinned Object Heap (POH, via GC.AllocateArray<byte>(n, pinned: true) ) solves this cleanly: it gives you buffers that are pinned from the moment they're born , in a region set aside specifically for that purpose, so they never fragment the normal object heap at all."},{"c":"memory-gc","s":"finalizers-the-gcs-last-resort-and-why-its-slow","h":"Finalizers: the GC's last resort, and why it's slow","t":"# Finalizers: the GC's last resort, and why it's slow Intermediate Every so often, an object wraps something the GC genuinely can't clean up on its own — a native handle, say. For those cases, C# offers a finalizer , written as ~T() . It sounds like a safety net. In practice, it's a safety net you should almost never actually rely on, and understanding why tells you a lot about how the GC treats these objects differently from everything else. Here's the sequence, once an object with a finalizer becomes unreachable: The GC notices it's unreachable — but, because it has a finalizer still waiting to run, it can't free it yet . Instead, it moves the object to something called the f-reachable queue , which — notice the name — is itself a GC root. The object just got re-rooted , temporarily, by the very mechanism that was about to collect it. It also gets promoted to the next generation in the process. At some later, genuinely unpredictable time, the single dedicated finalizer thread gets around to actually running that object's Finalize method. Only after that has happened does a later GC finally free the memory for real. Walk back through that and you'll see why finalizable objects are expensive: they survive at least one extra collection they otherwise wouldn't have, they get promoted to an older generation earlier than their actual usage pattern justifies, and their cleanup timing is completely outside your control. That's precisely why deterministic cleanup in .NET uses IDisposable plus using instead — and why a well-written Dispose method calls GC.SuppressFinalize(this) , which tells the runtime \"cleanup already happened properly, you can skip the finalizer and the extra generation hop entirely.\" Where you genuinely need to wrap a native handle, prefer SafeHandle over writing a finalizer by hand — it already does this correctly. See Exceptions & disposal for the full pattern. The GC doesn't release unmanaged resources promptly Everything above is about managed memory — the heap the GC actually tracks. File handles, sockets, database connections and native memory held by managed wrapper objects are a different story entirely: they get released when you dispose them, or, failing that, whenever a finalizer eventually gets around to it. A DbConnection left undisposed is a connection-pool leak the instant it happens, regardless of how much free RAM the machine has — the GC not having run yet is irrelevant."},{"c":"memory-gc","s":"weak-references-and-caches","h":"Weak references and caches","t":"# Weak references and caches Advanced Sometimes you want to hold onto an object if it's still around , without that holding being the reason it stays around. That's what WeakReference<T> is for: it references an object without keeping it alive, so the GC is still free to collect it, and TryGetTarget simply returns false afterwards if it did. ConditionalWeakTable<TKey,TValue> does something similar but for attaching extra data to an object without extending its lifetime. These show up in some cache and event-pattern implementations, but for the vast majority of web app caching you're better off with MemoryCache , configured with real size limits and expirations — it gives you predictable eviction, whereas weak references leave eviction entirely up to the GC's own timing, which is much harder to reason about in production."},{"c":"memory-gc","s":"memory-leaks-in-net-still-possible-just-a-different-shape","h":"Memory leaks in .NET: still possible, just a different shape","t":"# Memory leaks in .NET: still possible, just a different shape Intermediate Earlier this chapter said something worth repeating exactly: an object is only garbage once it's unreachable — not once it's useless . A managed memory leak is precisely the gap between those two: objects that are still technically reachable from some root, but that your application will never actually use again. Every single row in this table traces back to one of the five GC roots from earlier, holding a reference it shouldn't be: Cause Example Fix Static collections that only grow static List<Order> _processed , a static Dictionary cache without eviction Bounded caches ( MemoryCache with SizeLimit /expiry), or don't keep them Event handlers never unsubscribed A transient subscribes to a singleton's event; the singleton keeps every subscriber alive Unsubscribe in Dispose ; weak events; avoid events between lifetimes Captive dependencies A singleton holding a scoped DbContext whose change tracker grows forever Correct DI lifetimes ( DI ) Unbounded in-memory queues Channel.CreateUnbounded with a slow consumer Bounded channels with backpressure Timers System.Threading.Timer callbacks referencing big objects; timers never disposed Dispose timers; PeriodicTimer in hosted services Closures capturing too much A long-lived lambda capturing this or a large object Capture only what's needed; static lambdas Resolving disposable transients from the root provider app.Services.GetService<TransientDisposable>() repeatedly: tracked until shutdown Resolve inside scopes Large caches of EF entities / tracked graphs Long-lived DbContext Short-lived contexts, AsNoTracking HttpClient /connection objects created per call Sockets and native buffers pile up IHttpClientFactory , singletons for SDK clients Native memory Leaked unmanaged buffers, un-disposed native wrappers Dispose , SafeHandle ; diagnose with native tools How do you actually notice one of these is happening, as opposed to just normal memory use going up and down? The tell is specific: Gen2 size and total working set grow steadily under constant, steady load, and never come back down even after full GCs run. A healthy app's memory goes up and down as load varies; a leaking one's floor keeps rising. Left alone, that ends in either an OutOfMemoryException or a container getting OOMKilled ."},{"c":"memory-gc","s":"actually-finding-a-leak-a-real-diagnostic-workflow","h":"Actually finding a leak: a real diagnostic workflow","t":"# Actually finding a leak: a real diagnostic workflow Advanced Knowing the table above is one thing; actually finding which row applies, in a production service you didn't write the leaking code in, is another. Here's a workflow that works, step by step, using tools you install once with dotnet tool install : Shell Copy dotnet tool install -g dotnet-counters dotnet tool install -g dotnet-gcdump dotnet tool install -g dotnet-dump # 1. Watch live: GC heap size per generation, % time in GC, allocation rate, Gen0/1/2 counts, LOH size, working set dotnet-counters monitor -n Shop.Api --counters System.Runtime # 2. Snapshot the managed heap (lightweight: types, counts, sizes, roots), twice, minutes apart, then compare dotnet-gcdump collect -n Shop.Api -o before.gcdump dotnet-gcdump collect -n Shop.Api -o after.gcdump # open both in Visual Studio / PerfView and diff: which types grew? # 3. Full process dump for deep analysis (who roots this object?) dotnet-dump collect -n Shop.Api dotnet-dump analyze core_20261027.dmp > dumpheap -stat # objects by type and total size > dumpheap -mt <MethodTable> # instances of a type > gcroot <address> # the reference chain keeping it alive (THE leak answer) > eeheap -gc # heap/segment overview Step 1 tells you whether you have a problem and roughly how fast it's growing. Step 2 tells you which type is growing — diffing two snapshots taken minutes apart under steady load shows exactly which object counts kept climbing when they should have stayed flat. Step 3's final command, gcroot , is the one that actually answers the question: given one specific surviving instance, it walks backward through live references and shows you the exact chain of objects keeping it alive — which, cross-referenced against the roots table from earlier, tells you exactly which of the ten leak patterns above you're looking at. Beyond these three CLI tools, you'll also find Visual Studio's Diagnostic Tools / memory profiler , JetBrains dotMemory , PerfView (deep GC event and allocation sampling), Application Insights' own memory metrics and profiler, dotnet-trace with GC events enabled, and — specifically inside Kubernetes — kubectl debug with the diagnostics tools attached as an ephemeral container. The numbers actually worth watching: % time in GC (sustained above roughly 10% is a real problem, not noise), allocation rate in MB/s, Gen2 collection count , heap size right after a Gen2 collection finishes (this is the single clearest leak signal — it should be flat under steady load, not climbing), GC pause times (directly affects your p99 latency), and LOH size."},{"c":"memory-gc","s":"writing-allocation-friendly-code","h":"Writing allocation-friendly code","t":"# Writing allocation-friendly code Advanced Given everything above — that allocation rate is what drives GC frequency, and GC frequency is what drives latency and throughput — here's the direct, practical payoff: a table of common allocation-heavy patterns, and the lower-allocation alternative for each. Technique Instead of StringBuilder , string.Join , string.Create , interpolated string handlers += concatenation in loops Span<T> , ReadOnlySpan<char> slicing and span-based parsing Substring , Split on hot paths ArrayPool<T>.Shared.Rent/Return , stackalloc (small) new byte[64 * 1024] per request Structs ( readonly struct , record struct ) for small values Classes for tiny, short-lived data Generics Boxing via object /non-generic collections Pre-sized collections new List<T>(n) Repeated resize-and-copy Static lambdas / avoiding captures in hot paths Closures allocating per call ValueTask for frequently-synchronous async methods Task allocation per call Cached JsonSerializerOptions , source-generated serializers and [LoggerMessage] New options per call; boxing log arguments AsNoTracking + projections in EF Core Materialising and tracking full entity graphs Streaming ( IAsyncEnumerable , Stream , PipeReader ) Buffering whole payloads in memory FrozenDictionary for static lookups Rebuilding dictionaries Object pooling ( ObjectPool<T> ) for expensive reusable objects Allocating them per operation One honest caveat before you go rewrite everything: measure first (see Performance engineering ). Most business code spends almost all of its time waiting on I/O, not allocating — and readability is worth more than shaving a handful of allocations off code that runs once per request and was never the bottleneck to begin with. Don't call GC.Collect() It's tempting, when you're debugging a memory issue, to just force a collection and see what happens. Resist it in production code. The GC's internal heuristics are already tuned to your app's actual allocation pattern over time, and forcing a collection out of turn almost always hurts : it promotes objects to older generations before they've earned it, wastes CPU on a collection that wasn't actually needed yet, and causes a pause at a moment you didn't choose. The legitimate uses are narrow: tests, benchmarks, and right after a genuinely one-off huge cleanup in a batch job — and even then, measure before and after to confirm it actually helped. Q How does the .NET garbage collector work? It's a generational, tracing, mark-and-compact collector. Allocation bumps a pointer in Gen0. When a generation's budget fills, a GC suspends threads, marks all objects reachable from roots (statics, stack locals, GC handles, the finalization queue), reclaims the rest, compacts survivors and promotes them to the next generation. Gen0/1 collections are frequent and cheap; Gen2 (full, including the LOH) is expensive and mostly runs concurrently in background GC. Q What are GC generations and why do they exist? Gen0 holds new objects, Gen1 is a buffer, and Gen2 holds long-lived objects. Based on the generational hypothesis that most objects die young, collecting the small young generation frequently reclaims most garbage cheaply, without scanning the whole heap each time. Q What is the Large Object Heap? A separate heap region for objects of 85,000 bytes or more. It's collected only during Gen2 collections and not compacted by default, so frequent large allocations cause full GCs and fragmentation. Pool or stream large buffers instead. Q Workstation vs Server GC? Workstation GC uses a single heap and favours low latency and memory, and is the default for client apps. Server GC uses a heap and dedicated GC thread per core with parallel collection for higher throughput, and is the default for ASP.NET Core. .NET 9+ adds DATAS so Server GC adapts heap count to load, keeping memory proportional in containers. Q What are GC roots? The starting points for reachability: static fields, local variables and parameters on th"},{"c":"memory-gc","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Fundamentals of garbage collection Runtime configuration options for garbage collection Debug a memory leak in .NET Maoni Stephens' blog posts on the .NET GC (she leads the GC team). Book: Pro .NET Memory Management by Konrad Kokosa: the definitive deep dive."},{"c":"memory-gc","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run (a plain console app, .NET 8/9, no extra packages). It checks generation assignment, the LOH size threshold, promotion after a collection, ArrayPool<T> reuse, and WeakReference<T> behavior directly against the live GC in your process. Server GC/DATAS and container heap limits are process-wide startup configuration, not something an in-process console app can flip and observe, so those claims are left to the Microsoft Learn links above instead of faked here. C# Run ▶ Copy // Verify this chapter: run with `dotnet run`. No external packages needed. using System ; using System . Buffers ; void Check ( string name, bool ok) { if (!ok) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: {name}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: {name}\" ); } // 1. A new object starts life in Gen0. var fresh = new byte [ 16 ]; Check ( \"new small object starts in Gen0\" , GC . GetGeneration (fresh) == 0 ); // 2. Objects >= 85,000 bytes go straight to the Large Object Heap, which is // logically part of Gen2 (GC.GetGeneration reports 2 for LOH objects). var small = new byte [ 1000 ]; var large = new byte [ 100_000 ]; Check ( \"small array (1,000 bytes) is not on the LOH (Gen0/1)\" , GC . GetGeneration (small) < 2 ); Check ( \"array >= 85,000 bytes is allocated on the LOH (reported as Gen2)\" , GC . GetGeneration (large) == 2 ); // 3. Surviving a collection promotes an object to the next generation. var survivor = new object (); int genBefore = GC . GetGeneration (survivor); GC . Collect ( 0 , GCCollectionMode . Forced , blocking: true ); // collect Gen0 only int genAfter = GC . GetGeneration (survivor); Check ( \"an object still referenced after a Gen0 collection is promoted (or already older)\" , genAfter >= genBefore); // 4. GC.CollectionCount increases after a forced collection of that generation. long countBefore = GC . CollectionCount ( 0 ); GC . Collect ( 0 , GCCollectionMode . Forced , blocking: true ); long countAfter = GC . CollectionCount ( 0 ); Check ( \"GC.CollectionCount(0) increases after forcing a Gen0 collection\" , countAfter > countBefore); // 5. ArrayPool<T>.Shared.Rent can return the exact same backing array once // it has been returned to the pool -- demonstrating the \"no new allocation\" // reuse the chapter recommends for hot paths. var pool = ArrayPool < byte >. Shared ; var rented1 = pool. Rent ( 4096 ); pool. Return (rented1); var rented2 = pool. Rent ( 4096 ); bool sameArray = ReferenceEquals (rented1, rented2); pool. Return (rented2); Console . WriteLine (sameArray ? \"OK: ArrayPool<T>.Shared reused the same backing array (no fresh heap allocation)\" : \"OK: ArrayPool<T>.Shared returned a different array this run (pool reuse is a best-effort optimization, not guaranteed every time)\" ); // 6. WeakReference<T> lets the GC collect an object even though a reference // to it technically still exists, unlike a normal (strong) reference. object ? strongTarget = new object (); var weak = new WeakReference < object >(strongTarget); Check ( \"WeakReference.TryGetTarget finds the object while a strong reference keeps it alive\" , weak. TryGetTarget ( out _)); strongTarget = null ; // drop the only strong reference GC . Collect (); GC . WaitForPendingFinalizers (); GC . Collect (); // Not asserted as a hard failure: the GC is not obligated to collect on any // particular call, but under GC.Collect() + WaitForPendingFinalizers() in a // console app with no other roots, this reliably clears. bool collected = !weak. TryGetTarget ( out _); Console . WriteLine (collected ? \"OK: after dropping the only strong reference and forcing a collection, WeakReference no longer finds the object\" : \"OK: object survived this run (GC timing is not guaranteed) -- see Further reading: Fundamentals of garbage collection\" ); // 7. Server GC vs Workstation GC and DATAS are process-wide runtime // configuration (csproj / runtimeconfig.json / env vars), not something a // console app can flip at runtime and observe in-process -- see Further // reading: \"Runtime"},{"c":"threading-concurrency","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Concurrency = several things in progress at once (maybe interleaved); parallelism = several things literally running at the same instant on different cores; asynchrony = not blocking a thread while waiting on something slow. A web server is heavily concurrent, mostly asynchronous, and only occasionally parallel. _count++ looks like one step but is really three (read, add, write) — two threads can interleave those steps and silently lose an update. That's a race condition , and it never throws an exception; it just produces a wrong number. Interlocked fixes a single shared variable with one atomic CPU instruction (no lock needed). A lock / Monitor protects more than one variable at once, but you can never await inside it — use SemaphoreSlim when you need both mutual exclusion and await . A deadlock happens when two threads each hold something the other is waiting for. The practical fix is almost always: always acquire locks in the same order everywhere. The BCL ships thread-safe collections ( ConcurrentDictionary , Channel<T> , immutable snapshots) specifically so you don't have to hand-roll locking for common patterns like caches and producer/consumer queues. The one rule that matters most in ASP.NET Core: every singleton must be thread-safe , because it's the one instance every concurrent request shares."},{"c":"threading-concurrency","s":"three-words-people-mix-up-constantly","h":"Three words people mix up constantly","t":"# Three words people mix up constantly Beginner Before anything else, let's pin down three words that sound almost interchangeable and genuinely aren't — because half the confusion in this topic comes from people using them loosely. Term Meaning .NET tools Concurrency Several tasks in progress at the same time — they might be truly simultaneous, or just interleaved, taking turns Everything in this chapter Parallelism Several tasks actually executing at the same instant , on different CPU cores, specifically to speed up CPU-bound work Parallel.For/ForEach(Async) , PLINQ, Task.Run , Task.WhenAll over CPU work Asynchrony Not blocking a thread while it waits for something slow, like I/O async / await , Task Here's a sentence worth re-reading until it clicks: a web server is massively concurrent — thousands of requests in flight — but mostly asynchronous , because almost all of that \"in flight\" time is spent waiting on a database or a downstream API, not computing. It's only occasionally, genuinely parallel , when one request deliberately spreads CPU-bound work across cores. Most of what this chapter covers is really about concurrency: many things happening at once, and the bugs that come from them touching shared state at the same time. One more related word you'll meet below: multithreading , which just means using more than one OS thread. It's the underlying mechanism both parallelism and (some) concurrency are built on."},{"c":"threading-concurrency","s":"threads-are-expensive-the-pool-exists-because-of-that","h":"Threads are expensive; the pool exists because of that","t":"# Threads are expensive; the pool exists because of that Beginner A thread is a path of execution the operating system schedules onto a CPU core, and every single one gets its own stack — about 1 MB reserved by default. Creating a thread from scratch costs real time (milliseconds, not microseconds) and real memory, and once you have too many of them, the OS spends more time switching between them than actually running your code. So .NET doesn't create a fresh thread for every piece of work. Instead, the thread pool keeps a set of reusable worker threads sitting ready, and hands them queued work items as they become free. Nearly everything concurrent in a typical ASP.NET Core app ends up running on it: Every incoming request (Kestrel dispatches each one to the pool) Every await continuation — the \"resume here later\" code you saw in the async/await chapter — since ASP.NET Core has no SynchronizationContext to route continuations elsewhere Task.Run , Parallel , and timer callbacks C# Run ▶ Copy var t = new Thread (() => DoWork ()) { IsBackground = true , Name = \"legacy-worker\" }; // dedicated thread: rare today t. Start (); await Task . Run (() => ComputeHash (bytes)); // queue CPU work to the pool await Task . Factory . StartNew ( LongRunningLoop , TaskCreationOptions . LongRunning ); // hint: gets a dedicated thread Here's the mechanism worth really understanding, because it's the root cause of a production incident nearly every .NET backend team eventually has: the pool doesn't start out huge. It begins with roughly one thread per CPU core, and when more work arrives than it can currently handle, it grows gradually — a \"hill-climbing\" algorithm adds threads a little at a time, and a separate starvation-detection mechanism injects roughly one new thread every half-second to one second under sustained pressure. That's deliberately slow, because spinning up threads too eagerly would waste the exact resources the pool exists to conserve. Now put that together with one more fact: calling .Result , Thread.Sleep , or any blocking I/O call ties up a pool thread for the entire duration of the block — it can't go do other work in the meantime. Under load, with the pool only growing a thread or two a second, a sudden burst of blocked threads can genuinely starve the queue faster than the pool can backfill it. That specific failure mode has a name — thread-pool starvation — and it's covered in detail in async/await , because it's really a consequence of not using async / await properly."},{"c":"threading-concurrency","s":"race-conditions-when-it-depends-on-timing-becomes-a-bug","h":"Race conditions: when \"it depends on timing\" becomes a bug","t":"# Race conditions: when \"it depends on timing\" becomes a bug Beginner Here's a piece of code that looks completely harmless: C# Run ▶ Copy private int _count; public void Increment () => _count++; It isn't harmless, and the reason is worth walking through slowly, because _count++ looks like one operation but is actually three : read the current value, add one to it, write the new value back. Each of those three steps is a separate moment in time — and another thread can slip in between any of them. Walk through exactly what the diagram shows: Thread A reads _count and gets 5 . Before A writes anything back, Thread B also reads _count — and also gets 5 , because A hasn't written yet. Thread A computes 5 + 1 = 6 and writes 6 . Thread B, working from the 5 it already read, also computes 5 + 1 = 6 , and writes 6 . Two increments happened. The final value is 6 , not 7 . One of the increments vanished without a trace, without throwing any exception, without any sign anything went wrong — which is exactly what makes race conditions so nasty: the result depends entirely on the unpredictable timing of two threads, so the bug might not show up once in a thousand test runs and then appear under real production load. This exact \"read, modify, write\" shape hides in more places than a bare counter. Check-then-act code — if (!dict.ContainsKey(k)) dict.Add(k, v); — has the identical flaw: two threads can both pass the ContainsKey check before either calls Add . Lazy initialisation done by hand has the same shape. So does iterating a collection while another thread modifies it, which in .NET usually surfaces as an InvalidOperationException , or — worse — silently corrupted state in a plain Dictionary . # Where does this actually bite in a web app? The good news: most of your code is completely safe from this by default, because most of it isn't shared between threads. Shared? Example Safe? ✘ Not shared Locals, request-scoped services, HttpContext (one request) Yes — nothing else can see them ✔ Shared Singleton services , static fields, IMemoryCache entries, hosted services, IOptionsMonitor callbacks Must be made thread-safe deliberately That gives you a simple, memorable rule: every singleton must be thread-safe , because a singleton is, by definition, the one instance every concurrent request calls into at the same time. Everything from here through the rest of this chapter is really a toolbox for making that true."},{"c":"threading-concurrency","s":"interlocked-the-fastest-fix-when-it-applies","h":"Interlocked: the fastest fix, when it applies","t":"# Interlocked: the fastest fix, when it applies Intermediate The simplest tool in that toolbox handles exactly the counter problem from above, when all you need to protect is a single variable: C# Run ▶ Copy private long _requests; public void OnRequest () => Interlocked . Increment ( ref _requests); // atomic ++ public long Snapshot () => Interlocked . Read ( ref _requests); private int _initialised; public void InitOnce () { if ( Interlocked . CompareExchange ( ref _initialised, 1 , 0 ) == 0 ) Init (); } // CAS: set to 1 only if still 0 Interlocked . Add ( ref _bytes, n); var old = Interlocked . Exchange ( ref _currentConfig, newConfig); // atomic reference swap (great for immutable snapshots) Interlocked.Increment doesn't fix the race by making the three steps faster — it fixes it by making them one indivisible step , a single atomic CPU instruction that no other thread can observe halfway through. There's no window left for another thread to sneak a read in between, because there's no \"in between\" any more. That's also exactly why Interlocked is the fastest synchronisation tool in .NET: it needs no lock, no waiting, nothing but one CPU instruction. The trade-off is right there in the name — it only ever covers one variable at a time . The moment you need to keep two or more related pieces of state consistent with each other, Interlocked can't help, and you need a real lock."},{"c":"threading-concurrency","s":"locks-protecting-more-than-one-variable-at-once","h":"Locks: protecting more than one variable at once","t":"# Locks: protecting more than one variable at once Intermediate # lock / Lock / Monitor C# Run ▶ Copy private readonly Lock _gate = new (); // C# 13 / .NET 9: dedicated Lock type (faster); use a private readonly object before that private readonly Dictionary < string , Price > _prices = new (); public void Update ( string sku, Price p) { lock (_gate) // only one thread at a time inside; others wait { _prices[sku] = p; } } public bool TryGet ( string sku, out Price ? p) { lock (_gate) return _prices. TryGetValue (sku, out p); } lock (obj) is really shorthand — it compiles down to Monitor.Enter / Monitor.Exit wrapped in a try/finally , which guarantees the lock is released even if the code inside throws. Four rules keep this safe in practice: lock on a private, readonly object (never this , never typeof(X) , never a string — all of those could accidentally be locked on by other unrelated code too, defeating the whole point); keep what's inside the lock short ; never await inside a lock (the compiler actively forbids this — you'll see exactly why in a moment); and never call unknown code — an event, a callback — while holding a lock, because you have no idea what it might do. # Why await and lock don't mix Here's the \"why\" behind that compiler rule, because it's one of those facts that's easy to memorise and hard to actually understand. A Monitor lock is thread-affine : whichever thread acquired it is the one that must release it. But you already know from the async/await chapter that when an await suspends and later resumes, the continuation can land on a completely different thread pool thread than the one that started it. Put those two facts together and a lock held across an await becomes nonsensical — the thread that's supposed to release it might not be the thread that's running by the time execution comes back. So the compiler simply won't let you write it. # SemaphoreSlim: the async-friendly alternative When you genuinely need mutual exclusion and the ability to await inside the protected section, reach for SemaphoreSlim instead: C# Run ▶ Copy private readonly SemaphoreSlim _refreshGate = new ( 1 , 1 ); // mutual exclusion usable with await public async Task < string > GetTokenAsync ( CancellationToken ct) { if (_token is { IsValid : true } t) return t. Value ; // fast path without locking await _refreshGate. WaitAsync (ct); try { if (_token is { IsValid : true } t2) return t2. Value ; // double-check after acquiring _token = await FetchTokenAsync (ct); return _token. Value ; } finally { _refreshGate. Release (); } } private readonly SemaphoreSlim _throttle = new ( 10 ); // at most 10 concurrent calls to a fragile API Notice the shape of GetTokenAsync : it checks the fast path before acquiring the semaphore, and checks it again right after — a pattern called \"double-checked locking\". Why check twice? Because between the first check failing and this method actually acquiring the gate, some other call might have already done the refresh for you — checking again avoids doing the work a second time unnecessarily. A SemaphoreSlim(1, 1) behaves like a lock that's safe to hold across an await ; one more detail worth knowing is that a plain SemaphoreSlim(n) with n greater than one isn't mutual exclusion at all — it's a concurrency limiter , letting up to n callers through at once, which is exactly what _throttle above is doing. # The rest of the toolbox, briefly Primitive Use ReaderWriterLockSlim Many concurrent readers, exclusive writers (sync only). Often an immutable snapshot + Interlocked.Exchange is simpler and faster Mutex Cross- process mutual exclusion (named mutex), e.g. single-instance apps ManualResetEventSlim , AutoResetEvent , CountdownEvent , Barrier Signalling between threads (mostly replaced by tasks and channels) Lazy<T> / LazyInitializer Thread-safe lazy initialisation AsyncLocal<T> / ThreadLocal<T> Per-async-flow / per-thread values Distributed locks (Redis, SQL app locks, Blob leases) Across machines : a C# lo"},{"c":"threading-concurrency","s":"deadlocks-when-locking-itself-becomes-the-bug","h":"Deadlocks: when locking itself becomes the bug","t":"# Deadlocks: when locking itself becomes the bug Intermediate Locks solve the race-condition problem — but used carelessly, they create a different one, and this one doesn't just lose an update, it freezes your app solid. Here's the simplest possible version: C# Run ▶ Copy // Thread 1: lock(A) { … lock(B) { } } // Thread 2: lock(B) { … lock(A) { } } → each holds one and waits forever for the other Walk through it: Thread 1 grabs lock A , then tries to grab lock B — but Thread 2 already has B , and is itself waiting on A , which Thread 1 is holding. Neither thread will ever let go, because neither can proceed. They're not crashed — they're just frozen, forever, waiting on each other in a perfect, silent circle. There's a name for the four conditions that all have to be true at once for this to happen ( Coffman's conditions ): each lock can only be held by one thread (true of every lock you've seen); a thread keeps what it already has while it waits for more; nothing can force a thread to let go of a lock early; and the chain of \"who's waiting for whom\" loops back on itself. The useful takeaway isn't memorising the four names — it's this: break just one of them, and the deadlock becomes impossible. That's exactly where the fixes below come from: Lock ordering : if every piece of code that needs both A and B always acquires them in the same order — say, always A before B — a circular wait becomes structurally impossible. This single discipline prevents the overwhelming majority of real deadlocks. Don't hold a lock while calling out — to an event, a callback, I/O, another service — because you have no idea what that outside code might itself try to lock. Use timeouts instead of waiting forever: Monitor.TryEnter(obj, timeout) , SemaphoreSlim.WaitAsync(timeout) . A deadlock that times out and retries is recoverable; one that waits forever is not. Prefer designs that need no locks at all where you can: immutable data, Interlocked , concurrent collections, channels and message passing — all covered below. And the async-specific version of this bug, which you met properly in the async/await chapter: never block on async code ( .Result ) anywhere a SynchronizationContext exists — it's a two-thread deadlock with exactly the same circular-wait shape as the example above, just dressed up differently."},{"c":"threading-concurrency","s":"why-the-compiler-and-cpu-are-allowed-to-reorder-your-code","h":"Why the compiler and CPU are allowed to reorder your code","t":"# Why the compiler and CPU are allowed to reorder your code Advanced There's a subtler category of concurrency bug that has nothing to do with locks at all, and it trips people up because it violates an assumption that feels completely obvious: that code runs in the order you wrote it. For a single thread, it effectively does. For multiple threads watching each other, it sometimes doesn't — and here's why that's actually allowed. Compilers, the JIT, and CPUs are all permitted to reorder reads and writes, and to cache values in registers instead of main memory, as long as the single thread running that code can't tell the difference . That last clause is the key: the guarantee is only ever about what one thread observes about itself. Nobody promised anything about what a different thread, watching from outside, will see — and that gap is exactly where this bug lives. C# Run ▶ Copy private bool _stop; public void Run () { while (!_stop) { /* work */ } } // the JIT may hoist _stop into a register → loops forever public void Stop () => _stop = true ; From Run 's own point of view, nothing here looks unsafe at all, so the JIT is completely within its rights to decide _stop can't possibly change during the loop and cache it once in a register instead of re-reading memory every iteration. The loop then runs forever, even though some other thread genuinely did call Stop() — that write just never became visible to this thread's cached copy. The fix is to tell the runtime, explicitly, \"don't cache this — always go back to real memory\": mark the field volatile ( private volatile bool _stop; ), or use Volatile.Read / Volatile.Write directly. Locks and Interlocked operations also happen to act as full memory barriers, which is a second reason they fix more bugs than just the ones they're obviously designed for. In practice, though, don't reach for volatile by hand for a stop signal — use a CancellationToken , which does this correctly for you, and save hand-rolled lock-free code for the rare cases that genuinely need it. It's expert territory, and the bugs in it are exactly this subtle."},{"c":"threading-concurrency","s":"concurrent-collections-built-so-you-dont-have-to-lock-by-han","h":"Concurrent collections: built so you don't have to lock by hand","t":"# Concurrent collections: built so you don't have to lock by hand Intermediate Everything so far has been about protecting your own state with locks or atomics. The BCL also ships a set of collections that are already safe to use from multiple threads, without you writing any locking code at all: Collection Notes ConcurrentDictionary<K,V> Fine-grained locking; atomic GetOrAdd , AddOrUpdate , TryRemove , TryUpdate . The factory may run more than once, so wrap it in Lazy<T> for expensive work ConcurrentQueue<T> / ConcurrentStack<T> Lock-free FIFO/LIFO ConcurrentBag<T> Unordered; thread-local optimised BlockingCollection<T> Blocking bounded producer/consumer (sync) Channel<T> Async producer/consumer: bounded or unbounded, single/multi reader/writer, backpressure ImmutableDictionary / ImmutableList + ImmutableInterlocked Snapshot semantics: readers never lock FrozenDictionary Built once, read concurrently, no locks needed The immutable-snapshot pattern deserves its own look, because it's a genuinely elegant way to sidestep locking entirely for data that's read constantly but written rarely: C# Run ▶ Copy // Copy-on-write snapshot: readers are lock-free, writers swap a new immutable instance atomically private ImmutableDictionary < string , FeatureFlag > _flags = ImmutableDictionary < string , FeatureFlag >. Empty ; public bool IsOn ( string name) => _flags. TryGetValue (name, out var f) && f. Enabled ; // no lock public void Reload ( IEnumerable < FeatureFlag > all) => Interlocked . Exchange ( ref _flags, all. ToImmutableDictionary (f => f. Name )); Here's why this works without a single lock anywhere: readers never modify _flags , they just read whatever reference happens to be there right now — and reading a reference is already atomic. When new flags arrive, Reload doesn't mutate the existing dictionary at all; it builds a brand new one and then uses Interlocked.Exchange to atomically swap the reference _flags points at. Any reader that grabbed the old reference a moment earlier keeps working with a perfectly consistent, if slightly stale, snapshot. Nobody ever sees a half-updated dictionary, because nobody ever updates one — they only ever replace the whole thing at once."},{"c":"threading-concurrency","s":"producer-consumer-pipelines-with-channels","h":"Producer/consumer pipelines with Channels","t":"# Producer/consumer pipelines with Channels Intermediate Channel<T> is the async-aware queue that connects a producer doing one job to one or more consumers doing another, with built-in backpressure: C# Run ▶ Copy var channel = Channel . CreateBounded < ImportRow >( new BoundedChannelOptions ( 1_000 ) { FullMode = BoundedChannelFullMode . Wait , // producer awaits when full: backpressure SingleWriter = true , SingleReader = false }); var producer = Task . Run ( async () => { await foreach ( var row in ReadCsvAsync (path, ct)) await channel. Writer . WriteAsync (row, ct); channel. Writer . Complete (); // no more items }); var consumers = Enumerable . Range ( 0 , 4 ). Select (_ => Task . Run ( async () => { await foreach ( var row in channel. Reader . ReadAllAsync (ct)) // ends when the writer completes and the channel is drained await SaveAsync (row, ct); })). ToArray (); await Task . WhenAll (consumers. Append (producer)); The word bounded is doing real work here. If the channel had no capacity limit, a slow consumer couldn't push back on a fast producer at all — the queue would simply grow without limit until memory ran out, which is a memory leak with extra steps. FullMode = Wait means that once the channel hits its 1,000-item capacity, WriteAsync itself starts await ing until a consumer makes room, which is backpressure in its purest form: the producer is automatically throttled to match how fast the consumers can actually keep up. This exact pattern — one bounded queue, several workers, cancellation, a clean completion signal — covers the large majority of in-process pipeline needs you'll run into. For genuinely multi-stage pipelines with branching and merging, look at TPL Dataflow ( System.Threading.Tasks.Dataflow ), which generalises this idea into a graph of connected blocks."},{"c":"threading-concurrency","s":"data-parallelism-parallel-and-plinq","h":"Data parallelism: Parallel and PLINQ","t":"# Data parallelism: Parallel and PLINQ Intermediate Everything above this point has mostly been about correctness under concurrency. This last tool is about speed : deliberately spreading genuinely CPU-bound work — not I/O waiting, actual computation — across multiple cores at once. C# Run ▶ Copy // Synchronous CPU work across cores Parallel . For ( 0 , images. Length , new ParallelOptions { MaxDegreeOfParallelism = Environment . ProcessorCount }, i => thumbnails[i] = Resize (images[i])); // each index written by one iteration: no shared mutable state // Async work with bounded concurrency (I/O-bound fan-out) await Parallel . ForEachAsync (urls, new ParallelOptions { MaxDegreeOfParallelism = 8 , CancellationToken = ct }, async (url, token) => await DownloadAsync (url, token)); // PLINQ var primes = Enumerable . Range ( 2 , 5_000_000 ). AsParallel (). WithDegreeOfParallelism ( 4 ). Where ( IsPrime ). Count (); Three things worth knowing before reaching for this. First, parallelism isn't free: partitioning the work, scheduling it across threads, and merging the results back together all cost something, so it only pays off once the per-item work is substantial enough to be worth that overhead — parallelising a trivial loop can easily make it slower . Second, if the body of your loop touches shared mutable state (as opposed to the thumbnails[i] example above, where each iteration writes to a different, non-overlapping index), you're right back to the race-condition problem from the start of this chapter — use Parallel.For 's localInit / localFinally overloads to accumulate per-thread results safely instead. And third, specifically for ASP.NET Core: avoid heavy parallel CPU work inside a request handler . Every thread that work pulls onto a core is a thread stolen from the shared pool other requests are also waiting on — offload genuinely heavy CPU work to a background worker instead, so one expensive request can't starve everyone else."},{"c":"threading-concurrency","s":"thread-safety-checklist-for-asp-net-core","h":"Thread safety checklist for ASP.NET Core","t":"# Thread safety checklist for ASP.NET Core Advanced Pulling every idea in this chapter together into something you can actually check a pull request against: Singletons hold only thread-safe or immutable state (concurrent collections, Interlocked , snapshots, locks around compound operations). No mutable static fields used as caches without synchronisation and bounds. DbContext is never shared across concurrent operations (one per scope; IDbContextFactory for parallel work). HttpContext is never used outside its request or from background threads. No blocking on async code ( .Result / .Wait() ), and no Thread.Sleep in request paths. lock sections are short and contain no awaits or external calls; use SemaphoreSlim for async exclusion. Background work is bounded (channels with capacity, MaxDegreeOfParallelism ). Cross-instance coordination uses distributed locks or the database, not C# locks. Q What's the difference between concurrency, parallelism and asynchrony? Concurrency is multiple tasks in progress at once (possibly interleaved). Parallelism is executing multiple tasks simultaneously on multiple cores for CPU-bound speed-up. Asynchrony is not blocking a thread while waiting for I/O, so threads can do other work. A web server is highly concurrent and mostly asynchronous. Q What is the thread pool and why does blocking it hurt? A pool of reusable worker threads that runs queued work items: ASP.NET Core requests, await continuations, Task.Run. It grows slowly when saturated, so blocking threads (sync-over-async, Thread.Sleep, sync I/O) causes thread-pool starvation, where queued work waits and latency spikes while CPU stays low. Q What is a race condition and how do you prevent it? When correctness depends on thread timing, e.g. two threads doing count++ and losing an update, or check-then-act on a dictionary. Prevent it with atomic operations (Interlocked), locks around compound operations, concurrent collections' atomic methods, immutability, or avoiding shared mutable state altogether. Q Why can't you use await inside a lock statement? What do you use instead? Monitor locks are thread-affine (they must be released by the thread that acquired them), but after an await the continuation may run on a different thread, and holding a lock across awaits risks long blocking and deadlocks. The compiler forbids it. Use SemaphoreSlim(1,1) with WaitAsync/Release in try/finally. Q How do deadlocks occur and how do you avoid them? Two or more threads each hold a resource another needs and wait in a cycle. Avoid them with consistent lock ordering, short critical sections without external calls, timeouts on acquisition, lock-free or message-passing designs, and not blocking on async code where a SynchronizationContext exists. Q How do you make a singleton service thread-safe? Prefer immutable state, or swap immutable snapshots atomically with Interlocked.Exchange. Use concurrent collections with their atomic methods, Interlocked for counters, and locks or SemaphoreSlim around compound operations. Don't hold per-request state or non-thread-safe dependencies (like DbContext) in it. Q What does the volatile keyword do? It gives reads acquire semantics and writes release semantics, preventing certain compiler/JIT/CPU reorderings and register caching, so other threads see updates. It doesn't make compound operations atomic. In practice, prefer locks, Interlocked, Volatile.Read/Write or CancellationToken for signalling. Q When would you use Parallel.ForEachAsync vs Task.WhenAll? Parallel.ForEachAsync for processing a (possibly large) collection with a bounded degree of parallelism and cancellation. Task.WhenAll to await a known, small set of already-started independent operations. Unbounded WhenAll over thousands of items can overwhelm downstream services. Q What are Channels used for? In-process asynchronous producer/consumer queues (System.Threading.Channels), with bounded capacity for backpressure, completion signalling, and async reading "},{"c":"threading-concurrency","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Managed threading best practices Overview of synchronization primitives The managed thread pool Data and task parallelism (TPL) Joseph Albahari, Threading in C# (free online). Stephen Cleary, Concurrency in C# Cookbook (2nd ed.)."},{"c":"threading-concurrency","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run (a plain console app, .NET 8/9; System.Collections.Concurrent and System.Threading.Channels are part of the BCL, no extra packages). It reproduces the race condition, then shows Interlocked , lock and ConcurrentDictionary all fixing it exactly; verifies SemaphoreSlim(n) genuinely caps concurrency at n ; verifies a bounded Channel<T> applies real backpressure while still delivering every item; and verifies consistent lock ordering avoids a deadlock that a real two-lock deadlock would otherwise hang on. Deliberately reproducing a volatile -missing infinite loop or an actual deadlock on demand is timing/JIT-dependent and not something it's safe to force in a short script, so those are described rather than triggered — see Further reading. C# Run ▶ Copy // Verify this chapter: run with `dotnet run`. No external packages needed // (System.Threading, System.Collections.Concurrent and System.Threading.Channels // are all part of the BCL). using System ; using System . Collections . Concurrent ; using System . Diagnostics ; using System . Linq ; using System . Threading ; using System . Threading . Channels ; using System . Threading . Tasks ; void Check ( string name, bool ok) { if (!ok) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: {name}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: {name}\" ); } const int threads = 8 ; const int perThread = 200_000 ; const int expected = threads * perThread; // 1. Race condition: plain `_count++` across many threads loses updates, // because read-increment-write is three separate steps, not one. int unsafeCount = 0 ; void BumpUnsafe () { for ( int i = 0 ; i < perThread; i++) unsafeCount++; } Parallel . Invoke ( Enumerable . Repeat ( 0 , threads). Select (_ => ( Action ) BumpUnsafe ). ToArray ()); Console . WriteLine (unsafeCount < expected ? $\"OK: unsynchronized increments lost updates under contention ({unsafeCount} of {expected} survived)\" : $\"OK: unsynchronized increments happened not to lose any this run ({unsafeCount} of {expected}) -- races are timing-dependent, not guaranteed on every run/machine\" ); // 2. Interlocked.Increment under the exact same contention never loses one: // it's a single atomic CPU instruction, not three separate steps. long safeCount = 0 ; void BumpSafe () { for ( int i = 0 ; i < perThread; i++) Interlocked . Increment ( ref safeCount); } Parallel . Invoke ( Enumerable . Repeat ( 0 , threads). Select (_ => ( Action ) BumpSafe ). ToArray ()); Check ( $\"Interlocked.Increment under the same contention is always exact ({safeCount} == {expected})\" , safeCount == expected); // 3. lock (Monitor) protects a compound operation (read a dict, write it back) // the same way, for state Interlocked can't cover (more than one variable). var gate = new Lock (); var prices = new System . Collections . Generic . Dictionary < string , int >(); void BumpPrice ( string sku) { for ( int i = 0 ; i < 10_000 ; i++) lock (gate) { prices[sku] = prices. GetValueOrDefault (sku) + 1 ; } } Parallel . Invoke ( Enumerable . Repeat ( 0 , threads). Select (_ => ( Action )(() => BumpPrice ( \"KB-01\" ))). ToArray ()); Check ( $\"a lock-protected read-modify-write is exact under contention ({prices[\" KB - 01 \"]} == {threads * 10_000})\" , prices[ \"KB-01\" ] == threads * 10_000 ); // 4. ConcurrentDictionary.GetOrAdd / AddOrUpdate are atomic: many threads // racing to initialize the SAME key still end up with one consistent value. var concurrentCounts = new ConcurrentDictionary < string , int >(); Parallel . For ( 0 , 50_000 , _ => concurrentCounts. AddOrUpdate ( \"hits\" , 1 , (_, old) => old + 1 )); Check ( $\"ConcurrentDictionary.AddOrUpdate is exact under contention ({concurrentCounts[\" hits \"]} == 50000)\" , concurrentCounts[ \"hits\" ] == 50_000 ); // 5. SemaphoreSlim(n) as a concurrency LIMITER: never more than n callers // inside the guarded section at once, verified by tracking the peak. var throttle = new SemaphoreSlim ( 3 ); int current = 0 , peak = 0 ; var peakGate = new Lock (); async Task Thr"},{"c":"performance-engineering","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Measure before you optimise. Find the real bottleneck with traces/profilers first; guessing wastes effort on code that was never slow. Use percentiles (p95, p99), not averages — averages hide the slow requests that real users actually feel. Most backend slowness is the database (missing indexes, N+1), not CPU-bound C# code. BenchmarkDotNet , not a raw Stopwatch loop, for trusting micro-benchmark numbers — a bare loop is skewed by JIT warm-up, GC pauses and dead-code elimination. dotnet-counters / dotnet-trace / dotnet-dump are the day-to-day diagnostic toolbox; load testing (k6, NBomber) checks the whole system under concurrency, not just one method. Fix things in order of impact: indexes/N+1 first, then async/concurrency, then payload size, then micro-level allocations — in that order, because each later category's wins are usually dwarfed by the earlier ones."},{"c":"performance-engineering","s":"the-method-measure-dont-guess","h":"The method: measure, don't guess","t":"# The method: measure, don't guess Intermediate Every other technique in this chapter — profilers, benchmarks, the optimisation catalogue — only pays off once you've committed to one discipline: find out where time is actually going before changing anything. The alternative, guessing based on intuition about what \"feels slow,\" reliably sends people optimising the wrong 95% of the code while the real bottleneck sits untouched. Diagram Copy 1. Define the goal \"p95 of GET /products < 150 ms at 500 RPS\", not \"make it faster\" 2. Measure the baseline in a production-like environment, with production-like data 3. Find the bottleneck traces → profiler → the ONE thing dominating the time 4. Change one thing the cheapest fix for that bottleneck 5. Measure again did the target metric improve? any regressions elsewhere? 6. Repeat or stop stop when the goal is met. Further optimisation has a cost (complexity) \"Premature optimization is the root of all evil\" (Knuth). The full quote continues: \"…yet we should not pass up our opportunities in that critical 3%.\" Find the 3% with measurements."},{"c":"performance-engineering","s":"latency-throughput-and-percentiles","h":"Latency, throughput and percentiles","t":"# Latency, throughput and percentiles Intermediate Metric Meaning Latency Time for one operation (request duration) Throughput Operations per unit of time (requests/s) Percentiles p50 (median), p95 , p99 , p99.9: the latency below which that % of requests fall Utilisation / saturation How busy a resource is / how much work is queued for it Use percentiles, not averages. Here's a concrete illustration of why: out of 100 requests, if 98 take 50 ms and 2 take 5,000 ms, the average is about 148 ms — already looks bad, but still hides what's going on — while the p99 is 5,000 ms, the number that actually describes the experience of the unluckiest 2% of users. (Source: this is just latency[⌈p·N⌉ − 1] on the sorted array — verified below.) At real traffic volumes, that 2% is thousands of people, and a user loading a page that fires ten API calls hits that unlucky tail on nearly every page load, not just rarely. Little's Law links the three core metrics precisely: concurrency = throughput × latency (so 500 RPS × 0.2 s = 100 concurrent requests in flight) — a formula worth keeping handy when sizing a thread pool or a connection pool, since it tells you exactly how many requests you need to support simultaneously , not just per second."},{"c":"performance-engineering","s":"where-time-goes-in-a-net-backend","h":"Where time goes in a .NET backend","t":"# Where time goes in a .NET backend Intermediate Typical request time, in order of how often it's actually the culprit — notice this ordering is itself a product of experience across many real investigations, and it's worth checking top-down rather than jumping straight to whichever cause feels most interesting: Database : missing indexes, N+1 queries, over-fetching, lock contention, chatty round trips ( EF Core querying ). Remote calls : sequential calls that could be concurrent, no caching, no timeouts, slow dependencies. Thread pool starvation : sync-over-async, blocking calls ( async/await ). Serialization : huge payloads, reflection-based serializers, returning entire entity graphs. Allocations and GC : per-request large buffers, LINQ-heavy hot paths, boxing, string concatenation ( Memory & GC ). Lock contention : a global lock in a singleton serialising all requests. Logging : synchronous sinks, excessive logging, interpolated log strings. Pure CPU work in your code: rarely the problem, but measurable when it is. Traces ( Observability ) show which of these dominates for a slow request before you open a profiler — exactly the workflow the trace diagram in that chapter walked through, where one SQL span turned out to account for nearly the entire request's duration. Starting there, rather than guessing from this list, is what step 3 of the measurement loop above actually means in practice."},{"c":"performance-engineering","s":"micro-benchmarking-with-benchmarkdotnet","h":"Micro-benchmarking with BenchmarkDotNet","t":"# Micro-benchmarking with BenchmarkDotNet Advanced Timing code with Stopwatch in a loop gives misleading numbers. Four things throw it off: JIT warm-up : your first iterations run on slow, unoptimised Tier 0 code before the JIT recompiles the hot method into fast Tier 1 code (the two-tier curve from How code runs ). An unwarmed loop measures a mix of slow-then-fast, not the steady state you actually care about. GC pauses landing at unpredictable moments. Dead-code elimination : the optimiser may notice nobody uses the result and skip the work entirely, timing nothing. CPU frequency scaling changing clock speed mid-run. BenchmarkDotNet handles all four: it runs a separate process in Release mode, does warm-up and pilot runs, takes many iterations, and applies statistics — so the number you get back reflects your code, not noise. C# Run ▶ Copy // dotnet new console; dotnet add package BenchmarkDotNet; run with: dotnet run -c Release [MemoryDiagnoser] // allocations per operation + GC counts [SimpleJob(RuntimeMoniker.Net80)] [SimpleJob(RuntimeMoniker.Net10_0, baseline: true)] // compare runtimes public class SkuParsing { private const string Line = \"2026-10-27;KB-01;3;49.90\" ; [Benchmark(Baseline = true)] public decimal Split () => decimal . Parse ( Line . Split ( ';' )[ 3 ], CultureInfo . InvariantCulture ); [Benchmark] public decimal Span () { ReadOnlySpan < char > s = Line ; return decimal . Parse (s[(s. LastIndexOf ( ';' ) + 1 )..], CultureInfo . InvariantCulture ); } [Params(10, 1_000)] public int N ; private int [] _data = []; [GlobalSetup] public void Setup () => _data = Enumerable . Range ( 0 , N ). ToArray (); [Benchmark] public int LinqSum () => _data. Where (x => x % 2 == 0 ). Sum (); [Benchmark] public int LoopSum () { int s = 0 ; foreach ( var x in _data) if (x % 2 == 0 ) s += x; return s; } } BenchmarkRunner . Run < SkuParsing >(); Diagram Copy | Method | Mean | Ratio | Allocated | |------- |----------:|------:|----------:| | Split | 112.40 ns | 1.00 | 216 B | | Span | 38.15 ns | 0.34 | - | Look at both columns of that result, not just Mean : Span is roughly three times faster and allocates zero bytes, where Split allocates 216 B per call purely from the intermediate array Split(';') creates. Multiply that 216 B by a hot path called a million times a minute, and it's 216 MB of garbage the GC now has to collect — exactly the allocation-pressure mechanism from Memory & GC turning a \"tiny\" per-call cost into real, measurable GC time under load. Rules: always Release , never with a debugger attached; return results (or use Consumer ) so the JIT can't eliminate the work; benchmark realistic inputs; look at Allocated , not just time; remember a 70 ns win matters only if the code runs millions of times — on a code path called once per request at 50 RPS, 70 ns is immeasurable against a 150 ms database call, and chasing it would be exactly the \"premature optimisation\" Knuth warned about. Micro-benchmarks validate a hypothesis about a hot path that profiling already identified ; they're a verification step, not where you start."},{"c":"performance-engineering","s":"the-diagnostics-toolbox","h":"The diagnostics toolbox","t":"# The diagnostics toolbox Advanced Tool What it does When dotnet-counters Live runtime and ASP.NET Core metrics: CPU, GC heap/gen counts, % time in GC, allocation rate, thread pool queue length & thread count , exceptions/s, requests/s, connection counts First look at a running process; starvation and GC pressure dotnet-trace Collects an EventPipe trace (CPU samples, GC, allocations, contention, exceptions, custom events) CPU hot paths, allocation sources, lock contention dotnet-dump Full process dump + SOS analysis ( clrstack , dumpheap , gcroot , syncblk ) Hangs, deadlocks, memory leaks, crashes dotnet-gcdump Lightweight heap snapshot Memory growth comparison dotnet-monitor A sidecar/agent exposing those diagnostics over HTTP with triggers (\"collect a trace when CPU > 80% for 1 min\") Production containers / Kubernetes Visual Studio Profiler / JetBrains dotTrace & dotMemory CPU, allocation, async and database profiling with rich UIs Development and staging deep dives PerfView Microsoft's free, powerful ETW/EventPipe analyser GC and CPU analysis, flame graphs Speedscope / flame graphs Visualise traces ( dotnet-trace convert --format Speedscope ) Seeing where CPU time goes Application Insights Profiler , continuous profilers (Pyroscope, Datadog) Sampled production profiling Real-traffic hot paths SQL tools Execution plans, Query Store, SET STATISTICS IO , Azure SQL Insights Database time Shell Copy dotnet-counters monitor -n Shop.Api --counters System.Runtime,Microsoft.AspNetCore.Hosting,System.Net.Http dotnet-trace collect -n Shop.Api --profile cpu-sampling --duration 00 : 00 : 30 -o cpu.nettrace dotnet-trace convert cpu.nettrace --format Speedscope # open at speedscope.app dotnet-dump collect -n Shop.Api && dotnet-dump analyze <file> # > clrstack -all (look for threads blocked in Wait/GetResult) # Reading the symptoms This table is really a decision tree disguised as a table: each row's symptom narrows down which tool from above you reach for next, and why each symptom points where it does is worth tracing through once: Symptom Likely cause Confirm with High latency, low CPU , rising thread count Thread-pool starvation (sync-over-async) or waiting on I/O/locks counters (ThreadPool Queue Length), dump clrstack -all High CPU, latency rises with load Hot code path, excessive GC, serialization, regex backtracking dotnet-trace CPU sampling → flame graph High % time in GC, saw-tooth memory Allocation rate too high, LOH churn counters (alloc rate, gen2 count), trace allocations Memory grows forever Leak gcdump diff, gcroot ( Memory & GC ) Slow only under concurrency Lock contention, connection pool exhaustion, DB locking trace contention events, pool counters, DB waits A specific endpoint is slow Its SQL, its remote calls Distributed trace spans The first row is the one people most often get wrong: high latency with low CPU sounds like \"the machine isn't working hard, so why is it slow?\" But low CPU is exactly what thread-pool starvation looks like. The CPU really isn't busy — your requests are just sitting in a queue waiting for a free worker thread, because something upstream is blocking synchronously on async work and holding threads that should have gone back to the pool (the same mechanism as Async and await's deadlock section ). So \"the server isn't even busy\" is a clue pointing at a thread-pool problem, not evidence against one."},{"c":"performance-engineering","s":"load-testing","h":"Load testing","t":"# Load testing Advanced Micro-benchmarks test methods; load tests test the system under realistic concurrency — a different question entirely, since a method that benchmarks fast in isolation can still fall over once a hundred requests are all contending for the same connection pool at once. JavaScript Copy // k6 script (JavaScript): ramp to 500 virtual users, assert SLOs import http from 'k6/http' ; import { check, sleep } from 'k6' ; export const options = { stages : [{ duration : '2m' , target : 100 }, { duration : '5m' , target : 500 }, { duration : '2m' , target : 0 }], thresholds : { http_req_duration : [ 'p(95)<150' , 'p(99)<400' ], http_req_failed : [ 'rate<0.01' ] }, }; export default function () { const res = http.get( 'https://staging.shop.com/api/products?page=1' ); check(res, { 'status 200' : r => r.status === 200 }); sleep( 1 ); } Test type Purpose Load Expected peak traffic: do we meet the SLOs? Stress Beyond peak: where and how does it break? (Gracefully, with 429/503, or by falling over?) Spike Sudden bursts: does autoscaling react in time? Soak / endurance Hours at steady load: memory leaks, connection leaks, degradation Capacity How many instances for N RPS → cost planning The Stress row's parenthetical — \"gracefully, with 429/503, or by falling over\" — is the whole point of a stress test. A system that starts returning 503s past its limit, while still serving the requests it can handle, is behaving correctly. A system that falls over entirely past that same limit is a future incident, waiting for the next traffic spike. Tools: k6 (Grafana), NBomber (C#), Azure Load Testing (managed, runs JMeter or Locust scripts, integrates with CI and App Insights), JMeter, Locust, bombardier / wrk for quick HTTP blasts. Test against production-like environments and data volumes, watch server-side metrics during the test, and watch out for the load generator itself becoming the bottleneck: a test that's really measuring how fast the test machine can send requests, rather than how fast your API answers them, is an easy trap to fall into."},{"c":"performance-engineering","s":"high-impact-optimisations-by-layer","h":"High-impact optimisations, by layer","t":"# High-impact optimisations, by layer Advanced # Data access (usually the biggest wins) Add or fix indexes for the actual query patterns; check plans ( Relational fundamentals ). Eliminate N+1 ; project to DTOs; AsNoTracking ; split queries; pagination with sensible limits. Batch round trips ( WHERE Id IN … , ExecuteUpdate , bulk copy); avoid chatty loops. Cache hot, rarely changing reads (HybridCache, output cache, CDN) ( Caching ). Read replicas for read-heavy workloads; connection pooling and DbContext pooling. This category tops the list because the gaps here are so much bigger than anywhere else: a missing index can turn a 2 ms query into a 2-second table scan, a thousand-fold difference no amount of C# optimisation elsewhere in the request can claw back. Fix the database layer first; everything else is comparatively marginal gains. # Concurrency and I/O Async all the way ; remove every .Result / .Wait() . Run independent remote calls concurrently ( Task.WhenAll ), with timeouts. Reuse connections: IHttpClientFactory , singleton SDK clients, HTTP/2 or HTTP/3. Move slow work off the request path (queues + workers; 202 Accepted). Remove global locks from hot singletons; use concurrent or immutable structures. # Payloads and serialization Smaller responses: projections, paging, field selection, compression (Brotli/gzip), JsonIgnoreCondition.WhenWritingNull . System.Text.Json source generation for hot types; cache JsonSerializerOptions (never create one per call). Stream large results ( IAsyncEnumerable , Stream ) instead of buffering. gRPC/Protobuf for internal high-volume calls. # Allocations and CPU Pool large buffers ( ArrayPool ), use Span / Memory for parsing, avoid boxing and per-call closures in hot paths, pre-size collections, StringBuilder , FrozenDictionary for static lookups, [GeneratedRegex] instead of new Regex instances, [LoggerMessage] for hot logging ( Advanced C# ). Seal classes, avoid unnecessary virtual/interface dispatch in tight loops (the JIT devirtualises sealed types), and let Dynamic PGO work (it's on by default). Use Vector<T> /SIMD-accelerated BCL APIs ( span.IndexOfAny , SequenceEqual , Sum on spans). # Platform Upgrade .NET : each release brings significant free performance improvements (Stephen Toub's yearly posts document hundreds). Server GC with DATAS; correct container CPU/memory requests ( Kubernetes ). Scale out horizontally; autoscale on the right signal (queue length, RPS, CPU). CDN and edge caching for static and cacheable content."},{"c":"performance-engineering","s":"startup-performance","h":"Startup performance","t":"# Startup performance Advanced Startup matters for serverless cold starts , scale-out speed , and container rolling deployments — anywhere a brand-new process has to become ready to serve traffic as fast as possible, rather than a long-running process where startup cost is amortised over hours of uptime. Technique Effect ReadyToRun ( <PublishReadyToRun> ) Precompiled code: less JIT at startup Native AOT ( <PublishAot> ) No JIT at all; ~10× faster startup, much lower memory; requires AOT-compatible code ( How code runs ) WebApplication.CreateSlimBuilder Fewer default services Trimming Smaller deployments EF Core compiled models Faster first query for big models Avoid heavy work in startup Defer cache warm-ups to a background service; lazy initialisation Source generators (JSON, config binding, RDG) No reflection at startup Warm-up before taking traffic Startup/readiness probes, slot warm-up ( applicationInitialization ) Native AOT's speedup isn't vague \"it's more optimised\" — it's the literal removal of a pipeline stage. With no JIT at all, there's no Tier 0 → Tier 1 warm-up period to pay for (the same curve from How code runs ), which is exactly where the roughly tenfold startup improvement comes from."},{"c":"performance-engineering","s":"preventing-regressions","h":"Preventing regressions","t":"# Preventing regressions Advanced Performance budgets and SLOs (p95 targets per critical endpoint) tracked on dashboards, with alerts. Benchmarks in CI for performance-critical libraries (BenchmarkDotNet with result comparison against the main branch). Load tests in the pipeline (Azure Load Testing with pass/fail criteria) before production for high-traffic services. Canary deployments with automated latency and error comparisons ( CI/CD ). Code review checklists: async all the way, no N+1, projections, timeouts, pagination limits, no per-request large allocations. The common thread: left unguarded, performance degrades gradually and invisibly. One small regression per PR, each too small to notice on its own, compounds over months into a system that's quietly twice as slow — with no single commit anyone could point to as \"the cause.\" Each of these five practices exists to catch that drift automatically, before it compounds. Q How would you approach a performance problem in a .NET API? Define a measurable goal (p95 latency at a given load). Measure a baseline in a production-like environment. Use distributed traces to see which component dominates (DB, remote calls, app code). Drill in with the right tool (execution plans for SQL, dotnet-counters/trace or a profiler for CPU/GC/thread-pool issues). Fix the single biggest bottleneck, re-measure, and repeat until the goal is met. Avoid optimising without evidence. Q Why use percentiles instead of averages for latency? Averages hide the tail: a few very slow requests barely move the mean but hurt real users, and users making many requests encounter the tail frequently. p95/p99 show what most users and the worst-affected users experience, and SLOs are usually defined on them. Q What is BenchmarkDotNet and why not just use a Stopwatch? A micro-benchmarking library that runs code in an isolated Release process with warm-up, many iterations and statistical analysis, and reports allocations with MemoryDiagnoser. A Stopwatch loop is skewed by JIT tiering, warm-up, GC, dead-code elimination and noise, so it produces unreliable numbers. Q High latency but low CPU: what do you suspect and how do you confirm it? Thread-pool starvation from blocking calls (sync-over-async, sync I/O), or waiting on slow dependencies, locks or connection pools. Confirm with dotnet-counters (growing thread pool queue length and thread count), a dump showing threads blocked in Wait/GetResult, and traces showing long dependency spans. Q What tools would you use to diagnose a production performance issue in a container? Distributed traces and metrics (App Insights / OpenTelemetry) first, then dotnet-counters for runtime health, dotnet-trace for CPU/allocation/contention profiles, dotnet-dump for hangs and leaks, typically via dotnet-monitor as a sidecar or kubectl debug with an ephemeral container, plus database execution plans and Query Store. Q Name high-impact optimisations for a typical CRUD API. Fix indexes and N+1 queries, project to DTOs with AsNoTracking, paginate, cache hot reads (HybridCache, output caching), make everything async and parallelise independent remote calls, reuse HttpClient connections, compress and trim responses with System.Text.Json source generation, move slow work to background queues, and upgrade to the latest .NET. Q How do you improve cold-start time for a .NET service? Native AOT (or ReadyToRun), the slim builder, trimming, source generators instead of reflection, EF compiled models, deferring heavy initialisation out of startup, pre-warming instances (Premium/Flex always-ready, readiness probes, slot warm-up), and smaller container images."},{"c":"performance-engineering","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading .NET diagnostic tools overview ASP.NET Core performance best practices BenchmarkDotNet Stephen Toub, Performance Improvements in .NET 8 / 9 / 10 (.NET Blog): enormous, invaluable. Azure Load Testing · k6 documentation Book: Writing High-Performance .NET Code (2nd ed.) by Ben Watson."},{"c":"performance-engineering","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run -c Release (plain console app, .NET 8/9, no extra packages — GC.GetAllocatedBytesForCurrentThread and Stopwatch -free comparisons are enough to see the allocation difference this chapter talks about). C# Run ▶ Copy using System . Globalization ; // 1) Percentiles vs averages: 98 fast requests + 2 slow ones. var latenciesMs = new List < double >(); for ( int i = 0 ; i < 98 ; i++) latenciesMs. Add ( 50 ); latenciesMs. Add ( 5000 ); latenciesMs. Add ( 5000 ); latenciesMs. Sort (); double average = latenciesMs. Average (); double p99 = latenciesMs[( int ) Math . Ceiling ( 0.99 * latenciesMs. Count ) - 1 ]; if (!(average is > 140 and < 160 )) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected average ~148ms, got {average}\" ); if (p99 != 5000 ) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected p99 = 5000ms, got {p99}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: average={average:F1}ms looks tolerable, but p99={p99}ms reveals the real tail latency averages hide.\" ); // 2) Little's Law: concurrency = throughput (req/s) x latency (s) double throughputRps = 500 ; double latencySeconds = 0.2 ; double concurrency = throughputRps * latencySeconds; if (concurrency != 100 ) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected concurrency=100, got {concurrency}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: Little's Law — {throughputRps} RPS x {latencySeconds}s latency = {concurrency} concurrent requests in flight.\" ); // 3) Span-based slicing avoids the allocation that string.Split causes. const string line = \"2026-10-27;KB-01;3;49.90\" ; long before = GC . GetAllocatedBytesForCurrentThread (); decimal viaSplit = decimal . Parse (line. Split ( ';' )[ 3 ], CultureInfo . InvariantCulture ); long splitAllocated = GC . GetAllocatedBytesForCurrentThread () - before; before = GC . GetAllocatedBytesForCurrentThread (); ReadOnlySpan < char > s = line; decimal viaSpan = decimal . Parse (s[(s. LastIndexOf ( ';' ) + 1 )..], CultureInfo . InvariantCulture ); long spanAllocated = GC . GetAllocatedBytesForCurrentThread () - before; if (viaSplit != viaSpan) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: Split and Span parsing disagree on the value\" ); if (!(splitAllocated > spanAllocated)) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected Split ({splitAllocated}B) to allocate more than Span ({spanAllocated}B)\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: string.Split allocated {splitAllocated}B (the intermediate array), Span-based slicing allocated {spanAllocated}B — confirms the chapter's allocation claim, though exact byte counts vary by runtime/JIT.\" ); // Claims NOT verified here (they need an external profiler/benchmark harness, not a plain console check): // - BenchmarkDotNet's own warm-up/statistics methodology and exact ns timings — see Further reading: https://benchmarkdotnet.org/ // - dotnet-counters/dotnet-trace/dotnet-dump behavior on a live process — see Further reading: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/diagnostics/ // - Native AOT's ~10x startup improvement and k6/load-testing throughput numbers — environment-dependent; see the Further reading links above. Console . WriteLine ( \"All runtime-checkable claims verified.\" );"},{"c":"web-security","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Security is the same principles this book has already used (least privilege, secure-by-default, never trust input) now organised around specific attacks, not a separate discipline bolted on at the end. Broken access control (IDOR/BOLA) is the #1 real-world risk: checking \"is this user logged in\" is not the same as checking \"may this user see this specific record.\" Always use parameterized queries, never string-concatenated SQL — the value should never become part of the command text at all. Store passwords with a slow, salted, adaptive hash (PBKDF2, Argon2id, bcrypt), never a fast hash like SHA-256 or plain text. XSS steals/runs script in someone else's browser; CSRF abuses cookies being sent automatically — they need different, sometimes opposite, defences (HttpOnly cookies help XSS, SameSite/antiforgery tokens help CSRF). Treat every external input — request bodies, file uploads, outbound URLs you fetch on a user's behalf (SSRF) — as hostile until validated, and keep dependencies patched; most real breaches exploit a known, unfixed issue, not a novel attack."},{"c":"web-security","s":"security-principles","h":"Security principles","t":"# Security principles Beginner Principle Meaning Example Defence in depth Multiple independent layers; one failure isn't fatal WAF + authn + authz + parameterised SQL + least-privilege DB user + encryption at rest Least privilege Every identity gets only the access it needs The app's managed identity has db_datareader/db_datawriter , not db_owner ; data-plane roles scoped to one container Secure by default The safe option is the default Fallback policy requiring authentication; HTTPS only; deny-by-default CORS Fail securely Errors don't open doors or leak details Generic ProblemDetails, no stack traces; authorization errors deny Never trust input Validate, encode and parameterise everything from outside Request bodies, headers, query strings, files, messages, and data from other services Minimise attack surface Fewer endpoints, ports, packages and privileges Remove unused endpoints, disable Swagger UI in prod, chiseled images Keep secrets out of code Use identities and vaults Managed identity, Key Vault, User Secrets for dev Assume breach Detect, limit blast radius, recover Logging and alerts, segmentation, short token lifetimes, backups Notice how many of these principles have already shown up by name earlier in this book — least privilege drove every RBAC example in Identity & secrets , secure-by-default was the entire justification for Auth 's fallback-policy advice. Security here isn't a separate discipline bolted on at the end; it's the same instincts this book has been building all along, now organised explicitly around attacks. Threat modelling (e.g. STRIDE : Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information disclosure, Denial of service, Elevation of privilege) at design time finds problems before they're built."},{"c":"web-security","s":"owasp-top-10-mapped-to-net","h":"OWASP Top 10 mapped to .NET","t":"# OWASP Top 10 mapped to .NET Intermediate The OWASP Top 10 (web apps; 2021 edition, with a 2025 update) and OWASP API Security Top 10 (2023) list the most critical risks. Risk What it is .NET defences Broken access control (#1 web; API1 BOLA, API5 BFLA) Users access data or functions they shouldn't: IDOR, missing checks on admin endpoints, CORS misconfig Authorization on every endpoint (fallback policy), resource-based authorization , scope queries by tenant/user, deny by default Cryptographic failures Weak or missing encryption, plain-text secrets, weak hashing TLS everywhere, HSTS, TDE / encryption at rest, PasswordHasher /PBKDF2/Argon2, Data Protection API, Key Vault Injection SQL, NoSQL, OS command, LDAP, log injection Parameterised queries (EF Core LINQ, Dapper parameters, FromSql interpolation); never build commands from input Insecure design Missing security controls by design Threat modelling, abuse cases, rate limits, secure defaults Security misconfiguration Debug pages in prod, default credentials, verbose errors, open storage Environment-specific config, UseExceptionHandler , Azure Policy, IaC reviews, disabled public access Vulnerable and outdated components Known-CVE packages and base images NuGet audit, dotnet list package --vulnerable , Dependabot, image scanning, regular .NET patching Identification and authentication failures Weak passwords, credential stuffing, broken session handling External IdP (Entra ID), MFA, lockout, short-lived tokens, secure cookies Software and data integrity failures Untrusted deserialization, unsigned updates, compromised CI No BinaryFormatter (removed in .NET 9), signed packages and images, pinned actions, protected pipelines Security logging and monitoring failures Attacks go unnoticed Log auth failures and access denials, alert on anomalies, Defender for Cloud SSRF (Server-Side Request Forgery) The server fetches attacker-controlled URLs (cloud metadata, internal services) Allow-list destinations, block private/metadata IP ranges, no raw user URLs to HttpClient API3: Broken object property level authorization / mass assignment Clients read or write properties they shouldn't Request/response DTOs , never bind entities, explicit mapping API4: Unrestricted resource consumption No limits on size, rate or cost Rate limiting, request size limits, pagination caps, timeouts, upload limits The rest of this chapter walks through the table's top few rows in detail — they're ranked by real-world frequency for a reason, and that ranking is also the order worth spending review time in."},{"c":"web-security","s":"broken-access-control-and-idor","h":"Broken access control and IDOR","t":"# Broken access control and IDOR Beginner The #1 risk in practice. An IDOR (Insecure Direct Object Reference, called BOLA in the API Top 10) is when an endpoint trusts an ID from the client without checking that the caller is actually allowed to see that specific resource: C# Run ▶ Copy // ✘ Any authenticated user can read ANY order by changing the id app. MapGet ( \"/orders/{id:int}\" , async ( int id, ShopDb db) => await db. Orders . FindAsync (id)). RequireAuthorization (); // ✔ Scope the query to the caller (and return 404, not 403, so you don't reveal what exists) app. MapGet ( \"/orders/{id:int}\" , async ( int id, ClaimsPrincipal user, ShopDb db, CancellationToken ct) => { var customerId = user. GetCustomerId (); var order = await db. Orders . AsNoTracking () . Where (o => o. Id == id && o. CustomerId == customerId) . Select (o => o. ToDto ()). FirstOrDefaultAsync (ct); return order is null ? Results . NotFound () : Results . Ok (order); }). RequireAuthorization (); Notice the first version isn't missing authorization entirely — RequireAuthorization() is right there. What it's missing is authorization scoped to this specific resource , which is exactly the resource-based authorization distinction Auth drew out: \"is logged in\" and \"may see order 1042\" are different questions, and the broken version only ever asked the first. Also: function-level checks ( /admin/* must require an admin policy, not just \"authenticated\"); multi-tenant isolation (global query filters by TenantId plus database row-level security as defence in depth; see EF Core advanced ); don't rely on hidden UI or unguessable IDs (GUIDs aren't access control); and test authorization explicitly in integration tests ( Integration testing )."},{"c":"web-security","s":"injection","h":"Injection","t":"# Injection Beginner C# Run ▶ Copy // ✘ SQL injection: input = \"x' OR '1'='1\" or \"'; DROP TABLE Orders; --\" var sql = \"SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Email = '\" + email + \"'\" ; db. Users . FromSqlRaw (sql); // ✔ Parameterised (all of these send values as parameters) db. Users . Where (u => u. Email == email); // LINQ db. Users . FromSql ( $\"SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Email = {email}\" ); // FormattableString → parameter conn. QueryAsync < User >( \"SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Email = @email\" , new { email }); // Dapper cmd. Parameters . Add ( \"@email\" , SqlDbType . NVarChar , 256 ). Value = email; // ADO.NET Every one of the four safe lines above was already covered in detail when it was first introduced — ADO.NET & Dapper made the same point about parameterisation, and this chapter is really collecting that advice under its actual name: injection defence. Dynamic identifiers (sort column, table name) can't be parameters : allow-list them ( sort switch { \"price\" => …, _ => … } ), exactly the whitelisting approach API design used for sortable fields. Other injection forms: OS command injection ( Process.Start with user input: avoid it, or pass arguments with ArgumentList , never through a shell), LDAP/XPath , NoSQL (build queries with SDK APIs, not string concatenation), log injection/forging (structured logging encodes values; never log raw multi-line user input as the message), and header injection (CRLF in redirects; ASP.NET Core validates headers)."},{"c":"web-security","s":"xss-csrf-and-clickjacking","h":"XSS, CSRF and clickjacking","t":"# XSS, CSRF and clickjacking Intermediate Cross-Site Scripting (XSS): attacker-supplied script runs in another user's browser. Razor encodes output by default ( @Model.Name ); Html.Raw and MarkupString bypass it, so avoid them with user data or sanitise with HtmlSanitizer. APIs returning JSON are safe if served with Content-Type: application/json . The risk moves to the SPA , which must not inject untrusted HTML ( innerHTML , dangerouslySetInnerHTML ). Content-Security-Policy header restricts script sources: a strong second layer. Keep tokens out of localStorage where XSS can steal them (prefer HttpOnly cookies with the BFF pattern) — precisely the XSS-vs-CSRF tradeoff table Auth laid out: a cookie's HttpOnly flag is specifically the mitigation for this exact theft. Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF): a malicious site makes the victim's browser send an authenticated request, because cookies are attached automatically . Affects cookie-authenticated apps (MVC/Razor Pages, BFFs), not APIs using bearer tokens in the Authorization header — the mirror image of the XSS point above, which is exactly why that same table framed cookies and bearer tokens as trading one risk for the other rather than one simply being safer. Defences: antiforgery tokens (automatic with Razor form tag helpers and [ValidateAntiForgeryToken] / UseAntiforgery ; minimal API form endpoints validate by default in .NET 8+), SameSite cookies ( Lax or Strict ), and checking Origin / Referer for state-changing requests. Clickjacking: framing your site invisibly. Use X-Frame-Options: DENY or CSP frame-ancestors 'none' ."},{"c":"web-security","s":"authentication-failures-and-password-storage","h":"Authentication failures and password storage","t":"# Authentication failures and password storage Intermediate Prefer an external identity provider (Entra ID, Entra External ID, Auth0, Okta) with MFA and Conditional Access , over building login yourself ( OAuth & OIDC ) — the exact \"don't roll your own token service\" warning Auth gave, extended to the whole login experience rather than just token issuance. If you store passwords: never plain text, reversible encryption, or fast hashes (MD5, SHA-1, SHA-256). Use a slow, salted, adaptive algorithm: PBKDF2 (ASP.NET Core Identity's PasswordHasher<T> : HMAC-SHA512 with 100,000 iterations since .NET 7), Argon2id , bcrypt or scrypt . Account lockout and rate limiting on login (credential stuffing); generic error messages (\"invalid email or password\"), with no user enumeration on login, registration or password reset. Check new passwords against breached-password lists (the Have I Been Pwned k-anonymity API); favour length over complexity rules (NIST SP 800-63B). Session/token hygiene: short-lived access tokens, rotating refresh tokens, revocation on password change (security stamp), secure cookie flags ( HttpOnly , Secure , SameSite ). C# Run ▶ Copy var hasher = new PasswordHasher < AppUser >(); string hash = hasher. HashPassword (user, password); // includes algorithm version, salt, iterations var result = hasher. VerifyHashedPassword (user, hash, attempt); // Success / SuccessRehashNeeded / Failed Notice why \"slow\" is listed as a requirement, not a drawback: a fast hash like SHA-256 lets an attacker who steals a password database try billions of guesses per second on commodity hardware. PBKDF2's deliberate 100,000 iterations make each individual guess expensive enough that brute-forcing even a weak password back from its hash becomes impractical at scale — the slowness is the entire point."},{"c":"web-security","s":"cryptography-and-the-data-protection-api","h":"Cryptography and the Data Protection API","t":"# Cryptography and the Data Protection API Advanced Rules: don't invent crypto ; use vetted libraries and current algorithms; manage keys properly (Key Vault); and pick the right primitive: Need Use Avoid Protect data your app will read back (tokens, cookies, temp data) ASP.NET Core Data Protection API ( IDataProtector ): authenticated encryption, key ring with rotation Rolling your own AES code Encrypt data at rest Platform encryption (TDE, Storage SSE), Always Encrypted, AesGcm with keys in Key Vault ECB mode, hard-coded keys Integrity / signatures HMAC-SHA256, RSA-PSS/ECDSA (Key Vault keys) Plain hashes as \"signatures\" Passwords PBKDF2 / Argon2 / bcrypt SHA-256 Random tokens, API keys RandomNumberGenerator.GetBytes(32) System.Random , Guid.NewGuid() for secrets Comparing secrets CryptographicOperations.FixedTimeEquals == (timing attacks) C# Run ▶ Copy public sealed class InviteTokens ( IDataProtectionProvider provider) { private readonly ITimeLimitedDataProtector _p = provider. CreateProtector ( \"Shop.Invites.v1\" ). ToTimeLimitedDataProtector (); public string Create ( Guid inviteId) => _p. Protect (inviteId. ToString (), TimeSpan . FromDays ( 7 )); public Guid ? Read ( string token) { try { return Guid . Parse (_p. Unprotect (token)); } catch ( CryptographicException ) { return null ; } } } // Multi-instance: persist keys to Blob and protect them with Key Vault (see Identity & secrets)"},{"c":"web-security","s":"transport-security-and-security-headers","h":"Transport security and security headers","t":"# Transport security and security headers Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy if (!app. Environment . IsDevelopment ()) app. UseHsts (); // Strict-Transport-Security: browsers refuse plain HTTP app. UseHttpsRedirection (); app. Use ( async (ctx, next) => { var h = ctx. Response . Headers ; h[ \"X-Content-Type-Options\" ] = \"nosniff\" ; h[ \"X-Frame-Options\" ] = \"DENY\" ; h[ \"Referrer-Policy\" ] = \"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\" ; h[ \"Content-Security-Policy\" ] = \"default-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'\" ; // tailor for HTML apps; APIs can be strict h[ \"Permissions-Policy\" ] = \"camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()\" ; await next(ctx); }); builder. WebHost . ConfigureKestrel (k => k. AddServerHeader = false ); // don't advertise \"Server: Kestrel\" TLS 1.2+ only (the platform usually enforces it), managed certificates with automatic renewal, TLS for service-to-service traffic too (or mTLS through a mesh), and Encrypt=True for SQL."},{"c":"web-security","s":"ssrf-and-outbound-calls","h":"SSRF and outbound calls","t":"# SSRF and outbound calls Advanced Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) : any feature where your server fetches a URL the user supplied (webhooks, \"import from URL\", image proxies, PDF renderers) can be tricked into fetching a URL it was never meant to reach. The attacker just needs to supply an internal address instead of a normal one — for example the cloud metadata endpoint ( 169.254.169.254 ), a special local address cloud VMs expose so code can ask \"who am I\" and get back managed-identity tokens. If an attacker can make your server request that address on their behalf, they get those tokens too — which is exactly the thing Identity & secrets relied on to avoid storing secrets in the first place. Defences: allow-list domains and schemes; resolve DNS and block private, link-local and loopback ranges (and re-check after redirects, or disable automatic redirects); run fetchers in an isolated network segment with egress restrictions; enforce timeouts and size limits; and never return raw fetched content or errors to the user."},{"c":"web-security","s":"mass-assignment-over-posting-and-data-exposure","h":"Mass assignment, over-posting and data exposure","t":"# Mass assignment, over-posting and data exposure Intermediate C# Run ▶ Copy // ✘ Binding the entity: a client can send {\"isAdmin\": true, \"balance\": 1000000} app. MapPut ( \"/users/{id}\" , async ( int id, User input, ShopDb db) => { db. Update (input); await db. SaveChangesAsync (); }); // ✔ Request DTO with only the allowed fields, mapped explicitly; response DTO with only the safe fields public sealed record UpdateProfileRequest ( string DisplayName , string ? Bio ); public sealed record ProfileResponse ( Guid Id , string DisplayName , string ? Bio ); // no PasswordHash, no Email of others, no internal flags This is exactly the over-posting trap from Building APIs , repeated here because it's common enough to earn a dedicated OWASP API category of its own. Also avoid excessive data exposure : returning full entities and relying on the front end to hide fields. The API response is what an attacker sees — a front end hiding a field in its UI does nothing to stop someone reading the raw network response."},{"c":"web-security","s":"resource-consumption-and-dos-protection","h":"Resource consumption and DoS protection","t":"# Resource consumption and DoS protection Intermediate Rate limiting per user/IP/API key, with stricter limits on expensive and auth endpoints ( Caching, rate limiting & CORS ), plus WAF/Front Door/APIM limits at the edge. Request limits: Kestrel MaxRequestBodySize , form/multipart limits, header size and timeouts, [RequestSizeLimit] . Pagination caps (max page size), query complexity limits (GraphQL depth and cost), timeouts on every outbound call and query. Regex DoS (ReDoS): use RegexOptions.NonBacktracking or timeouts for patterns over user input; [GeneratedRegex] with a match timeout. JSON limits: MaxDepth ; avoid deserializing huge payloads into memory."},{"c":"web-security","s":"secure-file-uploads","h":"Secure file uploads","t":"# Secure file uploads Advanced C# Run ▶ Copy app. MapPost ( \"/documents\" , async ( IFormFile file , IBlobStore store, CancellationToken ct) => { if ( file . Length is 0 or > 10 * 1024 * 1024 ) return Results . BadRequest ( \"File must be 1 byte–10 MB\" ); var ext = Path . GetExtension ( file . FileName ). ToLowerInvariant (); if (ext is not ( \".pdf\" or \".png\" or \".jpg\" )) return Results . BadRequest ( \"Unsupported type\" ); await using var s = file . OpenReadStream (); if (! await FileSignatures . MatchesAsync (s, ext, ct)) return Results . BadRequest ( \"Content doesn't match extension\" ); // magic bytes var safeName = $\"{Guid.NewGuid():N}{ext}\" ; // never use the client's file name as a path await store. UploadAsync ( $\"quarantine/{safeName}\" , s, ct); // scan (Defender for Storage malware scanning) before use return Results . Accepted (); }). RequireAuthorization (). DisableAntiforgery (); Notice the comment on FileSignatures.MatchesAsync : checking the file extension alone is trivially bypassed by renaming a malicious file, which is exactly why this checks the actual byte signature (\"magic bytes\") inside the file content too — a .png extension on a file that doesn't start with PNG's real magic bytes is a clear sign something's wrong, regardless of what the client claimed. Store uploads outside the web root (Blob Storage), never execute them, serve them with Content-Disposition: attachment from a separate domain if they're user-visible, and scan them for malware."},{"c":"web-security","s":"supply-chain-and-dependencies","h":"Supply chain and dependencies","t":"# Supply chain and dependencies Intermediate NuGet audit on restore (warnings on known CVEs, fail CI on high severity), dotnet list package --vulnerable --include-transitive , Dependabot/Renovate PRs. Package source mapping and private feeds (prevents dependency confusion), lock files, Central Package Management for consistent versions ( Projects & NuGet ). Container images: minimal or chiseled base images, regular rebuilds, scanning (Trivy, Defender for Containers), signing, SBOMs — the exact security practices Docker detailed. CI/CD: least-privilege workflow tokens, OIDC instead of stored secrets, pin third-party actions by SHA, protected branches and environments, secret scanning with push protection. Unsafe APIs: BinaryFormatter is removed (.NET 9); avoid TypeNameHandling.All in Newtonsoft.Json (deserialization gadget attacks); validate XML with DTD processing disabled (XXE; .NET's defaults are safe)."},{"c":"web-security","s":"logging-and-monitoring-securely","h":"Logging and monitoring securely","t":"# Logging and monitoring securely Intermediate Log security events (authentication failures, access denials, privilege changes, rate-limit hits, validation anomalies) with user and correlation IDs, and alert on spikes. Never log passwords, tokens, API keys, full card numbers or sensitive personal data; use redaction ( Microsoft.Extensions.Compliance.Redaction ) — exactly the \"what not to log\" table from Errors & logging , now framed as a security control rather than just a logging hygiene tip. Protect log stores with RBAC and retention policies. Use Microsoft Defender for Cloud for posture management and threat detection across App Service, SQL, Storage, Key Vault and containers. Q What is the most common web/API vulnerability and how do you prevent it in ASP.NET Core? Broken access control (IDOR/BOLA): endpoints that trust client-supplied IDs or lack function-level checks. Prevent it by requiring authorization by default (fallback policy), applying policies to every endpoint, using resource-based authorization or scoping every query by the current user or tenant, returning 404 for inaccessible resources, and testing authorization in integration tests. Q How do you prevent SQL injection in .NET? Never concatenate input into SQL. Use EF Core LINQ, FromSql/ExecuteSql with interpolated FormattableString (converted to parameters), Dapper parameter objects, or ADO.NET parameters. Allow-list dynamic identifiers like sort columns, and run with a least-privilege database identity. Q What is CSRF and when does it apply? Cross-Site Request Forgery tricks a logged-in user's browser into sending a state-changing request to your site with their cookies attached automatically. It applies to cookie-authenticated apps; bearer-token APIs aren't vulnerable because browsers don't attach Authorization headers automatically. Defend with antiforgery tokens, SameSite cookies and Origin checks. Q How should passwords be stored? Hashed with a slow, salted, adaptive algorithm (PBKDF2 as in ASP.NET Core Identity's PasswordHasher, Argon2id, bcrypt or scrypt), with a per-user salt and a high work factor, re-hashing when parameters improve. Never plain text, encrypted, or fast hashes like SHA-256. Better yet, delegate authentication to an identity provider. Q What is the ASP.NET Core Data Protection API? A built-in cryptographic API for protecting data your app needs to read back (auth cookies, antiforgery tokens, temp data, custom tokens) with authenticated encryption, purpose strings for isolation, and automatic key rotation. In multi-instance deployments, the key ring must be persisted to shared storage and protected (e.g. Blob Storage plus Key Vault). Q What is SSRF and how do you prevent it? Server-Side Request Forgery: an attacker makes your server send requests to URLs of their choosing, reaching internal services or cloud metadata endpoints. Prevent it with allow-lists of destinations, blocking private/link-local/metadata IP ranges after DNS resolution (and on redirects), network egress restrictions, timeouts and size limits. Q What is mass assignment / over-posting? When model binding sets properties the client shouldn't control (IsAdmin, Price) because the request is bound directly to an entity. Prevent it with dedicated request DTOs and explicit mapping, and use response DTOs to avoid excessive data exposure. Q Which security headers would you add to an ASP.NET Core app? Strict-Transport-Security (UseHsts), X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff, X-Frame-Options: DENY or CSP frame-ancestors, Content-Security-Policy, Referrer-Policy and Permissions-Policy, plus removing the Server header and enforcing HTTPS redirection. Q How do you keep dependencies secure? NuGet vulnerability auditing in restore and CI, automated update PRs (Dependabot/Renovate), Central Package Management with lock files and package source mapping, regular .NET runtime/SDK patching, minimal container base images that are scanned and rebuilt often, and avoiding dangerous serializers l"},{"c":"web-security","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading ASP.NET Core security overview OWASP Top 10 · OWASP API Security Top 10 · OWASP Cheat Sheet Series (start with the .NET Security cheat sheet) Prevent CSRF attacks in ASP.NET Core Data Protection in ASP.NET Core Microsoft Defender for Cloud"},{"c":"web-security","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run (a plain console app, .NET 8/9, only base-class-library crypto and regex APIs — no extra packages). It checks injection, password hashing, constant-time comparison, secure random tokens, file-signature validation and ReDoS mitigation directly. Things that need a live HTTP pipeline or browser to observe (CSRF cookie behavior, CSP/HSTS enforcement, SSRF against a real metadata endpoint) are left to the Further reading links instead of faked here. C# Run ▶ Copy // Verify this chapter: run with `dotnet run`. No external packages needed // (uses only System.Security.Cryptography and System.Text.RegularExpressions // from the base class library -- the same primitives the chapter names). using System ; using System . Security . Cryptography ; using System . Text ; using System . Text . RegularExpressions ; void Check ( string name, bool ok) { if (!ok) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: {name}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: {name}\" ); } // 1. Injection: string concatenation lets attacker-controlled input change the // shape of the query; a parameterized call never merges the value into the // command text at all, so it has nothing to \"escape out of\". string email = \"x' OR '1'='1\" ; string concatenatedSql = \"SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Email = '\" + email + \"'\" ; Check ( \"concatenation lets the payload's quote characters reach the SQL text (the injection)\" , concatenatedSql. Contains ( \"OR '1'='1\" )); var parameterizedCommandText = \"SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Email = @email\" ; var parameters = new System . Collections . Generic . Dictionary < string , object > { [ \"@email\" ] = email }; Check ( \"a parameterized command's TEXT never contains the value at all -- the payload has nowhere to break out to\" , !parameterizedCommandText. Contains ( \"OR\" ) && parameters[ \"@email\" ]. Equals (email)); // 2. Password storage: PBKDF2 (Rfc2898DeriveBytes) with different salts // produces different hashes for the SAME password, and verification means // re-deriving with the STORED salt and comparing, not re-hashing blindly. byte [] Pbkdf2 ( string password, byte [] salt, int iterations = 100_000 ) => Rfc2898DeriveBytes . Pbkdf2 ( Encoding . UTF8 . GetBytes (password), salt, iterations, HashAlgorithmName . SHA512 , 32 ); var salt1 = RandomNumberGenerator . GetBytes ( 16 ); var salt2 = RandomNumberGenerator . GetBytes ( 16 ); var hashA = Pbkdf2 ( \"Tr0ub4dor&3\" , salt1); var hashB = Pbkdf2 ( \"Tr0ub4dor&3\" , salt2); Check ( \"the same password hashed with two different salts produces two different hashes\" , !hashA. AsSpan (). SequenceEqual (hashB)); var verifyAttempt = Pbkdf2 ( \"Tr0ub4dor&3\" , salt1); Check ( \"re-deriving with the ORIGINAL salt reproduces the original hash (this is how verification works)\" , hashA. AsSpan (). SequenceEqual (verifyAttempt)); var wrongPassword = Pbkdf2 ( \"wrong-password\" , salt1); Check ( \"a wrong password with the same salt produces a different hash (login correctly rejected)\" , !hashA. AsSpan (). SequenceEqual (wrongPassword)); // 3. Comparing secrets: CryptographicOperations.FixedTimeEquals exists // specifically to avoid the timing side-channel a plain == comparison has. Check ( \"FixedTimeEquals returns true for identical byte sequences\" , CryptographicOperations . FixedTimeEquals (hashA, verifyAttempt)); Check ( \"FixedTimeEquals returns false for different byte sequences\" , ! CryptographicOperations . FixedTimeEquals (hashA, hashB)); // 4. Random tokens: RandomNumberGenerator.GetBytes is the recommended source // for secrets (unlike System.Random, which is predictable, not CSPRNG). var token1 = RandomNumberGenerator . GetBytes ( 32 ); var token2 = RandomNumberGenerator . GetBytes ( 32 ); Check ( \"two calls to RandomNumberGenerator.GetBytes(32) produce different tokens\" , !token1. AsSpan (). SequenceEqual (token2)); // 5. Secure file uploads: checking the file extension alone is not enough -- // the content's actual \"magic bytes\" must match. PNG files start with a // fixed 8-byte signatur"},{"c":"oauth-oidc-identity","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR OAuth 2.0 lets an app get a limited, revocable token to act on a user's behalf, without ever seeing their password. OpenID Connect (OIDC) adds a standard way to learn who that user is (the ID token) on top of OAuth. Use the authorization code flow with PKCE for anything with a user; it never puts tokens in the browser URL, and PKCE stops a stolen authorization code from being redeemed by someone who didn't start the login. The access token is for the API; the ID token is for the client to learn who signed in; the refresh token gets new access tokens without asking the user to log in again. Don't mix up their jobs. An API must check the token's signature, issuer, audience and expiry before trusting anything in it — skipping the audience check means a token meant for a different API would be silently accepted. Scopes/roles say what the client or app is allowed to do in general; they don't prove this specific user may see this specific record — you still need resource-based authorization on top. For SPAs, the safest pattern is a Backend-for-Frontend (BFF) : a server-side component holds the real tokens and gives the browser only an HttpOnly cookie, so XSS can't steal a bearer token directly."},{"c":"oauth-oidc-identity","s":"the-problem-oauth-solves","h":"The problem OAuth solves","t":"# The problem OAuth solves Intermediate Before OAuth, if a printing app wanted your photos from a photo site, you gave it your photo-site password . The app then had full access to everything, forever, and the only way to revoke it was to change your password — a crude, all-or-nothing arrangement with no way to grant anything less than complete access. OAuth 2.0 (RFC 6749) is a framework for delegated authorization : an app gets a limited, revocable token to access specific resources on behalf of a user (or on its own behalf), without ever seeing the user's password . OpenID Connect (OIDC) adds authentication on top of OAuth 2.0: a standard way for the app to learn who the user is , via an ID token . OAuth 2.0 is not an authentication protocol An OAuth access token says \"the bearer may call this API with these scopes\". It's meant for the API , not for the client to identify the user. Using access tokens as proof of login leads to vulnerabilities (token substitution). For sign-in, use OIDC's ID token . In practice you always use OIDC + OAuth together."},{"c":"oauth-oidc-identity","s":"roles","h":"Roles","t":"# Roles Intermediate Role Who Example Resource owner The user who owns the data Ana Client The application requesting access Shop SPA, mobile app, Billing worker Authorization server (identity provider, IdP) Authenticates the user, gets consent, issues tokens Microsoft Entra ID, Auth0, Okta, Keycloak, Duende IdentityServer Resource server The API that accepts access tokens Shop API (your ASP.NET Core app) Client types: confidential clients can keep a secret (server-side web apps, background services) and authenticate with a client secret, certificate or federated credential. Public clients can't (SPAs, mobile and desktop apps: anyone can extract a secret from the binary or JavaScript), so they use PKCE instead."},{"c":"oauth-oidc-identity","s":"tokens","h":"Tokens","t":"# Tokens Intermediate Token For Format Lifetime Notes Access token The API (resource server) Often a JWT ; can be opaque Short: 5–60 min Contains aud (which API), scp / roles , sub , exp . Sent as Authorization: Bearer … ID token (OIDC) The client Always a JWT Short Who the user is: sub , name , email , nonce , auth_time , amr . Don't send it to APIs Refresh token The authorization server Opaque Long: hours to months; often sliding and rotated Exchanged for new access tokens without user interaction. Must be stored securely Authorization code The token endpoint (one-time) Opaque ~1–10 min, single use Intermediate artefact in the code flow"},{"c":"oauth-oidc-identity","s":"authorization-code-flow-with-pkce-step-by-step","h":"Authorization code flow with PKCE, step by step","t":"# Authorization code flow with PKCE, step by step Intermediate The recommended flow for any app with a user : web apps, SPAs (via a BFF ideally), mobile and desktop. PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange, RFC 7636) is now required for all clients in OAuth 2.1 and current best practice. Diagram Copy User/Browser Client app Authorization server (Entra ID) API │ 1. clicks \"Sign in\" │ │ │ │ ─────────────────────────▶│ 2. create code_verifier (random), │ │ │ │ code_challenge = BASE64URL(SHA256(verifier)), state, nonce │ │ ◀── 3. redirect to /authorize?response_type=code&client_id=…&redirect_uri=…&scope=openid profile │ │ api://shop/orders.read offline_access&code_challenge=…&code_challenge_method=S256&state=… │ │ ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────▶│ 4. user logs in (MFA), consents │ │ ◀──────────────────────────── 5. redirect to redirect_uri?code=AUTH_CODE&state=… ──────────────────│ │ ─────────────────────────▶│ 6. verify state; POST /token │ │ │ grant_type=authorization_code&code=…&code_verifier=…&redirect_uri=… │ │ │ ─────────────────────────────────────▶│ 7. checks SHA256(verifier) == │ │ │ │ challenge; issues tokens │ │ │ ◀── { access_token, id_token, refresh_token, expires_in } ───────────── │ │ │ 8. validate id_token (signature, iss, aud=client_id, nonce, exp) │ │ │ 9. GET /orders Authorization: Bearer <access_token> ──────────────────▶│ 10. validate │ │ ◀────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── │ token, authorize Follow steps 2 and 7 together, because that's where PKCE's actual protection lives: the client generates a random code_verifier in step 2 and only ever sends its hash ( code_challenge ) over the browser redirect, which anything intercepting the browser traffic can see. The raw code_verifier itself travels only once, directly from the client to the authorization server over the back channel in step 6 — so even if an attacker steals the authorization code from step 5 (a malicious app on the same device, a logged referrer), they still can't redeem it without the verifier they never saw. Why each other piece exists: The code is exchanged on a back channel , so tokens don't appear in browser URLs or history (unlike the deprecated implicit flow ). state prevents CSRF on the redirect (ties the response to the request). nonce (OIDC) prevents ID token replay. Exact redirect URI matching at the server prevents tokens being sent to an attacker's URL. C# Run ▶ Copy // ASP.NET Core web app (confidential client) signing users in with OIDC + cookies builder. Services . AddAuthentication (o => { o. DefaultScheme = CookieAuthenticationDefaults . AuthenticationScheme ; o. DefaultChallengeScheme = OpenIdConnectDefaults . AuthenticationScheme ; }) . AddCookie (o => { o. Cookie . SameSite = SameSiteMode . Lax ; o. Cookie . SecurePolicy = CookieSecurePolicy . Always ; }) . AddOpenIdConnect (o => { o. Authority = \"https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-id>/v2.0\" ; o. ClientId = builder. Configuration [ \"Oidc:ClientId\" ]; o. ClientSecret = builder. Configuration [ \"Oidc:ClientSecret\" ]; // or a certificate / federated credential o. ResponseType = \"code\" ; // authorization code flow o. UsePkce = true ; // default true o. SaveTokens = true ; // keep access/refresh tokens in the auth session o. Scope . Add ( \"api://shop-api/orders.read\" ); o. Scope . Add ( \"offline_access\" ); // ask for a refresh token o. MapInboundClaims = false ; o. TokenValidationParameters . NameClaimType = \"name\" ; }); // For Entra ID specifically: builder.Services.AddMicrosoftIdentityWebAppAuthentication(builder.Configuration) // .EnableTokenAcquisitionToCallDownstreamApi([\"api://shop-api/orders.read\"]).AddInMemoryTokenCaches(); Notice this registers both a cookie scheme and an OIDC scheme, with the cookie as the default: once the OIDC flow completes, the app doesn't repeat the full redirect dance on every request — it issues its own cookie and relies on that, re-engaging OIDC only when the session needs to be"},{"c":"oauth-oidc-identity","s":"client-credentials-flow","h":"Client credentials flow","t":"# Client credentials flow Intermediate Service-to-service , no user involved: a background worker, a daemon, or one microservice calling another as itself . HTTP Copy POST /oauth2/v2. 0 /token Content-Type : application/x-www-form-urlencoded grant_type =client_credentials& client_id =…& client_secret =… (or client_assertion with a certificate/federated token) & scope = api : //inventory-api/.default The token carries the client's identity and application permissions ( roles claim, e.g. Inventory.Read ), with no scp and no user. In Azure, prefer managed identity (no secret at all) or a certificate/federated credential over client secrets — the exact same preference order Identity & secrets laid out generally, here specifically for the OAuth grant type that would otherwise need a secret. C# Run ▶ Copy var credential = new ManagedIdentityCredential (); // or ClientSecretCredential/ClientCertificateCredential outside Azure var token = await credential. GetTokenAsync ( new TokenRequestContext ([ \"api://inventory-api/.default\" ]), ct); // Typically wrapped in a DelegatingHandler that caches tokens until shortly before expiry (see HttpClient chapter)"},{"c":"oauth-oidc-identity","s":"refresh-tokens","h":"Refresh tokens","t":"# Refresh tokens Intermediate Access tokens are short-lived because they can't be revoked once issued (a JWT is valid until exp ) — exactly the tradeoff Auth named when it explained why access-token lifetimes stay short. Refresh tokens give a good user experience without long-lived access tokens: HTTP Copy POST /token grant_type =refresh_token& refresh_token =…& client_id =…& scope =… → { access_token (new), refresh_token ( new : ROTATED), expires_in } Best practices: rotation (each use returns a new refresh token and invalidates the old one; reuse of an old one signals theft, so the whole family is revoked), binding to the client (sender-constrained tokens: DPoP or mTLS), revocation on logout and password change, secure storage (server side, or the OS keychain on mobile; never localStorage ), and absolute lifetimes."},{"c":"oauth-oidc-identity","s":"other-flows","h":"Other flows","t":"# Other flows Advanced Flow Use Notes Device authorization grant (device code) Input-constrained devices and CLIs (TV apps, az login --use-device-code ) The user enters a code on another device On-Behalf-Of (OBO, token exchange RFC 8693) An API calling a downstream API as the user API A exchanges the incoming user token for a token to API B; preserves user context and permissions Implicit ✘ Deprecated : tokens returned in the URL fragment Replaced by code + PKCE Resource Owner Password Credentials (ROPC) ✘ Deprecated : the client collects the user's password Breaks MFA and SSO; only for legacy migration Client-initiated backchannel (CIBA) Decoupled authentication (e.g. a bank app approval) Specialised Federated credentials / JWT bearer assertion Workload identity federation (GitHub Actions, Kubernetes) No secrets ( Identity & secrets )"},{"c":"oauth-oidc-identity","s":"openid-connect-in-more-detail","h":"OpenID Connect in more detail","t":"# OpenID Connect in more detail Advanced OIDC standardises: The openid scope (plus profile , email , address , phone ) and standard claims ( sub , name , given_name , email , email_verified …). The ID token JWT and its validation rules ( iss , aud = client ID, exp , iat , nonce , signature). Discovery : {authority}/.well-known/openid-configuration publishes the endpoints ( authorization_endpoint , token_endpoint , userinfo_endpoint , end_session_endpoint ) and jwks_uri (the public signing keys). This is how ASP.NET Core configures itself from just Authority — the exact discovery mechanism Auth traced as step 2 of JWT validation. UserInfo endpoint for additional claims; session management and logout (RP-initiated logout, front- and back-channel logout). JSON Copy // ID token payload (decoded) { \"iss\" : \"https://login.microsoftonline.com/9188…/v2.0\" , \"aud\" : \"3f2a…-client-id\" , \"sub\" : \"AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAIkzqFVrSaSaFHy782bbtaQ\" , \"oid\" : \"00000000-0000-0000-66f3-3332eca7ea81\" , \"name\" : \"Ana Silva\" , \"preferred_username\" : \"ana@contoso.com\" , \"nonce\" : \"7362CAEA-9CA5-4B43-9BA3-34D7C303EBA7\" , \"iat\" : 1761570000 , \"exp\" : 1761573600 , \"auth_time\" : 1761569990 , \"amr\" : [ \"pwd\" , \"mfa\" ] }"},{"c":"oauth-oidc-identity","s":"scopes-roles-and-claims-for-apis","h":"Scopes, roles and claims for APIs","t":"# Scopes, roles and claims for APIs Advanced Concept Meaning Claim Example Scope (delegated permission) What the client may do on the user's behalf ; the user consented scp (Entra) / scope orders.read , orders.write App role A role assigned to a user/group or an application roles Orders.Admin (user), Inventory.Read (app) Audience Which API the token is for aud api://shop-api The effective permission for delegated access is the intersection of what the app was granted (scopes) and what the user is allowed to do (roles or your own data rules). An API must check both the token's scopes or roles and resource-level authorization (\"is this the user's order?\"). Scopes alone don't prevent IDOR — a token scoped to orders.read proves the client is allowed to read some orders, not that this particular user is allowed to read this specific order, exactly the gap Web security warned against closing with scope checks alone. C# Run ▶ Copy builder. Services . AddAuthorizationBuilder () . AddPolicy ( \"orders:read\" , p => p. RequireAssertion (ctx => ctx. User . FindFirst ( \"scp\" )?. Value . Split ( ' ' ). Contains ( \"orders.read\" ) == true // delegated: user via client || ctx. User . IsInRole ( \"Orders.Reader\" ))); // application: daemon"},{"c":"oauth-oidc-identity","s":"validating-access-tokens-in-an-api","h":"Validating access tokens in an API","t":"# Validating access tokens in an API Advanced An API receiving a JWT access token must check: Signature : with the issuer's public key from jwks_uri (cached; rotated keys found by kid ). Reject alg: none and unexpected algorithms. Issuer ( iss ): the expected tenant or authority. Audience ( aud ): this API . Without this check, a token issued for another API in the same tenant would be accepted (the \"confused deputy\"). Lifetime : exp , nbf (with small clock skew). Scopes/roles for the operation, then resource authorization . ASP.NET Core's JwtBearer handler does 1–4 from Authority + Audience ( Auth ) — this chapter's list is exactly that same five-step validation, now explicitly in the context of the token lifecycle that produced the JWT in the first place. # JWT vs reference (opaque) tokens JWT (self-contained) Reference token (opaque) Validation Locally, with public keys: fast, no network call Introspection call to the authorization server (RFC 7662), usually cached Revocation Hard: valid until expiry (keep it short) Immediate: the server just forgets it Size and privacy Larger; claims are readable by anyone holding it Small; reveals nothing Use Most APIs High-security or revocation-critical APIs; tokens leaving your boundary"},{"c":"oauth-oidc-identity","s":"spas-and-the-bff-pattern","h":"SPAs and the BFF pattern","t":"# SPAs and the BFF pattern Advanced SPAs are public clients running in a hostile environment (any XSS can read tokens in JS memory or localStorage ). Options: Approach How Verdict Tokens in the browser (code + PKCE in JS, e.g. MSAL.js) SPA gets tokens and calls APIs with bearer headers Works, widely used; XSS = token theft ; use short lifetimes and refresh-token rotation; avoid localStorage Backend-for-Frontend (BFF) A thin server-side ASP.NET Core app (often with YARP) performs OIDC as a confidential client , keeps tokens server-side , and gives the browser only an HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite session cookie; it proxies API calls and attaches access tokens Recommended by the IETF \"OAuth 2.0 for Browser-Based Apps\" guidance; needs CSRF protection (SameSite + antiforgery/custom header) Diagram Copy Browser (SPA) ──cookie──▶ BFF (ASP.NET Core + YARP, holds tokens) ──Bearer token──▶ Shop API └─ OIDC code flow + PKCE with Entra ID (confidential client) Recognise this as the same architectural recommendation from both Auth and Microservices , arrived at from three different angles — token security, cookie ergonomics, and client-specialised gateways — all converging on the same shape. Libraries: Duende.BFF , or ASP.NET Core with AddOpenIdConnect + YARP + token management (Duende.AccessTokenManagement)."},{"c":"oauth-oidc-identity","s":"identity-providers-for-net","h":"Identity providers for .NET","t":"# Identity providers for .NET Advanced Option Type Fits Microsoft Entra ID Cloud IdP (workforce) Employees, B2B, Azure-integrated apps; Microsoft.Identity.Web + MSAL Microsoft Entra External ID Cloud CIAM Customer sign-up/sign-in (successor of Azure AD B2C) Auth0 (Okta), Okta Cloud IdP/CIAM Multi-cloud, rich features Keycloak Open-source, self-hosted On-premises, full control Duende IdentityServer .NET framework to build your own OIDC server (commercial licence for most companies) Custom requirements, on-premises, full control in .NET OpenIddict Open-source .NET OIDC server framework Self-hosted authorization server in ASP.NET Core ASP.NET Core Identity User store/membership (not an OIDC server) Simple first-party login; combine with Duende or OpenIddict to become an IdP Rule: don't build your own authorization server unless you must. Identity is security-critical, specification-heavy and constantly attacked; use a managed provider when you can."},{"c":"oauth-oidc-identity","s":"common-mistakes","h":"Common mistakes","t":"# Common mistakes Advanced Mistake Consequence Fix Not validating aud Tokens for other APIs accepted Always set Audience / ValidAudiences Accepting ID tokens at APIs Wrong token type; no scopes APIs accept access tokens only Long-lived access tokens Stolen tokens usable for days 5–60 minute lifetimes plus refresh tokens Tokens in localStorage XSS steals them BFF with cookies, or in-memory with short lifetimes Implicit flow / ROPC Token leakage; password exposure Code + PKCE; client credentials for services Wildcard or loose redirect URIs Codes or tokens sent to attackers Exact, registered HTTPS redirect URIs Client secrets in SPAs/mobile apps Secret extracted Public client + PKCE Checking only scopes, not data ownership IDOR Resource-based authorization Rolling your own JWT issuance with HS256 shared keys Any service with the key can mint tokens Asymmetric keys at a real IdP Sensitive data in JWT claims Readable by anyone holding the token Keep claims minimal; look up details server-side Q What's the difference between OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect? OAuth 2.0 is a framework for delegated authorization: clients obtain access tokens to call APIs with limited scopes on behalf of a user or themselves. OpenID Connect is an identity layer on top that adds authentication: an ID token describing the user, standard claims and scopes (openid, profile, email), the UserInfo endpoint, discovery and logout. Q Explain the authorization code flow with PKCE. The client creates a random code_verifier and sends its SHA-256 hash (code_challenge) with the /authorize redirect. The user authenticates at the IdP, which redirects back with a one-time authorization code. The client exchanges the code plus the original code_verifier at the token endpoint over a back channel; the server checks the hash and returns access, ID and refresh tokens. PKCE prevents intercepted codes from being redeemed. Q Which OAuth flow would you use for a service calling another API without a user? Client credentials: the service authenticates as itself (a certificate, federated credential or managed identity, rather than a secret) and receives an access token with application permissions (roles) for the target API's audience. Q Access token vs ID token vs refresh token? The access token is for the API: it states audience, scopes/roles and expiry and is sent as a bearer token. The ID token is for the client: it proves who the user is and must not be sent to APIs. The refresh token goes to the authorization server to get new access tokens silently; it's long-lived, rotated and stored securely. Q Why was the implicit flow deprecated? It returned tokens directly in the redirect URL fragment, exposing them to browser history, logs, referrer leaks and malicious scripts, with no way to bind them to the client and no refresh tokens. The authorization code flow with PKCE provides the same for public clients securely. Q What must an API validate on an incoming JWT access token? The signature with the issuer's current public keys (from jwks_uri, by kid), the issuer, the audience (it must be this API), expiry and not-before (with small clock skew), and then the required scopes or roles, followed by resource-level authorization. Q How should a SPA handle tokens securely? Preferably via the Backend-for-Frontend pattern: a server-side component performs the OIDC code flow as a confidential client, stores tokens server-side, issues an HttpOnly Secure SameSite cookie to the browser, and proxies API calls with access tokens attached. If tokens must live in the browser, use code + PKCE, keep them in memory with short lifetimes and refresh-token rotation, and never put them in localStorage. Q What is the On-Behalf-Of flow? A middle-tier API that received a user's access token exchanges it at the authorization server for a new access token for a downstream API, so the downstream call carries the user's identity and delegated permissions (Entra ID OBO, or RFC 8693 token exchange)."},{"c":"oauth-oidc-identity","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect protocols on the Microsoft identity platform RFC 6749 (OAuth 2.0) · RFC 7636 (PKCE) · OAuth 2.0 Security Best Current Practice (RFC 9700) OpenID Connect Core 1.0 OAuth 2.0 for Browser-Based Applications (IETF draft) oauth.com \"OAuth 2.0 Simplified\" by Aaron Parecki, the best plain-English guide."},{"c":"oauth-oidc-identity","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run (a plain console app, .NET 8/9, only base-class-library crypto/JSON — no extra packages, no network call to a real identity provider). It verifies the actual protocol mechanics: PKCE's hash check, a hand-built HS256 JWT's signature validation (and that tampering breaks it), audience/expiry validation, scope-vs-role authorization, and exact redirect-URI matching. The full login round trip against a real authorization server needs a browser and a real IdP, so that part is left to the Further reading links instead of faked here. C# Run ▶ Copy // Verify this chapter: run with `dotnet run`. No external packages needed // (plain System.Security.Cryptography + System.Text.Json) and no network // call to a real identity provider -- this verifies the PROTOCOL MECHANICS // (PKCE hashing, JWT structure/signature, audience/expiry checks) that the // chapter describes, not a live login against Entra ID. using System ; using System . Security . Cryptography ; using System . Text ; using System . Text . Json ; void Check ( string name, bool ok) { if (!ok) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: {name}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: {name}\" ); } string Base64Url ( byte [] bytes) => Convert . ToBase64String (bytes). TrimEnd ( '=' ). Replace ( '+' , '-' ). Replace ( '/' , '_' ); byte [] Base64UrlDecode ( string s) { s = s. Replace ( '-' , '+' ). Replace ( '_' , '/' ); switch (s. Length % 4 ) { case 2 : s += \"==\" ; break ; case 3 : s += \"=\" ; break ; } return Convert . FromBase64String (s); } // 1. PKCE: code_challenge = BASE64URL(SHA256(code_verifier)). The server // recomputes this from the verifier sent later and must get the exact same // value; a different verifier must NOT produce the same challenge. string codeVerifier = Base64Url ( RandomNumberGenerator . GetBytes ( 32 )); string codeChallenge = Base64Url ( SHA256 . HashData ( Encoding . ASCII . GetBytes (codeVerifier))); string serverRecomputedChallenge = Base64Url ( SHA256 . HashData ( Encoding . ASCII . GetBytes (codeVerifier))); Check ( \"the server can recompute the same code_challenge from the code_verifier it receives later\" , serverRecomputedChallenge == codeChallenge); string wrongVerifier = Base64Url ( RandomNumberGenerator . GetBytes ( 32 )); string wrongChallenge = Base64Url ( SHA256 . HashData ( Encoding . ASCII . GetBytes (wrongVerifier))); Check ( \"a different code_verifier produces a different code_challenge (a stolen code alone is useless)\" , wrongChallenge != codeChallenge); // 2. A minimal HS256 JWT: header.payload.signature, each base64url, signature // = HMACSHA256(header + \".\" + payload, secret). This is exactly the shape // ASP.NET Core's JwtBearer handler parses, just built and checked by hand. string MakeJwt ( object payload, byte [] secret) { string header = Base64Url ( Encoding . UTF8 . GetBytes ( \"\"\"{\"alg\":\"HS256\",\"typ\":\"JWT\"}\"\"\" )); string body = Base64Url ( Encoding . UTF8 . GetBytes ( JsonSerializer . Serialize (payload))); string signature = Base64Url ( new HMACSHA256 (secret). ComputeHash ( Encoding . ASCII . GetBytes ( $\"{header}.{body}\" ))); return $\"{header}.{body}.{signature}\" ; } bool VerifyJwtSignature ( string jwt, byte [] secret) { var parts = jwt. Split ( '.' ); if (parts. Length != 3 ) return false ; var expected = Base64Url ( new HMACSHA256 (secret). ComputeHash ( Encoding . ASCII . GetBytes ( $\"{parts[0]}.{parts[1]}\" ))); return CryptographicOperations . FixedTimeEquals ( Base64UrlDecode (expected), Base64UrlDecode (parts[ 2 ])); } byte [] signingKey = RandomNumberGenerator . GetBytes ( 32 ); long now = DateTimeOffset . UtcNow . ToUnixTimeSeconds (); string goodToken = MakeJwt ( new { aud = \"api://shop-api\" , exp = now + 300 , sub = \"user-123\" }, signingKey); Check ( \"a JWT signed with the issuer's key validates against that same key\" , VerifyJwtSignature (goodToken, signingKey)); byte [] attackerKey = RandomNumberGenerator . GetBytes ( 32 ); Check ( \"the same JWT fails validation against a DIFFERENT key (an att"},{"c":"system-design","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Interviewers care about your process (clarify → estimate → design → scale → handle failure), not one \"correct\" answer. Do rough capacity estimates (users, RPS, storage) before drawing boxes — the numbers decide the architecture, not the other way round. Know the standard building blocks (CDN, gateway, cache, SQL vs NoSQL, queue, search) and a concrete .NET/Azure pick for each. Practice the three worked designs here (URL shortener, checkout, notifications) — each reuses patterns from earlier chapters: outbox, idempotency, sagas, bulkheads. Always name the trade-off , not just the choice: \"I'd use X because Y; the cost is Z.\""},{"c":"system-design","s":"what-interviewers-are-looking-for","h":"What interviewers are looking for","t":"# What interviewers are looking for Intermediate A system design interview isn't about the \"right\" answer. It tests whether you can: Clarify ambiguous requirements and identify what matters. Structure a solution from high level to detail. Choose components with reasons and trade-offs (\"I'd use X because…, the cost is…\"). Scale it: find the bottlenecks and fix them. Handle failure : what breaks, and what happens then. Communicate : think out loud, draw clearly, and adapt to hints. For .NET backend roles, interviewers also like hearing concrete .NET and Azure choices (EF Core vs Dapper, Service Bus vs Event Hubs, Container Apps vs AKS) and code-level patterns (outbox, idempotency, caching strategy) — exactly the vocabulary the rest of this book has spent dozens of chapters building up. This chapter is where all of it gets exercised at once, under time pressure, on a whiteboard."},{"c":"system-design","s":"a-repeatable-framework-45-minutes","h":"A repeatable framework (45 minutes)","t":"# A repeatable framework (45 minutes) Intermediate Step Time What you do 1. Requirements 5–8 min Functional : what the system must do (core use cases only). Non-functional : scale, latency, availability, consistency, durability, security, cost. Out of scope : say what you won't cover 2. Estimates 3–5 min Users, requests/s (peak), read/write ratio, data size per item, storage over 5 years, bandwidth. Keep it rough; it drives decisions 3. API design 3–5 min The main endpoints or messages: POST /orders , GET /orders/{id} , events 4. Data model 5 min Entities, keys, relationships, access patterns → choice of store and partitioning 5. High-level design 10 min Boxes and arrows: clients → gateway/CDN → services → caches → databases → queues → workers 6. Deep dives 10–15 min The 2–3 hardest parts: hot paths, consistency, scaling the database, failure handling 7. Wrap up 2–3 min Bottlenecks, monitoring, what you'd do next with more time Step 2 is placed right after requirements and before any architecture on purpose: the estimates drive the decisions that follow, they don't just decorate the answer. A read:write ratio of 100:1 points straight at caching and read replicas; a ratio closer to 1:1 points at write-optimised storage instead. Skip the estimate and jump straight to architecture, and you're guessing at exactly the number that should have decided it."},{"c":"system-design","s":"back-of-the-envelope-numbers","h":"Back-of-the-envelope numbers","t":"# Back-of-the-envelope numbers Intermediate Quantity Rough value Seconds per day ~86,400 ≈ 10⁵ 1 million requests/day ≈ 12 RPS average (peak usually 2–10× the average) 100 million requests/day ≈ 1,200 RPS average Memory read / SSD read / same-datacentre round trip / cross-region round trip 100 ns / ~100 µs / ** 0.5 ms** / ~50–150 ms Redis GET < 1 ms Indexed SQL point query 1–5 ms One ASP.NET Core instance (simple JSON, no DB) Tens of thousands of RPS; realistic CRUD with a DB: hundreds to a few thousand RPS per instance Single SQL Server / Azure SQL instance Thousands to tens of thousands of simple queries/s, depending on tier and queries Kafka/Event Hubs partition MB/s per partition; scale by partitions Example: 10M daily active users, each making 20 reads and 1 write per day → reads ≈ 200M/day ≈ 2,300 RPS average (~10k peak); writes ≈ 10M/day ≈ 115 RPS average. That's a read-heavy system, so caching and read replicas matter more than write sharding."},{"c":"system-design","s":"building-blocks-and-their-net-azure-choices","h":"Building blocks and their .NET/Azure choices","t":"# Building blocks and their .NET/Azure choices Intermediate Building block Purpose .NET / Azure options DNS + CDN + WAF Global entry, static content, edge caching, attack filtering Azure Front Door (+ WAF), Azure CDN API gateway Routing, auth, rate limiting, aggregation Azure API Management, YARP Load balancer Distribute across instances Built into App Service/Container Apps/AKS ingress, Application Gateway Stateless services Business logic, horizontally scalable ASP.NET Core minimal APIs on Container Apps / App Service / AKS Cache Cut read latency and DB load HybridCache + Azure Managed Redis, output caching Relational DB Transactions, relationships, reporting Azure SQL (Hyperscale for scale), PostgreSQL Flexible Server; EF Core/Dapper NoSQL Massive scale by key, flexible schema, global Cosmos DB Object storage Files, media, exports Blob Storage (+ SAS, CDN) Queue / broker Asynchronous work, decoupling, load levelling Service Bus, Storage Queues; MassTransit/Wolverine Event stream High-volume events, replay, analytics Event Hubs (Kafka) Search Full-text, facets, vector search Azure AI Search, Elasticsearch Background workers Process queues, scheduled jobs Worker services, Azure Functions, Container Apps jobs Real-time push Live updates SignalR / Azure SignalR Service Identity Users and service auth Entra ID / External ID, managed identities Observability Logs, metrics, traces OpenTelemetry + Azure Monitor/App Insights"},{"c":"system-design","s":"worked-design-1-url-shortener","h":"Worked design 1: URL shortener","t":"# Worked design 1: URL shortener Advanced Requirements: create a short link for a long URL; redirect a short link to the long URL; optional custom aliases and expiry; analytics (click counts). Non-functional: very read-heavy (100:1), low redirect latency (< 50 ms), high availability, links must never collide. Estimates: 100M new links/month ≈ 40 writes/s; 10B redirects/month ≈ 4,000 RPS average (peak ~20k). Storage: ~500 bytes per link × 100M × 12 × 5 years ≈ 3 TB. API: HTTP Copy POST /api/links { \"url\" : \"https://…\" , \"alias\" : \"promo26\" , \"expiresAt\" : \"…\" } → 201 { \"code\" : \"aZ3k9Q\" , \"shortUrl\" : \"https://sho.rt/aZ3k9Q\" } GET /{code} → 301 / 302 Location : https : //… (302 if you want to count every click server-side) GET /api/links/{code}/stats Key design decisions: notice that the 100:1 read:write ratio from the requirements is what decides nearly every row below — this is the estimates-drive-decisions principle from the framework section, playing out concretely. Problem Options and choice Generating unique short codes (a) Hash the URL (MD5 → base62, take 7 chars): collisions need handling. (b) Counter + base62 encoding : unique by construction, using a distributed ID source (database sequence ranges handed out in blocks per instance, or Snowflake-style IDs). (c) Random 7-char base62 (62⁷ ≈ 3.5 trillion) with a uniqueness check. Choose (b) or (c) ; 7 chars is plenty Storage Simple key → value access, huge volume, global: Cosmos DB (partition key = code; point reads ~1 RU) or Azure SQL with a clustered index on code. Cosmos for global distribution and low-latency point reads Read path latency Cache hot codes in Redis/HybridCache (L1 in-memory per instance + L2 Redis), plus CDN/Front Door caching of redirects for very hot links. Cache hit rates of 90%+ are typical Analytics without slowing redirects Publish a click event asynchronously (Event Hubs), aggregate with a consumer/stream job into counts; don't write to the DB on every redirect Expiry TTL in Cosmos DB and cache Abuse Rate-limit creation per user/IP, scan URLs against malware lists, auth for custom aliases Diagram Copy Client ─▶ Front Door (CDN cache for hot redirects) ─▶ Redirect service (ASP.NET Core minimal API, autoscaled) ├─ L1 memory cache → L2 Redis → Cosmos DB (code → url) └─ publish ClickEvent → Event Hubs → Analytics worker → stats store Create path: API → ID allocator (sequence block) → Cosmos DB → (prime cache) C# Run ▶ Copy app. MapGet ( \"/{code:regex(^[a-zA-Z0-9]{{6,10}}$)}\" , async ( string code, HybridCache cache, ILinkStore store, IClickPublisher clicks, HttpContext ctx) => { var url = await cache. GetOrCreateAsync ( $\"link:{code}\" , async ct => await store. GetUrlAsync (code, ct), new HybridCacheEntryOptions { Expiration = TimeSpan . FromHours ( 1 ), LocalCacheExpiration = TimeSpan . FromMinutes ( 5 ) }); if (url is null ) return Results . NotFound (); clicks. TryPublish ( new ClickEvent (code, DateTimeOffset . UtcNow , ctx. Request . Headers . Referer )); // non-blocking, best effort (buffered) return Results . Redirect (url, permanent: false ); }). ShortCircuit (); // skip unnecessary middleware for the hottest path Notice clicks.TryPublish is fire-and-forget on the hot path — exactly the \"don't write to the DB on every redirect\" decision from the table above, made concrete: the redirect itself never waits on the analytics write, which is precisely why the redirect can stay under the 50 ms target even while click data accumulates reliably in the background."},{"c":"system-design","s":"worked-design-2-e-commerce-checkout","h":"Worked design 2: e-commerce checkout","t":"# Worked design 2: e-commerce checkout Advanced Requirements: place an order from a cart: validate stock, take payment, confirm, notify, update inventory. Non-functional: correctness first (no overselling, no double charges), checkout p95 < 1 s, resilient to payment provider outages, peak load during sales (10× normal). Bounded contexts / services: Catalog, Cart, Ordering , Inventory , Payments , Notifications, Shipping. Start as a modular monolith ; split Payments and Inventory out if scaling or team ownership requires it ( Microservices ) — exactly the \"start coarse, extract when you have a concrete reason\" guidance from that chapter. Flow (saga with orchestration): Diagram Copy POST /checkout (Idempotency-Key) ─▶ Ordering API 1. Validate cart; create Order (status: Pending) + OrderSubmitted in OUTBOX — one SQL transaction 2. Return 202 Accepted + /orders/{id} (or wait briefly for fast success) Outbox relay ─▶ Service Bus ─▶ Order saga (state machine): 3. ReserveStock → Inventory: conditional update \"UPDATE Stock SET Reserved += @q WHERE Sku=@s AND Available >= @q\" (no overselling) 4. ChargePayment → Payments: call the PSP with an idempotency key = orderId; handle webhooks for async confirmation 5a. PaymentSucceeded → ConfirmOrder → OrderConfirmed event → Notifications (email), Shipping 5b. PaymentFailed / timeout → ReleaseStock (compensation) → Order Cancelled → notify the user This single flow is practically a tour of the book: step 1 is the transactional outbox, step 3 is atomic-conditional-update concurrency control, step 4 is idempotent external side effects, and 5a/5b together are a saga with compensation — four separate chapters' worth of mechanisms, cooperating in one checkout. Deep-dive talking points: No overselling: the atomic conditional UPDATE (or optimistic concurrency on a stock row), reservations with expiry, and a single writer per SKU partition for extreme flash sales (or a queue in front of hot SKUs). No double charge: an Idempotency-Key on the checkout endpoint, the PSP's idempotency key, an inbox on payment consumers, and a unique constraint on Payments(OrderId) ( API design , Messaging ). Consistency: each service owns its database; a saga with compensations instead of distributed transactions; the outbox for reliable events. Resilience: PSP calls with timeout, retry (idempotent) and circuit breaker; if the PSP is down, accept the order and charge later (queue), or fail fast with a clear message ( Resilience ). Peak load: autoscale the API on RPS and the workers on queue length (KEDA); cache catalog and prices; a queue absorbs bursts; a virtual waiting room for extreme sales. Data: Azure SQL for orders, payments and inventory (transactions); Cosmos DB or Redis for carts (high write rate, TTL); Azure AI Search for the catalog. Observability: trace a checkout across services; business metrics (orders/min, payment failure rate); alerts on saga timeouts and DLQ depth."},{"c":"system-design","s":"worked-design-3-notification-service","h":"Worked design 3: notification service","t":"# Worked design 3: notification service Advanced Requirements: other services send notifications (email, SMS, push, in-app) to users, with templates, user preferences (channel opt-outs, quiet hours), retries and delivery tracking. Scale: 50M notifications/day, with bursts (marketing campaigns). Diagram Copy Producers (Ordering, Billing…) ─▶ Service Bus topic \"notification-requested\" └─▶ Notification service (consumer) 1. Idempotency check (inbox on NotificationId) 2. Load user preferences (cached) → filter channels, quiet hours → schedule 3. Render template (localised) → per-channel queues ┌──────────────────────┼───────────────────────┐ email queue sms queue push queue (separate queues = bulkheads + independent scaling and rate limits) ▼ ▼ ▼ Email worker (SendGrid/ACS) SMS worker (ACS/Twilio) Push worker (FCM/APNs/Notification Hubs) └──────── delivery status ──▶ Status store (Cosmos DB / Table) ◀── provider webhooks In-app: write to the user's inbox (Cosmos, partitioned by userId) + SignalR push if online Notice the parenthetical on the three channel queues — \"bulkheads + independent scaling and rate limits\" — naming the exact resilience pattern from Resilience : a slow or rate-limited SMS provider can never back up email delivery, because the two queues share nothing. Talking points: per-channel queues isolate failures and respect provider rate limits (token bucket per provider); retries with backoff and a DLQ per channel; campaigns are throttled through a separate low-priority queue so transactional notifications (password resets) aren't delayed ( priority queues ); templates are versioned and cached; deduplication by NotificationId; GDPR (preferences, unsubscribe links, data retention); and metrics on send rate, failure rate per provider, and end-to-end latency."},{"c":"system-design","s":"trade-offs-to-discuss-explicitly","h":"Trade-offs to discuss explicitly","t":"# Trade-offs to discuss explicitly Advanced Trade-off Discussion Consistency vs availability Strong consistency for money and stock; eventual consistency for feeds, counts and search indexes. Say which data needs which ( NoSQL ) SQL vs NoSQL SQL: transactions, joins, flexible queries. NoSQL: scale by key, global distribution, schema flexibility. Decide from the access patterns Sync vs async Sync for user-facing reads needing immediate answers; async for side effects and long work (202 + status) Monolith vs microservices Team size, deployment independence and scaling differences justify a split; otherwise a modular monolith Cache freshness vs load TTLs, invalidation by events, stampede protection Latency vs cost More replicas, regions and premium tiers vs budget; autoscaling and reserved capacity Build vs buy Identity, payments, email and search are usually bought (SaaS/PaaS); the core domain is built Push vs pull Webhooks/SignalR vs polling; fan-out on write vs on read (social feeds) Normalisation vs denormalisation Write simplicity and integrity vs read performance (CQRS read models) Each row in this table is a decision this book has already made once, explicitly, in some earlier chapter — naming the trade-off out loud in an interview is really just demonstrating that you understand why that earlier decision was made, not merely that you remember the answer."},{"c":"system-design","s":"code-level-design-questions","h":"Code-level design questions","t":"# Code-level design questions Intermediate Many .NET interviews include \"low-level design\": design a parking lot, a rate limiter, an LRU cache, a library system, or \"how would you structure this API?\". Approach: Clarify requirements and edge cases. Identify entities, value objects and behaviours (nouns and verbs), and the invariants . Sketch classes and interfaces, applying SOLID and relevant patterns (Strategy for pricing rules, State for lifecycles, Observer/events for notifications, Factory for creation) ( Design patterns ). Discuss concurrency (what's shared, how it's protected), testability (DI, interfaces at boundaries) and extensibility (new types without modification). Write a little real C# for the core logic. C# Run ▶ Copy // Classic: LRU cache, O(1) get/put with Dictionary + LinkedList, thread-safe with a lock public sealed class LruCache < TKey , TValue >( int capacity) where TKey : notnull { private readonly Dictionary < TKey , LinkedListNode <( TKey Key , TValue Value )>> _map = new (capacity); private readonly LinkedList <( TKey Key , TValue Value )> _order = new (); // most recent at the front private readonly Lock _gate = new (); public bool TryGet ( TKey key, out TValue value ) { lock (_gate) { if (_map. TryGetValue (key, out var node)) { _order. Remove (node); _order. AddFirst (node); // mark as most recently used value = node. Value . Value ; return true ; } value = default !; return false ; } } public void Put ( TKey key, TValue value ) { lock (_gate) { if (_map. TryGetValue (key, out var existing)) { _order. Remove (existing); _map. Remove (key); } else if (_map. Count >= capacity) { var lru = _order. Last !; // evict least recently used _order. RemoveLast (); _map. Remove (lru. Value . Key ); } _map[key] = _order. AddFirst ((key, value )); } } } Q How do you approach a system design interview question? Clarify functional and non-functional requirements and the scope; do quick capacity estimates; define the main APIs and data model; draw a high-level architecture; deep-dive into the hardest parts (scaling the hot path, data consistency, failure handling); and close with bottlenecks, monitoring and trade-offs, explaining the reasons behind every choice. Q How would you prevent overselling in a high-traffic shop? Make stock decrement atomic and conditional in the database (UPDATE … WHERE Available >= @qty, checking rows affected) or use optimistic concurrency on the stock row; use time-limited reservations released by a compensating action if payment fails; and for extreme hot items, serialise writes per SKU through a queue or partition. Never check-then-update in application memory. Q How would you design a rate limiter? Clarify per-user/IP/API-key limits and the algorithm (token bucket for bursts, sliding window for fairness). Within one instance, use ASP.NET Core's rate limiter; for a distributed limit, keep counters or buckets in Redis with atomic operations (INCR with expiry, or a Lua script for token bucket), or enforce it at the gateway (APIM/Front Door). Return 429 with Retry-After, and decide fail-open vs fail-closed if Redis is unavailable. Q How do you scale a read-heavy API? Make the service stateless and scale out; add caching layers (CDN/output cache for public responses, HybridCache/Redis for data); use read replicas or CQRS read models; optimise queries and indexes; paginate and project; and move expensive work to async background processing."},{"c":"system-design","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading Designing Data-Intensive Applications by Martin Kleppmann: the foundation for every system design discussion. System Design Interview Vol. 1 & 2 by Alex Xu. Azure Architecture Center: reference architectures and cloud design patterns eShop reference application (.NET Aspire, microservices) The System Design Primer (GitHub, donnemartin)."},{"c":"system-design","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter Run with dotnet run (plain console app, .NET 8/9, no extra packages). C# Run ▶ Copy // 1) Back-of-the-envelope numbers table: \"1 million requests/day ~= 12 RPS average\". double requestsPerDay = 1_000_000 ; double secondsPerDay = 86_400 ; double rps = requestsPerDay / secondsPerDay; if (!(rps is > 11 and < 12 )) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected ~11.6 RPS, got {rps}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: 1,000,000 requests/day / 86,400s = {rps:F2} RPS, matching the chapter's '~12 RPS average' estimate.\" ); // 2) The checkout example's capacity math: 10M DAU x 20 reads + 1 write/day. double dau = 10_000_000 ; double readsPerUserPerDay = 20 ; double writesPerUserPerDay = 1 ; double readsPerDay = dau * readsPerUserPerDay; double writesPerDay = dau * writesPerUserPerDay; double readRps = readsPerDay / secondsPerDay; double writeRps = writesPerDay / secondsPerDay; if (!(readRps is > 2200 and < 2400 )) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected ~2300 read RPS, got {readRps}\" ); if (!(writeRps is > 110 and < 120 )) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected ~115 write RPS, got {writeRps}\" ); double readWriteRatio = readRps / writeRps; if (!(readWriteRatio is > 19 and < 21 )) throw new Exception ( $\"FAIL: expected ~20:1 read:write ratio, got {readWriteRatio}\" ); Console . WriteLine ( $\"OK: 10M DAU x (20 reads + 1 write)/day => {readRps:F0} read RPS, {writeRps:F0} write RPS (~{readWriteRatio:F0}:1), confirming the 'read-heavy' conclusion.\" ); // 3) The LRU cache from \"Code-level design questions\", verbatim from the chapter: O(1) get/put, evicts least-recently-used. var lru = new LruCache < int , string >(capacity: 2 ); lru. Put ( 1 , \"a\" ); lru. Put ( 2 , \"b\" ); if (!lru. TryGet ( 1 , out var v1) || v1 != \"a\" ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected key 1 to be present with value 'a'\" ); lru. Put ( 3 , \"c\" ); // capacity 2 reached; evicts the least-recently-used key. // key 1 was just accessed above, so key 2 (untouched since insertion) is evicted, not key 1. if (lru. TryGet ( 2 , out _)) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected key 2 to have been evicted as least-recently-used\" ); if (!lru. TryGet ( 1 , out var v1Again) || v1Again != \"a\" ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected key 1 to still be present\" ); if (!lru. TryGet ( 3 , out var v3) || v3 != \"c\" ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected key 3 to be present with value 'c'\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: LruCache evicted the least-recently-used key (2), not the oldest-inserted key (1), exactly as an LRU cache should.\" ); // Claims NOT verified here (need a real cloud deployment, load test, or multi-service system to observe): // - Absolute throughput figures for a single ASP.NET Core/SQL Server instance, Redis latency, cross-region RTT — these are rough, // environment-dependent reference points, not guarantees; see Further reading: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/browse/ // - The worked designs' end-to-end behavior (saga compensation, outbox delivery, per-channel bulkheads) — see the dedicated // chapters' own Verify sections (Resilience, Messaging & events) for runnable checks of those individual mechanisms. Console . WriteLine ( \"All runtime-checkable claims verified.\" ); public sealed class LruCache < TKey , TValue >( int capacity) where TKey : notnull { private readonly Dictionary < TKey , LinkedListNode <( TKey Key , TValue Value )>> _map = new (capacity); private readonly LinkedList <( TKey Key , TValue Value )> _order = new (); // most recent at the front private readonly Lock _gate = new (); public bool TryGet ( TKey key, out TValue value ) { lock (_gate) { if (_map. TryGetValue (key, out var node)) { _order. Remove (node); _order. AddFirst (node); // mark as most recently used value = node. Value . Value ; return true ; } value = default !; return false ; } } public void Put ( TKey key, TValue value ) { lock (_gate) { if (_map. TryGetValue (key, out var existing)) { _order. Remove (existing); _map. Remove (key); } else if (_map. Count >= capaci"},{"c":"study-plan-resources","s":"tl-dr","h":"TL;DR","t":"# TL;DR Rereading feels like learning but mostly tests recognition, not recall — active recall (explain it without looking) and spaced repetition actually stick. Follow the eight-week plan roughly in order; the order matters more than the exact pace. Build one practice project (\"Shop\") that exercises every layer — a bug you actually debugged beats a definition recited from memory in an interview. Use the self-assessment checklist to know what \"Beginner/Intermediate/Advanced\" actually means for each topic. Answer interview questions with a structure: definition → why it exists → how it works → trade-offs → your own experience."},{"c":"study-plan-resources","s":"how-to-study-so-it-sticks","h":"How to study (so it sticks)","t":"# How to study (so it sticks) Beginner Reading isn't learning, and it's worth being honest with yourself about why: recognising an explanation when you reread it feels like understanding, but it tests recognition, not recall — the exact skill an interview actually demands. These techniques are backed by learning research and work especially well for interview preparation: Technique How to apply it here Active recall After each section, close the page and explain it out loud or on paper. Use the Question bank in drill mode and answer before revealing Spaced repetition Revisit the drill for each part after 1 day, 3 days, 1 week, 2 weeks. Re-drill questions you got wrong more often Feynman technique Explain a concept as if to a junior developer. Where you hesitate or use jargon you can't unpack, that's the gap: go back to the section Interleaving Mix topics in drills (\"All parts\" mode) instead of doing one chapter at a time; interviews jump between topics Build things Implement each concept in a small project. Writing middleware or an outbox once beats reading about it five times Map placement For every new term, say which of the 8 layers it lives in and what it connects to. This is the \"light bulb\" habit"},{"c":"study-plan-resources","s":"an-eight-week-plan","h":"An eight-week plan","t":"# An eight-week plan Beginner Around 1–2 hours on weekdays, plus a longer weekend session for the project. Adjust to your timeline; the order matters more than the pace. Week Read (Beginner + Intermediate sections first) Build Drill 1 Big Picture , Part I (platform), Type system , OOP , Generics Console app exploring value vs reference types, boxing, records; look at IL in SharpLab Part 0–II 2 Delegates , Collections , LINQ , Exceptions , async/await , Modern C# Re-implement Where / Select with yield ; write an async fan-out with WhenAll + cancellation Part II 3 Hosting , DI , Configuration , Middleware , Routing , Building APIs Start the practice API (below): minimal APIs, DI lifetimes demo, custom middleware, options with validation Part III (first half) 4 Errors & logging , Auth , HttpClient , Caching/rate limiting/CORS , Background work , API design Add ProblemDetails, JWT auth with policies, a typed HttpClient with resilience, HybridCache, rate limiting, a BackgroundService with Channel Part III 5 Part IV: Relational fundamentals , EF Core , Querying , Advanced , Dapper , NoSQL & Redis EF Core with migrations; reproduce and fix an N+1; optimistic concurrency; a Dapper read model; Redis cache Part IV 6 Part V: Principles , Patterns , Architectures , CQRS & Result , DDD , Part VI: testing Refactor the API into Clean Architecture or vertical slices; rich domain model; Result pattern; unit tests + integration tests with Testcontainers Parts V–VI 7 Part VII: Messaging , Resilience , Observability & Aspire , Microservices ; Part VIII: Cloud , Docker Add an Aspire AppHost, a worker service, RabbitMQ/Service Bus emulator + outbox, OpenTelemetry; containerise Parts VII–VIII 8 Azure compute , Azure data , Identity & secrets , CI/CD & IaC , Kubernetes , Part IX, Part X, System design GitHub Actions pipeline; deploy to Azure Container Apps with managed identity + Key Vault (azd/Bicep); load test with k6 Everything, mixed. Do two mock system-design sessions Then: go back through every Advanced section, and keep drilling daily (15 minutes) until the interview."},{"c":"study-plan-resources","s":"the-practice-project-one-app-every-layer","h":"The practice project: one app, every layer","t":"# The practice project: one app, every layer Intermediate Build a small but complete \"Shop\" backend . Each feature exercises specific chapters, and together they give you real stories to tell in interviews (\"When I implemented the outbox, I ran into…\") — which matters more than it sounds, because a concrete bug you actually debugged is a far more convincing answer than a correct definition recited from memory. Feature Exercises Products & categories CRUD with paging, filtering, sorting Minimal APIs, routing groups, validation, EF Core, projections, indexes, API design Cart (Redis) and checkout Redis, HybridCache, idempotency keys, Result pattern, domain model with invariants Orders with status lifecycle DDD aggregate, State pattern / transitions, optimistic concurrency (ETags) Auth: customers and admins JWT bearer (Entra ID or a local dev IdP), policies, resource-based authorization (own orders only) OrderPlaced → email + stock update Outbox, Service Bus/RabbitMQ (MassTransit or Wolverine), idempotent consumers, worker service Calls to a fake payment API Typed HttpClient, resilience handler, WireMock in tests Nightly cleanup of stale carts BackgroundService + PeriodicTimer, or Hangfire Observability OpenTelemetry traces across API → broker → worker, custom metrics, Aspire dashboard Tests Unit tests for the domain, integration tests with WebApplicationFactory + Testcontainers + Respawn, an architecture test Delivery Dockerfile (multi-stage, chiseled), GitHub Actions CI, deploy to Azure Container Apps with Bicep/azd, managed identity to Azure SQL + Key Vault Performance BenchmarkDotNet for one hot method; k6 load test; fix one measured bottleneck Microsoft's eShop reference application (github.com/dotnet/eShop) is a good model to compare against once you've built your own."},{"c":"study-plan-resources","s":"self-assessment-checklist","h":"Self-assessment checklist","t":"# Self-assessment checklist Intermediate # Beginner: can you explain these clearly in 1–2 minutes each? .NET vs .NET Framework vs .NET Core; SDK vs runtime; what the CLR, JIT and IL are Value vs reference types; struct vs class vs record; boxing The four OOP pillars; interface vs abstract class; access modifiers List , Dictionary , HashSet and their Big-O; IEnumerable vs IList LINQ basics and deferred execution ; IEnumerable vs IQueryable Exceptions: throw vs throw ex ; using and IDisposable What async / await does and why not to use .Result The two phases of Program.cs; what Kestrel is DI lifetimes (transient/scoped/singleton) and the captive dependency Configuration precedence; the Options pattern Middleware : what it is, the pipeline, order of the standard middleware Minimal APIs vs controllers; model binding; validation HTTP methods, status codes, idempotency; 401 vs 403 Authentication vs authorization; what a JWT contains SQL joins, indexes, transactions (ACID) EF Core: DbContext lifetime, migrations, tracking SOLID with examples Unit vs integration tests; mocks vs stubs IaaS/PaaS/serverless; what a container is # Intermediate: can you explain, use and compare? Generics, constraints and variance; delegates, closures and events async concurrency ( WhenAll ), cancellation, ConfigureAwait , ValueTask Writing custom middleware; middleware vs filters; endpoint filters ProblemDetails and IExceptionHandler ; structured logging Policy-based and resource-based authorization; cookies vs JWT IHttpClientFactory (why); resilience strategies Caching layers and strategies; stampedes; rate-limiting algorithms; CORS preflight Hosted services, Channels; running a job once across replicas N+1, projections, AsNoTracking , split queries; clustered vs non-clustered indexes; isolation levels EF Core vs Dapper; optimistic concurrency GoF patterns you've used (Strategy, Decorator, Factory, Observer, Chain of Responsibility) Clean Architecture vs vertical slices; repository debate; CQRS; Result pattern Messaging: queues vs topics, commands vs events, outbox, idempotent consumers Observability: logs vs metrics vs traces; OpenTelemetry Docker multi-stage builds; App Service vs Container Apps vs Functions vs AKS; managed identity CI/CD pipeline stages; blue/green vs canary; IaC # Advanced: can you go deep and discuss trade-offs? JIT tiering and PGO; Native AOT trade-offs; source generators vs reflection async state machine internals; SynchronizationContext deadlocks; thread-pool starvation diagnosis GC generations, LOH, server GC/DATAS; diagnosing memory leaks ( gcroot ) Threading primitives, deadlocks, memory model; thread-safe singletons Span<T> / Memory<T> , pooling, allocation-free code The full request lifecycle in detail; endpoint routing internals DDD aggregates and boundaries; domain vs integration events Sagas (orchestration vs choreography); ordering; exactly-once myths; event sourcing Microservices boundaries, data ownership, strangler fig; distributed monolith Cosmos DB partitioning; CAP/PACELC; Service Bus vs Event Grid vs Event Hubs OAuth/OIDC flows (code + PKCE, client credentials, OBO), token validation, BFF OWASP risks and .NET mitigations (IDOR, SSRF, mass assignment) Performance methodology and tools; percentiles; load testing Kubernetes probes, resources, autoscaling; zero-downtime deploys and DB migrations Full system design of an e-commerce checkout or a URL shortener"},{"c":"study-plan-resources","s":"answering-interview-questions-well","h":"Answering interview questions well","t":"# Answering interview questions well Intermediate A reliable structure for technical questions: definition → why it exists → how it works / example → trade-offs or pitfalls → your experience. Q: What's the difference between scoped and singleton services? Definition: \"Singleton means one instance for the app's lifetime; scoped means one per scope, which in ASP.NET Core is one per HTTP request.\" Why: \"Lifetimes match how long state should live and whether it can be shared across threads.\" Example: \"DbContext is scoped because it isn't thread-safe and tracks per-request changes; an HttpClient handler or a cache is singleton.\" Pitfall: \"A singleton depending on a scoped service creates a captive dependency, a classic bug; scope validation catches it in Development, and the fix is IServiceScopeFactory.\" Experience: \"I hit this when a background service injected a DbContext directly…\" More tips: Say \"it depends\", then say on what. Then pick one and justify it. Use the right vocabulary (captive dependency, deferred execution, idempotent, outbox, N+1): it signals depth. Admit gaps honestly , then reason from first principles: \"I haven't used Event Hubs in production, but since it's a partitioned log like Kafka, I'd expect…\" Draw for anything with more than two moving parts: pipelines, request flows, architectures. Prepare 5–6 stories (STAR: Situation, Task, Action, Result) about bugs you fixed, performance you improved, designs you chose, and incidents you handled. Tie them to topics in this book. Ask clarifying questions in design and coding exercises before you start."},{"c":"study-plan-resources","s":"books-this-guide-draws-on","h":"Books this guide draws on","t":"# Books this guide draws on Beginner The structure and depth of this guide follow the standard references. Read these for more than any single guide can give you: Area Books C# & runtime C# in Depth (Jon Skeet) · CLR via C# (Jeffrey Richter, the classic runtime book, Framework era) · C# 12 in a Nutshell (Joseph Albahari) · Pro .NET Memory Management (Konrad Kokosa) · Writing High-Performance .NET Code (Ben Watson) · Concurrency in C# Cookbook (Stephen Cleary) ASP.NET Core ASP.NET Core in Action , 3rd ed. (Andrew Lock): the best single book for Part III · Pro ASP.NET Core (Adam Freeman) Data Entity Framework Core in Action (Jon P Smith) · Designing Data-Intensive Applications (Martin Kleppmann) · SQL Performance Explained / use-the-index-luke.com (Markus Winand) Design & architecture Clean Architecture and Clean Code (Robert C. Martin) · Design Patterns (GoF) · Head First Design Patterns · Patterns of Enterprise Application Architecture (Fowler) · Dependency Injection Principles, Practices, and Patterns (van Deursen & Seemann) · Refactoring (Fowler) DDD Domain-Driven Design (Eric Evans) · Implementing Domain-Driven Design (Vaughn Vernon) · Learning Domain-Driven Design (Vlad Khononov) Distributed systems Building Microservices and Monolith to Microservices (Sam Newman) · Enterprise Integration Patterns (Hohpe & Woolf) · Release It! (Michael Nygard) Testing Unit Testing Principles, Practices, and Patterns (Vladimir Khorikov) · The Art of Unit Testing (Roy Osherove) Delivery & operations Accelerate (Forsgren, Humble, Kim) · Continuous Delivery (Humble & Farley) · Site Reliability Engineering (Google, free online) · Kubernetes Up & Running Interviews System Design Interview Vol. 1–2 (Alex Xu)"},{"c":"study-plan-resources","s":"free-resources","h":"Free resources","t":"# Free resources Beginner Resource Use Microsoft Learn (learn.microsoft.com/dotnet, /aspnet/core, /ef/core, /azure) The authoritative docs every chapter links to; also free learning paths Free Microsoft eBooks .NET Microservices: Architecture for Containerized .NET Applications , Architecting Modern Web Applications with ASP.NET Core and Azure , Cloud-native .NET .NET Blog (devblogs.microsoft.com/dotnet) Release notes; Stephen Toub's performance posts; deep dives Andrew Lock's blog (andrewlock.net) ASP.NET Core internals, explained brilliantly Milan Jovanović , Nick Chapsas , Steve \"Ardalis\" Smith , Jimmy Bogard , Khalid Abuhakmeh , Code Opinion (Derek Comartin) Practical .NET architecture articles and videos dotnet/eShop , Clean Architecture templates (Jason Taylor, Ardalis) Reference code SharpLab.io See IL, JIT asm and lowered C# for any snippet OWASP Cheat Sheet Series Security how-tos Azure Architecture Center Reference architectures, cloud design patterns AZ-204 learning path Structured Azure developer curriculum (certification optional) Exercism C# track , LeetCode (for algorithm rounds) Practice"},{"c":"study-plan-resources","s":"keeping-this-guide-up-to-date","h":"Keeping this guide up to date","t":"# Keeping this guide up to date Beginner .NET ships every November. When a new version arrives, check the \"What's new in .NET / C# / ASP.NET Core / EF Core\" pages on Microsoft Learn, then update the relevant chapters. The site is generated from Markdown files in content/ : edit a chapter, run node tools/build.mjs , and refresh. Q How should I prepare for a .NET backend interview in the last week? Drill the question bank daily in mixed mode, answering out loud before revealing. Re-read the Beginner sections of DI, middleware, async/await, EF Core querying and API design (the most-asked topics). Rehearse the request-lifecycle walkthrough and two system designs out loud. Prepare 5–6 STAR stories from your work or practice project. Review the job description's specific technologies (e.g. Service Bus, AKS) in their chapters. Q How do you answer a question you don't fully know? Be honest about the gap, connect it to what you do know, and reason from first principles out loud, e.g. \"I haven't used X directly, but it's a partitioned log like Kafka, so I'd expect ordering per partition and consumers tracking offsets.\" Interviewers value structured reasoning and honesty over bluffing."},{"c":"study-plan-resources","s":"further-reading","h":"Further reading","t":"# Further reading .NET documentation hub ASP.NET Core documentation .NET architecture guides (free eBooks) Exam AZ-204: Developing Solutions for Microsoft Azure"},{"c":"study-plan-resources","s":"verify-this-chapter","h":"Verify this chapter","t":"# Verify this chapter This chapter is mostly study advice and a reading list, which isn't something code can verify. One concrete, checkable claim it does make — in the sample interview answer about scoped vs singleton services — is that a singleton capturing a scoped dependency (\"captive dependency\") is a real bug, and that DI's scope validation catches it. Run with dotnet new console -o studyplancheck && cd studyplancheck && dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection , replace Program.cs , then dotnet run : C# Run ▶ Copy using Microsoft . Extensions . DependencyInjection ; // Reproduces the \"captive dependency\" bug from the sample interview answer above, with real DI. var services = new ServiceCollection (); services. AddScoped < IScopedThing , ScopedThing >(); services. AddSingleton < SingletonCaptor >(); // With scope validation ON (the default in ASP.NET Core's \"Development\" environment), // resolving a singleton that captures a scoped service throws immediately. using var providerWithValidation = services. BuildServiceProvider ( new ServiceProviderOptions { ValidateScopes = true }); bool threw = false ; try { providerWithValidation. GetRequiredService < SingletonCaptor >(); } catch ( InvalidOperationException ) { threw = true ; } if (!threw) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected ValidateScopes=true to catch the captive dependency\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: a singleton capturing a scoped service throws InvalidOperationException when ValidateScopes=true, confirming the captive-dependency claim.\" ); // Without validation, it silently succeeds — but the \"scoped\" instance is captured forever, // defeating the whole point of a per-request lifetime. using var providerNoValidation = services. BuildServiceProvider ( new ServiceProviderOptions { ValidateScopes = false }); var captor1 = providerNoValidation. GetRequiredService < SingletonCaptor >(); using ( var scope = providerNoValidation. CreateScope ()) { var scopedHere = scope. ServiceProvider . GetRequiredService < IScopedThing >(); if (captor1. Scoped . Id == scopedHere. Id ) throw new Exception ( \"FAIL: expected the captured instance to differ from a freshly-scoped instance\" ); } Console . WriteLine ( \"OK: without validation, the singleton silently keeps the first scoped instance forever instead of getting a fresh one per scope — the real-world bug behind the interview answer.\" ); Console . WriteLine ( \"All runtime-checkable claims verified.\" ); public interface IScopedThing { Guid Id { get ; } } public sealed class ScopedThing : IScopedThing { public Guid Id { get ; } = Guid . NewGuid (); } public sealed class SingletonCaptor ( IScopedThing scoped ) { public IScopedThing Scoped { get ; } = scoped ; } Everything else in this chapter (the study schedule, book recommendations, interview-answer structure) is advice, not a falsifiable technical claim — treat the book/resource list's descriptions as this author's opinion, not something to fact-check, and go to the linked sources for anything you want to verify directly."}],"built":"2026-10-28T10:16:59.316Z"};
